window.XFULL=[["sec","cl:t-abs~s1-1","Definition","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Overview","Inflammation of the nose and paranasal sinuses; chronic disease is covered in Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps . The term rhinosinusitis is preferred, because the nasal mucosa is always involved too."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s1-2","Classification by time","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Overview","Classification of rhinosinusitis Type Duration Acute Under 12 weeks, with complete resolution (older American texts: under 4 weeks) Chronic 12 weeks or more Recurrent acute 4 or more episodes a year, with symptom-free intervals Acute on chronic Sudden worsening of chronic disease, returning to baseline EPOS 2020 dropped the term \"subacute\". Within acute rhinosinusitis it separates three phases: Common cold (acute viral rhinosinusitis): symptoms for less than 10 days. Acute post-viral rhinosinusitis: symptoms that worsen after five days or persist beyond ten. Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis: at least three of discoloured discharge (often one-sided), severe local pain (often one-sided), fever above 38 °C, raised ESR or CRP, and \"double sickening\" (worsening after initial improvement). Severe symptoms from the start (high fever and purulent discharge) also suggest it. Only 0.5 to 2% of viral episodes become bacterial, and discoloured mucus alone does not mean bacteria."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s1-3","Symptoms","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Overview","Nasal blockage, anterior or posterior discharge, facial pain or pressure, and reduced smell, with cough, fever, fatigue, dental pain, halitosis, headache or ear fullness."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s2-1","Drainage","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Applied anatomy","The frontal, anterior ethmoid and maxillary sinuses drain into the middle meatus through the ostiomeatal complex ; the posterior ethmoids drain into the superior meatus and the sphenoid into the sphenoethmoidal recess. Mucociliary clearance in each sinus follows a fixed path towards its natural ostium. Figure 1 First, a coronal section showing the maxillary and ethmoid sinuses in relation to the orbit and nasal cavity. Second, mucociliary clearance patterns of the sinuses. Dhingra, figs 35.1 and 35.4 Figure 2 The ostiomeatal complex in coronal section, and the drainage of each sinus into its meatus. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-abs~s2-2","Development","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Applied anatomy","The maxillary and ethmoid sinuses are present at birth; the frontal sinus appears around the sixth year and the sphenoid later in childhood. So ethmoiditis is the sinusitis of infants, and frontal sinusitis an adult disease."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s2-3","The thin walls","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Applied anatomy","The ethmoids are separated from the orbit only by the paper-thin lamina papyracea , and the frontal, ethmoid and sphenoid sinuses all border the anterior cranial fossa. This is why complications go to the eye and the brain."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s3-1","Acute viral rhinosinusitis","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Pathogenesis","Rhinoviruses, influenza and parainfluenza viruses, spread by droplets, with an incubation of one to four days. Congestion, rhinorrhoea, sneezing and low fever improve within seven to ten days. Figure 3 Pathophysiology of acute viral rhinosinusitis. Dhingra, fig. 36.1"],["sec","cl:t-abs~s3-2","Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Pathogenesis","The virus damages cilia and epithelium; oedema blocks the ostia; secretions stagnate and bacteria multiply: Streptococcus pneumoniae , Haemophilus influenzae , Moraxella catarrhalis and Staphylococcus aureus , with rising resistance to penicillin and macrolides. Anaerobes accompany dental infection. Streptococcus anginosus (milleri group) is the commonest organism in complicated sinusitis in older children and adolescents, with a tendency to form intracranial abscesses. Figure 4 The sequence from acute viral to acute bacterial rhinosinusitis. Dhingra, fig. 36.2"],["sec","cl:t-abs~s3-3","Predisposing factors","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Pathogenesis","Viral infections, swimming and diving, dental infection, trauma, a deviated septum or concha bullosa, polyps, allergy, adenoids, immune deficiency and cystic fibrosis."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s4-1","Acute maxillary sinusitis","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Clinical picture by sinus","4.1.1 Causes Spread from a cold; swimming in contaminated water; dental infection from the premolar and molar roots, which lie close to the sinus floor, or a root pushed into the sinus, or an oroantral fistula after extraction; trauma. 4.1.2 Clinical features Fever and malaise; pain over the cheek, often referred to the teeth or gums (so the patient may see a dentist first), worse on stooping, coughing or chewing; tenderness over the anterior wall; redness and swelling of the cheek in children; pus in the middle meatus and postnasal discharge. 4.1.3 Complications Chronic sinusitis, frontal sinusitis, osteomyelitis of the maxilla, and orbital cellulitis."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s4-2","Acute frontal sinusitis","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Clinical picture by sinus","4.2.1 Clinical features Severe frontal headache with typical periodicity: it starts on waking, peaks around midday and fades by evening (the office headache ). Exquisite tenderness on pressing upwards on the sinus floor above the medial canthus; upper lid oedema; a streak of pus high in the middle meatus. 4.2.2 Complications Orbital cellulitis, osteomyelitis of the frontal bone, meningitis, extradural and frontal lobe abscess, and chronic sinusitis."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s4-3","Acute ethmoiditis","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Clinical picture by sinus","4.3.1 Clinical features The sinusitis of infants and young children. Pain over the bridge of the nose and deep to the eye, worse on eye movement; swelling of both lids; a swollen middle turbinate. Orbital cellulitis comes early."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s4-4","Acute sphenoiditis","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Clinical picture by sinus","4.4.1 Clinical features Rarely isolated. Headache at the occiput or vertex, sometimes referred to the mastoid, with postnasal discharge seen on the roof of the nasopharynx. Isolated sphenoid opacity needs a mucocele or tumour excluded."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s5-1","Examination","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Diagnosis","Nasal endoscopy shows pus in the middle meatus; a swab from the middle meatus can be cultured."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s5-2","Imaging","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Diagnosis","Not needed for uncomplicated acute disease. CT of the sinuses (with contrast and of the orbits and brain) is requested when a complication is suspected or treatment fails. Plain radiographs (Waters' view) and transillumination are historical."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s6-1","Viral rhinosinusitis","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Management","Symptomatic: saline, analgesics (non-aspirin), short-term decongestants and fluids. Antibiotics are not needed. For post-viral symptoms beyond ten days, a nasal steroid spray helps."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s6-2","Bacterial rhinosinusitis","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Management","Analgesics: NSAIDs or paracetamol. Antibiotics: watchful waiting is reasonable in mild disease with a safety net. Antibiotics are greatly overused (given to over 90% of patients in UK primary care in some series), and early antibiotics do not prevent complications; keep them for severe or clearly bacterial disease. Doxycycline or a respiratory quinolone in penicillin allergy. Saline irrigation. Intranasal steroids: reduce oedema and shorten the illness. Decongestants: topical for a few days only; oral ones where safe. Antihistamines: only with coexisting allergy, as they thicken mucus. Steam inhalation: symptomatic relief. Where the books differ · First-line antibiotic Textbooks and the American guideline: Amoxicillin, with or without clavulanate, for five to ten days. NICE (NG79, 2017): Phenoxymethylpenicillin for five days; a back-up prescription for symptoms beyond ten days, and an immediate antibiotic only for the systemically very unwell or those at high risk. In practice: Follow local guidance; in the UK, phenoxymethylpenicillin for five days when an antibiotic is needed."],["ev","cl:t-abs~s6-3","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications: current evidence","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Management","Guidelines. EPOS 2020 recommends symptomatic treatment for the common cold and post-viral rhinosinusitis, nasal steroids for post-viral symptoms, and antibiotics only for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis (Fokkens et al., Rhinology 2020). NICE (NG79, 2017) advises no antibiotic for symptoms under ten days, and a nasal steroid for symptoms beyond ten days. The American guideline allows watchful waiting for uncomplicated bacterial sinusitis with a safety net (Rosenfeld et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2015). Antibiotics. A Cochrane review of 15 trials in adults found that antibiotics cure about one extra patient in 19 at 7 to 14 days, but cause side effects in one in eight (Lemiengre et al., 2018). Nasal steroids. A Cochrane review found a modest benefit in symptom resolution, greater with higher doses (Zalmanovici Trestioreanu and Yaphe, 2013)."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s6-3","Surgery for acute disease","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Management","6.3.1 Maxillary Antral washout is rarely needed now; endoscopic middle meatal antrostomy if drainage is required. 6.3.2 Frontal If pain, fever or lid swelling worsen despite 48 hours of treatment, trephine the frontal sinus through a short incision below the medial end of the eyebrow, take pus for culture, and irrigate through a tube until methylene blue reaches the nose. Endoscopic frontal drainage is the modern alternative. Figure 5 Trephination of the right frontal sinus through its floor. Dhingra, fig. 36.3 6.3.3 Ethmoid Drain an orbital abscess and the ethmoids, endoscopically or through an external ethmoidectomy."],["sec","cl:t-abs~c7","Complications","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Complications","Local, orbital and intracranial. Figure 6 Complications of sinusitis. Dhingra, fig. 38.1"],["sec","cl:t-abs~s7-1","Local complications","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Complications","7.1.1 Mucocele and pyocele An expanding, mucus-filled sinus, from long-standing ostial obstruction, that slowly thins and erodes its bony walls; infected, it is a pyocele (mucopyocele). Frequency: frontal, ethmoid, maxillary, sphenoid. Frontal: a non-tender cystic swelling above the medial canthus (90%), pushing the eye forward, down and out; sometimes a forehead swelling; egg-shell crackling. Ethmoid: expands the medial orbital wall and bulges into the middle meatus. Sphenoid: presents with superior orbital fissure or orbital apex syndrome and proptosis. Treatment is endoscopic marsupialisation into the nose, which has replaced external operations ( Nose and sinus operations: principles ). Figure 7 First, a frontal mucocele presenting above the left medial canthus. Second, CT of a frontoethmoid mucocele displacing the eye down and out. Dhingra, figs 38.2 and 38.3 7.1.2 Retention cyst A cyst of an obstructed mucous gland, usually in the maxillary sinus, found incidentally; it does not erode bone and needs no treatment. 7.1.3 Osteomyelitis Maxilla: in infants, because the anterior wall contains spongy bone; cheek swelling, lid oedema and fistulae in the infraorbital region, alveolus or palate, with risk to tooth buds. Frontal bone: in adults; pus under the pericranium forms a soft, doughy forehead swelling, Pott's puffy tumour , and often an extradural abscess beneath. Antibiotics, drainage, trephination and removal of sequestra. Figure 8 First, osteomyelitis of the maxilla with an infraorbital fistula. Second, chronic frontal sinusitis with a fistula in the floor of the sin"],["sec","cl:t-abs~s7-2","Orbital complications","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Complications","Most come from the ethmoids, through the lamina papyracea or by thrombophlebitis of the ethmoidal veins. Other causes of proptosis are covered in Proptosis and the orbit . Chandler classification Stage Name Features I Preseptal cellulitis Lid oedema; vision and eye movements normal II Orbital cellulitis Diffuse orbital oedema, proptosis, chemosis, restricted movement III Subperiosteal abscess Pus between bone and periosteum; eye displaced away from the abscess IV Orbital abscess Pus within the orbit; severe proptosis, ophthalmoplegia, falling vision V Cavernous sinus thrombosis Spread to the cavernous sinus; signs in both eyes Figure 9 Orbital complications: (A) normal, (B) subperiosteal abscess, (C) orbital abscess. Dhingra, fig. 38.7 7.2.1 Displacement by the source An ethmoid subperiosteal abscess pushes the eye forward, down and out; a frontal one, down and out; a maxillary one, up and forward. 7.2.2 Orbital apex and superior orbital fissure syndromes Posterior ethmoid or sphenoid infection: deep orbital pain and paralysis of the sixth, third and fourth nerves (superior orbital fissure syndrome), with loss of vision and of V2 sensation in the orbital apex syndrome. The swollen eye Check visual acuity, colour vision, pupils, eye movements and proptosis. Any loss of vision, colour vision or movement, or a relative afferent pupil defect: urgent contrast CT and surgical drainage. Involve ophthalmology early; examine both eyes for cavernous sinus spread. 7.2.3 Treatment Admission, intravenous antibiotics and nasal decongestion; contrast CT. Drain a subperiosteal or orbital a"],["sec","cl:t-abs~s7-3","Intracranial complications","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Complications","Meningitis, extradural, subdural and brain (frontal lobe) abscess, and cavernous sinus thrombosis, from the frontal, ethmoid and sphenoid sinuses. 7.3.1 Cavernous sinus thrombosis Spread through the valveless ophthalmic veins from the ethmoids, sphenoid, orbit, the danger area of the face, or the pharynx. Abrupt onset with rigors; both eyes become involved; chemosis and proptosis; sequential palsies of the third, fourth and sixth nerves up to total ophthalmoplegia; a fixed, dilated pupil; reduced V1 sensation; papilloedema. Diagnosed with contrast CT or MRI. Orbital cellulitis or cavernous sinus thrombosis? Orbital cellulitis Cavernous sinus thrombosis Source Usually ethmoid sinus Nose, sinuses, orbit, face, pharynx, ear Onset Gradual Abrupt, with rigors Eyes One Spreads to both Chemosis and proptosis Moderate Marked Cranial nerves III, IV, VI Movement limited by swelling Progressive palsies Pupil Usually normal Dilated, fixed General state Unwell Toxic, very ill"],["sec","cl:t-abs~s7-4","Descending and focal infections","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · Complications","Infected discharge running down the pharynx causes pharyngitis, laryngitis and bronchitis; sinus infection can also act as a focus for distant disease."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s8-1","On call","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · For the examination","Swollen eye with sinusitis Check vision, colour vision, pupils, movements and proptosis, and examine the other eye. Admit; start intravenous antibiotics and nasal decongestion. Contrast CT of the sinuses, orbits and brain. Involve ophthalmology; drain an abscess if vision is threatened or there is no improvement. Headache, drowsiness or meningism: image the brain and involve neurosurgery."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s8-2","Viva essentials","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Definitions by duration, and when to diagnose bacterial infection. Organisms and first-line antibiotic. Features of each sinus, including the office headache. Frontal trephination. Chandler's classification and the direction of eye displacement. Mucocele and Pott's puffy tumour. Orbital cellulitis versus cavernous sinus thrombosis."],["sec","cl:t-abs~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications · For the examination","Common pitfalls Antibiotics for every cold. Calling an orbital abscess preseptal cellulitis without checking vision and movement. Missing the extradural abscess beneath a Pott's puffy tumour."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s1-1","Anatomy","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · The adenoid","The adenoid lies at the junction of the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx, as vertical ridges of lymphoid tissue separated by deep clefts. It is covered by ciliated columnar, stratified squamous and transitional epithelium and, unlike the palatine tonsil, has no crypts and no capsule . Figure 1 An adenoid mass after removal with a curette: ridges of lymphoid tissue separated by deep clefts. Dhingra, fig. 48.1 Figure 2 The nasopharynx in sagittal section, and the adenoids compared with the palatine tonsils. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s1-2","Growth and regression","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · The adenoid","Present at birth, it enlarges physiologically to a peak at about three to seven years, regresses from puberty and has largely gone by twenty; allergy or infection can keep it large beyond fifteen. Figure 3 Size of the adenoid with age: maximum at six to seven years, regressing at puberty; persistence beyond fifteen with allergy or infection. Dhingra, fig. 48.2"],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s1-3","Supply","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · The adenoid","Arteries: ascending palatine (facial), ascending pharyngeal, the pharyngeal branch of the maxillary artery, and the ascending cervical branch of the inferior thyroid. Lymph: to the upper jugular nodes, directly or through the retropharyngeal and parapharyngeal nodes. Nerves: glossopharyngeal and vagus, which explains the earache of adenoiditis."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s2-1","Causes","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · Adenoid hypertrophy","Physiological enlargement in childhood, a general tendency to lymphoid hyperplasia, recurrent rhinitis, sinusitis and tonsillitis, and allergy."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s2-2","Clinical features","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · Adenoid hypertrophy","2.2.1 Nasal Obstruction: the commonest symptom, with mouth breathing; infants struggle to feed because they cannot breathe and suck at once, and fail to thrive. Discharge: secretions cannot drain back through the blocked choanae, and chronic rhinitis adds to them: the \"wet, bubbly nose\". Sinusitis: chronic maxillary sinusitis and adenoid infection feed each other. Epistaxis when the adenoid is inflamed. Voice: hyponasal ( rhinolalia clausa ), losing the nasal consonants. 2.2.2 Aural Eustachian tube obstruction: a retracted drum and conductive deafness. Recurrent acute otitis media from spread of infection up the tube. Otitis media with effusion: the adenoid is an important cause; the fluctuating size gives a fluctuating hearing loss. Chronic suppurative otitis media that will not settle while the adenoid is infected. 2.2.3 General Adenoid facies: an elongated face with a dull expression, open mouth, prominent crowded upper teeth, short upper lip, pinched nose from disuse of the alae, and a high-arched palate because the tongue no longer moulds it. Sleep-disordered breathing: snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea, with daytime tiredness and poor concentration ( aprosexia ). Pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale with long-standing severe obstruction. Figure 4 Adenoid facies: the open mouth of the mouth breather. Dhingra, fig. 48.3 Size is relative A large adenoid in a large nasopharynx may cause no symptoms; a modest one in a small nasopharynx can block it completely. Judge the airway, not the adenoid."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s2-3","Diagnosis","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · Adenoid hypertrophy","Flexible nasendoscopy in a cooperative child: the best view of the adenoid, the choanae and the tube openings, without radiation. Lateral soft tissue radiograph of the nasopharynx: shows the adenoid and the remaining airway; useful in children who will not tolerate a scope, though crying lifts the palate and can exaggerate the size. Mirror examination is possible in some older children. Examine the whole nose for other causes of obstruction, and the ears with tympanometry and audiometry. Figure 5 Enlarged adenoids (arrows) on a lateral radiograph of a seven-year-old girl, leaving very little airway. Dhingra, fig. 48.4 Figure 6 Endoscopic grading of adenoids. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s2-4","Treatment","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · Adenoid hypertrophy","2.4.1 Medical For milder symptoms: intranasal steroid sprays, with montelukast in some children, treatment of allergy, saline and decongestant drops for short periods, and breathing exercises. Many children improve as the adenoid regresses. 2.4.2 Adenoidectomy For marked symptoms. The traditional indications: Adenoid hypertrophy causing snoring, mouth breathing, sleep apnoea ( Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea ) or hyponasal speech. Recurrent rhinosinusitis. Otitis media with effusion associated with adenoid hyperplasia, usually with grommets; NICE now advises considering it alongside grommets unless there is a palatal abnormality ( Otitis media: acute and with effusion ). Recurrent ear discharge in chronic otitis media associated with adenoiditis. Dental malocclusion: adenoidectomy does not correct it but helps prevent relapse after orthodontic treatment. Contraindications Cleft palate or submucous cleft: removing the adenoid that helps close the velopharynx causes velopharyngeal insufficiency ( Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency ). Look for a bifid uvula, a notch in the hard palate and a translucent midline zone ( zona pellucida ). Bleeding disorders. Acute upper respiratory infection."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s3-1","Technique","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · The operation","General anaesthesia with an oral tube; the same position as tonsillectomy, but never hyperextend the neck. With the Boyle–Davis gag in place, the nasopharynx is examined by retracting the palate and by palpation (feeling also for a submucous cleft). An adenoid curette with a guard is passed until it touches the back of the septum, pressed back and swept down, with the head slightly flexed to protect the atlas; lateral tags are removed with smaller curettes or punch forceps, taking care of the Eustachian tube openings. Bleeding is stopped by packing, with diathermy for persistent points; rarely a postnasal pack is left for 24 hours. When the tonsils are also to come out, the adenoid is removed first and the nasopharynx packed. Figure 7 Adenoidectomy with the curette. Dhingra, fig. 95.1"],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s3-2","Endoscopic techniques","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · The operation","Suction diathermy, a microdebrider or coblation under endoscopic or mirror vision remove the adenoid more completely, including tissue around the tube openings and choanae."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s3-3","Complications","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · The operation","Haemorrhage: usually primary; watch for a rising pulse and the vomiting of dark swallowed blood. A postnasal pack under general anaesthesia may be needed. Injury to the Eustachian tube openings. Injury to the pharyngeal muscles and cervical spine from hyperextension or pressure; 10 to 20% of children with Down syndrome have atlantoaxial instability. Grisel syndrome: neck pain and torticollis from inflammatory subluxation of the atlantoaxial joint, needing a collar or traction. Velopharyngeal insufficiency: hypernasal speech and nasal regurgitation, usually transient, but persistent with an unrecognised submucous cleft. Nasopharyngeal stenosis from scarring. Regrowth of tissue left behind; biopsy recurrent regrowth to exclude lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s4-1","Acute nasopharyngitis","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · Other nasopharyngeal inflammations","Part of a common cold or influenza, or a bacterial infection (streptococcus, pneumococcus, Haemophilus ). Dryness and burning behind the nose, then pain and difficulty in swallowing; in severe cases fever and nodes. The mucosa is red, swollen and covered in exudate. Most settle with analgesia; antibiotics for severe bacterial infection; decongestant drops for the associated adenoiditis of children."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s4-2","Chronic nasopharyngitis","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · Other nasopharyngeal inflammations","Adults with chronic nasal, sinus and pharyngeal infection, heavy smokers and drinkers, and those exposed to dust and fumes. Postnasal discharge and crusting, constant hawking and snorting back of secretions; congested mucosa with mucopus or crusts. Treat the source, stop smoking and drinking, and use alkaline douches and steam inhalation."],["ev","cl:t-adenoid~s4-3","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis: current evidence","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · Other nasopharyngeal inflammations","Otitis media. An individual patient data meta-analysis of ten trials found that adenoidectomy, with or without grommets, gives a small benefit in children with recurrent acute otitis media or glue ear, mainly in children aged two years and over, and not as a first operation in younger children (Boonacker et al., Health Technol Assess 2014). NICE (NG233, 2023) now advises considering adenoidectomy alongside grommets for glue ear, unless there is a palatal abnormality, because it reduces the need for repeat surgery. Sleep-disordered breathing. Adenotonsillectomy is first-line for children with obstructive sleep apnoea and enlarged tonsils; adenoidectomy alone suits those with small tonsils, and partial (intracapsular) tonsillectomy is increasingly used in young children. Medical treatment. A Cochrane review found that intranasal steroids may improve nasal obstruction symptoms and adenoid size in the short term, with limited, low-quality evidence (Chadha et al., 2009); montelukast may help, but carries a warning about neuropsychiatric side effects."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s4-3","Thornwaldt disease","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · Other nasopharyngeal inflammations","Infection of the pharyngeal bursa , the midline recess within the adenoid marking the embryonic attachment of the notochord. Persistent postnasal discharge with crusting. Nasal obstruction, Eustachian tube blockage and otitis media with effusion. A dull occipital headache, recurrent sore throat and low fever. A cystic, fluctuant midline swelling sits on the posterior nasopharyngeal wall. Antibiotics, then marsupialisation and removal of the lining. Asymptomatic Thornwaldt cysts found on MRI need no treatment. The adult with a one-sided nasopharyngeal problem An adult with unilateral otitis media with effusion, a nasopharyngeal mass or a neck node is examined and, if needed, biopsied for nasopharyngeal carcinoma ( Nasopharyngeal carcinoma ). Adenoid tissue in an adult is unusual; consider HIV and lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s5-1","Viva essentials","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Adenoid versus palatine tonsil: no crypts, no capsule. Symptoms under nasal, aural and general headings; adenoid facies. Lateral radiograph and its limits. Indications and contraindications for adenoidectomy, especially submucous cleft. Complications, including Grisel syndrome and atlantoaxial instability in Down syndrome. Thornwaldt disease."],["sec","cl:t-adenoid~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Adenoids and nasopharyngitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Removing the adenoid without feeling the palate for a submucous cleft. Hyperextending the neck of a child with Down syndrome. Calling a nasopharyngeal mass in an adult \"adenoids\" without a biopsy."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s1-1","Classification","Fungal sinusitis · Overview","Fungal rhinosinusitis Non-invasive Fungal ball (mycetoma) a mass in one sinus, healthy host Non-invasive Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis hypersensitivity, polyps, eosinophilic mucin Non-invasive Saprophytic infestation fungal growth on crusts after surgery Invasive Acute fulminant immunocompromised; Mucor, Aspergillus; days Invasive Chronic invasive slow, months; often diabetic Invasive Granulomatous invasive immunocompetent; Aspergillus flavus; Sudan, India, Pakistan"],["sec","cl:t-afs~s1-2","Organisms","Fungal sinusitis · Overview","Aspergillus (fumigatus, flavus, niger), the Mucorales ( Mucor , Rhizopus ), dematiaceous fungi ( Alternaria , Bipolaris , Curvularia ) and, rarely, Candida , Histoplasma and Blastomyces ."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s1-3","Mucor or Aspergillus?","Fungal sinusitis · Overview","Mucor or Aspergillus? Mucor Aspergillus Hyphae Broad Narrow Septa Rarely septate Always septate Branching At right angles At acute angles, about 45 degrees Typical disease Rhino-orbito-cerebral invasion, black necrosis Fungal ball, allergic disease, chronic invasive disease Figure 1 Aspergillus: septate hyphae branching at acute angles (arrow), on H&E and silver stain. Dhingra, fig. 37.3"],["sec","cl:t-afs~s2-1","Picture","Fungal sinusitis · Fungal ball","A dense, cheesy or greyish-black mass of fungal hyphae, usually in one maxillary sinus (then sphenoid, ethmoid, frontal), in an otherwise healthy, often older patient. Symptoms are those of one-sided chronic sinusitis, or none."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s2-2","CT","Fungal sinusitis · Fungal ball","A single opacified sinus with a hyperdense centre or calcific flecks, and no bone erosion; the wall may be thickened."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s2-3","Treatment","Fungal sinusitis · Fungal ball","Endoscopic removal of the ball and a wide opening of the sinus. No antifungal drugs are needed."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s3-1","What it is","Fungal sinusitis · Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis","A hypersensitivity (type I and III) reaction to fungi growing in the sinus mucin, without tissue invasion, in immunocompetent, often young, atopic patients; commoner in warm, humid climates."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s3-2","Bent and Kuhn criteria","Fungal sinusitis · Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis","Type I hypersensitivity to fungi. Nasal polyposis. Characteristic CT findings. Eosinophilic mucin without fungal invasion of tissue. Positive fungal stain of the mucin."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s3-3","Clinical picture","Fungal sinusitis · Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis","Gradual, often one-sided polyposis, thick, dark, peanut-butter mucin, and slow expansion of the sinuses that can displace the eye (proptosis, telecanthus) or erode the skull base. The mucin contains eosinophils, Charcot-Leyden crystals and scattered hyphae. Figure 2 Proptosis and lateral displacement of the eye from allergic fungal polyposis. Dhingra, fig. 41.2"],["sec","cl:t-afs~s3-4","Imaging","Fungal sinusitis · Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis","CT shows expanded, opacified sinuses with heterogeneous high-density material (the double density sign), thinning and remodelling of bone. On MRI the mucin is dark on T2."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s3-5","Treatment","Fungal sinusitis · Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis","Complete endoscopic clearance of mucin and polyps with wide drainage, combined with oral steroids before and after surgery, long-term topical steroids and saline irrigation, and close follow-up because recurrence is common. Immunotherapy and antifungals have a limited role."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s4-1","Who gets it","Fungal sinusitis · Acute invasive fungal sinusitis","Patients with uncontrolled diabetes, particularly with ketoacidosis (the acid environment favours Mucor), neutropenia, leukaemia, chemotherapy, organ transplants, HIV, and high-dose steroids. After the second wave of COVID-19, thousands of cases of rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis, popularly called black fungus , followed steroid treatment in diabetics."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s4-2","Mechanism","Fungal sinusitis · Acute invasive fungal sinusitis","The hyphae invade arteries, causing thrombosis and ischaemic necrosis, and spread rapidly along vessels and nerves to the orbit, cribriform plate, cavernous sinus and brain."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s4-3","Clinical picture","Fungal sinusitis · Acute invasive fungal sinusitis","Fever, facial pain or numbness, nasal blockage and bloodstained discharge; then lid swelling, proptosis, ophthalmoplegia and loss of vision; palatal or facial skin discolouration; confusion. Endoscopy shows pale, insensate mucosa early, then black necrotic eschar on the turbinate, septum or palate. Figure 3 Mucormycosis: (A) slight swelling of the right lower lid, (B) dark discolouration of the right palate and alveolus, (C) black necrotic tissue in the right nasal cavity. Dhingra, fig. 28.4 Figure 4 Mucormycosis with cheek and orbital involvement, and destruction of the palate. Dhingra, fig. 28.5"],["sec","cl:t-afs~s4-4","Diagnosis","Fungal sinusitis · Acute invasive fungal sinusitis","Urgent endoscopic biopsy of the middle turbinate or suspicious mucosa, with frozen section and fungal stains (silver, PAS), and culture. Contrast CT and MRI show the extent of soft tissue, orbital and intracranial spread; MRI may show non-enhancing, devitalised mucosa."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s4-5","Treatment","Fungal sinusitis · Acute invasive fungal sinusitis","Suspected invasive fungal sinusitis Urgent endoscopy and biopsy with frozen section. Start high-dose liposomal amphotericin B (about 5 mg/kg/day; the global guideline allows 5 to 10) immediately; monitor renal function. Correct ketoacidosis and glucose; stop or reduce immunosuppression; support neutropenia. Urgent surgical debridement back to bleeding tissue, endoscopic or open, repeated as needed. Involve ophthalmology, infectious diseases and the medical team. Antifungals: liposomal amphotericin B for Mucor; posaconazole or isavuconazole for step-down or intolerance; voriconazole for invasive Aspergillus (ineffective against Mucor). Surgery: debridement may extend to medial or total maxillectomy and orbital exenteration in extensive disease, with later obturator rehabilitation of the palate."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s4-6","Outcome","Fungal sinusitis · Acute invasive fungal sinusitis","Mortality is high, especially with intracranial spread; survival depends on reversing the immune defect and early, complete debridement."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s5-1","Chronic invasive","Fungal sinusitis · Chronic invasive fungal sinusitis","Slowly progressive invasion over months, often in diabetics or patients on long-term steroids, presenting as chronic sinusitis with bone erosion and orbital apex syndrome or skull base spread. Biopsy shows fungal invasion of the submucosa. Surgical debridement and long-term antifungals."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s5-2","Granulomatous invasive","Fungal sinusitis · Chronic invasive fungal sinusitis","In immunocompetent patients in Sudan, India and Pakistan, usually Aspergillus flavus : a slowly enlarging mass of the cheek, orbit or nose with non-caseating granulomas and giant cells. Surgery and long-term azoles."],["ev","cl:t-afs~c6","Fungal sinusitis: current evidence","Fungal sinusitis · Differential diagnosis","Mucormycosis. The global guideline recommends early complete surgical debridement plus high-dose liposomal amphotericin B (5 to 10 mg/kg/day) as first-line treatment, isavuconazole or posaconazole as alternatives and for step-down, and urgent control of the underlying condition, especially diabetic ketoacidosis (Cornely et al., Lancet Infect Dis 2019). Aspergillosis. The American guideline recommends voriconazole as primary treatment for invasive aspergillosis, with surgical debridement for invasive sinus disease (Patterson et al., Clin Infect Dis 2016). Non-invasive disease. Evidence for antifungal drugs and immunotherapy in allergic fungal rhinosinusitis is weak."],["sec","cl:t-afs~c6","Differential diagnosis","Fungal sinusitis · Differential diagnosis","Mimics. Bacterial chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps ( Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps ). Inverted papilloma and sinonasal malignancy (one-sided mass with bone erosion; Sinonasal tumours ). Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and NK/T-cell lymphoma (destructive, crusted nose; Granulomatous disease of the nose ). Mucocele (expansion without the hyperdense mucin)."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s7-1","Viva essentials","Fungal sinusitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Classification: invasive versus non-invasive. Mucor versus Aspergillus hyphae. Fungal ball: CT and treatment without antifungals. Allergic fungal sinusitis: Bent and Kuhn criteria, mucin, double density. Mucormycosis: risk factors, black eschar, amphotericin B and debridement."],["sec","cl:t-afs~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Fungal sinusitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a diabetic with facial numbness and a black turbinate as simple sinusitis. Waiting for culture before starting amphotericin. Giving antifungals for a fungal ball, or omitting steroids in allergic fungal disease."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s1-1","Preoperative assessment","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Planning together","Preparation is part of perioperative medicine : stopping smoking even for a few weeks improves respiratory function and reduces complications; malnourished patients (weight loss over 10% or a BMI under 18.5) benefit from nutritional support before major head and neck surgery; anaemia, diabetes and cardiac disease are optimised; and treatment escalation plans (such as ReSPECT) are discussed. For any patient with airway pathology, the surgeon and anaesthetist review the history, flexible endoscopy findings and imaging together and agree a primary plan with backup plans before induction, including who will provide a surgical airway and where the cricothyroid membrane is (marked beforehand, with ultrasound if the neck is difficult)."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s1-2","Children and the setting of care","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Planning together","Most paediatric ENT surgery, including tonsillectomy, is safely done in district hospitals for children from about two years (younger in high-acuity centres), with tertiary referral for children with major comorbidity, syndromes, severe obstructive sleep apnoea at a young age or extremes of weight. A reinforced laryngeal mask is suitable for most tonsillectomies in children over about three, and dexamethasone (about 0.15 mg/kg) reduces nausea and vomiting after tonsillectomy."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s2-1","The obstructed airway","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · The airway options","There is no single proven method for a patient with upper airway obstruction. Options are awake fibreoptic intubation under topical anaesthesia (often with high-flow nasal oxygen and light sedation), inhalational induction keeping spontaneous breathing, intravenous induction with paralysis when intubation is judged straightforward, and awake tracheostomy under local anaesthesia when the larynx is likely to be impassable, which every head and neck surgeon must be able to perform ( Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation ). The surgeon is scrubbed, with the neck prepared, before induction in any patient at risk. Figure 1 Surface marking of the thyroid and cricoid cartilages and the cricothyroid membrane: mark it before induction. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s2-2","Sharing the airway in endoscopic surgery","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · The airway options","For microlaryngoscopy and pharyngeal surgery the options are: Ways of sharing the airway Technique How it works Points Microlaryngeal tube Small cuffed tube (5.0 to 6.0 mm) Secure airway; obscures the posterior glottis Laser-resistant tube Metal or wrapped tube, saline-filled cuff Required for laser near the airway Jet ventilation (supraglottic or transglottic) High-pressure oxygen through a needle in the laryngoscope or a fine catheter through the glottis Unobstructed view; risk of barotrauma and pneumothorax if outflow is blocked Spontaneous ventilation (tubeless) Total intravenous anaesthesia, topical anaesthesia, patient breathes Good for dynamic airway assessment in children High-flow nasal oxygen (apnoeic) Humidified oxygen at up to about 70 L/min Extends safe apnoea time; best in fit patients for short procedures Jet ventilation needs a clear expiratory path, because gas trapped below an obstruction causes barotrauma; laser-resistant transglottic catheters such as the Hunsaker Mon-Jet allow jetting through a tight lesion with care. Figure 2 Direct laryngoscopy in the sniffing position. Dhingra, fig. 91.1"],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s2-3","Laser safety","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · The airway options","Airway fire needs fuel (the tube or swabs), oxygen and a spark (the laser; Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen ). Prevention is a laser-safe tube with saline in the cuff (ideally coloured, so a breach is obvious), the lowest inspired oxygen possible (under about 30%), no nitrous oxide, wet swabs protecting tissues and the cuff, eye protection for everyone and signs on the doors. If a fire starts, stop the laser and ventilation, remove the tube, pour saline into the airway, ventilate with air by mask, then reintubate and inspect the airway with a bronchoscope."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s3-1","The bleeding tonsil","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Common scenarios","A child or adult who returns with a post-tonsillectomy bleed is hypovolaemic (often more than they look, especially children), has a stomach full of swallowed blood and an airway filling with blood. Resuscitate first with cannulas, fluids and blood, and correct clotting. In theatre, a senior anaesthetist uses a rapid sequence induction in the head-down, lateral or sitting position with two suctions ready and a smaller tube available; the surgeon is scrubbed. After haemostasis, the stomach is emptied with a wide-bore tube and the patient is extubated awake on the side."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s3-2","Nasal and sinus surgery","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Common scenarios","Topical vasoconstriction gives a drier field: cocaine 4% (the often-quoted 200 mg limit has no firm scientific basis), Moffett's solution or adrenaline-soaked pledgets with co-phenylcaine. Total intravenous anaesthesia with propofol and remifentanil, a slightly head-up position and avoidance of tachycardia reduce bleeding, and a second-generation laryngeal mask with spontaneous breathing gives good airway protection and a smooth emergence."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s3-3","Throat packs and extubation","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Common scenarios","A retained throat pack is a \"never event\". The joint Difficult Airway Society, ENT UK and maxillofacial review found little evidence for routine throat packs, so many anaesthetists no longer use them; if one is needed, it is part of the surgical swab count. After intraoral, pharyngeal or airway surgery the patient may have a patent airway but be very difficult to reintubate, so extubation is planned like intubation: awake, with suction, sometimes over an airway exchange catheter, or with a tracheostomy left in place when swelling is expected. Figure 3 Skin incisions for tracheostomy: vertical and transverse. Dhingra, fig. 64.1"],["ev","cl:t-anaes~s3-4","Anaesthesia and the shared airway: current evidence","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Common scenarios","High-flow nasal oxygen. Transnasal humidified rapid-insufflation ventilatory exchange (THRIVE) extended safe apnoea times in patients with difficult airways, allowing tubeless laryngeal surgery (Patel and Nouraei, Anaesthesia 2015). Major complications. The UK fourth national audit project found that head and neck cases made up a large share of airway disasters, often from failure to plan for difficulty and from failed emergency front-of-neck access (Cook et al., Br J Anaesth 2011). Guidelines. The Difficult Airway Society guidelines set out scalpel cricothyroidotomy for \"can't intubate, can't oxygenate\" (Frerk et al., Br J Anaesth 2015) and a structured plan for extubation after airway surgery (Popat et al., Anaesthesia 2012). Tonsillectomy analgesia. Paracetamol and ibuprofen are standard; codeine is contraindicated in children under 12, and under 18 having surgery for sleep apnoea, because of fatal respiratory depression in ultra-rapid metabolisers ( Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess )."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s3-4","Environmental impact","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Common scenarios","Desflurane and nitrous oxide are potent greenhouse gases (desflurane accounts for most of the climate impact of volatile agents), so total intravenous anaesthesia and low-flow techniques are increasingly preferred."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s4-1","Viva essentials","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for NAP4 and why ENT patients are at airway risk. The obstructed airway: awake fibreoptic intubation, inhalational induction and awake tracheostomy; front-of-neck access. Ways of sharing the airway: microlaryngeal tubes, jet ventilation, tubeless techniques and high-flow nasal oxygen. Laser safety and the management of an airway fire. The bleeding tonsil: resuscitation and rapid sequence induction. Throat packs and planned extubation."],["sec","cl:t-anaes~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · For the examination","Common pitfalls Inducing anaesthesia in a patient with an obstructing tumour before the surgeon is scrubbed and the plan agreed. Using jet ventilation without a clear route for expiration. Running high oxygen concentrations during laser surgery. Underestimating blood loss in a child with a bleeding tonsil."],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s1-1","The physiology that matters","Approach to nasal obstruction · How the nose blocks","The nose provides about two-thirds of total airway resistance, and most of it lies at the internal nasal valve , the slit between the septum, the caudal edge of the upper lateral cartilage and the head of the inferior turbinate. It is the narrowest part of the airway, and because resistance rises steeply as the radius falls (Poiseuille's law), a small narrowing here causes a large symptom. The valve is also richly supplied with receptors, so obstruction at this point feels worse than obstruction further back. The mucosa of the turbinates is erectile tissue under sympathetic control. In about 80% of people it congests and decongests alternately on each side in the nasal cycle , every two to six hours, keeping total resistance constant. A patient who notices the cycle, or whose fixed deviation is unmasked by the congested phase, describes alternating blockage. The sensation of airflow is carried by trigeminal cold receptors, not by the volume of air. This is why the subjective complaint and objective measures often disagree, why menthol makes the nose feel clearer, and why a nose that has lost too much turbinate can feel blocked although it is wide open ( empty nose syndrome ). Do not miss Unilateral obstruction with blood-stained discharge, pain, facial swelling, numbness, loose teeth, diplopia or a neck node: sinonasal ( Sinonasal tumours ) or nasopharyngeal ( Nasopharyngeal carcinoma ) malignancy. An adolescent boy with unilateral obstruction and heavy epistaxis: juvenile angiofibroma — do not biopsy in clinic. A child with unilateral foul discharge: foreign body . A newbo"],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s2-1","The questions","Approach to nasal obstruction · History","Ask whether the blockage is one-sided or both, constant or variable, and whether it alternates. Fixed, unilateral obstruction points to the septum, a valve problem, a polyp, a tumour or a foreign body; fluctuating, bilateral obstruction points to rhinitis or chronic rhinosinusitis. Ask about onset (after trauma or surgery, or gradual), and whether the patient sniffs inward and the nostril collapses, which suggests valve collapse. Ask about the company the symptom keeps: sneezing, itch and watery discharge in rhinitis; loss of smell, facial pressure and discoloured discharge in rhinosinusitis; bleeding, pain and crusting in tumours, granulomatous disease and septal perforation; and snoring and mouth breathing. Take a full drug history, including decongestant sprays (rhinitis medicamentosa), antihypertensives, the oral contraceptive pill and recreational cocaine, and ask about asthma, aspirin sensitivity, smoking and occupation. The NOSE score and SNOT-22 quantify the symptom and its effect on life."],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s3-1","The nose","Approach to nasal obstruction · Examination","Look at the external nose from the front, side and below for deviation, a twisted pyramid, a narrow or collapsing ala and a caudal septum dislocated into the nostril. Watch the alae on deep nasal inspiration. Cottle's test , in which the cheek is drawn laterally to open the valve, suggests a valve problem when it improves breathing; a modified version supports the valve from inside with a cotton bud or a curette. Examine the vestibule, the septum and the turbinates with a headlight and speculum, then with an endoscope before and after topical decongestant. Mucosal swelling that shrinks well is reversible (rhinitis); a deviation, a spur or a polyp does not change. The endoscope also reveals middle meatal pus and polyps, the posterior septum, the choanae and the nasopharynx. Figure 2 The lateral nasal wall: turbinates and meatuses. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s3-2","Measuring the airway","Approach to nasal obstruction · Examination","Peak nasal inspiratory flow is the quickest objective test, normally around 120 to 140 L/min in adults. Anterior rhinomanometry measures resistance from flow and pressure and is the reference standard. Acoustic rhinometry maps cross-sectional area along the cavity; a large improvement after decongestion points to mucosal disease. CT is reserved for suspected sinus disease or before surgery, and imaging with MRI for any suspected tumour."],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~c4","Causes","Approach to nasal obstruction · Causes","Inflammatory, structural, and the rest. Causes of nasal obstruction Group Causes Typical clue Inflammatory Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis; rhinitis medicamentosa; chronic rhinosinusitis with or without polyps Bilateral, fluctuating, shrinks with decongestant Septum Deviation, spur, dislocated caudal septum, haematoma Fixed, unilateral or S-shaped Valves Internal valve narrowing (high septal deviation, weak upper laterals, post-rhinoplasty); external valve collapse Worse on inspiration; Cottle positive Turbinates Hypertrophy, concha bullosa Seen on endoscopy, partly reversible Masses Polyps, antrochoanal polyp, inverted papilloma, malignancy, angiofibroma Unilateral, progressive; blood Granulomatous Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, sarcoidosis, cocaine ( Granulomatous disease of the nose ) Crusting, bleeding, perforation Other Adenoids, choanal atresia, foreign body, empty nose syndrome, pregnancy and hypothyroidism Age and context"],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s4-1","Rhinitis and rhinosinusitis","Approach to nasal obstruction · Causes","Allergic rhinitis ( Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis ) affects about one person in five to a quarter of people and chronic rhinosinusitis about one in ten. Allergic rhinitis mainly swells the inferior turbinates and responds well to decongestant; chronic rhinosinusitis affects the middle meatus and responds less. Both are common partners of a deviated septum, and treating the mucosa often settles symptoms without surgery."],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s4-2","The septum and the valves","Approach to nasal obstruction · Causes","Cottle's classification ( Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess ) divides the septum into five areas from the nostril backwards. Deviations in areas 1 to 3, at or in front of the internal valve, cause the most obstruction; deviations further back matter less. A deviation at the valve or the caudal septum is not adequately corrected by a standard septoplasty and needs a functional septorhinoplasty ( Facial plastic surgery ), with spreader grafts for the internal valve and struts or alar grafts for the external valve. Figure 3 First, the types of septal deviation. Second, a C-shaped deviation nearly blocking one side. Dhingra, figs 26.4 and 26.6"],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~c5","Pathway","Approach to nasal obstruction · Pathway","Mucosa or skeleton? The blocked nose 1 Red flags? unilateral with blood, pain or cranial nerve signs endoscopy, MRI and CT, biopsy unless angiofibroma suspected adolescent boy with bleeding contrast imaging first, no biopsy child with unilateral foul discharge remove the foreign body 2 Fixed or fluctuating? bilateral, fluctuating, shrinks with decongestant rhinitis — treat the mucosa smell loss, discharge, polyps chronic rhinosinusitis pathway fixed or unilateral step 3 3 Where is the narrowing? mid or posterior septum septoplasty high or caudal septum, collapsing valve functional septorhinoplasty turbinates medical first, then reduction 4 Still blocked after surgery or with a wide nose? recurrent deviation or valve collapse revision wide cavity with paradoxical obstruction empty nose syndrome — avoid further resection"],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s6-1","Medical treatment","Approach to nasal obstruction · Management","Every patient with an inflammatory component should have a proper trial of saline irrigation and a topical steroid spray, used correctly with the head bent forward and the nozzle aimed away from the septum, for at least six to eight weeks. Antihistamines, allergen avoidance, a leukotriene antagonist in selected patients, and immunotherapy for confirmed allergy follow. Decongestant sprays are limited to a few days, because rebound congestion ( rhinitis medicamentosa ) follows longer use. Chronic rhinosinusitis is treated on its own pathway ( Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps )."],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s6-2","Surgery","Approach to nasal obstruction · Management","Two randomised trials have shown that septoplasty, with or without turbinate reduction, improves nasal obstruction more than continued medical treatment in patients with a deviated septum. Septoplasty corrects mid and posterior deviations through mucoperichondrial flaps, preserving an L-shaped strut of at least 1 cm dorsally and caudally. Turbinate reduction by submucosal diathermy, radiofrequency, outfracture or submucosal resection reduces the turbinates while preserving mucosa; its effect fades with time and it does not replace medical treatment. Functional septorhinoplasty treats the valve and the twisted nose, often through an external approach. Over-resection of the turbinates is avoided because of the risk of empty nose syndrome."],["ev","cl:t-ap_block~s6-3","Approach to nasal obstruction: current evidence","Approach to nasal obstruction · Management","Measuring it. The NOSE scale is a validated five-item score of nasal obstruction and is used to judge benefit from surgery; objective tests (peak nasal inspiratory flow, rhinomanometry, acoustic rhinometry) correlate only loosely with symptoms. Septal surgery. Two randomised trials showed septoplasty, with or without turbinate reduction, to be more effective than medical treatment for obstruction with a deviated septum (van Egmond et al., Lancet 2019; Carrie et al., Health Technol Assess 2024, NAIROS). Turbinates. Mucosa-sparing reduction (submucosal diathermy, coblation, microdebrider) is preferred, because radical turbinectomy risks crusting and empty nose syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s6-3","Children","Approach to nasal obstruction · Management","In a child, adenoidal hypertrophy is the commonest cause of bilateral obstruction, with mouth breathing, snoring and hyponasal speech. Rhinitis is common, and a unilateral foul discharge is a foreign body until proven otherwise. A neonate who is an obligate nasal breather and blocked may have choanal atresia : bilateral atresia causes cyclical cyanosis relieved by crying and needs an oral airway, CT and endoscopic repair, while unilateral atresia presents later with a unilateral discharge. Piriform aperture stenosis , nasolacrimal duct cysts and midline masses (dermoid, glioma, encephalocele) are rarer neonatal causes that need imaging before intervention. Figure 4 An antrochoanal polyp, a cause of unilateral obstruction in older children and young adults. Dhingra, figs 32.1 and 32.2"],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s7-1","Viva essentials","Approach to nasal obstruction · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The internal nasal valve: boundaries and why it matters. Inflammatory versus structural obstruction: the decongestant test. Cottle's test and Cottle's areas. Objective tests: peak nasal inspiratory flow, rhinomanometry, acoustic rhinometry. Septoplasty versus functional septorhinoplasty; the L-strut. Red flags for unilateral obstruction; choanal atresia in the newborn."],["sec","cl:t-ap_block~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to nasal obstruction · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a deviated septum when the obstruction is rhinitis that was never treated. Performing a standard septoplasty for a high or caudal deviation that needs valve surgery. Biopsying a vascular mass in an adolescent boy in clinic. Over-reducing the turbinates."],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s1-1","Mechanisms","Approach to nasal crusting · Why the nose crusts","Crusts form when mucus dries faster than it is cleared. This happens when the mucosa is damaged and replaced by squamous or scarred epithelium, when airflow is abnormally fast or turbulent across one area (the edge of a perforation, a wide postoperative cavity), when the cilia fail, or when the mucosa is inflamed by a granulomatous or vasculitic disease. Crusts then harbour bacteria, cause obstruction, bleeding and odour, and damage the mucosa further. Do not miss Crusting with systemic symptoms (cough, haemoptysis, arthralgia, rash, red eyes, deafness, kidney disease, weight loss): granulomatosis with polyangiitis, EGPA or sarcoidosis — ANCA, ACE, urinalysis, chest imaging, biopsy. Crusting with a perforation or palatal defect in a cocaine user: cocaine-induced midline destructive lesion; levamisole adds a vasculitis. Unilateral crusting with ulceration, a mass or bleeding: NK/T-cell lymphoma, squamous carcinoma ( Sinonasal tumours ). Collapse of the nasal bridge (saddle nose): granulomatosis with polyangiitis, relapsing polychondritis, cocaine, previous surgery."],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s2-1","History","Approach to nasal crusting · Assessment","Ask how long the crusting has lasted, whether it followed surgery, and whether there is bleeding, a whistle, a bad smell (often noticed by others more than by the patient), blockage or smell loss. Ask about digital trauma, nasal sprays and drugs (including long-term decongestants and intranasal cocaine), occupational exposure to chrome compounds and industrial dusts, radiotherapy and nasogastric tubes. A full systems review looks for features of vasculitis, sarcoidosis and tuberculosis, and for chest disease, infertility and childhood infections suggesting ciliary dyskinesia or cystic fibrosis."],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s2-2","Examination and investigation","Approach to nasal crusting · Assessment","Examine the nose with an endoscope, before and after the crusts are softened and removed, looking for perforation, granulations, ulceration, a cobblestoned or yellow nodular mucosa (sarcoidosis), a wide cavity, and synechiae. Look at the palate, the eyes, the ears and the skin. When the cause is not obvious, blood tests include a full blood count, renal function, inflammatory markers, ANCA (c-ANCA directed against proteinase-3 in granulomatosis with polyangiitis, p-ANCA against myeloperoxidase in other vasculitides), serum ACE and calcium, and, as appropriate, syphilis and tuberculosis tests. Urinalysis looks for blood and protein from renal involvement. A biopsy is taken from the edge of active disease, not from the crusts, when malignancy or vasculitis is suspected; it may need repeating, because a single biopsy is often non-diagnostic. CT shows bony destruction and sinus disease. Figure 1 First, a septal perforation. Second, a septum deliberately perforated for an ornament. Dhingra, figs 26.10 and 26.11"],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~c3","Causes","Approach to nasal crusting · Causes","Local and systemic. Causes of nasal crusting Group Causes Clues Environmental and local Dry air, digital trauma, decongestant overuse Anterior septum, picking, Little's area Surgical After sinus surgery or turbinate reduction; empty nose syndrome History of surgery; wide cavity Septal perforation Surgery, trauma, picking, cocaine, chrome workers, vasculitis Whistling, bleeding, crusts around the edge Atrophic rhinitis Primary (ozaena); secondary after surgery, radiotherapy, granulomatous disease Wide cavity, foul crusts, smell loss Vasculitis Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, EGPA ANCA, systemic features, saddle nose Granulomatous Sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, leprosy, syphilis, rhinoscleroma Yellow nodules, systemic features, culture Drugs Cocaine, levamisole Destruction, palatal perforation Malignancy NK/T-cell lymphoma, squamous carcinoma Unilateral, progressive, ulceration Clearance Primary ciliary dyskinesia, cystic fibrosis Lifelong symptoms, chest disease"],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s3-1","Atrophic rhinitis","Approach to nasal crusting · Causes","Described in full in Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis . Primary atrophic rhinitis (ozaena) occurs mainly in women in tropical and low-income settings. The mucosa and turbinates atrophy, the cavities widen, and greenish, foul-smelling crusts form, with anosmia, so the patient does not notice the odour that others do. Klebsiella ozaenae is often cultured. Secondary atrophic rhinitis follows excessive surgery, radiotherapy, trauma and granulomatous disease. Empty nose syndrome is a related condition after over-resection of the turbinates: the cavity is wide and clear, yet the patient feels blocked and short of breath."],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s3-2","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Approach to nasal crusting · Causes","Described in full, with sarcoidosis and the other granulomatous diseases, in Granulomatous disease of the nose . This necrotising vasculitis of small vessels affects the upper airway in most patients and may be limited to it. Nasal features are crusting, bleeding, obstruction, septal perforation and saddle collapse; there may also be otitis media with effusion, sensorineural hearing loss, subglottic stenosis, lung nodules and glomerulonephritis. c-ANCA supports the diagnosis, which is confirmed by biopsy and managed with rheumatology and renal teams."],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s4-1","General measures","Approach to nasal crusting · Management","Regular high-volume saline irrigation , several times a day, is the foundation for every cause. Lubricating ointments or oils and humidification help, and glucose-glycerine drops or warm alkaline douches soften crusts in atrophic rhinitis. Topical antibiotic ointment helps when the crusts are infected or Staphylococcus aureus carriage is a problem. Decongestant and steroid sprays are reviewed and stopped if they worsen crusting."],["ev","cl:t-ap_crust~s4-2","Approach to nasal crusting: current evidence","Approach to nasal crusting · Management","Classification. The ACR/EULAR 2022 classification criteria for GPA give heavy weight to nasal crusting, bloody discharge and septal perforation with a positive PR3-ANCA (Robson et al., Ann Rheum Dis 2022). Cocaine cut with levamisole can produce ANCA positivity that mimics vasculitis."],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s4-2","Specific treatment","Approach to nasal crusting · Management","Septal perforations ( Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess ) that cause symptoms are treated with irrigation, a silicone button or surgical repair. Atrophic rhinitis is treated with irrigation and, for Klebsiella infection, appropriate antibiotics; surgery to narrow the cavity (Young's operation, closing the nostrils for months, or submucosal implants) is reserved for severe cases. Vasculitis and sarcoidosis are treated systemically with steroids and immunosuppression or biologics such as rituximab. Cocaine-related disease requires abstinence before any reconstruction. Malignancy is treated on oncological principles. Nasal crusting 1 Red flags? systemic symptoms, saddle nose or perforation ANCA, ACE, urinalysis, chest imaging, biopsy unilateral ulceration or mass biopsy and imaging 2 Clean and examine perforation perforation pathway wide cavity after surgery secondary atrophic rhinitis or empty nose foul green crusts with wide cavities primary atrophic rhinitis — culture anterior septum only dryness, picking, decongestants 3 Treat saline irrigation for all treat the cause stop drugs that worsen crusting repeat biopsy if disease progresses without a diagnosis Figure 2 A silicone septal button closing a perforation. Dhingra, fig. 26.12"],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to nasal crusting · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Local versus systemic causes of nasal crusting. The work-up: ANCA, ACE, urinalysis, chest imaging and biopsy from the edge. Atrophic rhinitis: primary and secondary, Klebsiella ozaenae, Young's operation. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis in the upper airway. Cocaine and levamisole."],["sec","cl:t-ap_crust~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to nasal crusting · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating vasculitis as chronic rhinosinusitis. Biopsying crusts instead of the active edge. Repairing a perforation in an active cocaine user. Over-resecting turbinates and creating an empty nose."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s1-1","What kind of dizziness?","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · The history","Ask the patient to describe the sensation without using the word \"dizzy\". Vertigo is an illusion of movement, usually spinning, and points to the vestibular system. Disequilibrium is unsteadiness on the feet without a feeling in the head, typical of bilateral vestibular loss, neuropathy and cerebellar disease. Presyncope is light-headedness and a sense of impending faint, typical of orthostatic and cardiac causes. Visual or motion sensitivity , feeling worse in supermarkets, crowds or scrolling screens, suggests migraine, persistent postural-perceptual dizziness or an uncompensated vestibular loss. Vestibular migraine can produce any of these."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s1-2","Timing","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · The history","Timing points to the cause Pattern Typical duration Most likely Episodic, triggered by head position Seconds (under a minute) BPPV Episodic, triggered by pressure, straining or loud sound Seconds Superior canal dehiscence, perilymph fistula Episodic, spontaneous, many brief spells a day Seconds to a minute Vestibular paroxysmia Episodic, spontaneous, with fluctuating hearing, tinnitus and fullness 20 minutes to 12 hours Ménière's disease Episodic, spontaneous, with headache, photophobia or visual aura (though not always) 5 minutes to 72 hours Vestibular migraine Single acute episode, continuous, with nausea and imbalance Days, then slowly recovers Vestibular neuritis (with hearing loss: labyrinthitis); posterior circulation stroke Transient attacks with other brainstem symptoms Minutes Vertebrobasilar TIA Chronic, most days for more than three months Persistent Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness, uncompensated or bilateral vestibular loss, multisensory imbalance of the elderly"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s1-3","Triggers and associations","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · The history","Ask what brings the dizziness on. Rolling over in bed, looking up or bending down suggests BPPV. Standing up suggests orthostatic hypotension. Straining, lifting, coughing or loud noise suggests a third window. Sleep loss, stress, caffeine, menstruation and bright light suggest migraine. Then ask about the ear and the brain. Hearing loss, tinnitus and fullness put the labyrinth at the centre, and fluctuating hearing suggests Ménière's disease. But acute vertigo with sudden hearing loss can also be an anterior inferior cerebellar artery stroke, because that artery supplies both the labyrinth and the lateral pons. Headache, double vision, dysarthria, dysphagia, numbness, weakness and falls point to a central cause. Ask about migraine past and present, motion sickness as a child, anxiety, ototoxic drugs (gentamicin), head injury, recent ear surgery and barotrauma. Central red flags in the dizzy patient Cannot stand or walk unaided during an attack. New headache, especially occipital, or neck pain (vertebral dissection). Diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia, facial numbness, limb weakness or ataxia. Direction-changing, vertical or purely torsional nystagmus, or nystagmus not suppressed by fixation. A normal head impulse test in a patient with acute continuous vertigo and nystagmus. Vascular risk factors with acute vertigo and sudden hearing loss."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s2-1","Eyes and head","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Examination","Look for spontaneous nystagmus with the eyes in the primary position and on gaze to each side, with and without fixation (Frenzel lenses remove fixation). Peripheral nystagmus is horizontal-torsional, direction-fixed with the fast phase away from the affected ear, increases on gaze towards the fast phase (Alexander's law) and is suppressed by fixation. Central nystagmus may change direction with gaze, be purely vertical or torsional, and is not suppressed by fixation. The head impulse test looks at the vestibulo-ocular reflex. The patient fixes on your nose while you turn the head briskly through about 15° to each side. A corrective saccade back to the target after a turn towards one side means that canal is weak. The test of skew , alternate covering of each eye, reveals a vertical refixation in skew deviation, a central sign. Check smooth pursuit and saccades, which are normal in peripheral disease. Figure 1 The head impulse at the bedside and on video: a weak canal shows low gain and catch-up saccades. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s2-2","Positional testing","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Examination","The Dix–Hallpike test looks for posterior canal BPPV. Turn the head 45° towards the test ear, lie the patient back quickly with the head hanging about 20° below horizontal, and watch the eyes for 30 seconds. A positive test gives upbeating, torsional nystagmus with the upper pole of the eye beating towards the lower ear. It starts after a latency of a few seconds, lasts under a minute, reverses on sitting up and fatigues with repetition. The supine roll test , turning the head to each side while lying flat, looks for horizontal canal BPPV, which gives horizontal geotropic or apogeotropic nystagmus. Positional nystagmus that has no latency, does not fatigue, is purely vertical (especially downbeating), or comes without vertigo suggests a central lesion. Figure 2 The Dix–Hallpike manoeuvre. Dhingra, fig. 6.1"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s2-3","The rest of the examination","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Examination","Otoscopy, tuning forks and a fistula test (pressure on the tragus or with a pneumatic speculum) cover the ear. A cranial nerve and cerebellar examination covers the brain: finger–nose, heel–shin, rapid alternating movements, Romberg and tandem gait. Lying and standing blood pressure looks for orthostatic hypotension, defined as a fall of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing (some definitions allow up to ten); a heart rate rising by 30 or more without hypotension suggests postural tachycardia syndrome. Finally, ask the patient to walk."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s3-1","HINTS","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Acute continuous vertigo","In a patient who has been continuously vertiginous for hours or days, with nausea and spontaneous nystagmus, the question is whether this is vestibular neuritis or a stroke in the posterior circulation, which accounts for roughly one in ten such patients. HINTS (Head Impulse, Nystagmus, Test of Skew), done by a trained examiner, is more sensitive for stroke in the first 48 hours than early MRI, which misses small infarcts. A peripheral pattern is reassuring only when all three agree: an abnormal head impulse test (a corrective saccade towards the affected side), direction-fixed horizontal nystagmus, and no skew. Any central feature (a normal head impulse, direction-changing nystagmus or skew) means stroke until proved otherwise. HINTS-plus adds new hearing loss as a central warning, because of anterior inferior cerebellar artery infarcts. Inability to stand unaided is an additional central sign. HINTS in acute continuous vertigo Test Peripheral (neuritis) Central (stroke) Head impulse Abnormal: catch-up saccade Normal Nystagmus Direction-fixed, horizontal-torsional, suppressed by fixation Direction-changing on gaze, vertical or purely torsional Test of skew No vertical refixation Vertical refixation (skew) Plus: hearing Usually normal (labyrinthitis if lost) New loss suggests AICA infarct Gait Leans but can stand Cannot stand unaided"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s3-2","Management","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Acute continuous vertigo","A central pattern needs urgent MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging and the stroke team. A peripheral pattern is managed with short-term vestibular sedatives and antiemetics (prochlorperazine, cyclizine) for no more than a few days, fluids, and early mobilisation with vestibular rehabilitation exercises. A short course of oral steroids may be given early for vestibular neuritis ( Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes ). Sedatives slow compensation if they are continued."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s4-1","BPPV","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Recurrent episodic vertigo","BPPV is the commonest cause of vertigo: brief spinning on head movement, often first noticed on getting out of bed, typically in older adults and after head injury or a period in bed. The posterior canal is affected in about 85 to 90%. The diagnosis is made by the Dix–Hallpike test, and the cure is a repositioning manoeuvre: the Epley (or Semont) manoeuvre for the posterior canal, and the Lempert (barbecue) roll or Gufoni manoeuvre for the horizontal canal. Recurrence is common, so patients can be taught self-treatment. Medication has no role, and atypical or refractory cases need MRI. The disease itself is described in Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes . Figure 3 The Epley manoeuvre for posterior canal BPPV, with the position of the debris in the canal at each step. Dhingra, fig. 7.1"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s4-2","Ménière's disease","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Recurrent episodic vertigo","Definite Ménière's disease (Bárány Society) requires two or more spontaneous episodes of vertigo, each lasting 20 minutes to 12 hours, an audiometrically documented low- to mid-frequency sensorineural loss in the affected ear on at least one occasion, and fluctuating aural symptoms (hearing, tinnitus or fullness) in that ear, not better explained by another diagnosis. An audiogram is essential, and asymmetric sensorineural loss means MRI. Treatment aims to reduce the number and severity of attacks. It runs from acute vestibular sedatives through lifestyle measures (moderation of salt, caffeine and alcohol, though the evidence is weak), betahistine (in widespread use despite a large trial showing no benefit over placebo) and diuretics, to intratympanic steroid, intratympanic gentamicin and, rarely, surgery. Figure 4 (A) The normal cochlear duct. (B) Endolymphatic hydrops: the duct distended and Reissner's membrane bulging. Dhingra, fig. 15.1"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s4-3","Vestibular migraine","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Recurrent episodic vertigo","Vestibular migraine is the commonest cause of spontaneous recurrent vertigo, and it is underdiagnosed because many attacks come without headache. Definite vestibular migraine (Bárány Society and International Headache Society) requires at least five episodes of moderate or severe vestibular symptoms lasting 5 minutes to 72 hours. The patient must have current or previous migraine, and at least half the episodes must carry at least one migrainous feature: a migraine-type headache, photophobia with phonophobia, or a visual aura. There is no confirmatory test, so the diagnosis is clinical. Treatment begins with sleep, regular meals, hydration and the identification of triggers such as caffeine and alcohol. Acute attacks are treated with prochlorperazine and, for headache, a triptan with an NSAID and an antiemetic. Frequent attacks need prophylaxis: propranolol, amitriptyline or nortriptyline, or candesartan. The trial evidence comes from migraine headache rather than vestibular migraine itself, and CGRP antibodies are emerging. Ménière's or vestibular migraine? The two overlap more than the textbooks admit, and some patients have both. Documented, progressive low-frequency hearing loss favours Ménière's disease. Headache, photophobia, visual aura, motion sensitivity, a long migraine history and attacks lasting more than 12 hours or only minutes favour migraine. When in doubt, a trial of migraine prophylaxis is safe and often diagnostic."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s4-4","Less common episodic causes","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Recurrent episodic vertigo","Superior canal dehiscence causes vertigo with straining or loud sound, autophony and pulsatile tinnitus, with low bone conduction thresholds; it is confirmed by VEMPs and CT. Perilymph fistula follows stapes surgery, head injury or barotrauma. Vestibular paroxysmia , from a vascular loop compressing the eighth nerve, causes many brief spells a day and responds to carbamazepine. Episodic ataxia type 2 (CACNA1A) is rare and responds to acetazolamide. Vertebrobasilar TIAs cause brief vertigo with other brainstem symptoms in patients with vascular risk."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s5-1","The causes","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Chronic dizziness","Chronic dizziness usually has one of five causes, alone or in combination. An uncompensated unilateral vestibular loss after neuritis or surgery leaves symptoms on quick head turns. Bilateral vestibular loss , from gentamicin, meningitis, bilateral Ménière's disease or idiopathic causes, gives oscillopsia and unsteadiness in the dark. Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness is non-spinning dizziness and unsteadiness present on most days for more than three months, worse when upright, moving or in busy visual surroundings; it usually follows a vestibular event, migraine or anxiety, examination and tests are normal, and it is treated with vestibular rehabilitation, cognitive behavioural therapy and SSRIs. The multisensory imbalance of the elderly combines vestibular, visual, proprioceptive and musculoskeletal decline with drugs. Central causes , cerebellar degeneration, multiple sclerosis and posterior fossa tumours, are rare but must be sought. Vestibular rehabilitation is the treatment for most chronic vestibular dizziness, and vestibular sedatives should be stopped."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s5-2","Medical causes","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Chronic dizziness","Many dizzy patients have no ear disease at all. Orthostatic hypotension, arrhythmias and aortic stenosis cause presyncope. Hypoglycaemia, anaemia and thyroid disease, drugs (antihypertensives, anticonvulsants, sedatives), anxiety and panic, and hyperventilation complete the list. Hyperventilation causes dizziness without nystagmus, unless the eighth nerve is demyelinated or compressed."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s6-1","Tests that help","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Investigations","A pure tone audiogram is needed for every patient with ear symptoms and is required to diagnose Ménière's disease. MRI of the internal auditory meati and brain is needed for asymmetric hearing loss or tinnitus ( Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours ), central signs, atypical positional nystagmus and suspected stroke. Vestibular tests ( Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory : videonystagmography, calorics, video head impulse test, VEMPs, rotational chair) quantify and localise vestibular loss; they are useful when the history is unclear, before ablative treatment, and in suspected bilateral loss or dehiscence. CT temporal bones is for suspected dehiscence or fistula. An ECG , lying and standing blood pressure, glucose and blood count are for presyncope. The dizzy patient 1 Describe the dizziness and its timing single acute continuous attack go to step 2 recurrent episodes go to step 3 chronic, most days go to step 4 presyncope lying and standing BP, ECG, medical review 2 Acute continuous vertigo: do HINTS-plus any central sign, cannot stand, new headache or neurology urgent MRI-DWI and stroke team all peripheral vestibular neuritis — short sedation, early mobilisation, consider steroids 3 Recurrent episodes: what triggers them? head position, seconds Dix–Hallpike, then Epley spontaneous, 20 minutes to 12 hours, fluctuating hearing audiogram, MRI — Ménière's spontaneous with migrainous features vestibular migraine straining or sound CT, VEMP — dehiscence or fistula 4 Chronic dizziness head-movement related uncompensated loss — vestibular rehabilitation oscillopsia,"],["ev","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s6-2","Approach to dizziness and vertigo: current evidence","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Investigations","Acute vestibular syndrome. The HINTS evidence above comes from Kattah et al. ( Stroke 2009); HINTS also outperformed the ABCD2 score (Newman-Toker et al., Acad Emerg Med 2013). Timing and triggers. The TiTrATE approach sorts dizziness by timing and triggers rather than by the patient's word for it. BPPV. The American guideline recommends the Dix-Hallpike test and repositioning manoeuvres, and advises against routine imaging, vestibular testing and vestibular suppressants (Bhattacharyya et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s6-2","Children","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · Investigations","Dizziness in children ( The dizzy child ) is uncommon and is usually benign. Vestibular migraine and benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood (brief attacks in toddlers, a migraine precursor) are the commonest causes. Otitis media with effusion, vestibular neuritis, head injury and BPPV after trauma follow, along with the third-window effect of an enlarged vestibular aqueduct. Posterior fossa tumours, seizures and cardiac causes are the rare serious ones to exclude."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s7-1","Viva essentials","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Classification by timing and trigger, not by the word \"dizzy\". Peripheral versus central nystagmus; Alexander's law. HINTS and HINTS-plus: why a normal head impulse is alarming. Dix–Hallpike technique and the Epley manoeuvre; horizontal canal BPPV. Diagnostic criteria for Ménière's disease and vestibular migraine. Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness. When to scan; the role of vestibular rehabilitation."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dizzy~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to dizziness and vertigo · For the examination","Common pitfalls Labelling acute vertigo \"labyrinthitis\" without examining the eyes. Prescribing long-term vestibular sedatives, which delay compensation. Missing vestibular migraine because the patient has no headache during attacks. Giving betahistine for BPPV instead of doing an Epley manoeuvre."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s1-1","Two patterns","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Where is the problem?","Oropharyngeal dysphagia is difficulty starting a swallow: food sticks in the throat at once, the patient coughs or chokes on swallowing, food or fluid comes back through the nose, the voice is wet, and repeated swallows are needed. It is usually neuromuscular (stroke, Parkinson's disease, motor neurone disease, myasthenia, myopathy) or caused by a pharyngeal pouch, a cricopharyngeal bar, head and neck cancer or its treatment. Oesophageal dysphagia is a sensation of food sticking after swallowing, usually felt behind the sternum. Difficulty with solids that progresses to liquids suggests a mechanical obstruction: cancer if rapid with weight loss, a peptic stricture if slow with reflux, a ring or web if intermittent. Difficulty with solids and liquids from the start suggests a motility disorder such as achalasia. Patients localise the level poorly: a lower oesophageal obstruction can be felt in the neck. Red flags in dysphagia Progressive dysphagia, weight loss, odynophagia, haematemesis or anaemia: upper GI endoscopy urgently, at any age. Dysphagia with hoarseness, otalgia, a neck lump or a smoking and drinking history: head and neck cancer. Total dysphagia with inability to swallow saliva: food bolus obstruction — emergency. Coughing on swallowing, chest infections and weight loss: aspiration; assess safety of oral intake. New neurological signs (ptosis, fatigable weakness, fasciculation, dysarthria): neurological cause."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s2-1","History","Approach to difficulty swallowing · History and examination","Ask what is difficult (solids, liquids, tablets, saliva), where food sticks, and how long the problem has lasted and whether it is getting worse. Ask about coughing and choking, nasal regurgitation, a wet voice, regurgitation of undigested food (often hours later, with halitosis and gurgling in a pouch), chest infections, heartburn, weight loss, pain, voice change and neurological symptoms. Review medications that cause oesophagitis (doxycycline, bisphosphonates, potassium, NSAIDs) or dry the mouth. The EAT-10 questionnaire screens for and measures dysphagia."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s2-2","Examination","Approach to difficulty swallowing · History and examination","Examine the mouth, dentition, tongue movement and palate, the cranial nerves, the neck (nodes, thyroid, a pouch that gurgles), and the larynx and pharynx with a flexible endoscope, looking for pooling of saliva in the piriform fossae (a sign of obstruction or poor clearance), lesions and vocal fold movement. Observe a trial swallow of water when safe, and look for general signs of neurological disease."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~c3","Investigation","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Investigation","Choose by the question. Swallowing investigations Test What it shows Use Flexible endoscopy in clinic Mucosa, pooling, cord movement First test in ENT FEES (fibreoptic endoscopic evaluation of swallowing) Pharyngeal phase, residue, penetration and aspiration Oropharyngeal dysphagia, at the bedside Videofluoroscopy All phases of the swallow, aspiration, cricopharyngeal opening Oropharyngeal dysphagia Barium swallow Pouch, webs, rings, strictures, motility Structural and pouch assessment Upper GI endoscopy (OGD) Oesophageal mucosa, biopsy, dilatation Oesophageal dysphagia, red flags High-resolution manometry Oesophageal motility (Chicago classification) Achalasia, motility disorders CT neck and chest Malignancy and staging Suspected cancer In eosinophilic oesophagitis, biopsies are taken even when the oesophagus looks normal."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~c4","Causes","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Causes","Structural, neuromuscular, oesophageal. The oesophageal causes are described in full in Oesophagus and dysphagia , and swallowing in children in The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow ."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s4-1","Pharyngeal pouch","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Causes","A pharyngeal pouch (Zenker's diverticulum) is a pulsion diverticulum through Killian's dehiscence , between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus parts of the inferior constrictor, with failure of cricopharyngeal relaxation. It affects older adults and causes dysphagia, regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis, gurgling, aspiration and weight loss. A barium swallow confirms it. Symptomatic pouches are treated by endoscopic stapling or laser diverticulotomy , which divides the wall between pouch and oesophagus together with the cricopharyngeus, or by open excision or inversion with cricopharyngeal myotomy for small or difficult pouches. Figure 1 Killian's dehiscence and a pharyngeal pouch. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s4-2","Other structural causes","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Causes","Cricopharyngeal dysfunction without a pouch may respond to dilatation, botulinum toxin or myotomy. Post-cricoid webs with iron-deficiency anaemia form the Plummer–Vinson (Paterson–Brown-Kelly) syndrome, a risk factor for post-cricoid cancer . Cervical osteophytes , an enlarged thyroid and head and neck cancer, or the fibrosis that follows its treatment by radiotherapy, also narrow the pharynx. Figure 2 Large cervical osteophytes pressing on the oesophagus on barium swallow. Dhingra, fig. 69.1"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s4-3","Neuromuscular causes","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Causes","Stroke is the commonest cause of oropharyngeal dysphagia; most patients improve in the first weeks. Progressive neurological diseases (Parkinson's disease, motor neurone disease, multiple sclerosis, myasthenia gravis, inclusion body myositis, oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy) need neurological diagnosis and long-term swallow management."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s4-4","Oesophageal causes","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Causes","Oesophageal cancer is the cause not to miss. Peptic strictures follow long-standing reflux. Schatzki rings cause intermittent food bolus obstruction. Achalasia is a failure of lower oesophageal sphincter relaxation with absent peristalsis, treated with pneumatic dilatation, Heller myotomy or peroral endoscopic myotomy. Eosinophilic oesophagitis typically affects young atopic men, presents with food bolus obstruction, and responds to proton pump inhibitors, topical steroids and dietary elimination. Figure 3 The oesophagus and its normal constrictions, with distances from the upper incisors. Dhingra, figs 67.1 and 67.2"],["ev","cl:t-ap_dysph~s5-1","Approach to difficulty swallowing: current evidence","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Management","Referral. NICE suggested-cancer guidance (NG12) recommends urgent direct-access upper gastrointestinal endoscopy (within two weeks) for dysphagia at any age, and a suspected-cancer referral for persistent unexplained neck lumps or hoarseness ( Approach to a lump in the neck , Approach to the hoarse voice ). Stroke. Guidelines recommend a validated swallow screen before any oral intake after stroke, followed by specialist assessment, because early screening reduces pneumonia."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s5-1","Principles","Approach to difficulty swallowing · Management","Every patient needs an assessment of whether oral intake is safe, by a speech and language therapist , who can advise on texture modification, postures (chin tuck, head turn), exercises and compensatory manoeuvres. Nutrition and hydration are protected, by nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding when necessary. The cause is then treated. A food bolus obstruction in the oesophagus needs endoscopic removal if it does not pass, and every patient needs subsequent endoscopy to exclude a stricture, eosinophilic oesophagitis or cancer. Difficulty swallowing 1 Red flags? progressive, weight loss, odynophagia, anaemia urgent OGD hoarseness, otalgia, neck lump head and neck examination and imaging cannot swallow saliva food bolus — urgent endoscopy 2 Oropharyngeal or oesophageal? coughing, nasal regurgitation, wet voice oropharyngeal, step 3 food sticks after swallowing oesophageal — OGD, then barium or manometry 3 Oropharyngeal flexible endoscopy, FEES or videofluoroscopy regurgitation and gurgling barium swallow for pouch — endoscopic stapling neurological signs neurology, speech therapy 4 Unsafe swallow speech therapy, texture changes, postures non-oral feeding if needed"],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s6-1","Viva essentials","Approach to difficulty swallowing · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Oropharyngeal versus oesophageal dysphagia; solids versus liquids. Red flags and urgent endoscopy. FEES versus videofluoroscopy. Pharyngeal pouch: Killian's dehiscence, presentation, endoscopic stapling and its limits. Plummer–Vinson syndrome, achalasia and eosinophilic oesophagitis."],["sec","cl:t-ap_dysph~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to difficulty swallowing · For the examination","Common pitfalls Attributing progressive dysphagia to reflux without endoscopy. Performing a rigid oesophagoscopy blind in a patient with a pouch, risking perforation. Forgetting a swallow safety assessment before restarting oral intake."],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s1-1","Two mechanisms","Approach to the watering eye · Oversupply or poor drainage?","Hypersecretion comes from an irritated ocular surface: dry eye with reflex tearing (the cause in up to 40% of referrals), blepharitis, trichiasis, entropion, ectropion, allergy, foreign bodies and, rarely, gustatory tearing (\"crocodile tears\") after facial nerve regeneration ( Facial nerve palsy ). Impaired drainage comes from a failure of the lid pump (facial palsy, lax lower lid), a malpositioned or stenosed punctum, a canalicular block, or an obstruction of the sac or nasolacrimal duct. Nasolacrimal obstruction is common in older adults and in women, and becomes more frequent with age."],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s1-2","Lacrimal anatomy","Approach to the watering eye · Oversupply or poor drainage?","Tears drain through the upper and lower puncta into the canaliculi , which run vertically for about 2 mm and then horizontally for about 8 to 10 mm before joining as the common canaliculus , which enters the lacrimal sac in the lacrimal fossa. The nasolacrimal duct runs down from the sac for about 12 mm and opens beneath the inferior turbinate, about 1 to 1.5 cm behind its anterior end, under the mucosal fold called the valve of Hasner . The lower canaliculus carries most of the flow. In the nose, the sac lies deep to the frontal process of the maxilla, in front of the attachment of the middle turbinate and the uncinate process. Do not miss A firm mass above the medial canthal tendon, blood-stained tears or bloody reflux on syringing: lacrimal sac tumour — image and biopsy. A watering eye with nasal blockage, bleeding or numbness: sinonasal tumour invading the duct. Acute dacryocystitis with orbital cellulitis signs: urgent treatment ( Proptosis and the orbit ). A neonate with a blue swelling at the medial canthus: dacryocystocele, which can extend into the nose and obstruct the airway. Figure 1 Lacrimal drainage: canaliculi, sac, nasolacrimal duct and Hasner's valve. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s2-1","History and examination","Approach to the watering eye · Assessment","Ask whether the watering is constant or intermittent, unilateral or bilateral, and associated with stickiness, discharge, swelling at the inner canthus, pain or a red eye. Ask about previous facial trauma, nasal or sinus surgery, radiotherapy, sarcoidosis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and drugs (topical glaucoma drops, docetaxel). Examine the lids for position and laxity, the puncta for size and position, and the ocular surface for dryness and inflammation. Press over the lacrimal sac: mucus or pus regurgitating from the puncta indicates a mucocoele or dacryocystitis with a blocked duct. Examine the nose with an endoscope for septal deviation, turbinate or polyp disease, previous surgery, tumours and access to the lacrimal region."],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s2-2","Tests","Approach to the watering eye · Assessment","The fluorescein dye disappearance test places 2% fluorescein in both eyes; retention after five minutes indicates impaired drainage. Jones tests look for dye in the inferior meatus: the primary test (dye recovered under the turbinate) shows a patent system under physiological conditions, and the secondary test, which syringes saline after a failed primary test, separates partial obstruction (dye recovered) from complete obstruction or pump failure. Lacrimal syringing with a cannula in the lower canaliculus is the key test: free flow to the nose means a patent system; reflux through the same punctum means a canalicular block; reflux through the opposite punctum means a block in the common canaliculus or below, typically the nasolacrimal duct. Dacryocystography and dacryoscintigraphy show anatomy and function respectively, and CT or MRI is used after trauma or surgery, for suspected tumour, or with sinus disease."],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~c3","Causes of obstruction","Approach to the watering eye · Causes of obstruction","By level. Levels of lacrimal obstruction Level Causes Treatment Punctum Stenosis, eversion with ectropion, inflammation Punctoplasty, lid surgery Canaliculi Viral infection, chemotherapy drugs, trauma, canaliculitis ( Actinomyces ) Intubation, canaliculo-DCR, Lester Jones tube Common canaliculus Membrane, scarring DCR with membrane removal and intubation Sac and nasolacrimal duct Primary acquired obstruction (idiopathic), dacryolithiasis, sinus disease and surgery, trauma, granulomatous disease, tumours Dacryocystorhinostomy Lower duct in infants Imperforate valve of Hasner Massage, probing"],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s3-1","Primary acquired nasolacrimal duct obstruction","Approach to the watering eye · Causes of obstruction","Primary acquired obstruction is idiopathic inflammation and fibrosis of the duct, most common in middle-aged and older women. It causes constant watering and often a mucocoele, and predisposes to acute dacryocystitis."],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s3-2","Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction","Approach to the watering eye · Causes of obstruction","About 20% of newborns have a watering eye from an imperforate valve of Hasner. Most resolve by the age of one year with lacrimal sac massage and, if needed, antibiotic drops for infection. Persistent cases are treated by probing, with intubation or balloon dilatation for failures, and endoscopy of the inferior meatus helps in complex cases. A dacryocystocele may extend into the nose as an intranasal cyst and needs endoscopic marsupialisation if it causes respiratory distress."],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s4-1","Dacryocystorhinostomy","Approach to the watering eye · Management","Dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR) ( Nose and sinus operations: principles ) creates a permanent opening from the lacrimal sac into the nose, bypassing an obstructed nasolacrimal duct. The endonasal approach raises a mucosal flap over the frontal process of the maxilla and the lacrimal bone, removes bone with a drill or punches to expose the whole sac, opens the sac widely and lays its flaps against the nasal mucosa. It avoids a skin scar, preserves the lacrimal pump and allows simultaneous treatment of nasal disease. Silicone stents are used selectively, especially for canalicular disease. Success rates for endonasal DCR are comparable to the external approach, at about 90% for nasolacrimal duct obstruction when the sac is fully exposed. The external approach through a skin incision remains widely used by ophthalmologists. Failure is usually from an opening that is too small or too high, scarring, or synechiae to the middle turbinate or septum."],["ev","cl:t-ap_epiph~s4-2","Approach to the watering eye: current evidence","Approach to the watering eye · Management","Endonasal or external DCR. Published success rates for modern endonasal surgery range from about 85 to 95%, though randomised comparisons with external surgery are few. Mitomycin C applied at surgery improved success in meta-analyses (Xue et al., Orbit 2014)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s4-2","Other treatments","Approach to the watering eye · Management","Dry eye is treated with lubricants and, if needed, punctal plugs. Canalicular obstruction that cannot be bypassed is treated with a Lester Jones tube , a glass bypass from the medial canthus to the nose. Acute dacryocystitis is treated with systemic antibiotics and, if needed, drainage, followed by DCR once the infection settles. The watering eye 1 Ocular surface and lids dry eye, blepharitis, lid malposition ophthalmic treatment, lubricants normal step 2 2 Syringe the system free flow functional block or pump failure — Jones tests reflux same punctum canalicular block reflux opposite punctum or mucopus sac or duct obstruction, step 3 3 Sac or duct obstruction endoscopy of the nose mass, bloody tears or sinus disease CT or MRI, biopsy primary acquired obstruction endonasal or external DCR 4 Infant massage and wait to one year persisting probing, then intubation intranasal cyst with breathing difficulty endoscopic marsupialisation"],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to the watering eye · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Hypersecretion versus impaired drainage; dry eye as a common cause. Lacrimal anatomy: canaliculi, sac, duct and the valve of Hasner. Syringing and Jones tests: interpretation. Endonasal DCR: steps, landmarks, success, causes of failure. Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction and dacryocystocele."],["sec","cl:t-ap_epiph~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to the watering eye · For the examination","Common pitfalls Performing DCR for a dry eye or a lid problem. Performing DCR for a canalicular block, where it does not help without additional steps. Making the sac opening too small or too high. Missing a lacrimal sac tumour when syringing produces blood."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s1-1","The first distinction","Approach to facial weakness · Upper or lower?","The upper face receives fibres from both cerebral hemispheres, while the lower face receives fibres only from the opposite hemisphere. A supranuclear (upper motor neuron) lesion therefore weakens the lower face on the opposite side and spares the forehead, and emotional smiling may still move the mouth. A lower motor neuron lesion, at or beyond the facial nucleus in the pons, paralyses the whole of one side of the face, forehead included. A forehead-sparing palsy of sudden onset is a stroke until proven otherwise and goes down the stroke pathway, not to the ENT clinic. A pontine lesion can weaken the face together with the sixth nerve, because the motor fibres loop around the abducens nucleus. Red flags in facial weakness Forehead sparing, limb weakness, dysarthria or other brainstem signs: stroke pathway now. Slow onset over weeks, twitching or spasm before the weakness, or a palsy that recurs on the same side: tumour until imaging says otherwise. No recovery at all by three to four months, or an outcome worse than House–Brackmann III: look again for a cause. A discharging ear, cholesteatoma or acute otitis media: an otological palsy that needs surgery or drainage. A parotid mass or a facial skin cancer: perineural malignancy. Bilateral palsy: Lyme disease, Guillain–Barré syndrome, sarcoidosis, leukaemia, HIV. Figure 1 The forehead receives bilateral cortical innervation and is spared in supranuclear palsy. Dhingra, fig. 14.1"],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s2-1","Onset and course","Approach to facial weakness · History","The single most useful question is how quickly the weakness developed. Bell's palsy reaches its worst within 72 hours, often within 24 to 48 hours, and is frequently preceded by pain behind the ear. A palsy that progresses over weeks, starts with twitching, or never begins to improve suggests a tumour of the nerve, the temporal bone or the parotid."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s2-2","Associated symptoms","Approach to facial weakness · History","Ask about taste disturbance and hyperacusis, which place the lesion proximal to the chorda tympani and the nerve to stapedius but are common in Bell's palsy; about ear pain, a rash or blisters in the ear or mouth (Ramsay Hunt syndrome); about hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo (zoster, cholesteatoma, a temporal bone fracture or a tumour of the internal auditory meatus or cerebellopontine angle); about discharge, previous ear disease and ear surgery; and about head injury and recent parotid or ear operations. Ask also about tick bites, travel and rashes (Lyme disease), ascending weakness and numbness (Guillain–Barré syndrome), diabetes and hypertension, pregnancy, and previous episodes. Recurrent alternating palsies with facial swelling and a fissured tongue suggest Melkersson–Rosenthal syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s3-1","The face and the eye","Approach to facial weakness · Examination","Look at the face at rest and with each movement: raising the brows, closing the eyes gently and tightly, smiling, puffing the cheeks and showing the teeth. Grade the weakness with the House–Brackmann scale , which runs from I (normal) to VI (total paralysis). The key break lies between III and IV: in grade III the eye still closes completely with effort, and from grade IV it no longer closes. Note Bell's phenomenon (the eye rolls up on attempted closure) and any exposure of the cornea, because an eye that does not close and does not roll up is at high risk. House–Brackmann grading Grade Description Eye closure Forehead I Normal Normal Normal II Slight weakness Complete, minimal effort Moderate to good III Obvious, not disfiguring Complete with full effort Slight to moderate IV Disfiguring weakness Incomplete None V Barely perceptible movement Incomplete None VI Total paralysis None None Figure 2 First, the features of a left lower motor neuron facial palsy. Second, Bell's palsy in an adult and a child. Dhingra, fig. 14.7"],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s3-2","Looking for a cause","Approach to facial weakness · Examination","Examine the ear under the microscope for cholesteatoma, otitis media, a mass behind the drum or blood, and look for vesicles in the concha, the canal, the soft palate and the anterior tongue. Test hearing with tuning forks. Palpate the parotid and the neck, and look at the facial skin and scalp for a cancer that may spread along the nerve. Examine the other cranial nerves, the limbs and the gait. Figure 3 Topographical localisation of facial nerve lesions. Dhingra, fig. 14.12"],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s4-1","The common causes","Approach to facial weakness · Causes","Bell's palsy accounts for about 70% of acute lower motor neuron palsies. It affects about 30 people per 100,000 per year, peaks around 40 years and recurs in about 7%. Ramsay Hunt syndrome , caused by reactivation of varicella zoster in the geniculate ganglion, is the second commonest and carries a worse prognosis, with only about 20% recovering completely. The vesicles may appear late or not at all ( zoster sine herpete ), so a \"Bell's palsy\" with severe pain, hearing loss or vertigo deserves antiviral treatment. Otological causes include acute otitis media in children, where the canal is often dehiscent and the prognosis is excellent with antibiotics and a myringotomy; cholesteatoma , which causes a palsy in under 1% of cases but in up to 50% of petrous apex disease and needs surgery; and necrotising otitis externa , with pain, discharge and progressive cranial neuropathies in an elderly diabetic. Trauma includes temporal bone fractures , especially those crossing the otic capsule, and surgical injury in the middle ear, the mastoid, the cerebellopontine angle and the parotid. Tumours may arise in the nerve (schwannoma and haemangioma), press on it (paraganglioma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma ) or invade it ( parotid cancer , cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma with perineural spread). Neurological and systemic causes include stroke, Guillain–Barré syndrome, Lyme disease, sarcoidosis and, in children, congenital palsies such as Möbius syndrome and CHARGE. Where the lesion lies Site Clues Examples Supranuclear Forehead spared; limb or speech signs Stroke, tumour Pons Six"],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s5-1","Tests","Approach to facial weakness · Investigation","A typical Bell's palsy in an adult with a normal ear and no other neurological findings needs no tests at presentation, but a pure tone audiogram is useful in every patient and essential if there is hearing loss or vertigo. Blood glucose is checked when diabetes is possible, Lyme serology in endemic areas or with a tick bite, and a PCR swab of vesicles confirms zoster. Imaging is needed for any atypical feature: slow onset, twitching, recurrence on the same side, no recovery by three to four months, other cranial neuropathies, hearing loss, ear disease or a parotid mass. Gadolinium-enhanced MRI covers the nerve from the brainstem to the parotid, and high-resolution CT shows the bony canal, fractures and cholesteatoma. Electroneuronography compares the compound muscle action potential on the two sides in a complete palsy between day 3 and day 21. More than 90% degeneration predicts a poor recovery and is used in some centres to select patients after trauma for exploration. Electromyography after three weeks shows fibrillation potentials (degeneration with viable muscle) and polyphasic potentials (early reinnervation); electrical silence after a long palsy means the muscle is no longer viable."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~c6","Pathway","Approach to facial weakness · Pathway","From weak face to plan. The weak face 1 Forehead moving? spared stroke pathway, neurology paralysed lower motor neuron, step 2 2 Eye care now incomplete closure or poor Bell's phenomenon lubricating drops by day, ointment and taping at night, ophthalmology if exposed 3 Ear, parotid and nerves examined vesicles, pain, hearing loss or vertigo Ramsay Hunt — steroid and antiviral cholesteatoma or otitis media CT and surgery or drainage parotid mass or skin cancer imaging and fine needle aspiration other neurology or bilateral neurology, Lyme serology 4 Typical sudden palsy within 72 hours Bell's palsy — prednisolone within 72 hours, eye care, review 5 Not recovering by three to four months, slow, twitching or recurrent MRI with gadolinium and CT of the temporal bone refer to a facial nerve clinic"],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~c7","Management","Approach to facial weakness · Management","Principles for each group. Each cause is described in full in Facial nerve palsy ."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s7-1","Bell's palsy","Approach to facial weakness · Management","Prednisolone started within 72 hours improves the chance of full recovery; a typical adult course is 50 to 60 mg daily for 5 to 10 days, with or without a taper. Antivirals alone do not help, and adding them to steroids offers at most a small benefit, so they are reserved for severe palsy or suspected zoster. Every patient with incomplete closure needs eye protection. About 85% begin to recover within three weeks and most recover fully; complete palsy at onset, age over 60, severe pain, diabetes, hypertension and no recovery by three weeks predict a worse outcome. Surgical decompression of the labyrinthine segment, the narrowest part of the canal at about 0.6 mm, remains controversial."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s7-2","Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Approach to facial weakness · Management","Treat with steroids and an antiviral such as aciclovir or valaciclovir, protect the eye, manage pain and vertigo, and refer for facial therapy early."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s7-3","Trauma","Approach to facial weakness · Management","An immediate complete palsy after a temporal bone fracture, with CT evidence of disruption of the canal or more than 90% degeneration on electroneuronography, is considered for exploration and repair. A delayed or incomplete palsy almost always recovers with steroids and eye care. A nerve known to have been cut at surgery is repaired at once."],["ev","cl:t-ap_face~s7-4","Approach to facial weakness: current evidence","Approach to facial weakness · Management","Bell's palsy guideline. The American guideline underlies the management above, including no electrodiagnosis in incomplete palsy and referral if recovery is incomplete at three months (Baugh et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2013). Evidence. Prednisolone raised full recovery at three months from 64% to 83% (Sullivan et al., NEJM 2007). Grading. House–Brackmann remains standard; the Sunnybrook and eFACE scales capture recovery and synkinesis better."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s7-4","Long-term weakness","Approach to facial weakness · Management","Patients left with weakness need facial therapy, protection of the eye with gold or platinum weights in the upper lid and lower lid tightening, and botulinum toxin for synkinesis. Reanimation ( Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation ) depends on whether the muscles are still viable, which is unlikely after 18 to 24 months: nerve repair, grafting or nerve transfer (masseteric or hypoglossal) while they are, and muscle transposition or free gracilis transfer once they are not."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s8-1","Viva essentials","Approach to facial weakness · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Upper versus lower motor neuron: the forehead and why. Bell's palsy as a diagnosis of exclusion: the features that make you look for another cause. House–Brackmann grading and the importance of eye closure. Ramsay Hunt syndrome: zoster sine herpete, antivirals, prognosis. Electroneuronography and electromyography: timing and meaning. Steroids within 72 hours; the limited role of antivirals."],["sec","cl:t-ap_face~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to facial weakness · For the examination","Common pitfalls Labelling a slowly progressive or twitching palsy as Bell's palsy. Forgetting the eye: an unprotected cornea can be lost while the face recovers. Never looking in the ear or feeling the parotid. Not reviewing the patient: a palsy that has not begun to recover by three to four months needs imaging."],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s1-1","Four places","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Where is the block?","The sensation of fullness has four main sources. The canal contributes wax, debris, a foreign body, otitis externa or exostoses. The middle ear contributes an effusion, a retracted or perforated drum, haemotympanum or, rarely, a mass. The Eustachian tube fails to open (obstructive dysfunction) or stays open (patulous tube). The inner ear produces fullness in Ménière's disease, sudden sensorineural loss, superior canal dehiscence and other third-window lesions, and with raised intracranial pressure. Temporomandibular joint disorders and anxiety can also make an ear feel full. Do not miss Sudden hearing loss with fullness: test the hearing that day. A sensorineural loss of 30 dB or more over three frequencies within 72 hours needs steroids now and MRI later. A unilateral middle ear effusion in an adult: examine and, if in doubt, biopsy the nasopharynx. Fullness with low-frequency loss and episodic vertigo: Ménière's disease. Fullness with pulsatile tinnitus, headache and visual blurring: idiopathic intracranial hypertension."],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s2-1","The questions","Approach to a blocked or full ear · History","Ask whether the fullness came on suddenly or gradually, and whether it is constant or comes and goes. A sudden onset, especially on waking, with muffled hearing and tinnitus, is a sudden sensorineural loss until tested. Fullness after a cold, a flight or a dive suggests Eustachian tube dysfunction or barotrauma. Ask whether the patient can \"pop\" the ear and whether that relieves it. Obstructive tube dysfunction improves when the ear pops; a patulous tube makes the patient hear their own voice and breathing loudly ( autophony ), improves when lying down or with a cold, and is common after weight loss and in pregnancy. Autophony with hearing one's own eye movements or footsteps, or vertigo with loud noise or straining, points to superior canal dehiscence. Ask about episodic vertigo, fluctuating hearing and roaring tinnitus (Ménière's disease), nasal blockage, rhinitis and reflux (tube dysfunction), jaw pain and clicking (temporomandibular disorder), and in adults about epistaxis, nasal blockage, neck lumps and ethnicity (nasopharyngeal carcinoma)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s3-1","Examination","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Examination and tests","Otoscopy shows wax, otitis externa, a perforation, a dull retracted drum with an air–fluid level or bubbles, or a blue drum of haemotympanum. Watch the drum during deep breathing: in a patulous tube it moves in and out with respiration. Tuning forks are essential, because they separate a conductive from a sensorineural loss at the bedside within minutes. With Weber to the blocked ear and Rinne negative, the loss is conductive; with Weber away from the blocked ear and Rinne positive, it is sensorineural. Examine the nose and nasopharynx with an endoscope, and palpate the temporomandibular joints and the neck."],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s3-2","Tests","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Examination and tests","Pure tone audiometry is the key test ( Hearing tests: how to do and read them ). A conductive loss points to the canal or middle ear, a low-frequency sensorineural loss to hydrops, and a sudden flat or high-frequency sensorineural loss to sudden sensorineural hearing loss. Tympanometry shows a flat type B trace with normal canal volume in an effusion, a negative-pressure type C trace in tube dysfunction, and a normal type A trace in inner ear causes, with a large volume if the drum is perforated. Respiratory variation on tympanometry supports a patulous tube. The seven-item Eustachian Tube Dysfunction Questionnaire (ETDQ-7) scores symptoms such as pressure, pain, a clogged feeling, symptoms with colds, popping, tinnitus and muffled hearing, and is used to select patients for treatment. Tubomanometry measures how the tube opens under nasopharyngeal pressure in specialist centres. MRI is used for an asymmetric sensorineural loss and to examine the nasopharynx, and CT for suspected dehiscence. Figure 1 Tympanograms in the blocked ear: B with normal volume in an effusion, C in tube dysfunction, A in inner ear causes. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s4-1","Wax and the canal","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Causes in turn","Wax is the commonest cause. It is removed by microsuction, softening drops or irrigation, provided there is no perforation, previous surgery or infection. Otitis externa is treated as such."],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s4-2","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Causes in turn","Obstructive dysfunction is usually mucosal inflammation in the cartilaginous tube, from allergic rhinitis, chronic rhinosinusitis, laryngopharyngeal reflux and smoke; in children it is adenoidal hypertrophy ( Adenoids and nasopharyngitis ) and infection, with cleft palate as an important cause. It affects about 1% of adults, and around 40% of children have a temporary episode before age ten. The tube is described in Eustachian tube disorders . Management starts with autoinflation (Valsalva or a nasal balloon) and treatment of the nose with saline and a steroid spray. Ventilation tubes treat persistent effusion and retraction. Balloon dilation Eustachian tuboplasty is an option for chronic obstructive and baro-challenged dysfunction: a balloon passed through the nose under endoscopic vision is inflated in the cartilaginous tube to about 10 bar for two minutes. Patients are selected by symptoms (ETDQ-7) and testing. Figure 2 The Eustachian tube with the levator and tensor veli palatini, which open it on swallowing. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s4-3","Patulous Eustachian tube","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Causes in turn","A patulous tube causes autophony, hearing one's own breathing, and fullness. It is linked to weight loss, pregnancy, hormones, radiotherapy and neuromuscular disease, and some patients \"sniff\" to close the tube, which can lead to retraction and cholesteatoma. Treatment is explanation, hydration, saline nasal drops and lying down, with augmentation or reconstruction of the tube for disabling cases."],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s4-4","Middle ear effusion in adults","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Causes in turn","An adult with a persistent unilateral middle ear effusion needs the nasopharynx examined with an endoscope, and an MRI or biopsy if anything looks abnormal, to exclude nasopharyngeal carcinoma , especially in patients from endemic areas. Other causes are tube dysfunction after a cold, barotrauma, radiotherapy and sinonasal disease. Ventilation tubes relieve persistent effusions once the nasopharynx is clear. Figure 3 Middle ear effusion: bubbles behind the drum on Valsalva. Dhingra, fig. 10.2"],["ev","cl:t-ap_full~s4-5","Approach to a blocked or full ear: current evidence","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Causes in turn","Exclude sudden hearing loss. The case for testing hearing before blaming wax or the tube, because steroid treatment works best early, comes from the American guideline (Chandrasekhar et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019). Eustachian tube dysfunction. Balloon dilation helped selected adults with persistent obstructive dysfunction in a randomised trial (Poe et al., Laryngoscope 2018)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s4-5","The inner ear","Approach to a blocked or full ear · Causes in turn","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss often presents as a blocked ear with tinnitus; an audiogram confirms it and treatment with oral or intratympanic steroids begins at once, followed by MRI. Ménière's disease produces fluctuating fullness, roaring tinnitus and low-frequency loss before or during vertigo attacks. Superior canal dehiscence produces fullness, autophony, sound- or pressure-induced vertigo and an apparent conductive loss with normal tympanometry and preserved reflexes. Raised intracranial pressure , typically idiopathic intracranial hypertension, produces fullness with pulsatile tinnitus and headache. The blocked or full ear 1 Otoscopy wax or debris remove and re-test effusion, retraction or haemotympanum go to step 3 normal drum go to step 2 2 Tuning forks and audiogram today sensorineural loss, sudden steroids now, MRI low-frequency sensorineural, fluctuating, with vertigo Ménière's pathway normal hearing step 4 3 Effusion or negative pressure adult, unilateral, persistent endoscope the nasopharynx, biopsy if abnormal after a cold or flight autoinflation, nasal treatment, review persistent with hearing loss ventilation tube 4 Normal hearing and drum autophony, better lying down patulous tube popping and pressure with colds obstructive tube dysfunction — ETDQ-7, consider balloon dilation jaw clicking and tenderness temporomandibular disorder sound or pressure vertigo CT for dehiscence"],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to a blocked or full ear · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The four sources of fullness; tuning forks and tympanometry to separate them. Sudden sensorineural loss presenting as a blocked ear. The adult unilateral effusion and the nasopharynx. Obstructive versus patulous tube dysfunction: symptoms, tests, treatment, balloon dilation. Third-window disorders that mimic conductive loss."],["sec","cl:t-ap_full~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to a blocked or full ear · For the examination","Common pitfalls Telling a patient with sudden sensorineural loss that \"it's just wax\" or \"Eustachian tube\". Inserting a grommet in an adult unilateral effusion without examining the nasopharynx. Treating a patulous tube with decongestants, which make it worse."],["sec","cl:t-ap_globus~s1-1","The pattern","Approach to a lump in the throat · Typical globus","Typical globus is felt in the midline, usually around the level of the cricoid or the thyroid notch. It is present between meals and when swallowing saliva, and often improves when eating or drinking . It is not painful and does not cause true dysphagia, odynophagia, weight loss or regurgitation. It fluctuates, worsens with stress, tiredness and throat clearing, and has often been present for months. It is most common in middle age and slightly more common in women."],["sec","cl:t-ap_globus~s1-2","What lies behind it","Approach to a lump in the throat · Typical globus","The cause is usually multifactorial. Suggested contributors include laryngopharyngeal reflux ( Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections ), raised cricopharyngeal tone, postnasal drip, throat clearing, muscle tension around the larynx, stress and anxiety, and heightened sensitivity of the pharynx. Thyroid swelling ( Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre ), cervical osteophytes, a long uvula and tongue-base lymphoid hypertrophy are occasionally relevant. Features that are not globus Pain, odynophagia or referred otalgia. True dysphagia, food sticking, regurgitation or weight loss. Unilateral or lateralised symptoms, or a sensation that is progressive. Hoarseness, haemoptysis or a neck lump. A smoker or heavy drinker over 50. Any of these features calls for investigation for malignancy ( Carcinoma of the hypopharynx , Cancer of the larynx , Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ) and other causes ( Approach to difficulty swallowing ), not a diagnosis of globus. Figure 1 Divisions of the pharynx and their vertebral levels. Dhingra, fig. 47.3"],["sec","cl:t-ap_globus~s2-1","Examination","Approach to a lump in the throat · Assessment","Examine the mouth and oropharynx, palpate the tongue base and tonsils, examine the neck and thyroid, and perform a flexible endoscopy of the nasal cavity, nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx and larynx, including pooling in the piriform fossae and the postcricoid area. Look for signs associated with reflux (interarytenoid thickening, oedema), but recognise that these are common and non-specific. Figure 2 The piriform fossa, where saliva pools in obstruction and where hypopharyngeal cancer hides. Dhingra, fig. 47.8"],["sec","cl:t-ap_globus~s2-2","Investigation","Approach to a lump in the throat · Assessment","In typical globus with a normal examination, no further investigation is needed . A routine barium swallow adds little and should not be requested just to reassure. Investigations are reserved for atypical features: an upper GI endoscopy or barium swallow for dysphagia, an ultrasound for a thyroid swelling, and imaging or an examination under anaesthesia when malignancy is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-ap_globus~s3-1","For most patients","Approach to a lump in the throat · Management","A clear explanation that the sensation is common, real, benign and not a cancer, after a full examination, is the most effective treatment, and many patients improve with reassurance alone. Reduce throat clearing (sipping water or swallowing instead), stop smoking and keep well hydrated. Speech and language therapy helps patients with throat clearing, muscle tension and voice symptoms. A trial of reflux treatment (diet and lifestyle changes, alginates after meals and at bedtime, and a proton pump inhibitor for patients with typical reflux symptoms) is reasonable, though its effect in isolated globus is unproven. For persistent, troublesome symptoms, low-dose amitriptyline and help with anxiety may be tried."],["ev","cl:t-ap_globus~s3-2","Approach to a lump in the throat: current evidence","Approach to a lump in the throat · Management","Reassurance is evidence-based. Isolated globus without the features listed above is very rarely due to cancer, and NICE suggested-cancer guidance does not list it as a red flag. Treatment. Trials of proton pump inhibitors for globus alone have been disappointing."],["sec","cl:t-ap_globus~s3-2","Review and discharge","Approach to a lump in the throat · Management","Patients with typical globus and a normal examination can be discharged with advice to return if new features appear: pain, dysphagia, weight loss, hoarseness or a lump. A lump in the throat 1 Typical? midline, between meals, eased by eating, no pain or dysphagia: step 2 pain, dysphagia, weight loss, hoarseness, unilateral or neck lump: investigate for malignancy — endoscopy and imaging 2 Full examination flexible endoscopy, neck and thyroid abnormal investigate the finding normal step 3 3 Treat explanation and reassurance reduce throat clearing, hydrate, stop smoking speech therapy trial of reflux treatment if reflux features discharge with safety-net advice"],["sec","cl:t-ap_globus~s4-1","Viva essentials","Approach to a lump in the throat · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The typical features of globus and why eating eases it. The red flags that exclude a diagnosis of globus. A thorough examination, including flexible endoscopy. Why routine barium swallow is not needed. Explanation, speech therapy and a reflux trial."],["sec","cl:t-ap_globus~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to a lump in the throat · For the examination","Common pitfalls Labelling a unilateral or painful throat symptom as globus. Investigating typical globus repeatedly, reinforcing the patient's fear. Prescribing proton pump inhibitors indefinitely without review."],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s1-1","Physiology in brief","Approach to the hoarse voice · The voice and its failure","The vocal fold has a layered structure. The cover (epithelium and the jelly-like superficial lamina propria, Reinke's space) slides over the transition (the vocal ligament) and the body (the vocalis muscle). Air from the lungs sets the cover vibrating as a mucosal wave . The voice fails when the folds cannot close (paralysis, atrophy, a gap), when the cover is stiff or heavy (scar, sulcus, Reinke's oedema, cancer), when a lesion interrupts closure (nodules, polyps, cysts), or when the muscles around the larynx work against it (muscle tension dysphonia, dystonia). Red flags in hoarseness Hoarseness over three weeks in a smoker or drinker, or any persistent hoarseness over 45: urgent laryngoscopy on a suspected cancer pathway. Stridor or breathlessness: airway assessment first. Hoarseness with dysphagia, odynophagia, referred otalgia, haemoptysis, weight loss or a neck lump. A breathy voice and weak cough after thyroid, chest, neck or cardiac surgery, or with no obvious cause: vocal fold paralysis — image the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerve course. Hoarseness in a child with breathing difficulty: recurrent respiratory papillomatosis ( The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow ). Figure 1 Layers of the vocal fold: the cover (epithelium and Reinke's space) over the vocal ligament and muscle. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s2-1","The questions","Approach to the hoarse voice · History","Ask how the voice changed and when: sudden onset after shouting suggests a haemorrhage or polyp; onset after a cold suggests laryngitis; onset after an operation or intubation suggests nerve injury or arytenoid dislocation; gradual progression suggests a lesion or cancer. Ask whether it fluctuates, worsens with use or improves with rest. Ask about voice demands (teachers, singers, call-centre workers), smoking and alcohol, reflux symptoms, inhaled steroids, thyroid disease, neurological symptoms (tremor, weakness, swallowing problems), breathing, swallowing, pain and weight. The Voice Handicap Index (VHI-10) measures the effect on the patient's life."],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s3-1","Laryngeal examination","Approach to the hoarse voice · Examination","Listen to the voice: breathy (a gap), rough (irregular vibration), strained (muscle tension or dystonia), tremulous or with voice breaks. Examine the neck for lymph nodes, the thyroid and laryngeal tenderness. Examine the larynx with a flexible nasendoscope , ideally a distal-chip scope, looking at the mucosa, any lesion, the movement of each fold and arytenoid, and the pattern of closure; ask the patient to sniff, say \"ee\" and cough. Videostroboscopy shows the mucosal wave and is needed to distinguish nodules from cysts, to detect scar and sulcus, and to judge early cancer. Perceptual rating (GRBAS) and acoustic analysis document the voice. Figure 2 Flexible laryngoscopy in clinic. Dhingra, fig. 62.6"],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~c4","Causes","Approach to the hoarse voice · Causes","By mechanism. The causes are described in full in Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders , Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness , Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections , Vocal fold paralysis and Cancer of the larynx . Causes of hoarseness Group Causes Clues Inflammatory Acute laryngitis, chronic laryngitis, laryngopharyngeal reflux, inhaled steroid laryngitis Infection, smoking, reflux, inhalers Benign lesions Nodules, polyps, cysts, Reinke's oedema, sulcus, granuloma Voice use, smoking (Reinke's), intubation or reflux (granuloma) Premalignant and malignant Leukoplakia, dysplasia, squamous cell carcinoma Smoker, progressive, white or irregular lesion Papillomatosis Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis (HPV 6 and 11) Children and young adults, recurrence Neurological Vocal fold paralysis, laryngeal dystonia, essential tremor, Parkinson's disease, motor neurone disease, myasthenia Breathy voice, strangled voice breaks, tremor, other signs Functional Muscle tension dysphonia, functional aphonia Normal folds, strained voice, normal cough Other Hypothyroidism, ageing (presbyphonia), arthritis of the cricoarytenoid joint, trauma, amyloid Context"],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s4-1","Some key causes","Approach to the hoarse voice · Causes","Vocal fold nodules are bilateral, symmetrical swellings at the junction of the anterior and middle thirds, caused by voice overuse; voice therapy is the treatment. Polyps are usually unilateral, often after a haemorrhage, and are removed by microlaryngoscopy if therapy does not help. Reinke's oedema is diffuse swelling of the superficial lamina propria in smokers, giving a low-pitched voice, especially in women; stopping smoking is essential, and surgery reduces the swelling while preserving the cover. Leukoplakia carries a risk of malignant change that depends on the grade of dysplasia and is excised for histology. Vocal fold paralysis is more often left-sided, because the left recurrent laryngeal nerve has a longer course around the aortic arch. Causes are surgery (thyroid, cardiothoracic, anterior cervical spine), malignancy (lung, oesophagus, thyroid, skull base), and idiopathic. Unexplained paralysis is investigated with CT from the skull base to the aortic arch. Muscle tension dysphonia is the commonest functional voice disorder, with a strained voice, laryngeal tension and normal or compensatory supraglottic squeezing; it responds to voice therapy. Laryngeal (spasmodic) dystonia produces strangled voice breaks, and is treated with botulinum toxin. Figure 3 First, the site of vocal nodules at the junction of the anterior and middle thirds. Second, nodules on both folds. Dhingra, figs 61.1 and 61.2"],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s5-1","For all patients","Approach to the hoarse voice · Management","Voice hygiene and voice therapy with a speech and language therapist are central to most benign and functional voice problems. Smoking cessation, hydration, treatment of reflux when its features are present, and review of inhaled steroid technique are also important."],["ev","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s5-2","Approach to the hoarse voice: current evidence","Approach to the hoarse voice · Management","Guidelines. The American guideline uses four weeks rather than three, or sooner when a serious cause is suspected, and advises against antibiotics, steroids and antireflux drugs before laryngoscopy (Stachler et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018); NICE (NG12) sets the suspected-cancer referral at 45 and over. The rules are compared in Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders ."],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s5-2","Surgery","Approach to the hoarse voice · Management","Phonosurgery under general anaesthesia uses cold steel or lasers to remove lesions while preserving the superficial lamina propria. Suspicious lesions are biopsied or excised for histology. Vocal fold paralysis that does not recover is treated with injection medialisation (temporary or long-lasting), medialisation thyroplasty , arytenoid adduction or laryngeal reinnervation. Cancer is staged and treated on the head and neck pathway ( Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles ). The hoarse voice 1 Red flags or over three weeks? yes laryngoscopy, urgently if smoker, over 45, stridor or other head and neck symptoms recent cold, improving wait and review 2 Laryngoscopy lesion suspicious for cancer or dysplasia microlaryngoscopy and biopsy, staging benign lesion voice therapy, phonosurgery if needed inflammation smoking, reflux and inhaler review 3 Mobility fold paralysed CT skull base to aortic arch, injection or thyroplasty if no recovery normal folds, strained voice muscle tension dysphonia — voice therapy voice breaks dystonia — botulinum toxin Figure 4 First, a pedunculated vocal fold polyp. Second, bilateral Reinke's oedema in a smoker. Dhingra, figs 61.4 and 61.5"],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s6-1","Viva essentials","Approach to the hoarse voice · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three-week rule and urgent referral for persistent hoarseness. The body–cover model and the mucosal wave; stroboscopy. Nodules, polyps, cysts and Reinke's oedema: features and treatment. Vocal fold paralysis: causes, investigation and treatment options. Functional dysphonia and the role of voice therapy."],["sec","cl:t-ap_hoarse~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to the hoarse voice · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing antibiotics or a proton pump inhibitor for months without examining the larynx. Missing a lung cancer in a patient with left vocal fold paralysis. Operating on nodules before voice therapy. Stripping the vocal fold and leaving it scarred."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s1-1","Character of the discharge","Approach to the discharging ear · The first look","The discharge is not diagnostic, but it is a strong first clue. What the discharge suggests Discharge Usual source Think of Mucopurulent, often profuse Middle ear mucosa through a perforation or grommet Acute otitis media with perforation; mucosal chronic otitis media; grommet otorrhoea Scanty, foul-smelling, sometimes with white flakes Keratin and anaerobes in a pocket Cholesteatoma Thin, serous or watery Inflamed canal skin or drum surface Chronic otitis externa, eczema, granular myringitis Creamy debris with spores or hyphae Canal Otomycosis (white in Candida , black in Aspergillus niger , yellow in A. flavus ) Bloody Trauma, granulation, polyp, tumour Acute perforation, granulation tissue, aural polyp, carcinoma of the canal, glomus tumour Clear and watery, profuse, worse on straining or bending forwards Subarachnoid space CSF otorrhoea after temporal bone fracture, surgery, or a spontaneous tegmen defect Mucus is the useful discriminator: the canal has no mucous glands, so a mucoid discharge means the middle ear is open."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s1-2","Red flags","Approach to the discharging ear · The first look","When a discharging ear is more than an infection Diabetes, immunosuppression or old age with pain out of proportion and granulation on the canal floor: necrotising otitis externa. Facial weakness, vertigo, sensorineural loss, severe headache, fever, neck stiffness or drowsiness: an intratemporal or intracranial complication. Blood-stained discharge, a polyp or granulation that persists, or pain at night: carcinoma of the canal or middle ear until biopsy says otherwise. Clear watery discharge after head injury or surgery: CSF leak. Postauricular swelling pushing the pinna forwards: mastoiditis with a subperiosteal abscess."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s2-1","The ear","Approach to the discharging ear · History","Ask how long the ear has been discharging and whether this is a first episode or the latest of many. A short, painful history after swimming, scratching or a cold points to otitis externa or acute otitis media; a long history of intermittent painless discharge with hearing loss points to chronic otitis media. Ask whether pain came before the discharge and was then relieved by it, which is the story of an acute otitis media that has perforated, or whether pain persists despite the discharge, which is otitis externa, furunculosis or something deeper. Ask about hearing loss, tinnitus, vertigo and facial weakness, about previous ear surgery or grommets, and about what the patient puts in the ear: cotton buds, hearing aids, ear plugs, water."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s2-2","The patient","Approach to the discharging ear · History","Diabetes, immunosuppression, chemotherapy and steroids all change the list. Ask about headache, fever and drowsiness. In an adult with a long-standing discharging ear, ask also about smoking and other head and neck symptoms, because pain and bleeding can be a sign of malignancy."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s3-1","The ear","Approach to the discharging ear · Examination","Look at the pinna, the postauricular sulcus and the mastoid before touching the canal. Tenderness when the pinna is pulled or the tragus pressed is the sign of otitis externa or a furuncle; a red, tender, oedematous pinna is cellulitis or, if the lobule is spared, perichondritis. Then clean the canal under the microscope or endoscope . Meticulous microsuction is the key to diagnosis and to treatment, because drops cannot reach a canal full of debris and the drum cannot be judged through pus. Assess the canal skin for oedema, fungal debris, granulations on the floor and exposed bone, then the drum: intact, perforated centrally, perforated at the margin or in the attic, retracted, or obscured by a polyp. Test the facial nerve every time. Perform the fistula test when there is an attic or posterosuperior lesion. Use tuning forks, and arrange audiometry for any ear that is not settling or where the inner ear may be involved. Figure 1 An aural polyp in the canal, and how a polyp from the promontory can present through a perforation. Dhingra, fig. 11.9"],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s3-2","Beyond the ear","Approach to the discharging ear · Examination","If malignancy is possible, examine the neck, oral cavity and pharynx and pass a flexible endoscope. The nose and nasopharynx are worth examining in recurrent middle ear infection. When the drum cannot be seen If the canal is too swollen or tender to clean fully, start antibiotic and steroid drops, with a wick if the canal is closed, and bring the patient back to finish the microsuction and see the drum. Too many cholesteatomas are treated for months as \"otitis externa\"."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s4-1","The canal","Approach to the discharging ear · Causes","Acute otitis externa is bacterial in almost all cases, mostly Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus . It is driven by water, cotton buds, narrow canals, hearing aids and dermatitis. It begins with itch and fullness, then pain, a swollen canal and discharge. Otomycosis follows antibiotic drops or arises in humid climates, and its debris is often visibly fungal. Furunculosis is a staphylococcal abscess of a hair follicle in the outer canal and causes pain out of proportion to the findings. Chronic otitis externa means inflammation lasting more than three months or four acute episodes in a year; it is itchy more than painful and is often eczematous or due to contact allergy to drops. Necrotising otitis externa is osteitis of the skull base, most often in elderly diabetics. Keratosis obturans , canal cholesteatoma ( Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis ) and carcinoma ( External ear tumours and bony growths ) complete the canal list. Figure 2 Otomycosis: (A) yellow and (B) black fungal debris in the canal. Dhingra, fig. 8.11"],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s4-2","The drum and middle ear","Approach to the discharging ear · Causes","Acute otitis media perforates with sudden relief of pain and a mucopurulent discharge. Grommet otorrhoea is a perforation with a tube in it and is treated the same way. Mucosal chronic otitis media gives intermittent profuse mucoid discharge through a central perforation, usually after colds or water. Cholesteatoma gives scanty, foul, persistent discharge from an attic or marginal defect. Granular myringitis ( Myringitis ) produces granulation on the outer surface of an intact drum, often after surgery, with watery or bloody discharge. Tuberculous otitis media , now rare, gives painless discharge through multiple perforations and fails to respond to the usual drops. Figure 3 Sites of perforation: central perforations belong to the mucosal type; attic and posterosuperior marginal defects suggest cholesteatoma. Dhingra, fig. 11.8"],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s4-3","Beyond the ear","Approach to the discharging ear · Causes","CSF otorrhoea follows temporal bone fracture , surgery, erosive disease and spontaneous tegmen defects linked to raised intracranial pressure; it is confirmed by beta-2 transferrin in the fluid. A mastoid abscess or a parotid or skin process can discharge into the canal."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~c5","Pathway","Approach to the discharging ear · Pathway","From discharge to diagnosis. The discharging ear 1 Clean the canal under the microscope canal cannot be cleaned drops with a wick, review within days cleaned look at the drum 2 Any red flag? diabetic with pain and granulation CT, ESR or CRP, biopsy — treat as necrotising otitis externa palsy, vertigo or neurological signs urgent CT, admit watery after trauma beta-2 transferrin 3 Drum intact? yes, canal inflamed otitis externa or otomycosis yes, granulation on the drum granular myringitis yes, polyp or granulation in the canal biopsy 4 Drum perforated? central, mucoid mucosal chronic otitis media or perforated acute otitis media attic or marginal, foul cholesteatoma — CT grommet grommet otorrhoea 5 Not settling after two courses of appropriate drops? swab for culture and fungi biopsy granulation CT temporal bone consider tuberculosis"],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s6-1","Otitis externa and otomycosis","Approach to the discharging ear · Management","Treatment is aural toilet, topical drops, analgesia and keeping the ear dry. Combination antibiotic and steroid drops for 7 to 10 days are the most effective treatment. When the canal is closed, an expanding wick is inserted with aqueous drops, which make it swell, and changed after two to seven days. Acidifying drops such as acetic acid suit mild cases and prevention. Fungal otitis externa needs thorough removal of debris and two to three weeks of antifungal drops (clotrimazole) or acidifying drops. Systemic antibiotics are reserved for infection spreading beyond the canal: furunculosis, cellulitis, perichondritis (with antipseudomonal cover) and necrotising otitis externa. Water precautions follow, with ear plugs or cotton wool coated in petroleum jelly, and no cotton buds."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s6-2","Active middle ear disease","Approach to the discharging ear · Management","A perforated acute otitis media in an adult needs analgesia and often oral amoxicillin. Grommet otorrhoea and active mucosal chronic otitis media need microsuction and topical antibiotic drops, with a swab only when the ear does not settle. Once the ear is dry, a perforation that keeps discharging is repaired by tympanoplasty ( Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles ). Cholesteatoma needs imaging and surgery, not repeated courses of drops. Where the books differ · Ear drops when the drum is open Dhingra Topical antibiotic drops with steroid for both otitis externa and active chronic otitis media, with aural toilet. Lee Prefers non-ototoxic quinolone drops whenever the middle ear is open. Oxford Textbook Takes the ENT-UK consensus view. The risk of an untreated infection to the inner ear outweighs the small risk of aminoglycoside drops through a perforation, provided the course is short and the patient is told to stop if dizziness or hearing loss appears. Quinolones are preferred where available, but quinolone-resistant Pseudomonas is a growing problem. In practice: use quinolone drops when the middle ear is open; if only aminoglycoside drops are available, a short course is acceptable, with the patient told to stop if dizziness or hearing loss appears."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s6-3","Necrotising otitis externa and complications","Approach to the discharging ear · Management","Suspected necrotising otitis externa means admission, glycaemic control, culture before antibiotics, biopsy to exclude cancer, CT and MRI, and prolonged antipseudomonal treatment. Any complication of otitis media, such as Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis , facial palsy, labyrinthitis or intracranial infection , needs intravenous antibiotics, imaging and usually drainage. Figure 4 Sensory supply of the canal and drum: why canal disease is so painful. From the anatomy atlas"],["ev","cl:t-ap_otorr~s6-4","Approach to the discharging ear: current evidence","Approach to the discharging ear · Management","Otitis externa. Topical drops are first line, with aural toilet; systemic antibiotics only for spreading infection or the immunocompromised (Rosenfeld et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2014). Grommet otorrhoea. Antibiotic–steroid drops beat oral antibiotics and observation (van Dongen et al., NEJM 2014). Chronic suppurative otitis media. Cochrane reviews found that topical quinolones probably resolve discharge better than antiseptics or systemic antibiotics, though evidence is limited (Brennan-Jones et al., 2020)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s6-4","Children","Approach to the discharging ear · Management","In children the discharging ear is usually acute otitis media with perforation or grommet otorrhoea. Grommet otorrhoea is treated with non-ototoxic quinolone drops after cleaning; recurrent episodes need a look at the adenoids and a swab. A child with a red, swollen, tender mastoid and an ear pushed forward has acute mastoiditis and needs admission, intravenous antibiotics and often myringotomy with or without cortical mastoidectomy."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s7-1","Viva essentials","Approach to the discharging ear · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Clean before you diagnose: microsuction and a view of the drum. Reading the discharge: mucoid means the middle ear; foul and scanty means cholesteatoma; watery after trauma means CSF. The red flags: necrotising otitis externa, complications, malignancy. Ototopical drops through a perforation: the consensus position. When to swab, scan and biopsy."],["sec","cl:t-ap_otorr~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to the discharging ear · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a canal full of pus with drops alone and never seeing the drum. Repeating antibiotic drops for months in what turns out to be cholesteatoma or carcinoma. Missing necrotising otitis externa in an elderly diabetic with \"severe otitis externa\". Using oil-based drops with an expanding wick."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~s1-1","A structured history","Approach to facial pain and headache · The pain history","Use the same headings for every patient. Site : unilateral or bilateral, and the dermatome. Character : throbbing, pressing, stabbing or burning. Severity , out of ten: migraine and cluster headache often reach ten. Timing : how long each attack lasts, how often attacks come, and whether there are clusters and remissions. Associated features : nausea, photophobia and aura (migraine); a red, watering eye, rhinorrhoea, blockage and a drooping lid on the side of the pain (trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias); nasal symptoms (rhinosinusitis). Triggers : alcohol in cluster headache, touch or chewing in trigeminal neuralgia, the menstrual cycle in migraine. Relieving factors and what has been tried, including analgesic overuse, which itself causes daily headache. Rhinosinusitis pain accompanies an attack of nasal symptoms and settles when they do. Pain that has not changed with years of antibiotics and nasal sprays is unlikely to be sinogenic. Red flags in headache and facial pain Thunderclap headache , new headache with fever, neck stiffness, confusion or focal neurological signs: emergency imaging and neurology. New headache over 50 with jaw claudication, scalp tenderness or visual symptoms: giant cell arteritis — ESR, CRP and steroids the same day. Headache worse lying down, on waking or with straining, with visual obscurations or pulsatile tinnitus: raised intracranial pressure. Facial numbness, diplopia, proptosis, a loose tooth or a nasal mass: sinonasal malignancy . Acute sinusitis with periorbital swelling, reduced vision or severe headache: orbital or intracranial complica"],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~s2-1","The examination","Approach to facial pain and headache · Examination and tests","Examine the nose with an endoscope: mucopus or oedema in the middle meatus supports rhinosinusitis, and its absence makes it very unlikely. Examine the cranial nerves, especially the trigeminal nerve and the eyes, the temporomandibular joints, the teeth, the temporal arteries and the neck muscles. Figure 1 The trigeminal ganglion and its divisions, the pathway for most facial pain. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~s2-2","Investigation","Approach to facial pain and headache · Examination and tests","CT of the sinuses is not a screening test for facial pain, because incidental mucosal thickening is found in about a third of people without symptoms. It is used when malignancy, fungal disease or a complication is suspected, and in chronic rhinosinusitis that has failed medical treatment when surgery is planned. Dental radiographs and CT show odontogenic disease. MRI is used for trigeminal neuralgia, to look for a compressing vessel or demyelination, and for atypical or neurological features. Figure 2 The paranasal sinuses. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~c3","The causes","Approach to facial pain and headache · The causes","Recognising each. Facial pain and headache — features Condition Pain and timing Associated features First-line treatment Rhinosinusitis Pressure with nasal symptoms; settles when they do Blockage, discharge, smell loss; pus on endoscopy Treat the sinuses ( Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps ) Migraine Unilateral, throbbing, 4 to 72 hours Nausea, photophobia, aura in about a quarter; may cause nasal congestion Triptan acutely; propranolol, topiramate or amitriptyline to prevent Tension-type headache Bilateral, band-like pressing, mild to moderate Few; stress, poor sleep Simple analgesia, amitriptyline Midfacial segment pain Symmetrical pressure over the nasal bridge, cheeks or between the eyes Normal endoscopy; hyperaesthesia of the skin Low-dose amitriptyline Cluster headache Severe, orbital, 15 to 180 minutes, up to eight a day, in bouts Ipsilateral tearing, rhinorrhoea, ptosis, restlessness; alcohol trigger High-flow oxygen, subcutaneous sumatriptan; verapamil to prevent Paroxysmal hemicrania Like cluster but 2 to 30 minutes, many a day Autonomic features Indometacin (diagnostic response) SUNCT and SUNA Seconds to minutes, very frequent Conjunctival injection and tearing Lamotrigine Trigeminal neuralgia Electric shocks for seconds, usually V2 or V3 Triggered by touch, chewing, washing Carbamazepine; MRI; microvascular decompression Giant cell arteritis Temporal, new, over 50 Jaw claudication, visual loss, raised ESR Steroids at once Temporomandibular disorder ( Approach to earache ) Preauricular, with chewing Clicking, tender joint and muscles Soft diet, splint, phys"],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~s3-1","Midfacial segment pain","Approach to facial pain and headache · The causes","Midfacial segment pain is a form of tension-type headache affecting the middle third of the face. Patients describe a symmetrical pressure or tightness across the bridge of the nose, behind the eyes or over the cheeks, often feel their nose is blocked although it is not, and may have tender skin over the area. Endoscopy is normal, and the CT is normal or shows incidental changes. It responds to low-dose amitriptyline, starting at 10 mg at night and increased if needed, given for at least six weeks before its effect is judged and continued for about six months. Sinus surgery does not help."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~s3-2","Migraine and the nose","Approach to facial pain and headache · The causes","Migraine affects about one in seven people. Attacks can cause nasal congestion, discharge and tearing, and facial pain may be the only symptom, so migraine is commonly misdiagnosed as sinusitis. Acute attacks are treated with analgesics and triptans, used on no more than a few days a week to avoid medication-overuse headache. Prevention uses propranolol, topiramate (contraindicated in pregnancy) or amitriptyline, then candesartan, botulinum toxin for chronic migraine, or CGRP-targeted antibodies for refractory cases."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~s3-3","Contact point headache","Approach to facial pain and headache · The causes","The idea that a septal spur ( Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess ) touching a turbinate causes pain is controversial. Most patients with contact points have no pain, and most with pain and contact points improve with medical treatment for midfacial segment pain. Surgery is considered only when a temporary local anaesthetic block abolishes the pain and other causes have been treated."],["ev","cl:t-ap_pain~c4","Approach to facial pain and headache: current evidence","Approach to facial pain and headache · Pathway","Most \"sinus headache\" is migraine. In a large study of patients with self-reported or physician-diagnosed sinus headache, 88% met criteria for migraine (Schreiber et al., Arch Intern Med 2004). EPOS 2020 states that facial pain without nasal symptoms, a normal endoscopy and a normal CT is unlikely to be rhinosinusitis."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~c4","Pathway","Approach to facial pain and headache · Pathway","Sinus or not? Facial pain 1 Red flags? thunderclap, neurological signs, fever emergency imaging over 50 with jaw claudication ESR, CRP, steroids today numbness, diplopia, nasal mass imaging and biopsy 2 Nasal symptoms with the pain? yes, pus or polyps on endoscopy rhinosinusitis pathway, CT if surgery considered no nasal symptoms, normal endoscopy step 3 3 Pattern of the pain unilateral throbbing with nausea migraine symmetrical pressure over the midface midfacial segment pain — amitriptyline short, severe, with ipsilateral autonomic features cluster headache or another trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia electric shocks triggered by touch trigeminal neuralgia — MRI, carbamazepine 4 Not improving review analgesic overuse refer to neurology or a pain clinic"],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to facial pain and headache · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why most \"sinus headache\" is not sinusitis: pain needs nasal symptoms and endoscopic signs. A structured pain history with timing and associated features. Midfacial segment pain: features and amitriptyline. The trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias: cluster headache, paroxysmal hemicrania, SUNCT and SUNA. Why CT is not a screening test for facial pain. Red flags, especially giant cell arteritis and raised intracranial pressure."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pain~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to facial pain and headache · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on the sinuses for facial pain with a normal endoscopy and incidental CT changes. Missing cluster headache because the patient also has a runny nose. Giving repeated antibiotics for recurrent \"sinusitis\" that is migraine. Missing medication-overuse headache."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pnd~s1-1","Definition and mechanism","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · What the patient means","Normally, a litre or so of mucus a day is carried backwards by the cilia and swallowed without being noticed. A sensation of postnasal drip arises when the volume rises, when the mucus becomes thicker or more irritant, when mucociliary clearance slows, or when the sensory nerves of the pharynx become hypersensitive. Many patients have more than one of these. In chest medicine, a cough attributed to the nose is called upper airway cough syndrome , but many patients with postnasal drip have no cough, and many with chronic cough have a sensitised cough reflex rather than a nasal cause. Do not miss Unilateral nasal symptoms, blood-stained mucus or a neck lump: nasopharyngeal ( Nasopharyngeal carcinoma ) and sinonasal ( Sinonasal tumours ) tumour. Hoarseness, dysphagia, weight loss or pain on swallowing: laryngeal or pharyngeal malignancy. A smoker with a cough that has changed, haemoptysis or breathlessness: chest X-ray and respiratory referral. Persistent cough in a patient taking an ACE inhibitor: the drug."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pnd~s2-1","The questions","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · History","Ask what exactly is felt: mucus that can be spat out, or a sensation that nothing relieves; and whether the mucus is clear, thick or discoloured. Ask about the nose, blockage, discharge, sneezing, smell loss and facial pressure, which point to rhinitis or rhinosinusitis. Ask about heartburn, a sour taste, hoarseness worse in the morning, globus and throat clearing, which point to reflux. Ask about cough, its duration (chronic cough lasts eight weeks or more), smoking, asthma and medication, including ACE inhibitors and drugs that dry the mucosa (antidepressants, anxiolytics, anticholinergics). The Reflux Symptom Index and SNOT-22 quantify the symptoms."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pnd~s3-1","Nose, nasopharynx and larynx","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · Examination","Examine the nose with an endoscope, looking for mucopurulent discharge in the middle meatus or sphenoethmoidal recess, polyps, adenoidal tissue, a Thornwaldt cyst and any nasopharyngeal mass. Pass the scope down to the larynx and look for signs associated with reflux (posterior commissure thickening, oedema, erythema) and for any lesion that explains the throat symptoms. Examine the neck. A CT of the sinuses is arranged only if chronic rhinosinusitis is suspected and not settling on medical treatment. Figure 1 The region of the Eustachian tube, sphenoethmoidal recess, choana and fossa of Rosenmüller, examined at endoscopy. Dhingra, fig. 89.1"],["sec","cl:t-ap_pnd~c4","Causes","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · Causes","Where the drip comes from. Causes of postnasal drip Source Examples Clue Nose Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis, rhinitis of the elderly Sneezing, watery discharge, triggers Sinuses Chronic rhinosinusitis, dental sinusitis, fungal ball Pus in the meatus, smell loss, unilateral Nasopharynx Adenoiditis, Thornwaldt cyst, tumour Endoscopic findings Clearance Primary ciliary dyskinesia, cystic fibrosis, dry atrophic nose ( Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis ), after sinus surgery Thick mucus, crusting, lifelong symptoms Throat and reflux Laryngopharyngeal reflux, globus ( Approach to a lump in the throat ) Throat clearing, hoarseness, normal nose Sensory Cough hypersensitivity, laryngeal hypersensitivity Cough triggered by talking, cold air, scents Drugs ACE inhibitors, drying drugs Medication history"],["sec","cl:t-ap_pnd~s5-1","When a cause is found","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · Management","Rhinitis is treated with saline irrigation and a steroid spray, with an antihistamine for allergy and ipratropium for watery discharge. Chronic rhinosinusitis follows its own pathway: saline, steroids, a long course of a macrolide in selected patients, and surgery if medical treatment fails. Reflux symptoms ( Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections ) are managed with diet and lifestyle changes, alginates and, for typical acid symptoms, a proton pump inhibitor trial; evidence for proton pump inhibitors in throat symptoms alone is weak. Any drying or causative medication is reviewed with the prescriber."],["ev","cl:t-ap_pnd~s5-2","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh: current evidence","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · Management","Mechanisms. Postnasal drip and catarrh usually reflect rhinitis or chronic rhinosinusitis, but many patients with persistent catarrh have normal endoscopy and CT, and their symptom reflects altered sensation rather than excess mucus. Treatment. The nasal treatment above follows EPOS 2020, which also advises against repeated courses of antibiotics when examination is normal. Cough. Upper airway cough syndrome is one of the commonest causes of chronic cough, along with asthma and reflux."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pnd~s5-2","When the sensation persists","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · Management","Many patients have a normal nose and larynx and still feel the drip. Explanation, reassurance that nothing serious has been found, adequate hydration and steam inhalation help. Throat clearing perpetuates the irritation, so speech and language therapy for laryngeal hygiene and cough suppression techniques is effective (chronic refractory cough is described in Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders ). In refractory chronic cough, a neuromodulator such as low-dose amitriptyline or gabapentin may be tried under specialist care. Postnasal drip 1 Red flags? unilateral symptoms, blood, neck lump, hoarseness or dysphagia: full endoscopic examination and imaging smoker with changed cough chest X-ray 2 Nasal endoscopy rhinitis saline and steroid spray, ipratropium if watery pus or polyps chronic rhinosinusitis pathway nasopharyngeal lesion biopsy 3 Throat and larynx reflux features lifestyle, alginate, trial of proton pump inhibitor normal step 4 4 Normal nose and larynx review drugs explanation and hydration speech therapy for throat clearing neuromodulator for refractory cough"],["sec","cl:t-ap_pnd~s6-1","Viva essentials","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The mechanisms: volume, viscosity, clearance, hypersensitivity. Upper airway cough syndrome and cough hypersensitivity. The red flags that need endoscopy and imaging. Treating the nose, the sinuses and reflux; speech therapy when nothing is found."],["sec","cl:t-ap_pnd~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh · For the examination","Common pitfalls Repeating antibiotics for \"catarrh\" with no evidence of infection. Missing the ACE inhibitor in a patient with chronic cough. Operating on the sinuses for postnasal drip alone when the CT and endoscopy are normal."],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~s1-1","Sorting the discharge","Approach to a runny nose · Which side, and what colour?","Rhinorrhoea by side and character Pattern Think of Key features Bilateral, watery, with sneezing and itch Allergic rhinitis Seasonal or perennial triggers, itchy eyes, atopy Bilateral, watery, no itch Non-allergic rhinitis Triggered by cold air, smells, food, alcohol; older patients Bilateral, thick, discoloured Acute or chronic rhinosinusitis Blockage, pain, smell loss, pus in the middle meatus Unilateral, clear, watery, salty, positional CSF leak After trauma or surgery, or spontaneous; worse bending forward Unilateral, foul, purulent Foreign body (child), dental sinusitis, fungal ball Smell noticed by others; dental history Unilateral, blood-stained Tumour Obstruction, pain, numbness, proptosis Do not miss Clear, watery, unilateral discharge , especially after head injury or sinus surgery, worse on bending forward or straining, or with a salty taste: collect it for beta-2 transferrin or beta-trace protein. Any history of meningitis in a patient with a runny nose: a CSF leak until proven otherwise. Blood-stained unilateral discharge in an adult: endoscopy and imaging for a tumour. Unilateral foul discharge in a child: foreign body. Figure 1 Where each sinus and the nasolacrimal duct drains into the meatuses: the site of discharge on endoscopy points to its source. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~s2-1","Allergic rhinitis","Approach to a runny nose · Bilateral rhinorrhoea","Allergic rhinitis ( Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis ) is an IgE-mediated inflammation producing sneezing, watery discharge, itch of the nose and eyes, and blockage. The ARIA classification divides it by duration into intermittent (fewer than four days a week or for less than four consecutive weeks) and persistent , and by severity into mild and moderate to severe , depending on effects on sleep, daily activities, work and troublesome symptoms. It is diagnosed from the history and confirmed with skin-prick tests or specific IgE. A subgroup has local allergic rhinitis , with typical symptoms and negative skin tests but IgE in the nasal mucosa. Treatment follows the ARIA steps: allergen avoidance and saline; a non-sedating oral or topical antihistamine for mild intermittent disease; an intranasal steroid, or combined steroid and antihistamine spray, for persistent or moderate to severe disease; and allergen immunotherapy , sublingual or subcutaneous, for selected patients whose allergy is confirmed and who do not respond. Leukotriene antagonists help some patients with asthma. Depot and intranasal steroid injections are not recommended."],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~s2-2","Non-allergic rhinitis","Approach to a runny nose · Bilateral rhinorrhoea","Non-allergic rhinitis produces similar symptoms without atopy. It includes vasomotor (idiopathic) rhinitis, triggered by temperature change, strong smells and alcohol; gustatory rhinitis with eating, especially hot or spicy food; rhinitis of the elderly , a profuse clear drip; hormonal rhinitis in pregnancy and hypothyroidism; drug-induced rhinitis from decongestant overuse, antihypertensives, the oral contraceptive pill, aspirin and NSAIDs; occupational rhinitis; and NARES (non-allergic rhinitis with eosinophilia syndrome), in which nasal eosinophilia without atopy may precede polyps, asthma and aspirin sensitivity. When watery discharge dominates, ipratropium bromide spray, an anticholinergic, used before meals or triggers, is the drug of choice. Intranasal steroids help NARES and congestion. For intractable cases, the parasympathetic supply can be interrupted surgically: vidian neurectomy is effective but risks a dry eye, so the more selective posterior nasal nerve procedures (neurectomy, cryoablation or radiofrequency) are now preferred."],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~s2-3","Infective rhinitis and rhinosinusitis","Approach to a runny nose · Bilateral rhinorrhoea","The common cold is the commonest cause of all and settles in about ten days. Discoloured discharge with blockage, facial pain or smell loss for more than twelve weeks is chronic rhinosinusitis and is managed on its own pathway; shorter episodes are acute rhinosinusitis ."],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~s3-1","CSF rhinorrhoea","Approach to a runny nose · Unilateral rhinorrhoea","CSF rhinorrhoea ( CSF rhinorrhoea ) is a communication between the subarachnoid space and the nose. About 80 to 90% are traumatic , from head injury or surgery; the rest are non-traumatic , and many of these are spontaneous leaks in women with obesity and raised intracranial pressure (idiopathic intracranial hypertension), often through the cribriform plate or a lateral sphenoid recess. Spontaneous leaks have the highest recurrence rates. The discharge is clear, watery and unilateral, worse on bending forward (the teapot sign ) or straining, and may run backwards when lying down. It does not cause sneezing or itchy eyes. Diagnosis is confirmed by beta-2 transferrin or beta-trace protein in the fluid; the \"halo\" and glucose tests are unreliable. High-resolution CT shows the bony defect, MRI shows any encephalocele or herniated meninges, and CT cisternography or intrathecal fluorescein at surgery helps find small or intermittent leaks. Traumatic leaks are first treated conservatively, with bed rest, head elevation, stool softeners and avoidance of nose blowing, and a lumbar drain if needed; many close within a week. Persistent and spontaneous leaks are repaired endoscopically with a graft or a nasoseptal flap, and spontaneous leaks need their raised pressure treated to prevent recurrence. Pneumococcal vaccination is recommended. Figure 2 Sites of CSF leakage into the nose and ear. Dhingra, figs 29.4 and 29.5"],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~s3-2","Other unilateral causes","Approach to a runny nose · Unilateral rhinorrhoea","A foreign body in a child, or after dental or sinus surgery in an adult, gives a foul unilateral discharge. Dental (odontogenic) sinusitis and a fungal ball ( Fungal sinusitis ) affect one maxillary sinus, often with a foul smell. Choanal atresia ( Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses ) occasionally presents with unilateral discharge. Tumours , inverted papilloma and sinonasal malignancy ( Sinonasal tumours ), produce discharge with blockage, bleeding and pain. Unilateral watery discharge with tearing during attacks of severe orbital pain is cluster headache ."],["ev","cl:t-ap_rhin~c4","Approach to a runny nose: current evidence","Approach to a runny nose · Pathway","Allergic rhinitis. The ARIA steps above follow the 2020 update, whose combined spray is azelastine–fluticasone (Bousquet et al., J Allergy Clin Immunol 2020)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~c4","Pathway","Approach to a runny nose · Pathway","Discharge to diagnosis. The runny nose 1 Which side? bilateral step 2 unilateral step 3 2 Bilateral — itch, sneezing, triggers? atopic, itchy allergic rhinitis — avoidance, antihistamine, steroid spray, immunotherapy watery, triggered by cold, food or age non-allergic — ipratropium discoloured with smell loss over 12 weeks chronic rhinosinusitis decongestant overuse stop it 3 Unilateral — clear and watery? yes collect for beta-2 transferrin, CT for defect, MRI foul endoscopy, remove foreign body, dental review, CT bloody endoscopy and imaging, biopsy 4 CSF leak confirmed traumatic conservative first, repair if persisting spontaneous endoscopic repair and treat raised pressure"],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to a runny nose · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Unilateral clear rhinorrhoea is CSF until proven otherwise; beta-2 transferrin. The ARIA classification and stepwise treatment of allergic rhinitis. Non-allergic rhinitis subtypes, NARES and ipratropium. Vidian and posterior nasal nerve surgery: what they do and the dry eye risk. Spontaneous CSF leak and idiopathic intracranial hypertension."],["sec","cl:t-ap_rhin~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to a runny nose · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a unilateral watery discharge as rhinitis for months. Relying on glucose testing to diagnose CSF. Giving steroid sprays for rhinorrhoea in the elderly, where ipratropium works better. Repairing a spontaneous leak without addressing raised intracranial pressure."],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s1-1","Sorting the swelling","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · The pattern","Salivary swelling by pattern Pattern Think of Clues Recurrent, swelling at meals Stone or stricture Submandibular more than parotid; pain with eating, settles in hours Acute, painful, red, fever Acute bacterial sialadenitis Elderly, dehydrated, postoperative; pus from the duct Acute, bilateral, with malaise in a child or young adult Mumps or other viral parotitis Contacts; unvaccinated; orchitis Recurrent parotid swelling in a child Juvenile recurrent parotitis Episodes settle by puberty; sialectasis on ultrasound Bilateral, painless, persistent Sialosis, Sjögren's syndrome, IgG4-related disease, sarcoidosis, HIV Dry eyes and mouth; alcohol, diabetes; cysts Discrete persistent lump Neoplasm Painless, slow (benign); pain, fixity, palsy, nodes (malignant) Red flags for malignancy Facial nerve weakness with a parotid lump. Pain, rapid growth, fixity to skin or deep tissues, skin ulceration or trismus. Cervical lymphadenopathy. A lump in the submandibular, sublingual or minor salivary glands, where the proportion of malignant tumours is higher. A parapharyngeal mass pushing the tonsil medially (deep lobe tumour). Sjögren's syndrome with a new persistent swelling: lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s2-1","History","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · History and examination","Ask how long the swelling has been present, whether it fluctuates or is constant, whether it is related to eating, whether it is painful, and whether it is one gland or several. Ask about a bad taste or pus in the mouth, dry eyes and dry mouth, joint pains, fever, weight loss and night sweats, alcohol intake, diabetes, medications (anticholinergics, diuretics), radiotherapy, HIV risk, and previous skin cancers of the face and scalp, which metastasise to parotid nodes."],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s2-2","Examination","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · History and examination","Inspect and palpate the gland, bimanually for the submandibular gland and the floor of mouth, feeling for a stone along Wharton's duct. Look at the duct openings: the parotid (Stensen's) duct opposite the upper second molar and the submandibular duct beside the frenulum; massage the gland to see whether clear saliva, cloudy saliva or pus comes out. Test every branch of the facial nerve, examine the oropharynx for medial displacement of the tonsil, check the neck for nodes, and inspect the skin of the face and scalp. Figure 1 First, the parotid gland and Stensen's duct. Second, the sublingual and submandibular glands with their ducts. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s3-1","Imaging and tissue","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · Investigation","Ultrasound is the first-line investigation for almost all salivary swellings. It shows stones, duct dilatation, sialectasis, diffuse inflammatory change, cysts and solid masses, and guides fine-needle aspiration cytology or core biopsy . Cytology is reported using the Milan system, which grades the risk of malignancy; core biopsy gives more tissue when cytology is inconclusive or lymphoma is suspected. MRI defines the extent of a tumour, deep lobe involvement and perineural spread; CT shows stones and bony invasion; and sialography or MR sialography shows ducts and strictures. Sialendoscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic for obstruction. Blood tests include inflammatory markers, autoantibodies (anti-Ro, anti-La) for Sjögren's syndrome, serum IgG4, ACE and HIV when indicated."],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~c4","Causes","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · Causes","Obstruction, infection, systemic, neoplasm. The non-neoplastic causes are described in full in Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings ."],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s4-1","Stones and strictures","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · Causes","About 80 to 90% of salivary stones occur in the submandibular gland, because its saliva is more alkaline and mucous and its duct runs uphill. Patients have meal-time syndrome : pain and swelling with eating that settle over hours. Stones near the duct opening are removed transorally; stones up to about 4 to 5 mm can be retrieved with a sialendoscope basket, and larger ones fragmented by laser or extracorporeal lithotripsy or removed by a combined endoscopic and open approach. Gland excision is reserved for failure. Parotid problems more often come from strictures, which are dilated endoscopically. Figure 2 Submandibular sialadenitis, and a radio-opaque stone in the submandibular duct. Dhingra, figs 45.2 and 45.3"],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s4-2","Infection","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · Causes","Acute bacterial sialadenitis , usually Staphylococcus aureus , occurs in dehydrated, elderly or postoperative patients with reduced salivary flow; treatment is rehydration, sialogogues, massage, oral hygiene and antibiotics, with drainage of any abscess. Mumps causes bilateral painful parotitis with malaise and is managed supportively. Juvenile recurrent parotitis causes repeated parotid swellings in children and usually resolves by puberty."],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s4-3","Systemic and infiltrative causes","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · Causes","Sjögren's syndrome causes dry eyes, dry mouth and parotid swelling, and carries a markedly raised risk of MALT lymphoma. IgG4-related disease causes firm bilateral submandibular swellings (Küttner tumour) that respond to steroids. Sarcoidosis can cause parotid swelling; with uveitis, fever and facial palsy it forms Heerfordt's syndrome. HIV ( HIV and ENT ) causes lymphoepithelial cysts. Sialosis is a painless bilateral enlargement associated with alcohol, diabetes, malnutrition and drugs."],["ev","cl:t-ap_saliv~s4-4","Approach to a swollen salivary gland: current evidence","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · Causes","Systemic disease. The ACR/EULAR 2016 criteria classify Sjögren's syndrome (Shiboski et al., Arthritis Rheumatol 2017), and the ACR/EULAR 2019 criteria classify IgG4-related disease (Wallace et al., Ann Rheum Dis 2020), which commonly causes bilateral submandibular swelling and responds to steroids."],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s4-4","Tumours","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · Causes","Most salivary tumours arise in the parotid, and about 80% of parotid tumours are benign, most commonly pleomorphic adenoma , followed by Warthin's tumour (bilateral in a minority, in older male smokers). The tumours are described in full in Salivary gland tumours . The smaller the gland, the more likely a tumour is malignant: about half of submandibular tumours and most sublingual and minor salivary gland tumours are malignant. Malignant tumours include mucoepidermoid carcinoma (the commonest), adenoid cystic carcinoma (with perineural spread), acinic cell carcinoma, salivary duct carcinoma and carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma, as well as metastases from cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma and melanoma in parotid nodes ( Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck ). Benign parotid tumours are removed by superficial or partial parotidectomy, or extracapsular dissection, preserving the facial nerve; malignant tumours are managed by the head and neck multidisciplinary team. The swollen salivary gland 1 Pattern meal-related stone or stricture — ultrasound, sialendoscopy acute painful with pus bacterial sialadenitis — fluids, antibiotics, drain abscess bilateral painless bloods for Sjögren's, IgG4, sarcoid, HIV discrete lump step 2 2 Discrete lump ultrasound with fine-needle cytology or core biopsy MRI if malignant, deep or fixed 3 Red flags? facial weakness, pain, fixity, nodes treat as malignant — MDT benign cytology parotidectomy or extracapsular dissection, or observation in selected patients 4 Sjögren's with persistent swelling biopsy to exclude lymphoma Figure 3 Squamous "],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Pattern recognition: meal-time, acute, bilateral and discrete swellings. Why stones form in the submandibular gland. Ultrasound and fine-needle cytology as first-line tests; the Milan system. The proportion of malignancy by gland. Facial palsy with a parotid lump means malignancy. Sjögren's syndrome and lymphoma risk."],["sec","cl:t-ap_saliv~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to a swollen salivary gland · For the examination","Common pitfalls Performing an open biopsy of a parotid lump, which risks seeding a pleomorphic adenoma and the facial nerve. Missing a cutaneous primary in a patient with a parotid node. Removing a submandibular gland for a stone that could be removed endoscopically."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~c1","Terms and anatomy","Approach to loss of smell · Terms and anatomy","What can go wrong, and where. The physiology of smell is described in Nasal physiology and disorders of smell ."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s1-1","Definitions","Approach to loss of smell · Terms and anatomy","Anosmia is total loss of smell; hyposmia is a reduced sense of smell. Parosmia is a distorted perception of a familiar odour, usually unpleasant (coffee smells of sewage); phantosmia is perceiving a smell that is not there. Parosmia is often a sign that the olfactory nerve is regenerating and carries a reasonable prognosis."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s1-2","How smell works","Approach to loss of smell · Terms and anatomy","The olfactory epithelium lies in the narrow olfactory clefts, on the upper septum, the superior turbinate and the cribriform plate. About 10 to 15% of inspired air reaches it with each breath, more with sniffing. The receptor neurons send axons through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb and regenerate throughout life from basal stem cells, which is why recovery is possible. Retronasal smell, from food in the mouth reaching the nose, makes up most of what patients call flavour, so a patient with anosmia often complains of losing taste. Do not miss Unilateral loss with nasal blockage or bleeding: a sinonasal tumour , including olfactory neuroblastoma. Loss with headache, personality change or visual symptoms: a frontal or olfactory groove meningioma ( Skull base and the parapharyngeal space ). Phantosmia in brief episodes: temporal lobe seizures. Gradual loss in an older adult with tremor or memory problems: Parkinson's disease or dementia, in which smell loss can come years first. Absent smell from birth with delayed puberty: Kallmann syndrome. Figure 1 First, the olfactory region of the nasal cavity. Second, the Keros classification of the olfactory fossa. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s2-1","History","Approach to loss of smell · History and examination","Ask whether the loss came on suddenly or gradually, whether it fluctuates, and what preceded it: a cold or COVID-19, a head injury, nasal surgery. Fluctuating loss that improves with steroids is conductive and inflammatory, typically chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps. Sudden loss after a viral infection or a blow to the head is sensorineural. Ask about distortions and phantom smells, nasal symptoms, smoking, chemical exposure, drugs, neurological symptoms and family history, and about safety at home (smoke alarms, gas detectors, food dates)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s2-2","Examination and testing","Approach to loss of smell · History and examination","Examine the nose with an endoscope, paying particular attention to the olfactory clefts, which may be blocked by polyps or oedema when the rest of the nose looks normal. Examine the cranial nerves. Smell should be measured, not estimated. Validated tests include the 40-item University of Pennsylvania Smell Identification Test (UPSIT) , the Sniffin' Sticks (threshold, discrimination and identification, giving a TDI score) and short screening tests. Smell tests also detect malingering, because a patient who scores well below chance must be recognising the odours to avoid them."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s2-3","Imaging","Approach to loss of smell · History and examination","MRI of the anterior skull base and brain is indicated for unexplained loss, unilateral loss, or neurological symptoms; it may show reduced olfactory bulb volume. CT of the sinuses is indicated when chronic rhinosinusitis or a sinonasal mass is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~c3","Causes","Approach to loss of smell · Causes","Conductive and sensorineural. Causes of smell loss Type Cause Features Conductive Chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps ( Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps ) Fluctuating, improves with steroids; commonest cause in clinic Conductive Allergic rhinitis, septal deviation, tumours Blockage; unilateral if a mass Sensorineural Post-viral, including COVID-19 Sudden after infection; parosmia common Sensorineural Head injury Shearing of olfactory fibres at the cribriform plate; worse with occipital blows Sensorineural Neurodegeneration Parkinson's (about 90% at diagnosis), Alzheimer's Sensorineural Ageing (presbyosmia) Common over 65 Sensorineural Toxins and drugs Smoking, chemicals, radiotherapy, some drugs Sensorineural Congenital Kallmann syndrome, isolated congenital anosmia Other Idiopathic About a fifth of specialist clinic patients"],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s3-1","Post-viral and COVID-19 smell loss","Approach to loss of smell · Causes","Viral damage to the supporting cells and receptor neurons is the commonest sensorineural cause. COVID-19 typically caused a sudden, complete anosmia that patients noticed sharply, and most recovered within weeks, but a substantial minority were left with lasting loss and parosmia."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s3-2","Post-traumatic smell loss","Approach to loss of smell · Causes","Head injury shears the olfactory fibres at the cribriform plate or bruises the olfactory bulbs and frontal lobes. Recovery is less likely than after viral loss; only about a third to a half notice improvement over years."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s4-1","Conductive loss","Approach to loss of smell · Management","Chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps is treated with saline and topical steroids, preferably as drops in the head-down position, with a short course of oral steroids that also shows whether the loss is reversible. Endoscopic sinus surgery ( Nose and sinus operations: principles ) opens the olfactory clefts, and biologics (such as dupilumab) can restore smell in severe polyp disease."],["ev","cl:t-ap_smell~s4-2","Approach to loss of smell: current evidence","Approach to loss of smell · Management","Guidance. The 2023 international position paper recommends psychophysical testing (such as Sniffin' Sticks or UPSIT) rather than self-report, endoscopy, and MRI only when the cause is unclear or other neurological features exist; it recommends olfactory training for post-viral and other smell loss, and advises against routine systemic steroids except in chronic rhinosinusitis (Whitcroft et al., Rhinology 2023)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s4-2","Sensorineural loss","Approach to loss of smell · Management","Olfactory training is the best-supported treatment: the patient sniffs four strong odours (traditionally rose, lemon, eucalyptus and clove) for about 20 seconds each, twice a day, for at least three to six months. Changing the odours every few months may improve results. Oral steroids are most likely to help early after a viral or traumatic loss, and topical steroids when the olfactory cleft is swollen. Other treatments, including omega-3 supplements, vitamin A drops and platelet-rich plasma, have limited evidence. Every patient needs safety advice: smoke and gas detectors, checking food dates, and care with personal hygiene. Recovery after a viral loss can take years, and parosmia often signals regeneration. Smell loss 1 Red flags? unilateral with blockage or bleeding endoscopy and imaging, biopsy neurological symptoms MRI and neurology 2 Endoscopy of the olfactory clefts polyps or oedema conductive — steroids, chronic rhinosinusitis pathway, surgery or biologic normal sensorineural, step 3 3 Measure smell anosmia or hyposmia confirmed identify the cause — viral, trauma, ageing, neurological, congenital scores below chance consider non-organic loss 4 Sensorineural loss olfactory training for at least three to six months oral and topical steroids early safety advice MRI if no clear cause"],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to loss of smell · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Conductive versus sensorineural loss; the olfactory cleft. Anosmia, hyposmia, parosmia and phantosmia. Validated smell tests: UPSIT and Sniffin' Sticks. Olfactory training: the method and evidence. Smell loss as an early sign of Parkinson's disease. Safety advice for patients with anosmia."],["sec","cl:t-ap_smell~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to loss of smell · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not examining the olfactory clefts. Telling a patient with post-viral loss that nothing can be done, when training helps. Missing a unilateral tumour behind \"polyps\"."],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s1-1","The first questions","Approach to a sore throat · Acute or chronic?","An acute sore throat lasts days to two weeks and is usually infective. A chronic sore throat persists beyond three weeks and has a different list: reflux, chronic tonsillitis, tonsilloliths, postnasal drip, smoking, voice misuse, and malignancy. In either case the first judgement is about the airway and the swallow. Red flags in sore throat Drooling, inability to swallow saliva, muffled voice, stridor or sitting forward: supraglottitis or a deep neck abscess — protect the airway, do not lie the patient flat, do not examine the throat with a tongue depressor in a child. Trismus with unilateral swelling and uvular deviation: quinsy. Neck swelling, torticollis or reduced neck movement, especially in a child: retropharyngeal or parapharyngeal abscess. Persistent sore throat with fever, rigors and neck tenderness after tonsillitis: Lemierre's syndrome. Sore throat in a patient taking carbimazole, clozapine or chemotherapy: neutropenia — urgent full blood count. Persistent unilateral sore throat, referred otalgia, dysphagia, hoarseness, weight loss or a neck lump, especially in a smoker: cancer until proven otherwise."],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s2-1","History and examination","Approach to a sore throat · The acute sore throat","Ask about the duration, fever, ability to swallow food and fluids, voice change, breathing, earache and trismus, and about cough and coryza, which point to a viral cause. Examine the oropharynx, the tonsils, the neck nodes and the airway, and, when the history suggests supraglottitis, examine the larynx with a flexible endoscope in a safe setting rather than with a tongue depressor. Figure 1 First, the base of the tongue and valleculae. Second, the structures seen in the oropharynx. Dhingra, figs 47.6 and 47.7"],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s2-2","Who needs antibiotics?","Approach to a sore throat · The acute sore throat","Most acute sore throats are viral, and antibiotics shorten symptoms by only about a day in bacterial tonsillitis. Scoring systems identify patients more likely to have streptococcal infection. FeverPAIN scores one point each for fever, purulence, attending rapidly (within three days), inflamed tonsils and no cough or coryza: a score of 0 to 1 needs no antibiotic, 2 to 3 a delayed or no antibiotic, and 4 to 5 an immediate antibiotic or a delayed one. The Centor criteria (tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes, fever and absence of cough) are used similarly, with antibiotics considered at three or four. Phenoxymethylpenicillin for five to ten days is the first choice, with clarithromycin in penicillin allergy. Amoxicillin is avoided because it causes a rash in infectious mononucleosis. Admission is needed for patients who cannot swallow fluids, are dehydrated, have a suspected collection or airway compromise, or are systemically unwell. They need intravenous fluids, analgesia, intravenous benzylpenicillin and often a single dose of dexamethasone."],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s2-3","The important acute causes","Approach to a sore throat · The acute sore throat","Acute tonsillitis is viral or bacterial (group A streptococcus is the commonest bacterium). Infectious mononucleosis from Epstein–Barr virus gives a severe exudative tonsillitis with palatal petechiae, generalised lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly; it is confirmed by a monospot or EBV serology and atypical lymphocytes. Patients avoid contact sports for several weeks because of splenic rupture, and steroids are used for airway compromise. Peritonsillar abscess (quinsy) ( Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess , Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling ) produces trismus, a hot potato voice, unilateral swelling above the tonsil and a deviated uvula; it is drained by needle aspiration or incision. Supraglottitis ( Croup and epiglottitis ) produces severe odynophagia with relatively normal oropharynx, a muffled voice and drooling. Deep neck space infections need contrast CT and drainage. Lemierre's syndrome is a septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein, usually with Fusobacterium necrophorum , after a sore throat. Other causes ( Acute and chronic pharyngitis ) include HIV seroconversion, diphtheria in the unvaccinated, gonococcal pharyngitis, and herpangina or hand, foot and mouth disease in children. Figure 2 First, the spaces around the pharynx where abscesses form. Second, the deep spaces of the head and neck. Dhingra, figs 52.6 and 52.9"],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s3-1","Causes","Approach to a sore throat · The chronic sore throat","Causes of a chronic sore throat Cause Clues Chronic or recurrent tonsillitis Repeated episodes, halitosis, enlarged nodes Tonsilloliths Halitosis, white stones, foreign body sensation Laryngopharyngeal reflux ( Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections ) Throat clearing, hoarseness, globus, morning symptoms Postnasal drip ( Approach to postnasal drip and catarrh ) Nasal symptoms, throat clearing Smoking, alcohol, dry mouth History; mouth breathing; drugs Voice misuse and muscle tension Pain with voice use Glossopharyngeal neuralgia and Eagle syndrome ( Approach to earache ) Sharp pain triggered by swallowing; elongated styloid Malignancy ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ) Unilateral, progressive, otalgia, dysphagia, neck node, smoker Figure 3 An elongated styloid process (Eagle syndrome): (A) on radiograph, (B) excised. Dhingra, fig. 53.6"],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s3-2","Assessment","Approach to a sore throat · The chronic sore throat","Every adult with a sore throat lasting more than three weeks, especially if unilateral, needs a full head and neck examination with flexible endoscopy of the nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx and larynx, palpation of the tongue base and tonsils, and examination of the neck. Asymmetrical tonsils in an adult, a firm or ulcerated tonsil, or a neck node lead to biopsy or tonsillectomy for histology, and imaging."],["ev","cl:t-ap_sore~s4-1","Approach to a sore throat: current evidence","Approach to a sore throat · Tonsillectomy","Who needs antibiotics. The scoring above follows NICE (NG84), which also advises analgesia (paracetamol or ibuprofen) for all. Steroids. A single dose of oral dexamethasone speeds recovery modestly in some adults (Hayward et al., JAMA 2017)."],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s4-1","Indications","Approach to a sore throat · Tonsillectomy","In adults and children, the SIGN criteria for tonsillectomy in recurrent tonsillitis are sore throats due to acute tonsillitis that are disabling and prevent normal functioning, with seven or more well-documented, clinically significant, adequately treated episodes in the preceding year, five or more in each of the preceding two years, or three or more in each of the preceding three years. Other indications include two episodes of quinsy, suspected malignancy, and obstructive sleep-disordered breathing in children. The sore throat 1 Airway and swallow drooling, stridor, muffled voice airway team, endoscopy in a safe setting, admit trismus with unilateral swelling quinsy — drain neck swelling or torticollis contrast CT for deep neck abscess 2 Acute, airway safe FeverPAIN 0–1 no antibiotic 2–3 delayed or none 4–5 penicillin unable to swallow fluids admit, IV penicillin, fluids, steroid on carbimazole or clozapine urgent blood count 3 Glandular fever features? monospot, avoid amoxicillin, no contact sport, steroids for airway compromise 4 Chronic, beyond three weeks full endoscopic examination asymmetric tonsil, unilateral pain or neck node biopsy and imaging recurrent tonsillitis meeting criteria tonsillectomy reflux or postnasal drip treat the cause"],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to a sore throat · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The airway red flags and how to examine safely. FeverPAIN and Centor; antibiotic choice and avoiding amoxicillin. Infectious mononucleosis: diagnosis and advice. Quinsy, deep neck infection and Lemierre's syndrome. SIGN criteria for tonsillectomy. The persistent unilateral sore throat as a cancer symptom."],["sec","cl:t-ap_sore~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to a sore throat · For the examination","Common pitfalls Examining a child with suspected supraglottitis with a tongue depressor. Giving amoxicillin in glandular fever. Missing agranulocytosis in a patient on carbimazole. Treating a unilateral chronic sore throat with repeated antibiotics in a smoker."],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s1-1","Sorting ulcers","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Patterns","Mouth ulcers by pattern Pattern Think of Clues Single, persistent over three weeks Squamous cell carcinoma; traumatic ulcer; tuberculosis; syphilis Induration, rolled edge, fixed; lateral tongue or floor of mouth; risk factors Single, with a clear cause Traumatic ulcer Sharp tooth, denture, bite; heals within two weeks once the cause is removed Recurrent, multiple, healing Recurrent aphthous stomatitis Onset in youth; minor, major or herpetiform Acute, multiple, with fever Viral infection Primary herpes, hand, foot and mouth disease, herpangina Recurrent with genital ulcers and eye disease Behçet's disease Uveitis, skin lesions, pathergy With gut symptoms or anaemia Crohn's disease, coeliac disease, haematinic deficiency Diarrhoea, weight loss, low ferritin, B12 or folate Blisters, erosions and desquamative gingivitis Pemphigus vulgaris, mucous membrane pemphigoid, lichen planus Nikolsky sign, skin or eye involvement, white striae Target lesions and crusted lips Erythema multiforme Recent herpes or drug Drug-related Nicorandil, NSAIDs, methotrexate, chemotherapy, bisphosphonates Timing with the drug Immunosuppressed Neutropenia, HIV ( HIV and ENT ), CMV, fungal Systemic disease or treatment Do not miss A single ulcer over three weeks, especially indurated, with a raised edge, on the lateral tongue or floor of mouth, or with a neck node: urgent biopsy for squamous cell carcinoma. Leukoplakia or erythroplakia ( Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches ) next to an ulcer. Widespread erosions with skin blisters: pemphigus vulgaris — urgent dermatology. Ulcers with eye invol"],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s2-1","History","Approach to a mouth ulcer · History and examination","Ask how long each ulcer lasts and how often they come, the age of onset, the number and size, any trigger (trauma, stress, menstruation, foods, stopping smoking), and whether they scar. Ask about genital ulcers, eye symptoms, rashes, joint pain, bowel symptoms, weight loss, fatigue and fevers, and take a full drug history. Ask about smoking, alcohol, betel quid and HIV risk."],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s2-2","Examination","Approach to a mouth ulcer · History and examination","Note the site, size, number, base and edge of each ulcer, and feel it: induration and fixity suggest malignancy. Look for a sharp tooth or ill-fitting denture, white or red patches, blisters, gingival involvement and lichenoid striae. Examine the neck, skin, eyes and, when relevant, genitalia."],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~c3","Key causes","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Key causes","Recognising each. The oral diseases are described in full in Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis ."],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s3-1","Recurrent aphthous stomatitis","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Key causes","This is the commonest cause of recurrent ulcers, beginning in childhood or adolescence. Minor aphthae are under 1 cm, on non-keratinised mucosa, and heal in 10 to 14 days without scarring. Major aphthae are larger, deeper and last weeks, healing with scarring. Herpetiform ulcers are crops of many pinhead ulcers that coalesce. Triggers include trauma, stress, hormonal changes, sodium lauryl sulphate toothpastes, and deficiencies of iron, vitamin B12 and folate. Patients with recurrent ulcers should have a full blood count, ferritin, B12, folate and coeliac serology. Figure 1 Multiple aphthous ulcers on the uvula and faucial pillars. Dhingra, fig. 43.1"],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s3-2","Malignancy","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Key causes","Oral squamous cell carcinoma presents as a non-healing ulcer, often painless at first, with induration and a raised everted edge. Risk factors are smoking, alcohol, betel quid and chewing tobacco. Biopsy is taken from the edge, including normal tissue. The tongue and the floor of mouth are the highest-risk sites. Figure 2 First, a traumatic ulcerated mass of the tongue from sharp teeth. Second, a carcinoma to compare. Dhingra, figs 43.2 and 43.3"],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s3-3","Mucocutaneous disease","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Key causes","Lichen planus produces white lacy striae with erosions, often bilateral on the buccal mucosa; erosive lichen planus carries a small risk of malignant change and needs follow-up. Pemphigus vulgaris produces fragile blisters that rupture into painful erosions, often starting in the mouth; mucous membrane pemphigoid produces tougher blisters and desquamative gingivitis and can scar the eyes and larynx. Both need biopsy of perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence , as well as routine histology."],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s3-4","Systemic disease","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Key causes","Behçet's disease combines recurrent oral ulcers with genital ulcers, uveitis and skin lesions. Crohn's disease, coeliac disease, SLE, reactive arthritis, cyclic neutropenia and the PFAPA syndrome in children (periodic fever, aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis and adenitis) are other causes. Figure 3 Palatal ulceration in a patient with a white cell count of 2,500 per cubic millimetre during chemotherapy. Dhingra, fig. 43.4"],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s4-1","Investigation","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Investigation and management","Blood tests for recurrent ulcers include a full blood count, ferritin, B12, folate, and coeliac serology; inflammatory markers and an HIV test when indicated. Any persistent single ulcer is biopsied, and perilesional tissue is sent fresh for immunofluorescence when a blistering disease is suspected. Swabs for herpes simplex PCR help in acute vesicular ulcers."],["ev","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s4-2","Approach to a mouth ulcer: current evidence","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Investigation and management","Referral. NICE suggested-cancer guidance (NG12) recommends urgent referral for unexplained oral ulceration lasting more than three weeks, or a persistent lump or red or red-and-white patch. Potentially malignant lesions. Erythroplakia transforms in about a third and leukoplakia in about one in ten (Iocca et al., Head Neck 2020), so biopsy any unexplained red or white patch."],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s4-2","Treatment","Approach to a mouth ulcer · Investigation and management","The cause is treated: a sharp tooth smoothed, a denture adjusted, a deficiency replaced, a causative drug stopped with the prescriber. Aphthous ulcers are managed with chlorhexidine or benzydamine mouthwashes, topical corticosteroids (such as betamethasone dissolved as a mouthwash, or beclometasone spray), avoiding triggers including sodium lauryl sulphate, and colchicine or other systemic agents in severe cases under specialist care. Blistering diseases, Behçet's disease and lichen planus are managed with oral medicine, dermatology and rheumatology colleagues. The mouth ulcer 1 Single and persistent over three weeks? yes urgent biopsy — exclude squamous cell carcinoma single with obvious trauma remove the cause, review in two weeks, biopsy if not healed 2 Recurrent and multiple? bloods full blood count, ferritin, B12, folate, coeliac serology minor or major aphthae topical treatment genital ulcers or eye disease Behçet's disease 3 Blisters, erosions or desquamative gingivitis? biopsy with immunofluorescence pemphigus, pemphigoid, lichen planus — specialist care 4 Acute with fever herpes or other viral infection — supportive care, antiviral if severe on carbimazole or clozapine blood count"],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s5-1","Viva essentials","Approach to a mouth ulcer · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three-week rule and biopsy from the edge. Recurrent aphthous stomatitis: types and blood tests. Pemphigus versus pemphigoid: level of the split, immunofluorescence. Behçet's disease and other systemic causes. Drug-induced ulceration: nicorandil."],["sec","cl:t-ap_ulcer~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to a mouth ulcer · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving repeated topical steroids for a single persistent ulcer that is a cancer. Biopsying only the necrotic centre of an ulcer. Missing iron, B12, folate or coeliac disease behind recurrent ulcers."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s1-1","Definitions","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Overview","Microtia: a small, malformed pinna; anotia is its complete absence. Congenital aural atresia: absence or narrowing of the external canal, usually with a malformed middle ear. The two usually occur together, on the same side."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s1-2","Epidemiology","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Overview","Microtia occurs in roughly 1 in 6,000 to 1 in 12,000 births, more often in boys and on the right. About 10% are bilateral."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s1-3","Embryology","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Overview","The pinna develops from the hillocks around the first branchial cleft, the canal from the first branchial groove, and the malleus and incus from the first and second arches. The inner ear develops separately from the otic placode, so it is usually normal. Figure 1 Development of the external auditory canal and middle ear. Dhingra, fig. 1.25 Figure 2 The temporal bone at birth: an incomplete tympanic ring and no mastoid process. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s1-4","Associated syndromes","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Overview","Treacher Collins, Goldenhar and hemifacial microsomia, Crouzon, and branchio-oto-renal syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s2-1","Microtia","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Classification","Grade I: a recognisable but smaller or deformed pinna. Grade II: a deformed framework with some parts missing. Grade III: a soft-tissue remnant, the peanut ear . Grade IV: anotia. Figure 3 Grade III microtia of the right ear (peanut ear). Dhingra, fig. 8.3"],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s2-2","Middle ear","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Classification","Class I: normal pinna with an ossicular defect, the commonest. Class II: microtia and atresia with an aerated middle ear and mastoid. Class III: poorly aerated, underdeveloped middle ear and mastoid. Pearl A normal pinna with atresia implies the insult happened later in development, after the pinna had formed."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s3-1","Hearing","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Clinical features","Atresia: a conductive loss of about 60 to 70 dB. Ossicular malformation alone: about 25 to 50 dB. Congenital stapes fixation: about 50 to 65 dB. Bilateral atresia threatens speech and language development."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s3-2","Associated findings","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Clinical features","Preauricular tags, pits and sinuses, facial asymmetry, jaw underdevelopment, and syndromic features. Airway problems in some syndromes."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s4-1","Audiology","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Investigations","OAE and ABR from birth, including bone-conduction ABR, to measure cochlear function on each side."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s4-2","Imaging","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Investigations","Thin-section CT of the temporal bones before any surgery, usually around five or six years: the size and aeration of the middle ear and mastoid, the ossicles, the oval and round windows, the inner ear, and the course of the facial nerve. MRI if there is a sensorineural loss."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s4-3","Other tests","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Investigations","Renal ultrasound when preauricular pits accompany neck fistulae (branchio-oto-renal syndrome)."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s5-1","Hearing","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Management","Bilateral atresia: a bone-conduction aid on a headband within the first months, by six months at the latest. Unilateral atresia with a normal other ear: speech usually develops normally, but these children have more educational difficulty than once thought. Preferential seating at school and monitoring; early bone-conduction amplification is increasingly offered. A bone-anchored implant from around five or six years."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s5-2","The pinna","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Management","Reconstruction with autologous rib cartilage in stages, once there is enough cartilage, or a porous polyethylene framework, or an osseointegrated prosthesis. The pinna is reconstructed before any canal surgery. Where the books differ · Age for rib cartilage reconstruction Some texts About five to eight years. Others About eight to ten years. In practice Plan rib cartilage reconstruction for about eight to ten years, when there is enough cartilage; some centres start earlier."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s5-3","The canal","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Management","5.3.1 Selection Canal surgery suits ears with a conductive loss over 30 dB, bone conduction better than 20 dB, and a well-aerated middle ear with a favourable facial nerve on CT. The Jahrsdoerfer score (10 points from CT and the appearance of the pinna) predicts hearing results: a score of 7 or more gives a good chance of a useful result. The pinna is reconstructed first so the canal can be placed correctly. 5.3.2 Results Even in good hands, only about half of patients reach an air–bone gap under 30 dB long term, and canal restenosis and facial nerve risk are real, so bone-conduction devices (headband in infancy, then an implant) are now chosen by most families ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ). 5.3.3 Risks Facial nerve injury, restenosis of the new canal, lateralisation of the graft and sensorineural loss."],["ev","cl:t-atresia~s5-4","Microtia and congenital canal atresia: current evidence","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Management","Candidacy for atresia repair. The Jahrsdoerfer score comes from Jahrsdoerfer et al. ( Am J Otol 1992). Long-term series confirm the modest hearing results of canal surgery, which is why most families now choose bone-conduction devices. Unilateral atresia. Educational difficulty in these children is now recognised, and early amplification is increasingly offered."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s5-4","Preauricular sinus","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · Management","Leave it alone unless it becomes infected; then excise the whole tract ( Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus ). The facial nerve In atresia the facial nerve often runs more anteriorly and laterally than usual, crossing the area where the canal would be drilled. First branchial cleft anomalies also run close to it."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s6-1","On call","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · For the examination","A newborn with bilateral atresia Early audiology with bone-conduction testing. A bone-conduction aid as soon as possible. Look for syndromic features and airway problems."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s6-2","Viva essentials","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Embryology: why the inner ear is spared. Grades of microtia. Hearing before cosmetics, and the timing of each intervention. Selection for canal surgery, and the facial nerve risk."],["sec","cl:t-atresia~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Microtia and congenital canal atresia · For the examination","Common pitfalls Delaying amplification in bilateral atresia. Opening a canal before reconstructing the pinna. Drilling without a CT map of the facial nerve."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s1-1","The family","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Overview","Infective and chronic rhinitis Acute Viral common cold, influenza, exanthemas, COVID-19 Acute Bacterial and irritative non-specific bacterial, diphtheritic, chemical Chronic Simple and hypertrophic swollen or thickened turbinates Chronic Atrophic and crusting atrophic rhinitis (ozaena), rhinitis sicca, rhinitis caseosa Allergic and vasomotor rhinitis are covered in Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis , and the specific granulomatous infections in Granulomatous disease of the nose ."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s2-1","Common cold (coryza)","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Acute rhinitis","2.1.1 Cause Droplet spread of rhinovirus, adenovirus, coxsackie and other enteroviruses and coronaviruses. Incubation is one to four days; the illness lasts two to three weeks. 2.1.2 Clinical picture Burning at the back of the nose, then stuffiness, sneezing and profuse watery rhinorrhoea, chills and low fever. The discharge becomes mucopurulent if bacteria (streptococci, pneumococci, staphylococci, H. influenzae , Moraxella ) invade secondarily. 2.1.3 Treatment Rest, fluids, analgesics (non-aspirin preferred, as aspirin increases viral shedding), antihistamines and short-term decongestants. Antibiotics only for bacterial complications. 2.1.4 Complications Sinusitis ( Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications ), pharyngitis, tonsillitis, otitis media, bronchitis and pneumonia."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s2-2","Other viral rhinitis","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Acute rhinitis","Influenza A, B and C cause a similar but more severe illness, with more frequent bacterial complications. Measles, rubella and chickenpox cause rhinitis two to three days before the rash. COVID-19 begins like a cold and may cause sudden loss of smell and taste; during its second wave it was followed by a surge of rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis in diabetics and steroid-treated patients ( Fungal sinusitis )."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s2-3","Bacterial rhinitis","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Acute rhinitis","2.3.1 Non-specific Primary infection in children with pneumococcus, streptococcus or staphylococcus, sometimes with a greyish-white membrane that bleeds when removed; or secondary infection after a cold. 2.3.2 Diphtheritic Rare with immunisation. A greyish, tenacious membrane over the inferior turbinate and floor that bleeds when removed, with excoriation of the nostrils and upper lip. Isolate; give penicillin or erythromycin and diphtheria antitoxin."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s2-4","Irritative rhinitis","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Acute rhinitis","From dust, smoke, ammonia, formalin or acid fumes, or trauma during intranasal procedures: immediate sneezing, rhinorrhoea and congestion that settle once the irritant is removed."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s3-1","Chronic simple rhinitis","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Chronic simple and hypertrophic rhinitis","3.1.1 Causes Repeated colds with persistent infection in the sinuses, tonsils or adenoids; smoke and dust; nasal obstruction from a deviated septum or synechiae; vasomotor rhinitis; hypothyroidism. 3.1.2 Pathology Hyperaemic, oedematous mucosa with hypertrophied glands, more goblet cells and distended sinusoids over the turbinates: an early stage of hypertrophic rhinitis. 3.1.3 Clinical picture Blockage worse on lying, affecting the dependent side; thick mucoid or mucopurulent discharge and postnasal drip; headache. The turbinates are dull red and swollen, but they pit on pressure and shrink with a vasoconstrictor . 3.1.4 Treatment Treat the cause (sinuses, tonsils, adenoids, allergy, smoking, workplace), saline or alkaline irrigation, short-term decongestants, and antibiotics for infection."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s3-2","Hypertrophic rhinitis","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Chronic simple and hypertrophic rhinitis","Figure 1 The lateral nasal wall with the turbinates. From the anatomy atlas 3.2.1 Pathology Thickening of the mucosa, submucosa, glands, periosteum and bone, most marked on the inferior turbinate. 3.2.2 Causes Recurrent infections, chronic sinusitis, irritants, long-term nasal drops, and vasomotor or allergic rhinitis. 3.2.3 Clinical picture Obstruction is the main symptom, with thick sticky discharge and headache. The turbinate mucosa is thick, does not pit on pressure and shrinks little with vasoconstrictors, because of fibrosis. The inferior turbinate may be enlarged throughout, or at its anterior end, posterior end or lower border, giving a mulberry appearance. 3.2.4 Treatment Remove the cause, then reduce the turbinates: linear cautery, submucosal diathermy, cryosurgery, laser, coblation or radiofrequency, submucous resection of the turbinate bone, or partial turbinectomy. Avoid excessive removal, which leaves crusting and an empty nose."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s3-3","Compensatory hypertrophy","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Chronic simple and hypertrophic rhinitis","With a severely deviated septum, the turbinates on the wider side enlarge to narrow the airway and protect it from drying. The change is not reversible after septoplasty, so the turbinate is often reduced at the same operation. Simple or hypertrophic? Chronic simple rhinitis Hypertrophic rhinitis Mucosa Dull red, oedematous Thick, fibrosed Probe Pits on pressure Does not pit Vasoconstrictor Shrinks well Shrinks little Treatment Medical Turbinate reduction"],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s4-1","Definition","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Atrophic rhinitis","A chronic inflammation with atrophy of the nasal mucosa and turbinate bones. The nasal cavities become roomy and fill with foul-smelling crusts."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s4-2","Primary atrophic rhinitis","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Atrophic rhinitis","Figure 2 The lateral nasal wall: in atrophic rhinitis the turbinates shrink and the cavity widens. From the anatomy atlas 4.2.1 Causes Unknown; several theories (the mnemonic HERNIA ): Hereditary: several family members affected. Endocrine: begins at puberty, commoner in women, tends to settle after the menopause. Racial: commoner in white and East Asian populations than in equatorial Africa. Nutritional: deficiency of vitamins A and D or iron; rarer in well-off families. Infective: Klebsiella ozaenae (Perez bacillus) is the commonest organism cultured, with diphtheroids, Proteus , E. coli and cocci, but these are secondary invaders responsible for the smell. Autoimmune: a destructive reaction to antigens from the nasal mucosa. 4.2.2 Pathology Ciliated columnar epithelium becomes squamous; seromucinous glands, venous sinusoids and nerves atrophy; arteries show obliterative endarteritis; the turbinate bones are resorbed, widening the nose. The sinuses stay small and thick-walled because their development was arrested. 4.2.3 Clinical picture Usually a young woman, starting at puberty. A foul smell that makes the patient a social outcast, though the patient cannot smell it ( merciful anosmia ). Paradoxical nasal obstruction: the nose is wide, but damaged sensory nerves cannot feel the airflow. Epistaxis when crusts are removed. 4.2.4 Signs Greenish or greyish-black crusts cover the turbinates and septum; when removed, the cavities are roomy, the turbinates reduced to ridges, the mucosa pale, and the posterior nasopharyngeal wall easily seen. There may be a septal perforation, "],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s4-3","Treatment","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Atrophic rhinitis","4.3.1 Medical: nasal hygiene Irrigation: warm saline, or an alkaline douche (sodium bicarbonate, sodium biborate and sodium chloride in water), two or three times a day at first, then every few days; loosen and remove hard crusts. 25% glucose in glycerine: painted on after the crusts are removed; it inhibits the proteolytic organisms that cause the smell. Antibiotics: topical, or a 7 to 10 day systemic course (for example a fluoroquinolone with metronidazole) for active infection; not long-term. Mucolytics such as potassium iodide. Diet rich in vitamins A and D, folic acid, iron and protein. 4.3.2 Surgical Young's operation: both nostrils are closed with flaps just inside the vestibule and reopened after six months or more; the mucosa may recover. A modified Young's operation closes them partially. Narrowing the nasal cavities: submucosal implants of fat, cartilage, bone or acellular dermis under the floor, lateral wall and septum, or medial displacement of the lateral wall ( Lautenschlaeger operation )."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s4-4","Secondary atrophic rhinitis","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Atrophic rhinitis","Destruction from syphilis, lupus, leprosy or rhinoscleroma ( Granulomatous disease of the nose ); long-standing purulent sinusitis; radiotherapy; and excessive turbinate surgery (the empty nose syndrome ). A severely deviated septum can cause one-sided atrophic rhinitis on the wide side. Pearls Merciful anosmia: the patient is the only one who cannot smell it. Paradoxical obstruction: the widest nose in clinic, yet it feels blocked. Klebsiella ozaenae is a secondary invader, not the cause."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s5-1","Rhinitis sicca","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Rhinitis sicca and caseosa","A crusting disease of people who work in hot, dry, dusty places (bakers, ironsmiths, goldsmiths), confined to the anterior septum. Squamous metaplasia leads to crusts whose removal causes ulceration, bleeding and perforation. Change the working environment, apply a bland or antibiotic-steroid ointment, use douches, and stop picking."],["ev","cl:t-atrophic~s5-2","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis: current evidence","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Rhinitis sicca and caseosa","Common cold. Cochrane reviews find no benefit from antibiotics (Kenealy and Arroll, 2013), small benefits from nasal decongestants for adults (Deckx et al., 2016) and from antihistamine–decongestant combinations, and little or no benefit from vitamin C taken after symptoms start. Atrophic rhinitis. Evidence for every treatment is limited to case series; topical antibiotics such as aminoglycoside drops are used against Klebsiella ozaenae . Secondary atrophic rhinitis after radical turbinate surgery ( empty nose syndrome ) is the reason modern turbinate reduction preserves mucosa."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s5-2","Rhinitis caseosa","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · Rhinitis sicca and caseosa","Rare, usually one-sided and in men: the nose fills with offensive pus and cheesy material, probably from chronic sinusitis; the sinus walls may be eroded, mimicking malignancy. Remove the debris and granulation and drain the sinus; the outlook is good."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s6-1","Viva essentials","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Chronic simple versus hypertrophic rhinitis: the probe and vasoconstrictor tests. Atrophic rhinitis: theories, pathology, merciful anosmia, paradoxical obstruction, treatment including glucose-glycerine and Young's operation. Diphtheritic rhinitis: membrane and treatment. Rhinitis sicca and caseosa."],["sec","cl:t-atrophic~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Removing too much turbinate and creating an empty nose. Forgetting that a crusted, destroyed nose may be secondary to a granulomatous disease or lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s1-1","The toolkit","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Overview","Hearing tests Bedside Voice and tuning forks conductive or sensorineural, roughly how much Behavioural Pure-tone and speech audiometry thresholds and understanding Middle ear Tympanometry and stapedial reflexes drum, ossicles, tube, reflex arc Objective OAE, ABR, ASSR, ECochG cochlea and neural pathway without a response"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s1-2","Choosing a test","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Overview","Forks at the bedside; audiogram and tympanogram in clinic; OAE and ABR in newborns and uncooperative patients; ASSR for profound loss, beyond the range of ABR."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s2-1","Decibel scales","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Sound and the decibel","dB SPL: sound pressure relative to 20 µPa. dB HL: relative to average normal hearing at each frequency, so 0 dB HL is normal on the audiogram. dB SL: relative to the patient's own threshold."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s2-2","Everyday levels","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Sound and the decibel","Whisper about 30 dB, conversation about 60 dB, a shout about 90 dB, discomfort about 120 dB and pain about 130 dB."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s2-3","How the middle ear amplifies","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Sound and the decibel","The area ratio of the drum to the footplate (about 17 to 1) and the lever action of the ossicles (about 1.3 to 1) give a gain of about 22 times, around 25 dB. Without it, most sound energy would bounce off the fluid of the inner ear."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s2-4","Where frequencies are heard","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Sound and the decibel","The base of the cochlea hears high frequencies, the apex low frequencies. The canal resonates at about 3000 Hz. Figure 1 The organ of Corti, and the central auditory pathway that the ABR waves trace. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s3-1","Voice tests","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Bedside tests","A whisper heard at 6 m is taken as normal; the other ear is masked by rubbing the tragus."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s3-2","Tuning fork tests","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Bedside tests","The 512 Hz fork is the routine choice: lower forks are felt as vibration, higher ones fade too quickly. Figure 2 Tuning fork tests: (A) air conduction, (B) bone conduction, (C) the Weber test. Dhingra, fig. 4.1 3.2.1 Rinne test Compares air conduction with bone conduction in the same ear. Positive (air louder) is normal, and also seen in sensorineural loss; negative (bone louder) means a conductive loss of at least about 20 dB. 3.2.2 Weber test The fork on the forehead or vertex lateralises to the poorer ear in a conductive loss and to the better ear in a sensorineural loss. It is sensitive, lateralising with quite small differences between the ears. 3.2.3 Absolute bone conduction and Schwabach tests Compare the patient's bone conduction with the examiner's (with the canal occluded in the ABC test). Reduced in sensorineural loss. 3.2.4 Bing and Gellé tests Bing: occluding the canal makes the tone louder in normal and sensorineural ears but not in a conductive loss. Gellé: raising canal pressure normally reduces hearing, but not with a fixed or broken chain; tympanometry has replaced it. Reading tuning forks Test Normal Conductive Sensorineural Rinne Air louder Bone louder Air louder Weber Central To poorer ear To better ear ABC Same as examiner Same Reduced Schwabach Equal Lengthened Shortened 3.2.5 Estimating the gap from Rinne A negative Rinne at 256 Hz only suggests a gap of about 20 to 30 dB; at 256 and 512 Hz, 30 to 45 dB; at all three forks, 45 to 60 dB. Where the books differ · Gap that turns each fork negative Dhingra 15 dB at 256, 30 dB at 512 and 45 dB at 1024 Hz."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s4-1","How it is done","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Pure-tone audiometry","In a sound-treated room, the softest tone the patient hears at each frequency from 250 to 8000 Hz is recorded, first by air conduction through headphones, then by bone conduction with a vibrator on the mastoid. Figure 3 A two-room audiometry set-up: the audiologist watches the patient through a glass partition. Dhingra, fig. 4.2"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s4-2","The symbols","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Pure-tone audiometry","Frequency runs across the top from low (250 Hz) to high (8000 Hz), and loudness runs down the side, so a worse hearing threshold sits lower on the chart. By convention the right ear is red and the left ear blue. Air conduction is a circle (right) or a cross (left), joined by a line; bone conduction is an angle bracket pointing away from the ear tested, < for right and > for left, and a square bracket, [ or ], when the other ear was masked. An arrow below a symbol means no response at the machine's maximum output, which is about 120 dB for air but only about 60 to 70 dB for bone. Figure 4 The symbols, and a normal audiogram: every threshold sits in the shaded band of 20 dB HL or better, and air and bone conduction coincide. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s4-3","Reading it, step by step","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Pure-tone audiometry","Read every audiogram in the same order, so nothing is missed. Reading a pure-tone audiogram 1 Check the chart right and left, masked or unmasked, reliability noted by the tester 2 Air conduction normal (20 dB or better) how much loss mild, moderate, severe, profound which frequencies flat, sloping, rising, notched, cookie-bite 3 Bone conduction equal to air sensorineural better than air by more than 10 dB conductive component both raised with a gap mixed 4 Compare the ears symmetrical asymmetric by 15 dB or more at two adjacent frequencies, or 20 dB at one: MRI of the internal auditory meatus 5 Check masking ears differ by 40 dB or more on air, or any air–bone gap masked thresholds needed — was it done? 6 Fit the clinical picture otoscopy, tuning forks and tympanometry must agree if they do not, repeat or think non-organic Bone conduction is cochlear function. The air–bone gap is the size of the conductive loss: a gap of up to about 60 dB is possible from the middle ear alone (complete ossicular discontinuity behind an intact drum), so a larger gap means the bone thresholds are wrong or the loss is mixed. Bone conduction can be better than normal in a third-window lesion such as superior canal dehiscence, giving an apparent conductive loss with normal tympanometry and present reflexes."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s4-4","Masking","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Pure-tone audiometry","Sound presented to one ear can cross the skull and be heard by the other, so the non-test ear is masked with narrow-band noise. Interaural attenuation is 40 to 65 dB for air conduction with headphones (more with insert earphones) and close to 0 dB for bone, because the vibrator shakes the whole skull. Mask whenever the ears differ by 40 dB or more on air conduction, and for bone conduction whenever there is an air–bone gap in the test ear. The usual method is the plateau (Hood) technique : noise in the non-test ear is raised in 10 dB steps while the threshold in the test ear is re-measured; while the threshold keeps rising with the noise, the other ear was answering, and once it stays the same across several steps (the plateau), the true threshold has been found. Without masking, a dead ear produces a shadow curve , a threshold that mirrors the good ear about 40 to 60 dB lower for air and almost level with it for bone. The masking dilemma arises in large bilateral conductive losses, as in bilateral atresia, when the noise needed to mask one ear crosses over and masks the test ear too; insert earphones and ABR by bone conduction help. Figure 5 A right dead ear. Unmasked, the right ear seems to have a 65 to 80 dB loss with near-normal bone conduction: a shadow of the left ear. With the left ear masked, there is no response by bone conduction at the limits of the machine. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s4-5","Repeatability","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Pure-tone audiometry","Normal repeat audiograms agree within about 5 dB, so a change of 10 dB or more at two frequencies is significant. Grades of loss use the average of 500, 1000, 2000 and 4000 Hz in the better ear (the WHO grades are under Current evidence below)."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s5-1","Conductive and mixed losses","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Patterns on the audiogram","Otosclerosis produces a conductive loss, often worse in the low frequencies, with a characteristic dip of bone conduction of about 10 to 15 dB at 2 kHz, the Carhart notch . The notch is mechanical, not cochlear: it disappears after successful stapes surgery. Tympanometry is type A or As, and the stapedial reflexes are absent. Figure 6 Left otosclerosis. Air conduction is about 40 to 50 dB; masked bone conduction is near normal except for the dip at 2 kHz (Carhart notch). Air–bone gap about 35 dB. Drawn for The ENT Companion A mixed loss combines a raised bone conduction with an air–bone gap, as in chronic otitis media with cochlear damage from long-standing infection, otosclerosis with cochlear involvement, or a temporal bone fracture. Both components matter: the gap may be closed by surgery, but the sensorineural part sets the best possible result, so a patient with bone conduction of 40 dB will still need a hearing aid after a perfect tympanoplasty. Figure 7 Right mixed loss. Masked bone conduction is 30 to 55 dB (the sensorineural part), and air conduction 60 to 85 dB (the gap of about 30 dB is the conductive part). Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s5-2","Sensorineural patterns","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Patterns on the audiogram","The shape of a sensorineural loss points to its cause. Sensorineural shapes and what they suggest Shape Typical cause Clues Bilateral, symmetrical, sloping to the high frequencies Presbycusis, ototoxicity Age; aminoglycosides, cisplatin Notch at 3, 4 or 6 kHz with recovery at 8 kHz Noise Occupational or recreational noise; usually bilateral Low-frequency (rising) loss, fluctuating Ménière's disease, early hydrops Episodic vertigo, fullness, roaring tinnitus Mid-frequency \"cookie-bite\" (U-shaped) loss Genetic, congenital Family history; stable Flat, severe, sudden Sudden sensorineural loss, viral, vascular Onset within 72 hours Asymmetric Vestibular schwannoma until proven otherwise; also Ménière's, sudden loss, noise from one side (shooting) MRI of the internal auditory meatus Dead ear Labyrinthitis, meningitis, fracture, surgery Shadow curve without masking Figure 8 Left: presbycusis, a symmetrical high-frequency sloping loss. Right: noise damage, a notch at 4 kHz that recovers at 8 kHz; the recovery separates noise from ageing, although both often coexist. Drawn for The ENT Companion Figure 9 Left: early Ménière's disease in the left ear, a low-frequency (rising) sensorineural loss that fluctuates between tests. Right: an asymmetric right sensorineural loss; with no explanation, this needs an MRI. Drawn for The ENT Companion Figure 10 A mid-frequency \"cookie-bite\" loss, typical of some genetic hearing losses. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s5-3","When the audiogram does not fit","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Patterns on the audiogram","Non-organic (functional) hearing loss is suggested when thresholds vary between tests, the patient converses easily despite a \"severe\" loss, the speech reception threshold is much better than the pure-tone average, a unilateral \"dead ear\" shows no shadow curve, or acoustic reflexes are present at levels near or below the claimed threshold. Objective tests (otoacoustic emissions, ABR or cortical evoked response audiometry) establish the true thresholds; the Stenger test detects a feigned unilateral loss."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s6-1","Speech reception threshold","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Speech audiometry","The level at which half of two-syllable spondee words are repeated correctly; it lies within 10 dB of the pure-tone average."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s6-2","Speech discrimination","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Speech audiometry","Single-syllable words presented 30 to 40 dB above the SRT. Good in conductive loss, reduced in cochlear loss, very poor in retrocochlear loss."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s6-3","Roll-over","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Speech audiometry","Discrimination falling as loudness increases, a sign of a retrocochlear lesion."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s6-4","Reading the speech audiogram","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Speech audiometry","The speech audiogram plots the percentage of words repeated correctly against loudness. In a conductive loss the curve has a normal shape but is shifted to the right: once the sound is loud enough, the patient understands everything. In a cochlear loss the curve is shifted and may never reach 100%, because the damaged cochlea distorts the sound. In a retrocochlear loss the maximum is poor and falls again at high levels (roll-over), and speech discrimination is much worse than the pure-tone thresholds would suggest. Speech scores matter clinically: they predict hearing aid benefit and help select candidates for cochlear implants, which are assessed with sentence tests in quiet and in noise. Figure 11 Speech audiogram patterns: normal, conductive (shifted but reaching 100%), cochlear (shifted and reduced maximum) and retrocochlear (poor maximum with roll-over). Drawn for The ENT Companion Figure 12 Speech audiogram. Dhingra, fig. 4.3"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s7-1","Tympanometry","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Impedance audiometry","A probe in the canal changes the pressure while measuring how much sound is reflected from the drum. The peak of compliance shows the middle ear pressure and the mobility of the system. Figure 13 First, impedance audiometry in progress. Second, the principle of the impedance audiometer. Dhingra, figs 4.4 and 4.5 Tympanogram types Type Finding Think of A Normal peak Normal ear As Low peak Otosclerosis, malleus fixation Ad High peak Ossicular discontinuity, lax drum B Flat Fluid, thick drum; perforation or patent grommet if the volume is high C Peak below −100 mm H2O Retraction, tube dysfunction Figure 14 Types of tympanogram. Dhingra, fig. 4.6 Figure 15 Reading a tympanogram: the peak height (compliance), the peak position (middle ear pressure) and the ear canal volume (ECV). The shaded box is the normal range, roughly −100 to +50 daPa and 0.3 to 1.5 ml. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s7-2","Reading a tympanogram","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Impedance audiometry","Read three numbers, not just the shape. The peak pressure shows the middle ear pressure; normal is about −100 to +50 daPa (1 daPa is about 1 mm H₂O). The peak compliance shows how mobile the drum and chain are; normal in adults is about 0.3 to 1.5 ml. The ear canal volume is the air between the probe and the drum; normal is about 0.6 to 1.5 ml in adults and 0.3 to 0.9 ml in children. A flat tympanogram: what the volume tells you Ear canal volume Meaning Normal Middle ear fluid or a thickened, immobile drum High (over about 2 ml adults, 1 ml children) Perforation or a patent ventilation tube Low Wax or debris against the drum, or the probe against the canal wall An ear canal volume over about 2.5 mL in adults (2 mL in children) is a strong sign of a perforation or patent grommet. Infants under six months need a 1000 Hz probe tone, because the soft canal wall makes the standard 226 Hz test unreliable. A patulous Eustachian tube produces a trace that moves with breathing; a glomus tumour can produce pulsations in time with the heartbeat."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s7-3","Stapedial (acoustic) reflex","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Impedance audiometry","A loud sound, 70 to 100 dB above threshold, contracts the stapedius in both ears. Figure 16 The acoustic reflex pathway. Dhingra, fig. 4.7 7.3.1 What reflexes tell you Reflexes are measured in four conditions: ipsilateral and contralateral for each ear. Record the stimulus ear and the probe ear carefully: a contralateral right reflex means sound in the right ear, response measured in the left. A reflex at only 40 to 60 dB above threshold: recruitment, a cochlear lesion. Reflex decay to half within 10 seconds: an eighth nerve lesion. Absent with normal hearing: a facial nerve lesion above the nerve to stapedius. Present on the same side but absent on the other: a brainstem lesion. Absent: stapes fixation or any middle ear disease. Reflex patterns and the site of the lesion Finding Site Absent whenever the probe is in the affected ear Middle ear on that side (the probe cannot detect a reflex) Absent whenever the affected ear is stimulated Severe cochlear loss or eighth nerve lesion on that side Absent whenever the probe is in the affected ear, with normal hearing Facial nerve on that side, above the nerve to stapedius Ipsilateral reflexes present, both contralateral reflexes absent Brainstem lesion crossing the midline Present with an apparent severe loss Non-organic loss"],["sec","cl:t-audio~c8","Site-of-lesion tests","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Site-of-lesion tests","Cochlear or retrocochlear? Older behavioural tests, now largely replaced by MRI: Alternate binaural loudness balance: recruitment in cochlear loss. Short increment sensitivity index (SISI): high scores in cochlear loss. Tone decay: over 25 dB in retrocochlear loss. Bekesy audiometry. Figure 17 Alternate binaural loudness balance test for recruitment. Dhingra, fig. 4.8"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s9-1","Otoacoustic emissions","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Objective tests","Sounds produced by the outer hair cells and recorded in the canal. Transient emissions disappear with losses over about 30 dB, distortion-product emissions over about 50 dB. Absent with middle ear fluid or debris; present in auditory neuropathy. Used for newborn screening. Results are reported as pass (clear response) or refer . A refer is not a diagnosis of deafness: it may mean vernix or fluid in the canal, a noisy baby or environment, or a true loss, which is why screening repeats the test and then uses automated ABR. Because emissions reflect outer hair cell function, they can show early damage from noise or ototoxic drugs before the audiogram changes, and they monitor patients on cisplatin."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s9-2","Auditory brainstem response","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Objective tests","Five to seven waves recorded from the scalp within 10 ms of a click: I and II from the eighth nerve, III the cochlear nucleus, IV the superior olive, V the lateral lemniscus and inferior colliculus ( E. COLI ). Used for thresholds in infants (pass at about 30 to 40 dB nHL) and to look for retrocochlear lesions, though it misses small tumours. Unaffected by sleep and sedation. Figure 18 Brainstem auditory evoked potentials. Dhingra, fig. 4.10"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s9-3","Reading an ABR","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Objective tests","Look at three things. Wave latencies: wave V normally appears at about 5.5 to 6 ms with a loud click. Interpeak intervals: I–III about 2 ms, III–V about 2 ms, and I–V about 4 ms; the I–V interval measures conduction along the nerve and brainstem and is independent of the middle ear . Threshold: the softest level at which wave V can still be identified, used to estimate hearing in infants. In a conductive loss all the waves are delayed but the I–V interval is normal; in a cochlear loss wave I may be small or absent and the latency of V falls steeply as loudness rises (recruitment); in a retrocochlear lesion the I–V interval is prolonged or the waves after I are lost, and wave V on the affected side is delayed compared with the other ear. Figure 19 ABR traces. Conductive loss delays every wave; a retrocochlear lesion prolongs the I–V interval. Values are typical, not normative. Drawn for The ENT Companion Where the books differ · Abnormal interaural wave V difference Dhingra Over 0.2 ms. Lee 0.4 to 0.5 ms. In practice Treat a clearly prolonged difference as suspicious and get an MRI, which has replaced ABR for finding tumours ( Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours )."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s9-4","Auditory steady-state response","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Objective tests","Frequency-specific thresholds into the profound range, beyond the reach of ABR (about 80 dB)."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s9-5","Electrocochleography","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Objective tests","Records the summating potential, action potential and cochlear microphonic. An enlarged SP to AP ratio suggests Ménière's disease . Figure 20 Electrocochleography: normal ear and an ear with Ménière's disease. Dhingra, fig. 4.9 Figure 21 Reading electrocochleography: the summating potential (SP) is the shoulder before the sharp action potential (AP). A raised SP/AP ratio suggests endolymphatic hydrops (texts put the cut-off between about 0.3 and 0.4 to 0.5); a normal result does not exclude it. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-audio~s9-6","Auditory neuropathy","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Objective tests","Present otoacoustic emissions and cochlear microphonic with an absent or abnormal ABR: the hair cells work but the nerve does not carry the signal synchronously."],["sec","cl:t-audio~c10","Putting the tests together","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Putting the tests together","Patterns in common conditions. The test battery in common conditions Condition Tuning forks Audiogram Tympanogram Reflexes Other Wax or otitis media with effusion Rinne negative, Weber to the ear Conductive, flat or low-frequency B, normal volume (effusion) Absent OAE absent Perforation Rinne negative if gap over about 20 dB Conductive B, high volume Absent — Otosclerosis Rinne negative Conductive, Carhart notch A or As Absent Bone better than normal at 2 kHz after surgery Ossicular discontinuity Rinne negative Conductive, up to 60 dB Ad Absent — Superior canal dehiscence Weber to the ear Low-frequency gap, bone better than normal A Present VEMP thresholds low; CT Presbycusis Rinne positive Bilateral high-frequency sensorineural A Present, may be at reduced levels Speech scores fair Ménière's disease Weber away Low-frequency sensorineural, fluctuating A Present, recruitment ECochG SP/AP raised Vestibular schwannoma Weber away Asymmetric sensorineural A Absent or decay Poor speech, roll-over; ABR I–V prolonged; MRI Auditory neuropathy Variable Any, often fluctuating A Absent OAE present, ABR absent or abnormal Non-organic loss Inconsistent Variable, no shadow curve A Present near claimed threshold SRT better than PTA; objective tests normal"],["ev","cl:t-audio~c11","Hearing tests: how to do and read them: current evidence","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Testing children","Grading hearing loss. The WHO World Report on Hearing (2021) uses the better-ear average at 0.5, 1, 2 and 4 kHz: normal under 20 dB, mild 20 to under 35, moderate 35 to under 50, moderately severe 50 to under 65, severe 65 to under 80, profound 80 to under 95, and complete 95 dB or more; older textbooks use different cut-offs, so say which system you are quoting. Methods. The British Society of Audiology recommended procedure sets the standard technique and masking rules for pure-tone audiometry. Beyond the audiogram. Speech-in-noise tests and extended high-frequency audiometry (above 8 kHz) detect problems the standard audiogram misses, including early ototoxicity and possible hidden hearing loss."],["sec","cl:t-audio~c11","Testing children","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · Testing children","By age. Birth to six months: behavioural observation, OAE and ABR. Six to thirty months: visual reinforcement audiometry. From about thirty months: play audiometry. Older children: conventional audiometry. Normal thresholds in children are 15 dB or better. Visual reinforcement audiometry conditions the child to turn towards a sound, rewarded by a light or puppet, and uses insert earphones or free field; free-field results reflect the better ear only. Play audiometry turns the test into a game, such as putting a peg in a board each time a sound is heard. Tympanometry and otoacoustic emissions support behavioural tests at every age, and ABR under sleep or sedation is used when behavioural results are unreliable."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s12-1","Viva essentials","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Rinne and Weber: technique and interpretation, and the false-negative Rinne. Masking: when and why; the plateau method, shadow curve and masking dilemma. Reading an audiogram aloud: describe each ear, the type, degree and shape of loss, and what you would do next. Audiogram patterns: Carhart notch, noise notch, low-frequency loss, asymmetry, dead ear. Tympanogram types and the ear canal volume. Reflex patterns and the site of the lesion. Reading an ABR: latencies, the I–V interval and wave V asymmetry. Uses of the stapedial reflex. OAE versus ABR, and the waves of the ABR. Auditory neuropathy."],["sec","cl:t-audio~s12-2","Common pitfalls","Hearing tests: how to do and read them · For the examination","Common pitfalls Reading a false-negative Rinne as a conductive loss. Forgetting to mask. Calling a flat tympanogram fluid without checking the canal volume. Taking a shadow curve for a real moderate loss in a dead ear. Planning stapes surgery for an air–bone gap with present reflexes before excluding a third window. Accepting a severe loss in a patient who follows quiet conversation."],["sec","cl:t-benign~c1","Overview","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Overview","Lesions and tumours. Benign lesions and tumours of the larynx Non-neoplastic (commoner) Neoplastic Vocal nodules Squamous papilloma (juvenile and adult) Vocal polyp Chondroma Reinke's oedema Haemangioma Contact ulcer and granuloma; intubation granuloma Granular cell tumour Leukoplakia and keratosis Pleomorphic adenoma, oncocytoma Amyloid Neurilemmoma, neurofibroma Cysts: ductal, epidermoid, saccular Rhabdomyoma, lipoma, fibroma Laryngocele; sulcus vocalis"],["sec","cl:t-benign~s1-1","Assessment of the hoarse voice","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Overview","Any hoarseness lasting more than three weeks (four weeks in the American guideline), or sooner in a smoker or with pain, dysphagia, otalgia, stridor or a neck mass, needs laryngoscopy. Videostroboscopy shows the mucosal wave, which distinguishes superficial lesions (nodules, polyps) from those that stiffen the fold (cysts, scar, sulcus, carcinoma). Perceptual rating, acoustic analysis and voice handicap indices measure the voice before and after treatment."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s1-2","Surgery","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Overview","Phonomicrosurgery under suspension microlaryngoscopy with cold instruments or laser: a microflap is raised through a small incision on the upper surface, the lesion dissected from beneath, and the epithelium laid back, preserving the superficial lamina propria and the vocal ligament. The anterior commissure is never stripped on both sides at once, to avoid a web. Voice therapy before and after prevents recurrence. Figure 1 The set-up for microlaryngeal surgery. Dhingra, fig. 61.3"],["sec","cl:t-benign~s2-1","Vocal nodules","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Phonotraumatic lesions","Singers', screamers' or teachers' nodes. Bilateral, symmetrical swellings on the free edge at the junction of the anterior third and posterior two-thirds, the midpoint of the membranous fold, where vibration and collision are greatest. They arise from chronic misuse of the voice (shouting, singing too loudly or in the wrong register, speaking at an unnaturally low pitch): submucosal oedema and haemorrhage organise and hyalinise, and the epithelium thickens. Early nodules are soft, red and oedematous; mature ones grey-white and firm. Teachers, singers, actors, vendors and talkative children, especially boys, are typical; in adults, women more often. Figure 2 First, the site of vocal nodules at the junction of the anterior third and posterior two-thirds of the folds. Second, vocal nodules on laryngoscopy. Dhingra, figs 61.1 and 61.2 Symptoms: hoarseness, a breathy voice that tires, loss of the upper range in singers, and neck pain after prolonged speaking. Stroboscopy: an hourglass closure with a preserved mucosal wave. Treatment: voice therapy ( Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders ) is the cornerstone; most children's nodules disappear, and many adults'. Some go further: lesions that do not shrink after good, compliant therapy are by definition not nodules. Microsurgical excision for large, fibrotic nodules persisting after therapy, with care not to injure the vocal ligament, and continued therapy afterwards; intralesional steroid may help."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s2-2","Vocal polyp","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Phonotraumatic lesions","Usually a unilateral , soft, smooth, often pedunculated swelling at the same site, after sudden shouting causing haemorrhage into the fold, with smoking and allergy contributing; mostly men of thirty to fifty. It may be haemorrhagic, gelatinous or fibrous. Hoarseness, diplophonia (two pitches from two differently vibrating folds), and if large and pedunculated, choking or stridor as it flops through the glottis. Treatment is microsurgical excision followed by voice therapy. Figure 3 A pedunculated polyp on the right vocal fold. Dhingra, fig. 61.4"],["sec","cl:t-benign~s2-3","Reinke's oedema (polypoid degeneration)","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Phonotraumatic lesions","Bilateral, diffuse, symmetrical, pale, translucent swelling of the whole membranous folds from gelatinous fluid in Reinke's space; smoking is almost always responsible, with voice abuse, reflux and hypothyroidism. The voice becomes deep and rough, more noticeable in women, who may be mistaken for men on the telephone; large swellings obstruct breathing. Stop smoking; treat reflux and thyroid; voice therapy; decortication by a lateral incision, suction of the gel and trimming of excess epithelium, traditionally one side at a time. Figure 4 Bilateral Reinke's oedema in a 50-year-old woman who smoked. Dhingra, fig. 61.5"],["sec","cl:t-benign~s3-1","Contact ulcer and granuloma","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Granulomas and keratosis","On the vocal processes of the arytenoids, where they hammer together with hard glottal attacks, forceful low-pitched speech and habitual throat clearing; laryngopharyngeal reflux is a major factor ( Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections ), and intubation another ( Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis ). Hoarseness, pain on speaking radiating to the ear, a foreign-body feeling and constant throat clearing. Ulcers or granulomas sit on one or both vocal processes with red arytenoid mucosa. Anti-reflux treatment (twice-daily proton pump inhibitor), even without heartburn. Voice therapy to stop throat clearing and correct pitch. Inhaled or injected steroid. Botulinum toxin to the adductors (lateral cricoarytenoid and thyroarytenoid) for recurrent granulomas. Surgery only for airway obstruction, failure of treatment or suspicion of tumour; recurrence after excision is common. Send tissue for histology to exclude carcinoma and granular cell tumour."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s3-2","Intubation granuloma","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Granulomas and keratosis","Injury to the vocal processes from rough intubation, a large tube or prolonged intubation; usually bilateral on the posterior folds, with hoarseness and, if large, dyspnoea. Many mature into a pedunculated mass and drop off; treatment is as for contact granuloma."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s3-3","Leukoplakia and keratosis","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Granulomas and keratosis","White plaques or warty thickening on the upper surface of one or both folds with normal mobility, in smokers. Histology ranges from simple keratosis through dysplasia to carcinoma in situ and invasive carcinoma, so it is a precancerous condition . Biopsy or excisional microflap stripping for histology, stop smoking, treat reflux, and follow up for recurrence and new lesions; avoid repeated deep stripping that scars the voice. The white patch on a smoker's fold A keratotic or red lesion with a reduced mucosal wave on stroboscopy, irregular surface, or impaired mobility is suspicious for invasive carcinoma ( Cancer of the larynx ): biopsy rather than repeated observation."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s3-4","Amyloid","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Granulomas and keratosis","Men of fifty to seventy: a yellow or orange submucosal mass in the false fold, ventricle, true fold or subglottis, causing hoarseness or dyspnoea; apple-green birefringence with Congo red. Exclude myeloma and systemic amyloidosis; remove localised deposits endoscopically; the outlook is good."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s4-1","Ductal (retention) cysts","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Cysts and sulcus","From blocked seromucinous glands in the vallecula, aryepiglottic fold, false fold, ventricle and piriform fossa: silent when small; hoarseness, cough, throat pain and dyspnoea when large. Figure 5 (A) An aryepiglottic cyst causing intermittent laryngeal obstruction. (B) The cyst after removal. Dhingra, fig. 61.6"],["sec","cl:t-benign~s4-2","Vocal fold cysts","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Cysts and sulcus","Epidermoid cyst: an inclusion cyst of keratin, congenital or from phonotrauma, white and deep in the fold, often against the ligament. Mucous retention cyst: smooth, glistening and fluid-filled. Usually unilateral, sometimes with a reactive nodule on the opposite fold, and easily confused with nodules; stroboscopy shows a stiff segment with reduced mucosal wave, and they do not resolve with voice therapy. Microflap excision, ideally intact; residual wall recurs. Figure 6 An epidermoid cyst of the left vocal fold, full of cheesy material at excision. Dhingra, fig. 61.7"],["sec","cl:t-benign~s4-3","Saccular cysts and laryngocele","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Cysts and sulcus","Obstruction of the saccule's mouth fills it with mucus (a saccular cyst : anterior, bulging from the ventricle; or lateral, into the false fold and aryepiglottic fold and even the neck). Distension with air forms a laryngocele : internal, external (through the thyrohyoid membrane, a reducible neck swelling that grows with coughing or Valsalva) or combined, linked to raised pressure in wind players, glass blowers and weight lifters. Diagnosed by laryngoscopy, radiographs with Valsalva and CT. Endoscopic marsupialisation or excision for internal lesions; external excision for external and combined lesions. In an adult, a ventricular carcinoma obstructing the saccule must be looked for and biopsied. Figure 7 First, a laryngocele of the mixed type with internal and external components. Second, a left laryngocele as seen on Valsalva (arrow). Dhingra, figs 61.8 and 61.9"],["sec","cl:t-benign~s4-4","Sulcus vocalis","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Cysts and sulcus","A furrow along the free edge where the lamina propria is deficient and the epithelium dips to the ligament or muscle: congenital, or from phonotrauma, perhaps an opened epidermoid cyst. A breathy, weak voice with incomplete closure, a spindle-shaped gap and a reduced mucosal wave; confirmed by palpation at microlaryngoscopy. Prolonged voice therapy first; treat reflux and allergy. Surgery has limited success: steroid or filler injection, fat or fascia implantation, excision of the sulcus, slicing or multiple relaxing incisions, undermining, and medialisation thyroplasty for closure. Figure 8 (A) The layers of a normal vocal fold. (B) A sulcus vocalis in coronal section. (C) Its appearance on laryngoscopy. Dhingra, fig. 61.10 Figure 9 The layers of the vocal fold: Reinke's space and the vocal ligament. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-benign~s5-1","Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Neoplasms","The commonest benign tumour of the larynx (about 80% of benign neoplasms) and of the child's larynx, caused by HPV 6 and 11 . Juvenile onset: acquired from mothers with genital warts at birth; first-born children of young mothers by vaginal delivery are at greatest risk (caesarean section does not reliably prevent it). Presents at two to five years with hoarseness, a weak cry or aphonia and then stridor; often misdiagnosed as asthma or croup. Multiple, aggressive and recurrent, at junctions of squamous and ciliated epithelium (supraglottis, glottis, subglottis), spreading to the trachea and bronchi, especially after tracheostomy. Adult onset: men of thirty to fifty, often single, at the anterior fold or commissure, less aggressive; malignant change is rare (more with HPV 16 and 18, smoking and irradiation). Figure 10 Supraglottic papillomatosis. Dhingra, fig. 61.11 5.1.1 Treatment The aim is a safe airway and a useful voice with as few scars as possible; surgery debulks, it does not cure. Surgery: microdebrider, CO2 laser or pulsed KTP laser under suspension laryngoscopy, avoiding injury to the vocal ligament and the anterior commissure; repeated as often as needed. Office KTP laser in adults. Adjuvants for frequent recurrence or distal spread: intralesional cidofovir , systemic or intralesional bevacizumab , indole-3-carbinol, and (older) interferon alpha and retinoids; the HPV vaccine as therapy is under study, and as prevention will reduce maternal transmission. Tracheostomy is avoided if possible, because it encourages tracheal spread."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s5-2","Chondroma","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Neoplasms","Usually from the posterior lamina of the cricoid, also the thyroid, arytenoid or epiglottis; men of forty to sixty. A slow, hard, submucosal mass causing dyspnoea when subglottic, or a lump feeling and dysphagia when growing back into the hypopharynx. CT shows a calcified cartilaginous mass; biopsy (with the laser through hard tissue) to exclude chondrosarcoma. Excision through a laryngofissure or lateral pharyngotomy, preserving as much cricoid as possible; laryngectomy for large or recurrent tumours."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s5-3","Haemangioma","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Neoplasms","Infantile: subglottic, capillary, presenting with stridor in the first six months, half with skin haemangiomas; involutes, and is treated with propranolol ( Congenital lesions of the larynx ). Adult: cavernous, on the fold or supraglottis; left alone if silent, treated with steroids, laser or excision if symptomatic."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s5-4","Granular cell tumour","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Neoplasms","From Schwann cells, submucosal, usually on the posterior fold; the overlying pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia can be mistaken for well-differentiated carcinoma. Complete excision prevents recurrence."],["ev","cl:t-benign~s5-5","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness: current evidence","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Neoplasms","Nodules. Systematic reviews show that voice therapy reduces nodule size and improves voice in adults and children, and is first line; surgery is kept for nodules that persist after good-quality therapy (Alegria et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2020). Hoarseness guideline. Laryngoscopy for hoarseness lasting over four weeks, and voice therapy for hoarseness that affects quality of life (Stachler et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018). Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis. In a systematic review of systemic bevacizumab for severe juvenile-onset disease, every patient improved and more than half needed no further surgery, but disease returned when treatment stopped (Ryan et al., Laryngoscope 2021). Prophylactic HPV vaccination is expected to reduce juvenile-onset disease, as seen in Australia after its national programme."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s5-5","Others","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · Neoplasms","Pleomorphic adenoma, oncocytoma, neurilemmoma and neurofibroma, rhabdomyoma, lipoma and fibroma are rare and excised endoscopically or through an external approach."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s6-1","Viva essentials","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Site of nodules and why there. Nodule versus polyp versus cyst: bilaterality, stroboscopy and response to therapy. Reinke's oedema and smoking. Contact granuloma: reflux and voice misuse. Leukoplakia as a premalignant lesion. Laryngocele: types and the hidden carcinoma. Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis: HPV types, behaviour, treatment and adjuvants, and why tracheostomy is avoided."],["sec","cl:t-benign~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on nodules before a proper course of voice therapy. Stripping both sides of the anterior commissure at once. Repeatedly observing a white patch in a smoker without a biopsy. Diagnosing a child with papillomas as asthma."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s1-1","Applied anatomy","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Overview","The external auditory canal is about 24 mm long. Its outer third is cartilaginous, with hairs and sebaceous and ceruminous glands; its inner two-thirds is bony, with thin, sensitive skin. The canal narrows at the isthmus , about 6 mm from the drum: objects pushed past it become impacted. The anterior recess , in front of and below the drum, collects debris. Figure 1 The anterior recess of the canal, where discharge and debris collect. Dhingra, fig. 1.2 Figure 2 Skin of the cartilaginous canal, and the direction of the canal. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-canal~s1-2","Pulling the pinna","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Overview","In adults, pull the pinna up and back to straighten the canal; in infants, down and back."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s2-1","What it is","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Wax","Wax (cerumen) is a mixture of secretions of the ceruminous and sebaceous glands with shed keratin. It is acidic, water-repellent and antibacterial, and the canal normally clears it by epithelial migration."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s2-2","Why it impacts","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Wax","A narrow or tortuous canal, stiff hairs, exostoses, overactive glands, and pushing wax deeper with cotton buds or hearing aid moulds."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s2-3","Clinical picture","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Wax","A blocked ear and hearing loss, sometimes tinnitus or giddiness, and occasionally a reflex cough through the auricular branch of the vagus ( Arnold's nerve ). Sudden blockage after bathing happens when water swells dry wax."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s2-4","When to remove it","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Wax","Treat wax only when it causes symptoms or hides the drum that needs to be seen; wax that does neither is left alone. Cotton buds and ear candling are not recommended."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s2-5","Removal","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Wax","There are three methods, and the choice depends on where you are and on the ear. 2.5.1 Softening drops Hard wax is softened first, for a few days, with sodium bicarbonate glycerine drops, olive or almond oil, or hydrogen peroxide. Any drops, even water or saline, work better than none, with no clear winner among them. 2.5.2 Irrigation (syringing) Irrigation is the usual method in primary care and community services. Pull the pinna up and back and direct a stream of water at body temperature along the posterosuperior canal wall, so that it flows behind the wax and pushes it out. Dry the ear and inspect the drum afterwards. Figure 3 (A) Irrigation of the ear canal. (B) The jet passes behind the wax or foreign body and pushes it out. Dhingra, fig. 8.13 Do not irrigate when There is a perforation, a grommet, or a history of discharge or ear surgery. The patient has had a previous perforation that may be thin. There has been a complication from irrigation before. The patient has an only hearing ear. The canal holds vegetable matter, which swells, or a button battery. 2.5.3 Under the microscope In the ENT clinic, wax is removed under direct vision with the microscope: suction, or instruments such as a wax hook, Jobson-Horne probe or crocodile forceps. This is the method whenever irrigation is unsuitable. Figure 4 Other methods of removal: (A) suction and (B) forceps. Dhingra, fig. 8.14"],["sec","cl:t-canal~s2-6","Complications of removal","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Wax","Perforation, canal laceration and bleeding, otitis externa, and vertigo if the water is not at body temperature."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s3-1","Types","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Foreign bodies","3.1.1 Non-living Beads, stones, paper, cotton, seeds, peas and beans, pencil tips, and button batteries. Vegetable matter swells with moisture and can cause infection. 3.1.2 Living Insects (flies, cockroaches, ants), which cause intense discomfort and noise, and maggots, from flies laying eggs in a foul-discharging ear. Figure 5 An insect in the ear canal, seen with an endoscope (arrow). Dhingra, fig. 8.15"],["sec","cl:t-canal~s3-2","Removal","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Foreign bodies","3.2.1 General principles Remove under direct vision, with good light, the microscope and the right instrument. A cooperative patient is essential; use a general anaesthetic for children, for impacted objects, and after a failed first attempt. Most foreign bodies can wait for the right setting, because a bad first attempt makes the second harder. 3.2.2 Graspable objects Paper, cotton and other soft or irregular objects are taken with crocodile forceps. 3.2.3 Smooth, round objects Never grasp a bead, pea or stone with forceps: it slips and is pushed past the isthmus towards the drum. Pass an angled hook or curette beyond the object and roll it out from behind, or use suction. Where the books differ · Irrigation for a foreign body Dhingra Syringing is an option for a smooth object that is not vegetable matter. Oxford texts Irrigation is not recommended in the ENT clinic; hook the object out from behind instead. In practice Use the microscope and a hook or suction in the ENT clinic. Never irrigate vegetable matter or a button battery, or an ear with a perforation. 3.2.4 Insects Kill the insect first by filling the canal with oil or spirit, then remove it with forceps or suction. 3.2.5 Maggots Instil chloroform water or turpentine to kill them, then remove them with forceps over several sittings; do not irrigate. Treat the underlying ear disease. 3.2.6 Button batteries Remove urgently: they leak alkali and cause rapid liquefactive necrosis. Do not instil drops or water. 3.2.7 Impacted deep objects An object pushed medial to the isthmus may need a postaural approach. After any re"],["sec","cl:t-canal~s4-1","Acquired canal stenosis","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Narrowing and bony growths","Fibrous narrowing after chronic otitis externa, trauma, burns, surgery or radiation; congenital stenosis and atresia are covered in Microtia and congenital canal atresia . It is treated by meatoplasty, excising the fibrous tissue and resurfacing with skin flaps or grafts, with a stent while the canal heals."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s4-2","Exostoses and osteoma","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Narrowing and bony growths","Both are bony swellings of the bony canal, and they are told apart by number, shape, site and cause. Exostoses or osteoma? Exostoses Osteoma Number and sides Multiple, usually both ears Single, one ear Shape Broad-based (sessile) Pedunculated Site Deep in the bony canal, near the drum, from the anterior and posterior walls Outer bony canal, near the bony-cartilaginous junction, at a suture line (tympanosquamous or tympanomastoid) Cause Repeated cold water and wind (\"surfer's ear\") A true benign tumour, unrelated to cold water Both are usually symptomless, but they can trap wax and debris, causing recurrent otitis externa, a conductive loss and, rarely, canal cholesteatoma."],["ev","cl:t-canal~s4-3","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis: current evidence","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Narrowing and bony growths","Earwax. The American guideline recommends treating wax only when it causes symptoms or blocks the examination, using drops, irrigation or manual removal (including microsuction), and advises against cotton buds, ear candling and routine removal in asymptomatic patients (Schwartz et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017). NICE (NG98, 2018) advises against cotton buds and recommends microsuction or irrigation, in primary care or community services, when wax causes hearing loss or blocks the view. A Cochrane review found that any drops, including water or saline, are better than none, with no clear winner among them (Aaron et al., 2018)."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s4-3","Treatment","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · Narrowing and bony growths","Regular microsuction and drops first. Surgery when that fails: a postauricular or endaural approach, a posterior meatal skin flap, and removal of bone with a drill, protecting the canal skin, the drum and, posteriorly, the facial nerve. An osteoma can often be removed by fracturing its pedicle. Cold-water ear protection reduces the recurrence of exostoses, which return in surfers who keep swimming without it. In a child, an apparent bony stenosis may be a mild congenital atresia, so obtain a CT before operating. Tumours of the canal skin and glands are covered in External ear tumours and bony growths ."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s5-1","On call","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · For the examination","Something stuck in the ear Button battery: remove urgently, no drops. Live insect: kill it first with oil or spirit, then remove. Smooth round object: hook or suction, never forceps. Impacted object or failed attempt: microscope, and consider general anaesthesia. Inspect the drum afterwards."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s5-2","Viva essentials","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Anatomy: cartilaginous and bony parts, the isthmus, the anterior recess. Irrigation: technique, where it is used, and when not to irrigate. Removal of foreign bodies, living and non-living. Exostoses versus osteoma. Arnold's nerve and the ear cough reflex."],["sec","cl:t-canal~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Irrigating an ear with a perforation. Grasping a bead with forceps. Irrigating vegetable matter. Delaying removal of a button battery."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s1-1","Definition","Cholesteatoma · Overview","A cholesteatoma is a three-dimensional collection of keratinising stratified squamous epithelium in the middle ear cleft, with accumulated keratin debris in its centre. Despite the name it contains no cholesterol and is not a tumour; keratoma would be a better word."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s1-2","Structure","Cholesteatoma · Overview","1.2.1 Matrix The living wall: keratinising squamous epithelium resting on a layer of inflamed connective tissue, the perimatrix . 1.2.2 Contents White, flaky or pearly keratin debris, often infected and foul smelling. Figure 1 Structure of a cholesteatoma: a matrix of keratinising epithelium and perimatrix, enclosing keratin debris. Dhingra, fig. 11.1"],["sec","cl:t-chole~s1-3","Why it matters","Cholesteatoma · Overview","It is the unsafe chronic ear. Cholesteatoma destroys the ossicles, the labyrinth, the facial canal and the bony plates over the dura and sigmoid sinus, and it causes most of the serious complications of chronic otitis media."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s2-1","Congenital cholesteatoma","Cholesteatoma · Classification and genesis","A white mass behind an intact drum, usually anterosuperior, in a child with no history of otitis media. It arises from epithelial rests left in the temporal bone, and may also occur in the petrous apex and the cerebellopontine angle."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s2-2","Primary acquired cholesteatoma","Cholesteatoma · Classification and genesis","The commonest type. A retraction pocket of the pars flaccida, drawn in by negative middle ear pressure, deepens into the attic; keratin accumulates in it, the neck narrows, and it becomes a self-perpetuating sac. Figure 2 First, the theories of genesis: a retraction pocket, basal cell hyperplasia and epithelial invasion through a posterosuperior perforation. Second, the sequence leading to primary acquired cholesteatoma. Dhingra, figs 11.2 and 11.3"],["sec","cl:t-chole~s2-3","Secondary acquired cholesteatoma","Cholesteatoma · Classification and genesis","Squamous epithelium grows into the middle ear through a marginal or posterosuperior perforation, or is implanted by trauma or surgery. Figure 3 Pathways to secondary acquired cholesteatoma: metaplasia of the middle ear mucosa, and epithelial migration through a large central or marginal perforation. Dhingra, fig. 11.4"],["sec","cl:t-chole~s2-4","Theories of genesis","Cholesteatoma · Classification and genesis","Cell rests: for congenital cholesteatoma. Invagination or retraction (Wittmaack): for primary acquired. Basal cell hyperplasia (Ruedi): prickle cells of the pars flaccida invade the subepithelial tissue. Epithelial invasion or migration (Habermann): through a marginal perforation. Squamous metaplasia (Sade): of the middle ear mucosa after chronic inflammation."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s2-5","Retraction pockets","Cholesteatoma · Classification and genesis","Retraction of the pars tensa is staged by how deep it goes. Stage I: retracted, not touching the incus. Stage II: touching the incus. Stage III: lying on the promontory (atelectasis), but can be lifted off with suction. Stage IV: adherent to the promontory (adhesive otitis), with no middle ear space. Figure 4 A retracted drum with an attic retraction pocket (arrow). Dhingra, fig. 11.6 Ventilation of the attic The attic is ventilated through the narrow anterior and posterior tympanic isthmus. When the isthmus is blocked by swollen mucosa, the attic and Prussak's space become isolated and the pars flaccida retracts, which is why attic cholesteatoma can coexist with a normally ventilated mesotympanum."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s3-1","Bone erosion","Cholesteatoma · Pathology","Collagenases and other enzymes from the matrix and perimatrix, osteoclast activation by inflammatory cytokines, and infection all contribute. The mastoid is usually sclerotic. Where the books differ · How bone is eroded Dhingra Attributes erosion to enzymes from osteoclasts and inflammatory cells, and rejects pressure necrosis. Lee Includes pressure along with the inflammatory mediators. In practice Describe erosion as enzymatic and osteoclast-driven, promoted by infection; pressure is at most a contributor."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s3-2","Routes of spread","Cholesteatoma · Pathology","The cholesteatoma follows the path of least resistance: from Prussak's space into the attic, then through the aditus to the antrum and mastoid; or posteriorly into the sinus tympani and facial recess, the sites hardest to clear at surgery. Figure 5 Prussak's space, where attic cholesteatoma begins, and the folds and compartments that guide its spread. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-chole~s3-3","Hidden sites for residual disease","Cholesteatoma · Pathology","Sinus tympani, facial recess, the undersurface of the scutum, and the anterior epitympanic recess. Figure 6 The posterior wall of the middle ear: the facial recess and sinus tympani, the commonest hiding places for residual disease. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-chole~s4-1","Symptoms","Cholesteatoma · Clinical features","4.1.1 Discharge Scanty, foul smelling and sometimes bloodstained, from infected keratin and granulation. A discharge that suddenly stops Pus may have been blocked and may be tracking inwards. Ask about headache, fever, vertigo and facial weakness. 4.1.2 Hearing loss Conductive, or mixed when the cochlea is involved. A cholesteatoma that bridges an eroded chain can transmit sound well: the cholesteatoma hearer , who must be warned that hearing may worsen after surgery. 4.1.3 Symptoms of complications Earache, headache, vertigo, facial weakness, fever and vomiting, neck stiffness, double vision or unsteadiness, or a listless child who will not feed."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s4-2","Signs","Cholesteatoma · Clinical features","An attic perforation or posterosuperior marginal perforation. White keratin flakes or a pearly mass. Erosion of the scutum or posterosuperior canal wall. A red, fleshy polyp that bleeds when touched. A positive fistula test in some. Figure 7 (A) An attic retraction pocket. (B) A double perforation, in the pars tensa and in the attic. Dhingra, fig. 11.13 A blue drum A blue drum with dark fluid behind it suggests cholesterol granuloma, not cholesteatoma."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s5-1","Examination under the microscope","Cholesteatoma · Investigations","Clean with suction; look for keratin, the extent of the defect and polyps."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s5-2","Fistula test","Cholesteatoma · Investigations","Pressing on the tragus or using a Siegle speculum causes vertigo and nystagmus if the lateral canal is eroded. It is specific but insensitive, positive in only 30 to 50% of fistulae. Do it in every case."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s5-3","Audiogram","Cholesteatoma · Investigations","Measures conductive and sensorineural components before surgery."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s5-4","High-resolution CT","Cholesteatoma · Investigations","Shows the extent of disease and the state of the scutum, ossicles, tegmen, lateral semicircular canal, facial canal and sigmoid plate, and the anatomy for surgery."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s5-5","Non-echo-planar diffusion-weighted MRI","Cholesteatoma · Investigations","Keratin restricts diffusion and shows bright. Sensitivity and specificity are both about 90%, but pearls smaller than about 3 mm can be missed. It detects residual or recurrent disease after canal wall up surgery and can replace a planned second look when suspicion is low (a negative scan does not cancel a second look when suspicion is high). It also helps when an intracranial complication is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s5-6","Endoscopy in clinic","Cholesteatoma · Investigations","A rigid 0 degree endoscope shows the full depth of a retraction pocket and documents it with photographs for comparison over time."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s6-1","Aims","Cholesteatoma · Management","In order: eradicate disease and make the ear safe; obtain a dry ear; preserve or restore hearing."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s6-2","Conservative care","Cholesteatoma · Management","Regular microscopic cleaning suits small accessible disease, patients over 65 or unfit for surgery, or those who refuse an operation."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s6-3","Choice of operation","Cholesteatoma · Management","6.3.1 Canal wall up The posterior canal wall is kept (combined approach tympanoplasty). The ear looks normal, swimming is allowed and hearing aids are easy to fit, but residual disease is commoner, so a planned second look or, increasingly, non-echo-planar diffusion-weighted MRI (about 91% sensitive) at about one year is needed. 6.3.2 Canal wall down The posterior canal wall is removed and the attic, antrum and mastoid are exteriorised into one cavity (modified radical or radical mastoidectomy, Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles ). Exposure is better and recurrence lower, but the cavity needs lifelong cleaning, water precautions, and aids are harder to fit. Canal wall up or down Canal wall up Canal wall down Appearance Normal meatus Wide meatus and cavity Residual disease Higher Lower Second look Often needed Not routine Cavity care None Regular cleaning Swimming Allowed Limited Hearing aid Easy Harder Choose canal wall up only when the patient can return for long-term follow-up. Neither is a gold standard: in adults, a 2023 meta-analysis found lower recurrence after canal wall down with obliteration than after canal wall up, while in children recurrence did not differ and hearing was better with canal wall up; canal wall up surgery is often staged. Where the books differ · Timing of the second look Most texts 6 to 12 months after the first operation. Oxford Textbook 3 to 6 months. In practice Plan the second look, or non-echo-planar diffusion-weighted MRI, at about 6 to 12 months. 6.3.3 Mastoid obliteration About 40% of open cavities discharge or cannot toler"],["sec","cl:t-chole~s6-4","Lateral canal fistula","Cholesteatoma · Management","Present in 7 to 10% of cholesteatomas ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis ), 90% of them in the lateral canal; only about 60% are seen on preoperative CT. Assume one in every case. Small fistula: the matrix is often removed at the end of the operation and the fistula sealed. Large fistula or one in an only hearing ear: leave the matrix on it and convert to canal wall down. If the endosteum tears, cover it immediately. Never strip the matrix off a cochlear fistula."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s6-5","Hearing reconstruction","Cholesteatoma · Management","Ossiculoplasty, at the first stage or the second look."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s6-6","Staging and recidivism","Cholesteatoma · Management","The EAONO/JOS system divides the middle ear and mastoid into sites, with the sinus tympani and protympanum as difficult-access sites where residual disease is most often left. Stage I occupies one site, stage II two or more, stage III has an extracranial complication (facial palsy, labyrinthine fistula, postauricular abscess, canal wall destruction), and stage IV intracranial spread. Recidivism is either residual (incompletely removed) or recurrent (a new retraction); both demand follow-up for at least five years, longer in children."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s7-1","Intratemporal","Cholesteatoma · Complications","Ossicular erosion, usually the incus first. Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis. Facial palsy, usually from the tympanic segment ( Facial nerve palsy ). Mastoiditis and abscesses."],["ev","cl:t-chole~s7-2","Cholesteatoma: current evidence","Cholesteatoma · Complications","A meta-analysis of 26 studies found non-echo-planar diffusion-weighted MRI had a sensitivity of 91% and specificity of 92% for cholesteatoma, before and after surgery and in children and adults (Lingam et al., Otol Neurotol 2017). In 13 studies, endoscopic ear surgery had lower residual and recurrent disease than microscopic surgery (risk ratios 0.68 and 0.51), with similar graft take and hearing, though the studies were mostly non-randomised and in selected disease (Li et al., Am J Otolaryngol 2021). Across 1,534 patients, single-stage mastoid obliteration was followed by recurrent disease in 4.6% and residual disease in 5.4% (van der Toom et al., JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018)."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s7-2","Intracranial","Cholesteatoma · Complications","Extradural and subdural abscess, meningitis, brain abscess, lateral sinus thrombosis and otitic hydrocephalus, through an eroded tegmen or sinus plate ( Intracranial complications of otitis media ). At surgery Facial canal dehiscence and tegmen dehiscence often accompany a fistula. The epineurium does not need opening unless disease has invaded the nerve."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s8-1","On call","Cholesteatoma · For the examination","Chronic ear with a new symptom New vertigo, facial weakness, headache, fever or neck stiffness: treat as a complication until proven otherwise. Examine the facial nerve and do a fistula test. Request CT, and MRI if intracranial spread is possible. Start intravenous antibiotics and inform your senior."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s8-2","Viva essentials","Cholesteatoma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Definition and structure: matrix, perimatrix, keratin. Congenital, primary and secondary acquired, and the theories of genesis. Signs of the unsafe ear. Canal wall up versus down, and who is suitable for each. Fistula: test, incidence and management. Non-EPI diffusion MRI for residual disease."],["sec","cl:t-chole~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Cholesteatoma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating an attic crust with drops and never lifting it. Forgetting the fistula test before surgery. Choosing canal wall up for a patient who cannot attend follow-up. Stripping matrix off a large fistula in an only hearing ear."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s1-1","The lip","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Basics","The lip is built around the orbicularis oris , the sphincter of the mouth supplied by the facial nerve, with the superior labial artery running deep to it. Its surface landmarks are what the surgeon must restore: the white roll at the vermilion border, where a step of only 1 mm is visible; Cupid's bow ; and the two philtral columns , the lines of embryological fusion, with the philtral dimple between them. Figure 1 Lip landmarks: vermilion, philtrum, Cupid's bow and commissures. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s1-2","The palate","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Basics","The incisive foramen divides the palate in two. In front of it lies the primary palate : the premaxilla with the four incisors, the alveolus and the lip. Behind it lies the secondary palate : the hard palate, formed by the palatine processes of the maxillae and the palatine bones, and the soft palate. In the soft palate, the levator veli palatini forms a sling that lifts the velum against the posterior pharyngeal wall, and the tensor veli palatini opens the Eustachian tube. The tensor is the only palatal muscle supplied by V3 ; the levator, palatoglossus, palatopharyngeus and musculus uvulae are all supplied by the vagus through the pharyngeal plexus. The hard palate is supplied by the greater palatine artery through the greater palatine foramen, the pedicle of every palatal flap, and the soft palate by the lesser palatine, ascending palatine and ascending pharyngeal arteries. Figure 2 The hard and soft palate, and Passavant ridge where the velum closes against the pharyngeal wall. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s1-3","Embryology","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Basics","The lip and primary palate form between the fourth and seventh weeks. The paired maxillary prominences of the first arch grow medially to fuse with the medial nasal prominences of the frontonasal process, and the medial nasal prominences join to form the intermaxillary segment, which becomes the philtrum, premaxilla and four incisors. Failure of fusion causes cleft lip , with or without a cleft alveolus. The secondary palate forms between the sixth and twelfth weeks. The palatine shelves grow down beside the tongue, rise to the horizontal as the tongue drops, and fuse from front to back, from the incisive foramen to the uvula. Failure causes cleft palate . If the tongue cannot drop, as with a small mandible, the shelves cannot meet and the wide U-shaped cleft of the Pierre Robin sequence results. Figure 3 Rathke pouch and the buccopharyngeal membrane in the early embryo. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s1-4","Epidemiology and causes","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Basics","Two different conditions Feature Cleft lip with or without palate Isolated cleft palate Frequency About 1 in 700 to 1000 births; varies with ethnicity (commoner in Asian and Native American, less in African populations) About 1 in 1500 to 2000; uniform across ethnicities Sex Boys more than girls Girls more than boys Associated syndromes Up to about 30% Up to about 50% Side 80% unilateral, two-thirds of these on the left — Clefting is multifactorial. The risk to a sibling is about 4% and rises as more relatives are affected. Smoking and alcohol, anticonvulsants such as phenytoin and valproate, retinoids, steroids, folate deficiency and amniotic bands all contribute. Several syndromes must be recognised. Van der Woude syndrome , autosomal dominant through IRF6, combines lower lip pits with cleft lip or palate and is the commonest syndromic cleft. The Pierre Robin sequence runs from micrognathia to glossoptosis to a wide U-shaped cleft palate, with airway obstruction and feeding difficulty. Stickler syndrome is the commonest syndrome underlying Robin sequence; it is a collagen disorder with high myopia, retinal detachment (so eye screening is essential), hearing loss and joint problems. The 22q11.2 deletion (velocardiofacial or DiGeorge) syndrome brings cleft palate or velopharyngeal insufficiency with heart defects, hypocalcaemia, immune deficiency and learning difficulty, and its carotids may lie medially behind the pharynx, a danger at pharyngeal surgery. Treacher Collins syndrome, hemifacial microsomia, the CHARGE association and Apert syndrome complete the list ( The synd"],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s2-1","Lip and palate","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Classification","A cleft lip is unilateral or bilateral. It is complete when it runs through the nasal sill, incomplete when the sill is intact, sometimes with a Simonart band of tissue bridging the gap, and microform when there is only a notch and a muscle gap under intact skin. A cleft palate is unilateral when one palatal shelf remains attached to the vomer and bilateral when the vomer lies free in the midline. Veau's classification grades the extent: group I involves the soft palate only, group II the soft and hard palate up to the incisive foramen, group III is a complete unilateral cleft through lip, alveolus and palate, and group IV a complete bilateral cleft."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s2-2","The submucous cleft","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Classification","In a submucous cleft the mucosa is intact but the muscles are separated beneath it. The classic signs form Calnan's triad : a bifid uvula , a zona pellucida (a translucent bluish strip in the midline of the soft palate), and a notch in the posterior edge of the hard palate that can be felt with a finger. An occult submucous cleft has none of these signs and is seen only on nasendoscopy, as a missing muscle bulge on the nasal surface of the velum. Check the palate before adenoidectomy A bifid uvula, a palpable notch or hypernasal speech means a possible submucous cleft or velopharyngeal weakness. A full adenoidectomy can unmask velopharyngeal insufficiency , because the adenoid was helping the palate seal. Avoid adenoidectomy, or do a limited (superior) one, and involve the cleft team ( Adenoids and nasopharyngitis )."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s3-1","The newborn","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Early care","Many clefts are now diagnosed by antenatal ultrasound, which allows the family to be counselled before birth. After birth, feeding is the first problem: a baby with a cleft palate cannot create suction, so breastfeeding is usually impossible except with a cleft lip alone. Squeezy bottles or special teats such as the Haberman or pigeon, with feeding upright, are used, and weight gain is watched closely. The airway matters most in Robin sequence. Prone or side positioning and a nasopharyngeal airway manage most babies; severe obstruction needs a tongue–lip adhesion, mandibular distraction osteogenesis or a tracheostomy, guided by sleep studies. Every baby is examined for other anomalies, especially cardiac and syndromic, reviewed by a geneticist, and given a hearing screen . Presurgical orthopaedics, by lip taping or nasoalveolar moulding , narrows a wide cleft and shapes the nose before lip repair."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s3-2","The ears","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Early care","Because the tensor and levator insert abnormally, the Eustachian tube cannot open, and otitis media with effusion ( Otitis media: acute and with effusion ) affects almost every child (over 90%) with a cleft palate. Hearing is tested from birth and throughout childhood, and persistent hearing loss is treated with ventilation tubes or hearing aids, the tubes often being inserted at the time of lip or palate repair. The ears usually improve after palate repair and with growth, but the long-term risks of chronic otitis media, retraction pockets and cholesteatoma remain."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s4-1","The timetable","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Surgery","A typical cleft care timeline Age Step Antenatal and birth Diagnosis, counselling, feeding plan, airway, hearing screen, genetics Weeks 1 to 12 Presurgical orthopaedics (lip taping, nasoalveolar moulding) if needed About 3 months Cleft lip repair, with or without primary rhinoplasty (traditional \"rule of 10s\": 10 weeks, 10 lb, haemoglobin 10 g/dL) 6 to 12 (up to 18) months Cleft palate repair, with ear tubes as needed 3 to 5 years Speech assessment; velopharyngeal surgery if needed; fistula repair 8 to 11 years (mixed dentition, before the canine erupts) Alveolar bone graft (cancellous bone from the iliac crest) Teens Orthodontics; orthognathic surgery (maxillary advancement) after growth 16 to 18 years Definitive septorhinoplasty; lip revision"],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s4-2","Cleft lip repair","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Surgery","Lip repair has four aims: to join the orbicularis oris into a working sphincter, to align the white roll and vermilion exactly, to create a symmetrical philtrum, nostril sill and alar base, and to correct the nose. The unilateral cleft lip nose has a characteristic deformity. The cleft-side alar cartilage is displaced down, back and out with a flat, low dome, the alar base is wide, the columella and caudal septum deviate to the non-cleft side, and the maxilla on the cleft side is hypoplastic. In the Millard rotation–advancement repair , the non-cleft (medial) side is rotated down to level Cupid's bow, and the cleft (lateral) side is advanced into the gap beneath the nostril. The muscle is released from its abnormal insertions and repaired across the cleft, and the nasal floor is closed. The Fisher anatomical subunit repair and triangular-flap repairs are the alternatives. The bilateral cleft lip is harder, because of a protruding premaxilla, a prolabium without muscle and a short columella; it is repaired in one or two stages, building the muscle across behind the prolabium. The complications of lip repair are dehiscence, a vermilion notch (the \"whistle deformity\"), a mismatched white roll, scar hypertrophy and nasal asymmetry."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s4-3","Cleft palate repair","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Surgery","The palate is repaired between about 6 and 12 months (often 9 to 12), and certainly before 18 months; in the TOPS trial, repair at 6 months left less velopharyngeal insufficiency than at 12 (see Current evidence). The timing balances better speech with early repair against better maxillary growth with later repair . The aims are to separate the mouth from the nose and, above all, to rebuild the levator sling by intravelar veloplasty and lengthen the palate for normal speech, because in a cleft the levator runs front to back and inserts into the back of the hard palate instead of meeting its partner in the midline. The two-flap palatoplasty (Bardach) raises mucoperiosteal flaps on the greater palatine pedicles, closes the nasal and oral layers and adds an intravelar veloplasty; it is used for complete clefts. The von Langenbeck repair uses bipedicled flaps with lateral relaxing incisions. The Furlow double-opposing Z-plasty places opposite Z-plasties on the oral and nasal layers, which lengthens the palate and realigns the levator , and is used for soft palate clefts, submucous clefts and velopharyngeal insufficiency. The older Veau–Wardill–Kilner V–Y pushback leaves more raw bone and restricts growth. After repair, bleeding and airway obstruction, especially in Robin sequence, are the early risks. An oronasal fistula develops in up to about 10%, usually at the junction of hard and soft palate, velopharyngeal insufficiency persists in up to 20 to 25%, and maxillary hypoplasia appears with growth. A submucous cleft is repaired only if it causes velopharyngeal insufficiency or"],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s5-1","Definitions","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Velopharyngeal dysfunction","Three kinds of velopharyngeal dysfunction are distinguished. Insufficiency is a structural deficit: the palate is too short or scarred, there is a cleft or submucous cleft, or tissue has been lost after adenoidectomy or tumour surgery. Incompetence is a neuromuscular deficit, as in palsies or the 22q11 deletion. Mislearning is an articulation pattern that needs speech therapy only."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s5-2","Features","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Velopharyngeal dysfunction","The child has hypernasal resonance on vowels and voiced oral consonants, while the nasal sounds /m/, /n/ and /ng/ remain normal. Nasal air emission and turbulence accompany pressure consonants such as p, b, t, d and s; consonants are weak; compensatory articulations such as glottal stops develop; and food and liquid may regurgitate through the nose."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s5-3","Assessment","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Velopharyngeal dysfunction","A speech and language therapist makes a perceptual assessment, and the velopharynx is imaged during speech by nasendoscopy , which shows the size and site of the gap and the closure pattern, and by multiview videofluoroscopy . The closure pattern may be coronal (the commonest), sagittal, circular, or circular with a Passavant ridge, and it determines the operation. Before any pharyngeal surgery the child is checked for the 22q11 deletion with medialised carotids , ideally by MRA, and for obstructive sleep apnoea."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s5-4","Treatment","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Velopharyngeal dysfunction","Surgery corrects structure and therapy corrects articulation, so speech therapy alone is used for mislearning and small gaps. For a short palate or malpositioned muscle with a small gap, the palate is re-repaired with a Furlow double-opposing Z-plasty or a radical intravelar veloplasty. Small central gaps can be closed by augmenting the posterior pharyngeal wall with fat, implants or fillers, at the risk of migration and extrusion. For a large central gap with good lateral wall movement , a superiorly based pharyngeal flap is raised from the posterior wall and attached to the velum, leaving two lateral ports. For a circular or bow-tie gap with poor lateral wall movement , a sphincter pharyngoplasty (Orticochea, Jackson or Hynes) transposes the posterior tonsillar pillars with palatopharyngeus and sutures them to the posterior wall, narrowing the port while leaving a central opening. A speech bulb or palatal lift helps poor surgical candidates. The main risks of pharyngeal surgery are obstructive sleep apnoea ( Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea ) and hyponasality, so oxygen saturation is monitored after the operation."],["ev","cl:t-cleft~s6-1","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency: current evidence","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Later care","Timing of palate repair. In the TOPS trial, 558 infants with isolated cleft palate were randomised to repair at 6 or 12 months: velopharyngeal insufficiency at age five was less common after early repair (8.9% against 15.0%), with similar complications (Gamble et al., NEJM 2023). Ears. Almost all children with a cleft palate have glue ear; NICE (NG233, 2023) advises offering hearing aids or grommets after assessment by a cleft team, recognising that grommets in cleft children carry a higher risk of otorrhoea and perforation. Velopharyngeal dysfunction. Choice between Furlow palatoplasty, pharyngeal flap and sphincter pharyngoplasty depends on the size and pattern of closure on nasendoscopy and videofluoroscopy; no single operation has proved superior in trials. Robin sequence. Mandibular distraction relieves tongue-base obstruction in most infants who fail positioning and a nasopharyngeal airway."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s6-1","Through growth","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · Later care","The alveolar cleft is grafted in the mixed dentition, at about 8 to 11 years and before the permanent canine erupts, with cancellous bone from the iliac crest. The graft closes the alveolar cleft and any oronasal fistula there and supports the teeth and the alar base. About a quarter of children develop maxillary hypoplasia with a class III bite and need a Le Fort I advancement or distraction once growth stops. The cleft lip nose, corrected in part by a primary rhinoplasty at lip repair, is finally treated by a definitive septorhinoplasty with cartilage grafts after facial growth. Dental care and orthodontics continue throughout, and psychological support for the child and family matters most at school age and in adolescence."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s7-1","Viva essentials","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Primary versus secondary palate; the incisive foramen; the embryology of each. The nerve supply of the palatal muscles (tensor by V3). Cleft lip versus isolated cleft palate: epidemiology and syndromes (Van der Woude, Robin, Stickler, 22q11). The submucous cleft triad, and why it matters before adenoidectomy. The timetable of care, and the rule of 10s. Millard repair: which side rotates. Furlow palatoplasty and intravelar veloplasty. Otitis media with effusion in cleft palate: why, and how it is managed. Velopharyngeal insufficiency: signs, nasendoscopy, pharyngeal flap versus sphincter pharyngoplasty, OSA risk."],["sec","cl:t-cleft~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency · For the examination","Common pitfalls Adenoidectomy in a child with an unrecognised submucous cleft. Missing Stickler syndrome (retinal detachment) in Robin sequence. Pharyngeal surgery in 22q11 deletion without imaging the carotids. Neglecting hearing surveillance after the palate is repaired."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s1-1","The conditions","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Overview","Congenital nasal anomalies Airway Choanal atresia pyriform aperture stenosis, atresia of the nares Midline masses Nasal dermoid glioma, encephalocele Cysts Nasolacrimal duct cyst nasoalveolar cyst Other Haemangioma proboscis lateralis, arhinia (rare)"],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s1-2","Why it matters","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Overview","Newborns breathe preferentially, though not obligatorily, through the nose for the first weeks to months, so bilateral obstruction presents with distress at birth and is an emergency; midline masses carry a risk of meningitis and CSF leak."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s2-1","Embryology","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Choanal atresia","Persistence of the bucconasal membrane (or abnormal mesoderm) between the nasal cavity and the nasopharynx."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s2-2","Types","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Choanal atresia","One-sided or both; one-sided is commoner and may go unnoticed until adulthood. Complete or incomplete. Bony (about 90% in older texts; modern series show most are mixed bony and membranous) or purely membranous."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s2-3","Associations","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Choanal atresia","Associated anomalies are common, especially with bilateral atresia: CHARGE syndrome (coloboma, heart defects, choanal atresia, retarded growth and development, genital and ear anomalies), in which about half have bilateral atresia; Treacher Collins syndrome; and, more rarely, craniosynostoses such as Crouzon and Apert. Unilateral atresia is usually isolated."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s2-4","Clinical picture","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Choanal atresia","2.4.1 Bilateral Respiratory distress at birth: cyclical cyanosis , blue at rest or when feeding and pink when crying (the baby breathes through the mouth while crying), with difficulty feeding. 2.4.2 Unilateral One-sided nasal obstruction and persistent thick discharge from that side, often discovered in later childhood."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s2-5","Diagnosis","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Choanal atresia","Mucoid discharge without air bubbles. Failure to pass a soft catheter (a size 6 to 8 French) more than about 3 cm into the nose. Methylene blue in the nose fails to reach the pharynx; a wisp of cotton under the nostril does not move. Flexible nasal endoscopy. CT in the axial plane is the definitive test: it shows the atretic plate, narrowing of the posterior nasal cavity and thickening of the vomer, and separates bony from membranous atresia. In unilateral atresia, CT is best kept for planning once surgery is decided, to avoid repeated radiation and sedation."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s2-6","Management","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Choanal atresia","2.6.1 Emergency airway in bilateral atresia An oral airway, or a feeding nipple with the end cut off taped in the mouth ( McGovern's technique ), keeps the mouth open and avoids tracheostomy; orogastric feeding. Intubation if needed. Newborn with cyclical cyanosis Insert an oral airway or McGovern nipple. Try to pass a catheter through each side of the nose. Arrange CT of the nasal cavity after suction and decongestion. Look for CHARGE anomalies: heart, eyes, ears, genitalia. 2.6.2 Definitive repair Endoscopic transnasal repair with a drill: removal of the atretic plate and the posterior part of the vomer, with or without mucosal flaps and stents. The standard approach now. Transpalatal repair: better exposure, historically at about 18 months, but risks palatal growth disturbance. Unilateral atresia can be repaired electively in childhood. 2.6.3 After repair Restenosis is common, so regular review, dilatation and revision may be needed. Postoperative suction, saline and topical steroids reduce granulation and early scarring. Some surgeons stent bilateral repairs for two to six weeks with cut endotracheal tubes, but recent series show no difference in revision rates with or without stents, and because stents need intensive care by parents and sometimes removal under anaesthesia, their use is declining; unilateral stenting is not worthwhile."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s2-7","Pyriform aperture stenosis","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Choanal atresia","Narrowing of the bony nasal entrance from overgrowth of the nasal processes of the maxilla, presenting like bilateral choanal atresia (a catheter cannot be passed) but at the front of the nose; endoscopy and CT tell them apart. Mid-nasal stenosis can coexist. A single central maxillary incisor suggests holoprosencephaly, so pituitary and brain assessment is needed. Mild cases are managed medically; sublabial drilling of the aperture is reserved for persistent respiratory distress or problems with sleep, growth or feeding."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s3-1","Embryology","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Congenital midline nasal masses","In early development a projection of dura passes through the foramen caecum (between the frontal and ethmoid bones) into the prenasal space, or between the frontal and nasal bones through the fonticulus frontalis . Normally it regresses. If it does not, ectodermal elements (a dermoid), isolated glial tissue (a glioma) or a herniation of brain and meninges (an encephalocele) remain, sometimes with an intracranial connection. Incidence is about 1 in 20,000 to 40,000 births."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s3-2","Nasal dermoid","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Congenital midline nasal masses","3.2.1 Picture The commonest midline nasal mass: a firm, non-tender, non-pulsatile, non-compressible swelling anywhere from the glabella to the columella, often with a midline pit that may sprout a hair. It may become infected repeatedly. 3.2.2 Types Simple dermoid beneath the skin, in front of the nasal bones, without an opening. Dermoid with a sinus, whose tract runs under the nasal bones to the upper septum. Dermoid with a tract through the foramen caecum or cribriform plate to the dura, or intradural extension, which risks meningitis. Figure 1 Types of nasal dermoid: (A) simple, (B) with an external pit, lying deep to the nasal bones, (C) with a connection to the dura, (D) intradural. Dhingra, fig. 25.3 3.2.3 Treatment Complete excision of the cyst, the tract and any involved bone, cartilage and septum, often by an open rhinoplasty approach; combined neurosurgical repair when the tract reaches the dura."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s3-3","Nasal glioma","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Congenital midline nasal masses","3.3.1 Picture A mass of ectopic glial tissue, not a true tumour, sometimes called a nasal glial heterotopia. About 60% are extranasal (firm, reddish swelling on the bridge or side of the nose with telangiectasias), 30% intranasal (a pale, firm polypoid mass, mistaken for a polyp) and 10% both. About 15 to 20% have a fibrous stalk to the dura. Non-pulsatile and non-compressible. Figure 2 Extranasal glioma on the right side of a child's nose. Dhingra, fig. 25.4 3.3.2 Treatment Complete excision by an external approach; craniotomy if there is an intracranial connection."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s3-4","Encephalocele","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Congenital midline nasal masses","3.4.1 Picture A herniation of meninges (meningocele), or meninges with brain (meningoencephalocele), through a skull base defect, communicating with the subarachnoid space. It is soft, bluish, pulsatile and compressible , enlarges on crying, straining or jugular compression ( Furstenberg sign ), and may transilluminate. 3.4.2 Types by site Sincipital (frontoethmoid), presenting outside the nose: nasofrontal (glabella), nasoethmoidal (side of the nose) and naso-orbital (medial orbit). Basal, presenting inside the nose or nasopharynx: transethmoidal, sphenoethmoidal, transsphenoidal and sphenomaxillary, often mistaken for a polyp. 3.4.3 Treatment Neurosurgical or endoscopic: dividing the stalk, excising the extracranial part and repairing the dural and bony defect, as for CSF leaks ( CSF rhinorrhoea ). Image before you touch A midline nasal mass in a child may connect with the brain. Biopsy or simple removal can cause CSF leak and meningitis. Obtain MRI (with CT for the bone) first. Telling the midline masses apart Dermoid Glioma Encephalocele Consistency Firm Firm Soft Pulsatile or compressible No No Yes Furstenberg sign Negative Negative Positive Pit or hair Often No No Intracranial connection Sometimes About 15 to 20%, fibrous stalk Always, with CSF"],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s4-1","Nasolacrimal duct cyst","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Other congenital anomalies","A cystic dilatation of the lower nasolacrimal duct when Hasner's valve fails to open, seen as a bluish swelling under the inferior turbinate; bilateral cysts can obstruct a newborn's breathing. Endoscopic marsupialisation. Figure 3 The lacrimal drainage system and Hasner's valve in the inferior meatus. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s4-2","Haemangioma","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Other congenital anomalies","A strawberry swelling of the nasal tip that grows in the first year and regresses over several years; propranolol for problem lesions."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s4-3","Rare anomalies","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Other congenital anomalies","Arhinia (absent nose), proboscis lateralis, bifid nose and congenital atresia of the nares."],["ev","cl:t-congnose~s4-4","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses: current evidence","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Other congenital anomalies","Stents. A meta-analysis of bilateral choanal atresia repair found success of 65% with stents and 64% without, while stents caused alar injury, vestibular stenosis and blockage, so routine stenting is no longer justified (Strychowsky et al., Laryngoscope 2016). Endoscopic transnasal repair with mucosal flaps and wide posterior septectomy is the standard approach; topical mitomycin C has not been shown to prevent restenosis."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s4-4","The growing nose","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · Other congenital anomalies","Septal surgery in children was long avoided for fear of affecting facial growth, but conservative septoplasty for significant obstruction appears safe ( Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess ). Adenoids are largest in absolute terms at 6 to 10 years, but largest relative to the nasopharynx, and so most obstructive, at 2 to 6 years."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s5-1","Viva essentials","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Choanal atresia: embryology, cyclical cyanosis, tests, McGovern nipple, repair. CHARGE syndrome. Dermoid, glioma and encephalocele: embryology and differences. Furstenberg sign. Why not to biopsy a midline nasal mass."],["sec","cl:t-congnose~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing unilateral atresia in a child with one-sided discharge. Taking a nasal mass in a child for a polyp. Forgetting the associated anomalies of CHARGE."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s1-1","Causes","Croup and epiglottitis · Acute laryngitis","Infective: usually viral after a cold (parainfluenza, influenza, rhinovirus), followed by bacterial invasion (pneumococcus, H. influenzae , Moraxella , streptococci, staphylococci); with measles, chickenpox and whooping cough; candida in inhaled steroid users. Non-infective: vocal abuse (shouting, cheering), allergy, inhaled irritants, thermal and chemical burns, reflux, and intubation."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s1-2","Clinical features","Croup and epiglottitis · Acute laryngitis","Abrupt hoarseness, sometimes complete loss of voice; pain in the throat after talking; a dry, irritating cough, worse at night; and the features of a cold. Early the epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, arytenoids and false folds are red and swollen while the true folds stay white, standing out against the redness; later the folds become red and swollen, with sticky mucus between them and subglottic swelling. Vocal abuse may cause submucosal haemorrhage in a fold. Breathlessness is not a feature of simple laryngitis and suggests another diagnosis."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s1-3","Treatment","Croup and epiglottitis · Acute laryngitis","Voice rest: the most important measure; forcing the voice delays recovery and risks haemorrhage and nodules. No smoking or alcohol; hydration; steam inhalation. Cough suppressants, mucolytics and analgesics. Antibiotics only for secondary bacterial infection with fever or purulent sputum. Proton pump inhibitors where reflux is suspected. Steroids after burns, or for professional voice users who must perform. Most settle in one to two weeks; hoarseness persisting beyond three weeks needs laryngoscopy."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s1-4","Acute membranous laryngitis","Croup and epiglottitis · Acute laryngitis","Pyogenic membrane on the larynx, often spreading from the pharynx; it must be distinguished from diphtheria by swab and culture."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s2-1","Definition and cause","Croup and epiglottitis · Acute epiglottitis","Acute inflammation of the supraglottis (epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds and arytenoids), whose loose mucosa swells until it obstructs the airway; the swollen epiglottis acts like a ball valve. Classically in children of two to seven years from H. influenzae type b; since Hib vaccination it is rare in children and now seen mostly in adults, with streptococci, staphylococci and other organisms."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s2-2","Clinical features","Croup and epiglottitis · Acute epiglottitis","Abrupt onset, rapid progression over hours, high fever and toxicity (septicaemia). Children: stridor and difficulty breathing are the presenting features; the child sits upright, leaning forward with the chin out (tripod position), drooling because swallowing hurts, with a muffled voice and usually no cough. Adults: severe sore throat and painful swallowing out of proportion to a normal-looking oropharynx, a muffled \"hot-potato\" voice, and tenderness over the hyoid. Do not upset the child No tongue depressor, no throat examination, no cannula, no lying down, no unaccompanied trip to radiology. Keep the child with the parent, give oxygen without a struggle, and take them to theatre where an experienced anaesthetist and an ENT surgeon ready for tracheostomy can secure the airway under gaseous induction."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s2-3","Investigations","Croup and epiglottitis · Acute epiglottitis","Lateral soft tissue radiograph of the neck (only if the patient is stable and accompanied): a swollen epiglottis, the thumb sign , with ballooned hypopharynx. Flexible endoscopy in adults is usually safe and shows a cherry-red, swollen epiglottis and arytenoids. Blood cultures (more often positive than surface swabs) and epiglottic swabs after the airway is secure; CT for a suspected epiglottic abscess."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s2-4","Treatment","Croup and epiglottitis · Acute epiglottitis","Airway: intubation (or tracheostomy if intubation fails) in theatre for children and for adults with stridor or rapid progression; selected adults presenting later without stridor can be watched closely in high-dependency care. Intravenous antibiotics at once: a third-generation cephalosporin (ceftriaxone or cefotaxime), or ampicillin in sensitive strains. Steroids to reduce oedema; humidified oxygen; intravenous fluids. Rifampicin prophylaxis for household contacts of Hib cases, and vaccination. Intubation is usually needed for 24 to 72 hours, until the epiglottis has shrunk."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s3-1","Cause and pathology","Croup and epiglottitis · Croup","The commonest cause of acute stridor in children, between six months and three years, more in boys and in autumn and winter. Usually parainfluenza viruses (types 1 and 2), also RSV, influenza and adenovirus, with secondary bacterial infection. The loose areolar tissue of the subglottis swells inside the rigid cricoid ring; thick secretions and crusts add to the obstruction."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s3-2","Clinical features","Croup and epiglottitis · Croup","A day or two of a cold, then hoarseness, a harsh barking (seal-like) cough and inspiratory or biphasic stridor, worse at night; fever is usually low or moderate and the child is not toxic. Increasing obstruction shows as suprasternal, intercostal and subcostal recession, tachypnoea and agitation; restlessness, a rising pulse, pallor and cyanosis and finally exhaustion or drowsiness are late, ominous signs. Croup and epiglottitis in children Feature Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) Acute epiglottitis Organism Parainfluenza virus H. influenzae type b Age Six months to three years Two to seven years Site Subglottis Supraglottis Prodrome Cold for a day or two None Onset Gradual Sudden Fever Low or moderate High General state Not toxic Toxic Cough Barking Usually absent Voice Hoarse Muffled Drooling and painful swallowing Absent Present Posture Any Upright, leaning forward Radiograph Steeple sign on an AP view Thumb sign on a lateral view Treatment Steroids, nebulised adrenaline, humidified oxygen Airway in theatre, intravenous antibiotics"],["sec","cl:t-croup~s3-3","Treatment","Croup and epiglottitis · Croup","Mild: a single dose of oral dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg (doses up to 0.6 mg/kg are used; the lower dose appears as effective) or nebulised budesonide, and observation; most go home. Moderate to severe: humidified oxygen, steroids and nebulised adrenaline , which works within minutes but wears off in about two hours, so observe for rebound; admit. Severe or failing: intubation in theatre with a tube smaller than usual for age; tracheostomy rarely now. Antibiotics only for suspected secondary bacterial infection. Recurrent croup A child with repeated attacks, attacks under six months, or croup that will not settle needs microlaryngoscopy and bronchoscopy when well, to look for subglottic stenosis, a subglottic cyst or haemangioma, or a laryngeal cleft ( Congenital lesions of the larynx )."],["ev","cl:t-croup~s3-4","Croup and epiglottitis: current evidence","Croup and epiglottitis · Croup","A Cochrane review of 43 trials (4,565 children) found glucocorticoids improved croup within two hours, with the effect lasting at least 24 hours, halved return visits and readmissions (risk ratio 0.52) and shortened hospital stay by about 15 hours (Gates et al., 2018). Nebulised adrenaline improved croup scores at 30 minutes but not at two to six hours, so children are observed for rebound (Bjornson et al., 2013). In adult epiglottitis, a meta-analysis of 30 studies (10,148 patients) found about 1 in 9 need an airway intervention since Hib vaccination, with an epiglottic abscess, stridor or diabetes predicting the need (Sideris et al., Laryngoscope 2020)."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s3-4","Spasmodic croup","Croup and epiglottitis · Croup","Sudden nocturnal attacks of barking cough and stridor in an otherwise well child without fever, settling by morning; often allergic or reflux-related."],["sec","cl:t-croup~c4","Bacterial tracheitis","Croup and epiglottitis · Bacterial tracheitis","Croup that is not croup. A rare, serious bacterial infection ( Staphylococcus aureus , streptococci, pneumococcus), often following viral croup: high fever, toxicity, stridor and a cough with thick secretions, failing to respond to croup treatment. Bronchoscopy shows purulent tracheitis with oedema, sloughed mucosa and membranes that must be removed, often repeatedly; intubation and intravenous antibiotics; pneumonia frequently follows."],["sec","cl:t-croup~c5","Laryngeal diphtheria","Croup and epiglottitis · Laryngeal diphtheria","The membrane in the airway. Usually secondary to faucial diphtheria in unimmunised children under ten. An insidious onset with low fever but marked toxaemia (tachycardia, thready pulse); hoarseness, croupy cough, inspiratory stridor and progressive obstruction; an adherent grey-white membrane on the tonsils, palate and pharynx that bleeds when lifted and extends into the larynx and trachea; a bull neck. The exotoxin causes myocarditis and palatal, pharyngeal, ocular and diaphragmatic paralysis. Laryngeal diphtheria Treat on clinical suspicion; take smears and cultures. Antitoxin, 20,000 to 100,000 units intravenously after a test dose, according to severity and duration (laryngeal disease sits at the severe end; the doses for faucial diphtheria are in Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess ). Benzylpenicillin or erythromycin for 7 to 10 days. Airway: intubation after laryngoscopy and removal of membrane, or tracheostomy. Strict bed rest for two to four weeks because of myocarditis; cardiac monitoring. Isolation, immunisation of contacts."],["sec","cl:t-croup~c6","Laryngeal oedema","Croup and epiglottitis · Laryngeal oedema","Many causes, one danger. Oedema collects where the mucosa is loose, in the supraglottis and subglottis, and rarely in the vocal folds."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s6-1","Causes","Croup and epiglottitis · Laryngeal oedema","Infection: epiglottitis, croup, tuberculosis, syphilis; nearby quinsy, retropharyngeal abscess and Ludwig's angina ( Deep neck space infection ). Trauma: surgery of the tongue and floor of mouth, laryngeal trauma, foreign bodies, endoscopy (especially in children), intubation, burns, caustics and irritant gases. Neoplasm: ulcerating cancers of the larynx and laryngopharynx. Allergy: angioneurotic oedema, anaphylaxis; ACE inhibitor and hereditary angioedema ( Acute and chronic pharyngitis ). Radiotherapy. Systemic: nephritis, heart failure, myxoedema."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s6-2","Management","Croup and epiglottitis · Laryngeal oedema","Inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress call for an airway: intubation or tracheostomy. Lesser degrees are treated according to cause: intramuscular adrenaline (0.5 mL of 1:1000 in adults, repeated after five minutes if needed), antihistamines and steroids for allergy; steroids for infective, traumatic and post-radiation oedema; C1 esterase inhibitor or icatibant for hereditary angioedema; stopping the ACE inhibitor."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s7-1","Viva essentials","Croup and epiglottitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Croup versus epiglottitis: the table. How not to examine a child with suspected epiglottitis, and how to secure the airway. Treatment of croup: dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline. Bacterial tracheitis as croup that fails to respond. Laryngeal diphtheria. Causes and treatment of laryngeal oedema."],["sec","cl:t-croup~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Croup and epiglottitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using a tongue depressor on a drooling, stridulous child. Sending a child with epiglottitis to radiology alone. Discharging a child soon after nebulised adrenaline. Missing epiglottitis in an adult with severe throat pain and a normal oropharynx."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s1-1","Definition","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Overview","Inflammation of the nose and sinuses lasting 12 weeks or more, with at least two cardinal symptoms, one of which must be blockage or discharge, plus objective evidence on endoscopy or CT. 1.1.1 Cardinal symptoms Nasal blockage or congestion. Anterior or posterior (postnasal) discharge, often purulent. Facial pain or pressure. Reduced or lost sense of smell. 1.1.2 Objective signs Endoscopy showing oedema or polyps in the middle meatus, or mucopus there; or CT showing mucosal changes in the ostiomeatal complex or sinuses."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s1-2","Two phenotypes","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Overview","CRS without nasal polyps: more often infective and neutrophilic. CRS with nasal polyps: usually type 2 (eosinophilic) inflammation driven by IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, often with asthma."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s1-3","The EPOS 2020 classification","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Overview","Whether polyps are present says little about the disease underneath, so EPOS 2020 classifies chronic rhinosinusitis by mechanism. Primary, localised (one side or one sinus system): for example isolated sphenoid disease or allergic fungal rhinosinusitis. Primary, diffuse (both sides): split into type 2 (eosinophilic, polyps, asthma, recurrence) and non-type 2 endotypes. Secondary: arising from something else: odontogenic sinusitis, a tumour, immunodeficiency, primary ciliary dyskinesia, cystic fibrosis, or eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Localised non-type 2 disease has the best prognosis; diffuse type 2 disease is part of a wider airway disorder and tends to recur. Central compartment atopic disease is allergic inflammation centred on the middle turbinate head, superior septum and posterosuperior nasal cavity, in contrast to polyps from the middle meatus. Thick \"chewing gum\" eosinophilic mucin suggests eosinophilic or allergic fungal disease. A blood eosinophil count above 0.25 x 10⁹/L supports a type 2 endotype, and above 0.45 x 10⁹/L predicts recurrence."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s1-4","Acute exacerbation","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Overview","A sudden worsening that returns to the chronic baseline after treatment. Acute rhinosinusitis and its complications are covered in Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications ."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s2-1","Predisposing factors","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Causes and pathogenesis","2.1.1 Anatomical Structural variants that narrow the ostiomeatal complex: septal deviation, concha bullosa (a pneumatised middle turbinate), a paradoxical middle turbinate, Haller (infraorbital ethmoid) cells, prominent agger nasi cells, and a pneumatised or medially bent uncinate process. Figure 1 The ostiomeatal complex in coronal section, and a Haller cell narrowing the maxillary outflow. From the anatomy atlas 2.1.2 Mucociliary failure Infection, smoking and pollution impair cilia; in primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome with situs inversus and bronchiectasis) cilia do not beat; in cystic fibrosis the mucus is too thick to move; Young syndrome combines sinopulmonary disease with azoospermia. 2.1.3 Host and environment Allergy, asthma (about half of CRS patients), aspirin sensitivity, immune deficiency (IgG deficiency, HIV, chemotherapy, transplant drugs), smoking, occupational dusts and fumes, dental infection, and osteitis of the sinus bone after surgery. 2.1.4 Biofilms Colonies of bacteria in a protective polysaccharide matrix that resists antibiotics and keeps the disease going."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s2-2","Organisms","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Causes and pathogenesis","Polymicrobial: Staphylococcus aureus (sometimes methicillin-resistant), Pseudomonas , Klebsiella , E. coli and anaerobes, unlike the organisms of acute disease."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s2-3","The cycle","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Causes and pathogenesis","Ostial obstruction leads to stasis, low oxygen and infection; infection and inflammation damage cilia and swell the mucosa further, which blocks the ostium more. Figure 2 First, the sequence of events after sinus ostial obstruction. Second, the factors in the vicious cycle of chronic sinusitis. Dhingra, figs 37.1 and 37.2"],["sec","cl:t-crs~s3-1","Two kinds","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Nasal polyps","Antrochoanal or ethmoidal polyps? Antrochoanal polyp Ethmoidal polyps Age Children and young adults Adults Cause Infection Allergy, multifactorial inflammation Number and side Single, one-sided Multiple, both sides Origin Maxillary sinus, near the accessory ostium Ethmoid cells, uncinate, middle turbinate, middle meatus Growth Backwards to the choana; may hang behind the soft palate Forwards; may appear at the nostril Shape Three parts: antral, choanal and nasal Small, grape-like masses Recurrence Uncommon after complete removal Common"],["sec","cl:t-crs~s3-2","Antrochoanal polyp","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Nasal polyps","Also called Killian's polyp . It arises near the accessory ostium of the maxillary sinus and has three parts: a thin antral stalk, a round choanal part, and a flattened nasal part. It causes one-sided blockage that may become bilateral when the choanal part fills the nasopharynx, with a hyponasal voice. It can be missed on anterior rhinoscopy and is seen with an endoscope or hanging behind the soft palate. CT shows an opaque antrum and the mass passing through the ostium. Figure 3 First, an antrochoanal polyp at the left nostril of a 14-year-old. Second, one hanging into the oropharynx behind the soft palate, and the polyp after removal. Dhingra, figs 32.1 and 32.2 Figure 4 Endoscopic view of a choanal polyp. Dhingra, fig. 32.3 3.2.1 Treatment Endoscopic removal including its antral attachment, which prevents recurrence. Caldwell-Luc surgery is avoided, especially in children, because it damages the developing teeth."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s3-3","Ethmoidal (bilateral) polyps","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Nasal polyps","3.3.1 Associations Chronic rhinosinusitis, allergic and non-allergic, and NARES. Asthma (about 7% of asthmatics have polyps). Aspirin intolerance: about a third; Samter's triad (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease) is polyps, asthma and aspirin sensitivity. Cystic fibrosis (about 20%); polyps in a child should prompt a sweat test. Allergic fungal sinusitis (almost all; Fungal sinusitis ). Kartagener and Young syndromes, Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis), and nasal mastocytosis. 3.3.2 Pathology Oedematous mucosa of the middle meatus and turbinate prolapses into the nose; sessile polyps become pedunculated with gravity and sneezing. Large fluid-filled spaces and eosinophils lie in the stroma; the surface may become squamous where exposed. 3.3.3 Site Always from the lateral wall, usually the middle meatus (uncinate, bulla, sinus ostia, middle turbinate); allergic polyps almost never arise from the septum or floor. 3.3.4 Clinical picture Progressive bilateral blockage, loss of smell, headache from sinusitis, sneezing and watery discharge. Polyps are smooth, glistening, pale, grape-like, insensitive to probing and do not bleed. Long-standing polyposis broadens the nose and widens the intercanthal distance (the frog face , or Woakes syndrome in its destructive form). Figure 5 First, multiple polyps on endoscopy. Second, a polyp protruding from the left nostril. Dhingra, figs 32.4 and 32.5 3.3.5 Differential diagnosis A blob of mucus (disappears on blowing), a hypertrophied middle turbinate (pink and bony on probing), angiofibroma (bleeds, "],["sec","cl:t-crs~s4-1","Nasal endoscopy","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Assessment","Before and after decongestion: oedema, polyps and pus in the middle meatus and sphenoethmoidal recess. Polyps are graded by extent (for example the Lund-Kennedy score)."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s4-2","CT of the sinuses","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Assessment","Non-contrast CT in three planes is the gold standard: it shows the extent of disease (scored by the Lund-Mackay system), anatomical variants and bony erosion, and maps the anatomy for surgery. Use it to confirm disease when endoscopy is unclear and before any operation. Incidental changes are common: retention cysts in 10 to 15% and mucosal thickening in a quarter of normal people, who score up to about 4 on Lund-Mackay; a score above 5 strongly suggests disease. MRI is too sensitive to diagnose chronic rhinosinusitis, showing thickening in about half the general population."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s4-3","Other tests","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Assessment","Allergy testing, a sweat test or genetics for cystic fibrosis in children, ciliary studies, immunoglobulins, eosinophil count and ANCA when vasculitis is suspected ( Granulomatous disease of the nose ), and culture from the middle meatus."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s4-4","Symptom scores","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Assessment","Patient-reported scores such as SNOT-22 measure the burden and the response to treatment."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s5-1","CRS without polyps","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Medical treatment","Saline irrigation: high-volume rinses clear mucus, bacteria and allergens and disrupt biofilms. Intranasal corticosteroids: sprays or drops; better after decongestion and with correct technique. Antibiotics: culture-guided for exacerbations only. Long-term low-dose macrolides were once used for their anti-inflammatory effect, but in the MACRO trial three months of clarithromycin was no better than placebo, so they are no longer recommended routinely (some units still try them in non-type 2 disease with normal IgE). Treat allergy: antihistamines, leukotriene antagonists, immunotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s5-2","CRS with polyps","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Medical treatment","Steroid sprays or drops, long-term. Short oral steroid courses shrink polyps for a few weeks; limit their number because of side effects. Doxycycline for a few weeks reduces polyp size modestly. Treat asthma with the respiratory team. Aspirin-exacerbated disease: leukotriene modifiers, and aspirin desensitisation after surgery. Biologics: monoclonal antibodies against type 2 inflammation (dupilumab against the IL-4 receptor, omalizumab against IgE, mepolizumab against IL-5). EPOS 2020 indicates them for bilateral polyps after sinus surgery (or in patients unfit for it) with at least three of: evidence of type 2 disease (tissue eosinophils 10 or more per high-power field, blood eosinophils 0.25 x 10⁹/L or more, or total IgE 100 or more), need for systemic steroids (two or more courses a year or long-term use), SNOT-22 of 40 or more, anosmia on testing, and asthma needing inhaled steroids. Response is reviewed at 16 weeks and one year. Availability is limited by cost and national approval."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s6-1","Endoscopic sinus surgery","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Surgery","For patients whose symptoms persist despite adequate medical treatment (usually at least eight weeks of saline and intranasal steroids). It removes polyps and diseased tissue, opens the natural ostia, corrects obstructing anatomy, and makes a cavity that saline and steroids can reach. For diffuse type 2 disease the aim is a complete, wide cavity (a \"neo-sinus\") for topical steroid access; limited opening of the ostia suits localised disease. Medical treatment continues afterwards. Revision is common in polyposis. In cystic fibrosis and ciliary disease, wide dependent drainage of the maxillary and sphenoid sinuses is made. Figure 6 Functional endoscopic sinus surgery, without and with a camera and monitor. Dhingra, figs 37.4 and 37.5"],["sec","cl:t-crs~s6-2","Historical operations","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Surgery","Intranasal polypectomy alone, Caldwell-Luc operation, intranasal and external ethmoidectomy, and external frontoethmoidectomy (Howarth or Lynch); now largely replaced by endoscopic surgery ( Nose and sinus operations: principles )."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s7-1","Chronic maxillary sinusitis","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Chronic sinusitis by sinus","Purulent discharge, postnasal drip, cacosmia, often dental in origin; endoscopic middle meatal antrostomy has replaced antral washouts, inferior meatal antrostomy and Caldwell-Luc operations."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s7-2","Chronic frontal sinusitis","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Chronic sinusitis by sinus","Frontal headache and discharge; endoscopic frontal sinusotomy, extended to a Draf III (modified Lothrop) procedure when needed; external frontoethmoidectomy and osteoplastic obliteration are reserved for failures."],["ev","cl:t-crs~s7-3","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps: current evidence","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Chronic sinusitis by sinus","The European position paper EPOS 2020 defines the classification by endotype, the place of biologics and the care pathway (Fokkens et al., Rhinology 2020). In the UK MACRO trial of about 500 adults with persistent chronic rhinosinusitis on saline and steroids, endoscopic sinus surgery improved symptoms and quality of life compared with placebo at six months, while three months of clarithromycin did not (Philpott et al., Lancet 2025). Dupilumab reduced polyp size, nasal blockage and the need for systemic steroids or surgery in two phase 3 trials (Bachert et al., Lancet 2019). A Cochrane review supports large-volume saline irrigation as a low-cost adjunct (Chong et al., 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s7-3","Chronic ethmoiditis and sphenoiditis","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · Chronic sinusitis by sinus","Usually part of pansinusitis; endoscopic ethmoidectomy and sphenoidotomy."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s8-1","Viva essentials","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Definition and diagnostic criteria. Predisposing factors, especially anatomical variants and ciliary disorders. Antrochoanal versus ethmoidal polyps. Samter's triad, and polyps in a child (cystic fibrosis). Medical treatment, including biologics. Indications for endoscopic sinus surgery."],["sec","cl:t-crs~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps · For the examination","Common pitfalls Repeated antibiotics without steroids or saline. Calling a one-sided polyp benign without histology. Operating before medical treatment has been tried properly. Forgetting the sweat test in a child with polyps."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s1-1","Definition","CSF rhinorrhoea · Overview","Leakage of cerebrospinal fluid from the subarachnoid space into the nose through a defect in the dura and the bone of the skull base."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s1-2","CSF physiology","CSF rhinorrhoea · Overview","CSF is secreted by the choroid plexuses at about 20 mL an hour (350 to 500 mL a day) and absorbed by the arachnoid villi into the venous sinuses; the total volume is 90 to 150 mL, replaced three to five times a day. Normal lumbar pressure is 60 to 250 mm of water, rising with coughing, sneezing, nose blowing, straining and lifting, all of which a patient with a leak should avoid."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s1-3","Why it matters","CSF rhinorrhoea · Overview","Ascending infection causes meningitis, often pneumococcal and recurrent; air can enter the cranium (pneumocephalus)."],["sec","cl:t-csf~c2","Causes","CSF rhinorrhoea · Causes","Trauma first. Causes of CSF rhinorrhoea Trauma Accidental anterior skull base and temporal bone fractures Trauma Surgical endoscopic sinus surgery, pituitary and skull base surgery Inflammation and erosion Mucoceles, polyposis fungal disease, osteomyelitis Tumours Benign and malignant invading the skull base; pituitary tumours Congenital Meningocele, encephalocele skull base defects Spontaneous Raised intracranial pressure often obese middle-aged women; empty sella"],["sec","cl:t-csf~s2-1","Traumatic","CSF rhinorrhoea · Causes","The commonest cause: about 80% of leaks follow accidental injury, mostly fractures of the anterior skull base. Surgical injury, especially at the lateral lamella of the cribriform plate during ethmoid surgery ( Nose and sinus operations: principles ), may leak at once or later."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s2-2","Spontaneous","CSF rhinorrhoea · Causes","Congenital defects and encephaloceles in children are covered in Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses . Among adults with spontaneous leaks, many patients have idiopathic intracranial hypertension : typically obese middle-aged women, with an empty sella on MRI, and often small encephaloceles in the cribriform plate or lateral sphenoid recess. Their leaks recur unless the pressure is also treated."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s2-3","Sites","CSF rhinorrhoea · Causes","From the anterior cranial fossa: the cribriform plate (commonest), the ethmoid roof or the frontal sinus. From the middle cranial fossa: through the sphenoid sinus. After a temporal bone fracture, CSF passes into the middle ear and down the Eustachian tube ( CSF otorhinorrhoea ; Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea ). Figure 1 First, sites of leakage: (a) frontal sinus, (b) ethmoid, (c) sphenoid, (d) Eustachian tube after a temporal bone fracture. Second, bony defects in (a) the cribriform plate and (b) the ethmoid roof. Dhingra, figs 29.4 and 29.5 Figure 2 The fovea ethmoidalis, lateral lamella and cribriform plate on coronal CT, and the Keros classification of the olfactory fossa: a deep fossa (Keros 3) has a long, thin lateral lamella, the commonest site of surgical injury. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-csf~s3-1","History","CSF rhinorrhoea · Clinical picture","A clear, watery discharge from one side, increasing on bending forward, straining or lifting, sometimes in the morning when the patient first bends down and a sinus reservoir empties (the reservoir sign , typical of sphenoid leaks). It may taste salty or sweet, and cannot be sniffed back. There may be headaches (low pressure from the leak, or high pressure in idiopathic intracranial hypertension), loss of smell after trauma, or a history of meningitis."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s3-2","Halo sign","CSF rhinorrhoea · Clinical picture","After trauma, bloodstained fluid on filter paper gives a central red spot with a clear outer ring (the double ring or target sign ); it suggests, but does not prove, CSF. CSF or nasal secretion? CSF Nasal secretion History Head injury, sinus or skull base surgery, tumour Sneezing, itching, blockage Flow Drops or a stream on bending or straining; cannot be sniffed back Continuous; can be sniffed back Character Thin, watery, clear Mucoid, or watery like tears Handkerchief Stays soft Stiffens (mucus) Glucose Higher (over about 30 mg/dL), but the test is unreliable Lower Beta-2 transferrin Present Absent"],["sec","cl:t-csf~s3-3","Examination","CSF rhinorrhoea · Clinical picture","Endoscopy may show fluid from the olfactory cleft, the middle meatus, the sphenoethmoidal recess or the Eustachian tube, or an encephalocele; look in the ear for fluid behind the drum."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s4-1","Confirming CSF","CSF rhinorrhoea · Investigations","Beta-2 transferrin: a protein found only in CSF, perilymph and aqueous humour; the specific and sensitive test, needing only a few drops. Beta-trace protein: an alternative, also specific, used widely in Europe. Glucose testing is unreliable and no longer recommended."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s4-2","Localising the leak","CSF rhinorrhoea · Investigations","High-resolution CT of the skull base in fine coronal and axial slices: the first test, showing bony defects in the cribriform plate, ethmoid roof, frontal and sphenoid sinuses. MRI (heavily T2-weighted): shows fluid tracking through the defect and any encephalocele or intracranial pathology, without injections; also shows an empty sella. CT cisternography: intrathecal contrast with CT, when the leak is active but not localised. Intrathecal fluorescein: a small dose of diluted fluorescein by lumbar puncture, seen endoscopically as yellow or, with a blue filter, fluorescent green; used before or during surgery to find the site."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s5-1","Conservative treatment","CSF rhinorrhoea · Management","For early traumatic leaks: bed rest with the head raised, stool softeners, no nose blowing, sneezing through an open mouth, no straining. Acetazolamide reduces CSF production; a lumbar drain lowers the pressure. Most traumatic leaks close within one to two weeks. Routine prophylactic antibiotics are not recommended, because there is no evidence that they prevent meningitis; pneumococcal vaccination is advised instead."],["ev","cl:t-csf~s5-2","CSF rhinorrhoea: current evidence","CSF rhinorrhoea · Management","Endoscopic repair. A systematic review of 55 studies found endoscopic repair successful at the first attempt in about 90% of patients, and in about 97% after revision, regardless of graft material (Psaltis et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2012). The vascularised nasoseptal flap (Hadad et al., Laryngoscope 2006) brought postoperative leak rates after extended endoscopic skull base surgery down to about 5% in later series. Antibiotics. A Cochrane review found no evidence that prophylactic antibiotics prevent meningitis after basal skull fracture, with or without a leak (Ratilal et al., 2015); pneumococcal vaccination is recommended instead. A systematic review found little evidence for routine lumbar drains or prolonged antibiotics after repair."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s5-2","Surgical repair","CSF rhinorrhoea · Management","5.2.1 Indications A leak that persists beyond about one to two weeks, recurs, follows surgery and is recognised at the time, or is spontaneous; meningitis; a large defect or an encephalocele. 5.2.2 Endoscopic repair The standard approach, with success over 90% at the first attempt. The defect is exposed, mucosa removed from its edges, any encephalocele reduced or cauterised, and the defect closed in layers: Inlay (underlay) graft of fascia, fat or a dural substitute between the dura and the bone. Onlay graft of mucosa or fascia over the defect. Vascularised flap for large defects, most commonly the nasoseptal (Hadad-Bassagasteguy) flap on the posterior septal artery. Fibrin glue and absorbable packing to support the repair. Figure 3 Repair of a CSF fistula with fascia and a mucosal graft; frontal sinus leaks often need an external approach. Dhingra, fig. 29.6 5.2.3 Open approaches Intracranial repair by craniotomy for very large defects, multiple fractures or associated brain injury; external approaches for leaks high in the frontal sinus. 5.2.4 Afterwards Bed rest with the head up, avoidance of straining, stool softeners and no nose blowing for several weeks. In spontaneous leaks, treat raised pressure (weight loss, acetazolamide, sometimes a shunt) to prevent recurrence. Pearls Beta-2 transferrin is the test; the halo sign is only a hint. Spontaneous leak: think of idiopathic intracranial hypertension. The lateral lamella of the cribriform plate is the weak spot in ethmoid surgery."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s6-1","Viva essentials","CSF rhinorrhoea · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes and sites of CSF rhinorrhoea. CSF versus nasal secretions. Beta-2 transferrin, and localisation by CT, MRI, cisternography and fluorescein. Conservative treatment, and indications for repair. Endoscopic multilayer repair and the nasoseptal flap."],["sec","cl:t-csf~s6-2","Common pitfalls","CSF rhinorrhoea · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a one-sided clear drip as rhinitis. Relying on glucose testing. Repairing a spontaneous leak without treating raised intracranial pressure."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s1-1","Definition","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Overview","Chronic suppurative otitis media is long-standing infection of part or all of the middle ear cleft, with ear discharge and a permanent perforation. It is classically divided into two types: the tubotympanic or safe type, which involves the mucosa and the anteroinferior cleft, and the atticoantral or unsafe type, which involves bone and usually cholesteatoma. This chapter covers the safe, mucosal type. Figure 1 Classification of chronic otitis media into tubotympanic (mucosal) and atticoantral (squamous) types. Dhingra, fig. 11.7"],["sec","cl:t-csom~s1-2","Clinical stages","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Overview","1.2.1 Active mucosal disease Discharge through a pars tensa perforation. 1.2.2 Inactive mucosal disease A dry, permanent perforation that may become active with a cold or water. 1.2.3 Healed chronic otitis media An atrophic, healed drum, often with tympanosclerosis and some conductive loss."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s1-3","Why it matters","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Overview","It is a common cause of preventable hearing loss worldwide, especially where crowding, poverty and limited access to care allow acute infections to go untreated."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s2-1","Origins","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Pathogenesis","Most cases follow repeated acute otitis media in childhood, or a perforation from acute necrotising otitis or trauma. Chronic effusion and Eustachian tube dysfunction keep the mucosa inflamed."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s2-2","The permanent perforation","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Pathogenesis","Once the squamous epithelium of the drum grows round the edge of the hole and meets the mucosa on the inside, the perforation behaves like a lined fistula and will not close on its own. Figure 2 A permanent perforation of the pars tensa: the epidermal and mucosal layers meet across its edge, so it behaves like a non-healing fistula. Dhingra, fig. 11.5"],["sec","cl:t-csom~s2-3","Why the discharge recurs","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Pathogenesis","2.3.1 Reinfection through the tube Infection ascends from the nose, sinuses, adenoids and tonsils, especially in children, and nose blowing pushes secretions into the middle ear. 2.3.2 Reinfection through the canal Water entering through the perforation during bathing or swimming. 2.3.3 Biofilms Bacteria, particularly Pseudomonas aeruginosa , form biofilms on the mucosa that shield them from antibiotics and immune cells, explaining the relapses after apparently successful treatment."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s2-4","Organisms","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Pathogenesis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa , Proteus , Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus are the commonest aerobes, often with anaerobes such as Bacteroides . Fungi may follow repeated antibiotic drops."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s3-1","Symptoms","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Clinical features","3.1.1 Discharge Profuse, mucoid or mucopurulent, without a foul smell, and intermittent: it flares with colds and with water in the ear and dries between episodes. 3.1.2 Hearing loss Conductive, of variable degree, and rarely over 50 dB. Some patients hear better when the ear is wet, because the discharge shields the round window and restores a phase difference between the windows."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s3-2","Signs","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Clinical features","3.2.1 The perforation Central, with a rim of drum all round it. It may lie anterior, posterior or inferior to the handle of malleus, or be subtotal. A large perforation may expose the ossicles and the promontory. Figure 3 First, the types of perforation seen in the pars tensa. Second, a large central perforation. Dhingra, figs 11.10 and 11.11 Figure 4 A normal tympanic membrane and its parts, for describing the site of a perforation. From the anatomy atlas 3.2.2 The middle ear mucosa Normal and pale when inactive, red and oedematous when active. A pale polyp may fill the perforation. Figure 5 An aural polyp in the canal, and how a polyp from the promontory can present through the perforation. Dhingra, fig. 11.9 Never avulse a polyp A polyp may arise from the stapes, the facial nerve or the lateral semicircular canal. Pulling it out risks facial palsy or a dead ear; remove it under the microscope or at surgery, and send it for histology."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s3-3","Safe or unsafe?","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Clinical features","Mucosal versus squamous chronic otitis media Mucosal (safe) Squamous (unsafe) Discharge Profuse, mucoid, not foul Scanty, foul-smelling Perforation Central Attic or posterosuperior marginal Polyp Pale Red, fleshy Cholesteatoma Absent Present Complications Rare More common Hearing Mild to moderate conductive Conductive or mixed Figure 6 Sites of perforation: central perforations belong to the safe type; attic and posterosuperior marginal perforations to the dangerous type. Dhingra, fig. 11.8"],["sec","cl:t-csom~s4-1","Examination under the microscope","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Investigations","Every chronic ear should be cleaned and examined with the microscope. An apparently dry ear can hide discharge, and rarely cholesteatoma coexists with a central perforation. Figure 7 Examination of the ear under the operating microscope. Dhingra, fig. 11.12"],["sec","cl:t-csom~s4-2","Swab for culture","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Investigations","Guides the choice of drops when the ear does not respond."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s4-3","Tuning forks and audiogram","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Investigations","Measure the conductive loss and bone conduction. An air-bone gap over about 40 dB suggests ossicular discontinuity, often erosion of the long process of the incus."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s4-4","Imaging","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Investigations","CT is not routine for mucosal disease, where it shows no bone erosion. It is used when cholesteatoma or a complication is suspected, and before revision surgery. MRI is reserved for intracranial concerns."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s4-5","Biopsy","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Investigations","For granulation that persists despite treatment, to exclude tuberculosis and malignancy."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s4-6","Differential diagnosis","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Investigations","Cholesteatoma : scanty foul discharge, attic or marginal defect, white flakes. Tuberculous otitis media : painless thin discharge, pale granulation, multiple perforations, early facial palsy. Granular myringitis : granulation on the drum itself, without a perforation. Carcinoma of the temporal bone : pain, bleeding, and granulation that does not settle."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s5-1","Aural toilet","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Management","Drops cannot work through pus. Clean the ear under the microscope by suction, dry mopping or gentle saline irrigation; never use forceful syringing."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s5-2","Topical treatment","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Management","5.2.1 Drops Topical antibiotic drops are the main treatment once the ear is clean; they reach far higher concentrations than oral antibiotics. Quinolone drops (ciprofloxacin or ofloxacin, with or without a steroid) are preferred when the drum is perforated, because they are not ototoxic. Continue for one to two weeks after debridement, longer for a persistently active ear, and review. 5.2.2 Technique Tragal pumping after instilling the drops pushes them through the perforation into the middle ear. 5.2.3 Acidifying drops Acetic acid 1.5% helps against Pseudomonas . 5.2.4 Aminoglycoside drops and a perforation Gentamicin and neomycin-containing drops are ototoxic in animals, and manufacturers advise against them with an open middle ear. National specialist bodies, including ENT UK, have concluded that the risk from active infection outweighs the small risk from the drops: they may be used for active infection for up to about two weeks, with the patient warned to stop if hearing worsens or dizziness develops, and ideally a hearing test before treatment. Quinolone resistance in Pseudomonas is a growing concern, which is why aminoglycosides still have a role. Where the books differ · Ototoxic drops Dhingra Advises avoiding aminoglycoside and polymyxin drops with a perforation. Lee Lists neomycin and polymyxin preparations among the options. In practice Quinolones first; short courses of aminoglycoside drops are acceptable for active infection, with warnings."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s5-3","Systemic antibiotics","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Management","Mainly for spreading infection, complications or failure of topical treatment, chosen by culture; topical treatment works better than oral antibiotics for a discharging ear."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s5-4","Treating the source","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Management","Treat infected tonsils, adenoids and sinuses, and allergy; avoid hard nose blowing."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s5-5","Keep water out","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Management","Cotton wool with petroleum jelly, or a moulded plug, during bathing; no swimming while the ear is active."],["ev","cl:t-csom~s5-6","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma: current evidence","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Management","A series of Cochrane reviews in 2020 and 2021 found the evidence for treatments of chronic suppurative otitis media limited and largely old, but suggested that topical antibiotics improve resolution of discharge compared with placebo and are probably more effective than systemic antibiotics (Brennan-Jones et al., 2020; Chong et al., 2021). Quinolone and aminoglycoside drops could not be separated on effectiveness, and adverse effects were poorly reported; antibiotic drops may be more effective than antiseptics such as boric acid (Head et al., 2020). Results of tympanoplasty and the place of mastoidectomy are covered in Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles ."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s5-6","Surgery","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Management","Figure 8 A management pathway for chronic suppurative otitis media, from medical treatment to tympanoplasty and mastoid surgery. Dhingra, fig. 11.14 5.6.1 Tympanoplasty Once the ear has been dry for some weeks, repair of the perforation (myringoplasty), with ossicular reconstruction if needed, restores a closed, protected middle ear. Success is 60 to 90%, higher when air passes through the perforation on Valsalva, a sign of a working tube. 5.6.2 Mastoidectomy For discharge that persists despite proper medical treatment. Cortical mastoidectomy may be combined with tympanoplasty; on its own it does not improve the rate of drum closure. 5.6.3 Hearing aids For patients unfit for or unwilling to have surgery, ideally once the ear is dry. Pearls The other ear predicts how well the Eustachian tube works. Think of tuberculosis when an ear fails to respond, with pale granulation, multiple holes and facial weakness. No treatment of the Eustachian tube has proven benefit."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s6-1","Ossicular erosion","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Complications","The long process of the incus is a vascular watershed and erodes first, giving a larger conductive loss."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s6-2","Sensorineural hearing loss","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Complications","Toxins crossing the round window can damage the basal turn of the cochlea, producing a high-frequency loss."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s6-3","Tympanosclerosis","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Complications","Chalky hyaline plaques in the drum and middle ear, which may fix the ossicles."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s6-4","Others","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · Complications","Rarely, mastoiditis ( Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis ), facial palsy, labyrinthitis or intracranial infection ( Intracranial complications of otitis media ) in an acute flare."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s7-1","Viva essentials","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Safe versus unsafe: the six-row comparison. Why the perforation does not heal, and why the discharge recurs. Aural toilet, quinolone drops and water precautions. Hearing better when the ear is wet. Never avulse a polyp. Indications for tympanoplasty, and what predicts success."],["sec","cl:t-csom~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing drops into an ear full of pus. Using aminoglycoside drops through a perforation without need. Calling a chronic ear safe without examining it under the microscope. Missing tuberculosis or malignancy in granulation that will not settle."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s1-1","Why early matters","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Overview","The auditory pathways develop in response to sound during the first years of life. Without input, the auditory cortex is taken over by other functions and language develops late or not at all. Early fitting of hearing aids or a cochlear implant allows near-normal language in many children."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s1-2","Prelingual and postlingual","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Overview","Prelingual deafness occurs before speech has developed and has the greatest effect on language; postlingual deafness occurs after."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s1-3","The 1-3-6 rule","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Overview","Screen by one month, diagnose by three months, and start intervention by six months."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s2-1","Proportions","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Causes","About half of congenital sensorineural loss is genetic, about a quarter environmental and about a quarter unexplained. Of the genetic cases, about 70% are non-syndromic (mostly autosomal recessive) and 30% syndromic."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s2-2","Genetic","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Causes","2.2.1 Non-syndromic GJB2 mutations affecting connexin 26 (DFNB1) account for up to half of recessive non-syndromic deafness. Mitochondrial mutations (12S rRNA, 1555A>G) cause deafness after even a single dose of an aminoglycoside ( Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease ). 2.2.2 Syndromic Syndromes to know Syndrome Clue Hearing loss Waardenburg White forelock, odd-coloured eyes, dystopia canthorum (type 1) Sensorineural Usher Retinitis pigmentosa Sensorineural Pendred Goitre, enlarged vestibular aqueduct Sensorineural Jervell and Lange-Nielsen Fainting, long QT Sensorineural Alport Nephritis, X-linked Progressive sensorineural Treacher Collins Lower lid coloboma, small jaw and malar, microtia Conductive Crouzon and Apert Craniosynostosis, proptosis (syndactyly in Apert) Conductive or mixed Branchio-oto-renal Neck fistulae, pits, renal anomalies Conductive or mixed Stickler Small jaw, cleft, myopia Conductive or sensorineural Goldenhar Facial asymmetry, epibulbar dermoid Conductive or mixed Klippel-Feil Short neck, fused cervical spine Sensorineural or mixed Down Effusion, narrow canals Conductive Figure 1 Waardenburg syndrome: white forelock, heterochromia of the irides and depigmented skin. Dhingra, fig. 19.1 Syndrome pearls Usher type 1: profound loss and absent vestibular function; type 2: moderate loss, normal or reduced vestibular function. Pendred: the goitre appears around age eight; the perchlorate discharge test is positive. Waardenburg type 1 has dystopia canthorum; type 2 does not. Treacher Collins: lower lid coloboma; Goldenhar: upper lid."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s2-3","Inner ear malformations","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Causes","Michel: complete absence of the inner ear; no aid or implant can help. Mondini: a cochlea of only one and a half turns, often with an enlarged aqueduct. Scheibe: cochleosaccular dysplasia, the commonest. Alexander: the basal turn alone, losing high tones. Bing-Siebenmann: the membranous labyrinth fails to develop. Enlarged vestibular aqueduct: fluctuating, progressive loss after minor head injury. Where the books differ · Enlarged vestibular aqueduct Dhingra Wider than 2 mm. Lee 1.5 mm or more. In practice Treat an aqueduct of 1.5 mm or more as suspicious. Advise avoiding contact sports and head injury; surgical closure of the aqueduct causes hearing loss. Figure 2 The membranous labyrinth within the bony labyrinth, the structure that fails to form in these malformations. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s2-4","Environmental","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Causes","Environmental causes Prenatal TORCH infections cytomegalovirus, rubella, toxoplasmosis, syphilis, Zika; aminoglycosides, quinine, thalidomide, alcohol, radiation Perinatal Hypoxia, prematurity birth weight under 1500 g, jaundice (bilirubin over 20 mg%), sepsis, NICU stay, ventilation Postnatal Meningitis viral infections, otitis media with effusion, ototoxic drugs, trauma, noise 2.4.1 Cytomegalovirus The commonest non-genetic cause. The loss can be absent at birth and appear later, so a normal newborn screen does not exclude it. 2.4.2 Meningitis Labyrinthitis ossificans can close the cochlea within weeks; test hearing and image quickly ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis )."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s2-5","Risk factors","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Causes","Family history, craniofacial anomaly or syndrome stigmata, NICU stay of five days or more, ventilation, low Apgar scores, severe jaundice, congenital infection, and meningitis. Half of children with sensorineural loss have no risk factor at all, which is why screening must be universal."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s3-1","Newborn screening","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Evaluation","3.1.1 Otoacoustic emissions How these tests work is covered in Hearing tests: how to do and read them . They test the outer hair cells; quick and cheap. Absent with fluid or debris, or a cochlear loss. Normal in auditory neuropathy, which they therefore miss. 3.1.2 Automated ABR Tests the pathway to the brainstem; used for NICU babies, who are at risk of auditory neuropathy."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s3-2","Diagnostic audiology","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Evaluation","3.2.1 ABR Ear-specific thresholds, under natural sleep or sedation. 3.2.2 Behavioural tests by age Exact ages vary between services; the tests themselves are described in Hearing tests: how to do and read them . Under six months: behavioural observation. Six to eighteen months: distraction testing. Six months to two years: visual reinforcement audiometry. Two to five years: play audiometry. Older: conventional and speech audiometry."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s3-3","Finding the cause","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Evaluation","Imaging (CT and MRI) before implantation; ECG for long QT; urinalysis; thyroid function; eye examination; CMV PCR on newborn saliva, urine or dried blood spot (within 21 days); and genetic testing (GJB2, GJB6 and gene panels). Long QT A deaf child who faints needs an urgent ECG: Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome can cause sudden death. Treat with beta-blockers."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s3-4","Signs at home","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Evaluation","No startle to loud sound, sleeping through noise, no babbling or no words by one to two years, and poor school performance."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s4-1","Counselling","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Management","Parents need a clear explanation, support and early contact with educators."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s4-2","Hearing aids","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Management","As early as possible, in both ears if there is usable hearing."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s4-3","Cochlear implant","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Management","For severe to profound loss with little benefit from aids, ideally around the first birthday (earlier implantation gives better spoken language), with both ears implanted at the same sitting (NICE TA566), or urgently after meningitis, before the cochlea ossifies. Candidacy and the operation are covered in Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s4-4","Communication and education","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Management","Auditory-oral approaches, sign language, or total communication; radio aids at school; speech and language therapy; vocational guidance later. Figure 3 The faculties a hearing-impaired child can use for receptive and expressive communication. Dhingra, fig. 19.3"],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s4-5","Specific treatments","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Management","Symptomatic congenital CMV: valganciclovir for six months, started within the first month of life; six months gave better hearing and development at two years than six weeks (Kimberlin et al., NEJM 2015). Toxoplasmosis: pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine can prevent hearing loss. Long QT: beta-blockers. Pendred goitre: thyroid hormone. Enlarged aqueduct: avoid head injury and contact sport."],["ev","cl:t-deaf~s4-6","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss: current evidence","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Management","Timing. The Joint Committee on Infant Hearing position statement keeps the 1-3-6 targets and encourages 1-2-3 months in well-performing programmes; children with risk factors such as congenital CMV, ECMO, meningitis or a family history need ongoing surveillance even after passing the screen ( J Early Hear Detect Interv 2019). Cochlear implants. Implantation before about 12 to 18 months gives better spoken language than later implantation, and bilateral implants are standard for bilateral profound loss; NICE (TA566, 2019) uses 80 dB HL at two or more frequencies with inadequate benefit from aids. Congenital CMV. An international consensus recommends treatment, started within the first month, for moderately to severely symptomatic infants, including those with isolated hearing loss in some centres (Rawlinson et al., Lancet Infect Dis 2017). Genetics. Comprehensive gene panels find a cause in about 40% of children with non-syndromic loss, most often GJB2 ."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s4-6","Follow-up","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · Management","Children with risk factors need continued hearing checks even after passing the newborn screen; treated CMV infants continue audiology until about age six."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s5-1","On call","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · For the examination","A failed newborn hearing screen Repeat the screen; if it fails again, arrange a diagnostic ABR. Don't reassure. A deaf child with fainting: urgent ECG for long QT. After meningitis: audiology and imaging quickly, before ossification closes the implant window."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s5-2","Viva essentials","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes: genetic, prenatal, perinatal, postnatal. High-risk registry. OAE versus ABR, and auditory neuropathy. Behavioural tests by age. The syndromes table. Inner ear malformations, especially Michel and Mondini."],["sec","cl:t-deaf~s5-3","Common pitfalls","The deaf child and congenital hearing loss · For the examination","Common pitfalls Reassuring parents after a failed screen. Relying on OAE in a NICU baby. Missing late-onset CMV loss after a normal newborn screen. Forgetting the ECG."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s1-1","Deep cervical fascia","Deep neck space infection · Fascia and spaces","Superficial (investing) layer: surrounds the neck, splitting to enclose the sternomastoid, trapezius, submandibular and parotid glands. Middle (visceral) layer: surrounds the larynx, trachea, pharynx, oesophagus and thyroid; its buccopharyngeal part covers the constrictors. Deep layer: the prevertebral fascia in front of the vertebrae and muscles, with the thin alar fascia in front of it. All three contribute to the carotid sheath . Figure 1 The layers of the deep cervical fascia in sagittal and transverse section. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s1-2","Spaces","Deep neck space infection · Fascia and spaces","Spaces of the head and neck Space Extent and contents Usual source of infection Parotid Within the split superficial layer; gland, nodes, facial nerve, external carotid, retromandibular vein Ascending infection via Stensen's duct in dehydrated patients Submandibular (sublingual, submandibular, submental) Above and below the mylohyoid, between mandible and hyoid Teeth, sialadenitis, floor of mouth injury Peritonsillar Between the tonsil capsule and superior constrictor Tonsillar crypts Parapharyngeal Inverted pyramid from skull base to hyoid, beside the pharynx Tonsils, quinsy, teeth, parotid, Bezold abscess, other spaces Retropharyngeal Behind the buccopharyngeal fascia, in front of the alar fascia; skull base to about T2 Suppurating retropharyngeal nodes in children; trauma in adults Danger Between alar and prevertebral fascia; skull base to diaphragm Spread from retropharyngeal or parapharyngeal spaces Prevertebral Behind the prevertebral fascia; skull base to coccyx Tuberculosis and infection of the spine Visceral Around larynx, trachea, pharynx, oesophagus and thyroid, into the mediastinum Perforation of the oesophagus, thyroiditis, surgery Carotid sheath Skull base to the thoracic inlet; carotid, jugular vein, vagus Spread from adjacent spaces, drug injection Masticator Around the masseter, pterygoids and temporalis and the ramus Second and third molars Figure 2 The retropharyngeal, danger and prevertebral spaces, and the parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal spaces. From the anatomy atlas Figure 3 First, the spaces in relation to the pharynx where abscesses form. Second,"],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s2-1","Sources","Deep neck space infection · General principles","Dental infection is the commonest source in adults, and pharyngeal and tonsillar infection in children. Others: suppurating lymph nodes, sinusitis, mastoiditis (Bezold abscess), sialadenitis, trauma, intubation and endoscopy, foreign bodies, intravenous drug injection, and infected congenital cysts. In an adult, a \"neck abscess\" may be a necrotic metastasis from an oropharyngeal cancer."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s2-2","Organisms","Deep neck space infection · General principles","Usually mixed aerobic and anaerobic mouth flora: viridans and group A streptococci, peptostreptococci, staphylococci, Bacteroides and Fusobacterium . Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is common in infants; diabetics and the immunosuppressed acquire Gram-negative organisms; tuberculosis and actinomycosis cause chronic abscesses."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s2-3","Evaluation","Deep neck space infection · General principles","History: pain, fever, swelling, odynophagia, drooling, voice change, trismus, breathing difficulty; recent dental work, sore throat, trauma or intubation; diabetes and immune status. Examination: the teeth, floor of mouth, tonsils and posterior wall; trismus; crepitus (gas-forming organisms); cranial nerves; and flexible endoscopy of the airway whenever there is voice change, dyspnoea, stridor or unexplained dysphagia. Oxygen saturation can be normal with a critical airway. Blood: count, glucose, renal function, cultures. Imaging: contrast-enhanced CT is the key study, showing which spaces are involved, abscess against phlegmon, and spread to the mediastinum; clinical examination alone misjudges the spaces in many cases. Lateral neck radiographs show prevertebral swelling (over about 7 mm at C2 in adults, or more than half a vertebral body in children); chest radiographs for mediastinitis and aspiration; ultrasound in children and for guided aspiration; MRI and MR angiography for intracranial or vascular complications."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s2-4","Treatment","Deep neck space infection · General principles","Airway first. Sit the patient up, give humidified oxygen; steroids and nebulised adrenaline buy time. If the airway is narrowed by more than half, secure it by awake fibreoptic intubation or tracheostomy under local anaesthesia; never paralyse a patient whose airway you cannot see. Fluids: patients are dehydrated from not swallowing. Intravenous antibiotics at once, broad-spectrum with anaerobic cover: co-amoxiclav or ampicillin–sulbactam, or a third-generation cephalosporin with metronidazole; clindamycin in penicillin allergy and in small children; vancomycin for MRSA; antipseudomonal agents in the immunosuppressed. Adjust to cultures. Drainage when there is an abscess in a fascial space, gas, a threatened airway, or no improvement after 48 to 72 hours of antibiotics. Small contained abscesses and phlegmon, especially in children, often settle with antibiotics alone. Remove the source: extract the tooth, remove the foreign body. Control diabetes: diabetic patients have more multi-space spread and complications, and more often grow Klebsiella pneumoniae ."],["ev","cl:t-deepneck~s2-5","Deep neck space infection: current evidence","Deep neck space infection · General principles","A systematic review of 60 studies of deep neck infection in adults found contrast CT used for diagnosis in 78% and open drainage as the usual treatment, with almost no randomised evidence (Sheikh et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2023). In children, pooled studies suggest antibiotics alone may safely treat selected deep neck abscesses, although the evidence is weak (Carbone et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2012). In a meta-analysis of 20 studies, diabetes doubled the risk of multi-space spread (risk ratio 1.96) and complications (2.42), with more Klebsiella and fewer streptococci and anaerobes (Hidaka et al., Head Neck 2015)."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s2-5","Image-guided aspiration","Deep neck space infection · General principles","Ultrasound- or CT-guided needle aspiration, repeated if needed, suits a single unilocular abscess in a stable patient, avoids an incision and gives pus for culture. Multi-space, multiloculated or gas-forming collections and a threatened airway need open drainage."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s3-1","Applied anatomy","Deep neck space infection · Ludwig's angina","Three primary spaces surround the mandible: sublingual (above the mylohyoid, under the oral mucosa), submandibular (below the mylohyoid, under the investing fascia, containing the gland and nodes) and submental (between the anterior bellies of digastric). The roots of the premolars and first molar lie above the mylohyoid line and drain into the sublingual space; those of the second and third molars lie below it and drain into the submandibular space. The spaces communicate around the back of the mylohyoid and across the midline. Figure 4 First, the submandibular space below the mylohyoid and the sublingual space above it. Second, the roots of the molars project below and those of the premolars above the attachment of the mylohyoid. Dhingra, figs 52.1 and 52.2 Figure 5 The submandibular gland around the mylohyoid. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s3-2","Definition and cause","Deep neck space infection · Ludwig's angina","A rapidly spreading cellulitis of the submandibular, sublingual and submental spaces on both sides, usually without a discrete abscess. Dental infection accounts for about 80%; others are submandibular sialadenitis, floor-of-mouth injuries and mandibular fractures. Mixed aerobes and anaerobes."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s3-3","Clinical features","Deep neck space infection · Ludwig's angina","Painful swallowing, drooling and trismus; a swollen floor of mouth that pushes the tongue up and back; a tense, woody-hard , tender swelling below the jaw on both sides; a muffled voice; the patient sits up, leaning forward. As the tongue is forced back and the larynx swells, the airway closes. Figure 6 Ludwig's angina in a seven-year-old child. Dhingra, fig. 52.3"],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s3-4","Treatment","Deep neck space infection · Ludwig's angina","Secure the airway: awake fibreoptic intubation or tracheostomy under local anaesthesia, before it is lost. High-dose intravenous antibiotics with anaerobic cover; steroids reduce swelling. Drainage: intraorally if confined to the sublingual space; externally for submandibular and submental involvement, through a transverse incision below the mandible with blunt opening of the mylohyoid. Often only serous fluid is found, yet decompression relieves the airway. Extract the offending tooth. Never lie down a patient with Ludwig's angina A sitting, drooling patient with a raised floor of mouth can obstruct completely if laid flat or sedated. Move to theatre with the airway team present."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s3-5","Complications","Deep neck space infection · Ludwig's angina","Spread to the parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal spaces and mediastinum, airway obstruction, septicaemia and aspiration pneumonia."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s4-1","Applied anatomy","Deep neck space infection · Retropharyngeal abscess","The retropharyngeal space lies between the buccopharyngeal fascia in front and the alar fascia behind, from the skull base to the mediastinum. A midline raphe divides it into two lateral compartments (spaces of Gillette), each with retropharyngeal nodes that receive lymph from the nose, sinuses, adenoids, nasopharynx and middle ear and that regress after about four or five years of age."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s4-2","Acute retropharyngeal abscess","Deep neck space infection · Retropharyngeal abscess","4.2.1 Cause Mostly children under three to five, from suppuration of the retropharyngeal nodes after infection of the adenoids, nasopharynx, nose or sinuses. In adults, penetrating injury of the posterior pharyngeal wall or oesophagus (fish bones, instruments); rarely mastoid pus tracking under the petrous bone. 4.2.2 Clinical features Fever, irritability, refusal to feed, dysphagia and drooling. Difficulty in breathing, stridor and a croupy cough. Torticollis and neck stiffness, with the head held extended; torticollis is the best single indicator in children. A bulge on one side of the midline of the posterior pharyngeal wall. 4.2.3 Investigation A lateral soft tissue radiograph, taken with the neck extended, shows widening of the prevertebral shadow, sometimes with gas or a fluid level. Contrast CT shows the abscess, distinguishes it from phlegmon and shows extension below the hyoid or into the mediastinum. Figure 7 Retropharyngeal abscess: widening of the prevertebral space with gas (arrow) on a lateral radiograph. Dhingra, fig. 52.7 4.2.4 Treatment Intravenous antibiotics; many small abscesses and phlegmons in children resolve. A definite abscess is drained transorally with the child supine and head low, mouth held open by a gag and suction ready: aspirate first to relieve pressure, then make a vertical incision over the most fluctuant point, avoiding the carotid laterally. Under general anaesthesia, intubate gently to avoid rupture, and pack the pharynx. Tracheostomy if the airway is compromised. Low or extensive abscesses are drained through the neck."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s4-3","Chronic retropharyngeal (prevertebral) abscess","Deep neck space infection · Retropharyngeal abscess","Tuberculous, from caries of the cervical spine (in the midline, behind the prevertebral fascia) or from tuberculous retropharyngeal nodes (to one side, behind the buccopharyngeal fascia). Throat discomfort and mild dysphagia; a fluctuant swelling of the posterior wall; tuberculous neck nodes; radiographs show the spinal disease. Drain through the neck (along the anterior border of sternomastoid for a low abscess, the posterior border for a high one), never through the mouth when the spine is involved; antituberculous treatment, with the spinal surgeon. Figure 8 (A) A tuberculous prevertebral abscess seen in the oropharynx. (B) Radiograph of the same. Dhingra, fig. 52.8"],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s5-1","Applied anatomy","Deep neck space infection · Parapharyngeal abscess","An inverted pyramid with its base at the skull base and apex at the hyoid. Medially the buccopharyngeal fascia over the constrictors; laterally the medial pterygoid, ramus and deep lobe of the parotid; behind, the prevertebral fascia. The styloid process and its muscles divide it into: Anterior (prestyloid) compartment: related to the tonsillar fossa and medial pterygoid. Posterior (poststyloid) compartment: containing the internal carotid artery, internal jugular vein, cranial nerves IX to XII, the sympathetic trunk and upper deep cervical nodes."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s5-2","Causes","Deep neck space infection · Parapharyngeal abscess","The tonsils and adenoids, a ruptured quinsy ( Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess ), the last lower molar, Bezold abscess ( Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis ) and petrositis, the parotid, retropharyngeal and submandibular spaces, and penetrating injury, including local anaesthetic injections."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s5-3","Clinical features","Deep neck space infection · Parapharyngeal abscess","Anterior compartment: the triad of medial prolapse of the tonsil and tonsillar fossa, marked trismus from medial pterygoid spasm, and a swelling behind the angle of the jaw; severe odynophagia. Posterior compartment: a bulge behind the posterior pillar, palsies of IX, X, XI and XII and Horner syndrome, and parotid swelling, with little trismus. Both: fever, toxaemia, sore throat and torticollis."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s5-4","Complications","Deep neck space infection · Parapharyngeal abscess","Laryngeal oedema and airway obstruction; jugular thrombophlebitis and septicaemia; spread to the retropharyngeal space and down the carotid sheath to the mediastinum; mycotic aneurysm of the carotid and carotid blow-out."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s5-5","Treatment","Deep neck space infection · Parapharyngeal abscess","Intravenous antibiotics covering aerobes and anaerobes. Drainage under general anaesthesia (tracheostomy first if trismus is severe) through a horizontal incision 2 to 3 cm below the angle of the mandible, dissecting bluntly along the inner surface of the medial pterygoid towards the styloid process; a drain is left. Never drain a parapharyngeal abscess through the mouth , for the great vessels lie in its wall."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s6-1","Parotid abscess","Deep neck space infection · Other spaces","In dehydrated, postoperative or debilitated patients with poor oral hygiene, infection ascends Stensen's duct ( Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings ), usually Staphylococcus aureus . Five to seven days after an operation the parotid becomes swollen, red, indurated and tender; fluctuation is hard to feel through the thick capsule; pus can be expressed from the duct. Ultrasound or CT shows the loculi. Rehydrate, stimulate saliva, give intravenous antibiotics, and drain through a parotidectomy incision, opening the loculi bluntly in the line of the facial nerve branches. The thin deep fascia lets pus break into the parapharyngeal space."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s6-2","Masticator space","Deep neck space infection · Other spaces","Between the layers of the investing fascia around the masseter, pterygoids and temporalis, with the ramus, maxillary artery and inferior alveolar nerve; its three parts are masseteric, pterygomandibular and temporal. Infection of the second and third molars is the usual source; marked trismus is the cardinal sign. Drained through an incision lateral to the retromolar trigone, or above the zygomatic arch for the temporal space. Figure 9 The masticator space and its three parts: masseteric, pterygomandibular and temporal. Dhingra, fig. 52.10"],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s7-1","Mediastinitis","Deep neck space infection · Dangerous complications","Usually from the retropharyngeal or danger spaces: chest pain, dyspnoea, tachycardia and a widened mediastinum on the chest film. Mortality is high; combined cervical and thoracic drainage improves survival."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s7-2","Lemierre syndrome","Deep neck space infection · Dangerous complications","Septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein after pharyngitis, usually with Fusobacterium necrophorum : swinging fevers, tenderness along the sternomastoid, and septic emboli to the lungs and joints. Contrast CT shows a filling defect in the vein. Two to three weeks of intravenous antibiotics; anticoagulation is debated; ligation if the patient deteriorates."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s7-3","Carotid erosion","Deep neck space infection · Dangerous complications","A mycotic pseudoaneurysm or rupture from retropharyngeal or parapharyngeal infection. Warnings: a sentinel bleed from the mouth, nose or ear, a pulsatile swelling, Horner syndrome or lower cranial nerve palsies. Angiography, then ligation or stenting."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s7-4","Necrotising fasciitis","Deep neck space infection · Dangerous complications","In the elderly, diabetic and immunosuppressed, usually dental in origin: spreading oedematous \"peau d'orange\" erythema with crepitus and gas on CT. Resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics and repeated debridement to bleeding tissue."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s7-5","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Deep neck space infection · Dangerous complications","Infection from the upper teeth or face tracking back through the valveless ophthalmic veins: fever, orbital pain, proptosis, ophthalmoplegia and a dilated pupil ( Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications ). A neck abscess in an adult After the infection settles, examine the upper aerodigestive tract. A cystic \"abscess\" in an adult may be a necrotic node from an HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ) or an infected branchial cyst hiding one ( Approach to a lump in the neck )."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s8-1","Viva essentials","Deep neck space infection · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Layers of deep cervical fascia and the spaces between them. Ludwig's angina: spaces, cause, airway management. Retropharyngeal abscess: acute versus chronic, radiograph, transoral versus external drainage. Parapharyngeal abscess: compartments, signs, and why it is never drained through the mouth. Complications: mediastinitis, Lemierre syndrome, carotid blow-out."],["sec","cl:t-deepneck~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Deep neck space infection · For the examination","Common pitfalls Sending a patient with a threatened airway to the CT scanner unaccompanied. Incising a parapharyngeal swelling through the mouth. Draining a tuberculous prevertebral abscess transorally. Calling a cystic neck mass in an adult an abscess and not looking for a primary."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s1-1","Why children are different","The dizzy child · Overview","Young children cannot describe vertigo. They cling to a parent, fall, refuse to walk, go pale, vomit, or stop playing, and the parent reports \"clumsiness\" or \"falls a lot\". The examination is limited by cooperation and has to be built from play and observation. Children also compensate quickly after a vestibular loss, so a once-dramatic attack may leave few signs a week later."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s1-2","How common it is","The dizzy child · Overview","Dizziness is an uncommon referral in children and most causes are benign. Over half of dizzy children also have headache, and a first-degree family history of migraine is present in most children whose vertigo turns out to be a migraine variant. Common and important causes by type of attack Pattern Leading causes Clues Brief spells of seconds Benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood; BPPV after head injury Toddler, no hearing loss, normal between attacks; positional after trauma Attacks of minutes to hours Vestibular migraine; rarely Ménière's disease Headache, light and sound sensitivity, family history; fluctuating hearing and fullness A single attack lasting days Vestibular neuritis; labyrinthitis Viral prodrome; hearing normal in neuritis, reduced in labyrinthitis Unsteadiness rather than spinning Otitis media with effusion Conductive hearing loss, flat tympanogram Life-long imbalance, late walking Congenital or acquired severe vestibular loss Sensorineural hearing loss, meningitis, cytomegalovirus, inner ear malformation Spells with fatigue and screens Refractive error or convergence problems End of the day, headache, rarely vomiting Feeling faint on standing Orthostatic hypotension Rapid growth around puberty; presyncope, not spinning"],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-1","Benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood","The dizzy child · Causes","The commonest cause between 2 and 6 years, accounting for up to a third of dizzy children in that age group. A migraine precursor : sudden, intense spells of vertigo lasting seconds to minutes, often with pallor, fear, nystagmus and unsteadiness, sometimes nausea or vomiting, without a trigger and without hearing symptoms. The child is entirely normal between spells. About half go on to have migraine in adolescence, and most have a family history of migraine. The outlook is excellent and it resolves. Newer Bárány Society criteria (2021) call it recurrent vertigo of childhood , or vestibular migraine of childhood when migraine features accompany at least half of the attacks (see Current evidence below). Other childhood migraine variants Benign paroxysmal torticollis (episodic head tilt in infants under 2), cyclical vomiting and motion sickness (most marked between 4 and 10 years) belong to the same family and often precede vestibular migraine."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-2","Vestibular migraine","The dizzy child · Causes","The commonest cause in older children and adolescents: attacks of rotatory vertigo lasting minutes to hours, accompanied or followed by headache and often vomiting, with sensitivity to light and sound. A family history of migraine is present in most. It must be told apart from basilar-type migraine , in which vertigo is part of an aura with paraesthesia, ataxia or loss of consciousness, because the treatment differs: ergot preparations are contraindicated in basilar migraine."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-3","Otitis media with effusion","The dizzy child · Causes","Probably the commonest cause of balance complaints in young children overall. The child is unsteady and clumsy rather than spinning, with a conductive hearing loss. Proposed mechanisms are pressure changes at the oval and round windows and toxins from the middle ear fluid crossing into the inner ear. Balance often improves once the effusion clears or grommets are inserted ( Otitis media: acute and with effusion )."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-4","Vestibular neuritis and labyrinthitis","The dizzy child · Causes","As in adults: acute severe rotatory vertigo for days, with nausea, vomiting, imbalance and oscillopsia, often after a viral illness. Hearing is normal in neuritis and reduced in labyrinthitis. The adult picture is in Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes . Children recover faster than adults because they compensate well, but they are often admitted and a central cause is considered first."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-5","Chronic ear disease","The dizzy child · Causes","Cholesteatoma can erode the lateral semicircular canal and cause a fistula, with vertigo on pressure (a positive fistula test). Suppurative labyrinthitis is a complication of acute or chronic otitis media and leaves a dead ear ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis )."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-6","Inner ear malformations and syndromes","The dizzy child · Causes","Vestibular abnormalities accompany up to 70% of permanent sensorineural hearing losses in children. These children present with late motor milestones and imbalance rather than vertigo. Some malformations, especially the enlarged vestibular aqueduct and the incomplete partition anomalies, cause episodic vertigo with stepwise hearing loss after minor head injury, and are easily mistaken for Ménière's disease. Syndromic associations include Pendred, branchio-oto-renal and Waardenburg syndromes, and Usher syndrome , in which deafness and absent vestibular function are joined later by retinitis pigmentosa. A profoundly deaf child with absent vestibular function needs an eye examination with electroretinography. Meningitis and congenital cytomegalovirus can destroy both cochlear and vestibular function ( The deaf child and congenital hearing loss )."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-7","Head injury","The dizzy child · Causes","A temporal bone fracture through the labyrinth, or a perilymph fistula, causes vertigo with tinnitus and hearing loss. Post-concussion syndrome causes dizziness without hearing loss, and BPPV in children is almost always post-traumatic or after ear surgery such as cochlear implantation."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-8","Third-window disorders","The dizzy child · Causes","Superior canal dehiscence is rare in children but presents as in adults, with sound-induced vertigo ( Tullio phenomenon ), pressure-induced vertigo ( Hennebert sign ) and chronic disequilibrium."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-9","Drugs","The dizzy child · Causes","Aminoglycosides, including gentamicin drops into a perforated ear, are vestibulotoxic and cause ataxia and oscillopsia, often without hearing loss. Cisplatin and antimalarials are mainly cochleotoxic ( Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease )."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-10","Vision","The dizzy child · Causes","Refractive errors and convergence problems cause brief vertigo in about 10% of dizzy children over 5, usually at the end of the day or after long screen time, often with headache and nausea. An optometry review is part of the work-up."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-11","Psychological causes","The dizzy child · Causes","Anxiety and depression can present as dizziness, and long-standing vestibular symptoms can produce them. Prolonged school avoidance is a marker. Medically unexplained dizziness is a diagnosis of exclusion, and most children improve with therapy-based treatment."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s2-12","Central causes not to miss","The dizzy child · Causes","Posterior fossa tumours, Chiari malformation, hereditary ataxias, encephalitis, multiple sclerosis in adolescents, and epilepsy : temporal or occipital lobe seizures can produce a sensation of movement with visual or auditory hallucinations, and a prolonged recovery may be a post-ictal state. Cardiac arrhythmias cause presyncope rather than vertigo. Red flags in the dizzy child Persistent ataxia or unsteadiness between attacks. Headache on waking or with vomiting, or a headache that changes in character. Focal neurological signs, abnormal eye movements, or nystagmus that is vertical or changes direction with gaze. Loss of consciousness, aura with hallucinations, or confusion afterwards. Progressive or asymmetric sensorineural hearing loss. Regression of motor milestones. Any of these calls for MRI of the brain and internal auditory meati and paediatric neurology input."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s3-1","History","The dizzy child · Assessment","Take the history from the child and the parent, and try to make the child describe it (\"does the room spin, or do you feel wobbly inside?\"). Timing: how long each spell lasts, how often, and whether there was a single attack or many. Triggers: movement, position change, rolling or looking up, straining, loud noise, standing up, screens, tiredness, missed meals. Hearing: loss, fluctuation, tinnitus, fullness, ear discharge, previous grommets or ear surgery. Headache: before, during or after, with light or sound sensitivity; motion sickness; family history of migraine. Milestones: age of sitting (about 6 to 9 months) and walking (about 12 to 15 months), and balance tasks such as riding a bicycle without stabilisers. Delay suggests a congenital or early vestibular loss. Past history: prematurity, meningitis, cytomegalovirus, ototoxic drugs, head injury, surgery. Vision, school and mood."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s3-2","Examination","The dizzy child · Assessment","Grade the examination to the child's cooperation and get the most useful information first. Ears: for effusion, perforation, cholesteatoma; pneumatic otoscopy for a fistula sign. Eyes: spontaneous and gaze-evoked nystagmus, ideally with Frenzel lenses or video goggles to remove fixation; avoid extreme gaze, where physiological nystagmus appears. Head impulse test: the child fixes on a cartoon picture while the head is turned quickly through about 15 degrees; a corrective saccade marks the weak side. Head-shake test: eyes closed, head tilted down 30 degrees and shaken 20 times; nystagmus afterwards indicates asymmetry. Standing on one foot: briefly at 30 months, 2 seconds at 3 years, 5 seconds at 4 years and 10 seconds at 5 years. Romberg and Unterberger tests, gait, and a cerebellar and cranial nerve examination. Dix-Hallpike test only if the history suggests positional vertigo, usually after head injury. Blood pressure lying and standing if the spells are presyncopal. Peripheral or central? Peripheral Central Nystagmus Horizontal-torsional, one direction, suppressed by fixation Vertical or pure torsional, changes direction with gaze, not suppressed Head impulse Abnormal on the affected side Usually normal Balance Falls to one side but can stand Often cannot stand or walk Other signs Hearing loss may accompany it Ataxia, cranial nerve or long tract signs, headache"],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s3-3","Investigations","The dizzy child · Assessment","Audiometry and tympanometry in every child: play audiometry, pure tones with reflexes, or auditory brainstem response depending on age. This single test separates the glue ear, the sensorineural loss and the normal-hearing groups. Vestibular tests where available: video-oculography with calorics, video head impulse testing, rotational chair testing (in infants on a parent's lap), cervical VEMPs adapted to children, and posturography from about 3 years. They show whether there is a unilateral or bilateral loss and separate vestibular from visual and proprioceptive causes ( Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory ). MRI of the brain and internal auditory meati when there are red flags, an unexplained or asymmetric sensorineural loss, or symptoms that do not settle; it may need a general anaesthetic in young children. CT of the temporal bones for chronic ear disease, trauma or suspected malformation. Bloods are rarely useful unless the history points to anaemia, diabetes or infection. Optometry, ECG and EEG guided by the history. The dizzy child 1 History timing of spells hearing headache and family history milestones trauma and drugs 2 Examine ears nystagmus head impulse and head shake balance for age neurology 3 Test audiometry and tympanometry for all vestibular tests where available 4 Red flags present urgent MRI and paediatric neurology 5 Glue ear with imbalance treat the effusion watchful waiting or grommets 6 Normal ears and hearing BPVC or vestibular migraine explanation, triggers, review 7 Sensorineural loss aetiological work-up, MRI and CT, cytomegalovirus, geneti"],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s4-1","Reassurance and explanation","The dizzy child · Management","Most families need a clear diagnosis and the knowledge that the cause is benign. Benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood needs no treatment beyond explanation and review; it resolves, and parents should know that migraine may follow later."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s4-2","Acute attacks","The dizzy child · Management","Vestibular sedatives can help briefly in a severe acute attack such as vestibular neuritis. Phenothiazines are used with caution in children because of extrapyramidal reactions, and never for long, since they slow compensation."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s4-3","Vestibular migraine","The dizzy child · Management","Start with triggers: regular sleep, meals and fluids, limits on screen time, and attention to stress and vision. Drug prophylaxis, if needed, is best managed by a paediatrician or paediatric neurologist experienced in childhood migraine."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s4-4","Otitis media with effusion","The dizzy child · Management","Manage as for any glue ear; imbalance strengthens the case for grommets if the effusion persists."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s4-5","Vestibular rehabilitation","The dizzy child · Management","Physiotherapy exercises speed compensation after vestibular neuritis, head injury or a fixed vestibular loss, and help children with chronic imbalance and the anxious, deconditioned child."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s4-6","Specific conditions","The dizzy child · Management","BPPV, Ménière's disease, superior canal dehiscence and perilymph fistula are managed on the same principles as in adults. Cholesteatoma with a fistula needs surgery. Children with bilateral vestibular loss need early physiotherapy, safety advice about swimming and the dark, and occupational therapy input at school."],["ev","cl:t-dizzychild~s4-7","The dizzy child: current evidence","The dizzy child · Management","New diagnostic criteria. The Bárány Society and International Headache Society defined vestibular migraine of childhood (at least five episodes of moderate or severe vestibular symptoms lasting 5 minutes to 72 hours, with migraine features in at least half) and recurrent vertigo of childhood (the same attacks without migraine features), replacing the older term \"benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood\" for most purposes (van de Berg et al., J Vestib Res 2021). Commonest causes. Large series find vestibular migraine and its precursors, benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood, vestibular neuritis, otitis media with effusion and head trauma to be the commonest causes; central tumours are rare but are suggested by persistent headache, morning vomiting, ataxia or abnormal neurology. Rehabilitation. Vestibular rehabilitation is effective in children with peripheral vestibular loss, and screening for vestibular loss is recommended in children with sensorineural hearing loss or cochlear implants."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s4-7","Referral","The dizzy child · Management","Refer to a tertiary paediatric audiovestibular or neuro-otology service when symptoms persist despite first-line management, when vestibular testing is not available locally, or when surgery is being considered."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s5-1","Viva essentials","The dizzy child · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The causes by duration of attack , with BPVC, vestibular migraine and glue ear first. BPVC as a migraine precursor: seconds-long spells, normal between them, no hearing loss. Why every dizzy child needs audiometry and tympanometry . The red flags that need MRI and a neurologist. Milestones and the one-foot test as markers of vestibular loss. Vestibular loss with deafness: enlarged vestibular aqueduct, Usher syndrome, cytomegalovirus, meningitis ."],["sec","cl:t-dizzychild~s5-2","Common pitfalls","The dizzy child · For the examination","Common pitfalls Diagnosing BPPV in a child without a history of head injury. Missing glue ear because the child \"is just clumsy\". Calling enlarged vestibular aqueduct Ménière's disease. Forgetting the eyes, the blood pressure and the child's mood. Overlooking persistent ataxia between attacks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_5fu~s1-1","Summary","Fluorouracil · At a glance","Fluorouracil Summary Class Pyrimidine analogue antimetabolite (capecitabine is an oral prodrug) How it works Its metabolite FdUMP inhibits thymidylate synthase, blocking DNA synthesis; also incorporated into RNA ENT uses Induction TPF (docetaxel, cisplatin, 5-FU); platinum with 5-FU (with cetuximab or pembrolizumab) for recurrent or metastatic disease; some chemoradiotherapy regimens; topical 5% cream for actinic keratosis, Bowen's disease and superficial basal cell carcinoma Dose Commonly 750 to 1,000 mg/m2 daily as a continuous infusion for 4 to 5 days every 3 weeks (regimen-specific); topical cream once or twice daily for 2 to 4 weeks Before treatment DPD deficiency testing (DPYD genotyping)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_5fu~s2-1","Skin cancer","Fluorouracil · Using it in ENT","Topical 5-FU treats superficial lesions on the ears, nose and scalp; expect a brisk inflammatory reaction with redness, crusting and erosion before healing. It is not appropriate for nodular, infiltrative or recurrent basal cell carcinoma or for squamous cell carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-dr_5fu~s3-1","Adverse effects","Fluorouracil · Safety","Mucositis and diarrhoea, myelosuppression, palmar-plantar erythrodysaesthesia (hand-foot syndrome), coronary vasospasm with chest pain, and severe or fatal toxicity in DPD deficiency. Topically, local inflammation and photosensitivity."],["ev","cl:t-dr_5fu~s3-2","Fluorouracil: current evidence","Fluorouracil · Safety","DPD testing. About 3 to 8% of people have partial deficiency of dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase, which breaks down fluorouracil; complete deficiency can be fatal. The EMA and UK MHRA (2020) now recommend testing for DPD deficiency (genotype or blood uracil level) before starting fluorouracil or capecitabine, with dose reduction for partial deficiency. Head and neck use. Its role has narrowed: PF induction has given way to TPF, and first-line treatment of recurrent disease now centres on pembrolizumab, with or without platinum and fluorouracil (KEYNOTE-048)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_5fu~s3-2","Interactions","Fluorouracil · Safety","Warfarin (marked rise in INR); brivudine and sorivudine (fatal inhibition of DPD, contraindicated); phenytoin levels rise."],["sec","cl:t-dr_5fu~s4-1","Viva essentials","Fluorouracil · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism (thymidylate synthase). DPD deficiency testing. Topical use in skin cancer and its limits."],["sec","cl:t-dr_5fu~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Fluorouracil · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using topical 5-FU for a nodular basal cell carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-dr_acic~s1-1","Summary","Aciclovir and valaciclovir · At a glance","Aciclovir and valaciclovir Summary Class Nucleoside analogue antivirals (famciclovir is similar) How they work Phosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase, then by host kinases to aciclovir triphosphate, which inhibits viral DNA polymerase and terminates the DNA chain; active only in infected cells ENT uses Ramsay Hunt syndrome (with prednisolone); herpes zoster of the head and neck; severe primary herpetic gingivostomatitis; herpes simplex in the immunocompromised; severe Bell's palsy (adjunct, weak evidence); herpes simplex encephalitis (intravenous) Adult dose Zoster: aciclovir 800 mg five times daily or valaciclovir 1 g three times daily for 7 days, started within 72 hours; intravenous aciclovir 10 mg/kg every 8 hours for severe, disseminated, central or immunocompromised infection Child dose Aciclovir by age and weight (for example 20 mg/kg four times daily for zoster in children over 2, maximum 800 mg) Pregnancy Aciclovir has extensive safety data"],["sec","cl:t-dr_acic~s2-1","Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Aciclovir and valaciclovir · Using them in ENT","Facial palsy with a vesicular eruption in the ear canal, concha or palate, often with hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo. Start an antiviral and prednisolone within 72 hours, protect the eye, and arrange audiometry; recovery is worse than in Bell's palsy, so counsel accordingly."],["sec","cl:t-dr_acic~s2-2","Bell's palsy","Aciclovir and valaciclovir · Using them in ENT","Steroids alone are the evidence-based treatment; adding an antiviral may slightly improve outcomes in severe palsy, but the evidence is low quality."],["sec","cl:t-dr_acic~s3-1","Adverse effects","Aciclovir and valaciclovir · Safety","Nausea, headache, rash; with intravenous aciclovir, crystal nephropathy (hydrate well, infuse slowly) and neurotoxicity (confusion, tremor) in renal impairment."],["ev","cl:t-dr_acic~s3-2","Aciclovir and valaciclovir: current evidence","Aciclovir and valaciclovir · Safety","Bell's palsy. Antivirals alone are no better than placebo, and added to steroids they may give a small benefit with low-certainty evidence (Cochrane, Gagyor et al., 2019); the American guideline allows them only alongside steroids. Ramsay Hunt syndrome. A Cochrane review found no randomised evidence for antivirals (Uscategui et al., 2008), but they are given with steroids, early, by analogy with zoster elsewhere. Herpes zoster treatment is most effective when started within 72 hours of the rash."],["sec","cl:t-dr_acic~s3-2","Interactions","Aciclovir and valaciclovir · Safety","Other nephrotoxins; probenecid raises levels; mycophenolate (both levels raised)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_acic~s4-1","Viva essentials","Aciclovir and valaciclovir · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism and selectivity (viral thymidine kinase). Treatment of Ramsay Hunt syndrome . Dose adjustment and hydration in renal impairment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_acic~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Aciclovir and valaciclovir · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing a vesicle in the ear canal in a \"Bell's palsy\". Intravenous aciclovir without hydration in an elderly patient."],["sec","cl:t-dr_adren~s1-1","Summary","Adrenaline · At a glance","Adrenaline Summary Class Catecholamine: alpha and beta adrenoceptor agonist Concentrations 1:1,000 = 1 mg/ml (intramuscular anaphylaxis, nebulised, topical pledgets); 1:10,000 = 100 micrograms/ml (intravenous in cardiac arrest, some topical use); 1:80,000 to 1:200,000 = 12.5 to 5 micrograms/ml (with local anaesthetic infiltration) ENT uses Haemostasis in surgery (infiltration and pledgets); epistaxis (pledgets); croup and post-extubation stridor (nebulised); anaphylaxis and allergic angioedema (intramuscular) Anaphylaxis dose (intramuscular, 1:1,000, anterolateral thigh) Adult and child over 12: 500 micrograms (0.5 ml); 6 to 12 years: 300 micrograms (0.3 ml); 6 months to 6 years: 150 micrograms (0.15 ml); under 6 months: 100 to 150 micrograms; repeat after 5 minutes if no better Croup (nebulised) 400 micrograms/kg of 1:1,000 (maximum 5 mg), repeated after 30 minutes if needed Topical Pledgets soaked in 1:1,000 (or 1:10,000) in endoscopic sinus surgery"],["sec","cl:t-dr_adren~s2-1","Surgical haemostasis","Adrenaline · Using it in ENT","Infiltrated with lidocaine, adrenaline constricts arterioles; wait 7 to 10 minutes. Cotton pledgets with 1:1,000 adrenaline in endoscopic sinus surgery reduce bleeding and improve the view; count and remove them. Absorption from the nasal mucosa can cause tachycardia and hypertension, especially with high-concentration solutions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_adren~s2-2","Croup and stridor","Adrenaline · Using it in ENT","Nebulised adrenaline reduces mucosal oedema within minutes in severe croup or post-extubation stridor; it wears off in about 2 hours, so observe the child for at least 2 to 4 hours afterwards; dexamethasone treats the underlying swelling."],["sec","cl:t-dr_adren~s2-3","Anaphylaxis","Adrenaline · Using it in ENT","Recognise airway, breathing or circulation problems with skin or mucosal changes after exposure to a trigger (drugs, latex, chlorhexidine in theatre). Give intramuscular adrenaline immediately , lie the patient flat (or sit if breathing is difficult), give oxygen and fluids; repeat after 5 minutes; start an adrenaline infusion for refractory anaphylaxis under critical care. Antihistamines and steroids are not first-line. Take mast-cell tryptase samples and refer to allergy services."],["sec","cl:t-dr_adren~s3-1","Adverse effects","Adrenaline · Safety","Tachycardia, arrhythmia, hypertension, anxiety, tremor and pallor; myocardial ischaemia in patients with coronary disease; tissue necrosis if injected into compromised tissue or flaps. Never give 1:1,000 adrenaline intravenously in anaphylaxis outside critical care."],["ev","cl:t-dr_adren~s3-2","Adrenaline: current evidence","Adrenaline · Safety","Anaphylaxis. The Resuscitation Council UK guideline (2021) recommends intramuscular adrenaline 0.5 mg (0.5 ml of 1:1000) in adults, repeated after five minutes if needed, and calls refractory anaphylaxis (no improvement after two IM doses) an indication for a low-dose intravenous adrenaline infusion by an experienced clinician. Topical use. Concentrated adrenaline (1:1000) on patties in sinus surgery is absorbed; it has caused severe hypertension and arrhythmias, so use the smallest effective amount and tell the anaesthetist."],["sec","cl:t-dr_adren~s3-2","Interactions","Adrenaline · Safety","Non-selective beta blockers (severe hypertension with reflex bradycardia, and poor response in anaphylaxis: glucagon may help), tricyclics and MAOIs (enhanced pressor response), halothane (arrhythmias)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_adren~s4-1","Viva essentials","Adrenaline · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The concentrations and what each is used for. Anaphylaxis doses by age and route. Nebulised adrenaline in croup: dose, duration and observation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_adren~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Adrenaline · For the examination","Common pitfalls Intravenous instead of intramuscular adrenaline in anaphylaxis. Delaying adrenaline to give antihistamines. Discharging a child soon after nebulised adrenaline."],["sec","cl:t-dr_agno~s1-1","Summary","Silver nitrate · At a glance","Silver nitrate Summary Preparations Applicator sticks with a tip of silver nitrate (commonly 75% with potassium nitrate) How it works Silver ions denature proteins and form a coagulum (eschar) on contact with moisture ENT uses Anterior epistaxis from a visible vessel; prominent vessels in recurrent childhood epistaxis; granulation tissue in mastoid cavities, ear canals, tracheostomy stomas and wounds; small bleeding points Technique Anaesthetise and decongest; dry the area; touch the stick to the vessel and roll it around the bleeding point, starting at the periphery and working inward; a few seconds of contact only Avoid Cauterising opposing areas of both sides of the septum at one sitting; contact with skin and eyes"],["sec","cl:t-dr_agno~s2-1","Epistaxis","Silver nitrate · Using it in ENT","After topical co-phenylcaine or lidocaine with a decongestant, identify the vessel with a headlight or endoscope and suction. Cauterise the bleeding point; if both sides need treatment, wait 4 to 6 weeks between sides. Afterwards, an antiseptic cream (such as Naseptin, if no nut allergy) or petroleum jelly for 1 to 2 weeks helps the eschar heal."],["sec","cl:t-dr_agno~s2-2","Granulation tissue","Silver nitrate · Using it in ENT","Apply to granulations in a dry cavity or stoma, then treat the cause (infection, foreign body, a tube rubbing)."],["ev","cl:t-dr_agno~s3-1","Silver nitrate: current evidence","Silver nitrate · Safety","Technique. Cauterise only one side of the septum at a time, working from the edge of the bleeding area inwards, to avoid a septal perforation. Evidence. Chemical cautery with silver nitrate is effective for visible anterior bleeding points in clinic; electrocautery may be more effective for recurrent bleeds, but trials are small. In children with recurrent nosebleeds, a Cochrane review found no clear evidence that cautery is better than antiseptic cream or no treatment (Qureishi and Burton, 2012)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_agno~s3-1","Adverse effects","Silver nitrate · Safety","Pain and stinging (adequate anaesthesia); black staining of skin; chemical burns of the alar skin from run-off; septal perforation from bilateral or excessive cautery; failure if the field is wet."],["sec","cl:t-dr_agno~s4-1","Viva essentials","Silver nitrate · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Technique and why the field must be dry. Why not both sides of the septum at once."],["sec","cl:t-dr_agno~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Silver nitrate · For the examination","Common pitfalls Trying to cauterise an actively bleeding point without first controlling it with pressure and vasoconstrictor."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ahist~s1-1","Summary","Oral antihistamines · At a glance","Oral antihistamines Summary Agents Second generation: cetirizine, levocetirizine, loratadine, desloratadine, fexofenadine, bilastine. First generation: chlorphenamine, promethazine, hydroxyzine How they work Inverse agonists at the H1 receptor; stabilise it in its inactive state ENT uses Mild or intermittent allergic rhinitis; add-on when a nasal steroid is not enough; allergic conjunctivitis; urticaria and angioedema (histaminergic); itch Adult dose Cetirizine 10 mg once daily; loratadine 10 mg once daily; fexofenadine 120 mg once daily (180 mg for urticaria); chlorphenamine 4 mg every 4 to 6 hours (maximum 24 mg a day) Child dose Cetirizine 2 to 5 years 2.5 mg twice daily, 6 to 11 years 5 mg twice daily; loratadine 2 to 11 years 5 mg once daily (under 31 kg), 10 mg if heavier; fexofenadine 6 to 11 years 30 mg twice daily Avoid First-generation agents in the elderly, drivers and machine operators, glaucoma, prostatic hypertrophy and epilepsy Pregnancy Loratadine and cetirizine have the most data and are preferred if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ahist~s2-1","Generations","Oral antihistamines · How they work","Second-generation drugs are substrates of P-glycoprotein and are excluded from the brain, so they cause little sedation (cetirizine sedates a minority; fexofenadine is least sedating). First-generation drugs cross freely, sedate, impair driving even when the patient feels alert, and block muscarinic, alpha-adrenergic and serotonin receptors."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ahist~s3-1","Allergic rhinitis","Oral antihistamines · Using them in ENT","ARIA guidance: an oral antihistamine is reasonable for mild intermittent symptoms; for persistent or moderate to severe disease, a nasal steroid is more effective, and an intranasal antihistamine-steroid combination comes before adding an oral antihistamine. Oral antihistamines are useful for prominent eye symptoms and for patients who cannot use sprays."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ahist~s3-2","Angioedema and anaphylaxis","Oral antihistamines · Using them in ENT","In anaphylaxis, adrenaline is the treatment; antihistamines are third-line and only treat skin symptoms. In histaminergic angioedema without airway involvement, a non-sedating antihistamine at up to four times the licensed dose (specialist advice) is used. Antihistamines do not work in bradykinin-mediated angioedema (ACE inhibitor or hereditary)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ahist~s4-1","Adverse effects","Oral antihistamines · Safety","Second generation: headache, dry mouth, occasional drowsiness (cetirizine). First generation: sedation , psychomotor impairment, anticholinergic effects (urinary retention, confusion and falls in the elderly), paradoxical excitation in children. Older drugs such as terfenadine and astemizole caused QT prolongation and were withdrawn."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ahist~s4-2","Oral antihistamines: current evidence","Oral antihistamines · Safety","Guidelines. The ARIA guidance recommends second-generation oral antihistamines over first-generation ones, which impair attention, learning and driving even when the patient does not feel sleepy; intranasal steroids remain more effective for persistent symptoms and nasal blockage. Children. The UK MHRA advised in 2009 against over-the-counter cough and cold medicines containing first-generation antihistamines in children under six. Stopping cetirizine. The US FDA warned in 2025 that rare patients develop severe itching after stopping long-term cetirizine or levocetirizine."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ahist~s4-2","Interactions","Oral antihistamines · Safety","Alcohol and other sedatives (first generation); fruit juices reduce fexofenadine absorption; antacids with aluminium and magnesium reduce fexofenadine absorption."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ahist~s5-1","Viva essentials","Oral antihistamines · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for First- versus second-generation antihistamines. Which symptoms antihistamines do and do not treat. Their limited place in anaphylaxis and none in bradykinin angioedema."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ahist~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Oral antihistamines · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing chlorphenamine for daily allergic rhinitis. Relying on antihistamines in anaphylaxis. Treating ACE inhibitor angioedema with antihistamines."],["sec","cl:t-dr_algin~s1-1","Summary","Alginates · At a glance","Alginates Summary Agents Sodium alginate with bicarbonate and calcium carbonate (for example Gaviscon Advance, Gaviscon Double Action) How they work Form a gel raft above the gastric contents that acts as a physical barrier; the raft is itself refluxed preferentially ENT uses Suspected laryngopharyngeal reflux; reflux symptoms in pregnancy; adjunct to PPIs Adult dose 10 to 20 ml (or 1 to 2 tablets chewed) after meals and at bedtime, up to four times daily Child dose Infant preparations are different products; do not use adult products in infants Avoid Severe renal impairment; caution with sodium restriction (heart failure, hypertension)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_algin~s2-1","Laryngopharyngeal reflux","Alginates · Using them in ENT","Lifestyle measures (avoid late meals, alcohol, smoking, carbonated drinks and trigger foods; lose weight; raise the head of the bed) and an alginate at bedtime are the first step; small trials suggest benefit, though the evidence base is limited. The bedtime dose protects during sleep, when laryngopharyngeal exposure is greatest."],["sec","cl:t-dr_algin~s3-1","Adverse effects","Alginates · Safety","Generally minimal: bloating, constipation or diarrhoea; sodium and calcium loads."],["ev","cl:t-dr_algin~s3-2","Alginates: current evidence","Alginates · Safety","Laryngopharyngeal reflux. In a UK study, Gaviscon Advance alone improved reflux symptoms as much as Gaviscon combined with a high-dose PPI (Wilkie et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2018), supporting alginates as a first step, given after meals and at bedtime. Infants. NICE (NG1) suggests a one to two week trial of alginate for infants with frequent regurgitation and distress only after feeding changes have failed, and advises against routine acid suppression."],["sec","cl:t-dr_algin~s3-2","Interactions","Alginates · Safety","Antacid components reduce absorption of some drugs (tetracyclines, quinolones, iron, levothyroxine, azoles): separate doses by about 2 hours."],["sec","cl:t-dr_algin~s4-1","Viva essentials","Alginates · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism (raft) and why it may suit non-acid reflux. The bedtime dose."],["sec","cl:t-dr_algin~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Alginates · For the examination","Common pitfalls Taking an alginate at the same time as levothyroxine or a quinolone."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s1-1","Summary","Amoxicillin · At a glance","Amoxicillin Summary Class Aminopenicillin (beta-lactam) How it works Binds penicillin-binding proteins and blocks cross-linking of the bacterial cell wall; bactericidal Spectrum Streptococcus pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, non-beta-lactamase Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella, enterococci; not Staphylococcus aureus (most produce penicillinase), not Pseudomonas, not anaerobes of the beta-lactamase type ENT uses Acute otitis media; acute bacterial sinusitis; early Lyme disease; dental and minor soft tissue infection; endocarditis prophylaxis in selected cases; Helicobacter eradication Adult dose 500 mg three times daily by mouth; 1 g three times daily for severe infection Child dose 1 to 11 months 125 mg, 1 to 4 years 250 mg, 5 to 17 years 500 mg, each three times daily (or 30 mg/kg up to 1 g three times daily for severe infection) Duration 5 to 7 days for acute otitis media; 5 days for sinusitis Avoid Penicillin allergy; suspected infectious mononucleosis Pregnancy Safe; compatible with breastfeeding"],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s2-1","Mechanism","Amoxicillin · How it works","Amoxicillin inhibits the transpeptidases (penicillin-binding proteins) that cross-link peptidoglycan, so dividing bacteria lyse. Killing is time-dependent : what matters is how long the free drug stays above the minimum inhibitory concentration, which is why it is given three times a day and why higher doses overcome intermediate pneumococcal resistance. Resistance comes from beta-lactamase (about a fifth to a third of H. influenzae and almost all Moraxella catarrhalis) and from altered penicillin-binding proteins in pneumococci."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s2-2","Absorption and handling","Amoxicillin · How it works","Oral absorption is about 90% and is not reduced by food, unlike ampicillin. It is excreted unchanged by the kidney; reduce the dose when the eGFR is below 30 ml/min. It penetrates middle ear fluid and the sinuses well but crosses the uninflamed blood-brain barrier poorly."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s3-1","Acute otitis media","Amoxicillin · Using it in ENT","Most acute otitis media resolves without antibiotics, so NICE advises no antibiotic or a delayed (back-up) prescription for most children, with regular paracetamol or ibuprofen. Give an immediate antibiotic to children who are systemically unwell or at high risk of complications, those under 2 with bilateral disease, and children with otorrhoea from a perforation. Amoxicillin is first-line for 5 to 7 days ; clarithromycin or erythromycin if allergic; co-amoxiclav as second-line when symptoms do not improve after 2 to 3 days."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s3-2","Acute sinusitis","Amoxicillin · Using it in ENT","Symptoms for under 10 days need no antibiotic. For symptoms lasting more than 10 days, a back-up prescription can be considered; phenoxymethylpenicillin is the NICE first choice in adults, and amoxicillin is widely used elsewhere and in children. Co-amoxiclav is kept for patients who are systemically very unwell or have signs of a complication, who need assessment rather than a prescription."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s3-3","Other uses","Amoxicillin · Using it in ENT","Lyme disease (erythema migrans, facial palsy): amoxicillin or doxycycline, for 14 to 21 days. Dental infection with spreading cellulitis: often with metronidazole. Helicobacter pylori : as part of triple therapy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s4-1","Adverse effects","Amoxicillin · Safety","Diarrhoea , nausea and candidiasis; Clostridioides difficile infection is less common than with co-amoxiclav or cephalosporins. Rash. A delayed, itchy maculopapular rash appears in a few per cent of patients, often not true allergy. In infectious mononucleosis (and with cytomegalovirus and lymphatic leukaemia) a widespread maculopapular rash is common; it is not IgE-mediated and does not prove penicillin allergy, but it frightens patients and clinicians into lifelong labels. True hypersensitivity : urticaria, angioedema and anaphylaxis (about 1 in 5,000 to 10,000 courses). Rarely interstitial nephritis, seizures at high doses in renal failure, and haemolytic anaemia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s4-2","Contraindications and cautions","Amoxicillin · Safety","Penicillin allergy of any type (immediate or severe delayed). Glandular fever suspected: avoid; use phenoxymethylpenicillin or clarithromycin for a sore throat with exudate when an antibiotic is truly needed. Renal impairment: dose adjustment."],["ev","cl:t-dr_amox~s4-3","Amoxicillin: current evidence","Amoxicillin · Safety","Otitis media. A Cochrane review found that antibiotics relieve pain at two to three days in about one child in 20 treated, while causing vomiting, diarrhoea or rash in about one in 14 (Venekamp et al., 2015), which is why NICE (NG91) favours no or back-up prescriptions for most children. The American guideline uses high-dose amoxicillin (80 to 90 mg/kg/day in two doses) because of resistant pneumococci (Lieberthal et al., Pediatrics 2013). Penicillin allergy labels. Fewer than 5% of patients labelled allergic are truly allergic. In the PALACE trial, a direct oral amoxicillin challenge in adults at low risk on the PEN-FAST score was as safe as skin testing followed by challenge (Copaescu et al., JAMA Intern Med 2023), so removing false labels is now encouraged."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s4-3","Interactions","Amoxicillin · Safety","Increases the effect of warfarin (check the INR). Reduces the excretion of methotrexate . Allopurinol increases the risk of rash."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s5-1","Viva essentials","Amoxicillin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Spectrum and why it fails against beta-lactamase producers . NICE approach to acute otitis media : no or back-up antibiotic for most; who needs one at once; 5 to 7 days. Why amoxicillin is avoided in a sore throat that may be glandular fever . Time-dependent killing and dosing frequency."],["sec","cl:t-dr_amox~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Amoxicillin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving amoxicillin for an exudative tonsillitis in a teenager without considering mononucleosis. Labelling a mononucleosis rash as lifelong penicillin allergy. Prescribing amoxicillin for an otitis externa or a perichondritis (staphylococci and Pseudomonas). Prescribing for a viral upper respiratory infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ampho~s1-1","Summary","Amphotericin B · At a glance","Amphotericin B Summary Class Polyene antifungal How it works Binds ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane, forming pores that cause leakage and death; fungicidal Spectrum Mucorales (Rhizopus, Mucor, Lichtheimia), Aspergillus, Candida, Cryptococcus and most pathogenic fungi ENT uses Rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis; acute invasive fungal sinusitis of unknown cause; severe invasive aspergillosis when azoles cannot be used Dose Liposomal amphotericin 5 to 10 mg/kg once daily intravenously for mucormycosis (3 mg/kg for other indications); conventional (deoxycholate) amphotericin is far more toxic and is avoided where possible Pregnancy Use if needed (liposomal preferred)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ampho~s2-1","Mucormycosis","Amphotericin B · Using it in ENT","Suspect it in a diabetic with ketoacidosis or an immunocompromised patient with facial pain, swelling, numbness, nasal discharge or black eschar on the turbinate or palate, ophthalmoplegia or vision loss. Start liposomal amphotericin at once , before confirmation if the suspicion is high, and perform urgent, repeated surgical debridement to bleeding tissue, which may include orbital exenteration. Correct hyperglycaemia and acidosis, stop iron chelation with deferoxamine (a siderophore that feeds the fungus), and reduce immunosuppression. Step down to posaconazole or isavuconazole once stable. Mortality is high (often 30% to 50% or more)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ampho~s3-1","Adverse effects","Amphotericin B · Safety","Nephrotoxicity (less with liposomal forms): monitor creatinine daily; hydrate with saline. Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia from tubular leak: monitor and replace. Infusion reactions: fever, rigors, hypotension; slowing the infusion and premedication help. Anaemia with long courses."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ampho~s3-2","Amphotericin B: current evidence","Amphotericin B · Safety","Mucormycosis. The global guideline recommends liposomal amphotericin B 5 to 10 mg/kg/day, with early and complete surgical debridement and control of the underlying condition, as first-line treatment; isavuconazole or posaconazole are alternatives and are used for step-down (Cornely et al., Lancet Infect Dis 2019). Kidneys. Liposomal formulations are far less nephrotoxic than conventional amphotericin; hydrate, and monitor potassium, magnesium and creatinine."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ampho~s3-2","Interactions","Amphotericin B · Safety","Other nephrotoxins (aminoglycosides, ciclosporin, vancomycin), drugs affected by hypokalaemia (digoxin, QT-prolonging drugs)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ampho~s4-1","Viva essentials","Amphotericin B · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Risk factors and signs of mucormycosis . The triad of treatment: amphotericin, debridement, correcting the host. Renal and electrolyte monitoring."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ampho~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Amphotericin B · For the examination","Common pitfalls Waiting for culture before starting amphotericin. Giving deferoxamine to a patient with suspected mucormycosis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_anticoag~s1-1","Summary","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery · At a glance","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery Summary Warfarin Vitamin K antagonist; stop 5 days before surgery with an INR check (aim below 1.5, or as the surgeon requires) the day before; bridge with low molecular weight heparin only in high thrombotic risk (mechanical mitral valve, older valves, recent venous thromboembolism within 3 months, recent stroke) DOACs Apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban (factor Xa inhibitors) and dabigatran (thrombin inhibitor); stop about 24 hours before low bleeding-risk and 48 hours before high bleeding-risk surgery with normal renal function; dabigatran needs longer (up to 4 days) as renal function falls; no bridging is needed Heparins Therapeutic low molecular weight heparin: last dose 24 hours before surgery; unfractionated heparin infusion: stop 4 to 6 hours before Aspirin Usually continued for most ENT surgery; consider stopping 7 days before procedures where bleeding is catastrophic or hard to control (for example some skull base, middle ear and microvascular operations) if prescribed for primary prevention Clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor Stop 5 to 7 days before high bleeding-risk surgery, but not within the first 6 to 12 months after a coronary stent without cardiology advice (stent thrombosis is often fatal) Restarting Usually 24 hours after low-risk and 48 to 72 hours after high-risk surgery, once haemostasis is secure; venous thromboembolism prophylaxis in the meantime"],["sec","cl:t-dr_anticoag~s2-1","Bleeding risk of ENT operations","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery · Using them in ENT","Higher risk: tonsillectomy, sinus and skull base surgery, septorhinoplasty, thyroid and neck surgery, free flaps, and procedures in closed spaces (orbit, middle ear). Lower risk: endoscopy, minor skin surgery, grommets, nasal cautery, FNA (which can be done on anticoagulants). Agree each plan with the patient, the anticoagulation clinic and, for stents and valves, cardiology."],["sec","cl:t-dr_anticoag~s2-2","Epistaxis in the anticoagulated patient","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery · Using them in ENT","Most epistaxis can be controlled without stopping the anticoagulant: treat locally (cautery, packing, absorbable materials), check the INR on warfarin, and consider reversal only for life-threatening bleeding or a markedly raised INR. ENT UK consensus discourages routine cessation, because the stroke risk of stopping often outweighs the benefit."],["ev","cl:t-dr_anticoag~s3-1","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery: current evidence","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery · Safety","Bridging. In the BRIDGE trial, patients with atrial fibrillation who stopped warfarin for surgery had no fewer clots without heparin bridging, and had less bleeding (Douketis et al., NEJM 2015); bridging is now kept for very high-risk patients such as those with mechanical mitral valves or recent clots. DOACs. The PAUSE protocol stopped apixaban, rivaroxaban or dabigatran one to two days (low bleeding risk) or two to four days (high risk, depending on drug and kidney function) before surgery, without bridging or level testing, with low rates of bleeding and stroke (Douketis et al., JAMA Intern Med 2019). Antiplatelets. Do not stop dual antiplatelet therapy after a recent coronary stent without the cardiologist."],["sec","cl:t-dr_anticoag~s3-1","Key interactions","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery · Safety","Antibiotics (clarithromycin, metronidazole, co-trimoxazole, ciprofloxacin, fluconazole, miconazole gel) raise the INR on warfarin; enzyme inducers lower it. DOACs interact with strong CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein inhibitors and inducers (azoles, clarithromycin, rifampicin, carbamazepine). NSAIDs add bleeding risk to all."],["sec","cl:t-dr_anticoag~s4-1","Viva essentials","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Stopping times for warfarin, DOACs and clopidogrel . Who needs bridging . The coronary stent warning. Management of epistaxis on anticoagulants."],["sec","cl:t-dr_anticoag~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery · For the examination","Common pitfalls Stopping clopidogrel in a patient with a recent stent without cardiology advice. Bridging patients on DOACs. Stopping warfarin for a minor nosebleed."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azel~s1-1","Summary","Azelastine and combination sprays · At a glance","Azelastine and combination sprays Summary Preparations Azelastine 0.1% spray (137 micrograms per spray); azelastine with fluticasone propionate (137 and 50 micrograms per spray); olopatadine with mometasone is a newer combination How it works H1 antagonism, mast-cell stabilisation and reduced inflammatory mediator release ENT uses Seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis; moderate to severe allergic rhinitis not controlled by a nasal steroid; allergic conjunctivitis (eye drops); some non-allergic rhinitis Adult dose Azelastine 1 spray into each nostril twice daily; combination 1 spray into each nostril twice daily Child dose Azelastine from 6 years (1 spray each nostril twice daily); the combination from 12 years"],["sec","cl:t-dr_azel~s2-1","Place in treatment","Azelastine and combination sprays · Using it in ENT","Use a nasal steroid first for persistent or moderate to severe allergic rhinitis. When it is not enough, the steroid-antihistamine combination spray (ARIA and BSACI guidance) is the next step, before adding oral treatments. Its rapid onset helps patients who take sprays only when symptomatic."],["ev","cl:t-dr_azel~s3-1","Azelastine and combination sprays: current evidence","Azelastine and combination sprays · Safety","Combination spray. In large randomised trials, azelastine–fluticasone in one spray relieved nasal and eye symptoms of allergic rhinitis faster and more completely than either drug alone (Carr et al., J Allergy Clin Immunol 2012), and ARIA recommends it for patients not controlled on monotherapy. Side effects. A bitter taste is the commonest complaint; spraying with the head forward and not sniffing hard reduces it."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azel~s3-1","Adverse effects","Azelastine and combination sprays · Safety","A bitter taste (the commonest complaint; tilting the head forward helps), epistaxis, headache and, uncommonly, drowsiness. The combination adds the steroid's effects."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azel~s4-1","Viva essentials","Azelastine and combination sprays · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Where the combination spray fits in the ARIA stepwise approach. Speed of onset compared with nasal steroids."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azel~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Azelastine and combination sprays · For the examination","Common pitfalls Adding an oral antihistamine before trying the combination spray in a patient on a steroid alone."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s1-1","Summary","Azithromycin · At a glance","Azithromycin Summary Class Macrolide (azalide) How it works 50S ribosomal inhibition; immunomodulatory at low long-term doses Spectrum As clarithromycin, with better activity against H. influenzae and Moraxella and less against streptococci and staphylococci; atypicals; Chlamydia trachomatis ENT uses Penicillin allergy: respiratory infection; long-term anti-inflammatory therapy in chronic rhinosinusitis and in patients with bronchiectasis or cystic fibrosis; pertussis Adult dose 500 mg once daily for 3 days; long-term 250 mg once daily or 500 mg three times a week Child dose 10 mg/kg once daily for 3 days (maximum 500 mg) Avoid QT prolongation; hepatic impairment Pregnancy Use only if no alternative"],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s2-1","Mechanism","Azithromycin · How it works","The 15-membered azalide ring improves acid stability and Gram-negative penetration. Its antibacterial and anti-inflammatory actions are those of other macrolides."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s2-2","Handling","Azithromycin · How it works","Takes up into tissues and phagocytes, from which it is slowly released; terminal half-life is about 68 hours. Mostly excreted unchanged in bile. Antacids reduce absorption."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s3-1","Acute infection","Azithromycin · Using it in ENT","A 3-day course is equivalent to longer courses of other agents for acute otitis media and sinusitis in penicillin allergy, but pneumococcal and streptococcal macrolide resistance is a real cause of failure."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s3-2","Long-term use","Azithromycin · Using it in ENT","For chronic rhinosinusitis, especially with lower-airway disease, azithromycin three times a week is an option when clarithromycin interacts with other drugs. Long-term use selects macrolide resistance and has been linked to hearing loss (usually reversible), so check hearing if symptoms occur."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s4-1","Adverse effects","Azithromycin · Safety","Gastrointestinal upset; hepatitis; hearing loss and tinnitus with long-term use. QT prolongation and increased risk of cardiovascular death in patients with cardiac disease. Rarely, hypersensitivity including DRESS."],["ev","cl:t-dr_azi~s4-2","Azithromycin: current evidence","Azithromycin · Safety","Heart. The US FDA warned in 2013 that azithromycin can cause QT prolongation and fatal arrhythmias, particularly in patients with a long QT, hypokalaemia, bradycardia or on other QT-prolonging drugs. Long half-life. A three-day course gives tissue levels for about a week. Chronic rhinosinusitis. EPOS 2020 considers long-term low-dose macrolides an option in chronic rhinosinusitis without polyps, especially with low IgE, but evidence is weak and trials are mixed."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s4-2","Interactions","Azithromycin · Safety","Weaker CYP3A4 effect than clarithromycin, but still caution with warfarin, ciclosporin, digoxin and other QT-prolonging drugs; antacids reduce absorption."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s5-1","Viva essentials","Azithromycin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why a 3-day course works (tissue half-life). Differences from clarithromycin in spectrum and interactions. Long-term macrolide risks: QT, resistance, hearing."],["sec","cl:t-dr_azi~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Azithromycin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using azithromycin for streptococcal tonsillitis when penicillin can be given. Long-term therapy without an ECG baseline in patients at cardiac risk."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzo~s1-1","Summary","Benzodiazepines for vertigo · At a glance","Benzodiazepines for vertigo Summary Agents Diazepam (long-acting); lorazepam (shorter-acting); clonazepam (sometimes in persistent postural-perceptual dizziness or vestibular paroxysmia alternatives) How they work Positive allosteric modulators of the GABA-A receptor; vestibular suppression and anxiolysis ENT uses Severe acute vertigo for 1 to 3 days when other sedatives are insufficient; acute anxiety associated with vertigo Adult dose Diazepam 2 to 5 mg up to three times daily for a few days Avoid Respiratory depression, sleep apnoea, myasthenia, severe hepatic impairment; the elderly where possible"],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzo~s2-1","Acute vertigo","Benzodiazepines for vertigo · Using them in ENT","Reserve for the most distressing first days of an attack; combine with an antiemetic and stop as soon as possible. Persistent dizziness after an acute vestibular event (persistent postural-perceptual dizziness) is better treated with vestibular rehabilitation, cognitive behavioural therapy and SSRIs or SNRIs than with benzodiazepines."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzo~s3-1","Adverse effects","Benzodiazepines for vertigo · Safety","Sedation, ataxia, falls, confusion; respiratory depression with opioids; tolerance and dependence after 2 to 4 weeks; withdrawal symptoms. In patients with obstructive sleep apnoea, they worsen nocturnal apnoea."],["ev","cl:t-dr_benzo~s3-2","Benzodiazepines for vertigo: current evidence","Benzodiazepines for vertigo · Safety","Short courses only. Benzodiazepines and other vestibular suppressants relieve severe acute vertigo but slow central compensation if continued; guidelines advise stopping them within a few days and starting vestibular rehabilitation early. The American BPPV guideline recommends against routine vestibular suppressants for BPPV, which should be treated with repositioning manoeuvres (Bhattacharyya et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017). Falls. In older patients they increase falls and confusion."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzo~s3-2","Interactions","Benzodiazepines for vertigo · Safety","Opioids (fatal respiratory depression), alcohol, other sedatives; CYP3A4 inhibitors raise diazepam levels."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzo~s4-1","Viva essentials","Benzodiazepines for vertigo · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why benzodiazepines delay vestibular compensation. The dangers in the elderly and in sleep apnoea."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzo~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Benzodiazepines for vertigo · For the examination","Common pitfalls Long-term benzodiazepines for chronic dizziness. Combining with opioids after surgery in a patient with sleep apnoea."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzy~s1-1","Summary","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics · At a glance","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics Summary Agents Benzydamine 0.15% mouthwash and spray; lidocaine gels and 2% viscous solution; choline salicylate gel (not under 16 years); barrier gels (polyvinylpyrrolidone and hyaluronic acid products); doxepin rinse (specialist) How they work Benzydamine: local NSAID-like inhibition of inflammatory mediators and mild anaesthesia; lidocaine: sodium-channel block; barrier gels: coat and protect ENT uses Painful pharyngitis and tonsillitis; aphthous ulcers; pain after tonsillectomy (adjunct); oral mucositis during head and neck radiotherapy (benzydamine is recommended for prevention in moderate-dose radiotherapy by MASCC guidelines) Benzydamine dose Mouthwash 15 ml rinsed or gargled every 1.5 to 3 hours (dilute with water if it stings); spray 4 to 8 sprays every 1.5 to 3 hours Avoid Viscous lidocaine in young children and patients with impaired swallowing (aspiration, choking, toxicity); choline salicylate in children (Reye syndrome)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzy~s2-1","Radiotherapy mucositis","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics · Using them in ENT","Mouth care during head and neck radiotherapy includes regular saline or bicarbonate rinses, good oral hygiene, benzydamine from the start of treatment, systemic analgesia escalating to opioids, nutritional support (often a feeding tube), and treatment of candidal infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzy~s2-2","After tonsillectomy","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics · Using them in ENT","Regular oral analgesia matters most; benzydamine sprays may help before meals."],["ev","cl:t-dr_benzy~s3-1","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics: current evidence","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics · Safety","Radiation mucositis. The MASCC/ISOO guideline recommends benzydamine mouthwash to prevent oral mucositis in patients receiving moderate-dose radiotherapy (up to 50 Gy) for head and neck cancer, and photobiomodulation in selected centres; it advises against chlorhexidine for this purpose (Elad et al., Cancer 2020). Pain after tonsillectomy. Mucosal analgesics give short relief; regular paracetamol and ibuprofen are the mainstay."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzy~s3-1","Adverse effects","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics · Safety","Stinging, numbness and taste disturbance with benzydamine; lidocaine toxicity and aspiration when the throat is numbed; salicylate toxicity with large amounts of choline salicylate."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzy~s4-1","Viva essentials","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Prevention and treatment of radiation mucositis . Risks of viscous lidocaine."],["sec","cl:t-dr_benzy~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Benzydamine and mucosal analgesics · For the examination","Common pitfalls Recommending viscous lidocaine to a young child."],["sec","cl:t-dr_beta~s1-1","Summary","Betahistine · At a glance","Betahistine Summary Class Histamine analogue: weak H1 agonist and potent H3 antagonist How it works Proposed: increased inner ear microcirculation via H1 vasodilatation and H3-mediated increase in histamine release; reduced firing of vestibular nuclei ENT uses Ménière's disease (reduction of vertigo attacks); widely used, without good evidence, for other vertigo Adult dose 16 mg three times daily or 24 mg twice daily with food; some use higher doses (up to 48 mg three times daily) on specialist advice Child dose Not recommended under 18 years Avoid Phaeochromocytoma; caution in asthma, peptic ulcer disease and with antihistamines Pregnancy Avoid unless essential"],["sec","cl:t-dr_beta~s2-1","Ménière's disease","Betahistine · Using it in ENT","The first steps are explanation, salt restriction, attention to caffeine and stress, vestibular rehabilitation, and acute vestibular sedatives for attacks. Betahistine may be offered as a preventive; review it at 3 to 6 months and stop it if attacks have not reduced. Treatment escalates to intratympanic steroids, then intratympanic gentamicin or surgery for disabling, frequent attacks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_beta~s2-2","Evidence","Betahistine · Using it in ENT","In the BEMED trial, neither 24 mg twice daily nor 48 mg three times daily reduced attacks more than placebo over 9 months; attacks fell in all groups, illustrating the natural fluctuation of the disease."],["sec","cl:t-dr_beta~s3-1","Adverse effects","Betahistine · Safety","Nausea, dyspepsia, headache, and rarely rash or pruritus. It does not sedate."],["ev","cl:t-dr_beta~s3-2","Betahistine: current evidence","Betahistine · Safety","Evidence. In the BEMED trial of 221 patients, neither standard nor high-dose betahistine reduced vertigo attacks more than placebo over nine months (Adrion et al., BMJ 2016), and a Cochrane review found low-certainty evidence of benefit (Murdin et al., 2016). The American Ménière's guideline lists it only as an option for maintenance (Basura et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020). Counsel patients honestly: it is safe, cheap and of uncertain value."],["sec","cl:t-dr_beta~s3-2","Interactions","Betahistine · Safety","Antihistamines may in theory reduce its effect; MAO-B inhibitors raise its levels."],["sec","cl:t-dr_beta~s4-1","Viva essentials","Betahistine · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The proposed mechanism. The BEMED trial result and how to counsel patients. Where betahistine fits in the stepwise management of Ménière's disease."],["sec","cl:t-dr_beta~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Betahistine · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing betahistine indefinitely for any dizziness. Using it as an acute treatment for a vertigo attack."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bev~s1-1","Summary","Bevacizumab · At a glance","Bevacizumab Summary Class Anti-VEGF monoclonal antibody How it works Binds VEGF-A, preventing receptor activation; reduces angiogenesis and vascular permeability ENT uses Severe recurrent respiratory papillomatosis (systemic, as adjuvant to surgery); hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia with severe or transfusion-dependent bleeding; intralesional use in papillomatosis has been reported Dose Regimens vary: commonly 5 to 10 mg/kg intravenously every 2 to 3 weeks for an induction phase, then extended intervals for maintenance (specialist protocols) Avoid Recent major surgery (wait at least 4 weeks; stop before elective surgery), uncontrolled hypertension, active bleeding, pregnancy"],["sec","cl:t-dr_bev~s2-1","Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","Bevacizumab · Using it in ENT","For children and adults with frequent surgery or pulmonary spread, systemic bevacizumab with debulking surgery lengthens intervals between procedures and can induce remission in many case series; it is used under a multidisciplinary team."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bev~s2-2","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia","Bevacizumab · Using it in ENT","For severe epistaxis despite local treatments (laser, coblation, septodermoplasty, Young's procedure), systemic bevacizumab reduces bleeding and transfusion needs. Topical intranasal bevacizumab did not help in trials. Tranexamic acid and iron replacement continue."],["ev","cl:t-dr_bev~s3-1","Bevacizumab: current evidence","Bevacizumab · Safety","NF2. Bevacizumab shrank vestibular schwannomas and improved hearing in some patients with neurofibromatosis type 2 (Plotkin et al., NEJM 2009) and is now standard for progressive tumours threatening hearing. HHT. Intravenous bevacizumab reduces epistaxis and transfusion needs and is recommended for severe disease by the Second International HHT Guidelines (Faughnan et al., Ann Intern Med 2020); topical spray does not work (Whitehead et al., JAMA 2016). RRP. Systemic bevacizumab reduced disease and surgery in severe papillomatosis in a systematic review (Ryan et al., Laryngoscope 2021)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bev~s3-1","Adverse effects","Bevacizumab · Safety","Hypertension , proteinuria , epistaxis or bleeding, arterial and venous thrombosis, delayed wound healing and wound dehiscence, gastrointestinal perforation, osteonecrosis of the jaw, posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bev~s4-1","Viva essentials","Bevacizumab · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Its role in papillomatosis and HHT . Monitoring (blood pressure, urine protein) and the surgery interval."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bev~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Bevacizumab · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a patient who had bevacizumab within the last few weeks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_botox~s1-1","Summary","Botulinum toxin · At a glance","Botulinum toxin Summary Preparations OnabotulinumtoxinA (Botox), abobotulinumtoxinA (Dysport), incobotulinumtoxinA (Xeomin); botulinum toxin B (rimabotulinumtoxinB) for resistant cases How it works Cleaves SNARE proteins (SNAP-25 for type A) in the presynaptic terminal, preventing acetylcholine release; recovery requires new terminal sprouting (3 to 6 months) ENT uses Adductor spasmodic dysphonia (thyroarytenoid injection); abductor spasmodic dysphonia (posterior cricoarytenoid); essential vocal tremor; sialorrhoea (parotid and submandibular glands under ultrasound); Frey's syndrome (intradermal); cricopharyngeal achalasia (dysfunction); laryngeal contact granulomas (rest the folds); synkinesis and hyperkinesis after facial palsy; palatal myoclonus causing objective tinnitus; first bite syndrome; facial aesthetics Typical doses Adductor spasmodic dysphonia commonly 0.625 to 2.5 units of onabotulinumtoxinA per vocal fold through an EMG-guided needle; sialorrhoea in adults incobotulinumtoxinA 100 units in total (30 units into each parotid and 20 units into each submandibular gland); Frey's syndrome about 1 to 2.5 units per square centimetre of the area mapped by a starch-iodine (Minor) test Onset and duration Effect from 1 to 3 days, peak at 1 to 2 weeks, lasting about 3 to 4 months Avoid Myasthenia gravis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome; infection at the site; pregnancy and breastfeeding; caution with aminoglycosides (potentiation)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_botox~s2-1","Spasmodic dysphonia","Botulinum toxin · Using it in ENT","A focal laryngeal dystonia: strained, strangled, effortful speech with voice breaks (adductor type) or breathy breaks (abductor type), better with laughing, singing or whispering. Injections into the thyroarytenoid-lateral cricoarytenoid complex through the cricothyroid membrane under EMG guidance, or by a transoral or transnasal approach, restore fluency; patients expect a few days to a couple of weeks of breathiness and mild aspiration of thin fluids after each injection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_botox~s2-2","Sialorrhoea","Botulinum toxin · Using it in ENT","Injected into the parotid and submandibular glands under ultrasound, it reduces drooling in neurological disease (Parkinson's, cerebral palsy, motor neurone disease) for several months; dysphagia can worsen if the toxin spreads to the pharyngeal muscles."],["sec","cl:t-dr_botox~s2-3","Frey's syndrome","Botulinum toxin · Using it in ENT","Gustatory sweating and flushing over the parotid region after parotidectomy (aberrant regeneration of parasympathetic fibres into sweat glands): map the area with the Minor starch-iodine test and inject intradermally; benefit lasts many months."],["sec","cl:t-dr_botox~s3-1","Adverse effects","Botulinum toxin · Safety","Local weakness beyond the target (breathy voice and aspiration after laryngeal injection; dysphagia after salivary or cricopharyngeal injection; facial asymmetry and ptosis after facial injection), dry mouth, flu-like symptoms, antibody-mediated resistance after frequent high doses, and very rarely distant spread with generalised weakness."],["ev","cl:t-dr_botox~s3-2","Botulinum toxin: current evidence","Botulinum toxin · Safety","Laryngeal dystonia. The American Academy of Neurology rated botulinum toxin probably effective for adductor spasmodic dysphonia (Simpson et al., Neurology 2008). Sialorrhoea. In the SIAXI trial, incobotulinumtoxinA injected into the parotid and submandibular glands reduced drooling in adults with neurological disease (Jost et al., Neurology 2019), and NICE (NG62) recommends it for children with cerebral palsy when anticholinergic drugs fail. Frey syndrome and first-bite syndrome respond to intradermal and parotid injection respectively."],["sec","cl:t-dr_botox~s3-2","Interactions","Botulinum toxin · Safety","Aminoglycosides and other drugs that affect neuromuscular transmission enhance its effect."],["sec","cl:t-dr_botox~s4-1","Viva essentials","Botulinum toxin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism (SNAP-25 cleavage) and duration. Technique and side effects of injection for spasmodic dysphonia . Frey's syndrome and the Minor test. Units are product-specific ."],["sec","cl:t-dr_botox~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Botulinum toxin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Converting doses between products as if units were equal. Not warning a patient about breathiness and aspiration after laryngeal injection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_budir~s1-1","Summary","Steroid drops and irrigations · At a glance","Steroid drops and irrigations Summary Preparations Betamethasone sodium phosphate 0.1% drops; fluticasone propionate nasules (400 micrograms per unit dose); budesonide respules (0.5 to 1 mg) added to a saline rinse bottle (off-label); steroid-releasing sinus implants (mometasone) ENT uses Nasal polyps; chronic rhinosinusitis after surgery; olfactory loss from polyps or eosinophilic disease; postoperative oedema Doses Betamethasone 2 to 3 drops into each nostril two or three times daily in the head-down position, for short courses (commonly up to 4 to 6 weeks); fluticasone nasules one 400 microgram unit once or twice daily, divided between the nostrils; budesonide 0.5 to 1 mg in 240 ml saline once or twice daily Avoid Prolonged betamethasone drops (systemic absorption and adrenal suppression)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_budir~s2-1","Positions for drops","Steroid drops and irrigations · Using them in ENT","Drops must run along the roof of the nose: the head-down and forward (\"praying to Mecca\") position, the lying head back over the edge of the bed (Mygind) position, or lying on one side with the head turned down (lateral head low). Hold the position for 1 to 2 minutes for each nostril. The upright position wastes the dose down the throat."],["sec","cl:t-dr_budir~s2-2","Choosing the agent","Steroid drops and irrigations · Using them in ENT","Betamethasone drops are potent and work well for short courses, but are absorbed systemically; repeated courses can suppress the adrenal axis and cause Cushingoid features, so limit their use and reserve them for flares or before surgery. Fluticasone nasules have low bioavailability and suit longer use. Budesonide rinses after sinus surgery deliver steroid widely with acceptable systemic absorption and improve symptom scores and endoscopic appearance."],["ev","cl:t-dr_budir~s3-1","Steroid drops and irrigations: current evidence","Steroid drops and irrigations · Safety","High-volume steroid irrigation. Budesonide added to a large-volume saline rinse reaches the sinuses far better than a spray after surgery, and EPOS 2020 supports it in chronic rhinosinusitis, especially after sinus surgery. Studies of budesonide irrigation have not shown clinically important adrenal suppression at the usual doses, whereas betamethasone drops are absorbed more and should be used in short courses. Steroid-eluting stents placed at surgery reduce scarring and the need for postoperative intervention in randomised trials."],["sec","cl:t-dr_budir~s3-1","Adverse effects","Steroid drops and irrigations · Safety","With betamethasone drops: adrenal suppression , raised blood glucose, glaucoma and cataract risk with prolonged use. Local irritation, epistaxis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_budir~s4-1","Viva essentials","Steroid drops and irrigations · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why drops and rinses reach the sinuses better than sprays. The head-down positions. The risk of systemic absorption with betamethasone drops."],["sec","cl:t-dr_budir~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Steroid drops and irrigations · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing betamethasone drops indefinitely. Instilling drops with the head upright."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bupi~s1-1","Summary","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine · At a glance","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine Summary Class Long-acting amide local anaesthetics How they work Sodium-channel blockade; high lipid solubility and protein binding give long duration; slow dissociation from cardiac sodium channels causes cardiotoxicity Preparations 0.25% and 0.5% (bupivacaine also with adrenaline 1:200,000); levobupivacaine 0.25%, 0.5%, 0.75% ENT uses Wound infiltration after neck, thyroid, parotid and ear surgery; nerve blocks (superficial cervical plexus for thyroid and neck surgery); post-tonsillectomy infiltration (variable evidence); scalp blocks Maximum dose Bupivacaine 2 mg/kg (up to 150 mg in 4 hours), with or without adrenaline; levobupivacaine 150 mg as a single dose (400 mg in 24 hours) Quick conversion 0.25% = 2.5 mg/ml; 0.5% = 5 mg/ml Onset and duration Onset 10 to 20 minutes; duration 4 to 8 hours"],["sec","cl:t-dr_bupi~s2-1","Infiltration and blocks","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine · Using them in ENT","Infiltrating the wound at the end of surgery reduces early pain and opioid use. A superficial cervical plexus block at the midpoint of the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid reduces pain after thyroidectomy and neck dissection. Mixing with lidocaine combines fast onset with long duration, but the toxic doses are additive."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bupi~s2-2","Tonsillectomy","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine · Using them in ENT","Infiltrating the tonsillar fossae may reduce early pain; rare complications (airway obstruction from parapharyngeal spread, cranial nerve palsies, and vessel injection) mean it is used selectively."],["ev","cl:t-dr_bupi~s3-1","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine: current evidence","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine · Safety","Toxicity. Bupivacaine is the most cardiotoxic local anaesthetic; inadvertent intravascular injection can cause refractory arrhythmia. The Association of Anaesthetists guideline for local anaesthetic toxicity recommends 20% lipid emulsion and prolonged resuscitation. Tonsillectomy. Infiltration of the tonsil bed gives a small reduction in early pain in trials, but rare catastrophic complications (airway obstruction, brainstem injection) have been reported, so injection should be shallow and aspirated first."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bupi~s3-1","Adverse effects","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine · Safety","Cardiotoxicity (ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation, prolonged and difficult resuscitation) and neurotoxicity; levobupivacaine is slightly less cardiotoxic. Bupivacaine must never be used for intravenous regional anaesthesia. Manage toxicity as for lidocaine, with early lipid emulsion ."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bupi~s4-1","Viva essentials","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Maximum doses and why lower than lidocaine. Why bupivacaine toxicity is so dangerous to the heart. The superficial cervical plexus block."],["sec","cl:t-dr_bupi~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Bupivacaine and levobupivacaine · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating lidocaine and bupivacaine doses as separate when combined."],["sec","cl:t-dr_calc~s1-1","Summary","Calcium replacement · At a glance","Calcium replacement Summary Intravenous Calcium gluconate 10% (2.2 mmol of calcium per 10 ml): 10 to 20 ml in 50 to 100 ml of 5% glucose or saline over 10 minutes with ECG monitoring, then an infusion (for example 100 ml of 10% calcium gluconate in 1 litre, starting at about 50 ml/hour and titrated) for ongoing need Oral Calcium carbonate (for example 1.25 to 1.5 g tablets, 500 to 600 mg elemental calcium) two or three times daily, increased as needed; calcium citrate is better absorbed with acid suppression Indications for intravenous calcium Symptoms (tetany, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, seizures), ECG change (prolonged QT), or adjusted calcium below about 1.9 mmol/l Adjuncts Check and correct magnesium (hypomagnesaemia prevents correction of calcium); alfacalcidol or calcitriol when PTH is low Avoid Calcium chloride through a peripheral line (tissue necrosis on extravasation); rapid infusion; mixing with bicarbonate or phosphate"],["sec","cl:t-dr_calc~s2-1","After total thyroidectomy","Calcium replacement · Using them in ENT","Units use protocols based on PTH and calcium. A low PTH in the hours after surgery predicts hypocalcaemia: start oral calcium (with or without alfacalcidol) early, which allows safe early discharge; a normal PTH allows discharge without supplements. Check adjusted calcium on the evening of surgery and the next morning, and examine for Chvostek (facial twitch on tapping the facial nerve) and Trousseau (carpal spasm with a blood pressure cuff) signs. Most hypocalcaemia is temporary; hypoparathyroidism lasting beyond 6 to 12 months is permanent."],["sec","cl:t-dr_calc~s2-2","After parathyroidectomy","Calcium replacement · Using them in ENT","Hungry bone syndrome (rapid uptake of calcium into demineralised bone) follows surgery for severe primary or secondary hyperparathyroidism: calcium falls over days despite normal PTH and may need large doses of intravenous and oral calcium with alfacalcidol; low phosphate and magnesium accompany it."],["sec","cl:t-dr_calc~s3-1","Adverse effects","Calcium replacement · Safety","Intravenous: arrhythmia and bradycardia if given too fast (especially with digoxin), venous irritation, calcinosis and necrosis on extravasation. Oral: constipation, nausea, hypercalcaemia and nephrocalcinosis or stones with overtreatment."],["ev","cl:t-dr_calc~s3-2","Calcium replacement: current evidence","Calcium replacement · Safety","After thyroidectomy. A parathyroid hormone level measured on the day after surgery predicts hypocalcaemia: a normal level allows early discharge, and a low level (commonly under about 15 pg/ml or 1.6 pmol/l) prompts prophylactic calcium with or without activated vitamin D (American Thyroid Association statement, Orloff et al., Thyroid 2018). Acute severe hypocalcaemia. The Society for Endocrinology emergency guidance recommends 10 to 20 ml of 10% calcium gluconate in 50 to 100 ml of 5% glucose over ten minutes with ECG monitoring, followed by an infusion (Turner et al., Endocr Connect 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_calc~s3-2","Interactions","Calcium replacement · Safety","Separate oral calcium from levothyroxine (4 hours), quinolones and tetracyclines (2 to 3 hours), iron and bisphosphonates; thiazides raise calcium; digoxin toxicity is increased by hypercalcaemia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_calc~s4-1","Viva essentials","Calcium replacement · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Recognition of hypocalcaemia (symptoms, Chvostek and Trousseau signs, ECG). Intravenous calcium gluconate regimen and monitoring. The role of magnesium and of PTH-guided protocols. Hungry bone syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-dr_calc~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Calcium replacement · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving calcium chloride peripherally. Failing to correct magnesium. Discharging after total thyroidectomy without a calcium or PTH check."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbi~s1-1","Summary","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil · At a glance","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil Summary Agents Carbimazole (prodrug of methimazole); propylthiouracil How they work Inhibit thyroid peroxidase, blocking iodination of thyroglobulin and coupling of iodotyrosines; PTU also blocks peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 ENT uses Making Graves' disease and toxic nodular goitre euthyroid before thyroidectomy; definitive medical treatment of Graves' disease (12 to 18 months) Carbimazole dose 20 to 40 mg daily, titrated to free T4 (titration regimen), or 40 mg daily with levothyroxine (block-and-replace) PTU dose 200 to 400 mg daily in divided doses, then reduced; preferred in the first trimester of pregnancy and in thyroid storm Monitoring Thyroid function every 4 to 6 weeks; full blood count if any symptoms of infection; liver function with PTU Pregnancy PTU in the first trimester (carbimazole embryopathy), carbimazole thereafter"],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbi~s2-1","Preparing for thyroidectomy","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil · Using them in ENT","Aim for a euthyroid state on carbimazole (with propranolol for symptoms) before elective surgery. When antithyroid drugs cannot be used or the patient needs urgent surgery, prepare with potassium iodide, beta blockers, steroids and sometimes cholestyramine under endocrinology (see preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbi~s3-1","Agranulocytosis","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil · Safety","Occurs in about 0.1% to 0.5%, usually in the first 3 months. Every patient must be told to stop the drug and have an urgent full blood count if they develop a sore throat, mouth ulcers, fever or other signs of infection. In a patient with a sore throat on carbimazole, check the neutrophil count before diagnosing tonsillitis, and treat neutropenic sepsis urgently."],["ev","cl:t-dr_carbi~s3-2","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil: current evidence","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil · Safety","Pregnancy. The UK MHRA (2019) warned of congenital malformations with carbimazole in the first trimester and advised effective contraception; propylthiouracil is preferred in early pregnancy, then switched back. Liver. The US FDA added a boxed warning in 2010 for severe propylthiouracil hepatotoxicity, so carbimazole or methimazole is preferred otherwise. Agranulocytosis. Patients must stop the drug and have a blood count if they develop a sore throat or fever. Course. The European Thyroid Association recommends 12 to 18 months of treatment for Graves' disease, or longer low-dose treatment in selected patients (Kahaly et al., Eur Thyroid J 2018)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbi~s3-2","Other adverse effects","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil · Safety","Rash and pruritus (common), arthralgia, raised liver enzymes; PTU causes rare but severe hepatotoxicity (avoid it as long-term treatment); ANCA-associated vasculitis with PTU; carbimazole in early pregnancy causes aplasia cutis and other embryopathy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbi~s4-1","Viva essentials","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism and the difference between carbimazole and PTU. Agranulocytosis and the sore throat warning. Making the patient euthyroid before surgery."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbi~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Carbimazole and propylthiouracil · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a sore throat in a patient on carbimazole as tonsillitis without a blood count."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbo~s1-1","Summary","Carboplatin · At a glance","Carboplatin Summary Class Platinum compound ENT uses Palliative chemotherapy for recurrent or metastatic squamous cell carcinoma (with 5-FU, a taxane, or immunotherapy as in KEYNOTE-048); concurrent chemoradiotherapy in patients unfit for cisplatin (less evidence); some nasopharyngeal and sinonasal regimens Dose By the Calvert formula: dose (mg) = target AUC x (GFR + 25), commonly AUC 5 every 3 weeks, or lower weekly doses with radiotherapy Toxicity Myelosuppression (platelets most), less renal, nerve and ear toxicity than cisplatin"],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbo~s2-1","Choosing carboplatin","Carboplatin · Using it in ENT","When renal function is poor, hearing is already impaired, neuropathy is present or the patient is frail, carboplatin is substituted for cisplatin, accepting probably lower efficacy with radiotherapy. Ototoxicity is uncommon at standard doses but occurs with high doses and in children."],["ev","cl:t-dr_carbo~s3-1","Carboplatin: current evidence","Carboplatin · Safety","Cisplatin-unfit patients. Carboplatin, alone or with fluorouracil or paclitaxel, is a common substitute with radiotherapy when cisplatin is contraindicated (poor hearing, renal impairment, neuropathy), but its benefit has not been tested as rigorously as cisplatin's. Dosing. The Calvert formula uses measured or estimated GFR; capping the GFR at 125 ml/min avoids overdosing. Toxicity. Less ototoxic and nephrotoxic than cisplatin, but more thrombocytopenia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbo~s3-1","Adverse effects","Carboplatin · Safety","Thrombocytopenia, neutropenia and anaemia (nadir at 2 to 3 weeks); nausea; hypersensitivity after repeated cycles; hypomagnesaemia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbo~s4-1","Viva essentials","Carboplatin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for How its toxicity differs from cisplatin. The Calvert formula."],["sec","cl:t-dr_carbo~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Carboplatin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Assuming carboplatin carries no ototoxic risk."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s1-1","Summary","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · At a glance","Cefalexin and cefuroxime Summary Class Cephalosporins: cefalexin first generation, cefuroxime second generation How they work Beta-lactams that inhibit cell wall cross-linking; more stable to staphylococcal penicillinase than penicillin Spectrum Cefalexin: S. aureus (not MRSA), streptococci, some E. coli. Cefuroxime: adds H. influenzae and Moraxella, including beta-lactamase producers ENT uses Cellulitis and wound infection (cefalexin); acute otitis media and sinusitis in non-severe penicillin allergy (cefuroxime); surgical prophylaxis (cefuroxime or cefazolin intravenously) Adult dose Cefalexin 500 mg two or three times daily (up to 1 to 1.5 g three or four times daily); cefuroxime axetil 250 to 500 mg twice daily; intravenous cefuroxime 750 mg to 1.5 g three times daily Child dose Cefalexin 12.5 mg/kg twice daily (25 mg/kg twice daily for severe infection); cefuroxime 15 mg/kg twice daily (maximum 250 mg per dose) Avoid Previous immediate (anaphylactic) penicillin or cephalosporin allergy Pregnancy Safe"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s2-1","Mechanism and generations","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · How they work","Cephalosporins share the beta-lactam ring of penicillins but have a six-membered dihydrothiazine ring that resists many beta-lactamases. Moving from the first to the third generation, Gram-negative activity increases and Gram-positive activity falls; cefalexin is the best oral anti-staphylococcal cephalosporin, cefuroxime the best balanced for the respiratory organisms."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s2-2","Absorption and handling","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · How they work","Cefalexin is almost completely absorbed; cefuroxime axetil is a prodrug absorbed better with food. Both are excreted by the kidney: reduce the dose when renal function is poor."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s3-1","Penicillin allergy","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · Using them in ENT","Ask what actually happened. A non-severe delayed rash is not a contraindication to a cephalosporin; cross-reactivity is low (about 1% to 2%) and is driven mainly by similar side chains (cefalexin shares one with amoxicillin, cefuroxime does not). After anaphylaxis, angioedema, or a severe cutaneous reaction (Stevens-Johnson syndrome, DRESS), avoid all beta-lactams and choose a macrolide, doxycycline or clindamycin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s3-2","Skin, wound and soft tissue","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · Using them in ENT","Cefalexin covers the staphylococci and streptococci of cellulitis, wound infection and infected epidermoid cysts, and is an option for prophylaxis after ear and facial plastic surgery where cover is wanted."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s3-3","Surgical prophylaxis","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · Using them in ENT","Intravenous cefazolin or cefuroxime at induction (with metronidazole if the pharynx is opened) is standard for clean-contaminated head and neck surgery; prophylaxis should not run beyond 24 hours."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s4-1","Adverse effects","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · Safety","Diarrhoea and C. difficile , among the highest risks of any antibiotic class. Hypersensitivity, including rash, serum sickness-like reactions (cefaclor especially) and anaphylaxis. Rarely a positive Coombs test with haemolysis, and interstitial nephritis."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cefal~s4-2","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime: current evidence","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · Safety","Cross-reactivity. True cross-allergy between penicillins and cephalosporins is about 1 to 2% and depends mostly on shared side chains: cefalexin and cefaclor share the amoxicillin side chain, whereas cefuroxime, ceftriaxone and cefazolin do not, so a dissimilar cephalosporin can usually be given to a patient with a non-severe penicillin allergy. Stewardship. Oral cephalosporins carry a higher risk of C. difficile than penicillins and are not first line for otitis media or sinusitis in NICE guidance."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s4-2","Interactions","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · Safety","Mild potentiation of warfarin. Probenecid raises their levels."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s5-1","Viva essentials","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The generations and how the spectrum shifts. Cross-reactivity with penicillin and which allergy histories rule them out. That no oral cephalosporin covers Pseudomonas or anaerobes."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cefal~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Oral cephalosporins: cefalexin and cefuroxime · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing a cephalosporin after documented penicillin anaphylaxis. Using them for necrotising otitis externa or a dental abscess without anaerobic cover. Prolonged prophylaxis beyond 24 hours."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s1-1","Summary","Ceftazidime · At a glance","Ceftazidime Summary Class Third-generation (anti-pseudomonal) cephalosporin How it works Cell wall synthesis inhibitor with an affinity for pseudomonal penicillin-binding protein 3 Spectrum Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Enterobacterales, Haemophilus; poor against staphylococci and streptococci; not anaerobes, not MRSA, not enterococci ENT uses Necrotising otitis externa; Pseudomonas perichondritis with abscess or failure of oral treatment; Pseudomonas infection in cystic fibrosis sinus disease; febrile neutropenia Adult dose 1 to 2 g every 8 hours intravenously (2 g every 8 hours in severe infection) Child dose 25 to 50 mg/kg every 8 hours (maximum 6 g a day); up to 50 mg/kg every 8 hours in cystic fibrosis Avoid Beta-lactam anaphylaxis Pregnancy Use if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s2-1","Mechanism","Ceftazidime · How it works","Like other cephalosporins it blocks peptidoglycan cross-linking; its side chain gives it good penetration and affinity in Pseudomonas. Resistance arises from AmpC derepression, efflux and ESBL production; avibactam (ceftazidime-avibactam) restores activity against many resistant Gram-negatives and is reserved for specialist use."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s2-2","Handling","Ceftazidime · How it works","Intravenous or intramuscular; renally excreted, so the dose falls with the eGFR."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s3-1","Necrotising otitis externa","Ceftazidime · Using it in ENT","Ceftazidime, or piperacillin-tazobactam, is the intravenous anti-pseudomonal choice, frequently with oral ciprofloxacin. After clinical response, falling CRP and ESR, and a resolving scan, therapy is continued orally with ciprofloxacin for a total of 6 weeks or more; recurrence is common in the first year. If cultures grow Aspergillus, add voriconazole."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s3-2","Cartilage and wound infection","Ceftazidime · Using it in ENT","Pseudomonas perichondritis of the pinna with abscess, or not responding to oral ciprofloxacin, needs drainage and intravenous ceftazidime."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s4-1","Adverse effects","Ceftazidime · Safety","Diarrhoea and C. difficile; hypersensitivity. Neurotoxicity (encephalopathy, myoclonus, seizures) if the dose is not reduced in renal failure. Positive Coombs test, eosinophilia, raised liver enzymes."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s4-2","Ceftazidime: current evidence","Ceftazidime · Safety","Necrotising otitis externa. Ceftazidime remains a standard intravenous choice against Pseudomonas aeruginosa , often followed by oral ciprofloxacin, for six weeks or more guided by symptoms, CRP and imaging. Resistance. For multidrug-resistant Pseudomonas , newer combinations such as ceftazidime–avibactam and ceftolozane–tazobactam are reserved on microbiology advice."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s4-2","Interactions","Ceftazidime · Safety","Aminoglycosides (additive nephrotoxicity; do not mix in one bag); chloramphenicol (antagonism)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s5-1","Viva essentials","Ceftazidime · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The intravenous and oral options for Pseudomonas in ENT. How you monitor treatment of necrotising otitis externa: pain, granulation, CRP and ESR, imaging (gallium or MRI). Why ceftazidime is not a good drug for streptococcal or staphylococcal infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftaz~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Ceftazidime · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using ceftazidime as general broad cover in head and neck sepsis (it misses Gram-positives and anaerobes). Forgetting renal dose adjustment in elderly diabetics."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s1-1","Summary","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · At a glance","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime Summary Class Third-generation cephalosporins How they work Cell wall synthesis inhibitors, stable to most beta-lactamases of respiratory pathogens Spectrum S. pneumoniae (including many penicillin-resistant strains), H. influenzae, Moraxella, Neisseria, streptococci, Enterobacterales; poor against S. aureus compared with flucloxacillin; not Pseudomonas, not enterococci, not anaerobes, not MRSA, not Listeria ENT uses Meningitis, subdural empyema and brain abscess from ear or sinus disease (with metronidazole); orbital cellulitis and abscess; acute mastoiditis; severe deep neck infection; acute epiglottitis and supraglottitis; Lyme disease with neurological involvement Adult dose Ceftriaxone 2 g once daily; 2 g twice daily for meningitis and intracranial infection. Cefotaxime 1 to 2 g every 8 hours; 2 g every 6 hours for meningitis Child dose Ceftriaxone 50 to 80 mg/kg once daily (80 mg/kg in meningitis, maximum 4 g); cefotaxime 50 mg/kg every 8 to 12 hours (every 6 hours in meningitis); cefotaxime is preferred in neonates Avoid Previous anaphylaxis to beta-lactams; ceftriaxone with intravenous calcium in neonates Pregnancy Safe"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s2-1","Mechanism","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · How they work","Bactericidal inhibition of cell wall synthesis. Their stability to the beta-lactamases of Haemophilus and Moraxella, and good activity against pneumococci with reduced penicillin sensitivity, make them the reliable empirical choice for invasive respiratory-tract bacteria."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s2-2","Handling","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · How they work","Ceftriaxone has a long half-life (about 8 hours) that allows once-daily dosing and outpatient parenteral therapy; it is excreted in bile and urine, so it needs no adjustment in renal impairment alone. Cefotaxime is renally excreted and given more often. Both reach therapeutic levels in the cerebrospinal fluid when the meninges are inflamed."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s3-1","Intracranial complications","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · Using them in ENT","Meningitis, extradural or subdural abscess, brain abscess and venous sinus thrombosis from otitis media, mastoiditis or sinusitis need high-dose ceftriaxone (2 g twice daily) with metronidazole , often with vancomycin where penicillin-resistant pneumococcus or MRSA is a concern, plus neurosurgical drainage and treatment of the source. Courses are typically 4 to 8 weeks for abscesses."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s3-2","Orbital and neck infection","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · Using them in ENT","Orbital cellulitis (Chandler II and above): intravenous ceftriaxone with metronidazole (or co-amoxiclav), decongestants and nasal steroid, with CT and drainage for an abscess or failing vision. Acute epiglottitis : secure the airway first, then ceftriaxone. Deep neck infection and acute mastoiditis : ceftriaxone with metronidazole is a common empirical choice."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s3-3","Outpatient parenteral therapy","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · Using them in ENT","Once-daily ceftriaxone allows patients to complete long courses at home, for example in skull base osteomyelitis caused by a sensitive organism."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s4-1","Adverse effects","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · Safety","C. difficile infection and diarrhoea. Ceftriaxone biliary sludge (pseudolithiasis), occasionally causing biliary colic, especially in children on high doses. In neonates, ceftriaxone displaces bilirubin and precipitates with calcium (fatal lung and kidney deposits): avoid in jaundiced neonates and with calcium infusions. Hypersensitivity; neutropenia and thrombocytopenia on long courses."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s4-2","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime: current evidence","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · Safety","Practical use. Once-daily dosing suits outpatient parenteral antibiotic therapy. In bacterial meningitis, guidelines recommend ceftriaxone 2 g twice daily with dexamethasone 10 mg every six hours for four days, started before or with the first dose of antibiotic, because dexamethasone reduces death and hearing loss in pneumococcal meningitis (de Gans and van de Beek, NEJM 2002). Safety. Do not give ceftriaxone with intravenous calcium in neonates (fatal precipitates); it can cause biliary sludge and, rarely, immune haemolysis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s4-2","Interactions","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · Safety","Calcium-containing infusions (ceftriaxone, neonates); minor effect on warfarin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s5-1","Viva essentials","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Empirical regimen for an otogenic brain abscess : ceftriaxone, metronidazole, and drainage of both the abscess and the ear. What third-generation cephalosporins miss: Pseudomonas (except ceftazidime), anaerobes, enterococci, MRSA, Listeria. Ceftriaxone in neonates and with calcium."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ceftri~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Ceftriaxone and cefotaxime · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating an intracranial complication without dealing with the ear or sinus source. Omitting anaerobic cover in sinogenic and otogenic abscesses. Choosing ceftriaxone for necrotising otitis externa."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cetux~s1-1","Summary","Cetuximab · At a glance","Cetuximab Summary Class Anti-EGFR monoclonal antibody (IgG1, chimeric) How it works Blocks ligand binding and EGFR signalling, reduces proliferation and radioresistance, and triggers antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity ENT uses With radiotherapy in locally advanced squamous cell carcinoma when cisplatin is contraindicated; with platinum and 5-FU (EXTREME regimen) for recurrent or metastatic disease (largely replaced by pembrolizumab first-line); as a single agent in palliation Dose Loading 400 mg/m2 intravenously one week before radiotherapy, then 250 mg/m2 weekly Premedication Antihistamine and steroid before the first infusions"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cetux~s2-1","Choosing it","Cetuximab · Using it in ENT","Cetuximab with radiotherapy suits patients with renal impairment, hearing loss, neuropathy or poor performance status who cannot have cisplatin. In fit patients with HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer, cisplatin is better (RTOG 1016, De-ESCALaTE)."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cetux~s3-1","Cetuximab: current evidence","Cetuximab · Safety","Trials. Cetuximab with radiotherapy improved survival over radiotherapy alone (Bonner et al., NEJM 2006), and with platinum and fluorouracil it became first-line treatment for recurrent disease (EXTREME; Vermorken et al., NEJM 2008). Not a substitute for cisplatin. In HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer it was inferior to cisplatin with radiotherapy (Gillison et al., Lancet 2019; Mehanna et al., Lancet 2019). Current role. Radiotherapy with cetuximab for cisplatin-unfit patients, and second-line options after immunotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cetux~s3-1","Adverse effects","Cetuximab · Safety","Acneiform rash in most patients (its severity correlates with response); treat with emollients, topical and oral antibiotics (doxycycline). Infusion reactions , including anaphylaxis, more frequent in regions where tick bites cause IgE against galactose-alpha-1,3-galactose (alpha-gal). Worse radiation dermatitis and mucositis. Hypomagnesaemia , diarrhoea, interstitial lung disease rarely."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cetux~s4-1","Viva essentials","Cetuximab · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The target (EGFR) and the Bonner trial. Why it is not used for de-escalation in HPV-positive disease. Rash and infusion reactions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cetux~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Cetuximab · For the examination","Common pitfalls Choosing cetuximab over cisplatin in a fit HPV-positive patient."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cido~s1-1","Summary","Cidofovir · At a glance","Cidofovir Summary Class Acyclic nucleotide analogue of cytosine How it works Inhibits viral DNA polymerase after cellular phosphorylation (no viral kinase needed); in HPV-infected tissue, induces apoptosis ENT uses Adjuvant intralesional treatment of recurrent respiratory papillomatosis needing frequent surgery (for example more than four procedures a year), distal spread, or rapid regrowth Dose Intralesional injection of 5 to 7.5 mg/ml at the base of papillomas after debulking, at intervals of weeks, with a maximum per session related to body weight (often around 3 mg/kg) Avoid Renal impairment; pregnancy"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cido~s2-1","Adjuvant therapy in papillomatosis","Cidofovir · Using it in ENT","The mainstay is surgical debulking with microdebrider or laser while preserving normal tissue. Adjuvants are considered for severe disease: intralesional cidofovir, systemic bevacizumab , and the HPV vaccine (Gardasil), which may lengthen intervals between surgeries. Evidence from trials of cidofovir is mixed."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cido~s3-1","Cidofovir: current evidence","Cidofovir · Safety","Evidence. Intralesional cidofovir for recurrent respiratory papillomatosis is supported mainly by case series; controlled evidence of benefit over surgery alone is weak. Concerns about nephrotoxicity and, in animal studies, carcinogenicity have limited its use. Changing practice. Systemic bevacizumab is now preferred for severe, frequently recurring or distal disease (Ryan et al., Laryngoscope 2021), and prophylactic HPV vaccination is expected to reduce juvenile-onset disease."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cido~s3-1","Adverse effects","Cidofovir · Safety","Systemically nephrotoxic (proximal tubule) and neutropenic; with intralesional use, systemic toxicity is uncommon but renal function and blood counts are checked. Concern that it may promote dysplasia has led to cautious use, with biopsies before each treatment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cido~s4-1","Viva essentials","Cidofovir · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The adjuvant options for recurrent respiratory papillomatosis and when to use them. The safety concerns with cidofovir."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cido~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Cidofovir · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using cidofovir without biopsies to exclude dysplasia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cina~s1-1","Summary","Cinacalcet · At a glance","Cinacalcet Summary Class Calcimimetic (allosteric modulator of the calcium-sensing receptor) ENT uses Primary hyperparathyroidism when parathyroidectomy is contraindicated, refused or has failed; parathyroid carcinoma with hypercalcaemia; secondary hyperparathyroidism in end-stage kidney disease (alongside or instead of parathyroidectomy); before surgery in severe hypercalcaemia Dose Primary hyperparathyroidism and carcinoma: 30 mg twice daily, titrated up to 90 mg three or four times daily; secondary hyperparathyroidism: 30 mg once daily, titrated to a maximum of 180 mg daily Monitoring Calcium within a week of starting or changing the dose, then regularly; PTH Etelcalcetide An intravenous calcimimetic given at dialysis"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cina~s2-1","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Cinacalcet · Using it in ENT","Surgery is recommended for patients who meet guideline criteria (symptomatic, calcium more than 0.25 mmol/l above the upper limit, osteoporosis or fragility fracture, renal stones or impaired renal function, age under 50) and is curative in about 95% with an experienced surgeon. Cinacalcet controls calcium but does not cure the disease or reverse bone loss."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cina~s2-2","Secondary hyperparathyroidism","Cinacalcet · Using it in ENT","In dialysis patients, cinacalcet with vitamin D and phosphate binders controls PTH; parathyroidectomy (subtotal or total with autotransplantation) is used when medical treatment fails or causes intolerable effects."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cina~s3-1","Adverse effects","Cinacalcet · Safety","Nausea and vomiting (common), hypocalcaemia (paraesthesia, cramps, seizures, prolonged QT), dizziness, rash; gastrointestinal bleeding reported."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cina~s3-2","Cinacalcet: current evidence","Cinacalcet · Safety","Place in treatment. The Fifth International Workshop on asymptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism recommends cinacalcet to lower calcium in patients who need treatment but cannot have or refuse surgery; it normalises calcium in most but does not improve bone density, for which a bisphosphonate or denosumab is added (Bilezikian et al., J Bone Miner Res 2022). Parathyroid carcinoma. It controls hypercalcaemia when surgery cannot."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cina~s3-2","Interactions","Cinacalcet · Safety","A strong CYP2D6 inhibitor (raises levels of flecainide, tricyclics, metoprolol and others); levels raised by strong CYP3A4 inhibitors."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cina~s4-1","Viva essentials","Cinacalcet · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism (calcium-sensing receptor). Why surgery remains first choice in primary hyperparathyroidism."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cina~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Cinacalcet · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using cinacalcet in place of surgery in a fit patient with osteoporosis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s1-1","Summary","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · At a glance","Ciprofloxacin Summary Class Fluoroquinolone How it works Inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV; bactericidal, concentration-dependent Spectrum Pseudomonas, Enterobacterales, H. influenzae, Moraxella, Neisseria, some staphylococci; weak against streptococci and pneumococci; no anaerobic cover ENT uses Necrotising otitis externa (oral, for 6 weeks or more); perichondritis of the pinna after high piercing or ear surgery; Pseudomonas wound and cavity infection; topical drops (see quinolone ear drops) Adult dose 500 to 750 mg twice daily orally (750 mg twice daily in necrotising otitis externa); intravenous 400 mg two or three times daily Child dose 10 to 20 mg/kg twice daily orally (maximum 750 mg), used in children only where the benefit clearly outweighs risk (cystic fibrosis, Pseudomonas) Avoid History of quinolone-associated tendon disorder; QT prolongation; caution with steroids, in the elderly, in epilepsy, in aneurysm risk Pregnancy Avoid"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s2-1","Mechanism","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · How it works","Quinolones trap gyrase and topoisomerase IV on DNA and produce lethal double-strand breaks. Killing is concentration-dependent , so adequate dosing reduces resistance; Pseudomonas readily becomes resistant during treatment, especially at low doses."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s2-2","Handling","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · How it works","Good oral bioavailability (about 70%), so oral therapy is equivalent to intravenous; reduced by calcium, iron, zinc, magnesium, aluminium antacids and dairy taken at the same time. Partly renally excreted."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s3-1","Necrotising otitis externa","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · Using it in ENT","After biopsy and deep cultures, high-dose oral ciprofloxacin (often after an intravenous anti-pseudomonal start) for at least 6 weeks , with diabetic control and aural toilet. Rising quinolone resistance means cultures must guide treatment; recurrence occurs in up to a fifth, so follow patients for a year."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s3-2","Perichondritis of the pinna","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · Using it in ENT","High (transcartilaginous) piercing and ear surgery give Pseudomonas access to the avascular cartilage. Oral ciprofloxacin, with incision and drainage of any abscess, prevents cartilage necrosis and cauliflower deformity."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s4-1","Adverse effects","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · Safety","Tendinitis and tendon rupture (Achilles especially), sometimes within 48 hours; higher with corticosteroids, age over 60 and renal or solid organ transplant. Aortic aneurysm and dissection , heart valve regurgitation. QT prolongation , dysglycaemia (in diabetics on insulin or sulfonylureas). Peripheral neuropathy, confusion, seizures, psychiatric reactions; these may be long-lasting and disabling. C. difficile; photosensitivity; crystalluria."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cipro~s4-2","Ciprofloxacin (systemic): current evidence","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · Safety","Regulatory restrictions. The UK MHRA (2019, reinforced in 2024) and the EMA restricted systemic fluoroquinolones to infections where other recommended antibiotics are inappropriate, because of long-lasting, disabling side effects: tendinopathy and tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, psychiatric effects, and aortic aneurysm and dissection. Necrotising otitis externa and Pseudomonas infection, where alternatives are limited, remain appropriate uses. Ear drops do not carry these systemic risks and are the preferred topical choice with a perforation or grommet."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s4-2","Interactions","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · Safety","Theophylline and tizanidine (levels rise, contraindicated with tizanidine); warfarin (raised INR); cations (reduced absorption); other QT-prolonging drugs; NSAIDs (seizures)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s5-1","Viva essentials","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The only oral anti-pseudomonal antibiotic. Regimen and duration for necrotising otitis externa . Serious harms: tendon, aorta, QT, nerves, and the warnings you give the patient."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cipro~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Ciprofloxacin (systemic) · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using ciprofloxacin for streptococcal or pneumococcal infection. Prescribing it with steroids to an elderly patient without warning about tendon rupture. Taking it with calcium supplements or milk."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s1-1","Summary","Cisplatin · At a glance","Cisplatin Summary Class Platinum compound (alkylating-like agent) How it works Forms platinum-DNA cross-links (intrastrand adducts) that block replication and trigger apoptosis; sensitises tumour to radiation ENT uses Concurrent chemoradiotherapy (definitive or adjuvant) in locally advanced squamous cell carcinoma; induction chemotherapy (TPF); nasopharyngeal carcinoma (with radiotherapy and induction or adjuvant chemotherapy); recurrent and metastatic disease with other agents Dose Three-weekly 100 mg/m2 on days 1, 22 and 43 of radiotherapy (a cumulative dose of at least 200 mg/m2 matters for benefit), or weekly 40 mg/m2 Requirements Adequate renal function (eGFR usually above 50 to 60 ml/min), hearing, performance status and no significant neuropathy; pre- and post-hydration with magnesium and potassium; aggressive antiemetics"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s2-1","Chemoradiotherapy","Cisplatin · How it works","Cisplatin inhibits repair of radiation-induced DNA damage and kills hypoxic cells; meta-analysis (MACH-NC) shows about 6.5% absolute survival benefit at 5 years for concurrent chemoradiotherapy over radiotherapy alone, with the benefit falling in patients over 70."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s2-2","De-escalation trials","Cisplatin · How it works","In HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer, replacing cisplatin with cetuximab (De-ESCALaTE, RTOG 1016) reduced survival, so cisplatin remains standard even in HPV-positive disease."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s3-1","How and who","Cisplatin · Ototoxicity","Cisplatin enters the cochlea via the stria vascularis and outer hair cells, generating reactive oxygen species and causing apoptosis of outer hair cells in the basal turn first: bilateral, symmetrical, high-frequency sensorineural loss , often with tinnitus, that is permanent and may progress after treatment. It affects up to about half of adults and more children. Risk rises with cumulative dose (especially above 200 to 300 mg/m2), high single doses, young age, pre-existing hearing loss, renal impairment, cranial radiotherapy that includes the cochlea, noise and other ototoxic drugs, and genetic variants (for example TPMT, ACYP2)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s3-2","Monitoring and protection","Cisplatin · Ototoxicity","Baseline audiometry (including extended high frequencies where available) and before each cycle or every few weeks; consider changing to carboplatin if significant loss develops. Sodium thiosulfate , given 6 hours after cisplatin, reduces hearing loss in children with localised solid tumours. Hearing aids and, for profound loss, cochlear implants rehabilitate."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s4-1","Adverse effects","Cisplatin · Safety","Nephrotoxicity (proximal tubule): hydration, magnesium replacement, avoid nephrotoxins. Severe nausea and vomiting (highly emetogenic): 5-HT3 antagonist, NK1 antagonist, dexamethasone. Ototoxicity and tinnitus. Peripheral sensory neuropathy; myelosuppression; hypomagnesaemia, hypokalaemia; thrombosis."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cis~s4-2","Cisplatin: current evidence","Cisplatin · Safety","Benefit. The MACH-NC meta-analysis showed about a 6.5% absolute survival gain at five years from concurrent chemoradiation (Pignon et al., Radiother Oncol 2009). A cumulative dose of at least 200 mg/m² appears to matter. Weekly or three-weekly. In the postoperative setting, an Indian trial found better locoregional control with three-weekly cisplatin (Noronha et al., J Clin Oncol 2018), whereas the Japanese JCOG1008 trial found weekly 40 mg/m² non-inferior for survival with less toxicity (Kiyota et al., J Clin Oncol 2022). Hearing. Baseline and serial audiometry; sodium thiosulfate protects hearing in children (Brock et al., NEJM 2018)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s4-2","Interactions","Cisplatin · Safety","Aminoglycosides, loop diuretics and vancomycin (ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity); NSAIDs (nephrotoxicity)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s5-1","Viva essentials","Cisplatin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications for concurrent chemoradiotherapy (including adjuvant: positive margins and extracapsular spread). The ototoxicity pattern, risk factors and monitoring. Why cetuximab is not a substitute in fit HPV-positive patients."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cis~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Cisplatin · For the examination","Common pitfalls No baseline audiogram before cisplatin. Missing hypomagnesaemia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s1-1","Summary","Clarithromycin · At a glance","Clarithromycin Summary Class Macrolide How it works Binds the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks protein synthesis; bacteriostatic; also reduces neutrophil activity, mucus hypersecretion and biofilm formation Spectrum Streptococci (resistance in a minority of pneumococci and group A streptococci), Moraxella, H. influenzae (moderate), atypicals (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella), Bordetella, non-tuberculous mycobacteria, Helicobacter ENT uses Penicillin allergy: acute otitis media, sinusitis, tonsillitis; long-term macrolide therapy for chronic rhinosinusitis without polyps; non-tuberculous mycobacterial lymphadenitis (with rifampicin or rifabutin) when excision is not possible; pertussis Adult dose 250 to 500 mg twice daily for 5 to 7 days; long-term CRS regimen 250 mg once daily (or twice daily) for 12 weeks Child dose 7.5 mg/kg twice daily (maximum 500 mg), or by weight band: 8 to 11 kg 62.5 mg, 12 to 19 kg 125 mg, 20 to 29 kg 187.5 mg, 30 to 40 kg 250 mg, twice daily Avoid QT prolongation, hypokalaemia; with simvastatin, lomitapide, colchicine in renal impairment, ergotamine, ticagrelor Pregnancy Avoid, especially in the first trimester, unless benefit clearly outweighs risk; erythromycin is preferred"],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s2-1","Antibacterial and anti-inflammatory action","Clarithromycin · How it works","At usual doses it stops bacterial protein synthesis at the 50S subunit. At low doses given for months, macrolides reduce interleukin-8 and neutrophil recruitment, reduce goblet-cell mucus secretion and interfere with quorum sensing and biofilms; this immunomodulatory effect, not killing, is the rationale in chronic rhinosinusitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s2-2","Handling","Clarithromycin · How it works","Well absorbed, concentrates in tissues and macrophages; metabolised by CYP3A4 to an active metabolite and excreted partly in urine. Halve the dose when the eGFR is below 30 ml/min."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s3-1","Acute infection in penicillin allergy","Clarithromycin · Using it in ENT","NICE lists clarithromycin (or erythromycin, and doxycycline in adults) as the alternative in penicillin allergy for acute otitis media, acute sinusitis and sore throat."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s3-2","Chronic rhinosinusitis","Clarithromycin · Using it in ENT","Long-term low-dose macrolides are considered for chronic rhinosinusitis without polyps with low IgE and neutrophilic inflammation, after maximal medical therapy, as in the EPOS and BRS guidance: 250 mg once daily for 12 weeks, continued only if it helps. Benefit is modest and less clear in eosinophilic polyposis. Check an ECG for QT and a statin and drug history before starting."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s3-3","Mycobacterial lymphadenitis","Clarithromycin · Using it in ENT","Non-tuberculous mycobacterial node infection in young children is best treated by complete excision ; where excision would risk the facial nerve, clarithromycin with rifampicin or rifabutin for 3 to 6 months is an alternative."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s4-1","Adverse effects","Clarithromycin · Safety","Nausea, diarrhoea, abdominal pain and a metallic taste (common). QT prolongation and torsades de pointes, especially with other QT-prolonging drugs or hypokalaemia. Reversible hearing loss and tinnitus at high doses. Hepatotoxicity; C. difficile; increased cardiovascular mortality was reported years after a course in patients with coronary heart disease, so use with caution in them."],["ev","cl:t-dr_clari~s4-2","Clarithromycin: current evidence","Clarithromycin · Safety","Heart. The US FDA warned in 2018 that clarithromycin may increase the long-term risk of cardiovascular events and death in patients with heart disease, based on the CLARICOR trial; consider alternatives in such patients. Interactions. It is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor: dangerous with simvastatin, colchicine, ticagrelor and some antiarrhythmics. Chronic rhinosinusitis. In the UK MACRO trial, three months of low-dose clarithromycin gave no benefit over placebo in persistent chronic rhinosinusitis (Philpott et al., Lancet 2025)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s4-2","Interactions","Clarithromycin · Safety","A strong CYP3A4 inhibitor : contraindicated with simvastatin (rhabdomyolysis), and raises levels of atorvastatin, ciclosporin, tacrolimus, carbamazepine, theophylline, midazolam, DOACs, calcium-channel blockers and warfarin (raised INR). Additive QT effects with antipsychotics, quinolones, ondansetron and antiarrhythmics."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s5-1","Viva essentials","Clarithromycin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The anti-inflammatory macrolide rationale and who benefits in chronic rhinosinusitis. The key interactions (statins, warfarin, QT drugs) and the ECG before long-term use. Management of atypical mycobacterial lymphadenitis in a child."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clari~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Clarithromycin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting long-term clarithromycin in a patient on simvastatin. No ECG before long-term therapy in a patient with cardiac disease or on QT drugs. Expecting long-term macrolides to shrink eosinophilic nasal polyps."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s1-1","Summary","Clindamycin · At a glance","Clindamycin Summary Class Lincosamide How it works Binds the 50S ribosomal subunit; bacteriostatic at usual doses; inhibits toxin synthesis Spectrum Streptococci, S. aureus (including some community MRSA), anaerobes (Bacteroides, Prevotella, Fusobacterium, Peptostreptococcus), Actinomyces; not Gram-negative aerobes (H. influenzae, Moraxella, Pseudomonas, Enterobacterales), not enterococci ENT uses Tonsillitis and quinsy in penicillin allergy; deep neck and dental infection; recurrent tonsillitis with beta-lactamase producing flora; necrotising fasciitis and toxic shock (with a penicillin); osteomyelitis of the jaw; surgical prophylaxis in penicillin allergy Adult dose Oral 150 to 300 mg four times daily (up to 450 mg four times daily); intravenous 600 mg to 1.2 g three or four times daily (up to 4.8 g a day) Child dose Oral 3 to 6 mg/kg four times daily (maximum 450 mg); intravenous 3.75 to 6.25 mg/kg four times daily (up to 10 mg/kg in severe infection) Avoid Previous C. difficile infection where possible; diarrhoea Pregnancy Use if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s2-1","Mechanism","Clindamycin · How it works","Clindamycin blocks peptide chain elongation at the 50S subunit, the same site as macrolides (so macrolide-resistant staphylococci may carry inducible clindamycin resistance, detected by the D-test). By stopping protein synthesis it reduces exotoxin production in streptococcal toxic shock and necrotising fasciitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s2-2","Handling","Clindamycin · How it works","Well absorbed orally; distributes widely into bone, saliva, abscess fluid and tissues but not into cerebrospinal fluid; hepatically metabolised."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s3-1","Throat and neck","Clindamycin · Using it in ENT","Quinsy and deep neck abscess in penicillin allergy : intravenous clindamycin, often alone (it covers the streptococci and anaerobes), with drainage. Recurrent tonsillitis with failure of penicillin: co-colonising beta-lactamase-producing anaerobes may protect streptococci; clindamycin or co-amoxiclav may work."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s3-2","Necrotising soft tissue infection","Clindamycin · Using it in ENT","In cervical necrotising fasciitis, clindamycin is added to a beta-lactam for its anti-toxin action, alongside urgent radical debridement."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s3-3","Surgical prophylaxis","Clindamycin · Using it in ENT","For penicillin-allergic patients in clean-contaminated head and neck surgery, intravenous clindamycin (600 mg) with or without gentamicin is used; prophylaxis stops within 24 hours."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s4-1","Adverse effects","Clindamycin · Safety","C. difficile colitis: clindamycin carries one of the highest risks of any antibiotic; stop it at the first sign of diarrhoea. Diarrhoea, nausea, metallic taste; oesophageal ulceration if capsules are swallowed without water. Rash, raised liver enzymes, blood dyscrasias."],["ev","cl:t-dr_clinda~s4-2","Clindamycin: current evidence","Clindamycin · Safety","C. difficile. Clindamycin carries one of the highest risks of C. difficile infection of any antibiotic; stop it if diarrhoea develops. Toxin suppression. It inhibits toxin production by streptococci and staphylococci, so it is added to a beta-lactam in necrotising fasciitis and toxic shock. Penicillin allergy. It remains an alternative for dental and deep neck infection, but local streptococcal resistance should guide its use."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s4-2","Interactions","Clindamycin · Safety","Potentiates neuromuscular blockers. Erythromycin antagonises it at the ribosome."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s5-1","Viva essentials","Clindamycin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Spectrum: Gram-positive and anaerobes, no Gram-negative aerobes . Why it is used in necrotising fasciitis (toxin suppression). C. difficile risk and patient warning."],["sec","cl:t-dr_clinda~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Clindamycin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using clindamycin alone where Haemophilus or Gram-negative cover is needed (sinusitis, otitis media). Continuing it in a patient with new diarrhoea."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s1-1","Summary","Co-amoxiclav · At a glance","Co-amoxiclav Summary Class Aminopenicillin with a beta-lactamase inhibitor How it works Amoxicillin blocks cell wall synthesis; clavulanic acid binds and inactivates beta-lactamase Spectrum As amoxicillin plus beta-lactamase producing H. influenzae, Moraxella, methicillin-sensitive S. aureus and anaerobes (Bacteroides, Prevotella, Fusobacterium); not Pseudomonas, not MRSA ENT uses Second-line acute otitis media; sinusitis in a systemically unwell patient; peritonsillar abscess and deep neck space infection; acute mastoiditis (with or after intravenous therapy); dental abscess; human and animal bites; prophylaxis in clean-contaminated head and neck surgery; sialadenitis Adult dose Oral 625 mg (500/125) three times daily; intravenous 1.2 g three times daily Child dose Oral 0.25 ml/kg of 125/31 suspension three times daily (1 month to 5 years) or 5 ml of 250/62 suspension three times daily (6 to 11 years), doubled for severe infection; intravenous 30 mg/kg three times daily (maximum 1.2 g) Duration Usually 5 to 7 days; longer for deep infection by clinical response Avoid Penicillin allergy; previous cholestatic jaundice with co-amoxiclav or a penicillin Pregnancy Generally safe; avoid near term in preterm rupture of membranes (necrotising enterocolitis)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s2-1","Mechanism","Co-amoxiclav · How it works","Clavulanic acid has little antibacterial action of its own; it is a suicide inhibitor of many class A beta-lactamases, so the amoxicillin survives to kill the organism. It does not inhibit the chromosomal AmpC enzymes of Enterobacter and Pseudomonas, so co-amoxiclav does not cover them."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s2-2","Absorption and handling","Co-amoxiclav · How it works","Both components are well absorbed and renally excreted; reduce the dose in severe renal impairment. Giving it with food improves the absorption of clavulanate and reduces nausea."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s3-1","Infection of the throat and neck","Co-amoxiclav · Using it in ENT","Peritonsillar abscess is a mixed aerobic-anaerobic infection: drain it, then give co-amoxiclav (or benzylpenicillin with metronidazole) intravenously until the patient can swallow, then orally to complete 7 to 10 days. In deep neck space infection , intravenous co-amoxiclav is a standard empirical choice, adding or changing for MRSA risk, diabetes or a hospital source."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s3-2","Ear and sinus","Co-amoxiclav · Using it in ENT","Second-line for acute otitis media that has not improved after 2 to 3 days of amoxicillin. For acute mastoiditis , an intravenous broad-spectrum regimen (co-amoxiclav, or ceftriaxone with metronidazole) is started with imaging and surgical review. For acute sinusitis in a patient who is systemically very unwell or at risk of complications."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s3-3","Surgery and wounds","Co-amoxiclav · Using it in ENT","Single-dose or 24-hour prophylaxis for clean-contaminated head and neck surgery (opening the pharynx), the choice for bites to the face and ear, and treatment of acute suppurative sialadenitis with hydration and massage."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s4-1","Adverse effects","Co-amoxiclav · Safety","Diarrhoea is more common than with amoxicillin; co-amoxiclav is one of the antibiotics most associated with C. difficile . Cholestatic jaundice and hepatitis : rare but up to six times more frequent than with amoxicillin, commoner in men over 65 and with courses over 14 days; it can appear up to 6 weeks after stopping. Rash, candidiasis, and the hypersensitivity of any penicillin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s4-2","Contraindications and cautions","Co-amoxiclav · Safety","Penicillin allergy. History of co-amoxiclav or penicillin-associated jaundice. Hepatic impairment: monitor liver function on longer courses."],["ev","cl:t-dr_coamox~s4-3","Co-amoxiclav: current evidence","Co-amoxiclav · Safety","Stewardship. NICE keeps co-amoxiclav as second line for otitis media and sinusitis, and for systemically unwell patients, because its broad spectrum drives Clostridioides difficile infection and resistance. Liver. Cholestatic jaundice is the commonest cause of drug-induced liver injury in Europe; it is more likely in men over 65 and with courses over 14 days, and may appear up to six weeks after stopping. Penicillin allergy. Most labels are false; low-risk patients can now be delabelled by a direct oral challenge (Copaescu et al., JAMA Intern Med 2023)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s4-3","Interactions","Co-amoxiclav · Safety","As amoxicillin: warfarin (monitor the INR), methotrexate (reduced excretion), and the oral contraceptive is no longer considered significantly affected."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s5-1","Viva essentials","Co-amoxiclav · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why clavulanate is added and which organisms it brings back into range. What it still does not cover: Pseudomonas, MRSA, atypicals . Its role in quinsy, deep neck infection, mastoiditis and bites . Cholestatic jaundice and C. difficile as the important harms."],["sec","cl:t-dr_coamox~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Co-amoxiclav · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using co-amoxiclav first-line where amoxicillin or penicillin V would do. Using it for necrotising otitis externa or a Pseudomonas perichondritis. Long courses without liver function checks. Forgetting that an abscess needs drainage, not a broader antibiotic."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cocaine~s1-1","Summary","Cocaine (topical) · At a glance","Cocaine (topical) Summary Class Ester local anaesthetic and sympathomimetic How it works Sodium-channel block plus inhibition of noradrenaline, dopamine and serotonin reuptake, causing vasoconstriction and central stimulation Preparations Topical solution 4% to 10%; paste 10% to 25%; Moffett's solution (classically 2 ml of 8% cocaine, 2 ml of 1% sodium bicarbonate and 1 ml of 1:1,000 adrenaline, applied on pledgets or as a spray, with the patient in the head-down position) ENT uses Topical anaesthesia and decongestion for septal, turbinate, sinus and nasal fracture surgery Maximum dose 1.5 mg/kg (many units limit the total to 100 mg in an adult) Avoid Ischaemic heart disease, hypertension, arrhythmia, hyperthyroidism, phaeochromocytoma, pregnancy; patients on MAOIs, tricyclics or non-selective beta blockers Legal status Schedule 2 controlled drug: record, store and dispose of it as required"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cocaine~s2-1","Application","Cocaine (topical) · Using it in ENT","Apply on pledgets or as a measured spray, keep within the dose limit, and allow 10 to 15 minutes. With Moffett's solution, adrenaline adds vasoconstriction and the bicarbonate improves absorption of the cocaine base."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cocaine~s2-2","Recognising misuse","Cocaine (topical) · Using it in ENT","Chronic intranasal cocaine use causes septal perforation, loss of the turbinates, palatal perforation and destruction of the midline (cocaine-induced midline destructive lesion), which can mimic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, including a positive ANCA (often PR3 or HNE). Ask about drug use, and be aware that levamisole adulteration causes vasculitis and neutropenia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cocaine~s3-1","Adverse effects","Cocaine (topical) · Safety","Tachycardia, hypertension, arrhythmia, coronary vasospasm, myocardial infarction, stroke, agitation, hyperthermia and seizures; with adrenaline, the risk is additive. Treat toxicity with benzodiazepines, cooling and avoidance of beta blockers (unopposed alpha stimulation)."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cocaine~s3-2","Cocaine (topical): current evidence","Cocaine (topical) · Safety","Alternatives. Many units have replaced topical cocaine with lidocaine–phenylephrine (co-phenylcaine) or xylometazoline with lidocaine, which give comparable decongestion and anaesthesia for clinic procedures without the cardiac risk or controlled-drug paperwork. Safety. Reported deaths and myocardial ischaemia after topical cocaine are linked to high doses and to combination with adrenaline; keep within the unit's maximum dose and do not combine it with adrenaline-soaked packs."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cocaine~s3-2","Interactions","Cocaine (topical) · Safety","MAOIs, tricyclics, adrenaline and other sympathomimetics; non-selective beta blockers; halothane (arrhythmias)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cocaine~s4-1","Viva essentials","Cocaine (topical) · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism of vasoconstriction (reuptake inhibition). Moffett's solution and its components. The maximum dose and cardiovascular contraindications. Cocaine-induced midline destructive lesion versus GPA."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cocaine~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Cocaine (topical) · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving a beta blocker to treat cocaine-induced hypertension. Forgetting controlled drug procedures."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cophen~s1-1","Summary","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia · At a glance","Co-phenylcaine Summary Composition Lidocaine hydrochloride 5% and phenylephrine hydrochloride 0.5% (each spray about 5 mg lidocaine and 0.5 mg phenylephrine) How it works Sodium-channel block (lidocaine) and alpha-1 vasoconstriction (phenylephrine) ENT uses Rigid and flexible nasendoscopy; cautery for epistaxis; biopsy; nasal foreign body removal in older children and adults; packing removal; preparation for flexible laryngoscopy (with a throat spray) Dose Adults and children over 12: up to 8 sprays in total (commonly 2 to 5 sprays into each nostril); children 8 to 12: up to 5 sprays; wait about 5 to 10 minutes Avoid Under 8 years; MAOIs within 2 weeks; severe hypertension or ischaemic heart disease (caution); hyperthyroidism"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cophen~s2-1","Technique","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia · Using it in ENT","Spray with the patient's head slightly forward, aiming along the floor and toward the middle meatus; wait at least 5 minutes. Soaked cotton pledgets placed under the inferior and middle turbinates give deeper anaesthesia for cautery or biopsy. Warn the patient about the bitter taste and the numb throat, and advise nothing to eat or drink until sensation returns (about an hour) if the spray has reached the pharynx."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cophen~s2-2","Alternatives","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia · Using it in ENT","Lidocaine spray alone with xylometazoline, or for children, topical anaesthetic gel or a short examination without anaesthesia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cophen~s3-1","Adverse effects","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia · Safety","Bitter taste, numb throat, occasional palpitations and hypertension; vasovagal reactions during procedures; local anaesthetic toxicity if excess is used, especially in children."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cophen~s3-2","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia: current evidence","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia · Safety","Evidence. Topical lidocaine with phenylephrine reduces discomfort during nasal endoscopy and nasogastric tube insertion in randomised trials, though for a quick flexible nasendoscopy many patients tolerate the procedure equally well without it. Children. Avoid under eight years; for younger children a decongestant alone, or a carefully dosed lidocaine spray, is used."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cophen~s3-2","Interactions","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia · Safety","MAOIs, tricyclics and beta blockers (phenylephrine)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cophen~s4-1","Viva essentials","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Composition and maximum sprays. Advice after use (numb throat, aspiration risk)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cophen~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Co-phenylcaine and topical nasal anaesthesia · For the examination","Common pitfalls Exceeding the spray limit in a small child."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s1-1","Summary","Co-trimoxazole · At a glance","Co-trimoxazole Summary Class Sulfonamide with a dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor How it works Sulfamethoxazole blocks dihydropteroate synthase; trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase; synergistic and bactericidal Spectrum S. aureus including many MRSA, H. influenzae, Moraxella, Enterobacterales, Stenotrophomonas, Pneumocystis jirovecii, Nocardia; streptococci variable; not Pseudomonas, not anaerobes ENT uses Localised (limited) granulomatosis with polyangiitis, adjunct to maintenance; Pneumocystis prophylaxis during high-dose steroids, cyclophosphamide or rituximab; MRSA skin and soft tissue infection; Nocardia Adult dose 960 mg twice daily for treatment; 960 mg twice daily in GPA maintenance (some use 480 mg twice daily); Pneumocystis prophylaxis 480 mg once daily or 960 mg three times a week Child dose 24 mg/kg twice daily for treatment; prophylaxis by body surface area on specialist advice Avoid Folate deficiency, blood disorders, severe liver or kidney disease, porphyria, first trimester pregnancy, G6PD deficiency (haemolysis)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s2-1","Mechanism","Co-trimoxazole · How it works","Bacteria must make their own folate; blocking two steps in the pathway gives synergy. Human dihydrofolate reductase is far less sensitive to trimethoprim, but toxicity rises in folate-deficient patients and with methotrexate."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s2-2","Handling","Co-trimoxazole · How it works","Well absorbed; renally excreted, so the dose is reduced and potassium monitored in renal impairment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s3-1","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Co-trimoxazole · Using it in ENT","In limited sinonasal GPA, co-trimoxazole may help maintain remission and reduce relapses, possibly by eradicating nasal S. aureus; it does not replace immunosuppression in generalised disease. Patients on rituximab or cyclophosphamide receive it as Pneumocystis prophylaxis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s4-1","Adverse effects","Co-trimoxazole · Safety","Rash, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (stop at the first rash). Bone marrow suppression (leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, megaloblastic anaemia), especially in the elderly and folate deficient. Hyperkalaemia (trimethoprim acts like amiloride), raised creatinine without a true fall in renal function. Hepatitis, aseptic meningitis, haemolysis in G6PD deficiency."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s4-2","Co-trimoxazole: current evidence","Co-trimoxazole · Safety","GPA. In a randomised trial, co-trimoxazole 960 mg twice daily reduced relapses of granulomatosis with polyangiitis in remission (Stegeman et al., NEJM 1996), and it is used as Pneumocystis prophylaxis during rituximab or cyclophosphamide treatment. Safety. It causes hyperkalaemia (especially with ACE inhibitors or in renal impairment), rises in creatinine, marrow suppression, and severe skin reactions; it increases warfarin and methotrexate toxicity."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s4-2","Interactions","Co-trimoxazole · Safety","Methotrexate (severe, potentially fatal marrow toxicity), warfarin (raised INR), ACE inhibitors, spironolactone and other potassium-raising drugs, phenytoin, ciclosporin, azathioprine."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s5-1","Viva essentials","Co-trimoxazole · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Its role in GPA : adjunct in limited disease and Pneumocystis prophylaxis. Haematological, skin and potassium monitoring. The methotrexate and warfarin interactions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cotrim~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Co-trimoxazole · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using co-trimoxazole for routine sinusitis or tonsillitis. Combining it with methotrexate. Ignoring hyperkalaemia in a patient on an ACE inhibitor."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cromo~s1-1","Summary","Sodium cromoglicate · At a glance","Sodium cromoglicate Summary Class Mast-cell stabiliser (cromone) How it works Inhibits mast-cell degranulation, possibly through chloride channel effects ENT uses Prophylaxis of seasonal or exposure-related allergic rhinitis (pollen, animals); allergic conjunctivitis; patients wishing to avoid steroids, especially in pregnancy and children Dose Nasal spray 4%: 1 spray into each nostril two to four times daily (up to six times); eye drops 2%: 1 to 2 drops four times daily Pregnancy Considered safe"],["sec","cl:t-dr_cromo~s2-1","Place in treatment","Sodium cromoglicate · Using it in ENT","Less effective than nasal steroids; useful for short, predictable exposures when started a week before the season or taken shortly before contact. It has no effect on symptoms once they have started."],["ev","cl:t-dr_cromo~s3-1","Sodium cromoglicate: current evidence","Sodium cromoglicate · Safety","Place in treatment. Cromoglicate is less effective than intranasal steroids and antihistamines and must be used four times daily, so ARIA reserves it for patients who cannot or will not use other drugs, such as in pregnancy, or for prevention just before a known allergen exposure. Eye drops remain useful for allergic conjunctivitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cromo~s3-1","Adverse effects","Sodium cromoglicate · Safety","Local irritation, transient sneezing; very rarely bronchospasm."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cromo~s4-1","Viva essentials","Sodium cromoglicate · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism and why it is preventive only. Where it fits (pregnancy, children, predictable exposure)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_cromo~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Sodium cromoglicate · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using cromoglicate to relieve established symptoms."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ddavp~s1-1","Summary","Desmopressin · At a glance","Desmopressin Summary Class Synthetic vasopressin (V2 receptor) analogue How it works Stimulates release of von Willebrand factor and factor VIII from endothelial Weibel-Palade bodies; also antidiuretic ENT uses Prophylaxis and treatment of bleeding in type 1 von Willebrand disease, mild haemophilia A and carriers, uraemic and drug-induced platelet dysfunction; tonsillectomy, adenoidectomy and other surgery in these patients Dose 0.3 micrograms/kg intravenously or subcutaneously (maximum about 20 to 27 micrograms) over 20 to 30 minutes, 30 to 60 minutes before surgery, repeated every 12 to 24 hours for a few doses; intranasal 300 micrograms (adults) as an alternative Effectiveness A test dose beforehand shows whether the patient responds; tachyphylaxis after 2 to 3 doses Avoid Type 2B von Willebrand disease (thrombocytopenia); children under 2 years (hyponatraemic seizures); cardiovascular disease; fluid overload; habitual high fluid intake"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ddavp~s2-1","Tonsillectomy in bleeding disorders","Desmopressin · Using it in ENT","Screen for bleeding disorders by history (family history, easy bruising, prolonged bleeding after dental extraction, menorrhagia); routine clotting tests miss mild von Willebrand disease. Plan surgery with haematology: desmopressin before surgery, tranexamic acid for 7 to 10 days, overnight admission, fluid restriction and sodium checks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ddavp~s3-1","Adverse effects","Desmopressin · Safety","Hyponatraemia and water intoxication (fluid restriction to about two thirds of maintenance for 24 hours, sodium monitoring, particularly in children); facial flushing, headache, tachycardia, hypotension; rarely thrombosis."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ddavp~s3-2","Desmopressin: current evidence","Desmopressin · Safety","Hyponatraemia. Desmopressin causes water retention; restrict fluids to about maintenance for 24 hours after each dose and check sodium, especially in children and the elderly, because hyponatraemic seizures have occurred. Tachyphylaxis limits repeated doses to about three. Von Willebrand disease. The 2021 international guideline recommends a trial of desmopressin with factor levels to confirm response before relying on it for surgery, and tranexamic acid alongside for mucosal procedures such as tonsillectomy (Connell et al., Blood Adv 2021)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ddavp~s3-2","Interactions","Desmopressin · Safety","Drugs that increase antidiuretic hormone effect or cause hyponatraemia (SSRIs, carbamazepine, NSAIDs, opioids)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ddavp~s4-1","Viva essentials","Desmopressin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism (endothelial release of von Willebrand factor and factor VIII). Use in tonsillectomy with bleeding disorders. Hyponatraemia and fluid restriction."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ddavp~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Desmopressin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using desmopressin in type 2B von Willebrand disease. Free intravenous fluids after desmopressin in a child."],["sec","cl:t-dr_decon~s1-1","Summary","Topical nasal decongestants · At a glance","Topical nasal decongestants Summary Agents Xylometazoline 0.1% (adults) and 0.05% (children); oxymetazoline 0.05%; phenylephrine (in combinations) How they work Alpha-1 and alpha-2 adrenoceptor agonists: venous sinusoid constriction in the turbinates ENT uses Short-term relief of congestion in colds and acute sinusitis; preparation for nasendoscopy and procedures (often with lidocaine); epistaxis (on pledgets); before nasal surgery; Eustachian tube symptoms in air travel; testing whether obstruction is mucosal (shrinks) or structural (does not) Adult dose Xylometazoline 0.1%, 1 to 2 sprays into each nostril up to three times daily, for no more than 7 days Child dose In the UK, not recommended under 6 years; 6 to 11 years xylometazoline 0.05%, 1 spray up to twice daily for up to 5 days Avoid Within 2 weeks of an MAOI; after trans-sphenoidal surgery; dry rhinitis; narrow-angle glaucoma; caution in hypertension, heart disease, hyperthyroidism"],["sec","cl:t-dr_decon~s2-1","Mechanism and rebound","Topical nasal decongestants · How they work","They constrict the capacitance vessels of the turbinates. With repeated use, receptors downregulate and the mucosa congests again as the effect wears off ( rebound ), prompting more use. After about 5 to 10 days this becomes rhinitis medicamentosa : persistent congestion, a red, swollen, granular mucosa, and dependence on the spray."],["sec","cl:t-dr_decon~s3-1","In the clinic and theatre","Topical nasal decongestants · Using them in ENT","Applied with lidocaine as a spray or on cotton pledgets, they improve the view and comfort of nasendoscopy and procedures. In theatre, oxymetazoline or adrenaline pledgets reduce bleeding in endoscopic sinus surgery. In epistaxis , a pledget with decongestant before cautery is standard first aid."],["sec","cl:t-dr_decon~s3-2","Treating rhinitis medicamentosa","Topical nasal decongestants · Using them in ENT","Stop the spray (one nostril at a time if needed), cover the withdrawal with a nasal corticosteroid for several weeks, and sometimes a short course of oral prednisolone; treat the original cause of congestion."],["sec","cl:t-dr_decon~s4-1","Adverse effects","Topical nasal decongestants · Safety","Rebound congestion and rhinitis medicamentosa. Local stinging and dryness. Systemic absorption: hypertension, palpitations and, in infants and young children, CNS depression and bradycardia (hence the age limits)."],["ev","cl:t-dr_decon~s4-2","Topical nasal decongestants: current evidence","Topical nasal decongestants · Safety","Rebound. Use beyond about seven to ten days causes rebound congestion ( rhinitis medicamentosa ); stopping, with an intranasal steroid to cover the withdrawal, is the treatment. Children. The UK MHRA advised in 2009 against over-the-counter decongestants in children under six. Short-term use is still helpful for acute sinusitis, Eustachian tube problems before flying, and to improve access for a nasal steroid spray."],["sec","cl:t-dr_decon~s4-2","Interactions","Topical nasal decongestants · Safety","MAOIs (hypertensive crisis), tricyclics, beta blockers (reflex hypertension with phenylephrine)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_decon~s5-1","Viva essentials","Topical nasal decongestants · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism of rebound and rhinitis medicamentosa, and how to treat it. Decongestion as a test of mucosal versus structural obstruction. Age limits in children."],["sec","cl:t-dr_decon~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Topical nasal decongestants · For the examination","Common pitfalls Allowing patients to use decongestant sprays for weeks. Using them in infants."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s1-1","Summary","Dexamethasone · At a glance","Dexamethasone Summary Class Long-acting glucocorticoid (about 25 times the anti-inflammatory potency of hydrocortisone, biological half-life 36 to 54 hours) How it works Glucocorticoid receptor agonist; no salt retention ENT uses Croup; airway oedema before extubation and after airway surgery; angioedema (allergic, as an adjunct); peritonsillar abscess (single dose); tonsillectomy (nausea and pain); post-operative oedema after facial and thyroid surgery; bacterial meningitis (with the first antibiotic); intratympanic treatment; cerebral oedema Adult dose Airway oedema commonly 6 to 8 mg intravenously every 8 hours for 2 to 3 doses; nausea prophylaxis 4 to 8 mg at induction; quinsy 8 to 10 mg once; meningitis 10 mg every 6 hours for 4 days Child dose Croup 150 micrograms/kg orally once (up to 600 micrograms/kg in severe croup); tonsillectomy 150 micrograms/kg intravenously (maximum 8 to 10 mg); meningitis 150 micrograms/kg every 6 hours for 4 days Pregnancy Crosses the placenta (unlike prednisolone); use when indicated"],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s2-1","Mechanism","Dexamethasone · How it works","It reduces capillary permeability, mucosal oedema and cytokine release. Its long action means one dose covers the risk period of a tonsillectomy or an extubation. Its central antiemetic effect is not fully understood."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s3-1","Croup and the airway","Dexamethasone · Using it in ENT","A single oral dose of dexamethasone for croup reduces return visits, admissions and intubation, even in mild disease; nebulised adrenaline is added for severe stridor. Before extubation after prolonged intubation or airway surgery, steroids reduce post-extubation stridor in high-risk patients."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s3-2","Tonsillectomy","Dexamethasone · Using it in ENT","A single intravenous dose reduces post-operative vomiting and improves early oral intake, and is recommended by the AAO-HNS guideline; it has not been convincingly shown to increase bleeding."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s3-3","Bacterial meningitis","Dexamethasone · Using it in ENT","Dexamethasone given just before or with the first antibiotic dose reduces hearing loss and neurological sequelae in pneumococcal and Haemophilus meningitis; it is not started more than about 4 to 12 hours after antibiotics."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s3-4","Intratympanic use","Dexamethasone · Using it in ENT","Dexamethasone 10 to 24 mg/ml is injected through the drum for sudden hearing loss and Ménière's disease (see intratympanic steroids)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s4-1","Adverse effects","Dexamethasone · Safety","Hyperglycaemia (even after a single dose in diabetics), insomnia, agitation, perineal burning or itch with rapid intravenous injection. Long courses carry all glucocorticoid harms, with marked adrenal suppression. Tumour lysis syndrome in undiagnosed lymphoma: avoid steroids before tissue diagnosis when lymphoma is suspected (they can also make the biopsy non-diagnostic)."],["ev","cl:t-dr_dexa~s4-2","Dexamethasone: current evidence","Dexamethasone · Safety","Tonsillectomy. The American tonsillectomy guideline recommends a single intraoperative dose of dexamethasone in children, which reduces vomiting and speeds return to diet (Mitchell et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019; Cochrane, Steward et al., 2011); an early report of more bleeding has not been confirmed in later meta-analyses. Croup. A single oral dose works, and 0.15 mg/kg is as effective as higher doses (Cochrane, Gates et al., 2018). Meningitis. Dexamethasone with the first antibiotic dose reduces death and hearing loss in pneumococcal meningitis (de Gans and van de Beek, NEJM 2002)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s4-2","Interactions","Dexamethasone · Safety","Enzyme inducers (rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine) reduce its effect; it reduces the effect of some drugs metabolised by CYP3A4."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s5-1","Viva essentials","Dexamethasone · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Dexamethasone in croup : dose and evidence. Timing in bacterial meningitis and why ENT surgeons care (hearing). The tonsillectomy guideline position. Why steroids before a lymph node biopsy can mislead."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dexa~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Dexamethasone · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving dexamethasone to a child with a suspected lymphoma before the biopsy. Forgetting glucose checks in diabetics."],["sec","cl:t-dr_diur~s1-1","Summary","Diuretics in Ménière's disease · At a glance","Diuretics in Ménière's disease Summary Agents Bendroflumethiazide; hydrochlorothiazide with triamterene or amiloride; acetazolamide (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor) How they work Thiazides block the Na-Cl cotransporter in the distal tubule; acetazolamide reduces bicarbonate reabsorption and may reduce endolymph production; both proposed to reduce hydrops ENT uses Maintenance treatment in Ménière's disease to reduce vertigo attacks (alongside salt restriction); acetazolamide also in idiopathic intracranial hypertension with pulsatile tinnitus, and in some CSF leaks Adult dose Bendroflumethiazide 2.5 mg once daily; hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg with triamterene once daily; acetazolamide 250 mg twice daily (higher in intracranial hypertension) Avoid Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, gout, severe renal or hepatic impairment; acetazolamide in sulfonamide allergy, renal stones, metabolic acidosis"],["sec","cl:t-dr_diur~s2-1","Ménière's disease","Diuretics in Ménière's disease · Using them in ENT","Offered after lifestyle measures, often with or instead of betahistine; review at 3 to 6 months. Check sodium, potassium and renal function before and periodically. Salt restriction (about 1.5 to 2 g sodium a day) is part of the same strategy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_diur~s2-2","Pulsatile tinnitus and intracranial hypertension","Diuretics in Ménière's disease · Using them in ENT","Acetazolamide lowers CSF production in idiopathic intracranial hypertension , which presents with pulsatile tinnitus, headache, obesity and papilloedema; involve neurology and ophthalmology."],["sec","cl:t-dr_diur~s3-1","Adverse effects","Diuretics in Ménière's disease · Safety","Thiazides: hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperuricaemia and gout, hyperglycaemia, postural hypotension, erectile dysfunction, photosensitivity. Acetazolamide: paraesthesia, taste change (carbonated drinks taste flat), fatigue, metabolic acidosis, renal stones, rarely blood dyscrasias and Stevens-Johnson syndrome."],["ev","cl:t-dr_diur~s3-2","Diuretics in Ménière's disease: current evidence","Diuretics in Ménière's disease · Safety","Ménière's disease. The American guideline lists diuretics and dietary salt restriction as options for maintenance (Basura et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020), but systematic reviews find the evidence for diuretics and for salt restriction weak and very uncertain. They remain widely used because they are cheap and generally safe; check sodium and potassium. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension. Acetazolamide with weight loss improved vision in a randomised trial (NORDIC IIH, JAMA 2014), relevant to spontaneous CSF leaks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_diur~s3-2","Interactions","Diuretics in Ménière's disease · Safety","Lithium (toxicity), NSAIDs (reduced effect), digoxin (toxicity if hypokalaemic)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_diur~s4-1","Viva essentials","Diuretics in Ménière's disease · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The rationale and the weak evidence. Monitoring of electrolytes. Acetazolamide in idiopathic intracranial hypertension with pulsatile tinnitus."],["sec","cl:t-dr_diur~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Diuretics in Ménière's disease · For the examination","Common pitfalls No electrolyte checks in an elderly patient on a thiazide."],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s1-1","Summary","Doxycycline · At a glance","Doxycycline Summary Class Tetracycline How it works Binds the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks tRNA attachment; bacteriostatic; also inhibits matrix metalloproteinases Spectrum Streptococci (variable), S. aureus including some community MRSA, H. influenzae, Moraxella, atypicals, Borrelia, Rickettsia, Chlamydia, Actinomyces ENT uses Acute sinusitis in penicillin allergy (adults); Lyme disease and Lyme facial palsy; atypical pneumonia; acne-associated and rosacea conditions; MRSA skin infection; doxycycline sclerotherapy of macrocystic lymphatic malformations; long-term anti-inflammatory use in nasal polyps (a short course reduces polyp size) Adult dose 200 mg on day 1 then 100 mg daily (sinusitis: total 5 days); Lyme disease 100 mg twice daily or 200 mg once daily for 21 days (28 days for some neurological presentations); nasal polyps 200 mg then 100 mg daily for 20 days in trials Child dose Not under 12 years (except in specific severe infections such as Lyme or rickettsial disease on specialist advice); 12 to 17 years as adult Avoid Children under 12, pregnancy and breastfeeding; with antacids, iron, calcium or milk at the same time"],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s2-1","Mechanism","Doxycycline · How it works","It blocks aminoacyl-tRNA binding to the ribosome. The anti-matrix metalloproteinase and anti-inflammatory effects explain its use in rosacea and the reduction in polyp size and eosinophilic inflammatory mediators shown in polyposis trials."],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s2-2","Handling","Doxycycline · How it works","Well absorbed; absorption is reduced by divalent and trivalent cations (antacids, calcium, iron, zinc, milk). Excreted in bile and faeces, so no renal dose adjustment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s3-1","Lyme disease","Doxycycline · Using it in ENT","Lyme neuroborreliosis may present with isolated, sometimes bilateral, facial palsy in endemic areas. Treat with oral doxycycline for 21 days (amoxicillin or ceftriaxone are alternatives); steroids are not recommended for Lyme facial palsy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s3-2","Lymphatic malformations","Doxycycline · Using it in ENT","Interventional radiologists inject doxycycline (typically 10 to 20 mg/ml) into macrocystic lymphatic malformations; it causes inflammation and fibrosis of the cyst walls. Swelling after the injection can compromise an airway in neck and tongue lesions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s4-1","Adverse effects","Doxycycline · Safety","Oesophageal ulceration : take with a full glass of water, sitting or standing, not just before bed. Photosensitivity : warn about sun exposure. Nausea; candidiasis; benign intracranial hypertension (stop it if headache and visual symptoms appear). Permanent tooth discolouration and enamel hypoplasia in children; deposition in growing bone."],["ev","cl:t-dr_doxy~s4-2","Doxycycline: current evidence","Doxycycline · Safety","Nasal polyps. In a randomised trial, doxycycline for three weeks reduced polyp size modestly for up to twelve weeks, with effects distinct from those of oral steroids (Van Zele et al., J Allergy Clin Immunol 2010). Lyme disease. NICE (NG95, 2018) recommends doxycycline for 21 days for erythema migrans and for Lyme facial palsy. Children. Short courses do not stain teeth in modern studies, and doxycycline is now acceptable at any age for serious rickettsial infection, but UK licensing still restricts routine use under 12."],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s4-2","Interactions","Doxycycline · Safety","Absorption falls with antacids, iron, calcium and zinc (separate by 2 to 3 hours); it potentiates warfarin; carbamazepine, phenytoin and rifampicin increase its metabolism."],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s5-1","Viva essentials","Doxycycline · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why it is avoided in children under 12 and in pregnancy. Lyme disease as a cause of facial palsy and its treatment. Advice for taking it safely (water, upright, sun, antacids)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_doxy~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Doxycycline · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing doxycycline to a child with sinusitis. Taking it with milk or calcium supplements. Giving steroids for Lyme facial palsy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dupi~s1-1","Summary","Dupilumab · At a glance","Dupilumab Summary Class Monoclonal antibody against IL-4 receptor alpha (blocks IL-4 and IL-13) How it works Inhibits type 2 inflammation: B-cell class switching to IgE, eosinophil recruitment, goblet cell hyperplasia and mucosal oedema ENT uses Severe chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps (in many countries as add-on to nasal steroids); also atopic dermatitis, asthma, eosinophilic oesophagitis Adult dose 300 mg subcutaneously every 2 weeks for polyps (interval may be extended once controlled) Eligibility (EPOS 2020) Bilateral polyps after previous surgery (or unfit for it) plus at least three of: type 2 inflammation (blood eosinophils of 150 per microlitre or more, tissue eosinophils of 10 or more per high-power field, or total IgE of 100 or more), need for systemic steroids (2 or more courses a year or long-term), significantly impaired quality of life (SNOT-22 of 40 or more), anosmia, comorbid asthma Response Assess at 16 weeks and 1 year: polyp size, need for steroids, quality of life, smell, asthma Pregnancy Limited data; use only if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_dupi~s2-1","Mechanism","Dupilumab · How it works","IL-4 and IL-13 share the IL-4 receptor alpha chain. Blocking it reduces IgE production, eosinophil trafficking through eotaxins and VCAM-1, mucus hypersecretion and tissue remodelling. Blood eosinophils may rise transiently because their tissue recruitment is blocked."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dupi~s3-1","Who gets it","Dupilumab · Using it in ENT","Patients with severe, type 2 polyposis who still have uncontrolled disease after appropriate surgery and continued nasal steroids and saline, especially with asthma or aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease. National reimbursement criteria vary: in some health systems access is limited."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dupi~s3-2","Monitoring","Dupilumab · Using it in ENT","Nasal endoscopy with polyp score, SNOT-22, smell testing and steroid use at baseline, 16 weeks and annually. Stop if there is no meaningful response."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dupi~s4-1","Adverse effects","Dupilumab · Safety","Conjunctivitis and keratitis (more in atopic dermatitis). Injection-site reactions, arthralgia, transient eosinophilia. Rarely eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis becomes apparent when oral steroids are reduced; check for vasculitic symptoms."],["ev","cl:t-dr_dupi~s4-2","Dupilumab: current evidence","Dupilumab · Safety","Trials. In SINUS-24 and SINUS-52, dupilumab reduced polyp size, blockage and the need for steroids or surgery, and improved smell, in severe chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps (Bachert et al., Lancet 2019). In the head-to-head EVEREST trial in patients with polyps and asthma, dupilumab was superior to omalizumab for polyp size and smell (De Corso et al., Lancet Respir Med 2025). Selection. EPOS and EUFOREA criteria reserve biologics for bilateral polyps after sinus surgery (or unfit for it) with at least three of: type 2 inflammation, need for systemic steroids, poor quality of life, loss of smell, or asthma. Side effects: conjunctivitis and transient eosinophilia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dupi~s4-2","Interactions and vaccines","Dupilumab · Safety","Avoid live vaccines during treatment; treat helminth infection before starting."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dupi~s5-1","Viva essentials","Dupilumab · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The type 2 pathway and the target. EPOS criteria for biologics in polyposis. How you assess response."],["sec","cl:t-dr_dupi~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Dupilumab · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting a biologic without optimising nasal steroids and considering surgery. Missing EGPA when steroids are withdrawn."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eacid~s1-1","Summary","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations · At a glance","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations Summary Preparations Acetic acid 2% spray or drops (alone or with a steroid); aluminium acetate 8% to 13% solution (Burow's solution), often on a wick; boric acid powder (insufflation) or solution; spirit (alcohol) drops for prevention How they work Lower the canal pH, are bacteriostatic and antifungal, and dry and shrink inflamed skin ENT uses Mild acute otitis externa; prevention in swimmers and recurrent otitis externa; wet, discharging mastoid cavities; adjunct in otomycosis Dose Acetic acid spray, one spray three times daily for up to 7 days (until 2 days after symptoms resolve) Avoid Perforated drum for acetic acid and alcohol drops (pain and possible ototoxicity)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_eacid~s2-1","Mechanism","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations · How they work","Acid inhibits Pseudomonas and staphylococci, astringents precipitate proteins and reduce oedema and exudate, and boric acid both dries and acidifies."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eacid~s3-1","Mild otitis externa and prevention","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations · Using them in ENT","Acetic acid spray is an over-the-counter first step in mild otitis externa, with results close to antibiotic-steroid drops in the first week but less effective with longer courses. Swimmers with recurrent infection can use acidifying or alcohol drops after swimming."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eacid~s3-2","Cavities","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations · Using them in ENT","Insufflating boric acid powder after cleaning a wet mastoid cavity helps keep it dry; repeated sessions may be needed."],["ev","cl:t-dr_eacid~s4-1","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations: current evidence","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations · Safety","Guideline. The American guideline on acute otitis externa recommends topical drops as first-line treatment, aural toilet when debris blocks the canal, and no systemic antibiotics unless infection spreads beyond the canal or the patient is immunocompromised (Rosenfeld et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2014). Acetic acid. A Cochrane review found acetic acid drops roughly as effective as antibiotic–steroid drops in the first week, but less effective when treatment had to continue beyond a week (Kaushik et al., 2010). It remains useful for mild cases and for prevention in swimmers."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eacid~s4-1","Adverse effects","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations · Safety","Stinging and burning (especially in an inflamed canal), and irritation through a perforation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eacid~s5-1","Viva essentials","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The normal pH of the canal and why acidification helps. Where acidifiers fit in otitis externa and in mastoid cavity care."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eacid~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Acidifying and astringent ear preparations · For the examination","Common pitfalls Acetic acid or alcohol drops in an ear with a perforation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eaf~s1-1","Summary","Antifungal ear preparations · At a glance","Antifungal ear preparations Summary Preparations Clotrimazole 1% solution (first-line); miconazole or econazole creams; boric acid powder or solution; ciclopirox (where available); systemic itraconazole or voriconazole for invasive disease How they work Azoles block ergosterol synthesis in the fungal membrane (lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase); boric acid acidifies and is fungistatic ENT uses Otomycosis; fungal infection in mastoid cavities; prophylaxis in patients prone to recurrence Dose Clotrimazole 2 to 3 drops two or three times daily, continued for at least 14 days after clinical resolution Pregnancy Topical use acceptable"],["sec","cl:t-dr_eaf~s2-1","Mechanism","Antifungal ear preparations · How they work","Azoles deplete ergosterol and accumulate toxic sterols in the fungal membrane. They are active against Candida and Aspergillus, although Aspergillus is less sensitive to clotrimazole than to the newer triazoles."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eaf~s3-1","Treating otomycosis","Antifungal ear preparations · Using them in ENT","Clean the canal under the microscope: remove the fungal debris and spores by microsuction, repeated at intervals. Drops alone into a canal full of hyphae fail. Clotrimazole 1% drops for at least two weeks after the canal looks clean; stop the antibacterial drops that caused it. Keep the ear dry ; treat underlying eczema; control diabetes. With a perforation , clotrimazole has not been shown to be ototoxic and is commonly used; avoid gentian violet and alcohol-based preparations, which are irritant and potentially ototoxic."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eaf~s3-2","When to think further","Antifungal ear preparations · Using them in ENT","Persistent or recurrent fungal infection in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient, with pain and granulation, may be invasive fungal necrotising otitis externa (usually Aspergillus): biopsy and treat systemically with voriconazole."],["ev","cl:t-dr_eaf~s4-1","Antifungal ear preparations: current evidence","Antifungal ear preparations · Safety","Otomycosis. Thorough aural toilet under the microscope matters as much as the drug. Small trials suggest that azoles such as clotrimazole clear infection better than older antiseptics, and clotrimazole has not been shown to be ototoxic, so it is the usual choice when the drum is perforated. Prevention. Otomycosis often follows prolonged antibiotic–steroid drops, so limit their duration and review ears that are not settling."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eaf~s4-1","Adverse effects","Antifungal ear preparations · Safety","Local irritation and burning; rarely contact sensitivity."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eaf~s5-1","Viva essentials","Antifungal ear preparations · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Organisms and appearance (Aspergillus niger, Candida). The essential role of aural toilet . Duration of treatment and what to do with a perforation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eaf~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Antifungal ear preparations · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating otomycosis with more antibiotic drops. Stopping clotrimazole as soon as symptoms settle."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eamg~s1-1","Summary","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · At a glance","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops Summary Preparations Gentamicin 0.3% with hydrocortisone 1%; neomycin with polymyxin B and hydrocortisone; neomycin with dexamethasone and acetic acid spray; framycetin combinations (availability varies) How they work Aminoglycoside protein-synthesis inhibition, polymyxin membrane disruption, steroid anti-inflammatory action Spectrum Pseudomonas and staphylococci (gentamicin); neomycin weak against Pseudomonas, polymyxin added for it; no antifungal activity ENT uses Acute otitis externa with an intact drum; eczematous otitis externa with superadded infection; granulation tissue Dose Typically 2 to 4 drops three or four times daily (sprays one spray three times daily) for 7 days Avoid Perforated drum or grommet where a safer alternative exists; known neomycin allergy Pregnancy Low systemic absorption from an intact canal"],["sec","cl:t-dr_eamg~s2-1","Mechanism","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · How they work","Topical aminoglycosides reach very high concentrations in the canal. In the middle ear they diffuse through the round window membrane into the perilymph and damage hair cells, as systemic aminoglycosides do. The risk depends on the dose, the duration, and the state of the middle ear mucosa: inflamed, thickened mucosa may protect the round window, which is why toxicity is uncommon in actively discharging ears and more likely in a dry ear with a perforation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eamg~s3-1","With an intact drum","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · Using them in ENT","They are a reasonable choice for acute otitis externa when the drum is seen and intact."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eamg~s3-2","With a perforation or a tube","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · Using them in ENT","Guidance from ENT UK accepts that aminoglycoside drops may be used with caution in a discharging ear with a perforation when the benefit outweighs the risk, for no longer than 2 weeks , and with the patient warned; but quinolone drops avoid the dilemma and are preferred. Do not use them in a dry perforation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eamg~s4-1","Adverse effects","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · Safety","Ototoxicity : sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus and vestibular failure if they reach the inner ear. Contact dermatitis : neomycin sensitivity is common in chronic otitis externa (patch tests are positive in a substantial proportion); a red, weeping, itchy canal that worsens on treatment suggests it. Otomycosis with prolonged use; steroid-related local effects."],["ev","cl:t-dr_eamg~s4-2","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops: current evidence","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · Safety","Ototoxicity. Aminoglycoside drops reaching the middle ear through a perforation can cause sensorineural loss and vestibular failure, more often in a dry ear. The American otitis externa guideline recommends a non-ototoxic drop (a quinolone) whenever the drum is not intact or a grommet is present (Rosenfeld et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2014), and ENT UK guidance accepts aminoglycosides only for short courses (under two weeks) in a discharging ear, with the patient warned. Contact allergy. Neomycin sensitises up to a third of patients with chronic otitis externa."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eamg~s4-2","Interactions","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · Safety","Clinically insignificant systemically. Avoid with other ototoxic ear preparations."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eamg~s5-1","Viva essentials","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism of topical ototoxicity through the round window. The ENT UK position on aminoglycoside drops in a perforated ear. Neomycin contact sensitivity and its clinical picture."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eamg~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Aminoglycoside and combination ear drops · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing gentamicin drops for weeks in an ear with a perforation. Missing neomycin allergy in an otitis externa that gets worse with treatment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s1-1","Summary","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · At a glance","Quinolone ear drops Summary Preparations Ciprofloxacin 0.2% or 0.3% (single or with dexamethasone 0.1%); ofloxacin 0.3% (eye drop formulations often used in the ear) How they work Inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV; topical concentrations far exceed the MIC of most organisms, including many strains \"resistant\" on systemic breakpoints Spectrum Pseudomonas aeruginosa, S. aureus (including many MRSA at topical concentrations), Proteus, H. influenzae; not fungi, limited against streptococci and anaerobes ENT uses Acute otitis externa; tympanostomy tube otorrhoea; chronic suppurative otitis media with discharge; discharging mastoid cavity; after ear surgery Dose Typically 4 drops (or one single-dose vial) twice daily for 7 days, varying by product; ofloxacin 10 drops once daily in adults (5 in children) in some licences Children Licensed in children with grommets (ciprofloxacin-dexamethasone from 6 months) Pregnancy Negligible systemic absorption; generally acceptable"],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s2-1","Why topical treatment wins","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · How they work","Ear drops deliver antibiotic at concentrations a thousand times higher than oral treatment achieves in the canal or middle ear, so they cure acute otitis externa and tube otorrhoea more often than oral antibiotics, without systemic side effects or resistance pressure on the gut flora. Systemic antibiotics are needed only when infection spreads beyond the canal (cellulitis), in the immunocompromised, or in necrotising otitis externa."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s2-2","Getting the drops in","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · How they work","They work only if they reach the infection. Aural toilet (microsuction or dry mopping) first; a wick when the canal is swollen shut; lie with the ear up for 3 to 5 minutes after instilling, and tragal pumping to push drops through a tube or perforation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s3-1","Acute otitis externa","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · Using them in ENT","Topical antibiotic with or without steroid for 7 days is the treatment; adding a steroid speeds pain relief. Continue a few days after symptoms settle; review if no better in 48 to 72 hours."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s3-2","Discharging ear with a perforation or a tube","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · Using them in ENT","Quinolone drops are first-line for tympanostomy tube otorrhoea (better than oral amoxicillin or watchful waiting) and for active chronic suppurative otitis media , because they are not ototoxic. Swab persistent discharge; consider fungal infection, cholesteatoma or a tumour if it does not settle."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s4-1","Adverse effects","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · Safety","Otomycosis : prolonged antibacterial drops alter the canal flora; limit courses to 7 to 10 days and suspect fungi when discharge continues. Local irritation, itching, a bitter taste if drops pass through a perforation. Contact sensitivity is less common than with neomycin."],["ev","cl:t-dr_eqc~s4-2","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin: current evidence","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · Safety","Grommet otorrhoea. In a Dutch randomised trial in children with grommet otorrhoea, ciprofloxacin–hydrocortisone–bacitracin drops were superior to oral co-amoxiclav and to observation (van Dongen et al., NEJM 2014), and the American grommet guideline recommends topical drops, not oral antibiotics, for uncomplicated tube otorrhoea (Rosenfeld et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2022). Perforation. Quinolone drops are the safe choice with a perforation, as they are not ototoxic. Resistance and fungi. Long courses favour otomycosis; limit treatment to seven to ten days."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s4-2","Cautions","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · Safety","Avoid in known quinolone hypersensitivity. They do not cover fungi."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s5-1","Viva essentials","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why topical beats systemic for otitis externa and tube otorrhoea. Why quinolones are preferred when the drum is not intact . The role of aural toilet, wicks and tragal pumping. Otomycosis after prolonged use."],["sec","cl:t-dr_eqc~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Quinolone ear drops: ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing drops into a canal full of debris. Oral antibiotics for uncomplicated otitis externa. Courses of several weeks that produce otomycosis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ester~s1-1","Summary","Steroid ear drops · At a glance","Steroid ear drops Summary Preparations Betamethasone sodium phosphate 0.1% drops (ear, eye and nose); prednisolone sodium phosphate 0.5%; steroid in combination with antibiotics is covered elsewhere How they work Reduce inflammation, oedema and itch by suppressing cytokines and inflammatory cell activity ENT uses Chronic eczematous, seborrhoeic and psoriatic otitis externa; allergic contact dermatitis of the canal (including from neomycin); granulation after treated infection; persistent itch Dose Betamethasone 2 to 3 drops every 2 to 3 hours initially, reducing as it settles (commonly three or four times daily for 7 to 14 days) Avoid Untreated bacterial, fungal or viral infection of the canal Pregnancy Short courses acceptable"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ester~s2-1","Mechanism","Steroid ear drops · How they work","Topical corticosteroids act on keratinocytes and inflammatory cells to reduce cytokine release, vasodilatation and itch. They also reduce canal oedema, which reopens a narrowed canal."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ester~s3-1","Chronic otitis externa","Steroid ear drops · Using them in ENT","Treat the dermatosis: avoid scratching, cotton buds and water, keep the canal dry, use steroid drops or a steroid cream on a cotton bud for flares, and look for and treat co-existing fungal infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ester~s3-2","After infection","Steroid ear drops · Using them in ENT","Once a bacterial otitis externa has been treated, a short course of steroid drops can help granulation and residual swelling settle."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ester~s4-1","Steroid ear drops: current evidence","Steroid ear drops · Safety","Use. Steroid-only drops treat eczematous and seborrhoeic otitis externa and contact dermatitis, especially when neomycin allergy is suspected, but evidence comes from small trials and clinical experience. Caution. Steroids alone can let a bacterial or fungal infection spread; if the canal is wet, swollen or has debris, clean it and use an antibiotic–steroid drop instead."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ester~s4-1","Adverse effects","Steroid ear drops · Safety","Masking or worsening of fungal infection. Local skin atrophy with prolonged use. Rarely, sensitivity to excipients."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ester~s5-1","Viva essentials","Steroid ear drops · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The difference between infective and eczematous otitis externa and how treatment differs. Steroid drops as treatment for neomycin contact dermatitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ester~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Steroid ear drops · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using steroid alone in an acutely infected, discharging canal. Ignoring a fungal infection that flares under steroid."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluc~s1-1","Summary","Fluconazole · At a glance","Fluconazole Summary Class Triazole antifungal How it works Inhibits fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), depleting ergosterol; fungistatic against Candida Spectrum Candida albicans and most other Candida (C. krusei is resistant, C. glabrata often less sensitive), Cryptococcus; no activity against Aspergillus or Mucorales ENT uses Oropharyngeal candidiasis in the immunocompromised, after radiotherapy or failing topical treatment; oesophageal candidiasis; laryngeal candidiasis; candidal colonisation of voice prostheses; refractory candidal otomycosis Adult dose Oropharyngeal candidiasis 50 mg once daily for 7 to 14 days (100 to 200 mg in severe or immunocompromised cases); oesophageal 200 to 400 mg on day 1, then 100 to 200 mg daily for 14 to 21 days Child dose 3 to 6 mg/kg on day 1, then 3 mg/kg daily Pregnancy Avoid (high doses linked to fetal malformations)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluc~s2-1","Handling","Fluconazole · How it works","About 90% oral bioavailability, unaffected by food or acid; long half-life (about 30 hours) allows once-daily dosing; renally excreted (halve the dose when the creatinine clearance is under 50 ml/min)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluc~s3-1","Oral and laryngeal candidiasis","Fluconazole · Using it in ENT","Topical nystatin or miconazole first for uncomplicated thrush. Use fluconazole for extensive disease, failure of topical treatment, during or after radiotherapy, in HIV and other immunosuppression, in steroid inhaler users with laryngeal candidiasis (with advice to rinse after inhaler use), and for oesophageal involvement (odynophagia)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluc~s3-2","Voice prostheses","Fluconazole · Using it in ENT","Candida biofilm shortens the life of tracheo-oesophageal voice prostheses; antifungal treatment (oral or topical) can extend device life in patients with frequent leaks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluc~s4-1","Adverse effects","Fluconazole · Safety","Nausea, abdominal pain, rash, headache; hepatotoxicity (rare); QT prolongation; Stevens-Johnson syndrome rarely."],["ev","cl:t-dr_fluc~s4-2","Fluconazole: current evidence","Fluconazole · Safety","Guidelines. The American candidiasis guideline recommends topical clotrimazole or miconazole for mild oropharyngeal candidiasis and oral fluconazole for moderate to severe disease or oesophageal involvement (Pappas et al., Clin Infect Dis 2016). Safety. Fluconazole prolongs QT and raises levels of warfarin, sulfonylureas, phenytoin and some statins. Radiotherapy patients often need several courses; check for oesophageal involvement if swallowing is painful."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluc~s4-2","Interactions","Fluconazole · Safety","A moderate CYP3A4 and strong CYP2C9 and 2C19 inhibitor: raises warfarin (INR), phenytoin, sulfonylureas, ciclosporin, tacrolimus and some statins; additive QT effects with other QT-prolonging drugs."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluc~s5-1","Viva essentials","Fluconazole · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Spectrum: Candida yes, Aspergillus and Mucor no . Use in head and neck radiotherapy and voice prosthesis patients. Warfarin and QT interactions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluc~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Fluconazole · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using fluconazole for suspected invasive fungal sinusitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s1-1","Summary","Flucloxacillin · At a glance","Flucloxacillin Summary Class Penicillinase-resistant (antistaphylococcal) penicillin How it works Cell wall synthesis inhibitor, stable against staphylococcal penicillinase Spectrum Methicillin-sensitive S. aureus, streptococci; not MRSA, not Gram-negatives, not Pseudomonas ENT uses Furunculosis of the ear canal and nasal vestibule; cellulitis of the face, pinna and neck; infected post-operative wounds; acute suppurative parotitis (with an anaerobic or broader agent if unwell); infected auricular haematoma; impetigo; with benzylpenicillin for severe cellulitis Adult dose Oral 500 mg to 1 g four times daily; intravenous 1 to 2 g four times daily (up to 2 g every 4 hours in endocarditis or osteomyelitis) Child dose Oral 1 month to 1 year 62.5 to 125 mg, 2 to 9 years 125 to 250 mg, 10 to 17 years 250 to 500 mg, four times daily; intravenous 25 to 50 mg/kg four times daily Duration 5 to 7 days for cellulitis, by response Avoid Penicillin allergy; previous flucloxacillin-associated jaundice Pregnancy Safe"],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s2-1","Mechanism","Flucloxacillin · How it works","An isoxazolyl penicillin whose bulky side chain prevents penicillinase from reaching the beta-lactam bond. MRSA resists it through a different penicillin-binding protein (PBP2a, encoded by mecA), so methicillin resistance means flucloxacillin resistance."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s2-2","Absorption and handling","Flucloxacillin · How it works","Oral absorption is incomplete and reduced by food: take it an hour before or two hours after meals . It is highly protein-bound, excreted renally and partly by the liver."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s3-1","Staphylococcal skin and soft tissue infection","Flucloxacillin · Using it in ENT","Furuncle of the ear canal : a painful boil in the outer, hair-bearing canal; incise if pointing, give oral flucloxacillin for spreading infection, and a wick with antibiotic-steroid drops if the canal is shut. Nasal vestibulitis and furunculosis : topical antiseptic or mupirocin for mild disease, oral flucloxacillin for a furuncle, and urgent assessment if there is spreading cellulitis (the danger triangle drains to the cavernous sinus). Cellulitis of the face, pinna and neck : flucloxacillin, with benzylpenicillin or clindamycin in severe disease."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s3-2","Perichondritis of the pinna","Flucloxacillin · Using it in ENT","Perichondritis after piercing of the upper ear cartilage is often caused by Pseudomonas , which flucloxacillin does not cover; ciprofloxacin is the oral choice. Use flucloxacillin for simple cellulitis of the lobule and staphylococcal wound infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s3-3","Acute suppurative parotitis","Flucloxacillin · Using it in ENT","Usually S. aureus in a dehydrated, elderly or post-operative patient: rehydrate, massage, sialogogues and intravenous flucloxacillin (with metronidazole or as co-amoxiclav), and look for an abscess on ultrasound if it does not settle."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s4-1","Adverse effects","Flucloxacillin · Safety","Cholestatic jaundice and hepatitis , which may appear up to two months after stopping; commoner in the elderly and with courses longer than 14 days. Gastrointestinal upset; phlebitis with intravenous use. The hypersensitivity of all penicillins."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s4-2","Contraindications and cautions","Flucloxacillin · Safety","Penicillin allergy; previous flucloxacillin jaundice. Hepatic impairment."],["ev","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s4-3","Flucloxacillin: current evidence","Flucloxacillin · Safety","Paracetamol interaction. UK and European regulators warned in 2022 that flucloxacillin taken with paracetamol can cause high anion gap metabolic acidosis (pyroglutamic acidosis), especially in severe renal impairment, sepsis, malnutrition or chronic alcohol use; monitor patients at risk. Liver. Cholestatic hepatitis can occur up to two months after treatment, more often in older patients and longer courses. Indication. It remains the drug of choice for Staphylococcus aureus skin infection, perichondritis without Pseudomonas , and infected haematomas."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s4-3","Interactions","Flucloxacillin · Safety","Paracetamol with flucloxacillin, in malnourished or septic patients, has been linked to high anion gap metabolic acidosis (pyroglutamic acidosis). Methotrexate excretion is reduced."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s5-1","Viva essentials","Flucloxacillin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why flucloxacillin resists penicillinase, and why MRSA resists flucloxacillin. The organisms of otitis externa (Pseudomonas, S. aureus) and of perichondritis (Pseudomonas) , and which drug covers which. Take on an empty stomach ; delayed cholestatic jaundice."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluclox~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Flucloxacillin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a cartilage perichondritis with flucloxacillin alone. Oral flucloxacillin taken with food. Forgetting to drain a pointing furuncle or an abscess."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluor~s1-1","Summary","Intrathecal fluorescein · At a glance","Intrathecal fluorescein Summary Agent Sodium fluorescein (preservative-free 10% or 5% solution) How it works A fluorescent dye that mixes with CSF and appears at the site of a leak; a blue-light filter on the endoscope increases sensitivity ENT uses Localising CSF leaks at endoscopic repair (spontaneous, traumatic, iatrogenic, encephaloceles); confirming a watertight repair Dose Commonly 0.1 ml of 10% fluorescein (10 mg) diluted in 10 ml of the patient's own CSF, injected slowly over about 10 minutes through a lumbar drain or needle, about 30 to 60 minutes before the endoscopic search Consent Off-label use; explain the rare risks Avoid Higher concentrations or doses; known fluorescein allergy"],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluor~s2-1","Technique","Intrathecal fluorescein · Using it in ENT","After anaesthesia, insert a lumbar drain (also useful post-operatively in some patients), withdraw CSF, dilute the fluorescein in it, and inject slowly. Head-down positioning and a Valsalva manoeuvre help the dye reach the leak. Fluorescein may take time to appear; a negative search does not exclude a leak. Other localisation tools are CT cisternography, high-resolution CT, MRI cisternography and beta-2 transferrin or beta-trace protein testing of nasal fluid."],["ev","cl:t-dr_fluor~s3-1","Intrathecal fluorescein: current evidence","Intrathecal fluorescein · Safety","Safety. Intrathecal fluorescein is used off-label; the US FDA warns against it. Seizures, transverse myelitis and other neurological reactions have followed high doses or concentrated solutions, whereas large series using 0.1 ml of 10% fluorescein (10 mg or less) diluted in CSF and injected slowly report very low complication rates. Obtain specific consent and document the dose. Alternatives. High-resolution CT, MR cisternography and beta-2 transferrin testing localise and confirm most leaks without it."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluor~s3-1","Adverse effects","Intrathecal fluorescein · Safety","Seizures, lower limb weakness and numbness, opisthotonus, cranial nerve deficits, and very rarely death, mostly with higher doses or concentrations; headache and nausea; transient yellow discoloration of skin and urine with systemic absorption."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluor~s4-1","Viva essentials","Intrathecal fluorescein · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The dose and dilution. Its off-label status and complications. Other ways to localise a CSF leak."],["sec","cl:t-dr_fluor~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Intrathecal fluorescein · For the examination","Common pitfalls Injecting undiluted or high-dose fluorescein quickly."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s1-1","Summary","Gentamicin (systemic) · At a glance","Gentamicin (systemic) Summary Class Aminoglycoside (others: amikacin, tobramycin, streptomycin, neomycin) How it works Binds the 30S ribosomal subunit, causing misreading of mRNA; concentration-dependent, bactericidal, with a post-antibiotic effect Spectrum Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, S. aureus (synergy); synergy with penicillins against streptococci and enterococci; no anaerobic activity; poor in acidic, anaerobic pus ENT uses Gram-negative sepsis; surgical prophylaxis in penicillin allergy (single dose with clindamycin); topical ear drops; intratympanic chemical labyrinthectomy Adult dose Once-daily dosing 5 to 7 mg/kg (based on ideal or adjusted body weight) with levels by a nomogram (for example the Hartford nomogram); divided dosing for endocarditis synergy (1 mg/kg every 12 hours) Child dose 7 mg/kg once daily (neonates on specific regimens), guided by levels Avoid Myasthenia gravis; take care with renal impairment, pre-existing hearing loss, family history of aminoglycoside deafness, and other ototoxic or nephrotoxic drugs Pregnancy Avoid (fetal ototoxicity)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s2-1","Mechanism","Gentamicin (systemic) · How it works","Uptake into bacteria needs oxygen-dependent transport, so aminoglycosides do not work against anaerobes or in abscesses. Once-daily dosing exploits concentration-dependent killing and the post-antibiotic effect while giving the kidney and ear a drug-free interval."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s2-2","Handling","Gentamicin (systemic) · How it works","Not absorbed orally. Distributes in extracellular fluid, excreted unchanged by glomerular filtration; it accumulates in renal tubular cells and in the perilymph and endolymph , where it remains long after plasma levels fall."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s3-1","How it damages the ear","Gentamicin (systemic) · Ototoxicity","Aminoglycosides enter hair cells through the mechanotransduction channels, generate reactive oxygen species and trigger apoptosis. Gentamicin and streptomycin are mainly vestibulotoxic ; amikacin, kanamycin, neomycin and tobramycin are mainly cochleotoxic. Cochlear damage begins in the outer hair cells of the basal turn , so high frequencies go first. Damage is usually permanent and can progress for weeks after the drug stops."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s3-2","Who is at risk","Gentamicin (systemic) · Ototoxicity","Duration over 5 to 7 days, high cumulative dose, high troughs, renal impairment and older age. Concurrent loop diuretics , vancomycin, cisplatin. Mitochondrial 12S rRNA mutations (m.1555A>G, m.1494C>T) cause profound deafness after even a single dose; in the UK, neonates are now tested rapidly for m.1555A>G before gentamicin where possible, and patients with a maternal family history of aminoglycoside deafness should not receive it."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s3-3","Recognising vestibular toxicity","Gentamicin (systemic) · Ototoxicity","Bilateral vestibular loss presents with oscillopsia (the world jumps when walking) and imbalance in the dark, often noticed only when the patient is mobilised after an ICU stay; a head impulse test shows bilateral catch-up saccades. There is no vertigo, because both sides are lost together."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s3-4","Monitoring","Gentamicin (systemic) · Ototoxicity","Measure levels by the protocol (a trough before the next dose with once-daily dosing), check renal function, question patients about hearing, tinnitus and imbalance, and consider high-frequency audiometry on long courses."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s4-1","Adverse effects","Gentamicin (systemic) · Safety","Ototoxicity (cochlear and vestibular), as above. Nephrotoxicity : usually reversible acute tubular injury. Neuromuscular blockade, especially in myasthenia and with anaesthetic agents."],["ev","cl:t-dr_gent~s4-2","Gentamicin (systemic): current evidence","Gentamicin (systemic) · Safety","Genetic risk. The MT-RNR1 m.1555A>G variant, present in about 1 in 500 people, causes profound deafness after even one dose. A point-of-care genetic test returned results within about 26 minutes in neonatal units without delaying antibiotics (McDermott et al., JAMA Pediatr 2022), and NICE recommended it in 2023. Monitoring. Vestibulotoxicity can occur even with normal levels; ask daily about imbalance and oscillopsia, and avoid courses longer than needed."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s4-2","Interactions","Gentamicin (systemic) · Safety","Loop diuretics, vancomycin, ciclosporin, cisplatin, amphotericin and neuromuscular blockers."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s5-1","Viva essentials","Gentamicin (systemic) · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Which aminoglycosides are vestibulotoxic versus cochleotoxic . Mechanism of hair cell damage and why it starts in the basal turn. The m.1555A>G mitochondrial mutation. How bilateral vestibular loss presents (oscillopsia) and how you test for it. Monitoring and risk factors."],["sec","cl:t-dr_gent~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Gentamicin (systemic) · For the examination","Common pitfalls Continuing gentamicin beyond a few days without levels and review. Giving it with furosemide without considering ototoxicity. Missing bilateral vestibular failure in a patient who was on ICU."],["sec","cl:t-dr_glyco~s1-1","Summary","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling · At a glance","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling Summary Glycopyrronium Quaternary antimuscarinic that does not cross the blood-brain barrier well; oral solution (for example 320 micrograms/ml) for children from 3 years: start about 12.8 micrograms/kg three times daily, increased weekly to a maximum of about 64 micrograms/kg (or 6 ml) three times daily, by product Hyoscine hydrobromide Transdermal patch (1 mg over 72 hours) behind the ear; children by age (often half a patch to one patch every 3 days) How they work Block muscarinic M3 receptors in the salivary glands, reducing watery saliva Other options Speech and language therapy and positioning; botulinum toxin injection; submandibular duct relocation or ligation, gland excision, or ductal ligation (four-duct ligation); trihexyphenidyl in dystonic children"],["sec","cl:t-dr_glyco~s2-1","Assessing drooling","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling · Using them in ENT","Assess its severity and frequency, posture and head control, dental and oral health, reflux, and aspiration risk (posterior drooling causes chest infections and needs different priorities). Involve the multidisciplinary team."],["sec","cl:t-dr_glyco~s3-1","Adverse effects","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling · Safety","Constipation , urinary retention, flushing, dry skin and reduced sweating (overheating in hot weather), thickened secretions (harder to clear, a particular concern with posterior drooling), blurred vision, behavioural change and irritability, tachycardia; hyoscine crosses into the brain and can cause confusion and drowsiness, and local skin irritation."],["ev","cl:t-dr_glyco~s3-2","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling: current evidence","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling · Safety","Children with neurodisability. NICE (NG62, 2017) recommends glycopyrronium or trihexyphenidyl as first-line drugs for drooling in children with cerebral palsy, with botulinum toxin to the salivary glands if these fail and surgery for persistent severe drooling. A licensed oral glycopyrronium solution is now available for children. Side effects. Constipation, urinary retention, thick secretions and behavioural change are common; hyoscine patches cause more central side effects."],["sec","cl:t-dr_glyco~s3-2","Contraindications","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling · Safety","Glaucoma, bowel obstruction, urinary retention, myasthenia gravis, significant cardiac disease."],["sec","cl:t-dr_glyco~s4-1","Viva essentials","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Assessment of drooling and the multidisciplinary approach. Anticholinergic side effects in children. Surgical options when drugs fail."],["sec","cl:t-dr_glyco~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Glycopyrronium and hyoscine for drooling · For the examination","Common pitfalls Thickening secretions in a child who aspirates."],["sec","cl:t-dr_h2~s1-1","Summary","H2 receptor antagonists · At a glance","H2 receptor antagonists Summary Agents Famotidine; cimetidine; nizatidine (ranitidine withdrawn) How they work Competitive antagonism of histamine H2 receptors on parietal cells, reducing basal and nocturnal acid secretion ENT uses Nocturnal reflux symptoms with a PPI; PPI intolerance; premedication to reduce aspiration risk in some anaesthetic protocols Adult dose Famotidine 20 to 40 mg once or twice daily (reduce in renal impairment) Child dose Famotidine on specialist advice by weight"],["sec","cl:t-dr_h2~s2-1","Place in treatment","H2 receptor antagonists · Using them in ENT","Tolerance (tachyphylaxis) develops within weeks of regular use, limiting their long-term effect. At bedtime, a dose with a morning PPI is sometimes used for nocturnal symptoms."],["ev","cl:t-dr_h2~s3-1","H2 receptor antagonists: current evidence","H2 receptor antagonists · Safety","Ranitidine withdrawn. Ranitidine was withdrawn worldwide in 2020 after contamination with the probable carcinogen NDMA; famotidine is now the main H2 antagonist. Use. A night-time H2 antagonist can be added for nocturnal acid breakthrough on PPIs, but tolerance develops within weeks. Evidence for H2 antagonists alone in laryngopharyngeal reflux is weak."],["sec","cl:t-dr_h2~s3-1","Adverse effects","H2 receptor antagonists · Safety","Headache, dizziness, diarrhoea; confusion in the elderly and in renal impairment; gynaecomastia and many interactions with cimetidine (a CYP inhibitor raising warfarin, phenytoin and theophylline levels)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_h2~s4-1","Viva essentials","H2 receptor antagonists · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism compared with PPIs. Why ranitidine was withdrawn."],["sec","cl:t-dr_h2~s4-2","Common pitfalls","H2 receptor antagonists · For the examination","Common pitfalls Choosing cimetidine in a patient on warfarin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_hae~s1-1","Summary","Drugs for angioedema · At a glance","Drugs for angioedema Summary Allergic (histaminergic) Intramuscular adrenaline if there is airway or circulatory compromise (see adrenaline); non-sedating antihistamines; corticosteroids; nebulised adrenaline for laryngeal oedema ACE inhibitor angioedema Airway first; stop the ACE inhibitor permanently; antihistamines, steroids and adrenaline are usually ineffective; icatibant (30 mg subcutaneously) and C1-esterase inhibitor or fresh frozen plasma have been used, with mixed trial evidence Hereditary angioedema (C1-INH deficiency) Acute: C1-esterase inhibitor concentrate (for example 20 units/kg intravenously), icatibant 30 mg subcutaneously, or ecallantide; prophylaxis: lanadelumab, berotralstat, C1-INH; short-term prophylaxis before surgery and dental work (C1-INH, or danazol or tranexamic acid in some protocols) Key features of bradykinin angioedema No urticaria or itch; slower onset over hours; lasts 2 to 5 days; abdominal attacks in hereditary disease; family history; normal tryptase; low C4 in hereditary types"],["sec","cl:t-dr_hae~s2-1","ACE inhibitor angioedema","Drugs for angioedema · Using them in ENT","It affects about 0.1% to 0.7% of patients on ACE inhibitors, more often people of African descent, and can occur after years of uneventful treatment. It typically involves the tongue, lips and floor of mouth. Assess the airway with flexible nasendoscopy; the patient's voice, drooling, stridor and the extent of swelling (tongue base and larynx) decide whether to secure the airway, which may need awake fibreoptic intubation or a surgical airway. Admit patients with floor of mouth, tongue or laryngeal involvement. Switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker carries a small risk of recurrence."],["sec","cl:t-dr_hae~s2-2","Hereditary angioedema","Drugs for angioedema · Using them in ENT","Diagnosed by low C4 and low C1-esterase inhibitor level or function; triggered by trauma, surgery, dental work, oestrogens and ACE inhibitors. Before ENT surgery, arrange short-term prophylaxis with immunology."],["ev","cl:t-dr_hae~s3-1","Drugs for angioedema: current evidence","Drugs for angioedema · Safety","Mechanism decides treatment. Histaminergic angioedema responds to adrenaline, antihistamines and steroids; bradykinin-mediated angioedema (hereditary angioedema and ACE inhibitor angioedema) does not. Hereditary angioedema. The international WAO/EAACI guideline recommends C1 inhibitor concentrate, icatibant or ecallantide for attacks, and long-term prophylaxis with lanadelumab, berotralstat or C1 inhibitor (Maurer et al., Allergy 2022). ACE inhibitor angioedema. In a randomised trial, icatibant was no better than placebo (Sinert et al., J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract 2017); stop the ACE inhibitor and protect the airway."],["sec","cl:t-dr_hae~s3-1","Key points","Drugs for angioedema · Safety","Giving only antihistamines and steroids to a patient with bradykinin angioedema and an evolving airway problem delays the decisions that matter. Icatibant causes injection-site reactions; C1-esterase inhibitor carries a small thrombosis risk."],["sec","cl:t-dr_hae~s4-1","Viva essentials","Drugs for angioedema · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for How to tell histaminergic from bradykinin angioedema. Management of ACE inhibitor angioedema , starting with the airway. Hereditary angioedema: diagnosis and perioperative prophylaxis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_hae~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Drugs for angioedema · For the examination","Common pitfalls Discharging a patient with tongue swelling on an ACE inhibitor after antihistamines. Restarting the ACE inhibitor."],["sec","cl:t-dr_heliox~s1-1","Summary","Heliox · At a glance","Heliox Summary Composition Helium with 21% to 40% oxygen How it works Low density reduces the Reynolds number, converting turbulent to more laminar flow through a stenosis; resistance and work of breathing fall ENT uses Temporising severe upper airway obstruction (tumours, angioedema, subglottic stenosis, post-extubation stridor, croup) while preparing definitive treatment; supporting breathing during transfer Delivery Tight-fitting non-rebreathing mask at high flow; flowmeters calibrated for heliox Limitations The more oxygen needed, the less helium can be given (benefit falls when oxygen requirement exceeds about 40%)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_heliox~s2-1","In the airway emergency","Heliox · Using it in ENT","Heliox improves stridor and distress, sometimes dramatically, but it can mask how bad the obstruction is and lull the team. Use the time to bring the right people (senior ENT surgeon, anaesthetist), plan the airway (awake fibreoptic intubation, awake tracheostomy, or front-of-neck access), and treat the cause (steroids, nebulised adrenaline, antibiotics, drainage)."],["ev","cl:t-dr_heliox~s3-1","Heliox: current evidence","Heliox · Safety","Evidence. Heliox lowers the work of breathing through a narrowed airway by reducing turbulent flow, buying time while definitive treatment works. A Cochrane review in croup found insufficient evidence that heliox is better than oxygen with steroids and adrenaline (Moraa et al., 2018). Limits. Because the mixture contains only 20 to 30% oxygen, it is unsuitable for hypoxic patients, and it never replaces securing the airway."],["sec","cl:t-dr_heliox~s3-1","Problems","Heliox · Safety","Hypoxia if the oxygen fraction is too low for the patient's needs; a falsely reassuring clinical picture; voice distortion (high pitch)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_heliox~s4-1","Viva essentials","Heliox · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The physics (density and turbulent flow). That heliox is a bridge , not a treatment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_heliox~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Heliox · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using heliox instead of securing the airway."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ici~s1-1","Summary","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab · At a glance","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab Summary Class Immune checkpoint inhibitors (anti-PD-1) How they work Block PD-1 on T cells so tumour PD-L1 cannot switch them off ENT uses Recurrent or metastatic HNSCC first-line (pembrolizumab alone if PD-L1 combined positive score of 1 or more, or with platinum and 5-FU); after platinum failure (nivolumab, pembrolizumab); perioperative treatment of resectable locally advanced HNSCC (pembrolizumab, approved in some countries); cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (cemiplimab); nasopharyngeal carcinoma (toripalimab and others with chemotherapy) Dose Pembrolizumab 200 mg every 3 weeks or 400 mg every 6 weeks; nivolumab 240 mg every 2 weeks or 480 mg every 4 weeks Biomarker PD-L1 combined positive score (CPS) on tumour tissue"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ici~s2-1","Response patterns","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab · Using them in ENT","Responses can be slow and durable; pseudoprogression (apparent growth from immune infiltration) occurs occasionally, so scans are interpreted in context. About a fifth of patients respond, and some have long-term control."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ici~s3-1","Recognising them","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab · Safety","Any organ can be affected: Thyroid : hypothyroidism (very common, particularly after neck radiotherapy) or thyroiditis with transient thyrotoxicosis; check thyroid function regularly. Skin : rash, pruritus, vitiligo. Gut : diarrhoea and colitis. Lung : pneumonitis (cough, breathlessness). Liver : hepatitis. Endocrine : hypophysitis, adrenal insufficiency, type 1 diabetes. Rarer: myocarditis (often fatal), myositis, neuropathy, nephritis, sicca."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ici~s3-2","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab: current evidence","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab · Safety","Trials. In recurrent or metastatic disease, nivolumab (CheckMate 141) and pembrolizumab (KEYNOTE-040) improved survival after platinum, and first-line pembrolizumab alone (PD-L1 positive) or with chemotherapy beat EXTREME (Burtness et al., Lancet 2019, KEYNOTE-048). Around surgery, perioperative pembrolizumab improved event-free survival (KEYNOTE-689), and adjuvant nivolumab with chemoradiation improved disease-free survival (NIVOPOSTOP) (reviewed by Imamura et al., Cancer Treat Rev 2025). Toxicity. The ASCO guideline sets out management of immune-related effects; hypothyroidism is the commonest, affecting about one patient in ten (Schneider et al., J Clin Oncol 2021)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ici~s3-2","Management","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab · Safety","Grade the toxicity, withhold the drug for moderate or severe events, and treat with corticosteroids (with other immunosuppressants for refractory cases) in discussion with oncology; replace hormones for endocrine failure, which is usually permanent."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ici~s4-1","Viva essentials","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism (PD-1 and PD-L1) and the CPS biomarker. KEYNOTE-048 and CheckMate 141. Common immune-related adverse events, especially thyroid."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ici~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Pembrolizumab and nivolumab · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing hypothyroidism or adrenal insufficiency in a tired patient on immunotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_immuno~s1-1","Summary","Allergen immunotherapy · At a glance","Allergen immunotherapy Summary Forms Subcutaneous immunotherapy (SCIT): injections in a specialist clinic; sublingual immunotherapy (SLIT): daily tablets or drops at home, first dose under supervision How it works Induces regulatory T cells and IL-10 and TGF-beta, shifts responses from Th2 to Th1, raises allergen-specific IgG4 that blocks IgE, and reduces mast cell and eosinophil activity ENT uses Moderate to severe IgE-mediated allergic rhinitis (grass and tree pollen, house dust mite) with symptoms despite optimal medication, confirmed by history plus skin prick test or specific IgE Course Usually 3 years; SLIT pollen tablets started at least 2 to 4 months before the season and taken all year Avoid Severe or uncontrolled asthma (FEV1 under 70%); active autoimmune disease or malignancy; starting during pregnancy; beta blockers (relative, as anaphylaxis is harder to treat); poor adherence"],["sec","cl:t-dr_immuno~s2-1","Who benefits","Allergen immunotherapy · Using it in ENT","A patient with clearly seasonal or perennial symptoms that correlate with one or two relevant allergens, confirmed by testing, whose symptoms persist despite nasal steroids, antihistamines and allergen avoidance, and who will commit to 3 years. Polysensitised patients may still benefit if the clinically relevant allergen is clear."],["sec","cl:t-dr_immuno~s2-2","SCIT versus SLIT","Allergen immunotherapy · Using it in ENT","SCIT has a long track record but needs clinic visits and observation after each injection because of the risk of systemic reactions. SLIT is safer (local mouth itch and swelling are common, anaphylaxis rare) and convenient, but adherence falls with time."],["ev","cl:t-dr_immuno~s3-1","Allergen immunotherapy: current evidence","Allergen immunotherapy · Safety","Lasting benefit. Three years of grass pollen immunotherapy gives benefit that persists for years after stopping (Durham et al., NEJM 1999), while two years was not enough in the GRASS trial (Scadding et al., JAMA 2017). Asthma prevention. In the GAP trial, grass tablet immunotherapy in children with rhinitis reduced asthma symptoms and medication use, although not the formal diagnosis of asthma (Valovirta et al., J Allergy Clin Immunol 2018). Sublingual tablets can be started at home after the first supervised dose, which makes them the usual first choice."],["sec","cl:t-dr_immuno~s3-1","Adverse effects","Allergen immunotherapy · Safety","SCIT: local swelling; systemic reactions and anaphylaxis , so injections are given only where resuscitation is available, with 60 minutes of observation. SLIT: oral itch and swelling, throat irritation, gastrointestinal upset; eosinophilic oesophagitis rarely."],["sec","cl:t-dr_immuno~s4-1","Viva essentials","Allergen immunotherapy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications and contraindications. Mechanism (IgG4 blocking antibodies, regulatory T cells). The disease-modifying effect and course length. Safety arrangements for SCIT."],["sec","cl:t-dr_immuno~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Allergen immunotherapy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Offering immunotherapy without confirming IgE sensitisation to the relevant allergen. Giving SCIT to a patient with uncontrolled asthma."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s1-1","Summary","Intranasal corticosteroids · At a glance","Intranasal corticosteroid sprays Summary Agents Mometasone furoate; fluticasone propionate; fluticasone furoate; beclometasone dipropionate; budesonide; triamcinolone How they work Glucocorticoid receptor activation in the mucosa: fewer mast cells, eosinophils and T cells; less cytokine and chemokine release; less oedema and mucus ENT uses Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis; chronic rhinosinusitis with and without polyps; adjunct in acute post-viral and bacterial sinusitis; adenoidal hypertrophy and Eustachian tube dysfunction (modest evidence); after sinus surgery Adult dose Mometasone 2 sprays (50 micrograms each) into each nostril once daily, up to twice daily for polyps; fluticasone propionate 2 sprays each nostril once daily (maximum twice daily); fluticasone furoate 2 sprays each nostril once daily; beclometasone 2 sprays each nostril twice daily Child dose Mometasone 1 spray each nostril once daily from 3 years; fluticasone furoate 1 spray each nostril once daily from 6 years; fluticasone propionate 1 spray each nostril once daily from 4 years Onset Some effect within 12 hours; full effect after 1 to 2 weeks of regular use Pregnancy Budesonide and fluticasone have the most reassuring data; acceptable when needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s2-1","Mechanism","Intranasal corticosteroids · How they work","They suppress both the early and late phases of the allergic response, though their effect on the early phase builds over days rather than minutes, which is why they work best taken regularly and started before the pollen season."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s2-2","Systemic bioavailability","Intranasal corticosteroids · How they work","The molecules differ greatly in how much of the swallowed fraction survives first-pass metabolism: mometasone and fluticasone have under 1% to 2% systemic bioavailability, budesonide about a third and beclometasone almost half. Choose the low-bioavailability agents for children, for long-term use, and for patients also on inhaled steroids."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s3-1","Technique","Intranasal corticosteroids · Using them in ENT","Teach it at every visit: Blow the nose first; shake the bottle; prime a new bottle. Head bent forward (looking at the toes), not tipped back. Use the opposite hand (right hand for the left nostril) so the nozzle points laterally , away from the septum, toward the outer corner of the eye. Sniff gently, not hard (hard sniffing sends the dose to the throat)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s3-2","Allergic rhinitis","Intranasal corticosteroids · Using them in ENT","First-line for moderate to severe or persistent symptoms, alone or with an antihistamine; more effective than oral antihistamines for congestion."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s3-3","Chronic rhinosinusitis","Intranasal corticosteroids · Using them in ENT","Regular long-term use in every patient, in addition to saline irrigation; in polyposis, a higher dose, drops or rinses for better distribution (see steroid drops and irrigations), and short courses of oral steroids for exacerbations. After sinus surgery the opened cavities let sprays reach the sinuses better."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s4-1","Adverse effects","Intranasal corticosteroids · Safety","Epistaxis and crusting (up to 1 in 10), mostly from spraying onto the septum; rarely septal perforation . Dryness, irritation, headache, taste and smell disturbance. Systemic effects are rare with mometasone and fluticasone; small reductions in growth velocity have been seen with beclometasone in children. Adrenal suppression is possible when high nasal doses are combined with inhaled and other steroids."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ins~s4-2","Intranasal corticosteroids: current evidence","Intranasal corticosteroids · Safety","Effectiveness. Intranasal steroids are the most effective single treatment for persistent allergic rhinitis (ARIA). In chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps they reduce polyp size and symptoms, and in a Cochrane review higher doses and drops gave somewhat larger benefits, with more nosebleeds (Chong et al., 2016). Growth. Mometasone and fluticasone furoate at standard doses have not reduced growth in children over a year; beclomethasone has, so newer molecules are preferred. Technique (head forward, spray away from the septum) reduces crusting and bleeding."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s4-2","Interactions","Intranasal corticosteroids · Safety","Ritonavir and cobicistat (and other strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as itraconazole) markedly raise fluticasone levels, causing Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression: use beclometasone or budesonide with care, or avoid."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s5-1","Viva essentials","Intranasal corticosteroids · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism and onset , and why regular use matters. Bioavailability differences between agents and which to choose in children. Correct technique (head down, opposite hand, lateral aim). The ritonavir interaction."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ins~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Intranasal corticosteroids · For the examination","Common pitfalls Labelling a patient a treatment failure without checking technique and adherence. Spraying at the septum, producing bleeding and crusting. Using beclometasone long term in a small child."],["sec","cl:t-dr_iodine~s1-1","Summary","Potassium iodide and preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery · At a glance","Preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery Summary Potassium iodide Lugol's iodine (aqueous iodine oral solution, about 130 mg/ml total iodine) 0.1 to 0.3 ml three times daily in milk or water, or potassium iodide tablets, for 7 to 10 days before surgery in Graves' disease How iodine works High iodide concentrations acutely inhibit hormone synthesis (Wolff-Chaikoff effect) and release, and reduce gland vascularity; the effect wanes after 10 to 14 days (escape) Beta blockers Propranolol 40 mg three or four times daily (up to 80 mg four times daily) to control heart rate and tremor; also reduces peripheral T4 to T3 conversion at high doses; use cardioselective agents or diltiazem in asthma Steroids Hydrocortisone or dexamethasone reduce T4 to T3 conversion and treat relative adrenal insufficiency in storm Cholestyramine Binds thyroid hormone in the gut and reduces enterohepatic recirculation; an adjunct for rapid preparation Plasma exchange For refractory cases before urgent surgery"],["sec","cl:t-dr_iodine~s2-1","Elective surgery","Potassium iodide and preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery · Using them in ENT","The patient should be clinically and biochemically euthyroid on carbimazole (or PTU), with beta blockade for symptoms. Iodine is then added for the last 7 to 10 days in Graves' disease. Toxic nodular goitre does not usually need iodine."],["sec","cl:t-dr_iodine~s2-2","Thyroid storm","Potassium iodide and preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery · Using them in ENT","Recognise it by fever, tachycardia or atrial fibrillation, heart failure, agitation or confusion and gastrointestinal upset (the Burch-Wartofsky score helps). Treat in critical care: Thyroid storm 1 Block synthesis PTU (loading 500 to 1,000 mg, then 250 mg every 4 hours) or carbimazole or methimazole 2 Block release iodine, given at least an hour after the thionamide, so it is not used to make more hormone 3 Block peripheral effects propranolol (or esmolol) hydrocortisone 300 mg then 100 mg every 8 hours 4 Support cooling, fluids, treatment of heart failure and arrhythmias, paracetamol (not aspirin, which displaces T4 from binding proteins) find and treat the trigger"],["ev","cl:t-dr_iodine~s3-1","Potassium iodide and preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery: current evidence","Potassium iodide and preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery · Safety","Graves' disease. The American Thyroid Association guideline recommends potassium iodide (Lugol's iodine or saturated potassium iodide) for about ten days before thyroidectomy for Graves' disease, after the patient is made euthyroid with antithyroid drugs, because it reduces thyroid blood flow and intraoperative blood loss (Ross et al., Thyroid 2016). Not for toxic nodules (it can worsen thyrotoxicosis in nodular goitre). Urgent surgery in an uncontrolled patient needs beta blockers, steroids and iodine together, with an experienced anaesthetist."],["sec","cl:t-dr_iodine~s3-1","Adverse effects","Potassium iodide and preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery · Safety","Iodine: metallic taste, sialadenitis and salivary swelling (\"iodide mumps\"), rash, gastrointestinal upset, hypersensitivity. Beta blockers: bronchospasm, bradycardia, heart failure."],["sec","cl:t-dr_iodine~s4-1","Viva essentials","Potassium iodide and preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The Wolff-Chaikoff effect and escape. Preoperative preparation in Graves' disease. The order of treatment in thyroid storm ."],["sec","cl:t-dr_iodine~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Potassium iodide and preoperative preparation for thyroid surgery · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving iodine before the thionamide in thyroid storm. Proceeding with elective surgery in a thyrotoxic patient."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ipra~s1-1","Summary","Ipratropium nasal spray · At a glance","Ipratropium nasal spray Summary Class Short-acting muscarinic antagonist How it works Blocks acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors on nasal glands, reducing secretion ENT uses Non-allergic (vasomotor) rhinitis with predominant rhinorrhoea; gustatory rhinitis; elderly rhinitis (\"old man's drip\"); persistent rhinorrhoea in allergic rhinitis despite a steroid; common cold Adult dose 2 sprays (21 micrograms each, 0.03%) into each nostril two or three times daily Child dose Licensed from 12 years in the UK Avoid Caution in glaucoma (avoid spraying into eyes), prostatic hypertrophy and urinary retention Pregnancy Limited data; use if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ipra~s2-1","Choosing it","Ipratropium nasal spray · Using it in ENT","It does not help congestion , so it is not a substitute for a nasal steroid where blockage is the main problem; combine the two when both symptoms are present. For gustatory rhinitis, use it 15 to 30 minutes before meals."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ipra~s3-1","Ipratropium nasal spray: current evidence","Ipratropium nasal spray · Safety","Rhinorrhoea. Ipratropium nasal spray reduces watery rhinorrhoea in non-allergic (vasomotor) rhinitis, gustatory rhinitis and the common cold, but not blockage or sneezing; a Cochrane review found a modest benefit in the common cold, with nasal dryness and nosebleeds as side effects (AlBalawi et al., 2013). It combines usefully with an intranasal steroid when watery discharge persists."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ipra~s3-1","Adverse effects","Ipratropium nasal spray · Safety","Nasal dryness, epistaxis, irritation; rarely anticholinergic effects (dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ipra~s4-1","Viva essentials","Ipratropium nasal spray · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Which symptom it treats and why (parasympathetic secretomotor supply). Its place in non-allergic and elderly rhinitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ipra~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Ipratropium nasal spray · For the examination","Common pitfalls Expecting ipratropium to relieve nasal obstruction."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itgent~s1-1","Summary","Intratympanic gentamicin · At a glance","Intratympanic gentamicin Summary Indication Unilateral Ménière's disease with disabling vertigo despite medical treatment (and usually after intratympanic steroids), in a patient who accepts the risk to hearing Preparation Gentamicin 40 mg/ml buffered with sodium bicarbonate to about 26 to 30 mg/ml Regimen 0.5 to 0.8 ml through the drum under topical anaesthesia, the patient lying ear-up for 30 minutes without swallowing; a single injection, repeated after 4 weeks or more only if vertigo continues (titration) Outcomes Vertigo control about 90%; hearing loss in roughly 10% to 30% (lower with low-dose regimens); post-treatment imbalance until compensation Contraindications Only hearing ear; bilateral Ménière's disease or a weak opposite labyrinth on testing; poor capacity for central compensation (elderly, cerebellar disease, visual impairment) relative"],["sec","cl:t-dr_itgent~s2-1","Before treatment","Intratympanic gentamicin · Using it in ENT","Confirm the diagnosis (definite Ménière's disease by Bárány criteria), the side, and the function of the opposite labyrinth (video head impulse test, calorics). Record pure-tone audiometry. Explain that the aim is to stop vertigo, not to cure the disease; that tinnitus and fullness may continue; and that the other ear may later be affected in up to a third of patients."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itgent~s2-2","After treatment","Intratympanic gentamicin · Using it in ENT","Unsteadiness for days to weeks; early vestibular rehabilitation. Repeat audiometry before any further injection and 2 months after the last. Signs of effect include a reduced head impulse response and spontaneous nystagmus beating to the opposite ear."],["ev","cl:t-dr_itgent~s3-1","Intratympanic gentamicin: current evidence","Intratympanic gentamicin · Safety","Ménière's disease. A Cochrane review found that intratympanic gentamicin reduces vertigo in Ménière's disease (Pullens and van Benthem, 2011), and the American guideline offers it for active disease not controlled by non-ablative treatment (Basura et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020). In a randomised trial, intratympanic methylprednisolone was non-inferior to gentamicin for vertigo control, without the risk of hearing loss (Patel et al., Lancet 2016), so steroids are often tried first. Dosing. Low-dose, titrated injections repeated only when vertigo returns control most patients while keeping hearing loss to about one in five or fewer."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itgent~s3-1","Adverse effects","Intratympanic gentamicin · Safety","Sensorineural hearing loss (sometimes severe), persistent imbalance and oscillopsia (particularly if the other labyrinth is weak), tinnitus, perforation and otitis media."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itgent~s4-1","Viva essentials","Intratympanic gentamicin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism: vestibulotoxic more than cochleotoxic; type I hair cells. Patient selection and the contraindications. Titration versus fixed-dose regimens. Expected outcomes for vertigo and hearing."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itgent~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Intratympanic gentamicin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating without checking the opposite labyrinth. Repeating injections too soon, before the effect of the first is known."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itra~s1-1","Summary","Itraconazole · At a glance","Itraconazole Summary Class Triazole antifungal Spectrum Aspergillus, Candida, dermatophytes, dimorphic fungi (histoplasmosis, blastomycosis); not Mucorales reliably ENT uses Adjunct after surgery in allergic fungal rhinosinusitis (evidence weak); chronic invasive and granulomatous fungal sinusitis (step-down or alternative); otomycosis resistant to topical treatment (rarely) Adult dose 100 to 200 mg once or twice daily; levels are measured on long courses (therapeutic drug monitoring) Handling Capsules need gastric acid: take with food or an acidic drink, not with PPIs or antacids; oral solution on an empty stomach Avoid Heart failure (negative inotrope); pregnancy"],["sec","cl:t-dr_itra~s2-1","Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis","Itraconazole · Using it in ENT","The main treatment is complete surgical clearance of eosinophilic mucin followed by topical and systemic steroids and saline irrigation. Postoperative itraconazole may reduce recurrence and steroid needs in some patients, but trials are small; monitor liver function."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itra~s3-1","Adverse effects","Itraconazole · Safety","Hepatotoxicity, nausea, oedema and heart failure , hypokalaemia, peripheral neuropathy with long use, hypertension."],["ev","cl:t-dr_itra~s3-2","Itraconazole: current evidence","Itraconazole · Safety","Heart failure. The US FDA warned in 2001 that itraconazole is a negative inotrope and should not be used for non-life-threatening infection in patients with heart failure. Absorption. Capsules need food and acid; the oral solution is better absorbed on an empty stomach, and levels should be checked on long courses. Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis. Small trials and case series suggest postoperative itraconazole reduces recurrence, but evidence is weak and it is not routine."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itra~s3-2","Interactions","Itraconazole · Safety","A strong CYP3A4 inhibitor : raises levels of simvastatin and lovastatin (contraindicated), midazolam, ciclosporin, DOACs and many others; with fluticasone or budesonide it can cause iatrogenic Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression. Absorption falls with PPIs and H2 blockers."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itra~s4-1","Viva essentials","Itraconazole · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Its role and weak evidence in allergic fungal rhinosinusitis . The fluticasone interaction and heart failure warning."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itra~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Itraconazole · For the examination","Common pitfalls Itraconazole capsules with a PPI. Combining it with fluticasone without warning."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itster~s1-1","Summary","Intratympanic steroids · At a glance","Intratympanic steroids Summary Agents Dexamethasone 10 mg/ml to 24 mg/ml; methylprednisolone 40 mg/ml to 62.5 mg/ml ENT uses Sudden sensorineural hearing loss: salvage after incomplete recovery with oral steroids, or primary treatment when systemic steroids are contraindicated (diabetes, psychiatric illness, intolerance); Ménière's disease (non-ablative vertigo control); autoimmune inner ear disease (adjunct) Regimen Commonly 0.4 to 0.8 ml injected under topical phenol anaesthesia, repeated 3 or 4 times over 2 weeks for sudden hearing loss; for Ménière's disease, methylprednisolone injections repeated as needed Timing Salvage within 2 to 6 weeks of onset of sudden hearing loss Risks Pain, transient vertigo, perforation (about 1% to 4%), otitis media"],["sec","cl:t-dr_itster~s2-1","Pharmacokinetics","Intratympanic steroids · How it works","The round window membrane is the main route into the inner ear. Perilymph levels after intratympanic dexamethasone are many times those after oral or intravenous doses. Contact time matters: the patient lies with the ear up and avoids swallowing for about 20 to 30 minutes. A mucosal fold or a false round window membrane can block absorption."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itster~s3-1","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss","Intratympanic steroids · Using it in ENT","The AAO-HNS guideline recommends offering intratympanic steroid as salvage when recovery after initial treatment is incomplete, within 2 to 6 weeks of onset, and it is an option as primary treatment. Combined oral and intratympanic treatment is used in some centres for severe losses."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itster~s3-2","Ménière's disease","Intratympanic steroids · Using it in ENT","In a randomised trial, intratympanic methylprednisolone controlled vertigo about as well as intratympanic gentamicin, without the risk of hearing loss from the drug. It is therefore often tried before ablative gentamicin."],["ev","cl:t-dr_itster~s4-1","Intratympanic steroids: current evidence","Intratympanic steroids · Safety","Ménière's disease. In a randomised double-blind trial, intratympanic methylprednisolone (two injections two weeks apart) was non-inferior to intratympanic gentamicin in reducing vertigo attacks over two years (Patel et al., Lancet 2016). Sudden hearing loss. As first-line treatment, intratympanic and oral steroids give similar hearing gains (Mirian and Ovesen, JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020); as salvage within two to six weeks, intratympanic steroid adds a modest further gain (Chandrasekhar et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itster~s4-1","Adverse effects","Intratympanic steroids · Safety","Pain or burning during injection (less with dexamethasone than methylprednisolone), transient dizziness from cold or volume, persistent perforation in a small proportion (higher with repeated injections or a ventilation tube), otitis media, and dysgeusia from the chorda tympani."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itster~s5-1","Viva essentials","Intratympanic steroids · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The round window route and why the patient lies still. Indications in sudden hearing loss (salvage and primary) and in Ménière's disease. How the procedure is done and its risks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_itster~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Intratympanic steroids · For the examination","Common pitfalls Waiting more than 6 weeks before offering salvage. Forgetting to tell the patient not to swallow and to keep the head turned."],["sec","cl:t-dr_levo~s1-1","Summary","Levothyroxine and liothyronine · At a glance","Levothyroxine and liothyronine Summary Levothyroxine Synthetic T4, half-life about 7 days; converted in tissues to active T3 Liothyronine Synthetic T3, half-life about 1 day; quick onset and offset ENT uses Replacement after total or completion thyroidectomy, and after lobectomy if hypothyroid; TSH suppression in differentiated thyroid cancer; bridging before radioiodine (liothyronine, now largely replaced by recombinant TSH) Starting dose After total thyroidectomy in a healthy adult: about 1.6 micrograms/kg daily (for example 100 to 125 micrograms); elderly or with heart disease: 25 to 50 micrograms daily, increased every 4 to 6 weeks Monitoring TSH (and free T4) 6 to 8 weeks after starting or changing the dose, then every 6 to 12 months Taking it On an empty stomach, 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast, separate from calcium, iron and antacids by 4 hours Pregnancy Essential; dose usually needs to rise by 25% to 30% from early pregnancy"],["sec","cl:t-dr_levo~s2-1","TSH targets in differentiated thyroid cancer","Levothyroxine and liothyronine · Using them in ENT","Targets follow the risk of persistent or recurrent disease and the response to treatment (ATA and BTA guidance): High risk or structural persistent disease: TSH below 0.1 mU/l. Intermediate risk or biochemically incomplete response: TSH about 0.1 to 0.5 mU/l. Low risk with excellent response: TSH in the low normal range (about 0.5 to 2 mU/l). Avoid long-term suppression where it is not needed, particularly in older patients, those with atrial fibrillation, heart disease or osteoporosis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_levo~s2-2","After lobectomy","Levothyroxine and liothyronine · Using them in ENT","About one in five patients becomes hypothyroid after hemithyroidectomy; check TSH at 6 to 8 weeks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_levo~s3-1","Adverse effects","Levothyroxine and liothyronine · Safety","Over-replacement or suppression: atrial fibrillation , palpitations, angina, tremor, weight loss, insomnia and, long-term, osteoporosis (particularly in post-menopausal women). Under-replacement: fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, raised cholesterol."],["ev","cl:t-dr_levo~s3-2","Levothyroxine and liothyronine: current evidence","Levothyroxine and liothyronine · Safety","TSH targets in thyroid cancer. The American Thyroid Association guideline adapts suppression to risk: below 0.1 mU/l for high-risk or persistent disease, 0.1 to 0.5 for intermediate risk, and the low-normal range for low-risk patients with an excellent response (Haugen et al., Thyroid 2016). Overtreatment raises the risk of atrial fibrillation and osteoporosis, especially in older patients. Taking it. On an empty stomach 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast, or at bedtime, and apart from calcium, iron and PPIs."],["sec","cl:t-dr_levo~s3-2","Interactions","Levothyroxine and liothyronine · Safety","Absorption reduced by calcium carbonate, iron, aluminium and magnesium antacids, PPIs, sevelamer, bile acid sequestrants, soy and coffee; requirements increased by enzyme inducers (carbamazepine, rifampicin, phenytoin) and oestrogens; it increases the effect of warfarin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_levo~s4-1","Viva essentials","Levothyroxine and liothyronine · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Starting dose after total thyroidectomy and when to check the TSH. TSH targets by cancer risk. The calcium interaction after thyroidectomy (patients often take both)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_levo~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Levothyroxine and liothyronine · For the examination","Common pitfalls Taking levothyroxine with calcium supplements. Suppressing TSH indefinitely in a low-risk patient with osteoporosis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s1-1","Summary","Lidocaine · At a glance","Lidocaine Summary Class Amide local anaesthetic (also class Ib antiarrhythmic) How it works Blocks voltage-gated sodium channels from inside the axon, stopping action potential conduction; small, unmyelinated pain fibres are blocked first Preparations 0.5%, 1% and 2% injections, plain or with adrenaline (1:80,000 to 1:200,000); 4% topical solution; 10% spray (10 mg per spray); 2% gel; 5% with phenylephrine (co-phenylcaine) ENT uses Infiltration for incisions, flaps, biopsies and ear canal blocks; regional nerve blocks (auricle, scalp, infraorbital, mental); topical anaesthesia for nasendoscopy, laryngoscopy, transnasal oesophagoscopy and awake fibreoptic intubation; intravenous adjunct to reduce coughing at extubation Maximum dose Plain 3 mg/kg (maximum 200 mg); with adrenaline 7 mg/kg (maximum 500 mg); topical mucosal doses count toward the total and are absorbed rapidly Quick conversion 1% = 10 mg/ml; 2% = 20 mg/ml. A 70 kg adult may receive up to 21 ml of 1% plain or 49 ml of 1% with adrenaline Onset and duration Onset 2 to 5 minutes; duration 1 to 2 hours plain, 2 to 4 hours with adrenaline Pregnancy Safe in normal doses"],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s2-1","Mechanism","Lidocaine · How it works","Lidocaine is a weak base. The un-ionised form crosses the nerve membrane; inside, the ionised form blocks the sodium channel. In inflamed, acidic tissue (an abscess) less drug is un-ionised, which is why infiltration of infected tissue works poorly. Adding sodium bicarbonate (1 ml of 8.4% to 10 ml of lidocaine) reduces the sting of injection and speeds onset."],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s2-2","Handling","Lidocaine · How it works","Metabolised in the liver (CYP1A2 and 3A4) to active metabolites; clearance falls in liver disease and heart failure. Adrenaline slows absorption, prolongs the block, raises the safe dose and reduces bleeding."],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s3-1","Infiltration","Lidocaine · Using it in ENT","Inject slowly through a fine needle, aspirating before each injection in vascular areas, and wait 7 to 10 minutes for adrenaline's vasoconstriction. Adrenaline in the ear, nose and digits is safe in healthy tissue; avoid it in compromised flaps and use with care in patients with severe cardiovascular disease."],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s3-2","Topical airway anaesthesia","Lidocaine · Using it in ENT","Spray or atomise onto the mucosa; for awake fibreoptic intubation, the total topical dose up to about 9 mg/kg has been used under anaesthetic supervision, but in the clinic keep well below the injection limits. After laryngeal anaesthesia the patient should not eat or drink for about an hour (loss of protective reflexes)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s4-1","Recognise it","Lidocaine · Safety","Early: circumoral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus, light-headedness, slurred speech. Then: agitation, muscle twitching, seizures and loss of consciousness. Cardiovascular: bradycardia, arrhythmias, hypotension and cardiac arrest (more abrupt with bupivacaine). Toxicity can occur from inadvertent intravascular injection even at low doses. Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity 1 Stop stop injecting call for help 2 Airway and breathing 100% oxygen secure the airway avoid hypercapnia and acidosis 3 Seizures benzodiazepine (midazolam, lorazepam) or a small dose of propofol 4 Cardiovascular standard advanced life support arrhythmias may be refractory prolonged CPR may be needed 5 Lipid emulsion 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 ml/kg over 1 minute, then infusion 15 ml/kg/hour repeat boluses up to twice at 5-minute intervals and double the infusion rate if instability persists (maximum about 12 ml/kg in total)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s4-2","Other adverse effects","Lidocaine · Safety","Allergy to amide anaesthetics is very rare (often a reaction to preservatives or to adrenaline); methaemoglobinaemia is rare with lidocaine but more frequent with prilocaine and benzocaine."],["ev","cl:t-dr_lido~s4-3","Lidocaine: current evidence","Lidocaine · Safety","Maximum doses. Commonly quoted limits are 3 mg/kg for plain lidocaine and 7 mg/kg with adrenaline by infiltration; for topical airway anaesthesia the British Thoracic Society bronchoscopy guideline sets a maximum of 8.2 mg/kg. Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity. The UK AAGBI (Association of Anaesthetists) guideline advises stopping the injection, managing the airway and seizures, and giving 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 ml/kg as a bolus followed by an infusion if there is cardiovascular collapse."],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s4-3","Interactions","Lidocaine · Safety","Beta blockers and cimetidine reduce clearance; other antiarrhythmics add cardiac effects."],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s5-1","Viva essentials","Lidocaine · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Maximum doses with and without adrenaline, and converting a percentage to milligrams. Mechanism and why infiltration fails in infected tissue. Signs and management of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity , including lipid emulsion."],["sec","cl:t-dr_lido~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Lidocaine · For the examination","Common pitfalls Forgetting to add topical doses to injected doses. Injecting without aspirating in the scalp or face. Allowing a patient to drink straight after laryngeal anaesthesia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_linez~s1-1","Summary","Linezolid · At a glance","Linezolid Summary Class Oxazolidinone How it works Binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit and prevents formation of the initiation complex; bacteriostatic for staphylococci Spectrum MRSA, coagulase-negative staphylococci, streptococci including resistant pneumococci, enterococci including VRE, Nocardia, some mycobacteria; no Gram-negative activity ENT uses MRSA soft tissue, wound and neck infection; resistant Gram-positive infection needing oral treatment; part of regimens for drug-resistant tuberculosis Adult dose 600 mg twice daily orally or intravenously Child dose Under 12 years 10 mg/kg every 8 hours (maximum 600 mg); 12 years and over as adult Duration Usually up to 14 days without extra monitoring; longer courses need weekly blood counts Avoid Within 2 weeks of an MAOI; uncontrolled hypertension, phaeochromocytoma, thyrotoxicosis unless monitored Pregnancy Avoid unless essential"],["sec","cl:t-dr_linez~s2-1","Mechanism","Linezolid · How it works","A unique ribosomal site means no cross-resistance with other classes. It reduces toxin production, an advantage in toxin-mediated staphylococcal and streptococcal infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_linez~s2-2","Handling","Linezolid · How it works","About 100% oral bioavailability, so step-down from intravenous to oral therapy can happen early. Good soft tissue and bone penetration. Non-renal metabolism."],["sec","cl:t-dr_linez~s3-1","Adverse effects","Linezolid · Safety","Myelosuppression (thrombocytopenia, anaemia) with courses beyond 2 weeks: weekly full blood count. Peripheral and optic neuropathy with courses over 28 days; check vision. Lactic acidosis; hypoglycaemia in diabetics; C. difficile."],["ev","cl:t-dr_linez~s3-2","Linezolid: current evidence","Linezolid · Safety","Safety. Courses over about two weeks cause thrombocytopenia and anaemia (check the blood count weekly), and long courses cause peripheral and optic neuropathy and lactic acidosis. It is a weak MAOI, so serotonin syndrome can occur with SSRIs and other serotonergic drugs. Use. It is reserved for MRSA or resistant Gram-positive infection on microbiology advice; oral absorption is complete, allowing early switch from intravenous treatment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_linez~s3-2","Interactions","Linezolid · Safety","A weak, reversible MAO inhibitor : serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, SNRIs, tricyclics, triptans and pethidine; hypertensive reactions with sympathomimetics such as pseudoephedrine and large amounts of tyramine-rich food."],["sec","cl:t-dr_linez~s4-1","Viva essentials","Linezolid · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Its role in MRSA and VRE, and the advantage of oral bioavailability. Monitoring: blood count, vision, lactate. The serotonin syndrome and pseudoephedrine interactions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_linez~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Linezolid · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting linezolid in a patient on an SSRI without discussion and monitoring. Prolonged courses without blood counts."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mblue~s1-1","Summary","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes · At a glance","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes Summary Methylene blue (methylthioninium chloride) Historic intravenous parathyroid localisation; oral or topical use to demonstrate fistulas (pharyngocutaneous, salivary, tracheo-oesophageal puncture leaks) and to mark tracts; treatment of methaemoglobinaemia (1 to 2 mg/kg intravenously) Indocyanine green (ICG) Intravenous near-infrared fluorescent dye: assessing parathyroid perfusion after thyroidectomy, flap and skin perfusion, lymphatic mapping; contains iodide (caution in iodine allergy) Isosulfan blue and patent blue V Lymphatic mapping for sentinel node biopsy, usually with a radioactive tracer (technetium-99m) Parathyroid autofluorescence No dye needed: near-infrared light makes parathyroids glow, used to find and protect them at thyroidectomy"],["sec","cl:t-dr_mblue~s2-1","Fistula detection","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes · Using them in ENT","After laryngectomy or pharyngeal surgery, oral methylene blue appearing in the neck drain or wound confirms a pharyngocutaneous fistula; around a voice prosthesis, coloured water shows whether leakage is through or around the device."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mblue~s2-2","Sentinel node biopsy","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes · Using them in ENT","In early oral cavity cancer with a clinically negative neck, sentinel node biopsy uses a radioactive tracer injected around the tumour, lymphoscintigraphy, a gamma probe and sometimes a blue dye or fluorescent tracer."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mblue~s3-1","Methylene blue","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes · Safety","Serotonin syndrome when given intravenously to patients on SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, tricyclics, lithium or linezolid (it is a potent MAO-A inhibitor); confusion and neurotoxicity at high doses; haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; falsely low pulse oximetry readings; blue-green urine and skin."],["ev","cl:t-dr_mblue~s3-2","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes: current evidence","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes · Safety","Serotonin syndrome. The US FDA and UK MHRA warned that methylene blue (methylthioninium chloride), a potent MAO inhibitor, can cause serotonin syndrome in patients on SSRIs, SNRIs and other serotonergic drugs; avoid it or stop these drugs beforehand where possible. G6PD deficiency. It can cause haemolysis. Parathyroid localisation. Near-infrared autofluorescence and indocyanine green angiography are newer ways to find and assess parathyroid glands without these risks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mblue~s3-2","Other dyes","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes · Safety","Anaphylaxis to patent and isosulfan blue (around 1%); ICG anaphylaxis rarely, with iodide content."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mblue~s4-1","Viva essentials","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The serotonin syndrome warning with methylene blue. Modern parathyroid localisation and identification techniques. How to confirm a pharyngocutaneous fistula."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mblue~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Methylene blue and other surgical dyes · For the examination","Common pitfalls Intravenous methylene blue in a patient on an SSRI."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mepo~s1-1","Summary","Mepolizumab and anti-IL-5 biologics · At a glance","Anti-IL-5 biologics Summary Agents Mepolizumab and reslizumab (bind IL-5); benralizumab (binds the IL-5 receptor alpha and depletes eosinophils by antibody-dependent cytotoxicity) How they work Reduce eosinophil production, survival and tissue infiltration ENT uses Severe CRS with nasal polyps (mepolizumab is licensed in many countries); severe eosinophilic asthma; EGPA (mepolizumab 300 mg) Doses Mepolizumab 100 mg subcutaneously every 4 weeks (300 mg every 4 weeks in EGPA); benralizumab 30 mg every 4 weeks for 3 doses, then every 8 weeks; reslizumab intravenously by weight every 4 weeks Pregnancy Limited data; use only if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_mepo~s2-1","Polyps and asthma","Mepolizumab and anti-IL-5 biologics · Using them in ENT","For patients meeting the EPOS biologic criteria, especially those with blood eosinophilia and severe eosinophilic asthma, mepolizumab reduces polyp size and steroid use and the need for surgery. Response is assessed at 6 months and annually."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mepo~s2-2","Recognising EGPA","Mepolizumab and anti-IL-5 biologics · Using them in ENT","Adult-onset asthma, nasal polyps, eosinophilia above 10%, and then neuropathy, purpura, cardiac or renal involvement suggest EGPA (formerly Churg-Strauss syndrome); about 40% are ANCA (MPO) positive. Refer to rheumatology; mepolizumab is used for relapsing or refractory disease."],["ev","cl:t-dr_mepo~s3-1","Mepolizumab and anti-IL-5 biologics: current evidence","Mepolizumab and anti-IL-5 biologics · Safety","Trials. In SYNAPSE, mepolizumab every four weeks reduced polyp size and blockage and cut the need for surgery in severe recurrent nasal polyps (Han et al., Lancet Respir Med 2021). Choice. Anti-IL-5 drugs suit patients with high blood eosinophils and asthma; benralizumab, which depletes eosinophils, gave a smaller benefit in polyps. Tezepelumab, an anti-TSLP antibody, reduced polyp size, blockage and the need for surgery in the WAYPOINT trial (Lipworth et al., NEJM 2025)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mepo~s3-1","Adverse effects","Mepolizumab and anti-IL-5 biologics · Safety","Injection-site reactions, headache, back pain; hypersensitivity rarely. Herpes zoster has been reported: consider vaccination before starting. Helminth infection: treat before starting."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mepo~s4-1","Viva essentials","Mepolizumab and anti-IL-5 biologics · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The difference between IL-5 and IL-5 receptor antibodies. Features of EGPA and why ENT surgeons should spot it."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mepo~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Mepolizumab and anti-IL-5 biologics · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing EGPA in a patient with polyps, asthma and marked eosinophilia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s1-1","Summary","Meropenem · At a glance","Meropenem Summary Class Carbapenem (beta-lactam) How it works Cell wall synthesis inhibitor, stable to almost all beta-lactamases including ESBL and AmpC Spectrum Enterobacterales including ESBL producers, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter (some), streptococci, methicillin-sensitive S. aureus, anaerobes; not MRSA, not Enterococcus faecium, not Stenotrophomonas, not carbapenemase producers ENT uses Severe or hospital-acquired head and neck sepsis; descending mediastinitis; ESBL infection; meningitis and brain abscess with resistant Gram-negatives; failed treatment of skull base osteomyelitis Adult dose 1 g every 8 hours intravenously; 2 g every 8 hours for meningitis and severe infection Child dose 10 to 20 mg/kg every 8 hours; 40 mg/kg every 8 hours for meningitis (maximum 2 g per dose) Avoid Beta-lactam anaphylaxis (cross-reactivity with penicillin is low, about 1%) Pregnancy Use if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s2-1","Mechanism","Meropenem · How it works","Carbapenems bind multiple penicillin-binding proteins and resist hydrolysis by nearly all serine beta-lactamases. Resistance comes from carbapenemases (KPC, OXA-48, NDM) and porin loss, and it spreads; every unnecessary carbapenem prescription adds to the problem, which is why most hospitals restrict them to microbiology approval."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s2-2","Handling","Meropenem · How it works","Intravenous; renally excreted, so reduce the dose when the creatinine clearance is below 50 ml/min. It crosses the inflamed meninges and has a lower seizure risk than imipenem."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s3-1","Severe neck and mediastinal sepsis","Meropenem · Using it in ENT","Descending necrotising mediastinitis from a dental, pharyngeal or deep neck source carries a high mortality: secure the airway, drain the neck and mediastinum (with thoracic surgeons), and give broad cover such as meropenem with vancomycin or linezolid, narrowed by cultures."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s3-2","Resistant and hospital organisms","Meropenem · Using it in ENT","After major resection and free-flap reconstruction, fistula and wound infection may involve ESBL Enterobacterales or resistant Pseudomonas: meropenem is used when cultures demand it, not as a reflex."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s4-1","Adverse effects","Meropenem · Safety","Diarrhoea, C. difficile and candidiasis; selection of resistant organisms. Seizures (less than imipenem), particularly in renal failure or CNS disease. Hypersensitivity, raised liver enzymes, thrombocytopenia."],["ev","cl:t-dr_mero~s4-2","Meropenem: current evidence","Meropenem · Safety","Stewardship. Carbapenems are reserved for confirmed or strongly suspected resistant Gram-negative infection (ESBL producers) or severe intracranial infection when first-line drugs fail, because carbapenem resistance is rising worldwide, including in Egypt. Interaction. Meropenem lowers valproate levels sharply within a day, risking seizures; use another antibiotic or another antiepileptic."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s4-2","Interactions","Meropenem · Safety","Sodium valproate : carbapenems lower valproate levels profoundly within a day, and seizures follow; the combination should be avoided."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s5-1","Viva essentials","Meropenem · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for What carbapenems cover and what they do not ( MRSA , E. faecium, Stenotrophomonas). The valproate interaction. Antimicrobial stewardship: microbiology advice, cultures first, review at 48 to 72 hours."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mero~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Meropenem · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using meropenem first-line for community-acquired head and neck infection. Forgetting MRSA cover in the septic post-operative patient. Giving it to a patient taking valproate."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s1-1","Summary","Metronidazole · At a glance","Metronidazole Summary Class Nitroimidazole How it works Reduced inside anaerobic cells to reactive nitro radicals that break DNA; bactericidal Spectrum Obligate anaerobes (Bacteroides, Prevotella, Fusobacterium, Peptostreptococcus, Clostridia), protozoa (Trichomonas, Giardia, Entamoeba); no activity against aerobes or Actinomyces ENT uses With a beta-lactam for quinsy, deep neck infection, dental abscess, Lemierre syndrome, otogenic and sinogenic intracranial abscess, chronic and complicated sinusitis, necrotising ulcerative gingivitis; head and neck surgical prophylaxis; C. difficile (oral) in mild disease where vancomycin is not available Adult dose Oral 400 mg three times daily (or 500 mg three times daily); intravenous 500 mg every 8 hours; rectal 1 g every 8 hours Child dose 7.5 mg/kg three times daily orally or intravenously (maximum 500 mg) Avoid Alcohol during and for 48 hours after; caution in liver disease Pregnancy Avoid high-dose regimens; normal doses used when needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s2-1","Mechanism","Metronidazole · How it works","A prodrug activated only in the low-redox environment of anaerobes, which is why it is selective. Resistance is rare."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s2-2","Handling","Metronidazole · How it works","Almost completely absorbed orally (so intravenous use is only for patients who cannot swallow), penetrates abscesses and the brain very well, metabolised in the liver."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s3-1","Mixed infections of the head and neck","Metronidazole · Using it in ENT","Odontogenic, peritonsillar, parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal infections and Lemierre syndrome (Fusobacterium necrophorum thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein) are mixed or anaerobic: give a penicillin or cephalosporin with metronidazole, and drain collections."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s3-2","Intracranial complications","Metronidazole · Using it in ENT","For otogenic or sinogenic brain abscess , subdural empyema and venous sinus thrombosis, metronidazole with high-dose ceftriaxone is the standard empirical combination because of its excellent brain penetration."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s3-3","Prophylaxis","Metronidazole · Using it in ENT","Given with cefuroxime or co-amoxiclav at induction for clean-contaminated surgery such as laryngectomy and oral cavity resection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s4-1","Adverse effects","Metronidazole · Safety","Metallic taste, nausea, furred tongue, dark urine. Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol: flushing, vomiting and tachycardia. Peripheral and optic neuropathy and encephalopathy with prolonged courses (beyond about 10 days or high totals); seizures. Hepatotoxicity, severe in Cockayne syndrome."],["ev","cl:t-dr_metro~s4-2","Metronidazole: current evidence","Metronidazole · Safety","Alcohol. Evidence for a true disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol is weak, but the standard advice to avoid alcohol during treatment and for 48 hours after remains in product information. Neurotoxicity. Courses over about four weeks can cause peripheral and optic neuropathy and, rarely, encephalopathy. Anaerobes. It is added to co-trimoxazole, cefuroxime or ceftriaxone for deep neck space and odontogenic infections; it is not needed with co-amoxiclav or piperacillin–tazobactam, which already cover anaerobes."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s4-2","Interactions","Metronidazole · Safety","Warfarin (marked rise in INR), alcohol, lithium (toxicity), phenytoin and busulfan; phenobarbital lowers its levels."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s5-1","Viva essentials","Metronidazole · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Selectivity for anaerobes and why that is. Organisms of Lemierre syndrome and its treatment. The alcohol and warfarin interactions; neuropathy with long courses."],["sec","cl:t-dr_metro~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Metronidazole · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving intravenous metronidazole to a patient who can swallow. Forgetting to warn about alcohol. Long courses without review for neuropathy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_migr~s1-1","Summary","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis · At a glance","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis Summary Agents Propranolol; amitriptyline (or nortriptyline); topiramate; pizotifen; candesartan; venlafaxine; flunarizine (not licensed in the UK); CGRP monoclonal antibodies (erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab) for refractory migraine under specialists Indications Frequent (for example several a month) or disabling attacks despite lifestyle measures Adult doses Propranolol 40 mg twice or three times daily, up to 80 to 160 mg a day; amitriptyline 10 mg at night, increasing to 25 to 50 mg; topiramate 25 mg at night, increasing to 50 mg twice daily; pizotifen 1.5 mg at night; candesartan 8 to 16 mg daily; venlafaxine 75 to 150 mg daily Trial length At least 2 to 3 months at an adequate dose before judging"],["sec","cl:t-dr_migr~s2-1","Matching drug to patient","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis · Using them in ENT","Propranolol : good with anxiety or hypertension; avoid in asthma, heart block, and use with care in depression and diabetes. Amitriptyline : good with insomnia, tension-type headache or neck pain; causes drowsiness, dry mouth and weight gain; cautious in the elderly (anticholinergic, falls, cardiac). Topiramate : suits overweight patients; causes paraesthesia, cognitive slowing, kidney stones and weight loss; it is teratogenic , so women of childbearing potential need a pregnancy prevention programme and highly effective contraception (it also reduces the effect of oral contraceptives). Pizotifen : causes weight gain and drowsiness. Venlafaxine : when anxiety or depression coexists."],["sec","cl:t-dr_migr~s2-2","Acute treatment","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis · Using them in ENT","Triptans may help headache; vestibular sedatives for severe vertigo; antiemetics."],["ev","cl:t-dr_migr~s3-1","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis: current evidence","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis · Safety","Diagnosis. The Bárány Society and International Headache Society criteria define vestibular migraine (Lempert et al., J Vestib Res 2012, updated 2022). Prophylaxis. A Cochrane review found the evidence for drugs to prevent vestibular migraine very uncertain, with few good trials (Webster et al., 2023); propranolol, amitriptyline, venlafaxine, topiramate and flunarizine are used on the basis of migraine evidence and small studies. Newer CGRP monoclonal antibodies work for migraine and are being studied in vestibular migraine. Lifestyle (sleep, regular meals, trigger avoidance) and vestibular rehabilitation help many."],["sec","cl:t-dr_migr~s3-1","Key cautions","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis · Safety","Beta blockers mask hypoglycaemia; tricyclics are dangerous in overdose; topiramate and valproate must not be used in pregnancy; sodium valproate is generally avoided in women who could become pregnant."],["sec","cl:t-dr_migr~s4-1","Viva essentials","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Diagnostic criteria of vestibular migraine (Bárány Society and International Headache Society). The prophylactic drugs and how comorbidities guide choice. Topiramate and pregnancy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_migr~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Vestibular migraine prophylaxis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Stopping prophylaxis after 2 weeks for lack of effect. Prescribing topiramate without a contraception discussion."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mmc~s1-1","Summary","Mitomycin C · At a glance","Mitomycin C Summary Class Alkylating antitumour antibiotic How it works Cross-links DNA and inhibits fibroblast proliferation and collagen formation ENT uses Adjunct after endoscopic incision and dilatation of subglottic and tracheal stenosis; choanal atresia repair; endoscopic dacryocystorhinostomy; frontal sinus and other stenoses; glottic webs Dose Topical 0.4 to 0.5 mg/ml on a cottonoid or pledget for 2 to 5 minutes, then irrigation with saline Handling Cytotoxic: prepare and dispose of according to cytotoxic protocols; avoid contact with skin and eyes"],["sec","cl:t-dr_mmc~s2-1","Airway stenosis","Mitomycin C · Using it in ENT","After radial incisions and balloon dilatation of an idiopathic or post-intubation subglottic stenosis, topical mitomycin may lengthen the interval between procedures in some series; randomised evidence is limited. Inhaled steroids, co-trimoxazole in some idiopathic cases, and treatment of reflux are other adjuncts."],["ev","cl:t-dr_mmc~s3-1","Mitomycin C: current evidence","Mitomycin C · Safety","Evidence is mixed. Topical mitomycin C improved success of dacryocystorhinostomy in meta-analyses, but has not been shown to prevent restenosis after choanal atresia repair, and trials in laryngotracheal stenosis give conflicting results. It may help as an adjunct after endoscopic division of short airway stenoses. Safety. Handle it as a cytotoxic agent; avoid contact with large raw areas and repeated high doses."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mmc~s3-1","Adverse effects","Mitomycin C · Safety","Excessive fibrinous debris or granulation that can obstruct a small airway (check endoscopically), delayed healing, and theoretical carcinogenicity; systemic absorption is negligible at topical doses."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mmc~s4-1","Viva essentials","Mitomycin C · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism and concentration. Where it is used and the limits of the evidence."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mmc~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Mitomycin C · For the examination","Common pitfalls Leaving fibrinous debris in a child's airway after mitomycin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_montel~s1-1","Summary","Montelukast · At a glance","Montelukast Summary Class Leukotriene receptor antagonist How it works Blocks the CysLT1 receptor, reducing bronchoconstriction, mucosal oedema, mucus and eosinophil recruitment ENT uses Allergic rhinitis with asthma; aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (Samter's triad) with nasal polyps; mild paediatric obstructive sleep apnoea (with nasal steroids) as an alternative or bridge to adenotonsillectomy Adult dose 10 mg once daily in the evening Child dose 6 months to 5 years 4 mg; 6 to 14 years 5 mg; 15 years and over 10 mg; once daily in the evening Pregnancy Use if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_montel~s2-1","Mechanism","Montelukast · How it works","Cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4) are released by mast cells and eosinophils. In aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease , inhibition of cyclo-oxygenase shunts arachidonic acid into the lipoxygenase pathway and floods the airways with leukotrienes, which is why leukotriene modifiers help these patients."],["sec","cl:t-dr_montel~s3-1","Allergic rhinitis","Montelukast · Using it in ENT","Less effective than nasal steroids and similar to oral antihistamines; most useful when asthma coexists."],["sec","cl:t-dr_montel~s3-2","Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease","Montelukast · Using it in ENT","Asthma, nasal polyps and respiratory reactions to aspirin and NSAIDs: montelukast helps nasal and asthma symptoms alongside nasal steroids, surgery and, in selected patients, aspirin desensitisation or biologics."],["sec","cl:t-dr_montel~s3-3","Childhood sleep-disordered breathing","Montelukast · Using it in ENT","Montelukast with a nasal steroid for 12 weeks reduces the apnoea-hypopnoea index in children with mild sleep apnoea and adenotonsillar hypertrophy; it is an option when surgery is not wanted or not yet possible."],["ev","cl:t-dr_montel~s4-1","Montelukast: current evidence","Montelukast · Safety","Neuropsychiatric effects. The US FDA added a boxed warning in 2020, and the UK MHRA reinforced its warning in 2024, about serious neuropsychiatric reactions, including nightmares, agitation, depression and suicidal thoughts, in adults and children. Place in treatment. Montelukast is less effective than intranasal steroids for allergic rhinitis, so ARIA and the FDA advise keeping it for patients who also have asthma or who cannot use other treatments; its benefit for adenoid hypertrophy is uncertain."],["sec","cl:t-dr_montel~s4-1","Adverse effects","Montelukast · Safety","Neuropsychiatric reactions : sleep disturbance, nightmares, agitation, depression and, rarely, suicidal thoughts. Regulators (MHRA, FDA) require warnings; tell families and stop the drug if they occur. Headache, abdominal pain; rarely eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis is unmasked as steroids are reduced."],["sec","cl:t-dr_montel~s5-1","Viva essentials","Montelukast · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The leukotriene shunt in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease . Its limited role compared with nasal steroids. The neuropsychiatric warning."],["sec","cl:t-dr_montel~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Montelukast · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting montelukast in a child without warning the family about mood and sleep effects."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mpred~s1-1","Summary","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone · At a glance","Hydrocortisone and methylprednisolone Summary Agents Hydrocortisone (short-acting, with mineralocorticoid effect); methylprednisolone (intermediate-acting, potent, little mineralocorticoid effect) ENT uses Adrenal crisis; perioperative steroid cover; adjunct in anaphylaxis and angioedema; methylprednisolone pulses for severe GPA, autoimmune inner ear disease and severe inflammatory airway disease; intratympanic methylprednisolone for Ménière's disease Adrenal crisis Hydrocortisone 100 mg intravenously or intramuscularly at once, then 200 mg over 24 hours by infusion (or 50 mg every 6 hours), with fluids Perioperative cover For patients on 5 mg or more of prednisolone (or equivalent) for 4 weeks or more: hydrocortisone 100 mg at induction, then for major surgery 200 mg over 24 hours until eating, then double the usual oral dose for 48 hours (minor surgery: usual dose plus 100 mg at induction, then double dose for 24 to 48 hours) Pulses Methylprednisolone 500 mg to 1 g intravenously daily for 3 days Child dose Hydrocortisone in adrenal crisis 2 mg/kg (maximum 100 mg) then as advised; methylprednisolone pulses 10 to 30 mg/kg (maximum 1 g)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_mpred~s2-1","The patient on long-term steroids","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone · Using them in ENT","Any patient on long-term steroids, or who has stopped them within the past 3 months, may not mount a stress response. Surgery, vomiting or severe infection can precipitate an adrenal crisis : hypotension, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia and collapse. Plan perioperative cover with the anaesthetist (national guidance from the Association of Anaesthetists and the Society for Endocrinology) and give hydrocortisone at once to any such patient who is unwell."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mpred~s2-2","Vasculitis and autoimmune ear disease","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone · Using them in ENT","Severe or organ-threatening granulomatosis with polyangiitis (subglottic stenosis with airway compromise, orbital disease, rapidly progressive renal disease) is induced with methylprednisolone pulses and rituximab or cyclophosphamide under rheumatology. Autoimmune inner ear disease (rapidly progressive bilateral sensorineural loss) is treated with high-dose prednisolone (1 mg/kg for 4 weeks), sometimes after an intravenous pulse."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mpred~s2-3","Anaphylaxis","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone · Using them in ENT","UK resuscitation guidance no longer recommends routine steroids in the initial treatment of anaphylaxis: adrenaline and fluids come first. Steroids may be given for refractory reactions or asthma."],["ev","cl:t-dr_mpred~s3-1","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone: current evidence","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone · Safety","Vestibular neuritis. In a randomised trial, methylprednisolone improved recovery of peripheral vestibular function (Strupp et al., NEJM 2004). Adrenal crisis. UK guidance recommends hydrocortisone 100 mg intramuscularly or intravenously at once, then 200 mg over 24 hours, for patients on long-term steroids or with adrenal insufficiency who are unwell, vomiting or having surgery, and emergency steroid cards for all at risk (Society for Endocrinology, 2020). GPA. In PEXIVAS, a reduced-dose steroid regimen was as effective as standard dosing with fewer serious infections (Walsh et al., NEJM 2020)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mpred~s3-1","Adverse effects","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone · Safety","As for all glucocorticoids, magnified by pulses: hyperglycaemia, hypertension, fluid retention (hydrocortisone), psychosis, infection, arrhythmia with rapid pulses (infuse methylprednisolone over at least 30 minutes), avascular necrosis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mpred~s4-1","Viva essentials","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Who needs perioperative steroid cover and the regimen. Recognition and treatment of adrenal crisis . Pulsed methylprednisolone in GPA and its indications."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mpred~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Methylprednisolone and hydrocortisone · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing a patient's steroid history before a major head and neck operation. Delaying hydrocortisone in a collapsed patient on long-term steroids."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mtx~s1-1","Summary","Methotrexate · At a glance","Methotrexate Summary Class Antifolate antimetabolite and immunosuppressant How it works Inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (cytotoxic doses); at low doses, increases adenosine release and inhibits T-cell activation (anti-inflammatory) ENT uses Palliative treatment of recurrent or metastatic HNSCC (weekly intravenous); maintenance of remission in non-severe granulomatosis with polyangiitis; autoimmune inner ear disease (steroid-sparing, limited evidence); sarcoidosis Dose Palliative 40 mg/m2 intravenously weekly; immunosuppression commonly 15 to 25 mg orally or subcutaneously once weekly , with folic acid (typically 5 mg weekly on another day) Monitoring Full blood count, liver and renal function every 2 weeks at first, then every 2 to 3 months Avoid Pregnancy (teratogenic; stop 3 to 6 months before conception), significant renal or liver impairment, active infection"],["sec","cl:t-dr_mtx~s2-1","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Methotrexate · Using it in ENT","After induction with steroids and rituximab or cyclophosphamide, maintenance is often with rituximab, azathioprine or methotrexate; limited sinonasal disease may be treated with methotrexate and steroids. ENT management of crusting, septal perforation, saddle nose and subglottic stenosis continues alongside."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mtx~s3-1","Adverse effects","Methotrexate · Safety","Mucositis, myelosuppression, hepatotoxicity and fibrosis, pneumonitis (cough and breathlessness: stop and investigate), nausea, infection risk, teratogenicity."],["ev","cl:t-dr_mtx~s3-2","Methotrexate: current evidence","Methotrexate · Safety","Weekly dosing. Accidental daily dosing has caused deaths; the UK MHRA (2020) and safety bodies require a fixed weekly day, clear labelling and patient education, with folic acid on a different day. Monitoring. Full blood count, renal and liver function regularly; avoid trimethoprim and co-trimoxazole at treatment doses, which increase marrow toxicity. Head and neck use. Weekly methotrexate for recurrent squamous carcinoma has been largely replaced by immunotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mtx~s3-2","Interactions","Methotrexate · Safety","Trimethoprim and co-trimoxazole (severe marrow toxicity), NSAIDs, penicillins and PPIs (reduced excretion), other hepatotoxic drugs and alcohol."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mtx~s4-1","Viva essentials","Methotrexate · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Once-weekly dosing and folic acid. The co-trimoxazole interaction. Its role in GPA."],["sec","cl:t-dr_mtx~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Methotrexate · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing methotrexate daily. Adding co-trimoxazole for a sinus infection in a patient on methotrexate."],["sec","cl:t-dr_naseptin~s1-1","Summary","Naseptin and mupirocin · At a glance","Naseptin and mupirocin Summary Preparations Naseptin: chlorhexidine dihydrochloride 0.1% and neomycin sulfate 0.5% cream (contains arachis oil); mupirocin 2% nasal ointment How they work Chlorhexidine disrupts bacterial membranes; neomycin inhibits protein synthesis; mupirocin inhibits isoleucyl-tRNA synthetase ENT uses Recurrent childhood epistaxis; nasal vestibulitis and crusting; after cautery to reduce crusting and rebleeding; S. aureus carriage (Naseptin); MRSA decolonisation before surgery (mupirocin) Doses Naseptin applied to the inside of each nostril four times daily for 10 days to eradicate staphylococci, or twice daily for 2 to 4 weeks for recurrent epistaxis; mupirocin to the inside of each nostril two or three times daily for 5 days, with chlorhexidine body wash Avoid Naseptin in peanut or soya allergy; mupirocin for longer than 10 days or repeated courses (resistance)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_naseptin~s2-1","Recurrent epistaxis in children","Naseptin and mupirocin · Using them in ENT","Bleeding from Little's area in children is often associated with vestibulitis and crusting; antiseptic cream for 4 weeks reduces bleeding about as well as silver nitrate cautery. Petroleum jelly is a cheaper alternative that also helps."],["sec","cl:t-dr_naseptin~s2-2","MRSA decolonisation","Naseptin and mupirocin · Using them in ENT","Before elective head and neck surgery in carriers: mupirocin nasally for 5 days with daily chlorhexidine washes, then re-screen."],["ev","cl:t-dr_naseptin~s3-1","Naseptin and mupirocin: current evidence","Naseptin and mupirocin · Safety","Recurrent epistaxis in children. Antiseptic creams such as Naseptin are widely used and help many children in cohort studies, but a Cochrane review found no good evidence that any treatment, including cream or cautery, is better than none (Qureishi and Burton, 2012). Allergy. Naseptin contains arachis (peanut) oil and is contraindicated in peanut or soya allergy; mupirocin is an alternative. Mupirocin decolonisation of S. aureus carriers before major surgery reduces surgical site infection in randomised trials."],["sec","cl:t-dr_naseptin~s3-1","Adverse effects","Naseptin and mupirocin · Safety","Local irritation, rash; neomycin contact sensitivity; anaphylaxis in peanut allergy (Naseptin); mupirocin resistance with prolonged use."],["sec","cl:t-dr_naseptin~s4-1","Viva essentials","Naseptin and mupirocin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The arachis oil warning. Management of recurrent childhood epistaxis. MRSA decolonisation regimen."],["sec","cl:t-dr_naseptin~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Naseptin and mupirocin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing Naseptin without asking about nut allergy. Repeated courses of mupirocin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_neuro~s1-1","Summary","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain · At a glance","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain Summary Carbamazepine First-line for trigeminal and glossopharyngeal neuralgia: 100 mg once or twice daily, increased gradually to the lowest effective dose (commonly 200 mg three or four times daily, maximum 1.6 g a day); oxcarbazepine is an alternative Gabapentin 300 mg at night, increasing by 300 mg every few days to 900 to 3,600 mg a day in three doses Pregabalin 75 mg twice daily, increasing to 150 to 300 mg twice daily Amitriptyline 10 mg at night, increasing to 25 to 75 mg (nortriptyline is better tolerated) Duloxetine 60 mg once daily Burning mouth syndrome Topical clonazepam (dissolved in the mouth and spat out), alpha-lipoic acid, cognitive behavioural therapy, amitriptyline, gabapentin ENT uses Trigeminal and glossopharyngeal neuralgia; burning mouth syndrome; post-herpetic neuralgia (after Ramsay Hunt or trigeminal zoster); persistent idiopathic facial pain; post-surgical neuropathic pain; chronic tension-type headache presenting as \"sinus\" pain"],["sec","cl:t-dr_neuro~s2-1","Neuralgias","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain · Using them in ENT","Trigeminal neuralgia causes electric-shock pains triggered by touch, eating or talking; glossopharyngeal neuralgia causes similar pains in the throat, tongue base and ear on swallowing, occasionally with syncope. Carbamazepine helps most; MRI looks for a vascular loop, multiple sclerosis or a tumour; microvascular decompression is considered when drugs fail. Eagle syndrome (elongated styloid) is a differential."],["sec","cl:t-dr_neuro~s2-2","\"Sinus\" headache","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain · Using them in ENT","Most patients referred with facial pain and a normal nasal endoscopy and CT do not have sinusitis; migraine, tension-type headache and midfacial segment pain respond to amitriptyline or migraine treatment, not to sinus surgery."],["ev","cl:t-dr_neuro~s3-1","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain: current evidence","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain · Safety","Trigeminal neuralgia. The European Academy of Neurology guideline recommends carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine first line, and microvascular decompression for patients with neurovascular compression who fail or cannot tolerate drugs (Bendtsen et al., Eur J Neurol 2019). Gabapentinoids. Gabapentin and pregabalin became controlled drugs in the UK in 2019 because of misuse and respiratory depression with opioids, and the MHRA (2022) warned of a possible small increase in malformations with pregabalin in pregnancy. Neuropathic pain. NICE (CG173) recommends amitriptyline, duloxetine, gabapentin or pregabalin as first choices."],["sec","cl:t-dr_neuro~s3-1","Adverse effects","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain · Safety","Carbamazepine : dizziness, ataxia, diplopia, hyponatraemia, leucopenia, raised liver enzymes; Stevens-Johnson syndrome , strongly linked to HLA-B*15:02 in patients of Han Chinese, Thai and some other Asian ancestry (test before starting); potent enzyme inducer (reduces contraceptive pill, warfarin and many other drugs); teratogenic. Gabapentinoids : drowsiness, dizziness, weight gain, misuse and dependence (controlled drugs in the UK), respiratory depression with opioids. Tricyclics : anticholinergic effects, sedation, postural hypotension, cardiac toxicity in overdose."],["sec","cl:t-dr_neuro~s4-1","Viva essentials","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Features of glossopharyngeal neuralgia and its treatment. HLA-B*15:02 testing before carbamazepine. Facial pain that is not sinusitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_neuro~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Drugs for neuropathic and facial pain · For the examination","Common pitfalls Sinus surgery for facial pain with a normal CT. Starting carbamazepine at full dose."],["sec","cl:t-dr_nsaid~s1-1","Summary","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac · At a glance","NSAIDs Summary Agents Ibuprofen; naproxen; diclofenac; ketorolac (parenteral); celecoxib and etoricoxib (COX-2 selective) How they work Inhibit cyclo-oxygenase 1 and 2, reducing prostaglandins and thromboxane; analgesic, anti-inflammatory and antipyretic ENT uses Post-operative analgesia (tonsillectomy, ear, nasal, thyroid); acute otitis media and sore throat; inflammatory conditions (salivary, TMJ) Adult dose Ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily (maximum 2.4 g a day); naproxen 250 to 500 mg twice daily; diclofenac 50 mg two or three times daily (maximum 150 mg a day) Child dose Ibuprofen 5 to 10 mg/kg three or four times daily (maximum 30 mg/kg a day, up to 2.4 g), from 3 months and 5 kg Avoid Active peptic ulcer or bleeding; severe heart failure; severe renal impairment; aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease; third trimester of pregnancy; diclofenac in ischaemic heart, peripheral arterial or cerebrovascular disease; dehydration (renal injury); chickenpox (serious skin infection reported)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_nsaid~s2-1","Tonsillectomy and bleeding","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac · Using them in ENT","The Cochrane review and subsequent work show that ibuprofen and similar NSAIDs, given with paracetamol, give good analgesia after tonsillectomy without a significant increase in bleeding needing surgery, and reduce the need for opioids, which is particularly important in children with sleep apnoea. Many guidelines (including the AAO-HNS) support ibuprofen after tonsillectomy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_nsaid~s2-2","Asthma","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac · Using them in ENT","Most asthmatics tolerate NSAIDs. Those with aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (asthma, nasal polyps, and reactions to aspirin or NSAIDs) must avoid COX-1 inhibitors; COX-2 selective agents are usually tolerated."],["sec","cl:t-dr_nsaid~s3-1","Adverse effects","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac · Safety","Dyspepsia, peptic ulceration and gastrointestinal bleeding : give a PPI with long-term use or in the elderly. Acute kidney injury , particularly with dehydration, ACE inhibitors and diuretics (the \"triple whammy\"). Fluid retention, hypertension, heart failure, and increased cardiovascular events (diclofenac and COX-2 inhibitors more than naproxen and low-dose ibuprofen). Bronchospasm in aspirin-sensitive asthma."],["ev","cl:t-dr_nsaid~s3-2","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac: current evidence","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac · Safety","Tonsillectomy. A Cochrane review found that NSAIDs did not increase bleeding needing return to theatre after tonsillectomy (Bailey et al., 2013), and the American tonsillectomy guideline recommends ibuprofen and paracetamol for pain control (Mitchell et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019). Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease. Patients with asthma and nasal polyps may react to any COX-1 inhibitor; COX-2 inhibitors are usually tolerated. Kidneys and heart. Avoid in dehydration, renal impairment and heart failure."],["sec","cl:t-dr_nsaid~s3-2","Interactions","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac · Safety","Warfarin, DOACs, SSRIs and steroids (bleeding); ACE inhibitors and diuretics (renal failure); methotrexate and lithium (toxicity)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_nsaid~s4-1","Viva essentials","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The evidence on NSAIDs and post-tonsillectomy bleeding . Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease and NSAIDs. The triple whammy and other key cautions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_nsaid~s4-2","Common pitfalls","NSAIDs: ibuprofen and diclofenac · For the examination","Common pitfalls Withholding ibuprofen after tonsillectomy and prescribing codeine instead. NSAIDs in a dehydrated child who is not drinking."],["sec","cl:t-dr_omal~s1-1","Summary","Omalizumab · At a glance","Omalizumab Summary Class Anti-IgE monoclonal antibody How it works Binds the Fc region of free IgE, reduces circulating IgE and downregulates high-affinity IgE receptors on mast cells and basophils ENT uses Severe chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps; severe allergic asthma; chronic spontaneous urticaria Dose Subcutaneously every 2 or 4 weeks; for asthma and polyps the dose (75 to 600 mg) is set from a table by baseline total IgE and body weight; urticaria 300 mg every 4 weeks Pregnancy Use if clearly needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_omal~s2-1","Mechanism","Omalizumab · How it works","By removing free IgE it blunts both allergen-triggered and non-specific type 2 activation. Total IgE measured during treatment rises (complexes) and cannot be used to adjust the dose."],["sec","cl:t-dr_omal~s3-1","Polyposis","Omalizumab · Using it in ENT","Eligible patients are as for other biologics (EPOS criteria): severe, type 2 polyposis not controlled by surgery and medical therapy. Response is assessed at 16 to 24 weeks."],["ev","cl:t-dr_omal~s4-1","Omalizumab: current evidence","Omalizumab · Safety","Trials. In POLYP 1 and POLYP 2, omalizumab reduced polyp size and congestion and improved smell and quality of life compared with placebo (Gevaert et al., J Allergy Clin Immunol 2020). In the head-to-head EVEREST trial (De Corso et al., Lancet Respir Med 2025), dupilumab was more effective than omalizumab for polyp size and smell. Urticaria. Omalizumab is the standard next step for chronic spontaneous urticaria not controlled by high-dose antihistamines."],["sec","cl:t-dr_omal~s4-1","Adverse effects","Omalizumab · Safety","Anaphylaxis in about 0.1% to 0.2%, sometimes delayed or after several doses: give the first doses where anaphylaxis can be treated, observe after injection, and consider an adrenaline auto-injector. Injection-site reactions, headache, arthralgia. Possible helminth infection risk; caution in parasitic areas."],["sec","cl:t-dr_omal~s5-1","Viva essentials","Omalizumab · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism (free IgE) and dosing by IgE and weight. Anaphylaxis risk and precautions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_omal~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Omalizumab · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using total IgE during treatment to judge response."],["sec","cl:t-dr_opioid~s1-1","Summary","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · At a glance","Opioids Summary Agents Codeine and dihydrocodeine (weak); tramadol; morphine; oxycodone; fentanyl (anaesthesia); naloxone (antagonist) How they work Mu-opioid receptor agonists in the brain and spinal cord; codeine is a prodrug converted to morphine by CYP2D6; tramadol also inhibits noradrenaline and serotonin reuptake ENT uses Severe post-operative pain not controlled by regular paracetamol and NSAIDs; cancer pain in head and neck malignancy; pain from mucositis during radiotherapy Codeine Contraindicated in children under 12 and in under-18s having tonsillectomy or adenoidectomy for obstructive sleep apnoea (MHRA and EMA); avoid in breastfeeding and known ultra-rapid metabolisers Morphine Oral immediate-release adult 5 to 10 mg every 4 hours as needed (lower in the elderly and in sleep apnoea); child 1 to 12 years 200 to 300 micrograms/kg every 4 hours (specialist and monitored use) Naloxone 100 to 200 micrograms intravenously, repeated every 2 to 3 minutes, titrated to breathing (larger doses in opioid overdose); it wears off before most opioids, so infusion or repeat doses may be needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_opioid~s2-1","After tonsillectomy","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · Using them in ENT","Regular paracetamol and ibuprofen are the basis of analgesia; if more is needed, use a short course of an opioid other than codeine in adults, at the lowest dose, and in children only with monitoring in hospital. Children with obstructive sleep apnoea have increased sensitivity to opioids (possibly from intermittent hypoxia upregulating receptors), so doses should be reduced."],["sec","cl:t-dr_opioid~s2-2","Sleep apnoea and airway surgery","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · Using them in ENT","Patients with obstructive sleep apnoea need lower doses, CPAP on the ward, avoidance of other sedatives, and monitoring of oxygen saturation. Opioids with benzodiazepines or gabapentinoids sharply increase the risk of respiratory depression."],["sec","cl:t-dr_opioid~s2-3","Head and neck cancer pain","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · Using them in ENT","Follow the analgesic ladder, use liquid or transdermal preparations when swallowing is difficult, prescribe laxatives with every regular opioid, and involve palliative care."],["sec","cl:t-dr_opioid~s3-1","Adverse effects","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · Safety","Respiratory depression , sedation, nausea and vomiting, constipation, urinary retention, pruritus, confusion and falls; tolerance and dependence with longer use; serotonin syndrome and seizures with tramadol."],["ev","cl:t-dr_opioid~s3-2","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol: current evidence","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · Safety","Codeine and tramadol in children. The UK MHRA (2013), EMA and US FDA (2017) contraindicated codeine in children under 12, and in under-18s after tonsillectomy or adenoidectomy for sleep apnoea, after deaths in ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolisers; the FDA extended this to tramadol. After surgery. Prescribing small quantities for short periods reduces persistent opioid use; most ENT procedures need only paracetamol and an NSAID. Sleep apnoea. Opioids increase the risk of respiratory depression; monitor patients with severe apnoea."],["sec","cl:t-dr_opioid~s3-2","Interactions","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · Safety","Benzodiazepines, gabapentinoids, alcohol and other sedatives (respiratory depression); SSRIs and MAOIs with tramadol (serotonin syndrome); CYP2D6 inhibitors (fluoxetine, paroxetine) block codeine and tramadol activation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_opioid~s4-1","Viva essentials","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why codeine is contraindicated after tonsillectomy in children (CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolism). Opioid sensitivity in obstructive sleep apnoea. Naloxone dosing and its short duration."],["sec","cl:t-dr_opioid~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Opioids: codeine, morphine, oxycodone and tramadol · For the examination","Common pitfalls Discharging a child after tonsillectomy with codeine. Opioids plus gabapentin plus a benzodiazepine in an obese patient after airway surgery. No laxative with a regular opioid."],["sec","cl:t-dr_oralaf~s1-1","Summary","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel · At a glance","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel Summary Agents Nystatin oral suspension 100,000 units/ml (a polyene); miconazole oral gel 20 mg/g (an imidazole) ENT uses Oral thrush (in infants, inhaled steroid users, after antibiotics, during radiotherapy); denture stomatitis; angular cheilitis (miconazole cream or gel) Adult dose Nystatin 1 ml four times daily after food, held in the mouth, for 7 days and continued for 48 hours after resolution; miconazole gel 2.5 ml four times daily after food, held in the mouth, for 7 days and 7 days after resolution Child dose Nystatin 1 ml four times daily; miconazole gel 4 months to 2 years 1.25 ml four times daily, older children 2.5 ml four times daily Avoid Miconazole with warfarin; miconazole in infants under 4 months or with poor swallowing (choking)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_oralaf~s2-1","Practice","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel · Using them in ENT","Treat the cause (rinse after steroid inhalers, review antibiotics, check glucose and HIV status if unexplained). For dentures, remove them at night and soak them in an antifungal or chlorhexidine solution, and apply the gel to the fitting surface. Fluconazole is used for extensive or resistant infection and in the immunocompromised."],["sec","cl:t-dr_oralaf~s3-1","Adverse effects","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel · Safety","Nystatin: nausea, unpleasant taste. Miconazole: nausea, taste change."],["ev","cl:t-dr_oralaf~s3-2","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel: current evidence","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel · Safety","Warfarin. The UK MHRA warned in 2016 that miconazole oral gel, even topically, can markedly raise the INR in patients on warfarin, with serious bleeding; avoid the combination or monitor the INR closely. Choice. Nystatin is less effective than azoles in immunocompromised and irradiated patients; the American guideline prefers clotrimazole or miconazole for mild disease and fluconazole for moderate to severe disease (Pappas et al., Clin Infect Dis 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_oralaf~s3-2","Interactions","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel · Safety","Miconazole inhibits CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 even as an oral gel: it markedly raises the INR with warfarin (bleeding deaths have been reported) and raises levels of sulfonylureas, phenytoin and some statins. Avoid with warfarin, or monitor INR very closely."],["sec","cl:t-dr_oralaf~s4-1","Viva essentials","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The miconazole and warfarin interaction. Contributing causes of oral candidiasis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_oralaf~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Nystatin and miconazole oral gel · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing miconazole gel to a patient on warfarin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ototox~s1-1","Summary","Ototoxic drugs · At a glance","Ototoxic drugs Drug or group Mainly affects Reversible? Key points Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, streptomycin: vestibular; amikacin, neomycin, kanamycin, tobramycin: cochlear) Hair cells, basal turn first Usually permanent Mitochondrial m.1555A>G mutation; loop diuretics add risk; topical drops through a perforation Cisplatin (and carboplatin at high doses) Outer hair cells and stria vascularis Permanent Dose-related, worse in children; sodium thiosulfate protects in some children Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide) Stria vascularis Usually reversible Rapid intravenous doses, renal failure; strongly synergistic with aminoglycosides Salicylates (aspirin at high doses) Outer hair cells (prestin) Reversible Tinnitus and flat mild loss at high levels Quinine, chloroquine, hydroxychloroquine Cochlea Often reversible (quinine) Tinnitus, vertigo; cinchonism Macrolides (erythromycin, azithromycin) Cochlea Usually reversible High intravenous doses, renal or hepatic impairment Vancomycin Cochlea Variable Mainly with aminoglycosides Deferoxamine Cochlea Variable Chelation in transfusion-dependent patients Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors (sildenafil) Cochlea Variable Associated with sudden sensorineural hearing loss Topical agents in the middle ear Inner ear through the round window Variable Aminoglycoside drops, chlorhexidine and alcohol preparations, some antiseptics"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ototox~s2-1","Monitoring","Ototoxic drugs · Recognising and monitoring","Record baseline pure-tone audiometry (with extended high frequencies 9 to 16 kHz where available, and otoacoustic emissions in children) before treatment, repeat during treatment and afterwards. Significant change by ASHA criteria is a threshold shift of 20 dB or more at one frequency, 10 dB or more at two adjacent frequencies, or loss of response at three consecutive frequencies where there was one before. Ask about tinnitus and imbalance."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ototox~s2-2","Vestibular toxicity","Ototoxic drugs · Recognising and monitoring","Bilateral vestibular loss causes oscillopsia and unsteadiness, especially in the dark or on uneven ground, without vertigo. Test with the head impulse test (bilateral catch-up saccades), a positive Romberg on foam, dynamic visual acuity, and video head impulse or caloric testing. Treat with vestibular rehabilitation."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ototox~s2-3","Ototoxic drugs: current evidence","Ototoxic drugs · Recognising and monitoring","Monitoring. Baseline and serial audiometry, including extended high frequencies (above 8 kHz), detects cisplatin and aminoglycoside ototoxicity before speech frequencies are affected; children should be graded with a paediatric scale (such as SIOP Boston). Protection. Sodium thiosulfate halved cisplatin hearing loss in children with localised hepatoblastoma (Brock et al., NEJM 2018). Genetics. Testing for MT-RNR1 m.1555A>G before aminoglycosides in neonatal units avoids profound deafness in carriers (McDermott et al., JAMA Pediatr 2022)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ototox~s2-3","Prevention","Ototoxic drugs · Recognising and monitoring","Avoid ototoxic drugs where possible, use the lowest effective dose and shortest course, monitor drug levels and renal function, avoid combining ototoxic drugs, and test for the m.1555A>G mutation where available."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ototox~s3-1","Viva essentials","Ototoxic drugs · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Which drugs are cochleotoxic, vestibulotoxic, reversible or permanent . The mechanisms (hair cell entry, reactive oxygen species, stria vascularis). Monitoring criteria and how bilateral vestibular loss presents. The m.1555A>G mutation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ototox~s3-2","Common pitfalls","Ototoxic drugs · For the examination","Common pitfalls No baseline audiogram before cisplatin or long aminoglycoside courses. Missing bilateral vestibular failure after an ICU stay."],["sec","cl:t-dr_para~s1-1","Summary","Paracetamol · At a glance","Paracetamol Summary Class Non-opioid analgesic and antipyretic How it works Not fully understood: central inhibition of cyclo-oxygenase (in a low-peroxide environment), activation of descending serotonergic pathways, and effects of its metabolite AM404 on cannabinoid and TRPV1 receptors; little peripheral anti-inflammatory effect ENT uses Post-operative pain (tonsillectomy, ear, nasal and neck surgery); acute otitis media, pharyngitis, sinusitis; fever Adult dose 1 g every 4 to 6 hours orally or intravenously (maximum 4 g in 24 hours); under 50 kg or with risk factors for toxicity, 15 mg/kg (maximum 60 mg/kg a day) Child dose Oral by age band or 15 mg/kg every 4 to 6 hours (maximum 4 doses in 24 hours); intravenous 15 mg/kg (10 mg/kg in neonates and infants under 10 kg) with daily maxima by age Risk factors for toxicity Low body weight, malnutrition, chronic alcohol use, liver disease, enzyme-inducing drugs, dehydration and prolonged fasting Pregnancy First-choice analgesic"],["sec","cl:t-dr_para~s2-1","After tonsillectomy","Paracetamol · Using it in ENT","Give paracetamol and ibuprofen regularly , alternating or together, for at least the first 5 to 7 days, with clear written instructions on doses and maximums for parents. Pain often peaks on days 3 to 6, and poor intake from pain causes dehydration and readmission."],["sec","cl:t-dr_para~s2-2","Rectal and intravenous routes","Paracetamol · Using it in ENT","Rectal absorption is slow and variable; intravenous paracetamol gives reliable levels but must be dosed by weight to avoid tenfold errors in children (milligram and millilitre confusion)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_para~s3-1","Overdose","Paracetamol · Safety","Toxicity comes from the metabolite NAPQI, normally detoxified by glutathione. After overdose, measure levels from 4 hours after ingestion and treat with acetylcysteine according to the treatment line; staggered or uncertain-time overdoses are treated without waiting. Liver failure appears at 2 to 4 days."],["sec","cl:t-dr_para~s3-2","Adverse effects","Paracetamol · Safety","Rare at therapeutic doses: rash, thrombocytopenia, and hepatotoxicity in at-risk patients; high anion gap metabolic acidosis (pyroglutamic acidosis) with flucloxacillin in septic, malnourished patients."],["ev","cl:t-dr_para~s3-3","Paracetamol: current evidence","Paracetamol · Safety","Intravenous dosing. After accidental overdoses in small adults and children, the UK MHRA (2010) required weight-based intravenous dosing: 15 mg/kg for adults under 50 kg, with a maximum of 60 mg/kg a day, and clearer prescribing in millilitres versus milligrams. Combination. Paracetamol with ibuprofen gives better pain relief than either alone after tonsillectomy and other ENT surgery. Liver. Reduce the dose in malnutrition, chronic alcohol use and low body weight."],["sec","cl:t-dr_para~s3-3","Interactions","Paracetamol · Safety","Warfarin effect may increase with regular use; enzyme inducers increase toxic metabolite formation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_para~s4-1","Viva essentials","Paracetamol · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Doses and maxima for adults, low-weight adults and children. Regular analgesia after tonsillectomy. Mechanism of toxicity and acetylcysteine."],["sec","cl:t-dr_para~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Paracetamol · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing \"as required\" analgesia after tonsillectomy. Full adult doses in a frail 45 kg patient. Combination products adding hidden paracetamol."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s1-1","Summary","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · At a glance","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin Summary Class Natural penicillins (beta-lactam) How they work Inhibit cell wall cross-linking; bactericidal, time-dependent Spectrum Streptococci (group A, C, G and pneumococci), many oral anaerobes, Neisseria, Treponema, Actinomyces; not most staphylococci, not H. influenzae or Moraxella that produce beta-lactamase, not Gram-negative bacilli ENT uses Streptococcal tonsillitis and pharyngitis (penicillin V); acute sinusitis in adults (NICE first choice); severe tonsillitis, quinsy (with metronidazole) and actinomycosis (benzylpenicillin); scarlet fever; prophylaxis after splenectomy Adult dose Penicillin V 500 mg four times daily (or 1 g twice daily for sore throat), up to 1 g four times daily; benzylpenicillin 1.2 g four times daily intravenously, up to 2.4 g every 4 hours in severe infection Child dose Penicillin V 1 to 11 months 62.5 mg, 1 to 5 years 125 mg, 6 to 11 years 250 mg, 12 to 17 years 500 mg, four times daily; benzylpenicillin 25 mg/kg every 6 hours (50 mg/kg in severe infection) Duration Sore throat 5 to 10 days (10 days is still advised in many guidelines to eradicate the streptococcus); sinusitis 5 days Avoid Penicillin allergy Pregnancy Safe"],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s2-1","Mechanism","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · How they work","Like all penicillins they bind penicillin-binding proteins and stop peptidoglycan cross-linking. Their narrow spectrum is an advantage: less disruption of the gut and oral flora, less C. difficile and less resistance selection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s2-2","Absorption and handling","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · How they work","Penicillin V is acid-stable and absorbed by mouth but best taken on an empty stomach , an hour before food. Benzylpenicillin is destroyed by gastric acid and must be given parenterally. Both have short half-lives (about 30 to 60 minutes), hence dosing four times a day; probenecid prolongs their levels."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s3-1","Sore throat","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · Using them in ENT","Use a clinical score to decide: FeverPAIN (fever, purulence, attend rapidly within 3 days, inflamed tonsils, no cough or coryza) or Centor (exudate, tender anterior nodes, fever, absent cough). With FeverPAIN 0 to 1 or Centor 0 to 2, no antibiotic; FeverPAIN 2 to 3, consider a back-up prescription; FeverPAIN 4 to 5 or Centor 3 to 4, consider an immediate antibiotic. Penicillin V is first-line; clarithromycin or erythromycin in allergy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s3-2","Severe tonsillitis and quinsy","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · Using them in ENT","A patient who cannot swallow fluids or has a peritonsillar abscess is admitted: intravenous benzylpenicillin (often with metronidazole for anaerobes), fluids, analgesia, and drainage of any abscess; many units give a single dose of dexamethasone. Step down to oral penicillin V (with metronidazole) once swallowing."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s3-3","Other uses","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · Using them in ENT","Actinomycosis of the jaw and neck: high-dose benzylpenicillin for weeks, then oral penicillin for months. Acute sinusitis in adults where an antibiotic is indicated."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s4-1","Adverse effects","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · Safety","Hypersensitivity: from rash to anaphylaxis; the commonest cause of drug anaphylaxis. Diarrhoea and nausea, less than with broader agents. Very high doses of benzylpenicillin in renal failure can cause seizures (encephalopathy). The Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction in syphilis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s4-2","Contraindications and cautions","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · Safety","Penicillin allergy. About 10% of patients carry a label, but most are not truly allergic; formal de-labelling widens safe options. Renal impairment with high-dose intravenous benzylpenicillin."],["ev","cl:t-dr_penv~s4-3","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin: current evidence","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · Safety","Course length. Five days of penicillin V four times daily was non-inferior to ten days three times daily for streptococcal pharyngotonsillitis in a Swedish trial (Skoog Ståhlgren et al., BMJ 2019); NICE (NG84) allows five to ten days. Where rheumatic fever remains common, including Egypt, ten days or a single intramuscular benzathine penicillin injection is still advised. Benzylpenicillin remains first line for severe streptococcal infection and is combined with clindamycin for necrotising soft tissue infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s4-3","Interactions","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · Safety","Methotrexate (reduced excretion); probenecid (raised levels)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s5-1","Viva essentials","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for FeverPAIN and Centor scores and how they guide antibiotics. Why penicillin V, not amoxicillin, is first-line for a sore throat. Treatment of quinsy : drainage plus intravenous benzylpenicillin and metronidazole. Group A streptococcus remains universally penicillin-sensitive."],["sec","cl:t-dr_penv~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Phenoxymethylpenicillin and benzylpenicillin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing penicillin V to be taken with meals. Short courses that fail to eradicate streptococcus in a patient with recurrent tonsillitis. Relying on antibiotics alone for a peritonsillar abscess."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s1-1","Summary","Piperacillin with tazobactam · At a glance","Piperacillin with tazobactam Summary Class Ureidopenicillin with a beta-lactamase inhibitor How it works Cell wall synthesis inhibition; tazobactam protects against many beta-lactamases Spectrum Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Enterobacterales, methicillin-sensitive S. aureus, streptococci, enterococci (some), anaerobes; not MRSA, not ESBL reliably, not atypicals ENT uses Necrotising (malignant) otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis; hospital-acquired and post-operative deep neck and wound infection; aspiration pneumonia after head and neck surgery; febrile neutropenia Adult dose 4.5 g every 8 hours intravenously, increased to every 6 hours in severe infection; extended infusions over 3 to 4 hours improve time above the MIC Child dose 90 mg/kg every 6 to 8 hours (maximum 4.5 g per dose) Duration Review at 48 to 72 hours with cultures; necrotising otitis externa needs 6 weeks or more of anti-pseudomonal therapy, usually switched to oral ciprofloxacin Avoid Penicillin allergy Pregnancy Use if needed"],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s2-1","Mechanism","Piperacillin with tazobactam · How it works","Piperacillin's side chain lets it penetrate the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacilli including Pseudomonas; tazobactam inhibits many plasmid-mediated beta-lactamases. Extended-spectrum beta-lactamase (ESBL) and AmpC producers are often resistant; use a carbapenem for proven ESBL infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s2-2","Handling","Piperacillin with tazobactam · How it works","Given intravenously only; renally excreted, so reduce the dose when the creatinine clearance is below 40 ml/min."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s3-1","Necrotising otitis externa","Piperacillin with tazobactam · Using it in ENT","An elderly diabetic or immunocompromised patient with severe otalgia out of proportion, granulation at the bony-cartilaginous junction and cranial nerve palsy (VII first). Take deep cultures and biopsy (to exclude carcinoma) before antibiotics if possible, control the diabetes, start intravenous anti-pseudomonal therapy (piperacillin-tazobactam or ceftazidime, often with ciprofloxacin), and convert to oral ciprofloxacin for a total of at least 6 weeks guided by clinical response, inflammatory markers and imaging."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s3-2","Post-operative and hospital infection","Piperacillin with tazobactam · Using it in ENT","After major head and neck surgery, a wound or neck infection with a pharyngocutaneous fistula, or aspiration pneumonia, is often polymicrobial with Gram-negatives; piperacillin-tazobactam is a common empirical choice, narrowed when cultures return."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s4-1","Adverse effects","Piperacillin with tazobactam · Safety","Hypersensitivity; diarrhoea and C. difficile. Acute kidney injury , especially in combination with vancomycin. Hypokalaemia (sodium load), thrombocytopenia and neutropenia with prolonged courses, and hepatitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s4-2","Contraindications and cautions","Piperacillin with tazobactam · Safety","Penicillin allergy. Renal impairment (dose reduction)."],["ev","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s4-3","Piperacillin with tazobactam: current evidence","Piperacillin with tazobactam · Safety","Kidneys. Observational studies consistently show more acute kidney injury when piperacillin–tazobactam is combined with vancomycin than with other beta-lactams; monitor renal function or choose another partner. Infusion. Extended or continuous infusions keep levels above the MIC longer; meta-analyses suggest lower mortality in sepsis, although the large BLING III trial did not show a significant difference. Stewardship. Narrow treatment once cultures return."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s4-3","Interactions","Piperacillin with tazobactam · Safety","Vancomycin (additive nephrotoxicity: monitor renal function); methotrexate; aminoglycosides (inactivated if mixed in the same infusion: give separately)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s5-1","Viva essentials","Piperacillin with tazobactam · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Organisms of necrotising otitis externa (Pseudomonas in most; Aspergillus in some) and the treatment plan. Which beta-lactams cover Pseudomonas: piperacillin-tazobactam, ceftazidime, cefepime, carbapenems (not ertapenem). Why to culture and biopsy before treatment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_piptaz~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Piperacillin with tazobactam · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting antibiotics in necrotising otitis externa before taking deep cultures and a biopsy. Stopping too early on clinical improvement. Continuing broad-spectrum therapy without a 48-hour review."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ponv~s1-1","Summary","Antiemetics: ondansetron and others · At a glance","Antiemetics in ENT Summary Ondansetron 5-HT3 antagonist; adult 4 mg intravenously at the end of surgery; child 100 micrograms/kg (maximum 4 mg); QT prolongation, headache, constipation Dexamethasone Adult 4 to 8 mg at induction; child 150 micrograms/kg (maximum 8 to 10 mg) at tonsillectomy Cyclizine H1 antihistamine; 50 mg up to three times daily; useful after ear surgery; tachycardia and dry mouth; sedation Prochlorperazine D2 antagonist; 12.5 mg intramuscularly or 3 to 6 mg buccally; extrapyramidal effects Metoclopramide D2 antagonist and prokinetic; 10 mg up to three times daily for up to 5 days; extrapyramidal reactions (young women and children; avoid under 1 year, restricted in under-18s); no use in bowel obstruction Droperidol D2 antagonist; low dose (0.625 to 1.25 mg) for prophylaxis; QT prolongation Others Aprepitant (NK1 antagonist) for high-risk patients; hyoscine patch for motion sickness and prolonged vestibular nausea; total intravenous anaesthesia reduces risk"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ponv~s2-1","Assessing risk","Antiemetics: ondansetron and others · Using them in ENT","The Apfel score in adults counts female sex, non-smoking, history of PONV or motion sickness, and post-operative opioid use: each adds about 20% risk. In children, the risk is high after tonsillectomy and strabismus surgery, in those over 3 years, and with a history of PONV."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ponv~s2-2","Prevention","Antiemetics: ondansetron and others · Using them in ENT","Give one or two prophylactic agents for moderate risk and three or more for high risk, with different mechanisms; reduce baseline risk with propofol-based anaesthesia, avoidance of nitrous oxide, opioid-sparing analgesia and good hydration. Treat breakthrough vomiting with a drug from a class not already given."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ponv~s3-1","Antiemetics: ondansetron and others: current evidence","Antiemetics: ondansetron and others · Safety","Guideline. The fourth consensus guidelines on postoperative nausea and vomiting recommend assessing risk (Apfel score in adults: female, non-smoker, history of PONV or motion sickness, postoperative opioids), and multimodal prophylaxis with at least two drugs in patients with one or two risk factors, more with higher risk (Gan et al., Anesth Analg 2020). Children after tonsillectomy have a high risk; dexamethasone plus ondansetron is standard. Total intravenous anaesthesia with propofol also reduces vomiting."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ponv~s3-1","Key cautions","Antiemetics: ondansetron and others · Safety","QT prolongation with ondansetron and droperidol (check other QT drugs and electrolytes); dystonic reactions with metoclopramide and prochlorperazine (treat with procyclidine); sedation with cyclizine and hyoscine."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ponv~s4-1","Viva essentials","Antiemetics: ondansetron and others · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The Apfel score and multimodal prophylaxis. Why PONV matters after tonsillectomy and middle ear surgery."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ponv~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Antiemetics: ondansetron and others · For the examination","Common pitfalls Repeating the same antiemetic class for breakthrough vomiting."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ppi~s1-1","Summary","Proton pump inhibitors · At a glance","Proton pump inhibitors Summary Agents Omeprazole, esomeprazole, lansoprazole, pantoprazole, rabeprazole How they work Irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase (proton pump) of the gastric parietal cell after activation in acid ENT uses Typical reflux symptoms with throat complaints; oesophagitis; after some laryngeal surgery and with laryngeal granulomas; in combination with alginates in suspected laryngopharyngeal reflux (limited evidence); gastroprotection Adult dose Omeprazole 20 to 40 mg once daily; lansoprazole 30 mg once daily; twice-daily dosing is often used in suspected laryngopharyngeal reflux, for 8 to 12 weeks, then stopped or reduced Timing 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast (and before the evening meal if twice daily)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_ppi~s2-1","Throat symptoms","Proton pump inhibitors · Using them in ENT","Globus, throat clearing, chronic cough, hoarseness and catarrh have many causes. Examine with nasendoscopy and exclude red flags (dysphagia, weight loss, odynophagia, persistent hoarseness, a neck lump, smoking history) before considering reflux. In the TOPPITS trial, lansoprazole 30 mg twice daily for 16 weeks was no better than placebo for persistent throat symptoms. If a trial is given, set a time limit, combine with lifestyle measures and alginates, and stop if there is no benefit."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ppi~s2-2","Laryngeal granulomas and surgery","Proton pump inhibitors · Using them in ENT","Contact granulomas of the vocal process are often treated with anti-reflux therapy, voice therapy and sometimes botulinum toxin; surgery has a high recurrence rate."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ppi~s3-1","Adverse effects","Proton pump inhibitors · Safety","Headache, diarrhoea; with long-term use: C. difficile , hypomagnesaemia (check magnesium on long-term use, especially with diuretics), fractures, vitamin B12 deficiency, pneumonia, subacute cutaneous lupus, interstitial nephritis; rebound acid hypersecretion on stopping."],["ev","cl:t-dr_ppi~s3-2","Proton pump inhibitors: current evidence","Proton pump inhibitors · Safety","Laryngopharyngeal reflux. Meta-analyses show a modest symptom benefit over placebo but no effect on laryngoscopic signs (Guo et al., J Clin Gastroenterol 2016), so treat for a defined period and stop if there is no response. Long-term harms. The US FDA has warned of hypomagnesaemia (2011), fractures and C. difficile infection with long-term use; review and step down when possible. Hoarseness. The American guideline advises against PPIs for hoarseness without signs of reflux (Stachler et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ppi~s3-2","Interactions","Proton pump inhibitors · Safety","Omeprazole and esomeprazole reduce the activation of clopidogrel (choose pantoprazole); PPIs reduce absorption of itraconazole capsules, ketoconazole and some tyrosine kinase inhibitors; raise methotrexate levels at high doses."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ppi~s4-1","Viva essentials","Proton pump inhibitors · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The weak evidence in laryngopharyngeal reflux and the TOPPITS trial. Correct timing of doses. Long-term risks and the clopidogrel interaction."],["sec","cl:t-dr_ppi~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Proton pump inhibitors · For the examination","Common pitfalls Lifelong PPIs for globus without review. Missing a cancer by labelling symptoms as reflux."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s1-1","Summary","Prednisolone · At a glance","Prednisolone Summary Class Glucocorticoid How it works Binds the intracellular glucocorticoid receptor, switching off inflammatory genes (NF-kappaB, AP-1) and switching on anti-inflammatory ones; reduces cytokines, oedema and immune cell activation ENT uses Sudden sensorineural hearing loss; Bell's palsy and Ramsay Hunt syndrome; nasal polyps; allergic fungal sinusitis after surgery; autoimmune inner ear disease; laryngeal oedema, severe pharyngitis and post-operative swelling; acute vestibular neuritis (debated); granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other vasculitides; IgG4-related disease; sarcoidosis Doses Sudden hearing loss: 1 mg/kg daily (maximum 60 mg) for 7 to 14 days, often with a taper; Bell's palsy: 50 mg daily for 10 days or 60 mg daily for 5 days then a taper (within 72 hours of onset); polyps: commonly 25 to 50 mg daily for 7 to 14 days, no more than one or two courses a year where possible Child dose 1 to 2 mg/kg daily (maximum usually 40 to 60 mg) for the condition Equivalent doses Prednisolone 5 mg = hydrocortisone 20 mg = methylprednisolone 4 mg = dexamethasone 0.75 mg Pregnancy Acceptable when needed (largely inactivated by the placenta)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s2-1","Mechanism","Prednisolone · How it works","Glucocorticoids act through gene transcription, so their full effect takes hours, although some membrane effects are faster. In the inner ear they may act on ion and water transport as well as on inflammation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s2-2","Handling","Prednisolone · How it works","Well absorbed orally; take in the morning with food to mimic the circadian peak and reduce insomnia and dyspepsia. Courses of more than 3 weeks, or repeated courses, suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and must be tapered."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s3-1","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss","Prednisolone · Using it in ENT","Exclude a conductive cause with tuning forks and audiometry; give high-dose oral steroids as soon as possible, ideally within 2 weeks (benefit falls after that); arrange an MRI to exclude a vestibular schwannoma; offer intratympanic steroid as primary treatment when oral steroids are unsafe, or as salvage. The evidence for benefit is modest, so discuss it with the patient."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s3-2","Bell's palsy","Prednisolone · Using it in ENT","Steroids started within 72 hours increase complete recovery; antivirals add little except perhaps in severe palsy. Protect the eye."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s3-3","Nasal polyps","Prednisolone · Using it in ENT","A short course shrinks polyps and restores smell temporarily; use it for exacerbations and before surgery, while long-term control relies on topical steroid, saline, surgery and, for selected patients, biologics. Repeated courses accumulate harm: more than about 500 to 1,000 mg in a lifetime is associated with osteoporosis, cataract, diabetes and other effects."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s4-1","Adverse effects","Prednisolone · Safety","Short courses: hyperglycaemia, insomnia, mood change (euphoria, anxiety, depression, psychosis), dyspepsia, raised blood pressure, fluid retention, increased appetite. Avascular necrosis of the femoral head, rare but reported even after short courses: warn about hip or groin pain. Infection risk and masking of infection; reactivation of tuberculosis, varicella risk in non-immune patients. Longer use: osteoporosis, Cushingoid features, myopathy, cataract and glaucoma, skin thinning, growth suppression in children, adrenal suppression."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s4-2","Contraindications and cautions","Prednisolone · Safety","Systemic infection without cover; live vaccines during high doses. Take care with diabetes (monitor glucose), peptic ulcer disease (gastroprotection), psychiatric history, glaucoma, osteoporosis, hypertension and heart failure."],["ev","cl:t-dr_pred~s4-3","Prednisolone: current evidence","Prednisolone · Safety","Bell's palsy. Prednisolone within 72 hours raised complete recovery at three months from 64% to 83% (Sullivan et al., NEJM 2007). Nasal polyps. A Cochrane review found that a short course of oral steroids reduces polyp size and symptoms for up to about three months (Head et al., 2016). Cumulative harm. A European (EAACI) position paper found that a lifetime cumulative dose of about 500 to 1000 mg of prednisolone raises the risk of osteoporosis, diabetes, cataract and other harms, so repeated courses should prompt surgery or a biologic instead (Hox et al., Clin Transl Allergy 2020)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s4-3","Interactions","Prednisolone · Safety","NSAIDs (peptic ulcer), anticoagulants (INR change), fluoroquinolones (tendon rupture), hypoglycaemics and insulin (higher doses needed), enzyme inducers such as rifampicin and phenytoin (lower steroid levels), and diuretics (hypokalaemia)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s5-1","Viva essentials","Prednisolone · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Regimens for sudden hearing loss and Bell's palsy , and the time windows. Steroid equivalent doses . What you warn the patient about, including avascular necrosis and diabetes. When to taper (over 3 weeks of treatment or repeated courses)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pred~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Prednisolone · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting steroids in a diabetic patient without a plan for glucose. Repeated courses for polyps without counting the cumulative dose. Stopping a long course abruptly."],["sec","cl:t-dr_propr~s1-1","Summary","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma · At a glance","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma Summary Class Non-selective beta blocker How it works Vasoconstriction, reduced VEGF and bFGF signalling, and apoptosis of haemangioma endothelial cells ENT uses Subglottic and airway haemangioma; periocular, nasal tip, lip and parotid haemangiomas; ulcerated or segmental lesions; PHACE-associated haemangiomas (with care) Dose Start at about 1 mg/kg/day in 2 or 3 divided doses, increased over 1 to 2 weeks to 2 to 3 mg/kg/day (the licensed formulation uses 3 mg/kg/day in two doses); continued for at least 6 months, often until 12 months of age or beyond Before starting Heart rate, blood pressure and cardiac examination; ECG if bradycardic, arrhythmic or with a family history of heart block; in segmental facial lesions, assess for PHACE syndrome (MRI and MRA of head and neck, echocardiogram)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_propr~s2-1","Airway haemangioma","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma · Using it in ENT","Infants with stridor and a beard-distribution haemangioma need airway endoscopy. Propranolol is started in hospital with cardiovascular monitoring, often shrinking the lesion within days and avoiding tracheostomy; steroids, laser or open excision are reserved for non-responders. Stop gradually at the end; rebound growth can occur."],["sec","cl:t-dr_propr~s3-1","Adverse effects","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma · Safety","Hypoglycaemia (give with feeds, and stop if the infant is not feeding or is unwell with vomiting), bradycardia, hypotension, bronchospasm (stop during wheezy infections), sleep disturbance, cold extremities, diarrhoea."],["ev","cl:t-dr_propr~s3-2","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma: current evidence","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma · Safety","Evidence. Oral propranolol 3 mg/kg/day for six months cleared or nearly cleared infantile haemangioma in 60%, against 4% with placebo (Léauté-Labrèze et al., NEJM 2015). Guideline. The American Academy of Pediatrics guideline recommends referral by about one month of age for high-risk haemangiomas (airway, periocular, large facial or beard distribution, ulcerated), propranolol 2 to 3 mg/kg/day given with feeds, and no routine ECG unless there is bradycardia or a cardiac history (Krowchuk et al., Pediatrics 2019). Hypoglycaemia is the main risk: give with feeds and stop during illness with poor feeding."],["sec","cl:t-dr_propr~s3-2","Contraindications","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma · Safety","Asthma or bronchospasm, heart block or bradycardia, hypotension, heart failure, PHACE syndrome with significant cerebrovascular anomalies (stroke risk; specialist decision), premature infants under the corrected age threshold."],["sec","cl:t-dr_propr~s4-1","Viva essentials","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Presentation of subglottic haemangioma and the beard-distribution sign. Propranolol dosing, pre-treatment checks and hypoglycaemia advice. PHACE syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-dr_propr~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Propranolol for infantile haemangioma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Continuing propranolol in an infant who is not feeding. Missing PHACE assessment in a large segmental facial haemangioma."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pseudo~s1-1","Summary","Oral decongestants · At a glance","Oral decongestants Summary Agents Pseudoephedrine; phenylephrine (poorly absorbed, little better than placebo) How they work Sympathomimetic: release noradrenaline and stimulate alpha-adrenoceptors, constricting nasal venous sinusoids ENT uses Short-term congestion in colds and acute sinusitis; prevention of barotrauma during flights or dives in selected adults; Eustachian tube symptoms Adult dose Pseudoephedrine 60 mg every 4 to 6 hours (maximum 240 mg a day), short term; for flying, a dose about 30 to 60 minutes before descent Child dose Not recommended under 12 years in the UK Avoid Uncontrolled hypertension, severe kidney disease, ischaemic heart disease, within 2 weeks of an MAOI, phaeochromocytoma, hyperthyroidism, narrow-angle glaucoma"],["sec","cl:t-dr_pseudo~s2-1","Barotrauma and flying","Oral decongestants · Using them in ENT","Pseudoephedrine before descent reduces ear barotrauma in adults with a history of it, alongside autoinflation (Valsalva or a nasal balloon); it is not effective in children. For a diver, it can mask a problem that reappears at depth and is generally discouraged."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pseudo~s3-1","Adverse effects","Oral decongestants · Safety","Hypertension, tachycardia, palpitations, anxiety and insomnia ; urinary retention. Rare posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome and reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (sudden thunderclap headache): regulators have restricted their use. Misuse in illicit methamphetamine production explains sales controls."],["ev","cl:t-dr_pseudo~s3-2","Oral decongestants: current evidence","Oral decongestants · Safety","New warnings. The EMA (2023) and the UK MHRA (2024) warned that pseudoephedrine can cause posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) and reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (RCVS), and contraindicated it in severe or uncontrolled hypertension and severe kidney disease; stop it at once if sudden severe headache, seizures or visual disturbance occur. Phenylephrine. A US FDA advisory committee concluded in 2023 that oral phenylephrine does not work as a decongestant at standard doses, so it has no place in treatment."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pseudo~s3-2","Interactions","Oral decongestants · Safety","MAOIs and linezolid (hypertensive crisis), other sympathomimetics, beta blockers."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pseudo~s4-1","Viva essentials","Oral decongestants · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Cardiovascular contraindications. Their role in barotrauma prevention in adults."],["sec","cl:t-dr_pseudo~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Oral decongestants · For the examination","Common pitfalls Recommending pseudoephedrine to a hypertensive patient or a child."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rai~s1-1","Summary","Radioactive iodine · At a glance","Radioactive iodine Summary Agent Iodine-131 (sodium iodide), given orally How it works Taken up through the sodium-iodide symporter; beta emission (range about 1 to 2 mm) destroys cells; gamma emission allows imaging ENT uses Remnant ablation after total thyroidectomy (selected low and intermediate risk patients); adjuvant treatment (intermediate and high risk); treatment of residual, nodal or distant metastatic disease; Graves' disease and toxic nodular goitre Doses Remnant ablation commonly 1.1 GBq (30 mCi) in low-risk disease; 3.7 GBq (100 mCi) or more for adjuvant or metastatic treatment; lower doses for benign disease Preparation Low-iodine diet for about 2 weeks; TSH stimulation (above 30 mU/l) by withdrawal of levothyroxine or recombinant TSH; avoid iodinated CT contrast in the preceding months; pregnancy test Contraindications Pregnancy and breastfeeding; inability to follow radiation protection precautions"],["sec","cl:t-dr_rai~s2-1","Who needs it after thyroidectomy","Radioactive iodine · Using it in ENT","Decisions follow pathology and risk: not routinely in very low risk (unifocal tumours of 1 cm or less); considered in low to intermediate risk; recommended in high risk (gross extrathyroidal extension, distant metastases). Lobectomy alone is adequate for many low-risk cancers, and radioiodine requires a total thyroidectomy first."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rai~s2-2","Radioiodine refractory disease","Radioactive iodine · Using it in ENT","Disease that does not take up iodine, or progresses despite it, is treated by surgery, external beam radiotherapy or targeted therapy (see targeted therapy for thyroid cancer)."],["ev","cl:t-dr_rai~s3-1","Radioactive iodine: current evidence","Radioactive iodine · Safety","Lower doses. In the HiLo and ESTIMABL trials, 1.1 GBq ablated low-risk thyroid remnants as well as 3.7 GBq, with recombinant TSH as effective as withdrawal (Mallick et al., NEJM 2012; Schlumberger et al., NEJM 2012). No ablation for low risk. In ESTIMABL2, low-risk patients followed without radioiodine did as well as those ablated (Leboulleux et al., Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2022). The American Thyroid Association guideline adapts radioiodine to risk (Haugen et al., Thyroid 2016). Side effects: sialadenitis, dry mouth, nasolacrimal obstruction and, rarely, second cancers."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rai~s3-1","Adverse effects","Radioactive iodine · Safety","Radioiodine sialadenitis : the salivary glands concentrate iodine; acute swelling and pain within days, and chronic sialadenitis with recurrent swelling, xerostomia and taste change in a proportion; risk rises with dose. Hydration and sialogogues around treatment may help; sialendoscopy can treat duct stenosis. Nasolacrimal duct obstruction with epiphora; dental caries from dry mouth. Nausea, neck swelling and pain (thyroiditis of remnant), transient bone marrow suppression, gonadal effects (avoid pregnancy for 6 months, conception for men for about 4 months), a small increase in second cancers with high cumulative doses, pulmonary fibrosis in diffuse lung metastases. In Graves' disease, radioiodine can worsen thyroid eye disease : steroid cover, especially in smokers."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rai~s4-1","Viva essentials","Radioactive iodine · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications after thyroidectomy by risk. Preparation: low-iodine diet, TSH stimulation. Sialadenitis and other ENT side effects."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rai~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Radioactive iodine · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving iodinated contrast shortly before planned radioiodine. Missing radioiodine sialadenitis in a patient with recurrent parotid swelling."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rev~s1-1","Summary","Reversal of anticoagulation · At a glance","Reversal of anticoagulation Summary Warfarin Life-threatening bleeding: four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) 25 to 50 units/kg (by INR and weight) plus vitamin K 5 to 10 mg intravenously; non-urgent high INR without bleeding: oral vitamin K 1 to 5 mg and withhold doses Dabigatran Idarucizumab 5 g intravenously (two 2.5 g doses); haemodialysis removes it Apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban Andexanet alfa where available and indicated; otherwise PCC 25 to 50 units/kg; activated charcoal if taken within a few hours Unfractionated heparin Protamine 1 mg per 100 units given in the previous 2 to 3 hours (maximum 50 mg) Low molecular weight heparin Protamine gives partial reversal (about 60% of anti-Xa activity) Antiplatelets No specific reversal; platelet transfusion in life-threatening bleeding or emergency surgery; desmopressin in some cases Adjuncts Tranexamic acid; local measures; treat hypothermia, acidosis and hypocalcaemia in major haemorrhage"],["sec","cl:t-dr_rev~s2-1","The bleeding patient on an anticoagulant","Reversal of anticoagulation · Using them in ENT","Resuscitate, control the bleeding locally (packing, pressure, theatre), take blood for full blood count, clotting, renal function and group and save or crossmatch, establish which drug was taken and when, and involve haematology. Restart anticoagulation once bleeding is controlled and the risk allows, usually within days, because the thrombotic risk persists."],["ev","cl:t-dr_rev~s3-1","Reversal of anticoagulation: current evidence","Reversal of anticoagulation · Safety","Dabigatran. Idarucizumab reversed dabigatran completely within minutes in the REVERSE-AD study (Pollack et al., NEJM 2017). Factor Xa inhibitors. In ANNEXA-I, andexanet alfa gave better haemostasis than usual care in intracerebral haemorrhage but caused more thrombotic events, including strokes (Connolly et al., NEJM 2024); four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate is a common alternative. Warfarin. Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate with intravenous vitamin K 5 to 10 mg is preferred to plasma for major bleeding. Epistaxis usually needs local measures, not reversal."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rev~s3-1","Adverse effects","Reversal of anticoagulation · Safety","PCC and andexanet: thrombosis . Vitamin K intravenously: rare anaphylactoid reactions; give slowly. High doses make re-warfarinisation difficult. Protamine: hypotension, bradycardia, anaphylaxis (fish allergy, previous protamine-containing insulin, vasectomy)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rev~s4-1","Viva essentials","Reversal of anticoagulation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Warfarin reversal : PCC plus intravenous vitamin K. Specific reversal for dabigatran (idarucizumab) and the factor Xa inhibitors. Protamine dosing."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rev~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Reversal of anticoagulation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using fresh frozen plasma alone for warfarin reversal in life-threatening bleeding. Giving vitamin K without PCC in an emergency (it takes hours to work)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhinmed~s1-1","Summary","Drug-induced rhinitis and smell loss · At a glance","Drug-induced rhinitis and smell loss Group Examples Effect Topical decongestants Xylometazoline, oxymetazoline Rebound congestion and rhinitis medicamentosa after more than 5 to 10 days Recreational drugs Intranasal cocaine and other drugs Rhinitis, septal perforation, midline destruction; levamisole vasculitis Antihypertensives Alpha blockers (doxazosin, prazosin, tamsulosin), beta blockers, methyldopa, reserpine, ACE inhibitors (cough and sometimes rhinitis) Nasal congestion (neurogenic, from reduced sympathetic tone) Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors Sildenafil, tadalafil Congestion (vasodilatation) Hormones Oestrogens, combined oral contraceptive pill, pregnancy itself Congestion NSAIDs and aspirin All COX-1 inhibitors Rhinorrhoea, congestion and asthma in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease Psychotropics Chlorpromazine, risperidone, amitriptyline, gabapentin Congestion Smell and taste loss Intranasal zinc gluconate (anosmia), terbinafine, ACE inhibitors, metronidazole, clarithromycin, chemotherapy, radiotherapy Hyposmia, anosmia, dysgeusia"],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhinmed~s2-1","Taking a drug history","Drug-induced rhinitis and smell loss · Using it in ENT","In every patient with nasal obstruction or smell loss, ask about prescribed, over-the-counter and recreational drugs and sprays. Stopping or changing the drug, where possible, often resolves symptoms; treat rhinitis medicamentosa with a nasal steroid while the decongestant is withdrawn."],["ev","cl:t-dr_rhinmed~s2-2","Drug-induced rhinitis and smell loss: current evidence","Drug-induced rhinitis and smell loss · Using it in ENT","Olfactory training. Smelling four strong odours twice a day for at least three months improves recovery of post-viral smell loss, including after COVID-19 (Hummel et al., Laryngoscope 2009), and is recommended by the international position paper on olfactory dysfunction. Drugs that harm smell. Intranasal zinc gels caused permanent anosmia and were withdrawn after a US FDA warning in 2009. Drug-induced rhinitis from alpha-blockers, PDE5 inhibitors, ACE inhibitors, oral contraceptives and NSAIDs (in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease) is easily missed; take a full drug history."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhinmed~s2-2","Smell loss","Drug-induced rhinitis and smell loss · Using it in ENT","Most drug-related smell and taste disturbance is reversible on stopping the drug, but zinc nasal sprays have caused permanent anosmia. Olfactory training helps recovery from many causes of smell loss."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhinmed~s3-1","Viva essentials","Drug-induced rhinitis and smell loss · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism and treatment of rhinitis medicamentosa . Drugs that cause nasal congestion and smell loss. Cocaine-induced midline destructive lesions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhinmed~s3-2","Common pitfalls","Drug-induced rhinitis and smell loss · For the examination","Common pitfalls Turbinate surgery for congestion caused by a decongestant spray or an alpha blocker."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhtsh~s1-1","Summary","Recombinant TSH · At a glance","Recombinant TSH Summary Agent Thyrotropin alfa How it works Binds TSH receptors on thyroid cells, increasing iodine uptake and thyroglobulin release ENT uses Preparation for radioiodine remnant ablation in low and intermediate risk differentiated thyroid cancer; stimulated thyroglobulin and diagnostic whole-body scans in follow-up; patients who cannot tolerate hypothyroidism (heart failure, frailty, psychiatric illness) or cannot raise their own TSH (pituitary disease) Dose 0.9 mg intramuscularly on 2 consecutive days; radioiodine on day 3; thyroglobulin measured on day 5 Cautions Bulky residual or metastatic disease (TSH-stimulated tumour swelling can cause airway, spinal or brain compression; consider steroid cover)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhtsh~s2-1","Ablation and follow-up","Recombinant TSH · Using it in ENT","Trials (HiLo, ESTIMABL) showed that ablation with low-dose radioiodine after recombinant TSH is as successful as after withdrawal in low-risk patients, with better quality of life. A stimulated thyroglobulin after recombinant TSH is used to assess response in some follow-up protocols, although highly sensitive thyroglobulin assays have reduced the need."],["ev","cl:t-dr_rhtsh~s3-1","Recombinant TSH: current evidence","Recombinant TSH · Safety","Evidence. In the HiLo and ESTIMABL trials, remnant ablation after recombinant TSH was as successful as after thyroid hormone withdrawal, with better quality of life and no hypothyroid symptoms (Mallick et al., NEJM 2012; Schlumberger et al., NEJM 2012). Uses. Remnant ablation, stimulated thyroglobulin testing, and patients who cannot tolerate hypothyroidism or cannot raise their own TSH (pituitary disease). Its use for treating known metastatic disease is off-label."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhtsh~s3-1","Adverse effects","Recombinant TSH · Safety","Nausea, headache, fatigue, flu-like symptoms; transient tumour swelling and pain at metastatic sites."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhtsh~s4-1","Viva essentials","Recombinant TSH · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The advantage over withdrawal . The dosing schedule and timing of radioiodine and thyroglobulin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_rhtsh~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Recombinant TSH · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving recombinant TSH to a patient with tracheal or spinal metastases without steroid cover."],["sec","cl:t-dr_saline~s1-1","Summary","Saline irrigation · At a glance","Saline irrigation Summary Forms High-volume (about 240 ml) low-pressure squeeze bottle or neti pot; low-volume sprays and drops; isotonic (0.9%) or hypertonic (2% to 3%); buffered with sodium bicarbonate How it works Mechanical clearance of mucus, crusts and allergens; improved mucociliary clearance; dilution of inflammatory mediators ENT uses Chronic rhinosinusitis (first-line, with nasal steroid); after endoscopic sinus surgery; allergic rhinitis (adjunct); atrophic rhinitis and crusting; granulomatosis with polyangiitis; after radiotherapy to the nasal cavity Dose 150 to 240 ml into each nostril once or twice daily (high-volume); after surgery more often, starting the day after the operation Safety Use boiled and cooled, distilled or sterile water; clean and dry the bottle; replace it every few months"],["sec","cl:t-dr_saline~s2-1","Evidence","Saline irrigation · Using it in ENT","High-volume saline irrigation improves symptoms in chronic rhinosinusitis more than low-volume sprays and is recommended by EPOS and NICE. After sinus surgery it removes crusts and clot and reduces adhesions. In children with allergic rhinitis, saline reduces symptoms and medication use."],["sec","cl:t-dr_saline~s2-2","Isotonic or hypertonic","Saline irrigation · Using it in ENT","Hypertonic saline may improve ciliary beat and decongest a little more, but stings more; isotonic or buffered isotonic saline is usually better tolerated."],["sec","cl:t-dr_saline~s2-3","Technique","Saline irrigation · Using it in ENT","Lean forward over the sink, mouth open, head turned slightly, squeeze gently into the upper nostril so the solution runs round the septum and out of the other side, then blow gently. Use the solution at body temperature."],["ev","cl:t-dr_saline~s3-1","Saline irrigation: current evidence","Saline irrigation · Safety","Evidence. A Cochrane review found that large-volume saline irrigation improves symptoms in chronic rhinosinusitis, whereas sprays have little effect (Chong et al., 2016), and a randomised trial showed large-volume irrigation better than saline spray (Pynnonen et al., Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2007). Safety. Use boiled and cooled, distilled or sterile water: rare fatal Naegleria fowleri infections have followed irrigation with tap water. Clean the bottle regularly to prevent contamination."],["sec","cl:t-dr_saline~s3-1","Adverse effects","Saline irrigation · Safety","Stinging, ear fullness and fluid running from the nose later. Contaminated equipment can carry bacteria; tap water used without boiling has rarely caused fatal amoebic meningoencephalitis (Naegleria fowleri) and Acanthamoeba infection."],["sec","cl:t-dr_saline~s4-1","Viva essentials","Saline irrigation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for High-volume versus low-volume irrigation. Water safety. Its place as first-line therapy in chronic rhinosinusitis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_saline~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Saline irrigation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Unboiled tap water. Prescribing a low-volume spray where a high-volume rinse is needed."],["sec","cl:t-dr_scler~s1-1","Summary","Sclerosants · At a glance","Sclerosants Summary OK-432 (picibanil) Lyophilised Streptococcus pyogenes; triggers inflammation; macrocystic lymphatic malformations, ranulas; fever and swelling for days; not available everywhere Doxycycline 10 to 20 mg/ml injected into cysts; macrocystic and mixed lymphatic malformations; pain, swelling; avoid systemic dosing concerns in small children (tooth staining is rare with intralesional use) Bleomycin Antitumour antibiotic; microcystic and macrocystic lymphatic malformations and venous malformations; less swelling than OK-432; cumulative dose limits for pulmonary fibrosis (small doses are used) Sodium tetradecyl sulfate and polidocanol Detergent sclerosants (often as foam) for venous malformations Ethanol Powerful sclerosant for venous and arteriovenous malformations; risk of nerve injury, skin necrosis, and cardiopulmonary collapse; usually under general anaesthesia"],["sec","cl:t-dr_scler~s2-1","Lymphatic malformations","Sclerosants · Using them in ENT","Macrocystic lesions respond well (often more than 80% significant reduction); microcystic lesions respond poorly and may need sirolimus (an mTOR inhibitor, increasingly used for complex lymphatic malformations), surgery, or radiofrequency ablation of mucosal vesicles. Lesions of the tongue, floor of mouth and airway may swell after injection, so a planned tracheostomy or airway observation is needed for some."],["sec","cl:t-dr_scler~s2-2","Ranula","Sclerosants · Using them in ENT","Plunging and oral ranulas recur after drainage or marsupialisation; excision of the sublingual gland is the most reliable treatment, with OK-432 sclerotherapy an alternative in some centres."],["ev","cl:t-dr_scler~s3-1","Sclerosants: current evidence","Sclerosants · Safety","Lymphatic malformations. Systematic reviews show that macrocystic lymphatic malformations respond well to sclerotherapy, with similar results for OK-432, doxycycline, bleomycin and ethanol; microcystic lesions respond poorly. Sirolimus improved complicated vascular anomalies in a phase 2 trial (Adams et al., Pediatrics 2016) and is used for microcystic and diffuse disease. Safety. Airway swelling after sclerotherapy in the neck can be dangerous; ethanol carries nerve injury and cardiovascular risks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_scler~s3-1","Adverse effects","Sclerosants · Safety","Pain, fever and marked swelling (airway risk in neck and tongue lesions), skin necrosis and ulceration, nerve injury (facial, hypoglossal, recurrent laryngeal), infection; bleomycin pulmonary toxicity at cumulative doses; ethanol systemic toxicity."],["sec","cl:t-dr_scler~s4-1","Viva essentials","Sclerosants · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Macrocystic versus microcystic lymphatic malformations and the choice of treatment. Airway risk after sclerotherapy. Sirolimus as a medical option."],["sec","cl:t-dr_scler~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Sclerosants · For the examination","Common pitfalls Sclerotherapy of a tongue base malformation without an airway plan."],["sec","cl:t-dr_taxane~s1-1","Summary","Docetaxel and paclitaxel · At a glance","Docetaxel and paclitaxel Summary Class Taxanes How they work Bind beta-tubulin and stabilise microtubules, arresting cells in mitosis ENT uses TPF induction chemotherapy for larynx and hypopharynx preservation and for selected locally advanced tumours; palliative treatment of recurrent and metastatic disease (alone, with platinum, or after immunotherapy); some sinonasal and nasopharyngeal regimens Dose TPF: docetaxel 75 mg/m2 and cisplatin 75 mg/m2 on day 1, 5-FU 750 mg/m2 daily on days 1 to 5, every 3 weeks for 2 to 3 cycles; paclitaxel 80 mg/m2 weekly in palliation Premedication Dexamethasone (docetaxel: fluid retention and hypersensitivity); antihistamines and steroids (paclitaxel: hypersensitivity to the Cremophor vehicle)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_taxane~s2-1","Larynx preservation","Docetaxel and paclitaxel · Using them in ENT","In advanced laryngeal and hypopharyngeal cancer, responders to TPF induction proceed to radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy with preservation of the larynx; non-responders proceed to laryngectomy. Patients with a non-functioning larynx (aspiration, airway obstruction needing tracheostomy, cartilage destruction) are usually better served by laryngectomy."],["ev","cl:t-dr_taxane~s3-1","Docetaxel and paclitaxel: current evidence","Docetaxel and paclitaxel · Safety","TPF induction. Adding docetaxel to cisplatin and fluorouracil improved survival over PF induction (Vermorken et al., NEJM 2007, TAX 323; Posner et al., NEJM 2007, TAX 324) and improved larynx preservation (Pointreau et al., J Natl Cancer Inst 2009, GORTEC 2000-01). Induction chemotherapy has not consistently beaten concurrent chemoradiation alone, so it is used for selected patients (larynx preservation, bulky nodes, sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma). Toxicity. Neutropenia (prophylactic growth factor and antibiotics), neuropathy and fluid retention."],["sec","cl:t-dr_taxane~s3-1","Adverse effects","Docetaxel and paclitaxel · Safety","Neutropenia and febrile neutropenia (granulocyte colony-stimulating factor support with TPF), peripheral neuropathy, hypersensitivity reactions, fluid retention (docetaxel), alopecia, nail changes, mucositis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_taxane~s4-1","Viva essentials","Docetaxel and paclitaxel · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The TPF regimen and its role in larynx preservation . Premedication and neutropenia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_taxane~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Docetaxel and paclitaxel · For the examination","Common pitfalls Offering larynx preservation to a patient with a non-functioning larynx."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s1-1","Summary","Antituberculous drugs · At a glance","Antituberculous drugs Summary First-line drugs Rifampicin (R), isoniazid (H), pyrazinamide (Z), ethambutol (E) How they work Rifampicin blocks bacterial RNA polymerase; isoniazid blocks mycolic acid synthesis; pyrazinamide acts in acidic intracellular sites; ethambutol blocks arabinogalactan synthesis Standard regimen 2 months of RHZE (intensive phase) then 4 months of RH (continuation phase): 6 months in total for lymph node, laryngeal and most extrapulmonary tuberculosis; 12 months for central nervous system disease Adjuncts Pyridoxine 10 mg daily with isoniazid to prevent neuropathy; steroids for tuberculous meningitis and pericarditis Drug-resistant disease Bedaquiline, linezolid, fluoroquinolones, delamanid, pretomanid and injectables (amikacin) under specialist care Doses By weight: rifampicin about 10 mg/kg, isoniazid 5 mg/kg, pyrazinamide 25 mg/kg, ethambutol 15 mg/kg once daily, given as fixed-dose combination tablets Pregnancy First-line drugs are used; streptomycin is contraindicated (fetal ototoxicity)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s2-1","Why combinations and long courses","Antituberculous drugs · How they work","Mycobacteria divide slowly and exist in different populations: actively dividing extracellular bacilli (killed by isoniazid), slowly metabolising intracellular bacilli in acidic phagosomes (pyrazinamide), and intermittently active bacilli (rifampicin). Combinations prevent the emergence of resistance from spontaneous mutations; six months are needed to sterilise the lesions and prevent relapse."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s3-1","Diagnosis first","Antituberculous drugs · Using them in ENT","For a suspected tuberculous lymphadenitis (scrofula: a matted, painless, often cold neck mass with possible sinus formation), obtain tissue: ultrasound-guided core or FNA with samples for mycobacterial culture and PCR (GeneXpert, which also detects rifampicin resistance) as well as histology, or an excision biopsy. Do not incise and drain a tuberculous node: a chronic discharging sinus results. Send a chest radiograph, HIV test and contact history. Notify the case."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s3-2","Paradoxical reactions","Antituberculous drugs · Using them in ENT","Nodes may enlarge or new ones appear during the first months of treatment (paradoxical upgrading reaction) in up to a fifth: this is not treatment failure. Aspirate fluctuant nodes; consider steroids for severe reactions."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s3-3","Non-tuberculous mycobacteria in children","Antituberculous drugs · Using them in ENT","Atypical mycobacterial lymphadenitis (violaceous skin over a node in a well child aged 1 to 5) is not tuberculosis: excision is curative and antituberculous drugs are ineffective; clarithromycin with rifampicin is used when excision is unsafe."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s4-1","Adverse effects","Antituberculous drugs · Safety","Hepatotoxicity with rifampicin, isoniazid and pyrazinamide: baseline liver function and review if symptoms or abnormal results. Rifampicin : orange-red urine, tears and secretions (stains contact lenses); flu-like syndrome; potent enzyme induction. Isoniazid : peripheral neuropathy (prevent with pyridoxine), hepatitis, drug-induced lupus. Pyrazinamide : hyperuricaemia, gout, arthralgia, hepatotoxicity. Ethambutol : optic neuritis with loss of colour vision and acuity; baseline visual acuity and colour testing, and stop at once if vision changes. Streptomycin and amikacin : vestibular and cochlear ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity; audiometry during treatment."],["ev","cl:t-dr_tb~s4-2","Antituberculous drugs: current evidence","Antituberculous drugs · Safety","Regimens. WHO guidance (2022) keeps the six-month regimen (two months of isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol, then four of isoniazid and rifampicin) for most extrapulmonary tuberculosis, including cervical lymphadenitis, and longer courses for central nervous system and bone disease. Shorter four-month regimens are for pulmonary disease and selected children. Diagnosis. A rapid nucleic acid test (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra) on aspirate or tissue detects tuberculosis and rifampicin resistance within hours. Lymph nodes. Nodes may enlarge paradoxically during treatment; this is not failure."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s4-2","Interactions","Antituberculous drugs · Safety","Rifampicin induces CYP enzymes and lowers the levels of oral contraceptives , warfarin, DOACs, steroids (double the steroid dose), antiretrovirals, ciclosporin, azoles, methadone and many others."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s5-1","Viva essentials","Antituberculous drugs · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The standard RHZE regimen and its duration. How to sample a suspected tuberculous node (culture, PCR, not incision and drainage). The key toxicities: liver, ethambutol and vision , isoniazid and neuropathy, streptomycin and the ear . Rifampicin interactions, especially contraception. Atypical mycobacteria in children: excision, not antituberculous drugs."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tb~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Antituberculous drugs · For the examination","Common pitfalls Sending a node only for histology in formalin, with nothing for culture. Incising and draining a tuberculous node. Mistaking a paradoxical reaction for treatment failure. Forgetting pyridoxine or baseline vision testing."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tki~s1-1","Summary","Targeted therapy for thyroid cancer · At a glance","Targeted therapy for thyroid cancer Summary Multikinase inhibitors Lenvatinib (24 mg daily) and sorafenib (400 mg twice daily) for progressive radioiodine-refractory differentiated thyroid cancer; cabozantinib after progression Medullary thyroid cancer Selpercatinib and pralsetinib (selective RET inhibitors) for RET-mutant disease; vandetanib and cabozantinib (multikinase) Anaplastic thyroid cancer Dabrafenib with trametinib for BRAF V600E disease (including neoadjuvant use before surgery); immunotherapy in some regimens Other targets NTRK fusion (larotrectinib, entrectinib); RET fusion in differentiated cancer (selpercatinib) Testing BRAF, RET, NTRK and other alterations on tumour tissue; germline RET testing in all medullary cancer"],["sec","cl:t-dr_tki~s2-1","Who receives them","Targeted therapy for thyroid cancer · Using them in ENT","Patients with progressive, symptomatic or threatening disease that is refractory to radioiodine, after multidisciplinary discussion; asymptomatic, slowly progressive disease is often observed, because the drugs have significant toxicity. In anaplastic thyroid cancer , rapid BRAF testing at diagnosis matters, because treatment within days can change outcome."],["ev","cl:t-dr_tki~s3-1","Targeted therapy for thyroid cancer: current evidence","Targeted therapy for thyroid cancer · Safety","Radioiodine-refractory differentiated cancer. Lenvatinib (SELECT; Schlumberger et al., NEJM 2015) and sorafenib (DECISION; Brose et al., Lancet 2014) prolonged progression-free survival. Medullary cancer. Selpercatinib, a selective RET inhibitor, gave high response rates (LIBRETTO-001; Wirth et al., NEJM 2020) and beat cabozantinib or vandetanib (LIBRETTO-531; Hadoux et al., NEJM 2023). Anaplastic cancer. In BRAF V600E tumours, dabrafenib with trametinib gave responses in most patients (Subbiah et al., J Clin Oncol 2018), sometimes enough to allow surgery. Molecular testing of every advanced thyroid cancer is now standard."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tki~s3-1","Adverse effects","Targeted therapy for thyroid cancer · Safety","Multikinase inhibitors: hypertension , proteinuria, diarrhoea, fatigue, weight loss, hand-foot skin reaction, hypothyroidism (higher levothyroxine needs), impaired wound healing. Bleeding and fistula formation : tumours invading the trachea, oesophagus or carotid can bleed or form fistulas as they respond (especially with anti-VEGF agents such as lenvatinib), so assess airway and vascular invasion before starting and plan surgery and drug holidays around each other. Vandetanib: QT prolongation. RET inhibitors: hypertension, liver enzyme rise, QT prolongation. Dabrafenib and trametinib: fever, rash, cardiomyopathy, eye toxicity."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tki~s4-1","Viva essentials","Targeted therapy for thyroid cancer · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Definition of radioiodine-refractory disease. Which drugs for which cancers and alterations. The bleeding and fistula risk in invasive tumours."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tki~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Targeted therapy for thyroid cancer · For the examination","Common pitfalls Delaying BRAF testing in anaplastic thyroid cancer. Operating on a patient on lenvatinib without stopping it to allow healing."],["sec","cl:t-dr_topear~s1-1","Summary","Topical anaesthesia of the ear: phenol, EMLA and tetracaine · At a glance","Topical anaesthesia of the ear Summary Agents Phenol (liquefied, about 80% to 90%) applied to a spot; EMLA cream (lidocaine 2.5% and prilocaine 2.5%) filling the canal; tetracaine (amethocaine) 4% gel or solution; lidocaine infiltration of the canal ENT uses Myringotomy and grommet in the clinic; intratympanic injections; biopsy of the drum; removal of granulation or small polyps; clinic myringoplasty Phenol Apply a tiny amount with a fine applicator to a clean, dry drum at the planned site; the drum blanches white within seconds and the area is anaesthetic for minutes EMLA Fill the canal to the drum, leave for 30 to 60 minutes, then suction out completely Tetracaine Fill the canal for 15 to 45 minutes, then remove"],["sec","cl:t-dr_topear~s2-1","Choosing","Topical anaesthesia of the ear: phenol, EMLA and tetracaine · Using them in ENT","Phenol is quick and precise for a single puncture or a small incision. EMLA and tetracaine cover more of the drum for longer procedures but take time and must be removed fully. Injecting the canal at the bony-cartilaginous junction in four quadrants with lidocaine and adrenaline numbs both canal and drum and reduces bleeding."],["sec","cl:t-dr_topear~s2-2","Safety with a perforation","Topical anaesthesia of the ear: phenol, EMLA and tetracaine · Using them in ENT","Topical anaesthetics reaching the middle ear can cause transient vertigo or facial weakness; avoid filling an ear with a perforation."],["ev","cl:t-dr_topear~s3-1","Topical anaesthesia of the ear: phenol, EMLA and tetracaine: current evidence","Topical anaesthesia of the ear: phenol, EMLA and tetracaine · Safety","Clinic procedures. Topical anaesthesia of the drum (phenol, EMLA cream, tetracaine gel or iontophoresis) allows myringotomy, grommet insertion and intratympanic injection in adults in clinic; randomised comparisons are few, and the choice depends on local practice. Caution. EMLA and phenol in the middle ear can cause transient vertigo; keep them on the drum surface and remove the excess. Lidocaine injected into the canal skin (four-quadrant block) is the most reliable option for longer procedures."],["sec","cl:t-dr_topear~s3-1","Adverse effects","Topical anaesthesia of the ear: phenol, EMLA and tetracaine · Safety","Phenol: excess causes a burn and a persistent perforation. EMLA: methaemoglobinaemia from prilocaine, mainly in infants and with large areas (not a practical concern in the adult ear). Tetracaine: allergy (ester anaesthetic). All: vertigo if they reach the inner ear."],["sec","cl:t-dr_topear~s4-1","Viva essentials","Topical anaesthesia of the ear: phenol, EMLA and tetracaine · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for How to anaesthetise a drum for a clinic grommet. The four-quadrant canal block."],["sec","cl:t-dr_topear~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Topical anaesthesia of the ear: phenol, EMLA and tetracaine · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using too much phenol. Leaving EMLA in the canal."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tophaem~s1-1","Summary","Topical haemostatic agents · At a glance","Topical haemostatic agents Summary Passive scaffolds Oxidised regenerated cellulose (Surgicel); gelatin sponge (Gelfoam, Spongostan); microfibrillar collagen; bone wax. They give a framework for platelets and clot; oxidised cellulose is also acidic and mildly antibacterial Active agents Thrombin preparations; gelatin-thrombin matrices (Floseal, Surgiflo with thrombin): flowable gels that fill irregular bleeding surfaces Sealants Fibrin sealants (fibrinogen and thrombin, such as Tisseel); synthetic polyethylene glycol sealants. Used for haemostasis and to seal CSF leaks and grafts Absorbable packs Hyaluronic acid and carboxymethylcellulose packs (nasal), which hold the mucosa and dissolve ENT uses Epistaxis in patients on anticoagulants or with bleeding disorders (absorbable packs or matrices, avoiding packs that need removal); endoscopic sinus and skull base surgery; tonsil bed oozing; sigmoid sinus and emissary vein bleeding; bone bleeding (wax)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_tophaem~s2-1","Choosing","Topical haemostatic agents · Using them in ENT","Oozing from a sinus cavity : a gelatin-thrombin matrix applied directly and left in place, or an absorbable pack. Sigmoid sinus tear in mastoid surgery : oxidised cellulose or gelatin sponge held with a pledget and gentle pressure, then left; do not pack into the lumen. Bone bleeding (emissary veins, cut marrow): bone wax, used sparingly because it inhibits bone healing and is a foreign body. Skull base repair : fibrin or synthetic sealant over the graft layers, not between them."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tophaem~s2-2","In epistaxis","Topical haemostatic agents · Using them in ENT","Absorbable products avoid the trauma and rebleeding of pack removal, especially in patients with hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia and coagulopathy."],["ev","cl:t-dr_tophaem~s3-1","Topical haemostatic agents: current evidence","Topical haemostatic agents · Safety","Use. Haemostatic matrices (gelatin–thrombin such as Floseal) and oxidised cellulose control diffuse oozing in sinus surgery and epistaxis; in trials of endoscopic sinus surgery they reduced bleeding and the need for packing, though they can increase adhesions. Resorbable packing is preferred in HHT and in patients on anticoagulants. Caution. Microfibrillar collagen and thrombin products should not enter vessels or be used near the open dura."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tophaem~s3-1","Adverse effects","Topical haemostatic agents · Safety","Swelling of oxidised cellulose and gelatin, which can compress nerves in confined spaces (optic canal, foramina, spinal canal): remove excess in such sites. Foreign body reaction and granuloma; infection; adhesions. Bovine thrombin can induce antibodies against factor V; human or recombinant thrombin avoids this. Thrombin products must never enter the circulation (fatal thrombosis): do not inject into vessels."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tophaem~s4-1","Viva essentials","Topical haemostatic agents · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The difference between passive, active and sealant agents. Managing a sigmoid sinus bleed. Risks of swelling and intravascular thrombin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_tophaem~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Topical haemostatic agents · For the examination","Common pitfalls Leaving swelling haemostats around the optic nerve. Overusing bone wax."],["sec","cl:t-dr_triam~s1-1","Summary","Triamcinolone injection · At a glance","Triamcinolone injection Summary Class Long-acting particulate glucocorticoid suspension How it works Local anti-inflammatory and antifibroblast effect: reduces collagen synthesis and fibroblast proliferation, increases collagenase activity ENT uses Keloids and hypertrophic scars (ear lobe after piercing, neck incisions); subglottic and tracheal stenosis (with endoscopic incision or dilatation); vocal fold scar, nodules and granulomas (office injection); intralesional treatment of some inflammatory lesions Dose 10 to 40 mg/ml injected intralesionally; keloids every 4 to 6 weeks, typically after excision, until flat; airway lesions in endoscopic procedures as a single dose Avoid Intravascular injection; injection of the turbinates (embolic blindness); infection at the site"],["sec","cl:t-dr_triam~s2-1","Keloids","Triamcinolone injection · Using it in ENT","After excision of an ear lobe keloid, injecting the wound edges at surgery and at intervals afterwards reduces recurrence; pressure earrings and silicone help, and radiotherapy is used for resistant cases. Inject into the dermis, not below it."],["sec","cl:t-dr_triam~s2-2","The airway","Triamcinolone injection · Using it in ENT","In subglottic and tracheal stenosis, intralesional steroid at the time of radial incision and dilatation may delay restenosis; inhaled steroids and treatment of reflux are adjuncts. Office injection of the vocal fold is an option for scar and benign lesions."],["ev","cl:t-dr_triam~s3-1","Triamcinolone injection: current evidence","Triamcinolone injection · Safety","Injecting the nose. Intraturbinal and polyp injections of depot steroid have caused blindness by retrograde embolisation into the ophthalmic artery; most guidance now advises against them. Keloids. Intralesional triamcinolone remains first line, and meta-analyses show that combining it with 5-fluorouracil, or with surgery and early injection, gives better results with fewer side effects than triamcinolone alone. Side effects of repeated injections include skin atrophy, hypopigmentation and telangiectasia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_triam~s3-1","Adverse effects","Triamcinolone injection · Safety","Skin atrophy, hypopigmentation and telangiectasia, especially if injected too superficially or too often. Blindness after injection of the inferior turbinate or nasal and periocular tissues, from retrograde embolisation of crystals into the ophthalmic circulation; for this reason turbinate steroid injection has been abandoned by most surgeons. Systemic absorption with large or repeated doses: hyperglycaemia and adrenal suppression."],["sec","cl:t-dr_triam~s4-1","Viva essentials","Triamcinolone injection · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Its role in keloid management and how often to inject. The turbinate injection and blindness warning."],["sec","cl:t-dr_triam~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Triamcinolone injection · For the examination","Common pitfalls Injecting particulate steroid into the turbinates. Repeated superficial injections causing atrophy."],["sec","cl:t-dr_txa~s1-1","Summary","Tranexamic acid · At a glance","Tranexamic acid Summary Class Antifibrinolytic (lysine analogue) How it works Blocks the lysine-binding sites of plasminogen, so plasmin cannot bind and break down fibrin ENT uses Epistaxis (topical or oral adjunct); post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage; bleeding in von Willebrand disease, haemophilia and platelet disorders (with desmopressin or factor); reduction of blood loss in sinus, orthognathic and major head and neck surgery; hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia (oral, long-term in selected patients) Adult dose Oral 1 g two or three times daily (up to 1.5 g); intravenous 1 g slowly; topical 500 mg (5 ml of 100 mg/ml) on a pledget or swab, or a 5% mouthwash Child dose Oral 15 to 25 mg/kg two or three times daily (maximum 1.5 g); intravenous 10 to 15 mg/kg (maximum 1 g) Avoid Active thromboembolic disease; history of convulsions (high doses); severe renal impairment (dose reduction); disseminated intravascular coagulation with active clot formation Pregnancy Can be used"],["sec","cl:t-dr_txa~s2-1","Epistaxis","Tranexamic acid · Using it in ENT","Topical tranexamic acid on a pledget or nebulised has been used as a first step, but a large UK trial (NoPAC, 2021) found that topical tranexamic acid did not reduce the need for anterior packing in emergency department epistaxis. It remains useful as an adjunct, particularly in patients on antiplatelets, and orally in recurrent bleeding from hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_txa~s2-2","Tonsillar haemorrhage","Tranexamic acid · Using it in ENT","Intravenous or oral tranexamic acid is widely given as part of the conservative management of secondary post-tonsillectomy bleeding, alongside admission, intravenous access and assessment for return to theatre; the evidence is limited but harm is rare. A gargled mouthwash is an option in adults."],["sec","cl:t-dr_txa~s2-3","Surgery","Tranexamic acid · Using it in ENT","A dose at induction reduces blood loss in endoscopic sinus surgery (better field), orthognathic and major head and neck procedures, without an increase in thrombosis in trials."],["ev","cl:t-dr_txa~s3-1","Tranexamic acid: current evidence","Tranexamic acid · Safety","Epistaxis. In the NoPAC trial in UK emergency departments, topical tranexamic acid did not reduce the need for anterior packing compared with placebo (Reuben et al., Ann Emerg Med 2021). Trauma and bleeding. In CRASH-2, tranexamic acid given within three hours of injury reduced death from bleeding ( Lancet 2010); in HALT-IT, it did not help gastrointestinal bleeding and increased venous thrombosis ( Lancet 2020). ENT surgery. Meta-analyses show it reduces blood loss and improves the field in sinus surgery and rhinoplasty, without a clear rise in thrombosis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_txa~s3-1","Adverse effects","Tranexamic acid · Safety","Nausea and diarrhoea; seizures with high intravenous doses (cardiac surgery); visual disturbance rarely; thromboembolism in predisposed patients is a theoretical concern not borne out in large trials."],["sec","cl:t-dr_txa~s4-1","Viva essentials","Tranexamic acid · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism (plasminogen lysine-binding site). Its place in epistaxis, including the NoPAC result. Use in bleeding disorders with desmopressin."],["sec","cl:t-dr_txa~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Tranexamic acid · For the examination","Common pitfalls Relying on tranexamic acid and delaying definitive control of a bleeding tonsil."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s1-1","Summary","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · At a glance","Vancomycin and teicoplanin Summary Class Glycopeptides How they work Bind the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of peptidoglycan precursors and block cell wall synthesis; slowly bactericidal Spectrum MRSA and MSSA, coagulase-negative staphylococci, streptococci including resistant pneumococci, enterococci (not VRE), Clostridioides difficile (oral vancomycin); no Gram-negative activity ENT uses MRSA infection of wounds, flaps and cartilage; MRSA or resistant pneumococcal meningitis and intracranial abscess (with ceftriaxone); deep neck infection with MRSA risk; surgical prophylaxis in MRSA carriers; oral vancomycin for C. difficile Adult dose Vancomycin loading 25 to 30 mg/kg, then 15 to 20 mg/kg every 8 to 12 hours by slow infusion, adjusted to trough levels (target usually 10 to 20 mg/l, 15 to 20 mg/l in serious infection) or area-under-curve dosing; teicoplanin 6 to 12 mg/kg every 12 hours for 3 to 5 doses, then once daily; oral vancomycin 125 mg four times daily for 10 days for C. difficile Child dose Vancomycin 15 mg/kg every 6 to 8 hours, guided by levels; teicoplanin 10 mg/kg every 12 hours for 3 doses, then 6 to 10 mg/kg daily Avoid Previous severe reaction; take care in renal impairment, with other nephrotoxic or ototoxic drugs Pregnancy Use only if benefit outweighs risk"],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s2-1","Mechanism","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · How they work","Glycopeptides are too large to pass the outer membrane of Gram-negatives, which explains their spectrum. Killing is slow and time-dependent; vancomycin penetrates the cerebrospinal fluid adequately only when the meninges are inflamed."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s2-2","Handling","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · How they work","Neither is absorbed orally. Both are renally excreted; vancomycin has a narrow therapeutic window. Teicoplanin's long half-life allows once-daily dosing and intramuscular use."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s3-1","MRSA","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · Using them in ENT","Screen and decolonise MRSA carriers (nasal mupirocin and chlorhexidine wash) before elective head and neck surgery; give glycopeptide prophylaxis when MRSA is known. Treat established MRSA wound, cartilage or neck infection with a glycopeptide (or linezolid), with drainage."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s3-2","Intracranial infection","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · Using them in ENT","Add vancomycin to ceftriaxone for meningitis and abscesses where resistant pneumococci or MRSA are possible, for example after neurosurgery or cochlear implantation."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s4-1","Adverse effects","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · Safety","Vancomycin flushing syndrome (formerly red man syndrome): flushing, itch and hypotension when infused too fast; slow the rate (at least 60 minutes per gram). Nephrotoxicity , especially with high troughs, piperacillin-tazobactam and aminoglycosides. Ototoxicity (tinnitus, high-frequency loss), rare alone but more likely with aminoglycosides and loop diuretics. Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, DRESS; teicoplanin has fewer infusion and renal effects."],["ev","cl:t-dr_vanc~s4-2","Vancomycin and teicoplanin: current evidence","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · Safety","Dosing. The 2020 American consensus guideline recommends area-under-the-curve (AUC)-guided dosing, aiming for an AUC of 400 to 600 mg·h/l, rather than trough levels alone, because high troughs increase kidney injury without improving outcomes (Rybak et al., Am J Health Syst Pharm 2020). Kidneys. The risk of acute kidney injury rises when combined with piperacillin–tazobactam or other nephrotoxins."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s4-2","Interactions","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · Safety","Additive toxicity with aminoglycosides , loop diuretics, ciclosporin, cisplatin and piperacillin-tazobactam."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s5-1","Viva essentials","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Spectrum: Gram-positive only , MRSA. Monitoring: trough or AUC levels, renal function, hearing. Flushing syndrome and how to prevent it. Why oral vancomycin only treats C. difficile."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vanc~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Vancomycin and teicoplanin · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using vancomycin alone for a mixed or Gram-negative infection. Rapid infusion. Not checking levels and renal function, especially with other nephrotoxins."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vitd~s1-1","Summary","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol · At a glance","Active vitamin D Summary Alfacalcidol 1-alpha-hydroxycholecalciferol, converted in the liver to calcitriol; adult 0.5 to 1 microgram daily initially, adjusted to calcium (commonly 0.25 to 2 micrograms daily); child by weight Calcitriol 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (active hormone); 0.25 to 0.5 micrograms once or twice daily Colecalciferol Native vitamin D3 for deficiency (for example a loading course then maintenance by local protocol) How they work Increase intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate and bone resorption; raise serum calcium within days ENT uses Hypoparathyroidism after thyroidectomy (with oral calcium); hungry bone syndrome after parathyroidectomy (often started before surgery in severe disease); vitamin D deficiency before parathyroidectomy Monitoring Calcium two or three times a week at first, then weekly to monthly; aim for low normal calcium in hypoparathyroidism to limit hypercalciuria"],["sec","cl:t-dr_vitd~s2-1","After thyroidectomy","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol · Using them in ENT","When PTH is low after total thyroidectomy, alfacalcidol is often started with oral calcium; wean both as parathyroid function recovers, guided by calcium and PTH, usually over weeks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vitd~s2-2","Before parathyroidectomy","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol · Using them in ENT","Correct vitamin D deficiency before surgery for primary hyperparathyroidism (it does not usually worsen hypercalcaemia and reduces post-operative hungry bone effects)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vitd~s3-1","Adverse effects","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol · Safety","Hypercalcaemia (thirst, polyuria, constipation, confusion) and hypercalciuria with nephrocalcinosis and stones if overtreated; alfacalcidol and calcitriol have a short half-life, so hypercalcaemia resolves within days of stopping."],["ev","cl:t-dr_vitd~s3-2","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol: current evidence","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol · Safety","Hypoparathyroidism. The Second International Workshop on hypoparathyroidism recommends activated vitamin D and calcium to keep serum calcium in the low-normal range, avoiding hypercalciuria and kidney stones, with replacement PTH for patients not controlled on conventional treatment (Khan et al., J Bone Miner Res 2022). Palopegteriparatide, a long-acting PTH, allowed most patients to stop activated vitamin D and high-dose calcium in a phase 3 trial. After thyroidectomy. Early PTH-guided prophylaxis reduces symptomatic hypocalcaemia."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vitd~s3-2","Interactions","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol · Safety","Thiazides (hypercalcaemia); digoxin; enzyme inducers reduce vitamin D levels."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vitd~s4-1","Viva essentials","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why active vitamin D is needed in hypoparathyroidism. Calcium targets in long-term hypoparathyroidism."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vitd~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Active vitamin D: alfacalcidol and calcitriol · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using colecalciferol alone in post-surgical hypoparathyroidism. No calcium monitoring after starting alfacalcidol."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vori~s1-1","Summary","Voriconazole and posaconazole · At a glance","Voriconazole and posaconazole Summary Spectrum Voriconazole: Aspergillus, Candida (including fluconazole-resistant), Fusarium, Scedosporium; no Mucorales activity . Posaconazole and isavuconazole: Aspergillus, Candida and Mucorales ENT uses Invasive fungal sinusitis caused by Aspergillus (voriconazole); invasive Aspergillus otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis; mucormycosis step-down after amphotericin or salvage (posaconazole, isavuconazole) Voriconazole dose Intravenously 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for 2 doses, then 4 mg/kg every 12 hours; orally 400 mg every 12 hours for 2 doses, then 200 mg every 12 hours (lower in patients under 40 kg); trough levels of about 1 to 5.5 mg/l Posaconazole dose Tablets 300 mg twice daily on day 1, then 300 mg once daily, with level monitoring Isavuconazole dose 200 mg every 8 hours for 6 doses, then 200 mg once daily"],["sec","cl:t-dr_vori~s2-1","Invasive fungal sinusitis","Voriconazole and posaconazole · Using them in ENT","In an immunocompromised patient (neutropenia, transplant, poorly controlled diabetes) with facial pain or numbness, nasal crusting or pale, insensate mucosa, take urgent biopsies of the middle turbinate and septum for histology and culture. Aspergillus: voriconazole. Mucor or not yet identified: liposomal amphotericin, which covers both. Combine with aggressive surgical debridement to bleeding tissue and correction of the immunosuppression. Mortality remains high."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vori~s3-1","Adverse effects","Voriconazole and posaconazole · Safety","Voriconazole : visual disturbances in about a third (altered or bright vision, usually transient), hallucinations, hepatotoxicity, photosensitivity and skin cancer with long-term use, fluorosis and periostitis, QT prolongation. Posaconazole: gastrointestinal upset, hepatotoxicity, QT prolongation, pseudo-hyperaldosteronism. Isavuconazole shortens rather than prolongs the QT interval and has fewer interactions."],["ev","cl:t-dr_vori~s3-2","Voriconazole and posaconazole: current evidence","Voriconazole and posaconazole · Safety","Invasive aspergillosis. The American guideline recommends voriconazole as primary treatment, with isavuconazole as an alternative (Patterson et al., Clin Infect Dis 2016). Monitoring. Levels vary widely; therapeutic drug monitoring (trough about 1 to 5.5 mg/l) improves efficacy and safety. Side effects. Transient visual disturbance, hallucinations, liver injury and photosensitivity; long-term use is linked to cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma and periostitis. It has no activity against Mucor ."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vori~s3-2","Interactions","Voriconazole and posaconazole · Safety","All are strong CYP3A4 inhibitors: contraindicated with some drugs (rifampicin, carbamazepine and others reduce levels; they raise levels of ciclosporin, tacrolimus, sirolimus, vincristine and midazolam)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vori~s4-1","Viva essentials","Voriconazole and posaconazole · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Which azole covers Mucor and which does not. The treatment of acute invasive fungal sinusitis: debridement, antifungal, correct immunosuppression. Voriconazole visual and skin toxicity."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vori~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Voriconazole and posaconazole · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting voriconazole for suspected mucormycosis. Relying on antifungal drugs without surgery."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vsupp~s1-1","Summary","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics · At a glance","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics Summary Agents Prochlorperazine; cinnarizine; cyclizine; promethazine; betahistine is not a vestibular sedative How they work Prochlorperazine blocks D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone; antihistamines block H1 and muscarinic receptors in the vestibular nuclei and vomiting centre ENT uses Acute vertigo with nausea (vestibular neuritis, Ménière's attack, vestibular migraine, post-operative ear surgery); motion sickness (cinnarizine, promethazine, hyoscine) Adult dose Prochlorperazine 5 to 10 mg orally up to three times daily, 3 to 6 mg buccally twice daily, or 12.5 mg intramuscularly; cinnarizine 30 mg three times daily; cyclizine 50 mg up to three times daily orally or intravenously; promethazine 25 mg at night or up to three times daily Child dose Prochlorperazine is not for children under 1 year and is used with care in older children; cinnarizine 5 to 12 years 15 mg three times daily; cyclizine and promethazine by age Duration Ideally no more than 3 days Avoid Prochlorperazine in Parkinson's disease, and in the elderly where possible; all in glaucoma and urinary retention (anticholinergic)"],["sec","cl:t-dr_vsupp~s2-1","Acute vestibular syndrome","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics · Using them in ENT","In acute vestibular neuritis, short-term prochlorperazine (buccal if vomiting) for the first days, then stop and start early mobilisation and vestibular rehabilitation ; continued suppression delays compensation and prolongs symptoms. Always check for a central cause first (HINTS examination, new neurological signs, inability to walk)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vsupp~s2-2","Ménière's attacks","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics · Using them in ENT","Buccal prochlorperazine or oral cinnarizine during an attack; they do not prevent attacks."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vsupp~s3-1","Adverse effects","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics · Safety","Extrapyramidal effects with prochlorperazine: acute dystonia (oculogyric crisis, especially in young women), parkinsonism, akathisia; tardive dyskinesia with long-term use; neuroleptic malignant syndrome rarely. Sedation, dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention and constipation; confusion and falls in the elderly. Cinnarizine with long-term use can cause parkinsonism and depression in the elderly."],["ev","cl:t-dr_vsupp~s3-2","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics: current evidence","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics · Safety","Acute vertigo. Prochlorperazine, cyclizine and other vestibular sedatives are for the first days of an attack only; prolonged use delays compensation. The American BPPV guideline advises against them as routine treatment for BPPV (Bhattacharyya et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017). Vestibular neuritis. In a randomised trial, methylprednisolone improved recovery of canal function (Strupp et al., NEJM 2004), but a Cochrane review found insufficient evidence that steroids improve symptoms (Fishman et al., 2011). Long-term cinnarizine in older people can cause drug-induced parkinsonism."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vsupp~s3-2","Interactions","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics · Safety","Other sedatives and alcohol; QT-prolonging drugs (promethazine, prochlorperazine)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vsupp~s4-1","Viva essentials","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why vestibular sedatives should be used for days, not weeks (central compensation). Extrapyramidal side effects and their treatment (procyclidine for dystonia). Why to examine for a central cause before treating vertigo symptomatically."],["sec","cl:t-dr_vsupp~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics · For the examination","Common pitfalls Repeat prescriptions of prochlorperazine for chronic dizziness in an elderly patient. Missing a cerebellar stroke presenting as acute vertigo."],["sec","cl:t-dr_wax~s1-1","Summary","Cerumenolytics · At a glance","Cerumenolytics Summary Preparations Olive oil; sodium bicarbonate 5% drops; urea hydrogen peroxide; docusate; almond and arachis (peanut) oil products; water and saline How they work Water-based drops hydrate and disintegrate keratin squames; oils lubricate and soften; peroxide releases oxygen that breaks up wax ENT uses Impacted or symptomatic wax; before irrigation or microsuction; regular use in patients prone to impaction (hearing aid users) Dose Typically 3 to 5 drops two or three times daily for 3 to 5 days Avoid Perforated drum or grommet (for most products); otitis externa; arachis and almond oil in nut allergy"],["sec","cl:t-dr_wax~s2-1","Before removal","Cerumenolytics · Using them in ENT","NICE advises drops for 3 to 5 days, then irrigation if wax persists (or microsuction in specialist settings). Remove wax only if it causes symptoms, prevents examination or prevents taking an impression for a hearing aid."],["sec","cl:t-dr_wax~s2-2","When not to irrigate","Cerumenolytics · Using them in ENT","Do not irrigate an ear with a perforation or history of one, previous ear surgery, a grommet, a cleft palate repair, acute otitis externa or media, or an only hearing ear, or when the patient has had complications from irrigation before. Use microsuction instead."],["sec","cl:t-dr_wax~s2-3","Self-care","Cerumenolytics · Using them in ENT","Advise patients not to use cotton buds, which push wax deeper; olive oil sprays used regularly reduce impaction."],["ev","cl:t-dr_wax~s3-1","Cerumenolytics: current evidence","Cerumenolytics · Safety","Evidence. A Cochrane review found that wax-softening drops are better than no treatment, but no drop, including plain water or saline, is clearly better than another (Aaron et al., 2018). The American guideline recommends drops, irrigation or manual removal only for symptomatic wax, and advises against cotton buds and ear candling (Schwartz et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017). Practical point. Using drops for three to five days before irrigation improves success."],["sec","cl:t-dr_wax~s3-1","Adverse effects","Cerumenolytics · Safety","Temporary fullness, dizziness or irritation; otitis externa; bubbling and fizzing with peroxide; allergy to nut oils."],["sec","cl:t-dr_wax~s4-1","Viva essentials","Cerumenolytics · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Contraindications to irrigation . That water-based and oil drops work similarly. When wax removal is actually indicated."],["sec","cl:t-dr_wax~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Cerumenolytics · For the examination","Common pitfalls Irrigating an ear with a history of perforation or surgery. Arachis oil products in a peanut-allergic patient."],["sec","cl:t-dr_xero~s1-1","Summary","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes · At a glance","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes Summary Pilocarpine Muscarinic agonist that stimulates remaining salivary tissue; 5 mg three times daily with or after meals, increased to a maximum of 30 mg a day; review at 12 weeks (benefit takes weeks) Cevimeline Muscarinic agonist used for Sjögren's syndrome (not available in the UK) Saliva substitutes Carboxymethylcellulose, mucin or xanthan gum sprays, gels and lozenges; neutral pH preferred to protect teeth Dental protection High-fluoride toothpaste (5,000 ppm), fluoride trays, regular dental review; dental assessment and extractions before radiotherapy Prevention Intensity-modulated radiotherapy sparing at least one parotid; amifostine (radioprotector) is effective but rarely used because of hypotension and nausea; submandibular gland transfer in selected patients"],["sec","cl:t-dr_xero~s2-1","After radiotherapy","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes · Using them in ENT","Pilocarpine helps patients with residual gland function; it is less useful when glands are fibrotic. Sip water, sugar-free gum and humidification help. Watch for candidiasis and for osteoradionecrosis after dental extractions in irradiated bone."],["sec","cl:t-dr_xero~s3-1","Pilocarpine adverse effects","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes · Safety","Sweating (common), flushing, rhinitis, urinary frequency, diarrhoea, blurred vision (especially at night), bradycardia."],["ev","cl:t-dr_xero~s3-2","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes: current evidence","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes · Safety","Prevention first. Parotid-sparing IMRT halved severe xerostomia compared with conventional radiotherapy (Nutting et al., Lancet Oncol 2011, PARSPORT). Pilocarpine improves saliva and dryness in patients with residual gland function, after radiotherapy and in Sjögren's syndrome, but sweating and flushing limit its use. Saliva substitutes give short relief only. Submandibular gland transfer before radiotherapy preserves one gland in selected patients. Fluoride and dental review prevent radiation caries."],["sec","cl:t-dr_xero~s3-2","Contraindications","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes · Safety","Uncontrolled asthma and COPD, narrow-angle glaucoma, acute iritis, significant cardiovascular disease, and beta blockers (conduction effects)."],["sec","cl:t-dr_xero~s4-1","Viva essentials","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Prevention of radiation xerostomia (parotid-sparing radiotherapy). Pilocarpine's mechanism, dose and contraindications. Dental care and osteoradionecrosis."],["sec","cl:t-dr_xero~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Pilocarpine and saliva substitutes · For the examination","Common pitfalls Stopping pilocarpine after 2 weeks for lack of effect."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s1-1","What it is","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Pinna haematoma","Blood collects between the auricular cartilage and its perichondrium after a shearing blow, usually in contact sports (wrestling, rugby, boxing, judo). The cartilage has no blood supply of its own; it is fed from the perichondrium. Lifted off by blood, it starves, becomes infected ( perichondritis ) or forms disorganised new cartilage, giving the cauliflower ear . Congenital anomalies and other injuries of the pinna are covered in Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma . Look beyond the ear A head injury may take priority. A pinna haematoma in a baby who is not yet walking suggests non-accidental injury. Redness, heat and severe pain mean infection: perichondritis needs antipseudomonal antibiotics and drainage."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s1-2","Choosing the method","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Pinna haematoma","Pinna haematoma: which treatment Situation Treatment Small (under about a third of the pinna), fresh Aspiration with a wide-bore needle, then a compressive dressing; review and repeat if it re-collects Large, recurrent after aspiration, or organised Incision and drainage with a bolster (through-and-through sutures over dental rolls) Late presentation, infection, previous drainage As above with antibiotics Where the books differ · Aspiration, bolsters and antibiotics Oxford Specialist Handbook: Aspiration for haematomas under a third of the pinna, drainage with bolsters otherwise. Dhingra: Aspiration first, incision and dental-roll bolsters if it fails, antibiotics for all. K. J. Lee: Bolsters for 7 to 14 days, with antibiotics. In practice: Aspirate a small fresh haematoma; incise and bolster a large, recurrent or organised one. Leave the bolster about a week (up to two), and give antibiotics, as most units do."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s1-3","Incision and drainage","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Pinna haematoma","1.3.1 Anaesthesia A ring block of the pinna (local anaesthetic without adrenaline is traditional, though adrenaline is safe in the ear) or general anaesthesia in children. 1.3.2 Steps Incise along the inner curve of the helix (the scaphoid fossa side of the helical rim) or within the haematoma along a natural fold, where the scar hides. Milk out and wash out the clot; curette organised clot gently. Leave the wound open (or loosely closed) so blood can escape. Close the dead space: through-and-through mattress sutures tied over dental rolls or silicone splints on each side of the ear, or packing every concavity with soaked cotton wool under a head bandage. Senior's pearl Recurrence comes from the dead space, not from the method of evacuation. A bolster that holds skin and perichondrium against the cartilage on both sides is what prevents a second visit. 1.3.3 Aftercare Review at 4 to 7 days; bolsters are usually left for about a week. Give antibiotics for infection, delay or repeated drainage (most units give them routinely). Advise headgear for return to sport."],["ev","cl:t-earminor~s1-4","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus: current evidence","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Pinna haematoma","In an institutional series of 87 cases, recurrence was lower when an otolaryngologist treated or advised on the first drainage and when a bolster dressing was applied (Dalal et al., Laryngoscope 2020). Untreated or recurrent haematoma leads to the cauliflower ear, whose correction requires excision of deformed cartilage and reshaping (Greywoode et al., Facial Plast Surg 2010)."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s1-4","Complications","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Pinna haematoma","Re-accumulation, infection and perichondritis, cartilage necrosis, cauliflower ear, pressure necrosis from a tight bolster."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s2-1","What it is","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Preauricular sinus","A blind epithelial tract opening in front of the ascending crus of the helix, from incomplete fusion of the hillocks of His (first and second arch tubercles). It is distinct from a first branchial cleft anomaly, which runs towards the external canal and the facial nerve. The tract is fine, branches, runs close to or through the helical cartilage, and ends superficial to the temporalis fascia, above the parotid. That is why it is excised widely, as a block on the fascia."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s2-2","When to operate","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Preauricular sinus","Asymptomatic pits need nothing. Treat infection with antibiotics (co-amoxiclav) and drain an abscess by aspiration or a small incision. Excise after recurrent infection, once the infection has settled. Bilateral pits and the kidneys Ask about hearing and the family. Preauricular pits with branchial cysts or fistulae and hearing loss suggest branchio-oto-renal syndrome; examine the neck and consider a renal ultrasound."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s2-3","Excision","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Preauricular sinus","2.3.1 The supra-auricular approach Extend the elliptical incision around the opening upwards into the postauricular or temporal skin. Expose the temporalis fascia above the helical root and work downwards and forwards on the fascia, taking all the soft tissue between the fascia and skin as one block with the tract, including a sliver of helical cartilage where the tract touches it. 2.3.2 Classic sinectomy An ellipse around the opening and dissection of the tract, aided by a probe, methylene blue or the microscope. Dye can mislead Probing and dye injection can create false passages and miss branches. Excise widely down to the temporalis fascia and the helical root rather than following the tract. 2.3.3 Closure Undermine the edges, close the deep layer with absorbable sutures and the skin with fine sutures; a pressure dressing reduces haematoma."],["ev","cl:t-earminor~s2-4","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus: current evidence","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Preauricular sinus","A systematic review of 14 studies found recurrence of 5.5% after sinectomy against 2.2% after the supra-auricular approach; using the microscope brought sinectomy recurrence down to about 1.9%, and drains increased wound infection without a clear benefit (Bruijnzeel et al., Laryngoscope 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s2-4","Complications","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · Preauricular sinus","Recurrence (the main one), wound infection, haematoma, scar, injury to the helical cartilage, and rarely the frontal branch of the facial nerve if dissection strays too far forwards and low over the zygomatic arch."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s3-1","Viva essentials","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why a haematoma damages cartilage. Aspiration versus incision and how to stop recurrence. Perichondritis and its antibiotic cover. Embryology of the preauricular sinus and its difference from a first cleft anomaly. Supra-auricular approach and why it recurs less."],["sec","cl:t-earminor~s3-2","Common pitfalls","Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus · For the examination","Common pitfalls Draining a haematoma without a bolster. Missing a head injury or non-accidental injury. Operating on an acutely infected sinus. Chasing the tract instead of excising the block on the fascia."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s1-1","What hearing needs","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Overview","For the middle ear to work, it needs an intact drum, a mobile ossicular chain, two mobile windows with acoustic separation (sound must reach the oval window but not the round window at the same time), an aerated middle ear with a working Eustachian tube, and a working cochlea. Every reconstruction tries to restore as many of these as possible."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s1-2","Approaches","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Overview","Transcanal (Rosen): through the canal with a speculum, for stapes surgery, small perforations and exploratory tympanotomy. Endaural (Lempert): incisions in the canal extended up through the incisura terminalis , where there is no cartilage. Postaural (Wilde): behind the ear, for mastoid surgery and anterior perforations. In children under two, the mastoid tip is absent and the facial nerve is superficial, so the incision is shorter and more horizontal."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s2-1","Indications","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Myringotomy and grommets","Acute otitis media with a bulging, painful drum or complications; incomplete resolution; recurrent acute otitis media; otitis media with effusion; barotrauma; atelectasis; and access for intratympanic treatment."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s2-2","Site","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Myringotomy and grommets","Posteroinferior for draining acute pus; anteroinferior or inferior for effusion and tubes. An anterosuperior tube stays in longest. Figure 1 Grommets in the tympanic membrane. Dhingra, fig. 10.4"],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s2-3","Complications","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Myringotomy and grommets","Injury to the ossicles or a high jugular bulb, persistent perforation, tube blockage or extrusion, otorrhoea, granuloma and tympanosclerosis. Pearl Look for a vascular anomaly (a high jugular bulb or aberrant carotid) before cutting the drum."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s3-1","Myringoplasty","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Repairing the drum and chain","Repair of a perforation alone, without inspecting or reconstructing the middle ear. It differs from a type I tympanoplasty, in which the middle ear is examined. 3.1.1 Grafts Temporalis fascia is the commonest; tragal perichondrium, cartilage (resists retraction) and vein are alternatives. 3.1.2 Techniques Underlay: the graft goes medial to the drum remnant and handle of malleus. Risks: a narrow middle ear space and anterior reperforation. Overlay: the graft goes lateral to the fibrous layer after removing the epithelium. Risks: blunting of the anterior angle, epithelial pearls and lateralisation of the graft. Figure 2 Myringoplasty: (A) the underlay technique, with the graft medial to the annulus and supported by gelfoam; (B) the overlay technique. Dhingra, fig. 5.3 3.1.3 Small holes A paper patch after cauterising the rim, or a fat plug. 3.1.4 Contraindications Active discharge, uncontrolled nasal allergy, otitis externa, squamous epithelium growing into the middle ear, an only hearing ear, and young children under about three."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s3-2","Tympanoplasty","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Repairing the drum and chain","Eradication of middle ear disease and reconstruction of the hearing mechanism, with or without mastoidectomy. 3.2.1 Wullstein's types Type I: graft on the handle of malleus, chain intact. Type II: graft on the incus, malleus missing. Type III: graft on the stapes head (myringostapediopexy). Type IV: only a mobile footplate; the graft shields the round window, creating a small air pocket. Type V: fixed footplate; a window is made in the lateral canal (Va) or the footplate removed (Vb). Figure 3 Types of tympanoplasty: the graft rests progressively on the malleus, incus, stapes and footplate. Dhingra, fig. 5.2"],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s3-3","Ossicular reconstruction (ossiculoplasty)","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Repairing the drum and chain","3.3.1 What is missing The commonest defect is erosion of the long process of the incus and the incudostapedial joint, from chronic otitis media or retraction; trauma most often damages the incus. An air–bone gap over about 30 dB is a good indication for reconstruction, although smaller gaps are accepted when surgery is needed anyway for infection or cholesteatoma. 3.3.2 Classification The Austin–Kartush classification describes what remains: 0 all ossicles present; A malleus and stapes present (incus eroded); B malleus present, stapes superstructure absent; C malleus absent, stapes present; D both absent; E fixed ossicular head; F fixed stapes. The Middle Ear Risk Index (MERI) adds scores for otorrhoea, perforation, cholesteatoma, ossicular status, granulation or effusion, previous surgery and smoking to predict the result. 3.3.3 Options: it depends on the stapes Stapes present: incus interposition or a reshaped autograft ossicle, a partial ossicular replacement prosthesis (PORP) on the stapes head, bone cement (hydroxyapatite or glass ionomer) bridging a small incus defect, or myringostapediopexy. Only the footplate: a total ossicular replacement prosthesis (TORP) or autologous columella, sometimes with a footplate shoe for stability. Modern prostheses are titanium or hydroxyapatite and are covered by a cartilage plate to prevent extrusion. Figure 4 First, sculptured autograft ossicles. Second, hydroxyapatite and titanium partial and total prostheses. Dhingra, figs 5.4 and 5.5 3.3.4 Results A PORP gives better hearing than a TORP: an air–bone gap under 20 dB is reached in "],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s4-1","Landmarks","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Mastoid operations","MacEwen's (suprameatal) triangle marks the antrum, about 12 to 15 mm deep in adults. Körner's septum , a persistent petrosquamous plate, can be mistaken for the floor of the antrum. Other landmarks: the lateral semicircular canal, sinus plate, tegmen, digastric ridge and facial nerve. Figure 5 First, MacEwen's triangle. Second, Körner's septum, which may hide the antrum. Dhingra, figs 1.12 and 1.14 Figure 6 MacEwen's triangle, and the middle ear seen through a posterior tympanotomy. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s4-2","Cortical (Schwartze) mastoidectomy","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Mastoid operations","Removes the mastoid air cells and opens the antrum; the posterior canal wall and middle ear are left alone. For coalescent or masked mastoiditis, and as access for endolymphatic sac surgery, facial nerve decompression, cochlear implants and translabyrinthine surgery."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s4-3","Modified radical mastoidectomy","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Mastoid operations","The posterior canal wall is lowered and the attic, antrum and mastoid are exteriorised into one cavity, keeping the drum remnant, the working ossicles and the Eustachian tube. For cholesteatoma of the attic and antrum."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s4-4","Radical mastoidectomy","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Mastoid operations","Everything is exteriorised: the drum, malleus and incus and the middle ear lining are removed, and the Eustachian tube is plugged. For disease that cannot be cleared safely otherwise, failed surgery, and some tumours. Hearing loss is 50 dB or more. Mastoid operations compared Operation Posterior canal wall Ossicles Hearing Cortical Kept Kept Usually unchanged Modified radical Removed Working parts kept Can be reconstructed Radical Removed All but the stapes removed Loss of 50 dB or more"],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s4-5","Canal wall up and canal wall down","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Mastoid operations","The choice between keeping and removing the posterior wall is covered in Cholesteatoma ."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s4-6","Meatoplasty","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Mastoid operations","Every canal wall down cavity needs a wide meatus for ventilation and cleaning."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s4-7","Obliteration","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Mastoid operations","Filling a cavity with pedicled muscle, bone pâté or other material reduces discharge and care; disease must never be buried under it."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s4-8","Complications of mastoid surgery","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Mastoid operations","Facial nerve injury, lateral canal fistula with vertigo and hearing loss, dural injury and CSF leak, sigmoid sinus bleeding, and incus dislocation. About a quarter of radical cavities continue to discharge."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s5-1","Before","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Before and after","An audiogram and tuning forks before any ear operation; CT for cholesteatoma, revisions and suspected complications; check the other ear and Eustachian tube function."],["ev","cl:t-earsurg~s5-2","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles: current evidence","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Before and after","Grafts. A meta-analysis of 37 studies (3606 patients) found graft take of 92% with cartilage and 82% with temporalis fascia, with similar hearing results; cartilage is preferred for high-risk drums, retraction pockets and revision (Jalali et al., Laryngoscope 2017). Grommets. The American guideline recommends grommets for children with bilateral glue ear for three months or more with documented hearing loss, and advises against them for a single episode of glue ear lasting under three months or for recurrent acute otitis media without effusion at assessment (Rosenfeld et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2022); NICE (NG233, 2023) offers grommets or hearing aids after three months of active observation. Endoscopic ear surgery improves views of hidden areas such as the sinus tympani and reduces residual cholesteatoma in some series, but long-term comparative evidence is limited."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s5-2","After","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · Before and after","Keep water out of the ear while a tube or graft is healing; review repaired ears at about three and six weeks; the graft is fully epithelialised in six to eight weeks. Clean mastoid cavities every four to six months in the first year, then yearly."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s6-1","Viva essentials","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Myringoplasty versus tympanoplasty. Wullstein's five types. Underlay versus overlay. Cortical, modified radical and radical mastoidectomy: what each removes and keeps. Mastoid landmarks: MacEwen's triangle and Körner's septum. Indications for myringotomy and the choice of site."],["sec","cl:t-earsurg~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles · For the examination","Common pitfalls Repairing a drum while the ear is still discharging. Mistaking Körner's septum for the floor of the antrum. Burying cholesteatoma under an obliteration."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s1-1","Definition","Epistaxis · Overview","Epistaxis is bleeding from inside the nose. The blood may run forward out of the nostril, backward into the nasopharynx and throat, or both. It is always a sign of something else, never a final diagnosis, and one rule holds on every shift: an attempt should always be made to find its local or systemic cause."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s1-2","Epidemiology","Epistaxis · Overview","Most people have at least one nosebleed in their lives, and around one in six of the population is estimated to bleed in any given year. Only a small fraction ever seek medical help. The distribution is bimodal: children and young adults, who bleed from the front of the septum, and adults past middle age, who make up most hospital admissions and most posterior bleeds."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s1-3","Why it matters","Epistaxis · Overview","The patients who reach the emergency department are older, often hypertensive, frequently on an anticoagulant or antiplatelet drug, and bleeding from further back in the nose, where blood runs silently down the pharynx and is easy to underestimate. In them, epistaxis is as much a resuscitation problem as a nasal one."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s2-1","Arterial supply","Epistaxis · Applied anatomy","The nasal cavity draws blood from both the internal and the external carotid arteries, and the two systems anastomose freely on the septum and the lateral wall. That double supply is why nosebleeds can be persistent, and why ligating one vessel sometimes isn't enough. 2.1.1 Internal carotid system Anterior ethmoidal artery, from the ophthalmic artery, entering the nose through the roof. Posterior ethmoidal artery, also from the ophthalmic, smaller and further back. These supply the upper septum and lateral wall above the middle turbinate. 2.1.2 External carotid system Sphenopalatine artery, terminal branch of the maxillary artery: the main supply of the nose. Greater palatine artery, from the maxillary, reaching the septum through the incisive canal. Superior labial artery, from the facial, supplying the front of the septum and vestibule. Figure 1 Arterial supply of the nasal septum (first) and the lateral nasal wall (second). The anterior ethmoidal, septal branch of the sphenopalatine, greater palatine and superior labial arteries converge on Little's area. Dhingra, figs 33.1 and 33.2 Figure 2 The ophthalmic artery giving off the anterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries in the orbit, on their way to the roof of the nose. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s2-2","Little's area and Kiesselbach's plexus","Epistaxis · Applied anatomy","On the anteroinferior septum, just above the vestibule, four arteries meet to form Kiesselbach's plexus ; the region it occupies is Little's area . 2.2.1 The four contributors Anterior ethmoidal (internal carotid) Septal branch of the sphenopalatine (external carotid) Greater palatine (external carotid) Septal branch of the superior labial (external carotid) 2.2.2 Why it bleeds It sits exactly where inspired air dries and cracks the mucosa, where the mucosa is thin and closely bound to cartilage, and where a fingernail reaches. About nine in ten bleeds start here."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s2-3","The posterior territory","Epistaxis · Applied anatomy","2.3.1 Sphenopalatine artery It enters the nose through the sphenopalatine foramen, just behind and below the crista ethmoidalis of the palatine bone, near the posterior end of the middle turbinate, and often divides into several branches before emerging. It is the dominant supply to the nose in about 85% of people (the rest are anterior ethmoid-dominant). Bleeding from its posterior lateral nasal and posterior septal branches is the classic posterior epistaxis. 2.3.2 Woodruff's plexus A plexus on the lateral wall below the posterior end of the inferior turbinate, and the other classic posterior bleeding site in adults. 2.3.3 Ethmoidal territory Bleeding above the level of the middle turbinate points to the ethmoidal arteries and therefore to the internal carotid system, which matters because embolisation cannot safely reach it. Where the books differ · Woodruff's plexus Dhingra Woodruff's plexus is a plexus of veins below the posterior end of the inferior turbinate. Lee Woodruff's is the naso-nasopharyngeal plexus over the posterior centimetre of the nasal floor, inferior meatus and middle meatus. In practice For the exam, give the first definition, and add that posterior arterial bleeds come from sphenopalatine branches."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s2-4","Venous drainage","Epistaxis · Applied anatomy","Veins follow the arteries. The retrocolumellar vein , running vertically just behind the columella across the floor of the nose, is a common source of venous bleeding in young people."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~c3","Aetiology","Epistaxis · Aetiology","Local, systemic, or neither. Causes of epistaxis Local Trauma inflammation and drying, structural, neoplastic, iatrogenic Systemic Drugs coagulation and platelet disorders, vascular disorders, cardiovascular, organ failure Idiopathic No cause found despite assessment"],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s3-1","Local causes","Epistaxis · Aetiology","3.1.1 Trauma The commonest cause overall. Digital trauma (nose picking) dominates in children; in adults add forceful blowing, blows to the face, fractures of the nasal bones, midface and skull base, and foreign bodies. 3.1.2 Inflammation and drying Dry, heated air in winter, acute viral rhinitis and sinusitis, rhinitis sicca, atrophic rhinitis, and the crust-forming granulomatous infections all leave a fragile, cracked mucosa. 3.1.3 Structural A septal spur or deviation creates turbulent, drying airflow over its edge; a septal perforation bleeds from its crusted rim. 3.1.4 Neoplastic Benign: juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma, haemangioma, inverted papilloma. Malignant: carcinoma of the nose, sinuses and nasopharynx. Any unilateral, recurrent bleed, especially with obstruction, must be examined endoscopically. 3.1.5 Iatrogenic and topical Nasal and sinus surgery, nasogastric and nasotracheal tubes, nasal oxygen cannulae, topical steroid and decongestant sprays, and intranasal cocaine."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s3-2","Systemic causes","Epistaxis · Aetiology","The logic is simple: anything that impairs clotting, platelets or vessel walls will show up in the nose. 3.2.1 Drugs and supplements Warfarin and the direct oral anticoagulants, aspirin, clopidogrel and other antiplatelets, NSAIDs, and over-the-counter vitamin E, fish oil, garlic, ginkgo and ginseng. 3.2.2 Coagulation and platelet disorders Haemophilia, von Willebrand disease (the commonest inherited bleeding disorder), thrombocytopenia, leukaemia and aplastic anaemia. 3.2.3 Vascular disorders Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia ( Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia ), scurvy, and the vessel fragility of old age. 3.2.4 Cardiovascular Hypertension and atherosclerosis are associated with posterior bleeds in older adults. Whether raised pressure causes the bleed or simply keeps it going is still debated; either way, it should be measured and treated. 3.2.5 Organ failure Liver failure (reduced clotting factors) and renal failure (uraemic platelet dysfunction)."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s3-3","Idiopathic","Epistaxis · Aetiology","In a large share of patients no specific cause is found despite a careful history, examination and blood tests. The label is earned only after the search, not instead of it. Figure 3 Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia: lesions on the nasal septum, tongue, palate and fingertips (arrows). Always look at the lips, tongue and fingers of a recurrent bleeder. Dhingra, fig. 33.3 Red flags Adolescent boy, unilateral bleeding and obstruction: juvenile angiofibroma until proven otherwise ( Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma ). No biopsy in clinic. Adult with unilateral bleeding, obstruction, crusting or facial pain: endoscopy for a sinonasal tumour ( Sinonasal tumours ). A child under two: rare; think of non-accidental injury or a bleeding disorder. Massive or recurrent bleeding after trauma or skull base surgery: carotid pseudoaneurysm; needs angiography."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s4-1","By site: anterior or posterior","Epistaxis · Classification","With the patient sitting up, an anterior bleed runs mostly out of the nostril. A posterior bleed runs back into the throat, often from both sides, and no source is seen on anterior rhinoscopy. Anterior Posterior Frequency Common, about 90% Less common Site Little's area or the front of the lateral wall Posterosuperior cavity; often hard to localise Age Children and young adults Usually over 40 Cause Mostly trauma, dryness Often spontaneous; hypertension, atherosclerosis, anticoagulation Severity Usually mild; pressure, cautery or an anterior pack Often severe; admission, posterior pack or surgery"],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s4-2","By severity","Epistaxis · Classification","There is no universal scale; in practice the useful split is whether first aid is enough. 4.2.1 Minor Stops with pressure or a single cautery, haemodynamically stable, can go home. 4.2.2 Major Continues despite first aid, needs packing, shows haemodynamic change or a falling haemoglobin, or occurs in a frail or anticoagulated patient. These patients are admitted."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s4-3","By pattern: single or recurrent","Epistaxis · Classification","Recurrence changes the question from how to stop it to why it keeps happening. Recurrent unilateral bleeding needs endoscopy; recurrent bilateral bleeding, especially with a family history, needs HHT to be considered."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~c5","Clinical evaluation","Epistaxis · Clinical evaluation","Resuscitate first, then find the bleeding point, then ask why."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s5-1","Immediate assessment","Epistaxis · Clinical evaluation","The first question is not where the blood is coming from, but how much has been lost and whether the airway is safe. A posterior bleed can fill the stomach before it fills a basin, and swallowed blood that returns as dark vomit is easily mistaken for haematemesis. On the ward: the first five minutes Sit the patient up and leaning forward. Check pulse, blood pressure and saturation; secure IV access and send a full blood count, group and save, and clotting if anticoagulated or bleeding heavily. Firm pressure on the soft lower third of the nose for at least five minutes without peeking; many units ask for 10 to 15. Ice in the mouth adds a little reflex vasoconstriction. Suction the clots, spray topical vasoconstrictor and anaesthetic, and look with a headlight and speculum. Ask about anticoagulants, antiplatelets, previous bleeds, family history and alcohol, and which side bled first. Don't stop or reverse anticoagulants for a bleed that local measures can control. Discuss with the prescribing team if it is life-threatening."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s5-2","History","Epistaxis · Clinical evaluation","5.2.1 The bleed Side of onset, duration, estimated volume, whether it runs forward or into the throat, and previous episodes and their treatment. 5.2.2 Local symptoms Obstruction, discharge, crusting, facial pain or numbness, and trauma. 5.2.3 Systemic background Medications including supplements, hypertension, liver or renal disease, easy bruising, and a family history of bleeding or nosebleeds."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s5-3","Examination","Epistaxis · Clinical evaluation","5.3.1 General Pallor, pulse and blood pressure, signs of liver disease, bruising, and telangiectasias on the lips, tongue and fingertips. 5.3.2 Anterior rhinoscopy After clot removal and vasoconstriction: inspect Little's area first, then the front of the lateral wall. The oropharynx is checked for blood running down from behind. 5.3.3 Nasal endoscopy For recurrent bleeds, for bleeding not localised on rhinoscopy, and for any unilateral bleed with suspicious symptoms. It finds points hidden lateral to the turbinates or high in the nose and allows directed cautery instead of a blind pack."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s5-4","Investigations","Epistaxis · Clinical evaluation","5.4.1 Blood tests Not needed for a minor anterior bleed in a healthy patient. For significant bleeds: full blood count, group and save or crossmatch, clotting with INR in warfarinised patients, and liver and renal function where relevant. 5.4.2 Imaging CT or MRI when a tumour is suspected; for juvenile angiofibroma, contrast imaging replaces biopsy. 5.4.3 Angiography Diagnostic and therapeutic: for suspected pseudoaneurysm, vascular tumours, and as the first step of embolisation."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s6-1","The treatment ladder","Epistaxis · Local management","1 First aid and topical vasoconstriction Pressure on the soft nose, leaning forward; pledgets of vasoconstrictor and local anaesthetic. 2 Cautery of a visible point Silver nitrate or bipolar, to the bleeding point only. 3 Anterior packing Ribbon gauze, expanding sponge, or resorbable material. 4 Posterior packing Postnasal pack, Foley or dual balloon; admit and monitor. 5 Endoscopic cautery Directed at the source under local or general anaesthesia. 6 Ligation or embolisation For bleeding that persists despite packing."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s6-2","First aid","Epistaxis · Local management","Trotter's method : the patient sits up, leaning slightly forward over a basin, breathing through the mouth, while the soft part of the nose is pinched firmly against the septum. Leaning forward stops blood being swallowed and lets you see how much is lost. Sucking ice may add a little reflex vasoconstriction; ice packs on the nasal bridge have no proven benefit."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s6-3","Cautery","Epistaxis · Local management","Cautery works when you can see what you are treating. Anaesthetise first, and treat only the bleeding point. 6.3.1 Chemical Roll a silver nitrate stick over a small ring around the vessel, edge first and then inwards, before touching the vessel itself. Best for small, visible anterior points in a field that is not actively pouring. 6.3.2 Electrocautery Bipolar diathermy, usually under endoscopic view, is more effective for brisker or deeper points and for posterior sites. Where available it is preferred to silver nitrate, because it fails less often and reduces packing, admission and pain. After silver nitrate, wipe or irrigate the vestibule with saline to prevent skin burns. Pearl Never cauterise both sides of the septum at the same sitting: the mucoperichondrium on both sides may necrose and leave a perforation."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s6-4","Anterior packing","Epistaxis · Local management","6.4.1 Ribbon gauze About a metre of ribbon gauze, 2.5 cm wide in adults and 12 mm in children, soaked in liquid paraffin or bismuth iodoform paraffin paste and laid in layers so that it presses evenly on the whole cavity. Usually removed after 24 hours; antibiotics are given if it is left longer. 6.4.2 Expanding sponges and balloons Compressed sponges that swell when wetted, and inflatable balloon packs: quicker to insert, more comfortable, and now the standard in most emergency departments. 6.4.3 Resorbable packs Preferred when the patient is anticoagulated or has a bleeding disorder, because removing a non-resorbable pack can restart the bleed. Figure 4 Anterior nasal packing. (A) Vertical layers from the back forwards. (B) Horizontal layers from the floor upwards. Dhingra, fig. 33.4"],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s6-5","Posterior packing","Epistaxis · Local management","When blood runs back into the pharynx and an anterior pack fails, the choana must be blocked. 6.5.1 Postnasal gauze pack A rolled gauze cone with three silk ties is drawn into the nasopharynx on a catheter passed through the nose and out of the mouth, then pushed into place with a finger. An anterior pack goes in front, and two ties are secured over a dental roll at the columella. The third tie is left in the oropharynx, taped to the cheek, to pull the pack out later. 6.5.2 Foley catheter A 12 to 14 F Foley is passed until the balloon lies in the nasopharynx, inflated with saline (not air), drawn forward against the choana and backed by an anterior pack. Protect the ala and columella from pressure. 6.5.3 Dual-balloon devices Commercial catheters with a posterior and an anterior balloon, and a central tube that preserves a nasal airway. Figure 5 First, inserting a postnasal pack on a catheter. Second, a dual-balloon catheter with the posterior balloon at about 10 mL and the anterior at about 30 mL. Dhingra, figs 33.5 and 33.6 6.5.4 Care of the packed patient Admit and monitor: posterior packs fail in about a third. Oxygen: bilateral packs can cause hypoxia through the nasopulmonary reflex, especially in the elderly. Watch for: alar and columellar necrosis, sinusitis, otitis media, aspiration and toxic shock syndrome. Antibiotics are traditionally given while a non-resorbable pack is in, though the evidence for routine use is weak. Explain the pack: what type it is, when it comes out, and what should bring them back."],["ev","cl:t-epistaxis~s6-6","Epistaxis: current evidence","Epistaxis · Local management","The American nosebleed guideline (Tunkel et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020) recommends compression for at least five minutes, packing if the site cannot be seen, resorbable packs in patients on anticoagulants or antiplatelets, nasal endoscopy for recurrent or difficult bleeds, cautery confined to the bleeding site, ligation or embolisation when these fail, and local treatment before transfusion, reversal or stopping anticoagulants unless the bleed is life-threatening. In the NoPAC trial of 496 patients in UK emergency departments, topical tranexamic acid did not reduce the need for anterior packing (43.7% vs 41.3% with placebo) (Reuben et al., Ann Emerg Med 2021). A UK national audit of 1,826 cases found 30-day re-presentation in 13.9% and all-cause 30-day mortality of 3.4%, and wide variation from consensus guidance (National ENT Trainee Research Network, J Laryngol Otol 2017)."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s6-6","Medical adjuncts","Epistaxis · Local management","6.6.1 Blood pressure Treat pain and anxiety first; control persistent hypertension, but avoid dropping the pressure sharply. 6.6.2 Tranexamic acid Topical tranexamic acid did not reduce the need for packing in the NoPAC trial; it should not delay standard treatment. Some smaller trials suggest a benefit, especially in patients on antiplatelet drugs, and oral tranexamic acid helps in HHT."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s7-1","Choosing the vessel","Epistaxis · Surgery and intervention","Persistent bleeding Source seen Endoscopic cautery under local or general anaesthesia Posterior, below the middle turbinate Sphenopalatine ligation or embolisation Above the middle turbinate Ethmoidal ligation not embolisation Septal deviation Septal surgery to reach the bleed"],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s7-2","Endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation","Epistaxis · Surgery and intervention","7.2.1 Indications Posterior bleeding that persists or recurs despite adequate packing. TESPAL is now the operation of choice and has replaced the older open procedures. 7.2.2 Technique Under general anaesthesia, a mucosal incision on the lateral wall in front of the crista ethmoidalis; a mucoperiosteal flap is raised back to the crista, behind which the artery and its branches emerge from the foramen. They are clipped or coagulated, and the flap is replaced. 7.2.3 Outcomes and complications Control is reported in 95 to 98%, usually with discharge the next day; all branches emerging from the foramen must be controlled, and failure should prompt a search for an accessory branch or an ethmoidal source. Complications are minor: crusting, adhesions, epiphora and numbness of the palate or nose; visual loss is very rare."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s7-3","Ethmoidal artery ligation","Epistaxis · Surgery and intervention","7.3.1 Approach An external (Lynch) incision at the medial canthus, raising the periosteum of the medial orbital wall, usually for high bleeds after trauma. 7.3.2 The 24-12-6 rule The anterior ethmoidal foramen lies about 24 mm behind the anterior lacrimal crest, the posterior foramen about 12 mm further back, and the optic canal about 6 mm beyond that. The posterior artery is approached with great care, or left alone."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s7-4","Embolisation","Epistaxis · Surgery and intervention","7.4.1 Method The internal maxillary artery is catheterised by the femoral route and blocked with particles, gelfoam or coils, often bilaterally because of cross-flow. 7.4.2 Who it suits Patients unfit for anaesthesia, vascular tumours and traumatic pseudoaneurysms. It cannot treat the ethmoidal arteries, which arise from the internal carotid system. 7.4.3 Outcomes and risks Success around 88%. Complications include stroke, blindness, facial pain and skin necrosis."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s7-5","Septal surgery","Epistaxis · Surgery and intervention","Raising a mucoperichondrial flap divides the vessels feeding a recurrent septal bleed; septoplasty also straightens a deviation that hides a bleeding point or prevents adequate packing ( Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess )."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s7-6","Historical operations","Epistaxis · Surgery and intervention","7.6.1 Maxillary artery ligation Through a Caldwell-Luc approach and the posterior antral wall. Failure in 10 to 15%, with infraorbital numbness, oroantral fistula and dental injury. 7.6.2 External carotid ligation Above the origin of the superior thyroid artery. Too proximal to be reliable, given the collateral flow; of examination interest now. Pearls Bleeding above the middle turbinate is ethmoidal: embolisation won't help, think ligation. The crista ethmoidalis is the landmark for the sphenopalatine foramen: the artery lies just behind it. If endoscopic ligation fails, look for an ethmoidal source or a missed accessory branch before repacking."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~c8","Special situations","Epistaxis · Special situations","Children, the anticoagulated, HHT, and the bleed after trauma."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s8-1","Children","Epistaxis · Special situations","8.1.1 Typical picture Bleeding from Little's area, from crusting and picking, that stops on its own. 8.1.2 Treatment Antiseptic cream such as chlorhexidine with neomycin to the vestibule twice daily for four weeks settles most cases; silver nitrate for a visible vessel that keeps bleeding. Avoid creams containing arachis oil in peanut allergy. 8.1.3 When to investigate Recurrent bleeding with easy bruising or a family history needs a clotting screen including von Willebrand studies. Under two years of age, think of a bleeding disorder or non-accidental injury."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s8-2","The anticoagulated patient","Epistaxis · Special situations","Patients on warfarin, a direct oral anticoagulant or dual antiplatelets bleed longer and re-bleed more often, but stopping their drugs risks stroke or stent thrombosis. Control the bleed locally first with resorbable packing and check the INR in warfarinised patients. Reversal and withholding are for life-threatening bleeds, decided with the prescribing team: for warfarin with major bleeding, intravenous vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate (fresh frozen plasma only if that is unavailable); for a high INR with minor bleeding, withhold warfarin and give oral vitamin K. Ask about \"natural blood thinners\" such as garlic, ginger, ginkgo, turmeric and vitamin E."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s8-3","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia","Epistaxis · Special situations","Recurrent bleeding from multiple telangiectasias, usually from childhood, with a family history. Repeated packing and aggressive cautery make things worse; laser, bevacizumab, septodermoplasty and Young's procedure are the tools. The full account is in Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia ."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s8-4","Trauma and pseudoaneurysm","Epistaxis · Special situations","Delayed, massive or recurrent bleeding days to weeks after head injury or skull base surgery suggests a pseudoaneurysm of the internal carotid artery. It is a neurovascular emergency: pack, resuscitate and arrange urgent angiography."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s9-1","Of the bleed","Epistaxis · Complications and outcome","Hypovolaemic shock, anaemia, aspiration, and myocardial ischaemia in patients with coronary disease."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s9-2","Of treatment","Epistaxis · Complications and outcome","Cautery: septal perforation. Packing: pressure necrosis, sinusitis, otitis media, hypoxia, toxic shock syndrome. Surgery and embolisation: see 7.2 to 7.4 ."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s9-3","Prognosis","Epistaxis · Complications and outcome","Most bleeds settle with simple measures. A patient whose anterior bleed is stopped by cautery can go home with antiseptic or antibiotic cream for two weeks; after a significant posterior bleed, observe for 6 to 12 hours; anyone with a pack is admitted. Recurrence is common in the elderly, the anticoagulated and those with HHT, and patients admitted with epistaxis are often frail: in a UK national audit, 1 in 7 re-presented within 30 days and 3.4% died within 30 days of any cause."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s10-1","Viva essentials","Epistaxis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Little's area: the four arteries, their parent vessels, and why it bleeds. Trotter's method: position, mouth breathing, and pinching the soft nose. Anterior versus posterior: the five-row comparison. Postnasal pack: technique, the third tie, and why the patient is admitted. Which artery for which site, and why ethmoidal bleeds can't be embolised. Teenage boy, unilateral recurrent bleed: angiofibroma, and no biopsy in clinic."],["sec","cl:t-epistaxis~s10-2","Common pitfalls","Epistaxis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Underestimating blood loss in an elderly patient who is swallowing blood. Packing blindly before suctioning and looking, when a cauterisable point was visible. Cauterising both sides of the septum at once. Stopping anticoagulation reflexively for a bleed a pack would have controlled. Repeated packing in recurrent bilateral bleeds that are really HHT."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s1-1","Abscesses around the mastoid","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Signs","1.1.1 Bezold abscess Pus breaking through the medial side of a pneumatised mastoid tip and collecting under the sternocleidomastoid ( Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis ). 1.1.2 Citelli abscess Pus tracking from the tip along the posterior belly of digastric, behind the mastoid or in the digastric triangle. 1.1.3 Luc abscess A meatal abscess: pus from the antrum tracking under the periosteum of the bony canal."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s1-2","Signs of intracranial spread","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Signs","1.2.1 Griesinger's sign Oedema over the mastoid emissary vein in lateral sinus thrombosis ( Intracranial complications of otitis media ). 1.2.2 Tobey-Ayer test Compressing the jugular vein on the healthy side raises CSF pressure, on the thrombosed side does not; historical. 1.2.3 Crowe-Beck test Compressing the healthy jugular vein engorges the retinal veins in lateral sinus thrombosis; historical. 1.2.4 Gradenigo's triad Ear discharge, deep pain behind the eye and a sixth nerve palsy in petrous apicitis ( Petrous apicitis )."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s1-3","Labyrinth","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Signs","1.3.1 Hennebert's sign A positive fistula test with an intact drum: congenital syphilis, Ménière's disease, superior canal dehiscence ( Superior canal dehiscence ). 1.3.2 Tullio phenomenon Vertigo triggered by loud sound: superior canal dehiscence, perilymph fistula, hydrops."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s1-4","Otosclerosis","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Signs","1.4.1 Schwartze sign A red blush on the promontory through the drum, from an active, vascular focus ( Otosclerosis ). 1.4.2 Carhart's notch A dip of bone conduction maximal at 2000 Hz, mechanical, disappearing after stapes surgery. 1.4.3 Paracusis Willisii Hearing better in noisy surroundings."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s1-5","Tumours","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Signs","1.5.1 Brown's sign A glomus tumour pulsates, then blanches, as pressure in the canal is raised ( Middle ear and temporal bone tumours ). 1.5.2 Rising sun A red glomus tumour seen rising from the floor of the middle ear behind the drum. 1.5.3 Hitselberger's sign Reduced sensation of the posterior canal wall, from pressure on the sensory fibres of the facial nerve by a vestibular schwannoma ( Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours )."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s1-6","Facial nerve","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Signs","1.6.1 Bell's phenomenon The eye rolls up and out when the patient tries to close it ( Facial nerve palsy )."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s1-7","Mastoiditis","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Signs","1.7.1 Reservoir sign The canal refills with pus straight after it is mopped ( Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis ). 1.7.2 Light-house sign Pulsatile discharge through a pars tensa perforation."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s2-1","Facial nerve","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Syndromes","Ramsay Hunt: herpes zoster oticus with facial palsy, often with hearing loss and vertigo. Melkersson-Rosenthal: recurrent facial palsy, swelling of the lips and a fissured tongue. Heerfordt: sarcoidosis with uveitis, parotid swelling and facial palsy. Möbius: congenital bilateral facial palsy with sixth nerve palsy. Bogorad (crocodile tears): tearing while eating after facial nerve regeneration. Frey: gustatory sweating after parotid surgery. Bannwarth: facial palsy in Lyme meningoradiculitis."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s2-2","Inner ear","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Syndromes","Cogan: interstitial keratitis with audiovestibular loss. Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada: uveitis, alopecia, white hair and deafness. Lermoyez: hearing improves with the vertigo attack. Tumarkin's otolithic crisis: sudden drop attacks in Ménière's disease. Mal de débarquement: a rocking sensation persisting after travel by sea or air. van der Hoeve: osteogenesis imperfecta, blue sclerae and deafness."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s2-3","Pain","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Syndromes","Costen: a faulty bite with earache, fullness, tinnitus and dizziness. Eagle: an elongated styloid process with throat and ear pain."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s2-4","Jugular foramen","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Syndromes","Vernet: palsy of the ninth, tenth and eleventh nerves. Collet-Sicard: ninth to twelfth nerves. Villaret: ninth to twelfth nerves with Horner syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s3-1","Middle ear and mastoid","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Spaces and landmarks","Prussak's space: between the pars flaccida and the neck of the malleus; where attic cholesteatoma begins. Trautmann's triangle: bounded by the bony labyrinth, the sigmoid sinus and the superior petrosal sinus (dura); a route to the posterior fossa. Citelli's angle: the sinodural angle, between the sigmoid sinus and the middle fossa dura. MacEwen's triangle: the suprameatal triangle, marking the antrum. Körner's septum: a persistent petrosquamous plate in the mastoid, which can be mistaken for the floor of the antrum. Figure 1 Prussak's space, and MacEwen's triangle. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s3-2","Canal","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Spaces and landmarks","Foramen of Huschke: a defect in the anteroinferior bony canal in children, a route between the canal and the parotid. Fissures of Santorini: clefts in the cartilaginous canal through which infection passes to the parotid."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s3-3","Skull base","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Spaces and landmarks","Dorello's canal: where the sixth nerve passes under the petroclinoid ligament at the petrous tip. Meckel's cave: the trigeminal ganglion on the petrous apex. Hyrtl's fissure: an infant fissure from the hypotympanum to the subarachnoid space."],["ev","cl:t-eponym~s3-4","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance: current evidence","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Spaces and landmarks","Changing names. Several eponyms have been replaced by descriptive terms, and examiners expect both: Wegener's granulomatosis is now granulomatosis with polyangiitis (since 2011); vocal cord dysfunction is inducible laryngeal obstruction ; spasmodic dysphonia is increasingly called laryngeal dystonia (Simonyan et al., Neurology 2021); and keratocystic odontogenic tumour has returned to odontogenic keratocyst in the 2017 and 2022 WHO classifications. Use the current term and give the eponym in brackets."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~s3-4","Nerves","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · Spaces and landmarks","Arnold's nerve: the auricular branch of the vagus; explains the ear cough reflex. Jacobson's nerve: the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal; origin of glomus tympanicum and route for referred pain."],["sec","cl:t-eponym~c4","For the examination","Ear signs and eponyms at a glance · For the examination","Using this page. How to use the names Say what the sign is, then what it means, then which disease it points to. Know which tests are historical (Tobey-Ayer, Crowe-Beck) and what has replaced them (imaging). Link each landmark to the operation where it matters."],["sec","cl:t-et~s1-1","Functions","Eustachian tube disorders · Overview","Ventilation: equalising middle ear pressure with the atmosphere, for the drum to vibrate freely. Protection: from nasopharyngeal sound pressure and secretions. Clearance: drainage of middle ear secretions into the nasopharynx."],["sec","cl:t-et~s1-2","Disorders","Eustachian tube disorders · Overview","Eustachian tube disorders Fails to open Obstructive dysfunction mechanical or functional Fails to close Patulous tube autophony Pressure change Barotrauma the tube locks on descent"],["sec","cl:t-et~s2-1","The adult tube","Eustachian tube disorders · Applied anatomy","About 36 mm long, running downwards, forwards and medially from the middle ear to the nasopharynx at about 45 degrees. The posterolateral third is bony; the anteromedial two-thirds is cartilaginous. The narrowest point, the isthmus , lies at the junction of the two. Figure 1 First, a horizontal section of the tube with its bony and cartilaginous parts and the isthmus. Second, a vertical section: the cartilage forms the medial wall, roof and part of the lateral wall. Dhingra, figs 9.1 and 9.2"],["sec","cl:t-et~s2-2","Opening the tube","Eustachian tube disorders · Applied anatomy","The tube is closed at rest. Tensor veli palatini opens it during swallowing and yawning, helped by levator veli palatini. The pharyngeal opening lies behind the inferior turbinate, bounded by the torus tubarius , with the fossa of Rosenmüller behind it. Figure 2 Endoscopic view of the nasopharynx showing the right torus tubarius and the fossa of Rosenmüller. Dhingra, fig. 9.4 Figure 3 The tube with levator and tensor veli palatini, and its course through the sinus of Morgagni. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-et~s2-3","The infant tube","Eustachian tube disorders · Applied anatomy","About half the adult length, nearly horizontal (about 10 degrees), wider, with floppy cartilage and little elastin. It refluxes nasopharyngeal secretions and protects the middle ear poorly, which explains the frequency of otitis media in children. Function approaches the adult pattern by about age seven to ten."],["sec","cl:t-et~s3-1","Causes","Eustachian tube disorders · Obstructive dysfunction","3.1.1 Mechanical Inflammation and oedema from colds, sinusitis and allergy; adenoid hypertrophy, which also harbours bacteria; nasopharyngeal tumours, especially nasopharyngeal carcinoma in adults; and scarring after radiotherapy. 3.1.2 Functional A floppy cartilage or a weak tensor: infants, cleft palate (the tensor is poorly anchored) and Down syndrome (poor muscle tone)."],["sec","cl:t-et~s3-2","Clinical picture","Eustachian tube disorders · Obstructive dysfunction","Fullness, popping, mild pain, muffled hearing, tinnitus and sometimes unsteadiness. The drum is retracted, sometimes with amber fluid or a fluid level."],["sec","cl:t-et~s3-3","Consequences","Eustachian tube disorders · Obstructive dysfunction","Negative pressure, effusion ( Otitis media: acute and with effusion ), atelectasis, retraction pockets ( Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae ) and cholesteatoma , and ossicular erosion. Where the block is predicts the damage Tube itself: total atelectasis of the pars tensa. Middle ear: posterior retraction pocket. Tympanic isthmi: attic retraction pocket. Aditus: cholesterol granuloma in the mastoid."],["sec","cl:t-et~s3-4","Investigations","Eustachian tube disorders · Obstructive dysfunction","3.4.1 Valsalva and Toynbee tests Valsalva (forced expiration against a closed nose and mouth) inflates the middle ear, but only about 65% of normal people can do it. Toynbee's test (swallowing with the nose pinched) creates negative pressure. 3.4.2 Politzer test Air from a Politzer bag into the nose while the child swallows or says \"hick\"; useful in children. 3.4.3 Catheterisation A catheter passed along the nasal floor into the tubal opening; air is blown through while listening with an auscultation tube. Done gently, as it can injure the opening. Figure 4 Catheterisation of the Eustachian tube. Dhingra, fig. 9.3 3.4.4 Tympanometry Negative pressure or a flat curve; the inflation-deflation test assesses function through an intact or perforated drum. 3.4.5 Other tests Dye or saccharine clearance, sonotubometry, and endoscopy of the nasopharynx."],["sec","cl:t-et~s3-5","Treatment","Eustachian tube disorders · Obstructive dysfunction","Treat the nose, sinuses, allergy and adenoids; autoinflation; a grommet for persistent effusion or retraction. Balloon dilation of the cartilaginous tube helps selected adults with persistent obstructive dysfunction. In adults Persistent one-sided tube symptoms or effusion in an adult: examine the nasopharynx to exclude carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-et~s4-1","Causes","Eustachian tube disorders · Patulous tube","Idiopathic; rapid weight loss, which removes the fat pad around the tube; the last trimester of pregnancy; oral contraceptives; and neurological disease such as multiple sclerosis or stroke."],["sec","cl:t-et~s4-2","Clinical picture","Eustachian tube disorders · Patulous tube","Autophony : the patient hears their own voice and breathing loudly in the ear, relieved by lying down or putting the head between the knees (venous congestion closes the tube). The drum moves in and out with respiration, best seen while the patient breathes through one nostril."],["sec","cl:t-et~s4-3","Differential diagnosis","Eustachian tube disorders · Patulous tube","Superior canal dehiscence also causes autophony, but with vertigo or a conductive gap and an intact stapedial reflex."],["sec","cl:t-et~s4-4","Treatment","Eustachian tube disorders · Patulous tube","Reassurance; most acute cases settle on their own. Weight gain, nasal drops (saline, or in the past potassium iodide), cautery or injection around the tubal opening, a grommet, or plugging the tube in severe cases. Pearl Don't prescribe decongestants for fullness and autophony after weight loss: they make a patulous tube worse."],["ev","cl:t-et~c5","Eustachian tube disorders: current evidence","Eustachian tube disorders · Barotrauma","Balloon dilation. In a randomised trial of more than 200 adults with persistent obstructive dysfunction, balloon dilation plus medical treatment normalised the tympanogram in 52% at six weeks, against 14% with medical treatment alone, and symptom scores (ETDQ-7) improved more (Poe et al., Laryngoscope 2018); a second trial confirmed benefit at one year (Meyer et al., Otol Neurotol 2018). An American consensus statement supports balloon dilation for carefully selected adults with obstructive dysfunction confirmed by symptoms, examination and tympanometry, after failed medical treatment, and not for patulous tube (Tucci et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019); NICE (IPG665, 2019) considers it safe and effective. Medical treatment. Nasal steroids alone have little proven effect on Eustachian tube dysfunction without rhinitis. Assessment. The seven-item Eustachian Tube Dysfunction Questionnaire (ETDQ-7) is the standard symptom score."],["sec","cl:t-et~c5","Barotrauma","Eustachian tube disorders · Barotrauma","When the tube locks. On descent in an aircraft or when diving, a tube that fails to open can lock shut once the pressure difference reaches about 90 mm Hg, causing pain, effusion, haemorrhage or perforation. Its picture and treatment are covered in Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae ."],["sec","cl:t-et~s6-1","Viva essentials","Eustachian tube disorders · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Anatomy: length, angle, bony and cartilaginous parts, isthmus, tensor veli palatini. Infant versus adult tube. Tests of tubal function: Valsalva, Toynbee, Politzer, catheterisation, tympanometry. Patulous tube: causes, symptoms, signs. Unilateral effusion in an adult: look at the nasopharynx."],["sec","cl:t-et~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Eustachian tube disorders · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing nasopharyngeal carcinoma in an adult with one-sided fluid. Treating a patulous tube with decongestants. Performing Valsalva with an infected nose or an atrophic drum."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s1-1","Classification","External ear tumours and bony growths · Overview","External ear tumours Pinna, benign Cysts, keloid haemangioma, papilloma, cutaneous horn, keratoacanthoma, neurofibroma Pinna, malignant Basal cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma melanoma Canal, benign Osteoma, exostoses ceruminous adenoma, papilloma Canal, malignant Squamous cell carcinoma basal cell carcinoma, adenoid cystic and ceruminous carcinoma, melanoma"],["sec","cl:t-extca~s1-2","Where ear cancers arise","External ear tumours and bony growths · Overview","About 85% on the pinna, 10% in the canal and 5% in the middle ear ( Middle ear and temporal bone tumours )."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s2-1","Keloid","External ear tumours and bony growths · Benign lesions of the pinna","Overgrowth of scar after piercing, more common in darker skin. Excision alone recurs; combine with steroid injection, pressure clips or, rarely, radiotherapy. Figure 1 Keloid after ear piercing. Dhingra, fig. 16.1 Where the books differ · Radiotherapy for keloid Dhingra Postoperative radiation is an option. Lee Radiation is rarely used. In practice Combine excision with steroid injection or pressure clips; radiotherapy is used rarely."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s2-2","Haemangioma and vascular malformations","External ear tumours and bony growths · Benign lesions of the pinna","A strawberry haemangioma grows in the first year and regresses by about five years; a port-wine stain does not. Venolymphatic malformations may need excision. Figure 2 A venolymphatic malformation of the pinna in a child: before, during operation, and the excised specimen. Dhingra, fig. 16.2"],["sec","cl:t-extca~s2-3","Keratoacanthoma","External ear tumours and bony growths · Benign lesions of the pinna","A rapidly growing crater with a keratin plug that may regress on its own; it looks malignant and is usually excised."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s2-4","Others","External ear tumours and bony growths · Benign lesions of the pinna","Sebaceous and dermoid cysts, papillomas, cutaneous horns and neurofibromas."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s3-1","Risk factors","External ear tumours and bony growths · Malignant tumours of the pinna","Sun exposure, fair skin, male sex, older age and immunosuppression."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s3-2","Squamous cell carcinoma","External ear tumours and bony growths · Malignant tumours of the pinna","An ulcer, usually on the helix, with raised, everted edges. It spreads to the preauricular, postauricular, parotid and upper cervical nodes. Figure 3 Squamous cell carcinoma of the pinna. Dhingra, fig. 16.3"],["sec","cl:t-extca~s3-3","Basal cell carcinoma","External ear tumours and bony growths · Malignant tumours of the pinna","A nodule with a pearly, rolled, beaded edge and central crusting, usually behind the ear or on the helix; it rarely spreads to nodes but invades locally. Where the books differ · The commonest ear malignancy Dhingra Emphasises squamous carcinoma on the helix. Lee Basal cell carcinoma is the commonest, at about 45%. In practice Basal cell carcinoma is the commonest; squamous carcinoma is the typical helical ulcer and the one that spreads to nodes."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s3-4","Melanoma","External ear tumours and bony growths · Malignant tumours of the pinna","A pigmented, irregular lesion; spreads early to the parotid and neck nodes."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s3-5","Treatment","External ear tumours and bony growths · Malignant tumours of the pinna","Squamous carcinoma: excision with about 1 cm margins for small lesions; larger lesions, those within 1 cm of the canal, or with nodes need partial or total amputation of the pinna with parotidectomy and neck dissection. Basal cell carcinoma: radiotherapy for superficial lesions not involving cartilage; excision (Mohs surgery where available) for deeper lesions. Melanoma: wedge excision if small on the helix; wider resection with parotid and neck surgery as staged."],["sec","cl:t-extca~c4","Bony growths of the canal","External ear tumours and bony growths · Bony growths of the canal","Often just bone. Osteoma or exostoses? Osteoma Exostoses Number Single Multiple Sides One Usually both Shape Smooth, pedunculated Sessile, broad-based Site Outer bony canal, near the bony-cartilaginous junction, at a suture line Deep bony canal, near the drum, from the anterior and posterior walls Cause True benign tumour Reaction to cold water (swimmers, surfers) Figure 4 An osteoma arising from the anterior wall of the right ear canal (arrow). Dhingra, fig. 16.4"],["sec","cl:t-extca~s4-1","Treatment","External ear tumours and bony growths · Bony growths of the canal","None if small. When they block the canal, trap wax or cause recurrent otitis externa: an osteoma can be removed by fracturing its pedicle or drilling; exostoses are drilled away through the canal or a postauricular approach. Their effect on the canal, and recurrence after cold water, are covered in Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis . Pearl Use a drill, not a gouge and hammer, for exostoses: the facial nerve lies close to the posterior canal wall."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s5-1","Ceruminous gland tumours","External ear tumours and bony growths · Tumours of the canal","A firm, skin-covered mass in the outer canal. Malignant ones (adenoid cystic carcinoma, ceruminous adenocarcinoma) outnumber benign ones about two to one. Treated by wide excision, with radiotherapy for malignant tumours."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s5-2","Squamous cell carcinoma of the canal","External ear tumours and bony growths · Tumours of the canal","Often follows long-standing chronic discharge. It presents with bloody discharge, pain and granulation that will not heal; facial palsy means advanced disease. 5.2.1 Staging (Pittsburgh) T1: confined to the canal, without bone erosion. T2: limited bone erosion, or soft tissue extension under 0.5 cm. T3: full-thickness bone erosion with soft tissue under 0.5 cm, or middle ear or mastoid involvement. T4: cochlea, petrous apex, medial wall, carotid canal, jugular foramen, dura, soft tissue over 0.5 cm, or facial palsy. 5.2.2 Treatment Sleeve or lateral temporal bone resection, with parotidectomy and neck dissection as needed, and postoperative radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-extca~c6","Assessment","External ear tumours and bony growths · Assessment","Biopsy and image. Biopsy any non-healing lesion of the pinna or canal. CT and MRI for deep or canal tumours. Examine the anterior and posterior auricular, parotid and upper cervical nodes."],["ev","cl:t-extca~s6-1","External ear tumours and bony growths: current evidence","External ear tumours and bony growths · Assessment","Skin cancer of the ear. The ear is a high-risk site for both basal and squamous cell carcinoma; NCCN and British Association of Dermatologists guidelines recommend wider margins (or Mohs micrographic surgery) for high-risk tumours, with SCC of the ear carrying a higher risk of nodal spread to the parotid and neck than other sites. Melanoma of the ear is managed by wide local excision with sentinel node biopsy for tumours over 0.8 mm thick, following general melanoma guidelines. Chondrodermatitis nodularis responds in most cases to pressure relief (a pillow with a hole), with excision of cartilage for persistent lesions."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s6-1","Differential diagnosis","External ear tumours and bony growths · Assessment","Chondrodermatitis nodularis helicis, perichondritis , necrotising otitis externa and granulation from chronic otitis ( Chronic suppurative otitis media without cholesteatoma )."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s7-1","Viva essentials","External ear tumours and bony growths · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Squamous versus basal cell carcinoma of the pinna, and their treatment. Osteoma versus exostoses. Pittsburgh staging of canal carcinoma. Biopsy any non-healing lesion."],["sec","cl:t-extca~s7-2","Common pitfalls","External ear tumours and bony growths · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a non-healing helical ulcer with creams for months. Using a gouge on exostoses. Missing canal carcinoma in a patient with chronic discharge and new pain."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s1-1","Applied anatomy","External nose and nasal vestibule · Overview","The upper third of the nose is bony (paired nasal bones and the frontal processes of the maxillae); the lower two-thirds is cartilaginous (upper lateral cartilages, lower lateral or alar cartilages, and the septal cartilage). The skin is thin and mobile over the dorsum and thick and sebaceous over the tip and alae. The vestibule is the skin-lined entrance, with hairs (vibrissae), up to the limen nasi. Figure 1 First, the parts of the nose and related facial structures. Second, the osteocartilaginous framework. Dhingra, figs 23.1 and 23.2 Figure 2 The osteocartilaginous framework of the nose, and the change in skin thickness from nasion to tip. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s1-2","The danger area","External nose and nasal vestibule · Overview","Veins of the upper lip and nose drain through the facial and angular veins and the ophthalmic veins to the cavernous sinus, without valves. Infection here can spread to the cavernous sinus. Figure 3 The danger area of the face and its venous drainage to the cavernous sinus. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s2-1","Furuncle of the vestibule","External nose and nasal vestibule · Infections","2.1.1 Cause Acute staphylococcal infection of a hair follicle, usually after nose picking or plucking hairs. 2.1.2 Clinical picture A small, exquisitely tender red swelling in the vestibule, with redness and swelling spreading to the tip and dorsum; it may point and rupture. Figure 4 Furuncle of the right nasal vestibule. Dhingra, fig. 25.9 2.1.3 Treatment Warm compresses, analgesia, and topical and systemic antistaphylococcal antibiotics; incise only when fluctuant. Never squeeze a nasal furuncle Squeezing or early incision can spread infection through the angular and ophthalmic veins to the cavernous sinus. Other complications: cellulitis of the upper lip and septal abscess. Cavernous sinus thrombosis is described in Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications . Recurrent furuncles mean checking for diabetes and nasal staphylococcal carriage."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s2-2","Vestibulitis","External nose and nasal vestibule · Infections","2.2.1 Cause Diffuse dermatitis of the vestibular skin, usually Staphylococcus aureus , encouraged by nasal discharge from rhinitis or sinusitis and the trauma of wiping. 2.2.2 Clinical picture Acute: red, swollen, tender skin with crusts over erosions, sometimes involving the upper lip. Chronic: thickened skin with painful fissures and crusts. Figure 5 Acute vestibulitis on the left side. Dhingra, fig. 25.10 2.2.3 Treatment Clean the crusts with hydrogen peroxide, apply an antibiotic-steroid ointment and continue it for several days after apparent cure; cauterise a chronic fissure with silver nitrate; treat the source of discharge."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s2-3","Cellulitis","External nose and nasal vestibule · Infections","Streptococcal or staphylococcal infection making the nose red, swollen and tender, sometimes spreading from the vestibule; systemic antibiotics, warmth and analgesia."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s2-4","Specific infections","External nose and nasal vestibule · Infections","Lupus vulgaris (apple-jelly nodules), leprosy and syphilis also affect the external nose ( Granulomatous disease of the nose )."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~c3","Deformities","External nose and nasal vestibule · Deformities","Shape and function. Figure 6 Deformities of the nose. Dhingra, fig. 25.1"],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s3-1","Saddle nose","External nose and nasal vestibule · Deformities","A depressed dorsum, bony, cartilaginous or both. 3.1.1 Causes Depressed fractures (the commonest; Nasal and facial fractures ), excessive removal of septal cartilage at submucous resection, septal haematoma or abscess ( Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess ), granulomatosis with polyangiitis, cocaine, leprosy, tuberculosis, syphilis and relapsing polychondritis. 3.1.2 Treatment Augmentation rhinoplasty : septal, auricular or rib cartilage for a cartilaginous depression; bone (iliac crest or calvarial) for bony and cartilaginous loss. Autografts are preferred; synthetic implants tend to extrude."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s3-2","Hump nose","External nose and nasal vestibule · Deformities","A dorsal hump of bone, cartilage or both, corrected by reduction rhinoplasty : removal of the hump and lateral osteotomies to close the open roof."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s3-3","Crooked and deviated nose","External nose and nasal vestibule · Deformities","In a crooked nose, the dorsum from the root to the tip is C- or S-shaped; in a deviated nose, the dorsum is straight but lies to one side. Both usually follow injury, often in childhood. Correction by septorhinoplasty restores both appearance and airway ( Facial plastic surgery ). Figure 7 The nasal bridge is S-shaped in a crooked nose, and straight but displaced in a deviated nose. Dhingra, fig. 25.2"],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s3-4","Nasal valve collapse","External nose and nasal vestibule · Deformities","Weak or scarred lateral walls collapse on inspiration; Cottle's test is positive. Spreader grafts, alar batten grafts or functional rhinoplasty."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s4-1","Benign","External nose and nasal vestibule · Tumours of the external nose","Papilloma (wart), haemangioma, pigmented naevus, seborrhoeic keratosis, neurofibroma and sweat gland tumours. Congenital masses (dermoid, glioma, encephalocele) are covered in Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses ."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s4-2","Rhinophyma","External nose and nasal vestibule · Tumours of the external nose","The potato nose : a slow, lobulated, pink overgrowth of sebaceous glands and connective tissue over the tip and alae, with dilated vessels, in men past middle age with long-standing acne rosacea. It is cosmetic, but may block breathing or vision. Treatment is shaving it down with a scalpel, dermabrasion or carbon dioxide laser, letting the surface re-epithelialise from the remaining glands. Figure 8 Rhinophyma. Dhingra, fig. 25.4"],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s4-3","Basal cell carcinoma","External nose and nasal vestibule · Tumours of the external nose","The commonest malignant tumour of the nasal skin (about 87%), at 40 to 60 years, on the tip and ala: a pearly nodule or an ulcer with rolled edges ( rodent ulcer ). Slow, locally destructive, almost never metastasises. Excision with 3 to 5 mm margins, Mohs surgery for high-risk sites and recurrences, or radiotherapy or cryotherapy for early lesions; reconstruction with local flaps. Figure 9 Basal cell carcinoma of the nose. Dhingra, fig. 25.6"],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s4-4","Squamous cell carcinoma","External nose and nasal vestibule · Tumours of the external nose","The second commonest (about 11%), an infiltrating nodule or ulcer with everted edges on the side of the nose or columella; nodal metastases in about 20%. Radiotherapy for early lesions; wide excision with reconstruction, and neck dissection for nodes. Figure 10 Squamous cell carcinoma of the nose, and of the nasal tip. Dhingra, fig. 25.7"],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s4-5","Melanoma","External nose and nasal vestibule · Tumours of the external nose","The least common: superficial spreading or nodular; wide excision."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s4-6","Total rhinectomy","External nose and nasal vestibule · Tumours of the external nose","For advanced cancers, followed by a prosthesis or a forehead flap reconstruction."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s5-1","Stenosis and atresia of the nares","External nose and nasal vestibule · The vestibule","Trauma, burns, surgery, destructive inflammation (formerly smallpox) and Young's operation can narrow the nostrils; congenital atresia from failure of canalisation is rare. Corrected by excision of scar with flaps or grafts and stenting. Figure 11 Stenosis of the left naris after smallpox (A) and after trauma (B). Dhingra, fig. 25.11"],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s5-2","Nasoalveolar (nasolabial) cyst","External nose and nasal vestibule · The vestibule","A smooth swelling in the floor and lateral wall of the vestibule, raising the ala and filling the nasolabial fold; excised through a sublabial incision. Figure 12 A nasoalveolar cyst at operation (arrow). Dhingra, fig. 25.12"],["ev","cl:t-extnose~s5-3","External nose and nasal vestibule: current evidence","External nose and nasal vestibule · The vestibule","Vestibulitis and furunculosis. Staphylococcus aureus is the usual cause; topical mupirocin or fusidic acid for mild disease and an oral anti-staphylococcal antibiotic for spreading infection. Recurrent cases suggest nasal carriage, which is decolonised with mupirocin. Rhinophyma is treated by surgical shaving, CO₂ laser or electrosurgery, with good long-term results in case series; doxycycline and isotretinoin treat the underlying rosacea. Skin cancer of the nose is the commonest site of basal cell carcinoma on the face; Mohs surgery gives the lowest recurrence for high-risk and recurrent lesions, with reconstruction by local flaps (bilobed, nasolabial, paramedian forehead)."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s5-3","Vestibular papilloma and carcinoma","External nose and nasal vestibule · The vestibule","Warts are excised. Squamous carcinoma of the vestibule may spread to the floor, columella and upper lip, and to the parotid and submandibular nodes."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s6-1","Viva essentials","External nose and nasal vestibule · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Furuncle: the danger area, and why not to squeeze. Vestibulitis: cause and treatment. Causes of saddle nose, and its correction. Crooked versus deviated nose. Rhinophyma. Basal versus squamous cell carcinoma of the nose."],["sec","cl:t-extnose~s6-2","Common pitfalls","External nose and nasal vestibule · For the examination","Common pitfalls Squeezing or incising an early furuncle. Treating a non-healing ulcer with creams without biopsy. Using synthetic implants that later extrude."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s1-1","Regions","Nasal and facial fractures · Overview","Upper third: frontal bone, frontal sinus and supraorbital ridges. Middle third: nasal bones and septum, naso-orbito-ethmoid complex, zygoma, orbital floor and maxilla. Lower third: mandible."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s1-2","General management","Nasal and facial fractures · Overview","Facial injury in the emergency department Airway (with cervical spine control), breathing and circulation; a midface fracture can drop back and block the airway. Control bleeding: packs, and reduction of a mobile maxilla. Examine the eyes now: acuity, pupils, movements, proptosis; retrobulbar haemorrhage needs a lateral canthotomy within minutes. Look for CSF rhinorrhoea and otorrhoea, and other injuries. Examine the septum for a haematoma in every nasal injury. CT of the face (with 3D reconstruction) for anything beyond a simple nasal fracture."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s1-3","Soft tissue injuries","Nasal and facial fractures · Overview","Clean and close lacerations early, preserving tissue; look for injury to the parotid duct and the facial nerve in cheek wounds and repair them primarily."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s2-1","Mechanism and types","Nasal and facial fractures · Nasal fractures","The projecting nose is the most frequently fractured bone of the face. Frontal blow: depressed fracture of the thinner lower nasal bones; with more force, an open-book fracture (septum collapsed, nasal bones splayed) and comminution with a flat, wide dorsum. Lateral blow: depression of one nasal bone, or fracture of both with the septum, deviating the bridge. The septum is often buckled, dislocated or fractured, and a septal haematoma may form. Figure 1 Types of nasal fracture: (A) normal, (B) a frontal blow with depressed or open-book fracture, (C) a lateral blow deviating the bridge or depressing one nasal bone. Dhingra, fig. 34.1"],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s2-2","Clinical features","Nasal and facial fractures · Nasal fractures","Swelling within hours, periorbital bruising, tenderness, deformity, crepitus and mobility, epistaxis, obstruction from septal injury or haematoma, and lacerations exposing bone in compound fractures. Figure 2 A nasal fracture with the bridge displaced to the right (A), and after manual correction (B). Dhingra, fig. 34.2"],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s2-3","Diagnosis","Nasal and facial fractures · Nasal fractures","Clinical. Radiographs are not needed for an isolated nasal fracture and may miss it; CT is used when other facial fractures are suspected. Figure 3 A fractured nasal bone (arrow) on a radiograph. Dhingra, fig. 34.3"],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s2-4","Treatment","Nasal and facial fractures · Nasal fractures","2.4.1 Timing Reduce before swelling develops, or after it settles at about 5 to 7 days. After about two weeks the bones have set and reduction becomes difficult; healing is faster in children, so reduce them sooner. 2.4.2 Closed reduction Under local or general anaesthesia: a blunt elevator lifts depressed bones, guided by fingers outside; digital pressure realigns a deviated bridge; Walsham's forceps disimpact the nasal bones and Asch's forceps straighten the septum. Drain any septal haematoma. External splint, and packing for unstable fractures. 2.4.3 Open reduction When closed reduction fails, and for severe septal fractures; late deformity is corrected by septorhinoplasty after six months or more ( Facial plastic surgery ). A third or more of patients need later septorhinoplasty when the septum is also displaced. Pearl Always look inside the nose. A septal haematoma missed today becomes a septal abscess and a saddle nose ( Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess )."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s3-1","Mechanism","Nasal and facial fractures · Naso-orbito-ethmoid fractures","A blow over the nasion drives the nasal bones, perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, ethmoid cells and medial orbital walls backwards. The cribriform plate, frontal sinus outflow, lacrimal system, medial canthal tendon and eye may all be injured."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s3-2","Clinical features","Nasal and facial fractures · Naso-orbito-ethmoid fractures","Telecanthus (widened distance between the medial canthi), a flattened bridge with an upturned pug tip, periorbital bruising, orbital haematoma from the ethmoidal arteries, CSF rhinorrhoea, epiphora and displacement of the eye."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s3-3","Treatment","Nasal and facial fractures · Naso-orbito-ethmoid fractures","CT to map the injury; open reduction and fixation with miniplates through existing lacerations, a coronal or local incision, with repair of the medial canthal tendon and the lacrimal system as a priority over the nasal contour. Treat CSF leaks ( CSF rhinorrhoea )."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s4-1","Zygomatic (tripod) fracture","Nasal and facial fractures · Zygoma and orbital floor","4.1.1 Mechanism The second commonest facial fracture. The zygoma separates at its frontal, temporal and maxillary attachments, usually rotated inwards and backwards. Figure 4 A fractured left zygoma. Dhingra, fig. 34.4 4.1.2 Clinical features Flattening of the cheek. Step at the infraorbital margin. Numbness of the cheek, side of the nose and upper lip (infraorbital nerve). Trismus, as the depressed zygoma presses on the coronoid process. A slanted palpebral fissure from displacement of the lateral canthus. Diplopia from entrapment of the inferior rectus. Surgical emphysema of the lid after nose blowing. 4.1.3 Diagnosis CT in axial and coronal planes, or 3D reconstruction; Waters' view in the past. 4.1.4 Treatment Only displaced fractures need treatment: elevation through a temporal (Gillies) or intraoral approach, and open reduction with plate fixation at the frontozygomatic suture, infraorbital rim and zygomaticomaxillary buttress, repairing the orbital floor if needed."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s4-2","Zygomatic arch fracture","Nasal and facial fractures · Zygoma and orbital floor","The arch breaks in three places, depressing two fragments: a dimple over the arch, pain on chewing and trismus as fragments touch the coronoid. Elevated through a temporal ( Gillies ) incision with an elevator passed deep to the temporalis fascia; usually stable without fixation."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s4-3","Orbital floor blow-out fracture","Nasal and facial fractures · Zygoma and orbital floor","4.3.1 Mechanism A blunt object larger than the orbit (a fist or ball) raises orbital pressure, and the thin floor gives way; orbital fat and the inferior rectus herniate into the maxillary sinus while the rim stays intact. Figure 5 First, a blow-out fracture with orbital contents herniating into the maxillary sinus. Second, CT of a blow-out fracture of the right orbital floor. Dhingra, figs 34.5 and 34.6 4.3.2 Clinical features Bruising of the lid, conjunctiva and sclera. Diplopia , especially on looking up, from entrapment or swelling of the inferior rectus and oblique. Enophthalmos and downward displacement of the eye, apparent once the swelling settles. Numbness of the cheek and upper lip. 4.3.3 Diagnosis CT in coronal planes. A forced duction (traction) test distinguishes mechanical entrapment from nerve or muscle injury. Ophthalmology review for globe injury. In children, a white-eyed blow-out with a trapped muscle and vagal symptoms (vomiting, nausea, bradycardia), a trapdoor fracture, needs release within 24 to 48 hours. 4.3.4 Treatment Surgery, within about two weeks in adults, for persistent diplopia with entrapment, enophthalmos over about 2 mm, or a large floor defect (over half the floor): the floor is explored through a lower lid or transconjunctival incision (or endoscopically through the maxillary sinus), herniated tissue is freed, and the floor is reconstructed with titanium mesh, bone or a synthetic plate. Figure 6 The bony orbit, its walls, fissures and foramina. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s5-1","Classification","Nasal and facial fractures · Maxillary fractures","Le Fort fractures Type Line Moves on testing Le Fort I (transverse) Above and parallel to the palate, across the lower septum, maxillary sinuses and pterygoid plates Tooth-bearing segment only Le Fort II (pyramidal) Root of nose, lacrimal bone, orbital floor, upper maxilla, pterygoid plates The nose and maxilla together Le Fort III (craniofacial dysjunction) Root of nose, ethmofrontal junction, orbit, frontozygomatic and zygomatic arch sutures, pterygoid plates The whole midface Figure 7 Fractures of the maxilla: (A) Le Fort I, (B) Le Fort II, (C) Le Fort III. Dhingra, fig. 34.7"],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s5-2","Clinical features","Nasal and facial fractures · Maxillary fractures","Malocclusion with an anterior open bite, elongation and flattening of the midface ( dish face ), mobility of the maxilla, CSF rhinorrhoea in Le Fort II and III (the cribriform plate is involved), bilateral periorbital bruising, numbness, and airway compromise as the maxilla slides back."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s5-3","Treatment","Nasal and facial fractures · Maxillary fractures","Secure the airway (tracheostomy may be needed); restore occlusion with maxillomandibular fixation; open reduction and internal fixation with plates along the facial buttresses."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s6-1","Mandibular fractures","Nasal and facial fractures · Mandible and frontal sinus","The condyle is the commonest site, then the angle, body and symphysis, often fractured in two places. Malocclusion, pain, trismus, step deformity, numbness of the lower lip (inferior alveolar nerve) and sublingual haematoma. Orthopantomogram and CT; treated by open reduction and plating or maxillomandibular fixation. Figure 8 Fractures of the mandible by site (Dingman classification); condylar fractures are the commonest. Dhingra, fig. 34.8"],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s6-2","Frontal sinus fractures","Nasal and facial fractures · Mandible and frontal sinus","Anterior table: cosmetic depression; reduce and plate if displaced. Frontal sinus outflow injury: risk of mucocele; obliteration or endoscopic drainage. Posterior table: dura and brain at risk, with CSF leak; cranialisation of the sinus with neurosurgery for displaced fractures. Supraorbital ridge and frontal bone fractures are managed with the neurosurgeons."],["ev","cl:t-facefx~c7","Nasal and facial fractures: current evidence","Nasal and facial fractures · Complications","Nasal fractures. Closed reduction is best done within about two weeks, after swelling settles and before the bones set; local and general anaesthesia give similar results in trials. Mandible. Miniplate fixation (Champy lines) allows early function; antibiotics beyond 24 hours after surgery do not reduce infection in randomised studies. Imaging. Thin-slice CT with three-dimensional reconstruction is the standard for all fractures except isolated nasal ones."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~c7","Complications","Nasal and facial fractures · Complications","Early and late. Airway obstruction and haemorrhage. Retrobulbar haemorrhage and blindness. CSF leak, meningitis and loss of smell. Diplopia, enophthalmos and epiphora. Malocclusion and trismus. Facial asymmetry, saddle or crooked nose, and nasal obstruction."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s8-1","Viva essentials","Nasal and facial fractures · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Nasal fracture: timing of reduction, Walsham's and Asch's forceps, septal haematoma. Naso-orbito-ethmoid fracture: telecanthus. Tripod fracture: features and treatment. Blow-out fracture: mechanism, diplopia on upgaze, enophthalmos, forced duction test, indications for repair. Le Fort I, II and III."],["sec","cl:t-facefx~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Nasal and facial fractures · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing a septal haematoma. Waiting more than two weeks to reduce a displaced nasal fracture. Missing a trapdoor fracture in a child with vomiting and a quiet eye. Forgetting the cervical spine and the airway in a midface fracture."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s1-1","Definition","Facial nerve palsy · Overview","Facial nerve palsy is weakness of the muscles of facial expression from a lesion anywhere between the motor cortex and the muscles. The approach depends on where the lesion is, how complete it is, and whether the nerve is likely to recover."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s1-2","Why it matters","Facial nerve palsy · Overview","The eye is at risk within hours, the face carries a person's expression and identity, and a palsy may be the first sign of a tumour, a cholesteatoma or a fracture."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s2-1","Central connections","Facial nerve palsy · Applied anatomy","The part of the facial nucleus that supplies the forehead receives fibres from both cerebral hemispheres; the part that supplies the lower face is controlled only from the opposite side. An upper motor neurone ( supranuclear ) lesion therefore spares the forehead. Emotional movement travels by separate pathways, so a smile may survive a stroke that abolishes voluntary movement. Figure 1 The forehead receives bilateral cortical innervation and is spared in supranuclear paralysis; emotional movements travel by separate pathways. Dhingra, fig. 14.1"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s2-2","The course of the nerve","Facial nerve palsy · Applied anatomy","2.2.1 Intracranial From the pons across the cerebellopontine angle with the eighth nerve. 2.2.2 Intratemporal Meatal segment: in the internal acoustic meatus, anterosuperior. Labyrinthine segment: about 4 mm long, the narrowest part of the canal, ending at the geniculate ganglion. Swelling here is thought to cause Bell's palsy. Tympanic segment: along the medial wall of the middle ear above the oval window; the commonest site of bony dehiscence. Mastoid segment: vertically down to the stylomastoid foramen. 2.2.3 Extratemporal Through the parotid gland, dividing into temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular and cervical branches. Figure 2 Course of the facial nerve: meatal, labyrinthine, tympanic and mastoid segments, and its branches. Dhingra, fig. 14.2"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s2-3","Branches inside the temporal bone","Facial nerve palsy · Applied anatomy","Greater superficial petrosal nerve: lacrimation. Nerve to stapedius: the stapedial reflex. Chorda tympani: taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and submandibular and sublingual secretion. These allow a lesion to be localised (see 4.3 ). Figure 3 The course of the facial nerve through the temporal bone, and the petrosal nerves and tympanic plexus of the middle ear. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s2-4","Surgical landmarks","Facial nerve palsy · Applied anatomy","Figure 4 First, landmarks for finding the nerve in parotid surgery. Second, variations in its course, including bony dehiscence. Dhingra, figs 14.4 and 14.5"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s2-5","Nerve injury","Facial nerve palsy · Applied anatomy","Sunderland classification Degree What is damaged Outcome 1 Conduction block (neurapraxia) Full recovery 2 Axons, tubes intact (axonotmesis) Full recovery, slower 3 Endoneurium Incomplete, synkinesis likely 4 Perineurium Poor without repair 5 Whole nerve (neurotmesis) No recovery without repair"],["sec","cl:t-facial~c3","Causes","Facial nerve palsy · Causes","Think beyond Bell's palsy. Causes of facial palsy Central Stroke, tumour forehead spared Infection Bell's palsy, Ramsay Hunt otitis media, cholesteatoma, necrotising otitis externa, Lyme disease Trauma Temporal bone fracture surgery, birth (forceps), facial wounds Tumour Parotid cancer facial schwannoma, haemangioma, glomus, vestibular schwannoma, temporal bone cancer Systemic Diabetes sarcoid (Heerfordt), Guillain-Barré, leukaemia, granulomatosis Congenital Möbius syndrome birth injury"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s3-1","Bell's palsy","Facial nerve palsy · Causes","3.1.1 What it is An acute idiopathic lower motor neurone palsy, 60 to 75% of all facial palsies. Reactivation of herpes simplex virus is the likely cause; the nerve swells inside the tight labyrinthine segment. It is commoner in diabetes and pregnancy. 3.1.2 Course Weakness comes on over hours and peaks within 72 hours, often after a day of pain behind the ear. About 85 to 90% recover fully, and 95% if the palsy never becomes complete. Figure 5 First, the features of a left-sided facial palsy. Second, Bell's palsy on the left in (A) an adult and (B) a child. Dhingra, fig. 14.7"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s3-2","Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Facial nerve palsy · Causes","Herpes zoster oticus: vesicles in the concha, canal or palate, severe ear pain, facial palsy, and often sensorineural hearing loss and vertigo. The outlook for the face is worse than in Bell's palsy. Zoster of the ear is also covered in Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections . Figure 6 Ramsay Hunt syndrome: facial palsy with vesicles in the concha. Dhingra, fig. 14.9"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s3-3","Otitis media and cholesteatoma","Facial nerve palsy · Causes","Acute otitis media can inflame a dehiscent nerve; cholesteatoma ( Cholesteatoma ) erodes the canal, usually in the tympanic segment. Both need urgent otological care."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s3-4","Trauma","Facial nerve palsy · Causes","Temporal bone fractures and the facial nerve Longitudinal Transverse Frequency About 80% About 20% Blow Side of the head Back of the head Bleeding from the ear Common Usually absent Hearing Conductive Sensorineural Vertigo Less often Severe Facial palsy 10 to 20%, often delayed About 50%, often immediate The fractures themselves are covered in Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea . Figure 7 Longitudinal and transverse fractures of the temporal bone. Dhingra, fig. 14.10"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s3-5","Tumours","Facial nerve palsy · Causes","A slowly progressive palsy, a recurrent palsy on the same side, or palsy with twitching suggests a tumour. A facial palsy with a parotid lump is almost always malignant."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s3-6","Bilateral or recurrent palsy","Facial nerve palsy · Causes","Bilateral: Lyme disease, Guillain-Barré, sarcoidosis, leukaemia, Möbius syndrome, HIV, neurofibromatosis 2 and bilateral fractures. Recurrent on the same side: a tumour in about a third; Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome combines recurrent palsy, lip swelling and a fissured tongue."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s4-1","History","Facial nerve palsy · Assessment","Speed of onset, pain, vesicles, hearing loss and vertigo, ear discharge, trauma or surgery, previous episodes, and systemic illness."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s4-2","Examination","Facial nerve palsy · Assessment","4.2.1 The face Ask the patient to raise the eyebrows, close the eyes tightly, smile and puff the cheeks. On attempted eye closure the eye rolls up and out ( Bell's phenomenon ). Note drooling and tearing. 4.2.2 Grading House-Brackmann grading Grade Description I Normal II Slight weakness; complete eye closure with minimal effort III Obvious weakness, complete eye closure with effort, synkinesis may appear IV Obvious disfiguring weakness, incomplete eye closure V Barely perceptible movement VI No movement 4.2.3 Everything else Otoscopy, the parotid and neck, the other cranial nerves, and hearing. A sixth nerve palsy with it points to the brainstem; fifth, eighth or lower nerve involvement points to the cerebellopontine angle; a motor-only palsy points to the parotid."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s4-3","Topodiagnosis","Facial nerve palsy · Assessment","Localising the lesion Lost function Lesion lies Lacrimation (Schirmer test) At or above the geniculate ganglion Stapedial reflex Above the nerve to stapedius Taste and submandibular flow Above the chorda tympani Motor only Below the chorda, in the mastoid or parotid Figure 8 Topographical localisation of facial nerve lesions. Dhingra, fig. 14.12"],["sec","cl:t-facial~s4-4","Electrical tests","Facial nerve palsy · Assessment","4.4.1 Electroneuronography (ENoG) Compares the compound muscle response on the two sides. Degeneration of 90% or more within the first three weeks predicts a poor recovery. 4.4.2 Electromyography (EMG) Fibrillation potentials at 14 to 21 days mean denervation; polyphasic potentials appear 6 to 12 weeks before visible recovery; electrical silence means the motor end plates are lost. Where the books differ · The ENoG window Dhingra From day 4 to day 21. Lee Days 3 to 12 when deciding on decompression. In practice ENoG is meaningful between about day 3 and day 14 to 21 of a complete palsy; decompression, if offered at all, is within 14 days of onset for over 90% degeneration."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s4-5","Imaging","Facial nerve palsy · Assessment","Contrast MRI of the whole course, and CT of the temporal bone for fractures and cholesteatoma. Indicated for an atypical, slow, recurrent or bilateral palsy, or no recovery by three to four months."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-1","Eye protection","Facial nerve palsy · Management","Lubricating drops by day, ointment and taping at night, sunglasses or a moisture chamber. If closure stays poor, a gold or platinum weight in the upper lid, a lateral tarsal strip or tarsorrhaphy."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-2","Bell's palsy","Facial nerve palsy · Management","Oral steroids started within 72 hours of onset, for example prednisolone 50 mg daily for ten days, or 60 mg daily for five days followed by a five-day taper. With steroids, about 83% recover fully by three months, against 64% without. An antiviral alone does not help; added to steroids it may give a small benefit, so it is an option, not a rule, mainly in severe or complete palsy. Steroids are recommended in patients aged 16 and over; children recover well spontaneously, and the evidence in them is weaker. Where the books differ · Steroids in Bell's palsy Dhingra Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day, extended if the palsy stays complete; notes that benefit is not proved beyond doubt. Lee Recommends steroids within 48 to 72 hours. In practice Early steroids are standard; two large trials have settled the question."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-3","What not to do in Bell's palsy","Facial nerve palsy · Management","The American guideline advises against routine laboratory tests and imaging at presentation, and against electrodiagnostic testing in incomplete palsy. Image, or refer, if the palsy is atypical: slow onset, no recovery at all by three to four months, recurrence on the same side, other cranial nerve signs, or a parotid mass. Be cautious with steroids in diabetes, hypertension, peptic ulcer, tuberculosis, glaucoma and pregnancy."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-4","Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Facial nerve palsy · Management","High-dose steroids with an antiviral such as aciclovir or valaciclovir, and analgesia."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-5","Otitis media and cholesteatoma","Facial nerve palsy · Management","Acute otitis media: antibiotics with myringotomy. Chronic disease: urgent exploration, clearing disease from the nerve; granulation that has invaded the nerve is left alone."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-6","Trauma","Facial nerve palsy · Management","An immediate, complete palsy with a fracture through the canal is explored and decompressed or repaired. A delayed palsy is managed conservatively, with serial ENoG. Where the books differ · Delayed traumatic palsy Dhingra Conservative treatment. Lee Serial ENoG; decompress if the response falls below 10% of normal. In practice Delayed or incomplete palsy is observed with steroids, because recovery is usually good; exploration is kept for immediate complete palsy with over 90% degeneration on ENoG."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-7","Palsy after ear surgery","Facial nerve palsy · Management","Remove the packing and any tight dressing first. If the nerve was not seen and the palsy is complete, explore unless continuity is certain."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-8","Decompression","Facial nerve palsy · Management","Opening the bony canal, and sometimes the sheath, at the site of compression. For Bell's palsy it is controversial and, if done, must include the labyrinthine segment within two weeks."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s5-9","Repair and reanimation","Facial nerve palsy · Management","5.9.1 Nerve repair Tension-free primary anastomosis, or a cable graft from the great auricular (not when there are neck metastases), sural or medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve. 5.9.2 Nerve transfer When the distal nerve and muscle survive but the proximal stump is unavailable, ideally within 18 months: the masseteric nerve is most used; hypoglossal transfer costs some tongue function; a cross-face graft can give a spontaneous smile. 5.9.3 Muscle transfer For long-standing palsy with atrophied muscle: temporalis transfer, or a free gracilis transfer powered by the masseteric nerve (one stage, about 92% success, not spontaneous) or by a cross-face graft (two stages, about 80%, spontaneous). 5.9.4 Static procedures Brow lift, fascia lata sling and lid procedures."],["sec","cl:t-facial~c6","Complications","Facial nerve palsy · Complications","When recovery is incomplete. Exposure keratitis. Synkinesis: the eye closes when the patient smiles, from aberrant regeneration; treated with physiotherapy and botulinum toxin. Contracture and hemifacial spasm. Crocodile tears (Bogorad syndrome): tearing while eating, as salivary fibres regrow into the lacrimal pathway. Psychological distress. Figure 9 Hemifacial spasm: all the facial muscles and platysma contract together. Dhingra, fig. 14.13"],["ev","cl:t-facial~s6-1","Facial nerve palsy: current evidence","Facial nerve palsy · Complications","Steroids work, antivirals add little. In the Scottish trial of 551 patients treated within 72 hours, prednisolone raised complete recovery at three months from 64% to 83%, and aciclovir gave no benefit (Sullivan et al., NEJM 2007). A Swedish and Finnish trial of more than 800 patients confirmed the benefit of prednisolone and found none from valaciclovir (Engström et al., Lancet Neurol 2008). A Cochrane review found that steroids reduce incomplete recovery by about a third, with moderate-certainty evidence (Madhok et al., 2016); another found that adding an antiviral to steroids may slightly reduce incomplete recovery, with low-certainty evidence, and that antivirals alone are no better than placebo (Gagyor et al., 2019). The American guideline recommends steroids within 72 hours for patients aged 16 and over, allows an antiviral only alongside steroids, and advises against routine tests, imaging and electrodiagnosis in incomplete palsy, and against surgical decompression outside research or selected cases (Baugh et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2013)."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s6-1","Hemifacial spasm","Facial nerve palsy · Complications","Usually from a vascular loop compressing the nerve at the brainstem. Treated with botulinum toxin, or microvascular decompression."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s7-1","On call","Facial nerve palsy · For the examination","Acute facial weakness Forehead spared with emotional movement kept: central; follow the stroke pathway if acute. Protect the eye now: drops by day, ointment and tape at night. Look in the ear: vesicles mean Ramsay Hunt, so add an antiviral; a foul ear or cholesteatoma needs urgent otology review. Examine the parotid, neck and other cranial nerves. After trauma, record whether the palsy was immediate or delayed. Start steroids within 72 hours for Bell's palsy."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s7-2","Viva essentials","Facial nerve palsy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Upper versus lower motor neurone palsy, and why the forehead is spared. Segments of the nerve, and the narrow labyrinthine segment. Topodiagnosis: Schirmer, stapedial reflex, taste. House-Brackmann grading. ENoG and EMG: timing and meaning. Longitudinal versus transverse fractures. Bell's palsy is a diagnosis of exclusion."],["sec","cl:t-facial~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Facial nerve palsy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling it Bell's palsy without looking in the ear or feeling the parotid. Forgetting eye care. Missing a slowly progressive or recurrent palsy that needs imaging. Not recording the timing of a post-traumatic palsy."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s1-1","Landmarks","Facial plastic surgery · Facial analysis","Analysis uses a small vocabulary of points. In the midline from above down lie the trichion (the hairline), the glabella (the most prominent point of the forehead) and the nasion , the deepest point of the nasofrontal angle at the nasofrontal suture. On the dorsum, the rhinion marks the junction of bone and cartilage, where the skin is thinnest, and the light reflexes of the domes are the tip-defining points . The subnasale is where the columella meets the lip and the stomion where the lips meet. On the chin, the pogonion is the most anterior point and the menton the lowest, and the cervical point is the deepest point between the submental region and the neck. Figure 1 Surface landmarks and the osteocartilaginous framework of the external nose. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s1-2","Proportions","Facial plastic surgery · Facial analysis","In height the face divides into thirds : trichion to glabella, glabella to subnasale, and subnasale to menton are about equal, and within the lower third the upper lip takes a third and the lower lip and chin two-thirds. In width it divides into fifths , each about one eye's width; the intercanthal distance equals an eye width and also the width of the nasal base. Width to height is about 3:4. Facial angles Angle Defined by Ideal Nasofrontal Glabella–nasion and nasion–tip 115 to 130° Nasofacial Facial plane (glabella–pogonion) and the dorsum 30 to 40° Nasolabial Columella and upper lip at subnasale 90 to 95° in men, 95 to 110° (up to 120°) in women Nasomental Dorsum and tip–pogonion 120 to 132° Mentocervical Facial plane and the submental line 80 to 95°"],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s1-3","The chin and occlusion","Facial plastic surgery · Facial analysis","In profile the chin should sit on a vertical line dropped from the lower lip, or just behind it in women. A weak chin is either microgenia , a small chin with normal occlusion that is corrected with an implant or a sliding genioplasty , or retrognathia , a retruded mandible with a class II bite that needs orthognathic surgery such as a sagittal split osteotomy. The distinction is made by looking at the bite. Angle's classification describes occlusion by the upper first molar. In class I , the normal state, its mesiobuccal cusp sits in the buccal groove of the lower first molar; in class II the upper molar sits forward of the lower, as in retrognathia; and in class III it sits behind, as in prognathism. Chin implants of silastic, porous polyethylene or ePTFE are placed in a subperiosteal pocket through a submental or intraoral incision, and can displace, become infected, cause bone resorption or injure the mental nerve. A sliding genioplasty adds both projection and height, at the risk of injury to the mental nerve and the tooth roots."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s1-4","Skin","Facial plastic surgery · Facial analysis","The Fitzpatrick types run from I, which always burns and never tans, to VI, which never burns; types III to VI are at more risk of pigment change after peels and lasers. Glogau's photoageing classes progress from I (no wrinkles) through II (wrinkles in motion) and III (wrinkles at rest) to IV (only wrinkles). Nasal skin is thick and sebaceous at the tip and supratip and thinnest at the rhinion: thick skin hides tip work and swells for months, while thin skin shows every irregularity."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s2-1","Surgical anatomy","Facial plastic surgery · Rhinoplasty","The nose is best thought of in thirds. The upper, bony third is the nasal bones. The middle third is the upper lateral cartilages fused to the dorsal septum, which together form the internal nasal valve , normally an angle of 10 to 15°. The lower third is the lower lateral (alar) cartilages, with their medial, intermediate and lateral crura and the domes, and the external valve. The junction of the upper and lower laterals is the scroll , and the area where the nasal bones meet the upper lateral cartilages and septum is the keystone , whose disruption lets the middle vault collapse. Tip support comes from three major mechanisms: the size, shape and strength of the lower lateral cartilages; the attachment of the medial crural footplates to the caudal septum; and the attachment of the upper to the lower laterals at the scroll. The minor mechanisms are the interdomal ligament, the dorsal cartilaginous septum, the sesamoid complex, the skin and soft-tissue envelope, the nasal spine and the membranous septum. Every incision or resection that disrupts one of these must be compensated for. The tripod concept makes tip surgery predictable. The two lateral crura and the conjoined medial crura form the three legs of a tripod; shortening or lengthening the legs changes projection and rotation in ways that can be planned. Figure 2 Muscles of the nose, and nasal skin thickness from nasion to tip. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s2-2","Analysis of the nose","Facial plastic surgery · Rhinoplasty","On the frontal view , assess deviation by thirds, the width of the bony base compared with the alar base, smooth brow–tip aesthetic lines running from the medial brow to the tip-defining point, tip definition, and the \"gull in flight\" outline of the alae and columella. On profile , note the radix position, which should lie between the lash line and the upper lid crease, any dorsal hump, and a supratip break, which is desirable in women. Tip projection is judged by the Goode ratio , in which projection is 0.55 to 0.6 of nasal length, or by the 3:4:5 triangle; rotation is read from the nasolabial angle; and 2 to 4 mm of columellar show is normal. On the base view , the nose should form an equilateral triangle with a columella-to-lobule ratio of 2:1, symmetrical nostrils and no caudal septal deviation. Inside the nose , examine the septum, turbinates and valves; the Cottle manoeuvre , in which lateral traction on the cheek improves the airway, suggests valve collapse, and the modified Cottle test with a cotton bud localises it. Take at least six standard photographs, and always weigh the patient as well as the nose: body dysmorphic disorder and unrealistic expectations are the commonest causes of an unhappy outcome after technically sound surgery. Every rhinoplasty assessment should include a patient-reported outcome measure, a screening questionnaire for body dysmorphic disorder, and a functional examination of the valves."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s2-3","Approaches and incisions","Facial plastic surgery · Rhinoplasty","The incisions are the marginal , along the caudal edge of the lateral crus; the intercartilaginous , between the upper and lower laterals; the transcartilaginous , through the lateral crus; the transcolumellar , an inverted V or stair step; and the transfixion or hemitransfixion , between the caudal septum and the medial crura. Endonasal (closed) rhinoplasty uses either a non-delivery approach, cartilage-splitting or retrograde, or a delivery approach in which the alar cartilages are brought out as bipedicled flaps. It leaves no external scar and causes less swelling, but exposure is limited. External (open) rhinoplasty joins transcolumellar and marginal incisions and lifts the whole skin envelope. It gives the best exposure for precise sutures and grafts and is preferred for revision, cleft and crooked noses, major tip work and teaching. Its costs are a columellar scar, more oedema and the disruption of a minor tip support. Common manoeuvres Problem Manoeuvre Dorsal hump Component reduction: rasp the bone, cut the cartilage; then osteotomies to close the open roof; spreader grafts to keep the middle vault and valve Wide or crooked bony vault Medial, lateral (low-to-high along the nasomaxillary groove, starting above the inferior turbinate attachment) and intermediate osteotomies Crooked middle third Septoplasty, asymmetric spreader grafts or spreader flaps, camouflage onlay grafts Bulbous tip Cephalic trim, keeping at least 6 to 10 mm of lateral crus; dome-binding (transdomal, interdomal) sutures; lateral crural strut grafts Under-projected tip Columellar strut, caudal sep"],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s2-4","Preservation rhinoplasty and powered tools","Facial plastic surgery · Rhinoplasty","Removing a dorsal hump in one piece (the Joseph technique) destroys the junction between the nasal bones, upper lateral cartilages and septum, which can lead to middle vault collapse, an inverted V deformity and internal valve narrowing; hence the routine use of spreader grafts or spreader flaps (folding the upper lateral cartilages inwards) after hump reduction, and preserving the mucoperichondrium under the dorsum as a soft-tissue spreader. Preservation rhinoplasty , revived in recent years, aims to avoid these problems. As described by Daniel, it has three parts: subperichondrial elevation of the soft tissue envelope, preservation of the nasal ligaments, and dorsal preservation , in which the hump is lowered by removing a strip of septum and bone beneath it (\"push-down\" or \"let-down\") rather than by resection; it suits a well-shaped dorsum with a hump. Piezoelectric instruments and powered burs allow precise osteotomies under direct vision after wide subperiosteal exposure."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s2-5","Complications and revision","Facial plastic surgery · Rhinoplasty","Between 5 and 15% of rhinoplasties are revised, and revision should wait about a year for the tissues to settle. The named deformities each have a mechanism and a remedy. A pollybeak is fullness above the tip, from an under-resected cartilaginous dorsum, supratip dead space and scar, or loss of tip support; it is treated with steroid injection, trimming or restoring support. A saddle nose follows over-resection of the dorsum, septal collapse, a septal abscess or haematoma, or granulomatous disease such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis ( Granulomatous disease of the nose ) or cocaine use, and needs grafts. An inverted-V deformity appears when the upper lateral cartilages collapse away from the nasal bones and is corrected with spreader grafts. An open roof results from omitting osteotomies after hump removal, and a rocker deformity from carrying an osteotomy too high into the thick nasal bone. Alar retraction and notching follow over-resection of the lateral crus and need alar rim or composite grafts. Tip bossae, a pinched tip and tip ptosis are the other tip problems. Functionally, the nose may end up more obstructed through valve collapse, synechiae or residual septal deviation. Septal haematoma or perforation, epistaxis, a rare CSF leak from a high septal or osteotomy fracture, and dissatisfaction complete the list."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s3-1","What ages","Facial plastic surgery · The ageing face","Every layer ages. The skin develops a thinner dermis with less collagen and elastin, photodamage and wrinkles. The soft tissues descend and the fat pads lose volume. The bone resorbs, especially at the orbital rims, piriform aperture and maxilla and in the pre-jowl mandible. The changes show differently in each third. The upper third gains forehead lines and brow ptosis; the middle third shows eyelid laxity and fat bulges, a tear trough and deeper nasolabial folds; and the lower third develops jowls, marionette lines, neck laxity and platysmal bands."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s3-2","Brow and forehead lift","Facial plastic surgery · The ageing face","The ideal brow begins over the medial canthus and ends on a line from the alar base through the lateral canthus. In women it arches above the orbital rim, peaking between the lateral limbus and the lateral canthus; in men it lies flatter, at the rim. A coronal lift hides the scar well but raises the hairline and leaves the scalp numb, so a pretrichial incision is chosen for a high hairline. The endoscopic lift uses small incisions and subperiosteal release with fixation. Direct and midforehead lifts suit older men with deep forehead lines and patients with facial palsy. The danger is the frontal branch of the facial nerve , which runs in the temporoparietal fascia along Pitanguy's line , from 0.5 cm below the tragus to 1.5 cm above the lateral brow. Lateral dissection is therefore kept deep , on the deep temporal fascia. Figure 3 Extracranial branches of the facial nerve. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s3-3","Blepharoplasty","Facial plastic surgery · The ageing face","Upper lid blepharoplasty removes excess skin ( dermatochalasis ) with a strip of orbicularis and a conservative amount of fat from the medial and central compartments, through a crease incision about 8 to 10 mm above the lashes. At least 20 mm of skin must remain between the lash line and the brow, and a ptotic brow, rather than the lid, may be the real problem. Before surgery, record vision and fields, test for dry eye with the Schirmer test , and look for thyroid eye disease, previous surgery and true blepharoptosis , which needs levator surgery rather than skin removal. In the lower lid , fat alone is removed through a transconjunctival approach, and excess skin through a subciliary skin or skin–muscle flap. Lid laxity is tested with the snap test and the distraction test, in which more than 6 to 10 mm is abnormal; a lax lid needs a canthopexy or canthoplasty to avoid ectropion. A negative vector , with the globe projecting ahead of the cheek, is a warning against removing fat. The feared complication is a retrobulbar haematoma , which can blind; it presents with pain, proptosis and falling vision, and is treated by emergency lateral canthotomy and cantholysis , opening the wound, and mannitol, acetazolamide and steroids. The others are lagophthalmos and dry eye, ectropion and scleral show, ptosis, diplopia from inferior oblique injury, and milia. Pain and proptosis after eyelid surgery Severe pain, a tense proptosed eye and falling vision after blepharoplasty mean a retrobulbar haematoma. Release the sutures and perform a lateral canthotomy and cantholysis at the bedsid"],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s3-4","Rhytidectomy (facelift)","Facial plastic surgery · The ageing face","The facelift depends on the SMAS , the superficial musculoaponeurotic system, continuous with the platysma below and the temporoparietal fascia above. The facial nerve branches run deep to it, which is why dissection above the SMAS is safe and dissection beneath it demands care. Techniques are graded by depth: a skin-only lift; SMAS plication ; SMAS imbrication or lateral SMASectomy; the deep-plane lift, which goes beneath the SMAS and carries the malar fat to give the strongest midface effect at more risk to the nerves; and the composite lift. The neck is treated with liposuction, a midline platysmaplasty and, in selected patients, contouring of the submandibular gland or digastric. Dedo's classification guides the neck: class I minimal deformity, II skin laxity, III fat, IV platysmal banding, V micrognathia and VI a low hyoid. Haematoma is the commonest complication, at about 3 to 15%, more often in men and hypertensive patients, and must be evacuated promptly. Skin necrosis occurs behind the ear, especially in smokers. The great auricular nerve is the nerve most often injured, leaving the ear lobe numb; facial nerve injury, usually of the temporal or marginal mandibular branch, is usually temporary. Alopecia, a shifted hairline, the pulled-down \"pixie ear\" and poor scars are the cosmetic failures."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s4-1","The prominent ear","Facial plastic surgery · The ear","The prominent ear is the commonest auricular deformity (other pinna anomalies: Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma ). It results from a deficient antihelical fold , a deep conchal bowl , or both, sometimes with a protruding lobule. Normally the auriculocephalic angle is about 20 to 30°, the helix sits 15 to 20 mm from the scalp at the top and about 20 mm at mid-ear, and the ear is about 6 cm long and 55% as wide as it is long. Surgery is usually done at five to six years, when the ear has reached 85 to 90% of adult size and before teasing at school begins. In newborns, ear moulding splints in the first weeks correct many deformities without surgery."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s4-2","Techniques and complications","Facial plastic surgery · The ear","Mustardé horizontal mattress sutures create the antihelical fold, and Furnas conchomastoid sutures set back the concha; they are placed posteriorly so as not to narrow the canal. Stiff cartilage is weakened by scoring or excision (the Chongchet, Stenström and Converse techniques), and the conchal bowl or lobule may be reduced directly. Haematoma is the urgent complication, because it leads to perichondritis and a cauliflower ear. Infection and chondritis, suture extrusion and asymmetry also occur. Under-correction or recurrence is the commonest problem overall, and over-correction of the middle third with prominent poles produces the \" telephone ear \". Figure 4 Surface landmarks of the auricle. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s5-1","Resurfacing","Facial plastic surgery · Skin and injectables","Every resurfacing method works the same way: it removes the epidermis and injures the papillary dermis, so that new collagen and epidermis form. Injury that reaches the reticular dermis scars. Chemical peels are superficial (glycolic acid, Jessner solution, 10 to 25% TCA), medium (Jessner solution followed by 35% TCA) or deep (Baker–Gordon phenol). Phenol is cardiotoxic, causing arrhythmias , as well as nephrotoxic and hepatotoxic, so the ECG is monitored, the patient hydrated and one facial unit treated at a time. Dermabrasion works mechanically down to the papillary dermis, signalled by pinpoint bleeding, and suits acne scars, rhinophyma and scar revision. Among lasers , the ablative CO2 laser penetrates deeper and tightens, while Er:YAG is shallower and heals faster; fractional lasers make microscopic columns of injury that heal rapidly, and non-ablative lasers treat beneath an intact epidermis. Resurfacing is avoided within 6 to 12 months of isotretinoin (some say two years), and in patients with keloids, previous irradiation or scleroderma. Active herpes needs antiviral prophylaxis, and darker skin types risk pigment change."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s5-2","Botulinum toxin","Facial plastic surgery · Skin and injectables","Botulinum toxin blocks the presynaptic release of acetylcholine by cleaving the SNARE proteins, SNAP-25 in the case of type A. It acts within 2 to 3 days, peaks at 1 to 2 weeks and lasts 3 to 4 months. Cosmetically it treats glabellar lines (corrugator and procerus), forehead lines (frontalis), crow's feet (orbicularis oculi), platysmal bands and the gummy smile. In ENT it treats spasmodic dysphonia, hemifacial spasm and blepharospasm, synkinesis after facial palsy and the opposite side for symmetry, Frey syndrome, sialorrhoea and sialoceles, cricopharyngeal dysfunction, palatal myoclonus and migraine. Its complications are bruising and headache, eyelid ptosis from spread to the levator (treated with apraclonidine drops), brow ptosis, an asymmetric smile and dysphagia. It is avoided in myasthenia gravis, Lambert–Eaton syndrome and other neuromuscular diseases, in patients taking aminoglycosides, and in pregnancy and breastfeeding."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s5-3","Fillers and fat","Facial plastic surgery · Skin and injectables","Hyaluronic acid is the commonest filler. It lasts 6 to 18 months and, importantly, can be dissolved with hyaluronidase . Calcium hydroxylapatite and poly-L-lactic acid, which stimulates collagen over months, last longer, and permanent fillers such as PMMA and silicone carry a risk of late granulomas. Autologous fat is harvested, centrifuged and injected in small aliquots in several layers, with unpredictable survival. The danger zones are the glabella, nasal dorsum, nasolabial fold and temple, where filler can enter an artery. Vascular occlusion from filler Skin occlusion: blanching, pain, then a livedo or dusky pattern after injection. Stop injecting, flood the area with high-dose hyaluronidase (for hyaluronic acid fillers), massage, and apply warm compresses. Visual loss: retrograde embolism into the ophthalmic artery, from glabellar, nasal or nasolabial injection. This is an ophthalmic emergency."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s5-4","Scar revision","Facial plastic surgery · Skin and injectables","Scars hide best in relaxed skin tension lines , facial creases and the borders of aesthetic units, so revision aims to move them there. The Z-plasty lengthens a scar and changes its direction. With limbs at 30°, 45° and 60° it lengthens the scar by about 25%, 50% and 75%, and turns it by up to 90°, which makes it the tool for contracted scars and for scars lying 30 to 60° off the tension lines. Long scars that run against the lines are broken up with a W-plasty or a geometric broken-line closure. Dermabrasion or laser at 6 to 8 weeks refines the result, and steroid injection or silicone controls hypertrophic scars and keloids."],["ev","cl:t-facplast~s5-5","Facial plastic surgery: current evidence","Facial plastic surgery · Skin and injectables","Body dysmorphic disorder is found in roughly 10 to 15% of patients seeking cosmetic rhinoplasty in screening studies, and surgery rarely helps them, so a validated screening questionnaire belongs in every assessment. Outcome measures. The Standardized Cosmesis and Health Nasal Outcomes Survey (SCHNOS) and NOSE scale record function and appearance before and after surgery. Functional rhinoplasty. The American consensus statement on nasal valve compromise supports spreader grafts, alar batten grafts and lateral crural strut grafts for nasal valve collapse (Rhee et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2010, AAO-HNS consensus statement). Fillers. Hyaluronic acid filler in the nose and glabella carries a small risk of vascular occlusion and blindness; hyaluronidase must be immediately available."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s5-5","Hair restoration","Facial plastic surgery · Skin and injectables","Male pattern baldness is androgenetic alopecia, graded by the Norwood classification . Minoxidil, finasteride and platelet-rich plasma slow it. Transplantation moves follicular unit grafts from the androgen-resistant occipital scalp, harvested as a strip or by follicular unit extraction , with single-hair units placed at the hairline. Transplanted hairs shed after a few weeks and regrow from about three months."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s6-1","Viva essentials","Facial plastic surgery · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Facial proportions (thirds and fifths) and the main angles. Major and minor tip support mechanisms; the tripod concept. The internal and external nasal valves: the Cottle test, spreader and batten grafts. Open versus closed rhinoplasty; osteotomies; the L-strut. Complications: pollybeak, saddle nose, inverted V. The frontal branch and its safe plane; the SMAS. Facelift haematoma; great auricular nerve injury. Blepharoplasty: tests, retrobulbar haematoma. Otoplasty: Mustardé and Furnas sutures; the telephone ear. Botulinum toxin: mechanism, uses, complications. Filler vascular occlusion and hyaluronidase. Z-plasty geometry."],["sec","cl:t-facplast~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Facial plastic surgery · For the examination","Common pitfalls Removing a dorsal hump without osteotomies or spreader grafts (open roof, inverted V). An aggressive cephalic trim leading to alar collapse and notching. Blepharoplasty without recognising true ptosis, dry eye or a lax lower lid. Injecting filler in danger zones without aspiration, slow low-pressure technique, or hyaluronidase available. Operating on a patient with body dysmorphic disorder."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s1-1","Who","Foreign bodies of the airway · Overview","Children, more than half under four, who inhale suddenly while laughing, crying, playing or running with something in the mouth; infants have no molars to grind nuts and a weak cough. In adults, aspiration occurs in coma, sleep, intoxication, seizures, under anaesthesia (teeth, dentures), with neurological disease, and in the elderly."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s1-2","What","Foreign bodies of the airway · Overview","Vegetable (organic): peanuts above all, also seeds (watermelon, pumpkin), beans, peas, grains, nuts, pieces of carrot or apple; regional patterns follow diet (areca nut in parts of India, watermelon seeds in Egypt). They absorb water and swell, and their oils provoke an intense vegetal bronchitis with oedema, granulation and suppuration. Non-vegetable: plastic toys and whistles, beads, pins and needles, nails, wires, ball bearings, coins, pen caps and springs; inert ones may stay silent for long periods. Food boluses that obstruct the larynx in adults (the \"café coronary\"). Figure 1 Laryngeal foreign bodies: (1) safety pin, (2) pin, (3, 4) twisted wire, (5) a fruit seed. Dhingra, fig. 65.1"],["sec","cl:t-fb~s1-3","Where","Foreign bodies of the airway · Overview","Large objects lodge above the glottis; small ones pass to the trachea and bronchi, most often into the right main bronchus , which is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left (in young children the difference is less). Sharp objects stick anywhere."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s2-1","Stage 1: the event","Foreign bodies of the airway · Clinical features","Sudden choking, gagging, coughing and wheezing, sometimes cyanosis, lasting minutes. The object may be coughed out or lodge. Often the event is not witnessed, or forgotten."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s2-2","Stage 2: the silent interval","Foreign bodies of the airway · Clinical features","The mucosa adapts and the cough settles, for hours to weeks, depending on the object."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s2-3","Stage 3: complications","Foreign bodies of the airway · Clinical features","Obstruction, inflammation and infection, according to the site. Features by site Site Features Larynx Complete obstruction: aphonia, inability to cough or breathe, rapid death. Partial: pain, hoarseness, croupy cough, stridor, dyspnoea, haemoptysis Trachea Choking, cough, biphasic stridor and wheeze heard at the open mouth; a mobile object gives an audible slap and palpable thud as it strikes the undersurface of the folds Bronchus The triad of cough, wheeze and reduced air entry on one side; later collapse, emphysema, pneumonia, bronchiectasis, lung abscess"],["sec","cl:t-fb~s2-4","Patterns of bronchial obstruction","Foreign bodies of the airway · Clinical features","Figure 2 Types of bronchial obstruction: (A) partial (bypass valve) with wheeze; (B) check-valve with obstructive emphysema; (C) complete (stop valve) with collapse; (D) ball valve. Dhingra, fig. 65.2 Bypass valve: air passes both ways; only a wheeze. Check valve: air enters but cannot leave; obstructive emphysema of the lobe or lung, with hyper-resonance, reduced breath sounds and mediastinal shift to the other side. Stop valve: complete obstruction; air is absorbed and the segment collapses ( atelectasis ), with dullness and shift towards the affected side. Ball valve: a mobile object enters on inspiration but blocks on expiration. An emphysematous bulla may rupture into a pneumothorax, and a mobile object may shift sides, changing the signs. The asthmatic child who is not asthmatic New wheeze that is one-sided, does not respond to bronchodilators, or follows a choking episode; recurrent pneumonia in the same lobe; a persistent cough after eating nuts: think foreign body and arrange bronchoscopy."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s3-1","History","Foreign bodies of the airway · Diagnosis","The single most important clue: a witnessed choking episode has high sensitivity. Ask directly, and ask other family members."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s3-2","Examination","Foreign bodies of the airway · Diagnosis","Respiratory rate and effort, stridor, voice, unilateral wheeze, reduced air entry, signs of collapse or hyperinflation, and the audible slap of a tracheal object."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s3-3","Imaging","Foreign bodies of the airway · Diagnosis","Soft tissue radiographs of the neck (anteroposterior and lateral, neck extended): radio-opaque objects, sometimes radiolucent ones in the larynx and trachea. A coin in the trachea lies edge-on in the PA view (sagittal), whereas one in the oesophagus lies flat (coronal). Chest radiograph (PA and lateral): the object if radio-opaque; lobar or segmental collapse ; unilateral hyperinflation with mediastinal shift away; pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum; pneumonia or bronchiectasis in late cases. Inspiratory and expiratory films, or fluoroscopy (lateral decubitus films in young children): air trapping on the affected side, which does not deflate on expiration, and the mediastinum swings towards the normal side on expiration. CT of the chest (low dose) in doubtful, stable cases; virtual bronchoscopy. A normal chest film does not exclude it Most inhaled foreign bodies in children are radiolucent nuts and seeds, and the film is normal in a large proportion, especially in the first day. If the story is suggestive, proceed to bronchoscopy. Figure 3 First, a radiolucent fruit seed (chiku) in the subglottis (arrow) in a child with a tracheostomy. Second, a nail in the right bronchus on a PA chest film. Dhingra, figs 65.3 and 65.4 Figure 4 First, a broken piece of a Fuller's tracheostomy tube in the left bronchus. Second, (A) a spring from a ball pen in the right bronchus, and (B) the spring after removal. Dhingra, figs 65.5 and 65.6"],["sec","cl:t-fb~s4-1","Complete obstruction: first aid","Foreign bodies of the airway · Management","A patient who cannot speak, breathe or cough needs immediate action: Infants under one year: five back blows with the infant prone along the forearm, head down, then five chest thrusts; repeat. No abdominal thrusts. Children and adults: five back blows, then five abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) : standing behind, fist below the xiphisternum, sharp inward and upward thrusts that use the residual lung air to expel the object; repeat. If unconscious: start cardiopulmonary resuscitation; look in the mouth and remove a visible object (no blind finger sweeps). If these fail and expertise is available: laryngoscopy and removal with Magill forceps, or cricothyroidotomy (needle in small children) or emergency tracheostomy. Figure 5 The Heimlich manoeuvre: a sudden thrust upwards and backwards below the epigastrium squeezes air from the lungs to dislodge the object. Dhingra, fig. 65.7 Partial obstruction A patient who can cough and breathe should be encouraged to keep coughing and taken calmly to theatre; back blows and thrusts may convert a partial into a complete obstruction."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s4-2","Laryngeal foreign bodies","Foreign bodies of the airway · Management","After the emergency, removal by direct laryngoscopy under general anaesthesia, or by laryngofissure for an impacted object; tracheostomy first if the airway is compromised."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s4-3","Tracheobronchial foreign bodies","Foreign bodies of the airway · Management","Removed by rigid ventilating bronchoscopy under general anaesthesia, ideally with spontaneous ventilation, by a prepared team: an anaesthetist and surgeon who agree a plan, bronchoscopes of the right size and one size either side, telescopes and optical forceps , two laryngoscopes, a range of forceps, Dormia baskets and Fogarty balloon catheters, and suction, all checked beforehand. Practise grasping a duplicate object before starting. Urgent removal is needed for airway obstruction and for vegetable matter that will swell; otherwise it is done on the next planned list with a full team, not in the middle of the night. Small objects are withdrawn through the scope; larger ones are held against the tip and the scope, forceps and object removed together; a sharp point is sheathed in the scope or turned to trail. Steroids reduce oedema; physiotherapy and antibiotics treat secondary infection. Fluoroscopy (C-arm) for peripheral metallic objects; flexible bronchoscopy in selected adults; tracheostomy and bronchoscopy through the stoma for large objects; thoracotomy and bronchotomy for objects that cannot be reached."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s4-4","Complications of removal","Foreign bodies of the airway · Management","Oedema of the subglottis, bronchospasm, bleeding from granulations, pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum, fragmentation of nuts with distal spread, and complete obstruction if the object is dropped in the glottis: push it back into a main bronchus and ventilate the other lung, then retrieve it."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s4-5","Late complications of a retained object","Foreign bodies of the airway · Management","Recurrent pneumonia, bronchiectasis, lung abscess, granulation tissue and stenosis."],["ev","cl:t-fb~s4-6","Foreign bodies of the airway: current evidence","Foreign bodies of the airway · Management","First aid. The European Resuscitation Council guidelines (2021) keep five back blows then five abdominal thrusts for children over one and adults, five back blows then five chest thrusts for infants, encouragement to cough while the cough is effective, and no blind finger sweeps. Imaging. Low-dose CT has a high negative predictive value when the history is doubtful. Swallowed objects and button batteries in the oesophagus are covered in Swallowed foreign bodies ."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s4-6","Prevention","Foreign bodies of the airway · Management","No nuts, popcorn, hard sweets or whole grapes for children under four or five; supervise meals and play; age-appropriate toys without small parts; never hold pins or small objects in the mouth."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s5-1","Viva essentials","Foreign bodies of the airway · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Three stages of symptoms and the silent interval. Why the right bronchus. Signs by site: audible slap and palpable thud; the triad. Valve types and their radiological signs; inspiratory and expiratory films. First aid: back blows, chest thrusts in infants, Heimlich in older children and adults. Rigid bronchoscopy: equipment and technique."],["sec","cl:t-fb~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Foreign bodies of the airway · For the examination","Common pitfalls Excluding a foreign body because the chest film is normal. Treating a sudden one-sided wheeze in a toddler as asthma. Performing back blows or the Heimlich manoeuvre on a patient who is coughing effectively. Attempting removal without the right-sized instruments checked and ready."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s1-1","The pharynx","Swallowed foreign bodies · Where they lodge","In the pharynx, fish bones and needles lodge in the tonsil , often in a crypt, where they can be seen and lifted out with forceps. Fish bones also catch in the tongue base and vallecula , where they are found with a mirror or endoscope and removed under topical anaesthesia with curved forceps; a needle buried in the tongue base may migrate and need imaging and an open pharyngotomy. Wires, pins and staples stick to the posterior pharyngeal wall . In the pyriform fossa , fish and chicken bones, needles and dentures are common; small objects can be removed in clinic, but large or impacted ones, and all objects in children, are removed under general anaesthesia. Figure 1 The hypopharynx and larynx as seen endoscopically, and the vallecula with the hyoepiglottic ligament. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s1-2","The oesophagus","Swallowed foreign bodies · Where they lodge","By far the commonest site, about 70%, is at or just below the cricopharyngeus , and coins are typically held at the sphincter itself. Objects that pass it may stop at the crossing of the aorta and left bronchus (about 20%) or at the cardia (about 10%), while sharp objects catch anywhere. An object that lodges below the cricopharyngeus in an adult suggests an underlying stricture, ring, eosinophilic oesophagitis or cancer, and the oesophagus should be endoscoped or imaged once the oedema settles ( Oesophagus and dysphagia )."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s1-3","Who swallows things","Swallowed foreign bodies · Where they lodge","About 80% of patients are children under five, who swallow coins, toys, beads, button batteries and magnets. Adults swallow food boluses, especially meat, and fish and chicken bones; they swallow dentures, partly because an upper denture dulls palatal sensation; and they are predisposed by hasty eating, poor chewing, alcohol, sleep, seizures and unconsciousness. Psychiatric patients and prisoners may swallow objects deliberately. In an older adult, a sudden bolus obstruction may be the first sign of a narrowed oesophagus from stricture or carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s2-1","Symptoms","Swallowed foreign bodies · Assessment","The patient usually describes choking or gagging at the time, followed by pain or a pricking feeling localised in the neck, often beside the trachea, and worse on swallowing. Pain localises well in the pharynx and cervical oesophagus but poorly lower down. Dysphagia may begin as partial and become complete as oedema develops, and drooling with inability to swallow saliva means complete obstruction and a risk of aspiration. In small children the respiratory tract may dominate (inhaled foreign bodies are covered in Foreign bodies of the airway ): stridor or cough from compression of the soft posterior tracheal wall or from laryngeal oedema, or wheeze and recurrent chest infection from a long-standing oesophageal object. Chest, back or abdominal pain, fever and emphysema warn of perforation. Some patients, especially children, have few symptoms at all, so normal eating and a normal X-ray do not exclude a foreign body."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s2-2","Signs","Swallowed foreign bodies · Assessment","The signs are tenderness low in the neck beside the trachea ( Jackson's sign ), pain on rocking the larynx against the spine, pooling of saliva in the pyriform fossae that does not clear with swallowing, and sometimes the object itself in the postcricoid region. Surgical emphysema, fever and tachycardia mean perforation."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s2-3","Imaging","Swallowed foreign bodies · Assessment","Plain posteroanterior and lateral films are taken of the neck, chest and abdomen, and in children from mouth to anus so that every object is found. A coin in the oesophagus lies flat (en face) on the PA view and edge-on on the lateral; a coin in the trachea lies the other way, edge-on on the PA view, because the glottis is a sagittal slit. A button battery shows a double ring (\"halo\") on the PA view and a step-off on the lateral, so every apparent coin must be looked at carefully. Many fish bones, wood, plastic, aluminium and glass are invisible on X-ray. On the lateral neck film their presence is suggested indirectly by air in the cervical oesophagus, prevertebral soft-tissue thickening , loss of the cervical lordosis, or air in the tissues from perforation. CT is the most sensitive test for bones, perforation and abscess, and should be used freely when symptoms persist despite a negative film. Barium is avoided because it coats the object and mucosa, can be aspirated and makes endoscopy harder; a water-soluble study is used only if perforation is suspected and CT is unavailable. Figure 2 First, a coin in the upper oesophagus: (A) flat in the PA view, (B) edge-on in the lateral. Second, a fish bone in the cervical oesophagus with a bubble of air (arrow). Dhingra, figs 70.1 and 70.2 Figure 3 Two metallic foreign bodies: a large ring at the cricopharyngeus and a small piece in the abdomen (arrows). Dhingra, fig. 70.3"],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s3-1","Timing","Swallowed foreign bodies · Removal","Some objects must come out within hours : a button battery in the oesophagus , any object causing complete obstruction with drooling or airway compromise, sharp or pointed objects in the oesophagus, and any case with signs of perforation. Other oesophageal foreign bodies, including coins and food boluses that do not pass, should come out within 24 hours , because prolonged impaction increases ulceration, perforation and stricture. A smooth coin in the lower oesophagus of a well child can be observed for 12 to 24 hours with a repeat film, since it may pass into the stomach."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s3-2","Methods","Swallowed foreign bodies · Removal","Rigid oesophagoscopy under general anaesthesia is the traditional ENT approach and the best for sharp or impacted objects and for objects at the cricopharyngeus. Sharp points are drawn into the end of the scope and withdrawn with it (\" ensheathing \"), open safety pins are closed or turned so that the point trails, and a hypopharyngoscope or long-bladed laryngoscope suits objects at the upper sphincter. Flexible endoscopy under sedation, usually by gastroenterologists, suits food boluses, coins and blunt objects, with an overtube to protect the oesophagus from sharp ones; it also reveals any underlying eosinophilic oesophagitis, stricture or cancer, which should be biopsied. Open surgery is needed occasionally: a cervical oesophagotomy for impacted hooked objects such as partial dentures above the thoracic inlet, and thoracotomy or thoracoscopy for impacted lower objects and perforations. Methods to avoid Blind removal with a Foley balloon catheter: the object can be aspirated as it is pulled into the pharynx. Pushing an object into the stomach blind. Meat tenderisers (papain): they can digest the oesophageal wall. Emetics. Glucagon, which relaxes the LES but does not help when there is a stricture or ring, and has little proven benefit."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s3-3","After removal","Swallowed foreign bodies · Removal","After removal the mucosa is re-examined for ulceration, perforation and an underlying cause. Oral fluids start if the procedure was uneventful, the patient is watched for pain, fever and emphysema, and follow-up endoscopy or a contrast study is arranged if a stricture or tumour is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s4-1","Button (disc) batteries","Swallowed foreign bodies · Special objects","A button battery lodged against moist mucosa passes a current that hydrolyses tissue fluid and generates hydroxide at the negative pole , producing an alkaline, liquefactive burn, to which leakage of the contents and pressure add. The injury is fast: the mucosa is damaged within an hour, the muscle within two to four hours, and the wall can perforate within 8 to 12 hours. Lithium cells of 20 mm or more are the most dangerous. Late complications include tracheo-oesophageal fistula, mediastinitis, stricture, vocal fold palsy and an aorto-oesophageal fistula with fatal haemorrhage , which can appear days or weeks later. A battery in the oesophagus is removed endoscopically at once , at any hour. If ingestion was within 12 hours, honey (in children over one year) or sucralfate is given on the way to theatre to coat and protect the mucosa, provided it does not delay removal. Afterwards CT or MRI looks for deep injury, and significant injuries are observed in hospital. Once past the oesophagus, most batteries in the stomach pass. They are removed if the child has symptoms, if a large battery has been swallowed by a young child, or if it remains in the stomach on a repeat film at about 48 hours; One textbook threshold is a child under six with a battery of 1.5 cm or more. Batteries in the nose ( Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis ) and ear are emergencies too. The halo sign Any round radio-opaque object in the oesophagus of a child is a button battery until a careful look excludes it. Look for the double contour (halo) on the frontal film and the step at the edge on the "],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s4-2","Magnets","Swallowed foreign bodies · Special objects","A single magnet usually passes. Two or more magnets, or a magnet with a metal object, attract each other across loops of bowel and cause pressure necrosis, fistulae and perforation. Remove them while they can be reached endoscopically, and seek early surgical advice if they have passed beyond."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s4-3","Sharp and long objects","Swallowed foreign bodies · Special objects","Pins, needles, bones, open safety pins and denture fragments with hooks carry the highest risk of perforation and are removed from the oesophagus urgently. Beyond the stomach, sharp objects and long objects (over about 5 cm in a small child and 6 cm or more in adults) may not negotiate the duodenum; they are removed endoscopically if reachable, or followed with daily films, and surgery is needed for pain, tenderness, failure to progress or perforation."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s4-4","Objects beyond the stomach","Swallowed foreign bodies · Special objects","Most objects that reach the stomach pass within a week on a normal diet, without purgatives, while the stools are checked and films repeated. Operation is needed for abdominal pain or tenderness, an object that does not progress on serial films, a sharp object likely to perforate, a long object in a small child, and pyloric stenosis or other obstruction."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s4-5","Food bolus impaction","Swallowed foreign bodies · Special objects","If the patient can swallow saliva and is comfortable, a short wait may allow the bolus to pass. If saliva cannot be swallowed, or impaction lasts beyond 12 to 24 hours, the bolus is removed endoscopically, gently pushed on if there is no stricture or removed piecemeal. Almost every adult then needs investigation, usually biopsy for eosinophilic oesophagitis and a search for rings, strictures and cancer."],["ev","cl:t-fbfood~s5-1","Swallowed foreign bodies: current evidence","Swallowed foreign bodies · Complications","Guidelines. The European Society of Gastrointestinal Endoscopy recommends emergency endoscopy (ideally within 6 hours, at most 24) for complete oesophageal obstruction, button batteries and sharp objects in the oesophagus, and urgent endoscopy within 24 hours for other oesophageal foreign bodies; it advises CT rather than plain films when perforation or a bone is suspected, and against barium swallow (Birk et al., Endoscopy 2016). Button batteries. Remove within two hours; honey or sucralfate while waiting, in children over one year who swallowed it within 12 hours (Mubarak et al., J Pediatr Gastroenterol Nutr 2021). The honey dose is 10 ml every ten minutes, up to six doses; it reduced injury in animal studies (Anfang et al., Laryngoscope 2019), but a systematic review found the human evidence limited (Schmidt et al., Front Pediatr 2023). Coins. An asymptomatic coin in the distal oesophagus passes spontaneously in about a quarter to a third of children within 8 to 16 hours, so a short period of observation with a repeat film is reasonable before endoscopy."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s5-1","What a neglected object does","Swallowed foreign bodies · Complications","A foreign body can obstruct the airway through laryngeal oedema or, in children, tracheal compression. Perforation leads to retropharyngeal or parapharyngeal abscess ( Deep neck space infection ) and mediastinitis , and a sharp object can erode into the aorta to form an aorto-oesophageal fistula, announced by a sentinel bleed before exsanguination; pericarditis and empyema can also follow. Periesophageal cellulitis and neck abscess, tracheo-oesophageal fistula, ulceration and stricture from a neglected object, and migration of needles and bones into the neck, sometimes needing open exploration, complete the list. Pharyngeal and oesophageal foreign bodies at a glance Situation Action Fish bone seen in tonsil or tongue base Remove under topical anaesthesia with forceps Persistent pricking with a normal examination Flexible endoscopy; CT if still suspected; oesophagoscopy if seen or symptoms persist Coin in the upper oesophagus Rigid or flexible removal within 24 hours Coin in the lower oesophagus, well child Observe up to 24 hours; repeat film; remove if still there Button battery in the oesophagus Immediate removal; honey or sucralfate en route if it does not delay Food bolus, cannot swallow saliva Urgent endoscopy; biopsy for eosinophilic oesophagitis Sharp object in the oesophagus Urgent rigid oesophagoscopy, sheathing the point Two or more magnets Remove while reachable; surgical review Pain, fever, emphysema Suspect perforation: nil by mouth, antibiotics, CT, surgical opinion"],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s6-1","Viva essentials","Swallowed foreign bodies · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Commonest site (just below the cricopharyngeus) and the three narrowings. Coin orientation: oesophagus versus trachea. Button battery: halo sign, mechanism and timing of injury, immediate removal, honey or sucralfate. Signs of an oesophageal foreign body and of perforation. Radiolucent objects; why barium is avoided; the role of CT. Rigid oesophagoscopy technique for sharp objects. What not to do: Foley catheter, pushing down, papain."],["sec","cl:t-fbfood~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Swallowed foreign bodies · For the examination","Common pitfalls Mistaking a button battery for a coin. Sending home a patient with a persistent foreign body sensation and a \"normal X-ray\". Using barium as the first study. Forgetting to look for an underlying stricture, eosinophilic oesophagitis or cancer in an adult with bolus impaction."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s1-1","Definitions","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Overview","1.1.1 Labyrinthine fistula Erosion of the bony labyrinth, usually the lateral semicircular canal, exposing the membranous labyrinth without infecting it. Also called circumscribed labyrinthitis . 1.1.2 Serous labyrinthitis Diffuse irritation of the labyrinth by bacterial toxins through the windows or a fistula, without bacteria in the inner ear. Reversible. 1.1.3 Suppurative labyrinthitis Bacteria and pus in the labyrinth: total, permanent loss of hearing and vestibular function. 1.1.4 Perilymph fistula A leak of perilymph through the oval or round window, after stapes surgery, barotrauma, diving, straining or trauma."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s1-2","Other forms","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Overview","Viral labyrinthitis (mumps, measles, rubella, cytomegalovirus) and syphilitic labyrinthitis."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s2-1","From the middle ear","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Pathways","Cholesteatoma eroding the lateral canal (the commonest), through the oval or round window in acute or chronic otitis media, and after surgery or trauma."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s2-2","From the meninges","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Pathways","Bacteria spread from the subarachnoid space ( Intracranial complications of otitis media ) through the cochlear aqueduct or the internal auditory canal. Streptococcus pneumoniae , Haemophilus influenzae and Neisseria meningitidis are the organisms; pneumococcus carries the highest risk of deafness, around 20%."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s2-3","Blood-borne","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Pathways","Viral and syphilitic infections."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s3-1","Causes","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Labyrinthine fistula","Cholesteatoma in most cases, then tumours, trauma and surgery. About 7 to 10% of cholesteatomas have a fistula (5 to 10% in some reviews), 90% of them in the lateral canal. Figure 1 The bony labyrinth: the lateral semicircular canal bulges into the medial wall of the attic and antrum, where cholesteatoma erodes it. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s3-2","Clinical picture","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Labyrinthine fistula","Brief vertigo provoked by pressure on the ear: pressing the tragus, cleaning the ear, or nose blowing. Hearing is often preserved."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s3-3","Fistula test","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Labyrinthine fistula","Pressure applied to the canal (tragal pressure or a Siegle speculum) moves the endolymph through the fistula and causes vertigo and nystagmus. With positive pressure the fast phase usually beats towards the tested ear; with negative pressure, away from it. The fistula test It is specific but insensitive: positive in only 30 to 50% of fistulae. A negative test does not exclude one; it may also be negative in a dead labyrinth or if cholesteatoma seals the fistula. Assume a fistula in every cholesteatoma."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s3-4","Other positive fistula tests","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Labyrinthine fistula","Ménière's disease (Hennebert's sign), superior canal dehiscence , perilymph fistula and congenital syphilis."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s3-5","Investigations","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Labyrinthine fistula","HRCT shows only about 60% of fistulae before surgery."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s3-6","Management","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Labyrinthine fistula","Surgery for the cholesteatoma; the matrix over a small fistula may be removed carefully at the end of the operation and the defect sealed with fascia or bone pâté; over a large fistula, or in an only hearing ear, the matrix is left and the ear exteriorised. Whether to remove the matrix at the first operation or leave it for a second stage is debated; in experienced hands, removal with immediate sealing preserves hearing in most patients, and steroids are often given."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s4-1","Serous labyrinthitis","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Serous and suppurative labyrinthitis","4.1.1 Clinical picture Vertigo with nausea, nystagmus towards the affected ear (an irritative lesion), and some sensorineural hearing loss that recovers if treated. 4.1.2 Treatment Bed rest with the affected ear uppermost, full-dose antibiotics, labyrinthine sedatives, and myringotomy for a bulging drum. Mastoidectomy treats a chronic source once medical treatment has begun."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s4-2","Suppurative labyrinthitis","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Serous and suppurative labyrinthitis","4.2.1 Clinical picture Severe vertigo with nausea and vomiting, nystagmus beating towards the healthy ear (the labyrinth has failed), total deafness in the ear, and a toxic patient. The vertigo settles over three to six weeks as the brain compensates. 4.2.2 Danger Infection can spread from the labyrinth to the meninges along the internal auditory canal. 4.2.3 Treatment Intravenous antibiotics, removal of the middle ear source, and rarely drainage of the labyrinth if it is feeding an intracranial infection. Serous or suppurative? Serous Suppurative Cause Toxins Bacteria Nystagmus Towards the affected ear Towards the healthy ear Hearing Partial loss, recovers Total, permanent loss Patient Unwell Toxic, may have meningitis Outcome Recovery Dead ear, ossification"],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s4-3","Labyrinthitis ossificans","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Serous and suppurative labyrinthitis","After suppurative labyrinthitis, especially meningitic, fibrous tissue and new bone fill the cochlea, starting in the basal turn near the round window, within weeks to months. Deafness after meningitis Test hearing in every patient after bacterial meningitis. Bilateral profound loss: image urgently (MRI and CT) for ossification and refer for early cochlear implantation ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ). Steroids reduce hearing loss in H. influenzae and pneumococcal meningitis."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s5-1","Causes","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Perilymph fistula","Stapes surgery, barotrauma, diving, heavy lifting, straining or coughing, and head injury. A leak in an unoperated ear usually sits around the annular ligament or the round window membrane. Spontaneous fistula is controversial."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s5-2","Clinical picture","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Perilymph fistula","Fluctuating or sudden hearing loss, roaring tinnitus, disequilibrium or vertigo, often after the precipitating event, and a positive fistula test (Hennebert's sign). A penetrating ear injury with vertigo and hearing loss suggests stapes subluxation into the vestibule."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s5-3","Management","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Perilymph fistula","Bed rest for 7 to 10 days with the head raised, no straining, and stool softeners. Explore and patch the window if hearing is worsening or symptoms persist."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s6-1","Viral","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Viral and syphilitic labyrinthitis","Mumps (usually one-sided profound loss), measles, rubella and cytomegalovirus. CMV is the commonest non-syndromic cause of congenital sensorineural hearing loss, and the loss may progress after a normal newborn screen ( The deaf child and congenital hearing loss )."],["ev","cl:t-fistula~s6-2","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis: current evidence","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Viral and syphilitic labyrinthitis","Labyrinthine fistula. High-resolution CT predicts most fistulas but misses some, so the surgeon should expect one when the patient has vertigo or a positive fistula test."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s6-2","Syphilis","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · Viral and syphilitic labyrinthitis","Congenital or acquired. Late congenital syphilis may first appear at 8 to 20 years as bilateral sudden or fluctuating loss with vertigo, and Hennebert's sign. Test with VDRL or RPR and FTA-ABS; treat with penicillin and steroids. Syphilitic otitis media is covered in Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media ."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s7-1","On call","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · For the examination","Vertigo in an infected ear Examine for nystagmus and its direction; do a gentle fistula test. Audiogram and HRCT. Intravenous antibiotics; myringotomy for acute otitis media. Plan surgery for cholesteatoma. Hearing loss with meningitis: treat urgently and image for ossification."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s7-2","Viva essentials","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Three types: circumscribed, serous, suppurative. The fistula test, its direction, and why it can be negative. Nystagmus direction in serous versus suppurative labyrinthitis. Labyrinthitis ossificans and early cochlear implantation. Perilymph fistula: causes and treatment."],["sec","cl:t-fistula~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Being reassured by a negative fistula test. Forgetting a hearing test after meningitis. Missing late congenital syphilis in a young adult with fluctuating bilateral loss."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s1-1","When","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Emergency front-of-neck access","Can't intubate, can't oxygenate (CICO): after failed intubation, failed supraglottic airway and failed face-mask oxygenation with falling saturations, in a fully paralysed patient. Also for upper airway obstruction where intubation is impossible: facial or laryngeal trauma, burns or angio-oedema, an obstructing tumour, an irretrievable foreign body, or bleeding or vomit obscuring the view. Declare the emergency out loud."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s1-2","The landmarks","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Emergency front-of-neck access","Extend the neck. Run a finger down the midline from the thyroid notch over the prominence to the soft dip just above the cricoid ring: the cricothyroid membrane. In an obese or swollen neck where it cannot be felt, make an 8 to 10 cm vertical midline incision and find it by blunt finger dissection. Ultrasound before induction marks it in a planned difficult airway."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s1-3","Scalpel cricothyroidotomy","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Emergency front-of-neck access","Stand on the patient's left (for a right-handed operator) and stabilise the larynx with the left hand (the \"laryngeal handshake\"). Make a transverse stab incision through skin and membrane with a size 10 blade, blade facing you (caudally). Rotate the blade 90 degrees so the sharp edge points towards the feet. Slide a bougie along the blade into the trachea, then pass a lubricated 6.0 cuffed tube over it (6 mm is the smallest tube that allows prolonged ventilation). Inflate the cuff, ventilate and confirm with capnography; secure the tube. The cricoid and the back wall Keep the incision above the cricoid: damaging the only complete ring causes subglottic stenosis. Do not push the blade deeply, which can perforate the back of the trachea and oesophagus."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s1-4","Needle and kit techniques","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Emergency front-of-neck access","A 14G cannula through the membrane with jet or high-flow oxygen buys time, especially in children under about 12 years, where the membrane is tiny and surgical cricothyroidotomy is avoided. It gives oxygen but not ventilation (carbon dioxide rises after 30 to 45 minutes), needs an exit route for gas to avoid barotrauma, and kinks or displaces easily. Seldinger and guarded-blade kits (Mini-Trach, Melker) take longer under stress."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s1-5","Then","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Emergency front-of-neck access","Convert to a formal tracheostomy ( Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy ) within about 24 to 72 hours and assess the larynx; cricothyroidotomy is a bridge, not a long-term airway."],["ev","cl:t-fona~s1-6","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation: current evidence","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Emergency front-of-neck access","The Difficult Airway Society guidelines make scalpel cricothyroidotomy (scalpel, bougie and 6.0 cuffed tube) the recommended rescue technique in CICO, with a vertical midline incision when the membrane cannot be felt (Frerk et al., Br J Anaesth 2015). A systematic review of 21 studies found fewer late complications after emergency cricothyroidotomy than after emergency tracheostomy, with no difference in early complications (Zasso et al., BMC Anesthesiol 2020); a systematic review of kit and surgical methods found no technique clearly superior, on low-quality evidence (Langvad et al., Scand J Trauma Resusc Emerg Med 2013)."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s1-6","Complications","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Emergency front-of-neck access","Bleeding, false passage into the pretracheal tissue, posterior tracheal or oesophageal perforation, surgical emphysema, tube displacement or blockage, subglottic stenosis, voice change."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s2-1","Where ligation fits","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Arterial ligation for bleeding","For epistaxis that continues despite packing, the first surgical step is endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation ( Epistaxis ), with anterior ethmoidal ligation if the bleeding is high or follows trauma. Embolisation is the alternative where interventional radiology is available. Maxillary and external carotid ligation are now rare, reserved for when these fail or cannot be done; external carotid ligation also controls an uncontrollable post-tonsillectomy or tumour bleed. Choosing the vessel Vessel Approach Notes Sphenopalatine Endoscopic, through a middle meatal antrostomy and mucosal flap First choice for posterior epistaxis Anterior (and posterior) ethmoidal External (Lynch-type) incision between the medial canthus and dorsum, subperiosteal behind the lacrimal sac; or endoscopic For high or traumatic bleeds; the posterior ethmoidal artery lies a few millimetres in front of the optic nerve Maxillary Transantral (Caldwell-Luc) through the posterior antral wall into the pterygopalatine fossa Useful when active bleeding hides the sphenopalatine area; failure about 10 to 15% External carotid Neck incision, ligated above the lingual or superior thyroid branch Quick, but far from the bleed: collaterals refill, rebleeding up to about 45%"],["sec","cl:t-fona~s2-2","Anterior ethmoidal artery ligation","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Arterial ligation for bleeding","Under general anaesthesia, protect the eye, and make a curved incision midway between the medial canthus and the nasal dorsum (with a small Z or W to avoid webbing). Elevate subperiosteally to the anterior lacrimal crest, lift the lacrimal sac laterally, and follow the frontoethmoidal suture back to the anterior ethmoidal artery, seen tethering the periosteum about 24 mm behind the crest. Clip it. The posterior ethmoidal artery is about 12 mm further back, and the optic nerve about 6 mm behind that (the 24-12-6 rule)."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s2-3","Maxillary artery ligation","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Arterial ligation for bleeding","Through a Caldwell-Luc antrostomy (or a gingivobuccal approach), remove the mucosa and bone of the posterior antral wall under the microscope, open the periosteum, find the maxillary artery in the fat of the pterygopalatine fossa (it lies superficial, in front of the maxillary nerve), and clip the trunk and its branches."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s2-4","External carotid artery ligation","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Arterial ligation for bleeding","Under local or general anaesthesia, make a horizontal incision at the level of the greater horn of the hyoid, raise subplatysmal flaps, retract sternomastoid, divide the common facial vein, and expose the carotid bifurcation. Identify the external carotid by its branches (the internal carotid has none in the neck) and the hypoglossal nerve crossing it; preserve the vagus. Tie the external carotid twice above the superior thyroid or lingual artery, having seen at least two branches. Never tie a vessel without branches Ligation of the internal carotid by mistake can cause a stroke. If you have not seen branches arising from the vessel, you have not identified the external carotid."],["ev","cl:t-fona~s2-5","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation: current evidence","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Arterial ligation for bleeding","A meta-analysis of 44 studies (1,664 patients) found embolisation for refractory epistaxis succeeded in 87%, but rebleeding occurred in about 16% and complications in about 14%, including stroke in about 2% and visual loss in about 2% (Hoffman et al., Interv Neuroradiol 2023)."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s2-5","Complications","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · Arterial ligation for bleeding","Ethmoidal: canthal webbing, injury to the lacrimal sac, orbital haematoma, optic nerve injury (posterior artery). Maxillary: cheek numbness, oroantral fistula, dental injury, ophthalmoplegia. External carotid: rebleeding, nerve injury (hypoglossal, vagus, superior laryngeal), mistaken internal carotid ligation, haematoma."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s3-1","Viva essentials","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for CICO recognition and the scalpel-bougie-tube technique. Landmarks of the cricothyroid membrane and what to do when it cannot be felt. Children: why needle techniques are preferred. The ligation ladder for epistaxis and why proximal ligation fails. 24-12-6 and identifying the external carotid."],["sec","cl:t-fona~s3-2","Common pitfalls","Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Delaying front-of-neck access while repeating failed intubation attempts. Cutting through the cricoid. Leaving a cricothyroidotomy as a long-term airway. Tying the external carotid without seeing its branches."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s1-1","Classification","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Overview","Granulomatous diseases of the nose Bacterial Rhinoscleroma tuberculosis, lupus vulgaris, leprosy, syphilis Fungal Aspergillosis, mucormycosis covered in Fungal sinusitis Parasitic Rhinosporidiosis leishmaniasis Inflammatory Granulomatosis with polyangiitis sarcoidosis, eosinophilic granulomatosis Neoplastic mimic NK/T-cell lymphoma the old lethal midline granuloma"],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s1-2","The rule","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Overview","Always biopsy, and always look for the systemic disease: chest, kidneys, eyes, skin and lymph nodes. Many of these conditions mimic a neoplasm, and a neoplasm can mimic them."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s1-3","Common findings","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Overview","Crusting, bloodstained discharge, nasal obstruction, granulations, septal perforation, saddle nose and, in some, palatal destruction."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s2-1","Rhinoscleroma","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Bacterial infections","2.1.1 Cause Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis (Frisch bacillus), a Gram-negative bacillus; endemic in parts of Africa, Central America, Eastern Europe, the Middle East (including Egypt) and North India. 2.1.2 Spread Starts in the nose and spreads to the nasopharynx, oropharynx, larynx (especially the subglottis), trachea and bronchi. 2.1.3 Stages Atrophic (catarrhal): like atrophic rhinitis , with foul purulent discharge and crusts. Granulomatous (proliferative): painless, non-ulcerating nodules; infiltration of the lower external nose and upper lip gives a woody feel (the Hebra nose ). Cicatricial (sclerotic): stenosis of the nostrils, distortion of the lip, adhesions in the nose and pharynx, and subglottic stenosis with stridor. Figure 1 Rhinoscleroma of the nose. Dhingra, fig. 28.1 2.1.4 Diagnosis Biopsy: submucosal plasma cells, lymphocytes and eosinophils, with Mikulicz cells (large foamy macrophages containing the bacilli) and Russell bodies (eosinophilic inclusions of immunoglobulin in plasma cells), which are diagnostic. Culture of the biopsy. Figure 2 Rhinoscleroma: foamy Mikulicz cells (arrow) among lymphocytes (arrowheads). Dhingra, fig. 28.2 2.1.5 Treatment Long antibiotic courses until two consecutive biopsy cultures are negative. Where the books differ · Antibiotics for rhinoscleroma Dhingra Streptomycin 1 g/day with tetracycline 2 g/day for at least four to six weeks, repeated if needed; steroids to reduce fibrosis. Lee Fluoroquinolones such as ciprofloxacin, or tetracyclines, for months. In practice A long course of a fluoroquinolone or a tetracycline, guided by c"],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s2-2","Tuberculosis","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Bacterial infections","Usually secondary to pulmonary disease. Nodular infiltration, then ulceration of the anterior septum and anterior end of the inferior turbinate, with perforation of the cartilaginous septum. Biopsy with acid-fast stains and culture; antituberculous drugs."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s2-3","Lupus vulgaris","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Bacterial infections","A low-grade tuberculous infection of the vestibule and the skin of the nose and face, with brownish apple-jelly nodules and chronic vestibulitis; may perforate the cartilaginous septum. Biopsy; antituberculous treatment."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s2-4","Leprosy","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Bacterial infections","Mycobacterium leprae , mainly in the lepromatous form. The anterior septum and inferior turbinate become red and swollen with excess discharge, then crusting, bleeding and nodules that ulcerate and perforate. Late sequelae: atrophic rhinitis, a depressed bridge and loss of the anterior nasal spine with a retracted columella. Diagnosis from scrapings and biopsy (lepra bacilli in foamy lepra cells ); multidrug therapy with dapsone and rifampicin; reconstruction when inactive. Figure 3 Leprosy of the nose. Dhingra, fig. 28.3"],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s2-5","Syphilis","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Bacterial infections","2.5.1 Acquired Primary: a rare chancre of the vestibule. Secondary: rhinitis with crusting and fissuring, with mucous patches, rash and lymphadenopathy. Tertiary: the stage that involves the nose: a gumma destroys the septum, both bony and cartilaginous, with sequestra, foul crusts, palatal perforation and a saddle nose. 2.5.2 Congenital Early: snuffles in the first three months, becoming purulent, with excoriation of the vestibule and lip. Late: around puberty, with gummatous destruction like tertiary disease, plus corneal opacities, deafness and Hutchinson's teeth. 2.5.3 Diagnosis and treatment Serology (VDRL with treponemal tests) and biopsy. Benzathine penicillin 2.4 million units weekly for three weeks; alkaline douches; removal of sequestra; reconstruction once inactive. Which part of the septum? Tuberculosis, lupus and leprosy perforate the cartilaginous septum; syphilis destroys the bony septum."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s3-1","Rhinosporidiosis","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Parasitic infections","3.1.1 Cause and spread Rhinosporidium seeberi , once thought a fungus, now classed as an aquatic protistan parasite. Acquired from ponds shared with animals; common in South India and Sri Lanka. 3.1.2 Clinical picture A leafy, polypoidal, pink to purple, strawberry-like mass on the septum or lateral wall, very vascular and bleeding easily, its surface studded with white dots (the sporangia). Bloodstained discharge or epistaxis may be the only complaint; it may hang into the nasopharynx. Figure 4 Rhinosporidiosis: a polypoidal mass protruding from the nostril, and its appearance inside the nose. Dhingra, fig. 28.6 3.1.3 Life cycle Endospores grow into trophocytes, which mature into sporangia 200 to 250 µm across containing thousands of endospores; the sporangium bursts through a pore and releases them. Figure 5 First, histology with sporangia packed with spores. Second, the life cycle of the organism. Dhingra, figs 28.7 and 28.8 3.1.4 Diagnosis and treatment Biopsy shows the sporangia; the organism cannot be cultured. Complete excision with a diathermy knife and cautery of the base; recurrence is common; dapsone helps some patients."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s3-2","Leishmaniasis","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Parasitic infections","Transmitted by the sandfly ( Phlebotomus ). Cutaneous lesions on exposed skin progress from papules to crusted ulcers; mucosal disease appears on the septum as a nodule or plaque that ulcerates and perforates, sometimes years after a skin lesion, and can spread to the palate, pharynx and larynx. Biopsy, culture and PCR; mucosal disease needs parenteral antimonials (sodium stibogluconate or meglumine antimoniate)."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s4-1","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Inflammatory diseases","4.1.1 What it is Formerly Wegener's granulomatosis: a necrotising granulomatous vasculitis of small vessels affecting the upper airway, lungs and kidneys (the classic triad), with skin, eye and ear involvement. 4.1.2 Nose and sinuses A persistent cold or sinusitis, bloodstained then purulent discharge, crusting, granulations, septal perforation and saddle nose; destruction may extend to the orbit, palate and middle ear. Subglottic stenosis develops in some. 4.1.3 Systemic features Fatigue, night sweats, arthralgia, anaemia; cough and haemoptysis with cavitating lung nodules; glomerulonephritis with blood, casts and protein in the urine and a rising creatinine, the usual cause of death untreated. 4.1.4 Diagnosis c-ANCA (anti-proteinase 3) is positive in most active systemic disease; raised ESR and CRP; urinalysis and creatinine; chest imaging. Nasal biopsy shows necrosis, granulomas and vasculitis, though often non-specific. 4.1.5 Treatment Steroids with cyclophosphamide or rituximab to induce remission, then rituximab, azathioprine or methotrexate to maintain it (rituximab gives fewer relapses than azathioprine), with the rheumatology and renal teams. Nasal douching for crusts; reconstruction only when quiescent."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s4-2","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Inflammatory diseases","Churg-Strauss syndrome: asthma, nasal polyps, peripheral eosinophilia and vasculitis, often p-ANCA positive."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s4-3","Sarcoidosis","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Inflammatory diseases","A systemic non-caseating granulomatous disease. In the nose: submucosal nodules on the septum and inferior turbinate with obstruction, crusting, pain and bleeding; nodules on the vestibule and facial skin ( lupus pernio ). Chest imaging shows hilar adenopathy; serum calcium and serum ACE are raised in active disease. Topical and systemic steroids."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s5-1","What it is","Granulomatous disease of the nose · NK/T-cell lymphoma","Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, associated with Epstein-Barr virus; the condition once called lethal midline granuloma or polymorphic reticulosis. Commoner in East Asia and Latin America."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s5-2","Clinical picture","Granulomatous disease of the nose · NK/T-cell lymphoma","Progressive, relentless destruction of the midline: nasal obstruction and discharge, ulceration and necrosis of the septum, palate and facial skin, with fever and weight loss. Figure 6 NK-cell lymphoma with midline destruction in a young man. Dhingra, fig. 28.9"],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s5-3","Diagnosis","Granulomatous disease of the nose · NK/T-cell lymphoma","Deep, repeated biopsies of viable tissue, with immunohistochemistry (CD56, cytotoxic markers) and EBV studies; superficial biopsies show only necrosis and inflammation."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s5-4","Treatment","Granulomatous disease of the nose · NK/T-cell lymphoma","Radiotherapy with chemotherapy. It must be told apart from granulomatosis with polyangiitis, because the treatments are completely different."],["ev","cl:t-gpa~c6","Granulomatous disease of the nose: current evidence","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Assessment","Classification. The ACR/EULAR 2022 criteria for granulomatosis with polyangiitis give heavy weight to nasal involvement (bloody discharge, crusting, ulceration, septal perforation) and to c-ANCA or PR3-ANCA (Robson et al., Ann Rheum Dis 2022). Induction. Rituximab was as effective as cyclophosphamide for inducing remission and better for relapsing disease (Stone et al., NEJM 2010, the RAVE trial). In PEXIVAS, plasma exchange did not reduce death or end-stage kidney disease, and a reduced-dose steroid regimen was as effective as the standard one with fewer infections (Walsh et al., NEJM 2020). Avacopan, a complement C5a receptor blocker, allowed steroids to be tapered and gave better sustained remission at one year (Jayne et al., NEJM 2021, ADVOCATE). Maintenance. Rituximab maintenance gave fewer relapses than azathioprine (Guillevin et al., NEJM 2014, MAINRITSAN). NK/T-cell lymphoma. Asparaginase-based chemotherapy combined with radiotherapy is now standard for localised nasal disease, and plasma EBV DNA is used to monitor response."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~c6","Assessment","Granulomatous disease of the nose · Assessment","The work-up of a destructive nose. Investigations by suspicion Suspected disease Key test Granulomatosis with polyangiitis c-ANCA, urinalysis, creatinine, chest imaging, biopsy Sarcoidosis Serum ACE, calcium, chest imaging Tuberculosis Acid-fast stains, culture, PCR, chest imaging Syphilis VDRL with treponemal tests Rhinoscleroma Biopsy for Mikulicz cells and Russell bodies, culture Lymphoma Deep biopsy with immunohistochemistry and EBV studies Cocaine damage History and urine toxicology Cocaine Cocaine-induced midline destruction can mimic vasculitis, and some patients develop ANCA from levamisole in the drug. Ask, and test."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s7-1","Viva essentials","Granulomatous disease of the nose · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Classification of granulomatous disease of the nose. Rhinoscleroma: organism, stages, Mikulicz cells and Russell bodies, treatment. Rhinosporidiosis: organism, appearance, sporangia, treatment. Syphilis of the nose: stages, snuffles, saddle nose, bony septal perforation. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis: triad, c-ANCA, treatment. NK/T-cell lymphoma versus vasculitis."],["sec","cl:t-gpa~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Granulomatous disease of the nose · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a destructive nose with antibiotics for months without a biopsy. Superficial biopsies of necrotic tissue in suspected lymphoma. Forgetting the kidneys in granulomatosis with polyangiitis."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s1-1","Definition","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Overview","HHT, also called Osler-Weber-Rendu syndrome , is an autosomal dominant disorder of angiogenesis. It produces fragile telangiectasias in the skin and mucous membranes and larger arteriovenous malformations (AVMs) in internal organs."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s1-2","Epidemiology","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Overview","About 1 in 5,000 to 10,000 people, reported mainly in white populations, affecting men and women equally. Penetrance increases with age."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s1-3","Why it matters","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Overview","Recurrent epistaxis causes chronic iron-deficiency anaemia and repeated transfusions, and treating it badly (repeated packing and aggressive cautery) makes it worse. Pulmonary AVMs cause paradoxical embolism, stroke and brain abscess; cerebral AVMs can bleed."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s2-1","Genetics","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Pathogenesis","Mutations disturb transforming growth factor-beta signalling in endothelial cells, so the connective tissue and matrix that should support new vessels during angiogenesis fail to form. HHT type 1: mutation in endoglin (chromosome 9q34); pulmonary and cerebral AVMs are commoner. HHT type 2: mutation in ACVRL1 , also called ALK1 (chromosome 12q); hepatic AVMs are commoner. Juvenile polyposis–HHT overlap: mutation in SMAD4 ; these patients need colonoscopy because of colonic polyps and cancer risk. Together, the three genes explain about 90% of clinically diagnosed cases, and genetic testing is mainly used to screen relatives."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s2-2","The lesion","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Pathogenesis","Postcapillary venules dilate and connect directly to dilated arterioles, losing the capillary bed between them. The walls lack elastic tissue and smooth muscle, so they cannot contract after injury: minor trauma from dry air or a sneeze causes brisk bleeding that does not stop easily."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s3-1","Epistaxis","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Clinical features","The commonest and earliest feature: recurrent, spontaneous bleeding from childhood, from both sides, becoming more frequent with age. About 80 to 90% have nosebleeds by age 21."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s3-2","Mucocutaneous telangiectasias","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Clinical features","Small red spots that blanch with pressure, on the lips, tongue, palate, fingertips, face and nasal mucosa. About 80 to 90% develop them by their thirties or forties. Figure 1 A patient with recurrent epistaxis from HHT, with telangiectasias on the nasal septum, tongue, palate and fingertips (arrows). Dhingra, fig. 33.3"],["sec","cl:t-hht~s3-3","Visceral arteriovenous malformations","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Clinical features","Organ involvement in HHT Organ Frequency What it causes Lungs About 30% Hypoxia, paradoxical emboli, stroke, brain abscess, haemoptysis Brain 5–15% Haemorrhage, seizures Liver 40–70% Usually silent; high-output heart failure, portal hypertension Gut 20–25% Bleeding and anaemia Spine Rare Spinal haemorrhage"],["sec","cl:t-hht~s3-4","Anaemia","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Clinical features","Chronic iron-deficiency anaemia from nasal and gut bleeding."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s4-1","Criteria","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Diagnosis","Epistaxis: spontaneous and recurrent. Telangiectasias: multiple, at characteristic sites (lips, mouth, fingers, nose). Visceral lesions: gastrointestinal telangiectasia, or pulmonary, hepatic, cerebral or spinal AVMs. Family history: a first-degree relative with HHT."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s4-2","Interpretation","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Diagnosis","Three or more criteria: definite . Two: possible or suspected. Fewer than two: unlikely. Genetic testing confirms the diagnosis in uncertain cases and in relatives."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s4-3","Work-up","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Diagnosis","Full head and neck examination with nasal endoscopy. Full blood count and iron studies. Contrast echocardiography (bubble study) to screen for pulmonary AVMs, in every patient. MRI brain with contrast for cerebral AVMs. Endoscopy for symptomatic gastrointestinal bleeding; Doppler ultrasound or CT for the liver. Genetic counselling. Screen the lungs A pulmonary AVM can cause a stroke or brain abscess in a patient who has only ever complained about nosebleeds. Screen every patient with contrast echocardiography and treat significant AVMs by embolisation."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s4-4","Differential diagnosis","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Diagnosis","Bleeding disorders (von Willebrand disease, platelet disorders), anticoagulant use, and a bleeding nasal tumour in one-sided bleeding."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s5-1","Principles","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Management of the nose","The goal is fewer and smaller bleeds and a better haemoglobin, not cure. Avoid trauma to the mucosa: repeated packing and aggressive cautery destroy healthy mucosa, encourage new telangiectasias and can cause septal perforation."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s5-2","The acute bleed","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Management of the nose","Bleeding is almost always from the anterior third, so posterior packs are rarely needed. Gentle pressure; if packing is needed, resorbable packs such as Nasopore or Surgicel, or a haemostatic matrix, because removing a conventional pack, or an expanding sponge, tears other lesions and restarts bleeding. Give antibiotic cover for any pack, and avoid cautery of large areas. If packing fails, embolise the facial and maxillary feeders without coils."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s5-3","Conservative and medical treatment","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Management of the nose","1 Humidification and emollients Saline sprays, ointments and humidified air keep the mucosa moist. 2 Iron and transfusion Correct anaemia; intravenous iron when oral iron fails. 3 Tranexamic acid Oral antifibrinolytic reduces the frequency and severity of bleeds. 4 Hormonal treatment Oestrogen or tamoxifen convert the mucosa to a thicker squamous type. 5 Systemic anti-angiogenic drugs Intravenous bevacizumab or oral pomalidomide reduce bleeding and transfusion needs in severe disease, and are now used by specialist HHT centres; topical bevacizumab spray does not work."],["ev","cl:t-hht~s5-4","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia: current evidence","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Management of the nose","Guidelines. The Second International HHT Guidelines recommend moisturising topical therapies, oral tranexamic acid for epistaxis not controlled by moisturisers, laser or radiofrequency ablation, septodermoplasty or nasal closure for refractory bleeding, and systemic anti-angiogenic treatment (intravenous bevacizumab) for severe disease; they advise screening every patient for pulmonary and cerebral arteriovenous malformations (Faughnan et al., Ann Intern Med 2020). Topical drugs. In the NOSE trial, topical bevacizumab, oestriol and tranexamic acid sprays were no better than placebo (Whitehead et al., JAMA 2016). Pomalidomide. In the PATH-HHT trial of 144 patients, oral pomalidomide reduced epistaxis severity scores significantly more than placebo over 24 weeks (Al-Samkari et al., NEJM 2024)."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s5-4","Procedures","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · Management of the nose","Treatment is stepped up by severity, judged by bleeding frequency, the haemoglobin and transfusion needs; the Epistaxis Severity Score helps track it. Benefit lasts months after laser, years after septodermoplasty, and permanently after nasal closure. 5.4.1 Photocoagulation KTP, argon, Nd:YAG or diode laser, bipolar diathermy or coblation, aimed at individual lesions and repeated every few months as new ones appear. The first intervention is usually an examination under anaesthesia with KTP or argon laser to stage and treat the disease: these green wavelengths are absorbed by haemoglobin, each lesion is circled and then ablated, tranexamic acid and antibiotic cover are given at induction, and no pack is needed. Bipolar diathermy works better than laser in a bloody field. If laser controls bleeding for less than about three months, move to septodermoplasty or systemic treatment. 5.4.2 Septodermoplasty The bleeding anterior septal mucosa is replaced with a split- or full-thickness skin graft, which is tougher. It halves the number of later laser treatments. 5.4.3 Young's procedure Closure of the nostrils, one or both sides, by suturing flaps inside the vestibule. Bleeding stops in most patients, often permanently, at the price of nasal breathing and smell; it is offered to transfusion-dependent patients who accept that. The Lund modification uses three vestibular flaps. Rebleeding after reopening is troublesome. 5.4.4 Embolisation Gives only temporary benefit because new collaterals form; reserved for emergencies (see the acute bleed, above). Pearls Repeated cautery makes HHT"],["sec","cl:t-hht~s6-1","Viva essentials","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Inheritance and genes: autosomal dominant; endoglin (type 1) and ACVRL1 or ALK1 (type 2). Curaçao criteria and how many make the diagnosis definite. Screening for pulmonary and cerebral AVMs. The treatment ladder, septodermoplasty and Young's procedure."],["sec","cl:t-hht~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating every bleed as simple epistaxis without asking about family history and looking at the lips and fingers. Repeated packing and wide cautery. Forgetting to screen the lungs."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s1-1","HIV","HIV and ENT · The virus and the disease","HIV is an enveloped, single-stranded RNA retrovirus of the lentivirus genus. HIV-1 causes most infections worldwide and is the more pathogenic; HIV-2 is mainly West African and less virulent. The virion is a lipid bilayer carrying the envelope glycoproteins gp120 , which binds CD4, and gp41 , which mediates fusion. Inside lie a matrix (p17), a conical capsid ( p24 ) and two copies of RNA, together with the three enzymes reverse transcriptase , integrase and protease . Each enzyme is a drug target. Figure 1 Structure of the HIV-1 virion. Dhingra, fig. 75.1"],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s1-2","Life cycle","HIV and ENT · The virus and the disease","gp120 binds CD4 and a co-receptor (CCR5 or CXCR4) on helper T cells, macrophages and dendritic cells. gp41 fuses the envelope with the cell membrane. Reverse transcriptase copies the RNA into DNA. Integrase inserts it into the host genome as a provirus . The provirus directs new viral RNA and polyproteins, which protease cleaves into functional proteins. New virions bud off. The progressive loss of CD4 helper T cells is what destroys cell-mediated immunity, and it explains the pattern of disease that follows."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s1-3","Transmission","HIV and ENT · The virus and the disease","HIV spreads by sexual contact, through blood and blood products, by shared needles, and from mother to child during pregnancy, delivery or breastfeeding. The infectious fluids are blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk, together with the deep body fluids: CSF and pleural, peritoneal, pericardial and amniotic fluid. Saliva, tears, sweat, urine and faeces carry a negligible risk unless they are visibly bloody."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s1-4","Course","HIV and ENT · The virus and the disease","Primary (acute) infection appears two to four weeks after exposure as a glandular-fever-like illness, with fever, pharyngitis, cervical nodes, a rash, mouth and genital ulcers, and sometimes meningoencephalitis. Viral load is very high at this stage and the patient is highly infectious. A clinical latency of about ten years on average follows. The patient is well, sometimes with persistent generalised lymphadenopathy , but the virus keeps replicating in lymphoid tissue, including the tonsils and adenoids, while CD4 counts fall. AIDS is defined by a CD4 count below 200 cells/mm³ (normal 500 to 1500) or by an AIDS-defining illness. These include Kaposi sarcoma, Pneumocystis pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis, extrapulmonary tuberculosis, cryptococcosis, cerebral toxoplasmosis and certain lymphomas."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s1-5","Diagnosis","HIV and ENT · The virus and the disease","The first-line test is a fourth-generation combined antigen and antibody immunoassay , which detects p24 antigen and antibodies and becomes positive at about two to six weeks. A positive result is confirmed with a second, differentiating assay; the Western blot has largely been replaced. HIV RNA by PCR (the viral load) diagnoses acute infection and monitors treatment, and the CD4 count measures immune damage and predicts opportunistic disease. Patients are staged clinically by the WHO stages 1 to 4, or by the CDC stages based on the CD4 count."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s1-6","Treatment","HIV and ENT · The virus and the disease","Antiretroviral therapy (ART) , formerly called HAART, combines usually three drugs, often in a single daily tablet. It is started at diagnosis, whatever the CD4 count , because immediate treatment halves serious illness and death (the START and TEMPRANO trials). A person with an undetectable viral load does not transmit HIV sexually: undetectable equals untransmittable . For prevention, pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) with tenofovir and emtricitabine protects people at high risk, and post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) is described below. Classes of antiretroviral drugs Class Action Examples Nucleoside and nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTI) Chain termination of viral DNA Tenofovir, emtricitabine, lamivudine, abacavir, zidovudine Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTI) Bind and block reverse transcriptase Efavirenz, nevirapine, rilpivirine Integrase inhibitors Block integration into the host genome Dolutegravir, bictegravir, raltegravir Protease inhibitors Block cleavage of polyproteins Darunavir, atazanavir (with ritonavir boosting) Entry and fusion inhibitors Block CCR5 or gp41 Maraviroc, enfuvirtide"],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s2-1","Categories of disease","HIV and ENT · ENT manifestations","Four kinds of disease account for almost everything the ENT surgeon sees. Infections , both opportunistic and ordinary, become more severe and more often complicated as the CD4 count falls; they may be bacterial, viral, fungal, mycobacterial or protozoal. Malignancies are either AIDS-defining , namely Kaposi sarcoma and non-Hodgkin lymphoma, or non-AIDS-defining , such as Hodgkin lymphoma, skin cancers and HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer. Neurological disease includes HIV encephalopathy, neuropathies and cranial nerve palsies, especially of the facial nerve. Finally there are inflammatory and lymphoproliferative conditions , chiefly lymphoepithelial cysts and diffuse infiltrative lymphocytosis."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s2-2","Ear","HIV and ENT · ENT manifestations","In the external ear , seborrhoeic dermatitis and otitis externa are common, including necrotising (malignant) otitis externa ( Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis ) from Pseudomonas or Aspergillus , and Kaposi sarcoma may involve the pinna and canal. In the middle ear , serous otitis media follows adenoid hyperplasia or a nasopharyngeal mass. A unilateral effusion in an adult with HIV therefore needs a nasopharyngeal biopsy to exclude lymphoma or Kaposi sarcoma ( Nasopharyngeal carcinoma ). Recurrent and chronic otitis media are frequent, especially in children, and Pneumocystis or mycobacterial otitis occurs. The inner ear and nerves suffer sensorineural hearing loss from HIV itself, from opportunistic infections (CMV, syphilis, cryptococcal meningitis) and from ototoxic drugs. Herpes zoster oticus ( Ramsay Hunt syndrome ) and facial palsy ( Facial nerve palsy ) are commoner than in the general population."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s2-3","Nose and sinuses","HIV and ENT · ENT manifestations","Rhinosinusitis is common. While the CD4 count is above 200 the usual organisms are found, but at low counts infections become atypical and complicated, with Pseudomonas , CMV and invasive fungal sinusitis ( Fungal sinusitis ) from Aspergillus or mucor. Herpes simplex can ulcerate the nose, and the tumours to remember are Kaposi sarcoma and B-cell, Burkitt and plasmablastic lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s2-4","Mouth and pharynx","HIV and ENT · ENT manifestations","Oral candidiasis is the commonest oral lesion ( Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis ). It takes four forms: pseudomembranous thrush, with white plaques that wipe off; erythematous (atrophic) candidiasis; angular cheilitis ; and hyperplastic candidiasis, whose plaques do not wipe off. Topical nystatin or clotrimazole suffices when the CD4 count is above 200; systemic fluconazole is used when it is lower or when odynophagia suggests oesophageal disease. Resistance develops with repeated courses. Oral hairy leukoplakia produces white, vertically corrugated, hair-like plaques on the lateral border of the tongue that cannot be wiped off. It is caused by EBV , is asymptomatic and is not premalignant, but it is a strong marker of HIV that predicts progression to AIDS and should prompt testing. Biopsy it if in doubt, because the differential includes leukoplakia, candida, lichen planus and carcinoma. Periodontal disease ranges from linear gingival erythema to necrotising ulcerative gingivitis and periodontitis , which is painful, foul and bleeding, with loss of the interdental papillae and bone. It is treated with chlorhexidine, debridement, metronidazole and dental care. Ulcers need particular care. Giant aphthous ulcers are large, deep and painful; biopsy them to exclude herpes, CMV, tuberculosis, histoplasmosis and lymphoma before giving topical or intralesional steroids or thalidomide. Chronic herpes simplex ulcers lasting more than a month are AIDS-defining, as is oral histoplasmosis with its granulomatous ulcers. In adults, adenotonsillar hypertrophy causes nasa"],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s2-5","Larynx","HIV and ENT · ENT manifestations","The larynx may be infected by candida, herpes, CMV or tuberculosis, and epiglottitis occurs. Kaposi sarcoma and lymphoma here can obstruct the airway."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s2-6","Salivary glands","HIV and ENT · ENT manifestations","Benign lymphoepithelial cysts (BLEC) make up about three-quarters of HIV parotid lesions. They present as painless, soft, often bilateral parotid swellings (up to 80% are bilateral on imaging) with cervical lymphadenopathy, and they may be the presenting sign of HIV, especially in children. They are thought to arise from intraparotid nodes ( Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings ). Ultrasound or CT shows multiple thin-walled cysts, and FNA confirms the diagnosis. The cysts regress on ART; serial aspiration, doxycycline or alcohol sclerotherapy and low-dose radiotherapy are alternatives, and parotidectomy is reserved for disfiguring lesions or suspected malignancy. Diffuse infiltrative lymphocytosis syndrome (DILS) resembles Sjögren's syndrome, with bilateral parotid enlargement and sicca symptoms, but the infiltrate is of CD8 lymphocytes, Sjögren antibodies are negative, and lymphocytic interstitial pneumonitis often coexists. It is treated with ART and symptomatic measures. Xerostomia (often drug-related), parotitis and lymphoma complete the salivary picture."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s2-7","Neck","HIV and ENT · ENT manifestations","Most patients have cervical lymphadenopathy , and persistent generalised lymphadenopathy is a reactive follicular hyperplasia. Nodes that are asymmetric, rapidly growing, painful or matted, or that come with B symptoms, need FNA or excision. The causes then include tuberculosis (the commonest serious cause in endemic areas; Approach to a lump in the neck ), atypical mycobacteria, histoplasmosis, toxoplasmosis, bacillary angiomatosis, Kaposi sarcoma and lymphoma . Deep neck abscesses are also more frequent."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s3-1","Kaposi sarcoma","HIV and ENT · HIV-associated tumours","Kaposi sarcoma is an angioproliferative tumour caused by human herpesvirus 8 (KSHV). Four variants are described: the classic form in elderly men of Mediterranean or Eastern European origin, the endemic African form, the transplant-related form, and the AIDS-associated form, which is the commonest and most aggressive. The head and neck are involved in most AIDS cases: the skin of the face, nose and ears; the oral cavity, especially the hard palate , gingiva and tongue; and the pharynx, larynx and nodes. The lesions are violaceous macules, plaques and nodules that do not blanch, and oral lesions may ulcerate, bleed, loosen teeth and obstruct. Biopsy shows spindle cells, slit-like vascular channels, extravasated red cells and haemosiderin; the differential includes haemangioma, pyogenic granuloma and bacillary angiomatosis. Stage the disease by looking for visceral spread to the gut and lungs and by checking the CD4 count and viral load. The foundation of treatment is ART , under which many lesions regress. Local lesions respond to radiotherapy, to which they are very sensitive, or to intralesional vinblastine, cryotherapy or laser. Extensive disease is treated with liposomal doxorubicin or paclitaxel, and surgery is used only to palliate or to secure the airway."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s3-2","Lymphoma","HIV and ENT · HIV-associated tumours","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is the second commonest malignancy in HIV. It is mostly of aggressive B-cell types: diffuse large B-cell, Burkitt and plasmablastic , the last often in the oral cavity. It is frequently EBV-driven, extranodal and advanced at presentation with B symptoms. In the head and neck it affects Waldeyer's ring, the sinonasal tract, oral cavity, orbit, salivary glands and nodes, and primary CNS lymphoma occurs when the CD4 count is very low. Diagnosis needs an excision or core biopsy, because FNA is usually not enough to classify it. Treatment is chemotherapy (R-CHOP, R-EPOCH) together with ART. Hodgkin lymphoma is the commonest non-AIDS-defining cancer in HIV. It presents at a more advanced stage, usually as the mixed cellularity or lymphocyte-depleted types."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s3-3","Other cancers","HIV and ENT · HIV-associated tumours","Basal and squamous cell carcinomas and melanoma of the skin ( Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck ) are commoner and more aggressive, and HPV-related oropharyngeal, anal and cervical carcinomas are more frequent. The practical rule is simple: keep a low threshold to biopsy any lesion that does not heal."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s4-1","Risk of occupational infection","HIV and ENT · Protecting the team","After a percutaneous injury from an HIV-positive source the risk of transmission is about 0.3%, or 1 in 300; after mucous membrane exposure it is about 0.09%. The risk is higher with a deep injury, a hollow-bore needle that has been in a vessel, visible blood on the device, and a source with a high viral load."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s4-2","Universal (standard) precautions","HIV and ENT · Protecting the team","Every patient's blood and body fluids are treated as potentially infectious. Wash hands before and after patient contact, wear gloves, and double-glove in surgery. An impermeable gown, mask and eye protection are needed wherever splashes are likely, as in tracheostomy, endoscopy, drilling, laser and oral surgery. Sharps are passed in a dish through a \"hands-free\" zone, needles are never recapped, and sharps go straight into rigid containers; blunt suture needles are used where possible. Soiled linen and specimens are handled as infectious, and staff with weeping dermatitis should avoid direct patient care."],["ev","cl:t-hivent~s4-3","HIV and ENT: current evidence","HIV and ENT · Protecting the team","Post-exposure prophylaxis. UK (BHIVA) and WHO guidance recommend a three-drug regimen for 28 days, started as soon as possible and within 72 hours, for significant exposures to a source with HIV who is not known to have an undetectable viral load; PEP is not recommended when the source has a sustained undetectable viral load, because transmission does not occur ( undetectable equals untransmittable ). Treatment changes the ENT picture. Effective antiretroviral therapy has made oral candidiasis, hairy leukoplakia, Kaposi sarcoma and parotid lymphoepithelial cysts much less common; persistent or new lesions should prompt a check of adherence and viral load. HPV. People with HIV have higher rates of HPV-related oropharyngeal and anal cancer, and HPV vaccination is recommended for them."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s4-3","After an exposure","HIV and ENT · Protecting the team","Wash the wound with soap and water and let it bleed. Do not scrub or squeeze. Irrigate mucous membranes and eyes with water. Report the injury at once and assess the risk. Test the source patient (with consent) for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C. Take baseline blood from the injured worker. Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) : start as soon as possible, ideally within hours and no later than 72 hours. Take three drugs for 28 days, typically tenofovir with emtricitabine (or lamivudine) plus dolutegravir or raltegravir. Follow-up HIV testing at about 6 weeks and 3 to 4 months. Protect sexual partners and avoid blood donation meanwhile. PEP regimens have changed Older texts give two NRTIs (such as zidovudine and lamivudine) for low-risk exposures and add a third drug for high-risk ones. Current guidance uses a three-drug regimen built around an integrase inhibitor for every exposure that warrants PEP, because it is better tolerated and more often completed. Diagnosis has moved from ELISA and Western blot to fourth-generation antigen and antibody assays."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s5-1","Viva essentials","HIV and ENT · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The virion and its life cycle: where the drugs act. CD4 count and AIDS; the stages of infection. ENT clues to HIV: oral hairy leukoplakia, persistent candida, necrotising gingivitis, bilateral parotid lymphoepithelial cysts, generalised lymphadenopathy, Kaposi sarcoma, and a unilateral effusion with adenoid hyperplasia. Kaposi sarcoma: cause, appearance, treatment. Universal precautions and PEP."],["sec","cl:t-hivent~s5-2","Common pitfalls","HIV and ENT · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a unilateral adult middle ear effusion with grommets without examining and biopsying the nasopharynx. Excising bilateral parotid cysts that would regress on ART. Relying on FNA alone to diagnose lymphoma. Delaying PEP beyond the first hours after a needlestick."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s1-1","Why a method","Approach to hearing loss · Overview","The causes of hearing loss run into hundreds. A structured approach, starting with tuning forks and an audiogram, narrows them quickly and catches the few that are urgent or dangerous."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s1-2","The three questions","Approach to hearing loss · Overview","Three questions Type Conductive or sensorineural? or mixed, or non-organic Side One ear or both? asymmetry needs imaging Speed Sudden, progressive or fluctuating? sudden loss is an emergency"],["sec","cl:t-hl~s2-1","By site","Approach to hearing loss · Classification","2.1.1 Conductive Any lesion from the canal to the stapediovestibular joint that stops sound reaching the cochlea. 2.1.2 Sensorineural A lesion of the cochlea ( sensory ), the cochlear nerve ( neural ), or the central pathways. 2.1.3 Mixed Both together, as in otosclerosis with cochlear involvement or chronic otitis media with labyrinthine damage. 2.1.4 Non-organic Claimed loss without an organic basis, deliberate or unconscious."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s2-2","By degree","Approach to hearing loss · Classification","WHO grades of hearing loss (2021) Grade Better-ear average at 500, 1000, 2000 and 4000 Hz Normal Below 20 dB Mild 20 to under 35 dB Moderate 35 to under 50 dB Moderately severe 50 to under 65 dB Severe 65 to under 80 dB Profound 80 to under 95 dB Complete or total 95 dB or more Where the books differ · Grading systems Dhingra Uses the older WHO scale averaged over 500, 1000 and 2000 Hz, with 0 to 25 dB counted as normal and 91 dB or more as profound. WHO 2021 The World Report on Hearing lowered the threshold for normal hearing to under 20 dB, added 4000 Hz to the average and added a complete hearing loss grade, because even mild losses affect communication. Many British services use the British Society of Audiology bands, in which normal hearing is up to 20 dB. In practice grade with the 2021 WHO table above, and know the older scale because older texts and exam questions still use it. Figure 1 Classification of hearing loss by threshold. Most people with normal hearing have thresholds between −10 and +10 dB. Dhingra, fig. 5.8"],["sec","cl:t-hl~s3-1","Causes","Approach to hearing loss · Conductive loss","Figure 2 The drum, ossicular chain and intratympanic muscles: every conductive loss interrupts this chain somewhere. From the anatomy atlas 3.1.1 Congenital Canal atresia , fixation of the stapes or malleus head, ossicular discontinuity, and congenital cholesteatoma. 3.1.2 Acquired Canal: wax, foreign body, otitis externa , stenosis, tumours ( External ear tumours and bony growths ). Drum: perforation, scarring. Middle ear: fluid, blood, pus, mass, cholesteatoma, tympanosclerosis. Ossicles: disruption (trauma, erosion) or fixation (otosclerosis, tympanosclerosis). Eustachian tube failure."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s3-2","Features","Approach to hearing loss · Conductive loss","Low tones are affected more; speech discrimination is good; the patient may hear better in noise; voice is soft. A conductive loss alone never exceeds about 60 dB. How big is the gap? Complete canal block: about 30 dB. Perforation: 10 to 40 dB, more for larger and posterior holes. Malleus fixation: 10 to 25 dB. Ossicular discontinuity behind an intact drum: about 54 dB; with a perforation, about 38 dB, because an intact drum blocks sound reaching the round window. Closed oval window: about 60 dB. Figure 3 Audiogram of a right ear with a conductive loss and an air-bone gap, with the standard audiogram symbols. Dhingra, fig. 5.1"],["sec","cl:t-hl~s3-3","Conductive loss behind an intact drum","Approach to hearing loss · Conductive loss","When the drum looks normal, the list is otosclerosis (the commonest, with a family history, onset in early adult life, worsening in pregnancy, a Carhart notch at 2 kHz and absent stapedial reflexes), ossicular discontinuity after trauma, malleus or incus fixation , tympanosclerosis , congenital cholesteatoma (a white mass behind an intact drum in a child), a small effusion , and congenital ossicular anomalies . A third-window lesion such as superior canal dehiscence produces an air–bone gap at low frequencies that is not truly conductive: bone conduction is better than normal, and the stapedial reflexes are present. Present reflexes with an apparent conductive loss should therefore stop a surgeon from operating on the stapes until a CT has excluded dehiscence."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s3-4","Management","Approach to hearing loss · Conductive loss","Clear the canal, drain fluid, remove a mass, repair the drum and chain (tympanoplasty; Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles ), stapes surgery, or a hearing aid; bone-conduction devices suit patients who cannot wear an air-conduction aid, such as those with chronic discharge or canal atresia."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s4-1","Causes","Approach to hearing loss · Sensorineural loss","Causes of sensorineural loss Congenital Genetic, syndromic intrauterine infection, prematurity, kernicterus Infection Viral, meningitis labyrinthitis, syphilis Trauma Head injury, surgery noise, barotrauma Toxic Ototoxic drugs Degenerative Presbycusis Inner ear Ménière's disease sudden hearing loss, autoimmune disease Neural Vestibular schwannoma multiple sclerosis, CP angle tumours Systemic Diabetes, thyroid, renal disease blood disorders, autoimmune disease"],["sec","cl:t-hl~s4-2","Features","Approach to hearing loss · Sensorineural loss","High tones are usually affected more; discrimination is reduced, especially in noise; the loss can exceed 60 dB; there may be recruitment or tinnitus. Figure 4 Audiogram of a right ear with sensorineural loss: air and bone conduction fall together, with no air-bone gap. Dhingra, fig. 5.6"],["sec","cl:t-hl~s4-3","Cochlear or retrocochlear?","Approach to hearing loss · Sensorineural loss","Cochlear or retrocochlear Test Cochlear Retrocochlear Speech discrimination Reduced, below 90% Very poor, out of proportion Roll-over Absent Present Recruitment Present Absent SISI Over 70% 0–20% Tone decay Under 25 dB Over 25 dB Stapedial reflex Present Absent Reflex decay Absent Present ABR Normal I–V Wave V delayed or absent Contrast MRI has largely replaced these special audiometric tests for excluding a retrocochlear lesion."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s4-4","Management","Approach to hearing loss · Sensorineural loss","Treat the cause where possible; protect what remains from noise and ototoxic drugs; then rehabilitate early with hearing aids or implants."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s5-1","History","Approach to hearing loss · Assessment","Onset and speed, one or both ears, fluctuation, tinnitus, vertigo, otalgia and discharge, noise and drug exposure, head injury, family history, and general health."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s5-2","Examination","Approach to hearing loss · Assessment","Otoscopy under the microscope, tuning forks, and the cranial nerves. Tuning fork patterns Test Conductive loss Sensorineural loss Rinne Negative (BC better than AC) Positive (AC better than BC) Weber To the poorer ear To the better ear Absolute bone conduction Normal Reduced"],["sec","cl:t-hl~s5-3","Audiometry","Approach to hearing loss · Assessment","Pure-tone audiogram with masking, speech audiometry, tympanometry and stapedial reflexes; otoacoustic emissions and ABR when needed ( Hearing tests: how to do and read them )."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s5-4","Further tests by suspicion","Approach to hearing loss · Assessment","MRI with contrast for asymmetric or retrocochlear loss; CT for conductive loss with a normal drum, trauma or malformation; blood glucose, thyroid and renal function, full blood count and syphilis serology."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s5-5","Non-organic hearing loss","Approach to hearing loss · Assessment","Clues to non-organic loss Inconsistent repeat audiograms (normal repeats agree within about 5 dB). No shadow curve with a claimed one-sided loss. Speech reception threshold more than 10 dB better than the pure-tone average. A positive Stenger test. Stapedial reflexes present despite claimed severe deafness. ABR or ASSR near the true threshold."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s6-1","Sudden sensorineural loss","Approach to hearing loss · Special situations","An emergency: steroids the same day and MRI ( Sudden sensorineural hearing loss )."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s6-2","Asymmetric sensorineural loss","Approach to hearing loss · Special situations","MRI to exclude a vestibular schwannoma ."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s6-3","Treatable causes","Approach to hearing loss · Special situations","Syphilis, hypothyroidism, autoimmune inner ear disease and ototoxic drugs ( Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease ), serous labyrinthitis, and perilymph fistula ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis )."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s6-4","Hearing loss in children","Approach to hearing loss · Special situations","Universal newborn hearing screening uses automated otoacoustic emissions, followed by automated auditory brainstem responses for babies who do not pass, and all babies who spend time in neonatal intensive care have both. Permanent childhood hearing loss affects about 1 to 2 per 1000 births; about half is genetic (most often GJB2 , connexin 26), and congenital cytomegalovirus is the commonest non-genetic cause, tested within the first three weeks of life when antiviral treatment may help. Every child with permanent loss needs an aetiological work-up ( The deaf child and congenital hearing loss ): imaging, genetics, CMV, ophthalmology, ECG for Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome, and renal assessment when indicated. Otitis media with effusion is the commonest cause of acquired hearing loss in children and is managed by watchful waiting for three months, then hearing aids or ventilation tubes if a bilateral loss persists."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s6-5","Age-related hearing loss","Approach to hearing loss · Special situations","Presbycusis is a symmetrical high-frequency sensorineural loss. Untreated hearing loss in mid-life is the largest single modifiable risk factor for dementia identified by the Lancet Commission, and hearing aids are associated with reduced cognitive decline in those at risk; this is a strong reason to offer amplification early."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s6-6","Implantable hearing","Approach to hearing loss · Special situations","Cochlear implants are offered in the UK to adults and children whose hearing thresholds are 80 dB HL or worse at two or more frequencies between 500 and 4000 Hz in both ears and who gain inadequate benefit from hearing aids, defined in adults as a score of 50% or less on the Arthur Boothroyd word test at 70 dB SPL (NICE TA566, 2019; Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ). Bone-conduction implants , middle ear implants and implants for single-sided deafness extend the options."],["ev","cl:t-hl~s6-7","Approach to hearing loss: current evidence","Approach to hearing loss · Special situations","Adults. NICE (NG98, 2018) recommends offering hearing aids to adults whose hearing loss affects their ability to communicate, without a threshold cut-off; same-day referral for unexplained sudden loss that developed over three days or less within the past 30 days, and referral within two weeks if it was longer ago; and MRI for asymmetric sensorineural loss. The Lancet Commission puts hearing loss at about 7% of dementia cases (Livingston et al., Lancet 2024). In the ACHIEVE trial of 977 adults aged 70 to 84, a hearing intervention did not slow cognitive decline overall over three years, but slowed it by about 48% in the subgroup at higher risk of dementia (Lin et al., Lancet 2023). Children. The American Joint Committee on Infant Hearing keeps the 1-3-6 targets (screen by one month, diagnose by three, intervene by six) and encourages 1-2-3 months where possible ( J Early Hear Detect Interv 2019)."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s6-7","Single-sided deafness","Approach to hearing loss · Special situations","Poor sound localisation and difficulty in noise, even with normal speech elsewhere. Bone-anchored devices, CROS aids or a cochlear implant help; the only hearing ear must be protected. In clinic Do the tuning forks first: they take seconds. Sudden sensorineural loss goes on the sudden hearing loss pathway today. Asymmetric sensorineural loss needs MRI. Look for treatable causes before fitting an aid. Warn anyone with one hearing ear to protect it."],["sec","cl:t-hl~c7","Pathway","Approach to hearing loss · Pathway","From complaint to plan. Hearing loss 1 Otoscopy and tuning forks wax, effusion, perforation or cholesteatoma treat the ear conductive with a normal drum audiogram, reflexes, CT if surgery considered sensorineural step 2 2 Speed sudden, within 72 hours sudden sensorineural loss pathway today fluctuating with vertigo Ménière's pathway gradual step 3 3 Symmetry asymmetric or unilateral MRI of the internal auditory meatus symmetrical age, noise, drugs, family history, systemic causes 4 Rehabilitation hearing aids bone-conduction device for discharging ears or atresia cochlear implant if thresholds 80 dB or worse with poor aided speech"],["sec","cl:t-hl~s8-1","Viva essentials","Approach to hearing loss · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Conductive versus sensorineural: causes, features and fork tests. WHO grades, old and new. Conductive loss behind an intact drum, including third-window lesions. Newborn screening and the work-up of a deaf child. Cochlear implant criteria. Cochlear versus retrocochlear tests. Non-organic hearing loss and how to detect it. Sizes of conductive loss for common lesions."],["sec","cl:t-hl~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to hearing loss · For the examination","Common pitfalls Fitting a hearing aid to an asymmetric loss without MRI. Missing a sudden loss labelled as a blocked ear. Forgetting the treatable causes."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s1-1","Burden","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Who and why","Head and neck cancer is about the seventh or eighth commonest cancer worldwide. In India it is among the leading cancers in men, mostly oral cavity cancer linked to smokeless tobacco and betel quid. More than 90% of tumours of the mucosa of the upper aerodigestive tract are squamous cell carcinomas, and in many low-resource settings more than 70% of patients present at stage III or IV."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s1-2","Risk factors","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Who and why","Tobacco is the principal cause, whether smoked as cigarettes, bidis or hookah, or used in smokeless forms: chewing tobacco, gutka, betel quid with areca nut and snuff. Reverse smoking , with the lit end inside the mouth, causes cancer of the palate. Alcohol acts synergistically with tobacco, so that the combined risk is multiplicative rather than additive. HPV-16 causes oropharyngeal cancer, especially of the tonsil and tongue base; it is linked to oral sex and the number of partners, and its incidence is rising in high-income countries. Epstein–Barr virus causes undifferentiated nasopharyngeal carcinoma , together with salted fish and preserved foods and genetic susceptibility in southern China. Other factors include poor oral hygiene and chronic dental trauma, submucous fibrosis from areca nut, Plummer–Vinson syndrome for postcricoid cancer, immunosuppression, radiation, occupational exposure to wood dust, leather and nickel for sinonasal adenocarcinoma and squamous carcinoma, Fanconi anaemia and dyskeratosis congenita, and diets poor in fruit and vegetables."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s1-3","Field cancerisation and second primaries","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Who and why","Carcinogens bathe the whole mucosa, so genetically altered fields surround a cancer and give rise to new ones. Second primary tumours develop at about 3 to 5% a year, in the head and neck, lung and oesophagus, and they are a leading cause of death after successful treatment of an early cancer. This is why every patient has a panendoscopy at diagnosis, is helped to stop smoking, and is followed for life."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s1-4","Premalignant lesions","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Who and why","Leukoplakia and erythroplakia ( Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches ), of which erythroplakia carries the highest risk, submucous fibrosis, erosive lichen planus and laryngeal dysplasia are the recognised premalignant states. The grade of dysplasia, from mild through moderate and severe to carcinoma in situ, predicts the risk of invasion. Figure 1 Leukoplakia of the cheek and of the lateral tongue. Dhingra, figs 44.7 and 44.8"],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s2-1","HPV-negative carcinoma","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Tumour biology","HPV-negative cancer is driven by tobacco carcinogens that cause accumulated mutations. Tumour suppressors are inactivated: TP53 is mutated in about 80%, CDKN2A (p16) is lost by mutation, deletion of 9p21 or methylation, and FAT1 , NOTCH1 and CASP8 are often affected. Oncogenes are activated: cyclin D1 by 11q13 amplification, PIK3CA at 3q26, EGFR , which is overexpressed in about 90% and amplified in some, and FGFR1 , MYC and HRAS . Califano's progression model describes the sequence: loss of 9p21 (p16), then 3p, then 17p (p53), then further changes, as normal mucosa moves through dysplasia to invasive cancer."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s2-2","HPV-positive carcinoma","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Tumour biology","In HPV-positive cancer the viral oncoprotein E6 degrades p53 and E7 inactivates Rb . With Rb gone, p16 is strongly over-expressed , which is why p16 immunohistochemistry serves as the surrogate marker for HPV in oropharyngeal cancer. p53 is usually wild-type , the mutational burden is lower, PIK3CA activation is frequent, and changes in TRAF3 and CYLD are linked to immune escape. Clinically the patients are younger and often non-smokers, with small primaries in the tonsil or tongue base and early, often cystic , nodes. The tumours respond much better to radiotherapy and chemotherapy, with three-year survival of 82% against 57% for HPV-negative tumours in RTOG 0129 ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ), although smoking more than 10 pack-years worsens the outlook."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s2-3","Prognostic factors","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Tumour biology","The tumour factors that matter are stage, especially nodal disease and extranodal extension ; depth of invasion in oral cancer; perineural and lymphovascular invasion; the margins; and HPV status. Comorbidity, performance status and continued smoking are the patient factors, and total treatment time and gaps in radiotherapy the treatment factors."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s3-1","Principles","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Staging","The T stage describes the primary, mostly by size, with important exceptions: vocal fold mobility and subsite spread in the larynx, depth of invasion in the oral cavity, and extension in the nasopharynx and sinuses. The N stage describes regional nodes by size, number and side and now by extranodal extension (ENE), and the M stage distant metastases. Clinical staging (cTNM) comes from examination and imaging, pathological staging (pTNM) from the resected specimen. T4 is subdivided into T4a , moderately advanced and resectable, and T4b , very advanced and usually unresectable, involving for example the masticator space, pterygoid plates, skull base, prevertebral fascia or mediastinum, or encasing the carotid. T stage: oral cavity (including lip) T Definition T1 2 cm or less, depth of invasion (DOI) 5 mm or less T2 2 cm or less with DOI over 5 mm, or over 2 to 4 cm with DOI 10 mm or less T3 Over 4 cm, or DOI over 10 mm T4a Invades through cortical bone of mandible or maxilla, the maxillary sinus or facial skin (lip: through bone, inferior alveolar nerve, floor of mouth or skin) T4b Masticator space, pterygoid plates, skull base, or encases the internal carotid T stage: larynx (glottis) T Definition T1a / T1b One vocal fold / both vocal folds, normal mobility T2 Extends to supraglottis or subglottis, or impaired mobility T3 Fixed vocal fold, paraglottic space or inner cortex of thyroid cartilage T4a Through the outer cortex of the thyroid cartilage or beyond the larynx (trachea, strap muscles, thyroid, oesophagus, deep tongue muscles) T4b Prevertebral space, encases carotid, o"],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s3-2","Nodal staging","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Staging","N stage: clinical (oral cavity, larynx, hypopharynx, HPV-negative oropharynx) N Definition N1 Single ipsilateral node 3 cm or less, ENE negative N2a Single ipsilateral node over 3 to 6 cm, ENE negative N2b Multiple ipsilateral nodes, none over 6 cm, ENE negative N2c Bilateral or contralateral nodes, none over 6 cm, ENE negative N3a Any node over 6 cm, ENE negative N3b Clinically overt ENE (skin involvement, fixation, cranial nerve or muscle invasion) Pathologically, a single ipsilateral node of 3 cm or less with ENE becomes pN2a, and larger or multiple nodes with ENE become pN3b. In the HPV-positive oropharynx , clinical N1 is ipsilateral nodes of 6 cm or less, N2 contralateral or bilateral nodes of 6 cm or less, and N3 any node over 6 cm, while pathologically pN1 is one to four nodes and pN2 five or more . In the nasopharynx , N1 is unilateral cervical, or unilateral or bilateral retropharyngeal nodes, of 6 cm or less above the cricoid; N2 is bilateral cervical nodes of 6 cm or less above the cricoid; and N3 is any node over 6 cm or below the caudal border of the cricoid. Figure 2 Lymph node levels of the neck. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s3-3","Stage groups","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Staging","Stage groups (oral cavity, larynx, hypopharynx, HPV-negative oropharynx, sinuses, salivary) Stage TNM I T1 N0 M0 II T2 N0 M0 III T3 N0, or T1 to T3 N1, M0 IVA T4a N0 to N1, or T1 to T4a N2, M0 IVB Any T N3, or T4b any N, M0 IVC Any M1 HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer has its own clinical groups: stage I is T0 to T2 with N0 to N1, stage II is T0 to T2 with N2 or T3 with N0 to N2, stage III is T4 or N3, and stage IV is M1. Many patients are downstaged compared with the seventh edition, which reflects their better prognosis."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s3-4","The eighth edition changes","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Staging","The 2017 AJCC eighth edition introduced: Depth of invasion for oral cancer. Extranodal extension in nodal staging. A separate staging system for p16-positive oropharyngeal cancer . Separate staging for unknown primaries by EBV and HPV status. Older texts and exam questions based on the seventh edition will give different stages for the same tumour. Newer versions have begun to appear (AJCC version 9 for nasopharyngeal cancer, and the UICC ninth edition in 2025), so always state which edition you are using."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s4-1","Assessment","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Work-up","The history covers symptoms, risk factors, comorbidity, performance status (ECOG or Karnofsky), nutrition and social support. The examination covers the whole head and neck, with palpation of the tongue base and floor of mouth, flexible nasendoscopy, the cranial nerves, and the neck by level. Tissue comes from biopsy of the primary and FNA of nodes, with p16/HPV testing for the oropharynx and unknown primaries and EBV for the nasopharynx."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s4-2","Imaging","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Work-up","CT and MRI of the primary and neck are complementary: MRI is better for the tongue, nasopharynx, skull base and perineural spread, CT for bone and the larynx. Distant staging uses CT of the chest for advanced disease and PET-CT for stage III and IV disease, unknown primaries and recurrence, and the neck is assessed with ultrasound and FNA."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s4-3","Panendoscopy","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Work-up","Examination under anaesthesia, with laryngoscopy, pharyngoscopy, oesophagoscopy and sometimes bronchoscopy, maps the tumour's extent into the tongue base, vallecula, postcricoid area and subglottis, takes biopsies, and searches for synchronous second primaries. For unknown primaries it includes tonsillectomy and tongue base mucosectomy ( Approach to a lump in the neck ). Figure 3 Laryngeal cancer by subsite: supraglottic, glottic and subglottic. Dhingra, figs 62.4 and 62.5"],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s4-4","Before treatment","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Work-up","Before treatment begins, teeth at risk are extracted ahead of radiotherapy, a dietitian reviews nutrition and a prophylactic gastrostomy is considered, and a speech and swallowing therapist makes a baseline assessment and starts prehabilitation . Smoking and alcohol cessation are supported, the anaesthetist plans the airway (some patients need a tracheostomy under local anaesthesia first), and the clinical nurse specialist begins psychosocial support."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s5-1","The team","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Treatment","Every patient is discussed at a multidisciplinary team (tumour board) of head and neck and reconstructive surgeons, clinical and medical oncologists, radiologist, pathologist, restorative dentist, speech and language therapist, dietitian, specialist nurse and palliative care. The patient's own priorities are central: cure, voice, swallowing, appearance and time."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s5-2","General principles","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Treatment","Early disease (stages I and II) is treated with a single modality, surgery or radiotherapy, which give similar cure rates. The choice is driven by function, access, morbidity, the patient's wishes and the wish to keep options open for second primaries: transoral laser surgery or radiotherapy for a T1 glottic cancer, for example, or surgery for a small oral tongue cancer. Advanced disease (stages III and IV) needs combined treatment; the radiotherapy and drug details are in Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer . Surgery followed by radiotherapy , or chemoradiotherapy for positive margins or extranodal extension, is preferred for oral cavity cancer and for cancers destroying cartilage or bone, such as T4a larynx and mandibular invasion. Primary concurrent chemoradiotherapy with surgical salvage is the organ-preservation approach for the larynx, hypopharynx and oropharynx, and the standard for the nasopharynx. Unresectable T4b disease is treated with chemoradiotherapy or palliation. Recurrent or metastatic disease is treated by salvage surgery or re-irradiation for local and regional recurrence, systemic therapy with pembrolizumab with or without platinum and 5-FU or cetuximab-based regimens, and palliative care."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s5-3","The neck","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Treatment","A clinically node-negative neck is treated electively, by selective neck dissection or elective radiotherapy, when the risk of occult nodes exceeds about 15 to 20%. In the oral cavity ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer ) this means a depth of invasion over 3 to 4 mm, and the D'Cruz trial showed a survival advantage for elective neck dissection in early oral cancer; sentinel node biopsy is an alternative. A clinically positive neck is treated by therapeutic neck dissection, modified radical or comprehensive, or by chemoradiotherapy with a PET-CT at 12 weeks deciding whether dissection is needed. Tumours of the tongue base, soft palate, supraglottis and floor of mouth crossing the midline drain to both sides, and both necks are treated."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s5-4","Surgery","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · Treatment","The aim of surgery is complete resection with clear margins, generally 5 mm or more on histology, a margin under 5 mm being close; frozen sections help. The approaches range from transoral laser microsurgery and transoral robotic surgery to the lip-split mandibulotomy, lateral pharyngotomy, total laryngectomy and pharyngolaryngectomy. Reconstruction usually takes place at the same operation, often with free flaps ( Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation ), and a tracheostomy protects the airway after major oral and pharyngeal resections."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s6-1","Follow-up","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · After treatment","Most recurrences occur within the first two years , so patients are seen every 1 to 3 months in the first year, every 2 to 4 months in the second, every 3 to 6 months in years three to five, and yearly thereafter. Each visit includes examination and nasendoscopy, with a PET-CT about 12 weeks after radiotherapy and further imaging as indicated. TSH is checked every 6 to 12 months after neck radiotherapy, the teeth are reviewed, smoking status is recorded, and the whole upper aerodigestive tract and chest are watched for second primaries."],["ev","cl:t-hncprinc~s6-2","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles: current evidence","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · After treatment","Staging. The AJCC eighth edition separated HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer because its prognosis is so much better: most patients previously staged IV now fall into stages I to II (Lydiatt et al., CA Cancer J Clin 2017). Chemotherapy. The MACH-NC meta-analysis of 107 trials, whose main result is given in Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer , found no benefit from adjuvant chemotherapy and less from induction (Pignon et al., Radiother Oncol 2009). Immunotherapy around surgery. In KEYNOTE-689, pembrolizumab before and after surgery improved event-free survival in resectable locally advanced squamous carcinoma (hazard ratio 0.73), and in NIVOPOSTOP, nivolumab added to postoperative chemoradiation improved disease-free survival in high-risk patients (reviewed by Imamura et al., Cancer Treat Rev 2025); overall survival data are awaited. Surveillance. PET-CT at 12 weeks after chemoradiation safely avoided planned neck dissection in most patients with nodal disease in the PET-NECK trial (Mehanna et al., NEJM 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s6-2","Rehabilitation and quality of life","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · After treatment","Swallowing is rehabilitated with exercises, dilatation of strictures, and a gastrostomy for those who cannot eat safely. After laryngectomy, voice is restored with a tracheo-oesophageal valve , an electrolarynx or oesophageal speech. Shoulder dysfunction after accessory nerve injury needs physiotherapy and lymphoedema manual drainage, and trismus, xerostomia, hearing loss and osteoradionecrosis are the other late effects to manage. Depression, fear of recurrence, alcohol relapse and suicide are all common in this group, so patients are screened and referred. Palliative care controls symptoms, manages a fungating tumour, and plans for carotid blow-out , airway obstruction and bleeding, with a crisis plan of dark towels and sedation."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s7-1","Viva essentials","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Risk factors: tobacco (including smokeless) and alcohol synergy; HPV; EBV. Field cancerisation and the second primary rate. HPV-positive versus HPV-negative cancer: E6 degrades p53, E7 inactivates Rb so p16 is over-expressed; prognosis; a separate AJCC staging system. The eighth edition changes: depth of invasion, ENE, p16 staging. Oral cavity and glottic T staging; the clinical N stages; the stage groups. The work-up: imaging, PET-CT, panendoscopy, the dental and nutrition review. Early versus advanced disease: single versus combined modality; when to treat the N0 neck; margins. Follow-up schedule and late effects."],["sec","cl:t-hncprinc~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles · For the examination","Common pitfalls Staging an oropharyngeal cancer without p16. Using seventh-edition definitions in an eighth-edition world. Forgetting the contralateral neck in midline tumours. Neglecting dental assessment and smoking cessation before treatment. Discharging patients without thyroid and second primary surveillance."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s1-1","What is missing","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Principles","Every defect is analysed in the same way; skin cancer defects follow the same ladder ( Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck ). First, which tissues have gone: skin, mucosal lining, muscle, bone, cartilage or nerve. Second, what each did, because the reconstruction must restore function as well as form: a skeleton for occlusion, contour and dental implants ; cover for vital structures such as the carotid, dura and brain; oral competence and a lip sphincter; pliable, mobile tissue for tongue movement, speech and swallowing; and volume, sensation and secreting mucosa. Third, the state of the bed: irradiation, infection, a fistula and the vessels available, remembering that irradiated or infected beds need vascularised tissue. Finally, the patient's health, prognosis and wishes."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s1-2","The reconstructive ladder (and the elevator)","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Principles","Healing by secondary intention: good for concave sites (medial canthus, temple, conchal bowl, nasolabial groove, glabella); bad near free margins (eyelid, ala, lip), which contract and distort. Primary closure: along relaxed skin tension lines, as a fusiform excision about three times as long as it is wide. Grafts: skin, cartilage, bone, nerve and composite grafts. Local flaps. Regional (pedicled) flaps. Free tissue transfer. Prostheses. Climbing the ladder rung by rung is not compulsory. The surgeon may take the \"elevator\" straight to the option that gives the best result, which for a major defect is often a free flap."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s2-1","Skin grafts","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Grafts","A skin graft survives for about 48 hours by plasmatic imbibition , and then by inosculation of its vessels with those of the bed and capillary ingrowth at three to five days. Anything that separates graft from bed in that time kills it: haematoma, seroma, shearing and infection, which are prevented with a bolster or tie-over dressing, quilting sutures, pie-crusting or negative pressure. Grafts do not take on bare bone or cartilage stripped of periosteum or perichondrium, nor on irradiated beds. Split-thickness grafts , taken with a dermatome from the thigh or buttock, take more reliably but contract more and match colour poorly; they line the mouth and maxillectomy cavities and cover the scalp over periosteum and free flap donor sites. Full-thickness grafts , defatted and taken from postauricular, preauricular, upper eyelid or supraclavicular skin, contract less and match better, so they are used on the nose, eyelids and ear."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s2-2","Cartilage, bone and nerve grafts","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Grafts","Septal cartilage is harvested leaving an L-strut of 1 to 1.5 cm; curved conchal cartilage suits the ala and ear; and costal cartilage is plentiful for the dorsum and microtia ( Microtia and congenital canal atresia ) but can warp. Composite skin–cartilage grafts from the ear repair alar rim and columella defects up to about 1 to 1.5 cm. Cortical bone from split calvaria, rib or iliac crest rebuilds the orbital floor, nasal dorsum and facial buttresses, and cancellous bone from the iliac crest fills alveolar clefts and small mandibular gaps. Every free bone graft needs a well-vascularised, unirradiated bed and rigid fixation, and all resorb to some degree. Nerve grafts come from the great auricular nerve, which lies in the operative field and gives up to about 10 cm; the sural nerve, up to 30 to 35 cm, used for cross-face grafts; and the medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s3-1","Principles","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Local flaps","Random-pattern local flaps live on the subdermal plexus. On the well-vascularised face they tolerate a length-to-width ratio of up to about 3:1, more than on the trunk. They are designed to place scars in the relaxed skin tension lines and at the borders of aesthetic subunits , to borrow skin from areas of laxity such as the cheek, glabella and neck, and to close the secondary defect without distorting a free margin. Smoking, tension, irradiation and haematoma are their enemies."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s3-2","Types","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Local flaps","Advancement flaps include the V-Y and the unipedicle and bipedicle flaps of the forehead and of the lip with a crescentic perialar excision. Rotation flaps include the cervicofacial and scalp flaps. Transposition flaps include the rhomboid (Limberg) flap, the bilobed (Zitelli) flap for defects up to about 1.5 cm on the lower third of the nose, the nasolabial (melolabial) flap for the ala, and the note flap. Interpolated flaps pass over or under intact skin and are divided later. The paramedian forehead flap , on the supratrochlear artery and divided at three weeks, is the workhorse for large or full-thickness nasal defects ( Facial plastic surgery ); the interpolated nasolabial flap and the Abbe cross-lip flap are the others. A full-thickness nasal defect needs all three layers rebuilt: lining from local mucosal flaps or a turnover flap, a framework of cartilage grafts, and cover from the forehead flap."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s3-3","Lip reconstruction","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Local flaps","Full-thickness lip defects Defect Reconstruction Under a third (up to about a half of the lower lip) Primary (V or W) closure A third to a half Abbe cross-lip flap (on the labial artery, divided at about three weeks); Estlander flap if the commissure is involved A half to two-thirds Karapandzic (neurovascular, sensate, innervated rotation–advancement); bilateral advancement Over two-thirds to total Bernard–Burow (cheek advancement), Gillies fan flap, or a free radial forearm flap with a palmaris longus sling What matters most is restoring the orbicularis sphincter , the vermilion line and the commissure. Microstomia is the price paid for large local flaps. Figure 1 The arterial supply of the lips, and the lip landmarks. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~c4","Regional flaps","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Regional flaps","Pedicled from nearby. Regional flaps Flap Pedicle Uses and notes Pectoralis major myocutaneous Pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial artery Workhorse salvage flap: oral, pharyngeal and neck defects, carotid cover; reaches about to the zygomatic arch; bulky, can sag Deltopectoral (fasciocutaneous) Perforators of the internal thoracic (mammary) artery, 2nd to 4th intercostal spaces Neck skin, pharyngostome; often needs delay and a second stage Temporalis muscle Deep temporal arteries Orbit, maxillectomy, skull base, facial reanimation Temporoparietal fascia Superficial temporal artery Ear, orbit and skull base; thin and vascular; can be free Supraclavicular island Supraclavicular branch of the transverse cervical artery Thin, pliable neck and face skin; pharynx Submental island Submental branch of the facial artery Oral cavity and face (beware nodes in oral cancer) Sternocleidomastoid Occipital, superior thyroid, transverse cervical Small buttress; parotid contour Trapezius (lateral, lower island) Transverse cervical and dorsal scapular arteries Posterior neck, scalp Latissimus dorsi (pedicled) Thoracodorsal artery Large defects of neck and scalp Nasoseptal, turbinate, pericranial Septal artery, sphenopalatine branches, supraorbital and supratrochlear Skull base ( Skull base and the parapharyngeal space ) Palatal island Greater palatine artery Palate and nasopharynx"],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s5-1","Principles","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Free flaps","Free flaps are joined to recipient arteries such as the facial, superior thyroid, lingual or transverse cervical, and to the internal jugular vein end-to-side or the external jugular or facial veins; a previously treated neck may need a contralateral approach or vein grafts. Two teams work together, ischaemia time is kept short (bowel is the most sensitive, then muscle, then bone), and the pedicle is laid out without kinking, twisting or tension. Success rates are about 95 to 99% in experienced units. Most failures occur within 48 to 72 hours , usually from venous thrombosis, so the flap is checked hourly at first for colour, capillary refill, temperature and pinprick bleeding, with a hand-held or implantable Doppler. The patient is kept warm, well filled and normotensive, vasoconstrictors are avoided where possible, and aspirin or heparin is given according to unit policy. Venous congestion makes the flap purple and swollen with brisk dark bleeding; arterial insufficiency makes it pale and cool with no bleeding. Either means an urgent return to theatre, because salvage is far more likely within hours."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s5-2","The workhorse flaps","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Free flaps","Common free flaps Flap Pedicle Tissue Typical use Radial forearm Radial artery and venae comitantes, cephalic vein Thin, pliable fasciocutaneous; sensate; can carry radius Tongue, floor of mouth, palate, pharynx (tubed), lip; check the Allen test Anterolateral thigh Descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery (perforators) Skin, fat, fascia, vastus lateralis; adjustable bulk Tongue (total glossectomy), cheek, skull base, pharynx; hidden donor site; variable perforators Fibula Peroneal artery Up to about 25 cm of straight bone, segmented by osteotomy; skin paddle Mandible , maxilla; implant-capable (double-barrel for height); image leg vessels first (peronea arteria magna) Scapula and parascapular (subscapular system) Circumflex scapular, thoracodorsal (angular branch to tip) Bone, skin, latissimus, serratus in combinations (\"chimeric\") Complex 3D maxillary and mandibular defects; elderly with poor leg vessels Iliac crest Deep circumflex iliac artery Thick curved bone with internal oblique Mandible, maxilla; donor pain and hernia Rectus abdominis Deep inferior epigastric artery Muscle and skin; bulky Skull base, maxillectomy, orbit, total glossectomy Latissimus dorsi Thoracodorsal artery Large thin muscle Scalp, large surface defects Jejunum Mesenteric arcade Tubed, secretory mucosa Circumferential pharyngo-oesophageal defects; peristalsis and voice quality are drawbacks Gracilis Medial circumflex femoral artery; anterior branch of the obturator nerve Functioning muscle Facial reanimation"],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s5-3","Choosing the flap","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Free flaps","In the mandible , a lateral segmental defect is usually rebuilt with a fibula, though a plate with a soft-tissue flap may suit a patient with a poor prognosis. An anterior arch defect must always be rebuilt with bone, or the \" Andy Gump \" deformity follows: a collapsed chin, drooling and loss of oral competence. The maxilla is restored with an obturator prosthesis or a free flap (fibula, scapula or anterolateral thigh), chosen by the size of the defect and the involvement of the palate and orbital floor. The tongue needs thin, mobile tissue after partial glossectomy, so a radial forearm flap is used, but bulk after total glossectomy, so an anterolateral thigh or rectus flap fills the mouth and reaches the palate. In the pharynx , partial defects take a patch of radial forearm or anterolateral thigh, while circumferential defects need a tubed anterolateral thigh or radial forearm flap, jejunum, or a gastric pull-up when the oesophagus is removed."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s6-1","Questions to ask","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Facial reanimation","What caused the palsy ( Facial nerve palsy ), and is the nerve in continuity? Is the proximal stump available? How long has the face been paralysed? Facial muscles survive denervation for about 18 to 24 months, after which motor end plates are lost and muscle atrophies. Which regions are affected, and what does the patient want: eye protection, symmetry at rest, a smile?"],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s6-2","Protect the eye first","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Facial reanimation","Whatever else is planned, the cornea is protected first: lubricating drops by day and ointment at night, taping and a moisture chamber. A gold or platinum weight in the upper lid helps it close, a lateral tarsal strip or canthoplasty tightens a lax lower lid, a brow lift corrects brow ptosis, and a tarsorrhaphy is done if the cornea is at risk. Botulinum toxin to the levator produces a temporary protective ptosis."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s6-3","Restoring the nerve (within about 18 to 24 months)","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Facial reanimation","A nerve cut at the time of injury is repaired at once by tension-free epineurial neurorrhaphy , and any transection in the temporal bone or at parotidectomy is repaired immediately. Where the gap is 1 cm or more, an interposition graft of great auricular or sural nerve is used; function returns at 6 to 12 months, usually with some synkinesis . Branches medial to a vertical line through the lateral canthus are usually not repaired, because of cross-innervation. When the proximal stump is unavailable but the distal nerve and muscle are still viable, a nerve transfer is used. The masseteric-to-facial (V to VII) transfer is now the commonest: fast, strong and with little morbidity, though the smile is driven by biting and is usually not spontaneous. The hypoglossal-to-facial (XII to VII) transfer, done as a partial split or jump-graft technique to limit tongue atrophy, gives good tone but synkinesis. Cross-face nerve grafting carries axons from the contralateral buccal branches through a sural graft, with a second stage once Tinel's sign shows they have crossed at about 8 to 12 months. It is the only method that gives a spontaneous, emotional smile , but it is weaker, so it is often combined with a masseteric transfer acting as a \" babysitter \" to keep the muscles alive."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s6-4","Long-standing palsy (muscle lost)","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Facial reanimation","Once the facial muscles are lost, a new muscle must be brought in. The free functional gracilis transfer , powered by the masseteric nerve in one stage or by a cross-face graft in two, is the gold standard for smile reanimation and is also used in Möbius syndrome . The temporalis transfer , as an orthodromic lengthening temporalis myoplasty from the coronoid or as the older turnover over the arch, is the alternative. Static procedures add support: fascia lata or acellular dermal slings to lift the commissure and support the nasal valve, a brow lift, and lower lid tightening."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s6-5","Synkinesis and hyperkinesis","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Facial reanimation","Aberrant regeneration after Bell's palsy or nerve repair produces involuntary co-contractions, such as the eye closing on smiling, and hypertonicity. These are treated with neuromuscular retraining by facial physiotherapy, botulinum toxin to the overactive muscles on the affected side and to the normal side for symmetry, and selective neurectomy or myectomy."],["ev","cl:t-hnrecon~s7-1","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation: current evidence","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Other options","The anterolateral thigh flap became a workhorse after a series of 672 flaps showed it to be reliable and versatile, with low donor morbidity (Wei et al., Plast Reconstr Surg 2002). Virtual surgical planning with cutting guides improves the accuracy of fibula reconstruction of the mandible and shortens operating time in comparative studies."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s7-1","Beyond tissue transfer","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · Other options","Auricular, nasal and orbital prostheses retained by osseointegrated implants or adhesives, and palatal obturators after maxillectomy, suit the elderly and the irradiated, follow failed reconstruction, and allow the cavity to be inspected for recurrence. Tissue expansion recruits skin for the scalp and neck. Virtual surgical planning uses CT-based 3D models, cutting guides and custom plates for mandibular and maxillary reconstruction. Facial allotransplantation of composite tissue has been done for devastating defects, but lifelong immunosuppression, rejection and ethical questions still limit it."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s8-1","Viva essentials","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The reconstructive ladder; sites that heal well by secondary intention. Split- versus full-thickness grafts: take, contraction, uses. Bilobed, rhomboid and paramedian forehead flaps: blood supply, three-layer nasal repair. Lip reconstruction by size of defect: Abbe, Estlander, Karapandzic. The pectoralis major flap: its pedicle and reach. Radial forearm, anterolateral thigh and fibula flaps: pedicles, uses, and pre-operative checks (Allen test, leg vessel imaging). Free flap monitoring and the failing flap. Facial reanimation by time since injury; masseteric versus hypoglossal versus cross-face grafting; the gracilis flap; the eye first."],["sec","cl:t-hnrecon~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Skin grafting onto irradiated tissue or bare bone. Leaving an anterior mandibular defect unreconstructed. Raising a radial forearm flap without an Allen test. Delaying return to theatre for a congested free flap. Waiting beyond two years before trying to reinnervate the face."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s1-1","Radiation and dose","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Radiation basics","Cancer is treated with ionising radiation of short wavelength and high energy: photons, either X-rays from machines or gamma rays from isotopes such as cobalt-60, and particles such as electrons, protons, neutrons and carbon ions. Dose is measured in grays ; 1 Gy is 1 joule absorbed per kilogram and equals 100 rad. Most treatment uses megavoltage photons of 4 to 25 MV from a linear accelerator , which spare the skin because the maximum dose builds up below the surface. Electrons deposit their dose near the surface and fall off sharply, which suits the skin, lip and superficial nodes overlying the spinal cord. Protons deposit most of their energy at a set depth, the Bragg peak , with almost no exit dose, which suits the skull base, orbit and brainstem, re-irradiation and children. Neutrons and carbon ions have a high linear energy transfer and a role in salivary and radioresistant tumours. Figure 1 First, the electromagnetic spectrum. Second, a modern linear accelerator. Dhingra, figs 73.1 and 73.2"],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s1-2","How radiation kills","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Radiation basics","Radiation damages DNA, mostly indirectly, by ionising water into free radicals that cause double-strand breaks ; cells then die when they try to divide, or by apoptosis. Oxygen \"fixes\" the damage, so hypoxic cells are two to three times more resistant. That is why anaemia is corrected to a haemoglobin of about 12 g/dL, smoking is stopped, and hypoxic sensitisers such as nimorazole have been tried. Fractionation works because of the four R's . Between fractions, normal tissues repair sublethal damage better than tumour cells; tumour cells reoxygenate ; and cells redistribute into sensitive phases of the cycle. Against this, tumour repopulation accelerates after about four weeks, which is why gaps in treatment are harmful and overall treatment time matters. The biological effect depends on dose per fraction as well as total dose, as described by the linear-quadratic model, BED = nd(1 + d/(α/β)). Late-reacting tissues, with a low α/β of about 3, are spared by small fractions, whereas tumours and acutely reacting tissues, with an α/β of about 10, are not."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s1-3","Fractionation","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Radiation basics","Conventional fractionation gives 1.8 to 2 Gy a day, five days a week. About 50 Gy sterilises microscopic disease such as the elective neck, 60 Gy is given after surgery, 66 Gy for close or positive margins, and 70 Gy in 35 fractions over seven weeks for gross tumour. Hyperfractionation gives smaller fractions of 1.1 to 1.2 Gy twice daily to a higher total of 74 to 80 Gy, improving local control with fewer late effects but more acute toxicity. Accelerated fractionation , with six fractions a week or a concomitant boost, shortens overall time to counter repopulation, and helps mainly patients treated without chemotherapy. Hypofractionation , with fractions of 2.5 Gy or more, is used for palliation, frail patients and stereotactic treatment."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s2-1","Planning","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Delivery","Immobilisation: a thermoplastic mask, with a mouth bite to displace the tongue. Planning CT, fused with MRI or PET. Contouring: the gross tumour volume (GTV); the clinical target volume (CTV), adding microscopic spread and nodal levels; the planning target volume (PTV), a margin for set-up error; and the organs at risk : spinal cord, brainstem, parotids, optic apparatus, cochlea, larynx, mandible and constrictors. Dose calculation on a planning system. Daily image verification. Figure 2 First, a thermoplastic immobilisation mask. Second, contouring of target volumes and organs at risk on the planning scan. Dhingra, figs 73.3 and 73.4"],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s2-2","Techniques","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Delivery","Conventional two-dimensional treatment with parallel opposed lateral fields is now historical, replaced first by CT-based three-dimensional conformal radiotherapy with several shaped beams and then by intensity-modulated radiotherapy (IMRT) , the current standard. IMRT uses inverse planning with many beams or arcs (VMAT), whose intensity is modulated by moving multileaf collimators, to \"curve\" the dose around the spinal cord and parotids and to give different doses to different targets at the same time, the simultaneous integrated boost . Parotid-sparing IMRT reduces permanent xerostomia. Image-guided radiotherapy images the patient on the machine each day with cone-beam CT, correcting set-up errors and allowing smaller margins, and adaptive radiotherapy re-plans during treatment as the tumour shrinks and the patient loses weight. Stereotactic radiosurgery and body radiotherapy deliver very precise high doses in one to five sessions, for vestibular schwannoma (12 to 13 Gy in one session), paraganglioma, small recurrences and re-irradiation."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s2-3","Brachytherapy","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Delivery","Brachytherapy places a sealed source in or against the tumour, interstitially or within a cavity, giving a high local dose that falls off rapidly. Iridium-192 is used with high-dose-rate remote afterloading, replacing caesium-137, radium and gold seeds. It treats cancers of the lip, oral tongue and floor of mouth and the nasopharynx, as a boost or for recurrence, and boosts external beam treatment. Figure 3 First, interstitial brachytherapy needles for cancer of the lip. Second, brachytherapy of the tongue (A–C). Dhingra, figs 73.5 and 73.6"],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s3-1","Roles","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Radiotherapy in practice","Definitive radiotherapy preserves the organ. Alone, it treats early T1 to T2 N0 tumours of the larynx (with control around 90% for T1 glottic cancer), oropharynx, nasopharynx and hypopharynx; with concurrent chemotherapy, it treats advanced tumours. Postoperative radiotherapy starts four to six weeks after surgery, and the whole interval from surgery to the end of radiotherapy should be under about 11 weeks. It is indicated for close (under 5 mm) or positive margins, extranodal extension, two or more positive nodes or a node over 3 cm, pT3 and pT4 tumours, perineural or lymphovascular invasion, nodes in levels IV or V from oral or oropharyngeal cancer, and poor differentiation. Positive margins and extranodal extension are the indications for adding concurrent cisplatin . Preoperative radiotherapy is now rarely used, because it increases wound complications and fistulae, and palliative radiotherapy uses short courses for pain, bleeding, fungation and obstruction. Pre- versus postoperative radiotherapy Preoperative Postoperative Shrinks borderline tumours and sterilises the edges Surgery done on unirradiated tissue: easier, better healing Oxygenated, undisturbed tumour bed Dose tailored to pathology (margins, nodes); a higher dose possible Smaller fields Larger fields; scarred, hypoxic tumour bed More fistulae, flap failures, carotid blow-out Delay from surgical complications allows regrowth"],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s3-2","Site by site","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Radiotherapy in practice","In the larynx ( Cancer of the larynx ), early glottic cancer is treated with radiotherapy alone or transoral laser surgery, and advanced cancer with concurrent cisplatin chemoradiation for larynx preservation, as established by RTOG 91-11; total laryngectomy is reserved for T4a disease with cartilage destruction or a non-functioning larynx. In the hypopharynx ( Carcinoma of the hypopharynx ), larynx preservation is possible but less often successful. In the nasopharynx ( Nasopharyngeal carcinoma ), radiotherapy is the primary treatment, covering levels II and V and the retropharyngeal nodes; T1N0 disease is treated with radiotherapy alone and more advanced disease with cisplatin chemoradiation, often with induction or adjuvant chemotherapy, and plasma EBV DNA guides follow-up. In the oropharynx ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ), radiotherapy or chemoradiation competes with transoral surgery followed by adjuvant treatment. HPV-positive disease has a much better outlook, with three-year survival of 82% against 57% in RTOG 0129, but de-escalation is done only within trials. Oral cavity cancer ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer ) is primarily surgical, with adjuvant radiotherapy according to the pathology. Salivary cancers ( Salivary gland tumours ) are operated on and then irradiated for high grade, positive margins, nodes or perineural spread; for adenoid cystic carcinoma the named nerve is covered to the skull base. After chemoradiation, the neck is assessed with a PET-CT at 12 weeks, and dissection is done only for PET-positive residual disease, as shown"],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s4-1","Goals","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Chemotherapy and drugs","Chemotherapy is given to sensitise the tumour to radiation and so improve locoregional control, to treat micrometastases, to preserve organs, to palliate recurrent or metastatic disease, and to debulk tumours before surgery or radiotherapy. On its own, it does not cure squamous carcinoma of the head and neck."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s4-2","How it is given","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Chemotherapy and drugs","Concurrent chemoradiation is the standard for locally advanced disease. The MACH-NC meta-analysis showed an absolute survival gain of about 6.5% at five years, all of it from concurrent treatment. Cisplatin , at 100 mg/m² every three weeks or 40 mg/m² weekly, is the drug of choice, and adding other drugs adds toxicity rather than cure. Induction chemotherapy with TPF (docetaxel, cisplatin and 5-FU), which is better than PF, is used to select patients for larynx preservation and for bulky nodal disease, but it has not consistently beaten chemoradiation alone (DeCIDE, PARADIGM). Adjuvant chemotherapy after definitive treatment brings no benefit, except in nasopharyngeal cancer. Postoperative chemoradiation is given for positive margins and extranodal extension, on the evidence of the EORTC 22931 and RTOG 9501 trials."],["ev","cl:t-hnrt~s4-3","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer: current evidence","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Chemotherapy and drugs","Trials cited above. MACH-NC (Pignon et al., Radiother Oncol 2009); the pooled EORTC 22931 and RTOG 9501 analysis (Bernier et al., Head Neck 2005); the Bonner trial (Bonner et al., NEJM 2006); cetuximab against cisplatin in HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer (Gillison et al., Lancet 2019, RTOG 1016; Mehanna et al., Lancet 2019, De-ESCALaTE); and KEYNOTE-048, in which pembrolizumab improved survival over the EXTREME regimen (Burtness et al., Lancet 2019). Postoperative cisplatin. Weekly cisplatin (40 mg/m²) was non-inferior to three-weekly in the postoperative setting (Kiyota et al., J Clin Oncol 2022, JCOG1008). IMRT. Parotid-sparing IMRT halved severe xerostomia compared with conventional radiotherapy (Nutting et al., Lancet Oncol 2011, PARSPORT)."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s4-3","Targeted therapy and immunotherapy","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Chemotherapy and drugs","Cetuximab is a monoclonal antibody against EGFR, which is overexpressed in over 90% of these tumours. Combined with radiotherapy it improved survival over radiotherapy alone in the Bonner trial, at the cost of an acneiform rash and infusion reactions, and it is now used for patients unfit for cisplatin. With platinum and 5-FU it was the first-line treatment for metastatic disease in the EXTREME regimen. The PD-1 checkpoint inhibitors nivolumab and pembrolizumab improved survival in recurrent or metastatic disease after platinum (CheckMate 141, KEYNOTE-040), and pembrolizumab, alone for PD-L1-positive tumours or with platinum and 5-FU, is now first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic disease (KEYNOTE-048). Their immune-related side effects include thyroiditis, pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, adrenal insufficiency and rash. Cetuximab is not a substitute for cisplatin In HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer, radiotherapy with cetuximab gave worse survival and control than radiotherapy with cisplatin, without less toxicity. Cisplatin remains the standard partner; cetuximab is reserved for patients who cannot receive platinum. Drugs and their characteristic toxicities Drug Use Key toxicity Cisplatin Concurrent, induction, palliative Nephrotoxicity (hydrate), high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, neuropathy, severe nausea, marrow Carboplatin Cisplatin-unfit, weekly concurrent Marrow (thrombocytopenia) 5-Fluorouracil Induction (PF, TPF), palliative Mucositis, diarrhoea, hand–foot syndrome, coronary spasm Docetaxel, paclitaxel Induction (TPF), palliative Neut"],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s5-1","Acute effects","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Toxicity","Acute effects appear from the second week and settle within weeks of finishing. Mucositis causes pain, odynophagia and weight loss, and is managed with salt and bicarbonate mouth rinses, analgesia including opioids, treatment of candida, and nutritional support from a dietitian with a nasogastric tube or gastrostomy when needed. The skin reddens and then desquamates, dry and later moist, and is washed gently without rubbing or unprescribed creams. Taste is lost, saliva becomes sticky, the larynx swells, fatigue sets in, and chemotherapy suppresses the marrow."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s5-2","Late effects","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Toxicity","Late effects are permanent and depend on dose per fraction and total dose. Xerostomia follows a mean parotid dose above about 26 Gy and brings caries, candida, dysphagia and taste loss; it is reduced by parotid-sparing IMRT and treated with pilocarpine, saliva substitutes and, prophylactically, submandibular gland transfer. Radiation caries is prevented by daily fluoride trays and lifelong dental care, and osteoradionecrosis is described below. Fibrosis of the soft tissues causes trismus , managed with jaw exercises and devices, and fibrosis of the constrictors causes dysphagia , stricture and aspiration, which is why swallowing exercises continue throughout treatment (\"use it or lose it\"). The larynx may develop oedema, chondronecrosis and a fixed cord. Hypothyroidism affects up to half of patients after neck radiotherapy, so TSH is checked every 6 to 12 months ( Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre ). The ear suffers serous otitis media, sensorineural hearing loss that is worse with cisplatin ( Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease ), and radionecrosis of the canal and temporal bone. The eye and nervous system are at risk of cataract, retinopathy, optic neuropathy , brainstem and temporal lobe necrosis, and radiation myelitis , which is avoided by keeping the cord dose under about 45 to 50 Gy; Lhermitte's sign is an early, reversible form. Radiation accelerates carotid atherosclerosis and stroke, sets the scene for carotid blow-out after salvage surgery, and occasionally causes second malignancies such as sarcomas and thyroid cancer. Early and late co"],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s5-3","Osteoradionecrosis (ORN)","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Toxicity","Osteoradionecrosis is exposed, devitalised irradiated bone that fails to heal over three months in the absence of tumour. It almost always affects the posterior body of the mandible , where radiation leaves the bone hypovascular, hypocellular and hypoxic, with radiation-induced fibrosis. The risk rises with doses above 60 Gy, dental extraction after radiotherapy, poor dentition, surgery on irradiated bone, smoking and alcohol. It presents with pain and exposed bone, trismus, an orocutaneous fistula or a pathological fracture, and recurrence must always be excluded by biopsy and imaging. Prevention is the key: a dental assessment before radiotherapy, extraction of unsalvageable teeth at least two weeks before starting, fluoride, and atraumatic dentistry afterwards. Established disease is treated conservatively with irrigation, antibiotics and removal of loose sequestra; medically with pentoxifylline and tocopherol, with or without clodronate (\"PENTOCLO\"), and hyperbaric oxygen in selected cases ( Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen ); and, when advanced, by resection and reconstruction with vascularised bone such as a fibula free flap."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s5-4","Care during treatment","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Toxicity","Before treatment the mouth is cleared of unsalvageable teeth, smoking is stopped, anaemia is corrected, and the dietitian, speech and swallowing therapist and nurse specialist see the patient, with a prophylactic gastrostomy for selected high-risk patients. Each week the weight, blood count (and renal function with cisplatin), mucositis, skin care and analgesia are reviewed, with psychological support and help to stop drinking. Admission is needed for dehydration, neutropenic sepsis, aspiration or uncontrolled pain."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s6-1","Benign and unusual tumours","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · Other tumours treated with radiation","Stereotactic radiosurgery controls growth in about 90 to 95% of small and residual vestibular schwannomas , and radiotherapy or radiosurgery halts the growth of about 90% of paragangliomas . Unresectable intracranial residual juvenile angiofibroma is irradiated and regresses slowly over years. Radiotherapy is central in sinonasal NK/T-cell lymphoma , combined with asparaginase-based chemotherapy, and is part of combined treatment for lymphoma of Waldeyer's ring and chloroma. Chordoma and chondrosarcoma of the skull base ( Skull base and the parapharyngeal space ) receive high-dose proton therapy after maximal safe resection, and olfactory neuroblastoma receives postoperative radiotherapy after craniofacial or endoscopic resection."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s7-1","Viva essentials","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The gray; the four R's; why fractionate; why gaps and anaemia are bad. IMRT and IGRT; what parotid sparing achieves; proton Bragg peak. Indications for postoperative radiotherapy, and when to add cisplatin (margins, extranodal extension). Chemoradiation: cisplatin and its ototoxicity; the roles of TPF induction, cetuximab and immunotherapy. Early and late toxicities; osteoradionecrosis and its prevention. PET-CT at 12 weeks to decide on neck dissection after chemoradiation."],["sec","cl:t-hnrt~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting radiotherapy before the teeth are assessed. Extracting teeth in an irradiated mandible without precautions. Long breaks in radiotherapy. Starting postoperative radiotherapy more than six weeks after surgery. Forgetting the TSH check and audiometry in follow-up."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s1-1","Subsites","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Overview","Their anatomy is described in Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx . Piriform sinus: on each side of the larynx, from the pharyngoepiglottic fold to the oesophagus. Postcricoid region: the anterior wall behind the cricoid lamina. Posterior pharyngeal wall: from the level of the hyoid to the cricoarytenoid joints. Figure 1 The three subsites of the hypopharynx, and an endoscopic view of the hypopharynx and larynx. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s1-2","Frequency and causes","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Overview","Common in India. More than 90% are squamous cell carcinomas. In one large Indian series the piriform sinus accounts for about 60%, the postcricoid region 30% and the posterior wall 10%; piriform sinus cancers predominate in North America, postcricoid cancers in parts of Europe. Tobacco and alcohol are the main causes; Plummer–Vinson (Paterson–Brown–Kelly) syndrome (iron deficiency anaemia, dysphagia, upper oesophageal webs, glossitis and koilonychia, in women of thirty to fifty) predisposes to postcricoid cancer."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s1-3","Benign tumours","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Overview","Exceptionally rare: papilloma, adenoma, lipoma, fibroma, leiomyoma, appearing as smooth, sometimes pedunculated masses."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s1-4","Behaviour","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Overview","Silent growth: the piriform sinus is roomy, so tumours become large before they obstruct. Submucosal spread: tumours extend well beyond their visible edge, so resection margins must be generous. Early lymphatic spread: 60 to 75% have palpable nodes at presentation, often bilateral, including retropharyngeal and paratracheal nodes. Second primaries: a synchronous oesophageal cancer is found in a significant minority; always examine the oesophagus ( Oesophagus and dysphagia ). Distant metastasis in about a fifth."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s2-1","Clinical features","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Carcinoma of the piriform sinus","Mostly men over forty. Early symptoms are few: a feeling of something sticking in the throat or a pricking on swallowing, localised to one side. Then pain on swallowing, referred otalgia (through the internal laryngeal and vagus nerves), progressive dysphagia, and a lump high in the neck that may be the first sign. Hoarseness and stridor indicate spread to the larynx, fixation of the hemilarynx or laryngeal oedema."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s2-2","Spread","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Carcinoma of the piriform sinus","Up to the vallecula and base of tongue. Down to the postcricoid region and oesophagus. Medially to the aryepiglottic fold, ventricle and paraglottic space (fixing the vocal fold). Laterally through the thyroid cartilage and thyrohyoid membrane into the thyroid gland and neck. Nodes: about three-quarters at presentation, half bilateral, to the upper and middle jugular nodes; nodes may appear long after the primary has been controlled. Pooling of saliva On flexible laryngoscopy, saliva pooling in one piriform sinus is a sign of obstruction there; ask the patient to puff the cheeks out to open the sinus and show the tumour."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s2-3","Treatment","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Carcinoma of the piriform sinus","Early tumours without nodes: radiotherapy (or chemoradiation), preserving the larynx, or selected transoral laser or robotic resection, or Ogura's extended partial laryngopharyngectomy, with neck treatment. Tumours confined to the piriform sinus but advanced: total laryngectomy with partial pharyngectomy, the remaining pharynx closed primarily, with neck dissection. Extension to the postcricoid region: total laryngopharyngectomy with neck dissection and reconstruction. Postoperative radiotherapy routinely, or chemoradiation for positive margins or extranodal spread. Many now treat advanced disease with concurrent chemoradiation, or with induction chemotherapy followed by chemoradiation in responders, to try to save the larynx, keeping surgery for salvage."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s3-1","Clinical features","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Carcinoma of the postcricoid region","Often women, sometimes young (in their twenties and thirties) with Plummer–Vinson syndrome, in about a third of cases. Progressive dysphagia, first for solids, with weight loss and malnutrition; voice change or aphonia when the tumour reaches the recurrent laryngeal nerve, the posterior cricoarytenoid muscle or the cricoarytenoid joint."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s3-2","Spread","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Carcinoma of the postcricoid region","Circumferentially (annular), causing tight stenosis; down into the cervical oesophagus; forwards into the arytenoids and cricoid; to the paratracheal nodes, which are not palpable, often on both sides."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s3-3","Signs","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Carcinoma of the postcricoid region","The tumour may be hidden on mirror examination; oedema and redness of the arytenoid region, pooling of saliva, and loss of laryngeal crepitus (the grating felt when the larynx is moved across the spine) suggest it. A lateral soft tissue radiograph shows a widened prevertebral shadow."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s3-4","Treatment","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Carcinoma of the postcricoid region","Prognosis is poor. Small tumours may be treated by radiotherapy or chemoradiation to preserve the larynx; most present late and need total laryngopharyngo-oesophagectomy with reconstruction by gastric pull-up or colon transposition, or a free jejunal graft if the oesophagus can be preserved below."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~c4","Posterior pharyngeal wall","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Posterior pharyngeal wall","The least common. Mostly men over fifty. Usually exophytic, sometimes ulcerative; it stays localised for a long time, then invades the prevertebral fascia, muscles and vertebrae. Nodal spread is often bilateral and retropharyngeal; about half have nodes at presentation. Dysphagia, spitting of blood, or a neck mass without other symptoms. Early lesions are treated by radiotherapy, or by excision through a lateral pharyngotomy (or transhyoid approach, or transorally) with repair, preserving the larynx; advanced lesions by laryngopharyngectomy and neck dissection. Fixation to the prevertebral fascia makes a tumour unresectable (T4b)."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s5-1","Examination","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Evaluation","Flexible laryngoscopy of the piriform sinuses, postcricoid region and posterior wall (puffing the cheeks opens the sinuses); vocal fold mobility; palpation of the larynx for crepitus and the neck for nodes, including the thyroid and the central compartment."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s5-2","Investigations","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Evaluation","Barium swallow: shows the lower limit of postcricoid and oesophageal disease, webs, and a second oesophageal tumour. Contrast CT of the neck and chest: the primary, cartilage and prevertebral invasion, and nodes; MRI for cartilage and soft tissue extent. PET-CT: distant metastases (lungs, liver, bone, mediastinal nodes) and second primaries. Panendoscopy under anaesthesia: biopsy, mapping of the extent (including submucosal spread) and a search for synchronous primaries, including oesophagoscopy. Thyroid function, haemoglobin and iron studies; nutrition and swallowing assessment."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s5-3","T staging","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Evaluation","T staging of hypopharyngeal cancer (AJCC 8th edition) Category Definition T1 One subsite and 2 cm or less T2 More than one subsite or an adjacent site, or over 2 cm up to 4 cm, without fixation of the hemilarynx T3 Over 4 cm, or fixation of the hemilarynx, or extension to the oesophageal mucosa T4a Thyroid or cricoid cartilage, hyoid, thyroid gland, oesophageal muscle or central compartment soft tissue T4b Prevertebral fascia, encasement of the carotid artery, or mediastinal structures"],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s6-1","Choosing","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Treatment","The decision balances cure against the larynx and swallowing: subsite, stage, nodal disease, the patient's fitness and wishes, and local expertise all count. T1 to T2, N0: radiotherapy (treating both sides of the neck and the retropharyngeal nodes), or larynx-preserving surgery (transoral laser or robotic resection, partial pharyngectomy, extended partial laryngopharyngectomy) with neck dissection. Larger T2 and T3, or any node-positive disease: concurrent chemoradiation; induction chemotherapy with response-guided treatment; or total laryngopharyngectomy with neck dissection and adjuvant therapy. T4a: usually total laryngopharyngectomy with neck dissection and postoperative (chemo)radiation; chemoradiation where the patient declines laryngectomy. Postcricoid tumours usually need laryngopharyngectomy, with oesophagectomy if the oesophagus is involved. Where the books differ · Surgery or larynx preservation Dhingra Advises total laryngectomy with partial pharyngectomy for piriform tumours, laryngopharyngectomy when the postcricoid region is involved, with routine postoperative radiotherapy, and radiotherapy for early tumours. Lee Gives a wider menu following the NCCN guidelines (induction chemotherapy, chemoradiation, transoral surgery, Ogura's partial laryngopharyngectomy) and notes that many prefer non-surgical treatment for larynx preservation. In practice Offer larynx preservation (induction chemotherapy or concurrent chemoradiation) to suitable patients with T2 to T3 disease who would otherwise need laryngectomy, and primary total laryngopharyngectomy for T4a disease wi"],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s6-2","Reconstruction","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Treatment","Partial pharyngeal defects: primary closure, or a pectoralis major, radial forearm or anterolateral thigh flap patch. Circumferential defects: a tubed fasciocutaneous free flap or a free jejunal graft. With oesophagectomy: gastric pull-up (or colon interposition). Voice is restored by tracheoesophageal puncture and valve after laryngectomy ( Cancer of the larynx )."],["ev","cl:t-hypoca~s6-3","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx: current evidence","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Treatment","Larynx preservation. In the EORTC 24891 trial in piriform sinus and aryepiglottic fold cancers, induction cisplatin and fluorouracil followed by radiotherapy in responders gave survival equivalent to laryngectomy with postoperative radiotherapy, preserving the larynx in about a third of survivors at ten years (Lefebvre et al., J Natl Cancer Inst 1996; Ann Oncol 2012). Adding docetaxel (TPF) to induction chemotherapy improved the larynx preservation rate over PF in the GORTEC 2000-01 trial (Pointreau et al., J Natl Cancer Inst 2009). Guidelines. The In practice line above follows the NCCN and European guidelines. Survival. Hypopharyngeal cancer still has the worst survival of the head and neck subsites, around 30 to 40% at five years overall, because most patients present with stage III or IV disease."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s6-3","Outcome","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · Treatment","Five-year survival is poor: roughly 20 to 50% for piriform sinus cancer, about a third for postcricoid cancer, and around a fifth for the posterior wall. One series reports a gross five-year cure rate of only 19% for posterior wall tumours. Distant metastases and second primaries cause many late deaths."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s7-1","Viva essentials","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Subsites of the hypopharynx and their relative frequency. Plummer–Vinson syndrome and postcricoid carcinoma. Why it presents late, submucosal spread and early nodes. Signs: pooling of saliva, loss of laryngeal crepitus, hemilarynx fixation. Investigations, including barium swallow and oesophagoscopy for a second primary. Treatment options and reconstruction (jejunum, gastric pull-up, flaps)."],["sec","cl:t-hypoca~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Carcinoma of the hypopharynx · For the examination","Common pitfalls Dismissing a localised, one-sided pricking on swallowing as globus. Missing a synchronous oesophageal cancer. Taking narrow margins in a tumour that spreads submucosally."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s1-1","Definition and scope","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Overview","Infection spreading from the middle ear and mastoid into the cranial cavity: extradural abscess, subdural empyema, meningitis, brain abscess, lateral (sigmoid) sinus thrombophlebitis and otitic hydrocephalus. Perforation, ossicular erosion, tympanosclerosis and atelectasis are sequelae of otitis media, not complications. Figure 1 The complications of suppurative otitis media and the routes of spread. Dhingra, fig. 12.1"],["sec","cl:t-icc~s1-2","Epidemiology","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Overview","Antibiotics have made these complications rare where care is good, but they remain important where chronic ears go untreated. Half of brain abscesses in adults and a quarter in children are otogenic, and a temporal lobe abscess is about twice as common as a cerebellar one."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s1-3","Acute or chronic disease","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Overview","Infants with acute otitis media tend to develop meningitis by blood-borne spread. Adults with chronic otitis media, especially cholesteatoma, develop abscesses and sinus thrombosis by bone erosion. Where the books differ · How often there is more than one Dhingra About one-third of patients with otogenic meningitis have another intracranial lesion. Lee About half. In practice Look for a second complication in every patient."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s2-1","Routes of spread","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Pathogenesis","2.1.1 Direct bone erosion Cholesteatoma, osteitis and granulation destroy the tegmen or the sinus plate, bringing infection to the dura. 2.1.2 Venous thrombophlebitis Infected thrombus in small veins crosses intact bone, which is why an abscess can arise without visible erosion. 2.1.3 Preformed pathways Congenital dehiscences, a patent petrosquamous suture or Hyrtl's fissure in children, inner ear malformations, fractures, previous surgery, and the round and oval windows via the labyrinth."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s2-2","Risk factors","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Pathogenesis","Extremes of age, poverty and poor access to care, virulent or resistant organisms, inadequate antibiotic courses, immunosuppression, preformed defects and cholesteatoma. Figure 2 Axial relations of the middle ear and mastoid: the dura, sigmoid sinus and labyrinth that infection can reach. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-icc~s2-3","Organisms","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Pathogenesis","Mixed: Staphylococcus aureus , streptococci, Proteus , Pseudomonas and E. coli , with anaerobes such as Bacteroides , particularly in abscesses."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s3-1","Extradural abscess","Intracranial complications of otitis media · The complications","3.1.1 Picture Pus between the bone and the dura of the middle or posterior fossa. Often silent and found at mastoidectomy; sometimes a deep, one-sided headache that eases when pus flows from the ear. 3.1.2 Treatment At mastoidectomy, remove bone until healthy dura is exposed. If pus is suspected behind an intact plate, take the plate down."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s3-2","Subdural empyema","Intracranial complications of otitis media · The complications","3.2.1 Picture Pus in the subdural space spreads quickly over the hemisphere: severe headache, fever, meningism, seizures, aphasia and rapidly progressive hemiplegia, with raised pressure. 3.2.2 Treatment A neurosurgical emergency: urgent drainage through burr holes or craniotomy, with high-dose antibiotics."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s3-3","Meningitis","Intracranial complications of otitis media · The complications","3.3.1 Picture The commonest complication. Headache, fever, photophobia, vomiting, neck stiffness, and positive Kernig and Brudzinski signs; later, drowsiness, fits and coma. 3.3.2 CSF Turbid, under raised pressure, with many polymorphs, high protein and low sugar. 3.3.3 Treatment Intravenous antibiotics that penetrate the CSF, and steroids. Treat the ear: myringotomy for acute otitis media, mastoidectomy for chronic disease once the patient is fit. After meningitis Check hearing early. Labyrinthitis ossificans can close the cochlea within weeks, and a cochlear implant must go in before it does ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants )."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s3-4","Brain abscess","Intracranial complications of otitis media · The complications","3.4.1 Stages Where the books differ · Stages of an otogenic brain abscess Dhingra Four stages: invasion (initial encephalitis), localisation (the latent, quiet stage), enlargement, and termination by rupture into the ventricle or subarachnoid space. Lee Three stages: encephalitis, a quiet period, and rupture. In practice The quiet stage is the trap: the patient seems better while the abscess is forming. 3.4.2 Raised intracranial pressure Morning headache, projectile vomiting, papilloedema, a slow pulse and a subnormal temperature, with drowsiness. 3.4.3 Temporal lobe abscess Nominal aphasia (with a dominant-side lesion), a contralateral upper quadrant field defect, contralateral weakness, and uncinate fits with hallucinations of taste and smell. An ipsilateral third nerve palsy with a dilated pupil warns of tentorial herniation. 3.4.4 Cerebellar abscess Suboccipital headache, nystagmus, ipsilateral ataxia with past-pointing, intention tremor and dysdiadochokinesia, and early papilloedema. Figure 3 First, CT of a left cerebellar abscess. Second, CT of a right otogenic cerebral (temporal lobe) abscess. Dhingra, figs 12.6 and 12.7 3.4.5 CSF Moderately raised cells and protein with normal sugar; but a lumbar puncture is dangerous and is avoided while there is mass effect. 3.4.6 Treatment Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics with anaerobic cover for six to eight weeks, steroids or mannitol for raised pressure, and neurosurgical drainage by aspiration through a burr hole or excision. The ear is then made safe through a separate operative field. Where the books differ · Timing o"],["sec","cl:t-icc~s3-5","Lateral sinus thrombophlebitis","Intracranial complications of otitis media · The complications","3.5.1 Picture Inflammation of the sigmoid sinus wall, mural thrombus, then an infected clot. The fever is the clue: high, swinging, picket-fence fever with rigors and well periods in between, like malaria but irregular. 3.5.2 Signs Griesinger's sign: oedema over the mastoid emissary vein. Tobey-Ayer test: compressing the internal jugular vein on the healthy side raises CSF pressure, but compressing the affected side does not. Crowe-Beck test: compressing the healthy jugular vein engorges the retinal veins. Papilloedema, more often with a right-sided thrombus as the right sinus is usually dominant. Tenderness along the jugular vein; septic emboli to the lungs, bones and joints. 3.5.3 Investigations Blood culture during a rigor, a smear to exclude malaria, and contrast CT (the empty delta sign ) or MR venography. 3.5.4 Treatment Intravenous antibiotics and mastoidectomy with exposure of the sinus. If it is needle-aspirated and yields pus or no blood, it is opened between packs placed above and below, and infected clot is removed; healthy clot is left alone. Anticoagulation is increasingly used: systematic reviews find it safe in otogenic thrombosis and associated with recanalisation, but there are no randomised trials. Ligation of the internal jugular vein, or thrombectomy, is rarely needed: it is kept for septic emboli or clot spreading down the neck."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s3-6","Otitic hydrocephalus","Intracranial complications of otitis media · The complications","3.6.1 Picture Raised intracranial pressure with normal CSF and normal-sized ventricles, usually after lateral sinus thrombosis that blocks venous outflow. Headache, vomiting, blurred vision, papilloedema and a sixth nerve palsy. 3.6.2 CSF Pressure over 300 mm of water, with normal contents. 3.6.3 Treatment Treat the ear and the sinus; lower the pressure with acetazolamide, steroids and mannitol, with repeated lumbar punctures or a shunt if needed. Serial eye examinations are essential, because the danger is blindness from optic atrophy."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s4-1","Imaging","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Investigations","4.1.1 Contrast CT of the brain and temporal bone The first study: abscess, empyema, bone erosion and the delta sign. 4.1.2 MRI with contrast and MR venography More sensitive for early cerebritis, subdural collections and sinus thrombosis."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s4-2","Lumbar puncture","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Investigations","Only when imaging has excluded mass effect and raised pressure. CSF by complication Complication Pressure Cells Sugar Meningitis Raised Very high, polymorphs Low Brain abscess Raised Moderate Normal Sinus thrombosis Raised Normal Normal Otitic hydrocephalus Over 300 mm H2O Normal Normal"],["sec","cl:t-icc~s4-3","Other tests","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Investigations","Blood cultures, full blood count, ear swab, audiogram when possible. Which complication? Complication Clue Next step Meningitis Neck stiffness CT, then lumbar puncture Temporal abscess Aphasia, field defect MRI, drain Cerebellar abscess Ataxia, nystagmus MRI, drain Sinus thrombosis Picket-fence fever MR venography Otitic hydrocephalus Papilloedema, clear CSF MRV, lower pressure"],["sec","cl:t-icc~s5-1","The sequence","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Management","On call: discharging ear with fever and headache Airway, breathing, circulation; treat sepsis. Full neurological examination, including fundi and cranial nerves. Contrast CT of brain and temporal bones, then MRI and MRV. Start intravenous antibiotics with CSF penetration and anaerobic cover. Call neurosurgery early. No lumbar puncture until mass effect and raised pressure are excluded."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s5-2","Treating the ear","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Management","Acute otitis media: myringotomy and grommet. Chronic disease or cholesteatoma: mastoidectomy to remove the source, once the intracranial problem is controlled and the patient is fit."],["ev","cl:t-icc~s5-3","Intracranial complications of otitis media: current evidence","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Management","Imaging. CT and MRI with MR venography are complementary; image before lumbar puncture when there are focal signs or reduced consciousness. Sigmoid sinus thrombosis. Evidence for anticoagulation comes from systematic reviews of observational series. Prevention. Mastoiditis and intracranial complications have become rare since antibiotics; most cases now arise from cholesteatoma or untreated chronic disease."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s5-3","Outcomes","Intracranial complications of otitis media · Management","Deafness, epilepsy, focal deficits, loss of vision and death. Rupture of an abscess into the ventricle carries a mortality of about 80%."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s6-1","Viva essentials","Intracranial complications of otitis media · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Routes of spread: erosion, thrombophlebitis, preformed pathways. Stages of brain abscess, and the danger of the quiet stage. Temporal versus cerebellar abscess signs. Lateral sinus thrombosis: picket-fence fever, Griesinger's sign, Tobey-Ayer and Crowe-Beck tests, delta sign. CSF in each complication. Otitic hydrocephalus: raised pressure, normal CSF, risk of blindness."],["sec","cl:t-icc~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Intracranial complications of otitis media · For the examination","Common pitfalls Doing a lumbar puncture before imaging. Being reassured by the quiet stage of an abscess. Treating swinging fever as malaria without examining the ear. Stopping at one complication and missing a second. Forgetting a hearing test after meningitis."],["sec","cl:t-im_ca~s1-1","CT, MRI and PET-CT","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · Choosing the scan","Contrast CT of the neck and chest is quick, reproducible and tolerated by patients with secretions or pain; it is the usual first staging study for the larynx, hypopharynx and most of the neck. MRI is preferred for the nasopharynx (skull base and intracranial spread), the oral cavity and oropharynx (amalgam, depth of invasion, small tonsillar primaries), the sinonasal tract (tumour versus secretions, orbit and dura) and for perineural spread ; in the larynx it is kept for equivocal cartilage invasion. PET-CT is used for advanced disease (to find distant metastases and second primaries), for the unknown primary before panendoscopy, and after treatment. A CT of the chest is part of every staging work-up for squamous carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-im_ca~s2-1","The core questions","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · The primary","The staging rules themselves are in Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles . Describe the subsite of origin, the size in three planes (and, in the oral cavity, the depth of invasion), and then go through the structures that change the stage or the operation for that site. These are the T4 structures: for a T4a tumour the disease is still usually resectable; for a T4b tumour (encasing the carotid, invading the prevertebral fascia or muscles, the skull base or the mediastinum, or the masticator space and pterygoid plates for some sites) it usually is not. Key structures to report by site Site Report specifically Nasopharynx Parapharyngeal space, skull base (clivus, foramen lacerum, foramen ovale), cavernous sinus, intracranial spread, retropharyngeal nodes Oral cavity Depth of invasion, lingual septum, neurovascular bundles, mandible cortex and marrow, inferior alveolar canal, skin Oropharynx Tongue base and vallecula, soft palate, parapharyngeal space, pterygoids, mandible, larynx, carotid Larynx Pre-epiglottic and paraglottic fat, commissures, subglottis, cartilage, extralaryngeal tissues, cord movement Hypopharynx Pyriform apex, postcricoid, oesophagus, thyroid and cricoid cartilage, prevertebral fascia Sinonasal Orbit (periorbita), anterior skull base and dura, pterygopalatine fossa, infratemporal fossa, skin Figure 1 Nasopharyngeal carcinoma. Left: coronal CT, an asymmetric bulky nasopharyngeal mass. Right: sagittal T1, the tumour extends up into the sphenoid and sella and replaces the bright fatty marrow of the clivus, the sign of skull base invasion. Koc"],["sec","cl:t-im_ca~s2-2","Perineural spread","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · The primary","Perineural tumour often causes no symptoms and is easily missed, but it changes the surgical margin and the radiotherapy field. Follow the nerves at risk for each site: V2 (palate, maxilla, cheek) through the pterygopalatine fossa and foramen rotundum; V3 (masticator space, retromolar trigone) through the foramen ovale; the facial nerve (parotid, skin of the face) through the stylomastoid foramen; the hypoglossal nerve (tongue base, skull base). Look for nerve enlargement and enhancement, lost fat at a foramen, widened foramina and denervation of muscle. Figure 3 Perineural spread. Left: drawing of tumour following a nerve through the skull base. Right: coronal post-gadolinium MRI in maxillary carcinoma, a grossly enlarged, enhancing V2 in the foramen rotundum compared with the normal side. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 536 Figure 4 The same pattern on CT. Left: loss of the fat in the right pterygopalatine fossa compared with the left. Right: bone CT, smooth widening of the right foramen rotundum and pterygopalatine fossa. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 536"],["sec","cl:t-im_ca~s3-1","Nodes","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · The nodes and beyond","Go through every level on both sides and the retropharyngeal nodes, as in the nodes lesson ( Lymph nodes ), and report the number, size, side and level of abnormal nodes, any necrosis , and any extranodal extension . Radiological extranodal extension (ill-defined margins, infiltration of fat, muscle or skin) matters: in the eighth edition of the TNM, clinically and radiologically overt extranodal extension makes HPV-negative nodal disease N3b. Report carotid contact in degrees: 270° or more suggests encasement. HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer is staged with its own, more favourable system. Figure 5 Extranodal extension. Left: pathological left neck nodes from a tongue base primary, the level IIB node with ill-defined contours. Right: a large level II nodal mass from a tongue cancer infiltrating the surrounding soft tissues. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 544 Figure 6 Advanced extranodal disease. Left: tumour surrounding the left common carotid and thickening the skin. Right: CT angiography showing outpouchings of a weakened carotid wall, a warning of carotid blowout. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 544"],["sec","cl:t-im_ca~s3-2","Distant disease and second primaries","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · The nodes and beyond","At staging, look at the lung apices , the bones (including the mandible and spine) and the mediastinal nodes on every neck scan, and review the chest CT. Patients with smoking-related cancers carry a high risk of a second primary in the upper aerodigestive tract, lung or oesophagus, most often with hypopharyngeal cancer; PET-CT helps to find them."],["ev","cl:t-im_ca~s4-1","Reading a head and neck cancer scan: current evidence","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · The unknown primary","Staging. NCCN and UK multidisciplinary guidance recommend contrast CT or MRI of the primary and neck, chest CT for advanced disease, and FDG PET-CT for stage III to IV disease, unknown primaries and suspected recurrence. Unknown primary. PET-CT before panendoscopy finds the primary in about a third of patients whose examination is negative. After treatment. The 12-week PET-CT is covered in the post-treatment lesson ( The treated neck )."],["sec","cl:t-im_ca~s4-1","A sequence that finds the primary","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · The unknown primary","When squamous carcinoma is found in a neck node and examination is normal, imaging is done before panendoscopy and biopsy, so that biopsy changes do not confuse it. Contrast CT or MRI looks for asymmetry at the usual hiding places (the tonsils, the tongue base, the nasopharynx and the pyriform apex), and PET-CT finds a further proportion. HPV and EBV status of the node points to the oropharynx or the nasopharynx. The search ends with panendoscopy, directed biopsies and often tonsillectomy and tongue base mucosectomy. Figure 7 Unknown primary. Left: a unilateral necrotic level IIA node; panendoscopy was negative but directed biopsy of the glossotonsillar sulcus found the primary. Right: a cystic, rim-enhancing level II node from an occult lingual tonsil carcinoma. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 543 Reading a head and neck cancer staging scan 1 Primary subsite size in three planes depth (oral cavity) crossing the midline 2 Local extent site-specific T4 structures cartilage, bone, skull base, orbit, dura parapharyngeal space and pterygoids 3 Perineural nerves at risk for the site foramina and fat pads denervation 4 Vessels carotid contact in degrees jugular vein 5 Nodes every level both sides and retropharyngeal size, necrosis, extranodal extension 6 Beyond the neck lung apices and chest CT bones mediastinum second primary 7 Summary TNM stage resectability what would change the plan (biopsy, PET-CT, MRI)"],["sec","cl:t-im_ca~s5-1","Viva essentials","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Which scan for which site: CT for larynx and neck, MRI for nasopharynx, oral cavity, oropharynx and sinonasal, PET-CT for advanced and unknown primary. A routine: primary, local extent, perineural, vessels, nodes, chest and second primary. T4a versus T4b: what makes a tumour unresectable. Perineural spread: the nerves at risk for each site and the signs. Extranodal extension and carotid encasement. The unknown primary: imaging before panendoscopy, PET-CT, HPV and EBV."],["sec","cl:t-im_ca~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Reading a head and neck cancer scan · For the examination","Common pitfalls Staging a nasopharyngeal cancer on CT alone. Forgetting the chest, the bones and the second primary. Missing perineural spread because the post-contrast images were not fat-suppressed. Imaging the unknown primary after biopsies have distorted the mucosa."],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s1-1","Hounsfield units","CT for the ENT surgeon · What a CT image is","CT measures how much each small volume of tissue (a voxel ) absorbs X-rays, and expresses it on the Hounsfield scale , where water is 0 and air is −1000. Fat is negative (about −100), soft tissues sit a little above water (muscle 40 to 50), fresh blood is denser (60 to 80), iodinated contrast in vessels reaches 100 to 300, and bone ranges from a few hundred to over 1000. Placing a region of interest on a lesion and reading its number is often the quickest way to tell fat from fluid, or fluid from blood. Figure 1 The Hounsfield scale and three common windows. The window decides which densities are shown as grey: everything below it is black and everything above it white. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s1-2","Windows","CT for the ENT surgeon · What a CT image is","A screen can show only about 256 shades of grey, and the eye distinguishes far fewer, so the radiologist chooses a window : a level (the centre value) and a width (the range displayed). A bone window (level about 500 to 700, width 2000 to 4000) shows ossicles, the otic capsule, the skull base foramina and thin sinus walls, but makes all soft tissue look the same grey. A soft-tissue window (level about 40 to 60, width 350 to 400) separates muscle, fat, nodes, tumour and abscess, but turns bone uniformly white. A wide lung window is used for the airway and the chest. Every CT must be read in both bone and soft-tissue windows, because each hides what the other shows: an orbital abscess is invisible on bone windows, and a dehiscent lamina papyracea is invisible on soft-tissue windows. Figure 2 The same patient in three settings. (a) Bone window: intact lamina papyracea (1) with ethmoid and sphenoid opacification. (b) Soft-tissue window: an orbital mass (1) compressing the medial rectus (2). (c) After contrast: the capsule of a subperiosteal abscess enhances (1) around fluid (2). Dunnebier, Imaging for Otolaryngologists Read the window before the scan Before interpreting any image, look at the window settings in the corner. A \"normal\" temporal bone in a soft-tissue window, or a \"normal\" neck in a bone window, means only that the wrong window was used."],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s2-1","Bone and soft-tissue reconstructions","CT for the ENT surgeon · How the images are made","The raw data from the scanner can be reconstructed with different mathematical filters ( kernels ). A bone (sharp, high-resolution) algorithm gives crisp edges and fine detail at the cost of noise, and is used for the temporal bone, sinuses and facial skeleton. A soft-tissue (smooth) algorithm reduces noise and shows subtle density differences, and is used for the neck. A good temporal bone or sinus study is therefore reconstructed twice, in a bone algorithm for the bones and a soft-tissue algorithm for the soft tissues, and each is then viewed in its own window."],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s2-2","Slice thickness and multiplanar reformats","CT for the ENT surgeon · How the images are made","Modern multidetector scanners acquire a volume of very thin data (0.5 to 0.6 mm) in one pass, from which images can be rebuilt in any plane. Temporal bone studies use slices of about 0.5 to 0.6 mm, with axial and coronal reformats, and often oblique planes along the cochlea or the semicircular canals (Pöschl and Stenvers views). Sinus studies use about 1 mm in axial, coronal and sagittal planes; the coronal plane mirrors the surgeon's endoscopic view, and the sagittal plane shows the frontal recess. Neck studies use 2 to 3 mm soft-tissue slices with thinner bone images of the skull base, mandible and larynx where needed. Because the volume can be reformatted, the patient needs to be scanned only once; direct coronal scanning with the neck extended is no longer needed and is avoided in trauma. Figure 3 Coronal bone-algorithm CT of the middle ear in thin section: the tympanic segment of the facial nerve, the oval window and stapes, the scutum and Prussak's space are all visible. Detail like this needs submillimetre slices. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 84"],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s2-3","Scrolling, not snapshots","CT for the ENT surgeon · How the images are made","On the workstation, anatomy is learnt by scrolling through the stack and following each structure from slice to slice, not by looking at single images. Follow the facial nerve from the internal auditory canal to the stylomastoid foramen, or the uncinate process from top to bottom, and the anatomy becomes three-dimensional."],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s3-1","Iodinated contrast","CT for the ENT surgeon · Contrast","Intravenous iodinated contrast opacifies vessels and enhances tissues with a blood supply. Contrast-enhanced CT is the standard for neck masses, cancer staging, nodes, abscesses and suspected vascular lesions. Many neck protocols use a split bolus or a delay of 60 to 90 seconds after injection, so that both arteries and mucosa enhance and tumours stand out from muscle. CT angiography times the scan to the arterial phase to show vessels, for trauma, paragangliomas, pulsatile tinnitus and bleeding. Contrast is not needed for routine sinus or temporal bone imaging, where the question is about bone and air. Before contrast, ask about previous reactions, renal function and metformin, and remember that iodine interferes with radioactive iodine treatment of thyroid cancer for weeks. What to request Question Study Chronic rhinosinusitis before surgery ( Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps ) Non-contrast CT sinuses, thin bone algorithm, three planes (or cone-beam CT) Cholesteatoma ( Cholesteatoma ), conductive loss, trauma to the ear ( Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea ) Non-contrast thin-section CT temporal bones Neck mass ( Approach to a lump in the neck ), cancer staging, nodes Contrast-enhanced CT neck (or MRI) Deep neck abscess ( Deep neck space infection ) or orbital complication ( Proptosis and the orbit ) Contrast-enhanced CT Pulsatile tinnitus ( Approach to tinnitus ), vascular tumour, trauma to vessels CT angiography (and venography) Facial fracture ( Nasal and facial fractures ) Non-contrast thin CT with 3D reconstructions"],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s4-1","Cone-beam CT","CT for the ENT surgeon · Cone-beam CT and dose","Cone-beam CT uses a cone of X-rays and a flat detector rotating once around the head, usually with the patient seated. It gives excellent bony detail of the sinuses, temporal bone and teeth at a lower dose than conventional CT, and suffers less from metal artefact around cochlear implants. Its soft-tissue contrast is poor, Hounsfield values are unreliable, and motion degrades it, so it cannot replace conventional CT for soft tissue, infection or tumour."],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s4-2","Radiation","CT for the ENT surgeon · Cone-beam CT and dose","A sinus or temporal bone CT gives a modest effective dose, but the lens of the eye lies in the field of sinus and orbital scans, and children are more sensitive to radiation. Use low-dose protocols for sinus CT, avoid repeat scans that will not change management, and prefer ultrasound or MRI in children when they can answer the question."],["ev","cl:t-im_ct~s5-1","CT for the ENT surgeon: current evidence","CT for the ENT surgeon · Artefacts","Radiation risk. In a UK cohort, children who received a cumulative CT dose of about 50 mGy to the marrow or 60 mGy to the brain had roughly three times the risk of leukaemia or brain tumour, though the absolute risk remained small (Pearce et al., Lancet 2012). Cone-beam CT is increasingly used in clinic for that reason."],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s5-1","The common artefacts","CT for the ENT surgeon · Artefacts","CT artefacts in head and neck imaging Artefact Cause Effect and remedy Streak (beam hardening) Dental amalgam, implants, dense bone Dark and bright streaks across the oral cavity and oropharynx; angle the gantry, use metal-reduction software, or MRI Motion Swallowing, breathing, a restless child Blurred or doubled edges, especially the larynx; repeat with breath-hold or quiet breathing Partial volume A thick slice containing two tissues Thin bony plates (lamina papyracea, tegmen, stapes) appear absent; use thin slices before calling a dehiscence Noise Low dose, large patient Grainy images; thicker reconstructions or a higher dose Positioning Head tilted Asymmetry that mimics disease; compare like with like and reformat Figure 4 Dental amalgam artefact hiding a left oral tongue cancer on CT; MRI showed the tumour. ESR Modern Radiology, Head and Neck Imaging Do not over-call a dehiscence Thin bony plates such as the tegmen, the bone over the superior semicircular canal and the lamina papyracea can disappear on CT through partial volume averaging. Confirm on thin slices in more than one plane, and ideally on reformats along the structure (Pöschl view for the superior canal), before diagnosing a dehiscence ( Superior canal dehiscence ). Reading any CT, every time 1 Check the basics patient name and date, region, contrast or not, slice thickness, planes available 2 Check the windows bone window for bone and air soft-tissue window for soft tissue adjust if needed 3 Scroll the whole stack follow structures slice to slice compare left with right 4 Use the planes axial, coronal and "],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s6-1","Viva essentials","CT for the ENT surgeon · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Hounsfield units of air, fat, water, soft tissue, blood and bone. Windows (level and width), and why every CT needs bone and soft-tissue viewing. Bone versus soft-tissue reconstruction kernels. Slice thickness and planes for temporal bone, sinus and neck studies. When to give contrast, and when it adds nothing. Cone-beam CT: advantages and limits. Artefacts , especially dental amalgam and partial volume."],["sec","cl:t-im_ct~s6-2","Common pitfalls","CT for the ENT surgeon · For the examination","Common pitfalls Reading a temporal bone in a soft-tissue window, or a neck in a bone window. Requesting contrast for chronic sinusitis, or forgetting it for a neck abscess. Calling a dehiscence on one thick slice. Missing an oral cavity cancer hidden by dental artefact without asking for MRI."],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s1-1","Space or structure of origin","Describing a lesion and reading a report · Where is it?","Every lesion is described first by where it arises: a mucosal surface (pharynx, larynx, sinus lining), a deep space of the neck, a gland, a bone, a nerve, a vessel or a node. In the neck, the deep spaces are separated by layers of deep cervical fascia, and each space contains a limited set of structures, so each has a short list of possible lesions. The neck-by-spaces lesson ( The neck by spaces ) teaches the map; here, the principle is enough: a mass is assumed to arise from the space it is centred in, and the way it displaces the surrounding fat and muscle confirms the space. Figure 1 A masticator space tumour. Left: T1 MRI, the mass replaces the fat planes of the muscles of mastication and pushes the parapharyngeal fat posteromedially. Right: the same pattern drawn: knowing which way the fat moves tells you where the mass began. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 43"],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s1-2","Laterality, size and extent","Describing a lesion and reading a report · Where is it?","Give the side, the maximum size in three planes, and the borders the lesion reaches or crosses: the midline, the skull base, the orbit, the larynx's framework, the carotid. Size matters for staging, and extent for surgery."],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s2-1","Margins and shape","Describing a lesion and reading a report · What is it like?","Well defined, smooth margins suggest a slow-growing or benign lesion (cyst, schwannoma, pleomorphic adenoma). Ill-defined, infiltrative margins suggest malignancy or infection. Lobulated, dumbbell or tubular shapes along a nerve suggest a nerve sheath tumour."],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s2-2","Density, signal and content","Describing a lesion and reading a report · What is it like?","On CT describe density compared with muscle (low, equal, high), and look for fat (lipoma, dermoid), fluid (cyst, abscess, necrosis), calcification (chondroid tumours, thyroid, phleboliths in a venous malformation) and air (abscess, fistula). On MRI give T1 and T2 signal, fat suppression, and diffusion. Fluid–fluid levels suggest haemorrhage in a lymphatic malformation."],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s2-3","Enhancement","Describing a lesion and reading a report · What is it like?","Describe whether the lesion enhances, how much and in what pattern: none (simple cyst, cholesteatoma), rim (abscess, necrotic node, infected cyst), avid and uniform (paraganglioma, haemangioma, schwannoma), heterogeneous (larger tumours with necrosis), flow voids (high-flow vascular tumours: the \"salt and pepper\" of paraganglioma)."],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s3-1","Bone: remodelling or destruction","Describing a lesion and reading a report · What is it doing?","The behaviour of adjacent bone is one of the most useful clues on CT. Remodelling means the bone is expanded, thinned and bowed but still present, with a smooth margin; it implies slow growth (mucocoele, polyposis, schwannoma, juvenile angiofibroma, many benign tumours). Destruction means the bone is eroded and missing, with irregular, permeative or moth-eaten margins; it implies aggressive disease (carcinoma, aggressive infection such as invasive fungal sinusitis ( Fungal sinusitis ) or necrotising otitis externa ( Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis ), metastasis). Smooth enlargement of a foramen suggests a nerve sheath tumour; irregular erosion of the foramen suggests malignancy. Figure 2 Remodelling. A large ethmoid mucocele expands the sinus and thins and bows its walls, including the lamina papyracea, without aggressive destruction. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 716 Figure 3 Destruction. Squamous cell carcinoma of the maxillary antrum breaks through the sinus walls into the cheek and the infratemporal fossa. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 754 Figure 4 Smooth enlargement of the foramen ovale by a trigeminal (V3) schwannoma, with an enhancing tubular tumour following the nerve. A benign nerve sheath tumour widens a foramen; carcinoma erodes it irregularly. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 56"],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s3-2","Nerves, vessels and airway","Describing a lesion and reading a report · What is it doing?","Look for perineural spread : enlargement and enhancement of a nerve, loss of the fat pad at its foramen (pterygopalatine fossa, stylomastoid foramen, foramen ovale), widening of the foramen, and denervation changes in the muscles it supplies (atrophy and fatty change, or acute oedema and enhancement). Describe the carotid : displaced, abutted or encased (more than 270° of contact suggests encasement and usually unresectability). Note any narrowing of the airway . Figure 5 Perineural spread along the mandibular nerve. Left: post-gadolinium fat-suppressed T1, the nerve is thickened and enhancing and the foramen ovale widened. Right: the pattern drawn, tumour passing through the skull base along the nerve. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 43"],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s3-3","Nodes","Describing a lesion and reading a report · What is it doing?","Every head and neck study includes the nodes ( Lymph nodes ). Name the level, size (short axis), shape, and features of malignancy: necrosis (the most reliable sign), round shape, loss of the fatty hilum, extranodal extension (irregular margins, fat stranding, invasion of muscle), and, in thyroid cancer, cystic change and calcification."],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s4-1","The three filters","Describing a lesion and reading a report · Building the differential","A differential diagnosis is built by passing the findings through three filters: location (what normally lives in this space or structure), imaging character (cystic or solid, fat, calcification, enhancement pattern, bone behaviour) and clinical context (age, symptoms, immune status, known cancer, previous surgery or radiotherapy). Give two to four diagnoses in order of likelihood, and say what would separate them: a further sequence, a biopsy, a blood test. A description in order Step Example Site and space Left parapharyngeal space, centred in the prestyloid compartment Size and extent 3.4 × 2.8 × 4.1 cm, abutting the skull base, no intracranial extension Margins and shape Smooth, lobulated, well defined Content Low density on CT; bright on T2; no fat or calcification Enhancement Heterogeneous moderate Effect on neighbours Displaces the parapharyngeal fat medially; widens the stylomandibular gap; carotid displaced posteriorly, not encased; no bone erosion Nodes None enlarged Differential Deep lobe pleomorphic adenoma most likely; minor salivary tumour; schwannoma less likely as the carotid is displaced posteriorly"],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s5-1","The structure of a report","Describing a lesion and reading a report · Reading a report","A radiology report gives the clinical details and question, the technique (modality, contrast, sequences), the comparison studies, the findings (usually organised by structure or space), and the impression , which should answer the clinical question and give the most important findings first. Standardised systems help: TI-RADS and the U grading for thyroid nodules, NI-RADS for the treated neck, Lund–Mackay scoring for sinus CT, and TNM stage for cancer."],["ev","cl:t-im_desc~s5-2","Describing a lesion and reading a report: current evidence","Describing a lesion and reading a report · Reading a report","Structured reporting. Structured templates and lexicons improve completeness and reduce ambiguity; the ACR Neck Imaging Reporting and Data System (NI-RADS) scores post-treatment scans from 1 (no evidence of recurrence) to 4 (definite recurrence), each linked to a management step (Aiken et al., J Am Coll Radiol 2018). Thyroid nodules (TI-RADS, U grading) and salivary cytology (Milan system) have similar systems."],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s5-2","Reading it as a surgeon","Describing a lesion and reading a report · Reading a report","Read the images yourself before or alongside the report; you know the patient and the operation. Check that the question was answered, that the whole region was covered, and that the findings fit the clinical picture. Discrepancies should be discussed with the radiologist, ideally in a multidisciplinary meeting. Describing any lesion 1 Where side space, structure or organ of origin size in three planes extent and borders crossed 2 What margins and shape density or signal fat, fluid, calcification, air enhancement pattern diffusion 3 What it does bone remodelled or destroyed nerves and foramina carotid and jugular airway orbit and dura 4 Nodes levels, size, necrosis, extranodal extension 5 Differential two to four diagnoses by likelihood what would separate them"],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s6-1","Viva essentials","Describing a lesion and reading a report · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Describe in order: site, size, margins, content, enhancement, effect, nodes, differential. Space of origin as the key to the differential. Remodelling versus destruction of bone. Perineural spread: the signs. Carotid encasement: more than 270°. Malignant node features: necrosis, shape, hilum, extranodal extension."],["sec","cl:t-im_desc~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Describing a lesion and reading a report · For the examination","Common pitfalls Jumping to a diagnosis before saying where the lesion is. Calling bone \"eroded\" when it is smoothly remodelled. Forgetting the nodes and the nerves on a cancer scan. Reading the impression only."],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s1-1","The normal gland and its relations","Thyroid and salivary glands · The thyroid","The thyroid wraps the front of the trachea from the level of the cricoid down, with the isthmus across the second to fourth tracheal rings. On ultrasound it is homogeneous and brighter than the strap muscles; on CT it is dense even before contrast because of its iodine, and enhances avidly. The carotid and jugular vein lie laterally, the oesophagus behind the left lobe, and the tracheoesophageal grooves hold the recurrent laryngeal nerves, the paratracheal (level VI) nodes and the parathyroid glands. Figure 1 Coronal contrast CT: the chevron-shaped thyroid in the visceral space, wrapping the trachea below the larynx. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 369"],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s1-2","Grading a nodule","Thyroid and salivary glands · The thyroid","The clinical pathway is in Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre . Ultrasound grades a nodule's risk by its features. The UK U grading (U1 normal, U2 benign, U3 indeterminate, U4 suspicious, U5 malignant) and the American TI-RADS (TR1 to TR5, scored by points) use the same features: Ultrasound features of thyroid nodules Feature Reassuring Suspicious Composition Cystic, spongiform Solid Echogenicity Iso- or hyperechoic, with a halo Hypoechoic, especially markedly hypoechoic Shape Wider than tall Taller than wide on transverse view Margin Smooth, well defined Lobulated, irregular, extrathyroidal extension Echogenic foci Comet-tail artefact (colloid), eggshell rim Microcalcifications , interrupted rim calcification Nodes Normal Round, cystic or calcified nodes in levels III, IV, VI Under the British Thyroid Association guidance, U3 to U5 nodules are sampled by FNA; under ACR TI-RADS the size threshold for FNA falls as the score rises (for example, 1 cm for TR5 and 2.5 cm for TR3). Cytology is reported in the Thy (UK) or Bethesda categories. Figure 2 Differentiated thyroid carcinoma on ultrasound: a well-defined nodule with many tiny hyperechoic microcalcifications. FNA showed papillary carcinoma. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 238"],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s1-3","When to add CT or MRI","Thyroid and salivary glands · The thyroid","Ultrasound cannot see behind the sternum or the trachea. CT (or MRI) is added for a retrosternal goitre (its lowest extent, tracheal narrowing and deviation, and whether it can be delivered through the neck), for a cancer that may invade the trachea, larynx, oesophagus or recurrent nerve , and for nodes in the mediastinum or behind the pharynx. In differentiated thyroid cancer, iodinated contrast delays radioactive iodine treatment by weeks, so the need for contrast is discussed with the team; MRI avoids the problem. Figure 3 Multinodular goitre. Left: a chest film, the trachea displaced by a mass in the neck and upper mediastinum. Right: coronal contrast CT, heterogeneous enlargement of both lobes, the left extending into the mediastinum. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 226 Figure 4 Differentiated thyroid carcinoma on CT. Left: drawing of a tumour in the left lobe. Right: non-contrast CT, a well-defined mass in the left lobe with fine, speckled microcalcifications. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 238"],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s1-4","The incidental nodule and the parathyroids","Thyroid and salivary glands · The thyroid","Thyroid nodules are often found by chance on CT, MRI or PET. Uptake of FDG in a focal nodule carries a significant risk of cancer and needs ultrasound; a small, non-avid nodule on CT in an older patient usually does not. For primary hyperparathyroidism , ultrasound and sestamibi scintigraphy (often with SPECT-CT) localise most adenomas, and four-dimensional CT helps when they disagree or after failed surgery. An adenoma is a small, oval, hypoechoic nodule behind the thyroid, often with a feeding vessel at one pole. Figure 5 Parathyroid adenoma. Left: delayed sestamibi scan, persistent uptake below the left lobe. Right: ultrasound, a hypoechoic oval nodule behind the lower left lobe. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 234"],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s2-1","The facial nerve plane","Thyroid and salivary glands · The parotid","The facial nerve cannot be seen on routine imaging within the parotid, so its plane is inferred. The retromandibular vein runs just medial to the nerve, and the nerve lies lateral to it; a tumour lateral to the vein is in the superficial lobe, one medial to it in the deep lobe. A deep lobe tumour passes through the stylomandibular gap (between the mandibular ramus and the styloid process and its ligament) and pushes the parapharyngeal fat medially. Ultrasound shows the superficial lobe well but not the deep lobe, which is shadowed by the mandible. Figure 6 Axial contrast CT at the mid oropharynx: the parotid space outlined, with the retromandibular vein lateral to the external carotid artery. The facial nerve runs just lateral to the vein. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 326 Figure 7 The deep lobe of the parotid projecting through the stylomandibular gap to touch the parapharyngeal space. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 325"],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s2-2","Benign tumours","Thyroid and salivary glands · The parotid","The tumours are described in Salivary gland tumours . A pleomorphic adenoma is well defined, lobulated and typically very bright on T2 , with a capsule; it is the commonest salivary tumour and can arise anywhere in the gland or its deep lobe. A Warthin tumour occurs in older men who smoke, in the tail of the parotid, may be multiple (up to a fifth) or bilateral (about 5 to 10%), may be partly cystic, and is avid on PET. Figure 8 Pleomorphic adenoma. Left: drawing of a small superficial lobe tumour. Right: fat-suppressed T2, a sharply marginated, very bright mass in the superficial lobe, close to the plane of the facial nerve. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 88 Figure 9 Warthin tumours. Left: drawing of bilateral tumours in the parotid tails. Right: contrast CT, a tumour in each parotid. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 92"],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s2-3","Features of malignancy","Thyroid and salivary glands · The parotid","Ill-defined or infiltrative margins, low or intermediate T2 signal , low ADC on diffusion, invasion of the masseter, skin or deep lobe, and abnormal nodes suggest malignancy, although a small low-grade carcinoma can look benign and FNA or core biopsy is still needed. Facial nerve weakness or pain with a parotid mass suggests perineural spread : follow the nerve up through the stylomastoid foramen and the mastoid segment of the facial canal on post-gadolinium MRI, and look for loss of the fat in the stylomastoid foramen. Figure 10 Mucoepidermoid carcinoma. Left: contrast CT, a large heterogeneous mass with cystic parts and ill-defined borders invading the masseter. Right: T2, irregular mixed signal with a low-signal solid part. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 98"],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s2-4","Infection, stones and Sjögren's","Thyroid and salivary glands · The parotid","Sialolithiasis ( Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings ) is shown by non-contrast CT or ultrasound; most stones are in the submandibular duct. Acute parotitis enlarges and enhances the gland, and contrast CT shows an abscess. Sjögren's syndrome gives enlarged glands with many small cysts or a speckled \"honeycomb\" pattern on ultrasound and MRI; a new dominant solid mass in such a gland raises the question of lymphoma."],["ev","cl:t-im_glands~s3-1","Thyroid and salivary glands: current evidence","Thyroid and salivary glands · The submandibular gland","Thyroid nodules. Ultrasound risk systems guide which nodules need fine-needle aspiration: the ACR TI-RADS (Tessler et al., J Am Coll Radiol 2017), the European EU-TIRADS (2017) and the British U1 to U5 grading; all reduce unnecessary biopsies of benign nodules. Salivary cytology is reported with the Milan system, which gives a risk of malignancy for each category."],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s3-1","Telling them apart","Thyroid and salivary glands · The submandibular gland","Masses in the submandibular space are either in the gland or in a level IB node next to it. A mass arising in the gland is surrounded by a \"claw\" of gland tissue; a node lies outside the gland, usually in front of the facial vein , whereas gland masses lie behind it. Submandibular gland tumours are more often malignant than parotid tumours, so a solid mass in the gland needs sampling. Reading a gland ultrasound or scan 1 Which gland and where thyroid lobe and pole parotid superficial or deep to the retromandibular vein submandibular gland or node 2 Lesion composition echogenicity or T2 signal shape (taller than wide) margins calcification vascularity 3 Grade U or TI-RADS for thyroid benign versus malignant features for salivary 4 Beyond the gland extrathyroidal extension trachea, larynx, oesophagus, recurrent nerve masseter, skin, facial nerve and stylomastoid foramen 5 Nodes levels III, IV, VI for thyroid intraparotid and level II for parotid 6 Next step FNA or core biopsy CT or MRI for retrosternal, deep or invasive disease"],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s4-1","Viva essentials","Thyroid and salivary glands · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Ultrasound first for thyroid, salivary and superficial lumps, with FNA. Suspicious thyroid features: solid, hypoechoic, taller than wide, irregular, microcalcification. U grading and TI-RADS , and when to sample. CT for retrosternal goitre and invasive thyroid cancer; the contrast and radioactive iodine issue. Parathyroid localisation: ultrasound, sestamibi, 4D-CT. Facial nerve plane and the retromandibular vein; the deep lobe and the stylomandibular gap. Pleomorphic adenoma versus Warthin tumour, and the features of malignancy."],["sec","cl:t-im_glands~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Thyroid and salivary glands · For the examination","Common pitfalls Ordering CT for a thyroid nodule instead of ultrasound. Giving iodinated contrast casually to a patient about to have radioactive iodine. Calling a deep lobe parotid tumour \"parapharyngeal\". Missing perineural spread to the stylomastoid foramen in a parotid cancer. Mistaking a level IB node for a submandibular gland tumour."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~c1","The study","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The study","Two sequences do most of the work. Who to scan is covered in Approach to hearing loss and Approach to tinnitus ."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s1-1","Heavily T2-weighted thin sections","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The study","A high-resolution heavily T2-weighted sequence (CISS, FIESTA, SPACE or DRIVE, depending on the manufacturer) in submillimetre slices makes CSF and labyrinthine fluid white and the nerves, vessels and tumours dark. It is the screening sequence for asymmetric hearing loss in many centres, and it shows the inner ear's anatomy in children before cochlear implantation."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s1-2","Post-gadolinium T1 with fat suppression","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The study","A thin post-gadolinium T1 shows enhancing lesions: schwannomas, meningiomas, labyrinthitis, facial neuritis, perineural tumour and metastases. It is added when the T2 screen is abnormal or equivocal, and used from the start when the question is inflammatory (facial palsy, sudden loss with vertigo) or when the T2 sequence would miss an intralabyrinthine lesion. Always look at the pre-contrast T1: blood and fat are bright before contrast and can mimic enhancement. Who needs what For unilateral sensorineural loss in an otherwise healthy adult, a lesion is found in fewer than 5% of scans, and about 90% of those are vestibular schwannomas. Meningioma, epidermoid and aneurysm make up most of the rest. In a child the emphasis changes: the scan looks for malformations, an absent cochlear nerve and post-meningitic ossification."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s2-1","The four nerves: \"seven up, Coke down\"","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The normal anatomy","On an oblique sagittal image across the mid internal auditory canal, four round dark nerves sit in white CSF. The facial nerve is anterosuperior, the cochlear nerve anteroinferior (and normally the largest of the four), and the superior and inferior vestibular nerves are posterior. At the fundus, the horizontal crista falciformis separates the facial and superior vestibular nerves above from the cochlear and inferior vestibular nerves below, and the vertical crest ( Bill's bar ) separates the facial nerve from the superior vestibular nerve. Closer to the porus, the cochlear and vestibular nerves merge into the vestibulocochlear nerve, which wraps around the facial nerve like a catcher's mitt around a ball . Figure 1 Oblique sagittal high-resolution T2 in the mid internal auditory canal: four nerves in bright CSF. The cochlear nerve, anteroinferior, is normally slightly larger than the facial nerve above it. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 62 Figure 2 At the porus: the vestibulocochlear nerve curves around the facial nerve, the \"catcher's mitt and ball\". Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 62"],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s2-2","The labyrinth","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The normal anatomy","Axial T2 shows the inner ear as white fluid within dark bone: the cochlea with its basal, middle and apical turns, divided by the osseous spiral lamina into scala tympani and scala vestibuli, the modiolus as a grey hub, the vestibule , and the semicircular canals . The cochlear nerve can be followed from the anteroinferior IAC through the cochlear nerve canal into the modiolus. Normal fluid signal in every part of the labyrinth is what you check; a dark filling defect means tumour, fibrosis or bone. Figure 3 Axial high-resolution T2 through the mid cochlea: scala tympani and scala vestibuli, the modiolus, the cochlear nerve entering through the cochlear nerve canal, the vestibule and the canals. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 89 Figure 4 Coronal T2 through the IAC fundus: the crista falciformis divides the canal into an upper compartment (facial and superior vestibular nerves) and a lower one (cochlear and inferior vestibular nerves). Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 107"],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s2-3","The cerebellopontine angle cistern","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The normal anatomy","The CPA cistern contains the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves running from the brainstem to the porus, the anterior inferior cerebellar artery looping near or into the canal, and, further up, the trigeminal nerve. Vascular loops touching the eighth nerve are very common in people without symptoms and should not be over-interpreted. Normal flocculus and choroid plexus can look like a small mass; compare the two sides."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~c3","A checklist","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · A checklist","Read every IAC MRI the same way. Reading an IAC and CPA MRI 1 Identify sequences thin T2 T1 before and after gadolinium DWI FLAIR if the brain is included 2 Each IAC on T2 four nerves visible any filling defect in CSF fundal cap of fluid porus width and symmetry 3 Each labyrinth on T2 bright fluid in cochlea (all turns), vestibule and three canals modiolus vestibular aqueduct and sac 4 After gadolinium enhancement in IAC, fundus, labyrinth, facial nerve segments compare with pre-contrast T1 5 CPA cistern mass its shape and angle with the petrous bone dural tail brainstem and fourth ventricle 6 The rest brainstem and cerebellum venous sinuses superficial siderosis mastoid and petrous apex"],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s4-1","Vestibular schwannoma","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The common lesions","The tumour is described clinically in Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours . The vestibular schwannoma arises in the IAC (usually from a vestibular nerve) and grows medially into the CPA, taking the shape of an ice cream on a cone : the cone in the canal, the ball in the cistern. It is centred on the porus, widens it, and meets the petrous bone at an acute angle. On T2 it is a dark filling defect in white CSF, often with a bright fundal cap of fluid lateral to it when it does not reach the fundus; it enhances avidly, often heterogeneously, and larger tumours become cystic or bleed. Report the size (intracanalicular and extracanalicular), the fundal cap, brainstem compression and the fourth ventricle (the basis of the Koos grade), and whether the cochlear nerve canal is involved, which matters for hearing preservation. Figure 5 A small intracanalicular vestibular schwannoma. Left: drawing of the tumour on the superior vestibular nerve. Right: axial T2, a dark mass in bright CSF, with a fluid cap at the fundus and a free cochlear nerve canal. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1222 Figure 6 A large vestibular schwannoma. Left: drawing of the ice-cream-on-cone shape compressing the middle cerebellar peduncle. Right: post-gadolinium T1, enhancing tumour in the canal and cistern, with a large intramural cyst. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1222"],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s4-2","Meningioma","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The common lesions","The CPA meningioma is the second commonest CPA mass. It has a broad dural base on the petrous bone, meets it at an obtuse angle , is usually eccentric to the porus , and seldom enters the canal far. A dural tail , calcification, and hyperostosis of the adjacent bone favour it. It enhances avidly and uniformly. Figure 7 A CPA meningioma. Left: post-gadolinium T1, a mass over the porus with a dural tail along the posterior temporal bone and a dot of enhancement in the fundus. Right: T2, a dural artery feeder as a flow void, and dark tumour inside the canal. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1228"],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s4-3","Epidermoid and arachnoid cysts","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The common lesions","An epidermoid cyst follows CSF on T1 and T2, does not enhance, and insinuates around nerves and vessels rather than displacing them. It does not suppress fully on FLAIR and is bright on diffusion . An arachnoid cyst follows CSF on every sequence including FLAIR and diffusion, and displaces structures. Diffusion separates the two at a glance. Figure 8 A CPA epidermoid. Left: FLAIR, incomplete suppression of the lesion. Right: DWI, bright restricted diffusion, which distinguishes it from an arachnoid cyst. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1202"],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s4-4","Lipoma and others","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The common lesions","A lipoma is bright on T1 before contrast and disappears with fat suppression; it can be mistaken for an enhancing tumour if the pre-contrast T1 is not checked. Facial nerve schwannoma may mimic a vestibular one but extends along the labyrinthine segment to the geniculate ganglion. Aneurysm , metastasis and sarcoidosis are rare."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s5-1","Labyrinthitis and labyrinthine ossification","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The labyrinth and the facial nerve","In labyrinthitis (viral, bacterial or after meningitis) the fluid on T2 is normal or slightly reduced but the labyrinth enhances after gadolinium. After bacterial meningitis, fibrous tissue and then bone replace the fluid ( labyrinthitis ossificans ): the T2 signal in the cochlea is lost before CT shows bone. In a child deafened by meningitis, early MRI and urgent cochlear implantation ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ) are needed before the cochlea ossifies. Figure 9 Labyrinthitis. Left: post-gadolinium T1 after meningitis, abnormal enhancement of both basal turns. Right: T2 in labyrinthitis, the cochlear fluid is still normal; enhancement, not loss of fluid, is the sign. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1112"],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s5-2","Intralabyrinthine schwannoma","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The labyrinth and the facial nerve","A small schwannoma inside the cochlea or vestibule appears as a dark filling defect on T2 and focal enhancement after gadolinium. Haemorrhage (bright before contrast) and labyrinthitis (enhancement without a filling defect) are the mimics. Figure 10 Intralabyrinthine schwannoma. Left: T2, a dark filling defect in the bright cochlear fluid. Right: post-gadolinium T1, focal enhancement in the cochlea. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1126"],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s5-3","Facial nerve enhancement","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The labyrinth and the facial nerve","The facial nerve has a venous plexus around its geniculate, tympanic and upper mastoid segments , so mild enhancement there can be normal. Enhancement in the IAC and labyrinthine segments is always abnormal. In Bell's palsy ( Facial nerve palsy ), the nerve enhances smoothly and linearly, typically with a \"fundal tuft\" at the lateral IAC and along the labyrinthine segment, without enlargement. Nodular or thickened enhancement, extension beyond the temporal bone, or a palsy that does not recover point to tumour or perineural spread from the parotid. Figure 11 Bell's palsy. Left and right: post-gadolinium T1 with fat suppression, a tuft of enhancement at the IAC fundus and enhancement of the labyrinthine and tympanic segments, smooth and not enlarged. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1166"],["ev","cl:t-im_iac~s5-4","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI: current evidence","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The labyrinth and the facial nerve","Protocol. High-resolution T2-weighted MRI (CISS or FIESTA) without contrast detects vestibular schwannomas almost as well as gadolinium-enhanced imaging and is widely used for screening asymmetric hearing loss and unilateral tinnitus; contrast is added when an abnormality is seen. Gadolinium. The US FDA (2017) warned that gadolinium is retained in the brain after repeated doses, of uncertain significance, and recommended macrocyclic agents and avoiding unnecessary contrast. Who needs a scan. NICE (NG98) recommends MRI for asymmetric sensorineural loss; definitions of asymmetry vary."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s5-4","The child before cochlear implantation","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · The labyrinth and the facial nerve","Before implantation, MRI (with CT in malformations) confirms that the cochlear nerve is present on oblique sagittal T2, that the cochlea is patent and fluid-filled (ossification after meningitis), and describes any malformation (common cavity, incomplete partition, large vestibular aqueduct; The deaf child and congenital hearing loss ). A small IAC with only one nerve usually means an absent cochlear nerve; that nerve is usually the facial."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s6-1","Viva essentials","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Sequences: high-resolution T2 and post-gadolinium fat-suppressed T1, and when each is needed. \"Seven up, Coke down\" in the IAC; crista falciformis and Bill's bar. Schwannoma versus meningioma: centred on the porus and acute angle, versus broad dural base, obtuse angle and dural tail. Epidermoid versus arachnoid cyst: diffusion. Labyrinthitis ossificans: loss of fluid signal on T2; urgency of implantation after meningitis. Normal versus abnormal facial nerve enhancement."],["sec","cl:t-im_iac~s6-2","Common pitfalls","The inner ear, IAC and CPA on MRI · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling a lipoma or haemorrhage \"enhancing\" without the pre-contrast T1. Reporting a normal vascular loop as the cause of tinnitus or hearing loss. Missing an intralabyrinthine schwannoma by looking only at the IAC. Calling normal geniculate enhancement abnormal. Mistaking the flocculus or choroid plexus for a small CPA mass."],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s1-1","Protocol","The larynx and hypopharynx · The study","Contrast-enhanced CT is the standard: thin slices (about 2.5 to 3 mm or thinner) from the skull base or mandible to the clavicles, during quiet breathing so the cords are abducted and the anterior commissure is open, with axial images angled along the plane of the cords and coronal and sagittal reformats. A second pass during a breath hold, phonation or modified Valsalva adducts the cords and opens the pyriform sinuses, showing whether a cord moves and whether a pyriform tumour is real or just collapsed mucosa. MRI is slower and degraded by swallowing, but it is more sensitive for cartilage invasion and helps when CT is equivocal. PET-CT helps for nodal disease, second primaries and recurrence after treatment."],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s2-1","The landmarks","The larynx and hypopharynx · Finding your level","Read a laryngeal CT from the hyoid down, naming each level from its landmarks. Supraglottis, upper: the free epiglottis, the valleculae in front of it, and the C-shaped, fat-filled pre-epiglottic space behind the hyoid. Supraglottis, lower (false cords): the aryepiglottic folds, the pyriform sinuses behind them, and fat in the paraglottic space beneath the false cords. Glottis (true cords): the arytenoid cartilages with their vocal processes, the top of the cricoid lamina , and muscle (thyroarytenoid) rather than fat beneath the cords. The anterior commissure, where the cords meet, should be thin. Subglottis: the complete cricoid ring with no arytenoids, and a thin rim of mucosa inside it. Figure 1 Axial contrast CT of the high supraglottis: the C-shaped pre-epiglottic space filled with fat, a common place for tumour to hide. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 379 Figure 2 The glottic level in quiet breathing: the true cords abducted, the vocal processes of the arytenoids, the thin anterior and posterior commissures, and the cricoid lamina behind. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 380"],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s2-2","Normal thickness","The larynx and hypopharynx · Finding your level","Some measurements are worth remembering. The mucosa of the anterior commissure , the posterior commissure and the subglottis is normally 1 mm or less; any visible soft tissue there in a patient with a laryngeal tumour suggests spread. The soft tissue between the cricoid and the spine in the postcricoid region (postcricoid hypopharynx and posterior hypopharyngeal wall together) is normally no more than about 1 cm. Figure 3 Axial contrast CT at the upper cricoid: the inferior margin of the true cords in front, and the postcricoid hypopharyngeal mucosa against the posterior pharyngeal wall behind. The tissue between cricoid and spine is normally 1 cm or less. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 400"],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s3-1","Pre-epiglottic and paraglottic spaces","The larynx and hypopharynx · The deep spaces and the cartilages","The pre-epiglottic space (in front of the epiglottis) and the paraglottic spaces (lateral to the ventricle and cords) are continuous fat-filled compartments with no fascia between them. Tumour can spread through them beneath intact mucosa, from the epiglottis to the false cord and down to the glottis, crossing the ventricle ( transglottic spread). Invasion of either space makes a supraglottic or glottic cancer at least T3 . On CT, look for tumour replacing the fat; on MRI, for intermediate signal replacing bright T1 fat. Figure 4 Coronal CT: the laryngeal ventricle, the air space between the false and true cords. A tumour that crosses it is transglottic. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 384"],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s3-2","Cartilage","The larynx and hypopharynx · The deep spaces and the cartilages","The thyroid, cricoid and arytenoid cartilages ossify irregularly with age, and unossified cartilage looks like soft tissue, so cartilage invasion is one of the hardest calls in head and neck imaging. Sclerosis next to a tumour is non-specific (it may be reactive); erosion or lysis is more specific; tumour outside the cartilage in the strap muscles or the thyroid is definite. On MRI, cartilage that is bright on T2 and enhances like the tumour suggests invasion, while much brighter T2 signal than tumour suggests inflammation. The distinction matters: erosion of the inner cortex makes a tumour T3, extension through the outer cortex makes it T4a, and full-thickness invasion usually means laryngectomy rather than voice-preserving treatment. Figure 5 Glottic carcinoma and cartilage. Left: a bulky anterior tumour across both cords and the anterior commissure, with erosion of the inner cortex of the thyroid cartilage. Right: post-gadolinium MRI of recurrent tumour invading the cricoid and penetrating the thyroid cartilage. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 530"],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s4-1","Supraglottic, glottic and subglottic","The larynx and hypopharynx · Cancer by site","The disease and its staging are in Cancer of the larynx . Supraglottic cancers have a rich lymphatic supply and often present with nodes, frequently on both sides. Look at the pre-epiglottic and paraglottic fat, the vallecula and tongue base, the aryepiglottic fold and pyriform sinus, and the cartilage. Glottic cancers seldom spread to nodes early; report the anterior commissure (and spread across it to the other cord), the posterior commissure, subglottic extension, the paraglottic space, cord movement on the breath-hold images, and the cartilage. Subglottic cancers are rare, spread to paratracheal nodes and are often advanced at presentation. Figure 6 Supraglottic carcinoma. Left: a small epiglottic tumour staged T3 because it invades the pre-epiglottic fat; the epiglottis should never be this thick. Right: a small aryepiglottic fold tumour with a collapsed pyriform sinus but no hypopharyngeal tumour. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 526"],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s4-2","Hypopharynx","The larynx and hypopharynx · Cancer by site","The hypopharynx ( Carcinoma of the hypopharynx ) has three subsites: the pyriform sinuses , the posterior pharyngeal wall and the postcricoid region. The pyriform apex reaches down to the level of the cricoid and is a clinical blind spot, a common hiding place for the primary when a patient presents with a neck node. Hypopharyngeal cancers spread submucosally, invade the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, the thyroid gland and the cervical oesophagus, and present with nodes. Report the lowest extent of tumour (oesophageal involvement changes the operation) and the relation to the prevertebral fascia (invasion makes it unresectable). Figure 7 Pyriform sinus carcinoma. Left: drawing of the tumour in the pyriform sinus. Right: contrast CT, a mass filling the right pyriform sinus and spreading along all its walls. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 518 Figure 8 Axial CT in left vocal cord paralysis: the left arytenoid is rotated medially; the left pyriform sinus and ventricle are widened. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 389"],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s5-1","Vocal cord paralysis","The larynx and hypopharynx · Other common questions","A paralysed cord ( Vocal fold paralysis ) produces a recognisable picture on CT: the ipsilateral arytenoid rotates medially , the ventricle and pyriform sinus dilate, the aryepiglottic fold thickens and moves medially, and the posterior cricoarytenoid muscle atrophies. The search for the cause follows the nerve: from the skull base (jugular foramen) down the carotid space, through the thyroid bed, to the aortopulmonary window on the left and the subclavian on the right. A scan that stops at the clavicles misses a left-sided cause in the chest. Figure 9 Left vocal cord paralysis. Left: a medially rotated left arytenoid with a dilated ventricle. Right: an enlarged left pyriform sinus and a thickened, medially placed aryepiglottic fold. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 296"],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s5-2","Laryngocele","The larynx and hypopharynx · Other common questions","A laryngocele ( Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness ) is a dilated saccule of the ventricle, filled with air or fluid. An internal one stays within the paraglottic space; an external or mixed one passes through the thyrohyoid membrane into the neck. Because a carcinoma at the ventricle can block the saccule, every laryngocele in an adult needs the ventricle examined for tumour. Figure 10 Laryngocele. Left: drawing of a laryngocele squeezing through the thyrohyoid membrane into the neck. Right: coronal CT of a mixed laryngocele with internal and external components. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 292"],["ev","cl:t-im_lx~s5-3","The larynx and hypopharynx: current evidence","The larynx and hypopharynx · Other common questions","CT or MRI. MRI can overcall cartilage invasion because of reactive inflammation; combining both with endoscopy gives the most accurate T stage for larynx preservation decisions. PET-CT response assessment after chemoradiation rests on the PET-NECK trial (Mehanna et al., NEJM 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s5-3","Trauma and stenosis","The larynx and hypopharynx · Other common questions","In laryngeal trauma , thin non-contrast CT shows fractures of the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, arytenoid dislocation, haematoma and air; a displaced fracture or one involving the cricoid usually needs repair. Subglottic and tracheal stenosis after intubation or tracheostomy is measured on CT with sagittal and coronal reformats: its level below the cords, its length and its narrowest diameter. Reading a laryngeal and hypopharyngeal CT 1 Level each slice hyoid and pre-epiglottic fat false cords and paraglottic fat true cords and arytenoids cricoid ring 2 Mucosal surfaces epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, false and true cords commissures ≤1 mm subglottis ≤1 mm 3 Deep spaces pre-epiglottic and paraglottic fat transglottic spread 4 Cartilage sclerosis, erosion, lysis tumour outside the larynx 5 Hypopharynx pyriform sinuses to the apex posterior wall postcricoid ≤1 cm oesophagus below 6 Movement cords on the breath-hold images signs of paralysis 7 Beyond thyroid gland, strap muscles, prevertebral fascia, carotid nodes both sides"],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s6-1","Viva essentials","The larynx and hypopharynx · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Protocol: contrast CT in quiet breathing plus a breath-hold or phonation pass; MRI for cartilage. Levels: how to tell false cord from true cord on axial CT. Pre-epiglottic and paraglottic spaces as blind spots, and their effect on T stage. Cartilage invasion: sclerosis versus erosion versus extralaryngeal spread. Normal thickness: commissures and subglottis ≤1 mm, postcricoid ≤1 cm. Pyriform apex and the unknown primary. Vocal cord paralysis signs, and scanning to the aortopulmonary window."],["sec","cl:t-im_lx~s6-2","Common pitfalls","The larynx and hypopharynx · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling a collapsed pyriform sinus a tumour without the breath-hold images. Calling cartilage invasion from sclerosis alone, or from unossified cartilage. Missing tumour in the pre-epiglottic fat under a normal-looking epiglottis. Stopping a scan for left cord palsy at the clavicles. Missing a ventricular carcinoma behind a laryngocele."],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s1-1","T1 and T2","MRI made simple · The basic sequences","On a T1-weighted image, fat is bright and fluid is dark . T1 gives the best anatomy, shows fat planes clearly (so tumour infiltrating fat stands out as grey replacing white), shows marrow, and is the baseline against which enhancement is judged. Bright signal on T1 before contrast has a short list: fat, subacute blood (methaemoglobin), proteinaceous fluid, melanin and gadolinium. On a T2-weighted image, fluid is bright . CSF, oedema, cysts, retained secretions and most inflammation are bright; most tumours are intermediate; fibrous tissue, calcification and air are dark. The quickest way to tell which is which: find the CSF. If it is white, the image is T2; if it is dark, it is T1. Figure 1 Schematic brightness of common tissues on T1, T2, post-gadolinium T1 with fat suppression, and diffusion. Abscess and cholesteatoma are the two classic \"bright on diffusion\" lesions in ENT. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s1-2","Fat suppression","MRI made simple · The basic sequences","Fat is bright on T1 and fairly bright on T2, so it can hide oedema and enhancing tumour lying in fat (in the orbit, neck, skull base marrow). Fat suppression removes its signal. STIR (short tau inversion recovery) is a robust fat-suppressed T2 for oedema and inflammation; fat-saturated T2 and fat-saturated post-contrast T1 are standard in head and neck protocols, because enhancing tumour and perineural spread show as bright against dark fat."],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s1-3","Gadolinium","MRI made simple · The basic sequences","Intravenous gadolinium shortens T1, so tissues with a blood supply or a broken blood–brain barrier become bright on T1. Post-gadolinium T1 with fat suppression is the workhorse for tumours, inflammation, meningeal and perineural spread , and for schwannomas in the IAC ( Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours ). Always compare with the pre-contrast T1: something bright before contrast is not enhancing. Figure 2 A small vestibular schwannoma in the internal auditory canal. Left: post-gadolinium T1 with fat suppression, the tumour enhances, with a cap of CSF lateral to it at the fundus. Right: CISS, the tumour is a dark filling defect in bright CSF; the screening sequence for asymmetric hearing loss. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1225"],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s2-1","Heavily T2-weighted thin-section images","MRI made simple · Special sequences for ENT","CISS , FIESTA and SPACE (names differ between manufacturers) give submillimetre heavily T2-weighted images in which CSF and labyrinthine fluid are white and nerves, vessels and tumours are dark. They show the facial, cochlear and two vestibular nerves in the IAC, the fluid-filled cochlea and canals (absent in labyrinthitis ossificans, abnormal in malformations), and small schwannomas. Many centres screen asymmetric hearing loss with a non-contrast high-resolution T2 study, adding gadolinium when it is abnormal. Figure 3 Oblique sagittal high-resolution T2 across the internal auditory canal. The four nerves sit in bright CSF: facial anterosuperior, cochlear anteroinferior, and the superior and inferior vestibular nerves posteriorly (\"seven up, Coke down\"). Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 105"],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s2-2","Diffusion-weighted imaging","MRI made simple · Special sequences for ENT","DWI shows how freely water molecules move. Water that cannot move (in thick pus, keratin, or densely packed tumour cells) is bright on DWI and dark on the ADC map ; checking the ADC map confirms true restricted diffusion rather than \"T2 shine-through\". In ENT, restricted diffusion means: Abscess , including orbital, intracranial, neck and periauricular collections. Cholesteatoma : non-echo-planar (non-EPI) DWI has few artefacts at the skull base and detects residual or recurrent cholesteatoma down to about 3 mm. Highly cellular tumours , especially lymphoma and undifferentiated carcinoma, and epidermoid cysts. Figure 4 Coalescent otomastoiditis with a periauricular abscess. Left: post-gadolinium T1 with fat suppression, an enhancing mastoid with thickened enhancing meninges and a dehiscent outer cortex. Right: DWI, the abscess is bright (restricted diffusion). Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1042 Figure 5 A congenital cholesteatoma read across modalities, part 1. Left: CT, a smooth expansile lesion of the mastoid and lateral clivus. Right: T2, bright and sharply marginated. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1030 Figure 6 Part 2, the same lesion. Left: post-gadolinium T1, no enhancement. Right: DWI, bright. Expansion, no enhancement and restricted diffusion together mean cholesteatoma. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1030"],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s2-3","Vessels and blood","MRI made simple · Special sequences for ENT","MR angiography (time-of-flight or contrast-enhanced) and MR venography show arteries and venous sinuses without radiation, for pulsatile tinnitus ( Approach to tinnitus ), paragangliomas ( Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies ) and sinus thrombosis ( Intracranial complications of otitis media ). Susceptibility sequences (T2* gradient echo or SWI) show old blood as dark blooming, as in superficial siderosis or microhaemorrhage in a schwannoma."],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s3-1","Find each sequence first","MRI made simple · Reading an MRI study","Open the series list and identify every sequence: T1, T2 (with or without fat suppression), post-contrast T1, DWI with ADC, and any thin T2. Then for each lesion ask the same questions in order. Characterising a lesion on MRI 1 T1 before contrast dark fluid or most tumours bright fat, subacute blood, protein, melanin 2 T2 very bright fluid, cyst, oedema, retained secretions intermediate most tumours dark fibrosis, calcification, air, dense secretions, fungus 3 After gadolinium none cyst, cholesteatoma, fluid rim only abscess or necrotic node solid tumour or inflammation along a nerve perineural spread 4 DWI and ADC bright DWI, dark ADC pus, keratin (cholesteatoma, epidermoid), lymphoma no restriction most other lesions 5 Put it together location, margins, effect on bone (CT) and neighbours, then the short differential Signal of lesions commonly met in ENT Lesion T1 T2 Gadolinium DWI Retained secretions Dark (bright if thick) Bright (dark if very thick or fungal) Rim of mucosa only Variable Mucocoele Variable with protein Variable Rim Usually no restriction Cholesteatoma Dark Bright None (rim if inflamed) Restricted Cholesterol granuloma Bright Bright None No restriction Abscess Dark Bright Rim Restricted Schwannoma Intermediate Bright, may be cystic Avid No restriction Squamous cell carcinoma Intermediate Intermediate Moderate Moderately restricted Lymphoma Intermediate Intermediate, homogeneous Mild, uniform Strongly restricted Lipoma or dermoid fat Bright Bright, suppresses with fat-sat None No restriction"],["ev","cl:t-im_mri~s4-1","MRI made simple: current evidence","MRI made simple · Practicalities","Cholesteatoma. In a meta-analysis, non-echo-planar diffusion-weighted MRI detected cholesteatoma with a sensitivity of about 94% (Li et al., Laryngoscope 2013), and it now replaces many second-look operations for residual or recurrent disease, though lesions under about 3 mm can be missed. Gadolinium safety. Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is now very rare with macrocyclic agents; the FDA (2017) warned of gadolinium retention in the brain after repeated doses. Implants. Check MRI compatibility of cochlear implants and other devices; most modern implants are conditional at 1.5 T."],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s4-1","When MRI is not the answer","MRI made simple · Practicalities","MRI is slow, so motion degrades it, and children often need sedation. It shows cortical bone poorly , so fine bony detail (ossicles, the otic capsule, sinus walls, fractures) belongs to CT; the two are complementary in the skull base and in cancer. Check for pacemakers, some cochlear implants and other implants, metal foreign bodies in the orbit, and claustrophobia. Cochlear implant magnets ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ) cause large artefacts and may need removal or a special protocol. Gadolinium is avoided in pregnancy and in severe renal impairment. Do not miss Calling a bright-on-T1 lesion \"enhancing\" without looking at the pre-contrast T1. Calling high DWI signal \"restricted\" without checking the ADC map. Missing perineural spread because post-contrast images were not fat-suppressed."],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s5-1","Viva essentials","MRI made simple · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for T1 versus T2: how to tell them apart, and what is bright on each. Fat suppression and why it matters in the head and neck. Gadolinium: what enhances; compare with the pre-contrast T1. DWI and ADC: abscess, cholesteatoma (non-EPI) and lymphoma. CISS or FIESTA for the IAC and the labyrinth. CT versus MRI: bone versus soft tissue."],["sec","cl:t-im_mri~s5-2","Common pitfalls","MRI made simple · For the examination","Common pitfalls Thinking that dark on T2 in a sinus excludes disease: thick secretions and fungus ( Fungal sinusitis ) are dark. Ordering MRI to look at ossicles or a fracture. Forgetting cochlear implants and other implants on the safety form."],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s1-1","The landmarks","Lymph nodes · Levels from landmarks","The surgical levels ( Approach to a lump in the neck ) are drawn on imaging from fixed landmarks that can be found on any axial slice: the mylohyoid and the posterior edge of the submandibular gland , the hyoid bone , the lower edge of the cricoid cartilage , the posterior edge of the sternocleidomastoid , the internal jugular vein , the carotid arteries and the top of the manubrium . Imaging levels Level Boundaries on axial imaging IA Submental: between the anterior bellies of the digastrics, below the mylohyoid IB Submandibular: lateral to the anterior digastric, in front of the posterior edge of the submandibular gland IIA Skull base to the lower body of the hyoid, behind the submandibular gland, in front of the posterior edge of the sternocleidomastoid; in front of, beside or inseparable from the internal jugular vein IIB As IIA, but behind the internal jugular vein with a fat plane between III Lower hyoid to the lower cricoid, in front of the posterior edge of the sternocleidomastoid IV Lower cricoid to the clavicle, in front of the posterior edge of the sternocleidomastoid VA Behind the posterior edge of the sternocleidomastoid, skull base to the lower cricoid VB Behind the sternocleidomastoid, lower cricoid to the clavicle VI Between the carotids, hyoid to the manubrium (prelaryngeal, pretracheal, paratracheal) VII Between the carotids, below the top of the manubrium, above the brachiocephalic vein Figure 1 Axial contrast CT above the hyoid: lines along the posterior edges of the submandibular glands and sternocleidomastoids separate level I from level II, and level "],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s1-2","Nodes without a number","Lymph nodes · Levels from landmarks","Some groups are named, not numbered, and are easily missed: the retropharyngeal nodes (lateral, or nodes of Rouvière, at the level of C1–C2 beside the longus capitis, and medial), the parotid and facial nodes, the occipital and supraclavicular nodes. Retropharyngeal nodes cannot be felt and are the first station for the nasopharynx and a common one for the oropharynx, hypopharynx and thyroid. Figure 4 Axial fat-suppressed T2: the lateral retropharyngeal nodes (of Rouvière) anterolateral to the prevertebral muscles, and the medial retropharyngeal nodes. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 435"],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s2-1","The malignant features","Lymph nodes · Is this node abnormal?","Size alone misclassifies one node in five or more, so use every feature together. Necrosis : a focal low-density defect in an untreated node is the most reliable sign of metastasis. Do not mistake the normal fatty hilum for necrosis. Shape : normal nodes are oval or kidney-shaped with a fatty hilum; malignant nodes become round (long-to-short axis ratio under 2) and lose the hilum. Size : thresholds vary; commonly used limits are about 10 mm in short axis (a little more for the jugulodigastric node) and 5 to 8 mm for a lateral retropharyngeal node. A cluster of three or more borderline nodes in the drainage area of a tumour is suspicious. Extranodal extension : an irregular, ill-defined margin, stranding of the surrounding fat, and invasion of muscle, vessel or skin. It changes the stage and the treatment. Asymmetry and enhancement : a node that enhances more than its neighbours, or that is new, is suspicious. Figure 5 The nodal network of the neck, drawn with the levels outlined, the hyoid plane (blue) and cricoid plane (orange-red) that divide them, and, in the axial slice above, the retropharyngeal and superficial nodes (arrows). Level II is the commonest site of metastasis from squamous carcinoma. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 540"],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s2-2","Ultrasound, MRI and PET","Lymph nodes · Is this node abnormal?","Ultrasound shows the hilum and its vessels best: a normal node is oval with an echogenic hilum and hilar vascularity ; a metastatic node is round, loses the hilum and shows peripheral vascularity, and can be sampled by FNA in the same sitting. On MRI , metastatic squamous carcinoma has a low ADC on diffusion. PET-CT improves detection in advanced disease and the unknown primary, but misses small and cystic nodes and gives false positives with reactive nodes. Figure 6 Ultrasound of a normal cervical node: an elliptical, hypoechoic node with an echogenic fatty hilum, in front of the common carotid artery. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 436"],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s2-3","Unresectability","Lymph nodes · Is this node abnormal?","A nodal mass that encases the carotid (contact of 270° or more), invades the prevertebral muscles or the skull base, or infiltrates the skin widely is usually unresectable. Contact under 180° usually means the artery can be peeled free."],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s3-1","Reading the nodes backwards","Lymph nodes · Where is the primary?","Each primary site drains to predictable levels, so the level of an abnormal node points to the primary. Nodes outside the usual pattern should prompt a search for a second primary. The node and the likely primary Nodes Likely primaries IA and IB Lip, anterior oral cavity, floor of mouth, skin of the face IIA (jugulodigastric) Oropharynx (tonsil, tongue base), oral tongue, supraglottis, nasopharynx Retropharyngeal and VA Nasopharynx; also oropharynx and hypopharynx III and IV Larynx, hypopharynx, thyroid VI Thyroid, subglottis, cervical oesophagus Parotid and preauricular Skin of the scalp, ear and face (squamous carcinoma, melanoma; Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck ) Left supraclavicular (Virchow) Chest and abdomen"],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s3-2","The cystic node","Lymph nodes · Where is the primary?","A cystic or largely necrotic node in an adult is a metastasis until proven otherwise . A thick-walled necrotic node most often comes from the tonsil or tongue base; a cystic node with a thin wall in a young adult suggests HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ); a cystic node with calcification , bright T1 signal (thyroglobulin) or avid enhancement suggests papillary thyroid carcinoma , even when the thyroid looks normal. Figure 7 Papillary thyroid carcinoma in a node. Left: contrast CT, a large heterogeneous level III node displacing the jugular vein, with calcification lower down. Right: ultrasound, marked internal heterogeneity; FNA confirmed papillary carcinoma. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 340"],["ev","cl:t-im_nodes~s4-1","Lymph nodes: current evidence","Lymph nodes · Not every abnormal node is cancer","Ultrasound with fine-needle aspiration is also used for surveillance of the N0 neck. Size thresholds in published series range from about 10 to 15 mm depending on level. Extranodal extension was added to nodal staging in the AJCC eighth edition ( Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles ). PET-CT is also used for response assessment."],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s4-1","Reactive, suppurative, tuberculous and lymphoma","Lymph nodes · Not every abnormal node is cancer","Reactive nodes are enlarged but oval, with a preserved hilum and no necrosis, often in several levels on both sides. Suppurative nodes, usually in children, have a thick enhancing rim around a fluid centre with inflammatory stranding of the fat and skin around them; they may progress to an abscess. Tuberculous nodes are often matted, necrotic and rim-enhancing, in the posterior triangle and lower neck, with less surrounding inflammation than their necrosis suggests, and calcify later. Lymphoma produces large, homogeneous, non-necrotic nodes in many chains, often with involvement of Waldeyer's ring, and restricts strongly on diffusion. Figure 8 Suppurative lymphadenitis. Right: contrast CT, a rim-enhancing, centrally necrotic level II node with cellulitis and oedema of the overlying skin and fat. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 314 Figure 9 Tuberculous lymphadenitis. Left: multiple necrotic, rim-enhancing nodes with extranodal inflammation. Right: matted necrotic nodes that had not responded to treatment. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 318 Figure 10 Non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Left: large, round, solid nodes in many chains on both sides without necrosis. Right: nodal and tonsillar lymphoma, with a partly necrotic level II mass. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 336 Reading the nodes on any head and neck scan 1 Every level, both sides I to VII retropharyngeal parotid, facial, occipital, supraclavicular 2 Each suspicio"],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s5-1","Viva essentials","Lymph nodes · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Imaging levels and their landmarks: hyoid, cricoid, submandibular gland, sternocleidomastoid, jugular vein, manubrium. Retropharyngeal nodes and the nasopharynx. Malignant features: necrosis, shape, size, extranodal extension. Ultrasound features of normal and abnormal nodes. Node to primary patterns; the cystic node in an adult. Carotid encasement and unresectability. Mimics: reactive, suppurative, tuberculous, lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-im_nodes~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Lymph nodes · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using size alone to call a node benign or malignant. Calling the fatty hilum necrosis. Forgetting the retropharyngeal nodes. Calling a cystic level II mass in an adult a branchial cyst. Missing a thyroid primary behind a calcified or cystic node."],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s1-1","Choosing the test","The oral cavity and oropharynx · The study","MRI with fat-suppressed post-gadolinium T1 and T2 is the best study for oral and oropharyngeal cancer: it is far less affected by dental amalgam than CT, shows the depth of a tongue tumour and the marrow of the mandible, and detects perineural spread. Contrast CT is quicker, images the neck and chest in one pass, and is the first test for infection , with bone windows of the mandible to find the tooth, the stone or the osteomyelitis responsible. Puffed-cheek or open-mouth techniques separate the cheek, gum and tongue surfaces. CT or cone-beam CT shows the cortex of the mandible; MRI shows its marrow."],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s2-1","Mucosa, sublingual space, submandibular space and root of tongue","The oral cavity and oropharynx · The anatomy","The oral cavity is divided by the mylohyoid muscle , a sling from one side of the mandible to the other, into two compartments. The oral mucosal surface : the lips, buccal mucosa, alveolar ridges, retromolar trigone, hard palate, floor of mouth and oral tongue. Squamous cell carcinoma arises here. The sublingual space , above and medial to the mylohyoid and lateral to the genioglossus: the sublingual gland, the deep part of the submandibular gland and its duct, the lingual nerve, artery and vein, and the hypoglossal nerve. The two sides connect anteriorly beneath the frenulum. The submandibular space , below the mylohyoid: the submandibular gland, level I nodes, the facial vein and the anterior belly of the digastric. It opens posteriorly into the sublingual space at the back edge of the mylohyoid and upwards into the parapharyngeal space. The root of the tongue : the genioglossus and geniohyoid muscles and the lingual septum in the midline, the landmark for midline crossing. Figure 1 Coronal T1 through the oral cavity: the mylohyoid sling separating the sublingual space above from the submandibular space below, with the genioglossus, hyoglossus and intrinsic tongue muscles and the midline lingual septum. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 483 Figure 2 Axial T2 fat-suppressed: the sublingual space between the mylohyoid laterally and the hyoglossus and genioglossus medially, with the sublingual gland and lingual artery inside it. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 466"],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s2-2","The oropharynx","The oral cavity and oropharynx · The anatomy","Behind the circumvallate papillae and the anterior tonsillar pillars lies the oropharynx: the base of tongue with the lingual tonsil, the palatine tonsils in the lateral walls, the soft palate and the posterior pharyngeal wall. The lymphoid tissue of the tonsils and tongue base is normally bulky and asymmetric, so a small tumour may be invisible on the scan; asymmetry, enhancement and restricted diffusion help."],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s3-1","Oral tongue","The oral cavity and oropharynx · Oral cancer","The disease is described in Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer . Report the depth of invasion (or tumour thickness on MRI), which drives the T stage and the decision to treat the neck; whether the tumour crosses the lingual septum ; its relation to the neurovascular bundle (lingual artery and hypoglossal nerve) on each side, because involvement of both bundles rules out tongue preservation; extension to the floor of mouth, tongue base and mandible; and the nodes. Tongue tumours often spread to level II and III and may skip levels. Figure 3 Oral tongue carcinoma. Left: post-gadolinium MRI, a wedge-shaped enhancing ulcer at the lateral tongue, well seen despite the amalgam. Right: the clinical lesion. Imaging measured its depth and showed no nodes. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 504"],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s3-2","Floor of mouth and gum","The oral cavity and oropharynx · Oral cancer","Floor of mouth cancers involve the sublingual gland and the submandibular duct (obstructing the gland), cross the midline easily, and reach the inner cortex of the mandible early. Alveolar and retromolar trigone cancers lie on the bone: the question is whether the mandible is invaded and how far. Cortical erosion is seen on CT or cone-beam CT; marrow replacement (loss of bright fat on T1) on MRI, which is sensitive but can overcall. Spread along the inferior alveolar canal is perineural spread to the skull base. The retromolar trigone also leads along the pterygomandibular raphe up to the masticator space and skull base. Figure 4 Floor of mouth carcinoma. Left: post-gadolinium MRI, a subtle anterior lesion involving the sublingual gland, with loss of the dark cortex of the mandible beside it. Right: the tumour at operation. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 508 Figure 5 Retromolar trigone carcinoma. Left: bone CT, a lytic defect in the mandible close to the inferior alveolar canal. Right: the mucosal tumour behind the last molar. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 512"],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s4-1","HPV and the cystic node","The oral cavity and oropharynx · Oropharyngeal cancer","HPV-related oropharyngeal carcinoma ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ) typically affects younger patients, has a small primary in the tonsil or tongue base that may be invisible on CT, and presents with large, often cystic level II nodes. A cystic level II mass in an adult should be treated as a metastasis until proven otherwise, not as a branchial cyst. Look carefully at both tonsils and the tongue base for asymmetry, and use PET-CT before panendoscopy when the primary is not seen. Figure 6 HPV-related tonsillar carcinoma. Left: a small enhancing mass in the right palatine tonsil, easily called normal. Right: a large necrotic level IIA node on the same side, the reason the patient presented. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 498"],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s4-2","What to report","The oral cavity and oropharynx · Oropharyngeal cancer","For tonsillar tumours report spread to the soft palate, tongue base, parapharyngeal space , medial pterygoid and mandible, and the relation to the internal carotid. For tongue base tumours report extension to the vallecula, pre-epiglottic space and larynx, and midline crossing. Encasement of the carotid or invasion of the prevertebral muscles or skull base makes the tumour T4b."],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s5-1","Abscess and Ludwig's angina","The oral cavity and oropharynx · Infection and cysts","Most oral cavity infections start in a tooth. The roots of the first molar and the teeth in front of it lie above the mylohyoid line, so their infections enter the sublingual space; the roots of the second and third molars lie below it, so their infections enter the submandibular space. Ludwig's angina ( Deep neck space infection ) is a spreading cellulitis of both sublingual and submandibular spaces on both sides that elevates the tongue and threatens the airway. Contrast CT shows the extent, any drainable collection, the airway, the source tooth and spread to the parapharyngeal space and mediastinum. Figure 7 Sublingual space abscess. Left: drawing of a horseshoe-shaped collection in both sublingual spaces joined beneath the frenulum. Right: contrast CT, the rim-enhancing collection above the mylohyoid on both sides. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 408"],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s5-2","Ranula","The oral cavity and oropharynx · Infection and cysts","A simple ranula ( Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches ) is a mucus retention cyst of the sublingual gland confined to the sublingual space. A diving (plunging) ranula passes behind the free edge of the mylohyoid, or through a gap in it, into the submandibular space, leaving a characteristic tail in the sublingual space that shows its origin. Figure 8 Diving ranula. Left: drawing of a ranula herniating backwards from the sublingual space. Right: contrast CT, a cystic mass in the submandibular space against the back of the mylohyoid, displacing the submandibular gland. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 400"],["ev","cl:t-im_oral~s5-3","The oral cavity and oropharynx: current evidence","The oral cavity and oropharynx · Infection and cysts","Depth of invasion. The AJCC eighth edition added depth of invasion to T staging of oral cancer, and MRI (or intraoral ultrasound) estimates it before surgery, helping decide on elective neck dissection; in a randomised trial, elective neck dissection improved survival over watchful waiting in early oral cancer (D'Cruz et al., NEJM 2015)."],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s5-3","The denervated tongue","The oral cavity and oropharynx · Infection and cysts","After hypoglossal nerve injury, one half of the tongue changes: at first it is swollen, bright on T2 and enhancing ; later it becomes fatty and atrophic , and the normal side can then look like a mass. The sharp midline border gives it away. Always follow the hypoglossal nerve back to the skull base to find the cause. Figure 9 Acute and subacute denervation. Left: T2, a swollen, bright left half of the tongue, sharply limited at the midline. Right: post-gadolinium T1, enhancement of the denervated muscle. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 388 Figure 10 Chronic denervation. Left: T1, fatty replacement of the left half of the tongue. Right: CT, fatty atrophy; the normal right side could be mistaken for a tumour. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 388 Reading an oral cavity or oropharynx scan 1 Site which mucosal subsite sublingual, submandibular or root of tongue oropharyngeal subsite 2 Tongue tumour depth of invasion lingual septum neurovascular bundles floor of mouth tongue base 3 Bone mandible cortex on CT marrow on MRI inferior alveolar canal maxilla and palate 4 Deep spread parapharyngeal space pterygoids pterygomandibular raphe skull base carotid 5 Infection collection above or below the mylohyoid airway source tooth or stone mediastinum 6 Nodes levels I to III both sides cystic or necrotic nodes"],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s6-1","Viva essentials","The oral cavity and oropharynx · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for MRI for oral cancer (amalgam, depth, marrow, nerves); CT for infection . The mylohyoid and the sublingual and submandibular spaces. Oral tongue: depth of invasion, lingual septum, neurovascular bundles. Mandible invasion: CT for cortex, MRI for marrow; the inferior alveolar canal. HPV-related cancer: small primary, cystic level II node. Dental infection above or below the mylohyoid line; Ludwig's angina. Diving ranula and the denervated tongue."],["sec","cl:t-im_oral~s6-2","Common pitfalls","The oral cavity and oropharynx · For the examination","Common pitfalls Relying on CT for a tongue tumour hidden by amalgam. Calling a cystic level II node in an adult a branchial cyst. Mistaking a normal half-tongue next to an atrophic one for a tumour. Calling marrow invasion from MRI alone when the cortex is intact."],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s1-1","CT and MRI","The orbit · The study","Contrast-enhanced CT with thin bone images is the first test for orbital infection, for trauma (non-contrast, thin bone algorithm in three planes) and for any process involving the sinus walls. MRI with fat suppression (STIR or fat-suppressed T2, and fat-suppressed post-gadolinium T1) is best for the optic nerve, the orbital apex, the cavernous sinus, tumours and intracranial spread. Fat suppression matters more here than anywhere: orbital fat is bright on T1 and hides enhancing tissue unless it is suppressed."],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s2-1","Preseptal and postseptal","The orbit · The compartments","The orbital septum , a fibrous sheet from the orbital rim to the tarsal plates, divides the eyelids and soft tissues in front ( preseptal ) from the orbit proper behind ( postseptal ). Infection limited to the preseptal tissues swells the lids but leaves the orbital fat clear; postseptal infection streaks the fat and may push the globe forwards."],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s2-2","Intraconal, conal and extraconal","The orbit · The compartments","The four rectus muscles and their fascia form a cone from the annulus of Zinn at the apex to the globe. Inside the cone ( intraconal ) are the optic nerve and its sheath, the ophthalmic artery and the superior ophthalmic vein; lesions here (cavernous venous malformation, optic nerve tumours) push the eye straight forwards. Conal disease involves the muscles themselves (thyroid eye disease, myositis, lymphoma). The extraconal space between the cone and the bony walls holds the lacrimal gland and is where sinus disease arrives first: a subperiosteal abscess or sinonasal tumour displaces the globe away from the involved wall. Figure 1 Coronal T1 in the mid orbit: the four rectus muscles and the superior oblique form the cone around the optic nerve-sheath complex in bright fat, with the superior ophthalmic vein beneath the superior rectus. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 213"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s2-3","The bony walls and the apex","The orbit · The compartments","The medial wall is the paper-thin lamina papyracea over the ethmoid sinuses; the floor is the roof of the maxillary antrum, with the infraorbital canal running through it; the roof borders the anterior cranial fossa and the frontal sinus. At the apex, the optic canal (optic nerve and ophthalmic artery) lies superomedially, the superior orbital fissure (III, IV, VI, V1, superior ophthalmic vein) between the greater and lesser wings, and the inferior orbital fissure opening to the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae. The nasolacrimal duct runs down from the lacrimal sac in the medial canthus to the inferior meatus. Figure 2 Coronal bone CT in the mid orbit: the thin lamina papyracea, the orbital floor and the infraorbital canal. The coronal plane shows wall integrity best. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 219 Figure 3 Axial bone CT of the upper orbit: the optic canals angling back towards the chiasm, with the superior orbital fissure lateral to them. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 218"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~c3","Infection","The orbit · Infection","The complications of sinusitis. The clinical management is in Proptosis and the orbit and Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications ."],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s3-1","Chandler's stages on imaging","The orbit · Infection","Orbital complications of sinusitis Stage Imaging What to look for I, preseptal cellulitis Swelling and enhancement in front of the septum; orbital fat clear Usually children; may follow skin infection II, orbital cellulitis Stranding of postseptal fat; no collection Proptosis, restricted movement III, subperiosteal abscess Lentiform, rim-enhancing collection along a wall, usually medial; displaced medial rectus Size, extent backwards, vision IV, orbital abscess Rim-enhancing collection within the orbital fat Needs drainage V, cavernous sinus thrombosis Enlarged superior ophthalmic vein with a filling defect; non-enhancing, bulging cavernous sinus Bilateral signs, cranial nerve palsies"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s3-2","The subperiosteal abscess","The orbit · Infection","A subperiosteal abscess sits between the bone and the periorbita, usually along the lamina papyracea next to opacified ethmoid cells. It is lentiform , rim-enhancing, displaces the medial rectus and the globe laterally, and restricts on diffusion. Features that favour surgical drainage include a large or posteriorly extending collection, visual compromise, age over about 10 years, a frontal sinus origin, and failure to improve on antibiotics. Figure 4 Subperiosteal abscess. Left: drawing of ethmoid infection passing through the lamina papyracea to form a collection displacing the cone. Right: contrast CT, opacified ethmoid cells and a large medial extraconal abscess displacing the medial rectus. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 810 Figure 5 Left: preseptal cellulitis after facial impetigo, swelling in front of the septum with clear orbital fat and clear sinuses. Right: STIR MRI, preseptal and postseptal cellulitis from ethmoid sinusitis, without an abscess. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 814"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s3-3","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","The orbit · Infection","Look for an enlarged superior ophthalmic vein containing a filling defect, an expanded cavernous sinus with filling defects or poor enhancement, and narrowing of the cavernous carotid. MRI with venography confirms it and shows intracranial complications. Figure 6 Contrast CT in septic cavernous sinus thrombosis: filling defects in both superior ophthalmic veins and the cavernous sinuses, with extension to the basilar venous plexus and superior petrosal sinus. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 235"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s4-1","Blowout fractures","The orbit · Trauma","The fractures are described clinically in Nasal and facial fractures . A pure blowout fracture breaks the floor or medial wall without involving the rim. Coronal CT shows the fracture, herniation of fat and of the inferior or medial rectus, and the volume of the defect. A rounded, displaced inferior rectus or muscle caught in the fracture, especially the trapdoor fracture of a child with little visible on CT but marked restriction and pain on upgaze, is a surgical emergency. Large defects risk enophthalmos. Figure 7 Orbital floor blowout fractures. Left: coronal CT, a mildly depressed floor fracture. Right: an older floor fracture with herniation of fat and part of the inferior rectus through the defect. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 980 Figure 8 Left: a lamina papyracea fracture with the medial rectus deformed into the defect. Right: retrobulbar air and blood with proptosis and tenting of the back of the globe, the warning sign of a compartment syndrome. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 980"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s4-2","Retrobulbar haemorrhage and the optic canal","The orbit · Trauma","Proptosis with tenting of the posterior globe (the globe drawn into a cone at the optic nerve insertion) means rising orbital pressure and threatens sight; it is treated clinically by lateral canthotomy and cantholysis without waiting for imaging. In high-energy trauma, look for fractures through the optic canal and the anterior clinoid."],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s5-1","Thyroid eye disease","The orbit · The muscles and masses","Thyroid eye disease enlarges the muscle bellies while sparing their tendons, usually on both sides, most often the inferior and medial recti (the mnemonic \"I'M SLow\": inferior, medial, superior, lateral). It also increases orbital fat. Crowding of the enlarged muscles at the apex compresses the optic nerve and is an indication for urgent treatment, sometimes endoscopic decompression of the medial wall and floor. STIR signal in the muscles suggests active inflammation. Figure 9 Thyroid eye disease. Left: drawing of symmetric muscle enlargement. Right: coronal CT, enlarged inferior, medial and superior recti on both sides. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 822"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s5-2","Idiopathic orbital inflammation and lymphoma","The orbit · The muscles and masses","Idiopathic orbital inflammation (pseudotumour) is usually unilateral and painful, involves the muscle including its tendon , the fat, the lacrimal gland or the sclera, and responds to steroids. Lymphoma is painless, moulds itself around the globe and orbital structures without destroying them, enhances uniformly and restricts strongly on diffusion; the lacrimal gland is a common site. Biopsy is often needed to separate them. Figure 10 Orbital lymphoma. Left: contrast CT, a lobulated retrobulbar mass moulding to the shape of the globe. Right: post-gadolinium T1, uniformly enhancing tumour in the lacrimal gland extending back along the lateral rectus. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 854"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s5-3","Cavernous venous malformation","The orbit · The muscles and masses","The commonest benign orbital mass in adults is the cavernous venous malformation (formerly cavernous haemangioma): a well-defined, oval, intraconal mass that is bright on T2 and fills in with contrast progressively from a point. It pushes the globe forwards and seldom threatens vision unless it sits at the apex. Figure 11 Cavernous venous malformation. Left: drawing of an oval intraconal mass displacing the optic nerve. Right: CT, a well-defined, slightly dense intraconal mass with the lateral rectus draped around it. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 804"],["ev","cl:t-im_orb~s5-4","The orbit: current evidence","The orbit · The muscles and masses","Orbital cellulitis. Contrast CT of the orbits and sinuses is needed when there is proptosis, ophthalmoplegia, reduced vision or colour vision, inability to examine the eye, or failure to improve in 24 to 48 hours; MRI is better for orbital apex and intracranial spread and for cavernous sinus thrombosis. Thyroid eye disease. STIR activity helps decide on steroids or teprotumumab."],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s5-4","Sinonasal tumours and the orbit","The orbit · The muscles and masses","For a sinonasal malignancy the key question is whether the periorbita has been breached. Thinning or loss of the lamina papyracea alone does not mean invasion; obliteration of the extraconal fat next to the tumour, nodular contact with the medial rectus, or tumour within the muscle does. MRI answers this best, and it often decides between preserving and removing the eye. Reading an orbit scan 1 Septum preseptal or postseptal lids and fat 2 Walls lamina papyracea, floor, roof fracture, erosion, remodelling adjacent sinuses 3 Extraconal collection, mass, lacrimal gland periorbita 4 Muscles size, belly or tendon, symmetry entrapment 5 Intraconal optic nerve and sheath superior ophthalmic vein mass 6 Globe and apex proptosis tenting optic canal superior orbital fissure cavernous sinus"],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s6-1","Viva essentials","The orbit · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Compartments: preseptal and postseptal; intraconal, conal and extraconal. Chandler's stages and what each looks like on CT. Subperiosteal abscess: imaging features and indications for drainage. Cavernous sinus thrombosis: the superior ophthalmic vein sign. Blowout fracture: muscle entrapment and the trapdoor fracture in children. Thyroid eye disease: muscle bellies, apical crowding, decompression. Periorbital invasion by sinonasal tumour."],["sec","cl:t-im_orb~s6-2","Common pitfalls","The orbit · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling preseptal cellulitis \"orbital\" without looking at the fat behind the septum. Missing a small medial subperiosteal abscess on a non-contrast scan. Reassuring a child with a trapdoor fracture because the CT looks almost normal. Calling invasion of the orbit from erosion of the lamina papyracea alone."],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s1-1","Choosing the test","The child's head and neck · Principles","Children are more sensitive to radiation than adults and have more years ahead in which harm could appear, so ultrasound is the first test for neck lumps, salivary and thyroid lesions, and MRI for soft tissue, the inner ear and nerves and intracranial connections. MRI under six months can often be done with a feed and wrap technique; older infants and young children may need sedation or anaesthesia, which can be combined with another procedure. CT is used when the question is about bone (choanal atresia, the temporal bone, fractures) or in an emergency (deep neck infection with airway concern), with a low-dose paediatric protocol."],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~c2","The neck lump","The child's head and neck · The neck lump","Position is the key. The clinical side is in Congenital neck masses and Head and neck cancer in children ."],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s2-1","Midline lumps","The child's head and neck · The neck lump","A thyroglossal duct cyst is the commonest congenital neck lump. It lies in the midline or just off it, anywhere from the foramen caecum of the tongue to the thyroid bed, most often at or just below the hyoid , and infrahyoid cysts are embedded in the strap muscles . Ultrasound confirms a cyst and, importantly, a normal thyroid gland in the lower neck. A solid component in an adult raises the question of carcinoma. A dermoid cyst of the floor of mouth or neck may contain fat globules (\"a sack of marbles\"); position relative to the mylohyoid decides the surgical approach. Figure 1 Thyroglossal duct cyst. Left: drawing of the course of the thyroglossal duct from the foramen caecum to the thyroid. Right: sagittal contrast CT, a well-defined cyst at the midline base of the tongue. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 590"],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s2-2","Lateral lumps","The child's head and neck · The neck lump","The second branchial cleft cyst is the commonest lateral cyst: it sits at the angle of the mandible, in front of the sternocleidomastoid , lateral to the carotid sheath and behind the submandibular gland, displacing all three. Infection thickens its wall. A cystic lump here in an adult must be treated as a cystic nodal metastasis until proven otherwise. A first branchial cleft anomaly lies near the external auditory canal, parotid or angle of the mandible, close to the facial nerve. Third and fourth branchial anomalies are usually on the left , run from the pyriform sinus, and present as recurrent abscesses or suppurative thyroiditis ; a barium swallow or CT after contrast may show the sinus tract. Figure 2 Second branchial cleft cyst. Left: contrast CT in a teenager, a well-defined low-density mass in front of the sternocleidomastoid and lateral to the carotid space. Right: coronal CT, a thicker wall inferiorly. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 602"],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s2-3","Vascular anomalies","The child's head and neck · The neck lump","Vascular anomalies ( Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies ) are divided into tumours and malformations . Infantile haemangioma appears in the first weeks, grows rapidly, then involutes; on imaging it is a lobulated, solid, intensely enhancing mass with high-flow vessels , often in the parotid. Lymphatic malformations are present from birth, are multicystic with thin septa, insinuate between structures across several spaces ( transspatial ), and show fluid–fluid levels after bleeding. Venous malformations are bright on T2, enhance slowly and contain phleboliths , best seen on CT or ultrasound. Figure 3 Lymphatic malformation. Left: contrast CT, a low-density lobulated mass in the submandibular space. Right: fat-suppressed T2, high fluid signal with lobular margins. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 580 Figure 4 Lymphatic malformation on ultrasound. Left: a well-defined multicystic mass with thin septa (macrocystic). Right: a largely anechoic, septated mass in the subcutaneous tissues of a newborn. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 580 Figure 5 Infantile haemangioma. Left: post-gadolinium MRI, a large lobulated, intensely enhancing mass replacing the parotid gland of a five-month-old, with prominent vessels. Right: a flow-sensitive sequence showing the high-flow vessels. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 620"],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s2-4","Malignancy and the rest","The child's head and neck · The neck lump","Most solid neck lumps in children are reactive or suppurative nodes , assessed by ultrasound. A firm, growing mass, a mass in the first months of life outside the usual sites, or nodes with systemic symptoms raise the possibility of lymphoma , rhabdomyosarcoma (orbit, nasopharynx, ear, parameningeal sites) or neuroblastoma (calcification). Fibromatosis colli is a fusiform enlargement of the sternocleidomastoid in the first weeks of life with torticollis, diagnosed on ultrasound and not to be biopsied. Figure 6 Orbital rhabdomyosarcoma in an eight-year-old. Left: coronal CT, a non-specific extraconal mass in the lower orbit without bone destruction. Right: post-gadolinium MRI, a heterogeneously enhancing solid mass. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 624"],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s3-1","Choanal atresia and pyriform aperture stenosis","The child's head and neck · The newborn nose","The clinical side is in Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses . A newborn who cannot breathe through the nose needs CT after suction and topical decongestion. Choanal atresia shows narrowing of the posterior choanae with a thickened vomer and medially bowed posterior maxilla , closed by bone, membrane or (most often) both; bilateral atresia presents with cyclical cyanosis and is associated with CHARGE syndrome, so look at the inner ears too. Congenital pyriform aperture stenosis narrows the front of the nose through overgrowth of the maxillae, and is associated with a single central incisor , which calls for assessment of the pituitary and brain. Figure 7 Choanal atresia. Left: axial CT, bony obstruction of the upper choana by an enlarged vomer and a thickened, medially placed posterior maxilla. Right: lower down, membranous atresia bridging the narrowed choana, with retained secretions. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 670 Figure 8 Congenital pyriform aperture stenosis. Left: axial CT in a newborn, overgrowth of the anterior maxillae narrowing the front of the nose. Right: a single median maxillary central incisor at the palate. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 686"],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s3-2","Midline nasal masses","The child's head and neck · The newborn nose","A midline nasal lump or pit in a child may be a dermoid (with a dermal sinus), a glioma (heterotopic brain) or an encephalocele , and any of them may connect with the cranial cavity through the foramen caecum . MRI (with thin CT of the anterior skull base) is mandatory before any biopsy or excision : an enlarged foramen caecum, a bifid crista galli or a tract to the dura means a combined approach. Figure 9 Nasal dermal sinus and dermoid. Left: drawing of a sinus tract from a nasal pit with dermoids outside and inside the skull. Right: coronal CT, a midline dermoid remodelling the margins of the foramen caecum. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 678"],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s4-1","Which test","The child's head and neck · The deaf child","The clinical side is in The deaf child and congenital hearing loss . In a child with sensorineural loss, MRI with high-resolution T2 shows the inner ear fluid, the IAC and, crucially, whether the cochlear nerve is present; CT shows the bony labyrinth, the cochlear nerve canal, the vestibular aqueduct and the middle ear, and is often added when a malformation is found or before cochlear implantation. In a child with conductive loss and a normal drum, CT looks for ossicular malformation, oval window atresia, and congenital cholesteatoma."],["ev","cl:t-im_paeds~s4-2","The child's head and neck: current evidence","The child's head and neck · The deaf child","Image gently. Children are more sensitive to radiation; cumulative CT dose is linked to a small increase in leukaemia and brain tumours (Pearce et al., Lancet 2012). CT remains right for acute complications such as mastoiditis. Cone-beam CT and low-dose protocols reduce exposure where CT is needed."],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s4-2","The malformations","The child's head and neck · The deaf child","Inner ear malformations are described by how far development went, in the Sennaroglu classification: complete labyrinthine aplasia (Michel), cochlear aplasia, common cavity , cochlear hypoplasia , and the incomplete partitions . IP-I is a cystic cochlea without a modiolus; IP-II (the Mondini deformity) has a normal basal turn but a cystic apex where the middle and apical turns fuse, with a dilated vestibule and a large vestibular aqueduct ; IP-III (X-linked) lacks the modiolus and carries a risk of a CSF gusher at surgery. A narrow or absent cochlear nerve canal or an absent cochlear nerve may make a cochlear implant ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ) pointless. Figure 10 IP-II (Mondini) malformation. Left: axial CT, a large vestibular aqueduct. Right: a mild large vestibular aqueduct with plump middle and apical turns that lack the interscalar septum and modiolus. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1102 Figure 11 Cochlear nerve deficiency. Left: axial T2 in a one-year-old with profound left loss: normal right cochlear nerve, absent left. Right: oblique sagittal T2, no cochlear nerve in the left IAC, with normal facial and vestibular nerves. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1108 Imaging a child 1 Choose the test ultrasound for lumps and glands MRI for soft tissue, inner ear and intracranial low-dose CT for bone and emergencies 2 Neck lump midline thyroglossal or dermoid lateral branchial cleft (which one) transspatial and cystic lymphatic malf"],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s5-1","Viva essentials","The child's head and neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Ultrasound and MRI first in children; when CT is right. Thyroglossal duct cyst: relation to the hyoid and a normal thyroid on ultrasound. Second branchial cleft cyst position; third and fourth arch anomalies and suppurative thyroiditis. Haemangioma versus vascular malformations. Choanal atresia versus pyriform aperture stenosis , and their associations. Midline nasal mass: image before biopsy. Inner ear malformations (common cavity, IP-I, IP-II, IP-III) and the cochlear nerve."],["sec","cl:t-im_paeds~s5-2","Common pitfalls","The child's head and neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls CT for a child's neck lump when ultrasound would do. Removing a thyroglossal cyst without confirming a normal thyroid. Biopsying a midline nasal mass with an intracranial connection. Implanting an ear with no cochlear nerve. Calling an adult's cystic level II mass a branchial cyst."],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s1-1","Timing","The treated neck · The baseline","Inflammation after radiotherapy makes early scans unreliable. The first post-treatment baseline is usually a PET-CT at about 12 weeks after chemoradiotherapy (the PET-NECK trial showed this is as safe as planned neck dissection while sparing most patients the operation), or a contrast CT or MRI at 8 to 12 weeks after surgery or radiotherapy. Scans earlier than this are more likely to show false-positive uptake and swelling. The baseline becomes the map against which every later scan is compared; surveillance then continues at intervals guided by the risk of recurrence, which is highest in the first two years."],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s1-2","Know the operation","The treated neck · The baseline","Read the operation note before the scan. A selective neck dissection may leave only subtle changes: loss of the fat around the carotid sheath and under the sternocleidomastoid. A radical neck dissection removes the sternocleidomastoid, the jugular vein and the accessory nerve; the trapezius wastes and the levator scapulae enlarges in compensation, which can be mistaken for a mass. Resection of one thyroid lobe at laryngectomy leaves the other, which can mimic a node. Figure 1 Neck dissection on imaging. Left: drawing of the nodal levels removed in different dissections. Right: contrast CT after selective neck dissection at level II on the right, with loss of the fat beneath the sternocleidomastoid and around the carotid space. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 548 Figure 2 After radical neck dissection. Left: the left sternocleidomastoid and jugular vein are absent and the levator scapulae has enlarged, a mimic of tumour. Right: absent sternocleidomastoid and jugular vein, a wasted trapezius and a pectoralis flap. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 551"],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s2-1","After radiotherapy","The treated neck · Expected changes","In the first months after radiotherapy, everything in the field swells: the skin and platysma thicken , the subcutaneous and deep fat becomes streaky , the mucosa and submucosa of the pharynx and larynx are oedematous and enhance, the retropharyngeal space fills with fluid (oedema, not a mass), and the salivary glands enhance and later shrink. Over months to years, swelling turns into fibrosis and atrophy : the tissues become thin, dark on T2 and enhance little. Expected changes are symmetric, smooth, diffuse and without a discrete mass . Figure 3 Expected changes after chemoradiotherapy. Left: a supraglottic carcinoma before treatment. Right: three months later, no residual tumour or nodes, but every fat plane and the aryepiglottic folds are swollen and hazy: expected oedema, not disease. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 556"],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s2-2","After reconstruction","The treated neck · Expected changes","Free and pedicled flaps ( Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation ) bring fat, muscle and sometimes bone. The fat component is easy to recognise; the muscle component is denervated , so it looks striated, may enhance at first and then wastes and becomes fatty. Bone flaps (fibula, iliac crest, scapula) show osteotomies and plates. The edges of a flap, where it meets native tissue, are where recurrence most often appears. Figure 4 A radial forearm free flap. Left: axial contrast CT, a well-defined, rounded fatty flap filling the defect, with surgical clips. Right: coronal CT, the flap restoring the contour of the tongue. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 552"],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s3-1","The signs","The treated neck · Is it recurrence?","Recurrence is suggested by a discrete, mass-like lesion rather than diffuse change, new or enlarging soft tissue compared with the baseline, nodular enhancement (especially at the margins of a flap or at an anastomosis), restricted diffusion (low ADC), intense, focal FDG uptake after the inflammatory period, bone destruction , new necrotic nodes , and new perineural enhancement. Most recurrences appear at the primary site or at the edge of the treated field, in the first two years. Figure 5 Left: a fibular free flap reconstructing the mandible, with osteotomies that allow the bone to be contoured. Right: recurrence at the junction of the normal tongue base and a myocutaneous flap, a discrete, uniformly enhancing nodule on the mucosal side. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 555"],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s3-2","NI-RADS","The treated neck · Is it recurrence?","The Neck Imaging Reporting and Data System (NI-RADS) gives a suspicion category for the primary site and for the neck on each surveillance scan, each tied to an action. NI-RADS categories Category Primary site Neck Usual action 1, no evidence of recurrence Expected changes only; non-mass-like distortion, diffuse mucosal enhancement No residual or new abnormal nodes Routine surveillance 2a, low suspicion Focal mucosal enhancement, not mass-like Residual or enlarging nodes without new necrosis or extranodal extension Direct inspection 2b, low suspicion Deep , ill-defined soft tissue with only mild differential enhancement (as 2) Short-interval follow-up or PET 3, high suspicion New or enlarging discrete nodule or mass with intense enhancement or FDG uptake New or enlarging necrosis or extranodal extension, or intensely FDG-avid node Biopsy 4, definite recurrence Proven by pathology, or definite progression Proven or definite Treat"],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s4-1","Osteoradionecrosis and chondronecrosis","The treated neck · Complications","Osteoradionecrosis of the mandible ( Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer , Cysts and tumours of the jaws ) appears on CT as mixed lysis and sclerosis, cortical breaks , gas in the bone, fragmentation and sequestra, and sometimes a pathological fracture, usually in the body of the mandible within the radiation field and often after a dental extraction. MRI shows marrow oedema and enhancement of the bone and surrounding soft tissue, which can look like tumour; the pattern of bone and gas on CT, and biopsy when doubtful, separate them. Chondronecrosis of the laryngeal cartilages shows fragmentation, collapse and gas. Figure 6 Osteoradionecrosis of the mandible. Left: bone CT, mixed lytic and sclerotic change with cortical interruption and gas bubbles in the bone. Right: a predominantly lytic pattern in another patient. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 564"],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s4-2","Other late effects","The treated neck · Complications","Radiotherapy can also cause temporal lobe necrosis after nasopharyngeal treatment, carotid stenosis , hypothyroidism, fibrosis of the pharynx with dysphagia and aspiration, and, rarely, a radiation-induced sarcoma years later."],["ev","cl:t-im_post~s4-3","The treated neck: current evidence","The treated neck · Complications","NI-RADS was introduced by the American College of Radiology (Aiken et al., J Am Coll Radiol 2018). PET-NECK is the trial behind the 12-week PET-CT (Mehanna et al., NEJM 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s4-3","After laryngectomy","The treated neck · Complications","After total laryngectomy ( Cancer of the larynx ) the neopharynx is a midline tube of muscle and mucosa from the tongue base to the oesophagus, in front of the spine. Recurrence appears as soft tissue thickening, often at the upper or lower anastomosis, the stoma or the paratracheal region. Figure 7 Total laryngectomy. Left: contrast CT of a T4 laryngeal carcinoma with subglottic and prelaryngeal extension. Right: drawing of the expected anatomy after laryngectomy: the neopharynx joins the oral cavity to the oesophagus, and the trachea ends at the stoma. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 568 Reading a post-treatment scan 1 Before you start what was done and when the pre-treatment scan the baseline after treatment 2 Expected change symmetric, smooth, diffuse oedema early, fibrosis late flaps and dissection changes 3 Primary site discrete mass or nodular enhancement flap margins and anastomoses DWI and PET 4 Neck new or enlarging nodes new necrosis or extranodal extension the other side 5 Complications osteoradionecrosis chondronecrosis carotid temporal lobes 6 Grade NI-RADS for primary and neck the action that goes with it"],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s5-1","Viva essentials","The treated neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Baseline scan timing: PET-CT at about 12 weeks after chemoradiotherapy; PET-NECK. Expected changes after radiotherapy and their time course. Neck dissection and flap appearances, and the mimics (levator scapulae, residual thyroid lobe). Signs of recurrence: discrete, new, nodular, restricted diffusion, focal FDG. NI-RADS categories and actions. Osteoradionecrosis versus recurrence."],["sec","cl:t-im_post~s5-2","Common pitfalls","The treated neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls Scanning too early after radiotherapy and acting on inflammatory uptake. Reading a surveillance scan without the baseline for comparison. Calling a hypertrophied levator scapulae or a remaining thyroid lobe a recurrence. Calling osteoradionecrosis tumour, or tumour osteoradionecrosis, without biopsy."],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s1-1","Protocol","Sinus CT before surgery · The study","A sinus CT for chronic rhinosinusitis is a non-contrast , low-dose, thin-section (about 1 mm or less) study, reconstructed in a bone algorithm, viewed in a bone window, and reformatted in coronal, axial and sagittal planes. The coronal plane mirrors the endoscopic view and shows the ostiomeatal unit; the sagittal plane shows the frontal recess and its cells; the axial plane shows the sphenoid, the posterior ethmoid and the relation of the optic nerve and carotid. Cone-beam CT is an alternative at lower dose. Most centres also use the dataset for image guidance during surgery ( Nose and sinus operations: principles ). Contrast and MRI are added when a complication, a tumour or invasive fungal disease is suspected. Scan after medical treatment, not during an acute attack A CT done during a cold or an acute exacerbation shows transient mucosal thickening that may not reflect chronic disease. Ideally the scan follows a course of maximal medical treatment, and it should be read with the patient's endoscopic findings in mind: up to a third of people without symptoms have some mucosal thickening."],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s2-1","The ostiomeatal unit","Sinus CT before surgery · The drainage pathways","The maxillary, anterior ethmoid and frontal sinuses all drain into the middle meatus through a common pathway called the ostiomeatal unit . On coronal CT, the uncinate process is a thin sickle of bone running up from the lateral nasal wall; lateral to it is the ethmoid infundibulum , into which the maxillary ostium opens; above it is the ethmoid bulla , the largest anterior ethmoid cell; and the gap between the free edge of the uncinate and the bulla is the hiatus semilunaris , opening into the middle meatus. A small obstruction here blocks three sinuses at once. Figure 1 Coronal CT through the ostiomeatal unit: the uncinate process, the maxillary infundibulum and ostium, the ethmoid bulla, the hiatus semilunaris and the middle meatus beneath the middle turbinate. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 250"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s2-2","The frontal recess","Sinus CT before surgery · The drainage pathways","The frontal sinus drains through its ostium into the frontal recess , an hourglass-shaped space bounded by the cells around it rather than by a true duct: the agger nasi cell in front, the ethmoid bulla (or a suprabullar cell) behind, and the lamina papyracea laterally. Where the uncinate attaches superiorly (to the lamina papyracea, the skull base or the middle turbinate) decides whether the frontal recess drains medial or lateral to it. The sagittal view is the best way to understand it. Figure 2 Sagittal CT of the frontal sinus drainage pathway: frontal sinus, ostium and infundibulum above, the frontal recess below, bounded by the agger nasi in front and the ethmoid bulla and a frontal bullar cell behind. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 266"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s2-3","The sphenoethmoidal recess","Sinus CT before surgery · The drainage pathways","The posterior ethmoid and sphenoid sinuses drain into the sphenoethmoidal recess , medial to the superior turbinate, behind the ostiomeatal unit. Disease confined to the posterior ethmoid and sphenoid means the obstruction is here, not at the middle meatus. Figure 3 Axial CT: the sphenoethmoidal recess, a narrow vertical slit in the posterior nose into which the posterior ethmoid and sphenoid sinuses drain. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 242 Patterns of obstruction Pattern Sinuses involved Site of the block Infundibular Maxillary alone Maxillary ostium or infundibulum Ostiomeatal unit Maxillary, anterior ethmoid, frontal on one side Middle meatus Sphenoethmoidal recess Posterior ethmoid and sphenoid Sphenoethmoidal recess Sinonasal polyposis All sinuses, both sides, with polyps in the nose Diffuse mucosal disease Sporadic Scattered, no drainage logic Retention cysts, mucosal thickening"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s3-1","Cells and turbinates","Sinus CT before surgery · Variants that change surgery","Variants matter when they narrow a drainage pathway or put a structure at risk. A concha bullosa (a pneumatised middle turbinate) may narrow the middle meatus. A paradoxical middle turbinate curves laterally. A Haller cell (infraorbital ethmoid cell) along the orbital floor narrows the maxillary infundibulum. A large agger nasi or other frontal cells narrow the frontal recess; these are now named by the International Frontal Sinus Anatomy Classification (agger nasi, supra agger, supra agger frontal, supra bulla, supra bulla frontal, supraorbital ethmoid and frontal septal cells), which has largely replaced the Kuhn types used in older texts. Figure 4 Coronal CT: bilateral aerated middle turbinates (concha bullosa), each narrowing its middle meatus. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 250 Figure 5 Coronal CT: a Haller (infraorbital ethmoid) cell along the orbital floor, at the roof of the maxillary infundibulum. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 250"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s3-2","The skull base: Keros","Sinus CT before surgery · Variants that change surgery","The cribriform plate sits lower than the fovea ethmoidalis (ethmoid roof), joined by the thin lateral lamella . The depth of the olfactory fossa is graded by Keros : type I 1 to 3 mm, type II 4 to 7 mm, type III 8 to 16 mm. The deeper the fossa, the longer and thinner the lateral lamella and the greater the risk of a CSF leak ( CSF rhinorrhoea ). Asymmetry between the two sides is common and should be noted. Figure 6 Coronal CT: the fovea ethmoidalis forming the ethmoid roof, stepping down to the lower cribriform plates either side of the perpendicular plate. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 23"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s3-3","The anterior ethmoid artery","Sinus CT before surgery · Variants that change surgery","The anterior ethmoid artery crosses the ethmoid roof from the orbit to the olfactory fossa, marked on coronal CT by a small notch in the medial orbital wall. When a supraorbital ethmoid cell is present, the artery may hang below the skull base in a bony mesentery and is at greater risk. Figure 7 Supraorbital ethmoid cells. Left: coronal CT, cells above the anterior ethmoidal notch, with the lateral lamella and fovea ethmoidalis. Right: axial CT, the cells lying behind the frontal sinus. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 268"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s3-4","The sphenoid and the posterior ethmoid","Sinus CT before surgery · Variants that change surgery","An Onodi (sphenoethmoidal) cell is a posterior ethmoid cell that extends lateral and above the sphenoid, carrying the optic nerve in its wall; the surgeon who mistakes it for the sphenoid may injure the nerve. Look on axial and coronal images for dehiscence of the bone over the optic nerve and the internal carotid artery in the lateral wall of the sphenoid, and for an intersinus septum that inserts onto the carotid canal: twisting it can tear the artery."],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s3-5","The lamina papyracea","Sinus CT before surgery · Variants that change surgery","The lamina papyracea is the thin lateral wall of the ethmoid. A dehiscence (congenital or after old trauma) lets orbital fat bulge medially and puts the orbit at risk. Disease that is pushing it outwards (mucocele, allergic fungal sinusitis) is also noted. The pre-operative sinus CT checklist (CLOSE) 1 C, cribriform plate Keros depth asymmetry lateral lamella any defect 2 L, lamina papyracea intact or dehiscent orbital fat herniation 3 O, Onodi cell present optic nerve in its wall 4 S, sphenoid optic nerve and carotid dehiscence septum onto carotid pneumatisation 5 E, ethmoid artery anterior ethmoid artery notch hanging below skull base supraorbital cells 6 Drainage uncinate attachment infundibulum and maxillary ostium frontal recess cells sphenoethmoidal recess 7 Disease pattern of opacification polyps osteitis hyperdensity bone erosion"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s4-1","Scoring: Lund–Mackay","Sinus CT before surgery · Reading the disease","The Lund–Mackay score grades each sinus (maxillary, anterior ethmoid, posterior ethmoid, sphenoid, frontal) on each side as 0 (clear), 1 (partial) or 2 (complete opacification), and each ostiomeatal complex as 0 or 2, for a total of 0 to 24. It is used for research and audit and correlates poorly with symptoms, but it gives a common language."],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s4-2","Chronic rhinosinusitis and polyposis","Sinus CT before surgery · Reading the disease","Chronic rhinosinusitis shows mucosal thickening and opacification in a pattern that follows the drainage pathways, often with osteitis : thickened, sclerotic sinus walls from long-standing inflammation. Polyposis fills the nose and all sinuses on both sides, often with widening of the ethmoid labyrinth. Figure 8 Chronic rhinosinusitis. Left: axial CT in cystic fibrosis, maxillary mucosal thickening with thickened sinus walls. Right: coronal CT, diffuse osteitis of the sinus walls. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 692 Figure 9 Sinonasal polyposis. Left and right: coronal CT, polyps filling the nasal cavity and occluding both middle meatuses, with opacified sinuses. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 708"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s4-3","Hyperdensity: think fungus or inspissated secretions","Sinus CT before surgery · Reading the disease","Dense material within an opacified sinus means thick, inspissated secretions, fungus or calcification. Allergic fungal sinusitis expands and fills several sinuses, usually on one side, with central high density and a low-density rim of mucosa (\"double density\"), and is dark on T2 . A fungal ball fills a single sinus (usually the maxillary) with dense material and fine calcifications, and the walls are often sclerotic. Figure 10 Allergic fungal sinusitis. Left: drawing of expanded sinuses filled with allergic mucin. Right: axial CT, unilateral expanded sinuses with central dense material and a low-density rim. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 700 Figure 11 The same disease on MRI. Left: T2, the ethmoid contents are very dark and could be mistaken for air. Right: post-gadolinium T1, only the mucosa enhances; the sinuses are full. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 700"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s4-4","Unilateral disease: exclude a tumour","Sinus CT before surgery · Reading the disease","Opacification of one side with a mass in the nose, bone destruction , or focal hyperostosis at one point of the sinus wall needs MRI. Inverted papilloma ( Sinonasal tumours ) typically arises from the lateral nasal wall near the middle meatus, often with a focal bony spur at its attachment, and has a convoluted, cerebriform pattern on T2 and post-contrast MRI. MRI separates tumour (intermediate T2, solid enhancement) from obstructed secretions (bright T2, rim enhancement). Figure 12 Inverted papilloma on MRI. Left: T2 fat-suppressed, the convoluted cerebriform pattern. Right: post-gadolinium T1, the tumour centred on the middle meatus with nasal and antral parts, separate from the obstructed secretions. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 744"],["ev","cl:t-im_sin~s4-5","Sinus CT before surgery: current evidence","Sinus CT before surgery · Reading the disease","When to scan. CT is not needed to diagnose uncomplicated acute or chronic rhinosinusitis; EPOS 2020 reserves it for failed medical treatment before surgery, unilateral disease and suspected complications. Before surgery. A systematic review of the key landmarks, as in the CLOSE checklist, reduces complications."],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s4-5","Emergencies","Sinus CT before surgery · Reading the disease","Acute invasive fungal sinusitis in an immunocompromised patient may show little on CT at first: unilateral mucosal thickening, stranding of the fat around the sinus (in front of the maxilla, in the pterygopalatine fossa, in the orbit) and subtle bone erosion. On MRI, devitalised mucosa does not enhance (the \"black turbinate\"). Endoscopy and biopsy should not wait for the scan. Orbital and intracranial complications of sinusitis need contrast-enhanced CT and often MRI; they are covered in the orbit lesson ( The orbit ). Figure 13 Acute invasive fungal sinusitis in leukaemia. Left: coronal T1, soft tissue in the right nasal cavity. Right: post-gadolinium T1, the inferior and middle turbinates do not enhance: dead mucosa. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 704"],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s5-1","Viva essentials","Sinus CT before surgery · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Protocol: thin non-contrast CT in three planes, after medical treatment. The ostiomeatal unit and the three drainage pathways. Variants: concha bullosa, Haller cell, agger nasi and frontal cells, Onodi cell. Keros classification and the lateral lamella. The CLOSE checklist before surgery. Lund–Mackay score. Hyperdense sinus: allergic fungal sinusitis versus fungal ball. Unilateral disease needs MRI; inverted papilloma features."],["sec","cl:t-im_sin~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Sinus CT before surgery · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating without reading the CT in all three planes. Mistaking an Onodi cell for the sphenoid. Calling dark T2 signal in a sinus \"air\" when it is fungal mucin. Reassuring a unilateral opacification without endoscopy or MRI. Waiting for imaging when invasive fungal sinusitis is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s1-1","Partners, not rivals","The skull base and its foramina · The study","Thin-section bone CT (1 mm or less, with multiplanar reformats) shows the foramina, the fine bony walls, erosion, remodelling and the matrix of a lesion (chondroid rings, ground glass, calcification). MRI shows the soft-tissue extent and the marrow: the pre-contrast T1 is the key sequence, because normal adult skull base marrow is fatty and bright, so tumour replacing marrow shows as grey against white. Post-gadolinium fat-suppressed T1 shows enhancement, dural spread and perineural tumour; DWI suggests epidermoid; MRA and MRV show the carotid and the venous sinuses. Read the pre-contrast T1 of the skull base first Normal marrow in the clivus, petrous apex, sphenoid and crista galli is fatty and bright on T1 in adults. A clivus that is darker than the pons on T1, or an asymmetric dark area of marrow, is the first sign of infiltration by tumour, myeloma or metastasis. In children the marrow is still red and darker, and the sphenooccipital synchondrosis stays open until about 20 years of age."],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s2-1","Anterior skull base","The skull base and its foramina · The three floors","The floor of the anterior cranial fossa is formed by the orbital plates of the frontal bone, the cribriform plate and ethmoid roof, and the planum sphenoidale. The cribriform plate sits lower than the fovea ethmoidalis (the ethmoid roof), and the two are joined by the thin lateral lamella , the weakest point and a common site of iatrogenic and spontaneous CSF leaks. Below it lie the nose, sinuses and orbits, so most anterior skull base lesions start there: sinonasal carcinoma, olfactory neuroblastoma, mucocele, osteoma and meningioma."],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s2-2","Central skull base","The skull base and its foramina · The three floors","The central skull base is the sphenoid bone and the temporal bone in front of the petrous ridge: the sella , the clivus above the synchondrosis, and the greater wings with their foramina. Below it lie the masticator, parotid and pharyngeal mucosal spaces, which is why nasopharyngeal carcinoma and masticator space tumours reach the middle cranial fossa through it. Figure 1 Coronal bone CT through the sphenoid: the foramen rotundum (maxillary nerve) above and lateral to the vidian canal (nerve of the pterygoid canal), with the pterygoid plates below. Two round holes in the sphenoid on each side. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 36 Figure 2 Axial bone CT: the foramen ovale in the greater wing of the sphenoid, the route of the mandibular nerve and of perineural spread from the masticator space. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 35"],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s2-3","Posterior skull base","The skull base and its foramina · The three floors","The posterior skull base is the bowl of the posterior fossa: the clivus below the synchondrosis, the occipital condyles and the posterior surface of the petrous bone. It contains the internal auditory canal , the jugular foramen (an anteromedial pars nervosa with the glossopharyngeal nerve and a larger posterolateral pars vascularis with the jugular bulb, vagus and accessory nerves), the hypoglossal canal below and medial to it, and the foramen magnum. On coronal CT, the jugular tubercle between the jugular foramen and the hypoglossal canal forms the \"beak\" of an eagle's head. Figure 3 Coronal bone CT: the \"eagle\" of the posterior skull base. The jugular tubercle (the beak) separates the jugular foramen above from the hypoglossal canal below. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 46 Figure 4 Axial bone CT at the mid jugular foramen: the smaller anteromedial pars nervosa and the larger posterolateral pars vascularis. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 43"],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~c3","The foramina that matter","The skull base and its foramina · The foramina that matter","Holes, contents and the disease that uses them. The ENT foramina Foramen or fissure Main contents Disease that uses it Cribriform plate Olfactory nerve fibres CSF leak, encephalocele, olfactory neuroblastoma, sinonasal carcinoma Optic canal Optic nerve, ophthalmic artery Fractures, sphenoid and orbital apex disease Superior orbital fissure III, IV, VI, V1, superior ophthalmic vein Orbital apex and cavernous sinus spread Inferior orbital fissure Infraorbital nerve and vessels Orbit to pterygopalatine fossa and infratemporal fossa Foramen rotundum Maxillary nerve (V2) Perineural spread from the cheek, palate and sinus Vidian canal Nerve of the pterygoid canal Juvenile angiofibroma; perineural spread to the petrous carotid Foramen ovale Mandibular nerve (V3) Perineural spread from the masticator space; trigeminal schwannoma Foramen spinosum Middle meningeal artery Landmark lateral to the foramen ovale Foramen lacerum Fibrocartilage; carotid passes above Nasopharyngeal carcinoma's route to the cavernous sinus Stylomastoid foramen Facial nerve Perineural spread from the parotid Jugular foramen IX, X, XI, jugular bulb Paraganglioma, schwannoma, meningioma Hypoglossal canal Hypoglossal nerve Schwannoma, metastasis, clival tumours"],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s3-1","The pterygopalatine fossa: the crossroads","The skull base and its foramina · The foramina that matter","The pterygopalatine fossa is a small fat-filled space behind the maxillary antrum, and on axial CT and T1 MRI it should contain fat . It connects medially to the nose through the sphenopalatine foramen , laterally to the infratemporal fossa through the pterygomaxillary fissure , upwards to the orbit through the inferior orbital fissure, backwards to the middle cranial fossa through the foramen rotundum and to the foramen lacerum through the vidian canal , and downwards to the palate through the palatine canals. Loss of its fat on either side is a key sign of perineural tumour, juvenile angiofibroma , invasive fungal disease or lymphoma. Figure 5 Axial bone CT: the pterygopalatine fossa behind the maxillary antrum, opening medially through the sphenopalatine foramen and laterally through the pterygomaxillary fissure. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 10 Figure 6 Juvenile angiofibroma. Left: post-gadolinium T1, an enhancing mass in the nasal cavity and nasopharynx arising near the sphenopalatine foramen. Right: the same tumour with flow voids, widening the pterygopalatine fossa, destroying the pterygoid base and entering the masticator space. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 740"],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s3-2","Perineural spread","The skull base and its foramina · The foramina that matter","Tumour can travel along nerves through the skull base, often silently. Look for an enlarged, enhancing nerve, loss of fat at the opening of a foramen (pterygopalatine fossa, foramen ovale, stylomastoid foramen), widening of the foramen, filling of Meckel's cave , and denervation of the muscles supplied (atrophy of the muscles of mastication with V3, of the tongue with XII). Adenoid cystic carcinoma ( Salivary gland tumours ), squamous carcinoma of the skin and lip ( Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck ), and lymphoma are the common culprits."],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s4-1","Normal variation first","The skull base and its foramina · The petrous apex","The petrous apex is pneumatised in about a third of people, often asymmetrically, and otherwise contains fatty marrow . Asymmetric marrow is bright on T1 and can be mistaken for a lesion; it suppresses with fat saturation and the bone is not expanded on CT. Fluid trapped in apical air cells after otitis is bright on T2 without expansion or bone loss. Neither needs treatment. Figure 7 Axial CT: asymmetric pneumatisation of the petrous apices, aerated on one side and marrow-filled on the other. Variation, not disease. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 97 Figure 8 Asymmetric marrow. Left: T1, a bright fatty focus in one petrous apex and dark air in the other. Right: CT, the bright side is simply marrow, with no expansion. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1140"],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s4-2","The lesions","The skull base and its foramina · The petrous apex","Petrous apex lesions on MRI and CT Lesion T1 T2 Gadolinium DWI CT Asymmetric marrow Bright, suppresses with fat-sat Intermediate None No restriction Normal, not expanded Trapped fluid Dark Bright None No restriction Opacified cells, intact septa, no expansion Cholesterol granuloma Bright, does not suppress Bright None No restriction Smooth expansion, thin cortex Cholesteatoma or epidermoid Dark Bright None Restricted Smooth expansion Apicitis Dark Bright Enhancing marrow and dura Abscess restricts Destruction of trabeculae and cortex Mucocele Variable Bright Rim No restriction Expanded air cell Figure 9 Cholesterol granuloma of the petrous apex. Left: drawing of an expansile cystic lesion. Right: axial CT, smooth expansion of the left apex with thinned, partly dehiscent cortex; the other apex is well pneumatised. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1152"],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s4-3","Apicitis","The skull base and its foramina · The petrous apex","Infection spreading from the middle ear into a pneumatised apex causes petrous apicitis , classically with Gradenigo's triad of otorrhoea, deep facial pain (V) and a sixth nerve palsy. CT shows destruction of the apical trabeculae, and MRI shows enhancing marrow and dura and any abscess."],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s5-1","Chordoma and chondrosarcoma","The skull base and its foramina · The clivus and the jugular foramen","Chordoma ( Skull base and the parapharyngeal space ) arises in the midline clivus , destroys bone, contains fragments of bone, is very bright on T2 and often \"thumbs\" the pons. Chondrosarcoma arises off the midline , at the petrooccipital fissure , contains chondroid rings and arcs of calcification on CT, and is also very bright on T2. Position, more than signal, separates them. Figure 10 Clival chordoma. Left: drawing of an expansile destructive midline mass indenting the pons. Right: sagittal T1, low-signal tumour replacing the clivus, with the classic \"thumb\" against the pons. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 874 Figure 11 Chondrosarcoma. Left: drawing of the lesion centred on the petrooccipital fissure with chondroid calcification. Right: T2, a very bright off-midline mass compressing the petrous carotid. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 952"],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s5-2","The jugular foramen","The skull base and its foramina · The clivus and the jugular foramen","Three tumours make up nearly all jugular foramen masses, and the bone margin on CT separates them. A paraganglioma (glomus jugulare; Middle ear and temporal bone tumours , Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies ) gives permeative, moth-eaten erosion, spreads superolaterally into the middle ear, and on MRI shows \"salt and pepper\" (flow voids and slow flow) with intense enhancement. A schwannoma smoothly enlarges and scallops the foramen, often extending along the nerve, without flow voids. A meningioma causes sclerotic, permeative change or hyperostosis and spreads along the dura with a dural tail. A high or dehiscent jugular bulb and asymmetric foramina are normal variants. Figure 12 Glomus jugulare paraganglioma on MRI. Left: T1, a jugular foramen mass with salt-and-pepper flow voids. Right: post-gadolinium T1, intense enhancement with intracranial extension against the medulla. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 892 Figure 13 The same tumour on CT. Left: drawing of its spread from the jugular foramen into the middle ear. Right: coronal CT, permeative-destructive margins and extension up into the middle ear. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 892"],["ev","cl:t-im_skb~s6-1","The skull base and its foramina: current evidence","The skull base and its foramina · CSF leaks and encephaloceles","Perineural spread along V2, V3 and the facial nerve is easily missed and changes treatment; contrast MRI with fat suppression is the test."],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s6-1","Imaging a leak","The skull base and its foramina · CSF leaks and encephaloceles","The clinical side is in CSF rhinorrhoea . A thin-section bone CT of the anterior and middle skull base finds most defects: the cribriform plate and lateral lamella, the ethmoid roof, the sphenoid (especially a pneumatised lateral recess ), the frontal sinus, and the tegmen in the ear. MRI (heavily T2-weighted) then shows whether the defect contains CSF, meninges or brain (meningocele or meningoencephalocele ), and separates fluid from retained mucus in an opacified sinus. CT cisternography is kept for difficult cases. In spontaneous leaks, look for signs of raised intracranial pressure: an empty sella, multiple defects and arachnoid pits. Figure 14 CSF leak. Left: coronal CT, a bony defect in the ethmoid roof lateral to the middle turbinate attachment with ethmoid opacification; MRI showed a meningoencephalocele. Right: CT after intrathecal contrast, contrast passing through a sphenoid defect. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 920 Reading a skull base study 1 Marrow pre-contrast T1 clivus, petrous apex, sphenoid dark or asymmetric marrow compare sides 2 Foramina rotundum, ovale, vidian, stylomastoid, jugular, hypoglossal widened, eroded or filled fat at their openings 3 Pterygopalatine fossa fat present on both sides connections widening 4 Bone remodelled or destroyed matrix (chondroid, ground glass, sclerosis) 5 Lesion by site anterior sinonasal, olfactory, meningioma clivus midline chordoma petrooccipital fissure chondrosarcoma jugular foramen paraganglioma, schwannoma, meningioma 6 Intracranial dura, cavernous sinus, "],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s7-1","Viva essentials","The skull base and its foramina · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for CT versus MRI for the skull base, and why the pre-contrast T1 matters. Foramina and contents , especially rotundum, ovale, vidian, stylomastoid and jugular. The pterygopalatine fossa and its connections. Signs of perineural spread. Petrous apex: marrow, fluid, cholesterol granuloma and cholesteatoma by signal. Chordoma versus chondrosarcoma , and the three jugular foramen tumours by their bone margins. CSF leak: CT for the defect, MRI for the content."],["sec","cl:t-im_skb~s7-2","Common pitfalls","The skull base and its foramina · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling asymmetric marrow or a non-pneumatised apex a lesion. Missing marrow infiltration by reading only post-contrast images. Missing loss of fat in the pterygopalatine fossa. Calling fat in the crista galli a dermoid, or an open synchondrosis a fracture in a teenager."],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s1-1","Three layers, many spaces","The neck by spaces · The fascia and the spaces","The superficial layer of the deep cervical fascia invests the neck: it surrounds the masticator and parotid spaces above the hyoid and the sternocleidomastoid, trapezius and strap muscles below. The middle layer lines the deep surface of the pharyngeal mucosa above and wraps the visceral space (larynx, trachea, thyroid, oesophagus) below. The deep layer surrounds the perivertebral space, and a slip of it, the alar fascia , separates the retropharyngeal space from the danger space behind it. All three contribute to the carotid sheath ."],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s1-2","The suprahyoid neck","The neck by spaces · The fascia and the spaces","Above the hyoid, a pair of fat-filled parapharyngeal spaces sits at the centre, surrounded by four key spaces: the pharyngeal mucosal space medially, the masticator space in front, the parotid space laterally and the carotid space behind. In the midline behind the pharynx are the retropharyngeal space and the perivertebral space . Figure 1 The spaces of the suprahyoid neck at the level of the nasopharynx, colour-coded: pharyngeal mucosal space (blue), masticator spaces (purple), parotid spaces (green), carotid spaces with the artery and vein (red), the fat of the parapharyngeal spaces (yellow-orange) in the middle of them, and the perivertebral space (magenta) behind. The fascial layers are drawn as coloured lines. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 6 Figure 2 The same spaces on axial T2 MRI: the pharyngeal mucosal space, masticator space, parotid space (outlined) and carotid space arranged around the parapharyngeal fat, with the prevertebral muscles behind. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 284"],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s1-3","The infrahyoid neck","The neck by spaces · The fascia and the spaces","Below the hyoid, the visceral space in the centre holds the larynx and hypopharynx, the trachea and oesophagus, the thyroid and parathyroid glands, and the recurrent laryngeal nerves. On either side are the carotid spaces , behind them the posterior cervical spaces (fat, the accessory nerve and level V nodes), and in the midline behind are the retropharyngeal and perivertebral spaces. The anterior cervical space is the fat between the carotid space and the visceral space. Figure 3 Axial contrast CT at the glottis: the visceral space with the larynx and strap muscles, the carotid spaces behind it on either side, the posterior cervical spaces and the perivertebral space. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 279"],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~c2","What each space contains","The neck by spaces · What each space contains","And therefore what arises there. The spaces, their contents and their common lesions Space Normal contents Common lesions Pharyngeal mucosal Mucosa, lymphoid tissue (adenoids, tonsils), minor salivary glands, constrictor muscles Squamous cell carcinoma, lymphoma, tonsillar abscess, minor salivary tumour Parapharyngeal Fat, occasional minor salivary rests Rare primary lesions; mostly displaced by its neighbours. Pleomorphic adenoma of salivary rests, lipoma Masticator Mandibular ramus and body, muscles of mastication, V3, pterygoid venous plexus Odontogenic abscess, sarcoma, perineural tumour on V3, benign masseter hypertrophy Parotid Parotid gland, facial nerve, intraparotid nodes, retromandibular vein Pleomorphic adenoma, Warthin tumour, carcinoma, nodes ( Salivary gland tumours ) Carotid Internal carotid, internal jugular vein, IX to XII (only X below the hyoid), sympathetic chain Paraganglioma, schwannoma, thrombosis, nodes alongside Retropharyngeal Fat; lateral (Rouvière) and medial retropharyngeal nodes above the hyoid Nodal metastasis, suppurative node, abscess, oedema Perivertebral Vertebrae, prevertebral and scalene muscles, brachial plexus, vertebral artery Discitis and osteomyelitis, metastasis, chordoma, brachial plexus schwannoma Visceral Larynx, hypopharynx, trachea, oesophagus, thyroid, parathyroids Carcinoma of each, thyroid and parathyroid lesions Posterior cervical Fat, accessory nerve, level V nodes Nodes, lymphatic malformation, lipoma, schwannoma"],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s3-1","The displacement rule","The neck by spaces · Where did it start?","The parapharyngeal space is mostly fat and rarely the origin of disease, so it acts as a marker. A mass growing in one of the four surrounding spaces pushes the fat away from itself: a pharyngeal mucosal mass pushes the fat laterally ; a masticator space mass pushes it posteriorly (and medially); a deep lobe parotid mass pushes it medially (and widens the stylomandibular gap); a carotid space mass pushes it anteriorly , and lifts the styloid process forwards; a lateral retropharyngeal mass pushes it anteriorly without displacing the styloid. A mass completely surrounded by fat arises in the parapharyngeal space itself, usually from salivary rests. Figure 4 Axial contrast CT at the level of the maxillary ridge: the pharyngeal mucosal space outlined between the paired parapharyngeal fat pads, with the masticator spaces in front and the carotid spaces behind. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 277 Figure 5 A true parapharyngeal space pleomorphic adenoma. Left: CT, a well-defined mass completely surrounded by parapharyngeal fat. Right: T1 MRI, the mass separate from the deep lobe of the parotid, with a rim of fat all round. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 14 Figure 6 A deep lobe parotid pleomorphic adenoma. Left: drawing of a pear-shaped tumour pushing the parapharyngeal fat medially. Right: T2, the bright tumour passing through a widened stylomandibular notch and displacing the fat and the tonsil medially. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 88"],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s3-2","The carotid space and the vessels","The neck by spaces · Where did it start?","In the carotid space, the position of the vessels refines the diagnosis. A carotid body paraganglioma ( Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies ) sits in the bifurcation and splays the internal and external carotid arteries apart. A vagal paraganglioma or schwannoma, higher up, pushes the internal carotid anteriorly (and medially). A sympathetic schwannoma, medial to the vessels, separates neither. Paragangliomas enhance intensely and show flow voids (\"salt and pepper\"); schwannomas enhance less and may be cystic. Figure 7 Carotid body paraganglioma. Left: drawing of the tumour in the bifurcation, splaying the internal and external carotids. Right: contrast CT, an avidly enhancing mass sitting in the notch between the two arteries. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 140"],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s4-1","Infection","The neck by spaces · Spaces as pathways","The clinical side is in Deep neck space infection . The spaces also explain how infection travels. A peritonsillar abscess can rupture through the superior constrictor into the parapharyngeal fat and on to the masticator or carotid spaces. Dental infection enters the masticator, submandibular and sublingual spaces. The retropharyngeal space extends down into the superior mediastinum (to somewhere between T1 and T6, depending on the text; Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx ), and the danger space behind it to the diaphragm, so infection there threatens the mediastinum. A suppurative retropharyngeal node in a child (a rim-enhancing node, often lateral) is not the same as a true retropharyngeal abscess (fluid distending the space from side to side, often with mass effect on the airway). Figure 8 Tonsillar abscess complications. Left: a tonsillar abscess extending through the capsule into the buccal space and the medial pterygoid. Right: a large abscess that has ruptured into the carotid space and the submandibular space. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 28 Figure 9 Retropharyngeal abscess. Left: drawing of the collection behind the pharynx. Right: contrast CT in an infant, a low-density collection distending the retropharyngeal space and pushing the pharynx forwards. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 168"],["ev","cl:t-im_spaces~s4-2","The neck by spaces: current evidence","The neck by spaces · Spaces as pathways","Deep neck infection. Contrast CT is the standard test for suspected deep neck abscess, but it overcalls abscess: a rim-enhancing low-density collection is pus in only about three-quarters of cases, so clinical response matters. Ultrasound helps in children and for superficial collections and can guide aspiration. Mediastinitis. Extension below the level of the carina on CT needs thoracic surgical involvement."],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s4-2","Abscess, phlegmon or cellulitis?","The neck by spaces · Spaces as pathways","On contrast CT, cellulitis is stranding of the fat; phlegmon is ill-defined, enhancing soft tissue without a fluid centre; an abscess is a fluid collection with a well-formed enhancing rim. Only a mature abscess is drained. Look also for the airway, the internal jugular vein (thrombosis, Lemierre syndrome; Approach to a sore throat ), the carotid, the mediastinum and the cause (tooth, stone, tonsil, foreign body). Placing a deep neck mass 1 Above or below the hyoid suprahyoid find the parapharyngeal fat infrahyoid visceral, carotid, posterior cervical 2 Which way is the fat pushed laterally mucosal posteriorly masticator medially deep parotid anteriorly carotid or retropharyngeal surrounded parapharyngeal itself 3 What normally lives there match to the contents table 4 Look at the vessels splayed carotid body ICA pushed forwards vagal stylomandibular gap widened deep parotid 5 Character cyst, fat, enhancement, flow voids, bone 6 Differential two to four diagnoses by space and character"],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s5-1","Viva essentials","The neck by spaces · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three layers of deep cervical fascia and what they wrap. The suprahyoid spaces around the parapharyngeal fat, and the infrahyoid spaces . The displacement rule for the parapharyngeal fat. Contents of each space and the lesions that arise there. Carotid space masses: carotid body versus vagal paraganglioma versus schwannoma. Danger space and mediastinal spread; abscess versus phlegmon."],["sec","cl:t-im_spaces~s5-2","Common pitfalls","The neck by spaces · For the examination","Common pitfalls Describing a deep neck mass without naming its space. Calling a deep lobe parotid tumour \"parapharyngeal\" when the fat is pushed medially. Draining a phlegmon. Calling a suppurative retropharyngeal node a retropharyngeal abscess."],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s1-1","Protocol","The temporal bone on CT · The study","A temporal bone CT is a non-contrast , thin-section study: slices of 0.5 to 0.6 mm through each ear separately, reconstructed with a bone algorithm and viewed in a wide bone window (width about 4000 HU). Axial images are made parallel to the lateral semicircular canal, and coronal images are reformatted from the same volume. Oblique reformats help with particular questions: the Pöschl plane, parallel to the superior canal, for superior canal dehiscence, and the Stenvers plane, perpendicular to the petrous ridge, for the cochlea and the vestibular aqueduct. Contrast adds nothing to the bone question; when soft tissue matters (a mass, the IAC, the labyrinth's fluid, recurrent cholesteatoma), MRI is the complementary study. Figure 1 Oblique reformat along the long axis of the cochlea: the whole basal turn in one image, with the superior vestibular nerve canal and the labyrinthine facial nerve just above it. Oblique planes rescue structures that run obliquely to the axial plane. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 90"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s1-2","How to scroll","The temporal bone on CT · The study","Read each ear alone, then compare the two sides at the same level. Follow tubular structures from slice to slice rather than looking for them on a single image: the facial nerve from the IAC to the stylomastoid foramen, the ossicular chain from the malleus head to the stapes, each semicircular canal around its loop. The normal tympanic membrane is barely visible; the ossicles, scutum, and the thin bony plates (tegmen, sigmoid plate, the bone over the superior canal) should be crisp."],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s2-1","Upper levels: the canals and the attic","The temporal bone on CT · The normal ear in axial","Start at the top. The first structure seen is the superior semicircular canal rising under the arcuate eminence. Moving down, the aditus ad antrum joins the epitympanum to the mastoid antrum, and the lateral semicircular canal comes into view, with the porus of the internal auditory canal medially and the vestibular aqueduct on the posterior surface of the petrous bone. Figure 2 Highest axial level: the superior semicircular canal projects upwards from the inner ear under the arcuate eminence. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 54 Figure 3 At the lateral semicircular canal: the porus of the internal auditory canal, the vestibule and the fovea of the vestibular aqueduct medially; the malleus head in the attic, Prussak's space, the antrum and Körner's septum laterally. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 54"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s2-2","The attic and the facial nerve","The temporal bone on CT · The normal ear in axial","At the level of the labyrinthine segment of the facial nerve, just above the cochlea, the attic shows the ice-cream cone : the malleus head (the ice cream) sitting on the body and short process of the incus (the cone). Lateral to the ossicles lies Prussak's space , the lateral epitympanic recess, which is where the common pars flaccida cholesteatoma begins. A little lower, the tympanic segment runs from the geniculate ganglion anteromedially, beneath the lateral canal, to the posterior genu, and the cog , a bony ridge, crosses the anterior attic. Figure 4 Through the labyrinthine facial nerve: malleus head and short process of incus (the ice-cream cone), Prussak's space lateral to them, the mastoid antrum and Körner's septum behind. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 55 Figure 5 The tympanic facial nerve runs from the geniculate ganglion to the posterior genu. The cog crosses the anterior epitympanum. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 55"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s2-3","The posterior wall: three recesses","The temporal bone on CT · The normal ear in axial","At the oval window level, the posterior wall of the middle ear shows three landmarks from medial to lateral: the sinus tympani , the pyramidal eminence (with the stapedius) and the facial recess , with the mastoid facial nerve just behind. The sinus tympani is hidden from the surgeon's view through a standard mastoidectomy, so disease there must be reported. Figure 6 Magnified axial view of the posterior mesotympanum: sinus tympani, pyramidal eminence and facial recess, with the mastoid segment of the facial nerve and the stapedius behind; the incudostapedial joint and manubrium in front. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 83"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s2-4","Lower levels: cochlea, carotid and jugular","The temporal bone on CT · The normal ear in axial","At the mid-cochlear level, the incudostapedial joint and the tensor tympani tendon from the cochleariform process are seen. Lower still, the sigmoid plate separates mastoid air cells from the sigmoid sinus, the horizontal petrous carotid runs anteromedially, and the jugular foramen sits posteromedially. At the bottom, the stylomastoid foramen lies just anteromedial to the mastoid tip, where the facial nerve leaves the skull. Figure 7 Mid-cochlear level: cochleariform process and tensor tympani tendon, and the incudostapedial joint between the lenticular process and stapes head. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 56 Figure 8 Mesotympanum: the faint normal tympanic membrane, the horizontal petrous carotid running anteromedially, and the jugular foramen. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 57"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s3-1","The planes that show the roof and the walls","The temporal bone on CT · The normal ear in coronal","Coronal images show the tegmen (the roof of the middle ear and mastoid), the scutum (the sharp upper attachment of the tympanic membrane), the tympanic annulus below, the oval window with the stapes, the lateral canal bulging into the attic, and the tympanic facial nerve under it. These are the structures cholesteatoma erodes, so the coronal plane is where the cholesteatoma questions are answered. Figure 9 At the oval window: the basal turn of the cochlea, the barely visible tympanic membrane, and the tympanic annulus as the lower landmark between middle ear and canal. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 59 Figure 10 Mid mesotympanum: Prussak's space bounded by the lateral attic wall and scutum laterally and the ossicles medially. A sharp scutum is normal; a blunted one is the earliest bony sign of attic cholesteatoma. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 60 Figure 11 The tegmen tympani roofing the attic, with the scutum, Prussak's space and the incus body beneath it; the tympanic annulus below, and the labyrinthine and anterior tympanic facial nerve above the cochlea. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 60 Figure 12 Anteriorly: a thick, well-defined tegmen, the geniculate ganglion on the cochlea and the horizontal carotid beneath it. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 61"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s4-1","Lateral to medial","The temporal bone on CT · A checklist for every temporal bone","Reading a temporal bone CT 1 External canal patency and width soft tissue bone erosion or new bone (exostoses, osteoma) atresia 2 Tympanic membrane and scutum thickening, retraction scutum sharp or blunted 3 Middle ear spaces aeration of attic, mesotympanum, hypotympanum Prussak's space sinus tympani and facial recess 4 Ossicles malleus head and incus body (ice-cream cone) long process and lenticular process of incus stapes erosion, dislocation, fixation 5 Walls and covers tegmen tympani and tegmen mastoideum lateral canal (fistula) facial canal (dehiscence) sigmoid plate 6 Mastoid pneumatisation opacification septal erosion (coalescence) outer cortex 7 Inner ear cochlea (two and a half turns) vestibule three canals and their bony covers vestibular aqueduct width lucency of the otic capsule 8 Nerves and canals IAC width and symmetry facial nerve course from IAC to stylomastoid foramen 9 Vessels carotid canal wall jugular bulb (high, dehiscent, eroded) sigmoid sinus 10 Petrous apex air, marrow or fluid expansion or erosion compare the two sides Always compare sides Asymmetry is often the first clue. Pneumatisation of the petrous apex, height of the jugular bulb and width of the vestibular aqueduct vary between people, so a finding is judged against the other ear before it is called abnormal."],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s5-1","Opacification: fluid, granulation or cholesteatoma?","The temporal bone on CT · Common patterns","CT shows soft tissue in the middle ear well but cannot tell its nature: fluid, inflamed mucosa, granulation, cholesterol granuloma and cholesteatoma all look the same density. What separates them on CT is what the tissue does to bone . Opacification with intact ossicles, scutum and septa favours effusion or granulation; a soft-tissue mass with blunting of the scutum, ossicular erosion and medial displacement of the ossicles favours cholesteatoma. Where doubt remains, non-EPI diffusion MRI shows keratin as bright. Figure 13 Chronic otitis media. Left: a normal short process of the incus and malleus head. Right: the other ear, with deossification of the short process of the incus and an underpneumatised mastoid. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1046"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s5-2","Cholesteatoma","The temporal bone on CT · Common patterns","The disease is described in Cholesteatoma . The pars flaccida (attic) cholesteatoma, about 80% of acquired cases, fills Prussak's space, blunts the scutum and pushes the malleus head and incus medially, then spreads back through the aditus into the antrum. The pars tensa cholesteatoma begins in the posterosuperior mesotympanum, erodes the long process of the incus and the stapes, and fills the sinus tympani and facial recess. The report should answer five surgical questions: is the tegmen intact, is there a labyrinthine fistula (usually the lateral canal), is the facial canal dehiscent, is the sinus tympani involved, and what has happened to the ossicles . Figure 14 A small pars flaccida cholesteatoma. Left: drawing of the attic sac in Prussak's space. Right: coronal CT, soft tissue in Prussak's space, a blunted scutum and a mildly eroded, medially displaced malleus head. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1050 Figure 15 A large pars flaccida cholesteatoma. Left: drawing of ossicular erosion, lateral canal fistula and tegmen scalloping. Right: coronal CT, a mass filling the middle ear and mastoid with a fistula into the lateral semicircular canal. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1050 Figure 16 Pars tensa cholesteatoma. Left: erosion of the short process of the incus and spread through the aditus into the antrum. Right: erosion of the incus and stapes, with disease in the facial recess and the sinus tympani, the blind spot for the surgeon. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and "],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s5-3","Otosclerosis","The temporal bone on CT · Common patterns","The disease is described in Otosclerosis . The otic capsule is normally uniformly dense, among the densest bone in the body. Fenestral otosclerosis appears as a lucent (otospongiotic) focus at the fissula ante fenestram , just anterior to the oval window, sometimes thickening the footplate. Cochlear otosclerosis adds lucent foci around the cochlea, the \"double ring\" or halo. The changes are subtle: look anterior to the oval window on every scan of a patient with conductive loss and an intact drum. Figure 17 Fenestral otosclerosis. Left: drawing of the plaque around the footplate. Right: coronal CT, a lucent focus just anterior to the oval window. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1120 Figure 18 Cochlear otosclerosis. Left: drawing of the pericochlear halo. Right: axial CT, lucent foci around the cochlea with the fenestral focus at the fissula. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1120"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s5-4","Superior canal dehiscence","The temporal bone on CT · Common patterns","The disease is described in Superior canal dehiscence . Absence of bone over the superior canal produces a \"third window\", with autophony, sound- or pressure-induced vertigo and an air–bone gap with normal stapedial reflexes. It must be confirmed on thin slices in the Pöschl plane, because partial volume can make thin normal bone disappear. Figure 19 Superior semicircular canal dehiscence. Left: drawing of the uncovered canal and a thin tegmen. Right: coronal CT, no bone over the roof of the canal. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 1134"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s5-5","Fractures","The temporal bone on CT · Common patterns","Temporal bone fractures are described by their relation to the petrous ridge and, more usefully, by whether they spare or violate the otic capsule . Longitudinal fractures run along the petrous ridge, through the canal and middle ear, and disrupt the ossicles (incus dislocation most often), giving conductive loss. Transverse fractures cross the ridge and the otic capsule, and cause sensorineural loss, vertigo and facial palsy. In every fracture look at the ossicles, the facial canal (the geniculate region is the commonest site of injury), the tegmen (CSF leak), the carotid canal and the jugular foramen. Sutures, fissures and small canals can mimic a fracture. Figure 20 A transverse, otic capsule-violating fracture. Left: the line crosses the vestibule and reaches the tympanic facial nerve canal. Right: lower down, it extends across the jugular foramen into the round window niche. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 968"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s6-1","Before atresia surgery","The temporal bone on CT · The congenital ear","In congenital aural atresia , CT answers the questions that decide whether reconstruction is feasible: the thickness and type of the atresia plate (bony or membranous), the size of the middle ear , the ossicles (usually fused malleus and incus), the course of the facial nerve (often displaced anteriorly and laterally over the atresia plate), the oval and round windows , any congenital cholesteatoma behind the plate, and the inner ear , which is usually normal because it develops separately. Scoring systems such as the Jahrsdoerfer grade combine these findings."],["ev","cl:t-im_tb~s6-2","The temporal bone on CT: current evidence","The temporal bone on CT · The congenital ear","HRCT of the temporal bones remains the standard for chronic ear disease, trauma and cochlear implant planning ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ). Non-echo-planar diffusion-weighted MRI detects cholesteatoma with a sensitivity of about 94% (Li et al., Laryngoscope 2013) and is used instead of second-look surgery. Cone-beam CT gives excellent bone detail at low dose and can confirm cochlear implant electrode position. Superior canal dehiscence is overcalled on standard CT; VEMP testing confirms it alongside the Pöschl reformat."],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s6-2","The vestibular aqueduct","The temporal bone on CT · The congenital ear","A large vestibular aqueduct is the commonest imaging finding in children with sensorineural loss ( The deaf child and congenital hearing loss ). The aqueduct is measured at its midpoint on axial images (the Cincinnati criteria: more than 1 mm at the midpoint or more than 2 mm at the operculum) and is often associated with an incomplete partition of the cochlea. Figure 21 Transverse oblique reformat profiling the vestibular aqueduct along its length, the plane in which its width is best judged. Chapman and Koontz, Imaging Anatomy: Head and Neck, p. 90"],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s7-1","Viva essentials","The temporal bone on CT · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Protocol: thin bone-algorithm CT, no contrast, axial and coronal, oblique planes when needed. Name the structures on an axial slice: ice-cream cone, Prussak's space, sinus tympani, facial recess, pyramidal eminence. The five cholesteatoma questions: tegmen, fistula, facial canal, sinus tympani, ossicles. Scutum blunting as the early sign of attic cholesteatoma. Fenestral otosclerosis at the fissula ante fenestram. Fractures: otic capsule-sparing versus violating, and the structures to check. Atresia checklist and the large vestibular aqueduct."],["sec","cl:t-im_tb~s7-2","Common pitfalls","The temporal bone on CT · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling middle ear opacification \"cholesteatoma\" without bony evidence; CT cannot tell its nature. Missing disease in the sinus tympani. Diagnosing a dehiscence of the tegmen, facial canal or superior canal on a single slice. Overlooking subtle fenestral otosclerosis in a patient with conductive loss and a normal drum. Calling a suture or a small canal a fracture."],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s1-1","Bone, soft tissue, vessel or function?","Choosing the test · Start from the question","Most ENT imaging questions fall into four kinds. Bone and air questions (sinus anatomy before surgery, ossicles, otic capsule, fractures, erosion by cholesteatoma) belong to CT . Soft tissue questions (the extent of a tumour, perineural or intracranial spread, the IAC, the labyrinth's fluid, cholesteatoma recurrence) belong to MRI . Superficial lumps and glands (thyroid, salivary glands, neck nodes, a lump in a child) belong to ultrasound , with fine-needle aspiration in the same sitting. Function questions (the swallow, aspiration, a pouch, metabolic activity of a tumour) belong to contrast swallow, videofluoroscopy, nuclear medicine or PET-CT . Common ENT questions and the test that answers them Question First test Why Chronic rhinosinusitis, planning sinus surgery Non-contrast CT sinuses (or cone-beam CT) Bony anatomy, variants, skull base and orbit Unilateral sinus opacification or suspected tumour CT and contrast MRI CT for bone; MRI separates tumour from secretions and shows orbit and dura Orbital or intracranial complication of sinusitis Contrast-enhanced CT; MRI if intracranial Abscess, cavernous sinus, empyema Chronic otitis media, cholesteatoma, conductive loss Non-contrast thin-section CT temporal bones Ossicles, scutum, tegmen, lateral canal, facial canal Residual or recurrent cholesteatoma ( Cholesteatoma ) Non-EPI diffusion MRI Detects keratin down to about 3 mm Asymmetric sensorineural loss or tinnitus ( Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours ) MRI of the internal auditory meatus (high-resolution T2 ± gadolinium) Vestibular schwannoma and other CPA lesion"],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s2-1","Ultrasound","Choosing the test · The tests one by one","Ultrasound is quick, cheap, free of radiation and dynamic, and in skilled hands is the most accurate way to characterise thyroid nodules, salivary lumps and neck nodes and to guide FNA or core biopsy. A normal lymph node is oval with a bright fatty hilum; an abnormal one is round, loses its hilum, and may show necrosis, microcalcification or peripheral vascularity. Ultrasound cannot see behind bone or air, so it does not show the deep lobe of the parotid well, the retropharyngeal space, the larynx or the skull base, and it depends on the operator. Figure 1 Abnormal neck nodes on ultrasound. Left: round, without a fatty hilum and with microcalcifications, typical of papillary thyroid cancer metastasis. Right: cystic change and loss of the hilum; fine-needle aspiration confirmed metastasis. ESR Modern Radiology, Head and Neck Imaging"],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s2-2","Plain films","Choosing the test · The tests one by one","Plain radiographs have a small, specific role: a lateral soft-tissue neck film for a radio-opaque foreign body or for the thumb sign of supraglottitis (only if the airway is safe and it will not delay care), an orthopantomogram for dental disease, osteoradionecrosis and mandibular fractures, and a chest film. Sinus X-rays are no longer used to diagnose sinusitis. Figure 2 Plain films that still help. Left: lateral neck in acute supraglottitis, the swollen epiglottis narrowing the airway. Right: orthopantomogram showing osteoradionecrosis of the mandible. ESR Modern Radiology, Head and Neck Imaging"],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s2-3","CT and MRI","Choosing the test · The tests one by one","CT and MRI are covered in their own lessons ( CT for the ENT surgeon , MRI made simple ). In short, CT is fast, widely available and best for bone and air, acute infection and trauma; MRI is best for soft-tissue extent, nerves, the labyrinth, the skull base and recurrence, but is slow, sensitive to motion and contraindicated with some implants. In cancer of the skull base, sinuses and nasopharynx the two are complementary."],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s2-4","PET-CT","Choosing the test · The tests one by one","PET-CT combines a CT with fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) uptake, which is high in most cancers but also in inflammation, infection, recent surgery, brown fat and normal lymphoid tissue (tonsils, adenoids). It is used for advanced-stage cancer and distant metastases, for the unknown primary, for suspected recurrence, and to assess response at about 12 weeks after chemoradiotherapy: the PET-NECK trial showed that surveillance by PET-CT, operating only on residual uptake, was as safe as planned neck dissection and spared most patients the operation. Too early a scan gives false positives from radiation inflammation. Figure 3 PET shows widespread disease in a child with lymphoma: the metabolic picture that CT alone would miss. ESR Modern Radiology, Head and Neck Imaging"],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s2-5","Swallow studies","Choosing the test · The tests one by one","A barium swallow shows the anatomy of the pharynx and oesophagus: pouch, web, stricture, ring, achalasia and tumour. Videofluoroscopy (modified barium swallow) with a speech therapist shows the dynamics of the oral and pharyngeal phases and aspiration with different textures. Water-soluble contrast is used when perforation or a leak is suspected. Figure 4 Contrast swallow images of the pharynx and oesophagus: a dynamic study shows both structure and transit. Dunnebier, Imaging for Otolaryngologists"],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s2-6","Angiography and nuclear medicine","Choosing the test · The tests one by one","Catheter angiography is now used mainly for treatment: embolisation of juvenile angiofibroma, paraganglioma and epistaxis, and balloon occlusion testing before carotid sacrifice. Nuclear medicine offers sestamibi and SPECT-CT for parathyroid adenoma ( Hyperparathyroidism ), iodine and technetium scans for the thyroid, and salivary scintigraphy for function."],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s3-1","Radiation, contrast and children","Choosing the test · Safety and stewardship","Each CT adds radiation, most important in children and for the lens of the eye. Ultrasound and MRI are preferred in children where they can answer the question; MRI in young children may need sedation or a \"feed and wrap\" approach in infants. Iodinated contrast is checked against allergy, renal function and planned radioactive iodine; gadolinium against renal function and pregnancy."],["ev","cl:t-im_test~s3-2","Choosing the test: current evidence","Choosing the test · Safety and stewardship","Choosing the test. The ACR Appropriateness Criteria and the Royal College of Radiologists' iRefer guidance rate imaging for common clinical problems; in the UK, the Ionising Radiation (Medical Exposure) Regulations 2017 require every exposure to be justified. Incidental findings are common (thyroid nodules, sinus mucosal thickening, small vestibular-region lesions) and should be managed by agreed pathways rather than repeated scanning."],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s3-2","Writing a good request","Choosing the test · Safety and stewardship","State the clinical findings, the specific question, and anything that changes the protocol: suspected cholesteatoma (thin bone CT), pulsatile tinnitus (add angiography), cancer staging (contrast, include chest), cochlear implant or other metal (MRI safety). A precise question gets a precise answer. Choosing the test 1 What do you need to see? bone, air, ossicles, fracture CT soft tissue, nerve, skull base, recurrence MRI superficial lump or gland ultrasound ± FNA swallow or aspiration barium or videofluoroscopy 2 Is it urgent? airway, abscess, complication, trauma contrast CT now otherwise plan the best test 3 Is it a child? prefer ultrasound or MRI CT for bone questions and emergencies 4 Is it cancer? primary and neck by CT or MRI chest CT PET-CT for advanced, unknown primary or response at 12 weeks"],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s4-1","Viva essentials","Choosing the test · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Match the test to the question: bone, soft tissue, superficial gland or function. Ultrasound features of an abnormal node. PET-CT: indications, false positives, and timing after chemoradiotherapy (PET-NECK). Barium swallow versus videofluoroscopy. Imaging children with the least radiation. A good imaging request."],["sec","cl:t-im_test~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Choosing the test · For the examination","Common pitfalls Ordering a CT for a thyroid nodule or a child's neck lump instead of ultrasound. Requesting PET-CT too soon after radiotherapy. Ordering MRI for a fracture or ossicular detail."],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s1-1","Protocol","Trauma · The study","Non-contrast thin-section CT (about 1 mm or less) in a bone algorithm, with multiplanar and 3D reformats , is the standard for facial, temporal bone and laryngeal fractures; it is done in one pass with the head CT and, where indicated, the cervical spine. CT angiography is added when a vessel may be injured: fractures through the carotid canal , the foramen transversarium or the skull base, a penetrating neck wound, an expanding haematoma, a neurological deficit not explained by the brain scan, or severe midface fractures. MRI is kept for the optic nerve, brain, ligaments and the facial nerve when needed later."],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~c2","The face","Trauma · The face","Buttresses and patterns. The clinical management of these fractures is in Nasal and facial fractures ."],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s2-1","The buttresses","Trauma · The face","The midface is a frame of thick vertical buttresses (nasomaxillary, zygomaticomaxillary and pterygomaxillary) joined by horizontal ones (the frontal bar, the infraorbital rims and orbital floors, the zygomatic arches and the palate and alveolus). Fractures are described by which buttresses are broken, because repair restores them. The pterygoid plates link the midface to the skull base, and are broken in every Le Fort fracture."],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s2-2","Le Fort fractures","Trauma · The face","Le Fort fractures separate part of the midface from the skull base, and each type has a unique fracture that identifies it: Le Fort I (horizontal, separating the alveolus and palate) breaks the lateral margin of the nasal aperture ; Le Fort II (pyramidal) breaks the inferior orbital rim ; Le Fort III (craniofacial dissociation) breaks the zygomatic arch and the lateral orbital wall. All three break the pterygoid plates. Fractures are often mixed and different on the two sides. Figure 1 Le Fort fractures. Left: drawing of the three levels. Right: 3D CT of a Le Fort I fracture separating the maxillary alveolus from the midface, through the nasal aperture. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 982 Figure 2 Left: 3D CT of a Le Fort II fracture through both inferior orbital rims, with rotation of the midface and malocclusion. Right: a Le Fort III fracture with nasofrontal separation, medial and lateral orbital wall and pterygoid plate fractures. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 982"],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s2-3","Zygomaticomaxillary complex","Trauma · The face","The zygoma is attached at four points, and a zygomaticomaxillary complex (tetrapod) fracture separates it at all of them: the zygomaticofrontal suture (lateral orbital rim), the zygomatic arch , the inferior orbital rim and floor , and the anterior and posterolateral maxillary walls , with the zygomaticosphenoid suture in the lateral orbital wall. Report the displacement and rotation of the zygoma, the orbital floor defect, and the infraorbital canal. Figure 3 Zygomaticomaxillary complex fracture. Left: axial CT, fractures of the anterior and lateral walls of the maxillary sinus with swelling over the cheek. Right: 3D CT, the classic pattern through the maxillary sinus walls, zygomatic arch and lateral orbital wall. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 986"],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s2-4","Nasoorbitoethmoid and frontal sinus fractures","Trauma · The face","Nasoorbitoethmoid fractures drive the nasal bones back between the orbits. The key question is whether the fragment carrying the medial canthal tendon is intact, displaced or comminuted, which decides the repair and the risk of telecanthus. Look also at the nasolacrimal ducts , the frontal recess and the anterior skull base. Frontal sinus fractures are described by the anterior table , the posterior table (dura and CSF leak) and the frontal sinus outflow through the frontal recess, which predicts later mucocele. Figure 4 Nasoorbitoethmoid fracture. Left: axial CT, comminuted fractures of the nasoorbitoethmoid complex. Right: lower down, the fractures involve both nasolacrimal ducts; epiphora is expected. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 990"],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s2-5","The orbit","Trauma · The face","Blowout fractures, muscle entrapment, retrobulbar haemorrhage and optic canal fractures are covered in the orbit lesson ( The orbit ). In any midface fracture, look at the orbital floor and medial wall, the globe, the optic canal and the superior orbital fissure."],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s2-6","The mandible","Trauma · The face","The mandible is a ring with the skull base, so it often breaks in two places (for example an angle on one side and the parasymphysis on the other). Report each fracture by site (condyle, ramus, angle, body, parasymphysis, symphysis), displacement, involvement of teeth (a fracture through a tooth socket is open), the inferior alveolar canal and the temporomandibular joint . A panoramic radiograph complements CT. Figure 5 Mandible fractures. Left: drawing of a ramus fracture crossing the inferior alveolar canal. Right: axial CT, displaced fractures of the right angle and left parasymphysis, the typical two-site pattern. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 992"],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s3-1","The pattern and the structures","Trauma · The temporal bone","The clinical side is in Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea . Temporal bone fractures are classed as longitudinal , transverse or oblique to the petrous ridge, but the classification that predicts complications is whether the otic capsule is spared or violated . Capsule-sparing fractures (the large majority) run through the squamous bone, external canal and middle ear, disrupt the ossicles (incudostapedial separation and incus dislocation are commonest) and cause conductive loss. Capsule-violating fractures cross the cochlea, vestibule or canals, cause sensorineural loss and vertigo, and are more often associated with facial palsy and CSF leak . In every fracture check the ossicles, the facial canal (the geniculate region is the commonest site of injury), the tegmen , the carotid canal (CT angiography if involved), the jugular foramen and the sigmoid sinus. Figure 6 Otic capsule-violating fractures. Left: an old transverse fracture through the otic capsule to the basal turn, with new bone in the cochlea (labyrinthitis ossificans). Right: a fracture through the labyrinthine facial nerve canal and the vestibule, typically with permanent deafness and facial palsy. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 968 Do not call a suture a fracture The temporal bone is full of sutures (petrotympanic, tympanosquamous, occipitomastoid), fissures and small canals (the cochlear and vestibular aqueducts, the subarcuate canal). Compare with the other side and follow the line across slices before calling it a fracture."],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s4-1","Laryngeal trauma","Trauma · The larynx and the neck","The clinical side is in Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis . After blunt injury to the larynx, CT (when the airway is secure) shows fractures of the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, displacement of fragments, arytenoid dislocation , haematoma narrowing the airway, and air in the soft tissues. A fractured cricoid or a displaced thyroid fracture usually needs open repair; isolated haematoma and minor oedema are usually managed conservatively. Endoscopy remains essential for the mucosa. Figure 7 Laryngeal fracture. Left: a step at the thyroid notch and a subtle fracture of the right thyroid lamina with inward rotation of the fragment. Right: lower down, displacement of the right arytenoid and widening of the cricothyroid space. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 278"],["ev","cl:t-im_trauma~s4-2","Trauma: current evidence","Trauma · The larynx and the neck","Head injury. NICE (NG232, 2023) and the Canadian CT head rule (Stiell et al., Lancet 2001) define who needs CT of the head after injury; CT of the cervical spine is included when indicated. Facial fractures. Thin-slice CT with three-dimensional reconstruction is standard for all but isolated nasal fractures, which are diagnosed clinically."],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s4-2","Penetrating neck injury","Trauma · The larynx and the neck","The neck is traditionally divided into zones : zone I from the clavicles to the cricoid, zone II from the cricoid to the angle of the mandible, zone III above the angle to the skull base. Unstable patients with hard signs (expanding haematoma, pulsatile bleeding, airway compromise, shock) go to theatre. In stable patients, CT angiography of the neck is now the first test whatever the zone (the \"no zone\" approach); it shows the track, vascular injury (occlusion, dissection, pseudoaneurysm, extravasation), and injury to the airway, pharynx and oesophagus, which may need contrast swallow or endoscopy if the track passes close to them. Figure 8 Penetrating laryngeal injury on CT angiography. Left: a pen through the supraglottis, its tip close to the left vertebral artery. Right: a gunshot wound with soft tissue air, shrapnel near the spine and comminuted fractures of the cricoid and arytenoid. Koch, Hamilton, Hudgins and Harnsberger, Diagnostic Imaging: Head and Neck, 3rd ed., p. 278 Reading a facial or neck trauma CT 1 Airway narrowing, haematoma, displaced fragments laryngeal cartilages 2 Midface buttresses pterygoid plates Le Fort unique fractures zygoma NOE and canthal fragment 3 Orbit floor and medial wall muscle globe retrobulbar blood or air optic canal 4 Frontal sinus and skull base anterior and posterior tables frontal recess cribriform plate pneumocephalus 5 Mandible each fracture site teeth inferior alveolar canal condyles and joints 6 Temporal bone otic capsule ossicles facial canal tegmen carotid canal 7 Vessels indications for CT angiography carotid and vertebral "],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s5-1","Viva essentials","Trauma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Thin CT with 3D reformats; indications for CT angiography. Facial buttresses and the pterygoid plates. Le Fort unique fractures: nasal aperture (I), inferior orbital rim (II), zygomatic arch (III). Zygomaticomaxillary complex fracture components. NOE fractures: the medial canthal tendon fragment. Frontal sinus: anterior table, posterior table, outflow. Mandible: two fractures, open fractures through teeth. Temporal bone: capsule sparing versus violating; structures to check. Penetrating neck injury: zones, hard signs, CT angiography."],["sec","cl:t-im_trauma~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Trauma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing the second mandibular fracture. Calling a Le Fort fracture without broken pterygoid plates. Calling a temporal bone suture a fracture. Missing a carotid canal fracture and the need for CT angiography. Missing a posterior table frontal sinus fracture and a CSF leak."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s1-1","Clinical assessment","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Approach","Most lesions present as a painless bony expansion of the jaw. When the cortex has thinned it gives a characteristic \"egg-shell crackling\" on pressure, and the teeth over it may be displaced, loose or, if they never erupted, missing. Pain and infection usually mean that a benign lesion has become secondarily infected, but numbness of the lower lip from involvement of the inferior alveolar nerve points towards malignancy or osteomyelitis and should never be dismissed. Test the vitality of the related teeth with cold or an electric pulp tester. The rule is simple and useful: every jaw cyst except the periapical (radicular) cyst is associated with vital teeth. Aspirate before any incisional biopsy. Blood suggests a vascular lesion such as an arteriovenous malformation ( Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies ) or aneurysmal bone cyst, and a pulsatile or blood-filled lesion must never be biopsied blind , because torrential bleeding may follow. Straw-coloured fluid suggests a dentigerous or radicular cyst, cheesy keratin an odontogenic keratocyst, and a dry tap a solid tumour or a traumatic bone cavity."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s1-2","Imaging","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Approach","A panoramic radiograph (OPG) with intraoral films is the first step. CT or cone-beam CT then defines size, any cortical breach, the relation to the inferior alveolar canal and the maxillary sinus, and features of malignancy, while MRI shows soft-tissue extension. Radiological groups Pattern Examples Radiolucent, unilocular Radicular, residual, dentigerous, lateral periodontal and nasopalatine cysts; odontogenic keratocyst; Stafne defect; unicystic ameloblastoma; adenomatoid odontogenic tumour Radiolucent, multilocular (\"soap bubble\") Ameloblastoma, odontogenic keratocyst, odontogenic myxoma, central giant cell granuloma, aneurysmal bone cyst, glandular odontogenic cyst, Pindborg tumour Radiolucent, multiple Keratocysts in Gorlin syndrome, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, cherubism, myeloma, metastases Radiolucent, ill-defined Osteomyelitis, osteoradionecrosis, MRONJ, malignancy, metastases, Langerhans cell histiocytosis (\"floating teeth\") Radio-opaque, well defined Odontoma, cementoblastoma, osteoma, focal cemento-osseous dysplasia Radio-opaque, ill-defined Fibrous dysplasia (\"ground glass\"), osteosarcoma (\"sunburst\"), chondrosarcoma, Paget disease (\"cotton wool\") Mixed Ossifying fibroma, calcifying odontogenic cyst, adenomatoid tumour, cemento-osseous dysplasia Figure 1 The teeth, palate and retromolar trigone, and the maxilla with its alveolar process. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-jaw~c2","Cysts of the jaws","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Cysts of the jaws","Odontogenic and non-odontogenic. A true cyst has an epithelial lining. Odontogenic cysts arise from the remnants of tooth-forming epithelium, and each remnant gives its own cyst: the rests of Malassez give radicular cysts, the reduced enamel epithelium gives dentigerous cysts, and the dental lamina (rests of Serres) gives keratocysts and lateral periodontal cysts."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s2-1","Radicular (periapical) cyst","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Cysts of the jaws","The radicular cyst is the commonest jaw cyst . It follows death of the pulp of a tooth, which leads to a periapical granuloma that then becomes cystic. On the radiograph it is a well-defined, round lucency at the root apex of the non-vital tooth, and it may announce itself with a sinus onto the gum or skin. It is treated by root canal treatment, or by extraction with enucleation of the cyst. A cyst that persists after the tooth has gone is a residual cyst , and is enucleated."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s2-2","Dentigerous (follicular) cyst","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Cysts of the jaws","The dentigerous cyst is the second commonest. It forms around the crown of an unerupted tooth , attached at the amelocemental junction, usually a lower third molar or an upper canine, in patients aged 10 to 30. It expands painlessly and may push the tooth a long way, into the ramus or the antrum. Most are enucleated with the tooth. Large cysts in children are better marsupialised or decompressed, which lets the cyst shrink and the tooth erupt. Rarely an ameloblastoma or carcinoma arises in the wall, which is why the lining is always sent for histology. Its soft-tissue counterpart in a child, the eruption cyst , is a bluish swelling of the gum over an erupting tooth that resolves as the tooth comes through."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s2-3","Odontogenic keratocyst (OKC)","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Cysts of the jaws","The keratocyst was renamed a \"keratocystic odontogenic tumour\" in 2005 and reclassified as a cyst in 2017 (and again in 2022), but whatever its label it behaves aggressively. It usually occupies the posterior body and ramus of the mandible of a young adult, and because it extends along the medulla with little expansion it is often large by the time it is found. Histology shows a thin, parakeratinised lining with a palisaded, hyperchromatic basal layer and daughter cysts in the wall, and the aspirate is cheesy keratin. The friable lining and satellite cysts explain recurrence rates of up to 30%. Large cysts are first decompressed, then enucleated with a peripheral ostectomy or Carnoy solution; multiple recurrences need resection, and follow-up is long. Multiple keratocysts, especially in a child, mean Gorlin syndrome (naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome), an autosomal dominant condition caused by a PTCH1 mutation. Look for multiple basal cell carcinomas ( Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck ), palmar and plantar pits, a calcified falx, bifid ribs, frontal bossing with hypertelorism, and medulloblastoma, and avoid radiation in these patients. The orthokeratinised odontogenic cyst looks similar but has an orthokeratinised lining without palisading, rarely recurs and carries no syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s2-4","Other odontogenic cysts","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Cysts of the jaws","The lateral periodontal cyst is a small lucency between the roots of vital lower premolars and canines in adults. The glandular odontogenic cyst of the anterior mandible can be confused with central mucoepidermoid carcinoma, recurs, and may need resection. The calcifying odontogenic (Gorlin) cyst contains \"ghost cells\" with calcification, and the paradental (buccal bifurcation) cyst lies buccal to an erupting lower molar in a child."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s2-5","Non-odontogenic cysts","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Cysts of the jaws","The nasopalatine (incisive canal) cyst is the commonest non-odontogenic cyst. It appears as a heart-shaped lucency between vital upper central incisors, with a swelling behind them, and is enucleated. The nasolabial (nasoalveolar) cyst lies in soft tissue beneath the ala, filling the nasolabial fold and lifting the nasal floor, mostly in women; it is lined by respiratory epithelium and is removed through a sublabial approach or marsupialised endoscopically into the nose. Three lesions are pseudocysts, without an epithelial lining. The traumatic (simple) bone cavity is an empty space in a teenager that scallops between the roots and heals after surgical exploration. The aneurysmal bone cyst is a blood-filled expansile \"soap bubble\" treated by curettage. The Stafne defect is a lingual depression below the inferior alveolar canal at the angle, filled by submandibular gland, and needs no treatment."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s3-1","Ameloblastoma","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Odontogenic tumours","The ameloblastoma is the commonest clinically significant odontogenic tumour: benign, but locally aggressive . It affects adults in the third to fifth decades, and about 80% arise in the mandible, mostly in the molar–ramus region . It grows slowly and painlessly, displacing and loosening teeth and resorbing their roots. The radiograph shows a multilocular \"soap-bubble\" or honeycomb lucency with cortical expansion and knife-edge root resorption . Histologically there are islands of odontogenic epithelium whose peripheral columnar cells palisade with reverse polarisation of their nuclei away from the basement membrane, in follicular, plexiform, acanthomatous or desmoplastic patterns. Most mandibular tumours carry BRAF V600E , and BRAF-targeted therapy is now used for unresectable or recurrent disease. Several variants matter. The unicystic ameloblastoma of young patients often mimics a dentigerous cyst; its luminal type can be enucleated with close follow-up, but the mural type behaves like a solid tumour. The peripheral form is a gum mass. The metastasising ameloblastoma has benign histology yet spreads to lung or nodes, and ameloblastic carcinoma is frankly malignant. Treatment is resection with about 1 to 1.5 cm margins beyond the radiological edge , including a soft-tissue margin wherever the cortex has been breached: a marginal or segmental mandibulectomy, or a partial maxillectomy, with reconstruction by a fibula free flap and dental implants. Enucleation and curettage give recurrence rates of 50 to 90%. Maxillary tumours are the more dangerous because they can reach th"],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s3-2","Other odontogenic tumours","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Odontogenic tumours","The odontoma is the commonest odontogenic tumour overall, though really a hamartoma. Compound odontomas in the anterior maxilla contain small tooth-like structures, and complex odontomas in the posterior mandible are a disorganised mass of enamel and dentine. Both are radio-opaque with a lucent rim, often stop a tooth from erupting, and are enucleated. The adenomatoid odontogenic tumour occurs in teenage girls in the anterior maxilla around an unerupted canine, as a lucency with fine calcifications, and is enucleated. The calcifying epithelial odontogenic (Pindborg) tumour of adults in the posterior mandible contains amyloid with concentric Liesegang ring calcifications and is resected with a margin. The ameloblastic fibroma of young patients is enucleated. The odontogenic myxoma , with its \"soap bubble\" or \"tennis racket\" lucency of stellate cells in myxoid stroma, is unencapsulated and infiltrative, and recurs in about a quarter even after resection. The cementoblastoma is a radio-opaque mass fused to a lower molar root with a lucent halo, removed with the tooth."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s4-1","Fibro-osseous lesions","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Non-odontogenic lesions","In fibrous dysplasia bone is replaced by fibrous tissue and woven bone as a result of a GNAS mutation. About 80% is monostotic; polyostotic disease with café-au-lait macules and endocrine overactivity such as precocious puberty is McCune–Albright syndrome . It causes painless facial asymmetry in a child or teenager, the maxilla more often than the mandible, and growth usually slows after puberty. The radiograph shows an ill-defined ground-glass expansion blending into normal bone. Most patients are observed, recontouring is done for deformity once growth stops, and the optic canal is decompressed if vision is threatened ( Proptosis and the orbit ). The ossifying fibroma , by contrast, is a true, well-demarcated neoplasm of mixed lucency and opacity, typically in the mandible of young women; its juvenile trabecular and psammomatoid forms are aggressive in children and in the sinuses. It is enucleated or resected. Cemento-osseous dysplasia , periapical, focal or florid, occurs in middle-aged women of African or Asian descent and is best left alone, because extraction is followed by infection."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s4-2","Giant cell lesions","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Non-odontogenic lesions","The central giant cell granuloma affects patients under 30 in the anterior mandible and may cross the midline. Its multinucleated giant cells are indistinguishable from those of a brown tumour of hyperparathyroidism , so calcium and PTH must always be checked ( Hyperparathyroidism ). It is treated by curettage or with intralesional steroids, calcitonin or denosumab. Cherubism is autosomal dominant and produces painless bilateral swelling of the mandibular angles, and often the maxillae, from the age of two to five, giving the \"cherub\" face with upturned eyes. The radiograph shows multilocular lucencies, and the condition regresses after puberty."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s4-3","Bone tumours","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Non-odontogenic lesions","An osteoma is a dense, well-defined mass. Multiple osteomas with colonic polyposis and soft-tissue tumours make up Gardner syndrome (an APC mutation), and colonoscopy is mandatory because the polyps become malignant. The osteoblastoma is painful, and any cartilaginous jaw tumour, even one called a chondroma, should be treated as a possible chondrosarcoma. Osteosarcoma of the jaw occurs a decade or two later than in the limbs, in the third and fourth decades, and also follows radiotherapy and Paget disease. It presents with swelling, pain, loose teeth and lip numbness, and the radiograph shows a sunburst periosteal reaction and symmetrical widening of the periodontal ligament space (Garrington sign). It needs radical resection with clear margins and chemotherapy. Chondrosarcoma affects older adults, the anterior maxilla more than the mandible, and needs radical resection because it resists radiotherapy and chemotherapy. Metastases from breast, lung, prostate, kidney and thyroid usually land in the posterior mandible and present with pain, lip numbness or an extraction socket that will not heal; the \" numb chin \" can be the first sign of cancer. Myeloma and lymphoma give punched-out lesions, and Langerhans cell histiocytosis in children produces punched-out lucencies around the roots so that the teeth seem to float (\" floating teeth \"); it is treated by curettage, steroids, low-dose radiotherapy or chemotherapy. Finally, squamous carcinoma of the overlying mucosa ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer ) invades the mandible through the occlusal ridge or the to"],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s4-4","Paget disease","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Non-odontogenic lesions","Paget disease is disordered bone remodelling in older people. The maxilla and skull enlarge with \" cotton-wool \" radiodensities, the roots show hypercementosis, the alkaline phosphatase rises, and bony overgrowth can compress cranial nerves and cause hearing loss. It is treated with bisphosphonates."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s5-1","Osteomyelitis","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Osteonecrosis and infection","Osteomyelitis usually affects the mandible after dental infection or a fracture, and diabetes and immunosuppression predispose to it. It causes pain, swelling, pus and sequestra, and numbness of the lower lip ( Vincent sign ). Treatment is debridement, sequestrectomy and prolonged antibiotics."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s5-2","Osteoradionecrosis","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Osteonecrosis and infection","Osteoradionecrosis follows radiotherapy, especially above 60 Gy, and is described fully in Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer . It is prevented by dental treatment before radiotherapy, and treated along a ladder from debridement and pentoxifylline–tocopherol to resection with a free fibula flap."],["ev","cl:t-jaw~s5-3","Cysts and tumours of the jaws: current evidence","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Osteonecrosis and infection","Medication-related osteonecrosis of the jaw (MRONJ). The AAOMS definition used above also counts bone that can be probed through a fistula; risk is about 1% with oncological doses and below 0.1% with osteoporosis doses, and drug holidays are not routinely recommended for osteoporosis doses (Ruggiero et al., J Oral Maxillofac Surg 2022). Osteoradionecrosis. In the HOPON trial, hyperbaric oxygen ( Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen ) did not reduce the risk of osteoradionecrosis after dental extractions in irradiated jaws (Shaw et al., Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys 2019); pentoxifylline with tocopherol is widely used, on observational evidence."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s5-3","Medication-related osteonecrosis (MRONJ)","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · Osteonecrosis and infection","MRONJ is exposed bone for more than eight weeks in a patient taking antiresorptive drugs such as bisphosphonates or denosumab, or antiangiogenic drugs, who has never had radiotherapy to the jaw. The risk is highest with intravenous treatment for cancer, and it usually follows an extraction, so dental treatment should be completed before the drugs are started. It is managed with chlorhexidine, antibiotics and conservative debridement, with resection for advanced disease."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s6-1","Viva essentials","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Systematic reading of a jaw radiograph, and why aspiration comes before biopsy. Radicular versus dentigerous cyst: the tooth relationship and vitality. Odontogenic keratocyst: its lining, why it recurs, and Gorlin syndrome. Ameloblastoma: age, site, soap-bubble picture, reverse polarity, resection margins, BRAF. Fibrous dysplasia (ground glass) versus ossifying fibroma (well-defined). Giant cell granuloma: exclude hyperparathyroidism. Osteosarcoma signs; the numb chin. Osteoradionecrosis and MRONJ."],["sec","cl:t-jaw~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Cysts and tumours of the jaws · For the examination","Common pitfalls Biopsying a jaw lucency without aspirating first, and meeting an AVM. Curetting an ameloblastoma. Missing Gorlin syndrome in a young patient with several keratocysts. Calling a central giant cell lesion without checking calcium and PTH. Extracting teeth in an irradiated jaw or in a patient on intravenous bisphosphonates without precautions."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s1-1","Definition","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Overview","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (also called nasopharyngeal fibroma) is a benign, locally aggressive, highly vascular tumour arising at the back of the nasal cavity, near the sphenopalatine foramen."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s1-2","Epidemiology","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Overview","The commonest benign tumour of the nasopharynx, yet under 1% of head and neck tumours. Almost exclusively in boys and young men of 10 to 20 years."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s1-3","Why it matters","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Overview","Profuse epistaxis, anaemia, and spread into the orbit and skull base. Biopsy in clinic can cause catastrophic bleeding. It does not regress on its own, so waiting is not an option."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s2-1","Why adolescent boys","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Aetiology and pathology","It is thought to be testosterone-dependent: a hamartomatous nidus of vascular tissue, perhaps a remnant of the first branchial arch artery, is activated at puberty. About three-quarters of tumours carry androgen receptors."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s2-2","Molecular clues","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Aetiology and pathology","Vascular endothelial growth factor and insulin-like growth factor II drive growth; stromal cells show beta-catenin activity, and C-MYC is found in large tumours. Angiofibroma is about 24 times commoner in familial adenomatous polyposis of the colon."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s2-3","Site of origin","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Aetiology and pathology","The posterior nasal cavity, at the superior margin of the sphenopalatine foramen, near the junction of the sphenoidal process of the palatine bone with the root of the pterygoid plate."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s2-4","Gross and microscopic pathology","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Aetiology and pathology","A reddish-purple, lobulated, sessile mass covered by mucosa, without a capsule. Two components: endothelium-lined vascular spaces lacking muscle and elastic tissue (so they cannot contract and bleed profusely), and a stroma of stellate and spindle cells laying down collagen, whose amount decides how firm the tumour is. Figure 1 Angiofibroma: dilated vascular spaces in a fibrous stroma. Dhingra, fig. 49.4"],["sec","cl:t-juv~s3-1","Three modes","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Spread","Along paths of least resistance: into the nose and nasopharynx, and through fissures and foramina: the foramen rotundum, vidian (pterygoid) canal, palatovaginal canal and inferior orbital fissure. By expansion: pushing the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus forwards from the pterygopalatine fossa (the antral sign). By bone destruction: at the root of the pterygoid and the greater wing of the sphenoid. Figure 2 First, the pterygopalatine fossa from the side, with its routes to the orbit, infratemporal fossa and cranial cavity. Second, the openings in its posterior wall: foramen rotundum, vidian canal and palatovaginal canal. Dhingra, figs 49.1 and 49.2 Figure 3 Communications of the pterygopalatine fossa, the crossroads through which the tumour spreads. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-juv~s3-2","Extensions","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Spread","Nasal cavity and nasopharynx, blocking the choanae and displacing the septum. Paranasal sinuses: maxillary, sphenoid, ethmoid. Pterygopalatine fossa, then through the pterygomaxillary fissure into the infratemporal and temporal fossae and the cheek. Orbit, through the inferior orbital fissure: proptosis, diplopia, optic nerve compression. Cranial cavity in 20 to 25%, mainly the middle fossa (lateral to the internal carotid if it erodes the floor, medial if it comes through the sphenoid and sella), less often the anterior fossa; usually extradural, sometimes into the cavernous sinus. Figure 4 Axial view of the spread: into the nose, the nasopharynx, behind the antrum into the pterygopalatine fossa, and on into the cheek. Dhingra, fig. 49.3 Recurrence hides in canals Tumour left in the vidian canal or the basisphenoid is the commonest source of recurrence: drill the canal at surgery."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s4-1","Nasal and nasopharyngeal","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Clinical features","Profuse, recurrent, spontaneous epistaxis: the commonest presentation, often with anaemia ( Epistaxis ). Progressive nasal obstruction with a hyponasal voice. Nasal discharge from obstruction and sinusitis."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s4-2","Ear","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Clinical features","Conductive hearing loss and otitis media with effusion from tubal obstruction."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s4-3","Eye and nerves","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Clinical features","Proptosis and diplopia; involvement of the second, third, fourth and sixth nerves with orbital and intracranial spread."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s4-4","Face","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Clinical features","Broadening of the nasal bridge, swelling of the cheek, and a frog face with bilateral proptosis."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s4-5","Examination","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Clinical features","Endoscopy shows a smooth or lobulated, pink to purplish mass with prominent surface vessels in the back of the nose and nasopharynx, obstructing one or both choanae, sometimes pushing the soft palate down or hanging into the oropharynx. Never palpate it with a finger outside the operating theatre. Figure 5 Endoscopic view of an angiofibroma (arrow) in the left nasal cavity, with prominent surface vessels; septum (S), inferior turbinate (IT), middle turbinate (MT). Dhingra, fig. 49.5 Never biopsy in clinic Diagnosis is made by imaging. If tissue is truly needed to exclude another tumour, biopsy is done under general anaesthesia with blood cross-matched and everything ready to control bleeding."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s5-1","Contrast CT","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Investigations","The investigation of choice. It shows the enhancing mass, bone erosion and the extent of spread. Anterior bowing of the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus (the antral or Holman-Miller sign) is pathognomonic. Widening of the pterygopalatine fossa and erosion of the vidian canal are typical."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s5-2","MRI","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Investigations","Complements CT for soft tissue extension: intracranial, cavernous sinus, orbit, infratemporal fossa. Flow voids give a salt and pepper appearance."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s5-3","Angiography and embolisation","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Investigations","Digital subtraction angiography shows the feeders, mainly from the external carotid (the internal maxillary artery's sphenopalatine and posterior nasal branches, the ascending pharyngeal and ascending palatine arteries), and from the internal carotid in large or intracranial tumours. External carotid feeders can be embolised; surgery should follow within 24 to 48 hours (up to 72 in some series), before collaterals develop. Embolisation carries risks of stroke, blindness and cranial nerve palsy, so some surgeons omit it for small tumours. Figure 6 First, the specimen of an extensive angiofibroma and its CT, showing bone destruction and spread into the pterygopalatine fossa. Second, angiography before and after embolisation. Dhingra, figs 49.6 and 49.7"],["sec","cl:t-juv~s5-4","Preparation","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Investigations","Group and cross-match two to three units of blood."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s5-5","Differential diagnosis","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Investigations","Antrochoanal polyp ( Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps ), enlarged adenoids, olfactory neuroblastoma ( Sinonasal tumours ), rhabdomyosarcoma, lobular capillary haemangioma and haemangiopericytoma."],["sec","cl:t-juv~c6","Staging","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Staging","Extent decides the approach. Several systems exist (Sessions, Fisch, Chandler, Radkowski, Andrews, and newer endoscopic systems). The older ones (Fisch, Radkowski, Andrews) stage by extent only: extension into the sinuses, pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae, orbit and cranial cavity. The University of Pittsburgh Medical Center (UPMC) system adds residual vascularity from the internal carotid artery after embolisation, and predicts blood loss and recurrence better. UPMC staging of angiofibroma Stage Extent I Nasal cavity, medial pterygopalatine fossa II One or more sinuses, lateral pterygopalatine fossa; no residual vascularity III Skull base erosion, orbit or infratemporal fossa; no residual vascularity IV Skull base erosion, orbit or infratemporal fossa; residual vascularity V Intracranial extension with residual vascularity (medial or lateral)"],["sec","cl:t-juv~s7-1","Principles","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Treatment","Complete surgical removal is the treatment of choice; the tumour does not regress with age. Embolisation reduces blood loss: early tumours are removed endoscopically, often without embolisation; advanced tumours with internal carotid supply have the external carotid branches embolised first. Hormonal treatment is no longer used."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s7-2","Approaches by extent","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Treatment","Extent and approach Extent Approach Nose and nasopharynx Endoscopic, or transpalatal Plus sinuses and pterygopalatine fossa Endoscopic, lateral rhinotomy with medial maxillectomy, midfacial degloving, or Le Fort I Plus infratemporal fossa Extended endoscopic, infratemporal fossa approach, or maxillary swing Plus cheek Maxillary swing (facial translocation) Plus intracranial extension Combined endoscopic or open skull base surgery with neurosurgery; radiotherapy if inaccessible Residual or recurrent Observation, revision surgery, or radiotherapy Figure 7 Angiofibroma exposed through a transpalatal approach. Dhingra, fig. 49.8 7.2.1 Endoscopic surgery Now used for most tumours, including many with infratemporal and limited intracranial extension, with less bleeding and no facial scars; recurrence rates are similar to open surgery once tumour stage is accounted for. 7.2.2 Open approaches Transpalatal (with Sardana's sublabial extension), lateral rhinotomy and midfacial degloving with medial maxillectomy, the Le Fort I approach, the maxillary swing (Wei's facial translocation), the infratemporal fossa approach, and combined intracranial-extracranial resection."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s7-3","Radiotherapy","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Treatment","For unresectable intracranial disease, residual tumour next to critical structures, and recurrence; stereotactic radiosurgery for small residuals. Long-term risks include growth disturbance, cataract and radiation-induced malignancy in young patients."],["ev","cl:t-juv~s7-4","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma: current evidence","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Treatment","Endoscopic or open. A systematic review of 1047 cases found that endoscopic resection had less blood loss (about 540 ml against 1580 ml) and fewer recurrences than open surgery, although the recurrence difference disappeared once tumour stage was accounted for (Boghani et al., Laryngoscope 2013). Staging. The UPMC system also predicts the need for staged surgery (Snyderman et al., Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2010). Embolisation 24 to 72 hours before surgery reduces blood loss in larger tumours; its value in small tumours removed endoscopically is debated. Hormones such as flutamide shrink some tumours but are not standard treatment."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s7-4","Recurrence","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · Treatment","Up to about a third, usually within two years, from residual tumour in the basisphenoid and vidian canal. Follow with contrast MRI for at least three years. Small residual tumour may be watched for growth; accessible recurrence is removed endoscopically, and disease in high-risk sites such as the cavernous sinus may be observed or treated with stereotactic radiosurgery."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s8-1","Viva essentials","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The patient: adolescent boy, epistaxis and obstruction. Site of origin and routes of spread through the pterygopalatine fossa. The antral (Holman-Miller) sign. Why no biopsy, and the role of embolisation and its timing. Surgical approaches by extent."],["sec","cl:t-juv~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Biopsying the mass in clinic. Treating recurrent epistaxis in a teenage boy without endoscopy. Leaving tumour in the vidian canal."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s1-1","Normal self-cleaning","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Overview","The skin of the drum and deep canal migrates outwards, carrying keratin and wax to the entrance of the canal. When this migration fails, keratin builds up in the deep canal."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s1-2","Two conditions","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Overview","Keratosis obturans or canal cholesteatoma? Keratosis obturans Canal cholesteatoma Keratin Plug filling the whole canal Focal, over an area of eroded bone Bone Widened all round, smooth Focal erosion and necrosis, usually posteroinferior Pain Acute, severe Dull, chronic, or none Hearing Conductive loss Usually normal Sides Often both Usually one Age Younger Older Associations Bronchiectasis, chronic sinusitis Previous surgery, trauma, radiation"],["sec","cl:t-ko~s2-1","Cause","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Keratosis obturans","Failure of outward migration, possibly from inflammation of the canal skin. In young people it is associated with bronchiectasis and chronic sinusitis, which should be looked for."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s2-2","Clinical picture","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Keratosis obturans","Acute, severe pain, conductive hearing loss and sometimes otorrhoea. A dense, white, laminated keratin plug fills the deep canal; once removed, the canal is widened and the drum may be thickened. Where the books differ · Age and pain Dhingra Typically 5 to 20 years; pain is among the presenting symptoms. Lee Typically 30 to 60 years; severe pain is characteristic. In practice Ages vary between texts, but keratosis obturans is the younger patient's disease, and severe pain is its hallmark."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s2-3","Treatment","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Keratosis obturans","Removal of the plug under the microscope, which may need a general anaesthetic in children, treatment of any secondary otitis externa, and regular cleaning. Salicylic acid 2% in alcohol reduces reaccumulation. Follow-up is lifelong."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s2-4","Complication","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Keratosis obturans","Progressive widening of the canal can erode into the mastoid (an auto-mastoidectomy ) and expose the facial nerve."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s3-1","Cause","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Canal cholesteatoma","Spontaneous in older patients, or after surgery, trauma, radiation or canal stenosis."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s3-2","Clinical picture","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Canal cholesteatoma","A dull, chronic ache and a foul, scanty discharge, with normal hearing. A localised ulcer on the floor or posterior wall of the bony canal, with keratin debris and exposed, necrotic bone."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s3-3","Investigations","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Canal cholesteatoma","CT shows the extent of the erosion and its relation to the mastoid and facial nerve. Biopsy granulation tissue to exclude carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s3-4","Treatment","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Canal cholesteatoma","Regular debridement and topical drops for limited disease; when it keeps returning or is extensive, surgery (canalplasty) removes the matrix and dead bone and covers the defect with fascia or a skin graft."],["ev","cl:t-ko~c4","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma: current evidence","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Differential diagnosis","Two conditions. Keratosis obturans and external canal cholesteatoma are now accepted as distinct diseases. Treatment. Regular microsuction and softening drops control keratosis obturans. Evidence comes from case series."],["sec","cl:t-ko~c4","Differential diagnosis","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · Differential diagnosis","What else causes a canal plug or erosion. Impacted wax ( Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis ). Necrotising otitis externa . Squamous cell carcinoma of the canal ( External ear tumours and bony growths ). Middle ear cholesteatoma eroding the canal wall."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s5-1","Viva essentials","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Failure of epithelial migration as the cause. The comparison table: circumferential versus focal. Association with bronchiectasis and sinusitis. Biopsy to exclude carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-ko~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Keratosis obturans and canal cholesteatoma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Syringing a keratin plug repeatedly instead of removing it under the microscope. Missing carcinoma in canal granulation."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s1-1","Causes","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Overview","Irritants: smoking (the main cause), alcohol, dust and fumes in mines and industry, polluted air. Laryngopharyngeal reflux. Voice misuse: prolonged loud or forced voice, habitual throat clearing. Infection nearby or below: chronic sinusitis with postnasal drip, dental and tonsillar sepsis, chronic bronchitis and bronchiectasis. Incomplete resolution of acute laryngitis. Allergy as part of the unified airway. Specific infections: tuberculosis, syphilis, leprosy, scleroma, fungi, and MRSA. Systemic disease: sarcoidosis, rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, relapsing polychondritis, amyloidosis, pemphigus and pemphigoid. Radiotherapy. Figure 1 Coronal section of the larynx: vestibular and vocal folds, ventricle and saccule. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s1-2","Approach","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Overview","Stroboscopic or high-quality endoscopic examination of the larynx; treat reflux, irritants and voice use; biopsy any lesion that persists, is asymmetric, ulcerated or exophytic, or occurs in a smoker, because tuberculosis, granulomas and carcinoma can look alike. Where the books differ · Chronic laryngitis as a diagnosis Dhingra Describes chronic laryngitis as an entity, in hyperaemic and hypertrophic forms, with treatment centred on voice rest, steam and removal of irritants. Lee Advises abandoning \"chronic laryngitis\" as a diagnosis, replacing it with \"prolonged laryngeal inflammation\" and a named cause, and gives reflux a central place. In practice Use \"chronic laryngitis\" as a description, name the cause (reflux, smoking, voice misuse), and biopsy anything that might be cancer or tuberculosis; both books insist on that. Figure 2 Flexible laryngoscopy in clinic. Dhingra, fig. 62.6"],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s2-1","Chronic hyperaemic laryngitis","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Chronic non-specific laryngitis","A diffuse, symmetrical inflammation of the whole larynx. Hoarseness is the commonest complaint: the voice tires and fails by evening. Constant hawking and clearing of the throat, dryness and tickling, discomfort and a dry cough. The folds look dull red and rounded with flecks of sticky mucus on them and between the arytenoids."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s2-2","Chronic hypertrophic (hyperplastic) laryngitis","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Chronic non-specific laryngitis","Mostly men of thirty to fifty. Diffuse thickening that begins at the glottis and spreads to the false folds, epiglottis and subglottis: the respiratory epithelium changes to squamous, the vocal fold epithelium becomes hyperplastic and keratinised, glands first enlarge then atrophy. The mucosa is dusky red and thick, the folds red, rounded and later bulky and nodular, the false folds swollen (they may be mistaken for prolapse of the ventricle), and fold movement may become sluggish. It can be localised, as vocal nodules, polyps, Reinke's oedema and contact ulcers ( Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness )."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s2-3","Treatment","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Chronic non-specific laryngitis","Treat infection in the sinuses, teeth, tonsils and chest. Stop smoking and alcohol; avoid dust and fumes. Voice rest for weeks, then voice therapy. Steam inhalations and expectorants. Treat reflux. Stripping of the hyperplastic mucosa under the microscope in selected cases, one fold at a time and preserving the vocal ligament, with histology to exclude dysplasia."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s3-1","Features","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Laryngopharyngeal reflux","Hoarseness, globus, chronic cough, throat clearing, excess mucus and dysphagia; only a quarter to a third have heartburn, because the larynx is injured by small amounts of acid and pepsin that the oesophagus tolerates. Symptoms are worse after late meals, alcohol and exertion; a bitter taste on waking suggests nocturnal reflux."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s3-2","Signs","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Laryngopharyngeal reflux","Oedema and redness of the posterior larynx and interarytenoid area, subglottic oedema (pseudosulcus), diffuse vocal fold oedema, thick endolaryngeal mucus, and granulomas or contact ulcers."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s3-3","Diagnosis","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Laryngopharyngeal reflux","Usually clinical, confirmed by response to treatment. Combined multichannel pH and impedance monitoring (detecting acid and non-acid reflux) is the most accurate test; oesophagoscopy excludes Barrett oesophagus."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s3-4","Treatment","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Laryngopharyngeal reflux","Diet and behaviour changes (no late meals, less fat, coffee, chocolate and alcohol, weight loss, head of the bed raised), a twice-daily proton pump inhibitor half an hour before breakfast and dinner for at least two to three months, an H2 blocker or alginate at night, and fundoplication for proven refractory reflux. Evidence for proton pump inhibitors is mixed: trials show a modest symptom benefit at best, so treat for a defined period, review, and stop if there is no response."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s4-1","Polypoid degeneration (Reinke's oedema)","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Special forms","Bilateral, symmetrical swelling of the whole membranous vocal folds from fluid in Reinke's space, in middle-aged smokers (women more often seek help because their voice drops), with voice misuse, reflux, chronic sinusitis and myxoedema. The voice is low-pitched and rough; the folds are pale, translucent fusiform swellings that flop with breathing. Stop smoking, treat reflux and hypothyroidism, voice therapy; microlaryngeal decortication by a lateral incision with aspiration of the gel and trimming of excess epithelium, traditionally one side at a time. Figure 3 Bilateral Reinke's oedema in a smoker. Dhingra, fig. 61.5"],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s4-2","Pachydermia laryngis","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Special forms","A hypertrophic laryngitis of the posterior larynx: heaped-up red or grey granulation tissue in the interarytenoid area and posterior folds, bilateral and symmetrical, sometimes with contact ulcers on the vocal processes. Associated with alcohol, smoking, forceful speech and reflux. Not premalignant, but biopsy is essential to exclude carcinoma and tuberculosis. Microlaryngoscopic removal, anti-reflux treatment and voice therapy."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s4-3","Atrophic laryngitis (laryngitis sicca)","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Special forms","Atrophy of the mucosa with foul crusts, usually in women with atrophic rhinitis and pharyngitis: hoarseness that improves after coughing up a crust, dry cough, and dyspnoea from obstructing crusts, which may reach the trachea. Humidification, sprays of glucose in glycerine, expectorants (ammonium chloride, iodides), and treatment of the nose."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s5-1","Tuberculosis","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Specific infections","Almost always secondary to active pulmonary tuberculosis, mostly in middle-aged men, through infected sputum (bronchogenic) or the bloodstream. It favours the posterior larynx : the interarytenoid fold, false folds, true folds and epiglottis in that order. Symptoms: weakness of the voice (earliest), hoarseness, then severe pain from ulceration radiating to the ears and painful swallowing. Signs: redness of one fold with impaired adduction (the first sign); a mamillated swelling of the interarytenoid area; mouse-nibbled ulceration of the fold edge; shallow ulcers and granulations on the arytenoids; turban epiglottis (pseudoedema); swollen false folds and aryepiglottic folds; pallor of the surrounding mucosa. Diagnosis: chest radiograph, sputum, and laryngeal biopsy, essential to exclude carcinoma. Treatment: antituberculous chemotherapy and voice rest; lesions heal, sometimes with stenosis."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s5-2","Lupus","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Specific infections","An indolent tuberculous infection accompanying lupus of the nose and pharynx, attacking the anterior larynx : the epiglottis first, which may be destroyed, then the aryepiglottic and false folds. Painless and often found incidentally; no lung disease; responds well to antituberculous drugs."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s5-3","Syphilis","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Specific infections","Now rare; the tertiary gumma forms a smooth swelling that ulcerates and heals with stenosis. Never involved in the primary stage. Diagnosis by biopsy and serology."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s5-4","Leprosy","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Specific infections","With nasal and skin leprosy: nodular infiltration of the epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds and arytenoids, ulceration, and deformity and stenosis on healing. Dapsone and multidrug therapy."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s5-5","Scleroma","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Specific infections","Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis ( Granulomatous disease of the nose ), endemic in parts of Africa, the Middle East, Asia and Latin America, usually with nasal disease: a smooth red subglottic swelling causing hoarseness, wheeze and dyspnoea, passing through catarrhal, granulomatous and sclerotic stages to subglottic stenosis. Long courses of tetracycline or ciprofloxacin with steroids; reconstruction for stenosis."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s5-6","Fungal","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Specific infections","Candidiasis in inhaled steroid users, after antibiotics and in immunosuppression: white plaques on red mucosa that can mimic leukoplakia; systemic antifungals (topical nystatin does not reach), and biopsy if they persist. Blastomycosis and histoplasmosis are rare and mimic carcinoma."],["ev","cl:t-laryngitis~c6","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections: current evidence","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Systemic diseases","Laryngopharyngeal reflux. A meta-analysis of 14 placebo-controlled trials found that proton pump inhibitors improved reflux symptoms modestly (a 15% higher response rate) but not the laryngoscopic findings (Guo et al., J Clin Gastroenterol 2016). The American hoarseness guideline advises against antireflux medication for hoarseness without signs of reflux laryngitis or gastro-oesophageal reflux (Stachler et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018). Diagnosis. Laryngoscopic signs of reflux are non-specific and poorly reproducible; 24-hour multichannel impedance-pH monitoring is the reference test when the diagnosis matters. Smoking and dysplasia. Leukoplakia needs biopsy, because dysplasia cannot be judged by eye; stopping smoking is the most effective treatment for chronic laryngitis and Reinke's oedema."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~c6","Systemic diseases","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · Systemic diseases","The larynx as a window. Sarcoidosis: pale, turban-like submucosal swelling of the supraglottis (epiglottis and aryepiglottic folds); non-caseating granulomas on biopsy; steroids systemically or by injection. Rheumatoid arthritis: cricoarytenoid arthritis (pain, hoarseness, referred otalgia, and when bilateral, stridor from fixed folds) and \"bamboo nodules\" in the folds; steroids and disease-modifying drugs; tracheostomy for obstruction. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis: subglottic stenosis, often the first sign; immunosuppression, steroid injection and dilatation. Relapsing polychondritis: inflammation of the cartilages of the ear, nose, larynx and trachea, with collapse of the airway. Amyloidosis: waxy yellow submucosal deposits, usually in the false folds and ventricles, with apple-green birefringence on Congo red staining; endoscopic excision and screening for systemic disease. Pemphigus and pemphigoid: blisters and scarring of the supraglottis."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s7-1","Viva essentials","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes of chronic laryngitis, and why biopsy matters. Laryngopharyngeal reflux: symptoms, signs and treatment. Reinke's oedema and pachydermia laryngis. Tuberculosis of the larynx: sites, signs (mouse-nibbled, turban epiglottis, mamillated interarytenoid) and diagnosis; lupus compared. Scleroma and cricoarytenoid arthritis."],["sec","cl:t-laryngitis~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating persistent hoarseness in a smoker with antibiotics instead of laryngoscopy. Forgetting the chest radiograph in a patient with posterior laryngeal ulceration. Giving a short, once-daily course of proton pump inhibitor for laryngopharyngeal reflux and calling it a failure."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s1-1","How a laser works","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Lasers","Pumping: an energy source (electric current or a flash lamp) excites the atoms of an active medium . The medium can be a gas (CO2, argon, helium–neon), a solid crystal (Nd:YAG, KTP, erbium, holmium), a liquid dye, or a semiconductor (diode). Spontaneous and stimulated emission: excited electrons fall back and release photons. A photon striking another excited atom makes it release an identical photon, of the same wavelength, direction and phase. This is stimulated emission . Population inversion: when more atoms are excited than not, amplification begins. Resonance: two mirrors at the ends of a resonant cavity (one fully reflecting, one partly) bounce the light back and forth to amplify it. The beam leaves through the partly reflecting mirror. The result is light that is monochromatic , coherent (in phase), collimated (parallel) and of high intensity, and that can be focused to a tiny spot."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s1-2","Laser and tissue","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Lasers","A laser beam meeting tissue is reflected , transmitted , scattered or absorbed , and only the absorbed energy has any effect. Absorption depends on the chromophore , the target molecule that takes up a particular wavelength. Water absorbs infrared, which is why the CO2 and Er:YAG lasers cut so cleanly. Haemoglobin absorbs green and yellow light, with peaks at 418, 542 and 577 nm, the target of the KTP laser at 532 nm and the pulsed dye laser at 585 to 595 nm. Melanin absorbs across the spectrum, mainly in the visible and near-infrared, the target of ruby, alexandrite and Nd:YAG lasers. The absorbed energy acts as heat. At about 60°C proteins denature and coagulate reversibly, around 80°C collagen breaks down, and at 100°C water boils and the tissue vaporises . A laser wound therefore has three zones: A crater of vaporisation. A zone of thermal necrosis, which seals small vessels, nerves and lymphatics. A zone of thermal conductivity and repair, which is reversible. Depth of penetration follows from absorption. Longer visible wavelengths penetrate further, but water-absorbed infrared light from CO2 and Er:YAG lasers penetrates only microns, giving precise cuts with little collateral damage. The Nd:YAG laser, by contrast, coagulates about 4 mm deep, which is good for haemostasis but imprecise. Beyond heat, lasers act photochemically in photodynamic therapy, photoacoustically in stone fragmentation, and by photoablation in the excimer laser. Figure 1 First, the fates of a laser beam in tissue: (a) reflection, (b) transmission, (c) scatter, (d) absorption with tissue destructio"],["sec","cl:t-laser~s1-3","Parameters the surgeon controls","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Lasers","The surgeon sets the power in watts and the spot size , which is smallest at the focal point: a focused beam cuts, while a defocused beam coagulates and ablates. Exposure can be continuous , pulsed , or superpulsed and Q-switched , in which very short pulses are separated by intervals longer than the tissue's thermal relaxation time , so that heat dissipates between pulses and collateral damage falls. Power density (W/cm²), the power divided by the spot area, sets the rate of cutting, and fluence (J/cm²), the power density multiplied by time, is the total energy delivered."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s1-4","Delivery","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Lasers","Visible and near-infrared lasers, the argon, KTP, Nd:YAG, diode and holmium lasers, travel down flexible optical fibres and can be used through endoscopes and bronchoscopes. The CO2 laser cannot pass through ordinary fibres; it travels by an articulated arm with mirrors to a micromanipulator on the operating microscope, or through hollow flexible waveguides. Because CO2 and Nd:YAG beams are invisible, a red helium–neon aiming beam shows where they will strike. Figure 2 The electromagnetic spectrum, with the wavelengths of the common surgical lasers. Dhingra, fig. 71.3 Common lasers in ENT Laser Wavelength Absorbed by Delivery Typical uses CO2 10,600 nm (far infrared, invisible) Water Articulated arm, micromanipulator, waveguide Workhorse of laryngology: nodules, polyps, cysts, papillomas, Reinke oedema, leukoplakia, T1 glottic cancer (transoral laser microsurgery), cordotomy and arytenoidectomy, stapedotomy, skin resurfacing KTP 532 nm (green) Haemoglobin, melanin Fibre Vascular lesions, papillomas (office angiolytic laser), stapedotomy, sinus surgery, HHT Argon 488 to 514 nm (blue–green) Haemoglobin, melanin Fibre Vascular skin lesions, stapes surgery (historical) Nd:YAG 1064 nm (near infrared, invisible) Pigment; deep penetration Fibre Deep coagulation: palliation of tracheobronchial and oesophageal tumours, vascular malformations, HHT Diode 800 to 980 nm Haemoglobin, melanin Fibre Turbinate reduction, stapedotomy, dacryocystorhinostomy, vascular lesions Er:YAG 2940 nm Water (peak) Fibre or arm Fine skin resurfacing, bone (stapes) Ho:YAG 2100 nm Water Fibre Bone, sinus, s"],["sec","cl:t-laser~s1-5","Advantages and disadvantages","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Lasers","Lasers offer precision, haemostasis and a bloodless field, cause little postoperative oedema and pain, and reach targets through endoscopes. Against this are their cost, the training they demand, thermal injury, the lack of tactile feedback, and the hazards of fire and eye injury ."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s1-6","Laser safety","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Lasers","Safety starts with organisation: a laser safety officer, trained and certified staff, and a \" laser in use \" sign on a closed door. Everyone in the room, including the patient, wears wavelength-specific goggles : clear glass or plastic for CO2, orange or amber for argon and KTP, and green-blue for Nd:YAG. The reason is anatomical. Visible and near-infrared lasers (400 to 1400 nm) pass through the transparent media and burn the retina , whereas CO2 and other far-infrared lasers are absorbed by water at the front of the eye and burn the cornea . The patient's eyes are covered with wet pads and exposed skin with wet towels. The smoke plume can carry viable viral particles, such as HPV from papillomas, and is mutagenic, so a separate smoke evacuator is used. The airway fire needs the three elements of the fire triangle : heat from the laser, fuel from the tube, drapes and cottonoids, and an oxidiser, oxygen or nitrous oxide. It is prevented by removing each element (the anaesthetic side is in Anaesthesia and the shared airway ). Use laser-safe tubes, metal or specially wrapped, or jet ventilation without a tube; fill the cuff with saline coloured with methylene blue so that a puncture shows at once; protect the cuff and tissues with wet cottonoids; keep oxygen at 30% or less, and never above 40%; use no nitrous oxide ; use the lowest effective power; and direct the beam only at the target. Airway fire drill Stop the laser. Stop ventilation and disconnect oxygen. Remove the tube. Pour saline into the airway. Ventilate by mask with air. Reintubate. Do bronchoscopy to assess the i"],["sec","cl:t-laser~s1-7","Photodynamic therapy","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Lasers","In photodynamic therapy a photosensitiser, given systemically (porphyrins, temoporfin) or topically to the skin (aminolaevulinic acid), concentrates in tumour cells. Light of a matching wavelength, around 630 to 652 nm, then generates oxygen free radicals that kill them. It is used for superficial cancers of the skin, oral cavity and larynx, for field cancerisation, and to palliate recurrences, and patients must avoid sunlight for weeks because the skin is photosensitive."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s2-1","Radiofrequency ablation","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Radiofrequency and coblation","Radiofrequency ablation passes a high-frequency alternating current, about 350 kHz to 4 MHz and commonly 460 kHz, through a probe placed within the tissue. Ionic agitation heats the tissue to about 70 to 85°C, coagulating it without charring, and fibrosis over the following three to six weeks shrinks it. It reduces the volume of the inferior turbinates, the soft palate and the tongue base in snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea, and is used for tonsil reduction, lingual thyroid, thyroid nodules and microcystic lymphatic malformations. At the tongue base the hypoglossal neurovascular bundle lies about 2.7 cm deep and 1.6 cm lateral to the foramen caecum, and must be respected. The procedure is done as an outpatient, causes little pain and can be repeated."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s2-2","Coblation (controlled ablation)","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Radiofrequency and coblation","Coblation passes radiofrequency energy through conductive saline to create a thin plasma field of energised sodium ions. These break molecular bonds and dissolve tissue at only 40 to 70°C , against 400 to 600°C for electrocautery, so there is little charring or collateral injury. A single wand ablates, suctions, irrigates and coagulates. Coblation is used for tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy, including intracapsular tonsillotomy in children; tongue base and palatal surgery; turbinates and nasal polyps; laryngeal papillomas and cysts; cordotomy; subglottic and tracheal lesions; and juvenile angiofibroma. After coblation tonsillectomy, pain and haemorrhage are similar to or slightly better than other hot techniques, and intracapsular coblation causes less pain and bleeding. Figure 3 First, the principle of coblation. Second, (A) a coblation generator and (B) the different wands. Dhingra, figs 71.5 and 71.6 Coblation compared with electrosurgery Feature Coblation Electrosurgery (diathermy) Temperature 40 to 70°C 400 to 600°C Mechanism Chemical: plasma breaks molecular bonds Thermal: heating, charring Collateral damage Superficial, minimal Deeper Field Saline irrigated, suction built in Dry"],["sec","cl:t-laser~s3-1","Principle","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Cryosurgery","Cryosurgery uses liquid nitrogen, by spray or probe at down to −196°C, or nitrous oxide and CO2 probes, which rely on the Joule–Thomson effect : compressed gas expanding through a small orifice cools the tip to about −70°C or below. The tissue is frozen rapidly to −20 to −30°C or colder, thawed slowly , and the cycle repeated, with a margin of normal tissue and, if needed, a thermocouple to monitor temperature. Cells die by several mechanisms: ice crystals and dehydration leave toxic concentrations of electrolytes; membrane lipoproteins are denatured ; thermal shock damages the cells; and vascular stasis with microthrombosis causes ischaemic necrosis, which is why vascular lesions respond so well. There may also be an immune \"cryoimmunisation\" effect. The slough separates in three to six weeks and heals by secondary intention with little scarring. Figure 4 Cryotherapy for a haemangioma of the cheek. Dhingra, fig. 72.1"],["sec","cl:t-laser~s3-2","Uses, advantages and limits","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Cryosurgery","Cryotherapy treats haemangiomas and vascular malformations, premalignant lesions such as leukoplakia and actinic keratosis, and superficial skin cancers such as Bowen's disease and small basal cell carcinomas, with cure rates above 90%, including lesions over cartilage, which resists freezing. It also palliates advanced and recurrent tumours, reduces turbinates, and was formerly used for the tonsils of high-risk patients. It can be done as an outpatient, often without anaesthesia, is safe in bleeding disorders and poor-risk patients, causes little pain, leaves minimal scarring and no keloid, and is cheap and repeatable. Its limits are that it provides no histology or margin control, its depth is imprecise, it leaves hypopigmentation and hair loss in skin, it causes swelling and slough, and it is unsuitable for recurrent or ill-defined cancers."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s4-1","Principle","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Hyperbaric oxygen","Breathing 100% oxygen at more than one atmosphere absolute, usually 2.0 to 2.5 ATA for 90 to 120 minutes over a series of 10 to 40 sessions, increases the oxygen dissolved in plasma several-fold. This raises tissue oxygen tension, stimulates angiogenesis and fibroblast activity, improves leucocyte killing, reduces oedema and shrinks gas bubbles. Figure 5 A multiplace hyperbaric chamber. Dhingra, fig. 71.4"],["sec","cl:t-laser~s4-2","ENT uses","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Hyperbaric oxygen","For osteoradionecrosis and soft-tissue radiation injury , hyperbaric oxygen is an adjunct to debridement and reconstruction, but the evidence is mixed: Marx's older protocol supported giving it before dental extraction in irradiated jaws, whereas the randomised HOPON trial found no reduction in osteoradionecrosis, and pentoxifylline–tocopherol is an alternative. It is also used for refractory necrotising otitis externa ( Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis ) and skull base osteomyelitis, for compromised flaps and grafts, and for sudden sensorineural hearing loss as salvage or with steroids within two weeks of onset in moderate to profound loss. Outside ENT it treats decompression sickness, including inner ear decompression sickness in divers, air embolism, carbon monoxide poisoning and necrotising fasciitis."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s4-3","Complications and contraindications","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Hyperbaric oxygen","Middle ear barotrauma is the commonest complication and may need grommets; sinus barotrauma, oxygen toxicity seizures, temporary myopia and claustrophobia also occur. An untreated pneumothorax is an absolute contraindication. Eustachian tube dysfunction, upper respiratory infection, emphysema with bullae, uncontrolled epilepsy and certain chemotherapy drugs such as bleomycin and doxorubicin are relative contraindications."],["ev","cl:t-laser~s5-1","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen: current evidence","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Narrow band imaging","Airway fire. The American Society of Anesthesiologists practice advisory recommends minimising inspired oxygen (ideally under 30%), avoiding nitrous oxide, using laser-safe tubes with saline-filled cuffs, and a rehearsed response: stop the laser, remove the tube, stop gases, pour saline, then re-establish the airway and inspect it (Apfelbaum et al., Anesthesiology 2013). Narrow band imaging. Meta-analyses show that NBI raises sensitivity for laryngeal and pharyngeal cancer and dysplasia compared with white light endoscopy, and helps define margins in transoral laser surgery. Hyperbaric oxygen. In the HOPON trial, hyperbaric oxygen before and after dental extraction in irradiated jaws did not reduce osteoradionecrosis (Shaw et al., Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys 2019); its role in sudden hearing loss is covered in Sudden sensorineural hearing loss ."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s5-1","Principle and value","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · Narrow band imaging","Narrow band imaging filters the endoscope's light into two bands, 415 nm (blue) and 540 nm (green) , which are absorbed by haemoglobin and penetrate only the superficial layers, so superficial capillaries appear brown and submucosal veins cyan. Abnormal intraepithelial papillary capillary loops , dilated and tortuous with brown dots, mark dysplasia and early cancer. This helps to detect small cancers under 1 cm and second primaries, to target biopsies, to define resection margins and to survey for recurrence after radiotherapy. Its accuracy is reduced by secretions, bleeding, keratosis and post-radiation change. Figure 6 Carcinoma of the epiglottis (A) under white light and (B) under narrow band imaging. Dhingra, fig. 71.7"],["sec","cl:t-laser~s6-1","Viva essentials","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for What LASER stands for, the properties of laser light, and its components. Chromophores; CO2 versus KTP versus Nd:YAG: wavelength, absorption, delivery, uses. The three zones of a laser wound; power density and fluence. Laser safety: eye protection by wavelength, laser-safe tubes and the blue-dyed saline cuff, low oxygen and no nitrous oxide, the airway fire drill. Coblation versus diathermy; radiofrequency for sleep surgery. Mechanisms of cryotherapy; freeze fast, thaw slowly. Hyperbaric oxygen: how it works, ENT indications, complications."],["sec","cl:t-laser~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using nitrous oxide or high oxygen during laser airway surgery. Giving the theatre team the wrong goggles for the wavelength. Treating an undiagnosed lesion with cryotherapy or laser vaporisation without taking a biopsy first. Forgetting ear barotrauma in patients starting hyperbaric oxygen."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s1-1","Position","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Framework","The larynx lies in front of the hypopharynx, opposite the third to sixth cervical vertebrae in the adult, ending at the lower border of C6. It rises and moves forwards in swallowing and can be moved from side to side over the spine with a grating feel, the laryngeal crepitus , which is lost when a postcricoid tumour fills the gap. Figure 1 The site of the larynx from C3 to C6, and its anterior and posterior aspects. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s1-2","Cartilages","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Framework","Three unpaired and three paired. Thyroid: the largest; two laminae meeting in front at about 90° in men (the Adam's apple) and 120° in women, with superior and inferior horns. The vocal folds attach to the inner surface of the angle at about its midpoint, so an airway can be made below them through the cricothyroid membrane. Cricoid: the only complete ring, a narrow arch in front and a tall lamina behind; it holds the airway open, and its loss or injury causes stenosis. Epiglottis: a leaf of elastic cartilage attached by its stalk (petiole) to the thyroid angle above the vocal folds, and by the hyoepiglottic ligament to the hyoid, which divides it into suprahyoid and infrahyoid parts. Its posterior bulge, the tubercle , hides the anterior commissure on mirror examination; pits and perforations in it let supraglottic cancer reach the pre-epiglottic space. It is not essential for swallowing and can be removed with little aspiration. Arytenoids: pyramids sitting on the cricoid lamina, each with a vocal process forwards (for the vocal ligament) and a muscular process laterally (for the cricoarytenoid muscles). Corniculate (Santorini) on the arytenoid apex, and cuneiform (Wrisberg) in the aryepiglottic folds. Thyroid, cricoid and most of the arytenoid are hyaline and ossify from about 25 years; ossified cartilage can be mistaken for a foreign body on radiographs. The epiglottis, corniculate, cuneiform and arytenoid tips are elastic and do not ossify. Figure 2 The laryngeal framework: thyroid, cricoid, epiglottis, hyoid and the thyrohyoid, cricothyroid and cricotracheal membranes"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s1-3","Joints","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Framework","Cricoarytenoid: synovial; the arytenoid rotates about a vertical axis (abducting and adducting the vocal fold) and glides towards or away from its fellow (closing or opening the posterior glottis). Rheumatoid arthritis and intubation injury can fix it. Cricothyroid: synovial; the cricoid tilts on a transverse axis, lengthening and tensing the folds. Figure 4 The cricoarytenoid joint. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s1-4","Membranes and ligaments","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Framework","Extrinsic: the thyrohyoid membrane (pierced by the superior laryngeal vessels and internal laryngeal nerve), the cricotracheal membrane and the hyoepiglottic ligament. Intrinsic: the cricovocal membrane (conus elasticus) , whose free upper edge is the vocal ligament and whose two halves funnel up from the cricoid, a place where subglottic foreign bodies lodge; the quadrangular membrane between epiglottis and arytenoid, whose lower edge is the vestibular ligament; the cricothyroid ligament (the thickened front of the conus); and the thyroepiglottic ligament. Figure 5 First, a sagittal section showing the cricovocal and quadrangular membranes and the pre-epiglottic space. Second, a coronal section: the quadrangular membrane ends in the false cord, the cricovocal membrane in the vocal ligament, forming the conus elasticus. Dhingra, figs 56.2 and 56.3 Figure 6 The membranes of the larynx, and the conus elasticus seen after removing a thyroid lamina. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s2-1","Intrinsic muscles","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Muscles","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles Action Muscles Abduct the vocal folds Posterior cricoarytenoid (the only abductor) Adduct the vocal folds Lateral cricoarytenoid, interarytenoid (transverse arytenoid), external thyroarytenoid Tense the vocal folds Cricothyroid (lengthens), vocalis (internal thyroarytenoid; fine tension) Relax the vocal folds Thyroarytenoid Open the inlet Thyroepiglottic Close the inlet Oblique interarytenoid and aryepiglottic Figure 7 First, the laryngeal muscles and their actions on the arytenoid. Second, the intrinsic muscles seen from the side. Dhingra, figs 56.4 and 56.5 Figure 8 The posterior cricoarytenoid abducts; the lateral cricoarytenoid and interarytenoid adduct; the cricothyroid stretches and the thyroarytenoid relaxes the folds. From the anatomy atlas The one abductor The posterior cricoarytenoid alone opens the glottis. Lose it on both sides and the folds lie near the midline with a good voice and a poor airway."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s2-2","Extrinsic muscles","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Muscles","Primary elevators (attached to the thyroid cartilage): stylopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus and thyrohyoid. Secondary elevators (through the hyoid): mylohyoid, digastric, stylohyoid and geniohyoid. Depressors: sternohyoid, sternothyroid and omohyoid."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s3-1","The cavity","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Cavity and spaces","From the inlet (bounded by the epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds and interarytenoid fold) to the lower border of the cricoid. Two pairs of folds divide it: Vestibule: from the inlet down to the vestibular (false) folds. Ventricle: the recess between false and true folds; from its front a pouch, the saccule , rises between the false fold and the thyroid lamina, lubricating the folds with mucus. Its pathological enlargement is a laryngocele . Subglottis (infraglottic space): from the vocal folds to the lower border of the cricoid. Figure 9 The laryngeal inlet and intrinsic muscles seen from behind. Dhingra, fig. 56.6 Figure 10 The laryngeal inlet, the cavity with its vestibule, ventricle and infraglottic part, and a coronal section with the saccule. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s3-2","The vocal folds and glottis","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Cavity and spaces","The true folds run from the thyroid angle to the vocal processes: pearly white because they are covered by closely bound squamous epithelium without glands. The glottis (rima glottidis) is the gap between them, about 24 mm long in men and 16 mm in women, the narrowest part of the adult airway. Its anterior two-thirds, between the membranous folds, is the phonatory glottis ; its posterior third, between the arytenoids, the respiratory glottis . Figure 11 The rima glottidis: the anterior two-thirds is the phonatory glottis, the posterior third (between the arytenoids) the respiratory glottis. Dhingra, fig. 56.7"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s3-3","Layers of the vocal fold","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Cavity and spaces","Squamous epithelium. Lamina propria: a superficial layer ( Reinke's space , loose and rich in hyaluronic acid), an intermediate layer (elastic) and a deep layer (collagen); the intermediate and deep layers form the vocal ligament . The vocalis muscle. The epithelium and superficial lamina propria form the cover , which ripples over the stiffer body as the mucosal wave; scarring of the cover ruins the voice, and fluid in Reinke's space gives Reinke's oedema. Figure 12 The layers of the vocal fold: Reinke's space and the vocal ligament. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s3-4","Regions","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Cavity and spaces","Supraglottis: epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, arytenoids, false folds and ventricles. Glottis: the vocal folds with the anterior and posterior commissures, extending about 1 cm below the free edge. Subglottis: from there to the lower border of the cricoid. Figure 13 Supraglottis, glottis and subglottis. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s3-5","Spaces","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Cavity and spaces","Pre-epiglottic space (of Boyer): fat in front of the infrahyoid epiglottis, behind the thyrohyoid membrane and thyroid cartilage, below the hyoepiglottic ligament; invaded by supraglottic and tongue base cancers. Paraglottic space: between the thyroid lamina laterally and the ventricle, quadrangular membrane and conus elasticus medially, the piriform sinus behind; continuous with the pre-epiglottic space. Cancers spreading in it are transglottic and can leave the larynx through the cricothyroid membrane. Reinke's space: under the epithelium of the membranous fold, between the superior and inferior arcuate lines. Figure 14 The paraglottic and pre-epiglottic spaces communicate with each other. Dhingra, fig. 56.8 Figure 15 The paraglottic space in coronal section. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s3-6","Mucosa","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Cavity and spaces","Ciliated columnar epithelium except over the vocal folds and upper vestibule (squamous). It is loosely attached, and swells readily, everywhere except over the posterior epiglottis, vocal folds and the corniculate and cuneiform cartilages. Glands are plentiful on the posterior epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds and saccule, and absent from the vocal folds."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s4-1","Superior laryngeal nerve","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Nerves, vessels and lymph","From the inferior (nodose) ganglion of the vagus, it descends behind the internal carotid artery and divides at the level of the greater horn of the hyoid: Internal branch: sensory to the mucosa above the vocal folds, piercing the thyrohyoid membrane with the superior laryngeal artery; it runs under the mucosa of the piriform fossa. External branch: motor to the cricothyroid , running with the superior thyroid artery, where it is at risk in thyroidectomy (loss of high pitch and vocal stamina)."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s4-2","Recurrent laryngeal nerve","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Nerves, vessels and lymph","Motor to every intrinsic muscle except the cricothyroid (the interarytenoid receives both sides), and sensory below the vocal folds. The left nerve leaves the vagus in the chest, hooks under the arch of the aorta beside the ligamentum arteriosum and climbs in the tracheo-oesophageal groove; the right hooks around the subclavian artery. Both enter the larynx behind the cricothyroid joint. The left, with its long course through the chest, is paralysed far more often ( Vocal fold paralysis ). A non-recurrent right nerve accompanies an aberrant retro-oesophageal right subclavian artery. Figure 16 The nerve supply of the larynx, the nerves and their relations, and the cervical trachea with the recurrent laryngeal nerve. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s4-3","Embryology","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Nerves, vessels and lymph","The larynx develops from the laryngotracheal groove of the foregut; the cartilages and muscles come from the fourth and sixth arch mesenchyme. The superior laryngeal nerve is the nerve of the fourth arch (hence the cricothyroid), and the recurrent laryngeal nerve that of the sixth (all other intrinsic muscles); the sixth arch artery on the left becomes the ductus arteriosus, which is why the left nerve hooks around it. The lumen is obliterated in the sixth week and recanalises by the tenth: failure gives webs and atresia ( Congenital lesions of the larynx )."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s4-4","Vessels","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Nerves, vessels and lymph","The superior laryngeal artery (from the superior thyroid) with the internal laryngeal nerve, and the inferior laryngeal artery (from the inferior thyroid) with the recurrent nerve; matching veins drain to the internal jugular and brachiocephalic veins."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s4-5","Lymphatic drainage","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Nerves, vessels and lymph","Supraglottis: rich lymphatics through the thyrohyoid membrane to the upper and middle deep cervical nodes, often both sides. Glottis: almost none; hence early glottic cancer rarely metastasises ( Cancer of the larynx ). Subglottis: through the cricothyroid membrane to the prelaryngeal (Delphian), pretracheal and paratracheal nodes, and to the lower deep cervical and mediastinal nodes."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~c5","The child's larynx","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · The child's larynx","Not a small adult larynx. High: opposite C3 or C4 at rest (some texts give C2 to C3, depending on the landmark used), rising to C1 or C2 in swallowing; the epiglottis meets the soft palate, so an infant can breathe through the nose while milk passes either side of the epiglottis. The larynx descends to C5 to C6 by adulthood (C6 to C7, by about fifteen, in other texts). Soft: the cartilages collapse easily; the epiglottis is omega-shaped and the arytenoids are relatively large. Overlapping: the hyoid overlaps the thyroid and the thyroid overlaps the cricoid, so the cricothyroid and thyrohyoid membranes are narrow and hard to find; cricothyroidotomy is avoided in young children ( Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation ). Funnel-shaped: the subglottis (cricoid ring) is the narrowest part, about 4 to 5 mm in the newborn; one millimetre of oedema there halves the airway. A diameter under 4 mm in a term baby is subglottic stenosis. Loose submucosa swells quickly with infection and trauma. The larynx grows rapidly in the first three years, which allows some congenital narrowings to be watched, and again at puberty, when the male vocal folds lengthen and the voice breaks. The folds measure about 6 to 8 mm in childhood and 15 to 23 mm in adults."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s6-1","Protection of the lower airway","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Physiology","The oldest function. During a swallow three sphincters close in turn: the inlet (aryepiglottic folds, epiglottic tubercle and arytenoids), the false folds , and the true folds , the last barrier; breathing stops reflexly; the larynx rises and moves forward under the base of tongue as the epiglottis tilts back. Anything that gets through provokes a cough : the folds close, pressure builds below them, and a sudden release blows the material out. The larynx is the watchdog of the lungs."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s6-2","Phonation","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Physiology","The myoelastic–aerodynamic theory : the adducted folds are blown apart by subglottic pressure from the lungs and fall back by their elasticity and the Bernoulli effect, releasing puffs of air that vibrate the cover as a mucosal wave. Loudness depends on subglottic pressure; pitch on the frequency of vibration, set by length, tension and mass of the folds. The pharynx, mouth, nose and chest resonate the sound, and the lips, tongue, palate and teeth articulate it into speech."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s6-3","Respiration","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Physiology","The folds abduct on inspiration and partly adduct on expiration, regulating airflow and providing back pressure to the lungs."],["ev","cl:t-lxanat~s6-4","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx: current evidence","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Physiology","Nerves at thyroid surgery. A non-recurrent laryngeal nerve occurs in about 0.5 to 1% on the right, with an aberrant right subclavian artery, and is very rare on the left. The International Neural Monitoring Study Group guideline standardised intraoperative nerve monitoring, including checking vagal and recurrent nerve signals before and after dissection, and recommends considering staged surgery if signal is lost on the first side (Randolph et al., Laryngoscope 2011). The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve is at risk at the upper pole; injury lowers pitch and voice projection."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s6-4","Fixation of the chest","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · Physiology","A closed glottis fixes the chest so that the arm, trunk and abdominal muscles can work against it in lifting, climbing, coughing, vomiting, defecation, micturition and childbirth. Figure 17 The muscles that close the laryngeal inlet, and the rima vestibuli and rima glottidis. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s7-1","Viva essentials","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Cartilages, which are paired, and which ossify. Intrinsic muscles and their actions; the only abductor. Conus elasticus, quadrangular membrane, pre-epiglottic and paraglottic spaces. Layers of the vocal fold and Reinke's space. Nerve supply and the course of the recurrent laryngeal nerves. Lymphatic drainage by region and its importance in cancer. The infant larynx. Functions of the larynx."],["sec","cl:t-lxanat~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx · For the examination","Common pitfalls Forgetting that the cricothyroid is supplied by the external laryngeal nerve. Calling the glottis the narrowest part of the infant airway; it is the subglottis. Mistaking ossified laryngeal cartilage for a foreign body on a radiograph."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s1-1","Epidemiology","Cancer of the larynx · Overview","The second commonest cancer of the upper aerodigestive tract and about 1 to 5% of all malignancies; men are affected four to eight times as often as women, mostly in the sixth and seventh decades. In the UK about 2,400 people a year develop it, many from deprived communities; tobacco and alcohol account for about three-quarters of head and neck cancer. Glottic cancer is the commonest in most series (about 60%), supraglottic about 35 to 40%, and subglottic only 1 to 2%; supraglottic cancer predominates in some populations."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s1-2","Causes","Cancer of the larynx · Overview","Tobacco: the dominant cause; current smokers carry a ten- to twentyfold risk, which falls substantially after ten years of stopping. Alcohol: especially for supraglottic cancer; with tobacco the effects multiply (about fifteenfold). Others: previous radiation to the neck, papillomatosis (HPV 6 and 11, rarely malignant), occupational exposures (asbestos, nickel, mustard gas, wood dust, paints, diesel fumes), reflux, and genetic susceptibility."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s1-3","Premalignant lesions","Cancer of the larynx · Overview","Hyperplasia, keratosis, mild, moderate and severe dysplasia and carcinoma in situ form a spectrum (the white patch is described in Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness ). Mild to moderate dysplasia progresses in about 7 to 11%, severe dysplasia in 18 to 30%; severe dysplasia and carcinoma in situ behave alike. Excisional biopsy (microflap or laser) is the diagnosis and usually the treatment; radiotherapy for multifocal disease or the frail; lifelong follow-up."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s2-1","Histology","Cancer of the larynx · Pathology and regions","Squamous cell carcinoma in 85 to 95%; glottic tumours tend to be well differentiated, supraglottic ones less so. Others: verrucous carcinoma (1 to 2%; exophytic, well-differentiated, with pushing margins; does not metastasise; treated by excision rather than radiation), spindle cell and basaloid carcinoma, neuroendocrine tumours, salivary carcinomas, chondrosarcoma (usually the cricoid) and lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s2-2","Regions","Cancer of the larynx · Pathology and regions","Figure 1 The regions of the larynx (AJCC): the glottis extends from the ventricle to 1 cm below the free edge of the fold; the subglottis from there to the lower border of the cricoid. Dhingra, fig. 62.1 Supraglottis: suprahyoid and infrahyoid epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, arytenoids, false folds and ventricles. Embryologically from the third and fourth arches without midline fusion, with rich, bilateral lymphatics. Glottis: the true folds with the anterior and posterior commissures, extending 1 cm below the free edge. From the sixth arch, with scanty lymphatics. Subglottis: to the lower border of the cricoid. Figure 2 Supraglottis, glottis and subglottis, and the paraglottic space. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s2-3","Barriers and pathways","Cancer of the larynx · Pathology and regions","The conus elasticus and the quadrangular membrane resist spread; the pre-epiglottic space (reached through pits in the epiglottis) and the paraglottic space (lateral to the membranes) carry tumour silently across the ventricle ( transglottic cancer) and up to the thyroid cartilage. At the anterior commissure, Broyles' ligament inserts into thyroid cartilage without perichondrium, a route into the cartilage."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s3-1","Sites","Cancer of the larynx · Supraglottic cancer","Epiglottis, false folds and aryepiglottic folds, in that order. Suprahyoid epiglottic tumours tend to be exophytic, infrahyoid ones ulcerative. Figure 3 First, a supraglottic cancer of the epiglottis and right aryepiglottic fold. Second, a supraglottic cancer also invading the right piriform fossa (arrow). Dhingra, figs 62.2 and 62.3"],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s3-2","Spread","Cancer of the larynx · Supraglottic cancer","To the vallecula, base of tongue, piriform fossa and pre-epiglottic space, and through the thyroid cartilage. Nodes (levels II to IV) appear early: 25 to 50% at presentation, often bilateral, with occult nodes in 20 to 40% of N0 necks."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s3-3","Symptoms","Cancer of the larynx · Supraglottic cancer","Often silent for a long time: discomfort or pain in the throat, a lump feeling, painful swallowing, referred otalgia, a neck mass; hoarseness, dyspnoea, halitosis and weight loss come late."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s4-1","Site","Cancer of the larynx · Glottic cancer","The free edge and upper surface of the anterior and middle thirds of the fold. Figure 4 First, cancer of the larynx: (A) supraglottic, (B) glottic, (C) subglottic. Second, a cancer involving the supraglottic, glottic and subglottic areas on the left, on rigid endoscopy. Dhingra, figs 62.4 and 62.5"],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s4-2","Spread","Cancer of the larynx · Glottic cancer","Forwards to the anterior commissure and opposite fold; backwards to the vocal process and arytenoid; upwards to the ventricle and false fold; downwards to the subglottis; deeply into the thyroarytenoid muscle and paraglottic space. Impaired mobility means deep invasion of muscle, and fixation means invasion of the paraglottic space, cricoarytenoid joint or nerve: a bad prognostic sign. Because the folds have almost no lymphatics, node metastases are rare (under 5% in T1 and T2) until the tumour leaves the membranous fold."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s4-3","Symptoms","Cancer of the larynx · Glottic cancer","Hoarseness early, because even a tiny lesion alters vibration; stridor when the tumour enlarges or fixes the fold."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~c5","Subglottic cancer","Cancer of the larynx · Subglottic cancer","Rare and late. It starts on one side, spreads around the front and down into the trachea, through the cricothyroid membrane into the thyroid gland and strap muscles; nodes go to the prelaryngeal (Delphian), pretracheal, paratracheal (level VI), lower jugular and superior mediastinal groups. Stridor and dyspnoea are often the first symptoms; hoarseness is late, meaning spread to the undersurface of the fold, the thyroarytenoid or the recurrent nerve. It is usually advanced and poorly differentiated, and prone to stomal recurrence."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s6-1","History and examination","Cancer of the larynx · Diagnosis and staging","Any patient of cancer age with hoarseness for more than two to three weeks needs laryngoscopy. Also ask about sore throat, otalgia, dysphagia, aspiration, dyspnoea, haemoptysis, weight loss, smoking and drinking, and lung function. Laryngoscopy: mirror, flexible or rigid video laryngoscopy shows the appearance (a nodule, ulcer, thickening or granular mass), mobility of each fold, and extent to the vallecula, base of tongue and piriform fossae. Narrow-band imaging helps separate malignant from benign lesions; office biopsy is possible with flexible scopes. Stroboscopy shows a stiff, non-vibrating segment in invasive cancer. Neck: extralaryngeal spread (a midline swelling over the cricothyroid membrane, tender cartilage from perichondritis, thyroid and strap muscle involvement, widening of the thyroid cartilage) and nodes (number, size, side, fixity). Figure 5 Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy in the clinic. Dhingra, fig. 62.6"],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s6-2","Imaging","Cancer of the larynx · Diagnosis and staging","CT of the neck with contrast: pre-epiglottic and paraglottic space invasion, cartilage destruction, extralaryngeal spread and nodes. MRI: more sensitive for cartilage invasion and for recurrence after radiotherapy. CT of the chest: lung metastases and second primaries (a solitary lung nodule is more likely a second primary). PET-CT for advanced disease and after chemoradiation (at 10 to 12 weeks) to detect residual disease. Ultrasound of the neck, with guided aspiration of suspicious nodes. When cancer is suspected and the airway is safe, cross-sectional imaging is done before biopsy , because post-biopsy swelling can distort staging. Speech and language therapy assesses voice and swallowing before treatment decisions."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s6-3","Endoscopy under anaesthesia","Cancer of the larynx · Diagnosis and staging","Direct laryngoscopy shows the hidden areas (infrahyoid epiglottis, anterior commissure, ventricle, subglottis), maps the extent and allows biopsy; microlaryngoscopy for small fold lesions; panendoscopy for second primaries. Toluidine blue staining once guided biopsy of keratotic patches."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s6-4","T staging (AJCC 8th edition)","Cancer of the larynx · Diagnosis and staging","T staging of laryngeal cancer Category Supraglottis Glottis Subglottis T1 One subsite, normal fold mobility Folds only, normal mobility (T1a one fold, T1b both) Limited to the subglottis T2 More than one subsite of supraglottis, or glottis, or outside (base of tongue, vallecula, medial piriform wall), without fixation Extends to supraglottis or subglottis, or impaired mobility Extends to the folds, with normal or impaired mobility T3 Fold fixation, or postcricoid area, pre-epiglottic space, paraglottic space, or inner cortex of thyroid cartilage Fold fixation, paraglottic space, or inner cortex of thyroid cartilage Fold fixation T4a Through the thyroid cartilage or beyond the larynx (trachea, soft tissues, deep tongue muscle, strap muscles, thyroid, oesophagus) As supraglottis Cricoid or thyroid cartilage, or beyond the larynx T4b Prevertebral space, encasing the carotid, or mediastinum As supraglottis As supraglottis N staging follows the general head and neck rules, now including extranodal extension , which upstages nodes (N2a and N3b); Older texts print the earlier (6th edition) tables, based on size alone."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s7-1","Principles","Cancer of the larynx · Treatment","Early cancers (stage I and II) are treated by a single modality, radiotherapy or surgery, with similar control; the choice turns on voice, swallowing, access, fitness and preference. Advanced cancers (stage III and IV) need combined treatment, with organ preservation where it is safe and total laryngectomy where it is not."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s7-2","Radiotherapy","Cancer of the larynx · Treatment","For early tumours with mobile folds and no cartilage invasion or nodes: about 60 to 70 Gy over six weeks ( Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer ). T1 glottic cancer is controlled in about 90% or more with good voice; T2 in about 85% with normal mobility but much less (about 63 to 70%) when mobility is impaired. It spares the voice and avoids surgery but causes mucositis, oedema, dryness and occasional chondronecrosis, makes recurrence harder to detect, and cannot be repeated. Poor results with fixed folds, subglottic extension, cartilage invasion and nodes."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s7-3","Transoral surgery","Cancer of the larynx · Treatment","Transoral laser microsurgery (CO2 laser under the microscope; Lasers, radiofrequency, cryosurgery and hyperbaric oxygen ), and more recently transoral robotic surgery, has largely replaced open partial laryngectomy for T1, T2 and selected T3 tumours: cordectomy for glottic cancer, supraglottic resection for supraglottic cancer. It is cheaper and quicker, avoids tracheostomy, keeps radiotherapy in reserve, and gives similar control to radiotherapy, but the voice may be poorer after wide cordectomy. It needs good exposure, and is contraindicated by fold fixation, interarytenoid involvement and poor lung function."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s7-4","Open conservation surgery","Cancer of the larynx · Treatment","Now used mainly in selected patients and after radiotherapy failure: Cordectomy through a laryngofissure. Vertical partial (frontolateral, hemi-) laryngectomy: one fold with the anterior commissure and adjacent thyroid cartilage. Supraglottic (horizontal partial) laryngectomy: removes the epiglottis, pre-epiglottic space, false folds and aryepiglottic folds above the ventricles; for T1 and T2 supraglottic cancer with mobile folds, clear of the anterior commissure, with good lung function, because every patient aspirates at first and must relearn swallowing. Supracricoid partial laryngectomy: removes both folds, the paraglottic spaces and the thyroid cartilage, rebuilt by cricohyoidopexy or cricohyoidoepiglottopexy; for selected T2 and T3 tumours with at least one mobile arytenoid."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s7-5","Total laryngectomy","Cancer of the larynx · Treatment","Removal of the whole larynx with the hyoid, pre-epiglottic space and upper trachea; the pharynx is closed and the trachea brought out as a permanent end stoma. The classic indications: T3 and T4 tumours (fold fixed, cartilage invaded, extralaryngeal spread). Invasion of the thyroid or cricoid cartilage; both arytenoids; posterior commissure. Transglottic tumours with fold fixation. Failure after radiotherapy or conservation surgery. A non-functioning larynx with intractable aspiration. Combined with neck dissection and postoperative radiotherapy as indicated; contraindicated by distant metastases. Figure 6 Treatment of T2N0 glottic cancer: radiotherapy when the fold is mobile and the commissure and arytenoid are spared; conservation laryngectomy when mobility is impaired or the commissure or arytenoid is involved; total laryngectomy for failure. Dhingra, fig. 62.7"],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s7-6","Organ preservation","Cancer of the larynx · Treatment","The VA Larynx trial (1991) showed that induction chemotherapy (cisplatin and fluorouracil) followed by radiotherapy in responders gave the same survival as laryngectomy, with two-thirds keeping their larynx. RTOG 91-11 (2003) showed that concurrent cisplatin with radiotherapy gave the best laryngeal preservation and locoregional control, although at ten years overall survival did not differ. Concurrent chemoradiation is now standard for many T3 and node-positive T2 cancers, with salvage laryngectomy for failure; cetuximab or carboplatin for those unfit for cisplatin. But population survival has fallen since practice shifted to non-surgical treatment, and salvage laryngectomy after chemoradiotherapy carries high complication rates and poor quality of life. NICE advises offering T3 patients a choice of chemoradiotherapy or surgery with adjuvant (chemo)radiotherapy, and considering surgery first for T4a. Factors favouring primary laryngectomy are a poorly functioning larynx (aspiration, weight loss, tube feeding), a tracheostomy, cartilage invasion (T4a) and patient preference. Quality of life is similar overall after surgical and non-surgical treatment, so decisions centre on the patient's values about voice and swallowing, made in the multidisciplinary team."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s7-7","By site","Cancer of the larynx · Treatment","Carcinoma in situ: transoral laser excision (or stripping) with histology; radiotherapy if multifocal or invasive. Glottic T1: radiotherapy or laser cordectomy, with similar local control (about 85 to 95%); laser is completed in a day and is cheaper, while radiotherapy takes three to seven weeks, and attempts at a randomised comparison have failed to recruit. With anterior commissure involvement, radiotherapy or a frontolateral laryngectomy. Glottic T2: radiotherapy if the fold is mobile and the commissure and arytenoid are free; otherwise conservation surgery; total laryngectomy if it recurs. The N0 neck is not treated (occult nodes under 5 to 25%). Glottic T3 and T4: chemoradiation for selected T3; total laryngectomy with neck dissection and postoperative radiotherapy for T4a. Supraglottic T1 and T2: radiotherapy, laser resection or supraglottic laryngectomy, always with bilateral elective treatment of levels II to IV . Subglottic: radiotherapy for early lesions; total laryngectomy with hemithyroidectomy or total thyroidectomy and level VI and paratracheal dissection, and radiotherapy including the superior mediastinum, for advanced lesions."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s7-8","The neck","Cancer of the larynx · Treatment","Clinically positive nodes need neck dissection or radiotherapy. After chemoradiation, a complete response on PET-CT at about twelve weeks allows surveillance instead of planned neck dissection. Postoperative chemoradiation is given for positive margins or extranodal extension."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s8-1","Voice restoration","Cancer of the larynx · After laryngectomy","Communication after total laryngectomy Method How it works Comments Tracheo-oesophageal speech A one-way valved prosthesis (Blom–Singer, Provox) in a puncture between trachea and oesophagus diverts lung air into the pharynx when the stoma is occluded The best voice; most widely used; valve care and replacement needed Oesophageal speech Air is swallowed or injected into the upper oesophagus and released to vibrate the pharyngo-oesophageal segment No device; rough but loud; hard to learn; six to ten words per breath Electrolarynx A battery-powered vibrator held against the neck (or a tube into the mouth) supplies sound that the mouth shapes Easy to learn; mechanical voice Writing and lip speech Temporary Figure 7 First, (A) an electrolarynx and (B) a laryngectomised patient using it. Second, tracheo-oesophageal speech: the thumb closes the stoma and air passes through the prosthesis into the oesophagus. Dhingra, figs 62.8 and 62.9"],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s8-2","Other rehabilitation","Cancer of the larynx · After laryngectomy","Smell: lost because air no longer passes through the nose; the polite yawning manoeuvre (lowering the jaw and tongue with the lips closed) draws air through the nose. Stoma care: humidifying heat-moisture exchangers, protection from water. Swallowing and thyroid function (hypothyroidism in a fifth to two-thirds after treatment)."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s8-3","Complications","Cancer of the larynx · After laryngectomy","Pharyngocutaneous fistula (more after radiotherapy; reduced by a pectoralis major or free flap), stomal stenosis and stomal recurrence, pharyngeal stricture, chondronecrosis after radiotherapy, and a non-functioning larynx after chemoradiation."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s8-4","Follow-up and recurrence","Cancer of the larynx · After laryngectomy","Every one to three months in the first year, less often thereafter, lifelong, looking for recurrence and second primaries. Recurrence shows as new oedema, pain, worse voice or reduced mobility; PET-CT and biopsy confirm it; about half are salvageable, usually by total laryngectomy (selected small recurrences by conservation surgery). Panendoscopy at diagnosis finds a synchronous primary in 1 to 6%. Recurrent or metastatic disease that cannot be cured is treated with immunotherapy (pembrolizumab), with or without chemotherapy."],["ev","cl:t-lxca~s8-5","Cancer of the larynx: current evidence","Cancer of the larynx · After laryngectomy","In the VA Larynx trial, induction chemotherapy then radiotherapy gave the same survival as laryngectomy with two-thirds keeping their larynx (1991); RTOG 91-11 showed concurrent chemoradiotherapy gave the best larynx preservation, with no difference in overall survival at ten years (Forastiere et al., J Clin Oncol 2013). In the US national cancer database, survival from laryngeal cancer fell as practice shifted from surgery to non-surgical treatment in the 1980s and 1990s, especially for advanced disease (Hoffman et al., Laryngoscope 2006), which underlies NICE's advice to offer T3 patients a choice and to consider surgery first for T4a (NG36, 2016). In the PET-NECK trial of 564 patients with nodal disease, PET-CT surveillance after chemoradiotherapy gave the same survival as planned neck dissection, with far fewer operations (Mehanna et al., N Engl J Med 2016). For recurrent or metastatic disease, first-line pembrolizumab, alone or with chemotherapy, improved survival in PD-L1 positive patients (Burtness et al., Lancet 2019)."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s8-5","Prognosis","Cancer of the larynx · After laryngectomy","Five-year survival is about 90% for T1 glottic cancer, falling with stage; supraglottic and subglottic cancers do worse than glottic. Nodes, extranodal extension, cartilage invasion and subglottic extension are adverse."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s9-1","Viva essentials","Cancer of the larynx · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Regions and subsites, and why glottic cancer rarely spreads to nodes. Pre-epiglottic and paraglottic spaces, transglottic spread, Broyles' ligament. Symptoms by site; the hoarseness rule. T staging and the significance of fold mobility. Treatment: radiotherapy versus laser for early cancer; organ preservation (VA and RTOG 91-11); indications for total laryngectomy. Voice rehabilitation after laryngectomy."],["sec","cl:t-lxca~s9-2","Common pitfalls","Cancer of the larynx · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating persistent hoarseness in a smoker with antibiotics and voice rest. Missing the anterior commissure, ventricle or subglottis without direct laryngoscopy. Forgetting bilateral neck treatment in supraglottic cancer. Offering organ preservation to a T4a tumour through cartilage."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s1-1","Lesions","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Overview","Laryngomalacia (the commonest). Vocal fold paralysis (the second commonest). Congenital subglottic stenosis. Laryngeal web and atresia. Subglottic haemangioma. Laryngeal cysts and laryngocele. Laryngo-oesophageal cleft. Tracheomalacia and vascular rings (in the trachea, outside the larynx)."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s1-2","Clues from the cry","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Overview","A normal cry with stridor points away from the glottis (laryngomalacia, subglottic stenosis, haemangioma); a weak, hoarse or absent cry points to the vocal folds (paralysis, web). Stridor eased by lying prone suggests laryngomalacia, micrognathia or macroglossia. Choking and cyanosis with feeds suggest a cleft, paralysis or a tracheo-oesophageal fistula."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s2-1","Features","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Laryngomalacia","Excessive flaccidity of the supraglottis, which is drawn into the airway on inspiration. Stridor appears at birth or within the first weeks, is inspiratory and fluttering, worse with crying, feeding, excitement and lying supine, and better prone; the cry is normal. Symptoms usually peak at six to eight months and resolve by 12 to 24 months. Reflux often coexists and aggravates it. Figure 1 Laryngomalacia: the epiglottis folded longitudinally into an omega. Dhingra, fig. 59.1"],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s2-2","Appearances","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Laryngomalacia","On flexible endoscopy in the awake infant: an omega-shaped , elongated epiglottis curled on itself; short, tight aryepiglottic folds tethering it; and redundant mucosa over large arytenoids that prolapses into the glottis on inspiration. Vocal fold movement is normal. One classification grades the collapse as type 1 (short aryepiglottic folds), type 2 (redundant arytenoid tissue) and type 3 (posterior collapse of the epiglottis, often with neuromuscular disease). It cannot be judged in a paralysed, anaesthetised child."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s2-3","Treatment","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Laryngomalacia","Most: reassurance and observation; positioning; anti-reflux treatment. Severe disease (failure to thrive, feeding difficulty, apnoeas, cyanosis, pulmonary hypertension): microlaryngoscopy and bronchoscopy to exclude a second lesion, then endoscopic supraglottoplasty : division of the tight aryepiglottic folds and trimming of redundant arytenoid mucosa with cold steel or laser. Tracheostomy rarely."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s3-1","Causes","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Vocal fold paralysis","Birth trauma ( Vocal fold paralysis ; stretching of the recurrent nerve in breech or forceps delivery), central nervous system anomalies (Arnold–Chiari malformation and hydrocephalus, usually bilateral), cardiac surgery and ligation of the ductus arteriosus, cardiomegaly and mediastinal lesions (usually left-sided), and idiopathic."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s3-2","Presentation","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Vocal fold paralysis","Unilateral: a weak, breathy cry, mild stridor, and choking or aspiration with feeds. Bilateral: a good cry but marked inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress, because both folds lie near the midline."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s3-3","Assessment","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Vocal fold paralysis","Flexible endoscopy in the awake child; imaging of the brain, neck and chest; and a swallowing assessment."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s3-4","Treatment","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Vocal fold paralysis","Observation for unilateral paralysis, with feeding support. Bilateral paralysis often needs a tracheostomy; definitive airway surgery is deferred, because many recover within the first year or two, and the larynx grows. Shunting for Arnold–Chiari malformation may restore movement."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s4-1","Congenital","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Subglottic stenosis","Thickening of the cricoid cartilage (cartilaginous) or of the submucosa (soft tissue) below the vocal folds. Diagnosed when the subglottic lumen is under 4 mm in a term neonate (normal 4.5 to 5.5 mm) or under 3 mm in a premature baby. The child may be well until a cold produces stridor, recurrent or prolonged croup; the cry is normal."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s4-2","Acquired","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Subglottic stenosis","Far commoner now ( Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis ): from prolonged or repeated intubation in premature and sick neonates, especially with oversized tubes and movement; presenting as failure to extubate or stridor after extubation."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s4-3","Grading","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Subglottic stenosis","Cotton–Myer: grade I up to 50% obstruction, II 51 to 70%, III 71 to 99%, IV no lumen; assessed by the largest endotracheal tube that allows a leak."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s4-4","Treatment","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Subglottic stenosis","Many mild congenital stenoses improve as the larynx grows. Options: observation; endoscopic balloon dilatation and radial incision; anterior cricoid split (in neonates failing extubation); laryngotracheal reconstruction with costal cartilage grafts; and cricotracheal resection for severe stenosis; tracheostomy where needed."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s5-1","Laryngeal web","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Webs, cysts and haemangioma","Incomplete recanalisation of the larynx in the tenth week. Usually an anterior glottic web between the vocal folds with a concave posterior edge; associated with 22q11 deletion (velocardiofacial syndrome; The syndromal child ), which should be tested for. A weak or absent cry from birth, and airway obstruction when the web is large. Thin webs are divided endoscopically with knife or CO2 laser; thick webs often have subglottic extension and need excision through a laryngofissure with a silicone keel and later dilatations. Laryngeal atresia causes complete obstruction at birth and death unless an emergency airway is made (or a tracheo-oesophageal fistula provides one). Figure 2 A laryngeal web. Dhingra, fig. 59.2"],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s5-2","Subglottic haemangioma","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Webs, cysts and haemangioma","Asymptomatic at birth; stridor appears at six weeks to six months as the haemangioma proliferates, often presenting as recurrent croup, with a normal cry; worse with crying because of venous engorgement. About half have a cutaneous haemangioma, especially in the beard distribution; some have mediastinal lesions. Endoscopy shows a soft, compressible, reddish-blue swelling, usually posterolateral in the subglottis; biopsy is not needed and may bleed. MRI or CT shows the extent. Propranolol is now first-line and often produces dramatic involution. Systemic steroids (classically dexamethasone, then prednisolone for many months) and intralesional steroid, now second line. CO2 or KTP laser or open excision for resistant lesions. Tracheostomy while awaiting involution, which is the natural history."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s5-3","Laryngeal cysts","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Webs, cysts and haemangioma","Saccular cysts (a mucus-filled saccule) and ductal cysts in the aryepiglottic fold, vallecula or subglottis appear as smooth, bluish, fluid-filled swellings that obstruct the airway. Needle aspiration can provide an emergency airway; endoscopic deroofing or excision with laser is definitive. Subglottic cysts follow intubation in premature infants."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s5-4","Laryngocele","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Webs, cysts and haemangioma","An air-filled dilatation of the saccule rising between the false fold and the thyroid lamina: internal (within the larynx, a supraglottic bulge), external (through the thyrohyoid membrane, a neck swelling that enlarges with Valsalva), or combined . Rare in infants; in adults ( Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness ) linked to wind instrument players and glass blowers, and sometimes to a ventricular carcinoma blocking the saccule, which must be excluded. Infection forms a laryngopyocele . Endoscopic excision for internal ones, external excision for external and combined. Figure 3 First, a laryngocele of the mixed type with internal and external components. Second, a left laryngocele as seen on Valsalva (arrow). Dhingra, figs 61.8 and 61.9 Figure 4 Laryngocele. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~c6","Laryngeal cleft","Congenital lesions of the larynx · Laryngeal cleft","A gap at the back. Incomplete fusion of the posterior cricoid lamina and tracheo-oesophageal septum, leaving a communication between larynx and oesophagus. Benjamin–Inglis classification Type Extent I Interarytenoid, above the vocal folds II Partly into the cricoid lamina III Through the whole cricoid, into the cervical trachea IV Into the thoracic trachea Choking, coughing and cyanosis with feeds, recurrent aspiration pneumonia, a hoarse cry and stridor. Diagnosed at microlaryngoscopy by palpating the interarytenoid area with a probe, since small clefts are hidden by redundant mucosa. Type I clefts may be managed by thickened feeds or endoscopic injection or repair; deeper clefts need endoscopic or open repair."],["ev","cl:t-lxcong~c7","Congenital lesions of the larynx: current evidence","Congenital lesions of the larynx · The trachea and beyond","Laryngomalacia. The International Pediatric Otolaryngology Group recommends supraglottoplasty for severe disease (feeding difficulty with failure to thrive, apnoeas, cyanosis, pulmonary hypertension), screening for synchronous airway lesions in severe cases, and treating reflux when present (Carter et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2016). Supraglottoplasty also improves obstructive sleep apnoea due to laryngomalacia: in a meta-analysis the apnoea-hypopnoea index fell from about 14 to 3 events an hour (Camacho et al., Laryngoscope 2016). Haemangioma. In a randomised trial, oral propranolol 3 mg/kg/day for six months cleared or nearly cleared infantile haemangioma in 60% against 4% with placebo (Léauté-Labrèze et al., NEJM 2015), and it has replaced steroids as first-line treatment for subglottic haemangioma. Subglottic stenosis. Endoscopic balloon dilation succeeds for many soft, acquired, low-grade stenoses; firm or high-grade (Myer–Cotton III to IV) stenoses usually need open laryngotracheal reconstruction or cricotracheal resection."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~c7","The trachea and beyond","Congenital lesions of the larynx · The trachea and beyond","Not always the larynx. Tracheomalacia: a soft, collapsing trachea, primary or after compression or tracheo-oesophageal fistula repair; expiratory stridor and a barking cough; most improve with growth; aortopexy, stenting or tracheostomy for severe cases. Vascular rings and slings: a double aortic arch, right arch with ligamentum, anomalous innominate artery or pulmonary artery sling compress the trachea and oesophagus, causing stridor and dysphagia. Contrast CT or MRI and a barium swallow; surgical division. Tracheo-oesophageal fistula and oesophageal atresia. Cri-du-chat syndrome (deletion of the short arm of chromosome 5): a cat-like cry with a narrow, diamond-shaped glottis, learning disability and hypotonia."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s8-1","Viva essentials","Congenital lesions of the larynx · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Laryngomalacia: presentation, endoscopic appearance, natural history, supraglottoplasty. Normal versus hoarse cry as a localising sign. Subglottic stenosis: the 4 mm rule, causes, grading and treatment. Subglottic haemangioma: age of onset, beard haemangioma, propranolol. Laryngeal web and its association with 22q11 deletion. Laryngeal cleft: palpate the interarytenoid area."],["sec","cl:t-lxcong~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Congenital lesions of the larynx · For the examination","Common pitfalls Labelling every noisy baby laryngomalacia without looking at the whole airway when symptoms are severe. Biopsying a subglottic haemangioma. Missing a cleft because the interarytenoid area was not palpated."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s1-1","Causes","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Overview","Blunt: road traffic accidents (the neck striking the steering wheel or dashboard with the head extended), blows and kicks, clothesline injuries from a stretched wire or cable, and strangulation. Penetrating: knives, glass and gunshot wounds. Internal (iatrogenic): intubation, endoscopy, inhalation and caustic burns, and foreign bodies."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s1-2","Why the larynx is usually spared","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Overview","It is shielded by the mandible above, the sternum below and the spine behind, and it is mobile. Fractures need a direct anterior blow with the head extended, and become commoner after forty, when the cartilages calcify and become brittle; in children the elastic cartilages bend rather than break, but soft tissue injury and airway swelling are more dangerous. Figure 1 The relations of the larynx in cross-section. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~c2","Pathology","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Pathology","What breaks. Haematoma and oedema of the supraglottis or subglottis. Mucosal tears of the larynx or pharynx, with surgical emphysema. Dislocation or avulsion of an arytenoid at the cricoarytenoid joint. Dislocation of the cricothyroid joint, injuring the recurrent laryngeal nerve that runs just behind it. Fracture of the hyoid. Fractures of the thyroid cartilage, vertical or transverse: an upper fracture can avulse the epiglottis and false folds; a lower one displaces or tears the true folds and anterior commissure. Fracture of the cricoid, the ring that holds the airway open. Fractures of the upper tracheal rings. Laryngotracheal separation : the trachea parts from the cricoid and retracts into the mediastinum, usually with bilateral recurrent nerve injury. The lucid interval A patient with a laryngeal fracture may talk and breathe normally on arrival, then obstruct suddenly hours later as haematoma and oedema accumulate. Every significant anterior neck injury is observed in hospital with the airway assessed by endoscopy."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s3-1","Symptoms","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Clinical features","Respiratory distress and stridor. Hoarseness or aphonia. Painful, difficult swallowing, with aspiration. Pain in the larynx, worse with speaking and swallowing. Haemoptysis from mucosal tears."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s3-2","Signs","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Clinical features","Bruising and abrasions of the neck. Tenderness over the larynx. Surgical emphysema , increasing with coughing. Loss of the thyroid prominence and flattening of the neck contour; the thyroid notch may be impalpable. A palpable step or gap between fragments, and crepitus. Separation of the cricoid from the larynx or trachea."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s3-3","Associated injuries","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Clinical features","Cervical spine (assume it is injured), great vessels, oesophagus and pharynx, chest (pneumothorax), head and abdomen."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~c4","Evaluation","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Evaluation","Only when the airway allows. Flexible nasendoscopy: the most useful first examination in a stable patient: oedema, haematoma, lacerations, exposed cartilage, arytenoid displacement, vocal fold movement, and the size of the airway. CT of the larynx (with 3D reconstruction): cartilage fractures, joint dislocation, displacement; also the cervical spine and vessels. Only when the airway is safe. Rigid endoscopy (laryngoscopy, oesophagoscopy, bronchoscopy) under anaesthesia, after the airway has been secured, to map injuries. Chest radiograph for pneumothorax and mediastinal air; contrast swallow for pharyngo-oesophageal tears; angiography if vascular injury is suspected. Schaefer–Fuhrman grading of laryngeal injury Group Findings Management 1 Minor haematoma or laceration, no fracture Observe, humidify, steroids 2 Oedema, haematoma, minor mucosal disruption, no exposed cartilage, non-displaced fracture Observe; tracheostomy if the airway is threatened 3 Massive oedema, mucosal tears, exposed cartilage, displaced fracture, cord immobility Tracheostomy and open repair 4 As group 3 with a disrupted anterior commissure or unstable framework Tracheostomy, open repair and stenting 5 Complete laryngotracheal separation Tracheostomy and repair by anastomosis"],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s5-1","The airway","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Management","Acute distress: awake tracheostomy under local anaesthesia ( Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation ), then laryngoscopy under general anaesthesia. Orotracheal intubation is risky: a tube can enter a false passage through torn mucosa or complete a laryngotracheal separation. If intubation is attempted, it is by the most experienced operator, fibreoptically and with the neck prepared for a surgical airway. Stable airway: flexible endoscopy, then CT."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s5-2","Conservative treatment","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Management","For minor injuries with a normal or near-normal endoscopy and no displaced fracture: admission and observation, voice rest, humidified air, head elevation, systemic steroids early and in full dose (to reduce oedema and scarring), antibiotics (to prevent perichondritis), and anti-reflux treatment."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s5-3","Surgical repair","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Management","Indicated for displaced or unstable fractures, exposed cartilage, large mucosal tears, disruption of the anterior commissure, arytenoid dislocation and cord immobility, and laryngotracheal separation. Repair early, ideally within 24 to 48 hours and certainly before ten days, before granulation and scar form. Exposure: a midline thyrotomy (laryngofissure) to inspect and close the mucosa. Mucosa: all lacerations sutured; exposed cartilage covered with local flaps or grafts; loose fragments removed. Framework: fractures reduced and fixed with wire or titanium miniplates. Epiglottis: reattached, or excised if avulsed. Arytenoid: reduced if dislocated; removed if completely avulsed. Anterior commissure: a silastic keel prevents a web. Stents: a soft laryngeal stent or silicone tube for comminuted fractures, left for two to six weeks; stents themselves provoke granulation. Laryngotracheal separation: end-to-end anastomosis, with assessment of both recurrent nerves."],["ev","cl:t-lxtrauma~s5-4","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis: current evidence","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Management","Intubation injury. Large tubes, prolonged intubation and high cuff pressures cause most acquired laryngotracheal stenosis; cuff pressure monitoring and early review of patients with prolonged intubation reduce it."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s5-4","Complications","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Management","Laryngeal stenosis (supraglottic, glottic or subglottic), perichondritis and abscess, chondronecrosis, vocal fold paralysis or fixation, webs, and a poor voice."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s6-1","Causes","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Laryngotracheal stenosis","Prolonged or traumatic intubation (the commonest cause, including in children, Congenital lesions of the larynx : pressure necrosis of the posterior glottis and subglottis by the tube and cuff), external trauma, high tracheostomy and cricothyroidotomy, burns and caustics, radiotherapy, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, relapsing polychondritis, sarcoidosis, reflux, and idiopathic subglottic stenosis in women."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s6-2","Presentation","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Laryngotracheal stenosis","Progressive dyspnoea and stridor, often biphasic, sometimes with hoarseness; many patients are misdiagnosed with asthma."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s6-3","Evaluation","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Laryngotracheal stenosis","History of intubation or injury; office endoscopy of the supraglottis and glottis and vocal fold movement; CT of the subglottis and trachea; and direct laryngoscopy and bronchoscopy to measure the length, grade and site and to palpate the arytenoids (fixation versus paralysis). Cotton–Myer grading of subglottic stenosis Grade I, up to 50% obstruction; grade II, 51 to 70%; grade III, 71 to 99%; grade IV, no detectable lumen."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s6-4","Treatment","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Laryngotracheal stenosis","Supraglottic: endoscopic excision of scar; recurrence is common after radiotherapy. Glottic: an anterior web affects voice more than breathing and is divided with a keel; posterior glottic scarring that fixes the folds is treated by division of the interarytenoid scar, or by procedures that enlarge the glottis at the expense of voice (as for bilateral paralysis). Subglottic: endoscopic radial incisions with laser and balloon dilatation (with topical mitomycin or steroid injection) for thin, soft stenoses; laryngotracheal reconstruction with cartilage grafts, or cricotracheal resection with anastomosis, for firm, long or high-grade stenoses. Tracheal: resection and end-to-end anastomosis is definitive; balloon dilatation for thin webs or unfit patients. Complex, multilevel stenosis: a Montgomery T-tube stents the airway while preserving voice. Expanding metal stents are avoided in benign disease because of granulation and erosion."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s6-5","Prevention","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Laryngotracheal stenosis","Use the right-sized tube, keep cuff pressure below about 25 to 30 cm of water, avoid prolonged translaryngeal intubation where tracheostomy is better, and avoid placing a tracheostomy through the first ring or cricoid."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s7-1","Zones","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Penetrating neck injury","Penetrating injuries are those that breach the platysma ; they carry a mortality of up to about 6%, and half of deaths are from bleeding. The anterior neck is divided into three zones. Zone I runs from the clavicles and sternal notch to the cricoid and contains the great vessels at the thoracic inlet, trachea, oesophagus, lung apices and thoracic duct. Zone II , from the cricoid to the angle of the mandible, is the commonest site and the easiest to expose, with the best prognosis. Zone III , from the angle of the mandible to the skull base, contains the distal carotid and vertebral arteries and cranial nerves IX to XII and is hard to reach surgically."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s7-2","Management","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · Penetrating neck injury","Management follows trauma life support principles, with control of the airway (often by early intubation or a surgical airway) and of bleeding by direct pressure. Hard signs , such as an expanding haematoma, active bleeding, air bubbling through the wound, shock not responding to fluids, absent pulse, bruit or evolving stroke, mean immediate exploration in theatre. Stable patients with soft signs (haemoptysis, dysphonia, dysphagia, surgical emphysema, a non-expanding haematoma) or none are investigated: CT angiography has replaced catheter angiography as the first test in all three zones, supplemented by endoscopy or contrast swallow for the aerodigestive tract. The old rule of mandatory exploration of every zone II wound has given way to this selective (\"no zone\") approach , favoured by trauma societies, which reduces negative explorations without missing important injuries. Carotid injuries found at exploration are generally repaired rather than ligated."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s8-1","Viva essentials","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mechanism of laryngeal fracture and why it is commoner after forty. Signs: loss of thyroid prominence, emphysema, crepitus. Airway first: tracheostomy under local anaesthesia, not intubation. Indications and timing of open repair. Laryngotracheal stenosis: causes, grading and treatment."],["sec","cl:t-lxtrauma~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Discharging a talking patient with a laryngeal injury who later obstructs. Intubating blindly through a disrupted larynx. Delaying repair beyond the first week or two. Treating post-intubation subglottic stenosis as asthma."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s1-1","Definition","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Overview","Every acute otitis media inflames the mastoid air cells to some degree, because the antrum is continuous with the middle ear; a CT in plain otitis media often shows an opaque mastoid. Acute mastoiditis means clinically evident infection of the mastoid process, and coalescent mastoiditis means the bony septa between air cells have been destroyed, turning the cells into a single cavity full of pus."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s1-2","Who gets it","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Overview","Half of children with acute mastoiditis are four years old or younger, and only about 30% have a history of recurrent otitis media; often it is the first severe ear infection."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s1-3","Why it matters","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Overview","Mastoiditis is the gateway to the other complications of otitis media: neck and facial abscesses, facial palsy, labyrinthitis ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis ) and intracranial infection ( Intracranial complications of otitis media ). Up to one in ten develop an intracranial complication, even on intravenous antibiotics."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s2-1","Organisms","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Pathology","Streptococcus pneumoniae leads, with Streptococcus pyogenes , Staphylococcus aureus and Haemophilus influenzae . Pneumococcal serotype 19A is both resistant and virulent. Cultures are negative in many children who have already had antibiotics. Where the books differ · Commonest organism Dhingra Beta-haemolytic streptococcus. Lee Streptococcus pneumoniae. In practice Cover Streptococcus pneumoniae first; more recent series find it the commonest."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s2-2","How the bone is destroyed","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Pathology","Two processes run together. Pus under pressure in the cells causes ischaemic necrosis of the thin bony septa, and inflamed mucosa activates osteoclasts that resorb bone ( hyperaemic decalcification ). The cells coalesce into an abscess cavity, which then looks for a way out. Figure 1 Types of mastoid (cellular, diploic, acellular) and the groups of air cells through which infection spreads. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s2-3","Classification","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Pathology","Mastoiditis Acute Acute mastoiditis otitis media with mastoid inflammation Bone loss Coalescent septa destroyed, pus in a single cavity After antibiotics Masked (latent) slow bone destruction, few symptoms Long-standing Chronic with perforation, cholesteatoma or grommet Figure 2 Routes by which suppurative otitis media spreads to cause its complications. Dhingra, fig. 12.1"],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s3-1","Symptoms","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Clinical features","3.1.1 Pain Persistent pain behind the ear, often after an apparently improving otitis media. 3.1.2 Fever Fever that persists or returns despite treatment. 3.1.3 Discharge Ear discharge that becomes profuse and creamy, or persists beyond three weeks. Mucoid discharge must come from the middle ear, because the canal has no mucous glands. The change is what matters Pain, fever or discharge that continues or comes back during an acute otitis media points to mastoiditis."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s3-2","Signs","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Clinical features","3.2.1 The mastoid Tenderness is most telling over the tip and posterior border, rather than over the suprameatal (MacEwen's) triangle, which is tender in plain otitis media too. Always compare with the healthy side. Later the area becomes red, boggy and fluctuant. 3.2.2 The pinna A subperiosteal abscess pushes the auricle forwards and downwards, and the postauricular sulcus is lost. 3.2.3 The canal and drum Sagging of the posterosuperior canal wall, from periostitis over the antrum. Reservoir sign: the canal refills with pus straight after mopping. A pars tensa perforation with pulsatile discharge, sometimes with a light-house reflex. Hearing loss is conductive. Figure 3 Acute mastoiditis: the pinna is pushed forward and downward by the swelling behind it. Dhingra, fig. 12.2"],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s3-3","Masked mastoiditis","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Clinical features","Repeated or inadequate antibiotic courses can sterilise the middle ear while bone destruction continues. The patient has little pain and no discharge, or only a mild ache or persistent otalgia. The drum is dull and thickened with lost landmarks, and hearing is reduced. It may hide an extradural or perisinus abscess. Where the books differ · Symptoms of masked mastoiditis Dhingra Painless, without discharge; mild ache at most. Lee Persistent, chronic otalgia after antibiotics. In practice A patient whose ear never quite settles after antibiotics needs a CT."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s4-1","Subperiosteal (postauricular)","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Abscesses","The commonest. Pus breaks through the cribriform area into the space under the periosteum over the mastoid, displacing the pinna."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s4-2","Zygomatic","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Abscesses","Infection of the zygomatic air cells produces swelling in front of and above the ear, with oedema of the upper eyelid."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s4-3","Bezold abscess","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Abscesses","In an adult or older child with a pneumatised tip, pus breaks through the thin medial side of the tip and tracks under the sternocleidomastoid, presenting as a tender upper neck mass. 4.3.1 Differential Upper jugular lymphadenitis, a parotid mass, an infected branchial cyst, parapharyngeal abscess and jugular vein thrombosis. 4.3.2 Treatment Cortical mastoidectomy, finding the fistula at the tip, and drainage of the neck through a separate incision."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s4-4","Meatal (Luc) abscess","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Abscesses","Pus from the antrum tracks between the posterior canal wall and the periosteum, presenting as a swelling in the deep canal."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s4-5","Citelli abscess","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Abscesses","Pus from the tip tracks along the posterior belly of digastric to form an abscess behind the mastoid or in the digastric triangle."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s4-6","Deep neck","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Abscesses","Parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal abscesses via the tip cells or petrous cells (petrous infection: Petrous apicitis ). Figure 4 First, abscesses around the mastoid: postauricular, zygomatic and Bezold, with Citelli's abscess. Second, a Bezold abscess, where pus bursts through the medial side of the tip into the neck. Dhingra, figs 12.4 and 12.5 Figure 5 A mastoid abscess that has burst through the skin. Dhingra, fig. 12.3"],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s5-1","Blood tests","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Diagnosis","Full blood count with raised white cells, inflammatory markers, and blood cultures. Pus from the ear or myringotomy is sent for culture."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s5-2","Imaging","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Diagnosis","5.2.1 CT temporal bone with contrast For a suspected complication (including an abscess) or failure to improve within 48 hours; it is not needed in every child. Shows coalescence (loss of the septa), cortical erosion and abscesses, and looks for intracranial spread. An opaque mastoid alone is not mastoiditis. 5.2.2 MRI and MR venography When an intracranial complication or sinus thrombosis is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s5-3","Differential diagnosis","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Diagnosis","Swelling behind the ear Distinguishing points Furuncle of the canal No preceding otitis media, painful tragus and pinna movement, normal drum, no mucoid discharge Suppurating postauricular node No ear history, superficial abscess, mastoid not tender Otitis externa with nodes Canal swollen and tender, drum normal Infected sebaceous cyst Superficial, punctum, ear normal"],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s6-1","First steps","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Management","On call: acute mastoiditis Admit; take blood and ear cultures. Start intravenous third-generation cephalosporin, with anti-pseudomonal cover in chronic ears. Examine the facial nerve, balance, neck and neurology. Request contrast CT of the temporal bones and brain if a complication is suspected or there is no improvement in 48 hours; MRI and MRV if intracranial spread is possible. Perform a wide myringotomy with a grommet, and send the pus for culture. Call your senior for an abscess, coalescence, or no response at 24–48 hours."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s6-2","Medical treatment","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Management","Intravenous antibiotics, changed according to culture, with oral step-down to complete three to six weeks in total. Uncomplicated acute mastoiditis without coalescence often settles with antibiotics and myringotomy alone."],["ev","cl:t-mastoiditis~s6-3","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis: current evidence","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Management","Conservative or surgical? A systematic review of 21 studies (564 children) found cure rates of 96% with medical treatment, 96% with conservative surgery (myringotomy with or without grommet, abscess drainage) and 89% with mastoidectomy, supporting conservative first-line treatment for uncomplicated mastoiditis (Loh et al., J Laryngol Otol 2018). Immediate needle aspiration of a subperiosteal abscess, with myringotomy and intravenous antibiotics, avoided mastoidectomy in most children in an Israeli series (Bartov et al., Otol Neurotol 2019). Imaging. MRI with venography is better than CT for sigmoid sinus thrombosis and intracranial spread. Prevention. Mastoiditis is rare, and avoiding antibiotics for uncomplicated otitis media raises its incidence only slightly in large population studies, so it is not a reason to treat every ear infection."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s6-3","Surgery","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · Management","6.3.1 Indications for cortical mastoidectomy Subperiosteal abscess. Coalescence on CT. Sagging of the posterosuperior canal wall or a positive reservoir sign. No improvement after 48 hours of adequate treatment. Any complication, or cholesteatoma. 6.3.2 Small children A small subperiosteal abscess may be aspirated or incised with myringotomy and antibiotics, an approach that succeeds in most children in recent series; cortical mastoidectomy is advised if it fails or the abscess recurs. The infant mastoid is small and vascular, landmarks are poor, and the facial nerve exits superficially. 6.3.3 The operation Cortical (Schwartze) mastoidectomy removes the diseased cells and opens the antrum to the middle ear, leaving the canal wall and ossicles intact."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s7-1","Viva essentials","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Coalescence: what it means and how it is shown on CT. Signs: forward-displaced pinna, sagging canal wall, reservoir sign. The abscesses: subperiosteal, zygomatic, Bezold, Luc and Citelli, and where each points. Indications for cortical mastoidectomy. Masked mastoiditis: antibiotics without cure."],["sec","cl:t-mastoiditis~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating an opaque mastoid on CT as mastoiditis without clinical signs. Relying on suprameatal tenderness alone. Missing masked mastoiditis in a child whose otalgia never settled. Forgetting the superficial facial nerve when incising behind an infant's ear."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s1-1","Definition","Ménière's disease · Overview","An idiopathic disorder of the inner ear characterised by endolymphatic hydrops : distension of the endolymphatic system. It produces the classic tetrad of episodic vertigo, fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus and aural fullness."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s1-2","Epidemiology","Ménière's disease · Overview","Onset is usually between 35 and 60 years. It begins in one ear, but the other ear becomes involved in a substantial minority over the years."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s1-3","Ménière's disease or Ménière's syndrome","Ménière's disease · Overview","Ménière's disease is idiopathic hydrops. Ménière's syndrome , or secondary hydrops, has an identifiable cause: head injury, viral labyrinthitis, syphilis, Cogan syndrome, autoimmune disease, or delayed hydrops years after a profound loss in that ear."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s2-1","Hydrops","Ménière's disease · Pathology","The cochlear duct and saccule distend, bowing Reissner's membrane into the scala vestibuli. Small ruptures let potassium-rich endolymph mix with perilymph, poisoning hair cells and nerve fibres and producing an attack; as the membrane heals, function partly recovers. Figure 1 (A) The normal cochlear duct. (B) In hydrops the duct is distended with endolymph and Reissner's membrane bulges into the scala vestibuli. Dhingra, fig. 15.1 Figure 2 The membranous labyrinth with the endolymphatic sac, and the composition of endolymph and perilymph. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s2-2","Why it happens","Ménière's disease · Pathology","The cause is unknown; several theories may each be true in some patients. Defective absorption by the endolymphatic sac, or a small, poorly pneumatised sac and duct. Vasomotor disturbance with spasm of the internal auditory artery. Allergy and autoimmunity. Salt and water retention. Hypothyroidism. Viral infection. Figure 3 Aetiological factors and symptoms of endolymphatic hydrops. Dhingra, fig. 15.2"],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s3-1","Vertigo","Ménière's disease · Clinical features","Sudden, rotatory vertigo lasting from 20 minutes to several hours, often with nausea, vomiting, sweating and pallor. Attacks come in clusters with remissions of weeks or months. The patient lies still with the bad ear up or down; there is no loss of consciousness."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s3-2","Hearing loss","Ménière's disease · Clinical features","Fluctuating and sensorineural, worse during an attack and recovering afterwards, at first in the low frequencies. Over the years the loss becomes permanent and flat. Distortion, diplacusis (hearing the same tone at a different pitch in each ear) and intolerance of loud sound are common."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s3-3","Tinnitus","Ménière's disease · Clinical features","Low-pitched and roaring, louder before and during attacks."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s3-4","Fullness","Ménière's disease · Clinical features","Pressure in the ear, which may change or worsen before an attack and act as a warning."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s3-5","Signs","Ménière's disease · Clinical features","The drum is normal. During an attack there is horizontal-rotatory nystagmus. Where the books differ · Direction of nystagmus in an attack Dhingra Fast phase towards the healthy ear. Lee Towards the affected ear at the start (irritative), away from it later (paretic), and back towards it during recovery. In practice The direction can change during an attack, so do not rely on it alone to tell which ear is affected. Tullio phenomenon Vertigo provoked by loud sound may occur when the distended saccule lies against the stapes footplate."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s3-6","Variants","Ménière's disease · Clinical features","3.6.1 Cochlear hydrops Hearing symptoms without vertigo. 3.6.2 Vestibular hydrops Vertigo without hearing symptoms. 3.6.3 Lermoyez syndrome Hearing gets worse before an attack and improves with the vertigo, the reverse of the usual pattern. 3.6.4 Tumarkin's otolithic crisis Sudden drop attacks without warning or loss of consciousness, from abrupt otolith stimulation. Dangerous falls make it an indication for definitive treatment."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s4-1","Diagnostic criteria","Ménière's disease · Diagnosis","The Bárány Society criteria (2015), now used internationally and accepted in the American guideline, have two categories. Diagnostic criteria (Bárány Society and AAO-HNS, 2015) Category Requirements Definite Two or more spontaneous episodes of vertigo, each 20 minutes to 12 hours; audiometrically documented low- to mid-frequency sensorineural loss in the affected ear on at least one occasion before, during or after an attack; fluctuating aural symptoms (hearing, tinnitus or fullness) in that ear; not better explained by another diagnosis Probable Two or more episodes of vertigo or dizziness, each 20 minutes to 24 hours; fluctuating aural symptoms in the affected ear; not better explained by another diagnosis The 12-hour upper limit helps separate Ménière's disease from vestibular migraine , which is about ten times more common, overlaps with it in many patients, and is the commonest misdiagnosis. Where the books differ · The older AAO-HNS categories (1995) Dhingra and older texts certain (with histology), definite, probable and possible, based on two or more episodes of 20 minutes or longer and documented hearing loss. In practice Use the 2015 definite and probable categories."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s4-2","Staging","Ménière's disease · Diagnosis","By the average of thresholds at 0.5, 1, 2 and 3 kHz in the worst audiogram of the previous six months: stage 1 up to 25 dB, stage 2 26 to 40 dB, stage 3 41 to 70 dB, stage 4 over 70 dB."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s4-3","Audiometry","Ménière's disease · Diagnosis","4.3.1 Pure-tone audiogram Sensorineural loss affecting the low frequencies, giving a rising curve; later the curve flattens. Figure 4 Audiogram in early Ménière's disease: sensorineural loss, worse in the low frequencies (a rising curve). Dhingra, fig. 15.3 4.3.2 Speech and recruitment Discrimination is moderately reduced, around 55 to 85% between attacks, and recruitment is present, marking a cochlear lesion."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s4-4","Electrocochleography","Ménière's disease · Diagnosis","The summating potential grows relative to the action potential in a hydropic ear. Where the books differ · Raised SP/AP ratio Dhingra Above 30%. Lee Above 0.35. In practice A ratio above roughly a third supports hydrops. Figure 5 Electrocochleography: (A) normal ear, (B) Ménière's disease with an enlarged summating potential relative to the action potential. Dhingra, fig. 15.4"],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s4-5","Glycerol test","Ménière's disease · Diagnosis","An osmotic diuretic, 1.5 mL/kg, reduces hydrops temporarily. An improvement of 10 dB at two adjacent frequencies, or 10% in discrimination, is positive."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s4-6","Caloric test","Ménière's disease · Diagnosis","Reduced response on the affected side in about three-quarters."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s4-7","MRI with contrast","Ménière's disease · Diagnosis","Essential to exclude a vestibular schwannoma or other retrocochlear lesion. Delayed contrast MRI can now show hydrops directly in specialist centres."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s4-8","Differential diagnosis","Ménière's disease · Diagnosis","Vestibular schwannoma . Vestibular migraine ( Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes ). Recurrent vestibulopathy. Superior canal dehiscence . Autoimmune inner ear disease ( Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease ) and otosyphilis ( Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media ). Transient ischaemic attacks in the vertebrobasilar territory."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s5-1","The acute attack","Ménière's disease · Management","An acute attack Reassure; let the patient lie still with the head supported. Check the neurology and the head impulse test: new neurological signs mean stroke imaging, not sedatives. Give an antiemetic or vestibular sedative: prochlorperazine, dimenhydrinate, or diazepam. Intravenous fluids if vomiting. Keep sedatives short: they slow compensation."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s5-2","Long-term medical treatment","Ménière's disease · Management","5.2.1 Lifestyle Salt restriction to about 1.5 to 2 g a day, adequate water, limiting caffeine and alcohol, stopping smoking, and managing stress. Avoid working at heights or driving during active periods. 5.2.2 Medicines Betahistine (with caution in asthma) and a thiazide diuretic are widely used, although a large placebo-controlled trial found no benefit from betahistine. Treat allergy and hypothyroidism, and treat migraine features when they overlap. Vertigo is eventually controlled in most patients, partly through the natural course of the disease. Where the books differ · Betahistine Dhingra Betahistine and a diuretic as the standard long-term medical treatment. Oxford A large randomised trial (BEMED, 2016) found no benefit of betahistine over placebo in reducing attacks, even at high dose. Many units still offer it as a low-risk first step, but patients with frequent attacks should not be held on it for long before moving to intratympanic steroids. American guideline (2020): betahistine and diuretics are options; offer intratympanic steroids for active disease not controlled by medical treatment, and intratympanic gentamicin for those who fail non-ablative treatment. In practice: A trial of betahistine is reasonable, but move on to intratympanic steroids promptly if attacks continue."],["ev","cl:t-meniere~s5-3","Ménière's disease: current evidence","Ménière's disease · Management","In the BEMED trial of 221 patients, betahistine at 48 mg or 144 mg a day for nine months was no better than placebo at reducing attacks (Adrion et al., BMJ 2016). In a double-blind trial of 60 patients, intratympanic methylprednisolone reduced vertigo attacks by 90% and gentamicin by 87% at two years, with no difference between them (Patel et al., Lancet 2016). The American guideline (Basura et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020) recommends audiometry and MRI to exclude retrocochlear lesions, offers intratympanic steroids for active disease and gentamicin or labyrinthectomy after other treatments fail, and advises against positive-pressure therapy."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s5-3","When medical treatment fails","Ménière's disease · Management","The choice depends on whether hearing in the ear is still useful. Intractable vertigo Hearing useful Preserve the labyrinth intratympanic steroid, endolymphatic sac surgery, pressure-pulse device Hearing useful Destroy vestibular function intratympanic gentamicin, vestibular neurectomy Hearing lost Labyrinthectomy removes all inner ear function 5.3.1 Intratympanic steroid Repeated injections of methylprednisolone or dexamethasone. In a randomised trial, intratympanic methylprednisolone controlled vertigo as well as gentamicin, without the risk of gentamicin to hearing, so it is now usually tried first. 5.3.2 Intratympanic gentamicin Chemical ablation of the vestibular hair cells, given in small doses through a grommet or by injection, repeated only if vertigo continues (\"titration\"). Vertigo is controlled in about 80 to 90%, at the price of some hearing loss in 4 to 30%. Check the other ear's vestibular function first: up to half of patients develop disease in the second ear over twenty years, and bilateral ablation leaves oscillopsia. 5.3.3 Pressure-pulse therapy A device (Meniett) delivers low-pressure pulses to the middle ear through a grommet. Trials have not shown a clear benefit over placebo, and the American guideline advises against it. Figure 6 The mechanism of intermittent low-pressure pulse therapy. Dhingra, fig. 15.6 5.3.4 Endolymphatic sac surgery Decompression of the sac, or a shunt to the mastoid, preserves hearing. A meta-analysis found vertigo controlled in at least 75% of patients who had failed medical treatment, with decompression and shunting equally ef"],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s6-1","Viva essentials","Ménière's disease · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The tetrad, and the pathology of hydrops. Bárány Society and AAO-HNS (2015) criteria and staging. Audiogram: low-frequency, fluctuating, rising curve. Electrocochleography and the glycerol test. Variants: Lermoyez and Tumarkin. Ladder of treatment by hearing status."],["sec","cl:t-meniere~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Ménière's disease · For the examination","Common pitfalls Labelling every dizzy patient as Ménière's without documented hearing loss. Forgetting MRI to exclude a schwannoma. Keeping patients on vestibular sedatives for months. Using gentamicin when the other ear may also be affected."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s1-1","Recognising the abscess","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Quinsy drainage","A peritonsillar abscess sits between the tonsil capsule and the superior constrictor. It gives a one-sided sore throat, fever, trismus, a muffled (\"hot potato\") voice, drooling and referred earache; the soft palate bulges above the tonsil and the uvula is pushed to the other side. Bilateral swelling without trismus is usually tonsillitis. Image (CT) if the patient is a young child, the diagnosis is unclear, or a parapharyngeal or deep neck extension is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s1-2","Preparation","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Quinsy drainage","Admit or treat in the ambulatory unit; give intravenous fluids, penicillin (with or without metronidazole) and often a single dose of steroid; analgesia. Sit the patient up at 45 degrees with head support, a headlight and a Yankauer sucker running. Spray the throat with lidocaine and infiltrate the mucosa over the swelling."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s1-3","Aspiration","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Quinsy drainage","Use a 5 ml syringe (2 ml if trismus is severe) with a 18G needle or a cannula. Wrap tape around it so only 1 cm protrudes. Aspirate at three points over the most prominent bulge, from upper (just above and lateral to the upper pole) downwards. Send pus for culture. The carotid is lateral and behind The internal carotid lies deep to the tonsil bed, posterolateral to the abscess. Guard the needle or blade to 1 cm and keep the point directed slightly medially and never posterolaterally."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s1-4","Incision and drainage","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Quinsy drainage","If aspiration finds no pus, or the abscess recurs or fails to settle within 24 hours, incise. Open with a guarded blade, then widen with sinus forceps and suction. Where the books differ · Where to incise Oxford handbooks Level with the base of the uvula, a third of the way from the medial edge of the tonsil to its lateral extent, or wherever the abscess points. Classic textbook landmark Just lateral to where a horizontal line through the base of the uvula meets a vertical line along the anterior pillar ( Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess ). In practice Both put the cut level with the base of the uvula, above and lateral to the tonsil; incise where the abscess points most, with the blade guarded to 1 cm. Peritonsillar swelling 1 Trismus, uvula pushed across? yes aspirate no treat as tonsillitis, review 2 Pus on aspiration? yes antibiotics, review at 24 h no incise or image 3 Not settling at 24 h? incise CT for deep extension 4 Second quinsy or tonsillitis history? offer interval tonsillectomy"],["ev","cl:t-mouthminor~s1-5","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling: current evidence","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Quinsy drainage","A Cochrane review of 11 trials (674 participants) found recurrence was higher after needle aspiration than after incision and drainage (risk ratio 3.74), but the evidence was of very low quality and adverse events were rare with both (Chang et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2016). A systematic review of three randomised trials found no benefit from adding metronidazole to penicillin, with more side effects (Moen et al., J Laryngol Otol 2023)."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s1-5","Aftercare","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Quinsy drainage","Switch to oral antibiotics when swallowing; review if pain, trismus or fever return. Consider interval tonsillectomy after a second episode or a history of recurrent tonsillitis (hot or \"quinsy\" tonsillectomy is reserved for selected cases, such as airway compromise or failure to drain)."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s2-1","When it matters","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Tongue-tie division","A short or thick lingual frenulum attaches near the tip of the tongue, giving a notched or heart-shaped tip on protrusion. It is present in about 4 to 11% of newborns; most cause no trouble. Indications are breastfeeding difficulty with maternal nipple pain (after assessment by a feeding specialist) in infants, and in older children restricted tongue protrusion and elevation affecting oral hygiene, eating or articulation of tongue-tip sounds. If the tongue protrudes beyond the lower incisors, speech is unlikely to be affected."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s2-2","Frenotomy in infants","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Tongue-tie division","In babies under a few months, no anaesthetic is needed. Swaddle the baby, elevate the tongue with a grooved director or two fingers, and divide the thin frenulum with blunt-ended scissors close to the tongue, short of the genioglossus and the submandibular ducts and veins. Pressure with gauze stops the few drops of blood; feed straight away."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s2-3","Frenuloplasty in older children","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Tongue-tie division","Under general anaesthesia, divide the thick band transversely, release fibres down to genioglossus, and close the diamond-shaped defect vertically with absorbable sutures (transverse release, vertical closure), or use a Z-plasty for a long band. Senior's pearl Stay close to the tongue and look for the submandibular duct papillae before you cut. Dividing them, or the sublingual veins, turns a snip into a bleeding floor of mouth."],["ev","cl:t-mouthminor~s2-4","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling: current evidence","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Tongue-tie division","A Cochrane review of five randomised trials (302 infants) found frenotomy reduced maternal nipple pain in the short term, with inconsistent effects on objective breastfeeding scores and no reported adverse effects; the trials were small and at high risk of bias (O'Shea et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2017). A systematic review found mother-reported improvements in breastfeeding but limited high-quality evidence (Francis et al., Pediatrics 2015)."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s2-4","Complications","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Tongue-tie division","Bleeding, ulcer, infection, re-attachment and scarring, injury to the submandibular ducts (ranula or swelling)."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s3-1","Why","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Lip biopsy of the minor salivary glands","A biopsy of the minor salivary glands in the lower lip shows focal lymphocytic sialadenitis in Sjögren syndrome and can also diagnose sarcoidosis, amyloidosis and IgG4-related disease. In the 2016 ACR/EULAR classification criteria, a focus score of 1 or more and anti-Ro (SSA) antibodies each score 3 points, and abnormal Schirmer's test, ocular staining and unstimulated salivary flow 1 point each; 4 or more classifies as primary Sjögren syndrome (Shiboski et al., Arthritis Rheumatol 2017)."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s3-2","Technique","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Lip biopsy of the minor salivary glands","Under local anaesthesia, evert the lower lip and hold it with a chalazion clamp or fingers. Make a 1.5 to 2 cm mucosal incision parallel to the vermilion, between the midline and the commissure, through mucosa only. The glands bulge into the wound; tease out four to six lobules (enough for at least 4 mm squared of gland) with fine scissors, avoiding the small sensory nerves running vertically. Close with absorbable sutures."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s3-3","Complications","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Lip biopsy of the minor salivary glands","Lower lip numbness or tingling (usually temporary), bleeding, haematoma, mucocele, swelling, and a non-diagnostic sample."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s4-1","Assess first","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Drooling","Drooling is normal up to about 4 years. Beyond that it is mostly due to poor oral motor control in neurodevelopmental disability (cerebral palsy) or neurodegenerative disease (Parkinson's disease, motor neurone disease), rarely too much saliva. Distinguish anterior drooling (out of the mouth, social) from posterior drooling (into the pharynx, causing aspiration and chest infections). Grade severity and frequency, check dental disease, posture, nasal obstruction and medications that increase saliva."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s4-2","The ladder","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Drooling","1 Conservative Positioning, oral motor therapy, treatment of dental disease and nasal obstruction 2 Medication Glycopyrronium or hyoscine patches, limited by anticholinergic side effects 3 Botulinum toxin Ultrasound-guided injection of the submandibular (largest effect) and parotid glands, lasting 3 to 6 months 4 Surgery Duct ligation, submandibular duct relocation, or submandibular gland excision, chosen by whether the child aspirates"],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s4-3","Botulinum toxin injection","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Drooling","Under ultrasound guidance (local anaesthetic, sedation or general anaesthesia), inject the submandibular glands and, if needed, the parotids. Suggested maximum doses of botulinum toxin A are about 20 units per submandibular gland and 10 units per parotid gland. The main risk is dysphagia from spread into the floor of mouth muscles, usually temporary."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s4-4","Duct operations","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Drooling","Duct ligation: the submandibular ducts (two-duct), plus one or both parotid ducts (three- or four-duct), clipped or tied through the mouth; a day-case procedure, with temporary gland swelling. Submandibular duct relocation: an island of mucosa containing both duct openings is raised, the ducts are mobilised and tunnelled back to the tonsillar fossa so saliva runs into the pharynx; usually with excision of the sublingual glands to avoid a ranula. Not for children who aspirate. Submandibular gland excision: for children with posterior drooling and aspiration, with or without parotid duct ligation. Not for the child who aspirates Duct relocation and any operation that redirects saliva backwards can worsen aspiration. In posterior drooling, reduce saliva production (gland excision, duct ligation, botulinum toxin) instead."],["ev","cl:t-mouthminor~s4-5","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling: current evidence","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Drooling","A meta-analysis of eight placebo-controlled trials (181 patients) found botulinum toxin significantly reduced drooling severity in adults and children, with similar effects from types A and B (Vashishta et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2013). A meta-analysis of 21 studies (708 patients) found very good to excellent control after submandibular gland excision in about 80 to 85%, with no added benefit from parotid duct ligation or rerouting (Schild et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2021)."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s4-5","Complications","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · Drooling","Ranula, gland swelling and sialadenitis, dental caries from a dry mouth, thick saliva, bleeding, nerve injury in gland excision (marginal mandibular, lingual, hypoglossal), and recurrence."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s5-1","Viva essentials","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Quinsy: signs, aspiration versus incision, the carotid, and interval tonsillectomy. Tongue-tie: who needs division and the evidence. Lip biopsy: technique, focus score and the classification criteria. Drooling: anterior versus posterior, the ladder, and why relocation is wrong for aspirators."],["sec","cl:t-mouthminor~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling · For the examination","Common pitfalls An unguarded needle in the tonsil bed. Calling bilateral tonsillar swelling a quinsy. Dividing a tongue-tie through the submandibular duct papillae. Relocating the ducts in a child who aspirates."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s1-1","Definition","Myringitis · Overview","Inflammation confined to the tympanic membrane, sometimes with the adjacent deep canal skin, without primary disease of the middle ear or mastoid. Figure 1 Parts of the tympanic membrane. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-myring~s1-2","Forms","Myringitis · Overview","1.2.1 Acute bullous myringitis Blebs or bullae on the outer surface of the drum and the deep canal. The same picture is also described as otitis externa haemorrhagica ( Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis ). 1.2.2 Chronic granular myringitis The epithelium of the drum is replaced by granulation tissue, without a perforation."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s2-1","Cause","Myringitis · Bullous myringitis","It follows an upper respiratory infection. The organisms are those of acute otitis media: Streptococcus pneumoniae , Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella . Mycoplasma is the classic association, but it has not been proven. Where the books differ · Mycoplasma Dhingra Lists it as a possible cause. Lee The link is unproven. In practice Treat it as acute otitis media; Mycoplasma is rarely responsible."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s2-2","Clinical picture","Myringitis · Bullous myringitis","Sudden, intense ear pain for three or four days that then settles, sometimes with a little bloody discharge when the blebs burst. Haemorrhagic bullae sit on the drum and deep canal. A third to two-fifths have a middle ear effusion as well."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s2-3","Hearing","Myringitis · Bullous myringitis","A sensorineural or mixed loss, often in the high frequencies, is common and usually recovers. Always check hearing."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s2-4","Treatment","Myringitis · Bullous myringitis","Analgesia; lancing the blebs relieves pain; keep the ear dry; topical drops to prevent secondary infection; treat acute otitis media if present."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s3-1","Cause","Myringitis · Granular myringitis","Usually Staphylococcus , Pseudomonas or Proteus . Where the books differ · Predisposing factors Dhingra Links it to wax, foreign bodies and external ear infection. Lee No predisposing factor has been identified. In practice Look for wax, a foreign body or canal infection and treat any you find."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s3-2","Clinical picture","Myringitis · Granular myringitis","Itching, fullness and a foul, painless discharge. Granulation tissue covers part of the drum, often posterosuperiorly; the drum may perforate and heal repeatedly. Hearing is normal or slightly reduced."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s3-3","Diagnosis","Myringitis · Granular myringitis","Clinical, under the microscope. CT only if chronic otitis media or cholesteatoma has to be excluded."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s3-4","Treatment","Myringitis · Granular myringitis","Regular cleaning, dilute vinegar irrigation and topical drops; cauterising the granulation with silver nitrate or trichloroacetic acid, or laser; removal of the granulation. Severe or recurrent cases need tympanoplasty with canalplasty. Recurrence is common after surgery."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s3-5","Complication","Myringitis · Granular myringitis","Late fibrosis can blunt the anterior angle between the drum and canal wall, causing a conductive loss."],["ev","cl:t-myring~c4","Myringitis: current evidence","Myringitis · Differential diagnosis","Bullous myringitis is now regarded as a variant of acute otitis media: the same organisms ( Streptococcus pneumoniae first) are found in the middle ear fluid, and Mycoplasma is rarely responsible. Treat it as acute otitis media, with analgesia and an antibiotic when indicated. It causes a sensorineural hearing loss in a minority, which usually recovers (Drendel et al., Auris Nasus Larynx 2012); evidence for steroids in that setting is limited to small series. Granular myringitis is treated with careful debridement and topical antibiotic–steroid drops, with cautery or laser for resistant cases; evidence is from case series only."],["sec","cl:t-myring~c4","Differential diagnosis","Myringitis · Differential diagnosis","Other painful or wet drums. Acute otitis media : bulging drum with poor mobility. Chronic otitis media : a perforation and middle ear disease. Herpes zoster oticus : vesicles on the concha and canal, facial palsy. Otitis externa : canal swelling and tragal tenderness."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s5-1","Viva essentials","Myringitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Bullous versus granular, and the symptoms of each. Sensorineural loss in bullous myringitis. Treatment of each form."],["sec","cl:t-myring~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Myringitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not checking hearing in bullous myringitis. Calling granular myringitis chronic otitis media without examining under the microscope."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s1-1","The age rule","Approach to a lump in the neck · Approach","In children and young adults, inflammatory and congenital masses dominate, and lymphoma and thyroid cancer are the malignancies to remember. In adults over about forty, neoplasms dominate, and most are metastases, usually from squamous carcinoma of the upper aerodigestive tract. The old \"rule of 80\" puts it memorably: about 80% of non-thyroid neck masses in adults are neoplastic, 80% of those are malignant, and 80% of the malignant ones are metastatic, mostly from a primary above the clavicles."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s1-2","The time rule","Approach to a lump in the neck · Approach","Duration is almost as informative as age. A mass present for days is usually inflammatory, one present for months neoplastic, and one present for years congenital or benign."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s1-3","Site","Approach to a lump in the neck · Approach","Figure 1 First, swellings in the midline of the neck. Second, lateral swellings grouped by the triangles of the neck. Dhingra, figs 77.1 and 77.2 Neck masses by site Site Common causes Midline, above the hyoid Submental nodes, sublingual dermoid, thyroglossal cyst (suprahyoid), plunging ranula Midline, below the hyoid Thyroglossal cyst (commonest at the hyoid), thyroid isthmus, Delphian and pretracheal nodes, laryngocele, suprasternal dermoid, thymic cyst Low in the neck, beside the trachea (one side or both) Thyroid lobe nodule, asymmetric or multinodular goitre; moves with swallowing Submandibular triangle Submandibular nodes and gland (sialadenitis, stone, tumour), plunging ranula Carotid triangle Jugulodigastric and deep cervical nodes, branchial cyst, carotid body tumour, vagal schwannoma, laryngocele, carotid aneurysm Parotid region and angle of the jaw Parotid tumour or node, parapharyngeal mass, sebaceous cyst Posterior triangle Accessory chain nodes (nasopharynx, lymphoma, tuberculosis), cystic hygroma, lipoma, neural tumours, cervical rib Supraclavicular Virchow node (stomach and other infraclavicular primaries), lung and breast metastases, lymphoma, tuberculosis Within sternomastoid Sternomastoid tumour of infancy Figure 2 The triangles of the neck and their subdivisions. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s2-1","The levels","Approach to a lump in the neck · Lymph nodes","Neck node levels Level Boundaries Drains IA (submental) Between the anterior bellies of digastric, above the hyoid Lower lip, chin, floor of mouth, tongue tip IB (submandibular) Digastric bellies and the mandible Oral cavity, anterior nasal cavity, face, submandibular gland II (upper jugular) Skull base to the hyoid, around the internal jugular vein. IIA in front of the accessory nerve, IIB behind it Oral cavity, oropharynx, nasopharynx, larynx, parotid III (middle jugular) Hyoid to the cricoid Oropharynx, hypopharynx, larynx IV (lower jugular) Cricoid to the clavicle Hypopharynx, larynx, thyroid, cervical oesophagus V (posterior triangle) Behind sternomastoid, in front of trapezius. VA above the cricoid, VB below Nasopharynx, oropharynx, scalp and skin, thyroid VI (central) Hyoid to the sternal notch, between the carotids Thyroid, larynx (subglottis), hypopharynx, cervical oesophagus VII (superior mediastinal) Below the sternal notch Thyroid, oesophagus Figure 3 The lymph node levels of the neck, and the deep cervical chain. From the anatomy atlas Reading the node back to the primary Jugulodigastric node (level II): tonsil, tongue base, nasopharynx, supraglottis. Posterior triangle nodes: suggest the nasopharynx. Level VI: suggests the thyroid. Left supraclavicular node (Virchow, Troisier sign ): suggests an abdominal or thoracic primary. Cystic level II node in an adult: a metastasis from HPV-positive oropharyngeal carcinoma (tonsil or tongue base) or from papillary thyroid carcinoma, not a \"branchial cyst\", until proved otherwise."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s3-1","History","Approach to a lump in the neck · Assessment","Ask how long the lump has been present, whether it is growing or painful, and whether it changes with meals or infections. Then ask about the upper aerodigestive tract: hoarseness, dysphagia, odynophagia, referred otalgia, nasal obstruction or bleeding, a unilateral serous otitis, and weight loss. Record the risk factors (tobacco, alcohol and betel quid; sexual history for HPV; exposure to tuberculosis; HIV risk; contact with cats for Bartonella or toxoplasmosis; travel; and previous skin cancer or radiation) and any \"B\" symptoms of fever, night sweats and weight loss that suggest lymphoma or tuberculosis."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s3-2","Examination","Approach to a lump in the neck · Assessment","Palpate the neck from behind, level by level, noting the size, consistency, fixity, pulsation and transillumination of the lump and whether it moves with swallowing (thyroid, thyroglossal cyst) or with tongue protrusion (thyroglossal cyst). Then examine the whole head and neck: scalp, skin, ears, nose and oral cavity, palpate the tonsils and tongue base, and look at the nasopharynx, larynx and hypopharynx with a flexible nasendoscope. Finish with the axillae, groins, liver and spleen."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s3-3","Investigation","Approach to a lump in the neck · Assessment","Ultrasound with FNA is the first test for most adult masses. Ultrasound shows whether the mass is cystic or solid, the node architecture and its vascularity; FNA gives cytology, with p16/HPV, EBV, thyroglobulin, flow cytometry for lymphoma, and culture and acid-fast stains as needed. A non-diagnostic aspirate is repeated or replaced by a core biopsy. Contrast CT or MRI of the neck shows the extent and may reveal a primary. PET-CT is used for an unknown primary, done before panendoscopy and biopsies, and for staging and distant disease. Chest imaging. Blood tests: count, ESR, serology for EBV, HIV, toxoplasma and Bartonella , and tests for tuberculosis. Panendoscopy under anaesthesia with directed biopsies, tonsillectomy and tongue-base mucosectomy to find an occult primary. Do not excise an adult neck node first An open biopsy of a node that turns out to be metastatic squamous carcinoma breaches the fascial planes and contaminates the field. It raises rates of local recurrence and complications. If open biopsy is unavoidable after negative FNA and endoscopy (for example, to type a suspected lymphoma), plan it so that the incision can be included in a neck dissection, with frozen section, and be ready to proceed to dissection."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s3-4","Referral","Approach to a lump in the neck · Assessment","In the UK, NICE (NG12) advises a suspected cancer pathway referral (seen within two weeks) for an unexplained lump in the neck in adults, persistent unexplained hoarseness in those aged 45 or over, and unexplained oral ulceration lasting more than three weeks. One-stop neck lump clinics, with ultrasound and needle sampling at the first visit, shorten the time to diagnosis."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s4-1","Reactive lymphadenitis","Approach to a lump in the neck · Inflammatory causes","Reactive nodes are tender, mobile and often bilateral, and accompany a sore throat, a dental infection or a skin infection. They settle within two to six weeks. A node that persists beyond that, grows, is hard, or lies in the supraclavicular fossa needs investigation."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s4-2","Acute suppurative lymphadenitis","Approach to a lump in the neck · Inflammatory causes","Suppurative lymphadenitis, caused by Staphylococcus aureus and group A streptococcus, mainly affects young children. The node is hot and tender and may form an abscess. It is treated with antibiotics, and an abscess is drained by aspiration or incision. A neck abscess in an adult should raise the suspicion of a necrotic metastasis or tuberculosis."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s4-3","Tuberculous lymphadenitis (scrofula)","Approach to a lump in the neck · Inflammatory causes","Tuberculous lymphadenitis is very common in endemic areas and more frequent with HIV. It affects any age, usually the deep cervical and posterior triangle nodes, and passes through recognisable stages: discrete nodes, then matted nodes with periadenitis, then caseation and a cold abscess , then a \"collar-stud\" abscess through the deep fascia, and finally sinus formation. The diagnosis rests on FNA or biopsy showing caseating granulomas, acid-fast bacilli on Ziehl–Neelsen staining, culture and GeneXpert with rifampicin resistance testing, supported by a chest radiograph, a tuberculin test or interferon-gamma release assay, and an HIV test. Treatment is standard antituberculous therapy: isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol for two months, then isoniazid and rifampicin for four. Nodes may enlarge temporarily during treatment, a paradoxical reaction . Surgery is reserved for drug failure or residual masses, and abscesses are aspirated rather than incised. Figure 4 First, multiple tuberculous nodes in the neck. Second, (A) a caseating suprasternal node and (B) supraclavicular tuberculous nodes. Dhingra, figs 77.8 and 77.9"],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s4-4","Atypical mycobacteria","Approach to a lump in the neck · Inflammatory causes","Atypical mycobacterial infection affects children aged one to five as a painless node, usually submandibular or preauricular, with violaceous, thin overlying skin and a tendency to form sinuses. The chest film is normal and the tuberculin reaction weak. Complete excision is the treatment, or curettage where the facial nerve is at risk, with clarithromycin as an alternative; incision and drainage leaves a chronic sinus."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s4-5","Other infections","Approach to a lump in the neck · Inflammatory causes","Cat-scratch disease from Bartonella henselae causes a tender, often suppurating node after a cat scratch; it is diagnosed by serology and treated with azithromycin when severe. Toxoplasmosis causes posterior cervical nodes in young adults and is diagnosed by serology. Infectious mononucleosis from EBV gives generalised nodes, exudative tonsillitis and splenomegaly. HIV ( HIV and ENT ) causes persistent generalised lymphadenopathy, and its nodes may harbour tuberculosis, lymphoma or Kaposi sarcoma. Actinomycosis produces a woody swelling with sinuses near the jaw."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s4-6","Non-infective inflammatory causes","Approach to a lump in the neck · Inflammatory causes","Sarcoidosis produces non-caseating granulomas. Kikuchi–Fujimoto disease , histiocytic necrotising lymphadenitis, affects young women with tender nodes and fever and settles on its own. Kimura disease affects young Asian men with a preauricular mass, eosinophilia and a high IgE. Castleman disease is unicentric or multicentric, and Rosai–Dorfman disease causes massive painless nodes."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s5-1","Metastatic squamous carcinoma","Approach to a lump in the neck · Neoplastic causes","The typical patient has a hard, painless, enlarging node, often in level II or III. He may be an older smoker and drinker, or a younger non-smoker with HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer, whose nodes are often cystic. A node fixed to deep structures or to the skin means extranodal spread. The primary is sought in the tonsil and tongue base ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ), nasopharynx ( Nasopharyngeal carcinoma ), pyriform sinus ( Carcinoma of the hypopharynx ), larynx ( Cancer of the larynx ) and oral cavity ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer ), by examination, endoscopy, imaging and panendoscopy. Supraclavicular nodes may come from below the clavicle: from the lung, breast, oesophagus, stomach, pancreas, kidney, testis or ovary."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s5-2","Carcinoma of unknown primary","Approach to a lump in the neck · Neoplastic causes","When a squamous metastasis has no primary on examination, endoscopy and imaging, the search proceeds in a set order: PET-CT. Panendoscopy with biopsies. Ipsilateral (or bilateral) tonsillectomy and transoral tongue-base mucosectomy. p16 and EBV staining of the node, which point to the oropharynx or nasopharynx. Treatment is neck dissection with radiotherapy to the neck and the likely mucosal sites, or primary chemoradiotherapy ( Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer ). The outcome is better than many expect, especially with HPV-positive disease."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s5-3","Lymphoma","Approach to a lump in the neck · Neoplastic causes","Hodgkin lymphoma affects young adults, with rubbery, painless, discrete nodes, often low in the neck or supraclavicular, sometimes with a mediastinal mass, B symptoms and pain after alcohol; the diagnostic cell is the Reed–Sternberg cell. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma affects older adults and involves both nodes and extranodal sites, including Waldeyer's ring (tonsil, nasopharynx, tongue base), the thyroid, the salivary glands and the sinonasal tract, where NK/T-cell lymphoma occurs. FNA with flow cytometry may suggest lymphoma, but a core or excision biopsy is usually needed to classify it, and fresh tissue must be sent. Treatment follows staging, with chemotherapy with or without radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s5-4","Other neoplasms","Approach to a lump in the neck · Neoplastic causes","Thyroid carcinoma, including cystic nodal metastases of papillary carcinoma, salivary tumours ( Salivary gland tumours ), paragangliomas and schwannomas ( Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies ), lipomas and, rarely, sarcomas complete the neoplastic list."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s6-1","Swellings that are not nodes","Approach to a lump in the neck · Other masses","A laryngocele ( Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness ) is an air-filled dilatation of the laryngeal saccule. An external laryngocele pierces the thyrohyoid membrane to form a lateral neck swelling that enlarges on a Valsalva manoeuvre, coughing or blowing, as in trumpeters and glassblowers; infection turns it into a laryngopyocele , and a ventricular or supraglottic carcinoma obstructing the saccule must always be excluded. A pharyngeal pouch forms a soft swelling low in the left neck that gurgles, with regurgitation. A plunging ranula ( Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches ) is a soft, transilluminant submandibular swelling from the sublingual gland, cured by removing that gland. A cervical rib is a hard supraclavicular lump with thoracic outlet symptoms: arm paraesthesia from pressure on the lower trunk of the brachial plexus, and coldness, claudication, aneurysm or emboli from compression of the subclavian artery. It is excised if it causes symptoms. A lipoma is soft and lobulated with a slipping edge, commonly in the posterior triangle and nape, and sebaceous and epidermoid cysts lie in the skin with a punctum. Finally, a carotid aneurysm , or an ectatic, tortuous carotid in an elderly hypertensive patient, can present as a pulsatile lump."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s6-2","Thyroid swellings","Approach to a lump in the neck · Other masses","A thyroid swelling rises on swallowing, because the ligament of Berry binds each lobe to the cricoid and upper trachea; a thyroglossal cyst also rises on tongue protrusion, which the thyroid does not. The isthmus lies in the midline over the second to fourth tracheal rings, but each lobe lies beside the trachea, so a nodule in one lobe or an asymmetric goitre presents low in the neck to one side rather than in the midline. Three pictures cover most patients. A diffuse goitre follows iodine deficiency, goitrogens, puberty and pregnancy, inherited defects of hormone synthesis ( Pendred syndrome , with sensorineural deafness) and autoimmunity: Graves disease gives a diffuse vascular goitre with a bruit, Hashimoto thyroiditis a firm, rubbery, symmetrical goitre, and rapid painless enlargement in Hashimoto suggests lymphoma. A multinodular goitre evolves slowly and may become toxic; sudden painful enlargement means haemorrhage into a nodule, and about 5 to 10% harbour a carcinoma. A solitary nodule is usually benign, but carcinoma accounts for about 5 to 15%. Ask about pressure: cough, choking, positional or nocturnal breathlessness, stridor and dysphagia. A goitre may extend behind the sternum, and superior vena caval obstruction then gives a positive Pemberton sign : raising the arms brings on facial plethora, distended neck veins and breathlessness. Features raising concern for thyroid cancer Age under 20 to 30 or over 60, and male sex. Childhood neck irradiation, or a family history of thyroid cancer or MEN 2. Rapid growth or pain. A hard, fixed nodule, or a nodule over 4 cm"],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s7-1","Neck lumps in children","Approach to a lump in the neck · Children","The congenital masses are described in Congenital neck masses and the malignancies in Head and neck cancer in children . Most neck lumps in children are reactive lymph nodes, which are common, small, mobile and settle within weeks. Congenital lesions follow: thyroglossal duct cysts in the midline, moving with tongue protrusion; branchial cysts, sinuses and fistulae , most from the second arch along the anterior border of sternocleidomastoid; dermoid cysts ; lymphatic malformations (cystic hygroma), which are soft, transilluminate and may respond to sclerotherapy; and haemangiomas . Infection gives acute suppurative lymphadenitis and abscess, and non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection gives a violaceous, painless node in a toddler, often in the submandibular region, which is best treated by excision when feasible. Malignancy is uncommon but must be considered. Features that call for urgent assessment are a node larger than 2 cm or growing, a hard, fixed or matted node, a supraclavicular node, nodes persisting beyond six weeks, and systemic symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss, bruising, pallor, hepatosplenomegaly). Lymphoma is the commonest head and neck malignancy in children, followed by rhabdomyosarcoma, neuroblastoma and thyroid cancer. Ultrasound is the first test, avoiding radiation; a chest X-ray excludes a mediastinal mass before any anaesthetic, and an excision biopsy is often needed for diagnosis."],["ev","cl:t-neckmass~c8","Approach to a lump in the neck: current evidence","Approach to a lump in the neck · Pathway","Ultrasound-guided core biopsy diagnosed malignancy in head and neck lesions with 96% accuracy and gave a specific diagnosis in 87%, outperforming fine-needle aspiration, with very rare needle-track seeding (Novoa et al., Head Neck 2012; Shah et al., Br J Oral Maxillofac Surg 2016). For squamous carcinoma of unknown primary, transoral tongue base mucosectomy or lingual tonsillectomy found the primary in about two-thirds of patients in whom examination, imaging and panendoscopy had found nothing (Fu et al., J Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2016). After chemoradiotherapy for nodal disease, PET-CT surveillance gave the same survival as planned neck dissection with far fewer operations (Mehanna et al., N Engl J Med 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~c8","Pathway","Approach to a lump in the neck · Pathway","Lump to diagnosis. The adult neck lump 1 Clinical picture acute, tender, with infection antibiotics and review in two to three weeks moves with swallowing TSH, ultrasound and FNA (the thyroid pathway) any red flag or persisting over three weeks step 2 2 Full head and neck examination flexible endoscopy, skin, oral cavity, tonsils and tongue base obvious primary biopsy it and stage 3 Ultrasound with FNA or core squamous carcinoma CT or MRI, PET-CT, panendoscopy, tonsillectomy and tongue-base mucosectomy if no primary lymphoma suspected core biopsy or planned excision for flow cytometry thyroid cytology Bethesda or Thy grade, thyroid multidisciplinary team tuberculosis culture, treat 4 Still no diagnosis repeat core biopsy open biopsy planned for possible neck dissection, with frozen section"],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s9-1","Types and indications","Approach to a lump in the neck · Neck dissection","Neck dissection removes the lymph nodes and fat of the neck en bloc. A radical dissection removes levels I to V with the accessory nerve, internal jugular vein and sternomastoid; a modified radical dissection removes the same levels while preserving one or more of those structures; a selective dissection removes only the levels at risk, such as the supraomohyoid dissection (I to III) for the oral cavity and the lateral dissection (II to IV) for the larynx and pharynx; and an extended dissection removes additional nodes or structures. A therapeutic dissection treats clinically positive nodes, while an elective dissection treats a clinically negative neck when the risk of occult metastases exceeds about 15 to 20% ( Head and neck cancer: biology, staging and principles ). Figure 5 Contents of the anterior and posterior triangles. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s10-1","Viva essentials","Approach to a lump in the neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for A systematic approach: age, duration and site. Node levels and drainage; Virchow node. The work-up of an adult neck mass: full examination and endoscopy, then ultrasound and FNA, then imaging, then panendoscopy. Why open biopsy is avoided. Unknown primary: PET-CT before panendoscopy, tonsillectomy and tongue-base mucosectomy, p16 and EBV. Tuberculous lymphadenitis: stages, diagnosis and treatment. Lymphoma: needs tissue for typing. Neck lumps in children and the features that call for biopsy."],["sec","cl:t-neckmass~s10-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to a lump in the neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling a cystic level II mass in an adult a branchial cyst. Excision biopsy of a node before examining the upper aerodigestive tract. Incising a tuberculous or atypical mycobacterial abscess, which leaves a chronic sinus. Missing the nasopharynx in a patient with posterior triangle nodes and a unilateral effusion."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s1-1","The conditions","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Overview","Foreign bodies, mostly in children. Rhinoliths: stones formed around a nucleus in the nose. Nasal myiasis: maggots in the nose. Nasal synechiae: adhesions, often the aftermath of injury or surgery."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s1-2","The dictum","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Overview","If a child presents with unilateral, foul-smelling nasal discharge, a foreign body must be excluded. Other causes of one-sided bloodstained discharge in a child are rhinolith, nasal diphtheria, myiasis, sinusitis and, rarely, a tumour."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s2-1","Types","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Foreign bodies","Inorganic: beads, buttons, pebbles, chalk, pieces of plastic and toys; non-irritant, may stay silent for a long time. Organic: seeds, peas, beans, nuts, paper and sponge; they absorb water and swell, and cause early inflammation. Dangerous: button (disc) batteries leak alkali and produce electrical currents that destroy mucosa within hours, causing septal perforation; paired magnets on either side of the septum cause pressure necrosis. Iatrogenic: cotton pledgets or swabs left behind."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s2-2","Clinical picture","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Foreign bodies","A history of insertion if it was seen; otherwise one-sided obstruction and foul, sometimes bloodstained discharge with excoriation of the nostril. The object often lies on the floor or between the septum and inferior turbinate, buried in granulation and discharge."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s2-3","Investigations","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Foreign bodies","Usually none. A radiograph helps with metallic or radio-opaque objects and button batteries (a double ring on the radiograph)."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s2-4","Removal","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Foreign bodies","Soft objects (paper, cotton): forceps under good light. Round, smooth objects: a blunt hook or a Eustachian catheter passed beyond the object and drawn forwards along the floor; never forceps, which push it deeper. Positive pressure: in a cooperative child, a parent blowing into the mouth while the clear nostril is blocked ( mother's kiss ), or a bag-valve device. Under general anaesthesia: for uncooperative children and posterior or impacted objects, with a cuffed tube, the head extended (Rose's position) and a pack in the nasopharynx to prevent inhalation; an endoscope helps to find and remove it. Afterwards, look at both nostrils and the ears, because children often place more than one object. Button batteries Remove immediately: liquefactive necrosis begins within hours. Do not instil drops or water, which speed the electrical burn. Paired magnets across the septum also need urgent removal. Check afterwards for septal necrosis and perforation."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s2-5","Complications","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Foreign bodies","Rhinitis and sinusitis, epistaxis, septal perforation, rhinolith formation, and inhalation into the airway during removal."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s3-1","Formation","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Rhinolith","Slow deposition of calcium and magnesium salts around a nucleus: a small foreign body ( exogenous ), or a blood clot or dried secretions ( endogenous ). Over years it grows into a large, irregular mass that can erode the septum or lateral wall."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s3-2","Clinical picture","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Rhinolith","More common in adults: one-sided obstruction with a foul, often bloodstained discharge, sometimes epistaxis or neuralgic pain. A grey-brown or greenish-black, irregular, stony-hard and brittle mass lies between the septum and turbinates, often surrounded by granulation. A radiograph or CT shows the calcified mass."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s3-3","Treatment","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Rhinolith","Removal under general anaesthesia, usually through the nostril, breaking large stones into pieces; rarely a lateral rhinotomy for very large, hard stones."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s4-1","How it happens","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Nasal myiasis","Flies, especially Chrysomya species, are attracted by the foul discharge of atrophic rhinitis , syphilis, leprosy, rhinosporidiosis or infected wounds. They lay about 200 eggs at a time, which hatch into larvae within a day. In India it is seen mostly from August to October."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s4-2","Clinical picture","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Nasal myiasis","For the first three or four days: intense irritation, sneezing, watering eyes, headache and a thin bloodstained discharge, often presenting as epistaxis. The lids and lips swell; then maggots crawl out of the nose, and the patient smells foul. They burrow and destroy the nose, sinuses, face, palate and eye, form fistulae, and can cause meningitis and death. Figure 1 First, nasal myiasis: swelling of the nose and lids with bloodstained discharge, destruction of the cheek and eye in a neglected case, and a palatal perforation (arrow). Second, the maggot and the fly that lays it. Dhingra, figs 29.1 and 29.2"],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s4-3","Treatment","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Nasal myiasis","Pick out every visible maggot with forceps; they retreat from light into dark recesses. Instil chloroform water (or turpentine, or ivermectin) to kill or suffocate them, and remove them over several sessions. Warm saline douches to clear slough and dead maggots; systemic ivermectin and antibiotics. Isolate the patient under a net, away from flies. Endoscopy and imaging to find hidden maggots, and treat the underlying disease."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s5-1","Cause","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Nasal synechiae","Scar bands between the septum and lateral wall where two raw mucosal surfaces touch: after septal or sinus surgery, polypectomy, removal of foreign bodies, reduction of fractures, cautery, packing, or ulcerating infections."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s5-2","Effects","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Nasal synechiae","Nasal obstruction, and blocked sinus drainage with sinusitis, headache and discharge. Figure 2 A synechia in the left nasal cavity. Dhingra, fig. 29.3"],["ev","cl:t-nfb~s5-3","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis: current evidence","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Nasal synechiae","Positive pressure first. A systematic review of the \"mother's kiss\" technique found it successful in about 60% of children, with no adverse effects reported, making it a sensible first attempt in a cooperative child with an occlusive object (Cook et al., CMAJ 2012)."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s5-3","Treatment","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · Nasal synechiae","Divide the adhesion and keep the raw surfaces apart with a thin silastic sheet (splint) until healed, about one to three weeks. Prevention: avoid opposing raw areas and use splints after septal and turbinate surgery."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s6-1","Viva essentials","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The dictum: one-sided foul discharge in a child. Removal technique for round objects, and why not forceps. Button battery as an emergency. Rhinolith: formation and appearance. Myiasis: predisposing diseases and treatment."],["sec","cl:t-nfb~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Nasal foreign bodies, rhinoliths and myiasis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating one-sided discharge in a child with antibiotics without looking. Grasping a bead with forceps and pushing it backwards. Putting drops into a nose with a button battery."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s1-1","Tumours of the nasopharynx","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Overview","Tumours of the nasopharynx Benign Malignant Juvenile angiofibroma (the commonest benign tumour) Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (the commonest malignancy) Antrochoanal polyp, squamous papilloma Lymphoma (mostly B-cell non-Hodgkin) Thornwaldt cyst Rhabdomyosarcoma (children) Pleomorphic adenoma, craniopharyngioma Chordoma, plasmacytoma Paraganglioma, hamartoma Adenoid cystic and other salivary carcinomas Hairy polyp, teratoma (congenital) Melanoma, haemangiopericytoma Angiofibroma is covered in Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma ; this topic is about carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s1-2","Epidemiology","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Overview","The highest incidence in the world is in southern China, Hong Kong and Taiwan, with high rates in South-East Asia, North Africa, the Mediterranean and among the Inuit and Aleut peoples. It is uncommon in India except in the North-East. Chinese born in North America have a lower risk than those born in China but still higher than other Americans, showing the interplay of genes and environment. Males outnumber females about three to one; the peak is in the fifth and sixth decades, but about one in five patients is under thirty, and these younger patients usually have undifferentiated tumours."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s1-3","Aetiology","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Overview","Genetic: HLA haplotypes confer susceptibility; the risk persists after migration. Epstein–Barr virus: EBV DNA and EBV-encoded RNA are found in virtually every tumour cell of the non-keratinising types; clonal EBV in premalignant lesions suggests infection is an early, possibly initiating, event. Environmental: nitrosamines in salted, preserved fish eaten from childhood, with a diet low in vitamin C; smoke from burning incense and wood; tobacco, opium and occupational fumes. Tobacco and alcohol: linked to the keratinising (sporadic) type."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s2-1","WHO classification","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Pathology","WHO types of nasopharyngeal carcinoma Type Description North American frequency EBV and radiosensitivity I Keratinising squamous cell carcinoma About 25% Weaker association; least radiosensitive II Non-keratinising, differentiated About 12% Strong association III Non-keratinising, undifferentiated (lymphoepithelioma; Regaud and Schmincke types) About 63% Strongest association; most radiosensitive The current WHO scheme groups types II and III as non-keratinising carcinoma (differentiated and undifferentiated). Under the electron microscope all are squamous; immunohistochemistry for epithelial (cytokeratin) and lymphoid markers separates undifferentiated carcinoma from lymphoma. In endemic areas the non-keratinising types predominate even more; non-keratinising, EBV-associated carcinoma is the commonest type everywhere, and EBV is shown in tissue by EBER in-situ hybridisation."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s2-2","Gross forms","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Pathology","Proliferative (a polypoid mass causing nasal obstruction), ulcerative (bleeding), and infiltrative (submucosal, easily missed)."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s3-1","Local","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Spread","The commonest site of origin is the fossa of Rosenmüller in the lateral wall. Forwards: into the choanae and nasal cavity. Laterally: through the sinus of Morgagni into the parapharyngeal space and infratemporal fossa; into the pterygoid muscles (trismus). Upwards: through the foramen lacerum and foramen ovale into the middle cranial fossa and cavernous sinus (cranial nerves III, IV, V and VI); the sixth nerve is usually the first affected; into the sphenoid sinus and orbit (proptosis, optic nerve). Backwards: to the jugular foramen (IX, X, XI) and hypoglossal canal (XII), and the cervical sympathetic chain (Horner syndrome), directly or through enlarged retropharyngeal nodes. Downwards: to the oropharynx. Figure 1 Routes of spread of nasopharyngeal carcinoma and the clinical features they produce. Dhingra, fig. 49.9 Figure 2 The lateral wall of the nasopharynx with the torus tubarius and fossa of Rosenmüller, and an endoscopic view. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-npc~s3-2","Lymphatic","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Spread","The nasopharynx is rich in lymphatics and nodal spread is early and often bilateral: to the lateral retropharyngeal node (of Rouvière), the upper jugular (level II) nodes high under the mastoid, and the spinal accessory chain in the posterior triangle (level V). Retropharyngeal nodes cause neck stiffness and press on the lower cranial nerves."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s3-3","Distant","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Spread","Bone, lung and liver. Nasopharyngeal carcinoma has the highest rate of distant metastasis of any head and neck cancer: present in about 4% at diagnosis, and developing in about a quarter after IMRT (a quarter to a third in older series)."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s4-1","Cervical nodes","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Clinical features","The commonest presentation (in 60 to 90%): a painless lump between the angle of the jaw and the mastoid, or in the posterior triangle. Nodes are present in about three-quarters at diagnosis, half of them bilateral; often the node is the only complaint."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s4-2","Ear","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Clinical features","Eustachian tube obstruction causes conductive hearing loss, aural fullness and otitis media with effusion ; tinnitus; rarely tumour grows up the tube. Unilateral effusion in an adult An adult with fluid behind one drum and no obvious cause has a nasopharyngeal examination, and a biopsy if anything is seen. Inserting a grommet without looking at the nasopharynx misses cancers."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s4-3","Nose","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Clinical features","Nasal obstruction, bloodstained discharge or epistaxis, and hyponasal speech."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s4-4","Eye and nerves","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Clinical features","Diplopia and squint from a sixth nerve palsy; ophthalmoplegia (III, IV, VI); facial pain and numbness with loss of the corneal reflex (V); proptosis and visual loss; the jugular foramen syndrome (IX, X, XI); deviation of the tongue (XII); Horner syndrome; headache from skull base erosion. Trotter's triad Conductive deafness from tubal blockage, ipsilateral temporoparietal neuralgia from the fifth nerve, and immobility of the soft palate on the same side from the vagus: the classic signs of a tumour invading the lateral nasopharynx and parapharyngeal space."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s4-5","Others","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Clinical features","Trismus, neck pain from retropharyngeal nodes, weight loss, and the effects of distant metastases. Presenting features in order of frequency Feature Notes Neck mass Most common; often the only symptom Hearing loss and aural fullness Effusion from tubal obstruction Nasal obstruction and epistaxis Bloodstained post-nasal discharge Cranial nerve palsy Sixth nerve most often Headache, earache, neck pain Skull base and retropharyngeal involvement"],["sec","cl:t-npc~s5-1","Endoscopy and biopsy","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Diagnosis and staging","Rigid or flexible nasendoscopy under local anaesthesia shows a proliferative, ulcerative or submucosal lesion, and allows biopsy. If nodes suggest the diagnosis but nothing is seen, take biopsies from the fossa of Rosenmüller and posterior wall under general anaesthesia, sometimes through a transpalatal exposure. Fine-needle aspiration of a neck node, with EBV testing of the cells, points to a nasopharyngeal primary."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s5-2","Imaging","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Diagnosis and staging","MRI: the best study for soft tissue extent, the parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal spaces, perineural spread, skull base marrow, intracranial extension and nodes. CT: bone erosion of the skull base, clivus and pterygoid plates. PET-CT: distant metastases and detection of residual or recurrent disease after treatment; chest and abdominal imaging if PET is unavailable."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s5-3","Other tests","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Diagnosis and staging","A baseline audiogram (radiation and cisplatin both damage hearing), dental assessment before radiotherapy, and EBV serology : IgA antibodies to the early antigen are highly specific and those to the viral capsid antigen more sensitive, so both are used; plasma EBV DNA load is now used for screening high-risk populations, for risk stratification and to monitor for recurrence, although assays still need standardisation."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s5-4","Staging","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Diagnosis and staging","TNM staging of nasopharyngeal carcinoma (AJCC) Category Definition T1 Confined to the nasopharynx, or extending to the oropharynx or nasal cavity without parapharyngeal involvement T2 Parapharyngeal extension, or involvement of the medial or lateral pterygoid or prevertebral muscles T3 Invasion of bony structures of the skull base, cervical vertebrae or pterygoid structures, or the paranasal sinuses T4 Intracranial extension, cranial nerves, hypopharynx, orbit, parotid, or soft tissue beyond the lateral pterygoid N1 Unilateral cervical, or unilateral or bilateral retropharyngeal, nodes 6 cm or less, above the caudal border of the cricoid N2 Bilateral cervical nodes 6 cm or less, above the caudal border of the cricoid N3 Node over 6 cm, or extension below the caudal border of the cricoid Older AJCC editions defined T1 as confined to the nasopharynx and N3b as any node in the supraclavicular fossa (Ho's triangle, bounded by the medial and lateral ends of the clavicle and the point where neck meets shoulder). In every edition the nodal staging differs from other head and neck sites: low neck nodes carry the worst prognosis whatever their size. Figure 3 Ho's triangle (the supraclavicular fossa): the medial (A) and lateral (B) ends of the clavicle and the point where the neck meets the shoulder (C). Dhingra, fig. 49.10"],["sec","cl:t-npc~s6-1","Primary treatment","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Treatment","The nasopharynx is hard to reach surgically, and these tumours are radiosensitive, so radiotherapy is the treatment of choice . Stage I: radiotherapy alone to the nasopharynx and both sides of the neck, about 70 Gy. Stage II to IVA: concurrent chemoradiation with cisplatin, which improves survival over radiotherapy alone; the cumulative cisplatin dose matters more than the schedule, with about 200 mg/m² as the effective threshold (160 mg/m² after induction). Induction gemcitabine and cisplatin before chemoradiotherapy improves survival in high-risk patients, and adjuvant metronomic capecitabine improves failure-free survival in high-risk disease. Intensity-modulated radiotherapy (IMRT) delivers a high dose to the tumour while sparing the brainstem, spinal cord, optic apparatus, temporal lobes, cochleae and parotids. Proton and carbon-ion therapy have dosimetric advantages without proven better outcomes yet. Metastatic disease: platinum-based chemotherapy, now with immunotherapy, and local treatment in selected cases."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s6-2","Residual and recurrent disease","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Treatment","Neck: persistent or recurrent nodes after radiation are treated by neck dissection. Older texts advise a radical dissection because disease is often more extensive than it seems; current practice is a comprehensive (modified radical) dissection, sacrificing structures only where tumour involves them. Nasopharynx: after restaging with MRI and PET-CT, resectable recurrences are treated by nasopharyngectomy ; in a randomised trial from China, endoscopic nasopharyngectomy gave better overall survival and fewer severe late effects than re-irradiation with IMRT (Liu et al., Lancet Oncol 2021). Approaches are endoscopic for limited central tumours, or open through a maxillary swing, transpalatal, Le Fort I, midfacial degloving or infratemporal fossa approach. Extension around the internal carotid, intracranially or widely into the parapharyngeal space contraindicates surgery. Re-irradiation: IMRT, proton therapy or brachytherapy (interstitial implants, gold grains), at greater risk of necrosis of the skull base and temporal lobe, cranial nerve palsies and carotid rupture."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s6-3","Effects of treatment","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Treatment","Xerostomia and dental caries, otitis media with effusion and sensorineural hearing loss, trismus, hypothyroidism and pituitary failure, osteoradionecrosis of the skull base, temporal lobe necrosis, cranial neuropathy, carotid stenosis, and second cancers. Grommets in irradiated ears often discharge chronically."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s6-4","Outcome","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Treatment","With concurrent chemoradiation, local and regional control is about 70 to 80% and five-year survival about 70 to 80%; distant metastasis is now the commonest mode of failure."],["ev","cl:t-npc~s6-5","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma: current evidence","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Treatment","In a phase 3 trial of 480 patients with locoregionally advanced disease, induction gemcitabine and cisplatin before chemoradiotherapy improved three-year recurrence-free survival (85.3% vs 76.5%) and overall survival (Zhang et al., N Engl J Med 2019). Adjuvant metronomic capecitabine for a year after chemoradiotherapy improved failure-free survival in high-risk patients (Chen et al., Lancet 2021). Screening 20,174 men in Hong Kong with plasma EBV DNA detected nasopharyngeal carcinoma earlier, with a sensitivity of 97.1% and specificity of 98.6% (Chan et al., N Engl J Med 2017)."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s6-5","Follow-up","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Treatment","Every three to four months for two years, six-monthly to five years, then yearly, with yearly CT or MRI for five years."],["sec","cl:t-npc~c7","Other malignancies","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · Other malignancies","Not all are carcinoma. Lymphoma: a bulky submucosal mass, usually B-cell non-Hodgkin, often with disease elsewhere in Waldeyer's ring; sinonasal NK/T-cell lymphoma in Asian patients. Rhabdomyosarcoma: the embryonal type in children, as a polypoid mass with rapid growth and cranial nerve involvement; chemotherapy and radiotherapy. Chordoma: from notochordal remnants in the clivus, destroying bone and presenting in the nasopharynx; surgery and proton therapy. Plasmacytoma, adenoid cystic carcinoma and melanoma are rare."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s8-1","Viva essentials","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Aetiology: EBV, genetics and salted fish. WHO types and their link to EBV and radiosensitivity. Routes of spread and the cranial nerves involved; Trotter's triad. Unilateral effusion in an adult. Investigations: endoscopy and biopsy, MRI, EBV serology and DNA. Treatment: radiotherapy, concurrent chemoradiation, salvage surgery."],["sec","cl:t-npc~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Inserting a grommet in an adult with a one-sided effusion without examining the nasopharynx. Excising a high level II neck node before endoscopy and needle aspiration. Missing a sixth nerve palsy as the sign of skull base invasion."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s1-1","Functions","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Overview","Respiration. Air conditioning: filtering, warming and humidifying. Protection of the lower airway. Vocal resonance. Nasal reflexes. Olfaction."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s1-2","Why it matters","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Overview","Understanding normal function explains the symptoms of nasal disease: dryness and crusting after excessive turbinate surgery, hypoxia with nasal packs, sinusitis when cilia fail, and loss of smell with polyps."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s2-1","Nose breathing","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Respiration","Breathing through the nose is the natural route; mouth breathing is learned. A newborn with bilateral choanal atresia can asphyxiate ( Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses ). The nose also lets us breathe and eat at the same time."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s2-2","The airflow","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Respiration","In quiet breathing, inspired air passes mainly through the middle of the nose, between the turbinates and septum; little goes through the inferior meatus or the olfactory cleft, which is why weak odours must be sniffed. On expiration, friction at the limen nasi creates eddies beneath the turbinates that ventilate the sinuses. Figure 1 Nasal airflow during (A) inspiration and (B) expiration. Dhingra, fig. 24.1"],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s2-3","The nasal valve and turbinates","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Respiration","The swelling and shrinking of the anterior end of the inferior turbinate regulate inflow; the nasal valve is the narrowest point and the main resistance."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s2-4","The nasal cycle","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Respiration","Rhythmic alternating congestion and decongestion of the two sides every 2.5 to 4 hours; total airflow stays constant because the open side compensates."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s2-5","Measuring the airway","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Respiration","Rhinomanometry measures pressure and flow to give nasal resistance; acoustic rhinometry uses reflected sound to map the cross-sectional area along the nose; peak nasal inspiratory flow is a simple bedside measure."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s3-1","Filtration","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Air conditioning","Vibrissae at the entrance catch large particles; the mucus traps particles down to about 0.5 µm; smaller particles pass on to the lungs."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s3-2","Temperature","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Air conditioning","The vascular turbinate and septal mucosa, with its cavernous sinusoids, acts as a radiator: air at 0°C or below reaches nearly body temperature by the time it reaches the nasopharynx, about a quarter of a second later; hot air is cooled."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s3-3","Humidification","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Air conditioning","Relative humidity is raised to 75% or more, using about a litre of water a day from the mucosal glands. At 50% relative humidity cilia stop working within minutes, so dry air predisposes to infection."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s3-4","The lungs depend on it","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Air conditioning","Nasal obstruction alters gas exchange: packing both sides lowers oxygen tension (the nasopulmonary reflex ), and long-standing obstruction from adenotonsillar hypertrophy can cause pulmonary hypertension in children."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s4-1","The mucous blanket","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Protection","Goblet cells and glands secrete a two-layer blanket: thick mucus on top, a thin serous layer beneath. Cilia beat 10 to 20 times a second in the serous layer, with a fast effective stroke that reaches the mucus and a slow recovery stroke within the serous layer, moving the blanket in one direction towards the nasopharynx at 5 to 10 mm a minute. The whole sheet is cleared every 10 to 20 minutes and swallowed. About 600 to 700 mL of secretion is made each day. Figure 2 The conveyor belt of the mucous blanket, carrying trapped organisms and dust to the nasopharynx. Dhingra, fig. 24.2"],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s4-2","Sinus clearance","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Protection","In each sinus, cilia sweep mucus along fixed paths towards the natural ostium, even if a second opening has been made; this is why endoscopic surgery enlarges natural ostia. Figure 3 Mucociliary clearance of the sinuses: (A) maxillary, (B) frontal, (C) ethmoid and sphenoid. Dhingra, fig. 35.4"],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s4-3","What harms the cilia","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Protection","Drying, heat or cold, smoking, infection, noxious gases, some drugs (including topical adrenaline and preservatives), and a change in pH from the normal of about 7. In primary ciliary dyskinesia the cilia are immotile, causing chronic rhinosinusitis, otitis media and bronchiectasis."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s4-4","Chemical defences","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Protection","Lysozyme (muramidase), secretory IgA, IgE and interferon in the secretions."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s4-5","Sneezing","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Protection","A protective reflex that expels irritants, followed by a flood of secretions to wash them out. The saccharin test measures mucociliary transit: a particle placed on the inferior turbinate is normally tasted within about 20 minutes."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s5-1","Vocal resonance","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Resonance and reflexes","The nasal consonants M, N and NG pass through the nose. A blocked nose or nasopharynx causes hyponasal (denasal) speech, so M, N and NG sound like B, D and G; velopharyngeal insufficiency causes the opposite, hypernasal speech."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s5-2","Reflexes","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Resonance and reflexes","Sneezing; salivary and gastric secretion at the smell of food; and nasobronchial and nasopulmonary reflexes linking nasal obstruction with bronchial resistance and lower oxygen levels."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s6-1","The olfactory pathway","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Olfaction","The olfactory epithelium lies high in the nose, over the cribriform plate, the upper septum and the superior turbinate. Bipolar receptor neurons carry cilia into the mucus; their axons gather into olfactory nerve bundles that pass through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb, then along the olfactory tract to the piriform cortex, amygdala and entorhinal cortex. Olfactory receptor neurons regenerate throughout life. Figure 4 The vestibule, respiratory and olfactory regions of the nasal cavity. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s6-2","Smell and flavour","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Olfaction","Taste (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami) comes from taste buds supplied by the seventh, ninth and tenth nerves; flavour is taste plus smell, so patients who lose smell often complain that food has no taste. Ammonia is not used to test smell, because it stimulates the trigeminal nerve."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s6-3","Terms","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Olfaction","Anosmia: total loss of smell; hyposmia: partial loss. Parosmia: a smell present but perceived wrongly, often as unpleasant; common during recovery from post-viral loss, from misdirected regeneration. Phantosmia: smelling an odour that is not there; intracranial causes (tumour, temporal lobe epilepsy) must be excluded. Ageusia, hypogeusia, dysgeusia: absent, reduced and distorted taste."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s6-4","Epidemiology","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Olfaction","Smell declines with age: most people over 80 have impaired smell and about half are anosmic. Smoking and poor nutrition add to the risk."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s7-1","Conductive loss","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Disorders of smell","Odorants cannot reach the olfactory cleft: Chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps (the commonest treatable cause; Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps ), allergic and viral rhinitis. Septal deviation, turbinate hypertrophy, tumours. Atrophic rhinitis."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s7-2","Sensorineural loss","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Disorders of smell","Damage to the receptors or the pathway: Post-viral: after a cold or influenza (parainfluenza 3 especially), and COVID-19, often sudden. Head injury: up to 15% of significant head injuries, from shearing of olfactory fibres at the cribriform plate or frontal contusion; usually anosmia, and only about 10% recover. Tumours: olfactory groove meningioma, olfactory neuroblastoma ( Sinonasal tumours ), frontal lobe tumours. Neurodegenerative disease: Parkinson's and Alzheimer's disease, where smell loss may be the first sign. Congenital: Kallmann syndrome (anosmia with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, from failed migration of olfactory and gonadotropin-releasing neurons). Toxins, drugs, radiation, endocrine and metabolic disease: smoking, hypothyroidism, diabetes, zinc or B12 deficiency."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s8-1","History","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Assessment","Timing points to the cause: sudden after a cold or injury, gradual or fluctuating with sinus disease and allergy, lifelong in congenital loss. Separate true taste loss from flavour loss."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s8-2","Examination","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Assessment","Head and neck and neurological examination, including cognition and mood. Endoscopy of the olfactory cleft and middle meatus for polyps and inflammation; avoid topical anaesthetic before testing, as it blunts smell temporarily."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s8-3","Smell testing","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Assessment","Bedside: familiar odours (coffee, clove, peppermint, lemon) to each nostril with the eyes closed; the alcohol sniff test. Sniffin' Sticks: odour pens testing threshold, discrimination and identification. University of Pennsylvania Smell Identification Test: a 40-item scratch-and-sniff test scored out of 40."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s8-4","Imaging","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Assessment","CT of the sinuses for inflammatory disease; MRI of the anterior cranial fossa and olfactory bulbs when the cause is unclear, after trauma, or with phantosmia or neurological signs."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s9-1","Conductive loss","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Management","Treat the inflammation: topical steroids (drops in the head-down position reach the cleft better), a short course of oral steroids to test reversibility, and endoscopic sinus surgery for polyps."],["ev","cl:t-nphys~s9-2","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell: current evidence","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Management","Nasal airflow and sensation. The feeling of a clear nose depends on trigeminal cool receptors (TRPM8) sensing airflow, not only on the size of the airway, which explains why menthol makes the nose feel clearer and why radical turbinate surgery can cause empty nose syndrome , assessed with the ENS6Q questionnaire. Nitric oxide from the sinuses is antiviral and improves ventilation–perfusion matching; it is low in primary ciliary dyskinesia, where nasal nitric oxide is a screening test. Smell. Olfactory loss predicts Parkinson's disease and dementia (Whitcroft et al., Rhinology 2023)."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s9-2","Sensorineural loss","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · Management","Olfactory training , sniffing a set of strong odours twice a day for months, improves recovery after post-viral and post-traumatic loss. Counsel about safety: smoke and gas alarms, food dates, and personal hygiene. Pearls A patient who says food has lost its taste usually has lost smell. Parosmia during recovery is often a good sign. Unexplained phantosmia or smell loss with neurological signs needs MRI."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s10-1","Viva essentials","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Functions of the nose, with numbers: humidity, temperature, mucus speed. The mucociliary blanket and what damages it. The nasal cycle. The olfactory pathway and why ammonia is not used. Causes of anosmia, conductive and sensorineural. Kallmann syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-nphys~s10-2","Common pitfalls","Nasal physiology and disorders of smell · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling smell loss a taste disorder. Missing polyps as a treatable cause of anosmia. Forgetting imaging in unexplained phantosmia."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s1-1","Definition","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Overview","Inflammation of the skin of the external auditory canal, bacterial, fungal or reactive. The canal is a blind, warm, dark tube lined by thin skin over bone and cartilage, and anything that breaks its defences (moisture, scratching, loss of wax) lets infection in."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s1-2","Forms","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Overview","Otitis externa Bacterial Acute diffuse otitis externa furuncle (localised) Fungal Otomycosis Aspergillus, Candida Reactive Eczematous, seborrhoeic contact dermatitis from drops or moulds, neurodermatitis Chronic Chronic otitis externa stenosis of the canal Invasive Necrotising (malignant) otitis externa skull base osteomyelitis"],["sec","cl:t-oe~s1-3","Applied anatomy","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Overview","The outer third of the canal is cartilaginous, with hair follicles and sebaceous and ceruminous glands; the inner two-thirds is bony with thin, gland-free skin. Infection spreads from the canal through the fissures of Santorini in the cartilage and the tympanomastoid suture to the parotid, the skull base and the temporomandibular joint. Figure 1 Skin of the cartilaginous canal, with its hair follicles and sebaceous and ceruminous glands. Dhingra, fig. 8.12 Figure 2 Fissures of Santorini and the foramen of Huschke, routes from the canal to the parotid and skull base. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oe~s2-1","Causes","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Acute diffuse otitis externa","Moisture (swimming, humid climates), trauma from cotton buds and fingernails, hearing aids and earbuds, eczema and loss of protective wax. Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus are the usual organisms."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s2-2","Clinical picture","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Acute diffuse otitis externa","2.2.1 Symptoms Pain that develops over a day or two, itching, a feeling of blockage and a scanty discharge. 2.2.2 Signs Pain on moving the tragus or pinna; a red, swollen canal with debris; tender nodes in front of or behind the ear. When visible, the drum moves normally on pneumatic otoscopy, unlike otitis media."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s2-3","Management","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Acute diffuse otitis externa","2.3.1 Aural toilet Clean the canal under vision by suction or dry mopping. Debris collects in the anteroinferior recess, so look there. 2.3.2 Wick If the canal is swollen shut, insert an expanding wick soaked in antibiotic-steroid drops, or in an astringent such as aluminium acetate 8% or silver nitrate 3%. Where the books differ · Changing the wick Dhingra Daily for two to three days. Lee Every three to five days. In practice a compressed sponge wick usually falls out as the swelling settles, and is reviewed or replaced at about two to three days. 2.3.3 Drops Antibiotic-steroid drops for a week to ten days. Acidifying with dilute vinegar helps mild cases and prevention. 2.3.4 Oral antibiotics Not needed, unless there is spreading cellulitis or the patient is diabetic or immunocompromised. 2.3.5 Keep it dry No swimming until healed; cotton wool with petroleum jelly when bathing. When drops fail Think of contact allergy to neomycin, fungal superinfection, or an underlying necrotising infection."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s3-1","Picture","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Furuncle","A staphylococcal infection of a hair follicle, so it occurs only in the hair-bearing outer cartilaginous canal. Pain is out of proportion to its size and worse on chewing; it can swell the postauricular groove and mimic mastoiditis."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s3-2","Distinguishing from mastoiditis","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Furuncle","See also Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis . The drum is normal, there is no preceding otitis media and no mucoid discharge, and pinna movement is painful."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s3-3","Treatment","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Furuncle","Analgesia, oral antistaphylococcal antibiotics, heat, and an ichthammol-glycerine pack; incise when it points. Recurrent furuncles call for testing for diabetes and treating nasal staphylococcal carriage."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s4-1","Organisms","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Otomycosis","Aspergillus niger (black-headed spores), Aspergillus fumigatus (pale blue or green) and Candida (creamy white). Repeated antibiotic drops, humidity and swimming predispose."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s4-2","Picture","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Otomycosis","Intense itching, discomfort, blockage and a watery discharge with a musty smell. The canal contains a mass like wet newspaper , with black, grey or white spores. Figure 3 Endoscopic view of otomycosis with yellow (A) and black (B) fungal spores. Dhingra, fig. 8.11"],["sec","cl:t-oe~s4-3","Treatment","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Otomycosis","Meticulous cleaning, then a topical antifungal, continued for a week beyond apparent cure: clotrimazole for most, nystatin for Candida . Salicylic acid in alcohol and povidone iodine are alternatives."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s5-1","Chronic otitis externa","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Chronic and reactive otitis externa","Long-standing itch and irritation with thick, dry, scaling skin, which may narrow the canal. Treat underlying eczema or seborrhoeic dermatitis, use steroid drops or ointment, and stop the patient scratching."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s5-2","Reactive forms","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Chronic and reactive otitis externa","Eczematous otitis externa from drops or hearing-aid moulds, seborrhoeic dermatitis with scalp involvement, and neurodermatitis from habitual scratching."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s5-3","Otitis externa haemorrhagica","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Chronic and reactive otitis externa","Blood blisters on the deep canal and drum, often with influenza (bullous myringitis, Myringitis )."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s5-4","Chronic stenosing otitis externa","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Chronic and reactive otitis externa","Fibrous narrowing of the deep canal ( Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis ). Treated by excising the thick skin, widening the bony canal and grafting."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s6-1","What it is","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Necrotising otitis externa","An invasive infection that spreads from the canal floor through the fissures of Santorini into the infratemporal fossa and the bone of the skull base: a skull base osteomyelitis. Most patients are older and diabetic, but about 1 in 10 has no obvious immunosuppression. Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the usual organism (about 60% of cultures in recent series, with rising resistance); fungi such as Aspergillus grow in a significant minority, more often in HIV with low CD4 counts, and some cultures grow nothing."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s6-2","Clinical picture","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Necrotising otitis externa","6.2.1 Symptoms Severe, deep, unrelenting pain, worse at night and out of proportion to what is seen, with a persistent discharge despite treatment. 6.2.2 Signs Granulation tissue on the floor of the canal at the bony-cartilaginous junction. Cranial nerve palsies mark spread: the facial nerve first at the stylomastoid foramen, then the ninth, tenth and eleventh at the jugular foramen, and the twelfth. Multiple palsies carry a worse prognosis."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s6-3","Investigations","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Necrotising otitis externa","Which scan? Test Use Limitation Technetium-99 bone scan Confirms early bone involvement Stays positive for months Gallium or indium scan Follows response; repeated every few weeks Less anatomical detail CT Bone erosion Changes appear late MRI Soft tissue, marrow and dura Not a guide to cure CT shows erosion only after about 30% demineralisation, so a normal early CT is falsely reassuring. Contrast MRI with fat suppression and diffusion shows soft tissue and marrow disease earlier and detects intracranial spread; FDG PET-CT is increasingly used to diagnose and monitor. CT and MRI changes persist for up to a year after cure, so they cannot decide when to stop. Add ESR or CRP (useful to follow), glucose and HbA1c, and send both a swab and a granulation biopsy for bacterial and fungal culture and histology, because squamous carcinoma of the temporal bone ( Middle ear and temporal bone tumours ) is the key differential."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s6-4","Treatment","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Necrotising otitis externa","Admit for multidisciplinary care (otology, diabetes, microbiology, radiology, elderly care). Control the diabetes, clean the canal regularly, and start empirical intravenous antipseudomonal treatment at once, often dual therapy (piperacillin-tazobactam or ceftazidime with a fluoroquinolone), adjusted to cultures; oral ciprofloxacin alone suits only early, limited disease; voriconazole for Aspergillus . There is no agreed duration, but consensus favours at least six weeks , often completed at home, guided by symptoms, inflammatory markers and imaging. Surgery is limited to biopsy, deep sampling (for example at cortical mastoidectomy when there is no tissue in the canal) and drainage of abscesses or sequestra; debridement and nerve decompression have no proven benefit, and hyperbaric oxygen is unproven. Where the books differ · Monitoring and duration Dhingra Gallium repeated every three weeks; treatment for six to eight weeks, including intravenous aminoglycoside combinations. Lee Gallium every four weeks; oral fluoroquinolone for about six weeks as first line. In practice There is no agreed regimen; most units follow the treatment above for at least six weeks, guided by pain, CRP and MRI or PET-CT, and use gallium scanning less."],["ev","cl:t-oe~s6-5","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis: current evidence","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Necrotising otitis externa","For acute otitis externa, the American guideline recommends topical drops as first-line treatment and advises against oral antibiotics unless infection spreads beyond the canal or the patient is at risk (Rosenfeld et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2014), supported by a Cochrane review (Kaushik et al., 2010). For necrotising otitis externa, a systematic review of 70 studies (2,274 patients, mostly retrospective) found 84% were diabetic, Pseudomonas grew in 62%, 14% grew no organism, and there was no evidence for the best imaging, regimen or duration (median 7.2 weeks) (Takata et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2023). Cure rates have fallen and culture-negative cases risen over time (Byun et al., Otol Neurotol 2020). A UK multidisciplinary guideline identifies high-risk patients for early intensive treatment (Hopkins et al., J Laryngol Otol 2020)."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s6-5","Outcome","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · Necrotising otitis externa","Mortality remains around 5 to 20%, from sepsis, sinus thrombosis and cranial neuropathies. Quinolone resistance is rising. Suspect it when A diabetic or immunocompromised patient has severe pain and granulation in the canal. Otitis externa fails to respond after a week of proper treatment. A cranial nerve palsy appears with ear pain."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s7-1","On call","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · For the examination","A painful, swollen ear canal Clean under vision; insert a wick if the canal is closed. Antibiotic-steroid drops; analgesia; keep dry. Check glucose in diabetics and anyone with recurrent infection. Diabetic or immunocompromised with severe pain and granulation: biopsy, ESR, imaging and admit."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s7-2","Viva essentials","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Tragal tenderness and a mobile drum versus otitis media. Furuncle in the cartilaginous canal only. Otomycosis: organisms and appearance. Necrotising otitis externa: who, organism, signs, scans and treatment."],["sec","cl:t-oe~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing oral antibiotics instead of cleaning and drops. Missing a necrotising infection in a diabetic. Neomycin contact allergy mistaken for treatment failure. Drops into a canal full of debris."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s1-1","Course and narrowings","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Applied anatomy","The oesophagus is about 25 cm long. It runs from the cricopharyngeus at C6 to the cardia at T11, deviating slightly to the left in the neck and again in the lower chest. Along the way it has three normal constrictions, measured at endoscopy from the upper incisors: The pharyngo-oesophageal junction at C6, about 15 cm . The crossing of the aortic arch and left main bronchus at T4, about 25 cm . The diaphragmatic hiatus at T10, about 40 cm , with the cardia at about 40 to 42 cm. These narrowings are where foreign bodies lodge, where caustic burns are worst and where instruments perforate, so their distances are worth knowing by heart. Figure 1 First, the oesophagus and its normal constrictions, with distances from the upper incisors. Second, the oesophagus with its sphincters and the change from striated to smooth muscle. Dhingra, figs 67.1 and 67.2"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s1-2","Wall and supply","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Applied anatomy","The wall has a mucosa of non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium, a submucosa carrying Meissner's plexus, and inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle with Auerbach's (myenteric) plexus between them. Outside there is only adventitia. The absence of a serosa is the reason leaks spread freely through the mediastinum and anastomoses are fragile. The muscle is striated in the upper third, mixed in the middle third and smooth in the lower third, which explains why diseases of skeletal muscle affect the top and scleroderma the bottom. The upper third is supplied by the inferior thyroid artery, the middle by branches of the aorta, and the lower by the left gastric and inferior phrenic arteries. The lower oesophagus is a portosystemic anastomosis and the site of varices . Lymph drains to deep cervical, mediastinal and coeliac nodes, and a long submucosal lymphatic network allows skip lesions in cancer. The nerve supply is from the vagus and the sympathetic chain. Figure 2 Constrictions of the oesophagus and their distances from the incisors, and the layers and innervation of the oesophagus. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s1-3","Sphincters","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Applied anatomy","The upper oesophageal sphincter is mainly the cricopharyngeus , a zone 3 to 5 cm long that stays tonically closed at rest to stop air entering the oesophagus and refluxate entering the pharynx. On swallowing it relaxes and is pulled open by elevation of the larynx. Above it, between the thyropharyngeus and the cricopharyngeus, lies Killian's dehiscence , the site of the pharyngeal pouch; below it, between the cricopharyngeus and the oesophageal muscle, lies Laimer's triangle . The lower oesophageal sphincter is a physiological high-pressure zone rather than an anatomical sphincter. It is helped by the \"pinch-cock\" of the diaphragmatic crura, the acute angle of His , the mucosal folds, and the positive intra-abdominal pressure acting on the abdominal segment. Figure 3 Killian and Laimer triangles, and Killian dehiscence with a pharyngeal pouch. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s2-1","The phases","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Swallowing","The oral phase is voluntary: food is chewed, mixed with saliva, formed into a bolus and pushed back by the tongue against the palate. It ends when the bolus touches the faucial pillars and triggers the reflex swallow. The pharyngeal phase is a reflex lasting under a second, a sequence of protective closures and propulsion. The soft palate rises against Passavant's ridge to close the nasopharynx, and the tongue presses against the palate to close the mouth. Breathing stops and the larynx closes from below upwards, at the true folds, then the false folds, then the arytenoids against the epiglottis. The larynx rises and moves forward under the tongue base, which tips the epiglottis down and pulls the upper sphincter open ; laryngeal elevation is the single most important factor in opening it. The constrictors then contract in sequence while the cricopharyngeus relaxes. In the oesophageal phase , peristalsis carries the bolus to the stomach in about 3 to 8 seconds, with the lower sphincter relaxing ahead of it."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s2-2","Peristalsis","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Swallowing","Primary peristalsis is the wave started by a swallow. Secondary peristalsis is set off locally by distension and clears what the first wave left behind. Tertiary contractions are non-propulsive and uncoordinated; they appear in the elderly ( presbyoesophagus ) and in spasm. Figure 4 Passavant ridge, and the pharyngeal constrictors. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s3-1","Terms","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Approach to dysphagia","Dysphagia is difficulty swallowing. Odynophagia is painful swallowing and suggests ulceration, inflammation or infection. Globus is a persistent feeling of a lump in the throat, worst between meals, with no true difficulty in swallowing food."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s3-2","Oropharyngeal versus oesophageal","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Approach to dysphagia","Two kinds of dysphagia Feature Oropharyngeal (pre-oesophageal) Oesophageal Complaint Difficulty starting a swallow; food sticks in the neck Food sticks behind the sternum (though it may be felt in the neck) Associated Coughing or choking on swallowing, nasal regurgitation, drooling, dysarthria, a wet voice Regurgitation, heartburn, chest pain, weight loss Typical causes Stroke, Parkinson disease, motor neurone disease, myasthenia, myositis, cranial nerve palsies, head and neck cancer and its treatment, pharyngeal pouch, cricopharyngeal dysfunction Stricture, cancer, achalasia, spasm, eosinophilic oesophagitis, rings and webs, extrinsic compression First test Examination, flexible endoscopy, videofluoroscopy or FEES Endoscopy (upper GI), barium swallow, manometry"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s3-3","Causes by site","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Approach to dysphagia","It helps to think of the tube from the inside out. In the lumen lies a foreign body or food bolus ( Swallowed foreign bodies ). In the wall are inflammation (reflux, infection, caustic, eosinophilic, radiation or pill oesophagitis), strictures, webs and rings, tumours, and motility disorders. Outside the wall are the pharyngeal pouch, goitre and thyroid tumours, cervical osteophytes in diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis, mediastinal nodes and tumours, an enlarged left atrium, aortic aneurysm, and vascular rings causing dysphagia lusoria . Figure 5 (A and B) Large cervical osteophytes pressing on the oesophagus on barium swallow. Dhingra, fig. 69.1"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s3-4","History","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Approach to dysphagia","The tempo points to the cause. Sudden dysphagia means a foreign body or bolus, often stuck on an existing stricture or tumour; progression over weeks to months means cancer; intermittent symptoms suggest a ring or spasm; and long-standing dysphagia suggests achalasia or a web. The consistency separates mechanical from motor disease. Solids first and liquids later indicate a mechanical obstruction such as a stricture or cancer, whereas liquids as bad as or worse than solids from the start indicate a motility disorder such as achalasia, or a neuromuscular cause. Other clues complete the picture. Night-time regurgitation of undigested food with gurgling and halitosis suggests a pharyngeal pouch; heartburn suggests reflux and a peptic stricture; nasal regurgitation suggests palatal weakness; and coughing on swallowing means aspiration. Odynophagia suggests candida or herpes in the immunosuppressed, a pill or a caustic. Weight loss, hoarseness or a neck mass suggests cancer, and recurrent bolus impaction in a young atopic man suggests eosinophilic oesophagitis."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s3-5","Examination and tests","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Approach to dysphagia","Examine the mouth and oropharynx, and look at the larynx and hypopharynx with a flexible endoscope for pooling in the pyriform sinuses and for cord movement; then examine the neck, the cranial nerves and the chest. A blood count and iron studies look for iron deficiency and Plummer–Vinson syndrome. The barium swallow shows pouches, strictures, webs, rings, achalasia, spasm and extrinsic compression. Videofluoroscopy , the modified barium swallow, studies the oral and pharyngeal phases with different consistencies and demonstrates aspiration, and flexible endoscopic evaluation of swallowing (FEES) shows residue, penetration and aspiration at the bedside. A chest film, a lateral soft-tissue neck film and CT are used for masses. Endoscopy , either oesophagogastroduodenoscopy or transnasal oesophagoscopy, sees and biopsies the mucosa, and every new oesophageal dysphagia in an adult needs it. For function, high-resolution manometry is the test for motility disorders, classified by the Chicago classification , and 24-hour pH and impedance monitoring quantifies reflux."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s4-1","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Inflammation and injury","Reflux disease is a failure of the barrier, through weakness or transient relaxation of the lower sphincter, hiatus hernia, obesity, pregnancy, smoking, alcohol, fatty meals, scleroderma, and drugs that relax smooth muscle such as calcium-channel blockers, nitrates and anticholinergics. Its typical symptoms are heartburn and regurgitation. For the ENT surgeon the extra-oesophageal effects matter as much. Laryngopharyngeal reflux ( Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections ) causes hoarseness, throat clearing, globus and chronic cough, with posterior laryngitis, contact ulcers and granulomas; reflux also contributes to subglottic stenosis, asthma, dental erosion and, in children, otitis media with effusion. The complications are erosive oesophagitis, peptic stricture and Barrett's oesophagus , in which columnar intestinal metaplasia replaces the squamous lining of the distal oesophagus. It appears as salmon-pink mucosa, is premalignant for adenocarcinoma , and needs endoscopic surveillance. Treatment starts with lifestyle change: weight loss, raising the head of the bed, no meals in the three hours before bed, stopping smoking, and less alcohol, caffeine, chocolate and fat. Proton pump inhibitors, H2 blockers and alginates follow, and antireflux surgery such as a Nissen fundoplication is reserved for refractory disease."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s4-2","Infective oesophagitis","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Inflammation and injury","Infection occurs in the immunosuppressed, after chemotherapy or steroids, in HIV and in diabetes, and presents with odynophagia. Candida produces white plaques and is treated with fluconazole; herpes simplex produces small punched-out ulcers and is treated with aciclovir; and cytomegalovirus produces large shallow ulcers and is treated with ganciclovir."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s4-3","Eosinophilic oesophagitis","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Inflammation and injury","Eosinophilic oesophagitis typically affects young atopic men with asthma, eczema or food allergy, and presents with dysphagia and recurrent food bolus impaction . Endoscopy shows rings (\"trachealisation\"), furrows and white exudates, and biopsy shows 15 or more eosinophils per high-power field . It is treated with a PPI, swallowed topical steroids such as budesonide or fluticasone, elimination diets and careful dilatation."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s4-4","Pill and radiation oesophagitis","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Inflammation and injury","Tetracyclines and doxycycline, potassium chloride, iron, NSAIDs and bisphosphonates can lodge at the aortic narrowing and ulcerate it, especially when taken at bedtime without water. Radiation causes acute ulceration and, later, stricture and dysmotility."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s4-5","Caustic (corrosive) injury","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Inflammation and injury","Two patterns are seen: a toddler who swallows a household product by accident, usually in small amounts with less severe injury, and an adolescent or adult who swallows a large amount on purpose, with severe injury. Alkalis such as drain cleaner and lye cause liquefactive necrosis and penetrate deeply into the oesophagus. Acids cause coagulative necrosis , which limits their penetration, and damage the stomach more, leading to pyloric stenosis or gastric perforation. The injury evolves in four stages: Acute necrosis in the first days. Sloughing and granulation over two to three weeks, when the wall is weakest and perforation most likely. Fibrosis and stricture from about the third week, over months. A lifelong increased risk of squamous carcinoma , decades later. Assessment begins with the airway: stridor, hoarseness and drooling mean laryngeal injury, which may need intubation or tracheostomy. Burns of the lips and mouth are unreliable, because their absence does not exclude oesophageal injury. Look for perforation, mediastinitis or peritonitis, and take chest and abdominal films, with CT if perforation is suspected. Endoscopy is then done gently within 12 to 48 hours, going no further than a severe circumferential burn, and graded by Zargar's system: grade 0 normal, 1 oedema and erythema, 2a superficial ulcers and exudates, 2b deep or circumferential ulcers, 3a focal necrosis and 3b extensive necrosis. Grades 0 to 2a heal without stricture; grade 2b and above often stricture. Management follows a sequence: Resuscitate and protect the airway. No induced vomiting, no neutra"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s4-6","Perforation","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Inflammation and injury","Instrumentation is the commonest cause of perforation: rigid oesophagoscopy, dilatation and the removal of foreign bodies, usually just above or at the cricopharyngeus at Killian's dehiscence, or in the distal oesophagus. Foreign bodies, trauma and malignancy are other causes, and Boerhaave syndrome is a spontaneous full-thickness rupture after vomiting, usually of the left lower oesophagus. It must be distinguished from the Mallory–Weiss tear, a mucosal tear at the gastro-oesophageal junction after retching that presents with haematemesis and is not a perforation. A cervical perforation causes neck pain, dysphagia, fever, tenderness and surgical emphysema in the neck. A thoracic perforation causes severe chest or interscapular pain, fever, tachycardia and shock, with surgical emphysema, Hamman's sign (a mediastinal crunch with each heartbeat), pneumothorax and pleural effusion. Mackler's triad of vomiting, chest pain and subcutaneous emphysema is the classic picture of Boerhaave syndrome. Neck and chest films show air in the prevertebral space, mediastinum or pleura, and a water-soluble contrast swallow and CT confirm the leak. Every patient is kept nil by mouth on intravenous fluids, broad-spectrum antibiotics and antifungals, with nutritional support. Small contained cervical perforations are often managed conservatively, with cervical drainage if an abscess forms. Thoracic perforations need early surgical repair, ideally within about 24 hours, with drainage, or endoscopic stents and clips in selected cases; late perforations need drainage, diversion or resection. Pain a"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s5-1","Achalasia","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Motility disorders","Achalasia results from loss of the inhibitory neurons of the myenteric (Auerbach's) plexus , producing aperistalsis and failure of the lower sphincter to relax, usually with a high resting pressure. A secondary form occurs in Chagas disease from Trypanosoma cruzi , and pseudoachalasia is produced by a cancer at the cardia, which must always be excluded by endoscopy, especially in an older patient with rapid weight loss. The patient has dysphagia to both solids and liquids from the outset, regurgitates undigested food especially at night with cough and aspiration, has chest pain, and loses weight slowly. The barium swallow shows a dilated oesophagus with a fluid level tapering smoothly to a \" bird's beak \" at the cardia, and a chest film may show a widened mediastinum and an absent gastric bubble. Manometry is the gold standard, and the Chicago classification divides achalasia into types I to III, which predicts the response to treatment. The options are pneumatic dilatation , laparoscopic Heller myotomy with a partial fundoplication, and peroral endoscopic myotomy (POEM) . Botulinum toxin injected into the sphincter gives temporary relief in the frail, and calcium-channel blockers and nitrates have only a weak effect. The long-term risk of squamous carcinoma is increased."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s5-2","Spastic disorders","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Motility disorders","Distal (diffuse) oesophageal spasm produces premature, non-propulsive contractions with normal relaxation of the lower sphincter. It causes intermittent dysphagia and chest pain that mimics angina, and the barium swallow shows a \" corkscrew \" or \" rosary bead \" oesophagus. It is treated with nitrates, calcium-channel blockers, botulinum toxin and sometimes myotomy. The hypercontractile (\"nutcracker\" or \"jackhammer\") oesophagus has very strong contractions that remain peristaltic, and causes chest pain."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s5-3","Systemic causes","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Motility disorders","In scleroderma (systemic sclerosis), smooth-muscle atrophy and fibrosis give aperistalsis of the lower two-thirds and a weak lower sphincter , with severe reflux, strictures and Barrett's oesophagus, as part of the CREST syndrome. Polymyositis and dermatomyositis affect the striated upper oesophagus, and myasthenia gravis, motor neurone disease, diabetes, hypothyroidism and presbyoesophagus also disturb motility."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s5-4","Cricopharyngeal dysfunction","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Motility disorders","Here the upper sphincter fails to relax or open, from fibrosis of the muscle, neurological disease such as stroke, Parkinson disease, motor neurone disease or muscular dystrophy, or reflux. Videofluoroscopy shows a posterior cricopharyngeal bar , though a bar is also seen in up to a third of asymptomatic elderly people, so it must be interpreted with the symptoms. It is treated by dilatation, botulinum toxin injection or cricopharyngeal myotomy , endoscopic or external; myotomy is avoided in severe reflux."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s6-1","Plummer–Vinson (Paterson–Brown-Kelly) syndrome","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Structural disorders","This syndrome affects middle-aged women and combines iron-deficiency anaemia with dysphagia from a postcricoid web , glossitis, angular stomatitis, koilonychia, atrophic mucosa and sometimes achlorhydria. It carries an increased risk of postcricoid ( Carcinoma of the hypopharynx ) and oesophageal carcinoma, about 10% in the older literature. It is treated with iron, with B12 and B6 if deficient, dilatation of the web, and surveillance."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s6-2","Schatzki ring","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Structural disorders","A Schatzki ring is a thin mucosal ring at the squamocolumnar junction, often with a small hiatus hernia, in people over 50. It causes intermittent dysphagia to solids and, once the lumen is under 13 mm, the \"steakhouse syndrome\" of bolus impaction. It is treated by dilatation and a PPI."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s6-3","Diverticula","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Structural disorders","The pharyngeal (Zenker's) pouch is a pulsion diverticulum through Killian's dehiscence and is covered in Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch . A mid-oesophageal traction diverticulum is pulled out by inflamed mediastinal nodes, classically tuberculous, and an epiphrenic diverticulum is a pulsion diverticulum just above the lower sphincter, usually secondary to a motility disorder."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s6-4","Hiatus hernia","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Structural disorders","In the sliding hernia, about 90% of the total, the gastro-oesophageal junction slides up into the chest, and reflux is common. In the para-oesophageal (rolling) hernia the junction stays below the diaphragm while the fundus rolls up beside it; reflux is slight, but the hernia can bleed, obstruct or strangulate, and repair is recommended."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s6-5","Strictures","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Structural disorders","Benign strictures may be peptic, caustic, radiation-induced, anastomotic after surgery, pill-induced, the result of a foreign body, eosinophilic or congenital. Every stricture is biopsied to exclude cancer. Treatment is dilatation with bougies, often over a guidewire, or balloons, together with treatment of the cause; stents or surgery are reserved for refractory cases."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s6-6","Congenital anomalies","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Structural disorders","Oesophageal atresia with tracheo-oesophageal fistula is commonest (about 85%) as proximal atresia with a distal fistula . The baby drools, chokes and becomes cyanosed at the first feed, the abdomen distends, and a nasogastric tube coils in the upper pouch at 9 to 13 cm. It is associated with the VACTERL anomalies. The H-type fistula (about 4%) presents later with coughing on feeds and recurrent pneumonia. Vascular rings and an aberrant right subclavian artery cause dysphagia lusoria, and the latter brings a non-recurrent right laryngeal nerve that is at risk in thyroid surgery."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s7-1","Benign tumours","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Tumours","The leiomyoma accounts for about two-thirds of benign tumours. It is a smooth, intramural submucosal mass that causes dysphagia only when large and is best seen with endoscopic ultrasound; mucosal biopsy is avoided, because it scars the plane needed for enucleation. Fibrovascular polyps of the upper oesophagus can be regurgitated into the mouth and obstruct the larynx, and papillomas, lipomas, haemangiomas and granular cell tumours also occur."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s7-2","Oesophageal cancer","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Tumours","Squamous cell carcinoma is the commonest type worldwide and in India, and arises mostly in the middle and upper oesophagus. Its risk factors are tobacco, smoked and chewed, and alcohol; very hot drinks; dietary nitrosamines and poor diet; achalasia ; caustic strictures ; Plummer–Vinson syndrome; tylosis (palmoplantar keratoderma); and a previous head and neck cancer through field change. Adenocarcinoma is the commonest type in Western countries, arises in the distal oesophagus and cardia, and develops in Barrett's oesophagus with reflux and obesity. The tumour spreads directly into the trachea, bronchi, aorta, pericardium and recurrent laryngeal nerves; through lymphatics to cervical, mediastinal and coeliac nodes, including skip lesions; and through the blood to liver, lung and bone. The cardinal symptoms are progressive dysphagia, first to solids and then to liquids , and weight loss . Odynophagia and back pain suggest spread outside the wall, hoarseness means recurrent nerve involvement, and coughing on swallowing means a tracheo-oesophageal fistula . A sudden bolus obstruction may be the first sign. Diagnosis is by endoscopy and biopsy. The barium swallow shows an irregular \" apple-core \" narrowing with shouldering, and staging uses CT of chest and abdomen, PET-CT and endoscopic ultrasound for depth and nodes, with bronchoscopy for tumours near the airway. Early tumours are resected endoscopically. Resectable locally advanced disease is treated with neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (the CROSS regimen) or perioperative chemotherapy followed by oesophagectomy; definitive che"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s8-1","Rigid oesophagoscopy","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Oesophagoscopy","Rigid oesophagoscopy is still the instrument for sharp or impacted foreign bodies, for biopsy of cricopharyngeal and upper oesophageal lesions, for dilatation, for endoscopic pouch surgery and for panendoscopy in head and neck cancer. It is contraindicated by cervical spine disease or injury, trismus, a receding jaw, aortic aneurysm and severe systemic illness. General anaesthesia with the tube to the left of the mouth. Supine with the neck flexed and the head extended (the \"sniffing\" position), so the mouth, pharynx and oesophagus align. Protect the upper teeth. Pass the scope down the right side of the tongue to the arytenoids, then behind the cricoid in the midline. Open the cricopharyngeus by gentle, sustained lifting , never force. This is where perforation happens. Keep the lumen in view at all times. Aortic pulsation marks the 25 cm level. The red, velvety gastric mucosa marks the cardia. Inspect again on withdrawal. The complications are injury to the teeth, lips and arytenoids, tracheal compression in children, and perforation , which is why pain, fever and emphysema after the procedure are taken seriously."],["ev","cl:t-oesoph~s8-2","Oesophagus and dysphagia: current evidence","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Oesophagoscopy","Eosinophilic oesophagitis. Biopsies are taken from at least two levels; budesonide orodispersible tablets are a swallowed steroid option, and dupilumab improved histology and dysphagia in a phase 3 trial (Dellon et al., NEJM 2022). Barrett's oesophagus. The British Society of Gastroenterology guideline recommends endoscopic surveillance according to length and dysplasia, and endoscopic resection and ablation for dysplasia (Fitzgerald et al., Gut 2014). Caustic ingestion. Endoscopy within 12 to 48 hours grades the injury (Zargar classification) and predicts stricture; CT is increasingly used to decide who needs surgery. Food bolus. Most impactions pass; endoscopy within 24 hours (sooner if the patient cannot swallow saliva), and do not use carbonated drinks or glucagon in place of it."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s8-2","Flexible and transnasal oesophagoscopy","Oesophagus and dysphagia · Oesophagoscopy","Flexible oesophagoscopy is done under topical anaesthesia or sedation in the left lateral position through a mouth guard. It sees the oesophagus, stomach and duodenum; allows biopsy, dilatation, banding, stents and removal of soft foreign bodies; is safe in neck and jaw disease; and has largely replaced the rigid scope for diagnosis. Transnasal oesophagoscopy is an unsedated office examination with a thin scope passed through the nose, used for dysphagia, reflux and Barrett's screening, the search for a second primary, and tracheo-oesophageal puncture after laryngectomy. Figure 6 Flexible oesophagoscopy in the left lateral position, through a mouth guard, viewing on a monitor. Dhingra, fig. 93.1 Rigid versus flexible oesophagoscopy Feature Rigid Flexible Anaesthesia General Topical with or without sedation Neck and jaw disease Difficult or contraindicated Possible Reach Oesophagus Oesophagus, stomach, duodenum Foreign bodies Best for sharp and impacted objects; can sheath sharp points Soft, blunt objects; needs an overtube for sharp ones Biopsy of the upper sphincter region Excellent Can miss the postcricoid area Setting Inpatient theatre Day case or office"],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s9-1","Viva essentials","Oesophagus and dysphagia · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three constrictions and their distances; Killian dehiscence. The three phases of swallowing and the role of laryngeal elevation. Oropharyngeal versus oesophageal dysphagia; solids versus liquids; the investigation sequence. Achalasia: bird's beak, manometry, pseudoachalasia; dilatation, Heller myotomy, POEM. Plummer–Vinson syndrome and postcricoid cancer. Caustic injury: alkali versus acid, the stages, grading, and the stricture and cancer risk. Perforation: causes and signs; Boerhaave syndrome. Barrett oesophagus and adenocarcinoma; risk factors for squamous carcinoma. Rigid oesophagoscopy: position, steps, complications."],["sec","cl:t-oesoph~s9-2","Common pitfalls","Oesophagus and dysphagia · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling progressive dysphagia in an older patient \"globus\" without endoscopy. Forcing a rigid scope through the cricopharyngeus. Missing perforation in a patient with pain after endoscopy. Treating achalasia without excluding a cancer at the cardia. Inducing vomiting or neutralising after a caustic ingestion."],["sec","cl:t-om~s1-1","Definitions","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Overview","1.1.1 Acute otitis media Rapid onset, within 48 hours, of middle ear inflammation with an effusion: pain, fever and a red, bulging drum. 1.1.2 Recurrent acute otitis media Three or more episodes in six months, or four or more in a year, with the child well in between. 1.1.3 Otitis media with effusion Fluid in the middle ear behind an intact drum, without signs of acute infection. Also called secretory otitis media or glue ear . It is chronic once it has lasted three months. 1.1.4 Acute necrotising otitis media A rare, destructive form, usually from beta-haemolytic streptococcus after measles or scarlet fever, which rapidly destroys the drum, annulus and ossicles."],["sec","cl:t-om~s1-2","Epidemiology","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Overview","Acute otitis media is one of the commonest reasons a young child sees a doctor, peaking between six months and two years. Effusion follows most acute attacks and is the commonest cause of hearing loss in childhood, with peaks around two and again around five years."],["sec","cl:t-om~s1-3","Why it matters","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Overview","Acute disease is about pain and the rare but serious complication. Effusion is about hearing, speech, behaviour and school, in a child who often does not complain at all."],["sec","cl:t-om~s2-1","The Eustachian tube in a child","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Pathogenesis","The child's tube is shorter, wider and more horizontal than the adult's, and its muscles open it less efficiently. Secretions from the nasopharynx reach the middle ear easily, and the middle ear drains poorly. Figure 1 The Eustachian tube with tensor and levator veli palatini, the muscles that open it. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-om~s2-2","The sequence","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Pathogenesis","A viral cold inflames the tube and the adenoid. The tube blocks, gas in the middle ear is absorbed, negative pressure develops and a transudate collects. Bacteria from the nasopharynx, where the adenoid acts as a reservoir, then infect the fluid."],["sec","cl:t-om~s2-3","Organisms","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Pathogenesis","2.3.1 Bacteria Streptococcus pneumoniae leads, followed by non-typeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis . About half of the H. influenzae and almost all Moraxella produce beta-lactamase. 2.3.2 Viruses RSV, rhinovirus and adenovirus are found in many aspirates, alone or with bacteria."],["sec","cl:t-om~s2-4","Risk factors","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Pathogenesis","Who gets otitis media Environment Daycare, siblings smoke exposure, dummy use, bottle feeding lying flat, no breastfeeding Anatomy Cleft palate Down syndrome, craniofacial syndromes Host Prematurity immunodeficiency, low socioeconomic status Adenoid Bacterial reservoir and tubal obstruction"],["sec","cl:t-om~s3-1","Stages","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Acute otitis media","Acute suppurative otitis media runs through a sequence of stages, and the drum changes at each. 3.1.1 Tubal occlusion The drum is retracted with a foreshortened handle of malleus; there is deafness and fullness, and little fever. 3.1.2 Presuppuration Hyperaemia spreads from the handle of malleus and the periphery in a cart-wheel pattern. Pain is throbbing, and a child may have a high fever. 3.1.3 Suppuration Pus collects under pressure: the drum is red and bulging, landmarks vanish, and a yellow spot may mark where it will rupture. Pain is severe. 3.1.4 Resolution The drum ruptures, usually with a small anteroinferior perforation, discharge appears and pain and fever settle. Most perforations heal. 3.1.5 Complication If infection spreads beyond the cleft: mastoiditis , facial palsy, labyrinthitis or intracranial disease ( Intracranial complications of otitis media )."],["sec","cl:t-om~s3-2","Clinical picture","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Acute otitis media","Otalgia, fever, irritability, ear pulling and poor feeding, often after a cold. These symptoms are non-specific on their own; the diagnosis rests on the drum."],["sec","cl:t-om~s3-3","Diagnosis","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Acute otitis media","Pneumatic otoscopy is the essential examination. A bulging drum with poor mobility makes the diagnosis. A red drum in a crying child is not enough, because crying flushes the drum. Tympanometry helps when the view is poor. Look for a complication first Facial weakness, vertigo, mastoid tenderness or swelling, neck stiffness, drowsiness. A child with a cochlear implant, immunodeficiency or a septic appearance is treated, not observed."],["sec","cl:t-om~s3-4","Management","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Acute otitis media","3.4.1 Pain relief for everyone Paracetamol or ibuprofen, whatever is decided about antibiotics. 3.4.2 Observation or antibiotics Most acute otitis media settles without antibiotics in three days to a week, and antibiotics make only a small difference to pain (about 1 in 20 children benefits at two to three days, while about 1 in 14 gets diarrhoea, rash or vomiting). They do not meaningfully prevent mastoiditis: thousands of children would need treating to prevent one case. So the default is analgesia, safety-netting, and either no antibiotic or a back-up prescription to use if not better in three days or worse at any time. When to give an immediate antibiotic (NICE) Situation Plan Systemically very unwell, or signs of a complication Immediate antibiotic; admit if a complication is suspected Under 3 months with fever Urgent paediatric assessment High risk (immunocompromised, cochlear implant, significant heart, lung, kidney or neuromuscular disease) Immediate antibiotic Under 2 years with infection in both ears Consider immediate antibiotic, or a back-up prescription Any age with otorrhoea (a perforation) Consider immediate antibiotic, or a back-up prescription All others No antibiotic or a back-up prescription; analgesia; review if worse Where the books differ · When to treat NICE (2018): analgesia for all; immediate antibiotics only for the groups above; back-up prescriptions otherwise. American Academy of Pediatrics (2013): antibiotics for severe disease (fever 39°C or more, or moderate to severe pain for over 48 hours) and for non-severe bilateral disease under two; obse"],["sec","cl:t-om~s3-5","Recurrent acute otitis media","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Acute otitis media","Defined above as three or more episodes in six months, or four or more in a year. Reduce risk factors (smoke exposure, dummies, day care where possible), encourage breastfeeding, and check vaccinations (pneumococcal and Hib); children who keep having infections despite vaccination may have poor antibody responses. The options are: Watchful management, treating each episode promptly; many children grow out of it. Grommets, which let infections be treated with antibiotic drops instead of oral antibiotics and may reduce episodes in the first six months; but in the largest randomised trial they did not reduce the number of episodes over two years compared with medical management (Hoberman et al., NEJM 2021). They are the usual choice when effusion persists between attacks. Antibiotic prophylaxis (for example amoxicillin at half dose, or azithromycin three times a week, for two to three months) roughly halves episodes while it is taken, but the effect stops with the drug and resistance is a concern; it is rarely used in the UK. Where the books differ · Recurrent otitis media Dhingra Mentions amoxicillin prophylaxis, while noting its drawbacks and that many prefer grommets; grommets for 4 episodes in 6 months or 6 in a year. Lee Does not recommend prophylaxis; defines recurrence as 3 in 6 months or 4 in 12 months. In practice Prophylaxis is rarely used because of resistance; grommets are offered when effusion persists between attacks, though they do not reduce episodes over two years."],["sec","cl:t-om~s4-1","Pathogenesis","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Otitis media with effusion","Tubal dysfunction leaves negative pressure and fluid behind; the mucosa responds with more goblet cells and mucous glands, and the fluid becomes thick and tenacious, the classic glue . Persistent adenoid infection, allergy and unresolved acute otitis media all contribute."],["sec","cl:t-om~s4-2","Clinical picture","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Otitis media with effusion","4.2.1 Symptoms Often none: a parent or teacher notices inattention, poor speech, or a child turning up the television. Older children and adults describe fullness and muffled hearing. 4.2.2 Signs The drum is dull and opaque, sometimes amber, grey or bluish, with fine radial vessels rather than redness. It is often retracted, and may show an air-fluid level or bubbles. Mobility on pneumatic otoscopy is reduced. Figure 3 Otitis media with effusion: bubbles appear behind the drum on Valsalva. Dhingra, fig. 10.2"],["sec","cl:t-om~s4-3","Investigations","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Otitis media with effusion","4.3.1 Tympanometry A flat (type B) curve, or one shifted far to the negative side (type C). 4.3.2 Audiometry Usually a 20 to 40 dB conductive loss. An audiogram is needed when the effusion has lasted three months, or sooner if there is a speech or developmental concern. 4.3.3 The nasopharynx in adults Unilateral effusion in an adult Always examine the nasopharynx: nasopharyngeal carcinoma can first present this way, especially in smokers and people from endemic regions. Where the books differ · Scoping the nasopharynx Dhingra Always exclude a nasopharyngeal tumour in an adult with one-sided effusion. Lee The value of endoscopy is clearest in high-risk patients. In practice Examine the postnasal space in every adult with a persistent one-sided effusion, and biopsy if anything is abnormal; the threshold is lowest in smokers and people from endemic regions."],["sec","cl:t-om~s4-4","Natural history","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Otitis media with effusion","Most effusions clear on their own: screen-detected effusions clear in about 63% by three months and 88% by a year, and around 90% of effusions after treated acute otitis media resolve. But once a bilateral effusion has lasted three months, only about a quarter clear by six months. It affects 60 to 85% of children with Down syndrome or cleft palate."],["ev","cl:t-om~s4-5","Otitis media: acute and with effusion: current evidence","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Otitis media with effusion","A Cochrane review of 13 trials found that antibiotics did not reduce pain at 24 hours and reduced it at two to three days in only 1 child in 20, while causing side effects in about 1 in 14 (Venekamp et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2015). In a randomised trial of 250 children with recurrent acute otitis media, grommets did not reduce the rate of episodes over two years compared with medical management (1.48 vs 1.56 a year), though the first episode came later (Hoberman et al., N Engl J Med 2021). For effusion, a Cochrane review found grommets improved hearing by about 10 dB in the first months, with the benefit fading by a year (Browning et al., 2010). NICE's 2023 guideline on effusion (NG233) added autoinflation, bone-conduction devices and consideration of adenoidectomy with grommets."],["sec","cl:t-om~s4-5","Management","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Otitis media with effusion","NICE's 2023 guideline (NG233) recommends shared decisions at each step. 4.5.1 Monitoring Children with effusion but no hearing loss need reassurance only. With hearing loss (thresholds worse than 20 dB HL), reassess hearing after three months for bilateral disease, and consider it for unilateral disease. Antibiotics, steroids, antihistamines, decongestants and anti-reflux drugs have no lasting benefit and are not recommended. Where the books differ · Medical treatment of effusion Dhingra Lists decongestants, antihistamines and antibiotics among the medical options. Lee Guidelines find no role for them. In practice Do not use them; follow current guidelines (NICE NG233). 4.5.2 Autoinflation Consider a nasal balloon in children old enough to use it; it can speed clearance. 4.5.3 Hearing aids and devices Air-conduction hearing aids are an equal alternative to surgery when the loss is stable; bone-conduction devices (on a soft band) when the hearing fluctuates or air-conduction aids are unsuitable (narrow canals, otorrhoea). Preferred in many children with Down syndrome. 4.5.4 Grommets Consider grommets for persistent effusion with hearing loss, especially bilateral loss affecting speech, learning, behaviour or quality of life, or in children at risk (speech delay, sensory or learning difficulty, cleft palate, Down syndrome). Discuss the risks: otorrhoea, perforation, atrophy and tympanosclerosis (the operation is described in Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles ). Thick glue may need two incisions, one to let air in behind the fluid. Figure 4 First, two incisions "],["sec","cl:t-om~s5-1","Of acute otitis media","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Complications and sequelae","Complications now follow only 0.05 to 0.25% of episodes. Perforation in about 5%; around 90% heal. Acute mastoiditis (fewer than 0.4% of episodes, mostly under two years) and subperiosteal abscess; intravenous antibiotics alone succeed in about three-quarters. Facial palsy and labyrinthitis. Meningitis and other intracranial complications ( Intracranial complications of otitis media )."],["sec","cl:t-om~s5-2","Of chronic effusion","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Complications and sequelae","Atrophic, retracted drum and atelectasis. Necrosis of the long process of the incus. Tympanosclerosis. Retraction pockets ( Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae ) and cholesteatoma . Cholesterol granuloma."],["sec","cl:t-om~s5-3","Masked disease","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · Complications and sequelae","Inadequate antibiotic courses can leave a smouldering ear that becomes a persistent effusion or masked mastoiditis."],["sec","cl:t-om~s6-1","On call","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · For the examination","A child with a painful ear Look for complications first: face, balance, mastoid, neck and conscious level. Give analgesia to every child. Perform pneumatic otoscopy; a red drum alone is not enough. Decide antibiotics, a back-up prescription or no antibiotic by risk, age, both ears and otorrhoea. Safety-net: return if worse at 48–72 hours, with neck swelling, weakness or drowsiness."],["sec","cl:t-om~s6-2","Viva essentials","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Stages of acute suppurative otitis media and the drum at each stage. Why children: the Eustachian tube and the adenoid. The three bacteria, and beta-lactamase production. Effusion: drum appearance, type B tympanogram, 20–40 dB conductive loss. Indications for grommets, hearing aids as an alternative, and when to add adenoidectomy (NICE 2023). Unilateral effusion in an adult: examine the nasopharynx."],["sec","cl:t-om~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Otitis media: acute and with effusion · For the examination","Common pitfalls Diagnosing otitis media from a red drum in a crying child. Withholding analgesia while deciding about antibiotics. Treating effusion with decongestants and antihistamines. Missing nasopharyngeal carcinoma in an adult with one-sided glue."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s1-1","The term and the lesions","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Potentially malignant disorders","In 2005 the WHO replaced \"precancer\" with oral potentially malignant disorders , because these lesions carry a risk of cancer but do not always precede it. Leukoplakia is a white patch that cannot be characterised as any other definable lesion; it is the commonest, and smoking increases its risk. Erythroplakia , a red velvety patch, is much rarer but carries the highest risk, and most erythroplakias already show severe dysplasia or carcinoma when biopsied. Proliferative verrucous leukoplakia is a multifocal, relentlessly spreading white lesion, typically in older women who do not smoke, with a very high lifetime cancer risk. Oral submucous fibrosis , from areca nut chewing, and oral lichen planus , especially the erosive form, complete the main list; their clinical pictures are described in Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis and Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches . Features that raise the risk of transformation Red or mixed red-and-white lesions (erythroleukoplakia), and proliferative verrucous leukoplakia. Sites on the lateral and ventral tongue and floor of mouth. Multiple, large or long-standing lesions. Lesions in non-smokers and in women. Epithelial dysplasia on biopsy, of which about 5 to 18% transform. Figure 1 First, leukoplakia of the cheek after pan and tobacco use. Second, leukoplakia on the lateral border of the tongue. Dhingra, figs 44.7 and 44.8"],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s1-2","Assessment of a white or red patch","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Potentially malignant disorders","Inspect and palpate the whole mouth, note size, colour, texture and induration, examine the neck, remove irritants (a sharp tooth, a denture), treat candida, and review in two to three weeks. Any lesion that persists is biopsied , taking the reddest, most indurated or most irregular part; histology grades dysplasia as mild, moderate or severe. Adjuncts such as toluidine blue or Lugol's iodine staining, autofluorescence and narrow-band imaging help choose biopsy sites and map margins, but none replaces a biopsy."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s1-3","Management","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Potentially malignant disorders","Stop tobacco, alcohol and areca nut. Moderate and severe dysplasia, erythroplakia and accessible high-risk lesions are excised, usually with a laser or scalpel, and all patients are followed long term, because new lesions and cancers arise elsewhere in the mouth (field change)."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s2-1","Epidemiology and risk factors","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Oral squamous cell carcinoma","More than 90% of oral cancers are squamous cell carcinomas. The main risk factors are tobacco (smoked and chewed), alcohol , which multiplies the risk of tobacco, and betel quid with areca nut , which makes oral cancer one of the commonest cancers in South Asia. HPV plays only a small part, unlike in the oropharynx. An increasing number of young non-smokers, often women, develop tongue cancer without the usual risk factors, and patients with Fanconi anaemia have a very high risk. Figure 2 Leukoplakia where a quid of tobacco and lime was held. Dhingra, fig. 44.9"],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s2-2","Sites and presentation","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Oral squamous cell carcinoma","In Western countries the lateral border of the tongue and the floor of mouth are the commonest intraoral sites (the lip leads only when lip cancers are counted as oral); in South Asia the buccal mucosa and gingivobuccal sulcus, where the quid is held, predominate. Patients present with a non-healing ulcer, a lump, a red or white patch, pain referred to the ear, bleeding, loose teeth or ill-fitting dentures, trismus, numbness of the lip (mental nerve involvement) or a neck lump. Any single ulcer that has not healed in three weeks must be biopsied. Oral cavity subsites and their particular problems Subsite Particular points Oral tongue Commonest intraoral site in the West; depth of invasion drives nodal spread; may cross the midline Floor of mouth Early spread to the mandible and both sides of the neck; can obstruct the submandibular ducts Buccal mucosa and gingivobuccal sulcus Commonest in betel users; spread to skin, mandible, maxilla and infratemporal fossa Lower alveolus and retromolar trigone Early bone invasion; retromolar disease spreads to the pterygoid region Hard palate and upper alveolus Minor salivary tumours are common here; maxillectomy defects need obturators or flaps Lip Usually lower lip, sun-exposed; excellent prognosis; treated by surgery or radiotherapy Figure 3 The subsites of the oral cavity. Dhingra, fig. 42.1 Each subsite, and oropharyngeal cancer, is described in detail in Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s3-1","Investigation","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Staging and investigation","Examination under anaesthesia confirms extent and allows biopsy. MRI shows soft tissue extent, depth, marrow invasion and perineural spread better; CT shows cortical bone erosion; and an orthopantomogram gives dental and mandibular detail. Ultrasound with FNA assesses neck nodes. FDG PET-CT is used for advanced or high-grade disease and low neck nodes, to find distant metastases and second primaries, and for suspected recurrence. Dental assessment and any extractions are done before radiotherapy to reduce osteoradionecrosis."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s3-2","The AJCC eighth edition","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Staging and investigation","The eighth edition added depth of invasion (DOI), measured from the level of the adjacent normal basement membrane to the deepest point of invasion, because it predicts nodal spread better than surface size; it is not the same as tumour thickness, which includes exophytic growth. T staging of oral cavity cancer (AJCC 8th) T Size and depth of invasion T1 2 cm or less, DOI 5 mm or less T2 2 cm or less with DOI over 5 mm, or 2 to 4 cm with DOI 10 mm or less T3 Over 4 cm, or DOI over 10 mm T4a Invades through cortical bone of mandible or maxilla, into the maxillary sinus or the skin of the face T4b Invades masticator space, pterygoid plates or skull base, or encases the internal carotid artery Superficial erosion of a tooth socket by a gingival primary is not enough to make a tumour T4. Extranodal extension now upstages nodal disease, clinically when obvious and pathologically on the specimen."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s4-1","The primary","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Treatment","Surgery followed by risk-based adjuvant therapy gives the best control for operable oral cancer at every stage. The aim is complete excision with a 5 mm margin of normal tissue in three dimensions; on the specimen, margins of 5 mm or more are clear , 1 to 5 mm close , and under 1 mm involved , and positive margins reduce survival. Tumours near the mandible need a marginal mandibulectomy (removing the alveolar rim and keeping continuity) when the bone is abutted or superficially eroded, and a segmental mandibulectomy when tumour invades the medullary bone or the mandible is thin and edentulous. Radiotherapy or brachytherapy may be preferred for some lip and hard palate cancers where surgery would cost too much function. Figure 4 First, carcinoma of the buccal mucosa. Second, carcinoma of the lateral tongue with leukoplakia of the floor of mouth. Dhingra, figs 44.11 and 44.12"],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s4-2","The neck","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Treatment","The neck is treated electively in early oral cancer. A large Indian randomised trial (D'Cruz, 2015) showed that elective neck dissection of levels I to III in clinically node-negative early cancer improved three-year overall survival compared with waiting and operating when nodes appeared (about 80% versus 68%). Elective treatment is therefore standard when DOI is more than about 3 to 4 mm, and is considered at lower depths for high-risk sites. Sentinel node biopsy is an emerging alternative in early disease, with less morbidity. Node-positive necks have a comprehensive dissection of levels I to V (sometimes I to IV), and the opposite side is treated for midline tumours or extensive nodal disease. Figure 5 The lymph node levels of the neck: levels I to III drain the oral cavity first. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s4-3","Reconstruction","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Treatment","Reconstruction restores swallowing, speech, mastication and appearance, replacing like with like. Small defects heal by secondary intention, are grafted or are closed with local flaps; tongue and floor of mouth defects are commonly reconstructed with a radial forearm or anterolateral thigh free flap, and segmental mandibular defects with a fibula or other vascularised bone flap, which can later carry dental implants."],["ev","cl:t-oral_ca~s4-4","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer: current evidence","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Treatment","In 500 patients with node-negative early oral cancer, elective neck dissection improved three-year overall survival compared with watchful waiting and therapeutic dissection (80.0% vs 67.5%) (D'Cruz et al., N Engl J Med 2015). A meta-analysis of three randomised trials (608 patients) found sentinel node biopsy gave survival and neck control similar to elective neck dissection, with better neck and shoulder function (Gupta et al., Oral Oncol 2022). The AJCC eighth edition incorporates depth of invasion into T staging because it predicts nodal spread better than surface size."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s4-4","Adjuvant therapy","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Treatment","Postoperative radiotherapy is given for pT3–T4 tumours, multiple or level IV–V nodes, close margins, perineural invasion and lymphovascular invasion. Concurrent chemoradiotherapy with cisplatin is given for extranodal extension and involved margins , the two features that most predict recurrence. Unresectable T4b disease and patients unfit for surgery receive chemoradiotherapy or palliative treatment; borderline resectable tumours may be downstaged with induction chemotherapy . The suspicious oral lesion 1 Persistent ulcer, red or white patch after removing irritants biopsy the most suspicious area dysplasia stop tobacco, alcohol and areca, excise moderate or severe dysplasia, follow up long term carcinoma step 2 2 Stage MRI or CT of primary and neck, orthopantomogram, chest imaging or PET-CT dental assessment 3 Treat the primary wide excision with 5 mm margins marginal or segmental mandibulectomy by bone invasion free flap reconstruction where needed 4 Treat the neck N0 with DOI over 3 to 4 mm elective neck dissection levels I to III or sentinel node biopsy N+ comprehensive neck dissection 5 Pathology close margins, PNI, LVI, pT3–4, N2+ radiotherapy ENE or involved margin chemoradiotherapy"],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s5-1","Complications","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Complications and follow-up","Haemorrhage, infection (more after reconstruction, in drinkers and with tracheostomy), seroma and chyle leak follow neck surgery; marginal mandibular and accessory nerve weakness are recognised risks. Carotid blowout is rare after primary surgery but feared in irradiated and recurrent necks, especially with salivary fistula or flap necrosis. Radiotherapy causes dry mouth, dental decay, trismus and osteoradionecrosis, so lifelong dental care and jaw exercises matter."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s5-2","Follow-up","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · Complications and follow-up","Patients are reviewed frequently for the first two years, when most recurrences occur, and for at least five years, with imaging as needed. Second primary cancers in the mouth, pharynx, larynx, oesophagus and lung are common in smokers, and stopping tobacco and alcohol is the single most valuable intervention after treatment."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s6-1","Viva essentials","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Potentially malignant disorders: leukoplakia, erythroplakia, proliferative verrucous leukoplakia, submucous fibrosis, and the risk features. Depth of invasion and the AJCC eighth edition T stages. Elective neck dissection in early oral cancer: the D'Cruz trial; sentinel node biopsy. Margins: clear, close and involved. Marginal versus segmental mandibulectomy. Adjuvant therapy: indications for radiotherapy and for chemoradiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-oral_ca~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a persistent ulcer with repeated topical steroids instead of biopsying it. Watching the N0 neck in a tongue cancer with significant depth of invasion. Confusing depth of invasion with tumour thickness. Forgetting dental assessment before radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s1-1","Extent","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Boundaries","The oral cavity runs from the vermilion border of the lips in front to the oropharyngeal isthmus behind: the junction of the hard and soft palate above, the line of the circumvallate papillae below, and the anterior tonsillar pillars and retromolar trigones at the sides. Everything behind this ring (soft palate, tonsils, base of tongue, valleculae) is oropharynx, a distinction that matters because the two regions are staged and treated differently ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer , Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ). Figure 1 The vestibule and the oral cavity proper, and the boundary between the oral cavity and the oropharynx. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s1-2","Subsites","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Boundaries","Figure 2 The subsites of the oral cavity: lip, buccal mucosa, gingiva, retromolar trigone, hard palate, oral tongue and floor of mouth. Dhingra, fig. 42.1 Lips: the mucosal lip, from the vermilion border to the line where the lips meet. Buccal mucosa: the lining of the cheeks and inner lips, back to the pterygomandibular raphe. Upper and lower alveolar ridges (gingivae): the mucosa over the tooth-bearing bone. Retromolar trigone: the triangle of mucosa over the front of the ascending ramus, its base behind the last molar and its apex at the maxillary tuberosity. Hard palate: the roof of the mouth. Oral tongue: the anterior two-thirds, in front of the circumvallate papillae, with its tip, lateral borders, dorsum and undersurface. Floor of mouth: the crescent between the lower gingiva and the undersurface of the tongue, with the frenulum and the sublingual papillae where the submandibular ducts open. Figure 3 The subsites of the oral cavity, and the retromolar trigone with the pterygomandibular raphe. From the anatomy atlas Examining the floor of mouth The anterior floor is seen best when the patient lifts the tongue tip to the palate; the lateral floor when a tongue depressor pushes the tongue to the other side. Always palpate the floor bimanually: small cancers here are felt before they are seen."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s1-3","Functions","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Boundaries","Intake and chewing of food, the oral phase of swallowing, the shaping of speech, and protection against noxious substances through saliva, taste and the gag reflex."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s2-1","Surface anatomy","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · The lips","The vermilion is non-keratinised squamous epithelium without hair or sebaceous glands; its colour comes from the capillaries beneath. The vermilion border marks the change from skin to mucosa; the philtral ridges and Cupid's bow shape the upper lip; the two lips meet at the oral commissures. Figure 4 Lip landmarks, and the arterial supply of the lips from the superior and inferior labial arteries. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s2-2","Muscles","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · The lips","All are supplied by the facial nerve. The orbicularis oris forms the sphincter. At each commissure several muscles meet in a tendinous knot, the modiolus : orbicularis oris, buccinator, levator and depressor anguli oris, zygomaticus major and risorius. The lip elevators (levator labii superioris, levator labii superioris alaeque nasi, zygomaticus minor) and depressors (depressor labii inferioris, mentalis, platysma) complete the set."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s2-3","Nerves, vessels and lymph","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · The lips","Sensation: upper lip from the infraorbital nerve (V2), lower lip from the mental nerve (V3). Arteries: the superior and inferior labial branches of the facial artery. Lymph: the lower lip drains to the submandibular nodes, its midline also to the submental nodes, and it may cross the midline; the upper lip drains to the submandibular, preauricular and infraparotid nodes."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s3-1","The tooth","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Teeth, gums and jaws","Enamel: the hardest outer layer, laid down by ameloblasts. Dentine: beneath it, formed throughout life by odontoblasts, and sensitive. Pulp: the inner core of vessels and nerves. Cementum: covers the root and anchors it through the periodontal ligament to the alveolar bone."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s3-2","Numbers","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Teeth, gums and jaws","Twenty deciduous teeth (no premolars) and thirty-two permanent teeth: in each quadrant two incisors, one canine, two premolars and three molars, the third molar being the wisdom tooth."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s3-3","Gingiva","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Teeth, gums and jaws","The attached gingiva is bound firmly to the alveolar bone; the looser alveolar mucosa lies beyond it. Figure 5 Gingiva and alveolar mucosa. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s3-4","Muscles of mastication","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Teeth, gums and jaws","All supplied by the mandibular nerve (V3): Masseter: from the zygomatic arch to the angle and lateral ramus; elevates the mandible. Temporalis: from the temporal fossa to the coronoid process; elevates and retracts. Lateral pterygoid: from the greater wing of the sphenoid and lateral pterygoid plate to the condyle; opens the mouth, protrudes and moves the jaw from side to side. Medial pterygoid: from the medial side of the lateral pterygoid plate and the maxillary tuberosity to the medial angle; elevates and moves the jaw from side to side. Infiltration of these muscles by infection or cancer produces trismus ."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s4-1","Surface","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · The tongue","The sulcus terminalis and the V of circumvallate papillae divide the oral tongue in front from the base of tongue behind. At the apex of the V lies the foramen caecum , from which the thyroid descends in the embryo; a lingual thyroid or thyroglossal cyst begins here ( Congenital neck masses ). Behind lie the lingual tonsil and the valleculae, both oropharynx. Figure 6 The dorsum of the tongue with its papillae, sulcus terminalis and lingual tonsil, and its undersurface with the floor of mouth. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s4-2","Papillae","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · The tongue","Filiform: the most numerous; texture, temperature and pain; no taste. Fungiform: mostly at the tip; taste buds on their upper surface. Foliate: on the lateral border; taste buds on their sides. Circumvallate: eight to twelve in a V in front of the sulcus terminalis; taste buds in their walls."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s4-3","Muscles","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · The tongue","The intrinsic muscles (longitudinal, transverse and vertical) change its shape; the extrinsic muscles move it. Extrinsic muscles of the tongue Muscle From Action Nerve Genioglossus Mental spine of the mandible Protrudes and depresses; the bulk of the tongue Hypoglossal Hyoglossus Hyoid bone Depresses and retracts Hypoglossal Styloglossus Styloid process Retracts and elevates Hypoglossal Palatoglossus Palatine aponeurosis Raises the back of the tongue, closes the isthmus Vagus (pharyngeal plexus) Figure 7 The extrinsic muscles of the tongue. From the anatomy atlas Testing the hypoglossal nerve With a lower motor neurone lesion the protruded tongue deviates towards the paralysed side, pushed across by the healthy genioglossus, and the paralysed half wastes and fasciculates."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s4-4","Nerves and taste","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · The tongue","Oral tongue: touch, pain and temperature by the lingual nerve (V3); taste by the chorda tympani (VII) to the geniculate ganglion and nucleus solitarius. Base of tongue: both general sensation and taste by the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). Epiglottis and valleculae: a little taste by the internal laryngeal branch of the vagus (X). The five tastes are sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami; flavour is taste combined with smell. Figure 8 The nerve supply and taste pathways of the tongue. From the anatomy atlas Referred otalgia The lingual nerve and the auriculotemporal nerve are both branches of V3, and the glossopharyngeal nerve also supplies the middle ear through Jacobson's nerve. A cancer of the tongue or tonsil therefore often presents as earache with a normal ear."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s4-5","Vessels and lymph","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · The tongue","The lingual artery, second branch of the external carotid, supplies the tongue; the lingual vein and the vein accompanying the hypoglossal nerve drain it. Lymph from the tip goes to the submental and jugulo-omohyoid nodes, from the lateral border to the ipsilateral submandibular and deep cervical nodes (sometimes skipping straight to level IV), and from the central tongue and base to the deep cervical nodes of both sides."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s5-1","Hard palate","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Palate and floor","The palatine processes of the maxillae in front and the horizontal plates of the palatine bones behind, covered by mucoperiosteum firmly bound to bone. Incisive foramen: in the midline behind the incisors; nasopalatine nerve and vessels. Greater palatine foramen: medial to the second or third molar; greater palatine nerve and vessels, the pedicle of palatal flaps. Lesser palatine foramina: behind it, to the soft palate. Sensation is from the nasopalatine and greater palatine nerves (V2); blood from the greater palatine artery; lymph to the upper deep cervical and lateral retropharyngeal nodes, the front of the palate also to the submandibular nodes. Figure 9 The hard palate, soft palate, retromolar trigone and oral tongue. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s5-2","Floor of mouth","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Palate and floor","The mylohyoid muscle forms the diaphragm of the floor. Above it lie the sublingual glands, the submandibular duct, the lingual nerve looping beneath the duct, and the hypoglossal nerve; below it, the submandibular space. The anterior floor drains to the submandibular nodes and crosses the midline freely; the posterior floor drains to the upper deep cervical nodes. Figure 10 The floor of the mouth with the sublingual papillae and folds, and the openings of the ducts. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s5-3","Saliva","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Palate and floor","One to one and a half litres a day. At rest about two-thirds comes from the submandibular glands; when stimulated, more than half comes from the parotids. The parotid (Stensen) duct opens opposite the upper second molar; the submandibular (Wharton) duct beside the frenulum; the sublingual glands by many small ducts into the floor. Minor salivary glands line the lips, cheeks, palate and tongue, and are the source of the palate's salivary tumours. Diseases of the glands are covered in Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings . Figure 11 Distribution of serous and mucous minor salivary glands. From the anatomy atlas"],["ev","cl:t-oralanat~c6","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity: current evidence","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Lymphatic drainage","Depth of invasion. Because the AJCC eighth edition uses depth of invasion (thresholds 5 and 10 mm) in T staging, the thickness of the tongue and floor of mouth tissues matters in planning. In early oral cancer, elective neck dissection improved survival over watchful waiting (D'Cruz et al., NEJM 2015). Lingual nerve. It lies close to the lingual cortex of the third molar and is injured in a small percentage of lower third molar removals; it is at risk too in submandibular gland excision."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~c6","Lymphatic drainage","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · Lymphatic drainage","The map for the neck. Lymphatic drainage of the oral cavity Subsite First nodes Lower lip Submental (midline) and submandibular (lateral); may cross the midline Upper lip Submandibular, preauricular and infraparotid Buccal mucosa Submental and submandibular Alveolar ridges Submental and submandibular Hard palate Upper deep cervical and lateral retropharyngeal; front of palate to submandibular Floor of mouth Anterior to submandibular, crossing the midline; posterior to upper deep cervical Tongue tip Submental and jugulo-omohyoid Lateral tongue Ipsilateral submandibular and deep cervical, sometimes skipping to level IV Central tongue and base Deep cervical nodes of both sides Figure 12 The lymph node levels of the neck. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s7-1","Viva essentials","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Boundaries of the oral cavity and its subsites. Nerve supply of the tongue: general sensation and taste, front and back. Extrinsic muscles and the test of the hypoglossal nerve. Lymphatic drainage of the lip, tongue and floor of mouth. Referred otalgia from the mouth and pharynx."],["sec","cl:t-oralanat~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Applied anatomy of the oral cavity · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling the base of tongue or the tonsil \"oral cavity\". Forgetting that the lateral tongue can skip to level IV nodes. Inspecting the floor of mouth without palpating it."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s1-1","Classification","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Overview","Benign tumours: solid (papilloma, fibroma, haemangioma, lymphangioma, torus, pyogenic granuloma, granular cell tumour, minor salivary gland adenoma) and cystic (mucocele, ranula, dermoid). Premalignant lesions and conditions: leukoplakia, erythroplakia, submucous fibrosis and others. Malignant lesions: squamous cell carcinoma and the non-squamous cancers ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer , Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer )."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s1-2","The rule","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Overview","Any lump that is growing, ulcerated, indurated or fixed, or any patch that is red, speckled or nodular, is biopsied."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-1","Papilloma","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","Common, usually in the third to fifth decades, on the palate, uvula, tongue and lips: a small (under 1 cm), white, pedunculated growth with finger-like projections, linked to HPV 6 and 11. Excisional biopsy; recurrence is rare."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-2","Fibroma (fibroepithelial polyp)","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","A smooth, mucosa-covered, pedunculated or sessile lump about 1 cm across, firm or soft, along the bite line of the cheek or on the tongue, caused by chronic irritation. Conservative excision. Figure 1 A fibroepithelial polyp in the left cheek (arrow). Dhingra, fig. 44.1"],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-3","Haemangioma","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","Mostly in children, on the lip, tongue and cheek: a soft, red or blue, compressible lump that may bleed when bitten. Capillary, cavernous and mixed types are described. Lesions present at birth are observed, since many regress; propranolol and steroids help proliferating haemangiomas. Large, persistent or growing lesions are treated by embolisation (alone or before surgery), laser or excision; sclerotherapy works poorly in diffuse lesions. In older adults, dilated veins ( phlebectasia ) can look similar. Figure 2 First, (A) a haemangioma on the lateral border of the tongue and (B) multiple haemangiomas of the lips, cheek and tongue in a man who bled when he bit them. Second, a haemangioma of the right cheek in a young girl. Dhingra, figs 44.2 and 44.3"],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-4","Lymphangioma","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","Usually in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, as a localised compressible swelling or a diffuse enlargement causing macroglossia . It does not regress. Small lesions are excised; large symptomatic ones are debulked, since complete removal is rarely possible."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-5","Torus","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","A bony outgrowth covered by mucosa: torus palatinus in the midline of the hard palate (the commoner), torus mandibularis on the lingual surface of the mandible near the premolars, usually on both sides. Harmless; removed only if it interferes with speech, chewing or dentures."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-6","Pyogenic granuloma","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","A reactive overgrowth of granulation tissue after trauma or irritation, usually on the anterior gums, sometimes the tongue, lips or cheek: a soft, red to purple lump that bleeds on touch. Excision; recurrence is unlikely after complete removal. Figure 3 A pyogenic granuloma. Dhingra, fig. 44.4 2.6.1 Pregnancy granuloma Identical clinically and histologically, arising in the first trimester ( epulis gravidarum ) and regressing after delivery; excised only if it persists, since it recurs if removed during pregnancy."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-7","Granular cell tumour","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","A firm submucosal nodule, usually on the dorsum of the tongue, once called a myoblastoma but derived from Schwann cells; its histology shows pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia that can be mistaken for carcinoma. Excision. Congenital epulis is a related tumour on the gums of newborn girls."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-8","Minor salivary gland tumours","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","Pleomorphic adenoma is the commonest benign tumour, usually a painless submucosal lump on the hard or soft palate. Because a large proportion of minor salivary gland tumours are malignant, every palatal lump is biopsied, and adenomas are widely excised to prevent recurrence."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s2-9","Other benign tumours","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Solid benign tumours","Neurofibroma and schwannoma (the tongue is a favoured site), lipoma (soft, yellow, in the cheek or floor), solitary fibrous tumour and verruciform xanthoma, all treated by excision."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s3-1","Mucocele","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Cysts","A retention or extravasation cyst of a minor salivary gland, almost always on the lower lip , after a bite: a soft, bluish, translucent swelling that may burst and refill. Excision with the underlying gland. Figure 4 (A) A mucocele of the lower lip. (B) A mucocele on the ventral surface of the tongue tip. Dhingra, fig. 44.5"],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s3-2","Ranula","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Cysts","A cystic, translucent, bluish swelling in the floor of mouth to one side of the frenulum, pushing the tongue up, from obstruction of the ducts of the sublingual gland . A plunging ranula passes through or behind the mylohyoid into the neck and presents as a soft submandibular or submental swelling. Figure 5 A ranula: a translucent swelling under the tongue. Dhingra, fig. 44.6 3.2.1 Treatment Older texts describe excision for small ranulas and marsupialisation for large ones, noting that the thin wall makes complete removal difficult. Recurrence after marsupialisation alone is common; excision of the ranula with the sublingual gland through the mouth, protecting the submandibular duct and lingual nerve, is now preferred and also cures most plunging ranulas."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s3-3","Dermoid","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Cysts","A sublingual dermoid lies above the mylohyoid, in or beside the midline, and shines white through the mucosa, unlike the translucent ranula; a submental dermoid lies below the mylohyoid and bulges under the chin. Excised through the mouth or the neck according to its relation to the muscle. Swellings in the floor of mouth Feature Ranula Sublingual dermoid Submandibular calculus Colour Bluish, translucent White, opaque Normal mucosa over the duct Consistency Soft, fluctuant Doughy Hard, palpable along the duct Position One side of the frenulum Midline or near it Along Wharton's duct Other May plunge into the neck Present from youth Pain and swelling with meals"],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s4-1","Definitions","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Premalignant lesions","A premalignant lesion is altered tissue in which cancer is more likely to develop (leukoplakia, erythroplakia); a premalignant condition is a general state with increased risk (submucous fibrosis, lichen planus, iron deficiency). The WHO now groups both as oral potentially malignant disorders ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer ); submucous fibrosis and lichen planus are described in Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis . Precancerous lesions and conditions of the oral cavity Leukoplakia and erythroplakia. Oral submucous fibrosis. Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis. Lichen planus (erosive and atrophic). Reverse-smoking palate and actinic cheilitis. Sideropenic anaemia (Plummer–Vinson syndrome). Pigmented lesions and melanosis; discoid lupus."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s4-2","Leukoplakia","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Premalignant lesions","4.2.1 Definition The WHO defines leukoplakia as a white patch that cannot be characterised clinically or pathologically as any other disease. It is a clinical term that says nothing about histology; lichen planus, candidiasis, leukoedema and white sponge naevus must be excluded. 4.2.2 Causes and sites Smoking, tobacco and areca chewing, alcohol (especially with smoking), chronic trauma from teeth or dentures, candida, submucous fibrosis and Plummer–Vinson syndrome. In India the buccal mucosa and commissures are the commonest sites, at the place where the quid is held; the floor of mouth, tongue, gingivobuccal sulcus and lips are also affected. Mostly men in their forties. Figure 6 First, leukoplakia of the cheek after pan and tobacco use. Second, leukoplakia on the lateral border of the tongue. Dhingra, figs 44.7 and 44.8 4.2.3 Types Homogeneous: a uniform, smooth or wrinkled white plaque; the lowest risk. Speckled (nodular): white nodules on a red base. Erosive (erythroleukoplakia): white mixed with red, with erosions and fissures. Proliferative verrucous leukoplakia: multifocal, warty and relentlessly progressive, often in older women without the usual risk factors; a very high risk of carcinoma. Figure 7 Leukoplakia at the site where a quid of tobacco and lime was held. Dhingra, fig. 44.9 4.2.4 Malignant potential About a quarter show some dysplasia, and the higher the grade the greater the risk. Quoted rates of malignant change range from under 1% to 17.5%, averaging about 5% in older series and about 9.5% in a recent meta-analysis (see Current evidence); speckled, eros"],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s4-3","Erythroplakia","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Premalignant lesions","A velvety red patch or plaque that cannot be characterised as any other disease, often slightly depressed with a sharp edge, on the floor of mouth, ventral tongue, soft palate and gingivobuccal sulcus. The redness is the vascular submucosa shining through thin, poorly keratinised epithelium. Most already show severe dysplasia, carcinoma in situ or invasive cancer when first seen, and the risk is many times that of leukoplakia. Excision biopsy and close follow-up. Red is worse than white Erythroplakia and the red parts of a speckled leukoplakia are where carcinoma is found. Biopsy the red."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s4-4","Pigmented lesions","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Premalignant lesions","Benign melanotic macules and naevi are common; about a quarter of mucosal melanomas look benign at first, so a new, enlarging or irregular pigmented lesion on the palate or gum is biopsied."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s4-5","Chemoprevention","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Premalignant lesions","Vitamin A, beta-carotene, vitamin E and retinoids such as 13-cis-retinoic acid can make leukoplakia regress in a quarter to a half of patients and may reduce second primary cancers, but the benefit lasts only as long as the drug is taken; stopping tobacco remains the most effective prevention."],["ev","cl:t-oralbenign~c5","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches: current evidence","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Clinical comparison","Malignant transformation. A meta-analysis of potentially malignant oral disorders found transformation rates of about 1.4% for lichen planus, 5% for submucous fibrosis, 9.5% for leukoplakia, 33% for erythroplakia and 50% for proliferative verrucous leukoplakia, with moderate or severe dysplasia carrying a much higher risk than mild (Iocca et al., Head Neck 2020). Terminology. A 2020 WHO workshop updated the list and definitions of oral potentially malignant disorders and recommended biopsy of any lesion that cannot be explained, with lifelong review (Warnakulasuriya et al., Oral Dis 2021). Management. Stopping tobacco, areca nut and alcohol is the most effective measure; excision of dysplastic lesions has not been shown in trials to prevent cancer, so surveillance continues after removal."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~c5","Clinical comparison","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · Clinical comparison","Telling them apart. Oral white patches Lesion Wipes off? Clue Thrush Yes, leaving a red base Infant, antibiotics, steroids, diabetes Leukoedema Disappears on stretching Bilateral buccal mucosa, a normal variant Lichen planus No Lace-like Wickham striae, both cheeks Hairy leukoplakia No Lateral tongue borders, HIV Hyperplastic candidiasis No Commissures; responds partly to antifungals Leukoplakia No Tobacco site; a diagnosis of exclusion"],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s6-1","Viva essentials","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Definition of leukoplakia, its types and malignant potential. Erythroplakia and why it is worse. List of premalignant lesions and conditions. Ranula: origin, the plunging type, and treatment by excision of the sublingual gland. Mucocele, pyogenic granuloma and torus."],["sec","cl:t-oralbenign~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using \"leukoplakia\" as a histological diagnosis. Marsupialising a ranula and leaving the sublingual gland. Biopsying the white part of a speckled leukoplakia instead of the red. Excising a pregnancy granuloma during pregnancy."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s1-1","Approach","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Overview","Take a history of duration, recurrence, pain, habits (tobacco, areca nut, alcohol), dentures, drugs, skin, eye and genital lesions, and general illness. Examine every subsite, palpate any lesion for induration, and feel the neck. Causes of oral ulcers Group Examples Viral Herpangina, primary and recurrent herpes simplex, hand, foot and mouth disease Bacterial Vincent infection (acute necrotising ulcerative gingivitis), tuberculosis, syphilis Fungal Candidiasis Immune Aphthous ulcers, Behçet syndrome Trauma Cheek bite, jagged tooth, ill-fitting denture; chemical (aspirin, phenol, silver nitrate) and thermal burns Neoplasm Squamous cell carcinoma, minor salivary gland carcinoma, lymphoma Skin disease Erythema multiforme, lichen planus, pemphigus, mucous membrane pemphigoid, lupus Blood disease Leukaemia, agranulocytosis, cyclic neutropenia, pancytopenia Other Drug reactions, vitamin B12 and folate deficiency, radiation and chemotherapy mucositis, diabetes, uraemia The non-healing ulcer An ulcer persisting beyond three weeks after any cause (a sharp tooth, a denture) has been removed, an indurated or rolled edge, a fixed lesion, numbness, or a hard neck node: biopsy for carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s2-1","Viral","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Infections","2.1.1 Herpangina Coxsackie A infection of children: small vesicles on the pillars, tonsils, soft palate and uvula rupture into 2 to 4 mm ulcers with a yellow base and red areola, and heal within a week. 2.1.2 Herpetic gingivostomatitis Primary herpes simplex in children: fever, malaise and clusters of vesicles anywhere in the mouth, with swollen, bleeding gums, healing in one to two weeks. Recurrent herpes in adults reactivates from the trigeminal ganglion after stress, fever, sunlight or immunosuppression, usually as herpes labialis at the vermilion border, sometimes on the hard palate and attached gingiva. Treatment is symptomatic; aciclovir shortens recurrent attacks if started early. 2.1.3 Hand, foot and mouth disease A childhood viral infection with ulcers on the palate, tongue and cheeks and vesicles on the hands and feet."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s2-2","Bacterial","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Infections","2.2.1 Vincent infection Acute necrotising ulcerative gingivitis, caused by a fusiform bacillus and the spirochaete Borrelia vincentii : it begins at the interdental papillae, spreads along the gum margins under a grey necrotic slough, and smells foul; on the tonsil it is Vincent angina . A smear confirms it. Penicillin or erythromycin with metronidazole, mouthwashes and dental hygiene. In malnourished children it can spread into the face as noma . 2.2.2 Specific infections Tuberculosis (a painful, undermined ulcer, often on the tongue), syphilis and actinomycosis (sulphur granules) cause chronic ulcers; biopsy distinguishes them from cancer."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s2-3","Fungal: candidiasis","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Infections","Candida albicans takes hold in infants, diabetics, the immunosuppressed, denture wearers, after antibiotics, inhaled steroids or radiotherapy, and in a dry mouth. Thrush (pseudomembranous): white plaques that wipe off to leave a raw red base. Erythematous (atrophic): red patches, often under dentures. Chronic hyperplastic (candidal leukoplakia): a white patch that will not wipe off, usually at the commissures; it can harbour dysplasia, so biopsy it. Angular cheilitis at the corners of the mouth, and median rhomboid glossitis on the midline dorsum. Topical nystatin or clotrimazole; fluconazole for severe disease; remove the cause. Oral hairy leukoplakia Corrugated white streaks on the lateral borders of the tongue that do not rub off, caused by Epstein–Barr virus in HIV or immunosuppression. It is harmless in itself but a marker of immune failure; it regresses with antiretroviral treatment."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s3-1","Aphthous ulcers","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Immune and skin disorders","The commonest non-traumatic ulcers: recurrent, shallow, painful, on the movable mucosa (lips, cheeks, tongue, floor, soft palate) and sparing the hard palate and attached gingiva. Figure 1 Multiple aphthous ulcers on the uvula and faucial pillars (arrowheads). Dhingra, fig. 43.1 Minor: the common form, 2 to 10 mm, multiple, with a necrotic centre and red halo; heal in about two weeks without a scar. Major: over 1 cm, deep, often at the back of the mouth, lasting six weeks or more and healing with a scar. Herpetiform: crops of tiny ulcers. The cause is unknown; associations include deficiency of vitamin B12, folate or iron, stress, hormones, food sensitivity, coeliac and Crohn's disease, and drugs such as NSAIDs and nicorandil. Unlike viral ulcers, they recur, spare fixed mucosa and come without fever or nodes. Treat with topical steroids, chlorhexidine or tetracycline mouthwashes and topical anaesthetic gels, and correct deficiencies."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s3-2","Behçet syndrome","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Immune and skin disorders","Recurrent aphthous-like oral ulcers with genital ulcers and uveitis, sometimes with skin, joint and nervous system disease; the ulcers are punched out."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s3-3","Erythema multiforme","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Immune and skin disorders","A hypersensitivity reaction, often to herpes simplex or drugs (sulphonamides, anticonvulsants): bullae rupture into ulcers under a pseudomembrane, the lips form haemorrhagic crusts , and target lesions appear on the skin; the severe form merges into Stevens–Johnson syndrome. Supportive care; steroids for severe disease."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s3-4","Lichen planus","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Immune and skin disorders","A T-cell mediated disease. The reticular form shows lace-like white Wickham striae on both buccal mucosae and needs no treatment; the erosive form produces painful ulcers with a keratotic edge and responds to topical steroids. Erosive and atrophic forms carry a small risk of carcinoma, so they are followed up ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer )."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s3-5","Pemphigus and pemphigoid","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Immune and skin disorders","Pemphigus vulgaris: autoantibodies to desmoglein 3 split the epithelium above the basal layer (intercellular \"chicken-wire\" immunofluorescence); fragile blisters break into painful erosions, often before skin lesions appear, in patients of fifty to seventy. Systemic steroids and immunosuppression. Mucous membrane pemphigoid: the split is beneath the epithelium (linear basement membrane immunofluorescence); blisters on the gums, cheek and palate, and scarring of the conjunctiva. Steroids and dapsone."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s3-6","Blood disorders and drugs","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Immune and skin disorders","Leukaemia swells and ulcerates the gums; agranulocytosis and cyclic neutropenia cause ulceration as defences fail. A blood film, counts and marrow examination are needed. Figure 2 Palatal ulceration in a patient whose white cell count fell to 2,500 per cubic millimetre on cisplatin and fluorouracil chemotherapy. Dhingra, fig. 43.4"],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s3-7","Mucositis","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Immune and skin disorders","Radiotherapy and chemotherapy (methotrexate, fluorouracil, bleomycin) redden, then ulcerate the mucosa from about the second week; treatment is supportive."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~c4","Trauma","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Trauma","A sharp tooth can mimic cancer. A jagged tooth or ill-fitting denture ulcerates the lateral tongue or cheek; a pencil or toothbrush injures the palate; acids, alkalis, hot fluids and an aspirin held against a painful tooth burn the mucosa. Figure 3 First, an exophytic ulcerated mass on the left side of the tongue caused by sharp teeth (white arrows) in a man who chewed tobacco and pan; it healed after the teeth were extracted. Second, an ulcer on the lateral border of the tongue that simulated carcinoma (A) and healed completely after extraction of the jagged tooth (B). Dhingra, figs 43.2 and 43.3 Remove the cause, then review Smooth the tooth or adjust the denture and look again in two to three weeks. A traumatic ulcer heals; one that does not is a biopsy."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s5-1","Variants","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · The tongue","Geographic tongue (migratory glossitis): red, smooth areas without papillae, outlined by a white keratotic margin, that change shape from day to day; harmless. Fissured tongue: congenital, or with deficiency states; with facial palsy and lip swelling it forms Melkersson–Rosenthal syndrome . Hairy tongue: elongated filiform papillae stained brown or black by bacteria, with smoking and antibiotics; scrape the tongue and treat the cause. Median rhomboid glossitis: a red, smooth, rhomboid area in front of the foramen caecum, now attributed to chronic candida. Fordyce spots: yellow ectopic sebaceous glands in the buccal and labial mucosa; normal. Leukoedema: a milky film on the buccal mucosa that disappears when the cheek is stretched, unlike leukoplakia. Figure 4 First, geographic tongue. Second, tongue-tie. Dhingra, figs 43.5 and 43.6"],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s5-2","Tongue-tie (ankyloglossia)","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · The tongue","A short frenulum. If the tongue can be protruded beyond the lower incisors it rarely affects speech; symptomatic ties are released (frenotomy, or transverse release with vertical closure; Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling )."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s5-3","Systemic clues","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · The tongue","Smooth, red tongue: iron, B12 or folate deficiency; ash-grey mucosa and atrophy in iron deficiency (Plummer–Vinson syndrome). Angular cheilitis: riboflavin or iron deficiency, candida. Gingival hyperplasia and bleeding: scurvy, leukaemia, phenytoin. Strawberry tongue: scarlet fever and Kawasaki disease; Koplik spots: measles."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s5-4","Pigmentation","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · The tongue","Racial melanosis, amalgam tattoo, Peutz–Jeghers syndrome, Addison disease, Kaposi sarcoma and heavy metals (the blue line of lead) all pigment the mouth; a new or changing pigmented lesion, especially on the palate, is biopsied to exclude melanoma."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s5-5","Macroglossia","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · The tongue","Lymphangioma, haemangioma, hypothyroidism, acromegaly, amyloidosis, Down syndrome and storage diseases."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s5-6","Nicotine stomatitis","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · The tongue","Red pin-points in the centre of white papules on the palate of pipe and reverse smokers, from inflamed minor salivary gland ducts; it regresses when smoking stops. The heat, not the nicotine, is to blame."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s6-1","Definition and cause","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Submucous fibrosis","Oral submucous fibrosis is a chronic, insidious fibrosis beneath the epithelium of the mouth and pharynx, first described in India by Joshi in 1953 and common across South and South-East Asia. Areca (betel) nut is the essential cause, chewed alone, with tobacco, or as pan; tobacco, alcohol, poor nutrition and an immune reaction to arecoline add to it."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s6-2","Pathogenesis","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Submucous fibrosis","Activated T-lymphocytes and macrophages release fibrogenic cytokines that drive fibroblasts to make more collagen while collagenase falls, so collagen is laid down faster than it is removed. The lamina propria becomes fibroelastic and avascular, the epithelium atrophies, and the fibrosis can reach the muscle. Figure 5 The cellular immune response to areca nut that leads to increased collagen production in submucous fibrosis. Dhingra, fig. 43.7"],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s6-3","Clinical features","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Submucous fibrosis","Usually between twenty and forty. Intolerance of chillies and spicy food, a burning mouth, recurrent vesicles on the palate and pillars, then difficulty in opening the mouth and protruding the tongue. The soft palate, pillars and buccal mucosa blanch and lose their suppleness, with palpable fibrous bands; trismus becomes progressive until the patient cannot brush the teeth. Figure 6 Submucous fibrosis: (A) blanching of the soft palate and faucial pillars; (B) marked trismus. Dhingra, fig. 43.8 A premalignant condition Trismus makes examination difficult, so look carefully for leukoplakia and early cancer ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer ), and biopsy anything suspicious. Where the books differ · Malignant change in submucous fibrosis Most series About 3 to 7.6%. Lee 7 to 30%. Meta-analysis (Iocca et al., Head Neck 2020): About 5%. In practice: The figures differ because the series differ; treat it as premalignant and follow it up."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s6-4","Treatment","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Submucous fibrosis","Stop areca nut, pan, tobacco and spicy food; correct anaemia and vitamin deficiency. Intralesional dexamethasone with hyaluronidase, given twice a week for eight to ten weeks, softens the bands and relieves trismus. Jaw-opening exercises. Surgery for advanced trismus: release of the bands with cover by skin graft, buccal fat pad, nasolabial or tongue flaps, a palatal island flap or radial forearm free flaps, with coronoidectomy and temporalis myotomy where needed; recurrence is common."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~c7","Odontogenic cysts","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Odontogenic cysts","Cysts in the jaws. Other jaw cysts and tumours are covered in Cysts and tumours of the jaws ."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s7-1","Dentigerous cyst","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Odontogenic cysts","A developmental cyst around the crown of an unerupted or impacted tooth, usually the third molar or the upper canine, presenting in the second or third decade as a painless swelling of the jaw. Radiographs show a unilocular cyst containing the crown; in the maxilla the tooth may be pushed up to the orbital floor. Small cysts are removed with the tooth; large ones are first marsupialised to avoid a fracture. Ameloblastoma or carcinoma can arise in the wall, so the lining is always examined. Figure 7 Dentigerous cyst in a 14-year-old boy: (A) swelling of the left cheek; (B) radiograph; (C) the specimen with its tooth. Dhingra, fig. 43.9"],["ev","cl:t-oraldis~s7-2","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis: current evidence","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Odontogenic cysts","Referral. NICE suggested-cancer guidance (NG12) recommends urgent referral for unexplained oral ulceration lasting more than three weeks, and for a persistent unexplained lump in the mouth or red or red-and-white patch. Recurrent aphthous stomatitis. Topical steroids and chlorhexidine reduce pain and healing time; severe or atypical ulcers need tests for Behçet disease, coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease and haematinic deficiency. Areca nut. The International Agency for Research on Cancer classifies areca nut, with or without tobacco, as a group 1 carcinogen; (see the box on malignant change above). Oral lichen planus is managed with topical steroids and periodic review because of its small malignant potential."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s7-2","Dental (radicular) cyst","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · Odontogenic cysts","An inflammatory cyst at the apex (periapical) or side (lateral radicular) of a dead tooth. Enucleated when the tooth is extracted; lining left behind forms a residual cyst."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s8-1","Viva essentials","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes of oral ulceration and the three-week rule. Aphthous versus herpetic ulcers. Forms of candidiasis, and why the hyperplastic form is biopsied. Lichen planus, pemphigus and pemphigoid. Submucous fibrosis: cause, features, premalignancy and treatment. Dentigerous cyst."],["sec","cl:t-oraldis~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Oral ulcers, white patches and submucous fibrosis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing repeated mouthwashes for a persistent single ulcer instead of a biopsy. Treating a white patch that will not rub off as thrush. Missing an early cancer in a mouth with submucous fibrosis and trismus."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s1-1","Definitions","Proptosis and the orbit · Overview","Proptosis is forward displacement of the eyeball; exophthalmos is often used for proptosis from thyroid eye disease. Measured with an exophthalmometer (Hertel): a difference of more than 2 mm between the eyes, or a reading above about 20 mm, is abnormal. Pseudoproptosis (a large eye in myopia, lid retraction, or a small opposite eye) must be excluded."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s1-2","Applied anatomy","Proptosis and the orbit · Overview","The orbit is a four-sided pyramid. Its medial wall (the lamina papyracea) separates it from the ethmoids, its floor from the maxillary sinus, its roof from the frontal sinus and anterior cranial fossa; the sphenoid lies at the apex beside the optic canal. The orbital periosteum and septum limit the spread of infection; the valveless ophthalmic veins drain to the cavernous sinus. Figure 1 Anatomy of the orbit: its bones and relations. Dhingra, fig. 41.1 Figure 2 The bony orbit with its walls, fissures and foramina, and the optic canal and tendinous ring at the apex. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s1-3","Why it matters for ENT","Proptosis and the orbit · Overview","Sinus infections, mucoceles, fungal disease, polyps, angiofibroma, inverted papilloma and sinonasal cancers all invade the orbit; endoscopic surgery can injure it; and the orbit can be decompressed and drained through the nose."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~c2","Causes","Proptosis and the orbit · Causes","VEIN. Causes of proptosis (VEIN) Group Examples Vascular Venous varix, cavernous haemangioma, carotid-cavernous fistula, orbital haematoma Endocrine Thyroid eye disease (Graves ophthalmopathy) Inflammatory and infective Orbital cellulitis and abscess, idiopathic orbital inflammation, fungal sinusitis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, dacryoadenitis Neoplastic Primary orbital tumours, spread from the sinuses or cranium, metastases"],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s2-1","From the sinuses","Proptosis and the orbit · Causes","Mucoceles of the frontal and ethmoid sinuses, allergic fungal polyposis, inverted papilloma, angiofibroma, olfactory neuroblastoma and sinus carcinoma, and orbital complications of sinusitis: see Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications , Fungal sinusitis , Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma and Sinonasal tumours . Figure 3 First, proptosis with lateral displacement of the eye from allergic fungal polyposis. Second, a left ethmoid mucocele displacing the eye. Dhingra, fig. 41.2"],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s2-2","Clues from the history and examination","Proptosis and the orbit · Causes","Unilateral or bilateral: thyroid eye disease is the commonest cause of both bilateral and unilateral proptosis in adults; orbital cellulitis is the commonest cause in children. Speed: sudden (haemorrhage, cellulitis), weeks (inflammation, malignancy), years (benign tumours, mucoceles, fibrous dysplasia). Direction: axial proptosis suggests a lesion inside the muscle cone (haemangioma, optic nerve tumour, thyroid eye disease); non-axial displacement points away from the lesion: down and out with a frontoethmoid mucocele, laterally with an ethmoid lesion, upwards with a maxillary tumour. Variable proptosis with straining or bending: venous varix. Pulsatile proptosis with a bruit and arterialised conjunctival vessels: carotid-cavernous fistula. Pain: inflammation, infection or malignancy."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s3-1","Thyroid eye disease","Proptosis and the orbit · Important conditions","The commonest cause of proptosis in adults, bilateral or one-sided, in hyperthyroid, euthyroid or hypothyroid patients. Lid retraction, lid lag, chemosis, restricted movement and exposure keratopathy; CT shows enlargement of the muscle bellies with sparing of their tendons. Optic nerve compression at the apex threatens vision."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s3-2","Idiopathic orbital inflammation","Proptosis and the orbit · Important conditions","Orbital pseudotumour: painful proptosis, worse on eye movement; may involve the muscles (myositis, including the tendons, unlike thyroid disease), lacrimal gland, sclera or optic nerve. Biopsy shows non-specific inflammation; treated with steroids, immunosuppression or radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s3-3","Vascular lesions","Proptosis and the orbit · Important conditions","Cavernous haemangioma: the commonest benign orbital tumour of adults; painless, slowly progressive axial proptosis from a well-defined, enhancing intraconal mass; excised by lateral orbitotomy or endoscopically. Capillary haemangioma: the commonest orbital tumour of infants; usually involutes by about seven; propranolol or steroids. Venous varix: proptosis that comes and goes with straining. Carotid-cavernous fistula: pulsatile proptosis, bruit, chemosis and arterialised vessels; endovascular closure."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s3-4","Tumours","Proptosis and the orbit · Important conditions","Lymphoma: the commonest malignant orbital tumour of adults, often anterior and palpable; biopsy, radiotherapy and chemotherapy. Rhabdomyosarcoma: the commonest primary orbital malignancy of children (around six or seven years), with rapid proptosis; chemoradiation. Dermoid cyst: the commonest benign orbital tumour of children, at the frontozygomatic suture or deeper; excised. Optic nerve glioma (children, neurofibromatosis) and optic nerve sheath meningioma (adults). Lacrimal gland tumours: pleomorphic adenoma and adenoid cystic carcinoma in the upper outer orbit. Metastases: breast most often, then lung, prostate and kidney; neuroblastoma in children. Figure 4 First, proptosis with medial displacement of the eye from lymphoma in a child. Second, a dermoid cyst of the right orbit on axial and coronal CT. Dhingra, figs 41.4 and 41.5"],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s4-1","Examination","Proptosis and the orbit · Evaluation","Visual acuity, colour vision and pupils (a relative afferent pupillary defect means optic nerve compromise), eye movements and diplopia, the degree and direction of proptosis, lids, conjunctiva and cornea, fundus, and nasal endoscopy."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s4-2","Investigations","Proptosis and the orbit · Evaluation","CT of the orbits and sinuses: bone, sinus disease, fractures, muscle size. MRI for soft tissue, the optic nerve, apex and intracranial extension. Thyroid function and antibodies; blood tests for vasculitis and lymphoma as indicated. Biopsy for undiagnosed masses, endoscopically or by orbitotomy."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s5-1","Endoscopic orbital decompression","Proptosis and the orbit · Procedures","Removal of the medial wall (lamina papyracea) and the medial part of the floor, opening the periorbita so orbital fat can prolapse into the ethmoid and maxillary spaces. For thyroid eye disease with optic neuropathy, severe exposure or disfiguring proptosis. Diplopia may follow."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s5-2","Optic nerve decompression","Proptosis and the orbit · Procedures","Removal of the bone of the optic canal through the sphenoid in traumatic or compressive optic neuropathy; its value in trauma is debated."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s5-3","Dacryocystorhinostomy","Proptosis and the orbit · Procedures","For epiphora from obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct: a window is made from the lacrimal sac into the middle meatus, through the lacrimal bone and frontal process of the maxilla, endoscopically or through an external skin incision, sometimes with silicone tubes. Figure 5 The lacrimal drainage system, and the lacrimal apparatus. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s5-4","Drainage of orbital abscess","Proptosis and the orbit · Procedures","Endoscopic or external drainage of a subperiosteal or orbital abscess complicating sinusitis ( Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications ). 5.4.1 Who needs drainage in children Most children with orbital complications are treated medically at first, with intravenous antibiotics covering Streptococcus anginosus (milleri), the commonest organism in complicated sinusitis in young people, plus nasal decongestant, and with vision, pupils, colour vision and eye movements checked at least every few hours. A small medial subperiosteal abscess in a child with normal vision often resolves without surgery. Features that favour early drainage (based on the criteria of Garcia and Harris) are age nine years or older, frontal sinusitis, a non-medial or large abscess, gas in the abscess, a dental source, any reduction in visual acuity or colour vision, an afferent pupillary defect, intracranial complications, or failure to improve within 24 to 48 hours. Adolescents with frontal sinusitis and orbital involvement need imaging of the brain as well, because S. anginosus readily forms intracranial abscesses and Pott's puffy tumour."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s5-5","Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis","Proptosis and the orbit · Procedures","An emergency release for retrobulbar haemorrhage after trauma or sinus surgery: a tense, proptosed eye with rising pressure and falling vision. Cut the lateral canthal tendon and its inferior crus within minutes. Retrobulbar haemorrhage Pain, tense proptosis, a hard globe, ophthalmoplegia and loss of vision after injury or sinus surgery. Do not wait for imaging: perform lateral canthotomy and cantholysis at once, then call ophthalmology."],["ev","cl:t-orbit~s5-6","Proptosis and the orbit: current evidence","Proptosis and the orbit · Procedures","Orbital complications of sinusitis. Chandler's classification (1970) remains the standard. The criteria of Garcia and Harris, under which children under nine with a small medial subperiosteal abscess and normal vision were treated medically, avoided surgery in most such children ( Ophthalmology 2000); EPOS 2020 endorses initial medical treatment for small medial abscesses with close monitoring. Traumatic optic neuropathy. The International Optic Nerve Trauma Study found no clear benefit from high-dose steroids or surgical decompression over observation (Levin et al., Ophthalmology 1999), and the CRASH trial showed that high-dose steroids increase death after head injury ( Lancet 2004), so steroids are now avoided in this setting. Thyroid eye disease. The European (EUGOGO) guideline recommends intravenous methylprednisolone for active moderate to severe disease and urgent decompression for optic neuropathy not responding to steroids (Bartalena et al., Eur J Endocrinol 2021); teprotumumab, an IGF-1 receptor antibody, reduced proptosis in randomised trials (Douglas et al., NEJM 2020)."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s5-6","Orbital exenteration","Proptosis and the orbit · Procedures","Removal of the orbital contents for malignancy invading beyond the periorbita."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s6-1","Viva essentials","Proptosis and the orbit · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes of proptosis (VEIN) and the commonest in adults and children. Direction of displacement as a clue. Thyroid eye disease and endoscopic orbital decompression. Commonest orbital tumours in adults and children. Retrobulbar haemorrhage and lateral canthotomy. Dacryocystorhinostomy."],["sec","cl:t-orbit~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Proptosis and the orbit · For the examination","Common pitfalls Waiting for a scan in retrobulbar haemorrhage. Missing a sinus cause in one-sided proptosis. Forgetting colour vision and the pupils when assessing the optic nerve."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s1-1","Terms","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Definitions","Apnoea: cessation of airflow for 10 seconds or more during sleep. Hypopnoea: a reduction in airflow (commonly by 30 to 50%) with a fall in oxygen saturation of 3 to 4% or an arousal. Apnoea–hypopnoea index (AHI): apnoeas and hypopnoeas per hour of sleep; also called the respiratory disturbance index. Under 5 is normal. Arousal: a brief awakening on the EEG caused by respiratory effort; the arousal index is their number per hour. Sleep efficiency: minutes asleep divided by minutes in bed. Multiple sleep latency test: timed daytime naps to measure sleepiness and detect narcolepsy. Severity of obstructive sleep apnoea (adults) Grade AHI (events per hour) Normal Under 5 Mild 5 to 14 Moderate 15 to 29 Severe 30 or more"],["sec","cl:t-osa~s1-2","Types of apnoea","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Definitions","Obstructive: the upper airway collapses while breathing effort continues. Central: the airway is open but the brain does not signal breathing; no effort is made. Mixed: a central start followed by obstruction."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s1-3","Spectrum of sleep-disordered breathing","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Definitions","Primary snoring (no arousals, AHI under 5, no daytime sleepiness), upper airway resistance (effort-related arousals and sleepiness without frank apnoeas), and obstructive sleep apnoea ."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s2-1","Snoring","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Mechanism","In sleep the pharyngeal muscles relax and the airway narrows; breathing through the narrowing sets the soft palate, uvula, pillars and tongue base vibrating, at up to 90 dB. Snoring affects about a quarter of men and fifteen per cent of women, and increases with age."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s2-2","Obstruction","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Mechanism","The pharynx has no bony support between the palate and the larynx; its patency depends on muscle tone against the suction of inspiration. Tone falls most in REM sleep and stage 2 sleep, when events are most frequent. Each apnoea ends with an arousal that restores tone, fragmenting sleep."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s2-3","Sites","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Mechanism","Obstruction may be at more than one level, and may differ from night to night: Level I: nose and nasopharynx: septal deviation, turbinate hypertrophy, valve collapse, polyps, adenoids, tumours. Level II: palate and tonsils: a long soft palate and uvula, large tonsils, bulky lateral walls. Level III: tongue base, hypopharynx and larynx: macroglossia, retrognathia, lingual tonsils, an omega-shaped or floppy epiglottis, tumours. Obesity and a thick neck (collar over about 42 cm in men) narrow every level; alcohol, sedatives and hypnotics relax the muscles further. Figure 1 Waldeyer's ring seen through the mouth, and the adenoids compared with the palatine tonsils. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-osa~s2-4","Sleep architecture","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Mechanism","An adult sleeps seven to eight hours in three to five cycles of 90 to 120 minutes. Non-REM sleep (75 to 80%) moves from light stages to deep slow-wave sleep; REM sleep (20 to 25%) brings dreaming, irregular breathing and heart rate, and near-paralysis of skeletal muscle, which is why obstruction is worst then."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s3-1","Personal","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Consequences","Loud snoring disturbs the partner (the snoring spouse syndrome); witnessed choking and gasping, restless unrefreshing sleep, excessive daytime sleepiness, morning headaches, fatigue, poor memory and concentration, irritability and depression, reduced libido, nocturia."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s3-2","Medical","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Consequences","Hypertension: apnoea is an independent risk factor; treatment lowers blood pressure, even resistant hypertension. Heart: arrhythmias, ischaemia, heart failure, pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale; untreated severe apnoea increases mortality. Brain: stroke. Metabolic: insulin resistance; polycythaemia. Perioperative risk: difficult airways and sensitivity to opioids and sedatives."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s3-3","Social","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Consequences","Road traffic and workplace accidents. Ask every patient about sleepiness while driving; in some jurisdictions the doctor must advise the patient about notifying the licensing authority."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s4-1","History","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Evaluation","Take it from the bed partner as well as the patient: snoring, pauses, gasping, choking, restlessness and sweating at night; sleepiness, fatigue, headaches, memory and mood by day. Ask about sleep habits and shift work, alcohol, sedatives and caffeine, sleep position, nasal obstruction, menopause and weight change, and driving. Epworth sleepiness scale Each situation is scored 0 (would never doze), 1 (slight chance), 2 (moderate chance) or 3 (high chance of dozing), for a total out of 24: Sitting and reading. Watching television. Sitting inactive in a public place, such as a theatre or meeting. As a passenger in a car for an hour without a break. Lying down to rest in the afternoon. Sitting and talking to someone. Sitting quietly after lunch without alcohol. In a car, stopped for a few minutes in traffic. A total over 10 indicates excessive daytime sleepiness. The STOP-BANG and Berlin questionnaires screen for apnoea."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s4-2","Examination","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Evaluation","General: body mass index (obese at 30 or more), neck circumference (over 42 cm in men or 37.5 cm in women), blood pressure, signs of heart failure and hypothyroidism. Nose: septum, turbinates, valve, polyps; endoscopy for adenoids. Mouth and oropharynx: tonsil size, palate length and position, uvula, tongue size and scalloping, lateral walls, dental occlusion and retrognathia. Hypopharynx and larynx: flexible endoscopy of the tongue base, lingual tonsil and epiglottis. Friedman grading Tonsils Palate position (tongue relaxed in the mouth) 0: removed I: tonsils, uvula and pillars all visible 1: hidden within the pillars II: uvula visible, not the tonsils 2: reach the pillars III: soft palate visible, not the uvula 3: beyond the pillars IV: only the hard palate visible 4: meeting in the midline Friedman, not Mallampati For sleep surgery the palate is graded with the tongue relaxed inside the mouth (Friedman), not protruded as in the anaesthetist's Mallampati score. Large tonsils with a favourable palate predict success from palatal surgery."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s4-3","Endoscopic tests","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Evaluation","Müller manoeuvre: with the nose pinched and mouth closed, the patient inhales forcefully while the endoscopist watches which level collapses; simple but poorly reproducible. Drug-induced sleep endoscopy (DISE): endoscopy under propofol or midazolam sedation shows collapse during sleep-like conditions; the VOTE system records the velum, oropharyngeal lateral walls, tongue base and epiglottis, with the degree and pattern (anteroposterior, lateral or concentric). Used before surgery, after failed surgery and when findings do not match the sleep study."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s4-4","Imaging","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Evaluation","Lateral cephalometry for craniofacial skeletal anomalies and the posterior airway space; a lateral neck radiograph for adenoids in children."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s4-5","Sleep studies","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Evaluation","Polysomnography is the gold standard. A full attended study (level 1) records EEG, electro-oculogram and chin and leg EMG (sleep staging), ECG, nasal and oral airflow, thoracic and abdominal effort, oxygen saturation, body position and snoring; it separates obstructive from central apnoea and primary snoring from apnoea. Home studies (level 3: airflow, effort, oximetry, position) are now widely used for uncomplicated adults; oximetry alone is insufficient. A split-night study diagnoses in the first half and titrates CPAP in the second, though events may be under-counted."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s5-1","Lifestyle","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Non-surgical treatment","Weight loss; bariatric surgery for morbid obesity. Tirzepatide reduced the apnoea–hypopnoea index by about half in obese adults in the SURMOUNT-OSA trials (Malhotra et al., NEJM 2024) and is now licensed for moderate to severe sleep apnoea with obesity in some countries. Avoid alcohol in the evening, sedatives and hypnotics; stop smoking. Positional therapy: sleeping on the side, with a device (or a ball sewn into the back of a shirt) when apnoea is supine-dependent. Treat nasal obstruction and allergy."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s5-2","Positive airway pressure","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Non-surgical treatment","Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) is a pneumatic splint that holds the airway open, usually at 5 to 20 cm of water, through a nasal, nasal-pillow or full-face mask. Bilevel (BiPAP) delivers a higher inspiratory and lower expiratory pressure for those who need high pressures; auto-titrating devices adjust continuously. It is the most effective non-surgical treatment and the standard for moderate to severe apnoea, but only about half of patients use it adequately (at least four hours a night, five nights a week) in the long term, because of discomfort, claustrophobia, dryness, leaks and inconvenience. Coaching and follow-up improve adherence."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s5-3","Oral appliances","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Non-surgical treatment","A mandibular advancement device holds the jaw forward and draws the tongue and soft tissues away from the posterior wall; a tongue-retaining device holds the tongue forward. Effective for snoring and mild to moderate apnoea, and useful in retrognathia; side effects are jaw pain, dental movement and dryness."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-1","Indications","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","Failure of or intolerance to CPAP, favourable anatomy, patient preference, and serious cardiovascular disease. Surgery is tailored to the level(s) of obstruction; multilevel procedures may be combined or staged."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-2","Nasal surgery","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","Septoplasty, turbinate reduction, polypectomy, valve surgery and adenoidectomy ( Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess , Nose and sinus operations: principles ). Nasal surgery seldom cures apnoea, because the nose is not a collapsing segment, but it improves sleep quality and CPAP tolerance and can lower the pressure needed."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-3","Palate and oropharynx","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","Tonsillectomy (with adenoidectomy): the main treatment in children ( Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess , Adenoids and nasopharyngitis ); in adults with grade 3 or 4 tonsils, isolated tonsillectomy can be very effective. Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP): introduced by Fujita in 1981; tonsillectomy with trimming of the uvula and free edge of the palate and suturing of the pillars to widen the oropharynx. Overall success is about 50%, rising above 80% in well-selected patients (Friedman tonsils 3 to 4, palate I to II). Complications: pain, bleeding, velopharyngeal insufficiency, nasopharyngeal stenosis and voice change. Uvulopalatal flap: the uvula is folded back onto the denuded palate, sparing the muscle. Expansion sphincter pharyngoplasty: the palatopharyngeus is rerouted superolaterally to widen the lateral dimension. Uvulopalatoplasty (UPP, laser or radiofrequency assisted) and palatal stiffening (cautery-assisted or implants): useful for snoring, much less for apnoea. Palatal surgery relieves snoring in about 80% but apnoea in only about half, with relapse when another level takes over."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-4","Tongue base and hypopharynx","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","Radiofrequency reduction of the tongue base in repeated sessions, lingual tonsillectomy, midline glossectomy (coblation, laser, robotic), tongue suspension, hyoid suspension (to the mandible or thyroid cartilage), genioglossus advancement, and supraglottoplasty for epiglottic collapse."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-5","Skeletal surgery","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","Maxillomandibular advancement (Le Fort I osteotomy of the maxilla and sagittal split of the mandible, fixed forwards with plates) enlarges the whole skeletal framework of the airway and is the most effective operation apart from tracheostomy, especially in retrognathic patients and after failed soft tissue surgery; it changes facial appearance and needs orthodontic care."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-6","Hypoglossal nerve stimulation","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","An implanted stimulator fires the hypoglossal nerve with inspiration, protruding the tongue; for moderate to severe apnoea in patients who cannot use CPAP, with a suitable BMI (usually under 32 to 35) and without complete concentric palatal collapse on DISE."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-7","Transoral robotic surgery","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","Tongue base reduction by robot requires a BMI below about 35 (ideally near 30) and a mouth opening of at least 2.5 cm."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-8","Tracheostomy","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","The only operation that reliably cures obstructive apnoea, by bypassing every level; reserved for life-threatening disease, severe cardiopulmonary complications and failure of everything else, because of its personal and social cost ( Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy )."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s6-9","Perioperative care","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Surgery","Patients with apnoea are sensitive to opioids and sedatives and may have difficult airways. Monitor oxygen saturation overnight after surgery in severe apnoea, with cardiovascular disease, after tongue base surgery, and in young children or those with comorbidities; restart CPAP after surgery when appropriate. Where the books differ · The gold standard Dhingra Calls tracheostomy the gold standard of surgical treatment, lists uvulopalatoplasty as the commonest procedure and quotes 80% success for snoring but 50% for apnoea. Lee Places CPAP as the gold standard of therapy, emphasises patient selection by Friedman stage and DISE, reporting UPPP success above 85% in favourable anatomy, and names maxillomandibular advancement the most effective operation short of tracheostomy. In practice Both agree that surgery must be matched to the level of collapse. CPAP is first line for moderate and severe apnoea; surgery is matched to the site of collapse on drug-induced sleep endoscopy, with multilevel surgery (MacKay et al., JAMA 2020) and hypoglossal nerve stimulation (Strollo et al., NEJM 2014) for selected CPAP failures, and tracheostomy as a last resort."],["sec","cl:t-osa~c7","Children","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Children","Big tonsils, small airway. Children are different In children the commonest cause is adenotonsillar hypertrophy. They show snoring, pauses and gasping, restless sleep with the neck hyperextended, sweating, bedwetting, morning headache, and by day hyperactivity, poor attention and school performance, and sometimes failure to thrive, rather than sleepiness. An AHI above 1 is abnormal in a child, and adenotonsillectomy is first-line treatment."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s7-1","Who and why","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Children","About 10 to 15% of children snore, and 1 to 4% have obstructive sleep apnoea, more in obese and Black children. It usually begins between two and six years, when the tonsils and adenoids are largest relative to the airway (tonsils grade 3 or more on the Brodsky scale). Prematurity is a risk factor, and apnoea can cause failure to thrive, poor behaviour and attention, and enuresis."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s7-2","Assessment","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Children","Most children can be treated on a careful history and examination, ideally with a parental video. Overnight oximetry helps triage (a normal trace does not exclude apnoea), and polysomnography is kept for uncertain cases and high-risk children: those under two, with obesity, Down syndrome, craniofacial or neuromuscular disease, or cardiac complications."],["ev","cl:t-osa~s7-3","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea: current evidence","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Children","Children. In the CHAT trial, children aged five to nine with non-severe apnoea who had early adenotonsillectomy did not improve in attention or executive function compared with watchful waiting, but did improve in symptoms, behaviour, quality of life and sleep studies; 46% of the waiting group normalised spontaneously (Marcus et al., N Engl J Med 2013). The PATS trial of 458 children with snoring and mild apnoea found the same: no difference in executive function or attention at a year, but better behaviour, sleepiness and quality of life after surgery (Redline et al., JAMA 2023). Adults. In 2,717 adults with cardiovascular disease and moderate to severe apnoea, CPAP (used a mean of 3.3 hours a night) did not prevent cardiovascular events, though it improved sleepiness, mood and quality of life (McEvoy et al., N Engl J Med 2016). In the SAMS trial of 102 adults in whom CPAP had failed, combined palatal and tongue surgery lowered the apnoea-hypopnoea index from 47.9 to 20.8, compared with 45.3 to 34.5 on medical management, and improved sleepiness (MacKay et al., JAMA 2020). Hypoglossal nerve stimulation reduced the median index by 68% (29.3 to 9.0) at a year in selected patients (Strollo et al., N Engl J Med 2014). NICE recommends CPAP for moderate and severe disease, and for mild disease with symptoms affecting daily life (NG202, 2021)."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s7-3","Treatment","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · Children","Adenotonsillectomy is the main treatment. Watchful waiting is reasonable for snoring and mild disease, because many children improve; intranasal steroids and montelukast help in the short term, especially with rhinitis or residual symptoms after surgery. Weight loss is recommended for obese children. Children with persistent apnoea after adenotonsillectomy, or without large tonsils, need drug-induced sleep endoscopy or MRI to find other levels of obstruction. Children under three, with severe apnoea or comorbidities, are monitored overnight after surgery."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s8-1","Viva essentials","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Definitions: apnoea, hypopnoea, AHI and severity grades. Obstructive versus central apnoea. Consequences of untreated apnoea. Evaluation: Epworth score, Friedman staging, Müller manoeuvre, DISE, polysomnography. CPAP and its adherence problem. Surgery by level, UPPP selection, and maxillomandibular advancement. Children: adenotonsillectomy first."],["sec","cl:t-osa~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on the palate when the tongue base is the main site of collapse. Using oximetry alone to diagnose or exclude apnoea. Giving opioids freely after surgery to a patient with severe apnoea. Promising that nasal surgery will cure apnoea."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s1-1","Definitions","Approach to earache · Overview","Otalgia is ear pain. It is primary (otogenic) when the cause lies in the external, middle or inner ear or the mastoid, and referred (secondary) when it arises in another structure that shares the ear's sensory supply."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s1-2","The rule","Approach to earache · Overview","If the canal, drum and hearing are normal, examine everything that shares the ear's nerves before calling the pain idiopathic or psychogenic, and keep the patient under review. Red flags in earache An elderly or diabetic patient with severe pain, discharge and granulation on the canal floor: necrotising otitis externa . A normal ear with dysphagia, odynophagia, hoarseness, weight loss or a neck lump, especially in a smoker or drinker: referred pain from upper aerodigestive tract cancer. Facial weakness, vertigo or hearing loss with ear pain: Ramsay Hunt syndrome ( Facial nerve palsy ), a complication of otitis media ( Intracranial complications of otitis media ), or a tumour ( External ear tumours and bony growths ). Pain with jaw claudication or scalp tenderness in an older patient: giant cell arteritis. Swelling behind the ear pushing the pinna forward: Acute, coalescent and masked mastoiditis ."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s2-1","Five nerves","Approach to earache · Nerve supply","Nerves of the ear and where their pain comes from Nerve Supplies in the ear Referred from Trigeminal (auriculotemporal) Tragus, anterior canal and drum Teeth, jaw joint, mouth, tongue, sinuses, parotid Facial Concha and posterior canal (sensory branch) Geniculate ganglion, herpes zoster Glossopharyngeal (tympanic) Middle ear Tonsil, base of tongue, pharynx, styloid, after tonsillectomy Vagus (auricular, Arnold's) Concha, posterior canal Larynx, hypopharynx, vallecula, oesophagus C2 and C3 Back of the pinna and mastoid skin Cervical spine, neck injury Figure 1 Sensory nerve supply of the pinna. Dhingra, fig. 1.6 Figure 2 Sensory supply of the canal and drum, and of the auricle. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s3-1","Causes in the ear","Approach to earache · Primary otalgia","The pinna ( Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma ) gives perichondritis, cellulitis, trauma, frostbite, chondrodermatitis nodularis helicis (a tender nodule on the helix that wakes the patient when lying on that side) and skin cancer. The canal ( Otitis externa and skull base osteomyelitis , Wax, foreign bodies and canal stenosis ) gives acute otitis externa, furunculosis (exquisite pain, a swelling in the outer canal), otomycosis, impacted wax or foreign body, necrotising otitis externa, keratosis obturans and carcinoma. The middle ear gives acute otitis media , which in adults is less common and should prompt a look at the nasopharynx , bullous myringitis, barotrauma, Eustachian tube dysfunction, mastoiditis and its complications, and carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s3-2","Herpes zoster oticus","Approach to earache · Primary otalgia","Ramsay Hunt syndrome begins with deep, burning ear pain, often days before the vesicles in the concha, canal or palate, and is accompanied by facial palsy and sometimes hearing loss and vertigo. Early pain without a rash is easily mistaken for otitis externa or Bell's palsy."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s4-1","Through the trigeminal nerve","Approach to earache · Referred otalgia","Dental disease (caries, apical abscess, impacted third molars, cracked teeth) and temporomandibular disorders are the commonest causes of referred earache. Temporomandibular disorders cause preauricular pain worse with chewing, clicking, limited opening, bruxism and tender masticatory muscles; Costen syndrome links a faulty bite with earache, a blocked feeling, tinnitus and headache. Sinusitis, parotitis, oral ulcers and cancers of the tongue and floor of mouth also refer pain this way."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s4-2","Through the glossopharyngeal nerve","Approach to earache · Referred otalgia","Tonsillitis, quinsy , the days after tonsillectomy, cancer of the tonsil and tongue base ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ), and an elongated styloid process or calcified stylohyoid ligament ( Eagle syndrome ), which causes throat and ear pain on swallowing and turning the head, with a palpable styloid in the tonsillar fossa. Glossopharyngeal neuralgia causes paroxysmal stabbing pain in the throat and ear triggered by swallowing or talking."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s4-3","Through the vagus","Approach to earache · Referred otalgia","Cancer or inflammation of the vallecula, epiglottis, larynx ( Cancer of the larynx ), piriform fossa ( Carcinoma of the hypopharynx ) and oesophagus, and reflux ( Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections ). Pain from the hypopharynx is classically felt in the ear, sometimes before any other symptom."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s4-4","Through C2 and C3","Approach to earache · Referred otalgia","Cervical spondylosis, whiplash injury and other neck disease refer pain behind the ear."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s4-5","Other causes","Approach to earache · Referred otalgia","Giant cell arteritis, migraine, cardiac ischaemia (rarely), and nervus intermedius (geniculate) neuralgia , a paroxysmal deep ear pain. Psychogenic pain is diagnosed only after a full search."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s5-1","History","Approach to earache · Assessment","Ask about the character and timing of the pain, its relation to chewing, swallowing, talking and neck movement, discharge, hearing loss, itching and the water history. Ask about voice change, dysphagia, weight loss, smoking and alcohol, dental problems, bruxism and jaw clicking, neck pain and headaches."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s5-2","Examination","Approach to earache · Assessment","After the pinna, otoscopy and tuning forks, examine the teeth and bite, palpate the temporomandibular joints and masticatory muscles while the patient opens and closes, and examine the mouth and tongue. Palpate the tongue base and tonsils with a gloved finger, examine the nasopharynx, and perform flexible laryngoscopy of the larynx and hypopharynx. Examine the neck for nodes, the parotid, the cervical spine and the temporal arteries, and test the cranial nerves. The smoker with a normal ear An adult smoker or drinker with one-sided ear pain, a normal ear, and any throat symptom (dysphagia, hoarseness, a lump in the neck) needs endoscopy of the upper aerodigestive tract. Referred otalgia is cancer until the base of tongue, piriform fossa and larynx have been seen; if the examination is normal and the pain persists, image the neck and consider examination under anaesthesia."],["ev","cl:t-otalgia~s5-3","Approach to earache: current evidence","Approach to earache · Assessment","Referred pain in adults. After dental and jaw causes, cervical spine disease and pharyngeal or laryngeal inflammation follow. The cancer search applies especially over about 45, and NICE NG12 recommends a suspected-cancer referral for persistent unexplained hoarseness or neck lump in people aged 45 and over. Neuralgias. MRI looks for vascular compression or a tumour."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s5-3","Investigations","Approach to earache · Assessment","Investigations follow the findings: an orthopantomogram and dental review, imaging of the temporomandibular joint or styloid, CT or MRI of the neck and skull base, ESR and CRP for suspected arteritis, and biopsy of any lesion. In suspected necrotising otitis externa, CT and MRI with inflammatory markers and biopsy."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~c6","Pathway","Approach to earache · Pathway","From pain to source. Earache 1 Otoscopy and hearing abnormal ear primary otalgia — treat the ear disease diabetic or elderly with granulation necrotising otitis externa pathway vesicles or facial weakness Ramsay Hunt — steroid and antiviral 2 Normal ear examine teeth, jaw joints, mouth, tongue base, tonsils, nasopharynx, larynx, neck, spine 3 Findings dental disease dentist joint tenderness and clicking temporomandibular disorder throat lesion or neck node biopsy and imaging triggered stabbing pain neuralgia 4 Nothing found smoker or drinker with persistent pain imaging and examination under anaesthesia others review, consider neuralgia or neck causes"],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~c7","Management","Approach to earache · Management","Treat the source. Treat the cause found. Temporomandibular disorders respond in most patients to explanation, a soft diet, warmth, simple analgesia, jaw exercises, avoidance of wide opening and gum chewing, and an occlusal splint from a dentist; a short course of low-dose amitriptyline helps chronic muscle pain. Eagle syndrome is treated with analgesia and, if symptoms persist, styloidectomy through the tonsillar fossa or the neck. Glossopharyngeal and nervus intermedius neuralgias are treated like trigeminal neuralgia, with carbamazepine first and microvascular decompression or nerve section for refractory cases. Ear pain after tonsillectomy needs analgesia and reassurance. Pearl Ear pain after tonsillectomy is almost always referred pain through the glossopharyngeal nerve, not an ear infection."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s8-1","Viva essentials","Approach to earache · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The five nerves and the sites that refer pain through each. Referred otalgia in a smoker: exclude cancer, including examination under anaesthesia. Temporomandibular disorders and Costen syndrome. Eagle syndrome and the neuralgias. Primary otalgia red flags: necrotising otitis externa, Ramsay Hunt syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-otalgia~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to earache · For the examination","Common pitfalls Prescribing ear drops for a normal ear. Labelling pain psychogenic before examining the throat and larynx. Missing early Ramsay Hunt syndrome before the vesicles appear."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~c1","Overview","Otosclerosis · Overview","A young adult, a normal drum, and a hearing loss that keeps creeping on."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s1-1","Definition","Otosclerosis · Overview","Otosclerosis, also called otospongiosis , is a primary disease of the otic capsule. Normal dense enchondral bone is replaced by vascular, spongy bone that later hardens again. When a focus reaches the oval window it fixes the stapes footplate and causes a conductive hearing loss; when it involves the cochlea it can add a sensorineural loss."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s1-2","Epidemiology","Otosclerosis · Overview","The histological disease is far commoner than the clinical one. Histological foci are found in about 3 to 4% of temporal bones (up to 8 to 12% in white adults in some series), but clinical otosclerosis affects only about 0.5 to 2% of adults, rising to 3% over 60. It is less common in Black and Asian populations. Hearing loss usually begins between 20 and 30 years of age; onset before 10 or after 40 is unusual."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s1-3","Why it matters","Otosclerosis · Overview","Otosclerosis is the classic cause of progressive conductive deafness behind an intact, normal-looking drum. It is entirely treatable, with a hearing aid or with surgery that restores hearing in nine patients out of ten. But the operation opens the inner ear, and a small number of patients lose the hearing in that ear altogether, so the decision to operate is a careful one."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s2-1","The otic capsule","Otosclerosis · Pathology","The bony labyrinth is formed from three layers: an outer periosteal layer, an inner endosteal layer, and a middle enchondral layer that normally stays dense and inert throughout life. Otosclerosis is a disease of that middle layer. Its favourite site is the fissula ante fenestram , a small cleft of fibrous tissue just in front of the oval window. Figure 1 The bony and membranous labyrinth. The otosclerotic focus usually begins just in front of the oval window, where it can fix the stapes footplate. Dhingra, fig. 13.1 Figure 2 The medial wall of the middle ear with the oval window, and the bony labyrinth: the oval window niche is where stapedial otosclerosis fixes the footplate. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s2-2","Histology: active and mature foci","Otosclerosis · Pathology","Grossly, a focus may look chalky white, grey or yellow; a red, vascular focus is active and likely to progress quickly. 2.2.1 Active (otospongiotic) phase Immature lesions are full of marrow and vascular spaces, with osteoclasts resorbing bone and osteoblasts laying down new bone. Cement substance around the vessels stains blue with haematoxylin and eosin, forming the blue mantles of Manasse , the histological mark of activity. 2.2.2 Mature (sclerotic) phase Older foci are less vascular, with more laminated bone and fibrillar matrix that stains red. Hearing loss reflects where the focus sits, not how active it is."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s2-3","Types of otosclerosis","Otosclerosis · Pathology","2.3.1 Stapedial otosclerosis The commonest clinical form, causing stapes fixation and conductive loss. The focus may be: Anterior: at the fissula ante fenestram, the site of predilection. Posterior: behind the oval window. Circumferential: around the margin of the footplate. Biscuit type: within a thickened footplate, the annular ligament still free. Obliterative: filling the whole oval window niche. Figure 3 Types of stapedial otosclerosis: (A) anterior focus, (B) posterior focus, (C) circumferential, (D) biscuit type with a thick footplate, (E) obliterative. Dhingra, fig. 13.2 2.3.2 Cochlear otosclerosis Foci around the round window or elsewhere in the cochlear capsule cause sensorineural loss, probably from enzymes and toxic products released into the inner ear fluids. It is often combined with stapedial disease, giving a mixed loss. 2.3.3 Histological otosclerosis Foci that touch neither the stapes nor the cochlea remain silent and are found only at post-mortem."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s3-1","Heredity","Otosclerosis · Aetiology","A family history is found in 25 to 50% of patients, about half in many series. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with incomplete penetrance, and several genetic loci have been described."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s3-2","Sex and hormones","Otosclerosis · Aetiology","Women outnumber men about two to one in surgical series, although population data suggest similar prevalence with more severe disease in women. The hearing loss may begin or accelerate during pregnancy, around the menopause, or after an accident or major operation."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s3-3","Associations","Otosclerosis · Aetiology","3.3.1 Osteogenesis imperfecta Brittle bones, blue sclerae and conductive deafness form van der Hoeve syndrome . The otic capsule lesions look identical to otosclerosis under the microscope, and both involve genes for type I collagen. 3.3.2 Measles virus Measles RNA and antigens have been found in active foci, and some argue for a viral cause, as has been proposed for Paget's disease."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s4-1","Symptoms","Otosclerosis · Clinical features","4.1.1 Hearing loss Painless, insidious and progressive, beginning in the twenties and usually affecting both ears, often one before the other. 4.1.2 Paracusis Willisii The patient hears speech better in a noisy room. Background noise makes normal-hearing speakers raise their voices, while it barely reaches the otosclerotic ear. 4.1.3 Tinnitus and vertigo Tinnitus is common, especially with cochlear involvement and active disease. Vertigo is uncommon. 4.1.4 Speech Because the patient hears their own voice loudly by bone conduction, they speak softly, in a monotonous, well-modulated voice."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s4-2","Signs","Otosclerosis · Clinical features","The tympanic membrane is normal and mobile, and Eustachian tube function is normal. In about one in ten, a pink blush shows through the drum over the promontory: Schwartze sign , which reflects increased vascularity in an active focus. Clinical pearl A conductive loss with a normal drum has a short differential: otosclerosis, ossicular discontinuity, malleus head fixation, and superior canal dehiscence. The stapedial reflex separates the last of these (see 5.4 and Superior canal dehiscence )."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s5-1","Tuning forks","Otosclerosis · Investigations","Rinne turns negative first at 256 Hz, then at 512 Hz and finally at 1024 Hz as fixation progresses, so the forks give a rough measure of the air-bone gap. Weber lateralises to the ear with the greater conductive loss. Absolute bone conduction is normal, unless the cochlea is involved. Where the books differ · Air-bone gap that turns each fork negative Dhingra 256 Hz at about 15 dB, 512 Hz at about 30 dB, 1024 Hz at about 45 dB. Lee 15, 25 and 35 dB respectively. In practice Both describe the same sequence; a negative Rinne at 1024 Hz means a large gap."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s5-2","Pure-tone audiometry","Otosclerosis · Investigations","Air conduction is reduced, most at the lower frequencies early on. Bone conduction is normal, but often shows a dip at 2000 Hz: Carhart's notch , about 5 dB at 500 Hz, 10 dB at 1000 Hz, 15 dB at 2000 Hz and 5 dB at 4000 Hz. The notch is a mechanical artefact of stapes fixation, not a cochlear loss, and it disappears after successful surgery. A pure conductive loss rarely exceeds about 60 dB; anything worse means cochlear involvement. Figure 4 Audiogram of a left ear with otosclerosis. Air conduction is reduced with an air-bone gap; bone conduction dips at 2000 Hz (Carhart's notch). Dhingra, fig. 13.3"],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s5-3","Speech audiometry","Otosclerosis · Investigations","Discrimination is good, unless there is cochlear disease. This matters for surgical selection."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s5-4","Impedance audiometry","Otosclerosis · Investigations","5.4.1 Tympanometry Normal early; later a shallow, stiff As curve. 5.4.2 Stapedial reflex Absent once the stapes is fixed. In early disease a characteristic biphasic (on-off) pattern may appear. Pearl A conductive loss with a normal drum and a present stapedial reflex is not otosclerosis. Think of superior canal dehiscence, which produces an inner-ear conductive loss with intact reflexes."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s5-5","Imaging","Otosclerosis · Investigations","High-resolution CT is not needed routinely. It is useful when the loss progresses rapidly, when cochlear otosclerosis is suspected (a pericochlear halo, the double ring sign ), when the gap is doubtful, before revision surgery, and when there are vestibular symptoms."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s6-1","Conductive loss with a normal drum","Otosclerosis · Differential diagnosis","How to tell them apart Clue Key test Otosclerosis Young adult, progressive, family history Absent reflex, Carhart's notch Superior canal dehiscence Autophony, sound- or pressure-induced vertigo Reflex present, low VEMP threshold, CT Malleus head fixation Similar history Malleus immobile on palpation at surgery, CT Ossicular discontinuity Trauma or previous infection Hypermobile drum, Ad tympanogram, gap often over 50 dB Congenital stapes fixation Childhood onset, non-progressive Flat 50–60 dB loss, no Carhart's notch"],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s6-2","Conductive loss with an abnormal drum","Otosclerosis · Differential diagnosis","Otitis media with effusion, adhesive otitis media and tympanosclerosis are separated by otoscopy and tympanometry."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s7-1","The three options","Otosclerosis · Management","Otosclerosis Mild loss Observation review hearing yearly Any loss Hearing aid effective, no risk; the choice for an only hearing ear Suitable gap Stapes surgery closes the gap in about 90% Far advanced Cochlear implant when aids no longer help Each is legitimate. The patient chooses after an honest account of the benefits and the small risk of losing the hearing in the operated ear."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s7-2","Natural history","Otosclerosis · Management","Untreated, the air-bone gap widens by about 1 dB a year, with a further 1 dB a year of sensorineural decline."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s7-3","Medical treatment","Otosclerosis · Management","No drug reverses fixation. Sodium fluoride has been used to hasten maturation of active foci and slow sensorineural progression in cochlear otosclerosis. Where the books differ · Sodium fluoride Dhingra Controversial, and not generally recommended. Lee Widely accepted for progressive cochlear disease, though not FDA approved. In practice Considered by some for active disease with progressive sensorineural loss, a positive Schwartze sign or a halo on CT; not used routinely in the UK because the evidence is weak. Bisphosphonates remain experimental."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s7-4","Hearing aid","Otosclerosis · Management","Offer one to every patient before surgery: an excellent alternative for those who do not want surgery or are unfit for it, and the treatment of choice for an only hearing ear. Conductive losses aid particularly well. A bone-conduction device suits those who cannot wear an aid and decline surgery ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants )."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s7-5","Stapes surgery","Otosclerosis · Management","The fixed stapes is removed, wholly or in part, and a prosthesis is placed between the long process of the incus and the inner ear. 7.5.1 Selection The patient should feel the handicap (air conduction around 30 dB or worse), have a real air-bone gap with Rinne negative at 256 and 512 Hz, and keep good speech discrimination. Where the books differ · Criteria for surgery Dhingra Air-bone gap at least 15 dB, discrimination 60% or more, ideal age 20–30. Lee Air-bone gap 20 dB or more (elsewhere 25–30 dB), discrimination 70% or better, typical age 30–50. In practice Operate when the gap is real (Rinne negative at 256 and 512 Hz) and discrimination is good, on the poorer-hearing ear first. 7.5.2 Contraindications Only or better-hearing ear: use a hearing aid. Active Ménière's disease : a hydropic ear is at higher risk of sensorineural loss. Active middle ear disease or perforation: treat first. Children: recurrent Eustachian tube problems and fast regrowth of the focus. Occupations: divers, pilots and frequent flyers, roofers and athletes (balance), and noisy workplaces, since the stapedius reflex is lost. Relative: otitis externa, exostoses and pregnancy; defer until settled. 7.5.3 Stapedectomy or stapedotomy In stapedectomy all or part of the footplate is removed and the window sealed with a graft; in stapedotomy only a small fenestra (about 0.6–0.8 mm) is made in the footplate. Stapedotomy disturbs the inner ear less, gives better high-frequency results and has largely replaced stapedectomy. 7.5.4 Steps The operation is usually done under local anaesthesia through the ear cana"],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s7-6","Far-advanced otosclerosis","Otosclerosis · Management","Thresholds worse than about 85 dB with unmeasurable bone conduction. Stapedotomy with a powerful hearing aid is tried first in selected patients, then a cochlear implant if that fails; an active middle ear implant coupled to a stapedotomy piston suits patients whose hearing is too good for an implant. Otosclerotic bone can cause facial nerve stimulation by the implant."],["ev","cl:t-otosc~s7-7","Otosclerosis: current evidence","Otosclerosis · Management","Surgery. Large series of small fenestra stapedotomy report closure of the air-bone gap to within 10 dB in about 90% and total sensorineural loss in under 1%, with laser and microdrill techniques giving similar results; stapedotomy has better high-frequency and safety results than stapedectomy in comparative series. Far-advanced disease. A review-based strategy recommends stapedotomy with a hearing aid first when speech discrimination with an aid is reasonable, and cochlear implantation otherwise, noting a higher risk of facial nerve stimulation in otosclerotic ears (Merkus et al., Laryngoscope 2011). Fluoride. A systematic review found only limited, low-quality evidence that sodium fluoride slows hearing deterioration (Hentschel et al., Otol Neurotol 2014); a 2026 meta-analysis of ten mostly observational studies (2352 patients) found fluoride associated with hearing stabilisation, but randomised evidence remains thin (Hughes et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2026)."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s7-7","Historical operations","Otosclerosis · Management","Stapes mobilisation gave only temporary benefit, as the stapes refixed. Lempert's fenestration created a new window in the lateral semicircular canal; it left a mastoid cavity and an inherent loss of about 25 dB, and is now obsolete."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s8-1","During the operation","Otosclerosis · Complications of stapes surgery","Tear of the tympanomeatal flap, which can leave a perforation. Chorda tympani injury, causing a metallic taste in 5–10% long term (up to 30% briefly), worse if the opposite chorda was already damaged. Incus dislocation. Floating footplate, which drops into the vestibule if pushed. Perilymph gusher, about 1 in 300, more likely with a wide cochlear aqueduct, especially in X-linked deafness. Facial nerve injury, more likely with a dehiscent or overhanging nerve."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s8-2","Early after surgery","Otosclerosis · Complications of stapes surgery","8.2.1 Vertigo Usually from surgical trauma, serous labyrinthitis or a prosthesis that is too long. Persistent severe vertigo needs urgent review. 8.2.2 Reparative granuloma Rising sensorineural loss and vertigo at one to two weeks; needs urgent re-exploration."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s8-3","Late complications","Otosclerosis · Complications of stapes surgery","8.3.1 Conductive loss returning A short, loose or displaced prosthesis, or erosion of the incus (the commonest finding at revision, in about 5%). 8.3.2 Perilymph fistula Fluctuating hearing and vertigo, often after straining or barotrauma ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis ). 8.3.3 Sensorineural loss About 2% have some sensorineural loss; about 0.5 to 1% lose all hearing in the operated ear (a dead ear ). A slow high-frequency loss may appear over the years. After stapes surgery Lie the patient with the operated ear up for the first hours; expect mild unsteadiness. Vertigo with nystagmus, sudden hearing loss or facial weakness needs same-day senior review. A delayed facial palsy usually recovers fully within about six weeks on oral steroids. Advise against straining, nose blowing, heavy lifting and flying for about two to four weeks. Avoid water in the ear until the flap has healed. Book an audiogram at about six weeks."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s9-1","Viva essentials","Otosclerosis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Site of predilection: the fissula ante fenestram. Paracusis Willisii, and why it happens. Schwartze sign: active, vascular focus. Carhart's notch: 2000 Hz dip in bone conduction, mechanical, disappears after surgery. Selection criteria and contraindications for stapes surgery, especially the only hearing ear. van der Hoeve syndrome: osteogenesis imperfecta, blue sclerae, deafness. Dead ear risk of about 0.5 to 1% (about 1 in 200), and gusher in X-linked deafness."],["sec","cl:t-otosc~s9-2","Common pitfalls","Otosclerosis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on the only or better-hearing ear. Missing superior canal dehiscence: the reflex is present. Reading Carhart's notch as a true cochlear loss and turning a good candidate away. Forgetting to ask about occupation, flying and diving before offering surgery."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s1-1","How common","Head and neck cancer in children · Overview","Cancer is still the second commonest cause of death in childhood after trauma, and around 12% of childhood malignancies arise in the head and neck. Squamous cell carcinoma is extremely rare in children. The malignancies of the child's head and neck Tumour Typical age Typical presentation Hodgkin lymphoma Adolescents Firm, painless nodes, often supraclavicular; fever, night sweats, weight loss Non-Hodgkin lymphoma 2 to 12 years Rapidly enlarging nodes, Waldeyer's ring, often widespread Rhabdomyosarcoma Under 5 years in almost half Painless mass in the orbit, nose, sinuses, nasopharynx, ear or neck Neuroblastoma Infants, under 1 year Firm fixed neck mass, Horner syndrome Thyroid carcinoma Older children and adolescents Thyroid nodule with cervical nodes Nasopharyngeal carcinoma Adolescents Neck nodes, glue ear in one ear, nasal obstruction Salivary malignancy Older children Solitary parotid mass, rapid growth Where the books differ Oxford Textbook lymphoma 27%, neural tumours 23%, thyroid tumours 21%, soft tissue sarcomas 12% of childhood head and neck malignancies. Oxford Specialist Handbook: lymphoma about 50%, rhabdomyosarcoma 20%, neuroblastoma 6%, nasopharyngeal carcinoma 6%, thyroid carcinoma 4%; rhabdomyosarcoma and neuroblastoma are the commonest under 2. Both agree that lymphoma leads and squamous carcinoma is rare; the difference reflects the series and the age range counted. In practice: think of lymphoma first in a child's malignant neck mass, and of rhabdomyosarcoma and neuroblastoma under 2."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s2-1","History","Head and neck cancer in children · Assessment","Duration and growth, fever, night sweats, weight loss, itching, pain, nasal obstruction or bleeding, one-sided hearing loss or ear discharge, change in the voice or breathing, swallowing, and contact with tuberculosis, travel or HIV."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s2-2","Examination","Head and neck cancer in children · Assessment","Ears, nose, mouth and teeth, and the whole neck, then the axillae, groins, abdomen (liver and spleen) and skin. In the eye look for proptosis, Horner syndrome and heterochromia. Re-examine once fever, pain and dehydration have been corrected, as the signs often change. The benign and congenital masses are in Congenital neck masses . Features that need urgent investigation A node larger than about 2 cm, or one that keeps growing or has not shrunk after about 6 weeks. A supraclavicular node at any size. A hard, fixed or matted mass. Fever, night sweats, weight loss, pallor, bruising, or an enlarged liver or spleen. Stridor, dysphagia, cranial nerve palsy, Horner syndrome or proptosis. A one-sided nasal or nasopharyngeal mass, or an aural polyp with facial weakness. Any large, rapidly growing or unusual lump needs urgent imaging and tissue."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s2-3","Investigations","Head and neck cancer in children · Assessment","Ultrasound first: it tells cystic from solid, shows the architecture of nodes and guides biopsy. Chest X-ray before any general anaesthetic, to look for a mediastinal mass . Full blood count and film, LDH and liver function ; urine catecholamines (HVA and VMA) if neuroblastoma is possible; EBV serology in suspected nasopharyngeal carcinoma. MRI (or CT) of the primary and neck to define the extent; staging CT, bone scan, MIBG scan or bone marrow as the oncologist directs. The mediastinal mass and the anaesthetic A child with lymphoma may have a large anterior mediastinal mass that compresses the trachea. Induction of general anaesthesia can cause complete airway or cardiovascular collapse. Get a chest X-ray first; if there is a mass, discuss with the anaesthetist ( Anaesthesia and the shared airway ) and oncologist, and consider biopsy under local anaesthetic or sampling another site."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s2-4","Taking tissue","Head and neck cancer in children · Assessment","Talk to the oncologist and the pathologist before the biopsy so that the sample answers the question. Excision biopsy of a whole node is preferred for suspected lymphoma: the architecture matters. Send it fresh , not in formalin, so that part can go for flow cytometry, cytogenetics and molecular tests. Core biopsy under ultrasound is an alternative if the pathologist is confident it will be diagnostic. Fine-needle aspiration is of limited use in children: it rarely gives enough for lymphoma typing. A deep primary (nasopharynx, sinuses, ear) is biopsied endoscopically or under anaesthetic."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s3-1","Hodgkin lymphoma","Head and neck cancer in children · The tumours","Commonest in adolescents and young adults. Enlarged, rubbery, non-tender nodes, especially supraclavicular; up to a third have fever, night sweats, weight loss or itching. Staged by the Ann Arbor system after CT of the neck, chest, abdomen and pelvis. Treatment is chemotherapy, sometimes with radiotherapy, and the ENT role is the diagnostic biopsy. Early disease is cured in about 90%."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s3-2","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Head and neck cancer in children · The tumours","The commonest lymphoma between 2 and 12 years. More often widespread at presentation, it may involve Waldeyer's ring (a unilateral enlarged tonsil) and grow very fast, as in Burkitt lymphoma. Immunophenotyping separates B-cell and T-cell types. Combination chemotherapy is the mainstay; prognosis is poorer than Hodgkin lymphoma but good for early disease. The asymmetrical tonsil A child with one tonsil much larger than the other, especially if it grows quickly, looks abnormal, or comes with nodes or systemic symptoms, needs the tonsil removed ( Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess ) for histology to exclude lymphoma. Send it fresh."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s3-3","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Head and neck cancer in children · The tumours","The commonest soft tissue sarcoma of childhood; about 43% occur under 5 years and the head and neck is the commonest site. Histological types are embryonal (about 60%, better prognosis, including the botryoid type), alveolar (about 20%, worse) and others. Sites are grouped as: Orbital: proptosis; the best outlook. Parameningeal: nasopharynx, sinuses, middle ear and mastoid, infratemporal fossa; spread to the skull base and meninges is common, so these are unfavourable. Other head and neck sites: face, scalp, mouth, neck, larynx. In the ear it mimics chronic otitis media with an aural polyp, discharge, bleeding and facial palsy. Treatment is multimodal under a paediatric oncology protocol: biopsy, chemotherapy and radiotherapy, with surgery when the tumour can be removed without disabling loss of function. Five-year survival ranges from about 90% for completely resected disease to about 40% for metastatic disease. The aural polyp in a young child An aural polyp with bleeding, facial weakness or rapid regrowth in a young child is a rhabdomyosarcoma until proved otherwise. Send every aural polyp for histology."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s3-4","Neuroblastoma","Head and neck cancer in children · The tumours","The commonest malignancy of infants under 1 year, from neural crest cells of the sympathetic chain. Only about 5% arise in the neck, where it presents as a firm, fixed, non-tender mass with ipsilateral Horner syndrome and heterochromia of the iris , and sometimes stridor, dysphagia or cranial nerve palsies. Urinary HVA and VMA are raised in about 90%. It is staged with MRI or CT, MIBG and bone scans and bone marrow biopsy. Localised disease is excised; unresectable or metastatic disease is treated with chemotherapy. Cervical neuroblastoma does better than other sites because it is noticed early, and infants under 1 often do well whatever the stage."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s3-5","Thyroid carcinoma","Head and neck cancer in children · The tumours","Mostly papillary carcinoma ( Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre ). A thyroid nodule in a child is more likely to be malignant than one in an adult, and children more often present with cervical node and lung metastases, yet survival is excellent. Previous radiation to the neck is a risk factor. Medullary carcinoma occurs in multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2, where children with a RET mutation are offered prophylactic thyroidectomy. Management is by a specialist thyroid team: total thyroidectomy with the appropriate neck dissection, and radioiodine for selected patients."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s3-6","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Head and neck cancer in children · The tumours","Rare in children ( Nasopharyngeal carcinoma ), mainly in adolescents, and usually the undifferentiated type associated with Epstein-Barr virus. It presents as in adults with neck nodes, a unilateral middle ear effusion, nasal obstruction or epistaxis and cranial nerve palsies. In a child with a nasopharyngeal mass the differential is rhabdomyosarcoma, non-Hodgkin lymphoma and, in an adolescent boy, angiofibroma . Treatment is chemoradiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s3-7","Salivary gland tumours","Head and neck cancer in children · The tumours","At least two thirds of salivary swellings in children are not neoplastic, most commonly mucoceles and sialadenitis, and benign tumours are more often haemangiomas or vascular malformations than adenomas. But a solitary solid salivary mass in a child is malignant in more than half (against about a quarter in adults); series that count all solid salivary tumours give up to half ( Salivary gland tumours ). Mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the commonest malignancy (about half), followed by acinic cell carcinoma. The parotid is affected more than the submandibular gland, and rapid growth without infection suggests malignancy. Management follows adult principles: imaging, fine-needle aspiration or core biopsy, and parotidectomy with facial nerve preservation."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s3-8","Other tumours","Head and neck cancer in children · The tumours","Langerhans cell histiocytosis (lytic skull and temporal bone lesions, otorrhoea, nodes; Cysts and tumours of the jaws ), teratomas and other germ cell tumours, Ewing sarcoma, osteosarcoma, chondrosarcoma, and olfactory neuroblastoma are rare."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s4-1","The multidisciplinary team","Head and neck cancer in children · Treatment and after","Childhood cancer is treated in a paediatric oncology centre under national or international protocols, with paediatric oncologists, radiation oncologists, surgeons, pathologists, radiologists, nurses, psychologists, dietitians and speech therapists. The ENT surgeon diagnoses, secures the airway, provides surgical resection or access when needed, and manages the ear, nose and throat effects of treatment."],["ev","cl:t-paeds_ca~s4-2","Head and neck cancer in children: current evidence","Head and neck cancer in children · Treatment and after","Referral. NICE NG12 recommends a very urgent referral for children with unexplained lymphadenopathy that is enlarging, hard, fixed or associated with fever, night sweats, weight loss or hepatosplenomegaly. Thyroid cancer in children. The ATA paediatric guideline recommends total thyroidectomy for most differentiated cancers because they are more often multifocal and node-positive than in adults, with radioactive iodine selected by risk, and genetic testing for medullary carcinoma ( RET ) in at-risk families, with prophylactic thyroidectomy timed by the mutation (Francis et al., Thyroid 2015). Late effects. Children treated with head and neck radiotherapy need lifelong follow-up for growth, dental, endocrine, hearing (especially after cisplatin) and second cancers."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s4-2","Late effects","Head and neck cancer in children · Treatment and after","Survivors live for decades with the consequences of treatment, so follow-up is long term. Radiotherapy to the head and neck: impaired growth of bone and teeth, facial asymmetry, Eustachian tube dysfunction and glue ear, sensorineural hearing loss, cataract, hypothyroidism, pituitary dysfunction, osteoradionecrosis and second cancers. Cisplatin: permanent high-frequency hearing loss, worse in young children; monitor hearing during and after treatment ( Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer ). Surgery: cosmetic deformity, cranial nerve deficits, swallowing and speech problems. Psychological and educational effects for the child and family."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s5-1","Viva essentials","Head and neck cancer in children · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The commonest malignancies in a child's neck, and that squamous carcinoma is rare. The red flags of a malignant node. Chest X-ray before the anaesthetic for a mediastinal mass. How to send a node for lymphoma: excision, fresh, not in formalin . Rhabdomyosarcoma: types, sites, parameningeal spread, the aural polyp. Neuroblastoma: Horner syndrome, heterochromia, HVA and VMA. Why a solitary salivary mass in a child is worrying. Late effects of radiotherapy and cisplatin."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_ca~s5-2","Common pitfalls","Head and neck cancer in children · For the examination","Common pitfalls Repeated courses of antibiotics for a persistent hard node. Putting a lymphoma node into formalin. Anaesthetising a child with stridor or a mediastinal mass without a chest X-ray and planning. Calling a bleeding aural polyp in a toddler chronic otitis media. Fine-needle aspiration as the only test for suspected lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s1-1","The branchial apparatus","Congenital neck masses · Embryology","Between the fourth and eighth weeks, five (some count six) branchial arches form on each side of the primitive pharynx. They are separated on the outside by clefts lined with ectoderm and on the inside by pouches lined with endoderm, and each arch contains a cartilage bar, an artery, a nerve and muscle-forming mesoderm. The second arch then overgrows the third and fourth to form the smooth contour of the neck, temporarily enclosing a space, the cervical sinus of His . When a cleft, a pouch or the cervical sinus fails to obliterate, it leaves a remnant: a cyst with no opening, a sinus with one opening, inside or out, or a fistula with both. The skin openings always lie anterior to the sternomastoid , and the tract runs deep to platysma. Arches, their nerves and derivatives Arch Nerve Skeletal and muscle derivatives Artery First (mandibular) Trigeminal (V3) Malleus head and neck, incus body and short process, mandible, sphenomandibular ligament; muscles of mastication, mylohyoid, anterior digastric, tensor tympani, tensor palati Degenerates (part of maxillary) Second (hyoid) Facial (VII) Manubrium of malleus, long process of incus, stapes superstructure, styloid process, stylohyoid ligament, lesser cornu and upper body of hyoid; facial muscles, stapedius, stylohyoid, posterior digastric Stapedial (degenerates) Third Glossopharyngeal (IX) Greater cornu and lower body of hyoid; stylopharyngeus Common and internal carotid Fourth Superior laryngeal (X) Thyroid and cuneiform cartilages; cricothyroid, pharyngeal constrictors, cricopharyngeus Aortic arch (left), proximal subclavian "],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s1-2","The thyroid and tongue","Congenital neck masses · Embryology","The thyroid begins at the foramen caecum at about four weeks and descends through the tongue base, passing in front of, through or behind the developing hyoid. The thyroglossal duct that connects it to the tongue normally disappears by the sixth to eighth week. The anterior two-thirds of the tongue come from the first-arch lingual swellings and the tuberculum impar, the posterior third from the copula of the second to fourth arches, and the muscles from the occipital somites, which is why they are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s2-1","Thyroglossal duct cyst","Congenital neck masses · Midline masses","The thyroglossal cyst is the commonest congenital neck mass. It usually presents in childhood but can appear at any age, as a smooth, round, cystic midline swelling of 2 to 4 cm, usually at or just below the hyoid and sometimes slightly to the left. Because it is tethered to the foramen caecum, it rises on swallowing and on protruding the tongue . It may become infected after an upper respiratory infection ( Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre covers the gland itself), and if it ruptures or is incised it leaves a thyroglossal sinus or \"fistula\". It can lie anywhere along the tract: lingual, suprahyoid, at the hyoid, subhyoid (the commonest) or suprasternal. Two rarities matter. The cyst may contain the only functioning thyroid tissue , and about 1% contain carcinoma, almost always papillary. Ultrasound therefore confirms not only the cyst but also a normal thyroid gland , with thyroid function tests; scintigraphy is done if the gland is not seen, and solid areas in adults are sampled by FNA. The treatment is the Sistrunk operation : excision of the cyst and tract together with the central body of the hyoid bone and a core of tongue-base muscle along the tract up to the foramen caecum. Simple cystectomy recurs in up to half of cases; the Sistrunk operation brings recurrence below 5 to 10% (under 5% in most series). Infection is first settled with aspiration or antibiotics, and the operation done afterwards. Figure 1 First, a thyroglossal cyst in the midline of the neck. Second, sites of thyroglossal cysts along the tract (subhyoid is the commonest). Dhingra, figs 77.3 and 77."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s2-2","Dermoid cyst","Congenital neck masses · Midline masses","A dermoid cyst is lined by skin with its adnexa, formed from ectoderm trapped at a line of fusion. It lies in the submental region or the floor of mouth (the sublingual dermoid ), or at the suprasternal notch, and it does not move with tongue protrusion. A sublingual dermoid above the mylohyoid can push the tongue up and must be told apart from a ranula. It is excised through the mouth or the neck according to its relation to the mylohyoid."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s2-3","Other midline masses","Congenital neck masses · Midline masses","A teratoma in a neonate can be huge and obstruct the airway, and when it is found antenatally an EXIT (ex-utero intrapartum treatment) delivery is planned. Ectopic thyroid, a Delphian node, a bronchogenic cyst at the suprasternal notch, and a thymic cyst, usually lateral and deep to the lower third of the sternomastoid, complete the differential."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s3-1","Second cleft anomalies","Congenital neck masses · Branchial anomalies","About 90% of branchial anomalies arise from the second cleft. The cyst usually appears in older children and young adults as a smooth, fluctuant, non-tender swelling at the junction of the upper and middle thirds of the anterior border of the sternomastoid. It often enlarges with upper respiratory infections and may become infected, and its fluid contains cholesterol crystals. The sinus or fistula is seen from infancy as a pit on the lower third of the anterior border of the sternomastoid that discharges mucus. Its tract runs up along the carotid sheath deep to platysma, passes deep to the second-arch derivatives (the external carotid, stylohyoid and posterior digastric) and superficial to the third-arch derivatives (the internal carotid and glossopharyngeal nerve), so that it runs between the two carotids, crosses over the hypoglossal nerve, and ends in the tonsillar fossa . Ultrasound and MRI define the tract, and the anomaly is excised completely once infection has settled. A fistula is removed through an elliptical incision around the opening, with a second, higher step-ladder incision to follow the tract. Recurrence follows incomplete excision or surgery during infection. Figure 2 A left branchial sinus discharging pus. Dhingra, fig. 77.5 The adult \"branchial cyst\" A new cystic lateral neck mass in an adult over about 40 is far more often a cystic metastasis from HPV-related oropharyngeal carcinoma or papillary thyroid carcinoma than a branchial cyst. Examine the tonsils and tongue base, aspirate under ultrasound, test p16 and thyroglobulin, and image before excision."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s3-2","First cleft anomalies","Congenital neck masses · Branchial anomalies","First cleft anomalies are duplications of the external ear canal. Work type I is ectodermal, a duplicate canal running parallel to the external canal; Work type II is ectodermal and mesodermal and contains cartilage. They present as a cyst, sinus or fistula near the angle of the mandible or within the parotid, draining into or near the ear canal, often as recurrent abscesses in the parotid region or as otorrhoea. Because the tract runs close to, and sometimes deep to, the facial nerve, excision needs a parotidectomy approach with the nerve identified first ( Salivary gland tumours )."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s3-3","Third and fourth pouch anomalies","Congenital neck masses · Branchial anomalies","Third and fourth pouch anomalies open into the pyriform sinus , almost always on the left. They present as recurrent left-sided neck abscesses , as acute suppurative thyroiditis in children, or as cysts low in the lateral neck. A third-arch tract runs deep to the internal carotid and glossopharyngeal nerve and superficial to the hypoglossal nerve, piercing the thyrohyoid membrane above the internal laryngeal nerve. A fourth-arch tract passes below the superior laryngeal nerve and loops round the subclavian artery on the right or the aortic arch on the left. The diagnosis is made with a barium swallow, CT and direct laryngoscopy to see the pyriform opening. The sinus opening can be closed endoscopically by chemical or electrocautery, or the tract excised openly with a partial thyroidectomy. Figure 3 The triangles of the neck, and the common carotid artery and its relations, which guide the course of branchial tracts. From the anatomy atlas Branchial anomalies compared Type External opening Internal opening Key relations First cleft Angle of mandible, near the ear canal External auditory canal Close to the facial nerve Second cleft (commonest) Lower third, anterior border of sternomastoid Tonsillar fossa Between the external and internal carotids, over XII Third pouch Lower anterior border of sternomastoid Upper pyriform sinus Behind the internal carotid, over XII, above the internal laryngeal nerve Fourth pouch Low in the neck (rare) Apex of the pyriform sinus Below the superior laryngeal nerve, around the subclavian or aorta"],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s4-1","Lymphatic malformation (cystic hygroma)","Congenital neck masses · Lymphatic and vascular","A lymphatic malformation arises from sequestration of the primitive jugular lymph sacs. About half are present at birth and 90% appear by two years. They occur most often in the posterior triangle and extend into the floor of mouth and tongue, the parotid, the axilla and the mediastinum. Macrocystic lesions, the classic cystic hygroma, are soft, multilocular, compressible and brilliantly transilluminant . Microcystic lesions infiltrate the tongue and face, and the overlying mucosa shows clear or blood-stained vesicles . Both swell suddenly with infection or bleeding into the cysts, and large ones can obstruct labour, the airway or feeding. MRI shows the extent, and when antenatal ultrasound detects a large lesion an EXIT delivery is planned. Macrocystic disease responds to sclerotherapy with bleomycin, doxycycline, OK-432 or ethanol. Surgical excision preserves nerves and vessels; recurrence is low after complete excision and about half after partial excision, and several stages may be needed. Extensive microcystic disease is treated with sirolimus , mucosal vesicles with laser, and airway obstruction with a tracheostomy. Figure 4 A cystic hygroma in an infant. Dhingra, fig. 77.7"],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s4-2","Infantile haemangioma and other vascular anomalies","Congenital neck masses · Lymphatic and vascular","The infantile haemangioma, the commonest tumour of infancy, appears after birth, proliferates for about a year and then slowly involutes. A lesion in a beard distribution means a subglottic haemangioma until proved otherwise, and a large segmental facial lesion suggests PHACES syndrome . Propranolol is the treatment. Subglottic haemangioma is described with congenital lesions of the larynx ; venous malformations, capillary malformations and AVMs, which grow with the child, are covered with the vascular anomalies ."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s5-1","The sternomastoid tumour and the thymus","Congenital neck masses · Other paediatric masses","Fibromatosis colli , the sternomastoid tumour, is a firm spindle-shaped mass within the sternomastoid of a baby two to four weeks old, often after a difficult delivery. It causes torticollis , with the head tilted towards the lesion and the chin turned away, and ultrasound confirms it. Stretching physiotherapy resolves most cases, and persistent cases need tenotomy to prevent plagiocephaly and facial asymmetry. A thymic cyst is a cervical remnant lying along the line of thymic descent from the angle of the jaw to the mediastinum, usually on the left, and it may extend into the chest."],["ev","cl:t-paeds_neck~s5-2","Congenital neck masses: current evidence","Congenital neck masses · Other paediatric masses","Non-tuberculous mycobacterial lymphadenitis. In a Dutch randomised trial, surgical excision cured 96% of children against 66% with clarithromycin and rifabutin, with a better cosmetic result, at the cost of a small risk to the marginal mandibular nerve (Lindeboom et al., Clin Infect Dis 2007); observation alone is an acceptable choice because most nodes resolve over months. Lymphatic malformations. Sirolimus for complex disease is supported by a phase 2 trial (Adams et al., Pediatrics 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s5-2","Nodes and malignancy","Congenital neck masses · Other paediatric masses","Reactive and infective nodes are the commonest childhood neck masses: suppurative adenitis, atypical mycobacteria and cat-scratch disease ( Approach to a lump in the neck ). Kawasaki disease , with more than five days of fever, conjunctivitis, mucosal changes, a rash and a large node, must not be missed, because coronary aneurysms follow when it goes untreated. Malignancy is uncommon but important: lymphoma, especially Hodgkin lymphoma in adolescents, rhabdomyosarcoma, neuroblastoma, thyroid carcinoma, and nasopharyngeal carcinoma in endemic areas. These are described in Head and neck cancer in children . A hard, fixed or matted node over 2 to 3 cm, a supraclavicular node, growth over two to six weeks despite antibiotics, and B symptoms are the warnings that call for biopsy. Salivary masses in children include parotid haemangioma, ranula ( Benign oral lesions and premalignant patches ) and juvenile recurrent parotitis ( Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings )."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s6-1","Viva essentials","Congenital neck masses · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Embryology: the arches with their nerves and derivatives; the pouches and what they form. Thyroglossal cyst: clinical signs; check for a normal thyroid; the Sistrunk operation and why the hyoid is removed. Second branchial fistula: its course and the step-ladder excision. Third and fourth pouch sinuses: recurrent left neck abscess and thyroiditis. Cystic hygroma: features and management. The adult \"branchial cyst\": a metastasis until proved otherwise."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_neck~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Congenital neck masses · For the examination","Common pitfalls Simple excision of a thyroglossal cyst without the hyoid and tract. Removing a thyroglossal \"cyst\" that is the patient's only thyroid tissue. Incising and draining a branchial cyst or atypical mycobacterial node. Missing a first cleft anomaly as a recurrent \"parotid abscess\" or discharging ear."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s1-1","Anatomy with age","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The developing larynx","The infant larynx sits high, at about the level of C2 to C3 (C3 to C4 by other landmarks; Applied anatomy and physiology of the larynx ), so the epiglottis can meet the soft palate and the infant can breathe while sucking. It descends through childhood to about C6 to C7 by fifteen (C5 to C6 in other texts). The infant larynx is about a third of adult size, the subglottis (bounded by the complete cricoid ring) is the narrowest point, and the vocal folds are short with an immature layered structure that matures through childhood and puberty. Babies breathe preferentially through the nose for about the first six months, so nasal obstruction disrupts the coordinated suck, swallow and breathe cycle. Mature chewing and the ability to manage most textures develop by about two years, following motor development more than age. Figure 1 Laryngomalacia: an omega-shaped epiglottis, a reminder of how the infant larynx differs. Dhingra, fig. 59.1"],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s2-1","Causes","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The hoarse child","Dysphonia affects between 4 and 23% of children at some stage, and most causes are benign and self-limiting. Causes of dysphonia in children Group Causes Points Behavioural Vocal fold nodules ( Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness ) Commonest cause; boys, loud voice use; voice therapy works well Infective Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis Second commonest; HPV 6 and 11; may threaten the airway Structural Cysts, sulcus, polyps Stroboscopy shows a disturbed mucosal wave Congenital Webs, laryngeal cleft, subglottic stenosis, cysts From birth; often with stridor or feeding problems Neurological Vocal fold paralysis ( Vocal fold paralysis ) Often after cardiac surgery; Arnold–Chiari malformation if bilateral Inflammatory Reflux, eosinophilic oesophagitis ( Oesophagus and dysphagia ) Arytenoid oedema and erythema Iatrogenic Intubation injury, webs after repeated surgery Granuloma, scar, posterior glottic stenosis Functional Puberphonia, functional dysphonia Normal larynx, abnormal use"],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s2-2","Assessment","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The hoarse child","Listen to the voice for pitch, range, projection, breaks and resonance, and ask about voice use, noisy breathing, feeding, cardiac surgery and intubation. Look for syndromic features. A paediatric flexible nasendoscope (as small as 2.1 mm) shows mobility and gross lesions in most children; rigid stroboscopy is tolerated by mature children from about three or four years and shows the mucosal wave. Laryngeal ultrasound is a useful, non-invasive way to assess vocal fold movement in uncooperative children. A paralysed fold needs imaging of the nerve's course, and a contrast swallow or oesophagoscopy is considered when reflux or eosinophilic oesophagitis is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s2-3","Vocal fold nodules","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The hoarse child","Nodules are bilateral swellings at the junction of the anterior and middle thirds, more common in boys, and caused by voice overuse. They respond well to voice therapy , often regress at puberty in boys, and are almost never operated on in children. Figure 2 The site of vocal nodules, and nodules on both folds. Dhingra, figs 61.1 and 61.2"],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s2-4","Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The hoarse child","RRP is caused by HPV types 6 and 11, usually acquired at birth from the mother's genital tract; juvenile-onset disease typically appears before five years with progressive hoarseness and later stridor, and can be mistaken for asthma or croup. Treatment is repeated microdebrider or cold-steel debulking , preserving normal tissue and avoiding the anterior commissure to prevent webs; KTP laser is an alternative. Tracheostomy is avoided where possible because it may spread disease distally. Adjuvant treatments for aggressive disease (more than about four procedures a year) include intralesional cidofovir , whose small cancer risk must be discussed with parents, and systemic bevacizumab , which has given striking responses. HPV vaccination prevents the disease in the next generation and may reduce recurrences. Figure 3 Laryngeal papillomatosis. Dhingra, fig. 61.11"],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s2-5","Vocal fold paralysis","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The hoarse child","Paralysis is the second commonest congenital laryngeal anomaly ( Congenital lesions of the larynx ). Unilateral paralysis, often left-sided after cardiac or aortic arch surgery (about 5% of such operations), causes a weak cry, feeding problems and aspiration; children having arch surgery should be screened before discharge. Bilateral paralysis causes stridor with a good cry and is associated with Arnold–Chiari malformation and hydrocephalus. Many idiopathic palsies recover, so observation is appropriate when breathing and feeding are safe. Persistent unilateral palsy is treated with injection medialisation (temporary materials such as Gelfoam for about six weeks or hyaluronic acid for months, and longer-lasting calcium hydroxylapatite or fat), which in children needs a general anaesthetic and overnight oximetry, or later with thyroplasty or ansa cervicalis to recurrent laryngeal nerve reinnervation , which gives good results in children."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s2-6","Webs and other lesions","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The hoarse child","Anterior glottic webs may be congenital, often with a subglottic extension and associated with 22q11.2 deletion, or acquired after repeated microlaryngoscopy. Thin webs are divided endoscopically with a keel or mitomycin; thick congenital webs may need an open repair. Phonosurgery in children follows adult principles but is more conservative: cold instruments, no laser on the true folds, and subepithelial infusion to protect the cover."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s3-1","Assessment","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The child who cannot swallow safely","Swallowing problems affect about 1% of children and many more in clinical groups such as prematurity, neurodisability and cardiac disease. A feeding history (breast or bottle, textures, duration of meals, coughing, colour change, vomiting, chest infections, weight gain) and observation of a meal by a speech and language therapist come first. Instrumental tests are videofluoroscopy , which shows every phase including oesophageal transit, and FEES , which can be done at the bedside or during breastfeeding, shows secretions and laryngeal sensation, and avoids radiation; the two are comparable for the pharyngeal phase. They show penetration (material in the laryngeal vestibule down to the vocal folds) and aspiration (material below the folds), which in children is often silent."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s3-2","Causes","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The child who cannot swallow safely","Causes of paediatric dysphagia Level Causes Nose and nasopharynx Choanal atresia ( Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses ), piriform aperture stenosis, mid-nasal stenosis, craniosynostosis, adenoids Mouth and oropharynx Cleft palate ( Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency ), Pierre Robin sequence, macroglossia, tongue-tie (rarely), tonsillar hypertrophy Larynx and pharynx Laryngomalacia, vocal fold paralysis, laryngeal cleft, cricopharyngeal dysfunction Oesophagus and trachea Oesophageal atresia and tracheo-oesophageal fistula, vascular rings, strictures, eosinophilic oesophagitis Neurological Cerebral palsy, hypoxic brain injury, Chiari malformation, neuromuscular disease Respiratory and cardiac Chronic lung disease of prematurity, congenital heart disease (breathlessness, fatigue)"],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s3-3","Laryngeal cleft","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The child who cannot swallow safely","A posterior laryngeal cleft is a failure of fusion between the larynx and oesophagus. In the Benjamin–Inglis classification, type I is confined to the interarytenoid area above the vocal folds; type II extends into the cricoid lamina; type III extends through the cricoid into the cervical trachea; and type IV extends into the thoracic trachea. Type I clefts present with coughing on feeds, chest infections and a weak or hoarse cry, and are easily missed unless the interarytenoid area is palpated at microlaryngoscopy. Thickened feeds help many; persistent aspiration is treated by injection augmentation of the interarytenoid area or endoscopic repair. Deeper clefts present earlier and more severely and need endoscopic or open repair, with tracheostomy and gastrostomy for type III and IV."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s3-4","Other important causes","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The child who cannot swallow safely","Oesophageal atresia with tracheo-oesophageal fistula presents at birth with drooling, choking and inability to pass a nasogastric tube, or later with an isolated H-type fistula and coughing on feeds. Vascular rings compress the trachea and oesophagus, causing stridor and dysphagia for solids. Eosinophilic oesophagitis causes food refusal, vomiting and food impaction in older children. Tongue-tie can interfere with breastfeeding and is divided by simple frenotomy when it clearly does, but it is not a common cause of feeding failure."],["ev","cl:t-paeds_vs~s3-5","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow: current evidence","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The child who cannot swallow safely","Vocal nodules in children. A systematic review found that voice therapy improves voice quality and reduces nodule size in children, although study quality was limited (Adriaansen et al., Int J Lang Commun Disord 2022); surgery is rarely needed and many nodules regress at puberty. Hoarseness guideline. The four-week laryngoscopy rule of the American guideline ( Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders ) applies to children too, to exclude papillomatosis, paralysis or a cyst (Stachler et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018). Dysphagia. Instrumental assessment (videofluoroscopy or fibreoptic endoscopic evaluation of swallowing) is needed before deciding on thickened feeds or tube feeding; type 1 laryngeal clefts that cause aspiration can be treated by injection augmentation or endoscopic repair."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s3-5","Management","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · The child who cannot swallow safely","Management is multidisciplinary: positioning, pacing, bottle teats and texture modification by the speech and language therapist, dietetic support, treatment of reflux, and non-oral feeding by nasogastric tube or gastrostomy when the swallow is unsafe or intake inadequate, sometimes with fundoplication. The structural cause is corrected where possible. The child with a voice or swallowing problem 1 Airway first stridor or distress airway assessment and microlaryngoscopy and bronchoscopy safe step 2 2 Voice flexible endoscopy or ultrasound nodules voice therapy papillomas debulking, adjuvant therapy if frequent paralysis image the nerve course, observe, inject or reinnervate 3 Swallow feeding history and observed meal videofluoroscopy or FEES aspiration thickened feeds, non-oral feeding if unsafe 4 Look for structure interarytenoid palpation for cleft contrast study for fistula, ring or stricture endoscopy and biopsy for eosinophilic oesophagitis nasal endoscopy for nasal causes"],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s4-1","Viva essentials","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why the child larynx differs: position, size, subglottis. Nodules and papillomatosis: the two commonest causes of a hoarse child. RRP adjuvants: cidofovir and bevacizumab; HPV vaccination. Vocal fold paralysis after cardiac surgery; injection medialisation and reinnervation in children. Penetration versus aspiration; FEES versus videofluoroscopy. Benjamin–Inglis classification of laryngeal clefts; palpate the interarytenoid area."],["sec","cl:t-paeds_vs~s4-2","Common pitfalls","The hoarse child and the child who cannot swallow · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating papillomatosis as asthma or croup. Missing a type I laryngeal cleft by not palpating the interarytenoid area. Operating on vocal fold nodules in a child. Lasering the anterior commissure and creating a web."],["sec","cl:t-para~s1-1","Embryology","Hyperparathyroidism · Applied anatomy","The two pairs of glands have different journeys, and their journeys explain where they are found. The superior parathyroids arise from the fourth pharyngeal pouch and travel only a short distance with the lateral thyroid anlage, so their position is fairly constant. The inferior parathyroids arise from the third pouch together with the thymus and descend much further, so they are more variable, lying anywhere from the angle of the jaw to the mediastinum and often in the thyrothymic ligament or the thymus itself."],["sec","cl:t-para~s1-2","The normal glands","Hyperparathyroidism · Applied anatomy","A normal gland measures about 5 × 3 × 1 mm and weighs about 40 mg. It is flat and bean- or leaf-shaped, caramel, tan or mahogany in colour, darker than the paler, brighter yellow fat around it, and it \"glides\" within that fat when gently pushed (the gliding sign ). Most people have four glands and about 5% have supernumerary ones. The glands are usually mirror images of each other, which helps in finding the opposite one. The superior gland lies at the level of the cricothyroid joint on the back of the upper thyroid lobe, about 1 cm above the crossing of the recurrent laryngeal nerve and inferior thyroid artery, and deep (dorsal) to the nerve . The inferior gland lies within 1 to 2 cm of the lower pole and superficial (ventral) to the nerve . Both draw most of their blood from the inferior thyroid artery, which is why its branches are divided on the thyroid capsule at thyroidectomy rather than in the trunk. Adenomas can lie in ectopic sites: retro-oesophageal and retrotracheal, in the anterior mediastinum within the thymus, inside the thyroid, in the carotid sheath, or undescended high in the neck. Figure 1 The inferior thyroid arteries and recurrent laryngeal nerves, landmarks for the parathyroids; and the relations of the thyroid lobe. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-para~s2-1","Parathyroid hormone","Hyperparathyroidism · Calcium physiology","Parathyroid hormone raises serum calcium in four ways: it mobilises calcium from bone through osteoclast activity; it increases renal tubular reabsorption of calcium while increasing excretion of phosphate; it stimulates renal 1α-hydroxylation of vitamin D; and, through vitamin D, it increases absorption of calcium from the gut. Calcitonin from the thyroid C cells has only a minor opposing role in humans. PTH has a half-life of only a few minutes, which is what makes intraoperative PTH monitoring possible."],["sec","cl:t-para~s2-2","Measuring calcium","Hyperparathyroidism · Calcium physiology","Total calcium varies with albumin and is corrected by adding about 0.8 mg/dL (0.2 mmol/L) for each 1 g/dL fall in albumin below normal. When albumin is abnormal, ionised calcium is measured instead."],["sec","cl:t-para~s3-1","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Hyperparathyroidism · Hyperparathyroidism","Primary hyperparathyroidism affects about 1 in 500 women and 1 in 2000 men, mostly after the menopause. About 85% of cases are due to a single adenoma , 10 to 15% to four-gland hyperplasia (sporadic, familial, or part of MEN 1 or MEN 2A; the syndromes are tabulated in Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre ), 2 to 5% to double adenomas , mostly in the elderly, and under 1% to carcinoma . Neck irradiation and lithium are risk factors. The biochemistry is high calcium with a raised or inappropriately normal PTH, a low phosphate, and a high or high-normal urinary calcium. Vitamin D is measured and replaced if low, because deficiency both confuses the picture and worsens bone disease."],["sec","cl:t-para~s3-2","Clinical features","Hyperparathyroidism · Hyperparathyroidism","Manifestations of hypercalcaemia System Features Bones Pain, osteoporosis, fractures; osteitis fibrosa cystica, brown tumours (rare now) Kidneys Stones, nephrocalcinosis, polyuria and polydipsia, renal impairment Gut Constipation, nausea, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis Mind and muscle Fatigue, weakness, depression, poor memory, confusion Other Hypertension, short QT, band keratopathy, gout and pseudogout A hypercalcaemic crisis , with dehydration, confusion and arrhythmia at very high calcium levels, is treated with intravenous saline, a bisphosphonate or denosumab, and calcitonin, followed by urgent surgery."],["sec","cl:t-para~s3-3","Differential diagnosis of hypercalcaemia","Hyperparathyroidism · Hyperparathyroidism","Causes of hypercalcaemia PTH raised or normal PTH suppressed Primary hyperparathyroidism Malignancy (PTH-related peptide from lung, breast, kidney cancers; bone metastases; myeloma) Tertiary hyperparathyroidism Granulomas: sarcoidosis, tuberculosis (excess vitamin D activation) Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia (low urinary calcium) Vitamin D or A excess, milk-alkali syndrome Lithium Thyrotoxicosis, adrenal insufficiency, immobilisation, Paget disease; thiazides Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia (FHH) Cause: an autosomal dominant mutation of the calcium-sensing receptor. Biochemistry: lifelong mild hypercalcaemia with normal or slightly raised PTH, and a low urinary calcium (calcium/creatinine clearance ratio under 0.01). Course: benign. Surgery: does not help. Always check the 24-hour urinary calcium before operating."],["sec","cl:t-para~s3-4","Secondary and tertiary hyperparathyroidism","Hyperparathyroidism · Hyperparathyroidism","Secondary hyperparathyroidism is a hyperplastic response of all the glands to low calcium, mostly in chronic kidney disease , where phosphate is retained and active vitamin D is lacking, but also in vitamin D deficiency and malabsorption. PTH is high and calcium low or normal. It is treated medically with phosphate binders, active vitamin D and calcimimetics such as cinacalcet. Refractory disease with bone pain, pruritus or calciphylaxis needs subtotal (three-and-a-half gland) parathyroidectomy , or total parathyroidectomy with autotransplantation into the forearm, together with thymectomy. Tertiary hyperparathyroidism is autonomous hypersecretion that persists after the cause has been corrected, typically after renal transplantation, and produces hypercalcaemia."],["sec","cl:t-para~s3-5","Parathyroid carcinoma","Hyperparathyroidism · Hyperparathyroidism","Carcinoma is suspected when calcium and PTH are very high (calcium often over 14 mg/dL), when there is a palpable neck mass or a recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy, and when bone and renal disease are present together. At surgery the gland is hard, grey-white and adherent, invading the thyroid or muscle. It must be resected en bloc with the thyroid lobe and adjacent tissue without rupturing the capsule. Recurrence is common, and death usually comes from uncontrollable hypercalcaemia."],["sec","cl:t-para~s3-6","Indications for surgery","Hyperparathyroidism · Hyperparathyroidism","Parathyroidectomy is the only cure, and it is recommended for every symptomatic patient. In asymptomatic patients it is recommended when the serum calcium is more than 1 mg/dL (0.25 mmol/L) above the upper limit of normal, when the patient is under 50, when there is osteoporosis (a T-score of −2.5 or less) or a vertebral fracture, when creatinine clearance (eGFR) is under 60 mL/min or there are kidney stones, nephrocalcinosis or high urinary calcium, and when the patient cannot be followed up. Many surgeons also offer surgery to fit asymptomatic patients, because of the benefits to bone and cognition."],["sec","cl:t-para~s4-1","The tests","Hyperparathyroidism · Localisation","Localisation does not make the diagnosis; the biochemistry does. Its purpose is to plan a focused operation, and it is essential before any reoperation. Ultrasound is cheap but operator-dependent. It shows a hypoechoic oval nodule behind the thyroid, often with a polar feeding vessel, but is poor for retrotracheal and mediastinal glands. Sestamibi (technetium-99m MIBI) scintigraphy relies on the fact that both thyroid and parathyroid take up the tracer but the mitochondria-rich adenoma retains it after the thyroid has washed out. It detects about 85 to 90% of single adenomas, less reliably small adenomas, double adenomas and hyperplasia, and SPECT or SPECT-CT gives a three-dimensional position. Four-dimensional CT shows the typical behaviour of an adenoma, with arterial enhancement and early washout, and is useful when the other scans disagree and before reoperation. ¹⁸F-fluorocholine PET-CT is increasingly used, finding more adenomas when the first-line scans are negative or discordant, and MRI and selective venous sampling for PTH help in difficult reoperations. Concordance matters A concordant ultrasound and sestamibi scan localise a single adenoma in most patients and permit a focused approach. Discordant or negative scans suggest multigland disease or an ectopic gland. These patients need bilateral exploration by an experienced surgeon."],["sec","cl:t-para~s5-1","Approaches","Hyperparathyroidism · Surgery","In focused (minimally invasive) parathyroidectomy , a small incision over the localised gland allows the adenoma to be removed by controlling its vascular pedicle while keeping clear of the recurrent nerve. Intraoperative PTH is measured before and 10 minutes after excision, and a fall of more than 50% from the highest value into the normal range (the Miami criterion ) confirms cure. Bilateral neck exploration identifies all four glands. It is used when the scans do not localise, in multigland disease, in MEN and familial disease, and when the PTH fails to fall after a focused excision. When a gland is missing, the search follows embryology. A missing superior gland is sought in the tracheo-oesophageal groove and retro-oesophageal space along the inferior thyroid artery; a missing inferior gland in the thymus and thyrothymic ligament, by cervical thymectomy, and in the carotid sheath. Intrathyroidal ultrasound or a lobectomy on that side follows, but a blind sternotomy is not performed at the first operation. Hyperplasia is treated by subtotal parathyroidectomy, leaving a well-vascularised remnant of about 50 mg marked with a clip, or by total parathyroidectomy with autotransplantation; thymectomy is added in MEN 1."],["sec","cl:t-para~s5-2","Complications","Hyperparathyroidism · Surgery","Temporary hypocalcaemia is common. Hungry bone syndrome , a severe and prolonged hypocalcaemia with low phosphate and magnesium, follows removal of a long-standing adenoma in a patient with bone disease, as the starved skeleton takes up calcium, and needs large doses of calcium and calcitriol. Permanent hypoparathyroidism follows about 1 to 5% of adenoma operations and more after surgery for hyperplasia or secondary disease. Recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy occurs in 0.4 to 4%, and haematoma can occur. Persistent or recurrent hyperparathyroidism has a short list of causes: a missed or second adenoma, unrecognised hyperplasia, a supernumerary or ectopic gland, parathyromatosis from rupture of the capsule, carcinoma, and a wrong diagnosis such as FHH."],["sec","cl:t-para~s6-1","Causes and features","Hyperparathyroidism · Hypoparathyroidism and hypocalcaemia","Hypoparathyroidism usually follows removal or devascularisation of the parathyroids during thyroidectomy or central neck dissection. Rarer causes are autoimmune disease, DiGeorge syndrome (absent third and fourth pouch derivatives; Congenital neck masses ), magnesium deficiency and infiltration. Symptoms usually appear 24 to 72 hours after surgery, starting with perioral and fingertip tingling and cramps and progressing to carpopedal spasm and tetany, laryngospasm with stridor, seizures, confusion, and a prolonged QT with arrhythmia. Chvostek's sign is twitching of the face when the facial nerve is tapped in front of the ear; because 5 to 10% of normal people have it, it should be recorded before surgery. Trousseau's sign is carpal spasm when a blood-pressure cuff is inflated above systolic pressure for three minutes."],["ev","cl:t-para~s6-2","Hyperparathyroidism: current evidence","Hyperparathyroidism · Hypoparathyroidism and hypocalcaemia","Who to operate on. The criteria under Indications for surgery are those of the Fifth International Workshop on asymptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism (Bilezikian et al., J Bone Miner Res 2022); the American Association of Endocrine Surgeons guideline is the basis for offering surgery to patients who do not meet them, because it is the only cure (Wilhelm et al., JAMA Surg 2016). Focused parathyroidectomy with intraoperative PTH cures about 95 to 98%."],["sec","cl:t-para~s6-2","Management","Hyperparathyroidism · Hypoparathyroidism and hypocalcaemia","Calcium is measured after total thyroidectomy, and a PTH level a few hours after surgery predicts who will become hypocalcaemic and who can safely go home. Mild symptoms are treated with oral calcium carbonate and calcitriol or alfacalcidol. Severe symptoms or tetany need intravenous 10% calcium gluconate, 10 to 20 mL over ten minutes followed by an infusion, with cardiac monitoring and correction of magnesium. Hypoparathyroidism lasting beyond six months is permanent and needs lifelong calcium and active vitamin D, aiming for a low-normal calcium to avoid hypercalciuria, with recombinant PTH in selected cases. It is prevented by capsular dissection, by identifying and preserving the glands with their blood supply, by autotransplanting any gland that is devascularised or removed by mistake, and by near-infrared autofluorescence to identify the glands."],["sec","cl:t-para~s7-1","Viva essentials","Hyperparathyroidism · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Embryology and anatomy: fourth pouch superior glands deep to the RLN, third pouch inferior glands superficial to it, and the ectopic sites. Biochemistry: high calcium with non-suppressed PTH. Exclude FHH with urinary calcium. Adenoma versus hyperplasia; the MEN syndromes. Localisation: ultrasound, sestamibi, 4D CT; why localisation is not diagnosis. Focused parathyroidectomy and the intraoperative PTH fall. Post-thyroidectomy hypocalcaemia: signs and treatment."],["sec","cl:t-para~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Hyperparathyroidism · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia. Treating negative localisation scans as a reason not to operate on proven disease. Stopping after one adenoma in MEN 1, where all glands are abnormal. Rupturing the capsule of a suspected carcinoma or adenoma, which seeds parathyromatosis."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s1-1","The conditions","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Overview","Red, painful pinna Skin Cellulitis, erysipelas staphylococci and streptococci Cartilage Perichondritis and chondritis Pseudomonas Virus Herpes zoster oticus Ramsay Hunt syndrome Autoimmune Relapsing polychondritis also the nose, larynx and trachea Degenerative Chondrodermatitis nodularis helicis painful nodule on the helix"],["sec","cl:t-perich~s1-2","Applied anatomy","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Overview","The cartilage of the pinna has no blood supply of its own and lives on its perichondrium. When pus or blood separates the two, the cartilage starves and necroses. The lobule has no cartilage, so a cartilage disease spares it. Figure 1 The auricular cartilage: everything except the lobule. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-perich~s2-1","Causes","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Perichondritis","Blunt trauma with an infected haematoma ( Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus ), lacerations and burns, high (cartilage) piercing, ear surgery, and spread from otitis externa . Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the usual organism, with staphylococci."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s2-2","Clinical picture","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Perichondritis","2.2.1 Early Pain and a red, hot, tender, thickened pinna, sparing the lobule. 2.2.2 Late Fluctuant swelling as pus collects between perichondrium and cartilage, then necrosis. Figure 2 Perichondritis of the pinna: the whole pinna is red and swollen. Dhingra, fig. 8.10"],["sec","cl:t-perich~s2-3","Treatment","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Perichondritis","2.3.1 Early, without abscess An oral fluoroquinolone such as ciprofloxacin, or intravenous anti-pseudomonal antibiotics for severe infection; cool aluminium acetate compresses. 2.3.2 Abscess Incise along a natural fold, remove dead cartilage, irrigate, and apply a bolster dressing for several days to keep the skin against the cartilage. Antibiotics continue for two to four weeks."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s2-4","Outcome","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Perichondritis","Loss of cartilage leaves a shrivelled, deformed cauliflower ear . Figure 3 A cauliflower ear, the end result of cartilage necrosis after haematoma or perichondritis. Dhingra, fig. 8.7"],["sec","cl:t-perich~s3-1","Cellulitis","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Cellulitis and erysipelas","Spreading skin infection, usually staphylococcal or streptococcal, often after a scratch, piercing or otitis externa. Unlike perichondritis, it may involve the lobule."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s3-2","Erysipelas","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Cellulitis and erysipelas","Streptococcal infection with a sharply defined, raised red edge, which can spread onto the face. Treated with penicillin."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s4-1","Cause","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Herpes zoster oticus","Reactivation of varicella-zoster virus in the geniculate ganglion."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s4-2","Clinical picture","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Herpes zoster oticus","Severe, burning ear pain, followed by vesicles in the concha, canal and sometimes the palate and tongue, then facial palsy. The eighth nerve is often involved, with tinnitus, sensorineural hearing loss and vertigo. Figure 4 Ramsay Hunt syndrome: facial palsy with small vesicles in the right concha. Dhingra, fig. 14.9"],["sec","cl:t-perich~s4-3","Treatment","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Herpes zoster oticus","High-dose prednisolone (about 1 mg/kg for 14 days) with an antiviral such as valaciclovir, analgesia, and eye protection. Gabapentin for post-herpetic neuralgia. Surgical decompression is not advised, because the nerve damage is diffuse."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s4-4","Outlook","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Herpes zoster oticus","Worse than Bell's palsy: only about 60% regain normal facial function. Facial palsy in general is covered in Facial nerve palsy ."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s5-1","Relapsing polychondritis","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Inflammatory conditions","An autoimmune disease of cartilage: recurrent painful red swelling of the pinna sparing the lobule, often both ears, with nasal (saddle nose), laryngeal and tracheal cartilage involvement that can threaten the airway. Treated with high-dose steroids and immunosuppression."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s5-2","Chondrodermatitis nodularis helicis","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Inflammatory conditions","A painful, tender nodule on the rim of the helix, usually in older men who sleep on that side. Relieve pressure; excise if persistent, and send for histology to exclude skin cancer ( External ear tumours and bony growths )."],["ev","cl:t-perich~s5-3","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections: current evidence","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Inflammatory conditions","Perichondritis. The link between Pseudomonas and high (cartilage) piercing is why oral ciprofloxacin is first line. Ramsay Hunt syndrome. Cochrane reviews found no randomised evidence that antivirals improve facial recovery (Uscategui et al., 2008) and insufficient evidence on steroids, so combined steroid and antiviral treatment, started early, rests on observational studies and on the analogy with zoster elsewhere. Relapsing polychondritis affects the cartilage but spares the lobule; it needs rheumatological assessment because airway cartilage may also be involved."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s5-3","Differential diagnosis","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · Inflammatory conditions","Gout (tophi on the helix), lymphoma and skin tumours."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s6-1","On call","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · For the examination","A red, swollen ear Does it spare the lobule? Then it is cartilage. Fluctuant: drain under anaesthesia and bolster. Start anti-pseudomonal antibiotics. Vesicles with facial weakness: steroids, antivirals and eye protection. Eye symptoms: check the cornea."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s6-2","Viva essentials","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why the lobule is spared. Pseudomonas as the usual organism. Drainage and bolster, and why cartilage dies. Ramsay Hunt: vesicles, palsy, eighth nerve involvement, treatment and prognosis. Relapsing polychondritis and the airway."],["sec","cl:t-perich~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Perichondritis, zoster and pinna infections · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using flucloxacillin alone for perichondritis. Leaving pus under the perichondrium. Calling Ramsay Hunt syndrome Bell's palsy because nobody looked in the ear."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s1-1","Definition","Petrous apicitis · Overview","Inflammation, and sometimes abscess, of the air cells of the petrous apex, spreading from acute or chronic otitis media and mastoiditis ."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s1-2","Why only some people get it","Petrous apicitis · Overview","Only about 30% of petrous apices are pneumatised; the rest are diploic (marrow-filled) or sclerotic. Infection can only track into an aerated apex. Figure 1 A pneumatised petrous apex. Dhingra, fig. 1.15"],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s1-3","Why it matters","Petrous apicitis · Overview","The apex lies against the sixth nerve, the trigeminal ganglion, the internal carotid artery and the meninges. Infection there can cause meningitis, carotid injury and skull base osteomyelitis."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s2-1","Air cell tracts","Petrous apicitis · Applied anatomy","The middle ear and mastoid connect with the apex by perilabyrinthine cell tracts: Posterosuperior: from the mastoid and antrum, above and behind the labyrinth, through Trautmann's triangle and the subarcuate region. Anteroinferior: from the hypotympanum and the region around the Eustachian tube, below the cochlea. Figure 2 The air cell tracts of the temporal bone. Dhingra, fig. 1.13 Figure 3 The anterior and posterior surfaces of the petrous bone. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s2-2","Neighbours","Petrous apicitis · Applied anatomy","2.2.1 Dorello's canal The sixth nerve passes under the petroclinoid (Gruber's) ligament, between the petrous tip and the sphenoid; inflammation here causes a lateral rectus palsy and double vision. 2.2.2 Meckel's cave The trigeminal ganglion sits in a depression on the apex; its irritation causes deep pain behind the eye."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s3-1","The triad","Petrous apicitis · Clinical features","Persistent ear discharge. Deep retro-orbital or facial pain, from the trigeminal ganglion. Diplopia from a sixth nerve palsy. The full triad is uncommon today; often only one or two features are present."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s3-2","Other features","Petrous apicitis · Clinical features","Fever, headache, neck stiffness and vomiting suggest meningeal spread; the seventh and eighth nerves are occasionally involved. When to suspect it Persistent discharge or deep pain despite an adequate mastoidectomy, or new pain after the ear has apparently settled."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s4-1","High-resolution CT","Petrous apicitis · Investigations","Shows the pneumatisation of the apex, breakdown of the cell septa and erosion of the bone."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s4-2","MRI with contrast","Petrous apicitis · Investigations","Separates marrow from fluid or pus (diffusion-weighted imaging separates apicitis from cholesterol granuloma, cholesteatoma and effusion), shows dural enhancement and intracranial spread, and assesses the carotid and cavernous sinus. MR venography looks for venous sinus thrombosis."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s4-3","Differential diagnosis","Petrous apicitis · Investigations","Apical effusion: fluid in an aerated apex, usually asymptomatic. Asymmetric marrow: a non-pneumatised apex full of fat that looks abnormal on MRI; a common false alarm. Cholesterol granuloma: bright on both T1 and T2, expanding slowly. Cholesteatoma of the apex. Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and other skull base tumours. Petrous apex lesions on MRI Lesion T1 T2 Diffusion and enhancement CT Asymmetric marrow (fat) Bright, suppresses with fat saturation Intermediate No restriction Normal bone, non-pneumatised apex Trapped fluid or effusion Dark Bright No restriction, no enhancement Intact septa Cholesterol granuloma Bright Bright No restriction; rim only Smooth expansion Cholesteatoma or epidermoid Dark Bright Restricted diffusion Smooth erosion Apicitis Dark Bright Enhancing, with dural reaction Destroyed septa Chordoma or chondrosarcoma Intermediate Very bright Heterogeneous enhancement Bone destruction; clivus or petroclival fissure Cholesterol granuloma is the commonest primary apex lesion, about ten times commoner than apical cholesteatoma, and forms when bleeding fills an obstructed air cell. Asymptomatic lesions are watched with serial imaging; symptomatic ones are drained, preserving hearing where possible, through an infracochlear or infralabyrinthine route, or endoscopically through the sphenoid when the lesion abuts it. Lower cranial nerve palsies with an apex lesion raise concern for malignancy."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s5-1","Medical treatment","Petrous apicitis · Management","Admission, prolonged intravenous antibiotics that cover the organisms of otitis media and penetrate bone, and treatment of the middle ear source with myringotomy or mastoidectomy. Most acute cases now settle on antibiotics alone."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s5-2","Surgery","Petrous apicitis · Management","For an abscess, bone necrosis, or failure of medical treatment. The route follows the cell tracts and the state of the hearing: Hearing preserved: through the mastoid via Trautmann's triangle or the subarcuate cells, infralabyrinthine or infracochlear routes, or through the middle cranial fossa. Dead ear: translabyrinthine or transcochlear routes, which give the widest access. Medially placed lesions: endoscopic transsphenoidal drainage is increasingly used."],["ev","cl:t-petrous~c6","Petrous apicitis: current evidence","Petrous apicitis · Complications","Treatment. Modern case series show that most petrous apicitis responds to intravenous antibiotics with myringotomy or grommet, or cortical mastoidectomy; apical drainage is kept for failure of medical treatment. Gradenigo's full triad is present in only a minority."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~c6","Complications","Petrous apicitis · Complications","Neighbours at risk. Meningitis and other intracranial infection ( Intracranial complications of otitis media ). Injury to or thrombosis of the internal carotid artery. Skull base osteomyelitis. Cavernous sinus thrombosis."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s7-1","Viva essentials","Petrous apicitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Gradenigo's triad, and the anatomy behind each part: Meckel's cave and Dorello's canal. Pneumatisation of the apex in about 30%. CT and MRI together. Surgical routes, chosen by the hearing."],["sec","cl:t-petrous~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Petrous apicitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Reading asymmetric apical marrow as infection. Missing the diagnosis because the ear seemed to be getting better. Waiting for the full triad."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s1-1","Extent","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · The pharynx in general","A conical fibromuscular tube 12 to 14 cm long, from the base of the skull (basisphenoid and basiocciput) to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at the level of the sixth cervical vertebra, where it becomes the oesophagus. It is about 3.5 cm wide at the top and narrows to 1.5 cm at the pharyngo-oesophageal junction, the narrowest part of the gut after the appendix, which is why swallowed foreign bodies lodge there. Figure 1 Divisions of the pharynx and the vertebrae related to the posterior wall: nasopharynx (C1), oropharynx (C2 and upper C3), and laryngopharynx (C3 to C6). Dhingra, fig. 47.3 Figure 2 The divisions of the pharynx, and its cavity in sagittal section. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s1-2","Layers of the wall","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · The pharynx in general","From within outwards: Mucous membrane: ciliated columnar in the nasopharynx, stratified squamous below; continuous with the lining of the Eustachian tubes, nose, mouth, larynx and oesophagus. Pharyngobasilar fascia (pharyngeal aponeurosis): thick at the skull base, where it fills the gap above the superior constrictor, thin below. Muscular coat: an outer circular layer (the three constrictors) and an inner longitudinal layer (stylopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus). Buccopharyngeal fascia: covers the constrictors and continues forwards over the buccinator. Figure 3 Structure of the pharyngeal wall: (a) mucous membrane, (b) pharyngobasilar fascia, (c) muscular coat, (d) buccopharyngeal fascia; the sinus of Morgagni lies above the superior constrictor. Dhingra, fig. 47.1 Figure 4 The layers of the pharyngeal wall. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s2-1","Constrictors","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Muscles and gaps","Each passes backwards to insert into the midline raphe, the lower overlapping the upper like stacked cups. All are supplied by the pharyngeal plexus (vagus). Superior: from the pterygoid hamulus, pterygomandibular raphe and mandible. Middle: from the hyoid bone and stylohyoid ligament. Inferior: from the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, in two parts: the oblique thyropharyngeus and the transverse cricopharyngeus , which is the upper oesophageal sphincter, tonically closed at rest and relaxing on swallowing. Figure 5 The pharyngeal constrictors in lateral and posterior views. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s2-2","Longitudinal muscles","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Muscles and gaps","Stylopharyngeus: from the styloid process; the only pharyngeal muscle supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve. Salpingopharyngeus: from the Eustachian tube cartilage; raises the salpingopharyngeal fold. Palatopharyngeus: from the palate; forms the posterior pillar and, with fibres of the superior constrictor, Passavant's ridge . All elevate the pharynx and larynx during swallowing."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s2-3","Gaps and what passes through them","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Muscles and gaps","Above the superior constrictor (sinus of Morgagni): the Eustachian tube, levator and tensor veli palatini, and the ascending palatine artery. Between superior and middle constrictors: stylopharyngeus and the glossopharyngeal nerve. Between middle and inferior constrictors: the internal laryngeal nerve and superior laryngeal vessels. Below the inferior constrictor: the recurrent laryngeal nerve and inferior laryngeal vessels. Figure 6 The gaps between the constrictors and the structures passing through them. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s2-4","Killian's dehiscence","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Muscles and gaps","A weak triangle in the posterior wall between thyropharyngeus above and cricopharyngeus below. Mucosa herniates through it to form a pharyngeal pouch (Zenker diverticulum; Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch ), and an oesophagoscope can perforate it, hence its old name, the gateway of tears . Below cricopharyngeus lie Laimer's triangle and the lateral Killian–Jamieson area. Figure 7 Killian's dehiscence and the pharyngeal pouch, and Killian's and Laimer's triangles. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s3-1","Components","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Waldeyer's ring","Lymphoid tissue lies throughout the pharyngeal submucosa and gathers into masses that ring the entrances of the air and food passages: Nasopharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) at the junction of roof and posterior wall. Palatine tonsils between the pillars. Lingual tonsil on the base of tongue. Tubal tonsils around the Eustachian tube openings. Lateral pharyngeal bands behind the posterior pillars. Nodules scattered on the posterior wall. Figure 8 Waldeyer's ring. Dhingra, fig. 47.2 Figure 9 Waldeyer's ring seen through the mouth, and the adenoids compared with the palatine tonsils. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s3-2","Immunology","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Waldeyer's ring","B-lymphocytes in the germinal centres produce secretory IgA, T-lymphocytes in the parafollicular areas give cell-mediated immunity, and plasma cells secrete IgM and IgG against organisms that enter the crypts. The tissue is most active in childhood. No lasting immune deficit has been shown after removal of the tonsils and adenoids."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s4-1","Boundaries","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Nasopharynx","Behind the nasal cavities, from the skull base to the level of the hard palate. Roof and posterior wall: basisphenoid and basiocciput, curving down onto the arch of the atlas. Floor: the upper surface of the soft palate in front; behind it the nasopharyngeal isthmus , closed by the palate in swallowing and speech. Anterior wall: the choanae and the posterior ends of the turbinates. Lateral wall: the Eustachian tube opening, about 1.25 cm behind the inferior turbinate, capped above and behind by the torus tubarius ; behind the torus, the fossa of Rosenmüller , the commonest site of nasopharyngeal carcinoma ( Nasopharyngeal carcinoma ); and the salpingopharyngeal fold descending from it. Figure 10 First, the nasopharynx in sagittal section with the adenoids, nasopharyngeal bursa and Rathke's pouch. Second, an endoscopic view of the torus tubarius and the fossa of Rosenmüller behind it. Dhingra, figs 47.4 and 47.5 Figure 11 The lateral wall of the nasopharynx with the torus tubarius and fossa of Rosenmüller, and an endoscopic view. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s4-2","Special features","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Nasopharynx","Adenoids: grow until about six years and then atrophy; the overlying mucosa is thrown into radiating folds. Nasopharyngeal bursa: a midline recess within the adenoid, marking where the notochord touched the pharyngeal endoderm; infection causes postnasal discharge, and an abscess or cyst is Thornwaldt disease . Rathke's pouch: a dimple above the adenoids, remnant of the invagination that forms the anterior pituitary; a craniopharyngioma can arise along its course. Passavant's ridge: a ridge around the isthmus against which the soft palate closes."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s4-3","Lymphatic drainage","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Nasopharynx","To the retropharyngeal (node of Rouvière) and parapharyngeal nodes, then the upper deep cervical and spinal accessory (posterior triangle) chains, often to both sides."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s4-4","Functions","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Nasopharynx","Air conduit; ventilation and pressure equalisation of the middle ear; velopharyngeal closure in swallowing, vomiting and speech; a resonator for the voice (hyponasal with obstruction, hypernasal with velopharyngeal insufficiency); and drainage of nasal mucus."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s5-1","Boundaries","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Oropharynx","From the plane of the hard palate to the plane of the hyoid bone, opening forwards into the mouth through the oropharyngeal isthmus (soft palate above, tongue below, anterior pillars at the sides). Anterior wall: the base of tongue with the lingual tonsil, and the valleculae , each between the median and lateral glossoepiglottic folds, a site for retention cysts. Lateral wall: the palatine tonsil in its fossa between the anterior (palatoglossal) and posterior (palatopharyngeal) pillars. Posterior wall: in front of C2 and C3, with the retropharyngeal space behind. Superior wall: the undersurface of the soft palate. The upper border of the epiglottis and the pharyngoepiglottic folds separate it from the hypopharynx. Figure 12 First, the base of tongue and valleculae. Second, the structures seen in the oropharynx: uvula, soft palate, anterior and posterior pillars, tonsil and posterior pharyngeal wall. Dhingra, figs 47.6 and 47.7 Figure 13 The oropharynx through the open mouth, and the vallecula with the hyoepiglottic ligament. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s5-2","Soft palate","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Oropharynx","A mobile flap hung from the back of the hard palate. When it rises it seals the nasopharynx from the oropharynx; when it falls against the tongue it seals the mouth, so we can chew and breathe together. Muscles of the soft palate Muscle Action Nerve Levator veli palatini Raises the palate Vagus (pharyngeal plexus) Tensor veli palatini Tightens the palate; opens the Eustachian tube Mandibular nerve (V3) Palatoglossus Narrows the isthmus; forms the anterior pillar Vagus Palatopharyngeus Raises the pharynx; forms the posterior pillar Vagus Musculus uvulae Shortens the uvula Vagus Figure 14 The muscles of the soft palate. From the anatomy atlas The uvula in a vagal palsy With a unilateral vagal (pharyngeal plexus) palsy the uvula and palate are pulled towards the healthy side on saying \"aah\". The tensor alone is supplied by the trigeminal nerve."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s5-3","The palatine tonsil","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Oropharynx","Its anatomy, blood supply and bed are described in Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess ; it drains to the jugulodigastric (tonsillar) node ."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s5-4","Lymphatic drainage","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Oropharynx","To the upper jugular chain, especially the jugulodigastric node; the soft palate, pharyngeal walls and base of tongue also drain through the retropharyngeal and parapharyngeal nodes, and the base of tongue drains to both sides."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s5-5","Functions","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Oropharynx","A passage for air and food, the pharyngeal phase of swallowing, shaping of speech sounds, taste (taste buds on the base of tongue, soft palate, pillars and posterior wall), and immune defence through Waldeyer's ring."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s6-1","Boundaries and subsites","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Hypopharynx","From the plane of the hyoid bone to the lower border of the cricoid, in front of C3 to C6. Piriform sinus (fossa): on each side of the larynx, from the pharyngoepiglottic fold to the oesophagus; bounded laterally by the thyrohyoid membrane and thyroid cartilage, medially by the aryepiglottic fold, arytenoid and cricoid. It is the lateral food channel where fish bones lodge. The internal laryngeal nerve runs under its mucosa, so it can be anaesthetised there, and through it a piriform cancer refers pain to the ear. Postcricoid region: the anterior wall behind the cricoid lamina; the site of carcinoma in women with Plummer–Vinson syndrome ( Carcinoma of the hypopharynx ). Posterior pharyngeal wall: from the hyoid to the level of the cricoarytenoid joints. Figure 15 First, the right piriform fossa with its lateral and medial walls, apex and the postcricoid area. Second, the pharynx opened from behind, showing the nasopharynx, oropharynx and laryngopharynx. Dhingra, figs 47.8 and 47.9 Figure 16 The three subsites of the hypopharynx, and the internal laryngeal nerve under the mucosa of the piriform fossa. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s6-2","Lymphatic drainage","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Hypopharynx","The piriform sinus is richly supplied; its lymphatics pierce the thyrohyoid membrane to the upper jugular nodes, which is why its cancers so often present with neck nodes. The posterior wall drains to the retropharyngeal and parapharyngeal nodes; the postcricoid region to the parapharyngeal, paratracheal and supraclavicular nodes."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s7-1","Nerve supply","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Nerves, vessels and spaces","Motor: the pharyngeal plexus (vagus) supplies all pharyngeal and palatal muscles except stylopharyngeus (glossopharyngeal) and tensor veli palatini (mandibular nerve). Sensory: nasopharynx by the maxillary nerve (V2); oropharynx by the glossopharyngeal nerve, the afferent limb of the gag reflex; hypopharynx by the internal laryngeal branch of the vagus. Referred otalgia from the throat The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies the middle ear through Jacobson's nerve, and the vagus supplies the ear canal through Arnold's nerve. Tonsillitis, a quinsy, a post-tonsillectomy throat and cancers of the tonsil, base of tongue or piriform sinus all present with earache."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s7-2","Blood supply","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Nerves, vessels and spaces","The ascending pharyngeal artery, the tonsillar and ascending palatine branches of the facial artery, branches of the maxillary and lingual arteries, and below, the inferior thyroid artery. Veins form a pharyngeal plexus draining to the pterygoid plexus and internal jugular vein."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s7-3","Pharyngeal spaces","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Nerves, vessels and spaces","Retropharyngeal space: behind the pharynx, in front of the prevertebral fascia, from the skull base into the superior mediastinum (to about T2, or to the tracheal bifurcation in some texts); contains the retropharyngeal nodes, which regress after childhood. Parapharyngeal space: beside the pharynx, an inverted pyramid from the skull base to the hyoid; its post-styloid part contains the carotid vessels, internal jugular vein, the last four cranial nerves and the sympathetic chain. Their infections are described in Deep neck space infection . Figure 17 The parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal spaces, and the retropharyngeal, danger and prevertebral spaces. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s8-1","Oral phase","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Swallowing","Voluntary. Food is chewed, mixed with saliva and formed into a bolus that the tongue pushes back. When the bolus touches the anterior pillars, receptors supplied by the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves trigger the pharyngeal phase."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s8-2","Pharyngeal phase","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Swallowing","Involuntary and lasting under a second, while breathing stops: The soft palate rises and meets Passavant's ridge, closing off the nasopharynx. The vocal folds, false folds and arytenoids close the glottis, and the epiglottis tilts down. The larynx and hyoid rise and move forwards under the suprahyoid and thyrohyoid muscles. The tongue base drives back and the constrictors contract from above down. Cricopharyngeus relaxes and is pulled open by the forward movement of the larynx, the most important factor in opening the sphincter."],["ev","cl:t-pharanat~s8-3","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx: current evidence","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Swallowing","HPV and the oropharynx. HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer arises in the reticulated crypt epithelium of the tonsils and tongue base, which explains its small primaries and early cystic nodal metastases; it now accounts for most oropharyngeal cancer in many Western countries and carries a better prognosis, recognised by separate AJCC eighth edition staging. Parapharyngeal space anatomy (pre- and post-styloid) predicts the likely tumour type on imaging ( Skull base and the parapharyngeal space )."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s8-3","Oesophageal phase","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · Swallowing","A primary peristaltic wave carries the bolus to the stomach in three to six seconds, and the lower oesophageal sphincter relaxes. Swallowing nerves Sensation by V, IX and X; muscles by V (jaw, mylohyoid, tensor), VII (lips, face), IX (stylopharyngeus), X (palate, pharynx, larynx, cricopharyngeus) and XII (tongue). Incoordination between pharyngeal contraction and cricopharyngeal relaxation is thought to underlie the pharyngeal pouch."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s9-1","Viva essentials","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Layers of the pharyngeal wall and the sinus of Morgagni. Killian's dehiscence. Waldeyer's ring. Boundaries of the nasopharynx and the fossa of Rosenmüller. Subsites of the hypopharynx and the internal laryngeal nerve. Nerve supply of the pharynx and referred otalgia. Phases of swallowing."],["sec","cl:t-pharanat~s9-2","Common pitfalls","Applied anatomy and physiology of the pharynx · For the examination","Common pitfalls Forgetting that stylopharyngeus (IX) and tensor veli palatini (V3) are the exceptions to vagal supply. Placing the valleculae in the hypopharynx; they belong to the oropharynx. Looking only at the ear in a patient with otalgia and a normal drum."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s1-1","Definition","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Overview","Pharyngitis is inflammation of the pharyngeal mucosa, acute or chronic; when the tonsils are chiefly involved it is called tonsillitis, and the two overlap. Causes of acute pharyngitis Group Organisms and notes Viral (most) Rhinovirus, coronavirus, influenza and parainfluenza, adenovirus (pharyngoconjunctival fever), coxsackie A (herpangina, hand, foot and mouth), Epstein–Barr virus (infectious mononucleosis), cytomegalovirus, herpes simplex, acute HIV infection, measles, chickenpox Bacterial Group A beta-haemolytic Streptococcus pyogenes ; groups C and G streptococci; mixed anaerobes (Vincent angina); Corynebacterium diphtheriae ; Neisseria gonorrhoeae ; Arcanobacterium ; Chlamydia and Mycoplasma ; syphilis Fungal Candida albicans Protozoal Toxoplasmosis (rare) Why group A streptococcus matters It is the cause of rheumatic fever and post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, and of peritonsillar abscess and scarlet fever. Most of the reasoning about swabs and antibiotics is about finding it."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s2-1","Clinical features","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Acute pharyngitis","Mild: throat discomfort, malaise, low fever; a congested pharynx without nodes. Moderate to severe: pain, painful swallowing, headache, high fever; red pharynx with exudate, enlarged tonsils and lymphoid follicles on the posterior wall. Very severe: oedema of the soft palate and uvula, and tender cervical nodes. Viral and bacterial infections cannot be separated reliably by examination. Cough, rhinorrhoea, conjunctivitis and hoarseness point to a virus; fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes and the absence of cough point to streptococcus. Centor criteria One point each for fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior cervical nodes and absence of cough (the McIsaac modification adds a point under 15 years and subtracts one over 45). With low scores streptococcus is unlikely and antibiotics are not needed; with high scores, test or treat. Criteria are a guide, not a diagnosis."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s2-2","Diagnosis","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Acute pharyngitis","A throat swab culture detects about 90% of group A streptococci; a rapid antigen test gives an answer in minutes. Diphtheria needs special media and gonococcal swabs must be plated at once. No growth suggests a viral cause. Monospot or EBV serology and a blood film when glandular fever is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s2-3","Treatment","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Acute pharyngitis","2.3.1 General Rest, plenty of fluids, warm saline gargles and analgesics (paracetamol or ibuprofen); viscous lidocaine before meals for severe pain. A single dose of oral dexamethasone shortens severe pain in some adults but is not routine. 2.3.2 Streptococcal pharyngitis Penicillin V orally for ten days (American guidance) or five to ten days (NICE), or a single intramuscular injection of benzathine penicillin; clarithromycin or another macrolide for penicillin allergy. Where rheumatic fever is common, as in Egypt and much of the developing world, the full ten days or benzathine penicillin is what prevents it. 2.3.3 Others Diphtheria with antitoxin and penicillin or erythromycin ( Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess ); gonococcal pharyngitis with appropriate antibiotics according to local guidance."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s3-1","Infectious mononucleosis","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Viral pharyngitis","Epstein–Barr virus in older children and young adults: fever, severe sore throat with a grey-white membranous exudate on the tonsils, palatal petechiae, large posterior as well as anterior cervical nodes, splenomegaly and hepatitis. Atypical lymphocytes on the film and a positive Monospot (heterophile) test, or EBV serology, confirm it. Glandular fever: three cautions Amoxicillin or ampicillin produces a widespread rash in most patients; avoid them. Airway: massive tonsillar enlargement can obstruct breathing; steroids, and rarely a nasopharyngeal airway or tonsillectomy. Spleen: avoid contact sports for several weeks because of the risk of rupture."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s3-2","Others","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Viral pharyngitis","Cytomegalovirus: a mononucleosis-like illness in transplant and immunosuppressed patients, with a negative heterophile test. Herpangina: coxsackie A; fever, sore throat and small vesicles with a red halo on the soft palate and pillars. Pharyngoconjunctival fever: adenovirus; sore throat, fever and conjunctivitis, sometimes abdominal pain. Acute lymphonodular pharyngitis: coxsackie; white-yellow nodules on the posterior wall. Measles: Koplik spots on the buccal mucosa opposite the molars, three or four days before the rash. Acute HIV infection: a mononucleosis-like illness with rash and mouth ulcers."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s4-1","Candida","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Other acute throats","Oropharyngeal thrush extending from the mouth in the immunosuppressed, debilitated, diabetic or those on steroids, inhaled steroids or antibiotics: pain and dysphagia with white plaques; hyphae on microscopy. Nystatin, clotrimazole, fluconazole or itraconazole."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s4-2","Quincke disease","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Other acute throats","Acute swelling of the uvula, with bacterial tonsillitis or after trauma (thermal burns, heavy snoring)."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s4-3","Angioedema","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Other acute throats","Rapid, painless, watery swelling of the lips, tongue, floor of mouth, uvula and larynx. Allergic angioedema is histamine-mediated; ACE inhibitor and hereditary (C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency) angioedema are bradykinin-mediated and respond poorly to antihistamines. Adrenaline, steroids and antihistamines for allergy; stop the ACE inhibitor; C1 inhibitor concentrate or icatibant for hereditary attacks; secure the airway by awake fibreoptic intubation or tracheostomy when the swelling threatens it. Airway warning signs Drooling, inability to swallow saliva, a muffled voice, stridor or a patient who must sit up and lean forward: do not lie them down or examine with a tongue depressor; call for senior airway help."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s5-1","Types","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Chronic pharyngitis","Chronic catarrhal pharyngitis: congestion of the posterior wall with engorged vessels and excess mucus. Chronic hypertrophic (granular) pharyngitis: a thick, oedematous wall studded with red nodules of hypertrophied lymphoid follicles, hypertrophied lateral pharyngeal bands, and sometimes an elongated, oedematous uvula. Atrophic pharyngitis: dry, glazed mucosa with crusts, usually with atrophic rhinitis. Figure 1 Granular pharyngitis: reddish nodules on the posterior pharyngeal wall. Dhingra, fig. 50.1"],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s5-2","Causes","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Chronic pharyngitis","Infection nearby: chronic rhinitis and sinusitis with postnasal drip; dental and tonsillar sepsis. Mouth breathing: from nasal obstruction (polyps, rhinitis, septal deviation, turbinate hypertrophy), adenoids, protruding teeth or habit, so the throat receives unfiltered, unwarmed, dry air. Irritants: smoking, tobacco and pan chewing, alcohol, highly spiced food, dust and industrial fumes. Laryngopharyngeal reflux ( Chronic laryngitis, reflux and specific infections ). Faulty voice use and habitual throat clearing , which keep the hypertrophic form going."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s5-3","Symptoms","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Chronic pharyngitis","Discomfort, worse in the morning; a foreign body or lump sensation with a constant urge to swallow or clear the throat; a voice that tires and cracks; an irritable cough; gagging on opening the mouth."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s5-4","Treatment","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Chronic pharyngitis","Find and remove the cause: treat the nose, sinuses, teeth and reflux; stop smoking, chewing and drinking. Voice rest and speech therapy for faulty voice use; stop habitual hawking and throat clearing. Warm saline gargles, especially in the morning. Mandl's paint to the mucosa. Cautery of prominent granules with silver nitrate, or diathermy. For atrophic pharyngitis: alkaline sprays and irrigations to remove crusts, Mandl's paint, and potassium iodide by mouth to promote secretion, with treatment of the atrophic rhinitis. Chronic sore throat in an adult smoker A persistent one-sided sore throat, earache, dysphagia, weight loss, haemoptysis or a neck lump is not \"chronic pharyngitis\" until the tongue base, tonsils, hypopharynx and larynx have been examined endoscopically and the neck palpated."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s5-5","Keratosis pharyngis","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Chronic pharyngitis","White or yellow horny spikes on the tonsils, posterior wall or lingual tonsils, firmly adherent, from hypertrophy and keratinisation of the epithelium. There is no inflammation and no fever, which separates it from follicular tonsillitis. It is harmless and often regresses; reassurance is all that is needed. Figure 2 Keratosis pharyngis: keratotic excrescences that resemble follicular tonsillitis. Dhingra, fig. 50.2"],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s6-1","Eagle syndrome (styalgia)","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Throat pain without inflammation","An elongated styloid process (normal about 25 mm; over 30 mm is elongated) or a calcified stylohyoid ligament causes pain in the tonsillar fossa and upper neck radiating to the ear, worse on swallowing and turning the head, often after tonsillectomy. The tip can be felt through the tonsillar fossa; an open-mouth or lateral radiograph or CT with 3D reconstruction shows it. Many people have long styloids without symptoms. Symptomatic processes are shortened through the tonsillar fossa or through the neck. Figure 3 An elongated styloid process: (A) on radiograph, (B) excised after tonsillectomy. Dhingra, fig. 53.6"],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s6-2","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Throat pain without inflammation","Brief, severe, electric pain in the tonsil, tongue base and ear, triggered by swallowing or yawning; carbamazepine, and microvascular decompression for resistant cases."],["ev","cl:t-pharyngitis~s6-3","Acute and chronic pharyngitis: current evidence","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Throat pain without inflammation","Who needs an antibiotic. NICE (NG84, 2018) uses FeverPAIN or Centor scores: FeverPAIN 0 to 1 or Centor 0 to 2, no antibiotic; FeverPAIN 2 to 3, consider no or a back-up prescription; FeverPAIN 4 to 5 or Centor 3 to 4, consider an immediate antibiotic, phenoxymethylpenicillin for five to ten days. The American guideline recommends a rapid antigen test or culture before treating, and penicillin or amoxicillin for ten days for confirmed group A streptococcus (Shulman et al., Clin Infect Dis 2012). Benefit. A Cochrane review found that antibiotics shorten symptoms by about 16 hours on average and reduce suppurative complications and rheumatic fever, but the absolute benefit is small in high-income settings (Spinks et al., 2021). Course length. In a Swedish trial, penicillin V four times daily for five days was non-inferior to three times daily for ten days in confirmed streptococcal pharyngotonsillitis (Skoog Ståhlgren et al., BMJ 2019). Steroids. In the TOAST trial, a single 10 mg dose of oral dexamethasone did not increase complete resolution at 24 hours but did at 48 hours (Hayward et al., JAMA 2017)."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s6-3","Globus","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · Throat pain without inflammation","A persistent lump feeling in the throat between meals, with normal swallowing of food. Examine fully and endoscopically; reflux and anxiety are common associations ( Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch covers dysphagia), and red flags (pain, dysphagia, weight loss, voice change, neck mass) call for investigation."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s7-1","Viva essentials","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes of acute pharyngitis, and why group A streptococcus matters. Centor criteria, swabs and rapid antigen tests. Infectious mononucleosis: features and the amoxicillin rash. Chronic pharyngitis: types, causes and treatment. Keratosis pharyngis versus follicular tonsillitis. Eagle syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-pharyngitis~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Acute and chronic pharyngitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Giving amoxicillin to a young adult with an exudative throat and big posterior nodes. Stopping penicillin after three days because the throat feels better. Labelling an adult smoker's persistent throat pain as chronic pharyngitis without endoscopy. Treating ACE inhibitor angioedema with antihistamines alone."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s1-1","Applied anatomy","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Overview","A single plate of elastic cartilage covered by skin that is tightly adherent on the lateral surface. The lobule has no cartilage. The incisura terminalis , between the tragus and the crus of the helix, also has no cartilage, which is why an endaural incision there avoids cutting it. Figure 1 (A) The divisions of the ear. (B) Elevations and depressions of the lateral surface of the pinna. (C) The auricular cartilage. Dhingra, fig. 1.1 Figure 2 Surface landmarks of the auricle, and its nerve supply. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s1-2","Development","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Overview","The pinna develops from six hillocks of His around the first branchial cleft. The tragus comes from the first arch and most of the rest from the second. It reaches its adult shape by about 20 weeks of gestation and most of its adult size by about nine years. Figure 3 Development of the pinna from six hillocks around the first branchial cleft. Dhingra, fig. 1.24 Where the books differ · Hillocks Dhingra The tragus from the first arch; the rest from the second arch. Lee The first three hillocks from the first arch, the last three from the second. In practice Both put the tragus on the first arch and most of the pinna on the second."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~c2","Congenital anomalies","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Congenital anomalies","From a tag to an absent ear. Figure 4 A normal pinna. Dhingra, fig. 8.1"],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s2-1","Absent or small","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Congenital anomalies","2.1.1 Anotia Complete absence of the pinna, often with an absent canal. 2.1.2 Microtia A small, malformed pinna, graded from mild deformity to a peanut-shaped vestige. It is often associated with canal atresia and middle ear anomalies, so hearing must be tested ( Microtia and congenital canal atresia ). Figure 5 First, anotia: no pinna or canal on the left. Second, microtia of the right ear (a peanut ear). Dhingra, figs 8.2 and 8.3"],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s2-2","Abnormal shape","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Congenital anomalies","Bat ear (prominent ear): the commonest anomaly, from an underdeveloped antihelical fold or deep concha. The normal angle between the pinna and the head is about 15 to 30 degrees. Cup and lop ear: folding or deficiency of the upper pinna. Cryptotia: the upper pinna is buried under the scalp skin. Stahl's ear: an extra crus giving a pointed helix. Satyr ear: a pointed upper pole. Darwin's tubercle: a small, harmless nodule on the helix. Coloboma: a cleft in the pinna. Figure 6 Stahl's ear, with an extra crus. Dhingra, fig. 8.4"],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s2-3","Accessory tissue","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Congenital anomalies","Preauricular tags: may contain cartilage. Preauricular pit or sinus: a small opening in front of the crus of the helix, from faulty fusion of the hillocks. It may become repeatedly infected. Figure 7 First, preauricular appendages. Second, an infected preauricular sinus discharging pus. Dhingra, figs 8.5 and 8.6"],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s2-4","Treatment","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Congenital anomalies","Bat ear: otoplasty, usually after age six, before teasing at school. Stahl's and other shape anomalies: a mould in the first six weeks of life, while the cartilage is soft, can correct them without surgery. Preauricular sinus: antibiotics for infection; excision of the whole tract when it has been repeatedly infected ( Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus )."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s3-1","Cause","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Haematoma","Blunt trauma from boxing, wrestling or rugby shears the perichondrium from the cartilage, and blood collects between them."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s3-2","Picture","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Haematoma","A tense, fluctuant, smooth swelling on the lateral surface that obliterates the landmarks."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s3-3","Treatment","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Haematoma","Drain early: aspiration for small, fresh collections, or incision for larger or recurrent ones. Then apply a pressure bolster with the concavities packed, through-and-through sutures or a moulded dressing, give antibiotics, and recheck for reaccumulation. The choice of method, the bolster and aftercare are covered in detail in Pinna haematoma and preauricular sinus ."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s3-4","Complication","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Haematoma","Untreated or infected, the cartilage necroses and new cartilage forms irregularly, giving a cauliflower ear . Figure 8 A cauliflower (boxer's) ear. Dhingra, fig. 8.7"],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s4-1","Lacerations","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Other injuries","Repair early, covering the cartilage with skin and keeping the perichondrium attached. Figure 9 A laceration of the left pinna. Dhingra, fig. 8.8"],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s4-2","Avulsion","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Other injuries","A partly avulsed segment on a pedicle is reattached. A completely avulsed part may be reattached as a composite graft if small, banked under postauricular skin, or replanted microvascularly."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s4-3","Bites","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Other injuries","The lobe is the commonest site; human bites are the most likely to become infected. Clean thoroughly, give antibiotics and repair."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s4-4","Frostbite","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Other injuries","The pinna is exposed and thin. Rewarm with moist pledgets at 38 to 42°C, give analgesia and antibiotics, avoid pressure dressings, and delay debridement for weeks to months until the dead tissue demarcates. Where the books differ · Rewarming Dhingra Moist pledgets at 38 to 42°C, noting that rapid rewarming is painful. Lee Favours slow rewarming. In practice Rewarm with moist pledgets at 38 to 42°C and give good analgesia, because rewarming hurts."],["ev","cl:t-pinna~s4-5","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma: current evidence","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Other injuries","Haematoma. A Cochrane review found no randomised trials comparing needle aspiration with incision and drainage, or different pressure dressings (Jones and Mahendran, 2004); case series favour incision and drainage with a bolster or through-and-through sutures, which reduce reaccumulation. Microtia. Autologous rib cartilage reconstruction, usually from about age eight to ten, remains the standard; porous polyethylene frameworks allow earlier, single-stage reconstruction but carry a higher risk of exposure. Prominent ears. Ear moulding in the first weeks of life corrects many deformities without surgery."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s4-5","Keloid","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · Other injuries","After piercing, often in darker skin. Excision alone recurs; combine with steroid injection, pressure or radiotherapy ( External ear tumours and bony growths )."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s5-1","On call","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · For the examination","A swollen pinna after a blow Aspirate or incise the haematoma. Pressure bolster with the concavities packed. Antibiotics; check for reaccumulation within a day or two, and review the bolster at 4 to 7 days. Lacerations: repair early, covering the cartilage."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s5-2","Viva essentials","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Development from the hillocks of His. Congenital anomalies, and the timing of otoplasty. Haematoma: why drain, and how to prevent cauliflower ear. Microtia: test the hearing and examine the canal and middle ear. Grafts from the pinna: tragal and conchal cartilage, perichondrium and lobule fat."],["sec","cl:t-pinna~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Pinna: congenital anomalies, haematoma and trauma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Leaving a haematoma to resolve. Draining without a pressure dressing. Excising a keloid without adjuvant treatment."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s1-1","The sella","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Anatomy","The pituitary lies in the sella turcica , a saddle in the body of the sphenoid, roofed by the diaphragma sellae through which the stalk passes up to the hypothalamus. The optic chiasm lies just above, so an expanding tumour presses on the crossing nasal fibres and causes a bitemporal hemianopia ."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s1-2","The cavernous sinuses","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Anatomy","On each side lies a cavernous sinus containing the internal carotid artery. Cranial nerves III, IV, V1 and V2 run in its lateral wall and VI runs free inside the sinus beside the carotid, which is why the abducens nerve is often the first to fail when a tumour spreads laterally."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s1-3","The sphenoid sinus","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Anatomy","The sinus is the corridor. Its pneumatisation decides how easy access is: sellar (most adults, the sinus extends below the sella), presellar (stops in front of it) or conchal (little air, mostly in children), in which the bone has to be drilled. In the lateral wall lie the bulges of the carotid artery and optic nerve, with the opticocarotid recess between them. The intersinus septum is often off the midline and may insert on the carotid prominence, so it is never twisted or fractured off blindly. A posterior ethmoid ( Onodi ) cell can run above the sphenoid and carry the optic nerve in its wall. Read the CT before the operation Look at the pneumatisation, where the intersinus septa insert, whether the carotid or optic canals are dehiscent, and whether there is an Onodi cell. These four answers decide where it is safe to open bone."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s1-4","Physiology","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Anatomy","The anterior pituitary secretes growth hormone, prolactin, ACTH, TSH, LH and FSH; the posterior pituitary stores ADH and oxytocin made in the hypothalamus. Prolactin is the only hormone under tonic inhibition by hypothalamic dopamine, so anything that compresses the stalk raises prolactin modestly (the stalk effect , usually under about 200 ng/ml), while the other hormones fall."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s2-1","Epidemiology","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Pituitary adenoma","About 90% of sellar tumours and 10 to 15% of intracranial tumours. Benign (WHO grade 1). Microadenomas are under 10 mm and macroadenomas 10 mm or more; giant adenomas exceed 4 cm."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s2-2","Functioning and non-functioning","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Pituitary adenoma","Most adenomas secrete a hormone. Prolactinomas are the commonest functioning type, followed by growth hormone and ACTH secreting adenomas; TSH secreting adenomas are rare. Non-functioning adenomas present late, as macroadenomas, through mass effect, and may raise prolactin slightly by the stalk effect. Adenomas can be part of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 with parathyroid and pancreatic islet tumours."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s2-3","Presentation","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Pituitary adenoma","How adenomas present Cause Features Mass effect Bitemporal hemianopia, headache, hypopituitarism (tiredness, low libido, amenorrhoea, hypothyroidism), cranial nerve palsies from cavernous sinus spread Prolactinoma Amenorrhoea, galactorrhoea, loss of libido, infertility, later osteoporosis; women notice it earlier Growth hormone (acromegaly) Enlarging hands, feet and jaw, coarse features, macroglossia, sweating, arthropathy, obstructive sleep apnoea, hypertension and diabetes ACTH (Cushing disease) Weight gain, striae, bruising, hirsutism, hypertension, diabetes, poor wound healing TSH Hyperthyroidism with a goitre and an unsuppressed TSH Acromegaly in the ENT clinic Acromegaly can present to ENT with snoring and sleep apnoea, a deepening voice, a large tongue or nasal obstruction from mucosal and turbinate hypertrophy. It also means a difficult airway at intubation."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s2-4","Pituitary apoplexy","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Pituitary adenoma","Sudden haemorrhage or infarction of an adenoma: a thunderclap headache, vomiting, visual loss, ophthalmoplegia, drowsiness and acute hypopituitarism, which can look like a subarachnoid haemorrhage. Suspected pituitary apoplexy Resuscitate and give intravenous hydrocortisone at once; an adrenal crisis kills. Check sodium, glucose, cortisol and the other pituitary hormones. Urgent MRI, or CT if MRI is not available. Formal visual acuity and fields, and eye movements. Neurosurgical and endocrine review; urgent decompression for reduced acuity, severe field loss or falling conscious level. Eye movement palsies alone often recover without surgery."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s3-1","Rathke cleft cyst","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Other sellar lesions","A benign epithelium-lined cyst from the remnant of Rathke's pouch, found in about 15% of people at post mortem but symptomatic in only about 2%. Centrally placed, non-enhancing, with signal that varies with its contents. Most are watched with yearly MRI; surgery is for visual loss or endocrine failure."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s3-2","Craniopharyngioma","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Other sellar lesions","A tumour of the squamous remnant of Rathke's pouch along the stalk, with two age peaks (childhood and middle age). The adamantinomatous type of children is cystic, calcified (about 90%) and wraps around vessels and nerves; the papillary type of adults is solid. It presents with visual loss, hypopituitarism, growth failure and diabetes insipidus. Complete removal is often impossible, so radiotherapy is added. Afterwards diabetes insipidus is common and usually permanent, most patients need hydrocortisone and thyroxine, and hypothalamic damage can cause uncontrollable appetite."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s3-3","Meningioma","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Other sellar lesions","Arising from the tuberculum sellae, planum or diaphragma, it enhances uniformly and often shows a dural tail . It causes visual loss with relatively preserved pituitary function. Surgery is for a deficit or growth that threatens one."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s3-4","Empty sella","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Other sellar lesions","The sella is filled with CSF and the gland is flattened. Primary empty sella comes from a defect in the diaphragma, often with raised intracranial pressure, and is linked to spontaneous CSF leaks ( CSF rhinorrhoea ). Secondary empty sella follows surgery, radiotherapy or apoplexy."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s3-5","Others","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Other sellar lesions","Chordoma of the clivus, chondrosarcoma, metastases, lymphocytic hypophysitis, and an internal carotid aneurysm, which must never be mistaken for a tumour."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s4-1","Blood tests","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Investigation","Prolactin, morning cortisol and ACTH, growth hormone and IGF-1, TSH and free T4, LH, FSH, testosterone or oestradiol, and sodium; paired plasma and urine osmolality if there is thirst or polyuria. Dynamic tests (suppression and stimulation tests) are arranged by the endocrinologist."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s4-2","Vision","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Investigation","Visual acuity, colour vision and formal perimetry. A field defect is the commonest reason for surgery in a non-functioning adenoma and the baseline for follow-up."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s4-3","Imaging","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Investigation","MRI with gadolinium is the key test. A microadenoma is a small area that enhances less than the normal gland, often seen only after contrast. A macroadenoma is usually iso- to hypointense on T1 and may be dumbbell-shaped where the diaphragma pinches it. Blood within it is bright on T1. CT shows the sphenoid anatomy, septa and bony dehiscence for planning and navigation, and calcification in a craniopharyngioma. CT or MR angiography if a lesion might be an aneurysm or the carotids are close together (\"kissing carotids\")."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s5-1","Prolactinoma","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Treatment","Treated first with a dopamine agonist , cabergoline first or bromocriptine, which shrinks the tumour and restores fertility in most patients, even for macroadenomas with visual loss. Surgery is for intolerance, resistance, visual loss that does not improve rapidly, or patient choice, and is increasingly offered as a first option for small, well-placed adenomas."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s5-2","Non-functioning adenoma","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Treatment","Small, asymptomatic tumours are watched with MRI and visual fields. Surgery is indicated for visual compromise, growth, or apoplexy."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s5-3","Growth hormone, ACTH and TSH adenomas","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Treatment","Surgery is the first treatment. Medical therapy and radiotherapy are used for residual or recurrent disease."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s5-4","Radiotherapy","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · Treatment","Fractionated radiotherapy or stereotactic radiosurgery for residual or recurrent tumour; hypopituitarism develops over the following years. First treatment by lesion Lesion First treatment Prolactinoma Dopamine agonist Non-functioning microadenoma, no symptoms Observation with MRI Non-functioning macroadenoma with visual loss Surgery Acromegaly, Cushing disease, TSH adenoma Surgery Rathke cleft cyst, no symptoms Yearly MRI Craniopharyngioma Surgery, often with radiotherapy Apoplexy Hydrocortisone, then decompression if vision or consciousness is affected"],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s6-1","The team","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · The operation","Pituitary surgery is a two-surgeon operation: the otolaryngologist provides and closes the access, the neurosurgeon removes the tumour, working through both nostrils with four hands. The endoscopic endonasal approach has largely replaced the older microscopic sublabial and transseptal routes."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s6-2","Steps in outline","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · The operation","Decongest; in most cases raise a nasoseptal flap on the posterior septal artery, or keep a \"rescue\" flap available. Lateralise the turbinates, remove the posterior part of the septum and open both sphenoid ostia widely. Take down the intersinus septa carefully, with drills rather than twisting. Identify the sella, carotid and optic prominences and the opticocarotid recesses. Open the sellar floor and dura, and remove the tumour. Reconstruct: free grafts for small defects, the nasoseptal flap for larger ones or a high-flow CSF leak."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s6-3","Extended approaches","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · The operation","The transplanum and transtuberculum approaches reach suprasellar extension and giant adenomas compressing the optic nerves; the transcavernous and transclival routes go further. These are tertiary skull base procedures ( Skull base and the parapharyngeal space ); the endoscopic technique in general is covered in Nose and sinus operations: principles ."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s6-4","Outcomes","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · The operation","Compared with the microscopic approaches, the endoscopic approach gives more complete removal and fewer septal perforations, with similar rates of meningitis, diabetes insipidus and endocrine problems."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s7-1","Diabetes insipidus","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · After the operation","Damage to the stalk or posterior pituitary stops ADH release: large volumes of dilute urine, thirst and a rising sodium. Monitor fluid balance, urine output and specific gravity, and serum sodium (for example every 6 hours at first). Urine specific gravity below 1.003 with a sodium above 145 mmol/l suggests it; treat with desmopressin."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s7-2","SIADH","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · After the operation","Some patients develop delayed hyponatraemia about a week after surgery from release of stored ADH, sometimes after an initial phase of diabetes insipidus. Check sodium after discharge as well."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s7-3","Steroid cover","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · After the operation","Give perioperative hydrocortisone to patients who are, or may become, ACTH deficient, and test the axis before stopping it."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s7-4","The nose","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · After the operation","Advise against nose blowing, straining and drinking through straws while the repair heals. Nasal douching and debridement reduce crusting and synechiae."],["ev","cl:t-pituitary~s7-5","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions: current evidence","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · After the operation","Prolactinoma. Pituitary Society consensus (Petersenn et al., Nat Rev Endocrinol 2023). Apoplexy. UK guideline (Rajasekaran et al., Clin Endocrinol 2011). Endoscopic or microscopic. Meta-analyses show similar or slightly better resection with the endoscope, fewer nasal complications and a shorter stay."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s7-5","Complications","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · After the operation","Complications to know Complication Notes CSF leak and meningitis The commonest serious problem; reduced by multilayer and flap repair Carotid injury Pack with pressure, then a crushed muscle patch, then urgent angiography for a pseudoaneurysm Visual loss From haematoma or chiasmal injury; urgent imaging Tension pneumocephalus Sudden confusion and falling consciousness; CT shows the \"Mount Fuji\" sign Hypopituitarism, diabetes insipidus, SIADH Endocrine follow-up Epistaxis Often from the sphenopalatine or posterior septal artery Nasal morbidity Crusting, synechiae, septal perforation, reduced smell"],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s8-1","Viva essentials","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Relations of the sella: chiasm above, cavernous sinuses with the carotid and nerves III to VI at the sides. Prolactinoma is treated with tablets ; other functioning adenomas with surgery. The stalk effect and why prolactin rises with a non-functioning adenoma. Pituitary apoplexy and the first dose of hydrocortisone. The sphenoid on CT : pneumatisation, septa on the carotid, Onodi cell. Diabetes insipidus and SIADH after surgery."],["sec","cl:t-pituitary~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a prolactinoma that tablets would have shrunk. Twisting off an intersinus septum that inserts on the carotid. Mistaking a carotid aneurysm for a sellar tumour. Forgetting steroid cover and sodium checks after discharge."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s1-1","Definitions","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Dysphagia","Dysphagia is difficulty in swallowing; odynophagia is pain on swallowing; globus is a lump feeling in the throat between meals without difficulty swallowing food; aspiration is passage of material below the vocal folds."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s1-2","Types by level","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Dysphagia","Oral: poor lip seal, chewing or bolus control; drooling and food left in the mouth (stroke, facial palsy, tongue surgery, neuromuscular disease). Oropharyngeal: difficulty starting a swallow, coughing or choking as soon as one swallows, nasal regurgitation, food sticking in the throat (stroke, motor neurone disease, myasthenia, Parkinson disease, head and neck cancer and its treatment, cricopharyngeal dysfunction, pouch). Oesophageal: food sticking behind the sternum seconds after swallowing (strictures, rings and webs, achalasia and spasm, tumours, extrinsic compression). Causes of dysphagia Group Examples Lumen Foreign body, food bolus Wall: inflammation Pharyngitis, tonsillitis, quinsy, oesophagitis (reflux, candida, herpes, eosinophilic, pill, caustic) Wall: tumour Carcinoma of the oropharynx, hypopharynx or oesophagus Wall: structural Pouch, webs (Plummer–Vinson), Schatzki ring, peptic and caustic strictures Wall: motility Achalasia, diffuse spasm, presbyoesophagus, scleroderma, polymyositis Outside the wall Goitre, cervical osteophytes, mediastinal nodes or tumour, aortic aneurysm, aberrant right subclavian artery (dysphagia lusoria) Neurological Stroke, motor neurone disease, multiple sclerosis, Parkinson disease, myasthenia, bulbar and pseudobulbar palsy, vagal palsy Red flags Progressive dysphagia for solids then liquids, weight loss, odynophagia, haematemesis or haemoptysis, voice change, referred otalgia, a neck mass, or age over fifty with new dysphagia: urgent endoscopy for malignancy."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s1-3","Evaluation","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Dysphagia","History: level, solids or liquids, progression, regurgitation, cough with eating, weight, voice, heartburn, neurological symptoms. Examination: mouth, oropharynx, flexible laryngoscopy (pooling, vocal fold movement, sensation), neck, cranial nerves. Videofluoroscopy (modified barium swallow): the oral and pharyngeal phases, penetration and aspiration, and the effect of postures and textures. Fibreoptic endoscopic evaluation of swallowing (FEES): swallowing coloured food under endoscopic view, with or without sensory testing. Barium swallow: pouches, webs, rings, strictures, motility. Endoscopy: rigid or flexible oesophagoscopy, or unsedated transnasal oesophagoscopy, with biopsy; transnasal oesophagoscopy under local anaesthetic in clinic also allows balloon dilatation of strictures and placement of feeding tubes. Manometry: motility disorders (achalasia, spasm). CT for tumours and extrinsic compression. Figure 1 The constrictions of the oesophagus and their distance from the incisors. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s1-4","Oropharyngeal dysfunction and its treatment","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Dysphagia","Tongue base weakness: residue in the valleculae; chin tuck, tongue base exercises. Pharyngeal sensory loss: silent aspiration, worse with thin liquids; thickened fluids, head turned to the weak side. Vocal fold palsy: aspiration of thin liquids and a breathy voice; medialisation. Poor laryngeal elevation: residue and failure of the sphincter to open; the Mendelsohn manoeuvre, exercises and stimulation. Failure of upper oesophageal sphincter opening: a cricopharyngeal bar (present in many asymptomatic elderly people) or a pouch; dilatation, botulinum toxin or cricopharyngeal myotomy. Speech and language therapists guide postures, textures and exercises; a gastrostomy feeds those who cannot swallow safely. 1.4.1 Cricopharyngeal dysfunction The upper oesophageal sphincter is formed by the lower inferior constrictor, the cricopharyngeus and the upper oesophagus. In cricopharyngeal dysfunction the sphincter opens too little or relaxes poorly; videofluoroscopy may show a cricopharyngeal bar at C5–C6, but a bar is seen in about a third of people without symptoms, so it must be matched to the complaint. High-resolution pharyngeal manometry with videofluoroscopy shows whether the pharynx contracts normally against a high residual sphincter pressure, which predicts benefit from dilatation, botulinum toxin or myotomy; myotomy gives the most durable result."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s2-1","Definition","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Pharyngeal pouch","A pulsion diverticulum in which pharyngeal mucosa herniates through Killian's dehiscence , the weak triangle in the posterior pharyngeal wall between the oblique thyropharyngeus above and the transverse cricopharyngeus below, the two parts of the inferior constrictor. Also called a hypopharyngeal diverticulum. Figure 2 First, a hypopharyngeal (Zenker) diverticulum herniating through Killian's dehiscence. Second, the potential sites of diverticula: Killian's dehiscence, the Killian–Jamieson area and Laimer's dehiscence. Dhingra, figs 54.1 and 54.2 Figure 3 Killian's dehiscence and the pharyngeal pouch, and Killian's and Laimer's triangles. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s2-2","Cause","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Pharyngeal pouch","Not fully known. Incoordination between pharyngeal contraction and cricopharyngeal relaxation, failure of the sphincter to open (fibrosis of cricopharyngeus, poor laryngeal elevation) and raised pressure above it force mucosa out through the weak area. Reflux may contribute. It is a disease of the elderly, usually over sixty, men more than women."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s2-3","Pathology","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Pharyngeal pouch","The pouch begins in the midline, lies first behind the oesophagus, then enlarges downwards and comes to lie to its left. As it grows, its mouth comes to lie in line with the pharynx while the oesophageal opening is pushed forward, so food enters the sac preferentially."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s2-4","Clinical features","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Pharyngeal pouch","Dysphagia: often appearing after a few swallows, once the pouch fills and presses on the oesophagus; progressive. Gurgling in the neck on swallowing ( borborygmi ); a swelling in the left side of the neck that empties with pressure. Regurgitation of undigested food, sometimes hours later and at night when lying down, with nocturnal cough and aspiration pneumonia . Halitosis , weight loss and malnutrition. Associated hiatus hernia; rarely carcinoma within a long-standing pouch."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s2-5","Diagnosis","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Pharyngeal pouch","A barium swallow shows the sac and its size, best in the lateral view. Endoscopy carries a risk of entering and perforating the sac, but is needed if carcinoma is suspected; nasogastric tubes are passed with care. Figure 4 Barium swallow showing an early Zenker's diverticulum (arrow) at the level of the sixth cervical vertebra in a 31-year-old woman. Dhingra, fig. 54.3"],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s2-6","Treatment","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Pharyngeal pouch","Small asymptomatic pouches can be watched. For symptoms: 2.6.1 Endoscopic Endoscopic stapling diverticulotomy: the party wall (the diverticular bar , containing cricopharyngeus) between the pouch and the oesophagus is divided and sealed with a linear stapler through a Weerda diverticuloscope; the pouch becomes part of the oesophagus and the muscle is divided at the same time. Now the commonest operation. Dohlman's procedure / CO2 laser diverticulotomy: the bar is divided by diathermy or laser through the diverticuloscope; suited to poor-risk and elderly patients. Endoscopic methods are quick, with rapid return to eating, but are less effective for very small pouches (too little bar to staple) and may be impossible when the neck cannot be extended or the mouth opened. Stapling therefore remains first line for medium and large pouches in fit patients; for small pouches (under about 2 to 3 cm), laser or open myotomy with diverticulectomy or inversion is preferred. Flexible endoscopic septotomy, including the submucosal tunnelling technique (Z-POEM), is increasingly offered by gastroenterologists, especially to frail patients who cannot extend the neck for rigid access. 2.6.2 External (cervical) Through an incision along the anterior border of sternomastoid, usually on the left, the pouch is exposed, and either excised, inverted or suspended (diverticulopexy), always with cricopharyngeal myotomy to prevent recurrence. Myotomy is the key Whatever is done to the sac, dividing cricopharyngeus treats the cause. Excision without myotomy invites recurrence and fistula. 2.6.3 Complica"],["ev","cl:t-pouch~c3","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch: current evidence","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Other causes","Which operation. A network meta-analysis of nine cohort studies (903 patients) found fewer persistent or recurrent symptoms after open diverticulectomy than after endoscopic laser diverticulotomy, with no difference between laser and stapling (Bhatt et al., JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2021). Stapling is quicker, with shorter admission and early feeding, at the cost of more recurrences. Flexible endoscopic techniques show high early success in case series, but long-term comparative data are limited."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~c3","Other causes","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · Other causes","Webs, rings and motility. Plummer–Vinson syndrome with its postcricoid web (a predisposition to postcricoid carcinoma, Carcinoma of the hypopharynx ), Schatzki rings and strictures, achalasia and diffuse spasm, eosinophilic oesophagitis, and extrinsic compression (goitre, osteophytes, mediastinal masses, dysphagia lusoria ) are described in Oesophagus and dysphagia ."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s4-1","Viva essentials","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes of dysphagia by lumen, wall and outside, and the red flags. Investigations: videofluoroscopy, FEES, barium swallow, endoscopy, manometry. Killian's dehiscence and the anatomy of a pouch. Clinical features of a pharyngeal pouch. Treatment: endoscopic stapling, Dohlman, excision with cricopharyngeal myotomy. Plummer–Vinson syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-pouch~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Dysphagia and pharyngeal pouch · For the examination","Common pitfalls Passing a rigid oesophagoscope blindly in a patient with a pouch. Excising a pouch without cricopharyngeal myotomy. Attributing progressive dysphagia in an older smoker to \"age\" without endoscopy."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~c1","Overview","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Overview","The right device for the loss. Which device? Situation Consider Sensorineural loss with useful speech understanding Air-conduction hearing aid Chronic discharge, canal atresia Bone-anchored device Single-sided deafness CROS aid, bone-anchored device, cochlear implant Moderate to severe loss, cannot tolerate an aid Middle ear implant Severe to profound loss with poor speech understanding Cochlear implant No cochlear nerve, NF2 Auditory brainstem implant"],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s1-1","Beyond devices","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Overview","Assistive listening devices (loop systems, FM and remote microphones), alerting devices, speech reading, auditory training and speech conservation. In adults, treating hearing loss may slow cognitive decline."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s1-2","Two ears are better than one","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Overview","Bilateral fitting gives the head shadow effect, binaural summation (sound seems about 3 dB louder) and squelch (better hearing in noise), and restores sound localisation."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s2-1","Components","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Hearing aids","A microphone, amplifier and receiver (speaker), powered by a battery, with digital processing that shapes gain by frequency and compresses loud sounds."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s2-2","Styles","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Hearing aids","Figure 1 Types of hearing aid: (A) body-worn, (B) behind-the-ear, (C) spectacle and (D) in-the-ear. Dhingra, fig. 20.1 Body-worn: high power; now rare. Behind-the-ear: the most versatile, from mild to profound loss, and the choice for children because the mould can be changed as the ear grows. Receiver-in-canal and open fit: for high-frequency loss with good low tones; avoid the occlusion effect. In-the-ear, in-the-canal, completely-in-canal: cosmetic, for mild to moderate loss in adults with good dexterity."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s2-3","Who does well","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Hearing aids","Speech discrimination of at least about 50%, and a wide dynamic range between the threshold and the level of discomfort."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s2-4","Problems","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Hearing aids","2.4.1 Recruitment Abnormal growth of loudness in a cochlear loss narrows the dynamic range: soft sounds are missed but loud sounds are intolerable. Compression circuits help. 2.4.2 Occlusion effect A blocked canal makes the patient's own voice boom. Venting reduces it, but venting also loses low-frequency gain. 2.4.3 Feedback Whistling from sound leaking back to the microphone; better moulds and feedback cancellation help."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s3-1","How they work","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Bone-conduction devices","Sound vibrates the skull directly and reaches both cochleas, bypassing the canal and middle ear."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s3-2","Types","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Bone-conduction devices","Conventional bone-conduction aids on a headband or spectacles. Skin-drive (passive transcutaneous, magnetic) devices, such as Baha Attract and the adhesive Adhear: the vibration passes through intact skin and loses energy on the way, so they are being superseded. Direct-drive devices, either percutaneous , with an abutment through the skin onto an osseointegrated titanium implant (Baha Connect, Ponto), or active transcutaneous , with the vibrator implanted under intact skin (Bonebridge, Osia). Figure 2 First, a bone-anchored device: titanium fixture, abutment and sound processor. Second, the device worn. Dhingra, figs 20.2 and 20.3"],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s3-3","Indications","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Bone-conduction devices","Chronic discharging ears that cannot tolerate an aid. Canal atresia and microtia. Conductive or mixed loss where surgery is unsuitable. Single-sided deafness with a good other ear (about 20 dB or better), sending sound across the skull. In aural atresia, a headband aid is started by about six months, with a definitive implant at about four to five years ( Microtia and congenital canal atresia ). 3.3.1 UK criteria A conductive or mixed loss, or single-sided deafness, with bone conduction in the fitting range and stable (no more than 15 dB change at two or more frequencies over two years); failure or unsuitability of a conventional or CROS aid after at least four weeks; and benefit from a trial on a headband for at least 14 days."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s3-4","Practical points","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Bone-conduction devices","Osseointegration takes about three months, longer in children. Complications include failed integration and skin reactions around the abutment. A bone-anchored device does not restore localisation."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s3-5","CROS and BiCROS aids","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Bone-conduction devices","A microphone on the deaf side sends sound wirelessly to the better ear; BiCROS adds amplification for a better ear that also has some loss. Where the books differ · Single-sided deafness Dhingra Bone-anchored devices have replaced CROS aids. Lee CROS aids remain an option. In practice A CROS aid is tried first (at least four weeks under the UK criteria); a bone-conduction device follows a headband trial; a cochlear implant, which gives better localisation and speech in noise, is increasingly offered."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~c4","Middle ear implants","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Middle ear implants","Driving the ossicles directly. A transducer fixed to an ossicle or the round window, driven electromagnetically or piezoelectrically. The commonest is the Vibrant Soundbridge, whose floating mass transducer drives the incus, stapes or round window. For adults with moderate to severe sensorineural or mixed loss who are unhappy with or cannot wear conventional aids but have a cochlea worth amplifying, and some conductive losses. The canal is left open."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s5-1","Principle","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Cochlear implants","An external microphone and processor convert sound into electrical patterns, sent across the skin to an internal receiver, which stimulates the spiral ganglion cells through an electrode array in the scala tympani. The cochlea's place coding is reproduced: basal electrodes for high pitches, apical for low. Figure 3 Principle of the cochlear implant. Dhingra, fig. 20.9 Figure 4 Cross-section of the cochlea: the electrode lies in the scala tympani, next to the spiral ganglion. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s5-2","Candidates","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Cochlear implants","5.2.1 Adults Severe to profound bilateral sensorineural loss with limited benefit from well-fitted aids. UK (NICE TA566, 2019): severe to profound loss (80 dB HL at two or more frequencies) with inadequate benefit from aids (Arthur Boothroyd words 50% or less). Another widely used set: moderate to profound loss with an aided sentence score of 50% or less in the ear to be implanted and 60% or less best-aided. 5.2.2 Children Severe to profound loss with little benefit after a three to six month trial of aids. The trial is waived after meningitis, when ossification threatens. Children with profound bilateral loss are implanted on both sides, simultaneously (NICE recommends bilateral simultaneous implants for children, and for adults with additional disabilities such as blindness). Where the books differ · Age of implantation Dhingra From twelve months. Lee Earlier implantation is safe and may help language. In practice Implant ideally between 9 and 12 months, and on both sides at once for profound bilateral loss. 5.2.3 Outcomes Duration of deafness and pre-operative speech scores explain most of the variation. Postlingually deafened adults often achieve high sentence scores; children implanted early commonly join mainstream schools; prelingually deaf adults gain mostly sound awareness, because the window for language (birth to about two years) has passed."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s5-3","Assessment","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Cochlear implants","Medical review; audiology with an aid trial; MRI to show the cochlear nerve and a fluid-filled cochlea; HRCT for bony anatomy, malformations, a narrow internal auditory canal (under 3 mm) and surgical hazards; psychological and speech assessment."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s5-4","Contraindications","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Cochlear implants","Complete cochlear or labyrinthine aplasia (Michel), complete ossification, and an absent cochlear nerve. Retrocochlear disease is a relative contraindication."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s5-5","The operation","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Cochlear implants","Cortical mastoidectomy, posterior tympanotomy through the facial recess, entry into the scala tympani through the round window or a cochleostomy, slow insertion of the array, sealing, and testing of the device. Implantation now aims to preserve the cochlea and any residual hearing: soft surgery uses a flexible array, the round window route, slow insertion over minutes to avoid basilar membrane rupture, no suction of perilymph, and steroids. In an ossified cochlea, the basal turn is drilled out or a split array used. Where the books differ · Route into the cochlea Dhingra Describes a cochleostomy. Lee Prefers the round window. In practice Both reach the scala tympani; the round window route is part of soft surgery to save residual hearing."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s5-6","After surgery","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Cochlear implants","Activation at about two to four weeks, then repeated mapping of each electrode and intensive auditory therapy; sound quality improves over three to six months."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s5-7","Vaccination","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Cochlear implants","Implant recipients are vaccinated against pneumococcus (PCV13, then PPSV23 at least eight weeks later), because meningitis is commoner in implanted children, especially with malformations or a CSF leak."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s5-8","Complications","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Cochlear implants","Flap breakdown and device extrusion. Device failure, about 4% over a lifetime; revision through the same track. Facial nerve injury (under 1%) and facial stimulation, managed by switching off electrodes. Taste change, vertigo, tinnitus. CSF gusher, especially with malformations. Meningitis. Pearls Bilateral implants restore head shadow, summation and squelch, and localisation. Inner ear malformations raise the risk of an anomalous facial nerve, gusher and meningitis. Auditory neuropathy can do well with an implant. Acute otitis media in an implanted child is treated promptly, intravenously if severe."],["ev","cl:t-rehab~c6","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants: current evidence","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Auditory brainstem implant","Hearing aids. A Cochrane review found that hearing aids improve hearing-specific quality of life, listening ability and communication in adults with mild to moderate loss, with high-certainty evidence (Ferguson et al., 2017). Cognition. In the ACHIEVE trial, hearing intervention slowed cognitive decline in older adults at higher risk of dementia (Lin et al., Lancet 2023). Single-sided deafness. Cochlear implantation gives better localisation and speech in noise than CROS or bone-conduction devices in randomised and comparative studies, and is increasingly offered."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~c6","Auditory brainstem implant","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · Auditory brainstem implant","When there is no nerve. An electrode placed on the cochlear nucleus, through the lateral recess of the fourth ventricle, when a cochlear implant cannot work: neurofibromatosis type 2, cochlear or nerve aplasia, or severe or complete cochlear ossification after meningitis or fracture. Results are more modest than with cochlear implants."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s7-1","Viva essentials","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Hearing aid types and who benefits. Recruitment and dynamic range. Bone-anchored devices: indications and osseointegration. Cochlear implant: principle, candidacy, assessment, contraindications, operation, complications. Auditory brainstem implant: indications."],["sec","cl:t-rehab~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants · For the examination","Common pitfalls Fitting an aid to an asymmetric loss without excluding a retrocochlear cause. Delaying implantation after meningitis. Forgetting pneumococcal vaccination."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s1-1","Definition","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Overview","Rhinitis is inflammation of the nasal mucosa, defined clinically by two or more of nasal obstruction, rhinorrhoea, sneezing and itching. Rhinosinusitis adds sinus involvement ( Acute rhinosinusitis and orbital complications , Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps )."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s1-2","Classification","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Overview","Rhinitis Allergic Seasonal or perennial intermittent or persistent (ARIA) Non-allergic, non-infective Vasomotor (idiopathic) drug-induced, rhinitis medicamentosa, hormonal, NARES, gustatory, emotional, occupational Infective Acute and chronic rhinitis atrophic rhinitis and specific infections Infective and atrophic rhinitis are covered in Acute, chronic and atrophic rhinitis ."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s1-3","Why it matters","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Overview","Allergic rhinitis affects about one person in five in Western countries (up to a quarter in some populations), disturbs sleep, school and work, and travels with asthma: rhinitis patients are about four times more likely to develop asthma, 20 to 30% of patients with rhinitis have asthma, and most with allergic asthma have rhinitis. Treating the nose often improves asthma control (the united airway ). Non-allergic rhinitis typically begins in early adulthood and is linked to adult-onset asthma."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s2-1","The erectile mucosa","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Applied physiology","The turbinate mucosa contains venous sinusoids surrounded by smooth muscle that fill and empty them. Sympathetic stimulation empties them and opens the airway; parasympathetic stimulation fills them and drives the glands to secrete. Their alternating congestion from side to side is the nasal cycle ."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s2-2","Hyperreactivity","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Applied physiology","In both allergic and non-allergic rhinitis the mucosa over-reacts to ordinary stimuli: temperature change, dust, smoke, perfume, cold air."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s3-1","Types","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Allergic rhinitis","Seasonal: symptoms in the season of the pollen the patient reacts to (tree, grass or weed pollens). Perennial: symptoms throughout the year, from house dust mite, moulds, cockroaches and animal dander."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s3-2","Genetic predisposition","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Allergic rhinitis","The chance of a child developing allergy is about 20% with one allergic parent and 47% with two."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s3-3","Pathogenesis","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Allergic rhinitis","3.3.1 Sensitisation In predisposed people, inhaled allergens drive production of specific IgE, which binds by its Fc end to mast cells and basophils. 3.3.2 Re-exposure Allergen bridges two IgE molecules on the cell surface; the mast cell degranulates, releasing preformed mediators (histamine) and newly made ones (leukotrienes, prostaglandins). Figure 1 (A) The IgE antibody: the Fc end fixes to the mast cell, the Fab end binds allergen. (B) Release of mediators when allergen bridges two IgE molecules. Dhingra, fig. 30.1 3.3.3 Early and late phases Early phase: within 5 to 30 minutes: sneezing, rhinorrhoea, blockage, from histamine. Late phase: 2 to 8 hours later, without further exposure: eosinophils, basophils and T cells infiltrate, causing congestion and thick secretions. 3.3.4 Priming Mucosa sensitised by one allergen reacts to smaller doses later, and to non-specific stimuli as well. Figure 2 First, mediators released from a mast cell. Second, allergic and non-specific stimuli acting through the same mast cells. Dhingra, figs 30.2 and 30.3"],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s3-4","Clinical features","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Allergic rhinitis","3.4.1 Symptoms It usually starts in childhood, around three years of age, after atopic dermatitis and before allergic asthma (the allergic march ), and is rare before two because sensitisation needs prolonged exposure; seasonal symptoms often peak in adolescence. Seasonal disease: paroxysmal sneezing (10 to 20 at a time), watery discharge, blockage and itching of the nose, eyes, palate or throat. Perennial disease is milder but persistent: frequent colds, a stuffy nose, reduced smell, postnasal drip, cough and ear symptoms. 3.4.2 Signs Nose: pale, oedematous, sometimes bluish mucosa; swollen turbinates; watery discharge; a transverse nasal crease from the allergic salute . Eyes: lid oedema, cobblestone conjunctiva, dark circles ( allergic shiners ). Ears: retracted drum or effusion. Pharynx and larynx: granular pharyngitis, hoarseness. Children: the long face and open mouth of chronic mouth breathing."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s3-5","ARIA classification","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Allergic rhinitis","ARIA classification Category Definition Intermittent Symptoms less than 4 days a week, or for less than 4 weeks Persistent Symptoms more than 4 days a week and for more than 4 weeks Mild None of: disturbed sleep, impaired daily activities or sport, impaired school or work, troublesome symptoms Moderate to severe One or more of these"],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s3-6","Investigations","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Allergic rhinitis","Nasal smear: eosinophils, best taken during active symptoms (also seen in NARES). Blood count: peripheral eosinophilia, inconsistent. Skin prick test: allergen pricked into the skin of the forearm or back; a wheal and flare at 10 to 15 minutes is positive, with histamine and diluent as controls. Specific IgE in serum (formerly RAST): correlates well with skin tests; useful when skin testing is impossible. Nasal provocation test: reproducing symptoms with allergen. Nasal endoscopy: to exclude polyps and structural causes. 3.6.1 Interpreting allergy tests A skin-prick wheal more than 3 mm larger than the negative control is positive, but only about half of people with positive tests have allergic disease, so tests are interpreted with the history and requested mainly when immunotherapy or targeted avoidance is planned. A small panel (house dust mite, grass, tree and weed pollen, cat, dog and moulds) covers most allergy. Serum specific IgE is a little less sensitive than skin testing."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s3-7","Complications","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Allergic rhinitis","Recurrent sinusitis, nasal polyps (in about 2%), otitis media with effusion, dental and facial growth problems from mouth breathing, and asthma."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s3-8","Treatment","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Allergic rhinitis","3.8.1 Avoidance Most effective with a single allergen: remove the pet, reduce dust mites, change work if necessary. Allergen-impermeable mattress and pillow covers are the best-studied measure for house dust mite, though alone they help little. 3.8.2 Drugs Intranasal corticosteroids: the most effective single treatment for persistent or moderate to severe disease. They take days to start working and about two weeks to reach full effect; prefer modern sprays with low systemic bioavailability (mometasone, fluticasone furoate) in children. Teach technique: aim away from the septum. Combined steroid-antihistamine spray (fluticasone with azelastine): for patients not controlled by either alone. Antihistamines: oral non-sedating or intranasal, for sneezing, itch and rhinorrhoea; less effect on blockage. Decongestants: a short course only; oral ones are combined with antihistamines. Sodium cromoglycate: stabilises mast cells; useful before exposure. Ipratropium bromide: for watery rhinorrhoea. Leukotriene receptor antagonists: montelukast, especially with asthma. Oral steroids: a short course for severe, uncontrolled episodes only. Depot steroid injections are not recommended. Anti-IgE (omalizumab): for severe allergic asthma; not a routine treatment for rhinitis. 3.8.3 Step care (ARIA) 1 Mild intermittent Oral antihistamine or intranasal cromoglycate. 2 Moderate or persistent Intranasal corticosteroid. 3 Severe Intranasal steroid with a non-sedating antihistamine. 4 Uncontrolled Short oral steroid course; consider immunotherapy. 3.8.4 Immunotherapy Gradually increasing doses of t"],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s4-1","What it is","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Vasomotor rhinitis","A non-allergic rhinitis that mimics allergy, with negative allergy tests, from autonomic imbalance with parasympathetic overactivity and a hyperreactive mucosa. Emotions influence it through the hypothalamus."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s4-2","Clinical features","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Vasomotor rhinitis","Paroxysmal sneezing on getting out of bed. Profuse, watery rhinorrhoea, sometimes the main complaint; dripping on leaning forward must be distinguished from CSF rhinorrhoea ( CSF rhinorrhoea ). Blockage alternating from side to side, worse at night and on the dependent side. Postnasal drip. The turbinate mucosa is congested and hypertrophic, or normal."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s4-3","Complications","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Vasomotor rhinitis","Hypertrophic rhinitis, polyps and sinusitis ( Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps )."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s4-4","Treatment","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Vasomotor rhinitis","4.4.1 Medical Avoid triggers; antihistamines and oral decongestants; topical steroids; ipratropium for rhinorrhoea; a short systemic steroid course in severe cases; address psychological factors. 4.4.2 Surgical Reduce the turbinates when obstruction persists; correct a deviated septum or polyps. Vidian neurectomy , cutting the parasympathetic secretomotor fibres, for intractable rhinorrhoea; now largely replaced by endoscopic posterior nasal nerve procedures."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s5-1","Drug-induced","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Other non-allergic rhinitis","Antihypertensives that block sympathetic activity (reserpine, methyldopa, beta-blockers), anticholinesterases, oral contraceptives, and aspirin or NSAIDs in sensitive patients."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s5-2","Rhinitis medicamentosa","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Other non-allergic rhinitis","Rebound congestion from overuse of topical decongestant drops beyond about five to seven days. Treated by stopping the drops, a topical or short systemic steroid course, and turbinate reduction if hypertrophy persists."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s5-3","Hormonal","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Other non-allergic rhinitis","Rhinitis of pregnancy: oedematous mucosa from oestrogen; treated with saline, topical steroids and limited decongestant use; avoid newer antihistamines. Hypothyroidism: parasympathetic predominance; relieved by thyroxine. Honeymoon rhinitis: congestion with sexual excitement."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s5-4","NARES","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Other non-allergic rhinitis","Non-allergic rhinitis with eosinophilia syndrome: eosinophils on smear but negative allergy tests; responds to topical steroids; may precede aspirin sensitivity and polyps."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s5-5","Gustatory rhinitis","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Other non-allergic rhinitis","Watery rhinorrhoea with spicy food, a cholinergic reflex; ipratropium before meals."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s5-6","Emotional rhinitis","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Other non-allergic rhinitis","Anxiety, tension or grief causing rhinitis; counselling."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s5-7","Non-airflow rhinitis","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Other non-allergic rhinitis","After laryngectomy or tracheostomy, when air no longer passes through the nose."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s5-8","Local allergic rhinitis","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Other non-allergic rhinitis","Allergy-like symptoms with negative skin and blood tests but specific IgE produced in the nasal mucosa, shown by nasal provocation; managed like allergic rhinitis."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s6-1","Indications","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Turbinate surgery","Persistent obstruction from inferior turbinate hypertrophy despite a proper trial of topical steroids, or rhinitis medicamentosa with fixed hypertrophy."],["ev","cl:t-rhinitis~s6-2","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis: current evidence","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Turbinate surgery","The British Society for Allergy and Clinical Immunology guideline recommends intranasal steroids as first-line treatment for persistent symptoms, adding an antihistamine or a combined steroid-azelastine spray when control is poor, and immunotherapy for uncontrolled disease with a confirmed allergen (Scadding et al., Clin Exp Allergy 2017). The ARIA 2020 care pathways follow the same steps, using patient-reported control to guide treatment (Bousquet et al., J Allergy Clin Immunol 2020). A Cochrane review of 49 trials found sublingual immunotherapy reduced symptoms and medication use in allergic rhinitis, with no serious systemic reactions (Radulovic et al., 2010)."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s6-2","Methods","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · Turbinate surgery","Submucosal reduction (microdebrider or radiofrequency), outfracture, partial turbinectomy, laser or cryosurgery. Reducing the conchal bone gives the longest-lasting effect. Preserve the mucosa: excessive removal leaves an empty nose , with crusting and paradoxical blockage."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s7-1","Viva essentials","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Pathogenesis of allergic rhinitis, and early versus late phase. Signs: allergic salute, transverse crease, allergic shiners, pale boggy mucosa. ARIA classification and step care. Skin prick test technique and controls. Immunotherapy: mechanism and duration. Vasomotor rhinitis and the forms of non-allergic rhinitis. Rhinitis medicamentosa."],["sec","cl:t-rhinitis~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling one-sided symptoms allergy without examining the nose. Prescribing decongestant drops without a time limit. Sending a patient for turbinate surgery before a proper trial of steroid spray. Missing CSF rhinorrhoea in a patient with clear, one-sided dripping."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s1-1","Parotid gland","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Applied anatomy","The largest, lying on the masseter and ramus, wrapped round the back of the mandible, from the zygomatic arch to below the angle (the tail), in front of the ear and sternomastoid; its deep surface reaches the prestyloid parapharyngeal space. It is enclosed in the parotidomasseteric fascia (part of the investing layer), whose unyielding nature makes parotitis so painful. Facial nerve: leaves the stylomastoid foramen, enters the gland and divides at the pes anserinus into temporofacial and cervicofacial divisions and five branches; it separates the larger superficial lobe (about 80%) from the deep lobe , the plane of parotid surgery. Landmarks for the trunk: the tragal pointer (the nerve lies about 1 cm deep and inferior to it), the tympanomastoid suture , and the posterior belly of digastric (the nerve is at its depth, above it). Other contents: the retromandibular vein (superficial to the external carotid), the external carotid artery dividing into maxillary and superficial temporal arteries, and intraparotid lymph nodes (about 20), the only salivary gland with intraglandular nodes. Stensen's duct: runs forward over the masseter, turns through the buccinator and opens opposite the upper second molar. Nerves: parasympathetic secretomotor fibres from the glossopharyngeal nerve via Jacobson's nerve, the lesser petrosal nerve, the otic ganglion and the auriculotemporal nerve; sensation of the overlying skin by the great auricular nerve (C2, C3). Figure 1 The parotid gland and Stensen's duct, and the facial nerve dividing it into superficial and deep lobes. From the anatomy atla"],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s1-2","Submandibular gland","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Applied anatomy","In the digastric triangle, wrapped round the back of the mylohyoid: a large superficial part below it and a deep part above it. Wharton's duct runs forwards between the mylohyoid and hyoglossus, crossed by the lingual nerve (which loops beneath it), to open beside the frenulum. The facial artery grooves the deep part, the facial vein lies superficial to it, and the marginal mandibular nerve runs over the gland within its capsule; the hypoglossal nerve lies deep. Secretomotor supply: chorda tympani via the submandibular ganglion. Figure 3 The submandibular gland around the mylohyoid, and the marginal mandibular nerve. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s1-3","Sublingual and minor glands","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Applied anatomy","The sublingual glands lie under the floor of mouth mucosa above the mylohyoid, draining by many small ducts (of Rivinus), some into Wharton's duct (Bartholin's duct). Six hundred to a thousand minor salivary glands line the mouth, palate (most), pharynx, larynx and sinuses. Figure 4 The major salivary glands and their ducts, and the sublingual and submandibular glands with their ducts. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s1-4","Saliva","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Applied anatomy","About a litre to a litre and a half a day. At rest, most comes from the submandibular glands (more mucinous, richer in calcium); when stimulated by taste or smell, the parotids (serous, watery) supply most. Saliva lubricates, begins starch digestion (amylase), buffers acid (bicarbonate), protects teeth and kills microbes (IgA, lysozyme, lactoferrin). Flow is driven mainly by parasympathetic stimulation; dehydration, anticholinergic drugs, radiotherapy and age reduce it."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s2-1","History and examination","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Assessment","Which gland, one or both sides; acute or recurrent; pain and swelling with meals (obstruction); fever and pus (infection); dry eyes and mouth (Sjögren); drugs, alcohol, diabetes; and red flags for tumour (persistent mass, facial weakness, pain, skin fixation, nodes). Inspect and milk the duct orifices, and palpate bimanually the submandibular gland and floor of mouth (stones are often felt along the duct). Test the facial nerve."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s2-2","Imaging","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Assessment","Ultrasound: first line for stones, ductal dilatation, abscess and masses, and for guided aspiration. Plain radiographs: about 80% of submandibular stones (fewer parotid stones) are radio-opaque; an occlusal film shows duct stones. Non-contrast CT: detects stones reliably; contrast CT for abscess. Sialography (conventional or MR sialography): ducts, strictures, sialectasis and radiolucent stones; contraindicated in acute infection. MRI: the best study for masses ( Salivary gland tumours ). Sialendoscopy: both diagnosis and treatment. Scintigraphy (technetium pertechnetate): gland function; uptake in Warthin tumour and oncocytoma."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s3-1","Mumps (epidemic parotitis)","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Infections","A paramyxovirus spread by droplets, saliva and urine; mostly children of four to six years in unvaccinated populations; incubation two to three weeks, infectious from before the swelling to about a week after. Features: fever, malaise and muscle pains, then painful swelling of one parotid and usually, within days, the other; sometimes the submandibular glands; swelling settles in about a week. Complications: orchitis (painful testis, usually one-sided; sterility rare), oophoritis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis and encephalitis, unilateral sensorineural deafness , sometimes sudden ( Sudden sensorineural hearing loss ), thyroiditis, myocarditis, nephritis and arthritis. Diagnosis: clinical; raised serum and urinary amylase; IgM or rising IgG serology, or PCR. Treatment: supportive: fluids, rest, analgesics, warm or cold compresses, avoiding sour foods; support for orchitis. Prevention: MMR vaccine."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s3-2","Acute suppurative sialadenitis","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Infections","Usually the parotid, in the elderly, dehydrated and debilitated, after abdominal or hip surgery, and with a dry mouth from drugs: stasis of saliva lets mouth bacteria ascend Stensen's duct. Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA), streptococci and anaerobes. Sudden, severe pain and swelling, worse on chewing, fever and toxicity; the duct orifice is red and swollen and pus can be milked from it. Treatment: rehydration, intravenous antibiotics covering staphylococci and anaerobes, sialogogues (lemon drops), massage from back to front, oral hygiene. Ultrasound or CT if it does not settle; an abscess (often hard to feel through the fascia) is drained through a parotidectomy incision with radial incisions in the line of the facial nerve branches. Figure 5 Acute suppurative parotitis on the left in a 58-year-old woman. Dhingra, fig. 45.1"],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s3-3","Neonatal and childhood parotitis","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Infections","Neonatal suppurative parotitis in premature babies (staphylococcal). Juvenile recurrent parotitis : recurrent swelling, often one-sided, in children aged three to ten without pus, with punctate sialectasis on imaging; usually remits at puberty; antibiotics during attacks, and sialendoscopic lavage with steroid."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s3-4","Chronic recurrent sialadenitis and sialectasis","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Infections","Recurrent painful swelling with pus during attacks and a firm, slightly enlarged gland between them, from duct obstruction, strictures, stones and reduced flow. Sialography shows ductal dilatation (punctate, globular or cavitary sialectasis ) or strictures. Treat attacks with antibiotics; between them, hydration, oral hygiene, sialogogues and avoiding drying drugs; sialendoscopy to dilate strictures and wash out plugs; gland excision for persistent symptoms. The Küttner tumour (chronic sclerosing sialadenitis, part of IgG4-related disease) can mimic a submandibular neoplasm."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s3-5","Granulomatous infections","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Infections","Tuberculosis: of the parotid (often its intraglandular nodes) or submandibular gland, as an acute sialadenitis or a chronic mass mimicking tumour, sometimes with skin changes or sinuses. Diagnosis by FNA (granulomas, acid-fast bacilli, culture) and tuberculin testing; antituberculous treatment. Atypical mycobacteria: in children of one to three years (one to five in some texts; Approach to a lump in the neck ), a painless mass with a violaceous skin and sinus formation, a negative chest film and weak tuberculin reaction; excision or curettage. Actinomycosis: after dental infection; a woody swelling with sinuses discharging sulphur granules; prolonged penicillin. Cat-scratch disease ( Bartonella ) and toxoplasmosis cause periparotid and cervical lymphadenitis."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s4-1","Pathology","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Sialolithiasis","Calcium phosphate (hydroxyapatite) deposited on a nidus of mucus or debris. About 80 to 90% occur in the submandibular system, because its saliva is more alkaline, viscous and calcium-rich and its duct is long and runs uphill; about 10% in the parotid (often in the gland), rarely the sublingual."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s4-2","Features","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Sialolithiasis","Pain and swelling of the gland at mealtimes ( salivary colic ), subsiding over an hour or two; recurrent infection with pus at the orifice; a stone may be seen at the papilla or felt along the duct bimanually. Figure 6 First, (A) an adult with submandibular sialadenitis (arrow) and (B) a radio-opaque stone in the submandibular gland on a lateral radiograph. Second, a radio-opaque stone in the right submandibular duct on an occlusal film. Dhingra, figs 45.2 and 45.3"],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s4-3","Diagnosis","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Sialolithiasis","Plain and occlusal films (radio-opaque stones), ultrasound, non-contrast CT, and sialography or MR sialography for radiolucent stones and strictures."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s4-4","Treatment","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Sialolithiasis","Small stones near the orifice: conservative (hydration, sialogogues, massage) may pass them. Stones in the anterior or middle duct: intraoral sialolithotomy : a stay suture behind the stone to stop it slipping back, an incision over it along the duct, protecting the lingual nerve, and marsupialisation. Sialendoscopy: small stones (under about 5 mm) retrieved with a basket; larger ones fragmented by laser or extracorporeal lithotripsy; combined endoscopic and transoral approaches remove many hilar stones while keeping the gland. Gland excision: for stones in the hilum or parenchyma that cannot be reached, and for a gland destroyed by recurrent infection."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s5-1","Sjögren syndrome","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Systemic and autoimmune","An autoimmune exocrinopathy with lymphocytic destruction of the salivary and lacrimal glands, mostly in women. Primary: dry mouth (xerostomia) and dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca), with parotid enlargement. Secondary: sicca features with another connective tissue disease, usually rheumatoid arthritis or lupus. Diagnosis: Schirmer test, anti-Ro (SS-A) and anti-La (SS-B) antibodies, rheumatoid factor and ANA, and labial minor salivary gland biopsy showing focal lymphocytic infiltrates; the gland shows a benign lymphoepithelial lesion (formerly Mikulicz disease). Treatment: saliva substitutes, frequent sips, sialogogues (pilocarpine, cevimeline), dental care, artificial tears. Lymphoma in Sjögren syndrome The risk of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (usually MALT lymphoma of the parotid) is greatly increased. A new, firm or enlarging mass in a Sjögren gland is biopsied."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s5-2","Sarcoidosis","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Systemic and autoimmune","Bilateral painless parotid swelling; Heerfordt syndrome (uveoparotid fever) combines parotitis, uveitis, fever and facial palsy ( Facial nerve palsy ). Steroids."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s5-3","HIV","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Systemic and autoimmune","Bilateral parotid enlargement ( HIV and ENT ) from benign lymphoepithelial cysts and lymphoid hyperplasia, sometimes the presenting sign of HIV; a sicca picture (diffuse infiltrative lymphocytosis syndrome). Antiretroviral treatment, aspiration or sclerotherapy for cosmetically troublesome cysts; a solid mass needs biopsy because of the risk of lymphoma."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s5-4","Sialadenosis (sialosis)","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Systemic and autoimmune","Painless, symmetrical, non-inflammatory enlargement of the parotids from acinar hypertrophy, with diabetes, alcoholism, malnutrition, bulimia, obesity and drugs. Distinguish from other causes of bilateral swelling by FNA or biopsy; treat the cause."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s5-5","Necrotising sialometaplasia","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Systemic and autoimmune","An ulcer or swelling of the hard palate (minor salivary glands) after ischaemic injury, mostly in men in their forties, whose pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia mimics squamous or mucoepidermoid carcinoma; it heals spontaneously in five to six weeks. Biopsy and wait. Causes of bilateral parotid swelling Group Examples Infective Mumps, HIV, recurrent parotitis of childhood Autoimmune and granulomatous Sjögren syndrome, sarcoidosis, IgG4-related disease Metabolic Sialadenosis (diabetes, alcohol, malnutrition, bulimia) Neoplastic Warthin tumour (bilateral in some), lymphoma, acinic cell carcinoma (rarely) Drugs Iodides, phenylbutazone and others"],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s6-1","Xerostomia","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Dry and wet mouths","From drugs (anticholinergics, antidepressants, antihistamines, diuretics), radiotherapy (irreversible damage above about 25 Gy), Sjögren syndrome, dehydration, diabetes and age. Effects: difficulty chewing, swallowing and speaking, caries, candida and altered taste. Treat with water, saliva substitutes, sugar-free gum, pilocarpine, fluoride and dental care; prevent radiation xerostomia with parotid-sparing IMRT and submandibular gland transfer ( Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer )."],["ev","cl:t-salivary~s6-2","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings: current evidence","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Dry and wet mouths","A systematic review of sialendoscopy found gland preservation and symptom relief in about 86% of patients with obstructive salivary disease, with few complications, making it the first-line approach for accessible stones and strictures (Strychowsky et al., Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2012). The 2016 ACR/EULAR criteria classify primary Sjögren syndrome by weighted points for focal lymphocytic sialadenitis on lip biopsy and anti-Ro antibodies (3 each) and for ocular staining, Schirmer's test and unstimulated salivary flow (1 each), with 4 or more classifying the disease (Shiboski et al., Arthritis Rheumatol 2017)."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s6-2","Sialorrhoea (drooling)","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · Dry and wet mouths","Drooling is normal up to about four years. Beyond that it is usually caused by poor oral and pharyngeal control, open lips and poor posture, as in cerebral palsy and adult neurological disease, rather than by too much saliva. At rest about 70% of saliva comes from the submandibular glands, the main target of treatment. Separate anterior drooling (spilling from the lips) from posterior drooling (pooling in the pharynx and aspiration, with cough, night choking and chest infections), because the choice of surgery depends on it. Treatment is stepwise: oral motor training and posture; glycopyrronium or a hyoscine patch; ultrasound-guided botulinum toxin into the submandibular and parotid glands; then surgery: duct ligation, submandibular duct relocation (not in children who aspirate), or bilateral submandibular gland excision for posterior drooling. The techniques are in Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling ."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s7-1","Viva essentials","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Parotid anatomy: the facial nerve and its landmarks; the course of Stensen's and Wharton's ducts. Mumps and its complications, including deafness. Acute suppurative parotitis: who gets it and why. Why stones form in the submandibular gland, and their management. Sjögren syndrome: diagnosis and the lymphoma risk. Causes of bilateral parotid swelling."],["sec","cl:t-salivary~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings · For the examination","Common pitfalls Performing sialography during acute infection. Calling a firm, persistent parotid lump \"chronic parotitis\" without imaging and FNA. Injuring the lingual nerve when removing a duct stone."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s1-1","Proportions","Salivary gland tumours · Overview","Parotid: about 80% of all salivary tumours; about 80% benign. Submandibular: about 10%; 50 to 60% benign. Minor salivary and sublingual: the remainder; most minor salivary tumours (and nearly all sublingual tumours) are malignant. Tumours of the salivary glands (simplified WHO) Benign Malignant Pleomorphic adenoma Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (low, intermediate, high grade) Warthin tumour (papillary cystadenoma lymphomatosum) Adenoid cystic carcinoma Oncocytoma Acinic cell carcinoma Basal cell, canalicular adenoma; myoepithelioma Carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma Haemangioma, lymphangioma (children) Salivary duct carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, polymorphous adenocarcinoma Lipoma, neurofibroma, schwannoma Epithelial–myoepithelial, secretory carcinoma Squamous and undifferentiated carcinoma (often metastatic), lymphoma, sarcoma"],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s1-2","Features of malignancy","Salivary gland tumours · Overview","Rapid growth, pain, fixation to skin or deep structures, ulceration, facial nerve weakness , trismus, and cervical nodes. Many malignant tumours, however, present as a painless mobile lump exactly like an adenoma."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s1-3","Evaluation","Salivary gland tumours · Overview","Ultrasound with fine-needle aspiration (or core biopsy): about 90% accurate in separating benign from malignant, with cytology reported in the Milan system categories; ultrasound-guided core biopsy gives a more specific diagnosis when FNA is indeterminate. Incisional biopsy of a parotid mass is avoided because it seeds tumour and endangers the nerve. MRI: the best study for extent, deep lobe and parapharyngeal extension, and perineural spread (especially of adenoid cystic carcinoma along the facial and trigeminal nerves); CT for bone erosion; PET-CT for high-grade cancers. Facial nerve function recorded before surgery. Figure 1 The parotid in cross-section with its relations, and the surgical landmarks of the facial nerve at parotidectomy. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s2-1","Pleomorphic adenoma","Salivary gland tumours · Benign tumours","The commonest salivary tumour in adults and children; about 85% in the parotid, usually the superficial lobe or tail; also the submandibular gland and palate. Women more than men, usually in the third to fifth decades. A slow, painless, firm, mobile, lobulated lump that may grow very large; from the deep lobe it can bulge into the parapharyngeal space and push the tonsil and soft palate medially. Pathology: a mixed tumour of epithelial and myoepithelial cells in a myxoid, chondroid or fibrous stroma, within an incomplete capsule from which finger-like pseudopodia protrude. Treatment: superficial parotidectomy (or partial superficial parotidectomy) with identification and preservation of the facial nerve, removing the tumour with a cuff of normal tissue; extracapsular dissection is used by some for small, superficial, mobile tumours. Enucleation leaves pseudopodia and gives high, often multinodular recurrence; spillage at surgery also seeds recurrence. Deep lobe and parapharyngeal tumours ( Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies covers the other parapharyngeal masses) are removed through the neck (occasionally with mandibulotomy), not through the mouth. Malignant change: carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma in long-standing or recurrent tumours (risk rising with time, several per cent at ten to fifteen years): sudden growth, pain or facial weakness in an old lump."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s2-2","Warthin tumour","Salivary gland tumours · Benign tumours","Papillary cystadenoma lymphomatosum: the second commonest benign parotid tumour, almost always in the tail of the parotid, in older men who smoke; up to a fifth multifocal and about 5 to 10% bilateral. A soft or cystic, sometimes fluctuating, lump that may become inflamed and painful. Histologically oncocytic epithelium in papillary cysts over a lymphoid stroma; it takes up technetium pertechnetate (a \"hot\" nodule). Not a true neoplasm and very rarely malignant: removal by partial parotidectomy or extracapsular excision, or observation in the elderly once FNA is confident."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s2-3","Oncocytoma","Salivary gland tumours · Benign tumours","Rare (about 1%), from oncocytes rich in mitochondria, in the elderly, in the superficial parotid, usually under 5 cm; hot on pertechnetate scanning. Superficial parotidectomy."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s2-4","Other benign tumours","Salivary gland tumours · Benign tumours","Basal cell adenoma (can mimic adenoid cystic carcinoma), canalicular adenoma (upper lip), myoepithelioma, lipoma (characteristic on imaging), and neural tumours."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s2-5","Tumours in children","Salivary gland tumours · Benign tumours","Vascular lesions ( Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies ) are the commonest. Haemangioma of the parotid in infants, usually girls: soft, bluish, enlarging with crying, growing in the first months then involuting by about five to seven years; half have skin haemangiomas; propranolol, surgery only if it fails. Lymphangioma does not involute and is excised or sclerosed. Among solid tumours in children, pleomorphic adenoma is the commonest benign one, but up to half are malignant (more than half of solitary solid masses in some series; Head and neck cancer in children ), mostly mucoepidermoid carcinoma ."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s3-1","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Salivary gland tumours · Malignant tumours","The commonest salivary malignancy in adults and children, usually in the parotid: a mixture of mucous, epidermoid and intermediate cells. Low grade: more mucous cells, cystic, slow, behaving almost like a benign tumour; five-year survival about 90%. High grade: more epidermoid cells, solid, aggressive, with nodal spread; survival about 30 to 50%. Low-grade tumours: parotidectomy (superficial or total according to site) preserving the nerve. High-grade: total parotidectomy, sacrificing only nerve that is invaded, with neck dissection and postoperative radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s3-2","Adenoid cystic carcinoma (cylindroma)","Salivary gland tumours · Malignant tumours","About 10% of salivary cancers, the commonest in the submandibular gland, sublingual gland and minor glands (especially the palate). Slow but relentless: it infiltrates widely along tissue planes and especially nerves (perineural spread, causing pain, facial palsy and skip lesions to the skull base), recurs locally decades later, and metastasises to the lungs (often with long survival) more than to nodes. Histology: cribriform (\"Swiss cheese\", best prognosis), tubular, and solid (worst). Treatment: wide excision with clear nerve margins and postoperative radiotherapy for almost all; survival keeps falling beyond five and ten years, so follow-up is lifelong."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s3-3","Acinic cell carcinoma","Salivary gland tumours · Malignant tumours","Usually in the parotid, sometimes bilateral, mostly in women: a small, firm, mobile lump resembling an adenoma; usually low grade. Parotidectomy; recurrence more often local than regional."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s3-4","Carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma","Salivary gland tumours · Malignant tumours","A carcinoma (often salivary duct carcinoma) arising in a long-standing or recurrent mixed tumour, up to 10% of salivary cancers: a sudden phase of rapid growth, pain or palsy. Radical surgery and radiotherapy; poor prognosis. (Rarer forms: true carcinosarcoma, and the metastasising pleomorphic adenoma.)"],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s3-5","Salivary duct carcinoma","Salivary gland tumours · Malignant tumours","A high-grade cancer resembling breast ductal carcinoma, in older men, with early nodal and distant spread; often HER2 or androgen receptor positive, which allows targeted treatment of recurrence."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s3-6","Others","Salivary gland tumours · Malignant tumours","Polymorphous adenocarcinoma (palate; good prognosis), epithelial–myoepithelial carcinoma, secretory carcinoma (ETV6–NTRK3 fusion, treatable with TRK inhibitors), adenocarcinoma NOS, and undifferentiated carcinoma (rapid, painful, fixed, with facial palsy and nodes)."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s3-7","Squamous cell carcinoma","Salivary gland tumours · Malignant tumours","Primary salivary squamous carcinoma is rare; most squamous cancer in the parotid is metastatic to intraparotid nodes from skin cancer of the scalp, ear, temple and face ( Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck ), which spreads to the parotid in about 5% of high-risk cases. Rapid growth, pain, ulceration, nodes. Radical parotidectomy with excision of skin, muscle, mandible or temporal bone as needed, neck dissection, and postoperative radiotherapy. Figure 2 Squamous cell carcinoma of the right parotid: (A) a parotid swelling and (B) facial palsy. Dhingra, fig. 46.1"],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s3-8","Lymphoma and metastases","Salivary gland tumours · Malignant tumours","Primary MALT lymphoma of the parotid (especially in Sjögren syndrome; Salivary gland infections, stones and swellings ) and secondary nodal lymphoma: biopsy and treat as lymphoma. Melanoma from the scalp and face also metastasises to the parotid."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~c4","Treatment principles","Salivary gland tumours · Treatment principles","Surgery first. Primary tumour: complete excision. In the parotid, superficial, total or radical parotidectomy according to site and extent; in the submandibular gland, excision of the gland with the contents of the triangle; minor gland cancers by wide local excision (often partial maxillectomy for the palate). Facial nerve: preserved whenever it can be separated from tumour, even with malignancy, provided it functions before surgery; a nerve that is paralysed or encased is resected and grafted immediately (great auricular or sural nerve; Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation ), with mastoidectomy if needed to reach a clear proximal margin. Neck: therapeutic dissection (levels I to V) for clinical nodes; elective dissection for high-grade tumours, T3 and T4 tumours, extraglandular extension and facial nerve involvement. Postoperative radiotherapy: high-grade histology, close or positive margins, perineural invasion, extraglandular extension, nodes, and recurrent tumours; neutrons and protons have special roles. Systemic treatment: for metastatic disease; targeted agents for HER2, androgen receptor and NTRK-driven tumours. T staging of major salivary gland cancer (AJCC) Category Definition T1 2 cm or less, no extraparenchymal extension T2 Over 2 cm up to 4 cm, no extraparenchymal extension T3 Over 4 cm, or extraparenchymal extension T4a Skin, mandible, ear canal or facial nerve T4b Skull base, pterygoid plates, or encasing the carotid"],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s5-1","Technique","Salivary gland tumours · Parotidectomy","A modified Blair (lazy S, preauricular-cervical) incision or a facelift incision; a skin flap raised superficial to the parotid fascia; the great auricular nerve identified (its posterior branch preserved where possible); the gland mobilised off the ear cartilage and sternomastoid; the facial nerve trunk found using the tragal pointer, tympanomastoid suture and posterior belly of digastric, then followed forwards along its branches, lifting the superficial lobe off them. Nerve monitoring is widely used. Retrograde dissection from a peripheral branch helps in revision surgery. Figure 3 Landmarks for the facial nerve trunk: tragal pointer, styloid process and posterior belly of digastric. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s5-2","Complications","Salivary gland tumours · Parotidectomy","Facial nerve weakness: temporary in a fifth to a third (about 22% with continuous monitoring), especially the marginal mandibular branch; permanent in about 1 to 2% after superficial parotidectomy for benign disease, rising to about 7% across all parotid surgery (deep lobe, revision, malignancy). Numbness of the ear lobe (great auricular nerve). Frey syndrome (gustatory sweating): sweating and flushing of the cheek skin while eating, months after surgery, because cut parasympathetic secretomotor fibres to the parotid regenerate into the sympathetic nerves of the sweat glands. Clinically apparent in about 20% after superficial parotidectomy, and in many more on testing; confirmed by the starch–iodine (Minor) test. Most need only reassurance; botulinum toxin injected into the skin is effective; an interposed barrier (SMAS flap, sternomastoid flap, acellular dermis, fat) reduces it; tympanic neurectomy is an old remedy. First bite syndrome: a sharp pain with the first mouthful of each meal, from loss of sympathetic supply, after deep lobe and parapharyngeal surgery. Sialocele and salivary fistula (usually settle with pressure, aspiration or botulinum toxin), haematoma, infection, a hollow contour, and tumour recurrence. Figure 4 The mechanism of Frey syndrome. From the anatomy atlas"],["ev","cl:t-saltum~s5-3","Salivary gland tumours: current evidence","Salivary gland tumours · Parotidectomy","A meta-analysis of ultrasound-guided core biopsy in head and neck lesions found an accuracy of 96% for malignancy and a correct specific diagnosis in 87%, better than fine-needle aspiration (Novoa et al., Head Neck 2012); tumour seeding along the needle track is very rare after either (Shah et al., Br J Oral Maxillofac Surg 2016). The Milan system grades salivary cytology by risk of malignancy, which helps with the up to 30% of aspirates that are indeterminate. For small, mobile, superficial benign parotid tumours, extracapsular dissection in experienced hands had recurrence no higher than superficial parotidectomy, with less facial weakness and Frey syndrome (Foresta et al., Oral Surg Oral Med Oral Pathol Oral Radiol 2014). In primary parotidectomy, facial nerve monitoring reduced immediate post-operative weakness but not permanent weakness (Sood et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2015)."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s5-3","Submandibular gland excision","Salivary gland tumours · Parotidectomy","The incision is placed 3 to 4 cm below the mandible, and the marginal mandibular nerve is protected by raising the fascia over the gland (the Hayes Martin manoeuvre) or by finding the nerve directly. A modified Blair incision is used whenever a neck dissection may be needed."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s6-1","Viva essentials","Salivary gland tumours · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The rule of glands: larger gland, more likely benign. Pleomorphic adenoma: pathology, why not to enucleate, superficial parotidectomy, malignant change. Warthin tumour: who, where, bilaterality, pertechnetate. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma grades; adenoid cystic carcinoma and perineural spread. Features of malignancy; FNA versus open biopsy. Parotidectomy: facial nerve landmarks, complications, Frey syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-saltum~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Salivary gland tumours · For the examination","Common pitfalls Enucleating a pleomorphic adenoma. Doing an open biopsy of a parotid lump. Removing a parapharyngeal deep lobe tumour through the mouth. Sacrificing a working facial nerve that could be dissected free."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s1-1","Definition","Superior canal dehiscence · Overview","A defect in the bone over the superior semicircular canal, where it rises towards the floor of the middle cranial fossa, leaving the membranous canal covered only by dura. Figure 1 The anterior surface of the petrous bone with the arcuate eminence, over the superior canal. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-scd~s1-2","Cause","Superior canal dehiscence · Overview","Probably a developmental thinness of the bone, with the final layer lost later in life through minor trauma, straining or the pulsation of the brain. It is often bilateral on CT, though usually symptomatic on one side."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s1-3","The third window","Superior canal dehiscence · Overview","Normally the inner ear has two mobile windows, the oval and the round. The dehiscence adds a third, so sound and pressure energy escape through it. Pressure in the middle ear or the cranial cavity now moves endolymph in the superior canal, causing vertigo; sound energy is diverted away from the cochlea, causing an apparent conductive loss; and bone-conducted sound is heard more loudly."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s2-1","Vestibular","Superior canal dehiscence · Clinical features","Tullio phenomenon: vertigo or oscillopsia triggered by loud sound. Hennebert's sign: vertigo with pressure in the canal, on Valsalva, coughing or straining. Chronic imbalance. The eye movements induced lie in the plane of the superior canal."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s2-2","Auditory","Superior canal dehiscence · Clinical features","Autophony: the patient's own voice sounds loud. Hyperacusis for bone-conducted sounds: hearing their footsteps, chewing, heartbeat, or even their eye movements. Pulsatile and gaze-evoked tinnitus. Aural fullness."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s3-1","Audiogram","Superior canal dehiscence · Investigations","A low-frequency air-bone gap, with bone conduction thresholds that may be better than 0 dB (supranormal). Stapedial reflexes are present , which an otosclerotic or other middle ear conductive loss would abolish."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s3-2","Vestibular evoked myogenic potentials","Superior canal dehiscence · Investigations","The cervical VEMP threshold is abnormally low and the ocular VEMP amplitude abnormally high ( Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory ). A true middle ear conductive loss normally abolishes VEMPs, so their presence with a gap is a strong clue."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s3-3","Electrocochleography","Superior canal dehiscence · Investigations","May show a raised SP to AP ratio."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s3-4","CT","Superior canal dehiscence · Investigations","High-resolution CT reformatted in the plane of the superior canal (Pöschl view) confirms the dehiscence. Thin bone can look dehiscent on CT, so imaging is read with the symptoms and VEMPs."],["sec","cl:t-scd~c4","Differential diagnosis","Superior canal dehiscence · Differential diagnosis","Mimics. How to tell them apart Condition Distinguishing features Otosclerosis Stapedial reflexes absent, VEMPs absent, no vestibular symptoms with sound Patulous Eustachian tube Autophony with breathing, drum moves with respiration, no air-bone gap Perilymph fistula Follows surgery, trauma or straining; fluctuating hearing Ménière's disease Attacks of vertigo for hours, fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss Otosyphilis Positive serology, Hennebert's sign"],["sec","cl:t-scd~s5-1","Conservative","Superior canal dehiscence · Management","Reassurance and explanation; avoiding triggers such as loud noise and straining. Many patients need nothing more."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s5-2","Surgery","Superior canal dehiscence · Management","For disabling vertigo or autophony: plugging the canal or resurfacing the defect, through the middle cranial fossa or a transmastoid approach. Plugging is more reliable for vestibular symptoms but sacrifices the canal; a systematic review favoured transmastoid plugging for disabled patients, because of lower complication and revision rates and a shorter stay. Surgery helps the vestibular symptoms more than the hearing loss. Round window reinforcement is an alternative in some centres."],["ev","cl:t-scd~s5-3","Superior canal dehiscence: current evidence","Superior canal dehiscence · Management","Original description. Minor described superior canal dehiscence in 1998, with sound- and pressure-induced vertigo and a dehiscence on CT ( Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 1998). Diagnosis. Low-threshold, high-amplitude cervical VEMPs and raised ocular VEMPs at 4 kHz are the most specific physiological tests. Surgery. The systematic review favouring transmastoid plugging is Ziylan et al. ( Otol Neurotol 2017)."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s5-3","Risks","Superior canal dehiscence · Management","Sensorineural hearing loss and vestibular loss after repair, and the usual risks of a middle fossa craniotomy. Before stapes surgery A conductive loss with a normal drum and present stapedial reflexes is not otosclerosis. Operating on the stapes in superior canal dehiscence does not help and may harm."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s6-1","Viva essentials","Superior canal dehiscence · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The third window and how it explains each symptom. Tullio phenomenon and Hennebert's sign. Conductive gap with present reflexes, and low VEMP thresholds. CT in the plane of the canal. Plugging or resurfacing."],["sec","cl:t-scd~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Superior canal dehiscence · For the examination","Common pitfalls Offering stapes surgery for a conductive loss with present reflexes. Diagnosing from CT alone, without symptoms and VEMPs."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s1-1","Scope","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Overview","Ototoxicity: hearing or balance damaged by drugs. Noise-induced hearing loss: from sustained or sudden loud sound. Presbycusis: age-related hearing loss. Immune-mediated inner ear disease: primary or as part of systemic disease."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s1-2","Why it matters","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Overview","Much of this hearing loss is preventable, by monitoring drugs and protecting ears from noise, and some is reversible."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s2-1","The drugs","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Ototoxicity","Ototoxic drugs Drug Main target Reversible? Streptomycin, gentamicin Vestibular Often permanent Neomycin, kanamycin, amikacin Cochlea Often permanent Loop diuretics Stria vascularis Usually Salicylates Cochlea Yes Quinine, chloroquine Cochlear vessels Usually; high doses may not be Cisplatin, carboplatin Outer hair cells Often permanent Others include deferoxamine, some ear drops (aminoglycosides and polymyxin) through a perforation, and some topical antiseptics. Where the books differ · Tobramycin Dhingra Mainly vestibulotoxic. Lee Mainly cochleotoxic. In practice Treat tobramycin as able to damage both, and monitor hearing and balance."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s2-2","Aminoglycosides","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Ototoxicity","Hair cells are damaged from the basal turn upwards, so high frequencies go first. Hearing loss may appear during or after treatment. Risk is higher with renal impairment, age over 65, high blood levels, long courses, previous exposure, loop diuretics, and genetic susceptibility: mitochondrial 12S rRNA mutations make even a single dose dangerous, so ask about family members who went deaf after these drugs."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s2-3","Cisplatin","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Ototoxicity","Bilateral, permanent high-frequency loss, worse in young children under five, the elderly, and with noise or radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s2-4","Prevention","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Ototoxicity","Avoid ototoxic drugs where alternatives exist, monitor levels and renal function, and obtain baseline and serial audiograms, including high-frequency audiometry. Sodium thiosulfate , given six hours after each cisplatin dose, roughly halves hearing loss in children with localised tumours, and is now licensed for this. Rapid genetic testing for the MT-RNR1 m.1555A>G variant before aminoglycosides is being introduced in neonatal units, so that carriers receive another antibiotic. On the ward New tinnitus, hearing loss or imbalance on an aminoglycoside or cisplatin: alert the treating team, check levels and renal function, and test hearing."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s2-5","Deliberate use","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Ototoxicity","Gentamicin's vestibular toxicity is used therapeutically in Ménière's disease ."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s3-1","Mechanism","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Noise-induced hearing loss","Noise first stiffens and then kills the outer hair cells of the basal turn, around the region that responds to 4 kHz; 3, 4 and 6 kHz are affected first. 3.1.1 Temporary threshold shift Hearing dulls after loud exposure and recovers. Where the books differ · Recovery from a temporary shift Dhingra Up to two weeks. Lee Within 24 hours. In practice Most temporary shifts recover within a day, but allow up to two weeks before calling a loss permanent. 3.1.2 Permanent threshold shift Repeated exposure turns the temporary shift into irreversible loss. 3.1.3 Acute acoustic trauma A single blast or impulse (around 140 dB or more) causes immediate loss without a preceding temporary shift, often with tinnitus. It can rupture the drum, disrupt the ossicles and damage the cochlea at once. Treat the inner ear loss like sudden sensorineural loss, with early steroids ( Sudden sensorineural hearing loss ). Figure 1 The organ of Corti: outer hair cells are the first casualties of noise and ototoxic drugs. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s3-2","Clinical picture","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Noise-induced hearing loss","High-pitched tinnitus and difficulty hearing in noise. The audiogram shows a symmetrical notch at 4 kHz , which deepens and widens into the speech frequencies with continued exposure. Figure 2 Early noise-induced hearing loss, with a dip at 4000 Hz. Dhingra, fig. 5.7"],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s3-3","Safe limits","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Noise-induced hearing loss","Limits differ by country, so check local regulations. Where the books differ · Exposure limits and the exchange rate Dhingra Occupational limit of 90 dBA for 8 hours; no continuous exposure over 115 dBA; no impulse over 140 dBA. The 5 dB rule : each 5 dB rise halves the safe duration. Hearing conservation (protection and audiometry) starts at 85 dBA. Oxford The UK Control of Noise at Work Regulations 2005 set a lower action value of 80 dB(A), when protection must be offered and information given, and an upper action value of 85 dB(A), when protection is compulsory and hearing surveillance required, with an absolute limit of 87 dB(A) at the ear allowing for protection. European and UK practice uses a 3 dB exchange rate (each 3 dB doubles the energy and halves the safe time); the 5 dB rule is the older American occupational convention. In practice In the UK, offer protection from 80 dB(A) and make it compulsory, with hearing surveillance, from 85 dB(A). Know the 90 dBA limit and the 5 dB rule for the examination."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s3-4","Prevention and management","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Noise-induced hearing loss","Remove from exposure, reduce noise at source, and use hearing protection: earplugs give about 15 to 30 dB, earmuffs 30 to 40 dB, and both together more. Protectors work better at high than low frequencies. Pre-employment and annual audiograms. Hearing aids for established loss."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s3-5","Medicolegal diagnosis","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Noise-induced hearing loss","The Coles, Lutman and Buffin (CLB) guidelines require high-frequency loss beyond that expected for age, a cumulative noise immission level of at least 100 dB (roughly 85 dB(A) daily for many years), and a notch or bulge at 3 to 6 kHz at least 10 dB deeper than the thresholds at 1 or 2 kHz and at 6 or 8 kHz."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s3-6","Hidden hearing loss","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Noise-induced hearing loss","Animal work showed that noise can destroy the synapses between inner hair cells and the cochlear nerve while thresholds recover ( cochlear synaptopathy ), which may explain difficulty hearing in noise with a normal audiogram; in humans the effect appears variable and is still debated. Recreational noise, from personal music players, concerts and shooting, is an increasingly important cause in young people."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s4-1","Picture","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Presbycusis","Gradual, bilateral, symmetrical high-frequency loss. The patient hears speech but cannot understand it, especially in noise, and is intolerant of loud sound because of recruitment."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s4-2","Types (Schuknecht)","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Presbycusis","Types of presbycusis Type Damage Audiogram Discrimination Sensory Hair cells, basal turn Steep high-tone loss Good Neural Cochlear neurones Variable Poor, out of proportion Strial (metabolic) Stria vascularis Flat Good Cochlear conductive Stiff basilar membrane Gently sloping Moderate"],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s4-3","Management","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Presbycusis","Hearing aids, assistive listening devices, speech reading and communication tactics; cochlear implants for severe loss ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ). Untreated hearing loss is linked with isolation and cognitive decline."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s5-1","Primary autoimmune inner ear disease","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Immune-mediated inner ear disease","Rapidly progressive, bilateral, often asymmetric sensorineural loss over weeks to months, usually in middle age (40 to 50), with vestibular symptoms in about half. About 15% have another autoimmune disorder."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s5-2","Secondary","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Immune-mediated inner ear disease","Part of systemic disease: Cogan syndrome (interstitial keratitis with audiovestibular loss), granulomatosis with polyangiitis, systemic lupus, antiphospholipid syndrome, and others."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s5-3","Diagnosis","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Immune-mediated inner ear disease","Bilateral loss of 30 dB or more with progression on serial audiograms within three months. Blood tests: ESR, rheumatoid factor, ANA, complement; anti-Hsp70 antibodies correlate with activity but are not specific. Contrast MRI to exclude other causes. A response to steroids supports the diagnosis."],["ev","cl:t-sensori~s5-4","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease: current evidence","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Immune-mediated inner ear disease","Cisplatin. In the SIOPEL 6 trial in children with standard-risk hepatoblastoma, sodium thiosulfate after cisplatin cut hearing loss from 63% to 33% without reducing survival (Brock et al., NEJM 2018); the American ACCL0431 trial found a similar reduction (Freyer et al., Lancet Oncol 2017). Aminoglycosides. The m.1555A>G variant, carried by about 1 in 500 people, can cause profound deafness after a single dose; a point-of-care test in neonatal intensive care identified carriers within about 26 minutes without delaying antibiotics (McDermott et al., JAMA Pediatr 2022), and NICE has recommended it (2023). Noise. Kujawa and Liberman showed in mice that noise causing only a temporary threshold shift destroys cochlear synapses permanently ( J Neurosci 2009). The World Health Organization estimates that more than a billion young people are at risk from unsafe listening, and its global standard for safe listening venues (2022) sets an average limit of 100 dB(A) over 15 minutes."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s5-4","Treatment","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Immune-mediated inner ear disease","Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day, up to 60 mg, for four weeks. Responders (an improvement of 15 dB at one frequency or 10 dB at consecutive frequencies, or better discrimination) continue to a plateau and taper to the lowest maintenance dose over months. For steroid intolerance or dependence: methotrexate, cyclophosphamide (more toxic), biological agents or intratympanic steroids; note that in a randomised trial methotrexate was no better than placebo at maintaining the hearing gained with steroids (Harris et al., JAMA 2003), so steroid-sparing drugs are given on specialist advice only. Cochlear implantation if hearing is lost."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~c6","Differential diagnosis","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · Differential diagnosis","Others to exclude. Vestibular schwannoma . Ménière's disease . Syphilis. Multiple sclerosis. Familial progressive sensorineural loss."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s7-1","Viva essentials","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Ototoxic drugs: vestibulotoxic versus cochleotoxic, risk factors, monitoring. Noise: 4 kHz notch, temporary and permanent shifts, safe limits and the 5 dB rule. Presbycusis: Schuknecht's four types. Autoimmune inner ear disease: picture and steroid trial."],["sec","cl:t-sensori~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not asking about drugs and noise. Missing a mitochondrial susceptibility to aminoglycosides in the family history. Calling rapidly progressive bilateral loss presbycusis."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s1-1","Applied anatomy","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Overview","The septum has three parts: the columellar septum (the columella, with the medial crura of the alar cartilages), the membranous septum (skin between the columella and the septal cartilage), and the septum proper , made of cartilage and bone covered by mucoperichondrium and mucoperiosteum. 1.1.1 The framework Quadrilateral (septal) cartilage: the front of the septum, supporting the nasal tip and dorsum. Perpendicular plate of the ethmoid: above and behind. Vomer: below and behind. Small contributions: the crest of the maxilla and palatine bones, the nasal spine of the frontal bone, and the rostrum of the sphenoid. Figure 1 Anatomy of the nasal septum: septal cartilage, perpendicular plate of the ethmoid and vomer. Dhingra, fig. 26.1 Figure 2 The septal cartilage and bones of the septum. From the anatomy atlas 1.1.2 Blood supply Little's area on the anteroinferior septum, where four arteries meet, is the commonest site of epistaxis ( Epistaxis )."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s1-2","Why it matters","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Overview","Septal problems cause nasal obstruction, sinusitis, epistaxis and external deformity, and septal surgery is one of the commonest operations in ENT."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s2-1","How it happens","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal trauma","Blows from the front, side or below buckle, fracture, dislocate or crush the septum. The septal cartilage can dislocate from the vomerine groove without any fracture of the nasal bones. Mucosal tears cause profuse epistaxis; an intact mucosa traps blood as a haematoma."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s2-2","Named fractures","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal trauma","Jarjaway fracture: from a frontal blow, running horizontally backwards just above the junction of the septal cartilage with the vomer. Chevallet fracture: from a blow from below, running vertically from the anterior nasal spine up to the junction of the bony and cartilaginous dorsum. Figure 3 Septal fractures: (A) Jarjaway type, (B) Chevallet type. Dhingra, fig. 26.2"],["sec","cl:t-septum~s2-3","Treatment","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal trauma","Drain any haematoma; reposition dislocated or fractured fragments, support them between the flaps with mattress sutures and packing, and treat any nasal bone fracture at the same time ( Nasal and facial fractures ). Ignored septal injuries leave a deviated cartilaginous nose and an asymmetric tip, columella or nostril."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s3-1","Causes","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Deviated nasal septum","3.1.1 Trauma A lateral blow dislocates the cartilage from the vomerine groove and maxillary crest; a frontal blow buckles and telescopes it. Childhood injuries, and birth trauma from a difficult delivery, are often forgotten. 3.1.2 Developmental Unequal growth of the palate and the skull base buckles the septum. Mouth breathers with adenoid hypertrophy often have a high-arched palate and a deviated septum; it is also seen with cleft lip and palate and dental anomalies. Figure 4 Deviated septum with a high-arched palate. Dhingra, fig. 26.3 3.1.3 Racial and hereditary Commoner in white populations, and often in several members of a family."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s3-2","Types","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Deviated nasal septum","Anterior (caudal) dislocation: the lower border of the cartilage projects into one nostril, best seen with the head tilted back. C-shaped deviation: a single curve; the wider side often shows compensatory turbinate hypertrophy. S-shaped deviation: two curves, which can block both sides. Spur: a shelf-like projection, usually at the junction of bone and cartilage, which may press on the lateral wall. Thickening: from an organised haematoma or overlapping fragments. Figure 5 First, the types of septal deviation. Second, a C-shaped deviation nearly blocking the left side. Dhingra, figs 26.4 and 26.6"],["sec","cl:t-septum~s3-3","Clinical features","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Deviated nasal septum","3.3.1 Nasal obstruction One-sided or both; high deviations block more than low ones, because the main airflow passes through the upper nose. Paradoxical obstruction on the wider side comes from compensatory turbinate hypertrophy. 3.3.2 Other symptoms Headache, from a spur pressing on the lateral wall. Sinusitis, from obstructed ostia and poor ventilation. Epistaxis, from crusting over the convexity or vessels stretched over a spur. Reduced smell, when air cannot reach the olfactory cleft. External deformity of the dorsum, tip or columella. Middle ear infections."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s3-4","Where is the obstruction?","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Deviated nasal septum","Levels of nasal obstruction Level Causes Vestibule Caudal septal dislocation, stenosis, synechiae Nasal valve Narrow valve angle, collapse after rhinoplasty, synechiae Attic (upper septum) High septal deviation Turbinates Hypertrophy, concha bullosa Choana Choanal atresia, choanal polyp, adenoids"],["sec","cl:t-septum~s3-5","The nasal valve","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Deviated nasal septum","The narrowest part of the airway, bounded by the septum, the lower edge of the upper lateral cartilage (normally at an angle of about 10 to 15 degrees) and the head of the inferior turbinate. Cottle's test : pulling the cheek laterally opens the valve; if breathing improves, the valve is part of the problem. Figure 6 First, the nasal valve area. Second, Cottle's test: pulling the cheek laterally opens the valve. Dhingra, figs 23.3 and 26.7"],["sec","cl:t-septum~s3-6","Management","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Deviated nasal septum","Minor, symptomless deviations need nothing. Treat coexisting rhinitis first. Operate when the deviation causes obstruction or the problems listed above. 3.6.1 Septoplasty The conservative operation: only the most deviated parts are removed; the rest is straightened (by scoring, morselising or repositioning) and put back. A flap is usually raised on one side only. It has largely replaced submucous resection, and can be done in children with severe obstruction because it preserves growth centres. 3.6.2 Submucous resection Flaps are raised on both sides and the deviated cartilage and bone are removed, leaving dorsal and caudal struts; now mostly historical ( Nose and sinus operations: principles ). 3.6.3 Associated procedures Turbinate reduction for hypertrophy, and septorhinoplasty when the external nose is deviated."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s4-1","Septal haematoma","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal haematoma and abscess","4.1.1 Cause Blood under the perichondrium or periosteum, after nasal trauma or septal surgery, or spontaneously in bleeding disorders. 4.1.2 Clinical picture Bilateral nasal obstruction, frontal headache and pressure over the bridge, with a smooth, rounded, soft swelling of the septum in both nostrils. Figure 7 Septal haematoma: smooth swellings of the septum in both nostrils. Dhingra, fig. 26.8 4.1.3 Treatment Aspirate small collections with a wide-bore needle; incise larger ones parallel to the floor, excise a small strip of mucosa to keep it draining, pack both sides to prevent reaccumulation, and give antibiotics to prevent abscess."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s4-2","Septal abscess","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal haematoma and abscess","4.2.1 Cause Usually an infected haematoma; occasionally after a furuncle of the nose or lip, or systemic infections such as typhoid or measles. 4.2.2 Clinical picture Severe obstruction, pain and tenderness over the bridge, fever and chills, a red, swollen nose, fluctuant septal swelling and tender submandibular nodes. Figure 8 Septal abscess. Dhingra, fig. 26.9 4.2.3 Treatment Drain urgently at the most dependent point, excise a piece of mucosa, remove pus and dead cartilage, reopen daily for two or three days if needed, and give antibiotics guided by culture for at least ten days."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s4-3","Complications","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal haematoma and abscess","Cartilage necrosis and saddle nose (supratip depression), corrected by augmentation rhinoplasty after two or three months ( Facial plastic surgery ). Septal perforation. Meningitis and cavernous sinus thrombosis, rare but serious. Never send home a swollen septum A child or adult with nasal obstruction after a blow needs the septum examined. A missed haematoma in a child may only show itself years later as a saddle nose."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s5-1","Causes","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal perforation","Causes of septal perforation Trauma Septal surgery repeated cautery, nose picking, piercing Infection and inflammation Septal abscess myiasis, rhinolith or foreign body, tuberculosis, lupus, leprosy, syphilis Destructive disease Granulomatosis with polyangiitis NK/T-cell lymphoma, carcinoma Drugs and chemicals Cocaine long-term steroid sprays, chromium, arsenic, soda ash Unknown Idiopathic Lupus, tuberculosis and leprosy perforate the cartilaginous septum; syphilis perforates the bony septum. Ask about cocaine use at any age. The granulomatous causes are covered in Granulomatous disease of the nose . Figure 9 First, a septal perforation. Second, a septum deliberately perforated for an ornament. Dhingra, figs 26.10 and 26.11"],["sec","cl:t-septum~s5-2","Clinical picture","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal perforation","Small anterior perforations whistle with breathing. Larger ones crust, obstruct and bleed when crusts are removed; very large ones can cause a saddle nose. Posterior perforations at the bony–cartilaginous junction are the ones that bleed heavily."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s5-3","Investigations","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal perforation","Look for the cause: blood tests (ANCA, ACE, syphilis serology), urine drug screen where relevant, and biopsy of granulation or of the edge of the perforation (taken from the posterosuperior edge, away from the healed anterior rim)."],["ev","cl:t-septum~s5-4","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess: current evidence","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal perforation","Septoplasty works. Two randomised trials have shown that septoplasty, with or without turbinate reduction, beats medical treatment for nasal obstruction with a deviated septum. In the Dutch trial, septoplasty improved quality of life at 12 and 24 months (van Egmond et al., Lancet 2019); in the UK NAIROS trial, SNOT-22 scores at six months were 20 points better after septoplasty than after nasal steroid spray and saline (Carrie et al., Health Technol Assess 2024). Perforation. Closure with endoscopic, pedicled flaps (such as the anterior ethmoidal artery flap) succeeds in most perforations under about 2 cm in published series, but evidence is from case series."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s5-4","Treatment","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · Septal perforation","Conservative: saline or alkaline douches and bland ointment to keep the nose free of crusts. Septal button: a silicone obturator. Practice has moved away from a routine \"trial\" of a button: some patients find it irritating and obstructive, and a button can enlarge the perforation. Custom-made obturators are more comfortable but lack strong evidence. Surgical closure: for small, inactive perforations, with local rotation or advancement flaps and an interposition graft. Perforations larger than about 2 cm are hard to close with local advancement flaps, but techniques borrowed from endoscopic skull base surgery, such as a pedicled flap based on the anterior ethmoidal artery , now allow closure of larger defects. Figure 10 A silicone septal button closing a perforation. Dhingra, fig. 26.12"],["sec","cl:t-septum~s6-1","On call","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · For the examination","A blocked nose after a blow Look at the septum in every patient with nasal trauma. A boggy, swollen septum is a haematoma: drain it the same day. Pack both sides and give antibiotics. Review for reaccumulation and abscess."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s6-2","Viva essentials","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Parts of the septum and its framework. Types of deviation, and the symptoms each causes. Cottle's test and the nasal valve. Septoplasty versus submucous resection. Septal haematoma and abscess: treatment and complications. Causes of septal perforation, and which diseases perforate the bony or cartilaginous part."],["sec","cl:t-septum~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing a septal haematoma after trauma. Operating on a septum when rhinitis is the real cause of obstruction. Biopsying the healed anterior edge of a perforation."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s1-1","The range","Sinonasal tumours · Overview","Sinonasal tumours Benign epithelial Papillomas squamous, inverted, oncocytic, exophytic Benign other Haemangioma, schwannoma, meningioma pleomorphic adenoma, chondroma, angiofibroma Benign bony Osteoma, fibrous dysplasia ossifying fibroma, ameloblastoma Malignant epithelial Squamous cell carcinoma adenocarcinoma, adenoid cystic carcinoma, undifferentiated carcinoma Malignant other Melanoma, olfactory neuroblastoma lymphoma, plasmacytoma, sarcomas, haemangiopericytoma"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s1-2","Epidemiology","Sinonasal tumours · Overview","Sinonasal cancer is rare (well under 1% of all cancers). The maxillary sinus is the commonest site, then the ethmoids; frontal and sphenoid primaries are rare."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s1-3","Risk factors","Sinonasal tumours · Overview","Hardwood dust (furniture workers): adenocarcinoma of the ethmoids and upper nose. Nickel refining: squamous and anaplastic carcinoma. Leather work, chromium, mustard gas, formaldehyde. Snuff rich in nickel and chromium (Bantu populations). Smoking and HPV for some squamous carcinomas."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s1-4","Histology","Sinonasal tumours · Overview","More than 80% of malignant tumours are squamous cell carcinoma; the rest are adenocarcinoma, adenoid cystic carcinoma, melanoma, olfactory neuroblastoma, lymphoma and sarcomas. The WHO classification (fourth edition 2017, fifth edition 2022) reorganised sinonasal tumours and added new entities, such as SMARCB1-deficient and HPV-related multiphenotypic carcinoma, so histology now carries more weight than ever. Figure 1 Adenocarcinoma with gland formation and intracytoplasmic mucin. Dhingra, fig. 40.4"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s2-1","Squamous papilloma","Sinonasal tumours · Benign tumours of the nasal cavity","A warty lesion of the vestibule or anterior septum; excised. Figure 2 Squamous papilloma of the left nasal vestibule. Dhingra, fig. 39.1"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s2-2","Inverted papilloma","Sinonasal tumours · Benign tumours of the nasal cavity","2.2.1 What it is A Schneiderian papilloma in which the epithelium grows inwards into the stroma. Typically in men of 40 to 70, almost always one-sided, arising from the lateral wall near the middle meatus and extending into the maxillary and ethmoid sinuses. HPV is found in some. 2.2.2 Three warnings It destroys bone by pressure. It recurs if incompletely removed. It harbours dysplasia or squamous cell carcinoma in about 5 to 10%, so the whole specimen must be examined; carcinoma arising in it behaves like de novo squamous carcinoma. 2.2.3 Clinical picture One-sided obstruction, discharge and bleeding, with a firm, greyish, irregular mass that can masquerade as a simple polyp. Figure 3 First, inverted papilloma in a 79-year-old man, with its CT. Second, an inverted papilloma in the right nose looking like a simple polyp. Dhingra, figs 39.2 and 39.3 Figure 4 Histology of a Schneiderian papilloma: ribbons of thickened epithelium growing into the stroma. Dhingra, fig. 39.4 2.2.4 Imaging CT shows a one-sided mass and focal hyperostosis at the site of attachment; MRI shows a convoluted cerebriform pattern. 2.2.5 Treatment Complete removal including the mucoperiosteum and drilling of the bone at the site of attachment, now usually endoscopic (endoscopic medial maxillectomy) and formerly by lateral rhinotomy or midfacial degloving. Long-term endoscopic follow-up."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s2-3","Vascular and other benign tumours","Sinonasal tumours · Benign tumours of the nasal cavity","Haemangioma (lobular capillary haemangioma, bleeding polyp of the septum ): a red, friable mass on the anterior septum, often in pregnancy; excised with its base. Schwannoma, meningioma, chondroma, pleomorphic adenoma of the septum. Angiofibroma ( Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma ). Encephalocele, glioma and dermoid ( Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses ). Figure 5 A bleeding polyp arising from the right side of the septum. Dhingra, fig. 39.5"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s3-1","Osteoma","Sinonasal tumours · Benign bony tumours","The commonest benign sinus tumour, usually in the frontal sinus, then ethmoid; often an incidental finding on CT. Symptoms arise when it blocks drainage (mucocele, sinusitis), bulges into the orbit or erodes the skull base. Multiple osteomas suggest Gardner syndrome (with colonic polyposis). Removed endoscopically or through an external approach when symptomatic. Figure 6 First, a frontoethmoidal osteoma invading the orbit. Second, an osteoma of the right frontal sinus. Dhingra, figs 40.1 and 40.2"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s3-2","Fibrous dysplasia","Sinonasal tumours · Benign bony tumours","Bone replaced by fibrous tissue and woven bone; monostotic , polyostotic , or with skin pigmentation and endocrine disorders ( McCune-Albright syndrome ). A painless swelling of the maxilla in a child or adolescent, with a ground-glass appearance on CT. Growth usually slows after puberty; conservative recontouring for deformity or compression. Figure 7 Fibrous dysplasia of the maxilla in a 13-year-old girl. Dhingra, fig. 40.3"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s3-3","Ossifying fibroma","Sinonasal tumours · Benign bony tumours","A well-defined, expansile lesion with a bony rim, more aggressive in its juvenile form; excised completely."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s3-4","Ameloblastoma","Sinonasal tumours · Benign bony tumours","An odontogenic tumour of the jaw, commonest in the mandible, that may invade the maxillary sinus; locally aggressive and recurrent unless widely excised."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s4-1","Clinical picture","Sinonasal tumours · Carcinoma of the maxillary sinus","Usually men of 40 to 60. Early symptoms are easily mistaken for sinusitis: nasal stuffiness, bloodstained discharge, facial pain or numbness, and epiphora. Late features depend on the direction of spread. Spread of maxillary carcinoma Direction Structures Features Medial Nasal cavity, ethmoids Obstruction, discharge, epistaxis Anterior Cheek Swelling, then skin invasion Inferior Alveolus, palate Toothache, loose teeth, ill-fitting dentures, palatal swelling Superior Orbit Proptosis, diplopia, pain, epiphora Posterior Pterygoid plates and muscles, infratemporal fossa Trismus, spread to nasopharynx and skull base Intracranial Through the ethmoids, cribriform plate or foramen lacerum Neurological signs Figure 8 Carcinoma of the left maxilla in a 75-year-old man: (A) swelling and redness of the cheek, (B) expansion of the upper alveolus with an intraoral growth, (C) CT showing destruction of the maxilla and orbital floor. Dhingra, fig. 40.6"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s4-2","Lymph node and distant spread","Sinonasal tumours · Carcinoma of the maxillary sinus","Nodes are uncommon and late (submandibular and upper jugular); the first-echelon retropharyngeal nodes cannot be felt. Distant metastases go to the lungs and bone."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s4-3","Investigations","Sinonasal tumours · Carcinoma of the maxillary sinus","CT in three planes: bone destruction and staging. MRI: separates tumour from retained secretions, and shows orbital, perineural and intracranial spread. Biopsy: endoscopic, or from the mouth or nose when the tumour presents there; the Caldwell-Luc route is no longer preferred. Staging: chest CT or PET-CT."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s4-4","Classifications","Sinonasal tumours · Carcinoma of the maxillary sinus","4.4.1 Ohngren's line An imaginary line from the medial canthus to the angle of the mandible. Tumours above and behind it (suprastructure) have a worse prognosis than those below and in front of it. 4.4.2 Lederman's classification Two horizontal lines of Sebileau, through the floors of the orbits and the floors of the antra, divide the region into suprastructure (ethmoid, sphenoid, frontal sinuses and olfactory area), mesostructure (maxillary sinus and respiratory nose) and infrastructure (alveolus). Figure 9 First, Ohngren's line. Second, Lederman's classification. Dhingra, figs 40.7 and 40.8 4.4.3 AJCC TNM for the maxillary sinus T staging of maxillary sinus carcinoma T Extent T1 Limited to the mucosa, no bone erosion T2 Bone erosion including the hard palate or middle meatus, but not the posterior wall or pterygoid plates T3 Posterior wall, subcutaneous tissue, floor or medial wall of the orbit, pterygoid fossa, ethmoids T4a Anterior orbit, cheek skin, pterygoid plates, infratemporal fossa, cribriform plate, sphenoid or frontal sinus T4b Orbital apex, dura, brain, middle cranial fossa, cranial nerves other than V2, nasopharynx, clivus"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s4-5","Treatment","Sinonasal tumours · Carcinoma of the maxillary sinus","4.5.1 Surgery Resection with clear margins, followed by radiotherapy for most stages: Medial maxillectomy (endoscopic or open) for medial tumours. Total maxillectomy through a Weber-Fergusson incision (lateral rhinotomy with lip split, extended below the eye), with orbital exenteration when the orbit is invaded beyond the periosteum. Craniofacial resection when the cribriform plate or anterior skull base is involved. Endoscopic resection now achieves oncological outcomes equivalent to open surgery for most tumours, with less morbidity, provided clear margins can be obtained. Figure 10 First, the Weber-Fergusson incision. Second, maxillectomy with orbital exenteration, before and after rehabilitation. Dhingra, figs 40.10 and 40.9 4.5.2 Reconstruction and rehabilitation A dental obturator closes the palatal defect, or a free flap reconstructs it; orbital prostheses after exenteration. 4.5.3 Radiotherapy and chemotherapy Postoperative radiotherapy for most; primary chemoradiotherapy for unresectable tumours; neoadjuvant chemotherapy in selected cases: advanced tumours involving the orbit or skull base may first have induction chemotherapy, with the response guiding radiotherapy or surgery. 4.5.4 Neck Neck dissection or irradiation for nodal disease, and elective treatment in some advanced tumours. Nodal metastases at presentation suggest a high-grade tumour such as squamous carcinoma or SNUC."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s4-6","Prognosis","Sinonasal tumours · Carcinoma of the maxillary sinus","Five-year survival is around a third to a half overall, worse with orbital apex, skull base or intracranial spread."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s5-1","Ethmoid","Sinonasal tumours · Tumours of the ethmoid, frontal and sphenoid","Often adenocarcinoma in woodworkers. Intestinal-type adenocarcinoma , linked to hardwood dust, looks like bowel cancer under the microscope but is not a metastasis; it is often multifocal, so wide removal of the sinus mucosa is advised. Low-grade non-intestinal adenocarcinoma may need surgery alone; high-grade disease needs radiotherapy too. Nasal obstruction, bloodstained discharge, a mass in the medial canthus, proptosis with lateral displacement of the eye, and early skull base spread. Craniofacial or endoscopic resection with radiotherapy. Figure 11 Carcinoma of the ethmoid. Dhingra, fig. 40.11"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s5-2","Frontal","Sinonasal tumours · Tumours of the ethmoid, frontal and sphenoid","Very rare: forehead swelling, frontal pain, displacement of the eye downwards, and early intracranial spread."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s5-3","Sphenoid","Sinonasal tumours · Tumours of the ethmoid, frontal and sphenoid","Usually secondary or from the nasopharynx; presents with cranial nerve palsies (second to sixth) and headache."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s6-1","Olfactory neuroblastoma","Sinonasal tumours · Other malignant tumours","Esthesioneuroblastoma , from the olfactory epithelium at the roof of the nose, at any age with peaks in the second and sixth decades. One-sided obstruction, epistaxis and loss of smell, with a mass high in the nose that may extend through the cribriform plate (a dumb-bell shape). Staged by the Kadish system (A nasal cavity, B sinuses, C beyond), which discriminates poorly, so the Dulguerov TNM-based system is often preferred. Somatostatin-receptor PET (DOTATATE) helps stage and follow it, as it does neuroendocrine tumours. Treated by craniofacial or endoscopic resection with radiotherapy. Up to 20% spread to the neck, sometimes late, so long-term neck surveillance is needed."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s6-2","Mucosal melanoma","Sinonasal tumours · Other malignant tumours","Older adults, on the septum or lateral wall; pigmented or amelanotic, bleeding; spreads by blood and lymph and has a poor prognosis; wide excision and radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s6-3","Lymphoma, plasmacytoma and sarcomas","Sinonasal tumours · Other malignant tumours","NK/T-cell lymphoma ( Granulomatous disease of the nose ), extramedullary plasmacytoma, and rhabdomyosarcoma in children. Figure 12 Rhabdomyosarcoma of the nose in a two-and-a-half-year-old boy. Dhingra, fig. 39.7"],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s6-4","Sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma (SNUC)","Sinonasal tumours · Other malignant tumours","Aggressive, often presenting with intracranial and nodal spread; it responds well to induction chemotherapy followed by chemoradiotherapy, with surgery when the response is poor."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s6-5","NUT carcinoma","Sinonasal tumours · Other malignant tumours","Defined by a NUTM1 rearrangement; affects young adults and has a very poor prognosis."],["ev","cl:t-sinus_ca~s6-6","Sinonasal tumours: current evidence","Sinonasal tumours · Other malignant tumours","Endoscopic surgery. The European position paper on endoscopic management of sinonasal and skull base tumours concluded that, in selected patients and experienced hands, endoscopic resection gives survival comparable to open surgery with less morbidity (Lund et al., Rhinology Suppl 2010); later large series and database studies support this for appropriately selected T1 to T3 tumours. SNUC. In a multicentre study, patients whose SNUC responded to induction chemotherapy did best with definitive chemoradiotherapy, whereas non-responders did best with surgery followed by chemoradiotherapy (Amit et al., J Clin Oncol 2019). Inverted papilloma. Endoscopic resection with drilling of the attachment site gives lower recurrence than older open surgery."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s6-6","Neuroendocrine carcinoma","Sinonasal tumours · Other malignant tumours","Small and large cell types, treated with combined chemotherapy and radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s7-1","Viva essentials","Sinonasal tumours · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Occupational risks: wood dust and adenocarcinoma, nickel and squamous carcinoma. Inverted papilloma: site, three warnings, imaging and treatment. Maxillary carcinoma: symptoms by direction of spread. Ohngren's line and Lederman's classification. T staging, and maxillectomy with or without orbital exenteration. Olfactory neuroblastoma and Kadish staging."],["sec","cl:t-sinus_ca~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Sinonasal tumours · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating one-sided sinus symptoms with antibiotics for months. Removing a one-sided polyp without sending it for histology. Calling numbness of the cheek sinusitis."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s1-1","The operations","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Overview","Nose and sinus operations Diagnostic Nasal endoscopy antral puncture (proof puncture) Septum and turbinates Septoplasty submucous resection, turbinate reduction Maxillary sinus Middle meatal antrostomy inferior meatal antrostomy, Caldwell-Luc Ethmoid, frontal, sphenoid Endoscopic sinus surgery external ethmoidectomy, frontal trephine, Lynch-Howarth, osteoplastic flap, Draf procedures Skull base Endoscopic skull base surgery CSF leak repair, pituitary surgery Other Oroantral fistula repair balloon sinuplasty"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s1-2","The danger areas","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Overview","The lamina papyracea (orbit), the cribriform plate and its thin lateral lamella (CSF leak), the anterior ethmoidal artery in the skull base (orbital haematoma), the optic nerve and carotid artery in the sphenoid and Onodi cells, and the nasolacrimal duct in front of the maxillary ostium. Figure 1 The ethmoid roof and anterior ethmoidal artery, and the relations of the sphenoid sinus: optic nerve, internal carotid artery and vidian nerve. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s2-1","Indications","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Diagnostic nasal endoscopy","Every chronic nasal complaint: obstruction, discharge, epistaxis, smell loss, headache, suspected tumour or CSF leak, and follow-up after surgery; biopsies and swabs under vision."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s2-2","Technique","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Diagnostic nasal endoscopy","After topical decongestant and anaesthetic (4% lignocaine with a vasoconstrictor), with the patient sitting or lying, a 4 mm 0° or 30° rigid endoscope makes three passes: First pass: along the floor to the nasopharynx: inferior turbinate and meatus, Eustachian tube, fossa of Rosenmüller. Second pass: between the middle and inferior turbinates to the sphenoethmoidal recess and superior meatus. Third pass: under the middle turbinate into the middle meatus: uncinate process, bulla, hiatus semilunaris and accessory ostia. Figure 2 The region of the Eustachian tube, sphenoethmoidal recess, choana and fossa of Rosenmüller. Dhingra, fig. 89.1 Figure 3 Endoscopic view of the left nasal cavity showing the uncinate process, with sites for injection on the lateral wall and middle turbinate. Dhingra, fig. 90.2"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s3-1","Antral puncture (proof puncture)","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Old maxillary operations","3.1.1 Indications Chronic or subacute maxillary sinusitis, to confirm the diagnosis and wash out pus; to obtain a specimen for culture or cytology. Contraindicated in acute sinusitis for fear of osteomyelitis. 3.1.2 Technique Under local anaesthesia, a Lichtwitz trocar and cannula pierce the thin lateral wall of the inferior meatus 1.5 to 2 cm behind the front of the inferior turbinate, directed towards the ear on the same side; the sinus is irrigated with warm saline until the return is clear. Figure 4 Antral puncture through the inferior meatus. Dhingra, fig. 84.1 3.1.3 What comes back Thin amber fluid with cholesterol crystals means a cyst; blobs of mucopus mean hyperplastic sinusitis; foul pus mixing with the fluid means suppuration. 3.1.4 Complications Cheek swelling (the cannula in the soft tissues), orbital injury through the roof, puncture of the posterior wall, bleeding and, rarely, fatal air embolism (never blow air into the antrum)."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s3-2","Inferior meatal antrostomy","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Old maxillary operations","A window under the inferior turbinate for drainage. It fails because mucociliary clearance carries mucus to the natural ostium, not to the new opening; risks bleeding and nasolacrimal duct injury. Now obsolete. Figure 5 Intranasal antrostomy in the inferior meatus. Dhingra, fig. 85.1"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s3-3","Caldwell-Luc operation","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Old maxillary operations","3.3.1 What it is Opening the maxillary sinus through its anterior wall in the canine fossa via a sublabial incision, with a nasoantral window. 3.3.2 Indications (historical and current) Irreversible sinus disease, foreign bodies and tooth roots, dental cysts, oroantral fistula, biopsy of antral tumours, recurrent antrochoanal polyp, maxillary and orbital floor fractures, access to the ethmoids (Horgan) or pterygopalatine fossa (maxillary artery ligation, vidian neurectomy). Avoided in children under about 17, whose developing teeth lie in the anterior wall. 3.3.3 Complications Bleeding, numbness of the cheek and teeth (infraorbital and superior alveolar nerves), nasolacrimal duct injury, sublabial (oroantral) fistula and, rarely, osteomyelitis. Figure 6 The Caldwell-Luc operation: (A) incision, (B) inside view and position of the antrostomy. Dhingra, fig. 86.1 3.3.4 Present status Most indications are now handled endoscopically; it remains useful for disease in the anterior and lateral recesses of the sinus and combined approaches."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s4-1","Submucous resection","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Septal and turbinate surgery","Flaps raised on both sides of the septum and all deformed cartilage and bone removed, leaving only dorsal and caudal struts. Not done before about 17. Figure 7 First, submucous resection: incision and elevation of the flaps. Second, the dorsal and caudal struts that must be preserved to avoid a supratip depression. Dhingra, figs 87.1 and 87.2"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s4-2","Septoplasty","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Septal and turbinate surgery","The conservative operation that has replaced submucous resection: a Killian (or hemitransfixion for caudal deviation) incision, a flap on one side, limited removal of bone and cartilage, and correction of the rest by scoring, cross-hatching, shaving or wedge excision and repositioning on the maxillary crest; can be done in children. Figure 8 First, straightening septal cartilage by scoring the concave side, shaving the convex side, or repositioning a dislocated cartilage. Second, septal incisions: (A) Killian's, (B) hemitransfixion. Dhingra, figs 88.1 and 88.2 Submucous resection or septoplasty? Submucous resection Septoplasty Dissection Extensive, flaps on both sides Limited, one flap Cartilage Removed, leaving dorsal and caudal struts Mostly preserved and repositioned Age Not before about 17 Children too Complications Supratip depression, columellar retraction, flapping septum, perforation Fewer Revision Difficult Easier"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s4-3","Indications for septoplasty","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Septal and turbinate surgery","Obstruction from a deviated septum, as part of septorhinoplasty, epistaxis from a spur, sinusitis or contact headache from deviation, access for endoscopic sinus, lacrimal or pituitary surgery, and sleep-disordered breathing."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s4-4","Complications of septal surgery","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Septal and turbinate surgery","Bleeding, septal haematoma and abscess, perforation, saddle nose and supratip depression, columellar retraction, synechiae, anosmia, and rarely CSF leak. Figure 9 A supratip depression after extensive septal surgery. Dhingra, fig. 87.3"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s4-5","Turbinate surgery","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Septal and turbinate surgery","Submucosal reduction (microdebrider, radiofrequency, coblation), outfracture, partial turbinectomy, laser; preserve mucosa to avoid an empty nose."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s5-1","Principle","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic sinus surgery","Functional endoscopic sinus surgery removes disease in the ostiomeatal complex and enlarges the natural ostia, so that ventilation and mucociliary clearance recover; the mucosa is preserved."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s5-2","Indications","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic sinus surgery","Chronic rhinosinusitis with or without polyps after failed medical treatment, recurrent acute sinusitis, complications of sinusitis (orbital abscess, mucocele), fungal sinusitis, antrochoanal polyp, and access for extended procedures: CSF leak repair, orbital and optic nerve decompression, dacryocystorhinostomy, sphenopalatine artery ligation, choanal atresia, tumours and the skull base. 5.2.1 Who should have surgery An international appropriateness consensus (RAND method) defined a minimum below which endoscopic sinus surgery is unlikely to help: in adults with uncomplicated chronic rhinosinusitis ( Chronic rhinosinusitis and nasal polyps ), a CT Lund–Mackay score of at least 1, a trial of at least eight weeks of topical nasal steroid plus a short course of systemic steroid (with polyps) or antibiotics (without polyps), and a SNOT-22 score of 20 or more after that treatment. Up to one in three patients do not achieve a clinically meaningful improvement after surgery, and the preoperative SNOT-22 is the best predictor of benefit, so selection matters. Equally, repeated rounds of medical treatment that delay surgery in patients who meet the criteria may worsen the final result."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s5-3","Preparation","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic sinus surgery","CT of the sinuses reviewed systematically before every operation (the CLOSE checklist: cribriform plate, lamina papyracea, Onodi cell, sphenoid and carotid, ethmoidal artery); topical decongestion and infiltration. Figure 10 The lateral nasal wall with the middle turbinate removed (uncinate, bulla and hiatus), and the ground lamella separating anterior from posterior ethmoid cells. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s5-4","Steps (anterior to posterior, Messerklinger-Stammberger)","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic sinus surgery","Medialise the middle turbinate; trim a concha bullosa by removing its lateral lamella. Uncinectomy. Middle meatal antrostomy: find and enlarge the natural maxillary ostium, which lies behind the lower uncinate, above the inferior turbinate. Bullectomy (anterior ethmoidectomy), staying away from the orbit, skull base and anterior ethmoidal artery. Posterior ethmoidectomy through the lower, medial part of the basal (ground) lamella; beware an Onodi cell around the optic nerve. Frontal recess clearance and frontal sinusotomy if diseased: the opening lies lateral to the middle turbinate attachment, medial to the orbit, in front of the anterior ethmoidal artery and behind the agger nasi; respect the mucosa to prevent stenosis. Sphenoidotomy through the natural ostium, about 1 cm above the choana, medial to the superior turbinate. The posterior-to-anterior ( Wigand ) technique starts at the sphenoid and follows the skull base forwards, for extensive polyposis and revisions. 5.4.1 Doing it well Revision cases most often show a residual uncinate process, a missed natural ostium with a separate antrostomy (causing mucus recirculation) and residual anterior ethmoid cells, so the basic steps done well prevent many revisions. Polyp disease does better with more complete surgery (wide antrostomies, clearing all ethmoid partitions, extended frontal and sphenoid surgery) to give topical steroids access. Total intravenous anaesthesia gives a drier field; routine preoperative steroids and antibiotics are not needed. Image guidance is useful in revisions and abnormal anatomy, but has not bee"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s5-5","Frontal sinus procedures","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic sinus surgery","Draf I (frontal recess clearance), Draf IIa and IIb (enlarging the frontal ostium), and Draf III (the endoscopic modified Lothrop, joining both frontal sinuses across the midline). External options: trephination, Lynch-Howarth frontoethmoidectomy, the osteoplastic flap with obliteration, and cranialisation after posterior wall fractures."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s5-6","Balloon sinuplasty","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic sinus surgery","Dilatation of the natural ostia with a balloon catheter. It cannot treat the ethmoids, and suits only limited disease without polyps."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s5-7","After surgery","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic sinus surgery","Saline irrigation, steroid sprays for polyposis or allergy, antibiotics as needed, and endoscopic cleaning of crusts and division of adhesions at weekly visits until the cavity heals. Revision rates are about 11% at three years, 19% at five and 17% at ten."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s5-8","Complications","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic sinus surgery","Complications of endoscopic sinus surgery Type Examples Minor Synechiae, crusting, bleeding, middle turbinate lateralisation, periorbital bruising, surgical emphysema of the lid, dental pain Orbital Breach of the lamina papyracea, orbital haematoma (anterior ethmoidal artery), injury to the medial rectus, nasolacrimal duct injury and epiphora, optic nerve injury and blindness Intracranial CSF leak, meningitis, pneumocephalus, brain injury Vascular Internal carotid artery injury, sphenopalatine and ethmoidal haemorrhage Other Anosmia, frontal sinus stenosis and mucocele Orbital haematoma during surgery Proptosis during or after surgery needs urgent assessment of vision, pupils and intraocular pressure. A tense eye with raised pressure and falling vision: stop, remove packs, and perform a lateral canthotomy and cantholysis at once; consider medical treatment and orbital decompression ( Proptosis and the orbit )."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~c6","Endoscopic skull base surgery","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Endoscopic skull base surgery","Through the nose to the brain. Extended endonasal approaches reach the sella (pituitary tumours), the anterior skull base and cribriform plate (olfactory neuroblastoma, meningioma), the clivus and the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae. The defect is closed in layers, and the nasoseptal flap on the posterior septal artery has greatly reduced postoperative CSF leaks ( CSF rhinorrhoea ). Pituitary surgery is covered in Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions , and the wider skull base in Skull base and the parapharyngeal space ."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s7-1","Causes","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Oroantral fistula","Extraction of the second premolar or upper molars, whose roots lie close to the sinus floor (especially with an apical abscess); a Caldwell-Luc incision that fails to heal; carcinoma; fractures; osteitis, syphilis and granulomatous disease. Figure 12 An oro-antral fistula: the thin floor of the maxillary sinus lies over the roots of the upper teeth. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s7-2","Clinical picture","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Oroantral fistula","Fluids and food regurgitate into the nose; a foul discharge from the infected sinus into the nose or mouth; the patient cannot drink through a straw or blow a wind instrument. A probe passes from the socket into the antrum."],["ev","cl:t-sinussurg~s7-3","Nose and sinus operations: principles: current evidence","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Oroantral fistula","Appropriateness. The international RAND consensus defined the minimum criteria for endoscopic sinus surgery in adults with uncomplicated chronic rhinosinusitis given above (Rudmik et al., Int Forum Allergy Rhinol 2016). Surgery or medicines. In the UK MACRO trial of about 500 adults with persistent chronic rhinosinusitis, compared with placebo, surgery improved symptoms and quality of life at six months, while three months of clarithromycin did not (Philpott et al., Lancet 2025). Balloon dilatation. A Cochrane review found no good-quality evidence comparing balloon sinuplasty with conventional surgery (Ahmed et al., 2011). Septoplasty. In the UK NAIROS trial of more than 300 adults, septoplasty improved SNOT-22 scores by 20 points more than a nasal steroid and saline at six months (Carrie et al., Health Technol Assess 2024); a Dutch trial reached the same conclusion (van Egmond et al., Lancet 2019)."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s7-3","Treatment","Nose and sinus operations: principles · Oroantral fistula","Fresh, small fistula without infection or a retained root: suture the gum margins and give antibiotics. Chronic or large fistula: treat the sinusitis (endoscopic antrostomy, removal of any root), excise the epithelialised track and close it with a buccal advancement or palatal rotation flap; or an obturator after cancer surgery."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s8-1","Viva essentials","Nose and sinus operations: principles · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Antral puncture: site, direction, findings and complications, including air embolism. Caldwell-Luc operation: incision, indications, complications, why not in children. Submucous resection versus septoplasty. Steps of endoscopic sinus surgery and the danger areas. Complications of endoscopic sinus surgery and their management. Oroantral fistula."],["sec","cl:t-sinussurg~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Nose and sinus operations: principles · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating without reviewing the CT for the dangerous variants. Making a second maxillary opening that does not join the natural ostium (recirculation). Stripping mucosa in the frontal recess."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s1-1","The cancers","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Overview","Basal cell carcinoma is the commonest human cancer and makes up about 70 to 75% of non-melanoma skin cancers; cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma accounts for about 20%. Merkel cell carcinoma is rare and aggressive. Between a fifth and a third of melanomas arise in the head and neck, and their incidence is rising. Rarer tumours include sebaceous carcinoma of the eyelid, adnexal carcinomas, dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans, angiosarcoma of the scalp in the elderly, and atypical fibroxanthoma."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s1-2","Risk factors","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Overview","Ultraviolet radiation is the dominant cause, but in two patterns: cumulative exposure drives BCC and SCC, while intense intermittent exposure and blistering sunburn in childhood drive melanoma. Tanning beds add to both. Fair skin of Fitzpatrick types I and II, red or blond hair, freckling and many or atypical naevi mark the susceptible patient. Immunosuppression transforms the risk of SCC. Organ transplant recipients have up to a hundredfold increase, and their tumours are aggressive; HIV ( HIV and ENT ) and chronic lymphocytic leukaemia have a similar effect. Reducing immunosuppression where possible, or switching to an mTOR inhibitor such as sirolimus, helps. Radiation, arsenic, and chronic scars and ulcers also predispose; SCC arising in a burn scar is a Marjolin ulcer . Among the inherited syndromes, xeroderma pigmentosum impairs DNA repair and produces skin cancers in childhood; naevoid BCC (Gorlin) syndrome , a PTCH1 mutation in the hedgehog pathway, gives multiple BCCs with jaw keratocysts ( Cysts and tumours of the jaws ), palmar pits and a calcified falx, and radiotherapy must be avoided; the familial atypical mole and melanoma syndrome is linked to CDKN2A ; and albinism removes the protection of pigment. The Merkel cell polyomavirus is found in most Merkel cell carcinomas."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s2-1","Examination","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Assessment","Record the lesion's size, borders, colour, ulceration and fixation, and then examine the whole skin. Palpate the draining nodes: the parotid nodes for the anterior scalp, temple, forehead, cheek and ear, the postauricular and suboccipital nodes for the posterior scalp, and the neck. Test the cranial nerves for perineural spread, looking for facial numbness and weakness (V and VII), especially with SCC of the cheek, temple and forehead. Figure 1 Lymph node regions of the head and neck, and the superficial nodes of the head (parotid, postauricular, occipital) that drain the scalp and face. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s2-2","Biopsy","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Assessment","A shave biopsy suits superficial, non-pigmented lesions, and a punch or incisional biopsy through the thickest part suits larger ones. A small suspicious pigmented lesion is removed by excision biopsy with narrow margins of 1 to 3 mm. Two errors are avoided: a shave biopsy of a suspected melanoma, which loses the depth on which staging depends, and a wide excision before diagnosis, which distorts the lymphatics needed for sentinel node mapping and may be the wrong size."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s2-3","Imaging","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Assessment","CT or MRI is used for large, recurrent or high-risk tumours, suspected bone invasion, nodes and perineural spread, with MRI following V and VII to the skull base. Nodes are assessed with ultrasound and FNA, and PET-CT stages advanced disease."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s3-1","Features","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Basal cell carcinoma","The classic BCC is a pearly, translucent papule with rolled edges and telangiectasia that ulcerates centrally, the rodent ulcer . The nodular subtype is commonest; the superficial type is a red scaly patch, usually on the trunk; and pigmented BCC can mimic melanoma. The aggressive subtypes are the morphoeic (sclerosing) BCC, which looks like a scar with ill-defined edges, and the infiltrative, micronodular and basosquamous types. BCC grows slowly but invades along the embryonic fusion planes of the nasal ala, inner canthus and preauricular region, and around cartilage and bone. Metastasis is very rare."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s3-2","High-risk BCC","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Basal cell carcinoma","A BCC is high risk when it lies in the H zone of the central face (the nose, eyelids, lips, ear, and periauricular and temple areas), when it measures 10 mm or more on the cheek, forehead, scalp or neck, or when it has ill-defined borders, an aggressive subtype or perineural invasion. Recurrent tumours, tumours in previously irradiated skin and tumours in immunosuppressed patients are also high risk."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s3-3","Treatment","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Basal cell carcinoma","Low-risk BCC is excised with margins of about 4 mm , checked histologically; small low-risk lesions off the face can be treated by curettage and cautery. High-risk, recurrent and H-zone BCC is best treated by Mohs micrographic surgery , in which horizontal frozen sections examine the whole margin, giving the lowest recurrence rates with maximum preservation of tissue; the alternative is excision with formal margin assessment before reconstruction. Radiotherapy suits patients unfit for surgery and positive margins that cannot be re-excised, but is avoided in Gorlin syndrome and connective-tissue disease. Superficial BCC responds to topical imiquimod or 5-fluorouracil, photodynamic therapy or cryotherapy. Locally advanced or metastatic BCC is treated with hedgehog pathway inhibitors such as vismodegib and sonidegib, whose side effects are muscle cramps, taste loss, alopecia and weight loss, and then with cemiplimab. Because new lesions are common, patients need annual skin checks and sun protection."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s4-1","Spectrum","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","SCC develops along a spectrum. Actinic keratoses are premalignant rough scaly patches on sun-damaged skin, and Bowen's disease is SCC in situ. The keratoacanthoma is a fast-growing crateriform nodule with a keratin plug that may regress, but it is treated as a well-differentiated SCC and excised. Invasive SCC is a keratotic nodule or ulcer that grows over months, most often on the lip, ear, scalp, temple and cheek."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s4-2","High-risk features","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","An SCC is high risk if it is 2 cm or more across, more than 2 mm deep or invading beyond the subcutaneous fat, poorly differentiated or of the desmoplastic, acantholytic or adenosquamous variant, or shows perineural invasion , especially of named nerves, or lymphovascular invasion. Site on the ear or lip, recurrence, origin in a scar and immunosuppression all add to the risk."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s4-3","Treatment of the primary","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","Field change and SCC in situ are treated with cryotherapy, topical 5-FU or imiquimod, photodynamic therapy or curettage. Low-risk invasive SCC is excised with 4 to 6 mm margins . High-risk SCC needs wider margins of 6 to 10 mm or Mohs surgery with complete margin assessment, resection of underlying bone, cartilage or parotid as required, and adjuvant radiotherapy for perineural invasion, close or positive margins, recurrence and T3 to T4 tumours."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s4-4","Nodes and the parotid","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","About 5% of high-risk SCCs metastasise, mostly to the parotid from primaries on the scalp, forehead, temple, ear and cheek, and to the neck. Nodal disease is treated by superficial or total parotidectomy with dissection of the at-risk neck levels, followed by postoperative radiotherapy, with cisplatin or a trial for extranodal extension and positive margins. Primaries on the posterior scalp and neck need a posterolateral neck dissection , which takes levels II to V together with the postauricular and suboccipital nodes. Sentinel node biopsy may be considered for selected high-risk tumours with a node-negative neck. For advanced, unresectable or metastatic disease, the PD-1 inhibitor cemiplimab , and also pembrolizumab, give high response rates and have replaced platinum and EGFR inhibitors as first-line systemic treatment."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s4-5","Lip cancer","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","Most lip SCCs ( Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer ) arise on the vermilion of the lower lip of outdoor workers; upper lip and commissure tumours are more aggressive. Lesions up to a third of the lip are removed by V or W wedge excision, and larger defects are closed with the Abbe, Estlander, Karapandzic or Bernard–Burow flaps. Actinic cheilitis is treated by vermilionectomy (a lip shave), and radiotherapy or brachytherapy is an alternative to surgery. Submental and submandibular nodes are treated when involved."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s5-1","Features and treatment","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Merkel cell carcinoma","Merkel cell carcinoma affects the elderly, the sun-damaged and the immunosuppressed, and is associated with the Merkel cell polyomavirus . It is a rapidly growing, painless, firm, red-violet dome-shaped nodule, remembered by AEIOU : asymptomatic, expanding rapidly, immunosuppressed, older than 50, and on a UV-exposed site. Histology shows small round blue cells that are CK20 positive in a perinuclear dot pattern and positive for neuroendocrine markers but TTF-1 negative , which distinguishes it from metastatic small-cell lung cancer. It spreads early to nodes, so that about a third of patients whose nodes feel normal have occult nodal disease, and it recurs locally and metastasises; five-year survival is about 30 to 65%. Treatment is wide excision with 1 to 2 cm margins and sentinel lymph node biopsy , the most sensitive staging test, with neck dissection or radiotherapy for nodal disease. Adjuvant radiotherapy to the primary and nodal basin is common, and checkpoint inhibitors such as avelumab, pembrolizumab and nivolumab treat advanced disease."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s6-1","Precursors and types","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Melanoma","Up to a third of melanomas arise in a pre-existing naevus. Giant congenital naevi over 20 cm carry the highest risk, dysplastic naevi a lesser one, and lentigo maligna (Hutchinson's melanotic freckle) is melanoma in situ on sun-damaged facial skin. Superficial spreading melanoma is the commonest, about 70%, growing first radially and then vertically. Nodular melanoma (10 to 15%) grows vertically from the start and is thick at diagnosis. Lentigo maligna melanoma appears on the face of the elderly after a long radial phase. Acral lentiginous melanoma of the palms, soles and nails is the commonest type in dark-skinned populations and is not sun-related. Desmoplastic melanoma is often amelanotic, a scar-like nodule on the head and neck that is strongly neurotropic , so its problem is local recurrence rather than nodes; it needs wide margins and adjuvant radiotherapy. Mucosal melanoma is considered separately below."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s6-2","Recognition","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Melanoma","The ABCDE rule describes a suspicious lesion: A symmetry, B order irregularity, C olour variation, D iameter over 6 mm, and E volution, meaning change in size, shape or colour, itching or bleeding. The \"ugly duckling\" sign, a mole unlike the patient's others, and dermoscopy add to clinical judgement. Head and neck melanomas carry a worse prognosis than those of the trunk and limbs, the scalp being worst, then the ear, then the face and neck. The mimics are seborrhoeic keratosis, pigmented BCC, blue naevus, Spitz naevus and naevus of Ota."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s6-3","Pathology and staging","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Melanoma","Breslow thickness , the depth from the granular layer to the deepest tumour cell, is together with ulceration the key prognostic factor and defines the T stage. Clark's levels of anatomical invasion are now historical. The report also gives the mitotic rate, microsatellites, lymphovascular and perineural invasion and the margins. Immunohistochemistry uses S-100, SOX10, HMB-45 and Melan-A, and tumours are tested for BRAF V600 (about half of cutaneous melanomas), NRAS, and c-KIT in mucosal and acral types. T stage of cutaneous melanoma (AJCC 8th edition) T Breslow thickness Ulceration Tis In situ — T1a Under 0.8 mm Absent T1b Under 0.8 mm with ulceration, or 0.8 to 1.0 mm Either T2 Over 1.0 to 2.0 mm a: absent; b: present T3 Over 2.0 to 4.0 mm a: absent; b: present T4 Over 4.0 mm a: absent; b: present Stages I and II are localised disease, stage III is nodal, satellite or in-transit disease, and stage IV is distant metastasis, with the LDH forming part of the M stage. Thin melanomas need no imaging; thick or ulcerated tumours and stage III and IV disease are staged with CT or PET-CT and brain MRI, with an LDH."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s6-4","Treatment of the primary","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Melanoma","Wide local excision margins depend on thickness: 0.5 to 1 cm for melanoma in situ, 1 cm up to 1 mm thick, 1 to 2 cm for 1 to 2 mm, and 2 cm beyond 2 mm, often modified on the face to preserve function. The depth of excision depends on site: down to the periosteum on the scalp, or the outer table if it is involved; a partial or total auriculectomy on the ear, or a lateral temporal bone resection if the canal is involved ( Middle ear and temporal bone tumours ); deep to the mimetic muscles on the face if needed; and to the parotid fascia, or including a superficial parotidectomy, over the parotid. Lentigo maligna is excised in stages with margin control, by the \"spaghetti\" technique or Mohs with immunostains, or treated with imiquimod or radiotherapy in the frail. Do not reconstruct before the margins are known Complex flaps should wait until the final pathology confirms clear margins. Otherwise residual tumour is buried under a flap. Use a temporary dressing or skin graft and reconstruct afterwards ( delayed reconstruction )."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s6-5","The neck","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Melanoma","Sentinel lymph node biopsy is offered for melanomas thicker than 0.8 mm (T1b and above) with a clinically negative neck. It is the strongest predictor of survival and guides adjuvant therapy. Preoperative lymphoscintigraphy with technetium-99m colloid, with SPECT-CT to localise nodes in the complex drainage of the head and neck, including parotid, postauricular and contralateral sites. An intraoperative gamma probe, with or without blue dye. Facial nerve monitoring in the parotid. A positive sentinel node is now usually followed by ultrasound surveillance rather than completion dissection, because the MSLT-II and DeCOG-SLT trials showed no survival benefit from completion dissection, only better regional control. Clinically or radiologically involved nodes need a therapeutic neck dissection, adding a superficial parotidectomy for primaries of the anterior scalp, temple, forehead, ear and cheek, and a posterolateral dissection with postauricular and suboccipital nodes for the posterior scalp. Adjuvant radiotherapy, often hypofractionated, improves regional control after extranodal extension or multiple or large nodes."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s6-6","Systemic therapy","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Melanoma","Checkpoint inhibitors , anti-PD-1 agents (nivolumab, pembrolizumab) alone or with the anti-CTLA-4 agent ipilimumab, are first-line treatment for unresectable and metastatic melanoma and give durable responses in about half of patients, at the cost of immune side effects: colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, thyroiditis and hypophysitis. BRAF with MEK inhibitors , dabrafenib with trametinib or encorafenib with binimetinib, give rapid responses in BRAF-mutant disease. After resection of stage III and high-risk stage II melanoma, adjuvant anti-PD-1 therapy, or BRAF/MEK inhibitors for BRAF-mutant tumours, reduces relapse. Intralesional oncolytic virus (T-VEC) treats in-transit disease, while dacarbazine and interferon are now historical."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s6-7","Mucosal melanoma","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Melanoma","Mucosal melanoma makes up only about 1% of melanomas, but the head and neck is its commonest site ( Sinonasal tumours ): the nasal septum and turbinates, the sinuses, the palate and gingiva, and the nasopharynx. It is often amelanotic and presents late with unilateral obstruction, epistaxis or a pigmented palatal lesion. Breslow depth does not apply, and every mucosal melanoma is at least T3. Treatment is complete endoscopic or open resection with postoperative radiotherapy for local control, and therapeutic rather than elective neck dissection. The prognosis is poor, with five-year survival of 20 to 30% because of distant metastasis, and immunotherapy and KIT inhibitors have a role."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s6-8","Melanoma of unknown primary","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Melanoma","Melanoma may present in the parotid or neck nodes with no primary, which has perhaps regressed. Examine the skin, scalp, mucosa and eyes, then treat it as stage III disease with dissection, adjuvant radiotherapy and systemic therapy. The prognosis is similar to, or better than, that of stage III disease with a known primary."],["ev","cl:t-skinca~s7-1","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck: current evidence","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Reconstruction","In MSLT-II (1,939 patients with a positive sentinel node), immediate completion lymph node dissection did not improve melanoma-specific survival compared with ultrasound surveillance, though it improved regional control (Faries et al., N Engl J Med 2017); DeCOG-SLT found the same (Leiter et al., Lancet Oncol 2016). Adjuvant nivolumab (Weber et al., N Engl J Med 2017) and pembrolizumab (Eggermont et al., N Engl J Med 2018) improved recurrence-free survival after resection of high-risk melanoma. For advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma not suitable for surgery or radiotherapy, cemiplimab produced responses in about half of patients (Migden et al., N Engl J Med 2018). For high-risk facial basal cell carcinoma, Mohs surgery halved recurrence over ten years compared with standard excision (van Loo et al., Eur J Cancer 2014)."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s7-1","The reconstructive ladder","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · Reconstruction","Defects are closed by the simplest reliable method on the reconstructive ladder : Healing by secondary intention, which works well in concave sites such as the conchal bowl and medial canthus. Primary closure along relaxed skin tension lines. Skin grafts: full-thickness grafts from postauricular or supraclavicular skin for the nose and eyelids, and split-thickness grafts for the scalp over periosteum. Local flaps: advancement, rotation, transposition (rhomboid, bilobed), and interpolated flaps (paramedian forehead, nasolabial). Regional flaps: cervicofacial, deltopectoral, pectoralis major, trapezius. Free tissue transfer. Scalp defects can also be closed by tissue expansion. The flaps themselves are described in Head and neck reconstruction and facial reanimation ."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s8-1","Viva essentials","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for BCC: features, high-risk features, Mohs surgery and its rationale, hedgehog inhibitors. cSCC: high-risk features, parotid metastasis, perineural spread, cemiplimab. Merkel cell carcinoma: CK20 and TTF-1; sentinel node biopsy. Melanoma: ABCDE, Breslow and ulceration, margins by thickness, sentinel node indications and lymphoscintigraphy, MSLT-II, checkpoint and BRAF/MEK inhibitors. Drainage: of the anterior versus posterior scalp, and when to add parotidectomy. Mucosal melanoma."],["sec","cl:t-skinca~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Skin cancer and melanoma of the head and neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls Shave biopsy of a pigmented lesion. Wide excision before diagnosis, which ruins sentinel node mapping. Flap reconstruction before margins are clear. Missing facial numbness or weakness as perineural spread from a \"treated\" facial SCC. Radiotherapy in Gorlin syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s1-1","Anterior skull base","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Applied anatomy","The anterior skull base separates the frontal lobes from the nose, ethmoid sinuses and orbits. In the midline lies the cribriform plate , its thinnest part, perforated by the olfactory fila; beside it the thin lateral lamella is a common site of CSF leak. The crista galli , where the falx attaches, rises in front, and the planum sphenoidale lies behind. Laterally are the orbital roofs and the fovea ethmoidalis of the frontal bone, with the anterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries crossing the ethmoid roof."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s1-2","Middle cranial fossa and the central skull base","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Applied anatomy","The middle fossa holds the temporal lobes. Centrally, the body of the sphenoid contains the sphenoid sinus, with the sella turcica above it holding the pituitary and a cavernous sinus on each side. In the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus run III, IV, V1 and V2, while VI runs free within the sinus beside the internal carotid artery, which is why VI is so often the first nerve to fail. The foramina of the region carry predictable contents: the superior orbital fissure (III, IV, V1 and VI), the optic canal, the foramen rotundum (V2), the foramen ovale (V3), the foramen spinosum (the middle meningeal artery), and the foramen lacerum with the carotid canal. The clivus slopes from the dorsum sellae to the foramen magnum; its upper part is the basisphenoid and its lower part the basiocciput. Seen from the sphenoid sinus, the carotid and optic nerve make bulges in the lateral wall, separated by the optico-carotid recess , and the bone over the carotid is dehiscent in some people. An Onodi cell is a posterior ethmoid cell that extends above the sphenoid and may contain the optic nerve."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s1-3","Posterior fossa and lateral skull base","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Applied anatomy","The petrous temporal bone contains the cochlea, labyrinth, facial nerve and internal carotid artery. The anterior surface of the petrous apex forms the floor of the middle fossa, with the arcuate eminence, the greater superficial petrosal nerve and Kawase's triangle , and its posterior surface carries the internal auditory canal. The jugular foramen transmits IX, X and XI with the jugular bulb, and the hypoglossal canal carries XII below and medial to it."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s1-4","Infratemporal and pterygopalatine fossae","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Applied anatomy","The infratemporal fossa lies below the middle cranial fossa, behind the maxilla and deep to the ramus, and contains the pterygoid muscles, V3, the maxillary artery and the pterygoid venous plexus. The pterygopalatine fossa is a small inverted cone behind the maxillary sinus that opens in every direction: to the orbit through the inferior orbital fissure, to the infratemporal fossa through the pterygomaxillary fissure, to the nose through the sphenopalatine foramen, to the middle cranial fossa through the foramen rotundum and vidian canal, and to the mouth through the greater palatine canal. It is therefore the crossroads by which juvenile angiofibroma and perineural cancer spread. Figure 1 Foramina of the skull base and their contents, and the cavernous sinus with its contents. From the anatomy atlas Figure 2 The pterygopalatine fossa, and the infratemporal fossa after removal of the ramus. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s2-1","Boundaries and compartments","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · The parapharyngeal space","The parapharyngeal space is an inverted pyramid with its base on the skull base and its apex at the greater cornu of the hyoid. Medially lie the buccopharyngeal fascia and superior constrictor, and so the tonsil; laterally the medial pterygoid, the ramus and the deep lobe of the parotid; posteriorly the prevertebral fascia; and anteriorly the pterygomandibular raphe. The tensor–styloid fascia , formed by the styloid process and its muscles, divides the space into two compartments. The prestyloid compartment contains fat, the deep lobe of the parotid, minor salivary rests, the maxillary artery and branches of V3. The poststyloid (carotid) compartment contains the internal carotid artery, the internal jugular vein, nerves IX to XII, the sympathetic chain and nodes. Figure 3 The parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal spaces. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s2-2","Tumours","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · The parapharyngeal space","Parapharyngeal space tumours by compartment Compartment Common tumours Imaging hint Prestyloid Pleomorphic adenoma (deep lobe or minor salivary), other salivary tumours, lipoma Fat plane between tumour and parotid lost (deep lobe origin) or preserved (minor salivary); carotid pushed posteriorly Poststyloid Schwannoma (vagus, sympathetic chain), paraganglioma (vagal, carotid body), neurofibroma, nodes (metastases, lymphoma), meningioma Carotid pushed anteriorly or splayed; flow voids in paragangliomas About half of parapharyngeal tumours are salivary, mostly pleomorphic adenoma, a fifth to a third are neurogenic, and the rest are paragangliomas and others; about 80% are benign. They are often silent until large, then appear as a smooth bulge pushing the tonsil and soft palate medially under normal mucosa, or as fullness behind the angle of the jaw, with a muffled voice, sleep apnoea, Eustachian tube obstruction or dysphagia. Cranial nerve palsies, Horner syndrome or pain suggest malignancy or a neural tumour. MRI, with CT, places the tumour in its compartment and shows the vessels; metanephrines are measured whenever a paraganglioma is possible, and prestyloid and nodal masses can be sampled by image-guided FNA. A transoral biopsy is never done , because it seeds a pleomorphic adenoma and makes a paraganglioma bleed. The transcervical approach through the submandibular triangle, dividing the stylomandibular ligament, is the workhorse. Deep lobe tumours need a transparotid–transcervical approach, large tumours reaching the skull base a transcervical approach with mandibulotom"],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s3-1","Anterior skull base","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Skull base tumours","The malignant tumours of the anterior skull base are mostly sinonasal ( Sinonasal tumours ): squamous carcinoma, adenocarcinoma in woodworkers, adenoid cystic carcinoma, olfactory neuroblastoma , sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma , neuroendocrine carcinoma and mucosal melanoma, together with sarcomas and lymphoma. The benign lesions are meningioma, inverted papilloma, juvenile angiofibroma, fibro-osseous lesions, mucoceles and encephaloceles."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s3-2","Sella and parasellar region","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Skull base tumours","The pituitary adenoma is the commonest sellar tumour; microadenomas are under 10 mm and macroadenomas larger. Mass effect produces bitemporal hemianopia , headache and hypopituitarism, and hormone excess produces the prolactinoma, with amenorrhoea, galactorrhoea and impotence, acromegaly or Cushing disease. A prolactinoma is treated first with cabergoline or bromocriptine , not surgery. The endocrine work-up measures prolactin, IGF-1, cortisol and ACTH, thyroid function, gonadotropins and sodium, with formal visual fields. The sellar lesions are covered fully in Pituitary tumours and sellar lesions . A Rathke cleft cyst is usually incidental and observed. The craniopharyngioma has two peaks, in children and in late middle age, is calcified and cystic, and is hard to remove completely. Meningiomas arise from the tuberculum, planum and olfactory groove and show a \"dural tail\". An empty sella is associated with idiopathic intracranial hypertension and spontaneous CSF leaks."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s3-3","Clivus and petrous apex","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Skull base tumours","The chordoma arises from notochordal remnants at the spheno-occipital synchondrosis in the midline. It is bright on T2 with bone destruction, causes diplopia from a VI palsy and headache, and is treated by maximal resection followed by high-dose, often proton, radiotherapy; recurrence is common. The chondrosarcoma arises at the petroclival synchondrosis, off the midline, and has a better prognosis. At the petrous apex, the cholesterol granuloma is bright on both T1 and T2; cholesteatoma, mucocele and petrous apicitis also occur, the last producing Gradenigo syndrome of otorrhoea, VI palsy and retro-orbital pain ( Petrous apicitis ). Asymmetric pneumatisation or retained fluid can only look like a lesion, and MRI settles the question."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s3-4","Lateral skull base and posterior fossa","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Skull base tumours","Here lie the paragangliomas (glomus jugulare, tympanicum and vagale) and temporal bone carcinoma ( Middle ear and temporal bone tumours ), vestibular schwannoma and other cerebellopontine angle lesions ( Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours ), schwannomas of the facial and lower cranial nerves, meningiomas, and the endolymphatic sac tumours of von Hippel–Lindau syndrome. MRI signal of cerebellopontine angle and petrous apex lesions Lesion T1 T1 with gadolinium T2 Diffusion Vestibular schwannoma Iso or low Marked enhancement Moderately high — Meningioma Iso or low Marked, with a dural tail Variable — Epidermoid (cholesteatoma) Low None High Restricted (bright) Arachnoid cyst Low (like CSF) None High (like CSF) Not restricted Cholesterol granuloma High None High — Lipoma High (suppressed by fat saturation) None Intermediate —"],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s3-5","Infratemporal fossa","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Skull base tumours","Primary infratemporal tumours are sarcomas, neurogenic tumours and salivary tumours, but more often the fossa is invaded from its neighbours by nasopharyngeal carcinoma, maxillary sinus cancer, juvenile angiofibroma or parotid cancer. The signs are trismus from invasion of the pterygoids, numbness of V3 over the lower lip and chin, serous otitis media from Eustachian tube invasion, and a mass deep to the ramus."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s4-1","Clinical picture","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Assessment","Skull base disease announces itself through whichever structure it reaches first. In the nose it causes unilateral obstruction, bleeding and anosmia; in the eye proptosis, diplopia and visual loss; in the face numbness of V2 or V3 and pain, which suggest perineural spread ; and in the jaw trismus. In the ear, a unilateral conductive loss from a middle ear effusion in an adult means the nasopharynx must be examined and biopsied, and pulsatile tinnitus suggests a vascular tumour. In the throat, hoarseness, dysphagia and aspiration mean IX to XI are involved, and tongue weakness means XII. CSF rhinorrhoea and headache complete the picture. The examination is a complete cranial nerve examination, nasal and nasopharyngeal endoscopy, otomicroscopy, and examination of the larynx and neck. Jugular foramen syndromes Vernet syndrome: IX, X and XI together, from jugular foramen lesions (paragangliomas, schwannomas, metastases). Collet–Sicard syndrome: adds XII. Villaret syndrome: adds Horner syndrome, from the retroparotid space."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s4-2","Imaging and tissue","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Assessment","CT and MRI are complementary and both are needed. CT shows bone detail: erosion, remodelling and widening of foramina. MRI with gadolinium and fat suppression shows soft tissue, dura, brain, orbit and perineural spread. Dural invasion is suggested by dural thickening of more than 5 mm or nodular thickening, and periorbital invasion by enhancement or enlargement of the extraocular muscles, stranding of the orbital fat and a nodular interface. Angiography is used for vascular tumours and embolisation, and balloon test occlusion of the internal carotid when it may have to be sacrificed; PET-CT stages disease and detects recurrence. Most lesions need tissue, often by endoscopic biopsy , but not all: paragangliomas, schwannomas, typical meningiomas and juvenile angiofibromas can be diagnosed on imaging alone."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s4-3","Unresectability","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Assessment","A tumour is generally unresectable when it invades the brainstem or spinal cord, both internal carotids, both cavernous sinuses or extensive brain parenchyma. Involvement of the optic chiasm, distant metastases and poor general health are relative contraindications. Lymphoma and other chemo- or radiosensitive tumours are not resected at all."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s5-1","Endoscopic endonasal surgery","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Approaches","Endoscopic skull base surgery works through both nostrils with the \" binostril, four-handed \" technique, using a wide sphenoidotomy and removal of the posterior septum, with ethmoidectomy, medial maxillectomy, transpterygoid access or a Draf III as needed. The transsphenoidal route reaches the sella and is the standard for pituitary surgery; the transplanum and transtuberculum routes reach the suprasellar region; the transcribriform route exposes the anterior skull base from the frontal sinus to the planum between the orbits; the transclival route reaches the clivus down to the foramen magnum and odontoid; and the transpterygoid route opens the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae, Meckel's cave and the petrous apex. The approach avoids facial and scalp incisions and brain retraction, gives magnified and angled views and speeds recovery, and for selected sinonasal cancers survival is comparable to open craniofacial resection. Its limits are lateral extension beyond the mid-orbit, involvement of the orbit, the skin or the anterior or lateral wall of the frontal or maxillary sinus, dural or brain invasion lateral to the orbit, and encasement of the carotid."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s5-2","Open approaches","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Approaches","Craniofacial resection combines a transfacial route, by lateral rhinotomy or midface degloving, with a bifrontal craniotomy to remove the cribriform plate, ethmoids and involved dura en bloc, and reconstructs with a pericranial flap on the supraorbital and supratrochlear vessels. The orbit is exenterated if the periorbital fat, extraocular muscles or orbital apex are invaded. Fisch's infratemporal fossa approaches close the ear canal and remove the middle ear. Type A reaches the jugular foramen and infralabyrinthine region with anterior rerouting of the facial nerve, and is used for glomus jugulare; type B reaches the clivus and petrous apex; and type C the parasellar region, nasopharynx and pterygopalatine fossa. Other routes include the lateral (preauricular) subtemporal–infratemporal approach, which removes the zygoma and condyle; the far lateral approach to the foramen magnum and lower clivus; the translabyrinthine, retrosigmoid, middle fossa, transotic and transcochlear approaches to the temporal bone and cerebellopontine angle, described with vestibular schwannoma and temporal bone tumours; and the maxillary swing, transpalatal and midface degloving routes to the nasopharynx."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s6-1","Principles","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Reconstruction","The aim is a durable barrier between the nose and the intradural space to prevent CSF leak, pneumocephalus and meningitis. Multilayer closure is standard: an inlay graft (usually fascia lata, sometimes with fat) tucked 5 to 10 mm under the dural edges for a watertight seal, then an onlay of free mucosa or, preferably, a vascularised flap , secured with sealant and supported by dressings or a balloon. Mucosa is stripped from the bone under the onlay so that it adheres and no mucocoele forms. Afterwards the patient avoids nose blowing, straining and mask ventilation."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s6-2","Choosing the repair","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Reconstruction","The choice depends on defect size and site, whether the intraoperative leak is low-flow (dura opened without entering a cistern or ventricle) or high-flow (a cistern or ventricle opened), and patient factors such as previous radiotherapy, long-term steroids and malnutrition. Small, low-flow leaks close reliably with free grafts, for example the bath-plug technique with a fat plug and a free mucosal onlay. Large or high-flow defects, high-risk sites and poor healers need a vascularised flap. The Hadad–Bassagasteguy nasoseptal flap , on the posterior septal branch of the sphenopalatine artery, transformed endoscopic skull base surgery by cutting leak rates from over 20% to about 5%; it should be planned before the sphenoidotomy, or a \"rescue flap\" incision made at the start of surgery to preserve its pedicle in case it is needed. Alternatives on sphenopalatine branches are inferior and middle turbinate flaps and lateral nasal wall flaps; regional options are the pericranial flap , on the supraorbital and supratrochlear vessels and passed through a window above the frontal sinus, and the temporoparietal fascia flap; free flaps close open defects."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s6-3","Lumbar drains","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Reconstruction","Lumbar drains reduce pressure on the repair, but with reliable multilayer closure and flaps many surgeons now use them only to treat a postoperative leak, for high-flow defects or in patients with raised intracranial pressure, or to give intrathecal fluorescein."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s7-1","Complications","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Complications and outcome","CSF leak is the commonest complication, and meningitis may follow. Tension pneumocephalus presents with sudden confusion and a falling conscious level, and CT shows the \" Mount Fuji sign \". Carotid injury is managed by packing with pressure, then a crushed muscle patch, then urgent angiography to look for a pseudoaneurysm. Stroke, cranial nerve deficits and visual loss can occur, sellar surgery can cause hypopituitarism and diabetes insipidus (so sodium and urine output are monitored), and the nose is left with crusting, synechiae and anosmia. Trismus and wound or flap failure complete the list."],["ev","cl:t-skullbase~s7-2","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space: current evidence","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Complications and outcome","Nasoseptal flap. Described by Hadad and colleagues ( Laryngoscope 2006), the vascularised nasoseptal flap reduced postoperative CSF leak after extended endoscopic approaches from double figures to around 5% in later series. Lumbar drains. In a randomised trial of high-risk defects (large dural openings or opening of a cistern or ventricle), a lumbar drain for three days reduced postoperative CSF leak from 21% to 8% (Zwagerman et al., J Neurosurg 2019), so many units now drain only high-flow defects. Endoscopic or open. For selected anterior skull base tumours, endoscopic resection gives oncological results comparable to craniofacial resection with less morbidity, provided clear margins are achieved (Lund et al., Rhinology Suppl 2010)."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s7-2","Outcome","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · Complications and outcome","Overall five-year survival for skull base malignancy is about 50%. Clear margins are the strongest predictor of survival, and postoperative radiotherapy is given for high-risk features."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s8-1","Viva essentials","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Contents of the cavernous sinus; why VI fails first; the skull base foramina. The pterygopalatine fossa: its communications and what spreads through it. The parapharyngeal space: prestyloid versus poststyloid, medial displacement of the tonsil, never biopsy through the mouth, the transcervical approach. Adult unilateral middle ear effusion: examine the nasopharynx. Chordoma versus chondrosarcoma; the MRI table for petrous apex and cerebellopontine angle lesions. Endoscopic endonasal approaches, their limits, and the nasoseptal flap and its pedicle. Craniofacial resection; the Fisch infratemporal fossa approaches. Complications: CSF leak, pneumocephalus, carotid injury."],["sec","cl:t-skullbase~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Skull base and the parapharyngeal space · For the examination","Common pitfalls Taking a transoral biopsy of a parapharyngeal mass. Treating a prolactinoma surgically before trying a dopamine agonist. Missing a VI palsy or facial numbness as signs of skull base spread. Calling an asymmetric petrous apex \"a lesion\" without MRI."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s1-1","Definition","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Overview","A sensorineural hearing loss of 30 dB or more over at least three contiguous frequencies, developing within three days."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s1-2","Epidemiology","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Overview","About 5 to 27 per 100,000 people a year, rising with age and commonest in middle and later life, equally in men and women. It is almost always one-sided; simultaneous loss in both ears is rare and points to a systemic or central cause."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s1-3","Why it matters","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Overview","Time matters: treatment works best in the first two weeks. And a minority have a serious underlying cause, including vestibular schwannoma and posterior circulation stroke."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s2-1","Idiopathic","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Causes","Most cases have no identifiable cause. Three theories compete, and each may be right for some patients: Viral: cochleitis or neuritis, supported by seasonal clusters and viral serology. Vascular: thrombosis, embolism, spasm or haemorrhage in the labyrinthine artery, an end artery. Membrane rupture: of the cochlear membranes or the round window, letting perilymph leak. Figure 1 Branches of the labyrinthine artery, an end artery, which explains the vascular theory. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s2-2","Identifiable causes","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Causes","Causes to exclude Infection Mumps, measles, herpes zoster meningitis, syphilis, acute otitis media, HIV Trauma Head injury, ear surgery blast, barotrauma, perilymph fistula Vascular Stroke (AICA) leukaemia, sickle cell, polycythaemia, embolism, bypass surgery Otological Ménière's disease Cogan syndrome, enlarged vestibular aqueduct Tumour Vestibular schwannoma other cerebellopontine angle tumours, metastases Other Ototoxic drugs multiple sclerosis, hypothyroidism, sarcoidosis, autoimmune disease, non-organic loss Perilymph fistula A leak at the oval or round window may follow stapes surgery, barotrauma, heavy lifting or straining. Spontaneous fistula is possible but probably over-diagnosed ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis )."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s3-1","Symptoms","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Clinical features","3.1.1 Hearing loss Sudden, one-sided, often noticed on waking or over a few hours. Patients frequently describe it as a blocked ear. 3.1.2 Tinnitus and fullness Often precede or accompany the loss. 3.1.3 Vertigo Present in some, and a sign of a worse prognosis."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s3-2","Signs","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Clinical features","The canal and drum are normal. Tuning forks are the key bedside test: Weber lateralises to the good ear, and Rinne remains positive in the affected ear. Not wax until proven otherwise A blocked ear after a cold, a swim or a flight can be sudden sensorineural loss. If you assume wax or fluid without doing forks, the window for treatment can close."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s4-1","Audiometry","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Investigations","Pure-tone audiogram with masking, and speech discrimination. The pattern (low-frequency, high-frequency, flat or total) helps prognosis. Figure 2 Audiogram of a right ear with sensorineural loss: air and bone conduction fall together, with no air-bone gap. Dhingra, fig. 5.6"],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s4-2","Tympanometry","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Investigations","Normal, which excludes middle ear fluid."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s4-3","MRI","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Investigations","MRI of the internal auditory canals and cerebellopontine angles with gadolinium, to exclude vestibular schwannoma and other retrocochlear lesions. If MRI is impossible, auditory brainstem response."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s4-4","Blood tests by history","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Investigations","Not routine for everyone: the American guideline advises against routine laboratory tests, because they rarely change management. Request them when the history suggests them: glucose (before steroids), full blood count and ESR, syphilis serology, and autoimmune tests for bilateral or fluctuating loss."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s4-5","Exploration","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Investigations","Exploratory tympanotomy only when a fistula is strongly suspected, after trauma or straining."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s4-6","Differential diagnosis","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Investigations","Wax or middle ear effusion: forks and tympanometry. Ménière's disease : fluctuation, low-frequency loss, attacks of vertigo. Vestibular schwannoma : MRI. Anterior inferior cerebellar artery stroke: vertigo, ataxia, facial weakness. Non-organic hearing loss: inconsistent results, objective tests normal."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s5-1","On the day","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Management","Sudden hearing loss in clinic or the emergency department Do the forks: Weber to the good ear, Rinne positive in the bad ear. Look for wax; don't accept a blocked ear without testing. Arrange an audiogram the same or the next day. Offer steroids early. Facial weakness, vertigo with ataxia, or vascular risk factors: treat as a posterior circulation stroke. Loss in both ears at once: urgent investigation for systemic, infective, autoimmune or central causes."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s5-2","Steroids","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Management","5.2.1 Oral Prednisolone 1 mg/kg (up to about 60 mg) a day for 7 to 14 days, then tapered, started as early as possible and ideally within two weeks (benefit is unlikely after six weeks). Check glucose and blood pressure, and warn about mood, sleep and stomach effects. An alternative regimen is 40 to 60 mg each morning for a week, tapered over three weeks. Higher doses do not help: in the HODOKORT trial, very high-dose glucocorticoids gave no better hearing than the standard dose and caused more side effects. 5.2.2 Intratympanic Dexamethasone or methylprednisolone injected through the drum, usually several injections over one to two weeks. As first-line treatment it works as well as oral steroids, so it suits patients in whom systemic steroids are unsafe (poorly controlled diabetes, for example). As salvage, within two to six weeks of onset when oral treatment has failed, it adds a modest further gain. Risks: pain, transient vertigo, and a persistent perforation in a few."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s5-3","Other treatments","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Management","Antivirals, vasodilators, vasoactive drugs and antioxidants have no proven benefit and are not recommended. Hyperbaric oxygen is an option alongside steroids if it can be started within two weeks of onset (or within a month as salvage), and meta-analyses show a modest gain in hearing; access and cost limit it. Older adjuncts such as bed rest, carbogen, dextran and a low-salt diet have not beaten natural recovery. Where the books differ · Adjunct treatments Dhingra Lists carbogen, low salt and hyperbaric oxygen among the options, while noting that no treatment has proven better than spontaneous recovery. Lee Recommends steroids, orally or intratympanically; finds no evidence for antivirals. American guideline (2019): steroids may be offered within two weeks; intratympanic steroid as salvage within two to six weeks; hyperbaric oxygen as an option; antivirals, vasodilators and routine CT not recommended. In practice: Steroids (oral, or intratympanic when oral steroids are unsafe) within two weeks; intratympanic salvage at two to six weeks; hyperbaric oxygen if available; no antivirals or vasodilators."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s5-4","Rehabilitation","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Management","If recovery is poor: a hearing aid for a partial loss, or a CROS aid, bone-conduction device or cochlear implant for single-sided deafness ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ). Tinnitus counselling as needed."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~c6","Prognosis","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Prognosis","What predicts recovery. Between a third and two-thirds of patients recover partly or fully, much of it spontaneously and mostly within the first two weeks; recovery after three months is unusual. Prognosis in sudden hearing loss Factor Better Worse Severity Mild Severe or total Frequencies Low or mid High or flat Vertigo Absent Present Age Younger Older Treatment Early Late Early recovery Within two weeks None by two weeks"],["ev","cl:t-ssnhl~s6-1","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss: current evidence","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Prognosis","The American clinical practice guideline (Chandrasekhar et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019) recommends audiometry and MRI for retrocochlear causes, steroids as an option within two weeks, intratympanic steroids for salvage within two to six weeks, and hyperbaric oxygen as an option; it advises against routine laboratory tests, CT and antivirals. In the HODOKORT trial of 325 patients in Germany, higher-dose glucocorticoids were no better than the standard dose at 30 days and caused more hyperglycaemia and hypertension (Plontke et al., NEJM Evid 2024). A Cochrane review found the evidence for systemic steroids weak and of low quality (Wei et al., 2013). As first-line treatment, intratympanic and systemic steroids gave similar hearing gains in a meta-analysis of seven trials (Mirian and Ovesen, JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020). Adding hyperbaric oxygen to medical therapy improved hearing by about 10 dB in a meta-analysis of three trials (Joshua et al., JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2022)."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s6-1","Follow-up","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · Prognosis","Repeat the audiogram during treatment and at the end. Counsel the patient to protect the other ear from noise and ototoxic drugs."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s7-1","Viva essentials","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The definition: 30 dB, three frequencies, three days. Tuning forks: Weber to the good ear, Rinne positive. Theories: viral, vascular, membrane rupture. MRI to exclude a schwannoma. Steroids, oral and intratympanic (first-line and salvage), and why higher doses do not help. Hyperbaric oxygen as an option. Prognostic factors."],["sec","cl:t-ssnhl~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling it wax or a cold without doing forks. Delaying treatment while waiting for an audiogram slot. Forgetting MRI once the acute phase has passed. Missing a stroke in a patient with vertigo and ataxia."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s1-1","Stridor","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Definition","A harsh, noisy breathing sound produced by turbulent airflow through a narrowed larynx or trachea. Stertor is the lower-pitched snoring of the nose, nasopharynx or oropharynx; wheeze is the musical expiratory sound of the smaller airways."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s1-2","Phase and site","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Definition","Inspiratory: supraglottis, pharynx and extrathoracic airway collapse on inspiration (laryngomalacia, epiglottitis, retropharyngeal abscess, supraglottic tumours). Biphasic: a fixed narrowing at the glottis, subglottis or cervical trachea (subglottic stenosis, bilateral cord palsy, papillomas, croup). Expiratory: the intrathoracic trachea and bronchi (tracheomalacia, bronchial foreign body, tracheal stenosis, compression by masses or vessels). Figure 1 Types of stridor and their site of origin. Dhingra, fig. 59.3"],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s1-3","Associated sounds","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Definition","Snoring or snorting: nose and nasopharynx. Gurgling and a muffled voice: pharynx and supraglottis. Hoarse voice or weak cry: the vocal folds. The cry is normal in laryngomalacia and subglottic stenosis. Barking cough: subglottis (croup). Expiratory wheeze: bronchi."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~c2","Causes","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Causes","From the nose to the bronchi. Figure 2 Causes of stridor in infants and children: congenital, and acquired with or without fever. Dhingra, fig. 59.5 Causes of stridor by site Site Congenital Acquired Nose Choanal atresia Severe nasal obstruction in neonates Tongue and jaw Macroglossia, lingual thyroid, micrognathia (Pierre Robin) Haemangioma, lymphangioma, Ludwig's angina Pharynx Dermoid, cysts Adenotonsillar hypertrophy, retropharyngeal abscess ( Deep neck space infection ), tumours Larynx Laryngomalacia, web, cysts, vocal fold paralysis, subglottic stenosis, haemangioma, cleft Epiglottitis, croup, diphtheria, oedema, foreign body, trauma, papillomas, carcinoma, bilateral palsy, post-intubation stenosis, tetany, laryngospasm Trachea and bronchi Tracheomalacia, stenosis, atresia Bacterial tracheitis, foreign body, stenosis after intubation or tracheostomy, tumours Outside the airway Vascular rings, oesophageal atresia and fistula, congenital goitre, cystic hygroma Oesophageal foreign body, goitre, mediastinal and neck masses"],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s2-1","In adults","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Causes","Laryngeal ( Cancer of the larynx ) and tracheal tumours, bilateral vocal fold paralysis (after thyroidectomy), post-intubation and tracheostomy stenosis, laryngeal trauma, angioedema, epiglottitis, deep neck infection, goitre and foreign bodies."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s3-1","Severity","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Assessment","Mild: stridor only when active or crying, no recession. Moderate: stridor at rest, suprasternal, intercostal, subcostal and sternal recession, tracheal tug, nasal flaring, tachypnoea, tachycardia. Severe: marked recession, agitation and restlessness (hypoxia), drooling, inability to speak or feed. Impending arrest: fatigue, falling respiratory rate and quieter stridor (less air moving, not improvement), pallor or cyanosis, drowsiness. Figure 3 A child with bilateral abductor paralysis: recession of the suprasternal notch, sternum, intercostal spaces and epigastrium on inspiration. Dhingra, fig. 59.4 A quieter child is not always a better child Diminishing stridor with tiring, drowsiness or a falling oxygen saturation means respiratory failure. Oximetry can stay normal until late; watch the work of breathing and the level of consciousness."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s3-2","History","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Assessment","Onset: at birth or soon after (congenital), or later (acquired). Speed: sudden (foreign body, oedema, epiglottitis) or gradual and progressive (laryngomalacia, haemangioma, papillomas, stenosis, tumours). Duration and course: short (infection, foreign body) or long-standing. Fever and toxicity: epiglottitis, tracheitis, retropharyngeal abscess, diphtheria. Feeding: choking, cyanosis and aspiration (cleft, paralysis, fistula, vascular ring, oesophageal foreign body). Cyanotic spells and apnoeas: need for airway support. Witnessed choking on a toy, nut or food ( Foreign bodies of the airway ). Previous intubation, surgery (thyroid, cardiac) or trauma. Voice and cry quality."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s3-3","Examination","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Assessment","Observe from a distance first: posture, colour, recession, drooling, voice. Note the phase of stridor, the effect of position (prone improves laryngomalacia, micrognathia and macroglossia), and auscultate over the nose, open mouth, neck and chest to localise. Examine the nose, tongue, jaw and pharynx; in adults, and in calm older children, flexible nasendoscopy shows the larynx. Flexible endoscopy in the clinic Under topical anaesthesia, the awake child can be examined for laryngomalacia, vocal fold movement, cysts, papillomas and webs, which an anaesthetised child cannot show: dynamic problems need a breathing patient."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s3-4","Investigations","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Assessment","Chosen by the history, and only when the airway allows: Lateral and anteroposterior soft tissue radiographs of the neck (steeple sign of croup, thumb sign of epiglottitis, prevertebral swelling, radio-opaque foreign bodies). Chest radiographs, with inspiratory and expiratory films or fluoroscopy for radiolucent bronchial foreign bodies (air trapping, mediastinal shift). CT or MRI for subglottic stenosis, haemangioma, masses and vascular rings; angiography for vascular anomalies. Contrast swallow for clefts, fistulae, rings and aspiration. Arterial blood gases in severe obstruction."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s3-5","Microlaryngoscopy and bronchoscopy","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Assessment","The definitive assessment of the paediatric airway, in theatre with full resuscitation facilities and an experienced anaesthetist. Inhalational induction maintains spontaneous breathing; a quick laryngoscopy is followed by bronchoscopy of the subglottis, trachea and bronchi, collection of secretions and removal of crusts or foreign bodies; then the child is intubated if needed. Look at the whole airway: a second lesion is common."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s4-1","The common paediatric causes","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Common causes in children","The congenital lesions are described in full in Congenital lesions of the larynx . Laryngomalacia is the commonest cause of stridor in infants, accounting for well over half of congenital cases. Inspiratory stridor begins in the first weeks, is worse with feeding, crying and lying supine, and usually resolves by 18 to 24 months. Short aryepiglottic folds, an omega-shaped epiglottis and prolapsing arytenoid mucosa are seen on awake flexible laryngoscopy. Most infants need only reassurance and treatment of reflux; poor weight gain, apnoeas, cyanosis or pectus excavatum are indications for supraglottoplasty . Vocal fold paralysis is the second commonest congenital cause; bilateral paralysis is associated with Arnold–Chiari malformation and hydrocephalus. Subglottic stenosis is congenital or, more often, acquired after intubation ( Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis ), and is graded by the Cotton–Myer system by the percentage of lumen obstructed: grade I up to 50%, grade II 51 to 70%, grade III 71 to 99%, grade IV no lumen. Subglottic haemangioma typically presents at a few weeks of age with biphasic stridor that worsens over the first months, often with a cutaneous haemangioma in a beard distribution, and responds to propranolol . Croup (viral laryngotracheobronchitis) produces a barking cough and stridor in children aged six months to three years and is treated with a single dose of oral dexamethasone, 0.15 mg/kg, with nebulised adrenaline for severe distress. Recurrent or atypical croup, or croup under six months, warrants airway endoscopy."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s5-1","Immediate","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Management","Keep the patient calm and upright (in a parent's arms for a child); give humidified oxygen without causing distress. Call senior anaesthetic and ENT help early. Temporising measures as appropriate: nebulised adrenaline, steroids (dexamethasone), heliox (a helium–oxygen mixture flows more smoothly through narrow airways). Treat the cause where possible: antibiotics, removal of a foreign body."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s5-2","Securing the airway","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Management","Intubation: in theatre, by the most experienced anaesthetist, with an ENT surgeon scrubbed and ready. Tracheostomy : when intubation is impossible or unsafe, for prolonged obstruction, laryngeal trauma, and for long-term airway management (bilateral paralysis, stenosis, severe congenital anomalies). Cricothyroidotomy ( Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation ): the emergency surgical airway in adults and older children when the patient can neither be intubated nor oxygenated; needle cricothyroidotomy with jet ventilation in young children."],["ev","cl:t-stridor~s5-3","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway: current evidence","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Management","Airway emergencies. The UK fourth national audit of major airway complications found that head and neck patients were heavily over-represented, that awake tracheostomy under local anaesthesia or awake fibreoptic intubation should be planned in advance, and that failed emergency front-of-neck access was common with needle techniques (Cook et al., Br J Anaesth 2011). The Difficult Airway Society guidelines are the source of the scalpel-bougie-tube technique above (Frerk et al., Br J Anaesth 2015); in small children surgical tracheostomy is the alternative to needle cricothyroidotomy, because the cricothyroid membrane is tiny. Children. A synchronous second airway lesion is found in a substantial minority of infants with laryngomalacia."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s5-3","The adult threatened airway","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Management","The Difficult Airway Society guidelines end with front-of-neck access when the patient \"can't intubate, can't oxygenate\": the recommended technique is a scalpel–bougie–tube cricothyroidotomy through the cricothyroid membrane, identified beforehand by palpation or ultrasound. In a patient with a known obstructing laryngeal tumour, the safest plan is often an awake tracheostomy under local anaesthesia , or awake fibreoptic intubation, agreed between surgeon and anaesthetist before any sedation ( Anaesthesia and the shared airway ). High-flow humidified nasal oxygen extends the time before desaturation during induction and airway surgery. Heliox and steroids buy time but do not treat the cause."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~c6","Pathway","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · Pathway","Assess, stabilise, secure. The child or adult with stridor 1 How severe? silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion or falling consciousness emergency airway now — theatre, senior anaesthetist and ENT severe recession or agitation oxygen, nebulised adrenaline, dexamethasone, senior help mild step 2 2 Phase and age inspiratory in an infant, worse feeding and supine laryngomalacia — awake flexible laryngoscopy biphasic, worsening in first months subglottic haemangioma or stenosis barking cough, toddler croup — dexamethasone sudden onset while eating or playing foreign body — bronchoscopy 3 Febrile and toxic? drooling, sitting forward supraglottitis — airway in theatre high fever, poor response to croup treatment bacterial tracheitis 4 Adult tumour awake tracheostomy or planned intubation, then biopsy post-thyroidectomy bilateral palsy reintubate or tracheostomy angioedema adrenaline, steroids, antihistamine stop ACE inhibitor"],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s7-1","Viva essentials","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Definition of stridor, and stertor and wheeze. Inspiratory, biphasic, expiratory and their sites. Causes in children by site, congenital and acquired. History points and severity signs. Laryngomalacia: features, natural history and indications for supraglottoplasty. Cotton–Myer grading and subglottic haemangioma with propranolol. Management: airway first, then diagnosis by endoscopy. Front-of-neck access and the awake tracheostomy."],["sec","cl:t-stridor~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to stridor and the threatened airway · For the examination","Common pitfalls Sending a child with stridor for radiographs unaccompanied. Mistaking quieter stridor in a tiring child for improvement. Stopping at the first lesion found and missing a second."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s1-1","The terms","The syndromal child · Definitions","A syndrome is a pattern of malformations known or assumed to have a single cause, such as Down syndrome or Treacher Collins syndrome. A sequence is a cascade of anomalies that follows from one initial malformation: in the Pierre Robin sequence , a small mandible lets the tongue fall back, which stops the palatal shelves from fusing and obstructs the airway. An association is a non-random clustering of anomalies without a known single cause, such as VACTERL. CHARGE was long called an association until mutations in CHD7 were found, and it is now a syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s1-2","The ENT presentations that should raise suspicion","The syndromal child · Definitions","Common ENT problems and the syndromes behind them Problem Syndromes to consider Conductive hearing loss Down, Goldenhar, Treacher Collins, Turner, primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener), cleft palate syndromes Sensorineural hearing loss Usher, Pendred, Jervell and Lange-Nielsen, Waardenburg, branchio-oto-renal, Alport, CHARGE, Alström Nasal obstruction in the newborn Choanal atresia (CHARGE), piriform aperture stenosis, solitary median maxillary central incisor Tongue base and pharyngeal obstruction Pierre Robin, Stickler, Beckwith–Wiedemann, mucopolysaccharidoses, craniosynostosis syndromes Laryngeal cleft and tracheo-oesophageal fistula Opitz G/BBB, Pallister–Hall, VACTERL Laryngeal web and velopharyngeal insufficiency 22q11.2 deletion (velocardiofacial) syndrome Every syndromal child in theatre Expect a difficult airway in mandibular hypoplasia (Treacher Collins, Pierre Robin, Goldenhar), macroglossia (Beckwith–Wiedemann, Down) and mucopolysaccharidoses: experienced anaesthetist and ENT surgeon present. Protect the cervical spine in Down syndrome and Goldenhar syndrome. Plan postoperative monitoring after adenotonsillectomy for obstructive sleep apnoea: many need high-dependency care. Image the carotids before pharyngeal surgery in 22q11.2 deletion, because the internal carotids may lie medially behind the pharyngeal wall."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s2-1","Genetics and general features","The syndromal child · Down syndrome","Down syndrome affects about 1 in 700 births and is the commonest chromosomal disorder. About 95% have trisomy 21, 3 to 4% a translocation (the only inheritable form) and a few are mosaic. Risk rises with maternal age. General features include learning disability, hypotonia, congenital heart disease in about 40% (atrioventricular septal defect most typically), hypothyroidism, low IgA with frequent respiratory infections, leukaemia, early Alzheimer's disease and obesity."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s2-2","The ears","The syndromal child · Down syndrome","The ear canals are narrow and fill with wax, and Eustachian tube dysfunction makes otitis media with effusion almost universal and longer-lasting than in other children. Ventilation tubes work, but insertion is technically hard, the benefit is shorter, and otorrhoea, early extrusion and recurrence are commoner, so hearing aids are often offered first . Chronic otitis media and cholesteatoma develop more often, so follow-up is long term. Sensorineural loss is less common but occurs earlier with age. Figure 1 Grommets for effusion: harder to place and shorter-lived in the narrow canals of Down syndrome, which is why hearing aids are often preferred. Dhingra, fig. 10.3"],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s2-3","The airway","The syndromal child · Down syndrome","Midfacial hypoplasia, relative macroglossia, hypotonia, obesity and adenotonsillar hypertrophy give obstructive sleep apnoea in about 60% . Oximetry or, ideally, polysomnography is done before adenotonsillectomy, because up to three-quarters need breathing support afterwards and surgery alone often does not cure it; CPAP or further surgery may follow. A mild congenital subglottic stenosis is common, so the endotracheal tube is usually half a size smaller than predicted for age."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s2-4","The neck","The syndromal child · Down syndrome","Atlantoaxial instability is present in about one in five (10 to 20% in other texts), and neck hyperextension during surgery such as adenotonsillectomy can injure the cord. Plain films do not predict who is at risk, so the neck of every child with Down syndrome is handled with care. Grisel syndrome, non-traumatic atlantoaxial subluxation after inflammation, has been linked to excessive diathermy during adenoidectomy."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s3-1","Treacher Collins syndrome","The syndromal child · First and second arch syndromes","Treacher Collins syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis) affects about 1 in 50,000. It is autosomal dominant, usually from mutations in TCOF1 (the \"treacle\" gene), with half arising as new mutations. It is bilateral and symmetrical : down-slanting palpebral fissures, lower lid colobomas , absent or hypoplastic zygomas, micrognathia, and microtia with canal atresia and a conductive loss, usually managed with bone-conduction hearing devices ( Microtia and congenital canal atresia ). About a third have a cleft palate. The small mandible, especially with choanal atresia, can cause airway obstruction at birth that needs a tracheostomy; distraction osteogenesis may later lengthen the mandible. Intubation is difficult throughout life. Figure 2 First, anotia. Second, microtia (a \"peanut\" ear), seen in first and second arch syndromes. Dhingra, figs 8.2 and 8.3"],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s3-2","Goldenhar syndrome (hemifacial microsomia)","The syndromal child · First and second arch syndromes","Goldenhar syndrome is the commonest first and second arch anomaly, affecting about 1 in 5,600 to 26,500, usually sporadically. It is unilateral and asymmetrical , more often on the right: hypoplasia of the mandible, temporomandibular joint, muscles and orbit, preauricular tags, microtia or anotia with canal atresia, ossicular anomalies, and sometimes facial nerve and inner ear anomalies. Associated features include epibulbar dermoids, upper lid colobomas , and cervical vertebral fusion. The OMENS+ system grades the orbit, mandible, ear, nerves and soft tissues. Lower lid or upper lid? A coloboma of the lower lid suggests Treacher Collins syndrome (bilateral and symmetrical). A coloboma of the upper lid, with an epibulbar dermoid, suggests Goldenhar syndrome (unilateral and asymmetrical)."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s3-3","Pierre Robin sequence and Stickler syndrome","The syndromal child · First and second arch syndromes","The Pierre Robin sequence combines micrognathia, glossoptosis and a U-shaped cleft palate ( Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency ). Airway obstruction is managed by prone positioning, a nasopharyngeal airway, tongue–lip adhesion, mandibular distraction or tracheostomy, and feeding often needs support. Stickler syndrome , a collagen disorder, is the commonest associated syndrome and adds high myopia with a risk of retinal detachment, joint problems and sensorineural or mixed hearing loss; every child with Robin sequence should have an eye examination."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~c4","Syndromes with sensorineural loss","The syndromal child · Syndromes with sensorineural loss","Look beyond the ear. The work-up of the deaf child is covered in The deaf child and congenital hearing loss ."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s4-1","The ones to know","The syndromal child · Syndromes with sensorineural loss","Syndromic sensorineural hearing loss Syndrome Inheritance and gene Features that matter Waardenburg Usually dominant; PAX3 (types 1 and 3), MITF (type 2), SOX10 (type 4) White forelock, heterochromia, dystopia canthorum in type 1; about 2 to 5% of congenitally deaf children Pendred Recessive; SLC26A4 (pendrin) Enlarged vestibular aqueduct, goitre later in childhood; fluctuating loss after minor head injury Jervell and Lange-Nielsen Recessive; KCNQ1 , KCNE1 Long QT syndrome, syncope and sudden death; ECG for every child with profound congenital loss Usher Recessive; several genes Retinitis pigmentosa; types 1 to 3 by severity of loss and vestibular function Branchio-oto-renal Dominant; EYA1 Preauricular pits, branchial fistulae, renal anomalies; mixed loss Alport Mostly X-linked; COL4A5 Haematuria and renal failure; progressive high-frequency loss; lens anomalies CHARGE CHD7 Coloboma, heart defects, choanal atresia, retardation of growth, genital and ear anomalies, with absent semicircular canals"],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s4-2","CHARGE syndrome in ENT","The syndromal child · Syndromes with sensorineural loss","CHARGE stands for coloboma, heart defects, choanal atresia, retarded growth and development, genital hypoplasia and ear anomalies. The ears are typically short and wide with a small or absent lobe; the inner ear often lacks semicircular canals, so balance and motor milestones are delayed; and cranial nerve palsies, including facial palsy and swallowing problems, are common. Bilateral choanal atresia can be the presenting emergency ( Choanal atresia and congenital nasal masses )."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s5-1","22q11.2 deletion syndrome","The syndromal child · Other syndromes ENT surgeons meet","The 22q11.2 deletion (velocardiofacial or DiGeorge syndrome) is one of the commonest microdeletion syndromes. It causes congenital heart disease, hypocalcaemia from absent parathyroids, immune deficiency from thymic hypoplasia, learning and psychiatric problems, and characteristic facies. In ENT it causes velopharyngeal insufficiency with hypernasal speech (often with an overt or submucous cleft), anterior glottic webs , and medially placed internal carotid arteries that must be imaged before pharyngoplasty."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s5-2","Craniosynostosis syndromes","The syndromal child · Other syndromes ENT surgeons meet","Crouzon and Apert syndromes, caused mainly by FGFR2 mutations, combine premature skull suture fusion with midfacial hypoplasia and proptosis; Apert adds fusion of the fingers and toes. Up to half develop obstructive sleep apnoea from the small midface, so overnight studies are done readily, and midface advancement by distraction may be needed. Conductive loss from middle ear anomalies and effusion is common."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s5-3","Macroglossia and storage disorders","The syndromal child · Other syndromes ENT surgeons meet","Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome (11p15) causes macroglossia, overgrowth, abdominal wall defects, neonatal hypoglycaemia and a risk of embryonal tumours; tongue reduction may be needed for airway or feeding problems. The mucopolysaccharidoses (Hurler, Hunter and others) deposit glycosaminoglycans in the airway, adenoids, tonsils and middle ear, causing progressive obstruction, sleep apnoea and conductive loss; the airway becomes increasingly difficult with age, and enzyme replacement or stem cell transplant changes the course."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s5-4","Turner syndrome","The syndromal child · Other syndromes ENT surgeons meet","Turner syndrome (45,X) affects about 1 in 2,500 girls: short stature, a webbed neck and ovarian failure, with recurrent otitis media, effusion and cholesteatoma in childhood and a characteristic mid-frequency sensorineural loss in adult life."],["ev","cl:t-syndr~s6-1","The syndromal child: current evidence","The syndromal child · Approach","Genetic testing. The American College of Medical Genetics recommends exome or genome sequencing as a first- or second-tier test for children with congenital anomalies or developmental delay (Manickam et al., Genet Med 2021), and gene panels find a cause in many children with hearing loss. Down syndrome. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends polysomnography for all children with Down syndrome by age four, because obstructive sleep apnoea is common and symptoms are unreliable (Bull et al., Pediatrics 2022). 22q11.2 deletion, CHARGE ( CHD7 ) and Treacher Collins syndromes each have airway, palate and hearing implications that need coordinated care."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s6-1","Principles","The syndromal child · Approach","Ask about pregnancy, birth, development, family history and other medical problems; look at the face, eyes, ears, neck, hands and skin; and listen to parents, who often know a great deal about their child's condition and should be asked what they expect from the consultation. When an ENT finding suggests a syndrome, refer to clinical genetics, and arrange the relevant tests: an ECG, renal ultrasound, eye examination, thyroid tests or imaging. Care is multidisciplinary, involving paediatrics, audiology, speech and language therapy, cleft and craniofacial teams, anaesthesia and teachers of the deaf."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s7-1","Viva essentials","The syndromal child · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Syndrome, sequence and association, with an example of each. Down syndrome: effusion and hearing aids, obstructive sleep apnoea and postoperative care, subglottic stenosis, atlantoaxial instability. Treacher Collins versus Goldenhar. Syndromes with sensorineural loss: Waardenburg, Pendred, Jervell and Lange-Nielsen, Usher, branchio-oto-renal, Alport. CHARGE and choanal atresia. 22q11.2 deletion: velopharyngeal insufficiency and the medial carotid."],["sec","cl:t-syndr~s7-2","Common pitfalls","The syndromal child · For the examination","Common pitfalls Hyperextending the neck of a child with Down syndrome during adenotonsillectomy. Discharging a child with Down syndrome and severe sleep apnoea on the day of adenotonsillectomy. Missing long QT syndrome in a profoundly deaf child. Operating on the pharynx in 22q11.2 deletion without imaging the carotids."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s1-1","Epidemiology","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Overview","The temporal bone is involved in 30 to 70% of head injuries, but only 14 to 22% of these produce a true fracture. Most fractures follow road traffic accidents, then assaults and falls. Young adult men in their second to fourth decades are affected about three times as often as women."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s1-2","Why it matters","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Overview","The temporal bone contains the cochlea, the labyrinth, the facial nerve, the ossicles, the carotid artery and the dura. A fracture can damage any of them, and early documentation of facial function and hearing shapes every later decision."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s2-1","Traditional: by the direction of the fracture line","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Classification","2.1.1 Longitudinal fracture About 80%. Usually from a blow to the side of the head. The line runs along the long axis of the petrous bone, through the squamous part, the posterosuperior canal wall, the tegmen and the middle ear, and tends to spare the otic capsule. 2.1.2 Transverse fracture About 20%. Usually from a blow to the back of the head. The line runs across the petrous bone, through the vestibule, cochlea or internal auditory canal. 2.1.3 Mixed Elements of both, common in severe injuries. Figure 1 First, the courses of longitudinal and transverse fractures of the temporal bone. Second, a right longitudinal fracture on CT and as seen at operation. Dhingra, figs 14.10 and 14.11 Longitudinal and transverse fractures Longitudinal Transverse Frequency About 80% About 20% Blow Side of the head Back of the head Drum Torn, bleeding from the ear Intact, haemotympanum Hearing Conductive Sensorineural Vertigo Less common Severe Facial palsy 10 to 20%, often delayed About 50%, often immediate"],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s2-2","Modern: does it involve the otic capsule?","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Classification","The old longitudinal–transverse scheme fails to describe the many mixed fractures. The modern scheme asks one question instead: is the otic capsule broken? Otic capsule sparing fractures are by far the commonest; otic capsule violating fractures, only about 6%, are much more likely to cause facial palsy, sensorineural hearing loss, CSF leak and meningitis. This system predicts outcome better. Where the books differ · Longitudinal or transverse Dhingra Teaches conductive loss with longitudinal and sensorineural loss with transverse fractures. Lee Modern series do not fit the old categories neatly; the otic capsule system predicts outcome better. In practice Describe the fracture as otic capsule sparing or violating to predict outcome, and know the old table, which is still examined."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s3-1","General","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Clinical features","Signs of head injury and of a base of skull fracture: Battle's sign (bruising over the mastoid, appearing after a day or two), periorbital bruising, and altered consciousness."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s3-2","The ear","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Clinical features","Bleeding from the ear with a torn drum or a step in the canal wall (longitudinal), or a blue-black drum from blood in the middle ear ( haemotympanum ) behind an intact drum (transverse)."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s3-3","Hearing","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Clinical features","3.3.1 Conductive loss From haemotympanum (the commonest early cause), a perforation, or ossicular injury: incudostapedial joint separation first (about 80% of ossicular injuries), then dislocation of the incus, then fracture of the stapes crura. An ossicular injury gives a conductive loss of at least 30 dB. 3.3.2 Sensorineural loss From fracture through the cochlea or a labyrinthine concussion; often permanent. Mixed losses are common."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s3-4","Vertigo","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Clinical features","Severe vertigo with spontaneous nystagmus after a transverse fracture through the labyrinth; milder, positional vertigo after concussion or BPPV."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s3-5","Facial nerve","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Clinical features","About a quarter of fractures cause facial palsy: about half of transverse fractures and 10 to 20% of longitudinal ones (texts give anywhere from a tenth to a fifth). Record whether the palsy was immediate or delayed, and complete or incomplete. An immediate complete palsy suggests transection or a bone spicule in the nerve. In about three-quarters the onset is delayed, by one to sixteen days; a delayed palsy suggests oedema and predicts a good recovery."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s3-6","CSF leak","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Clinical features","A CSF leak complicates about 17% of fractures: clear fluid from the ear (CSF otorrhoea) through a torn drum, or from the nose (CSF otorhinorrhoea) via the Eustachian tube behind an intact drum."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s4-1","High-resolution CT of the temporal bones","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Investigations","Shows the fracture line, the otic capsule, the ossicles, the facial canal and the tegmen. CT angiography if the fracture crosses the carotid canal."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s4-2","Beta-2 transferrin","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Investigations","Confirms that the fluid is CSF."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s4-3","Audiogram","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Investigations","Best done two to three days after injury, once the patient is awake, and repeated at six to eight weeks when blood has cleared from the middle ear."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s4-4","Electroneuronography","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Investigations","For a complete facial palsy, to guide decisions on exploration."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s5-1","The first assessment","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Management","Head injury with ear signs Follow trauma protocols and complete a neurological assessment. Document facial function as early as possible, before sedation if you can. Look for haemotympanum, a canal step, CSF, vertigo and nystagmus. Do not syringe, probe or pack the ear deeply. Arrange HRCT of the temporal bones, and CT angiography if the carotid canal is involved."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s5-2","CSF leak","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Management","Most stop within a week or two with the head raised, bed rest, stool softeners and avoiding nose blowing and straining. Routine prophylactic antibiotics are not recommended. A persistent leak, or meningitis, needs a lumbar drain or surgical repair. Patients with otic capsule violating fractures need pneumococcal vaccination and advice to seek help for fever and headache (see Complications below)."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s5-3","Facial palsy","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Management","Immediate complete palsy with evidence of nerve injury is explored and decompressed or repaired. Delayed palsy is managed with steroids and observation, with serial ENoG ( Facial nerve palsy ). During recovery, the Sunnybrook scale records synkinesis better than House–Brackmann."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s5-4","Hearing","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Management","Haemotympanum clears in weeks. A conductive loss persisting at six to eight weeks suggests ossicular injury and is explored and reconstructed. Sensorineural loss is rehabilitated with aids or, if profound, a cochlear implant."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s5-5","Vertigo","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Management","Short-term vestibular sedatives, then vestibular rehabilitation; treat post-traumatic BPPV with repositioning manoeuvres."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~c6","Complications","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Complications","Early and late. Meningitis, early or years later, through an unhealed fracture. Permanent sensorineural deafness. Facial palsy. Late cholesteatoma, from skin trapped in the fracture line. Chronic vertigo and perilymph fistula. Carotid injury and dissection."],["ev","cl:t-tbfx~s6-1","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea: current evidence","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Complications","Classification. In a review of 820 temporal bone fractures, otic capsule-violating fractures were far more likely to cause facial palsy, CSF leak and sensorineural loss than capsule-sparing ones (Brodie and Thompson, Am J Otol 1997); later comparisons confirmed that this scheme predicts complications better than the longitudinal–transverse system (Little and Kesser, Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2006). CSF leak. A Cochrane review found no evidence that prophylactic antibiotics prevent meningitis after basal skull fracture (Ratilal et al., 2015). Facial palsy. Delayed or incomplete palsies have an excellent prognosis with conservative management; evidence for decompression in immediate complete palsy comes from case series using ENoG thresholds (degeneration over 90% within two weeks)."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s6-1","Why capsule-violating fractures cause late problems","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · Complications","Bone of the otic capsule heals only by fibrous union, so these fractures leave a weak barrier: CSF leaks can recur with coughing or straining, meningitis can occur years later, and meningoceles or encephaloceles may present long after the injury with a conductive loss, an effusion or meningitis."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s7-1","Viva essentials","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Longitudinal versus transverse: the six-row table. Otic capsule sparing versus violating, and why it predicts outcome. Immediate versus delayed facial palsy, and how each is managed. Beta-2 transferrin for CSF. Ossicular injuries in order of frequency."],["sec","cl:t-tbfx~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea · For the examination","Common pitfalls Forgetting to record facial function before the patient is sedated. Syringing or packing a bleeding ear after head injury. Assuming a conductive loss is permanent before the blood has cleared. Missing late meningitis or cholesteatoma years after the injury."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s1-1","Definition","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Overview","Infection of the middle ear cleft by Mycobacterium tuberculosis . Syphilitic otitis media, much rarer, is included here because it shares the picture of bone destruction and a disproportionate hearing loss."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s1-2","Epidemiology","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Overview","Uncommon in low-burden countries, but important where tuberculosis is endemic, and rising with drug-resistant strains and immunosuppression, particularly HIV."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s1-3","Routes of infection","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Overview","Through the Eustachian tube, from pulmonary disease, classically present in most patients, although the chest film is normal in many. Through the blood, from a distant focus. In infants, through the tube from infected milk."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s2-1","Symptoms","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Clinical features","Discharge: painless, thin and cloudy, persisting despite treatment. Hearing loss: out of proportion to the symptoms, often mixed, from early ossicular and cochlear involvement."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s2-2","Signs","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Clinical features","A thickened drum early on, then multiple perforations that merge into one large defect. Pale, thick mucosa and abundant pale granulation. Bone sequestra in the mastoid. Non-tender enlarged cervical and preauricular nodes. Facial palsy in about 10%, sometimes the presenting feature in a child."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s2-3","Complications","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Clinical features","Facial palsy, sequestrum, postauricular fistula, Bezold abscess and labyrinthitis. Suspect it when A chronic ear fails ordinary treatment, a mastoidectomy fails, granulation is pale, or facial palsy appears early in a painless ear."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s3-1","From the ear","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Investigations","PCR of the discharge or tissue: gives an answer within days. Histology: caseating granulomas with Langhans giant cells. Acid-fast smear and culture: a negative smear does not exclude it."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s3-2","General","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Investigations","Chest imaging, tuberculin skin test or interferon-gamma release assay, and HIV testing."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s3-3","CT","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Investigations","Often looks like ordinary chronic otitis media, with more bone erosion and sequestra."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s3-4","Differential diagnosis","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Investigations","Ordinary chronic suppurative otitis media , carcinoma of the middle ear ( Middle ear and temporal bone tumours ), granulomatosis with polyangiitis, and fungal infection."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s4-1","Antituberculous chemotherapy","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Management","Standard multidrug therapy, guided by sensitivities, for at least six months: two months of isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol, then four of isoniazid and rifampicin (the WHO regimen for most extrapulmonary tuberculosis), with longer courses for bone or central nervous system involvement."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s4-2","Surgery","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Management","Limited to biopsy, removal of sequestra and drainage of abscesses or complications. Reconstruction is delayed until treatment is complete, because healing is slow and fistulae are common during active disease; afterwards, repair succeeds in most."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s4-3","Facial palsy","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Management","Usually recovers well with medical treatment."],["ev","cl:t-tbom~c5","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media: current evidence","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Syphilitic otitis","Diagnosis. Tuberculous otitis is easily missed: biopsy of granulations with histology, culture and a rapid nucleic acid test (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra) on tissue gives the best yield."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~c5","Syphilitic otitis","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · Syphilitic otitis","Rare, destructive. Congenital or acquired syphilis can cause osteitis of the temporal bone with necrosis, and labyrinthitis with profound, often fluctuating sensorineural loss and vertigo. Diagnose with treponemal tests (FTA-ABS) and treat with penicillin and steroids. Syphilitic labyrinthitis is covered in Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis ."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s6-1","Viva essentials","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The features: painless discharge, multiple perforations, pale granulation, early facial palsy, disproportionate hearing loss. Diagnosis by PCR, histology and culture. Treatment is medical; surgery for complications only."],["sec","cl:t-tbom~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Tuberculous and syphilitic otitis media · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a chronic ear without sending the tissue for tuberculosis. Being reassured by a negative acid-fast smear. Reconstructing during active treatment."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s1-1","Embryology","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Applied anatomy","The medial anlage starts at the foramen caecum of the tongue at about four weeks. It descends in front of the hyoid and larynx along the thyroglossal tract and reaches the root of the neck by seven weeks. Arrested descent leaves a lingual thyroid , often the only thyroid tissue the patient has; the lowest part of the tract persists as the pyramidal lobe in up to 40%; and remnants of the tract form thyroglossal cysts ( Congenital neck masses ). The lateral anlagen of the fourth and fifth pouches and the ultimobranchial bodies join the descending gland. The ultimobranchial bodies bring the neural-crest C cells , which concentrate in the upper and middle thirds of the lobes, and that is where medullary cancers arise."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s1-2","The gland","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Applied anatomy","The gland has two lobes joined by an isthmus over the second to fourth tracheal rings. It weighs 15 to 25 g, and each lobe measures about 5 × 3 × 2 cm. Its true capsule sends septa into the gland, while its false capsule , the perithyroidal sheath, is formed by the visceral (pretracheal) layer of the deep cervical fascia. Two suspensory ligaments hold it. The anterior ligament runs from the isthmus to the trachea, and the posterior ligament, the ligament of Berry , binds the back of each lobe to the cricoid and first tracheal rings, which is why the gland rises on swallowing. The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs deep to or through Berry's ligament beside small branches of the inferior thyroid artery. This is the commonest site of nerve injury, and thyroid tissue left here explains bed uptake and measurable thyroglobulin after a \"total\" thyroidectomy. The superior thyroid artery is the first branch of the external carotid. The inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk, passes behind the carotid sheath and supplies the parathyroids, and a thyroidea ima sometimes arises from the brachiocephalic trunk or the arch. The superior and middle veins drain to the internal jugular; the middle vein has no companion artery and is the first vessel divided at surgery; and the inferior veins form a plexus draining to the brachiocephalic veins. Lymph passes first to the central compartment (level VI), to the prelaryngeal ( Delphian ), pretracheal and paratracheal nodes, and then to the superior mediastinum (level VII) and the lateral neck (levels II to IV, sometimes V). Figur"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s1-3","Nerves at risk","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Applied anatomy","The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies all the intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid and carries sensation below the folds. The right nerve hooks round the subclavian artery and runs obliquely from lateral to medial; the left hooks round the aortic arch and runs more vertically in the tracheo-oesophageal groove. Each crosses the branches of the inferior thyroid artery, superficial to them, deep to them or between them, branches before entering the larynx in about a third of people, and enters behind the inferior cornu of the thyroid cartilage. It is found in the recurrent nerve triangle , bounded by the trachea medially, the retracted strap muscles laterally and the lower pole above, or close to the tubercle of Zuckerkandl . A non-recurrent laryngeal nerve occurs in about 0.5 to 1% of people on the right and almost never on the left. It runs directly from the vagus to the larynx and accompanies an aberrant retro-oesophageal right subclavian artery, the cause of dysphagia lusoria . The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve supplies cricothyroid, which tenses the cord for high pitch. It runs on the inferior constrictor close to the superior pole vessels and crosses them within 1 cm of the pole in about a fifth of people, so the superior vessels are ligated individually on the capsule with the gland pulled down. The ansa cervicalis supplies the strap muscles from below, so the straps are divided high when they must be divided. Figure 3 The recurrent laryngeal nerves and inferior thyroid arteries, and the relations of the lateral lobe. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s1-4","Lingual thyroid","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Applied anatomy","A lingual thyroid occurs in about 1 in 3000 to 4000 patients with thyroid disease, mostly women. It is a smooth, vascular mass at the tongue base that may cause dysphagia, dysphonia, airway obstruction or bleeding, particularly at puberty or in pregnancy. Before any treatment, a scintiscan and neck ultrasound establish whether any other thyroid tissue exists. Thyroxine shrinks it and radioiodine can ablate it; obstruction is relieved by excision through a transoral or suprahyoid transpharyngeal approach, followed by lifelong replacement if it was the only thyroid. Figure 4 Lingual thyroid at the tongue base, with its sagittal CT. Dhingra, fig. 66.3"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s2-1","Hormone synthesis","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Physiology and function tests","Follicular cells make thyroglobulin, thyroxine (T4) and tri-iodothyronine (T3), and the parafollicular C cells make calcitonin. Synthesis proceeds in steps: Iodide is actively trapped. Thyroid peroxidase oxidises it and attaches it to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin, forming MIT and DIT. Thyroid peroxidase couples them: DIT + DIT forms T4, and MIT + DIT forms T3. The hormones are stored as colloid. Colloid is taken back into the cell, thyroglobulin is cleaved, and the hormones are released. Uncoupled MIT and DIT are deiodinated so that their iodine is recycled. The gland releases mostly T4, with a half-life of about a week, and most T3, several times more potent and with a half-life of about a day, is made by peripheral deiodination. More than 99% of circulating hormone is protein-bound. Hypothalamic TRH stimulates pituitary TSH, TSH drives hormone synthesis, growth and vascularity of the gland, and T4 and T3 feed back negatively. Figure 5 First, thyroid follicles with follicular and parafollicular C cells. Second, the hypothalamic–pituitary–thyroid axis. Dhingra, figs 66.4 and 66.5"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s2-2","Tests and terms","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Physiology and function tests","TSH is the single best screening test: high in primary hypothyroidism and suppressed in hyperthyroidism. A high TSH with normal T4 is subclinical hypothyroidism, and a low TSH with normal T4 and T3 subclinical hyperthyroidism. Free T4 and T3 are measured because total levels vary with thyroid-binding globulin, which rises in pregnancy and with the pill. TPO (antimicrosomal) antibodies are present in almost all Hashimoto disease and most Graves disease; thyroglobulin antibodies occur in autoimmune thyroiditis and interfere with thyroglobulin assays; and stimulating TSH-receptor antibodies cause Graves disease. Thyroglobulin is made only by thyroid tissue; it is useless for diagnosing a nodule, but after total thyroidectomy and ablation it should be undetectable, so a rising level means recurrence. Calcitonin and CEA are the markers of medullary carcinoma. Ultrasound is the essential imaging study. Scintigraphy with technetium or I-123 shows hot and cold nodules, and CT , with non-iodinated contrast if radioiodine may follow, shows retrosternal extension and invasion."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s3-1","Hypothyroidism","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Benign disease","Iodine deficiency is the commonest cause of hypothyroidism worldwide and Hashimoto disease the commonest where iodine is sufficient. Thyroidectomy and radioiodine, neck irradiation (almost half of patients after laryngeal or pharyngeal radiotherapy, so TSH is checked routinely; Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for head and neck cancer ), drugs such as lithium, amiodarone and antithyroid drugs, inborn defects, and rare pituitary failure are the other causes. The patient is tired, intolerant of cold, gains weight, has dry coarse skin and hair, constipation, menorrhagia, slow thinking, bradycardia and non-pitting myxoedema . In ENT terms the voice becomes hoarse and low from oedematous cords, the tongue enlarges, and sensorineural hearing loss and serous otitis occur. Congenital hypothyroidism (cretinism), with stunted growth, intellectual disability and deafness, is prevented by neonatal screening. Treatment is levothyroxine, started low in the elderly and in heart disease. Anaesthesia and sedatives can precipitate myxoedema coma in untreated patients."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s3-2","Hyperthyroidism","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Benign disease","Hyperthyroidism causes weight loss despite a good appetite, heat intolerance, sweating, tremor, palpitations and atrial fibrillation, diarrhoea, anxiety, proximal myopathy and oligomenorrhoea. Graves disease is the commonest cause and toxic multinodular goitre the commonest in the elderly; toxic adenoma, transient thyroiditis, iodine excess (the Jod–Basedow phenomenon after contrast in a nodular goitre) and exogenous hormone follow, with rare TSH-secreting pituitary tumours, struma ovarii and functioning cancers. Causes of hyperthyroidism and their scans Cause Key features Isotope scan Graves disease Diffuse goitre, bruit, eye disease, pretibial myxoedema, TSH-receptor antibodies Diffuse high uptake Toxic multinodular goitre Elderly, nodular goitre, no eye signs Patchy hot areas, suppressed rest Toxic adenoma Single nodule, usually over 2.5 cm, often T3 toxicosis One hot nodule, rest suppressed Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis Painful tender gland after a viral illness, raised ESR Very low uptake Silent and postpartum thyroiditis Painless, transient Very low uptake"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s3-3","Graves disease","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Benign disease","Graves disease is an autoimmune disease in which stimulating TSH-receptor antibodies drive the gland. Women are affected five to ten times more often than men, usually in the third and fourth decades. It produces a diffuse vascular goitre with a bruit, thyrotoxicosis, ophthalmopathy with lid retraction, proptosis, diplopia and optic neuropathy, and dermopathy as pretibial myxoedema. Antithyroid drugs are first line: carbimazole or methimazole, or propylthiouracil in the first trimester despite its risk of liver failure. All carry a risk of agranulocytosis, so the patient is told to report a sore throat, and relapse after stopping is common. Beta-blockers control symptoms. Radioiodine is definitive but usually leads to hypothyroidism; it is avoided in pregnancy and breastfeeding, conception is deferred for at least six months, and it can worsen eye disease, especially in smokers. Total thyroidectomy is chosen for drug failure or side effects, a large goitre or nodules, active eye disease, pregnancy planning, or the patient's preference. Before surgery the patient must be made euthyroid on drugs to avoid thyroid storm , and Lugol's iodine for about ten days reduces vascularity through the Wolff–Chaikoff effect. Severe orbitopathy is treated by endoscopic transnasal orbital decompression ( Proptosis and the orbit )."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s3-4","Thyroiditis","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Benign disease","Hashimoto (chronic lymphocytic) thyroiditis is the commonest thyroid disease where iodine is sufficient, mostly in women. It produces a painless, firm, rubbery, symmetrical goitre with raised TPO antibodies, and the patient becomes hypothyroid over time; histology shows lymphoid follicles and Hürthle cell change. It is treated with thyroxine, but a discrete nodule still needs FNA, and a rapidly growing mass raises the possibility of lymphoma . Subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis is the commonest painful thyroid. It follows a viral infection, with neck pain radiating to the jaw and ear, fever, a tender gland and a high ESR, and passes through a transient toxic phase with very low isotope uptake, a hypothyroid phase and recovery. It is treated with NSAIDs, steroids and beta-blockers. Silent and postpartum thyroiditis are painless and transient but sometimes leave permanent hypothyroidism. Acute suppurative thyroiditis is rare: a bacterial infection with a tender red swelling and sometimes an abscess. In children it is usually left-sided and comes from a pyriform sinus fistula , a third or fourth branchial remnant ( Congenital neck masses ); after antibiotics and drainage, the fistula is sought with barium swallow, CT or endoscopy and excised or its opening cauterised. Riedel thyroiditis is very rare: fibrosis spreading out of the gland, part of the IgG4-related spectrum, making it woody, fixed and non-tender, compressing the trachea and paralysing the recurrent nerve, and mimicking anaplastic carcinoma or lymphoma. It is biopsied, often by isthmusectomy, which al"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s3-5","Goitre","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Benign disease","A goitre is an enlarged gland, diffuse or nodular. It results from endemic iodine deficiency, goitrogens, inherited defects of hormone synthesis ( Pendred syndrome , with sensorineural deafness and an enlarged vestibular aqueduct), puberty and pregnancy, autoimmunity and nodular hyperplasia. Endemic goitre is prevented by iodised salt. The multinodular goitre evolves slowly and may become toxic. A sudden painful enlargement means haemorrhage into a nodule, and about 5 to 10% harbour a carcinoma, usually a small papillary one. Pressure causes cough, choking, positional or nocturnal breathlessness, stridor and dysphagia, and superior vena caval obstruction gives a positive Pemberton sign : raising the arms brings on facial plethora, distended neck veins and breathlessness. Assessment is by TSH, ultrasound with FNA of suspicious or dominant nodules, laryngoscopy, and CT for retrosternal extension and tracheal compression, with flow–volume loops if needed. Surgery is indicated for compression, substernal extension that can no longer be examined or sampled, suspicion of cancer, toxicity and cosmesis. A retrosternal goitre is almost always removed through the neck; sternotomy is rarely needed, mainly for a primary mediastinal goitre or one extending below the aortic arch."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s4-1","Causes","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · The thyroid nodule","Most nodules are benign: a colloid (adenomatous) nodule in a nodular goitre, a follicular or Hürthle cell adenoma, a cyst, focal thyroiditis or a regenerative nodule, or a toxic adenoma. Carcinoma accounts for about 5 to 15%, and metastasis and lymphoma are rare. Some masses only look thyroid: a node, a parathyroid cyst, a thyroglossal cyst or a laryngocele. Clinical features raising concern for cancer Lower concern Higher concern Long-standing, stable nodule Age under 20 to 30 or over 60 years; male Multinodular gland without a dominant nodule Childhood neck irradiation; family history of thyroid cancer or MEN 2 Hashimoto or toxic features Rapid growth, pain; hard, fixed nodule; size over 4 cm Soft, mobile Cervical lymphadenopathy; vocal fold palsy; stridor, dysphagia Examine the larynx in every thyroid patient A paralysed vocal fold can coexist with a normal voice. Preoperative laryngoscopy finds unsuspected palsy, which suggests invasive cancer and changes the surgical plan. It also documents the baseline before surgery."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s4-2","Investigation","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · The thyroid nodule","TSH comes first. If it is low, a scintiscan follows, and a hot nodule is almost never malignant and is not aspirated. If it is normal or high, the patient goes on to ultrasound and FNA. Every patient has an ultrasound of the thyroid and neck, and the pattern sets the risk. High suspicion attaches to a solid hypoechoic nodule with microcalcifications, irregular or infiltrative margins, a taller-than-wide shape or extrathyroidal extension. A solid hypoechoic nodule with smooth margins is intermediate; an iso- or hyperechoic nodule, or a partly cystic one with an eccentric solid part, is low; a spongiform nodule is very low; and a purely cystic nodule is benign. Suspicious nodes show microcalcification, cystic change, loss of the fatty hilum, a round shape and peripheral vascularity. British practice grades the ultrasound from U1 (normal) and U2 (benign) to U3 (indeterminate), U4 (suspicious) and U5 (malignant), and aspirates U3 to U5; the American (ATA) and TI-RADS systems use the pattern and size together. Ultrasound-guided FNA is done for nodules of 1 cm or more with high or intermediate suspicion, 1.5 cm or more with low suspicion, and 2 cm or more with very low suspicion (or they are observed), but not for pure cysts. Suspicious nodes are also aspirated, with thyroglobulin measured in the needle washout. Calcitonin is measured when there is a family history of medullary cancer or MEN 2, and CT or MRI is used for retrosternal extension, invasion or bulky nodal disease. The Bethesda system for thyroid cytology (2023) and the UK Thy grades Bethesda category UK grade Average "],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s4-3","Cystic nodules","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · The thyroid nodule","About a quarter of nodules are wholly or partly cystic, and the colour of the aspirate is informative. Brown fluid is old haemorrhage into a colloid nodule. Water-clear fluid suggests a parathyroid cyst , confirmed by measuring PTH in the fluid. Bloody fluid that reaccumulates raises suspicion of cystic papillary carcinoma, and the solid part is aspirated under ultrasound. Cysts that recur repeatedly or exceed about 4 cm are excised, and ethanol ablation is an alternative for benign recurrent cysts. Figure 7 Management of a thyroid cyst according to the aspirate. Dhingra, fig. 66.7"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s4-4","Benign nodules","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · The thyroid nodule","Benign nodules are followed with examination and ultrasound, and aspirated again if they grow significantly or change pattern. TSH suppression with thyroxine is little used, because its effect is unpredictable and it harms bone and heart. A toxic adenoma is treated by lobectomy or radioiodine, and other benign nodules are removed only for compression or cosmesis, with radiofrequency or laser ablation as options for selected nodules."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~c5","Thyroid cancer","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroid cancer","Five types. Malignant tumours of the thyroid Type Proportion Cell of origin Spread Papillary 70 to 85% Follicular Lymphatic (central and lateral nodes) Follicular, including Hürthle (oncocytic) 10 to 15% Follicular Blood (lung, bone) Medullary 5% Parafollicular C cell Nodes early, then blood Anaplastic Under 5% Follicular (dedifferentiated) Local invasion, everywhere Lymphoma About 5% Lymphoid (in Hashimoto) Nodal and extranodal"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s5-1","Papillary carcinoma","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroid cancer","Papillary carcinoma is the commonest type and is becoming commoner as more small tumours are detected. It usually affects women of 30 to 50, and it is also the usual childhood thyroid cancer, often with nodes and lung spread but still with an excellent prognosis. The main risk factor is ionising radiation in childhood, from old low-dose treatment of tonsils, acne and thymus, radiotherapy for Hodgkin disease, and Chernobyl and atomic fallout, with a latency of decades; family history, familial adenomatous polyposis, Gardner and Cowden syndromes, and adequate iodine intake also contribute. Microscopically it forms papillae with characteristic nuclei, the ground-glass \" Orphan Annie eye \" nuclei with grooves and intranuclear inclusions, and psammoma bodies , and it is often multifocal. Because the diagnosis rests on nuclear features, cytology can make it. The tall cell, columnar, diffuse sclerosing and hobnail variants are aggressive, and BRAF V600E, the commonest mutation, is associated with more aggressive behaviour. It spreads by lymphatics: clinically evident nodes are present in about 30% of adults and in many more children, and the nodal deposits may be cystic and sometimes form the presenting lump behind an occult primary. Distant spread is uncommon. Ten-year survival exceeds 90%, and prognosis worsens with older age, male sex, larger size, extrathyroidal extension and distant metastases, as captured by the AGES (age, grade, extent, size) and AMES (age, metastases, extent, size) scores. Low-risk papillary microcarcinomas of 1 cm or less may be watched by active surveill"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s5-2","Follicular carcinoma","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroid cancer","Follicular carcinoma affects an older group than papillary carcinoma, around 50, mostly women, and is commoner in iodine-deficient areas. It is usually unifocal, and the diagnosis needs capsular or vascular invasion on histology; minimally invasive tumours behave well and widely invasive ones do not. It spreads by blood to lung and bone, with distant metastases in up to 15% at diagnosis, while nodal spread is uncommon. RAS mutations and the PAX8/PPARγ rearrangement are characteristic, and the prognosis is worse with age over 50, size over 4 cm and distant disease."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s5-3","Hürthle cell (oncocytic) carcinoma","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroid cancer","Hürthle cell carcinoma consists of large eosinophilic cells packed with mitochondria. It is more aggressive than follicular carcinoma, more often multifocal and with nodal and distant spread, and because it takes up radioiodine poorly , surgery is all-important."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s5-4","Medullary carcinoma","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroid cancer","Medullary carcinoma arises from the C cells, secretes calcitonin and CEA, which are used for diagnosis and follow-up, and shows amyloid stroma on histology. It is equally common in both sexes. About 75 to 80% are sporadic , unifocal tumours in the fifth decade. The other 20 to 25% are familial , caused by autosomal dominant germline RET mutations; they are preceded by C-cell hyperplasia, are bilateral and multifocal, and occur in MEN 2A, MEN 2B and familial medullary carcinoma, now counted as a variant of MEN 2A. It spreads early to central and lateral nodes, then to lung, liver and bone, and its hormones cause diarrhoea and flushing. The work-up measures calcitonin and CEA, tests every patient for RET, excludes a phaeochromocytoma with plasma or urinary metanephrines before any surgery (removing it first to avoid a hypertensive crisis), and checks serum calcium for hyperparathyroidism ( Hyperparathyroidism ). Treatment is total thyroidectomy with central (level VI) neck dissection, adding a lateral dissection for nodal disease or according to the calcitonin level. Radioiodine does not work, and RET and multikinase inhibitors such as selpercatinib, vandetanib and cabozantinib treat progressive metastatic disease. RET carriers have a prophylactic thyroidectomy within the first year for the highest-risk (MEN 2B) mutations, by about five years for high-risk MEN 2A mutations, and later, guided by calcitonin, for moderate-risk mutations. Multiple endocrine neoplasia Syndrome Gene Components MEN 1 (Wermer) MEN1 Parathyroid hyperplasia, pituitary tumours, pancreatic islet tumours "],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s5-5","Anaplastic carcinoma","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroid cancer","Anaplastic carcinoma is one of the most lethal cancers, with a median survival of about six months. It arises in old people, usually over 60, often by dedifferentiation of a long-standing differentiated cancer, and is always stage IV. It presents as a rapidly enlarging, hard, often painful mass fixed to its surroundings, with hoarseness, stridor, dysphagia and nodes, and half the patients have distant metastases. A core or open biopsy must provide enough tissue to exclude lymphoma , which is curable. The tumour is resected only if resection can be complete; otherwise it is treated with radiotherapy and chemotherapy, and BRAF-mutant tumours respond to dabrafenib with trametinib. Palliating the airway, including the hard decision about tracheostomy through tumour, and supportive and palliative care are central."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s5-6","Lymphoma","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroid cancer","Thyroid lymphoma usually affects older women and almost always arises in Hashimoto thyroiditis . It is mostly a B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma, diffuse large B-cell or MALT type, presenting as a rapidly enlarging, painless, firm goitre with compression, often in a hypothyroid patient. It is diagnosed by core or open biopsy with immunohistochemistry and flow cytometry, and treated with chemotherapy and radiotherapy, surgery being limited to biopsy or relief of the airway. The outlook is good."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s5-7","Metastases to the thyroid","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroid cancer","Metastases reach the thyroid from the kidney, breast, lung, melanoma and head and neck squamous carcinoma."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s6-1","Extent of thyroidectomy","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Treating differentiated cancer","Thyroid cancer incidence has risen sharply over three decades while mortality has changed much less, a sign that imaging finds many indolent cancers. Lobectomy with isthmusectomy is enough for low-risk, unifocal, intrathyroidal tumours up to about 4 cm without nodes or extrathyroidal extension. A solitary papillary microcarcinoma under 1 cm without adverse features needs only hemithyroidectomy without TSH suppression, and active surveillance is an option in selected patients. NIFTP (non-invasive follicular thyroid neoplasm with papillary-like nuclear features) is now regarded as benign and needs no further treatment after lobectomy. Total thyroidectomy is needed for tumours over 4 cm, gross extrathyroidal extension, nodal or distant metastases, bilateral disease, previous irradiation, and whenever radioiodine is planned. Completion thyroidectomy follows when lobectomy histology shows a higher-risk tumour. Where the books differ · Size cut-offs and lobectomy Dhingra Microcarcinomas under 1.5 cm and intrathyroidal tumours without nodes may have lobectomy; follicular neoplasms over 4 cm in the elderly go straight to total thyroidectomy; no elective neck dissection. Lee Following the 2015 ATA guidelines, lobectomy suffices for low-risk intrathyroidal tumours up to 4 cm; total thyroidectomy for larger, extrathyroidal or metastatic disease or when radioiodine is planned; prophylactic central dissection is debated. In practice follow the ATA approach, which uses risk stratification rather than size alone: lobectomy for low-risk intrathyroidal tumours up to 4 cm without nodes, tota"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s6-2","The neck","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Treating differentiated cancer","Clinically involved central nodes are removed by a therapeutic central (level VI) dissection . A prophylactic central dissection is debated; it is considered for T3 and T4 tumours and when lateral nodes are involved, at the cost of more hypoparathyroidism. Lateral nodes proven on FNA are treated by a compartmental selective neck dissection of levels II to IV, with V as needed, and never by \"berry picking\"."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s6-3","Invasive disease","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Treating differentiated cancer","A functioning recurrent nerve with tumour on its surface is shaved and kept, while a paralysed or encased nerve is resected. Involved strap muscles are removed en bloc, and invasion of the trachea, larynx or oesophagus is treated by shave or full-thickness window or sleeve resection according to its depth."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s6-4","After surgery","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Treating differentiated cancer","Radioactive iodine (I-131) ablates the thyroid remnant and treats microscopic or metastatic disease. It is given for high-risk disease (tumour over 4 cm, extrathyroidal extension, distant metastases), selectively for intermediate risk, and can usually be omitted in low-risk disease: in the ESTIMABL2 trial, low-risk patients followed without radioiodine did as well as those ablated. A low ablation dose (1.1 GBq) is as effective as a high dose (3.7 GBq) for low- and intermediate-risk patients. The patient is prepared with recombinant human TSH (which has largely replaced thyroxine withdrawal) and a low-iodine diet, and iodinated contrast is avoided beforehand. Its side effects are sialadenitis, xerostomia, taste change, nasolacrimal duct obstruction and second cancers. TSH suppression with levothyroxine aims for a TSH below 0.1 mU/L in high-risk disease and a low-normal level in low-risk disease. At 9 to 12 months, dynamic risk stratification using thyroglobulin, ultrasound and imaging re-grades each patient's risk and guides how hard TSH is suppressed and how closely they are followed. Surveillance follows thyroglobulin, with its antibodies, against the baseline, together with neck ultrasound, diagnostic radioiodine scans, and PET-CT for thyroglobulin-positive, iodine-negative disease. External beam radiotherapy treats unresectable or gross residual disease and tracheal invasion in older patients, and iodine-refractory progressive disease is treated with kinase inhibitors such as lenvatinib and sorafenib, and targeted agents for RET, NTRK and BRAF changes."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s7-1","Types","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroidectomy","Operations on the thyroid Operation What is removed Typical use Lobectomy (hemithyroidectomy) One lobe with the isthmus Benign nodule, follicular neoplasm, low-risk cancer, toxic adenoma Isthmusectomy The isthmus Isthmic nodule; biopsy and airway relief in anaplastic carcinoma or lymphoma Subtotal thyroidectomy Both lobes leaving a small posterior remnant Formerly Graves and multinodular goitre Near-total thyroidectomy One lobe totally, the other leaving under 1 g near the nerve and parathyroid Cancer, Graves, goitre Total thyroidectomy Both lobes and isthmus Cancer, Graves, large multinodular goitre Completion thyroidectomy The remaining lobe after lobectomy Lobectomy histology shows cancer needing total thyroidectomy"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s7-2","Indications and preparation","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroidectomy","The indications are the four Cs plus one: cancer, suspicion of cancer, compression, cosmesis, and hyperthyroidism. Before operating, the patient should be euthyroid; calcium and vitamin D are measured, the larynx is examined, the thyroid and neck are scanned by ultrasound with FNA, CT is obtained for retrosternal extension, calcitonin is measured when medullary cancer is suspected, and the usual anaesthetic assessment is made."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s7-3","Technique","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroidectomy","Incision: a collar incision in a skin crease about two fingerbreadths above the sternal notch. Flaps: subplatysmal flaps raised. Strap muscles: separated in the midline and lifted off the gland. Lateral mobilisation: the middle vein divided, and the gland rotated medially. Superior pole: the vessels divided individually on the capsule, in the avascular cricothyroid (Reeve) space, to spare the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. The recurrent nerve: identified and traced to its laryngeal entry. Nerve monitoring confirms and protects it, and loss of signal on the first side may stop a planned bilateral operation. Parathyroids: each gland identified and kept on its blood supply by dividing inferior thyroid artery branches on the capsule; a devascularised gland is confirmed by frozen section, minced and reimplanted into sternomastoid. Berry's ligament: divided carefully; cautery near the nerve is dangerous here. Closure: haemostasis checked with a Valsalva manoeuvre, then straps and platysma closed. Drains are optional; they do not prevent haematoma. Minimal-access approaches: video-assisted and remote-access robotic routes for selected patients. Figure 9 The thyroid with the strap muscles reflected, and a cross-section of the neck at the level of the thyroid. From the anatomy atlas"],["ev","cl:t-thyroid~s7-4","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre: current evidence","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroidectomy","The 2023 Bethesda system gives a single name to each cytology category, average risks of malignancy, and terminology aligned with the 2022 WHO classification (\"oncocytic\" for Hürthle cell) (Ali et al., Thyroid 2023). In the ESTIMABL2 trial of 776 patients with low-risk differentiated thyroid cancer after total thyroidectomy, omitting radioiodine was non-inferior to ablation at three years (95.6% vs 95.9% event-free) (Leboulleux et al., N Engl J Med 2022); the HiLo and ESTIMABL trials had already shown a low ablation dose (1.1 GBq) works as well as a high dose. The 2015 ATA guidelines set out risk-adapted surgery (lobectomy for low-risk tumours up to 4 cm), radioiodine and TSH suppression (Haugen et al., Thyroid 2016), and the British Thyroid Association guidelines (Perros et al., Clin Endocrinol 2014) define the U and Thy grading used in the UK."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s7-4","Complications","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · Thyroidectomy","Haematoma complicates under 2% of thyroidectomies, usually within hours, and is the complication that kills if it is not acted on at once. Neck haematoma after thyroidectomy Swelling, tight neck, stridor or distress: Open the skin and the strap muscles at the bedside. Evacuate the clot. Secure the airway. Return to theatre. Venous and lymphatic congestion makes the larynx oedematous, so intubation is difficult. Release of pressure comes first. Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury is temporary in up to about 5 to 10% and permanent in about 1%, with a higher risk in reoperation, cancer and retrosternal goitre. Unilateral injury causes hoarseness and aspiration ( Vocal fold paralysis ). Bilateral injury may leave a near-normal voice but causes stridor on extubation, often needing reintubation and tracheostomy. Injury to the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve costs high pitch and vocal projection, which matters greatly to singers. Hypocalcaemia from hypoparathyroidism is common and transient after total thyroidectomy and permanent in about 1 to 3% in expert hands, more after central dissection. It appears at 24 to 72 hours with perioral and fingertip tingling, cramps, Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs, tetany, laryngospasm and a long QT. Calcium and PTH are measured after surgery, and hypocalcaemia is treated with oral calcium and calcitriol, or intravenous calcium gluconate when severe ( Hyperparathyroidism ). Thyroid storm, rare tracheomalacia, expected hypothyroidism after total thyroidectomy, wound infection, seroma, keloid, pneumothorax, and chyle leak after latera"],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s8-1","Viva essentials","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Surgical anatomy: Berry's ligament; the course of the RLN on each side, and the non-recurrent nerve; the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve; the parathyroids and their blood supply. Lingual thyroid: scan before excision. Work-up of a nodule: TSH, ultrasound pattern, FNA, Bethesda categories; why FNA cannot diagnose follicular carcinoma. The five cancers: their spread, markers, MEN syndromes, RET testing, and phaeochromocytoma first. Extent of surgery, radioiodine, TSH suppression and thyroglobulin follow-up. Complications: haematoma (open at the bedside), nerve palsies, hypocalcaemia."],["sec","cl:t-thyroid~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating for medullary carcinoma without excluding phaeochromocytoma. Calling a cystic lateral neck node in a young adult a branchial cyst without considering metastatic papillary carcinoma. Excising a lingual thyroid without checking for other thyroid tissue. Giving iodinated contrast just before planned radioiodine. Failing to check the cords before and after surgery."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s1-1","Definition","Approach to tinnitus · Overview","The perception of sound, ringing, hissing, buzzing or roaring, in the absence of an external source."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s1-2","Epidemiology","Approach to tinnitus · Overview","Between 10 and 15% of adults have tinnitus, but only about one in thirty find it at least moderately annoying and about one in two hundred are severely affected. Most have some hearing loss, often age-related or noise-induced. About half of those with troublesome tinnitus also have reduced tolerance of sound."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s1-3","Why it matters","Approach to tinnitus · Overview","It can be the first symptom of a vestibular schwannoma, a glomus tumour or a dural arteriovenous fistula. And severe tinnitus disrupts sleep, concentration and mood."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s2-1","Subjective tinnitus","Approach to tinnitus · Classification","Heard only by the patient: by far the commonest."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s2-2","Objective tinnitus","Approach to tinnitus · Classification","Can be heard by the examiner with a stethoscope or a Toynbee tube. Vascular: pulsatile, from arterial or venous flow. Muscular: clicking from palatal myoclonus, or from spasm of the stapedius or tensor tympani. Patulous Eustachian tube: a blowing sound with breathing. Temporomandibular joint: clicks with jaw movement."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s2-3","A normal phenomenon","Approach to tinnitus · Classification","Sudden brief unilateral tapering tinnitus , a few seconds of ringing in one ear with fullness, happens to most people and needs no investigation."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s3-1","Otological","Approach to tinnitus · Causes","Wax, middle ear fluid, otitis media , otosclerosis , Ménière's disease , presbycusis and noise-induced hearing loss ( Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease ), sudden hearing loss , ototoxicity and vestibular schwannoma ."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s3-2","General","Approach to tinnitus · Causes","Anaemia, hypertension, thyroid disease, hyperlipidaemia, vitamin B12 deficiency, head injury and multiple sclerosis."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s3-3","Drugs","Approach to tinnitus · Causes","Aspirin and other salicylates, quinine, aminoglycosides, loop diuretics and some chemotherapy. Most drug-induced tinnitus reverses when the drug is stopped."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s3-4","Pulsatile tinnitus","Approach to tinnitus · Causes","Causes of pulsatile tinnitus Arterial Carotid stenosis, dissection, aneurysm aberrant internal carotid artery, persistent stapedial artery Venous High or dehiscent jugular bulb sigmoid sinus diverticulum or dehiscence ( Superior canal dehiscence ), idiopathic intracranial hypertension Tumour Glomus tympanicum or jugulare ( Middle ear and temporal bone tumours ) other vascular tumours ( Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies ) Shunt Dural arteriovenous fistula arteriovenous malformation High output Anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy conductive hearing loss makes body sounds louder"],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s3-5","How it arises","Approach to tinnitus · Causes","Reduced input from a damaged cochlea leads to reorganisation and increased spontaneous activity in the central auditory pathways. Everyone has some random activity in these pathways: in a silent room most people soon hear a faint hum or ringing. Normally, subconscious filters ignore it. When the brain labels the sound as a threat, the limbic and autonomic systems respond, attention is drawn to it, and a vicious circle of awareness, anxiety and amplification follows. This model explains why the loudness of tinnitus predicts distress so poorly, and why treatment aims at the reaction rather than the sound."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s3-6","Disorders of sound tolerance","Approach to tinnitus · Causes","Hyperacusis is intolerance of everyday sounds that others find comfortable, often with tinnitus. Misophonia is a strong emotional reaction to specific sounds, often mouth sounds such as chewing, regardless of loudness. Phonophobia is fear of particular sounds, and recruitment is the abnormally rapid growth of loudness in a cochlear hearing loss. Facial palsy , by removing the stapedial reflex, can cause hyperacusis. Sound tolerance is treated with explanation, graded sound exposure and cognitive behavioural therapy; ear plugs worn constantly make it worse."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s4-1","History","Approach to tinnitus · Assessment","Is it in one ear or both? Is it pulsatile? Is there hearing loss, vertigo or fullness? Noise exposure and drugs? How much does it disturb sleep, work and mood? Ask about mood Severe tinnitus is linked with anxiety and depression. Ask directly how the patient is coping, including any thoughts of self-harm, and arrange support where needed."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s4-2","Examination","Approach to tinnitus · Assessment","Otoscopy under the microscope (a red mass behind the drum, a blue drum, wax), tuning forks, cranial nerves, and auscultation over the ear, orbit and neck. Pressing on the carotid stops arterial tinnitus; pressing on the jugular vein or turning the head stops a venous hum. A rhythm with breathing points to a patulous tube; clicks with palatal movement mean myoclonus."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s4-3","Investigations","Approach to tinnitus · Assessment","4.3.1 Audiometry Pure-tone and speech audiometry, tympanometry ( Hearing tests: how to do and read them ), and tinnitus matching for pitch and loudness. Loudness and pitch do not predict how distressing it is. 4.3.2 Imaging One-sided tinnitus or asymmetric hearing: MRI of the internal auditory canals. Pulsatile tinnitus: CT angiography, MR angiography and venography; formal angiography if a dural fistula is suspected. Palatal myoclonus: MRI of the posterior fossa. 4.3.3 Blood tests As suggested by the history: full blood count, thyroid function, lipids, glucose, B12. 4.3.4 Long-duration tympanometry Shows rhythmic compliance changes in myoclonus or a patulous tube."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-1","Treat the cause","Approach to tinnitus · Management","Remove wax, treat otitis media, operate on otosclerosis, stop ototoxic drugs where possible, and treat anaemia or thyroid disease."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-2","Hearing aids","Approach to tinnitus · Management","The first step whenever there is hearing loss ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants ): restoring external sound reduces the contrast with the tinnitus."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-3","Explanation and counselling","Approach to tinnitus · Management","Explaining that tinnitus is common, real and not a sign of a brain tumour (once excluded) relieves much of the distress."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-4","Sound therapy","Approach to tinnitus · Management","5.4.1 Masking Background sound, a bedside sound generator or a wearable masker, alone or combined with a hearing aid. 5.4.2 Tinnitus retraining therapy Directive counselling plus low-level broadband sound, aiming at habituation: the tinnitus is still there but no longer noticed or disturbing. Where the books differ · Retraining sound exposure Dhingra At least 8 hours a day for 18 to 24 months. Lee Up to 16 hours a day for about a year. In practice in the Tinnitus Retraining Therapy Trial, TRT was no more effective than standard care with counselling, so sound therapy is now offered as part of stepped care rather than as a fixed long programme (Tinnitus Retraining Therapy Trial Research Group, JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019)."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-5","Psychological therapy","Approach to tinnitus · Management","Cognitive behavioural therapy has the strongest evidence: a Cochrane review of 28 trials found it reduces the impact of tinnitus on quality of life, with low-certainty evidence, though not its loudness (Fuller et al., 2020). NICE (NG155) recommends stepping up from digital tinnitus-specific CBT to group and then individual CBT. Relaxation and biofeedback also help. Treat anxiety, depression and insomnia, and refer immediately anyone at risk of suicide."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-6","Specific objective causes","Approach to tinnitus · Management","Dural arteriovenous fistula: embolisation, surgery or radiosurgery; fistulas with cortical venous drainage can bleed. Palatal myoclonus: muscle relaxants or botulinum toxin. Stapedial or tensor spasm: muscle relaxants, or division of the tendon. Patulous tube: see Eustachian tube disorders ."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-7","Treatments without proven benefit","Approach to tinnitus · Management","No drug reliably suppresses tinnitus, and betahistine is not recommended for it. Transcranial magnetic stimulation and implanted stimulators remain investigational. A cochlear implant often reduces tinnitus in patients who qualify for it because of their hearing loss."],["ev","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-8","Approach to tinnitus: current evidence","Approach to tinnitus · Management","Guidelines. NICE (NG155, 2020) recommends a full audiological assessment, immediate referral for tinnitus with suicidal risk or with acute neurological signs, and imaging for pulsatile or unilateral tinnitus when indicated; it offers psychological therapies (digital CBT first, then group and individual CBT) and hearing aids for those with hearing loss, and advises against drugs, dietary supplements, transcranial magnetic stimulation and electrical stimulation. The American guideline (Tunkel et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2014) recommends against routine imaging for non-pulsatile, symmetrical tinnitus and against drugs and supplements, and recommends CBT. A European multidisciplinary guideline reached the same conclusions (Cima et al., HNO 2019). Bimodal neuromodulation. Sound combined with tongue stimulation reduced tinnitus handicap in uncontrolled trials (Conlon et al., Sci Transl Med 2020); without a sham control its place remains uncertain."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s5-8","Stepped care","Approach to tinnitus · Management","Severity is measured with a validated questionnaire such as the Tinnitus Handicap Inventory or the Tinnitus Functional Index. Most patients need only explanation, hearing care and self-help; those with greater distress move to audiologist-led tinnitus support and sound therapy, and then to cognitive behavioural therapy delivered by a psychologist, with access to mental health services for those at risk."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~c6","Pathway","Approach to tinnitus · Pathway","From symptom to plan. Tinnitus 1 Pulsatile? yes, audible to examiner or not otoscopy, auscultation, CT angiography or MR angiography and venography young woman with obesity and headache fundoscopy — idiopathic intracranial hypertension red mass behind the drum glomus — imaging 2 Unilateral or asymmetric hearing? yes MRI of the internal auditory meatus sudden hearing loss with it treat as sudden sensorineural loss 3 Bilateral, non-pulsatile audiogram treat wax, effusion, otosclerosis hearing aids for hearing loss 4 Distress explanation and self-help sound therapy and tinnitus counselling cognitive behavioural therapy urgent help if risk of self-harm"],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~c7","Prognosis","Approach to tinnitus · Prognosis","Most get better with time. About a quarter of patients find their tinnitus nearly resolves, half improve, and a quarter stay the same. Habituation is the realistic goal. Pearl Most people adapt to tinnitus. What a clinician says in the first consultation strongly shapes how well they do."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s8-1","Viva essentials","Approach to tinnitus · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Subjective versus objective tinnitus and examples of each. Causes of pulsatile tinnitus, and how to investigate them. Unilateral tinnitus: MRI for schwannoma. Tinnitus retraining therapy and the aim of habituation. Hyperacusis and misophonia. Stepped care and cognitive behavioural therapy."],["sec","cl:t-tinnitus~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Approach to tinnitus · For the examination","Common pitfalls Telling a patient that nothing can be done. Missing pulsatile tinnitus because nobody asked. Forgetting to image one-sided tinnitus. Ignoring the patient's mood."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s1-1","The normal drum","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Overview","A three-layered membrane: outer squamous epithelium, a middle fibrous layer, and inner mucosa. The fibrous layer gives it strength and shape; when it is lost after healing, the drum becomes thin and floppy. On otoscopy the normal right drum shows the handle of malleus, the lateral process and a bright cone of light at five o'clock. Figure 1 A normal right tympanic membrane, with the cone of light at five o'clock. Dhingra, fig. 8.17 Figure 2 A normal tympanic membrane seen through the otoscope, and its parts. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s1-2","Conditions covered","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Overview","Traumatic perforation. Retraction, atelectasis and retraction pockets. Atrophic drum and tympanosclerosis. Barotrauma of the middle ear."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s2-1","Causes","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Traumatic perforation","Direct: cotton buds, hairpins, matchsticks, careless syringing or instrument use. Pressure: a slap on the ear, blast injury, a fall onto water, a kiss on the ear. Temporal bone fracture ( Temporal bone fracture and CSF otorrhoea ). Thermal: welding sparks or hot slag, which heal poorly."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s2-2","Clinical picture","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Traumatic perforation","Sudden pain, a little bleeding, deafness and tinnitus. The perforation is irregular or slit-like, often with blood clot and with its edges turned inwards. Figure 3 A traumatic perforation from blast, with ecchymosis (white arrow head) and tears of the drum (black arrows). Dhingra, fig. 8.19 Look deeper Vertigo, sensorineural hearing loss or facial weakness suggests stapes subluxation, perilymph fistula ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis ) or a temporal bone fracture. Arrange urgent assessment and consider exploration."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s2-3","Management","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Traumatic perforation","2.3.1 Assess Tuning forks, nystagmus and the facial nerve; an audiogram when practical. CT if a fracture is suspected. 2.3.2 Keep dry, keep out No drops, no syringing and no water in the ear. Antibiotics only if the ear becomes infected. 2.3.3 Repair Most perforations heal within weeks. Large ones with in-turned edges may be repositioned and splinted with a paper patch under the microscope. Those that persist beyond about three months are repaired by myringoplasty ( Ear and mastoid operations: terms and principles )."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s2-4","Complications","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Traumatic perforation","Persistent perforation, infection, ossicular dislocation, perilymph fistula, and implantation cholesteatoma from squamous epithelium driven into the middle ear."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s3-1","Why it happens","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Retraction and atelectasis","When the Eustachian tube fails ( Eustachian tube disorders ), middle ear gas is absorbed and negative pressure draws the drum inwards. Healed or atrophic areas, which lack the fibrous layer, retract first."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s3-2","Signs of a retracted drum","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Retraction and atelectasis","A dull drum with a broken or absent light reflex. A prominent lateral process of the malleus. A foreshortened handle of malleus. Prominent anterior and posterior malleolar folds. Figure 4 Features of a retracted tympanic membrane. Dhingra, fig. 8.18"],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s3-3","Retraction pockets and atelectasis","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Retraction and atelectasis","Stages of pars tensa retraction Stage Appearance I Retracted, not touching the incus II Touching the incus III Lying on the promontory (atelectasis), can still be lifted IV Adherent to the promontory (adhesive otitis), no middle ear space Figure 5 First, an atrophic drum with a retraction pocket and an adhesion from the malleus. Second, atelectasis with retraction pockets in the attic (white arrow) and the pars tensa (black arrow). Dhingra, figs 8.20 and 8.21"],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s3-4","Management","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Retraction and atelectasis","Treat the cause of tubal failure (adenoids, sinusitis, allergy). A grommet re-aerates the middle ear while the drum can still be lifted (stage III). Deep pockets that collect keratin, or that cannot be cleaned, are excised and reinforced with cartilage before they become cholesteatoma ( Cholesteatoma )."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s4-1","Atrophic drum","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Atrophy and tympanosclerosis","A thin, transparent segment without a fibrous layer, after healed perforations or long-standing effusion. It moves excessively and retracts easily."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s4-2","Tympanosclerosis","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Atrophy and tympanosclerosis","Chalky white hyaline and calcified plaques in the fibrous layer of the drum and sometimes in the middle ear around the ossicles, after recurrent otitis media or grommets. Plaques on the drum usually cause no symptoms; deposits around the ossicles can fix them and cause a conductive loss that is difficult to correct surgically. Figure 6 Tympanosclerosis with a central perforation: chalky plaques lie in front of and behind the hole. Dhingra, fig. 8.22"],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s5-1","Mechanism","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Barotrauma","On descent in an aircraft or when diving, outside pressure rises. If the Eustachian tube does not open, middle ear pressure falls relative to it. Once the difference reaches about 90 mm Hg, the soft tissues at the pharyngeal end of the tube are pushed together and the tube locks , so swallowing and Valsalva can no longer open it. A cold, allergy or sleeping through descent make this more likely."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s5-2","Clinical picture","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Barotrauma","Severe pain, deafness, tinnitus and sometimes vertigo. The drum is retracted and congested, with haemorrhage into it, and later an effusion or blood (haemotympanum), or a perforation."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s5-3","Inner ear barotrauma","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Barotrauma","Forceful Valsalva against a locked tube can rupture the round or oval window, causing sensorineural loss and vertigo: a perilymph fistula that may need exploration."],["ev","cl:t-tmperf~s5-4","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae: current evidence","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Barotrauma","Spontaneous healing. In a prospective series of traumatic perforations, most healed spontaneously within three months; small perforations closed faster than large ones, and a dry ear healed better than a wet one (Lou et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2011). In a randomised trial of perforations with inverted or everted edges, healing rates were similar with observation, gelfoam patching, and edge approximation plus patching, but closure was faster with intervention (Lou et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2011). Retraction pockets. Grommets, cartilage reinforcement or excision are used for progressive or unstable pockets, but there are no good randomised trials."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s5-4","Treatment","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · Barotrauma","Mild cases: decongestant drops or spray, analgesia and gentle Valsalva. Persistent effusion or failure: politzerisation or myringotomy, and a grommet for recurrent problems. Prevention Avoid flying with a cold, use a decongestant spray before descent, stay awake on descent, and swallow, yawn or perform Valsalva repeatedly."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s6-1","Viva essentials","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Traumatic perforation: causes, keep dry, most heal. Red flags: vertigo, sensorineural loss, facial weakness. Signs of a retracted drum. Retraction staging, and when a grommet helps. Barotrauma: the locked tube at about 90 mm Hg, treatment and prevention."],["sec","cl:t-tmperf~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Tympanic membrane: trauma, retraction, barotrauma and sequelae · For the examination","Common pitfalls Syringing or putting drops into an ear with a fresh traumatic perforation. Missing a perilymph fistula in a patient with vertigo after a slap or a dive. Ignoring a deep retraction pocket until it becomes a cholesteatoma."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s1-1","Position and surfaces","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Applied anatomy","Each tonsil is an ovoid mass of lymphoid tissue in the lateral wall of the oropharynx, in the tonsillar fossa between the anterior (palatoglossal) and posterior (palatopharyngeal) pillars. It is larger than it looks, extending up into the soft palate, down into the base of tongue and forwards under the anterior pillar. Medial surface: non-keratinising stratified squamous epithelium dipping into 12 to 15 crypts ; the largest, near the upper pole, is the crypta magna (intratonsillar cleft), the remnant of the second pharyngeal pouch. Crypts fill with cheesy debris that can be expressed by pressing on the anterior pillar. Lateral surface: a well-defined fibrous capsule , separated from the bed by loose areolar tissue: the plane of dissection at tonsillectomy and the space where pus collects in a quinsy. Upper pole: reaches into the soft palate under a semilunar fold enclosing the supratonsillar fossa . Lower pole: attached to the tongue, separated from it by the tonsillolingual sulcus , a hidden site for carcinoma. Figure 1 Primary and secondary crypts of the tonsil, with the crypta magna. Dhingra, fig. 51.1 Figure 2 Tonsillar crypts and blood vessels. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s1-2","Bed and relations","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Applied anatomy","The bed is formed by the superior constrictor and styloglossus. The glossopharyngeal nerve and an elongated styloid process may lie in the lower part of the fossa and can be reached through it after tonsillectomy. Beyond the constrictor lie the facial artery, submandibular gland, posterior belly of digastric, medial pterygoid and the angle of the mandible; the internal carotid artery lies about 2.5 cm behind and lateral to the tonsil. Figure 3 Relations of the tonsil: capsule (1), loose areolar tissue with the paratonsillar vein (2), superior constrictor (3), styloglossus (4), glossopharyngeal nerve (5), facial artery (6), medial pterygoid (7), angle of mandible (8), submandibular gland (9). Dhingra, fig. 51.2 Figure 4 The bed of the tonsil in horizontal section. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s1-3","Blood supply","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Applied anatomy","Five arteries: the tonsillar branch of the facial artery (the main supply, entering at the lower pole), the ascending palatine (facial), the ascending pharyngeal, the dorsal lingual branches (lingual) and the descending palatine (maxillary). Veins drain to the paratonsillar (external palatine) vein , which descends from the soft palate across the lateral surface of the capsule to the common facial vein and pharyngeal plexus; it is the usual source of bleeding at tonsillectomy. Figure 5 Arterial supply of the tonsil. Dhingra, fig. 51.3 Figure 6 Arterial supply of the palatine tonsil, and the paratonsillar vein with the tonsillar arteries. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s1-4","Lymph and nerves","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Applied anatomy","Lymphatics pierce the superior constrictor to the jugulodigastric (tonsillar) node below the angle of the mandible. Sensation comes from the glossopharyngeal nerve and lesser palatine nerves; hence the earache of tonsillitis and after tonsillectomy."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s1-5","Function","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Applied anatomy","The crypts and their specialised epithelium (M cells, antigen-presenting cells) bring inhaled and swallowed antigens to B-cell follicles that make antibody, and alert the wider immune system. The tonsils are most active between four and ten years and involute after puberty. Removal has not been shown to impair immunity, but should still be done only for a clear indication."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s2-1","Types","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Acute tonsillitis","Catarrhal (superficial): part of a generalised, usually viral, pharyngitis. Follicular: pus in the crypts appears as yellow spots at their openings. Parenchymatous: the whole tonsil is uniformly swollen and red. Membranous: exudate from the crypts coalesces into a membrane over the surface. Figure 7 First, acute follicular tonsillitis. Second, acute membranous tonsillitis. Dhingra, figs 51.4 and 51.5"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s2-2","Cause","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Acute tonsillitis","School children mostly, and young adults; uncommon in infants and over fifty. Group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus is the commonest bacterial cause; staphylococci, pneumococci and H. influenzae also, often after a virus. Most episodes overall are viral; viral and chronic pharyngitis are covered in Acute and chronic pharyngitis ."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s2-3","Clinical features","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Acute tonsillitis","Sore throat, painful swallowing (a child may refuse food), fever of 38 to 40°C with rigors, referred earache or true otitis media, malaise, and in children abdominal pain from mesenteric adenitis that can mimic appendicitis. The breath is foul, the tongue coated, the pillars and palate red; the tonsils are swollen with spots of pus or a membrane that wipes off easily; the jugulodigastric nodes are enlarged and tender. Figure 8 Acute follicular tonsillitis: pus beads on the left tonsil; on the right they have coalesced into a membrane. Dhingra, fig. 51.6"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s2-4","Treatment","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Acute tonsillitis","Rest, fluids and analgesia (paracetamol or ibuprofen; aspirin is avoided in children). For bacterial tonsillitis, penicillin for five to ten days, the full ten where rheumatic fever is common (erythromycin in allergy; who needs an antibiotic is covered in Acute and chronic pharyngitis ); amoxicillin is best avoided when glandular fever is possible."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s2-5","Complications","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Acute tonsillitis","Chronic tonsillitis with recurrent attacks. Peritonsillar abscess, parapharyngeal abscess, cervical abscess from suppurating nodes. Acute otitis media. Rheumatic fever, acute glomerulonephritis, and endocarditis in valvular heart disease."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~c3","The membrane on the tonsil","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · The membrane on the tonsil","A differential that matters. Membrane over the tonsil Condition Clues Membranous tonsillitis Abrupt onset, high fever; membrane on the tonsil only, wipes off easily Diphtheria Slower onset, low fever, toxic child; dirty grey membrane spreading beyond the tonsil to palate and pharynx, adherent, bleeds when removed; bull neck Vincent angina Insidious, little fever; membrane usually on one tonsil, lifting off to leave an irregular ulcer; fusiform bacilli and spirochaetes on smear Infectious mononucleosis Young adult; both tonsils large and coated; posterior cervical nodes, spleen; atypical lymphocytes, positive Paul–Bunnell (Monospot) test Agranulocytosis Very ill; necrotic ulcers across the oropharynx; neutrophils profoundly reduced Leukaemia Anaemia, bleeding gums; blast cells in blood and marrow Others Aphthous ulcer, traumatic ulcer (membrane within 24 hours), candida, malignancy Investigations: total and differential counts, blood film, throat swab and culture, Monospot test, marrow examination and biopsy as indicated."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s3-1","Faucial diphtheria","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · The membrane on the tonsil","Corynebacterium diphtheriae , spread by droplets, incubation two to six days; carriers exist. A grey-white tenacious membrane forms on the tonsils and spreads to the palate and posterior wall, bleeding when lifted; tender jugulodigastric nodes give a bull neck ; the child is toxic though the fever is modest. The exotoxin damages the heart (myocarditis, arrhythmia, circulatory failure) and nerves (palatal, ocular and diaphragmatic paralysis, weeks later); laryngeal membrane obstructs the airway. Treat diphtheria on suspicion Give antitoxin without waiting for culture: 20,000 to 40,000 units if under 48 hours and confined to the tonsils, 80,000 to 120,000 units if longer or more extensive, intravenously over an hour after a test dose, with adrenaline ready. Penicillin or erythromycin to kill the organism. Isolate; discharge after three negative swabs 24 hours apart; give a booster, since infection does not confer immunity. Immunise contacts and give them erythromycin for a week."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s4-1","Causes","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Chronic tonsillitis","Incomplete resolution of acute attacks with walled-off microabscesses in the follicles, subclinical infection, and infected sinuses or teeth; mostly children and young adults."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s4-2","Types","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Chronic tonsillitis","Chronic follicular: crypts full of infected cheesy material, seen as yellow spots. Chronic parenchymatous: hyperplasia makes the tonsils very large; they may meet in the midline and obstruct speech, swallowing and breathing, with sleep apnoea and eventually cor pulmonale. Chronic fibroid: small, infected tonsils with recurrent sore throats. Figure 9 Parenchymatous tonsillitis: the two tonsils almost touch. Dhingra, fig. 51.7"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s4-3","Features","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Chronic tonsillitis","Recurrent sore throats, chronic irritation and cough, bad taste and halitosis, and with large tonsils thick speech, difficulty swallowing and choking at night. Flushing of the anterior pillars compared with the surrounding mucosa and persistent enlargement of the jugulodigastric nodes are reliable signs; pressure on the pillar may express pus. Tonsil size grading Grade 0 tonsils are within the fossa; grade 1 occupy under 25% of the oropharyngeal airway; grade 2, 25 to 50%; grade 3, 50 to 75%; grade 4, more than 75% (\"kissing\" tonsils)."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s4-4","Local complications","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Chronic tonsillitis","Intratonsillar abscess: a blocked crypt fills with pus; a red, swollen, very painful tonsil; antibiotics, drainage, and later tonsillectomy. Tonsillolith: a calcified crypt plug felt as a gritty mass, removed or treated by tonsillectomy. Tonsillar cyst: a yellow swelling from a blocked crypt, often symptomless, easily drained. Figure 10 A tonsillar cyst on the left (arrow). Dhingra, fig. 51.8"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s4-5","Lingual tonsil","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Chronic tonsillitis","Acute lingual tonsillitis gives a one-sided lump feeling and dysphagia; compensatory hypertrophy follows tonsillectomy and causes a lump sensation, thick voice and cough; a lingual tonsil abscess causes severe dysphagia, salivation, painful protrusion and risks laryngeal oedema."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s4-6","Periodic fever (PFAPA)","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Chronic tonsillitis","In young children, recurrent episodes every three to eight weeks of fever, aphthous ulcers, pharyngitis and cervical adenitis lasting a few days, with complete health in between. Cyclic neutropenia must be excluded. A single dose of steroid aborts attacks; tonsillectomy is curative in most."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s5-1","Definition and cause","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Peritonsillar abscess","Pus in the peritonsillar space between the tonsil capsule and the superior constrictor. Usually a crypt, often the crypta magna, becomes blocked and forms an intratonsillar abscess that bursts through the capsule; some arise from the mucous (Weber) glands of the supratonsillar fossa. Cultures are usually mixed aerobic ( Streptococcus pyogenes , Staphylococcus aureus ) and anaerobic."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s5-2","Clinical features","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Peritonsillar abscess","Mostly young adults, usually one-sided. General: fever to 40°C, rigors, malaise, headache. Local: severe one-sided throat pain; odynophagia so marked that saliva dribbles from the mouth and the patient becomes dehydrated; a muffled hot-potato voice ; foul breath; ipsilateral earache; and trismus from spasm of the medial pterygoid. Examination findings in quinsy Sign Explanation Bulging of the soft palate and anterior pillar above the tonsil Pus in the supratonsillar part of the space Uvula swollen and pushed to the opposite side Mass effect Tonsil buried in oedematous pillars, pushed down and medially Displacement Mucopus over the tonsil, tender jugulodigastric nodes Infection Neck tilted to the affected side (torticollis) Muscle spasm Figure 11 A left peritonsillar abscess. Dhingra, fig. 52.4 Trismus is the key Trismus is the best single indicator of an abscess rather than peritonsillar cellulitis. If you are unsure, aspirate with a needle: pus settles the question and gives a culture."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s5-3","Investigation","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Peritonsillar abscess","Diagnosis is clinical. Needle aspiration confirms pus. Contrast-enhanced CT when the diagnosis is uncertain, trismus prevents examination, or spread to the parapharyngeal space is suspected."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s5-4","Treatment","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Peritonsillar abscess","Admit; intravenous fluids. High-dose intravenous antibiotics covering aerobes and anaerobes (penicillin with metronidazole, or co-amoxiclav; in trials, adding metronidazole to penicillin brought side effects without clear benefit); a single dose of steroid reduces pain and trismus. Analgesia; avoid aspirin. Saline or peroxide mouthwashes. Drain the pus: needle aspiration, or incision at the point of maximum bulge, which lies just lateral to where a horizontal line through the base of the uvula meets a vertical line along the anterior pillar. A guarded blade makes a small stab and sinus forceps open the cavity; the forceps may be reinserted the next day. The procedure step by step is in Quinsy drainage, tongue-tie, lip biopsy and drooling . Tonsillectomy: an interval tonsillectomy four to six weeks later is advised after a second quinsy in adults (recurrence is about 17%), and more readily in children or those with recurrent tonsillitis. Quinsy (hot) tonsillectomy during the acute illness is preferred by some, at the cost of more bleeding and the risk of rupture at induction. Figure 12 The site of drainage: just lateral to the junction of a vertical line along the anterior pillar and a horizontal line through the base of the uvula. Dhingra, fig. 52.5"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s5-5","Complications","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Peritonsillar abscess","Parapharyngeal abscess (a quinsy is a potential parapharyngeal abscess; Deep neck space infection ), laryngeal oedema needing tracheostomy, aspiration pneumonia or lung abscess after spontaneous rupture, septicaemia with endocarditis, nephritis or brain abscess, internal jugular vein thrombosis (Lemierre syndrome), and haemorrhage from erosion of the carotid or jugular vessels."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s6-1","Indications","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Tonsillectomy","6.1.1 Recurrent tonsillitis (SIGN and the Paradise criteria) Seven or more well-documented, clinically significant episodes in one year, five a year for two years, or three a year for three years, with the episodes disabling and preventing normal function. Below these thresholds, watchful waiting is advised, because many children improve with age. Obstructive sleep-disordered breathing is now the commonest indication in children ( Snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea ). In adults, the NATTINA trial showed that tonsillectomy roughly halves sore-throat days over two years compared with conservative management, so adults meeting these criteria should be offered surgery (Wilson et al., Lancet 2023). 6.1.2 Absolute (the traditional list) Recurrent throat infection as above, or two weeks or more of school or work lost in a year. Peritonsillar abscess: in children four to six weeks after treatment; in adults after a second attack. Tonsillitis causing febrile seizures. Hypertrophy causing obstructive sleep apnoea, difficulty swallowing or interference with speech. Suspicion of malignancy: a one-sided enlarged tonsil may be lymphoma in a child or squamous carcinoma in an adult ( Oral and oropharyngeal cancer ); excisional biopsy. Also in the search for an unknown primary. 6.1.3 Relative Diphtheria or streptococcal carriers unresponsive to antibiotics, chronic tonsillitis with halitosis unresponsive to treatment, and recurrent streptococcal tonsillitis in valvular heart disease. 6.1.4 As part of another operation Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty, glossopharyngeal neurectomy, and removal of "],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s6-2","Contraindications","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Tonsillectomy","Haemoglobin below 10 g/dL; acute upper respiratory infection, including acute tonsillitis; children under three (relative); overt or submucous cleft palate; bleeding disorders (von Willebrand disease, haemophilia, leukaemia, purpura, aplastic anaemia); uncontrolled diabetes, hypertension, heart disease or asthma; and, traditionally, during an epidemic of polio and during menstruation."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s6-3","Technique (dissection and snare)","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Tonsillectomy","General anaesthesia with a tube; Rose's position (head extended on a shoulder roll, head ring), avoiding hyperextension. The Boyle–Davis gag is opened and held by Draffin's bipods. The tonsil is grasped and drawn medially; the mucosa is incised where it reflects from the tonsil onto the anterior pillar, and the incision carried round the upper pole. The upper pole is freed with blunt scissors or a dissector in the plane outside the capsule, and dissection continues downwards to the lower pole. A snare is passed over the pedicle at the lower pole, tightened and the tonsil removed. A swab is pressed into the fossa; bleeding points are tied or coagulated. The other side is done in the same way. Figure 13 First, Rose's position: the neck extended over a sandbag and the head on a ring. Second, the Boyle–Davis gag held by Draffin's bipods. Dhingra, figs 94.1 and 94.3 Figure 14 First, the instruments for tonsillectomy. Second, (A) the tonsil dissected from its bed and (B) the pedicle at the lower pole cut with a snare. Dhingra, figs 94.2 and 94.4"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s6-4","Other techniques","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Tonsillectomy","Techniques are either extracapsular (total tonsillectomy) or intracapsular (partial tonsillectomy or tonsillotomy, which leaves the capsule as a biological dressing over the muscle). Cold: dissection and snare (the commonest), guillotine (largely abandoned), intracapsular tonsillotomy with a microdebrider, harmonic scalpel, cryosurgery. Hot: monopolar or bipolar electrocautery, coblation (plasma-mediated, lower temperature), KTP or CO2 laser, radiofrequency. Hot techniques reduce bleeding at operation but increase pain and secondary haemorrhage, which rises with the diathermy power used, so use the lowest effective setting. Intracapsular surgery, especially in young children with sleep-disordered breathing, gives less pain, a faster recovery and fewer bleeds, at the cost of occasional regrowth; after a cluster of child deaths from haemorrhage, Austria moved largely to intracapsular surgery for young children. There is still no consensus on the best method. Adenoidectomy , done with tonsillectomy in more than half of cases, is now often performed by suction diathermy or coblation under direct or mirror vision rather than with a blind curette. Figure 15 Tonsillectomy with coblation. Dhingra, fig. 94.5"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s6-5","Aftercare","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Tonsillectomy","Recovery position until awake; watch pulse, breathing and blood pressure, and look for bleeding or repeated swallowing. Cold fluids first, then a soft diet, then normal food early, which keeps the fossae clean; regular analgesia before meals (paracetamol and ibuprofen; not aspirin); mouthwashes. A single dose of dexamethasone at operation reduces vomiting. Routine post-operative antibiotics are not recommended. Codeine is contraindicated in children under 12, and in under-18s having surgery for sleep apnoea, because ultra-rapid metabolisers convert it to toxic levels of morphine. Pain is near-universal, peaks around days 5 to 7, and causes dehydration and readmission. Most go home the same or next day and return to work or school in about two weeks. Earache is referred pain, not otitis."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s6-6","Complications","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Tonsillectomy","6.6.1 Immediate Primary haemorrhage at operation: pressure, ties, diathermy. Reactionary haemorrhage within 24 hours: remove the clot (which stops the constrictor from clamping the vessels), apply pressure or vasoconstrictor; if it persists, return to theatre. Injury to pillars, uvula, palate, tongue, constrictor or teeth; aspiration of blood; facial oedema; surgical emphysema. 6.6.2 Delayed Secondary haemorrhage after 24 hours, most often from the fifth to tenth day, from infection and premature separation of the slough, often heralded by bloodstained saliva; it follows about 3 to 5% of operations (rates vary with definitions and technique). Admit every patient; a clot alone is observed, with antibiotics and peroxide gargles; active bleeding returns to theatre under general anaesthesia; check haemoglobin and clotting, transfuse if needed; external carotid ligation is very rarely required. Infection of the fossa, parapharyngeal abscess, otitis media. Lung complications from aspiration: atelectasis, lung abscess. Scarring of the palate and pillars; velopharyngeal insufficiency. Tonsillar remnants that become infected; compensatory lingual tonsil hypertrophy. Post-tonsillectomy bleeding A child who keeps swallowing, vomits dark blood or has a rising pulse is bleeding until proven otherwise. Children compensate until they suddenly do not: secure intravenous access, cross-match, and involve the anaesthetist early; induction in a bleeding child is a high-risk airway."],["ev","cl:t-tonsil~s6-7","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess: current evidence","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Tonsillectomy","In the NATTINA trial of 453 adults with recurrent tonsillitis, tonsillectomy halved the number of sore throat days over two years compared with conservative management (median 23 vs 30 days; adjusted rate ratio 0.53) (Wilson et al., Lancet 2023). The American guideline recommends watchful waiting below the Paradise thresholds, a single dose of intraoperative dexamethasone, no routine antibiotics, and ibuprofen and paracetamol for pain (Mitchell et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019). For children with sleep-disordered breathing, a Cochrane review found tonsillotomy probably gives a faster return to normal activity and slightly fewer early complications than tonsillectomy, with long-term outcomes uncertain (Blackshaw et al., 2020). In the Swedish national register, all hot techniques had more late haemorrhage than cold dissection with cold haemostasis (Söderman et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2015)."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s6-7","Who needs a specialist centre","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · Tonsillectomy","Mortality in more than 300,000 English paediatric tonsillectomies was about 1 in 27,000. Risk is higher in children under three with proven sleep apnoea and in those with craniofacial anomalies, Down syndrome, cerebral palsy, heart disease or bleeding disorders, who need surgery in a centre with paediatric high-dependency care."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s7-1","Viva essentials","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Anatomy: crypta magna, capsule, bed, blood supply and the paratonsillar vein. Types of acute tonsillitis and complications. Differential diagnosis of a membrane on the tonsil; diphtheria management. Quinsy: features, site of incision, and when to remove the tonsils. Indications, contraindications and complications of tonsillectomy; reactionary versus secondary haemorrhage."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Tonsillitis and peritonsillar abscess · For the examination","Common pitfalls Draining \"quinsy\" without trismus or a bulge: aspirate first. Giving amoxicillin to a young adult with glandular fever. Ignoring a one-sided enlarged tonsil in an adult. Sending home a child with a small \"warning\" bleed after tonsillectomy."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s1-1","Subsites","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Overview","Oral cavity: mucosal lip, buccal mucosa, oral tongue (anterior two-thirds), floor of mouth, upper and lower alveolus (gingiva), retromolar trigone and hard palate. Oropharynx: base of tongue, tonsil and tonsillar fossa with the pillars, soft palate and uvula (the faucial arch), and the posterior and lateral pharyngeal walls. The potentially malignant disorders, margins and adjuvant therapy for oral cancer are covered in more depth in Oral potentially malignant disorders and oral cancer . Figure 1 The subsites of the oral cavity, and the oropharynx through the open mouth. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s1-2","Epidemiology","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Overview","India carries one of the highest burdens of oral cancer in the world: lip, tongue and mouth together make up about a fifth of all male cancers there, with buccal mucosa and tongue the leading sites, driven by tobacco chewing with lime, pan and areca nut, bidi smoking and alcohol. In the West the lip is the commonest oral subsite when lip cancers are counted (many series group them with skin cancer); among intraoral sites the tongue and floor of mouth lead, and tongue cancer is rising in young adults. Oropharyngeal cancer is rising rapidly in Europe and North America because of HPV."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s1-3","Causes","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Overview","Tobacco: smoking increases oral cancer about sixfold; chewing tobacco places carcinogen at the site where the quid is held; reverse smoking (the burning end inside the mouth) causes palatal cancer. Alcohol: multiplies, rather than adds to, the effect of tobacco. Areca nut and pan: directly and through submucous fibrosis. Human papillomavirus (HPV 16): most cancers of the tonsil and base of tongue in North America; associated with oral sexual behaviour rather than tobacco. Others: chronic trauma from jagged teeth and dentures, poor oral hygiene, Plummer–Vinson syndrome, immunosuppression, sunlight (lower lip), and rare syndromes (Fanconi anaemia)."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s1-4","Field cancerisation","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Overview","Tobacco and alcohol alter the whole mucosa of the upper aerodigestive tract, so dysplasia and cancer arise in more than one place. About 15% of patients have, or will develop, a second primary in the head and neck, oesophagus or lung."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s1-5","Pathology","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Overview","Squamous cell carcinoma in about 90%; minor salivary gland carcinomas (adenoid cystic, mucoepidermoid, adenocarcinoma), lymphoma (especially tonsil and base of tongue), melanoma (palate and gum) and Kaposi sarcoma make up the rest. Verrucous carcinoma is a slow, warty, well-differentiated variant of the buccal mucosa that rarely metastasises."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s2-1","Gross forms","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Clinical picture","Exophytic, ulcerative (a non-healing ulcer with raised, rolled, everted edges, a shaggy base and induration), infiltrative, and superficially spreading (often arising in leukoplakia or erythroplakia)."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s2-2","Symptoms","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Clinical picture","An ulcer or lump that does not heal; bleeding. Pain, often referred to the ear through the lingual and auriculotemporal nerves (V3) or the glossopharyngeal nerve. Difficulty in chewing, swallowing and speaking; a hot-potato voice with tongue base tumours; loosening of teeth or ill-fitting dentures; trismus when the pterygoids or masseter are invaded; numbness of the lip from inferior alveolar nerve invasion. A lump in the neck, sometimes the only complaint. Foul breath, weight loss and, late, a fungating mass or orocutaneous fistula. Referral now A mouth ulcer or white or red patch that persists beyond three weeks, an unexplained lump in the mouth or neck, unexplained tooth mobility, persistent one-sided sore throat or earache with a normal ear: refer urgently for examination and biopsy."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s2-3","Examination","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Clinical picture","Inspect every subsite with good light, palpate the lesion for induration and depth, palpate bimanually the floor of mouth and tongue, examine the tongue base and tonsils by palpation and endoscopy, and examine both sides of the neck level by level. Cranial nerves V, VII and XII; mouth opening; teeth."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s3-1","Lip","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oral cavity cancer","The commonest oral subsite in the West when lip cancers are counted; more than 95% squamous, 95% on the lower lip , between the midline and the commissure, in older men with outdoor occupations; basal cell carcinoma is relatively commoner on the upper lip. Early blistering, crusting or ulceration on a background of actinic cheilitis. Nodes (submental and submandibular, bilateral near the midline) are late and occur in only about 10%. Figure 2 Carcinoma of the upper lip and oral commissure, with leukoplakia of the buccal mucosa. Dhingra, fig. 44.10 3.1.1 Treatment Excision with a safe margin. Defects up to a third of the lip close primarily after a V or W excision; a third to a half with lip-switch flaps (Abbe, or Estlander at the commissure); a half to two-thirds with a Karapandzic flap; larger defects with Gillies fan, Bernard–Burow or free flaps, always realigning the vermilion border. Radiotherapy is an alternative for small lesions; neck dissection for nodes. Cure exceeds 90% in early disease; upper lip and commissure tumours do worse."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s3-2","Buccal mucosa","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oral cavity cancer","Very common in India, equal in both sexes, at the angle of the mouth, along the occlusal line or in the gingivobuccal sulcus where the quid is kept; often multicentric because the whole cheek is \"condemned\". Exophytic or ulceroinfiltrative; it spreads deeply through buccinator and fat to the skin, back to the retromolar trigone and pterygoids (trismus), and up and down into the maxilla and mandible. About half have nodes (submandibular, then upper jugular). Verrucous carcinoma favours this site. Figure 3 Carcinoma of the buccal mucosa. Dhingra, fig. 44.11 3.2.1 Treatment T1 lesions by excision. Larger lesions, and any with muscle or bone involvement, by wide excision with marginal or segmental mandibulectomy or partial maxillectomy, reconstruction with local, pectoralis major or free flaps, neck dissection, and postoperative radiotherapy for stage III and IV."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s3-3","Oral tongue","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oral cavity cancer","Usually men of fifty to seventy, though increasingly young adults; may arise in leukoplakia, at a chronic dental ulcer, or in smokers and chewers. The commonest site is the middle of the lateral border and the ventral surface; the tip and dorsum less often. It appears as an exophytic papillary growth, an indurated ulcer with rolled edges, or a submucosal indurated nodule. It infiltrates the tongue musculature (fixation, slurred speech), the floor of mouth and mandible. Nodes: the lateral tongue drains to the submandibular and upper jugular nodes (and can skip to level IV), the tip to the submental and jugulo-omohyoid nodes; midline and ventral lesions spread to both sides. Figure 4 First, carcinoma of the lateral border of the tongue (arrow) with leukoplakia of the floor of mouth (double arrows). Second, an ulcerative squamous carcinoma of the tongue in a 40-year-old woman. Dhingra, figs 44.12 and 44.13 Figure 5 First, an exophytic growth on the right lateral border of the tongue. Second, cancer of the undersurface of the tongue. Dhingra, figs 44.14 and 44.15 Depth of invasion The thickness of a tongue cancer predicts nodal spread better than its width: occult metastases rise sharply beyond about 4 mm. Current staging (AJCC 8th edition) therefore includes depth of invasion in the T category, and elective neck dissection is advised for tumours deeper than about 3 to 4 mm. 3.3.1 Treatment T1 and T2: partial glossectomy (transoral or with a pull-through), healing by secondary intention, primary closure, graft or free flap; radiotherapy (external beam with brachytherapy) is an "],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s3-4","Floor of mouth","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oral cavity cancer","Squamous carcinoma, men four times more than women, usually starting anteriorly near the submandibular duct openings, which may obstruct and enlarge the gland. Ulcerative or infiltrative, spreading to the ventral tongue, lingual gingiva, mandible and submental space; a submandibular swelling may be gland or node. Small lesions by excision or radiotherapy; larger ones by wide excision with marginal or segmental mandibulectomy; elective neck treatment even for N0 stage II disease, since occult nodes occur in about 40%; nodes often bilateral. Figure 6 Ulcerative squamous carcinoma of the floor of mouth (arrows) in a 55-year-old man. Dhingra, fig. 44.16"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s3-5","Alveolus (gingiva)","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oral cavity cancer","Mostly men, on the lower jaw behind the first molar; early invasion of the bone and along the inferior alveolar nerve; nodes to the submandibular and upper jugular groups. Surgery is preferred because radiotherapy risks osteoradionecrosis: marginal mandibulectomy for superficial lesions, segmental or hemimandibulectomy when the medulla is invaded; partial maxillectomy for upper alveolar lesions."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s3-6","Retromolar trigone","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oral cavity cancer","Primary, or by extension from the gingiva, floor of mouth, buccal mucosa or faucial arch; it reaches the pterygomandibular raphe, pterygoids (trismus) and mandible early. Wide excision with mandibular resection and neck dissection. Figure 7 Cancer of the right retromolar trigone with trismus in a 36-year-old man. Dhingra, fig. 44.17"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s3-7","Hard palate","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oral cavity cancer","Squamous or, more often, minor salivary gland carcinoma (adenoid cystic, mucoepidermoid, adenocarcinoma); squamous cancer is linked to reverse smoking. A painless irregularity felt with the tongue, a superficial ulcer, or a submucosal mass; it may invade the nasal floor and antrum and must be told apart from an antral carcinoma breaking through. Resection with the underlying bone or a partial maxillectomy, closed with an obturator prosthesis or flap. Figure 8 A mixed salivary tumour of the palate. Dhingra, fig. 44.18"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s3-8","The mandible","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oral cavity cancer","Tumour reaches the mandible through the alveolar crest in the dentate jaw and through the occlusal surface in the edentulous jaw. Periosteal involvement needs at least a marginal (rim) mandibulectomy , preserving the lower border; invasion of the medulla, or an edentulous atrophic mandible, needs a segmental resection, reconstructed with a fibula free flap. Radiotherapy to a mandible already invaded leads to osteoradionecrosis."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s4-1","HPV and the changing disease","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oropharyngeal cancer","HPV-positive and HPV-negative oropharyngeal cancer Feature HPV-positive HPV-negative Typical patient Younger, healthier, male (about 9:1), non-smoker Older, smoker and drinker Cause HPV 16; oral sexual exposure Tobacco and alcohol Marker p16 overexpression on immunohistochemistry p16 negative Primary Often small, in the tonsil or tongue base crypts Often larger, infiltrative Nodes Early, often large and cystic Solid Prognosis Much better; staged separately (AJCC 8th) Worse The viral E6 and E7 oncoproteins inactivate p53 and Rb, and p16 is overexpressed as a result. Vaccination against HPV is expected to prevent most of these cancers. Local and regional failure is less common in HPV-positive disease, but distant metastases (mostly lung) occur at similar rates, so chest surveillance still matters. The AJCC eighth edition stages p16-positive and p16-negative tumours separately, with a separate pathological staging after surgery; but treatment is not yet changed on HPV status alone. Neck aspirates and biopsies are tested for p16 (and confirmed by HPV testing). The cystic neck lump in an adult A cystic lump in the upper neck of an adult is a metastasis from an HPV-related tonsil or tongue base cancer until proven otherwise, not a branchial cyst ( Approach to a lump in the neck ). Examine and image the oropharynx, aspirate for p16 or HPV, and if no primary is seen, perform tonsillectomy and tongue base biopsy or mucosectomy."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s4-2","Base of tongue","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oropharyngeal cancer","Silent for a long time; early symptoms (soreness, a lump feeling, mild dysphagia) are attributed to the lingual tonsil. Many present with nodes, often bilateral: about 70% have neck metastases at presentation, so the tongue base is a classic site of the occult primary. Late: referred otalgia, dysphagia, bleeding, a hot-potato voice and tongue fixation. Deeply infiltrative into the tongue, vallecula, pre-epiglottic space, epiglottis and tonsil. It is larger on palpation than it looks in the mirror: always palpate, preferably under anaesthesia. Figure 9 First, an exophytic growth at the base of the tongue. Second, carcinoma of the right base of tongue: (A) endoscopic view, (B) secondaries in the jugulodigastric nodes. Dhingra, figs 53.1 and 53.2"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s4-3","Tonsil and tonsillar fossa","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oropharyngeal cancer","Squamous carcinoma presents as an ulcer with a necrotic base, or as a smooth, enlarged tonsil when it lies within the crypts; lymphoma as a smooth, one-sided enlargement, sometimes ulcerated. Persistent sore throat, dysphagia, otalgia and a neck lump, later bleeding, fetor and trismus. It spreads to the palate, pillars, tongue base, pharyngeal wall, pterygoids and mandible, and the parapharyngeal space; about half have nodes at presentation, the jugulodigastric node first. Figure 10 First, squamous cell carcinoma involving the tonsil, pillars and soft palate. Second, lymphoma of the right tonsil presenting as one-sided tonsillar enlargement. Dhingra, figs 53.3 and 53.4 The one-sided enlarged tonsil Cause Clue Normal asymmetry or a medially placed tonsil Long-standing, smooth, no symptoms Unilateral tonsillitis, peritonsillitis, quinsy Pain, fever, trismus Tonsillolith, cyst Gritty or yellow Parapharyngeal tumour ( Skull base and the parapharyngeal space ) Tonsil pushed medially, normal mucosa Carcinoma or lymphoma Progressive, ulcerated, firm, nodes, otalgia; biopsy or tonsillectomy"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s4-4","Faucial arch (soft palate, uvula, anterior pillar)","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oropharyngeal cancer","Usually well-differentiated, superficially spreading squamous carcinoma on the oral surface, noticed early and slow to metastasise, behaving more like oral cancer; often accompanied by other primaries. Radiotherapy or transoral excision. Figure 11 An ulcerative lesion of the palatine arch: a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma. Dhingra, fig. 53.5"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s4-5","Posterior and lateral pharyngeal walls","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oropharyngeal cancer","Silent, spreading submucosally up to the nasopharynx and down to the hypopharynx, and back to the prevertebral fascia; nodes in about 60%, often bilateral and retropharyngeal. Radiotherapy or excision through a lateral pharyngotomy, often with mandibulotomy, and neck dissection."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s4-6","Benign tumours of the oropharynx","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Oropharyngeal cancer","Papilloma (pedunculated, on the tonsil, palate or pillars; excised), haemangioma (treated only if growing or bleeding), pleomorphic adenoma of the palate (excised completely), vallecular mucous cysts (excised or marsupialised), and rarely lipoma, fibroma and neuroma. Parapharyngeal tumours (deep-lobe parotid, schwannoma, paraganglioma, lipoma, carotid aneurysm) push the tonsil and palate medially and mimic oropharyngeal tumours: never biopsy through the mouth without imaging."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s5-1","Investigations","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Evaluation and staging","Biopsy of the primary; fine-needle aspiration of nodes; p16 immunohistochemistry (and HPV testing) for every oropharyngeal cancer. CT and MRI of the primary and neck: depth, bone invasion (CT and orthopantomogram for the mandible), extension into the pterygoids, parapharyngeal space, pre-epiglottic space and skull base; nodes, including retropharyngeal. Chest CT or PET-CT for distant metastases and second primaries; PET-CT also for the unknown primary. Panendoscopy under anaesthesia (laryngoscopy, pharyngoscopy, oesophagoscopy) to map the tumour and look for synchronous cancers. Dental assessment before radiotherapy; nutrition and swallowing assessment."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s5-2","TNM essentials","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Evaluation and staging","T staging (AJCC 8th edition) Category Oral cavity Oropharynx T1 2 cm or less, depth of invasion 5 mm or less 2 cm or less T2 2 cm or less with depth over 5 mm, or 2 to 4 cm with depth 10 mm or less Over 2 cm, up to 4 cm T3 Over 4 cm, or depth over 10 mm Over 4 cm, or extension to the lingual surface of the epiglottis T4a Cortical bone, maxillary sinus, skin of face (and, for lip, inferior alveolar nerve or floor of mouth) Larynx, extrinsic tongue muscle, medial pterygoid, hard palate or mandible T4b Masticator space, pterygoid plates, skull base, or encasing the internal carotid Lateral pterygoid, pterygoid plates, lateral nasopharynx, skull base, or encasing the carotid For oral cavity and HPV-negative oropharyngeal cancer, N staging follows node size, number and side, and extranodal extension (which upstages); for HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer the N categories are simpler and the stages lower, reflecting the better prognosis. Older texts print the 6th edition tables, based on size alone."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s6-1","Principles","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Treatment","Treatment is planned by a multidisciplinary team (surgeons, radiation and medical oncologists, radiologists, pathologists, reconstructive surgeons, dentists, speech and swallowing therapists, dietitians), balancing cure against speech, swallowing and appearance. Oral cavity: surgery first. Early (stage I and II): excision with a margin, with elective neck treatment according to depth. Advanced (III and IV): resection, reconstruction and neck dissection, followed by radiotherapy, or chemoradiation when margins are positive or there is extranodal extension. Oropharynx: early: radiotherapy (IMRT) or transoral surgery (robotic or laser) with neck dissection, both treating the neck. Surgery alone suits T1 and T2 tumours with no or a single node when margins are clear and there are no adverse features; patients must accept that adjuvant treatment may be needed, and those with gross extranodal extension on imaging are better treated by chemoradiotherapy to avoid triple therapy. Dental extractions needed before radiotherapy are best done at the staging anaesthetic. Advanced: concurrent chemoradiation with cisplatin (organ preservation), or surgery with adjuvant (chemo)radiation; surgery through a mandibulotomy is mostly reserved for salvage. Radiosensitive lymphoepithelioma and lymphoma are treated by radiotherapy or chemotherapy. Systemic agents: cisplatin is the standard radiosensitiser; cetuximab (an EGFR antibody) is an alternative for those unfit for cisplatin, though inferior in HPV-positive disease; immunotherapy (pembrolizumab, alone or with chemotherapy) as first-line trea"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s6-2","The neck","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Treatment","N0: elective selective neck dissection (levels I to III for oral cavity, II to IV for oropharynx) or elective radiotherapy when the risk of occult metastasis exceeds about 20%; sentinel node biopsy in early oral cancer. N+: comprehensive (modified radical) neck dissection, followed by radiotherapy for multiple nodes or extranodal extension. Figure 12 The lymph node levels of the neck. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s6-3","Reconstruction","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Treatment","Primary closure, skin grafts, local flaps (nasolabial, tongue, facial artery musculomucosal), regional flaps (pectoralis major, deltopectoral) and free flaps (radial forearm for the tongue and floor, anterolateral thigh for bulk, fibula for the mandible). Tracheostomy protects the airway after major resections."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s6-4","Palliation","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Treatment","Radiotherapy or chemotherapy for pain and bleeding, tracheostomy and gastrostomy for airway and nutrition, and good analgesia and palliative care."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s6-5","Prognosis","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Treatment","Early oral cancer is cured in most patients; advanced disease in roughly a third to a half. Extranodal extension, nodes low in the neck, positive margins and perineural invasion worsen survival. HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer has markedly better survival than HPV-negative. Lifelong follow-up looks for recurrence (most in the first two years) and second primaries, and stopping smoking improves outcome."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s6-6","Chemoprevention","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Treatment","Retinoids, beta-carotene and vitamin E can induce regression of leukoplakia and reduce second primaries while taken, but no agent has replaced stopping tobacco, areca nut and alcohol."],["ev","cl:t-tonsil_ca~c7","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer: current evidence","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Non-squamous cancers","HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer has much better survival than HPV-negative disease, and smoking worsens it whatever the HPV status (Ang et al., N Engl J Med 2010). In the De-ESCALaTE trial of 334 low-risk HPV-positive patients, replacing cisplatin with cetuximab did not reduce toxicity and worsened two-year survival (97.5% vs 89.4%) (Mehanna et al., Lancet 2019), and RTOG 1016 found the same (Gillison et al., Lancet 2019), so cisplatin remains standard and de-escalation belongs in trials. In the ORATOR trial, transoral robotic surgery with neck dissection and primary radiotherapy gave similar swallowing quality of life for early oropharyngeal cancer, with different toxicity profiles (Nichols et al., Lancet Oncol 2019). For early oral cancer, elective neck dissection improved three-year survival compared with waiting (80% vs 67.5%) (D'Cruz et al., N Engl J Med 2015), and pooled trials suggest sentinel node biopsy gives similar survival with better shoulder function (Gupta et al., Oral Oncol 2022)."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~c7","Non-squamous cancers","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · Non-squamous cancers","The others. Minor salivary gland carcinoma: 80 to 90% of minor salivary tumours are malignant in some series; the palate is the commonest site; adenoid cystic (about 40%, with perineural spread), adenocarcinoma and mucoepidermoid. Wide excision with neck dissection for nodes, and radiotherapy. Mucosal melanoma: palate and gum, sixth decade, men twice as often; pigmented, later ulcerated, sometimes amelanotic; wide excision including bone; five-year survival around 15%. Lymphoma: mostly non-Hodgkin, in the tonsil and tongue base, as a smooth bulky submucosal mass, with neck nodes in many; chemotherapy and radiotherapy. Kaposi sarcoma: reddish-purple palatal plaques, mainly in AIDS; responds to antiretroviral treatment, radiotherapy or chemotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s8-1","Viva essentials","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Risk factors, field cancerisation and second primaries. Commonest sites in India and in the West. Tongue cancer: site, spread, depth of invasion, referred otalgia and treatment, including the commando operation. Mandible: marginal versus segmental resection. HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer and the cystic neck node. Management of the N0 neck. Differential diagnosis of a one-sided enlarged tonsil."],["sec","cl:t-tonsil_ca~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Oral and oropharyngeal cancer · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a persistent ulcer with mouthwashes rather than a biopsy. Judging a tongue base cancer by looking without feeling. Excising a cystic neck lump as a branchial cyst in an adult without looking for a primary. Irradiating a mandible already invaded by tumour."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s1-1","Definitions","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Tracheostomy","Tracheotomy is opening the trachea; tracheostomy is making that opening into a stoma at the skin. The terms are used interchangeably."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s1-2","What a tracheostomy does","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Tracheostomy","Bypasses obstruction anywhere from the lips to the stoma. Improves alveolar ventilation by reducing dead space by 30 to 50% (normal dead space about 150 mL) and lowering airway resistance. Protects the airway with a cuffed tube against aspiration of secretions (coma, bulbar palsy) and blood (maxillofacial and laryngeal trauma), and allows the pharynx to be packed. Allows suction of secretions when the patient cannot cough (coma, head injury, respiratory paralysis) or coughing hurts (chest and upper abdominal injury or surgery). Permits prolonged ventilation: for intermittent positive pressure ventilation expected beyond a week or two (older texts say beyond 72 hours, but in the TracMan trial tracheostomy within four days did not reduce mortality), tracheostomy is better tolerated than translaryngeal intubation and protects the larynx. Allows anaesthesia when intubation is impossible (trismus, obstructing tumours)."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s1-3","Indications","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Tracheostomy","Indications for tracheostomy Group Examples Upper airway obstruction: infection Epiglottitis, croup, diphtheria, Ludwig's angina, quinsy, retropharyngeal and parapharyngeal abscesses Obstruction: trauma Laryngotracheal injury, maxillofacial and mandibular fractures, endoscopic and intubation injury Obstruction: other Tumours of the larynx, pharynx, tongue and thyroid; foreign bodies; laryngeal oedema (burns, irritants, allergy, radiation); bilateral abductor paralysis; congenital lesions (webs, cysts, subglottic stenosis, haemangioma) Retained secretions Coma, head injury, stroke, overdose; respiratory muscle paralysis (spinal injury, Guillain–Barré, myasthenia, polio); tetanus; painful cough; aspiration Respiratory insufficiency Prolonged ventilation; chronic lung disease (emphysema, bronchitis, bronchiectasis) Prophylactic Major surgery of the tongue, floor of mouth, mandible, pharynx and larynx; obstructive sleep apnoea unresponsive to other treatment In children under a year most indications are congenital (subglottic stenosis, haemangioma, cysts, webs, bilateral paralysis); in older children inflammatory or traumatic (croup, epiglottitis, diphtheria, burns, prolonged intubation, papillomatosis)."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s2-1","By urgency","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Types","Emergency: obstruction is complete or nearly so and intubation is impossible; ideally replaced by cricothyroidotomy or by intubation followed by an orderly tracheostomy. Elective (orderly): planned, with the patient intubated where possible; therapeutic or prophylactic. Permanent: an end stoma after laryngectomy, or a long-term tube for bilateral paralysis or stenosis. Percutaneous dilatational: at the bedside in intensive care. Minitracheostomy and cricothyroidotomy."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s2-2","By level","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Types","The thyroid isthmus lies over the second to fourth tracheal rings. High (above the isthmus, through the first ring): risks perichondritis of the cricoid and subglottic stenosis, and is avoided; it is acceptable, and preferred, only in laryngeal cancer when a laryngectomy is likely, so that the stoma can be excised with the specimen (the theoretical risk of tumour seeding is not clinically important). Mid (through the second to fourth rings, dividing or retracting the isthmus): the preferred site. Low (below the isthmus): the trachea is deep, close to great vessels, and the tube presses on the suprasternal notch. A thyroid ima artery is present in up to 5%, and a high-riding innominate artery can be injured low in the neck. Figure 1 The cervical trachea and recurrent laryngeal nerve, and surface marking of the thyroid and cricoid cartilages and cricothyroid membrane. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-trach~s3-1","Preparation","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Technique","Intubate first whenever possible, especially in children. Supine with a shoulder roll to extend the neck and bring the trachea forward (unless the cervical spine is injured). Local infiltration with lidocaine and adrenaline in the awake patient; general anaesthesia when intubated."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s3-2","Steps","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Technique","Incision: a vertical midline incision from the cricoid to just above the sternal notch gives fast access with little bleeding and suits emergencies; a 4 to 5 cm horizontal incision two fingerbreadths above the notch gives a better scar in elective cases. Dissect in the midline; retract or tie the anterior jugular veins. Separate the strap muscles in the midline and retract them laterally. Retract the thyroid isthmus, or divide it between clamps and suture-ligate it; preserving the isthmus where possible shortens surgery without adding complications. Instil a little lidocaine into the trachea to suppress coughing; steady the trachea with a cricoid hook. Open the trachea at the second to fourth rings: a vertical slit, a window, an inverted U (Björk) flap sutured to the skin to ease reinsertion, or a slit with stay sutures; never through the first ring. Withdraw the endotracheal tube to just above the opening under direct vision, insert the tracheostomy tube, inflate the cuff, confirm position with waveform capnography (as after any later tube change), and only then remove the endotracheal tube. Secure the tube with tapes (and sutures); do not close the skin tightly or pack the wound, which causes surgical emphysema. Figure 2 Skin incisions for tracheostomy: (A) vertical midline, (B) transverse. Dhingra, fig. 64.1"],["sec","cl:t-trach~s3-3","In infants and children","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Technique","Operate under general anaesthesia over an endotracheal tube or bronchoscope; the trachea is soft and easily lost, and straying laterally injures the recurrent nerve and carotid. Do not hyperextend the neck: it draws the pleura, innominate vessels and thymus into the field and invites too low an opening. Place stay sutures on either side of the midline before incising; they are the rescue if the tube comes out in the first days. Make a simple vertical incision; never remove cartilage. Do not push the blade deep: the posterior wall and oesophagus lie close. Choose a soft tube of the right diameter, length and curvature; confirm its position with a chest radiograph."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s3-4","Percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Technique","In the intubated intensive care patient under sedation, through a small incision, a needle, guide wire and dilators (single tapered or serial) enter between the second and third rings under bronchoscopic guidance through the endotracheal tube. It avoids transfer to theatre and is quicker and cheaper, with fewer wound infections. Avoid in obesity, neck masses, difficult intubation, poor extension, impalpable landmarks, coagulopathy and in children. Complications: paratracheal placement, bleeding, posterior wall injury and emphysema."],["sec","cl:t-trach~c4","Tracheostomy tubes","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Tracheostomy tubes","Choosing the tube. Features of tracheostomy tubes Feature Purpose Cuffed Seals the airway for ventilation and against aspiration; keep pressure below about 25 to 30 cm of water to avoid mucosal necrosis Uncuffed For patients who no longer need ventilation or protection; children Inner cannula Removed and cleaned to prevent blockage without changing the tube Fenestrated A window allows air to pass up through the larynx for speech when the inner cannula is removed and the tube occluded Speaking (one-way) valve Lets air in through the tube and out through the larynx (only with the cuff deflated) Adjustable flange For deep or obese necks Metal (silver, Jackson) Durable, for long-term permanent use; no cuff Figure 3 A patient with a suction-aid tracheostomy tube receiving oxygen through a side port. Dhingra, fig. 64.3"],["sec","cl:t-trach~s5-1","Why it matters","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Aftercare","Tracheostomy problems account for up to half of airway-related hypoxic brain injuries and deaths in hospital, and most are preventable. The UK National Tracheostomy Safety Project emergency algorithms for a blocked or displaced tube (separate versions for patients with a patent upper airway and for laryngectomy \"neck breathers\") should be at every bedside, with bedside equipment, a sign showing the airway type, and staff trained to use them; the Global Tracheostomy Collaborative promotes the same team-based approach internationally."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s5-2","The first days","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Aftercare","Constant observation by trained staff: bleeding, displacement, blockage and secretions; a call bell and writing pad. Suction as often as needed with a sterile catheter, applying suction only while withdrawing it, to avoid mucosal injury. Humidification of inspired air (heated humidifiers, nebulisers, heat-moisture exchangers) to prevent crusting and tracheitis; saline instillation and mucolytics for crusts. Inner cannula cleaned regularly; the outer tube is not changed for the first three to five days until a track forms, after which tube changes become straightforward. Cuff pressure checked; deflated when no longer needed. Spare tubes (same size and smaller), tracheal dilator, obturator, suction and oxygen at the bedside. The blocked or displaced tube Sudden respiratory distress in a patient with a tracheostomy: remove the speaking valve and inner cannula, pass a suction catheter; if it will not pass, deflate the cuff and remove the tube; oxygenate through the stoma and the face; if the patient was not a laryngectomy, they can also be oxygenated and intubated from above. Follow a standard emergency algorithm and colour-coded bedhead signs. In the first days, before a tract forms, displacement is an emergency: stay sutures and a Björk flap make reinsertion easier, and if the tube cannot be replaced the patient is oxygenated from above and intubated orally."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s5-3","Decannulation","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Aftercare","Remove the tube as soon as it is not needed, because prolonged use brings infection, ulceration, granulation, stenosis and scarring. Deflate the cuff, change to a smaller uncuffed tube, then occlude (cap) it; if the patient breathes, speaks and clears secretions comfortably for 24 hours (longer in children), remove the tube and tape the stoma, which usually closes within a week; persistent tracheocutaneous fistulae are closed surgically. In infants, decannulate in hospital with full reintubation equipment and monitoring."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s5-4","Difficult decannulation","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Aftercare","Look for persistence of the original disease, granulations at the stoma or the tube tip (often a suprastomal granuloma), subglottic stenosis or oedema, collapse of the anterior wall above the stoma, tracheomalacia, and psychological dependence; endoscopy of the larynx and trachea decides."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s6-1","At operation","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Complications","Haemorrhage (anterior jugular veins, isthmus, aberrant vessels). Apnoea after relief of long-standing obstruction, when the high carbon dioxide drive is suddenly removed; ventilate. Pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum (especially in children). Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury; injury to the oesophagus with a tracheo-oesophageal fistula. Aspiration of blood; false passage; cardiac arrest."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s6-2","Early (hours to days)","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Complications","Reactionary and secondary bleeding. Displacement and blockage of the tube: the commonest causes of death. Surgical emphysema, tracheitis and crusting, atelectasis and pneumonia, wound infection."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s6-3","Late","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Complications","Tracheo-innominate fistula with massive haemorrhage from erosion by the tube tip or cuff: under 1%, but it kills over 80%. A sentinel bleed demands immediate assessment; torrential bleeding is controlled by overinflating the cuff or by digital pressure through the stoma against the sternum while the patient goes to theatre. Subglottic and laryngeal stenosis (high tracheostomy), tracheal stenosis at the stoma or cuff site, tracheomalacia. Stenosis is promoted by cuff pressures above 30 cmH₂O, intubation beyond ten days, reflux, obesity, large tubes and percutaneous insertion ( Laryngotracheal trauma and stenosis ). Tracheo-oesophageal fistula from cuff pressure or the tube tip. Granulations, difficult decannulation, persistent tracheocutaneous fistula, ugly or keloid scar. Corrosion and fracture of old metal tubes with aspiration of fragments."],["sec","cl:t-trach~c7","Emergency airway","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Emergency airway","Buying time. When obstruction leaves no time for an orderly tracheostomy (the front-of-neck airway in detail is in Emergency front-of-neck airway and arterial ligation ): Jaw thrust and head tilt (not in spinal injury) lift the tongue off the pharyngeal wall. Oropharyngeal airway with bag-and-mask ventilation. Nasopharyngeal airway: better tolerated by an awake patient. Supraglottic airway (laryngeal mask): a rescue when mask ventilation and intubation fail; it does not help obstruction at or below the glottis. Intubation: the quickest definitive airway; then an orderly tracheostomy if needed. Cricothyroidotomy: through the cricothyroid membrane, between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, in the \"can't intubate, can't oxygenate\" situation. Scalpel cricothyroidotomy (a stab through the membrane, rotated, with a bougie and a 6 mm cuffed tube) is the recommended adult technique. Convert to a formal tracheostomy within about 72 hours, because perichondritis and subglottic stenosis follow prolonged cricothyroidotomy. It is avoided in children under about ten to twelve, whose membrane is tiny. Needle cricothyroidotomy with a large cannula and transtracheal jet ventilation : a temporary measure, the choice in small children; ensure a path for expiration or barotrauma follows. Emergency tracheostomy: with the trachea fixed between thumb and finger, a vertical incision from the thyroid to the notch, quickly through to the second and third rings; hazardous on a struggling patient without light, suction or help, so intubate first if at all possible. Figure 4 A laryngeal mask airway. "],["sec","cl:t-trach~s8-1","Direct laryngoscopy","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Airway endoscopy","To see the larynx and hypopharynx directly: when indirect examination is impossible (children, a strong gag, an overhanging epiglottis), to examine the hidden areas (base of tongue, valleculae, lower piriform sinuses, infrahyoid epiglottis, anterior commissure, ventricles and subglottis), to assess and biopsy tumours, and to treat (remove benign lesions and foreign bodies, dilate strictures). Contraindicated by cervical spine disease and by significant obstruction unless the airway has first been secured. Position: the Boyce (sniffing, barking-dog) position : head raised 10 to 15 cm on a pillow with the neck flexed on the chest and the head extended at the atlanto-occipital joint, aligning the oral, pharyngeal and laryngeal axes. Technique: protect the upper teeth; insert the laryngoscope along the right side of the tongue, move to the midline, lift the epiglottis, and examine every subsite systematically, lifting rather than levering on the teeth. Suspension and the operating microscope or angled telescopes allow microlaryngoscopy . Complications: injury to lips, teeth and tongue, bleeding, laryngeal oedema, laryngospasm. Figure 6 Direct laryngoscopy in the sniffing position, lifting the laryngoscope without levering on the teeth. Dhingra, fig. 91.1"],["ev","cl:t-trach~s8-2","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy: current evidence","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Airway endoscopy","Safety. The UK National Tracheostomy Safety Project published consensus emergency algorithms for the blocked or displaced tube (McGrath et al., Anaesthesia 2012). A UK national confidential enquiry found frequent and often preventable complications, mostly from displacement or blockage, and recommended bedhead signs, capnography and trained staff (NCEPOD, On the Right Trach? , 2014). Timing in the ICU. In the TracMan trial of 909 ventilated adults, tracheostomy within four days did not reduce 30-day mortality compared with waiting until day ten or later, and many late-group patients never needed one (Young et al., JAMA 2013); a Cochrane review found that early tracheostomy may shorten sedation and ICU stay, with low-certainty evidence on mortality (Andriolo et al., 2015). Technique. Percutaneous tracheostomy reduces wound infection and unfavourable scarring compared with open tracheostomy in selected ICU patients, with similar serious complications (Cochrane, Brass et al., 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s8-2","Bronchoscopy","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · Airway endoscopy","Indications: unexplained wheeze, haemoptysis or cough over four weeks; radiographic collapse, persistent opacity, obstructive emphysema, hilar or mediastinal shadows; vocal fold palsy; secretions for culture and cytology. Therapeutic: foreign bodies, retained secretions and mucus plugs. Rigid bronchoscopy under general anaesthesia allows ventilation through the scope, large biopsies, control of haemorrhage and is the instrument of choice for foreign bodies , especially in children. The bevel is turned to pass the glottis, and the head is moved to the left to enter the right bronchus and vice versa. Limit procedures in children to about twenty minutes to avoid subglottic oedema. Flexible bronchoscopy under topical anaesthesia (through the nose, an endotracheal tube or tracheostomy) reaches segmental and subsegmental bronchi, can be done at the bedside and in patients with neck or jaw problems, but controls ventilation and bleeding poorly and is not the tool for foreign bodies in children."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s9-1","Viva essentials","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Functions and indications of tracheostomy. Why not through the first ring; the levels. Steps of the operation, and differences in children. Tubes, aftercare, decannulation and causes of failed decannulation. Complications, especially displacement, blockage and tracheo-innominate fistula. Emergency airway options: cricothyroidotomy and jet ventilation. Direct laryngoscopy position and hidden areas; rigid versus flexible bronchoscopy."],["sec","cl:t-trach~s9-2","Common pitfalls","Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Making a high tracheostomy through the first ring. Changing the tube on day one without a mature track or stay sutures. Suturing the skin tightly around the tube. Leaving a cricothyroidotomy in place for weeks."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s1-1","The tumours","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Overview","Tumours of the middle ear and temporal bone Benign Paraganglioma (glomus) tympanicum, jugulare, vagale Benign Others adenomatous neuroendocrine tumour, facial schwannoma, haemangioma, choristoma, endolymphatic sac tumour Malignant Squamous cell carcinoma the commonest Malignant Others adenoid cystic carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, rhabdomyosarcoma (children), metastases Skull base Chordoma, chondrosarcoma petrous apex and clivus"],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s1-2","Why it matters","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Overview","Both key tumours present to the ear clinic looking like something common: pulsatile tinnitus or a polyp, and a discharging ear."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-1","Origin","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","Paragangliomas arise from neural-crest glomus bodies along the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson's nerve) and the auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold's nerve), and on the dome of the jugular bulb. They are benign, slow-growing and highly vascular. Figure 1 The tympanic plexus on the promontory (Jacobson's nerve), and the keel of bone between the carotid canal and jugular fossa. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-2","Who gets them","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","Usually in the fourth and fifth decades, with a strong female predominance (about 5 to 1)."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-3","Pathology","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","Nests of chief cells ( zellballen ) surrounded by sustentacular cells in a vascular stroma."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-4","Genetics","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","Some are familial, from mutations in the succinate dehydrogenase genes. SDHD mutations cause multiple tumours and are expressed only when inherited from the father; SDHB mutations carry a higher risk of malignancy. Where the books differ · How often Dhingra About 10% familial, about 3% secrete catecholamines, about 4% metastasise. Lee Up to 30% familial, 5% or fewer secrete, 2–4% metastasise. In practice Current series find about a third hereditary, so offer every patient genetic testing, and check for secretion before any anaesthetic."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-5","Types","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","2.5.1 Glomus tympanicum Arises on the promontory and fills the middle ear. 2.5.2 Glomus jugulare Arises in the jugular bulb, eroding upwards into the middle ear and medially to the jugular foramen and skull base. 2.5.3 Glomus vagale Arises on the vagus below the skull base, presenting in the neck."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-6","Clinical features","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","2.6.1 Symptoms Pulsatile tinnitus (about 80%), conductive hearing loss (about 60%), fullness, and later bleeding from the ear. Jugulare tumours add lower cranial nerve palsies (ninth to twelfth: hoarseness, dysphagia, wasting of the tongue and trapezius) and facial weakness. 2.6.2 Signs A red mass behind the drum, the rising sun when it emerges from the floor of the middle ear. Pneumatic pressure on the drum makes the mass pulsate, then blanch ( Brown's sign ). A bruit may be heard over the mastoid. In advanced cases a bleeding polyp fills the canal. Figure 2 Glomus jugulare: a red mass behind the drum. Dhingra, fig. 17.1 Never biopsy in clinic A red, pulsatile mass behind the drum, or a polyp with pulsatile tinnitus, is a glomus tumour until imaged. Biopsy can cause catastrophic bleeding. 2.6.3 Secreting tumours Hypertension, headache, palpitations and sweating mean catecholamine secretion: measure urinary or plasma metanephrines before any anaesthetic."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-7","Investigations","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","2.7.1 CT Erosion of the caroticojugular spine separates a jugulare tumour from a tympanicum. CT also excludes an aberrant carotid artery and a high jugular bulb. 2.7.2 MRI A salt-and-pepper appearance from flow voids; defines intracranial and neck extension. 2.7.3 Angiography Shows the feeding vessels (mainly the ascending pharyngeal artery), looks for multiple tumours, and allows embolisation. Balloon occlusion testing if the carotid may need to be sacrificed."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-8","Staging","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","Glasscock-Jackson classification Stage Glomus tympanicum Glomus jugulare I Limited to the promontory Jugular bulb, middle ear and mastoid II Fills the middle ear Extends under the internal auditory canal III Into the mastoid Into the petrous apex IV Through the drum into the canal, or beyond Beyond the apex into the clivus or infratemporal fossa The Fisch classification grades A (middle ear), B (tympanomastoid), C (infralabyrinthine and apex, C1 to C4 by carotid involvement) and D (intracranial)."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-9","Differential diagnosis","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","Red, white or blue behind the drum Colour Think of Key test Red, pulsatile Glomus tumour CT and MRI Pink, anterior Aberrant internal carotid artery CT, MR angiography Blue, inferior High jugular bulb CT, MRI Blue with dark fluid Cholesterol granuloma ( Petrous apicitis ) CT, MRI White Congenital cholesteatoma CT"],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s2-10","Treatment","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Paraganglioma","2.10.1 Surgery The treatment of choice for most, after embolisation 24 to 48 hours before and alpha-blockade if the tumour secretes. Small tympanicum tumours whose whole margin can be seen are removed through the canal; larger tumours need hypotympanic, extended facial recess or mastoid-neck approaches; jugulare tumours need the Fisch infratemporal fossa approach. 2.10.2 Radiotherapy Stereotactic radiosurgery or fractionated radiotherapy arrests growth in about 90 to 94%, though the tumour remains. For the elderly, the unfit, residual disease and tumours where surgery would cost lower cranial nerves. 2.10.3 Observation Slow-growing tumours in elderly patients may simply be watched. Pearls A glomus tympanicum is only certain when its whole rim is visible; otherwise image. Pain is more typical of carcinoma than of a glomus tumour."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s3-1","Who gets it","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Squamous cell carcinoma","Adults of 40 to 60, about three-quarters with a long history of chronic ear discharge."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s3-2","Clinical features","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Squamous cell carcinoma","Bloodstained, foul discharge; deep, boring pain, especially at night; friable granulation or a polyp that bleeds on touch; and later facial palsy, vertigo, trismus and a neck or parotid mass. It is easily mistaken for chronic otitis media or otitis externa."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s3-3","Investigations","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Squamous cell carcinoma","Staged by the modified Pittsburgh system, as for canal carcinoma ( External ear tumours and bony growths ). Biopsy of any non-healing granulation in the canal or middle ear. CT of the temporal bone and neck, MRI for soft tissue and dura, and chest imaging."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s3-4","Treatment","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Squamous cell carcinoma","Limited to the canal without bone involvement: sleeve resection of the canal. Bony canal involved: lateral temporal bone resection. Middle ear involved: subtotal temporal bone resection. Parotidectomy and neck dissection according to spread. Postoperative radiotherapy for most stages beyond the earliest, with chemotherapy for advanced disease. Outlook is poor once the middle ear and beyond are involved. The dura usually resists invasion."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s4-1","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Other tumours","The commonest malignant ear tumour in children: a polyp in a child's ear with early facial palsy. Treated with chemoradiation, with resection when feasible. Figure 3 A four-year-old child with rhabdomyosarcoma of the right middle ear and mastoid, with facial palsy on the same side. Dhingra, fig. 17.2"],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s4-2","Endolymphatic sac tumour","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Other tumours","A locally aggressive papillary tumour of the sac, sporadic or with von Hippel-Lindau disease (about 11% of patients, often bilateral). It mimics Ménière's disease , or causes sudden hearing loss from bleeding into the inner ear. Removed with the dura around the sac; VHL patients are screened by MRI every one to three years."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s4-3","Facial nerve schwannoma and haemangioma","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Other tumours","A schwannoma causes slowly progressive facial palsy ( Facial nerve palsy ) or hearing loss; a haemangioma near the geniculate ganglion causes a palsy out of proportion to its small size and should be removed early. Facial schwannomas arise most often near the geniculate ganglion, and many patients keep good facial function for years. Because removal means sacrificing and grafting the nerve, and the best result after a graft is House–Brackmann III, surgery is reserved for weakness worse than grade III; enlarging tumours with good function are treated by radiosurgery (control in about 90% at five years, with function preserved in 90%) or by bony decompression of the canal."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s4-4","Adenomatous neuroendocrine tumour","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Other tumours","A benign middle ear tumour presenting as a mass behind an intact drum with conductive loss."],["ev","cl:t-tumear~s4-5","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours: current evidence","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Other tumours","Genetics. About a third of head and neck paragangliomas are hereditary, so guidelines recommend genetic testing (SDHx and related genes) for every patient, with screening of relatives who carry a mutation. Radiosurgery. A meta-analysis found tumour control in about 95% of jugular paragangliomas after radiosurgery, with fewer new cranial neuropathies than surgery (Guss et al., Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys 2011), so radiosurgery or observation is increasingly preferred for large jugular tumours in older patients, and surgery for tympanic tumours and selected young patients. Temporal bone SCC. The modified Pittsburgh staging system guides treatment (Moody et al., Am J Otol 2000): lateral temporal bone resection for early tumours, subtotal resection with parotidectomy and neck dissection for advanced ones, and postoperative radiotherapy for all but the earliest."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s4-5","Chordoma and chondrosarcoma","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · Other tumours","Chordoma arises in the midline clivus in older patients, with headache and sixth nerve palsy; it is bright on T2 MRI and contains physaliferous cells. Chondrosarcoma arises off the midline at the petroclival junction. Both are treated by maximal resection followed by high-dose or proton radiotherapy."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s5-1","On call","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · For the examination","A mass or granulation in the ear Pulsatile red mass: do not biopsy; arrange CT and MRI. Non-healing, bleeding granulation in a chronic ear: biopsy it. Hypertension, sweating or palpitations with a glomus tumour: check catecholamines before any anaesthetic."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s5-2","Viva essentials","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Origin of glomus tumours: Jacobson's and Arnold's nerves, jugular bulb. Signs: rising sun, Brown's sign, pulsatile tinnitus. CT: caroticojugular spine; aberrant carotid and high bulb. Staging and treatment options. Squamous carcinoma: who, symptoms, and the types of temporal bone resection."],["sec","cl:t-tumear~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Middle ear and temporal bone tumours · For the examination","Common pitfalls Biopsying a red mass behind the drum in clinic. Treating a bleeding, painful ear as chronic otitis media for months. Forgetting catecholamines before surgery."],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s1-1","Biology","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Paragangliomas","Paragangliomas arise from the neural-crest paraganglia , the chemoreceptor tissue of the carotid body, the vagal ganglia, the jugular bulb and the middle ear. Histologically they are nests of chief (type I) cells surrounded by sustentacular (type II) cells , the pattern called Zellballen (\"balls of cells\"), in a highly vascular stroma. The carotid body is the commonest site in the head and neck, accounting for about half to 60%, followed by the jugular foramen (glomus jugulare), the middle ear (glomus tympanicum) and the vagus (glomus vagale, about 5 to 10%). Up to 40% are hereditary, mostly from mutations in the succinate dehydrogenase genes SDHB, SDHC, SDHD and others. Hereditary tumours appear at a younger age, are more often multiple and bilateral (about a third), and come with phaeochromocytomas; SDHB carriers have the highest risk of malignancy. Genetic testing is therefore offered to every patient. Chronic high-altitude hypoxia, by contrast, causes carotid body hyperplasia. About 5 to 10% of carotid tumours, and more vagal tumours, are malignant, and malignancy is defined only by metastasis to nodes or distant sites, not by histology or local invasion. Only 1 to 3% secrete catecholamines, but everyone is screened with plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines, both to find a functioning tumour and to find a phaeochromocytoma before anaesthesia. Figure 1 The carotid body at the bifurcation, and the carotid sheath and its contents. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s1-2","Carotid body tumour","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Paragangliomas","The carotid body tumour is a painless, slowly enlarging mass at the carotid bifurcation , at the anterior border of the sternomastoid at the level of the hyoid. It grows about 1 mm a year, doubling in four to five years. It moves from side to side but not vertically ( Fontaine's sign ), transmits or has its own pulsation, may have a bruit, and may bulge into the oropharynx. Cranial nerve palsies of X, XII or the sympathetic chain are uncommon before surgery, and pain or rapid growth suggests malignancy. Contrast CT or MRI shows an intensely enhancing mass splaying the internal and external carotids apart. On MRI it has a \" salt-and-pepper \" pattern, haemorrhage providing the salt and flow voids the pepper, and angiography shows the lyre sign of splayed carotids around a vascular blush. Functional imaging with Ga-68 DOTATATE PET looks for multiple tumours, and if carotid sacrifice may be needed, the circle of Willis and collateral flow are assessed by balloon occlusion testing. No needle, no biopsy FNA of a suspected carotid body tumour is avoided, and open or core biopsy condemned. They bleed, they rarely give a diagnosis, and imaging is characteristic. Figure 2 Carotid body tumour: (A) clinical swelling; (B) MRI; (C, D) angiography showing splaying of the internal and external carotids (lyre sign) with tumour blush; (E) the excised specimen. Dhingra, fig. 77.6 Shamblin classification Group Relation to the carotids Surgical meaning I Small, localised, easily separated from the vessels Low risk II Adherent to or partly surrounding the vessels Careful subadventitial dissectio"],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s1-3","Vagal paraganglioma","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Paragangliomas","The vagal paraganglioma usually arises from the inferior (nodose) ganglion, high in the parapharyngeal space. It displaces both carotids anteromedially without splaying them, separates the internal jugular vein from the carotid, and may extend to the jugular foramen and intracranially. About a third of patients already have vagal weakness and hoarseness, and malignancy is commoner than with carotid tumours. Because surgery almost always sacrifices the vagus, small tumours with normal nerve function are observed or irradiated, and surgery is kept for growing tumours and for those that have already paralysed the nerve."],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s1-4","Jugular and tympanic paragangliomas","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Paragangliomas","Glomus jugulare and tympanicum tumours present with pulsatile tinnitus , conductive hearing loss and a red mass behind the drum, the rising sun sign, which blanches on pneumatic pressure ( Brown's sign ), and jugulare tumours may cause lower cranial nerve palsies, the jugular foramen syndrome . They are described with tumours of the ear ; they are classified by Fisch or by Glasscock–Jackson and treated by surgery through transcanal, mastoid or infratemporal approaches, by radiotherapy, or by observation."],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s2-1","Schwannoma (neurilemmoma)","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Neural tumours","The schwannoma is a benign, encapsulated tumour of Schwann cells that grows eccentrically on its nerve, so the fascicles can often be separated from it. Histology shows cellular Antoni A areas with palisading Verocay bodies and loose Antoni B areas. Most head and neck schwannomas arise in the parapharyngeal space , where they are the second commonest tumour after salivary tumours ( Salivary gland tumours ), usually from the vagus or the cervical sympathetic chain, but also from IX, XI, XII and the cervical and brachial plexuses. They present as a slow, painless lateral neck or parapharyngeal mass that moves from side to side but not vertically, and pressure may send pain along the nerve. The nerve of origin usually still works. On imaging they enhance less intensely than a paraganglioma, have no flow voids , and show a target sign on T2. A vagal schwannoma pushes the carotids forward and separates the carotid from the jugular vein, while a sympathetic schwannoma pushes both carotids forward without separating the vessels. They are removed by intracapsular enucleation preserving the fascicles, but deficits remain common afterwards: hoarseness after vagal tumours, and Horner syndrome and first bite syndrome after sympathetic tumours. Small tumours in older patients can be observed."],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s2-2","Neurofibroma","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Neural tumours","The neurofibroma is unencapsulated and intertwined with its nerve, so the nerve has to be sacrificed to remove it. Multiple and plexiform neurofibromas are the hallmark of neurofibromatosis type 1, and rapid growth or pain suggests malignant change to a malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour . Distinguishing parapharyngeal and carotid space masses Feature Carotid body tumour Vagal paraganglioma Vagal schwannoma Sympathetic schwannoma Pleomorphic adenoma (prestyloid) Position of the carotids Splayed apart at the bifurcation Pushed anteromedially, not splayed Pushed anteriorly Pushed anteriorly, vein not separated Pushed posteriorly Flow voids Yes Yes No No No Nerve deficit before surgery Rare Vagal in about a third Uncommon Horner occasionally None Figure 3 The vagus nerve and the cervical sympathetic chain in the neck. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s3-1","Classification (ISSVA)","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Vascular anomalies","Haemangioma versus vascular malformation Feature Infantile haemangioma Vascular malformation Nature True tumour (endothelial proliferation) Error of vessel development At birth Absent or a faint precursor mark Present (may not be visible) Course Proliferates for about a year, then involutes over years Grows in proportion with the child; never involutes; growth spurts at puberty, pregnancy, infection, trauma Marker GLUT-1 positive GLUT-1 negative Treatment Observation; propranolol Laser, sclerotherapy, embolisation, surgery according to type"],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s3-2","Infantile haemangioma","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Vascular anomalies","The infantile haemangioma is the commonest tumour of infancy, affecting about 4 to 5% of infants, more often girls and premature or low-birth-weight babies. It appears in the first weeks, proliferates rapidly to about 9 to 12 months, and then involutes slowly, largely by five to ten years, often leaving fibrofatty tissue and telangiectasia. It may be superficial (the bright red \"strawberry\"), deep and bluish, or mixed, and focal, multifocal or segmental. Congenital haemangiomas are fully grown at birth and involute rapidly (RICH), partially (PICH) or not at all (NICH). Several associations matter. A haemangioma in a beard distribution is accompanied by a subglottic haemangioma ( Congenital lesions of the larynx ) in about two-thirds of children, with biphasic stridor appearing at about two to three months. A segmental facial lesion raises the possibility of PHACES syndrome (posterior fossa anomalies, haemangioma, arterial anomalies, cardiac and aortic anomalies, eye anomalies and sternal clefts), assessed with MRI and echocardiography. Five or more skin lesions prompt a search for hepatic haemangiomas. The Kasabach–Merritt phenomenon of platelet trapping and coagulopathy belongs to kaposiform haemangioendothelioma and tufted angioma, not to the common haemangioma. Most haemangiomas are left to involute. Treatment is needed when the airway is threatened, when a periocular lesion threatens vision through amblyopia, for ulceration and bleeding, for lesions of the lip, nose and ear that will deform, for large facial lesions, and for high-output heart failure. Propranolol , at a"],["ev","cl:t-vascneck~s3-3","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies: current evidence","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Vascular anomalies","Classification. The table above follows the ISSVA classification (2018); using the right name avoids wrong treatment. Infantile haemangioma. Oral propranolol cleared or nearly cleared haemangiomas in 60% against 4% with placebo (Léauté-Labrèze et al., NEJM 2015). Lymphatic and complex malformations. Sclerotherapy (doxycycline, bleomycin, OK-432) works best for macrocystic lymphatic malformations; sirolimus, an mTOR inhibitor, improved most complicated vascular anomalies in a phase 2 trial (Adams et al., Pediatrics 2016)."],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s3-3","Vascular malformations","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · Vascular anomalies","The capillary malformation , or port-wine stain, is a flat pink to purple patch present at birth, often in a trigeminal distribution, which darkens and thickens into \"cobblestones\" with age. Involvement of V1 raises the question of Sturge–Weber syndrome , with leptomeningeal angiomatosis, seizures and glaucoma. It is treated with the pulsed-dye laser. The venous malformation is a soft, bluish, compressible mass that swells when dependent or with a Valsalva manoeuvre. Imaging shows phleboliths , thrombosis makes it painful, and large lesions raise the D-dimer. It is treated by sclerotherapy with ethanol, sodium tetradecyl sulfate or bleomycin, by the Nd:YAG laser, or by excision of localised lesions, while compression and head elevation ease symptoms. The lymphatic malformation , macrocystic or microcystic, is described with the congenital neck masses . The arteriovenous malformation is a high-flow lesion with a nidus : warm and pulsatile, with a thrill and bruit. It is quiescent in childhood and expands at puberty, in pregnancy or after trauma, causing ulceration, bleeding, bone destruction and heart failure, and is staged by Schobinger as quiescence, expansion, destruction and decompensation. Treatment is superselective embolisation followed within days by complete surgical excision of the nidus with reconstruction. The feeding vessels must never be ligated proximally : ligation does not cure, it recruits collaterals, and it blocks future embolisation. An arteriovenous fistula , usually traumatic with one or two connections, is closed endovascularly or surgically."],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s4-1","Viva essentials","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Carotid body tumour: Fontaine sign and bruit; splaying, the lyre sign and salt-and-pepper MRI; no biopsy; metanephrines and SDH genetics; Shamblin groups; complications including first bite syndrome and baroreflex failure. Vagal paraganglioma versus schwannoma: vessel displacement and flow voids. Haemangioma versus malformation: natural history and GLUT-1; propranolol; subglottic haemangioma with beard-distribution lesions. AVM: embolise and excise; never ligate proximally."],["sec","cl:t-vascneck~s4-2","Common pitfalls","Paragangliomas, neural tumours and vascular anomalies · For the examination","Common pitfalls Needling a pulsatile mass at the bifurcation. Operating on a functioning paraganglioma, or on a patient with an undiagnosed phaeochromocytoma, without alpha blockade. Resecting both carotid body tumours at one sitting. Calling a venous malformation a \"haemangioma\" and waiting for it to involute."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s1-1","Definitions","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Overview","1.1.1 Vertigo A false sense of rotation or movement of oneself or the surroundings, from asymmetry in the vestibular system. 1.1.2 Other kinds of dizziness Disequilibrium: unsteadiness without a sense of rotation. Oscillopsia: the visual world jumps with head movement, typical of bilateral vestibular loss. Presyncope: light-headedness from reduced cerebral perfusion (cardiac, orthostatic, hypoglycaemic). Physiological: motion sickness and height vertigo."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s1-2","Peripheral or central","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Overview","Causes of vertigo Peripheral BPPV, vestibular neuritis labyrinthitis, Ménière's disease, perilymph fistula, superior canal dehiscence, ototoxicity, trauma, syphilis Central Posterior circulation stroke vertebrobasilar insufficiency, Wallenberg syndrome, vestibular migraine, multiple sclerosis, cerebellar and brainstem tumours, epilepsy Other Cervical ocular, psychogenic, cardiovascular"],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s2-1","Duration","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · The history","Duration of attacks Duration Typical causes Seconds Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo Minutes Vertebrobasilar ischaemia, vestibular migraine Minutes to hours Ménière's disease, recurrent vestibulopathy, vestibular migraine Days to weeks Vestibular neuritis, labyrinthitis, stroke Variable Perilymph fistula, superior canal dehiscence"],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s2-2","Triggers","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · The history","Head position (BPPV), loud sound or pressure (superior canal dehiscence, fistula), straining, neck movement, standing up (orthostatic hypotension), and migraine triggers."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s2-3","Hearing","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · The history","Hearing loss or tinnitus points to the labyrinth (labyrinthitis, Ménière's disease, fistula) or the cerebellopontine angle, or to an AICA stroke."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s2-4","Neurological symptoms","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · The history","Double vision, slurred speech, numbness, weakness, difficulty swallowing, new severe headache: central until proven otherwise."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s3-1","Nystagmus","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Examination","Look in the primary position and on gaze, with and without fixation (Frenzel glasses). Peripheral nystagmus is horizontal-torsional, beats away from the affected side and is suppressed by fixation; it is stronger looking towards the fast phase ( Alexander's law )."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s3-2","Head impulse test","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Examination","A rapid head turn: a catch-up saccade means the vestibulo-ocular reflex on that side has failed, a peripheral lesion."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s3-3","HINTS","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Examination","In acute continuous vertigo with nystagmus (the acute vestibular syndrome): a normal head impulse, direction-changing nystagmus, or a vertical skew deviation points to a stroke. New hearing loss with acute vertigo (\"HINTS plus\") also raises concern for an anterior inferior cerebellar artery stroke. HINTS is more sensitive than early MRI, which misses up to 1 in 5 posterior circulation strokes in the first 48 hours, but it is reliable only in the hands of someone trained to do it, and only in patients with continuous vertigo and nystagmus."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s3-4","Positional tests","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Examination","The Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre for posterior canal BPPV, and the supine roll test for the lateral canal."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s3-5","Other bedside tests","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Examination","Romberg and tandem gait, head shake, dynamic visual acuity (losing more than five lines suggests bilateral loss), fistula test, and a full neurological and cranial nerve examination. Peripheral or central findings Finding Acute peripheral Central Nystagmus Mixed horizontal-torsional Pure vertical or torsional, direction-changing Fixation Suppresses Does not suppress Head impulse Abnormal Normal Pursuit Normal Saccadic Saccades Normal Dysmetric Gait Can walk Often cannot stand Other CNS signs Absent Often present"],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s4-1","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Peripheral causes","4.1.1 Mechanism Otoconia dislodged from the utricle settle in a semicircular canal, most often the posterior canal, and move with gravity when the head changes position ( canalolithiasis ). It often follows head injury or ear infection, or occurs in older people without cause. Figure 1 The crista ampullaris with its cupula, and the macula of the utricle and saccule whose otoconia cause BPPV. From the anatomy atlas 4.1.2 Picture Brief vertigo, under a minute, on lying down, rolling over or looking up. No hearing loss. 4.1.3 Dix-Hallpike nystagmus Rotatory and geotropic, after a latency of a few seconds, rising then fading within a minute, reversing on sitting up, and fatiguing on repetition. 4.1.4 Treatment The Epley manoeuvre repositions the debris into the utricle; about 80% are cured by one manoeuvre and over 90% after up to four. The Semont manoeuvre is an equal alternative; Brandt-Daroff exercises are less effective. Most cases resolve in months anyway. Surgery (posterior canal occlusion or singular neurectomy) is rarely needed. Figure 2 The Epley manoeuvre for posterior canal BPPV, with the position of the patient and of the otolith debris in the canal at each step. Dhingra, fig. 7.1 4.1.5 Other canals The posterior canal causes about 90 to 95% of BPPV because it is the lowest canal when lying down; the horizontal (lateral) canal causes 5 to 15%, and the anterior canal very few. The occasional Epley manoeuvre converts posterior into horizontal canal BPPV. Horizontal canal: diagnosed with the supine roll test, which produces horizontal nystagmus on turning to either sid"],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s4-2","Vestibular neuritis","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Peripheral causes","4.2.1 Mechanism Probably viral inflammation of the vestibular nerve or ganglion. 4.2.2 Picture Sudden, severe, continuous vertigo with nausea and vomiting, lasting days and settling over two to three weeks, without hearing loss. Nystagmus beats away from the affected side; the head impulse is abnormal towards it; calorics are reduced. BPPV follows in up to 15%. 4.2.3 Treatment Short-course vestibular sedatives for the first few days, then early mobilisation and vestibular rehabilitation. Steroids started early speed recovery of caloric function in the short term, but do not clearly improve long-term symptoms. Failure to improve at two to three weeks means a central lesion, such as a cerebellar infarct, must be excluded."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s4-3","Labyrinthitis","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Peripheral causes","As neuritis, but with hearing loss, from viral or bacterial infection of the labyrinth itself ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis )."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s4-4","Ménière's disease","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Peripheral causes","Attacks of vertigo lasting hours with fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus and fullness ( Ménière's disease )."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s4-5","Recurrent vestibulopathy","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Peripheral causes","Ménière-like spells without hearing symptoms. About 60% remit; a minority become Ménière's disease or BPPV."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s4-6","Perilymph fistula","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Peripheral causes","Episodic vertigo and fluctuating hearing after stapes surgery, barotrauma, heavy lifting or coughing. Bed rest with the head up and laxatives; explore if worsening ( Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis )."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s4-7","Superior canal dehiscence","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Peripheral causes","Vertigo provoked by sound and pressure, autophony and a low-frequency conductive loss with intact reflexes ( Superior canal dehiscence )."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s4-8","Bilateral vestibular loss","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Peripheral causes","Oscillopsia and unsteadiness in the dark, without true vertigo, most often from aminoglycosides ( Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease ). Cerebellar ataxia with bilateral vestibular loss is called CABV , or CANVAS when sensory neuropathy is added."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s5-1","Posterior circulation stroke","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Central causes","Posterior circulation stroke syndromes Feature PICA AICA SCA Vertigo, nystagmus, ataxia Yes Yes Yes Hearing loss, tinnitus No Yes No Facial palsy Sometimes Yes No Dysphagia, hoarseness Yes No No Contralateral loss of vibration and position No No Yes Figure 3 Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome from occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery. Dhingra, fig. 7.2 5.1.1 Wallenberg syndrome Violent vertigo with hoarseness, dysphagia, ipsilateral Horner syndrome, ipsilateral facial and contralateral body loss of pain and temperature, and ataxia."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s5-2","Vertebrobasilar insufficiency","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Central causes","Attacks lasting minutes in patients over 50, with visual disturbance, diplopia or drop attacks, sometimes on neck movement."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s5-3","Vestibular migraine","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Central causes","Attacks of vertigo, minutes to hours, with or near migraine headache, often in young women with a family history; response to migraine treatment supports the diagnosis."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s5-4","Multiple sclerosis","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Central causes","Young adults with vertigo and pendular or dissociated nystagmus, and other neurological episodes."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s5-5","Other central causes","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Central causes","Cerebellar and brainstem tumours, Chiari malformation, idiopathic intracranial hypertension, encephalitis and epilepsy."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s6-1","Cervical vertigo","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Other causes","Vertigo with neck movement after a neck injury; controversial and probably over-diagnosed."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s6-2","Cardiovascular","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Other causes","Presyncope from arrhythmia, orthostatic hypotension or hypoglycaemia; not true rotatory vertigo."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s6-3","Psychogenic","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Other causes","Vague floating or swaying, with anxiety, no nystagmus and normal tests. Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness often follows an acute vestibular event."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~c7","Management","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Management","Principles. Acute vertigo on call Ask about timing, triggers, hearing and neurological symptoms. Examine nystagmus, head impulse, skew and gait (HINTS). Any focal sign, new severe headache, inability to stand, or a normal head impulse with nystagmus: treat as stroke until proven otherwise. Dix-Hallpike only once the neck and the patient's condition allow it. Give antiemetics or vestibular sedatives for a few days only."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s7-1","Vestibular rehabilitation","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Management","Head and eye exercises that drive central compensation, the mainstay after any peripheral loss. Formal vestibular tests are covered in Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory ."],["ev","cl:t-vertigo~s7-2","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes: current evidence","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Management","The American BPPV guideline recommends diagnosis with the Dix-Hallpike and supine roll tests, repositioning manoeuvres as treatment, and no routine imaging, vestibular testing or vestibular suppressants (Bhattacharyya et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017). A Cochrane review found the Epley manoeuvre resolved vertigo in 56% compared with 21% after a sham manoeuvre (Hilton and Pinder, 2014). In acute vestibular syndrome, HINTS had a pooled sensitivity of 95.5% and specificity of 71% for posterior circulation stroke (Krishnan et al., Eur Stroke J 2019). A Cochrane review found moderate to strong evidence that vestibular rehabilitation is safe and effective for unilateral peripheral vestibular loss (McDonnell and Hillier, 2015), while steroids for vestibular neuritis improved caloric recovery at one month but not at 12 months (Fishman et al., 2011)."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s7-2","Avoid long-term sedatives","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · Management","They slow compensation. Pearls An acoustic neuroma ( Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours ) usually causes unsteadiness, not severe vertigo. Fatigable, delayed, torsional positional nystagmus is peripheral; immediate, persistent or vertical nystagmus is central. A positive fistula test: perilymph fistula, cholesteatoma with a fistula, Ménière's disease, superior canal dehiscence, or syphilis."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s8-1","Viva essentials","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Peripheral versus central vertigo and nystagmus. BPPV: mechanism, Dix-Hallpike findings, Epley manoeuvre. Vestibular neuritis versus labyrinthitis. HINTS in acute vertigo. Wallenberg syndrome."],["sec","cl:t-vertigo~s8-2","Common pitfalls","Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes · For the examination","Common pitfalls Labelling a stroke as neuritis because the patient is vomiting. Missing a normal head impulse in continuous vertigo. Keeping patients on vestibular sedatives for weeks. Diagnosing BPPV without seeing the nystagmus."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s1-1","The end organs","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Overview","How to use these tests in the dizzy patient is covered in Vertigo: BPPV, neuritis and central causes . The three semicircular canals sense angular (rotational) acceleration; the two otolith organs, utricle and saccule, sense linear acceleration and head tilt. Figure 1 The crista ampullaris of a canal, and the macula of an otolith organ. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-vest~s1-2","The nerves","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Overview","The superior vestibular nerve supplies the lateral and superior canals and the utricle; the inferior vestibular nerve supplies the posterior canal and the saccule. Figure 2 Central vestibular pathways. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-vest~s1-3","Physiology to remember","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Overview","At rest both labyrinths fire equally. If the right labyrinth fails, the brain reads the imbalance as turning to the left: the eyes drift slowly to the right and flick back to the left. So nystagmus beats away from a dead labyrinth (a paretic lesion) and towards an irritated one."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s1-4","Ewald's laws","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Overview","The eyes move in the plane of the stimulated canal. In the lateral canal, flow towards the ampulla (ampullopetal) stimulates more than flow away from it; in the vertical canals the reverse is true."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s1-5","What each test measures","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Overview","What each test measures Test Measures Caloric test Each lateral canal, at low frequency Rotary chair Both lateral canals together Video head impulse test Each of the six canals, at high frequency Cervical VEMP Saccule and inferior vestibular nerve Ocular VEMP Utricle and superior vestibular nerve Posturography How the patient uses vestibular, visual and proprioceptive input"],["sec","cl:t-vest~s2-1","Spontaneous nystagmus","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Bedside examination","Look in the primary position and in each direction of gaze, with and without fixation (Frenzel glasses remove fixation). Peripheral nystagmus is horizontal-torsional, suppressed by fixation, and stronger looking towards the fast phase. 2.1.1 Degrees (Alexander's law) First degree: only when looking towards the fast phase. Second degree: also looking straight ahead. Third degree: in all directions of gaze. 2.1.2 Central patterns Downbeat: craniocervical junction (Chiari malformation, cerebellar degeneration). Upbeat: brainstem. Pure torsional: brainstem. Pendular: multiple sclerosis and congenital nystagmus."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s2-2","Head impulse test","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Bedside examination","A rapid head turn; a catch-up saccade shows a failed vestibulo-ocular reflex on that side."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s2-3","Dynamic visual acuity","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Bedside examination","Losing more than five lines on a chart while the head is shaken suggests bilateral vestibular loss."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s2-4","Positional tests","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Bedside examination","2.4.1 Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre With the patient sitting, turn the head 45 degrees to one side, then lie them back quickly with the head 30 degrees below horizontal. Watch for latency, duration, direction and fatigue. Figure 3 The Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre. Dhingra, fig. 6.1 Positional nystagmus Feature Peripheral Central Latency 2–20 seconds None Duration Under a minute Over a minute Direction Fixed, towards the undermost ear Changes Fatigue Yes No Vertigo Severe None or slight"],["sec","cl:t-vest~s2-5","Fistula test","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Bedside examination","Pressure on the tragus or with a Siegle speculum causes nystagmus when the labyrinth is open: lateral canal erosion, a fenestration cavity, superior canal dehiscence, or a window leak. It needs a working labyrinth: a dead ear, or cholesteatoma sealing the fistula, gives a false negative. Hennebert's sign is a positive test without a fistula, in congenital syphilis and some Ménière's disease. Fistulae themselves are covered in Labyrinthine fistula and labyrinthitis ."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s2-6","Balance and cerebellar tests","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Bedside examination","Romberg, tandem walking, Unterberger's stepping test, and past-pointing: falling and past-pointing go towards the side of the slow phase. Cerebellar hemisphere disease causes dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia and rebound; midline disease causes a wide gait and truncal ataxia."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s3-1","Caloric test","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Laboratory tests","Warm or cold water (or air) in the canal sets up convection currents in the lateral canal. With the patient lying and the head raised 30 degrees, the lateral canal is vertical and responds best. Figure 4 The Fitzgerald-Hallpike caloric test: the head is raised 30 degrees to make the lateral canal vertical. Dhingra, fig. 6.2 3.1.1 COWS Cold water makes nystagmus beat to the opposite side; warm to the same side. 3.1.2 Reading it Canal paresis: one side responds less (most labs use a cut-off of about 20 to 25%; texts quote 15 to 30%). Directional preponderance: nystagmus stronger in one direction (more than about 25 to 30%). Bilateral weakness: bilateral vestibular loss. 3.1.3 A worked example Each ear is irrigated with warm (44 °C) and cool (30 °C) water or air, and the peak slow-phase velocity of the nystagmus is measured in degrees per second. Jongkees' formula compares the two sides: canal paresis = [(RW + RC) − (LW + LC)] ÷ (RW + RC + LW + LC) × 100. For example, with right warm 30, right cool 28, left warm 12 and left cool 10°/s, the result is (58 − 22) ÷ 80 × 100 = 45%, a left canal paresis. Directional preponderance compares right-beating (RW + LC) with left-beating (LW + RC) responses in the same way. If the sum of all four responses is very small (under about 6 to 8°/s per irrigation), the result is bilateral weakness, confirmed by rotary chair or head impulse testing. Failure of fixation to suppress the nystagmus (fixation index) points to a central lesion. Figure 5 Calorigrams: (A) normal, (B) left canal paresis, (C) left directional preponderance. Dhingra, fig. 6."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s3-2","Electronystagmography and videonystagmography","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Laboratory tests","Record eye movements during calorics, positional tests, saccades, pursuit and optokinetic testing. Central signs: direction-changing nystagmus, failure of fixation suppression, abnormal saccades and pursuit, and hyperactive calorics."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s3-3","Video head impulse test","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Laboratory tests","Small, fast, unpredictable head turns are made while a lightweight camera records the eye. The gain is eye velocity divided by head velocity: normal for the lateral canals is about 0.8 or more (vertical canals about 0.7). A weak canal has low gain and catch-up saccades : overt saccades after the head stops, which the examiner can see at the bedside, and covert saccades during the movement, which only video can detect. The test is quick, tests all six canals, and measures the high frequencies of head movement used in daily life, whereas calorics test only the lateral canal at a very low frequency; the two are complementary, and Ménière's disease often shows abnormal calorics with a normal vHIT. A normal vHIT in acute continuous vertigo is a warning sign of a central cause. Figure 6 Reading a video head impulse test. On the right the eye matches the head (gain near 1). On the left the eye lags (gain 0.45) and catch-up saccades follow: a left lateral canal weakness, as in vestibular neuritis. Drawn for The ENT Companion"],["sec","cl:t-vest~s3-4","Vestibular evoked myogenic potentials","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Laboratory tests","Loud sounds stimulate the saccule (cervical VEMP from sternocleidomastoid) and utricle (ocular VEMP from the inferior oblique). They work even in deaf ears. In superior canal dehiscence thresholds are abnormally low and ocular amplitudes high; an enlarged ocular VEMP at 4 kHz is highly suggestive. Reading VEMPs Finding Cervical VEMP (saccule, inferior vestibular nerve) Ocular VEMP (utricle, superior vestibular nerve) Superior canal dehiscence Low threshold (often under 70 dB nHL) Large amplitude, low threshold: the best screening test Vestibular neuritis, superior nerve Usually preserved Absent on the affected side Vestibular neuritis, inferior nerve Absent on the affected side Preserved Ménière's disease Reduced or absent, or tuning shifted to higher frequencies Variable Vestibular schwannoma Often reduced or absent Often reduced or absent Conductive loss Air-conducted VEMP lost; bone-conducted VEMP preserved Same"],["sec","cl:t-vest~s3-5","Rotary chair","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Laboratory tests","Tests both labyrinths together over a range of frequencies; useful in bilateral loss, in children, and when a canal cannot be irrigated."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s3-6","Posturography","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Laboratory tests","Shows how the patient uses each sense to balance; useful in rehabilitation and in non-organic imbalance."],["ev","cl:t-vest~c4","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory: current evidence","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Using the tests","Classification. The Bárány Society's International Classification of Vestibular Disorders provides consensus definitions for BPPV, vestibular migraine, Ménière's disease, persistent postural-perceptual dizziness and bilateral vestibulopathy."],["sec","cl:t-vest~c4","Using the tests","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · Using the tests","When to order them. When the side or site of the lesion is unclear. Before a destructive procedure, to check the other ear. Planning rehabilitation and tracking recovery. Monitoring gentamicin effect; daily head impulse and visual acuity help detect toxicity ( Ménière's disease , Ototoxicity, noise, presbycusis and immune inner ear disease ). Pearls A canal paresis with directional preponderance away from it suggests Ménière's disease; preponderance towards the same side, a schwannoma. Fixation suppressing the nystagmus is peripheral; failure to suppress suggests a central cause."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s5-1","On call","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · For the examination","The dizzy patient at the bedside Spontaneous nystagmus, with and without fixation. Head impulse test and test of skew. Gait and cerebellar signs. Dix-Hallpike when the neck and condition allow."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s5-2","Viva essentials","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Ewald's laws and why nystagmus beats away from a dead ear. Dix-Hallpike technique and peripheral versus central positional nystagmus. Caloric test: technique, COWS, canal paresis and directional preponderance. Fistula test and Hennebert's sign. VEMP and what it tests."],["sec","cl:t-vest~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Vestibular testing: bedside and laboratory · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using water calorics with a perforation. Taking normal tests as proof of no vestibular disease. Missing central signs because the eyes were tested only with fixation."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s1-1","Motor","Vocal fold paralysis · Nerve supply","Every muscle that moves the vocal fold is supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve , except the cricothyroid (a tensor), which is supplied by the external laryngeal nerve , a branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. The interarytenoid receives fibres from both sides."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s1-2","Sensory","Vocal fold paralysis · Nerve supply","Above the vocal folds, the internal laryngeal nerve; below them, the recurrent laryngeal nerve."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s1-3","Course","Vocal fold paralysis · Nerve supply","Right recurrent nerve: leaves the vagus at the root of the neck, hooks under the subclavian artery and climbs in the tracheo-oesophageal groove. Left recurrent nerve: leaves the vagus in the mediastinum, hooks under the arch of the aorta beside the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs a much longer course; hence it is paralysed far more often. Superior laryngeal nerve: from the inferior ganglion of the vagus, behind the internal carotid artery, dividing at the greater horn of the hyoid into the internal (sensory, through the thyrohyoid membrane) and external (to cricothyroid, beside the superior thyroid artery) branches. Figure 1 The recurrent and superior laryngeal nerves and their course: the left recurrent nerve loops around the arch of the aorta, the right around the subclavian artery. Dhingra, fig. 60.1 Figure 2 The nerve supply of the larynx, and the nerves with their relations. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-vfp~c2","Positions of the cord","Vocal fold paralysis · Positions of the cord","What the folds show. Figure 3 Positions of the vocal folds: median, paramedian, intermediate (cadaveric), slight abduction and full abduction. Dhingra, fig. 60.2 Positions of the vocal fold Position Distance from midline In health In disease Median 0 Phonation Recurrent nerve paralysis Paramedian 1.5 mm Strong whisper Recurrent nerve paralysis Intermediate (cadaveric) 3.5 mm Neutral resting position Combined recurrent and superior nerve paralysis Gentle abduction 7 mm Quiet breathing Paralysis of adductors Full abduction 9.5 mm Deep inspiration None"],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s2-1","Why the paralysed cord lies near the midline","Vocal fold paralysis · Positions of the cord","Semon's law: in progressive organic lesions the abductor fibres, phylogenetically newer, are affected before the adductors, so the fold first lies near the midline. Wagner–Grossman hypothesis: with the recurrent nerve cut and the superior nerve intact, the cricothyroid still adducts the fold slightly into the paramedian position; with both nerves lost the fold lies in the cadaveric position. The position also reflects partial or misdirected regeneration , which restores tone (and synkinesis) without useful movement; a fold often lies laterally at first and medialises over months."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s3-1","By level","Vocal fold paralysis · Causes","Supranuclear: rare (bilateral cortical lesions). Nuclear (nucleus ambiguus, medulla): stroke (lateral medullary syndrome), tumours, motor neurone disease, polio, syringobulbia, multiple sclerosis, Arnold–Chiari malformation; other cranial nerves are involved. High vagal (with superior laryngeal involvement, a combined paralysis): posterior fossa tumours and basal meningitis; skull base fractures, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, glomus tumours; parapharyngeal tumours, metastatic nodes, lymphoma and penetrating injury. Low vagal and recurrent nerve: see the table. Systemic: diabetes, viral neuritis, diphtheria, syphilis, lead poisoning. Idiopathic: about 20 to 30%, many presumed viral; a diagnosis of exclusion. Causes of recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis Site Right Left Both Neck Thyroid surgery, thyroid cancer, cervical oesophageal cancer, nodes, trauma, subclavian aneurysm The same Thyroidectomy, thyroid cancer, cervical oesophageal cancer Chest Carcinoma of the right lung apex, tuberculosis of the pleura Bronchogenic carcinoma, mediastinal nodes, thoracic oesophageal cancer, aortic aneurysm, enlarged left atrium (Ortner syndrome), cardiac and thoracic surgery, ductus ligation Rare Left vocal fold palsy Bronchogenic carcinoma must be excluded in every unexplained left-sided paralysis: image the whole course of the nerve from the skull base to the aortic arch with contrast CT (or MRI and CT), and examine the thyroid, oesophagus and neck."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s3-2","Commonest causes overall","Vocal fold paralysis · Causes","Malignancy (lung, thyroid Thyroid nodules, cancer and goitre , oesophagus) and surgery (thyroid, cervical spine through an anterior approach, cardiothoracic) lead the list; thyroidectomy is the commonest cause of bilateral paralysis."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s4-1","Unilateral","Vocal fold paralysis · Recurrent nerve paralysis","All intrinsic muscles on that side are paralysed except cricothyroid, and the fold lies median or paramedian. About a third of patients have no symptoms; others have a weak, breathy voice that tires, diplophonia, loss of projection and range, and sometimes aspiration of thin liquids. The voice often improves over months as the healthy fold learns to cross the midline."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s4-2","Bilateral (bilateral abductor paralysis)","Vocal fold paralysis · Recurrent nerve paralysis","Usually after thyroidectomy, or neuritis. Both folds lie median or paramedian, held by the unopposed cricothyroids: the voice is good but the airway is poor , with stridor and dyspnoea that worsen on exertion and with any respiratory infection, sometimes requiring an emergency tracheostomy. The paradox Bilateral recurrent palsy gives a good voice and a bad airway; bilateral combined palsy gives a bad voice, no airway problem and dangerous aspiration."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s5-1","Superior laryngeal nerve paralysis","Vocal fold paralysis · Superior laryngeal and combined paralysis","Paralysis of the cricothyroid with numbness of the supraglottis on that side; usually part of a combined lesion, or the external branch injured in thyroid surgery. The voice is weak and cannot be raised in pitch, which singers notice at once. The glottis looks askew , with the anterior commissure rotated to the healthy side; the paralysed fold is shorter, lax and wavy, and flaps (sagging on inspiration, bulging on expiration). Electromyography of the cricothyroid confirms it. Voice therapy. Bilateral: paralysis with anaesthesia of the upper larynx causes repeated aspiration, cough and choking, and a weak, husky voice; it may recover after neuritis; tracheostomy with a cuffed tube and tube feeding, and epiglottopexy for persistent aspiration; cricothyroid approximation raises the pitch."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s5-2","Combined (complete) paralysis","Vocal fold paralysis · Superior laryngeal and combined paralysis","Unilateral: after thyroidectomy injuring both nerves, or with lesions of the nucleus ambiguus or vagus above the origin of the superior laryngeal nerve. The fold lies in the cadaveric position , the healthy fold cannot meet it, and the result is a hoarse, breathy voice, aspiration of liquids and a weak cough. Bilateral: both folds cadaveric with an insensitive larynx: aphonia, aspiration, inability to cough and recurrent pneumonia. Tracheostomy and gastrostomy, then procedures that protect the lungs: epiglottopexy, vocal fold plication (both reversible), laryngotracheal separation or diversion, and, as a last resort, laryngectomy."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~c6","Evaluation","Vocal fold paralysis · Evaluation","Paralysis or fixation? Laryngoscopy: flexible and stroboscopic, observing movement, position, the glottic gap, the level of the folds and arytenoid tilt; confirm with phonation, sniffing and coughing. Fixation versus paralysis: posterior glottic scarring, cricoarytenoid arthritis or dislocation and tumour can fix a fold; at direct laryngoscopy the arytenoid is palpated for passive mobility. Laryngeal electromyography: distinguishes paralysis from fixation, shows synkinesis and helps predict recovery (absent or synkinetic activity predicts poor recovery). Imaging: contrast CT from the skull base to the aortic arch, or MRI of the brainstem and skull base for high lesions; chest imaging; thyroid ultrasound. Swallowing assessment: FEES or videofluoroscopy for aspiration. Blood tests where indicated (glucose, serology)."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s7-1","Unilateral paralysis","Vocal fold paralysis · Treatment","The aim is to close the glottic gap. Voice therapy early, while awaiting recovery or compensation over six to twelve months. Injection laryngoplasty (medialisation by injection, through the mouth or the neck, awake or under anaesthesia): temporary fillers (carboxymethylcellulose 2 to 3 months, hyaluronic acid 4 to 9 months, collagen) early, even within weeks, especially if aspiration is a problem; longer-lasting calcium hydroxylapatite (about 18 months) and autologous fat (variable survival). Teflon is abandoned because of granulomas. Type I thyroplasty (medialisation laryngoplasty): a window in the thyroid lamina and a silastic or Gore-Tex implant in the paraglottic space push the fold medially; done under local anaesthesia so that the voice can be tuned; for permanent paralysis with a gap of a few millimetres and symmetrical arytenoids. Arytenoid adduction: a suture through the muscular process rotates the arytenoid inwards, mimicking the lateral cricoarytenoid; for a wide posterior gap, a lateralised vocal process or a level difference; usually with thyroplasty. Reinnervation: ansa cervicalis to recurrent nerve anastomosis restores bulk and tone (not movement); useful when the nerve is known to be cut and in children, who would outgrow an implant. In a randomised trial against thyroplasty, overall results were similar, but reinnervation did better under 52 years and thyroplasty better over 52 (Paniello et al., Laryngoscope 2011)."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s7-2","Bilateral abductor paralysis","Vocal fold paralysis · Treatment","The aim is an airway without ruining the voice; any procedure that widens the glottis worsens the voice. Tracheostomy ( Tracheostomy and airway endoscopy ) : the safest, the gold standard; the voice is preserved with finger occlusion or a speaking valve. Endoscopic glottic widening: posterior transverse cordotomy (Kashima), cutting the fold off the vocal process with the laser; partial medial arytenoidectomy ; suture lateralisation of the fold; repeated if needed. Older external procedures: arytenoidectomy and arytenoidopexy (Woodman), and type II thyroplasty. Botulinum toxin to the adductors (temporary); reinnervation and laryngeal pacing (selected, experimental). In the first months after thyroidectomy, a temporary tracheostomy and waiting is wise, since many palsies recover."],["ev","cl:t-vfp~s7-3","Vocal fold paralysis: current evidence","Vocal fold paralysis · Treatment","Early injection. Early temporary injection laryngoplasty, within six months of onset, was associated with fewer later open framework procedures in a retrospective series (Friedman et al., Laryngoscope 2010). Assessment. An international consensus recommends a minimum voice assessment for unilateral paralysis: videolaryngostroboscopy, perceptual rating (GRBAS), maximum phonation time and the Voice Handicap Index (Mattei et al., Eur Ann Otorhinolaryngol Head Neck Dis 2018). Thyroid surgery. The American guideline recommends documenting voice before thyroidectomy, laryngoscopy before surgery if voice is impaired or there is a cancer with possible extrathyroidal spread, and laryngoscopy after surgery when the voice has changed (Chandrasekhar et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2013). Intraoperative nerve monitoring helps identify the nerve and, with staged surgery when signal is lost on the first side, avoids most bilateral palsies."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s7-3","Isshiki's thyroplasties","Vocal fold paralysis · Treatment","Type I: medialisation. Type II: lateralisation, for the airway. Type III: shortening and relaxation, to lower the pitch (mutational falsetto, female-to-male voice). Type IV: lengthening and tensing, to raise the pitch (male-to-female voice, bowed or lax folds)."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~c8","Congenital paralysis","Vocal fold paralysis · Congenital paralysis","In infants. Unilateral (birth trauma, cardiac and great vessel anomalies, cardiac surgery) gives a weak cry and feeding problems; bilateral (Arnold–Chiari malformation, hydrocephalus, birth haemorrhage, meningocele) gives stridor needing tracheostomy. Many recover; definitive surgery is delayed ( Congenital lesions of the larynx )."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s9-1","Viva essentials","Vocal fold paralysis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Nerve supply and why the left recurrent nerve is more often paralysed. Positions of the cord, Semon's law and the Wagner–Grossman hypothesis. Causes by level; exclude carcinoma of the lung. Unilateral versus bilateral recurrent palsy: voice and airway. Superior laryngeal nerve palsy: askew glottis, loss of pitch. Treatment: injection, thyroplasty and its types, arytenoid adduction, cordotomy and tracheostomy."],["sec","cl:t-vfp~s9-2","Common pitfalls","Vocal fold paralysis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling a left palsy idiopathic without imaging to the aortic arch. Treating a fixed arytenoid as a paralysis without palpation or electromyography. Doing an irreversible glottic widening soon after thyroidectomy when the nerve may recover."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s1-1","Definition","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Hoarseness","Hoarseness (dysphonia) is roughness of voice from irregularity of the period or intensity of successive sound waves. Normal voice needs folds that (1) approximate properly, (2) have the right size, mass and stiffness, and (3) vibrate regularly in the air stream. Failed approximation: paralysis, fixation, a mass between the folds, sulcus, bowing. Altered size and mass: oedema, nodules, polyps, tumours (more); surgical loss or fibrosis (less). Altered stiffness: paralysis (less), scar and spasm (more). Irregular vibration: congestion, haemorrhage, lesions on the edge."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s1-2","Causes","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Hoarseness","Causes of hoarseness Group Examples Inflammation Acute viral laryngitis, diphtheria, irritant gases; chronic laryngitis from smoking, reflux, occupation and inhaled steroids; tuberculosis, syphilis, leprosy, fungi Benign lesions ( Benign vocal fold lesions and hoarseness ) Nodules, polyp, Reinke's oedema, cyst, contact granuloma, laryngocele, amyloid, sulcus Neoplasms Papilloma, haemangioma, chondroma, granular cell tumour; leukoplakia; carcinoma and sarcoma Trauma Shouting with fold haemorrhage, external injury, foreign body, intubation Neurological Recurrent, superior laryngeal or combined paralysis ( Vocal fold paralysis ); spasmodic dysphonia; Parkinson disease, myasthenia, motor neurone disease, multiple sclerosis, essential tremor Joint fixation Cricoarytenoid arthritis, dislocation Congenital Web, cyst, laryngocele, paralysis, sulcus Systemic and hormonal Hypothyroidism, acromegaly, virilising hormones, sarcoidosis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, amyloidosis Functional Muscle tension dysphonia, ventricular dysphonia, functional aphonia, puberphonia, phonasthenia Ageing Presbyphonia with bowed, thin folds"],["sec","cl:t-voice~s1-3","Evaluation","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Hoarseness","History: onset and duration, voice use and occupation, smoking, alcohol, reflux, inhalers, systemic symptoms, surgery (thyroid, chest, neck), and red flags (pain, otalgia, dysphagia, stridor, haemoptysis, weight loss, neck mass). Listening: breathy (incomplete closure), rough (irregular vibration), strained (hyperfunction or spasm), weak, diplophonic, tremulous. Laryngoscopy: the American guideline recommends laryngoscopy for any hoarseness lasting more than four weeks, and at once with red flags (smoking, a neck mass, airway symptoms, recent neck or chest surgery); UK practice uses three weeks in smokers, and NICE advises a suspected-cancer referral for persistent unexplained hoarseness at 45 or over. Do not give antibiotics, steroids or antireflux drugs for hoarseness before the larynx has been seen. Stroboscopy: the mucosal wave, closure pattern and symmetry. Neck, chest, cardiovascular and neurological examination for the cause of a paralysis; imaging, endoscopy and laboratory tests as indicated. Direct and microlaryngoscopy for biopsy, palpation of the arytenoids and treatment. Voice measures: maximum phonation time, acoustic analysis, the GRBAS rating, and patient-reported voice handicap indices."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s2-1","Muscle tension dysphonia","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Functional voice disorders","Excessive tension of the laryngeal and neck muscles during phonation: strained voice, vocal fatigue and neck ache, with supraglottic squeezing, a posterior gap or anteroposterior compression on laryngoscopy. It may be primary or compensatory. Voice therapy with laryngeal manual therapy is the treatment."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s2-2","Ventricular dysphonia (dysphonia plicae ventricularis)","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Functional voice disorders","The false folds take over phonation: a rough, low-pitched, unpleasant voice; on phonation the false folds meet and hide the true folds. Secondary: compensating for true fold paralysis, fixation, excision or tumour; difficult to treat. Functional: in a normal larynx, often psychogenic; the voice starts normally and then roughens. Voice therapy and counselling."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s2-3","Functional (psychogenic) aphonia","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Functional voice disorders","Sudden loss of voice, usually in emotionally stressed young women of fifteen to thirty, communicating in a whisper without other laryngeal symptoms. The folds stay apart on attempted phonation but close normally on coughing , and the cough is loud and clear. Reassure the patient that the larynx is normal, restore voice through coughing, humming and throat clearing exercises, and address the stress; psychotherapy as needed."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s2-4","Puberphonia (mutational falsetto)","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Functional voice disorders","Persistence of the high-pitched childhood voice in a physically normal adolescent boy or young man, often from emotional immaturity or social insecurity, while the larynx has matured. Gutzmann's pressure test : pressing the thyroid prominence backwards and downwards relaxes the overstretched folds and produces a low-pitched voice; the patient learns to reproduce it, first with pressure, then without, through syllables, words and sentences. The prognosis is good; type III thyroplasty is a last resort."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s2-5","Phonasthenia","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Functional voice disorders","Weakness and fatigue of the phonatory muscles after misuse or laryngitis: a voice that tires quickly. Laryngoscopy shows an elliptical gap between the folds (thyroarytenoid weakness), a triangular posterior gap (interarytenoid weakness), or a keyhole glottis (both). Voice rest, vocal hygiene and therapy. Figure 1 The glottis in phonasthenia: (A) weakness of the thyroarytenoids, (B) of the interarytenoid, (C) of both, giving a keyhole glottis. Dhingra, fig. 63.1"],["sec","cl:t-voice~s3-1","Nature","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Spasmodic dysphonia","A focal dystonia, thought to arise in the basal ganglia, with involuntary spasms of the laryngeal muscles during speech; the larynx is structurally normal and behaves normally for breathing, laughing, crying, whispering and often singing."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s3-2","Types","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Spasmodic dysphonia","Adductor (about 90%): spasms of the thyroarytenoid and lateral cricoarytenoid; a strained, strangled, effortful voice with breaks, especially on words beginning with vowels. Abductor: spasms of the posterior cricoarytenoid; a breathy voice with breathy breaks after voiceless consonants (p, t, k), worse with stress and on the telephone. Mixed. Voice tremor often coexists. Exclude Parkinson disease, essential tremor, multiple sclerosis, motor neurone disease and tardive dyskinesia."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s3-3","Treatment","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Spasmodic dysphonia","Botulinum toxin A is the treatment of choice: small doses into the thyroarytenoid–lateral cricoarytenoid complex on one or both sides for adductor spasm, through the cricothyroid membrane under EMG guidance or through a flexible scope. Benefit lasts about three to four months; side effects are temporary breathiness and choking on fluids. For abductor spasm, injection into the posterior cricoarytenoid is harder and less effective, and is staged one side at a time to avoid airway obstruction. Voice therapy helps alongside toxin but not alone; psychotherapy is ineffective. Surgery: recurrent nerve section (abandoned because symptoms return); selective adductor denervation–reinnervation; type II (lateralisation) thyroplasty; thyroarytenoid myectomy."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s3-4","Newer terms and variants","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Spasmodic dysphonia","Laryngeal dystonia is the term a recent multidisciplinary consensus proposed to replace \"spasmodic dysphonia\". It is task-specific: speaking is affected, but whispering, laughing and crying are not. Newer variants are singer's dystonia, affecting only singing, and adductor respiratory dystonia, with adductor spasms on inspiration causing stridor. Bedside test Ask the patient to count from 80 to 90 (voiced sounds, worse in adductor dystonia) and from 60 to 70 (voiceless sounds, worse in abductor dystonia)."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s4-1","Vocal tremor","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Other movement disorders","May be isolated or part of essential tremor of the head and hands, and is seen as rhythmic movement of the larynx and pharynx on sustained phonation. Botulinum toxin reduces it partly."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s4-2","Parkinson's disease","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Other movement disorders","A quiet, breathy, monotonous voice with bursts of rapid speech, usually after the limb signs. Intensive loudness-focused voice therapy (such as the Lee Silverman programme) helps."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s4-3","Inducible laryngeal obstruction","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Other movement disorders","Formerly paradoxical vocal fold movement or vocal cord dysfunction: inappropriate adduction of the folds on inspiration, triggered by exercise, irritants or cough, and often mistaken for asthma. Laryngoscopy is usually normal unless done during symptoms or provocation (continuous laryngoscopy during exercise for the exercise-induced form), and many patients describe throat tightness and altered breathing rather than visible closure."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s4-4","Chronic refractory cough","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Other movement disorders","Cough over eight weeks, affecting 5 to 10% of adults, often coexisting with inducible laryngeal obstruction. Both are best managed in a multidisciplinary upper airway clinic, with breathing and laryngeal control techniques taught by speech and language therapists; low-dose gabapentin or pregabalin helps some patients with refractory cough."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s5-1","Hyponasality (rhinolalia clausa)","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Resonance and fluency","Lack of nasal resonance on the nasal consonants m, n and ng (\"my nose\" sounds like \"by dose\"), from blockage of the nose or nasopharynx."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s5-2","Hypernasality (rhinolalia aperta)","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Resonance and fluency","Too much nasal resonance on oral sounds, with nasal escape, because the nasopharynx is not sealed off from the oropharynx or there is a communication between mouth and nose. Causes of altered nasal resonance Hyponasality Hypernasality Common cold, allergic rhinitis Velopharyngeal insufficiency Nasal polyps and tumours Cleft palate and submucous cleft Adenoids, nasopharyngeal mass Congenitally short palate, large nasopharynx Choanal atresia Palatal paralysis Habitual or familial After adenoidectomy, oronasal fistula; habitual or familial Hypernasality is assessed by perceptual rating, nasometry, nasendoscopy and videofluoroscopy of velopharyngeal closure, and treated by speech therapy, prosthetic palatal lifts, or surgery (pharyngeal flap, sphincter pharyngoplasty, palate repair, posterior wall augmentation; Cleft lip and palate and velopharyngeal insufficiency )."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s5-3","Stuttering","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Resonance and fluency","A disorder of fluency: hesitation, repetitions, prolongations and blocks, with secondary mannerisms such as grimacing, blinking and head jerks. Normal dysfluency is common between two and four years; parental overreaction may fix it. Prevention by educating parents; treatment by speech therapy and psychological support to reduce the fear of speaking."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s5-4","Dysarthria and aphasia","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Resonance and fluency","Dysarthria is a motor disorder of speech production (flaccid, spastic, ataxic, hypokinetic, hyperkinetic); aphasia is a disorder of language (expressive, receptive). Both are neurological and need neurological assessment."],["ev","cl:t-voice~c6","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders: current evidence","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Voice care","Hoarseness. The American guideline quoted under Evaluation also recommends voice therapy for hoarseness that impairs quality of life (Stachler et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018). Laryngeal dystonia. Botulinum toxin is the treatment of choice: the American Academy of Neurology rated it probably effective for adductor spasmodic dysphonia (Simpson et al., Neurology 2008), and a multidisciplinary panel convened by the US National Institutes of Health proposed the term \"laryngeal dystonia\" (Simonyan et al., Neurology 2021). Refractory cough. In a randomised trial, gabapentin improved cough-specific quality of life over placebo (Ryan et al., Lancet 2012)."],["sec","cl:t-voice~c6","Voice care","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · Voice care","Prevention and therapy. Vocal hygiene Hydrate well; humidify dry air. Avoid smoking, excess alcohol and caffeine; treat reflux. Avoid shouting, prolonged loud talking, whispering when hoarse, and habitual throat clearing. Rest the voice after heavy use; warm up before performing. Use amplification in noisy rooms and classrooms. Voice therapy by a speech and language therapist retrains breathing, onset, pitch, resonance and muscle tension; it is the first treatment for nodules, muscle tension dysphonia, functional disorders and many benign lesions, and supports surgery."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s7-1","Viva essentials","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Definition and causes of hoarseness, and the two-to-three-week rule. Functional aphonia: the folds close on coughing. Puberphonia and Gutzmann's test. Phonasthenia: elliptical, triangular and keyhole glottis. Spasmodic dysphonia: types and botulinum toxin. Hyponasality versus hypernasality with causes."],["sec","cl:t-voice~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Hoarseness, voice and speech disorders · For the examination","Common pitfalls Diagnosing functional aphonia without seeing the larynx. Calling spasmodic dysphonia psychogenic. Removing adenoids in a child with a submucous cleft and causing hypernasality."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s1-1","Definition","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Overview","A benign tumour of Schwann cells, usually arising from the vestibular division of the eighth nerve in the internal auditory canal. Acoustic neuroma is the traditional, inaccurate name: it is rarely acoustic and never a neuroma."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s1-2","Epidemiology","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Overview","It accounts for 8 to 10% of intracranial tumours and about 80% of cerebellopontine angle tumours, usually presenting between 40 and 60. Most are sporadic and one-sided. Bilateral tumours mean neurofibromatosis type 2 ."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s1-3","Why it matters","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Overview","It is the commonest serious cause of asymmetric sensorineural hearing loss, and every asymmetric loss is screened for it. Untreated large tumours compress the brainstem and cause hydrocephalus."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s2-1","Origin and growth","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Pathology","The tumour arises near the glial-Schwann cell junction in the internal auditory canal, widening the canal before it grows into the cerebellopontine angle. Growth is slow, around 1 to 2 mm a year, and some tumours never grow at all. Figure 1 The fundus of the internal auditory canal, showing the position of the facial, cochlear and superior and inferior vestibular nerves. Dhingra, fig. 18.1 Figure 2 The fundus of the internal auditory canal, with Bill's bar and the transverse crest separating the facial, cochlear and vestibular nerves. From the anatomy atlas"],["sec","cl:t-vs~s2-2","Histology","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Pathology","Two patterns: Antoni A , compact spindle cells with palisading nuclei (Verocay bodies), and Antoni B , loose, cystic tissue."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s2-3","Neurofibromatosis type 2","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Pathology","Autosomal dominant, from loss of the NF2 gene on chromosome 22. Bilateral vestibular schwannomas, often with meningiomas and other schwannomas, present in young adults."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s2-4","Size","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Pathology","Size and spread Stage Size What it affects Intracanalicular In the canal Eighth nerve Small Up to 1.5 cm Into the angle Medium 1.5 to 4 cm Fifth nerve, cerebellum Large Over 4 cm Brainstem, fourth ventricle, hydrocephalus Cut-offs vary between texts: for treatment decisions, small usually means up to about 1.5 to 2 cm and large over about 3 cm (see Management). Figure 3 Expansion of an acoustic neuroma: (A) intracanalicular, (B) into the cerebellopontine angle, (C) pressing on the fifth nerve, (D) onwards to the brainstem and lower cranial nerves. Dhingra, fig. 18.2"],["sec","cl:t-vs~s3-1","Eighth nerve","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Clinical features","3.1.1 Hearing loss Progressive, one-sided sensorineural loss with poor speech discrimination, often far worse than the pure-tone thresholds suggest. About one in ten presents as sudden hearing loss ( Sudden sensorineural hearing loss ). 3.1.2 Tinnitus One-sided and persistent. 3.1.3 Balance Unsteadiness rather than true vertigo, because the brain compensates as slowly as the tumour grows."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s3-2","Fifth nerve","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Clinical features","At around 2.5 cm: reduced corneal reflex, often the earliest sign, then facial numbness, which is present in only 7 to 9% of patients at diagnosis."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s3-3","Seventh nerve","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Clinical features","The sensory fibres fail first: numbness of the posterior meatal wall ( Hitselberger's sign ), loss of taste and reduced lacrimation. Motor weakness of the face comes late, in only about 5% at presentation, even with large tumours; a schwannoma with early facial palsy should make you think of another diagnosis, such as a facial nerve schwannoma, a meningioma or a malignant tumour."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s3-4","Lower cranial nerves","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Clinical features","Ninth and tenth: dysphagia, hoarseness and a reduced gag."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s3-5","Cerebellum and brainstem","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Clinical features","Ataxia, nystagmus, and finally raised intracranial pressure with headache, vomiting and papilloedema from obstructed CSF flow. Urgent Headache, vomiting, ataxia or papilloedema with a large tumour means hydrocephalus or brainstem compression: urgent neurosurgical review."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s4-1","Audiometry","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Investigations","Asymmetric sensorineural loss, poor discrimination, roll-over (discrimination falls as loudness rises), absent or decaying stapedial reflex, and abnormal tone decay: the signs of a retrocochlear lesion."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s4-2","MRI with gadolinium","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Investigations","The gold standard: finds tumours of a few millimetres. A brightly enhancing mass centred on the internal auditory canal, with a cone into the angle ( ice-cream cone )."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s4-3","Auditory brainstem response","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Investigations","Used when MRI is not possible. A prolonged inter-aural wave V latency or I–V interval suggests a lesion, but small tumours are missed. Where the books differ · Abnormal wave V difference Dhingra More than 0.2 ms. Lee 0.4 to 0.5 ms. In practice Treat a clearly prolonged difference as suspicious and get an MRI whenever possible, because ABR misses small tumours."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s4-4","Caloric test","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Investigations","Reduced on the affected side in most, normal in very small tumours."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s4-5","CT","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Investigations","If MRI cannot be done, and to show the bony canal and approach anatomy."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s4-6","Avoid lumbar puncture","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Investigations","CSF protein is raised, but a puncture with a large tumour risks herniation."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s4-7","Differential diagnosis","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Investigations","Other cerebellopontine angle lesions Lesion Distinguishing features Meningioma Broad dural base, dural tail, eccentric to the canal, calcification, hyperostosis Epidermoid Does not enhance; bright on diffusion-weighted MRI Facial schwannoma Facial palsy, enlarges the geniculate or labyrinthine segment Lipoma Bright on T1 before contrast; suppresses with fat saturation Haemangioma Ground-glass erosion; palsy out of proportion to size Arachnoid cyst Follows CSF on all sequences"],["sec","cl:t-vs~s5-1","Choosing","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Management","The decision rests on tumour size and growth, hearing in both ears, age and health, and the patient's wishes. Small tumours (mostly in the canal, up to about 1.5 to 2 cm): observation, radiosurgery or surgery are all reasonable; most are watched first. Medium tumours: radiosurgery or surgery. Large tumours compressing the brainstem (over about 3 cm): surgery, sometimes planned as near-total removal followed by radiosurgery to the remnant. Where the books differ · First choice Dhingra Favours surgical removal. Lee Presents observation, radiosurgery and microsurgery as equal options, chosen by patient and tumour. In practice Modern practice follows the second view: most small tumours are watched first."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s5-2","Observation","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Management","For small tumours, older or unfit patients, and those with good hearing. The first repeat MRI is at six months, to catch a fast-growing tumour or a wrong diagnosis, then yearly while stable, then less often. Treat if it grows. In large series only about 17% of intracanalicular and 29% of extrameatal tumours grew over four years, and growing tumours usually enlarge by 1 to 2 mm a year, so waiting rarely makes later treatment harder. The cost of waiting is hearing, which may decline even when the tumour does not grow."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s5-3","Stereotactic radiosurgery","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Management","A single session, usually 11 to 14 Gy at the margin (commonly 12 to 13 Gy), for tumours up to about 2.5 to 3 cm. Growth is controlled in 90 to 95%. A transient swelling of the tumour in the first year is expected and is not failure. Hearing is not reliably preserved and declines over the years; the facial nerve risk is about 1%; malignant change is very rare, about 1 in 1000. Surgery after failed radiosurgery carries a worse facial nerve outcome than primary surgery. Conventional external beam radiotherapy has no role."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s5-4","Microsurgery","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Management","Surgical approaches Approach Best for Hearing Main risk Translabyrinthine Any size Lost CSF leak Retrosigmoid (suboccipital) Medial, small or large tumours May be preserved Headache, cerebellar retraction Middle fossa Canal tumours up to about 1.5 cm Preserved in 60–70% Temporal lobe retraction, facial nerve 5.4.1 Translabyrinthine Through the mastoid and labyrinth: no brain retraction and the best view of the facial nerve, but hearing is sacrificed. It removes a tumour of any size, with good facial outcomes (House–Brackmann I or II) in most tumours under about 3.5 cm. 5.4.2 Retrosigmoid Through the posterior fossa behind the sigmoid sinus: hearing can be preserved for tumours under about 2 cm that do not fill the lateral third of the canal; postoperative headache is commoner. In a large review, useful hearing (class A or B) was kept in 57% of intracanalicular tumours, 37% under 2 cm and 12% over 2 cm. No approach has been shown clearly superior for facial function. 5.4.3 Middle fossa Above the temporal bone: for small intracanalicular tumours with good hearing. The facial nerve lies over the tumour and is at greater risk."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s5-5","Subtotal removal","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Management","Leaving a little tumour on the facial nerve preserves facial function; the remnant often stays stable and can be watched or irradiated."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s5-6","NF2","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Management","Bevacizumab may shrink tumours and improve hearing. An auditory brainstem implant or cochlear implant can restore some hearing ( Hearing rehabilitation: aids, bone devices and implants )."],["ev","cl:t-vs~c6","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours: current evidence","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Complications","Natural history. In a Danish national cohort of more than 700 patients followed with MRI, 17% of intracanalicular and 29% of extrameatal tumours grew, and most growth showed in the first years of follow-up (Stangerup et al., Otol Neurotol 2006). Upfront radiosurgery or wait and scan? In the V-REX trial, 100 patients with small or medium tumours were randomised: at four years tumour volume fell with upfront radiosurgery and rose with wait and scan (42% of the waiting group needed radiosurgery for growth), but 25 of 26 secondary outcomes, including hearing, balance and quality of life, did not differ, and no radiation complications occurred (Dhayalan et al., JAMA 2023). Guidelines. The European (EANO) guideline recommends observation for small asymptomatic tumours, radiosurgery for small to medium tumours that grow or cause symptoms, and surgery for large tumours with mass effect, with a team approach (Goldbrunner et al., Neuro Oncol 2020). The American Congress of Neurological Surgeons guidelines find no approach superior for facial nerve outcome and support MRI with gadolinium as the diagnostic standard (Olson et al., Neurosurgery 2018)."],["sec","cl:t-vs~c6","Complications","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · Complications","Of the tumour and of treatment. Facial palsy ( Facial nerve palsy ). Hearing loss. CSF leak and meningitis. Chronic headache, especially after the retrosigmoid route. Hydrocephalus and brainstem compression with large tumours."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s7-1","Viva essentials","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Origin: the vestibular nerve in the internal auditory canal. Presenting symptoms, and why vertigo is uncommon. Order of cranial nerve involvement: VIII, V (corneal reflex), VII (Hitselberger's sign), IX and X. Retrocochlear audiology: discrimination, roll-over, reflex decay. MRI with gadolinium as the gold standard. The three surgical approaches, with hearing outcome for each. NF2: bilateral tumours, chromosome 22."],["sec","cl:t-vs~s7-2","Common pitfalls","Vestibular schwannoma and CP angle tumours · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not requesting MRI for an asymmetric loss. Attributing sudden hearing loss to a virus without imaging. Doing a lumbar puncture with a large tumour. Promising hearing preservation after radiosurgery."],["osec","th:op-1~s1-1","The case at a glance","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Operating note","Office-based laryngeal procedures Summary Indications Injection augmentation for glottic insufficiency (paralysis, paresis, atrophy, scar); steroid injection to soften vocal fold, subglottic or tracheal scar and some benign lesions; botulinum toxin for spasmodic dysphonia; biopsy of a laryngeal lesion; laser (KTP and similar) for vascular lesions, papilloma, polyps, cysts, Reinke oedema, granuloma, webs, leukoplakia and dysplasia Contraindications Unstable heart or lungs; allergy to the local anaesthetic or the injectable; a larynx you cannot see well (prolapsing arytenoid, tight supraglottis); neck landmarks you cannot feel (for percutaneous routes); marked tremor of the larynx or pharynx Botulinum toxin, extra contraindications Pregnancy, breast-feeding, a neuromuscular disorder such as myasthenia gravis, current aminoglycoside treatment, and, for posterior cricoarytenoid injection, an already weak abductor Anaesthesia Topical and local only: nebulised and dripped lidocaine, with or without transtracheal lidocaine; an oral anxiolytic for an anxious patient Position Sitting upright in the examination chair for most procedures; lying supine is an option for EMG-guided toxin injection Antibiotics Not needed; infection is very rare even though the field is clean-contaminated Monitoring No active monitoring; record pulse, blood pressure and oxygen saturation before and after Imaging None needed for the procedure itself Tray Distal-chip flexible laryngoscope with a working channel (or a rigid 70-degree telescope for one-person per-oral work), camera and monitor, curved Abraham "],["osec","th:op-1~s1-2","Consent","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: a procedure done while you are awake, with the throat numbed by spray and drops; you will feel pressure and may cough, but should not feel pain. Why here and not in theatre: no general anaesthetic or tube, a quicker recovery, and the voice can be judged while we work. It may not be finished in one sitting: if the throat cannot be numbed well enough or the view is poor, we may stop and offer the operation under general anaesthetic. Fainting or feeling light-headed during the procedure. Bleeding from the nose or throat, usually minor. A worse voice for a time , or permanently if the material settles in the wrong layer of the vocal fold. Breathing difficulty from swelling or spasm of the voice box; rare, and we are ready to manage it. Choking on drinks while the throat is numb: nothing to eat or drink for an hour. Botulinum toxin: a breathy voice and some difficulty swallowing liquids for a few days to weeks while the dose takes effect; the effect wears off after a few months and the injection is repeated. Injection augmentation with most materials lasts months, not years, and may need repeating or replacing with a permanent operation. Laser: a small risk of burns to surrounding tissue and of scarring of the vocal fold."],["osec","th:op-1~s1-3","Before you start: choosing the patient","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Operating note","The procedure succeeds or fails on patient selection, so assess this in clinic before the day. 1.3.1 The history Ask about the voice complaint (hoarseness, weak or breathy voice, effortful or broken speech), chronic cough, breathlessness, throat pain and swallowing. Ask about recent surgery that put the recurrent laryngeal nerve at risk (thyroid, chest, neck, skull base), recent intubation or laryngeal surgery, neurological disease (Parkinson's disease, multiple system atrophy, essential tremor), reflux, smoking, and how the patient uses the voice at work and at play. 1.3.2 Listening to the voice Breathy voice suggests glottic insufficiency; breathy breaks suggest abductor spasmodic dysphonia. Strained, strangled voice with breaks on vowels suggests adductor spasmodic dysphonia; strain is also heard with granuloma. Rough voice or diplophonia suggests a paralysed or paretic fold, or a lesion on the fold. 1.3.3 Examining for the procedure Mouth and oropharynx: the gap between the incisors and how strong the gag reflex is. Neck: whether you can feel the thyroid notch, thyroid cartilage and cricoid easily, and any mass. Nose: whether it will take a 3 to 4 mm laryngoscope (5 mm for a transnasal oesophagoscope). Stroboscopy: atrophy, reduced or absent movement, scar with a poor mucosal wave, benign lesions (polyps, cysts, Reinke oedema, granuloma, papilloma), leukoplakia or keratosis, and dystonia or tremor. The ideal office patient Feature Why it matters Little or no gag Instruments pass over the tongue base without retching Can stay calm for 10 to 30 minutes Sudden movement wit"],["osec","th:op-1~s2-1","Inside: the superior arcuate line","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Anatomy you must see","The key internal landmark for augmentation is the superior arcuate line : the curved line on the upper surface of the fold where the lateral edge of the vocalis bulk meets the floor of the ventricle. Material is placed lateral to the thyroarytenoid muscle , through this line, so that it pushes the whole fold toward the midline. The ideal point is just in front of and lateral to the vocal process of the arytenoid, where most posterior gaps are. The superficial plane Material placed too medially or too shallowly lands in the superficial lamina propria (Reinke's space). It stiffens the vibrating cover and can make the voice worse instead of better. Deep and lateral is the rule for augmentation; only a steroid for a scar or benign lesion is meant to be superficial."],["osec","th:op-1~s2-2","Outside: the neck landmarks","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Anatomy you must see","Thyroid notch: the entry point for the thyrohyoid approach. Thyroid cartilage: the window for the trans-cartilage approach, entered 6 to 8 mm from the midline at the level of the folds. Cricothyroid membrane: the entry point for the transcricothyroid approach and for adductor toxin injection."],["osec","th:op-1~s2-3","The muscles for toxin","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Anatomy you must see","Adductor spasmodic dysphonia: the target is the thyroarytenoid and lateral cricoarytenoid complex, reached through the cricothyroid membrane just off the midline, angled up and laterally. Abductor spasmodic dysphonia: the target is the posterior cricoarytenoid , the only abductor, reached behind the larynx by rotating it, or through the cricoid."],["osec","th:op-1~s3-1","Anaesthetise the nose and larynx","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · The operation, stage by stage","Pack each side of the nose with pledgets (about half an inch by three inches) wet with oxymetazoline and 4% lidocaine, and leave them for five minutes or more. For per-oral work, nebulise 5 ml of 4% lidocaine over about ten minutes to blunt the gag. Remove the pledgets, pass the channel laryngoscope to the tongue base, and pass a drip catheter down the channel: drip 4% lidocaine onto the tongue base, epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, the vestibule and finally the vocal folds, asking the patient to make a high-pitched sound so the drops reach the glottis (the patient feels a \"gargle\"). A curved Abraham cannula through the mouth does the same job. A further option is to inject 4% lidocaine through the cricothyroid membrane into the airway, which makes the patient cough it over the subglottis and folds. For percutaneous routes, raise a skin bleb of 1% lidocaine with adrenaline if you wish. Touch the fold gently with the catheter tip to confirm that it is numb before you start. Figure 1 The office kit: flexible sheathed 27 gauge needle, orotracheal injector, malleable 25 gauge per-oral needle, syringe on an Abraham cannula, the end of the drip catheter, and nasal pledgets. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 1.1 What you should see A larynx that stays still and does not cough or close when the catheter tip touches the fold. Lidocaine toxicity Count every millilitre from the nebuliser, drips, sprays and injections. Keep the total within about 5 mg/kg , roughly 7 to 8 ml of 4% lidocaine (about 350 mg) in a 70 kg adult. Early signs are perioral tingling, a metallic taste, ringing"],["osec","th:op-1~s3-2","Per-oral injection augmentation","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · The operation, stage by stage","This route gives the best view and the best angle into the layers of the fold, but it has the steepest learning curve. An assistant holds the flexible scope through the nose (or you use a rigid 70-degree telescope in one hand). Spray the oropharynx with a little more lidocaine or a benzocaine spray to quieten the gag. The patient sits upright, leans forward with the chin pushed forward, and holds their own tongue out with gauze (or you hold it). Load the curved injector or malleable needle, and pass it over the tongue base into the larynx while watching the monitor. Aim for the superior arcuate line just in front of and lateral to the vocal process , lateral to the bulk of the thyroarytenoid. You feel a slight give as the needle enters; inject slowly while watching the fold move toward the midline and the free edge straighten. Inject through one puncture only. Figure 2 Per-oral augmentation: the assistant holds the flexible scope through the nose, the patient holds their tongue, and the surgeon passes the curved injector over the tongue base. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 1.2 Figure 3 The needle in place lateral to the vocal fold, seen through the flexible scope. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 1.4 What you should see The needle entering at the arcuate line, lateral to the muscle bulk, not on the vibrating edge. The fold rounding out and moving medially as you inject, and the glottic gap closing on phonation. Multiple punctures Every extra hole lets material leak out. Choose your site, commit, and inject through a single entry point; repeated passes also cause"],["osec","th:op-1~s3-3","Percutaneous augmentation","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · The operation, stage by stage","These routes are easier to learn and allow a view of the needle tip before injecting, but the depth is less predictable because of the angle. A second person usually holds the flexible scope and confirms where the needle is. What you should see On the scope, the needle tip tenting the fold lateral to the vocal process before you inject, then the fold moving to the midline as it fills. 3.3.1 Transcricothyroid Bend a 25 gauge 1.5 inch needle to about 45 degrees. Enter through the cricothyroid membrane just off the midline on the side to be injected, and angle up toward the undersurface of the fold. The endoscopist can see the tip appear under the fold in the subglottis; the tip is then advanced into the fold from below. 3.3.2 Thyrohyoid Enter at the thyroid notch at a steep angle, nearly perpendicular to the skin, with a 25 gauge needle or a spinal needle, so that the tip appears in the laryngeal vestibule above the petiole. Tilt it laterally toward the arytenoid on the side to be treated so that it runs parallel to the fold, without puncturing the mucosa, then place it at the arcuate line and inject while watching from above. Figure 4 The thyrohyoid route in a cadaver larynx: the needle passes through the notch and runs down into the supraglottis. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 1.5 3.3.3 Through the thyroid cartilage Pass a 20 gauge spinal needle straight through the thyroid ala, rotating it like a screw to ease it through, 6 to 8 mm from the midline at the level of the folds; then turn it forward and level so that it lies in the paraglottic space. Remove the stylet, "],["osec","th:op-1~s3-4","Through the channel: biopsy, laser and steroid","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · The operation, stage by stage","All three are done through the working channel of the flexible scope after the same anaesthesia. What you should see The instrument tip in the centre of the picture before it touches tissue, and a dry fold afterwards. 3.4.1 Biopsy Pass the forceps through the channel, grasp the abnormal tissue firmly, and pull away with a quick, decisive movement . A slow pull tears a long strip of mucosa from the fold and leaves a scar; the same applies anywhere in the larynx. 3.4.2 Laser Everyone in the room wears eyewear specific to the laser's wavelength. A laser mask is sensible for papilloma, because of the plume. Typical KTP settings are 30 to 35 W, 15 ms pulses, two pulses a second , with the fibre in a metal guide. Treat vascular lesions (ectasia, varices), papilloma, dysplasia and leukoplakia, polyps, granuloma, webs and, increasingly, scar, protecting the free edge of the fold. 3.4.3 Steroid injection For scar and some benign lesions, a fine needle through the channel places dexamethasone or triamcinolone superficially , within the lesion or scar itself. What you should see A clean biopsy bite with the mucosa around it intact. Blanching of the vessels or the lesion with the laser, without charring of the healthy edge. Laser burns and scar Too much energy or too long on one spot burns the surrounding tissue and leaves a stiff, scarred fold. Treat in short bursts and keep the vibrating edge in view."],["osec","th:op-1~s3-5","Botulinum toxin under EMG guidance","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · The operation, stage by stage","Use a hollow, insulated 25 or 26 gauge needle electrode, which both records the EMG and delivers the toxin; this is a one-person technique because the EMG, not the endoscope, tells you where the tip is. For adductor spasmodic dysphonia, enter through the cricothyroid membrane just off the midline and angle up and laterally into the thyroarytenoid; ask the patient to say \"eee\" and look for a burst of motor-unit activity, then inject. For abductor spasmodic dysphonia, rotate the larynx and reach the posterior cricoarytenoid behind the thyroid ala (or through the cricoid); confirm with a sniff, then inject. Toxin can also be given through the channel of a flexible scope. What you should see Brisk EMG activity on phonation (adductor) or sniffing (abductor) before you inject. The airway after abductor injection The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only muscle that opens the glottis. Weakening both sides at once can obstruct the airway, so inject one side first and stage the other if needed. Senior's pearl Toxin doses are set by trial and titration for each patient. Start low, note the response and the side effects at each visit, and adjust; a dose that leaves the voice breathy and swallowing awkward was too much."],["osec","th:op-1~s3-6","Finish","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · The operation, stage by stage","Withdraw the instruments, check the airway and the fold, and listen to the voice before the patient leaves the chair. Repeat the vital signs. What you should see Both folds moving (or the paralysed fold sitting medially) with a full-width airway, no bleeding or swelling, and a voice at least as strong as before. The numb larynx The throat stays anaesthetised for about an hour. Eating or drinking before then risks aspiration. Senior's pearl Keep the patient in the department for 15 to 20 minutes after any injection: the rare airway swelling or vasovagal collapse happens early, not at home."],["osec","th:op-1~s4-1","Crisis drills","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · When it goes wrong","The patient faints (vasovagal reaction) 1 Act stop, remove instruments, lie the patient flat with the legs raised maintain the airway and give oxygen 2 Recover observe until the pulse and colour return finish another day if needed 3 Next time consider doing the procedure lying down, and use an anxiolytic Breathing difficulty or stridor 1 Recognise laryngospasm, swelling after injection, or bilateral weakness after toxin 2 Act stop, sit up, oxygen, call for help reassure, slow breathing, gentle positive pressure for spasm 3 Escalate nebulised adrenaline and steroid for oedema anaesthetic and airway team if the patient does not settle Signs of lidocaine toxicity 1 Recognise tingling lips, metallic taste, tinnitus, confusion, then seizures or arrhythmia 2 Act stop giving local anaesthetic oxygen, airway and monitoring call for help 3 Escalate treat seizures with benzodiazepine give lipid emulsion for collapse or arrhythmia as per local protocol The voice is worse after augmentation 1 Find out stroboscopy is the material superficial (stiff, poorly vibrating fold) or simply over-injected? 2 Wait over-injection settles as temporary materials are absorbed 3 Act material in Reinke's space may need removal in theatre Coughing on drinks after toxin 1 Expect a short period of breathiness and thin-liquid dysphagia is common 2 Advise chin tuck, small sips and thicker fluids until it settles 3 Reduce lower the next dose watch for aspiration pneumonia if the dose was large"],["osec","th:op-1~s5-1","After the procedure","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Aftercare","Nothing to eat or drink for one hour , until the throat sensation returns, to avoid aspiration. Voice rest for 24 hours after injection augmentation. Voice rest for 3 to 6 days after laser treatment of benign lesions; after papilloma treatment, it depends on how much of the free edge was treated. Botulinum toxin takes 2 to 3 days to begin to work; warn the patient so they do not think it failed. Arrange stroboscopy to check closure after augmentation, and review timing for repeat toxin injections."],["osec","th:op-1~s5-2","Complications","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Aftercare","Complications of office laryngeal procedures Complication Cause or setting Vasovagal reaction Anxiety, gagging, upright position Respiratory compromise Laryngospasm, oedema, bilateral abductor weakness Poor voice from misplaced material Injection too superficial or too medial Burns and fold scarring Excess laser energy or too long on one spot Aspiration pneumonia Large adductor toxin dose; drinking while numb Lidocaine toxicity Total dose above about 5 mg/kg Bleeding and infection Uncommon"],["osec","th:op-1~s5-3","Injectable materials","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Aftercare","How long the materials last Material Approximate duration Notes Carboxymethylcellulose gel 2 to 3 months No preparation; a trial injection to test the effect Hyaluronic acid 2 to 4 months No preparation Micronised dermis 2 to 6 months Needs preparation and some over-injection Calcium hydroxylapatite About 18 months Longer-lasting; more care with depth Autologous fat Unpredictable, from weeks to years Harvested and injected under general anaesthetic Teflon Permanent Abandoned: granulomas form and removing them damages the larynx"],["ev","th:op-1~s5-4","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures: current evidence","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Aftercare","Office laryngology. Awake transnasal procedures (biopsy, KTP laser for papilloma and dysplasia, injection laryngoplasty, botulinum toxin) avoid general anaesthesia and are well tolerated, with lower cost and similar results to theatre in published series. Biopsy caution. Office biopsies are small and can miss invasive cancer; a benign or non-diagnostic result in a lesion that looks malignant needs biopsy under general anaesthesia. Hoarseness guideline. The American guideline recommends laryngoscopy for hoarseness over four weeks and voice therapy where it affects quality of life (Stachler et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018)."],["osec","th:op-1~s5-4","Alternatives","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · Aftercare","Augmentation: voice therapy (usually before and after any procedure) and laryngeal framework surgery (medialisation thyroplasty, arytenoid adduction). Spasmodic dysphonia: selective laryngeal adductor denervation and reinnervation; lateralisation thyroplasty has also been reported. Benign and premalignant lesions: microlaryngoscopy under suspension with microflap excision, with or without laser; voice therapy. Evidence: a 2013 meta-analysis of six studies found office steroid injection for benign fold lesions well tolerated, with measurable improvement in the voice."],["osec","th:op-1~s6-1","Where the books differ","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra covers these procedures through the diseases rather than as an office technique. For adductor spasmodic dysphonia, botulinum toxin relieves the breaks for up to about 16 weeks before repeat injection, too much causes breathiness and swallowing discomfort, and voice therapy alone helps little; recurrent laryngeal nerve section is kept for severe cases when injections fail. For abductor dysphonia, toxin goes into the posterior cricoarytenoid, percutaneously with EMG or endoscopically, only about half improve, and one side is injected first because of the airway. Lee lists the injectable materials and how long they last, explains why Teflon was abandoned, and states that adductor toxin goes into the thyroarytenoid and lateral cricoarytenoid complex to weaken rather than paralyse, with the dose found by titration, by EMG-guided percutaneous injection or through a flexible scope channel. Myers gives the practical detail: patient selection, layered topical anaesthesia with its dose limit, the superior arcuate line, the per-oral and three percutaneous routes, channel biopsy and KTP settings, and the aftercare rules."],["osec","th:op-1~s6-2","Viva essentials","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The ideal office patient : low gag, calm, mouth opening of at least 20 mm, a patent nose, a steady head. How to anaesthetise the larynx (nebuliser, drip catheter, transtracheal) and the lidocaine limit (about 5 mg/kg). The superior arcuate line and why augmentation goes deep and lateral . The four injection routes: per-oral, transcricothyroid, thyrohyoid and trans-thyroid cartilage , with one advantage and one drawback of each. The injectable materials and how long each lasts; why Teflon is no longer used. Toxin targets: thyroarytenoid-lateral cricoarytenoid for adductor, posterior cricoarytenoid for abductor, and why abductor injection is staged. The aftercare rules : an hour nil by mouth, 24 hours' voice rest after injection, 3 to 6 days after laser, and toxin taking 2 to 3 days to act."],["osec","th:op-1~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Office-Based Laryngeal Procedures · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting before the larynx is properly numb. Losing track of the total lidocaine dose. Several puncture sites, so the material leaks out. Injecting too superficially or medially into Reinke's space. A plugged needle in calcified cartilage, then forcing the plunger. A slow biopsy pull that strips the fold. Too much laser energy in one spot. Injecting both posterior cricoarytenoids at once. Letting the patient eat or drink before the throat sensation returns."],["osec","th:op-100~s1-1","The case at a glance","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · Operating note","Surgery for HHT epistaxis Summary Diagnosis Curaçao criteria: recurrent spontaneous epistaxis; mucocutaneous telangiectases; visceral AVMs (lung, brain, liver, gut); a first-degree relative. Three or four criteria are definite Medical first Humidification, emollients, saline; oral tranexamic acid; iron replacement; systemic bevacizumab for severe transfusion-dependent bleeding (specialist) Ladder Coagulation (KTP or Nd:YAG laser, coblation, bipolar) → septodermoplasty → closure of the nostrils (Young procedure) Avoid Repeated chemical or monopolar cautery; aggressive packing (use non-traumatic, absorbable packs) Before anaesthesia Screen for pulmonary AVMs (risk of paradoxical embolism and stroke: filter all intravenous lines) and brain AVMs Anaesthesia General Tray Endoscopes, KTP or Nd:YAG laser with fibre and suction, coblation wand, bipolar, eye protection, dermatome for skin graft, microdebrider, topical thrombin"],["osec","th:op-100~s1-2","Consent","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · Operating note","What to tell the patient Treatment reduces bleeding but does not cure HHT; most need repeat procedures. Bleeding during the procedure, sometimes heavy. Septal perforation and adhesions. After septodermoplasty: crusting, dryness, smell, graft loss. After nostril closure: no nose breathing , loss of smell, but often near-total control of bleeding; it can be reversed."],["osec","th:op-100~s1-3","Before you start","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · Operating note","Confirm the diagnosis and screen for visceral AVMs; record the Epistaxis Severity Score, haemoglobin, ferritin and transfusion history. Optimise iron and haemoglobin. Plan with the HHT team, especially before systemic bevacizumab. Figure 1 A cluster of telangiectases on the anterior septum. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 100.1 Figure 2 Telangiectases on the middle turbinate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 100.2"],["osec","th:op-100~s2-1","Prepare the nose gently","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · The operation, stage by stage","Protect the eyes and tube with moist gauze. Decongest with oxymetazoline on gently placed patties. Block the vessels: greater palatine foramen injection, a submucosal injection along the floor at the pyriform aperture, and an endoscopic injection over the sphenopalatine foramen, always aspirating first. What you should see A quiet, decongested nose with no new bleeding from patty trauma."],["osec","th:op-100~s2-2","Coagulate the telangiectases","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · The operation, stage by stage","With the KTP laser (for example 8 W, 20 ms pulses) through a suction, or a low-setting coblation wand, treat every visible lesion on the septum, turbinates, lateral wall and floor. For raised, broad lesions, work from the surrounding mucosa and edges inwards rather than firing at the centre. Irrigate with saline at the end. Figure 3 Radiofrequency ablation of telangiectases. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 100.3 What you should see Blanched, flattened lesions without bleeding, and septal mucosa intact between them. The septum Treat both sides of the septum at the same spot and you devascularise the cartilage between them. Stagger treatment across sides and sessions to avoid perforation."],["osec","th:op-100~s2-3","Septodermoplasty","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · The operation, stage by stage","When bleeding continues despite coagulation, replace the diseased anterior septal mucosa with split-thickness skin. Harvest a graft (about 6 by 3 cm per side), fenestrate it. With a 0 degree endoscope and a 4 mm microdebrider, remove septal mucosa from just in front of the middle turbinate forwards to the vestibule, keeping the perichondrium; control bleeding with adrenaline and thrombin. Lay the graft on the raw septum, suture it at the mucocutaneous junction and hold it with packing or a splint. What you should see The anterior septum covered by skin graft from the vestibule back to the middle turbinate."],["osec","th:op-100~s2-4","Closure of the nostrils (Young procedure)","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · The operation, stage by stage","For intractable, transfusion-dependent bleeding, close the nostrils. Test acceptance first with Vaseline-coated cotton wool plugs, changed three times a day. Then make a circumferential incision at the mucocutaneous junction of the vestibule, raise three anteriorly based flaps towards the alar rim, secure perfect haemostasis and close them without tension with absorbable vertical mattress sutures. One or both sides can be closed. What you should see A sealed vestibule with healthy flap edges and no bleeding behind it. Senior's pearl The cotton wool plug trial tells you two things at once: whether stopping airflow stops the bleeding, and whether the patient can live without nose breathing. Do it before closing anything."],["osec","th:op-100~s3-1","Crisis drills","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · When it goes wrong","Brisk bleeding during coagulation 1 Act pressure with adrenaline patties bipolar to the bleeding point 2 Still bleeding absorbable non-traumatic packing transfuse avoid non-absorbable packs that tear the mucosa on removal Acute HHT bleed in the emergency department 1 Act resuscitate tranexamic acid lubricated absorbable or low-pressure packs 2 Avoid chemical cautery and traumatic packing arrange laser when stable"],["osec","th:op-100~s4-1","After surgery","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · Aftercare","Saline and emollients daily for life; humidify the home. Monitor haemoglobin and ferritin; continue iron. Repeat coagulation when bleeding returns, often every few months to a year. Coordinate care with an HHT centre."],["ev","th:op-100~s4-2","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia: current evidence","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · Aftercare","International guidelines. The Second International HHT Guidelines (GRADE methodology, experts from 15 countries) recommend moisturising treatments, oral tranexamic acid, ablative treatment such as laser or radiofrequency for persistent bleeding, septodermoplasty or nasal closure for refractory cases, and systemic antiangiogenic treatment such as intravenous bevacizumab for severe bleeding (Faughnan et al., Ann Intern Med 2020). Tranexamic acid. A randomised, placebo-controlled crossover trial of 118 patients found oral tranexamic acid (3 g a day) reduced the duration of epistaxis by about 17%, with no thrombotic events (Gaillard et al., J Thromb Haemost 2014). Bevacizumab. In 34 patients with severe HHT bleeding (82% previously transfused), intravenous bevacizumab reduced bleeding and transfusion needs (Iyer et al., Mayo Clin Proc 2018). Newer drugs. In the PATH-HHT trial of 144 patients, oral pomalidomide reduced epistaxis severity significantly more than placebo (Al-Samkari et al., NEJM 2024). In the NOSE trial, topical bevacizumab, oestriol and tranexamic acid sprays were no better than placebo (Whitehead et al., JAMA 2016)."],["osec","th:op-100~s4-2","Complications","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · Aftercare","Bleeding, adhesions, septal perforation, crusting and dryness, graft loss, smell loss, closure dehiscence."],["osec","th:op-100~s5-1","Where the books differ","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers KTP or Nd:YAG laser and coblation for coagulation, septodermoplasty when coagulation fails, and the Young procedure as a last resort. Dhingra and K. J. Lee HHT as a cause of recurrent epistaxis and the dangers of cautery and packing. The guidelines tranexamic acid, ablation, and systemic bevacizumab before or alongside surgery."],["osec","th:op-100~s5-2","Viva essentials","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The genetics (ENG, ACVRL1) and Curaçao criteria . Screening for pulmonary and brain AVMs, and filters on intravenous lines. The treatment ladder from emollients to the Young procedure. Why to avoid cautery and traumatic packing. Medical options: tranexamic acid, bevacizumab."],["osec","th:op-100~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Surgical Management of Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia · For the examination","Common pitfalls Chemical cautery of the septum on both sides. Packing with materials that tear the mucosa on removal. Anaesthetising without screening for pulmonary AVMs. Closing the nostrils without a plug trial."],["osec","th:op-102~s1-1","The case at a glance","Balloon Sinus Dilation · Operating note","Balloon sinus dilation Summary Indications Mild to moderate chronic rhinosinusitis of the maxillary, frontal or sphenoid sinus despite medical treatment; recurrent acute rhinosinusitis; scarred or stenosed ostia after previous surgery; patients unfit for general anaesthesia (anticoagulated, frail); with endoscopic surgery as a hybrid Contraindications Headache or sleep apnoea without sinusitis; no CT evidence of disease; extensive polyps or ethmoid disease; osteitic or neo-osteogenic ostia; fungal sinusitis; tumour Must have A CT scan before treatment Anaesthesia Local (clinic) or general Position Supine or semi-sitting Tray 0 and 30 degree endoscopes, balloon system with bendable guide and light-guided wire or probe, inflation device, decongestants, dissolvable packs"],["osec","th:op-102~s1-2","Consent","Balloon Sinus Dilation · Operating note","What to tell the patient Symptoms usually improve, but the evidence is weaker than for endoscopic sinus surgery and some need further surgery. The risks are those of sinus surgery but less frequent: bleeding, scarring, orbital injury and vision loss, CSF leak (all rare). Facial pressure and congestion for a few days. If a sinus cannot be entered safely, it will be left alone."],["osec","th:op-102~s1-3","Before you start","Balloon Sinus Dilation · Operating note","Confirm chronic or recurrent rhinosinusitis by symptoms, endoscopy and CT. Study the CT for the uncinate attachment, frontal recess cells, the sphenoid ostium and its relation to the optic nerve and carotid, and any dehiscent lamina papyracea or skull base. Figure 1 Balloon dilation of the maxillary, frontal and sphenoid outflow tracts. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 102.1"],["osec","th:op-102~s2-1","Prepare","Balloon Sinus Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest and anaesthetise topically, and inject the lateral nasal wall and middle turbinate if working awake. Medialise the middle turbinate gently. Work in the order frontal, sphenoid, maxillary, bending the guide for each. What you should see The uncinate process, bulla and middle meatus in view."],["osec","th:op-102~s2-2","Frontal","Balloon Sinus Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Bend the guide to the frontal angle. Stay in front of the bulla ethmoidalis to find the frontal recess, pass the light wire or probe up the recess and confirm placement by transillumination of the forehead (and navigation if used). Advance the balloon over it and inflate. What you should see Bright transillumination of the frontal sinus before any inflation. Never force a balloon If the wire or probe will not pass easily, it may be heading for the orbit or the skull base. Abandon that sinus rather than push."],["osec","th:op-102~s2-3","Sphenoid","Balloon Sinus Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Find the natural ostium medial to the superior turbinate in the sphenoethmoidal recess, enter it gently and dilate. What you should see The guide passing into the sphenoid along the line of the ostium, not lateral towards the optic nerve and carotid."],["osec","th:op-102~s2-4","Maxillary","Balloon Sinus Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Probe the maxillary outflow tract behind the uncinate with a curved probe to make room, bend the guide to about 120 degrees, enter the ostium and confirm by transillumination of the cheek. Inflate to about 12 atmospheres for about 5 seconds, three times, angling the device forwards, downwards and backwards. What you should see A widened maxillary ostium on the 30 degree endoscope."],["osec","th:op-102~s2-5","Finish","Balloon Sinus Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Inspect each opened ostium, suction blood and mucus, and pack only if oozing. What you should see Patent, intact-mucosa ostia with little bleeding. Senior's pearl Balloon dilation works on the natural ostium. If the disease is in the ethmoid cells or there are polyps, the balloon cannot treat it; choose endoscopic sinus surgery or a hybrid."],["osec","th:op-102~s3-1","Crisis drills","Balloon Sinus Dilation · When it goes wrong","Bleeding during dilation 1 Act adrenaline-soaked patties 2 Continuing dissolvable middle meatal pack Orbital signs after a frontal or maxillary dilation 1 Act as for orbital injury in sinus surgery check vision and pressure lateral canthotomy for a tense orbit ophthalmology now"],["osec","th:op-102~s4-1","After surgery","Balloon Sinus Dilation · Aftercare","Sneeze with the mouth open and avoid nose blowing for a week; start saline irrigation at a week; review at a week. Congestion responds to decongestants or a short course of oral steroid."],["ev","th:op-102~s4-2","Balloon Sinus Dilation: current evidence","Balloon Sinus Dilation · Aftercare","Consensus. An expert consensus statement agreed that dilation should not be done for headache or sleep apnoea without sinusitis criteria, and that a sinus CT is required beforehand; most other statements did not reach consensus, reflecting limited evidence (Piccirillo et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2018). Outcomes. A systematic review of 17 studies, mostly of patients with limited disease, found improved quality of life (SNOT-20) and sinus opacification at a year, but called the evidence incomplete (Levy et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2016). A randomised trial of maxillary disease without severe CT findings found similar quality of life gains after balloon dilation and endoscopic sinus surgery (Bizaki et al., Rhinology 2014)."],["osec","th:op-102~s4-2","Complications","Balloon Sinus Dilation · Aftercare","Failure to cannulate, bleeding, scarring and restenosis, orbital injury, CSF leak, persistent symptoms needing endoscopic sinus surgery."],["osec","th:op-102~s5-1","Where the books differ","Balloon Sinus Dilation · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers balloon dilation for mild to moderate maxillary, frontal or sphenoid disease and stenosed ostia, alone or as a hybrid, with risks like those of endoscopic surgery but less frequent. K. J. Lee and the Oxford books: endoscopic sinus surgery remains the standard for chronic rhinosinusitis, particularly with polyps. The literature: benefit in selected limited disease; consensus that CT and a true sinusitis diagnosis come first."],["osec","th:op-102~s5-2","Viva essentials","Balloon Sinus Dilation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Who is suitable and who is not. How it works (fracture and displacement of the ostial walls, mucosal preservation). Transillumination to confirm placement. Evidence and its limits compared with endoscopic sinus surgery."],["osec","th:op-102~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Balloon Sinus Dilation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Dilating patients with headache but no sinusitis. Forcing a guide that will not pass. Using a balloon for polyps or ethmoid disease. Treating without a CT."],["osec","th:op-103~s1-1","The case at a glance","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Operating note","Antral lavage, middle meatal antrostomy and antrochoanal polyp Summary Indications Chronic rhinosinusitis or recurrent acute sinusitis that has failed medical treatment; nasal polyps; antrochoanal polyp; maxillary mucocele; fungal ball; benign or malignant tumours of the maxillary sinus; endoscopic access to the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae Contraindications An inadequate trial of medical treatment; odontogenic sinusitis that will usually (over 60%) settle once the tooth is treated; an asymptomatic opaque sinus or retention cyst Anaesthesia General with an endotracheal tube (mandatory with reactive airways), tube taped to the lower lip; total intravenous anaesthesia and controlled hypotension may help the view Position Supine, head turned slightly toward the surgeon (midline for four-handed surgery), reverse Trendelenburg if preferred; screens and navigation placed ergonomically Antibiotics A single dose of first-generation cephalosporin (clindamycin if allergic); broader cover and cultures if infection is active Monitoring None special; navigation for extensive or revision surgery Imaging Mandatory: thin coronal CT without contrast; look at the lamina papyracea and orbital floor for dehiscence, and find any Haller (infraorbital ethmoid) cells Tray Endoscopic sinus set; 0-, 30-, 45- and 70-degree telescopes; paediatric backbiting forceps; double-ball probe seeker; curved suckers; microdebrider; 90-degree ethmoid forceps; bipolar and suction monopolar diathermy; canine fossa trocar set Before theatre Stop antiplatelets and anticoagulants if possible; topical deco"],["osec","th:op-103~s1-2","Consent","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: enlarging the natural drainage opening of the cheek sinus from inside the nose; for a polyp, removing it completely from its root. Bleeding , usually minor. Watering eye from injury to the tear duct, which runs just in front of the sinus opening. Eye injury: bruising, swelling, double vision; rare. Numbness of the cheek or upper teeth, especially if a small puncture through the cheek is needed for a polyp. Scarring that can block the opening again. Persistent symptoms: facial pain alone, without sinus disease, is unlikely to be helped by surgery. Recurrence of an antrochoanal polyp if any root is left."],["osec","th:op-103~s1-3","Before you start","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Operating note","1.3.1 The history The four cardinal symptoms : blockage, discoloured discharge, facial pain or pressure, reduced smell; and how long they have lasted. Teeth: previous infections, root canals, implants or sinus lifts; think of an odontogenic source in one-sided maxillary disease. Previous medical treatment (antibiotics, steroids, sprays, rinses) and surgery (septoplasty, sinus surgery). Trauma to the nose, orbital rim, floor or medial wall. Systemic disease: sarcoidosis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, vasculitis; cystic fibrosis and ciliary dyskinesia. Bleeding history, antiplatelets, supplements (vitamin E, ginseng, ginkgo, garlic), smoking, alcohol, occupational exposure (wood dust, metals). 1.3.2 Nasal endoscopy Examine before and after decongestion and topical anaesthesia. Look for a deviated septum, concha bullosa, accessory ostia and recirculation , the state of the mucosa and pus (take a culture), tumours, and signs of previous surgery. Figure 1 Accessory ostia (arrows) in the left posterior fontanelle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 103.1 Recirculation The natural ostium is the only opening that the sinus's cilia clear toward; an accessory ostium in the fontanelle, or an inferior meatal window, is ignored. Mucus can loop out of the natural ostium and back in through an accessory opening: the patient has thick postnasal drip and recurrent infection, a 30-degree scope shows bubbly secretions moving between the two openings, and CT shows bubbles on the sinus floor. The cure is to join the natural ostium to the accessory opening. 1.3.3 The CT On standard window"],["osec","th:op-103~s2-1","The maxillary sinus","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Anatomy you must see","A single air cell bounded by the orbit above, the zygomatic process laterally, the hard palate below, the fontanelles and uncinate medially, the cheek in front and the pterygopalatine fossa behind. Its cilia carry mucus against gravity to the natural ostium , which lies at the back of the ethmoid infundibulum , between the uncinate process and the ethmoid bulla (the hiatus semilunaris). The natural ostium faces in a parasagittal plane and has an anterior elliptical extension; accessory ostia are round, punched-out holes in the fontanelle lying in the sagittal plane. Haller cells on the orbital floor can narrow the infundibulum. Figure 3 A Haller cell (asterisk) and the natural ostium of the right maxillary sinus (arrow) on coronal CT. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 103.4"],["osec","th:op-103~s2-2","The danger points","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Anatomy you must see","The nasolacrimal duct lies just in front of the uncinate, under the maxillary line ; cutting too far forward injures it. The lamina papyracea lies just lateral to the top of the uncinate; a sickle knife driven through the uncinate can enter the orbit. The infraorbital nerve runs in the roof of the sinus, sometimes dehiscent, especially after trauma. Branches of the sphenopalatine artery and the descending palatine nerve lie at the back of the antrostomy. The narrowest part of the ethmoid lies between the front of the middle turbinate and the uncinate and lacrimal processes; damaging the mucosa here causes adhesions."],["osec","th:op-103~s3-1","Prepare the field","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · The operation, stage by stage","Choose either oxymetazoline in the holding area or 1:1000 adrenaline on the table (the two compete for the same receptors, so the second works less well after the first). Place the soaked pledgets; colour the adrenaline with a drop of methylene blue and keep it in a labelled basin so it is never mistaken for local anaesthetic and injected. Inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline at the upper attachment of the middle turbinate, the lower uncinate and the tail of the middle turbinate, and wait 5 minutes. Keep the scope high in the nose so your instruments pass freely below it; keep the lens clean with irrigation or an irrigating sheath. Figure 4 Injection sites: the upper attachment of the middle turbinate (+), the lower uncinate (black arrowhead) and the tail of the middle turbinate (white arrowhead). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 103.5 What you should see A pale, dry middle meatus with the middle turbinate, uncinate and bulla clearly identified."],["osec","th:op-103~s3-2","Medialise the middle turbinate","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · The operation, stage by stage","Push the middle turbinate gently toward the septum, remembering that force is transmitted to the lateral lamella of the cribriform plate. Reduce a bulky, paradoxically curved turbinate or a concha bullosa with the shaver if needed; most need only gentle retraction. What you should see The middle meatus open, with the uncinate process, the bulla behind it and the hiatus semilunaris between them. The floppy turbinate Fracturing or over-thinning the turbinate destabilises it: it lateralises, scars to the lateral wall and blocks the very ostium you came to open."],["osec","th:op-103~s3-3","The uncinate window","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · The operation, stage by stage","Find the free edge of the uncinate. With paediatric backbiting forceps , bite backward to forward through the uncinate about a third of the way up from its attachment to the inferior turbinate. This retrograde route is the safest way into the infundibulum, because you are cutting away from the orbit. Remove the uncinate above the window with the shaver up to the agger nasi, as far as the rest of the operation needs. Figure 5 An uncinate window cut with paediatric backbiting forceps reveals the natural ostium (arrow); x marks the middle turbinate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 103.6 The sickle knife The orbit is entered most often during uncinectomy, when a sickle knife is pushed through the uncinate and on through the lamina papyracea. If you use a knife, keep it shallow and parallel to the lamina; the retrograde backbiter avoids the problem. What you should see The natural ostium, oval and lying obliquely in the floor of the infundibulum behind the cut edge of the uncinate; you cannot see it properly with a 0-degree scope until the uncinate is gone."],["osec","th:op-103~s3-4","Preserve the natural ostium","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · The operation, stage by stage","Below the window, shave away the nasal-side mucosa over the remaining uncinate bone, lift the bone out of its mucosal pocket with a double-ball probe, and lay the mucosal flaps together with the shaver. This removes the uncinate under the mucosa , opening the natural ostium widely while keeping its full 360-degree ring of lining and its parasagittal orientation. Figure 6 The lower uncinate lifted out of its mucosal pocket with a double-ball probe. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 103.7 Figure 7 The natural ostium with its full ring of lining preserved. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 103.8 What you should see The natural ostium, oval and pointing forward, with an intact mucosal ring and no raw bone around it. Senior's pearl An ostium left in its natural parasagittal plane cannot be blocked by a middle turbinate that later falls laterally. Once you convert it into a large sagittal antrostomy, a lateralised turbinate can seal it off."],["osec","th:op-103~s3-5","Lavage","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · The operation, stage by stage","To wash out the sinus, pass a small paediatric sucker gently through the natural ostium and irrigate; an accessory ostium or an inferior meatal puncture can also be used. This is enough for many cases of recurrent acute and chronic maxillary sinusitis. What you should see Returning fluid running clear, and mucus streaming from the natural ostium afterwards. Inferior meatal puncture Through the inferior meatus, enter high under the turbinate and about 1.5 to 2 cm behind its head, aiming at the ipsilateral ear, to avoid the nasolacrimal opening in front and the orbit above. Never inject air. Senior's pearl Send the washings for culture; in a recalcitrant or immunocompromised patient this is the most useful thing the procedure produces."],["osec","th:op-103~s3-6","Antrostomy, when needed","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · The operation, stage by stage","If a formal antrostomy is needed (polyps, fungal ball, tumour, mucocele, access for rinses or the pterygopalatine fossa), enlarge the confirmed natural ostium backward into the posterior fontanelle , keeping the lining around the front 270 degrees of the ostium, using a ball probe or curved sucker. If you decide to make an antrostomy, make it large ; a teardrop shape suits mucociliary clearance. Suction and wash out secretions; reduce polypoid mucosa to free trapped mucus, but do not strip it. Take care at the back, where the sphenopalatine branches and the descending palatine nerve lie. Missing the natural ostium An antrostomy made through the fontanelle or inferior meatus that does not include the natural ostium sets up recirculation. Always find the natural ostium and join your opening to it. Stripping the mucosa Stripped sinus lining regrows without organised cilia and never clears normally again; it also invites osteitis and scarring. Remove only what is diseased and cut it cleanly. What you should see One opening that includes the natural ostium, with the roof, the posterior wall and the floor of the sinus visible through a 30- or 70-degree scope. Senior's pearl Look into the sinus with an angled scope before you finish: the anterior wall and the alveolar recess are where a fungal ball or polyp stalk hides."],["osec","th:op-103~s3-7","Antrochoanal polyp","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the nasal and choanal parts endoscopically. Make a maximal antrostomy to see the sinus part and the stalk , which most often arises from the lateral wall of the sinus, and usually leaves through an accessory ostium in the posterior fontanelle. Grasp the neck with 90-degree ethmoid forceps to deliver the bulk, then clear the attachment with a curved 4 mm shaver under 30- and 70-degree telescopes. If you cannot reach the stalk, make a canine fossa puncture above the gingivobuccal sulcus over the canine root: keep a finger on the cheek so the trocar does not go through the posterior wall, and the other hand on the orbital rim so it does not slide up into the orbit. Pass a 30-degree scope through the puncture with a curved shaver through the nose, or the shaver through the puncture with an angled scope through the nose (or both, through a double puncture). No packing is needed, and the ethmoids rarely need surgery. A mini-Caldwell-Luc is the fallback. Figure 8 An antrochoanal polyp (arrow) leaving through an accessory ostium behind an intact uncinate (cross). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 103.3 What you should see The stalk's attachment clearly seen and removed, with a clean sinus wall left behind. Polyp from the natural ostium An antrochoanal polyp usually comes through an accessory ostium. A polyp coming out of the natural ostium should make you think of inverted papilloma : send every polyp for histology."],["osec","th:op-103~s4-1","Crisis drills","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · When it goes wrong","Orbital fat or bruising during uncinectomy 1 Act stop working there do not pull fat avoid packing 2 Watch for proptosis, tense eye and reduced vision an orbital haematoma needs canthotomy and cantholysis Watering eye afterwards 1 Suspect nasolacrimal duct injury from cutting in front of the maxillary line 2 Act many settle refer for dacryocystorhinostomy if it persists The sinus is blocked again at review 1 Look for a lateralised middle turbinate scarred over the antrostomy, ostial stenosis, or recirculation 2 Act divide adhesions, medialise the turbinate revise to include the natural ostium Brisk bleeding at the back of the antrostomy 1 Suspect sphenopalatine branch 2 Act bipolar or suction diathermy if needed, formal sphenopalatine control"],["osec","th:op-103~s5-1","After the operation","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Aftercare","Pain is minimal: paracetamol or low-dose opioid. High-volume saline irrigation from the day of surgery. Steroid sprays and other topical treatments from about a week, or after any oral steroid course ends. Routine postoperative antibiotics have no good evidence; culture and treat if infection is found. No nose blowing; lift less than about 5 kg for a while. A wide antrostomy allows the sinus to be checked in clinic with 30-degree or even flexible scopes, and to be rinsed and medicated at home."],["osec","th:op-103~s5-2","Complications","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Aftercare","Figure 9 A lateralised middle turbinate, and the CT of a scarred, lateralised turbinate obstructing the right maxillary sinus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 103.9 and 103.10 Complications of maxillary sinus surgery Complication Cause or prevention Adhesions The main pitfall; mucosal trauma; powered instruments and mucosal preservation reduce them Ostial stenosis Mucosa around the ostium damaged Recirculation Natural ostium not included in the antrostomy Lateralised middle turbinate blocking the sinus After a formal antrostomy in the sagittal plane Persistent disease Infection, polyps, secretions, a residual fungal ball or polyp Nasolacrimal duct injury Uncinectomy too far forward Orbital injury Sickle knife through the lamina Infraorbital nerve injury Rare; previous trauma Bleeding Sphenopalatine branches at the back, or turbinate resection Unrelieved facial pain Pain without objective disease; avoid surgery Recurrent antrochoanal polyp Stalk left behind after simple polypectomy"],["ev","th:op-103~s5-3","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp: current evidence","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Aftercare","Size of the antrostomy. Small randomised trials found no clear difference in outcomes between a minimal antrostomy and a wide one in limited disease; a wide antrostomy that includes the natural ostium is preferred in polyp disease and to allow topical steroids in. Mucus recirculation between the natural ostium and an accessory or surgical ostium is a common cause of persistent symptoms and must be avoided by joining them. Balloon dilatation treats only limited disease without polyps, and a Cochrane review found no good-quality comparison with conventional surgery (Ahmed et al., 2011)."],["osec","th:op-103~s5-3","Alternatives","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · Aftercare","Continued medical treatment, topical steroids and other topical medication. Because this is surgery for symptoms, with no malignant potential in inflammatory disease, keep it as limited as the disease allows. Odontogenic sinusitis often settles with dental treatment; a wide antrostomy helps in extensive or resistant cases. A middle meatal antrostomy works poorly in cystic fibrosis and ciliary dyskinesia, where mucus pools on the sinus floor because clearance depends on cilia."],["osec","th:op-103~s6-1","Where the books differ","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra describes proof puncture (antral lavage) through the inferior meatus with a Lichtwitz trocar about 1.5 to 2 cm behind the front of the inferior turbinate, aimed toward the ear on the same side, with the meaning of the washings (amber fluid with cholesterol crystals for a cyst, mucopus for hyperplastic sinusitis, foul pus for suppuration) and its complications, including fatal air embolism if air is blown in; inferior meatal antrostomy and the Caldwell-Luc operation, now largely replaced by endoscopic middle meatal surgery; and the antrochoanal (Killian) polyp with its antral, choanal and nasal parts, arising near an accessory ostium in children and young adults. Lee lists the antrochoanal polyp and silent sinus syndrome among indications for sinus surgery, stresses relieving ostial obstruction, and advises sending every antrochoanal polyp for histology to exclude inverted papilloma. Myers prefers a limited uncinate window that preserves the whole ostial ring when possible, a wide antrostomy that includes the natural ostium when more is needed, and complete removal of an antrochoanal polyp endoscopically, adding a canine fossa puncture when the stalk is out of reach."],["osec","th:op-103~s6-2","Viva essentials","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why the natural ostium must be included, and what recirculation is. How to tell the natural from an accessory ostium . Retrograde uncinectomy and the structures at risk (orbit, nasolacrimal duct). Haller cells and the maxillary line . The antrochoanal polyp : parts, site of origin, why simple polypectomy recurs, and the role of a canine fossa puncture. Inverted papilloma when a polyp comes from the natural ostium. Proof puncture: technique, interpretation and air embolism . Why antrostomy fails in cystic fibrosis ."],["osec","th:op-103~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Antral Lavage; Endoscopic Middle Meatal Antrostomy/Antrochoanal Polyp · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating without a proper trial of medical treatment or for pain alone. Driving a sickle knife through the lamina. Cutting in front of the maxillary line. An antrostomy that misses the natural ostium. Stripping the sinus lining. A small opening when an antrostomy was the plan. Simple polypectomy that leaves the stalk. Not sending a polyp for histology."],["osec","th:op-104~s1-1","The case at a glance","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · Operating note","Anterior antrostomy (Caldwell-Luc) Summary Indications Disease in the anterior, inferior or lateral antrum beyond endoscopic reach (fungal ball, antrochoanal polyp base, dental cysts and foreign bodies, calcified disease); biopsy or staging of maxillary tumours; access to the pterygopalatine fossa or orbital floor; first step in some maxillectomies; oroantral fistula closure Contraindications Unerupted teeth in children (risk to tooth buds); disease that endoscopic surgery can treat Anaesthesia General (or local with alveolar and greater palatine blocks) Position Supine, tube to the opposite corner of the mouth Tray Headlight, Army-Navy retractors, periosteal elevators, osteotome and Kerrison rongeurs, endoscopes, suction, absorbable sutures"],["osec","th:op-104~s1-2","Consent","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · Operating note","What to tell the patient Numbness of the cheek, upper lip, side of the nose or upper teeth (infraorbital or superior alveolar nerve), usually temporary. Facial swelling and discomfort in the gum. Tooth injury, devitalised teeth; ill-fitting dentures. Oroantral fistula. Eye injury and, very rarely, loss of vision if the orbital floor is breached. Injury to the tear duct (watering eye)."],["osec","th:op-104~s1-3","Before you start","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · Operating note","Review the CT: the disease, the height of the tooth roots, the orbital floor and the infraorbital canal. Combine with endoscopic middle meatal antrostomy so the sinus drains through its natural ostium. Figure 1 Mucociliary flow is towards the natural ostium, which is why mucosal stripping and inferior meatal drainage fail. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 104.1 Figure 2 CT of an antrochoanal polyp and of calcified chronic maxillary disease. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 104.2 and 104.3"],["osec","th:op-104~s2-1","Prepare and incise","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest the nose. Retract the lip and inject the sublabial region. Incise horizontally for 2 to 3 cm above the tooth roots, leaving about 5 mm of mucosa on the gum side of the sulcus for closure (over attached gingiva in edentulous patients so dentures will fit). Figure 3 The incision in the buccogingival sulcus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 104.4 What you should see Mucosa and periosteum divided down to the maxillary bone above the canine root."],["osec","th:op-104~s2-2","Expose the canine fossa","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · The operation, stage by stage","Elevate the periosteum up over the anterior wall of the sinus with a broad elevator, exposing about 2 by 2 cm of bone in the canine fossa above the tooth roots. Find and protect the infraorbital nerve as it leaves its foramen. Figure 4 Exposure of the canine fossa and the infraorbital nerve at its foramen. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 104.5 and 104.6 What you should see The thin bone of the canine fossa with the infraorbital nerve above it. Nerves and roots The infraorbital nerve is above and the canine and premolar roots below. Open the sinus in between, and do not retract hard on the nerve."],["osec","th:op-104~s2-3","Open the antrum","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · The operation, stage by stage","Break into the sinus with an osteotome or drill, then widen the opening with Kerrison rongeurs to the size you need (larger windows heal with a defect under the cheek). Figure 5 Opening the sinus with an osteotome and widening it with rongeurs. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 104.7 What you should see A view into the antrum through a window in the anterior wall."],["osec","th:op-104~s2-4","Deal with the disease","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the fungal ball, polyp base, cyst or foreign body, take biopsies, and preserve healthy mucosa. Open the natural ostium endoscopically (middle meatal antrostomy) for drainage. An inferior meatal antrostomy is now rarely needed. Figure 6 The historical inferior meatal antrostomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 104.8 What you should see A cleared sinus with healthy mucosa left and a wide natural ostium. Senior's pearl Strip nothing that is not diseased. Healthy maxillary mucosa recovers once the ostium is open; stripped mucosa is replaced by scar, and the patient is left with pain and a non-functioning sinus."],["osec","th:op-104~s2-5","Close","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · The operation, stage by stage","Close the sublabial incision in one layer with absorbable sutures. Apply a firm cheek dressing if bleeding. What you should see A watertight closure with no tension on the lip."],["osec","th:op-104~s3-1","Crisis drills","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · When it goes wrong","Orbital floor breached 1 Act stop leave periorbita undisturbed check the eye and vision after surgery 2 Swelling with vision change treat as orbital haematoma canthotomy, ophthalmology Wound breaks down with an oral leak 1 Act soft diet, mouthwash, antibiotics 2 Persisting treat as an oroantral fistula"],["osec","th:op-104~s4-1","After surgery","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · Aftercare","Ice and head elevation for swelling, soft diet, mouthwashes, avoid nose blowing, saline douching. Review for numbness and the fistula."],["ev","th:op-104~s4-2","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation: current evidence","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · Aftercare","Largely replaced. Endoscopic sinus surgery has replaced the Caldwell-Luc operation for most indications, because it preserves mucosa and causes fewer complications. Infraorbital nerve numbness or dysaesthesia, dental numbness and facial swelling are common after the open approach. Remaining uses. Access to the anterior and lateral maxillary sinus (some inverted papillomas, foreign bodies, dental cysts), often as a small canine fossa puncture combined with endoscopic surgery rather than a full open operation."],["osec","th:op-104~s4-2","Complications","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · Aftercare","Cheek swelling and pain, infraorbital and superior alveolar numbness, devitalised teeth, oroantral fistula, dacryocystitis, orbital injury, chronic facial pain, scarred non-functioning sinus."],["osec","th:op-104~s5-1","Where the books differ","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers keeps the anterior antrostomy for inflammatory disease beyond endoscopic reach, tumour biopsy, access to the pterygopalatine fossa and orbital floor, and oroantral fistula repair. Dhingra describes the classical radical antrostomy with mucosal stripping and an inferior meatal window, which is now obsolete for chronic sinusitis because drainage follows the natural ostium. The literature endoscopic techniques (middle meatal antrostomy, canine fossa puncture, endoscopic medial maxillectomy) now cover most of its old indications."],["osec","th:op-104~s5-2","Viva essentials","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mucociliary clearance and why the classical operation fails. Indications today. The steps and the structures at risk. Complications , especially numbness and oroantral fistula."],["osec","th:op-104~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Anterior Antrostomy: The Caldwell-Luc Operation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Stripping healthy mucosa. Operating on children with unerupted teeth. An incision in the sulcus itself, leaving nothing to close. Forgetting to open the natural ostium."],["osec","th:op-105~s1-1","The case at a glance","Oroantral Fistulas · Operating note","Closure of an oroantral fistula Summary Symptoms Fluid passing from mouth to nose when drinking, air leak on blowing or sucking, foul taste or discharge, pain, recurrent maxillary sinusitis Indications A persisting fistula or a communication over about 2 mm that has not closed Assessment Dental panoramic radiograph and CT for the bony defect, retained roots or foreign bodies, sinus disease and osteitis; biopsy any suspicious edge to exclude cancer Sinus Treat sinusitis first; open the natural ostium endoscopically (middle meatal antrostomy) at the same time for larger defects or sinus disease Flaps Buccal advancement flap (small to moderate); buccal fat pad flap (moderate, larger); palatal rotation flap (posterior, larger); combinations; free flaps for very large defects Anaesthesia General (local for small buccal flaps) Tray Oral surgery retractors, periosteal elevators, rongeurs and burrs, endoscopic sinus set, absorbable sutures"],["osec","th:op-105~s1-2","Consent","Oroantral Fistulas · Operating note","What to tell the patient The fistula may reopen, needing another repair. A buccal flap can make the sulcus shallower, affecting dentures. A palatal flap leaves a raw area on the palate that heals over a few weeks. Swelling, bleeding, infection, and numbness. Sinus surgery may be done at the same time."],["osec","th:op-105~s1-3","Before you start","Oroantral Fistulas · Operating note","Identify and remove the cause: a retained root, a foreign body (such as dental filling material) or necrotic bone. Clear sinusitis with antibiotics and decongestion. Plan the flap by the size and position of the defect and the state of the surrounding tissue. Figure 1 Dental filling material in the defect on a panoramic film, and the fistula with sinus disease on CT. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 105.1 and 105.2 Figure 2 An overview of the treatment options by size. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 105.4"],["osec","th:op-105~s2-1","Treat the sinus","Oroantral Fistulas · The operation, stage by stage","Open the maxillary natural ostium endoscopically, remove polyps, pus and any foreign body, and irrigate. For the fistula, the sinus must drain into the nose so that pressure and infection do not reopen the repair. Figure 3 A fistula with an obstructed maxillary sinus: drainage must be restored. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 105.10 What you should see A wide middle meatal antrostomy and a clear sinus."],["osec","th:op-105~s2-2","Excise the track","Oroantral Fistulas · The operation, stage by stage","Excise the epithelial lining of the fistula all round and freshen the edges. Remove necrotic bone back to bleeding bone, recessing it so the flap sits on healthy tissue. Figure 4 Bone recessed to remove all necrotic bone. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 105.3 Figure 5 The flap elevated, exposing the bony defect. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 105.6 What you should see A clean, bleeding bony margin around the communication."],["osec","th:op-105~s2-3","Buccal advancement flap","Oroantral Fistulas · The operation, stage by stage","Raise a broad-based trapezoidal mucoperiosteal flap from the buccal side, with releasing incisions angled outwards. Score the periosteum on its underside so the flap stretches across the defect, and suture it to the palatal mucosa without tension. Figure 6 The buccal advancement flap. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 105.7 What you should see The flap lying over the defect, reaching the palatal edge loosely. Senior's pearl The periosteal release incision is what gives a buccal flap its length. Without it you will be pulling, and a flap under tension breaks down."],["osec","th:op-105~s2-4","Buccal fat pad","Oroantral Fistulas · The operation, stage by stage","For larger defects, open the periosteum behind the zygomatic buttress, tease out the buccal fat pad on its pedicle and spread it over the defect, suturing it to the palatal mucosa; it mucosalises in a few weeks. A buccal flap can be laid over it as a second layer. What you should see A well-perfused fat pad covering the defect without tension."],["osec","th:op-105~s2-5","Palatal rotation flap","Oroantral Fistulas · The operation, stage by stage","For posterior defects, raise a full-thickness flap from the hard palate on the greater palatine artery, rotate it over the defect and suture it in. The exposed palatal bone heals by secondary intention under a protective plate. Figure 7 Designing and rotating a posteriorly based palatal flap. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 105.8 and 105.9 What you should see A rotated flap covering the defect, its pedicle untwisted. The greater palatine artery The palatal flap lives on the greater palatine artery emerging medial to the second molar. Keep it in the flap and do not kink the pedicle at the pivot point."],["osec","th:op-105~s3-1","Crisis drills","Oroantral Fistulas · When it goes wrong","The repair opens 1 Act gentle saline irrigation antibiotics if infected 2 Fully open wait for healing plan a different flap and check the sinus drains"],["osec","th:op-105~s4-1","After surgery","Oroantral Fistulas · Aftercare","Soft diet, chlorhexidine mouthwash, antibiotics and decongestants, no nose blowing, sucking through straws or smoking for 2 weeks, sneeze with the mouth open; a palatal plate after a palatal flap; denture adjustment later."],["ev","th:op-105~s4-2","Oroantral Fistulas: current evidence","Oroantral Fistulas · Aftercare","Communications smaller than about 2 mm may heal spontaneously, while larger ones need prompt closure to prevent sinusitis (Khandelwal and Hajira, World J Plast Surg 2017; Parvini et al., Int J Implant Dent 2019). In a randomised comparison, the pedicled buccal fat pad and the buccal advancement flap were equally successful in closing fistulae, with more early pain and reduced mouth opening after the fat pad (Nezafati et al., Int J Oral Maxillofac Surg 2012)."],["osec","th:op-105~s4-2","Complications","Oroantral Fistulas · Aftercare","Haematoma, dehiscence and recurrence, infection, sulcus obliteration, numbness."],["osec","th:op-105~s5-1","Where the books differ","Oroantral Fistulas · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers treat the sinus (often with endoscopic antrostomy) as well as the fistula; buccal, fat pad and palatal flaps by size; vascularised tissue for larger defects. Dhingra oroantral fistula as a complication of dental extraction and the Caldwell-Luc operation, closed with buccal or palatal flaps. The literature the buccal fat pad and the buccal flap give similar closure rates."],["osec","th:op-105~s5-2","Viva essentials","Oroantral Fistulas · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes and the 2 mm rule. Why the sinus must be treated as well as the fistula. The flap options and their blood supply. Post-operative precautions."],["osec","th:op-105~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Oroantral Fistulas · For the examination","Common pitfalls Closing the fistula without draining an infected sinus. A flap under tension. Leaving a root or foreign body in the sinus. Missing a carcinoma presenting as a \"fistula\"."],["osec","th:op-106~s1-1","The case at a glance","Medial Maxillectomy · Operating note","Medial maxillectomy Summary What is removed Lateral nasal wall and medial maxilla, bounded laterally by the infraorbital nerve , usually with a complete sphenoethmoidectomy Indications Tumours of the lateral nasal wall, nasal cavity, septum or medial maxillary wall; access to the pterygopalatine fossa, pterygoid plates, nasopharynx, sphenoid, clivus and medial infratemporal fossa; combined with an inferior maxillectomy (floor of nose, palate, upper gum) or a transcranial anterior skull base resection Not adequate Tumour extending lateral to the infraorbital nerve , into the palate , or into the facial soft tissue Approaches Lateral rhinotomy (best exposure); midfacial degloving (no facial scar, bilateral access, limited upward and backward reach); endoscopic endonasal (selected cases) Anaesthesia General, reinforced oral tube taped to the opposite commissure Position Supine, shoulder roll, head up about 30 degrees Antibiotics Broad-spectrum (aerobic and anaerobic nasal and oral flora) 30 minutes before incision and for 24 hours; topical antibiotic irrigation of the field Tray Soft tissue set; bone set with rongeurs and cutting and diamond irrigating drills; sinus set, with endoscopes if wanted Eye Temporary tarsorrhaphy or a corneal shield"],["osec","th:op-106~s1-2","Consent","Medial Maxillectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove a growth from the side wall of the nose and the inner part of the cheekbone. A scar beside the nose (lateral rhinotomy), or no facial scar with the degloving or endoscopic approach. Numbness of the cheek or teeth. A watery eye if the tear duct is involved; a new drainage opening is made. Eye problems: double vision, bleeding around the eye, rarely injury to the nerve of sight. Leak of brain fluid and meningitis. Widening between the eyes if the corner of the eye is not reattached. Bleeding , crusting, and rinses for some weeks. Radiotherapy or chemotherapy may be part of treatment."],["osec","th:op-106~s1-3","Before you start","Medial Maxillectomy · Operating note","Small intranasal tumours are often silent or mimic chronic sinusitis, allergy or polyps. Obstruction, sinus pressure or pain, discharge (sometimes bloody), epistaxis and anosmia are common. Symptoms that do not respond to treatment, or one-sided symptoms, should prompt imaging. Tearing, double vision and proptosis suggest orbital spread; a neck mass usually means nodal metastasis. Examine the nose with a rigid or flexible scope after decongestion: a mass, ulcer or contact bleeding suggests malignancy, and a unilateral \"polyp\" is more often neoplastic than inflammatory. Some tumours are submucosal. Biopsy suspicious lesions after imaging , to avoid heavy bleeding from a vascular tumour or a CSF leak from one that reaches the cranium. Figure 1 Endoscopic view of a tumour arising from the floor of the right nasal cavity; biopsy showed squamous cell carcinoma. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 106.1 Signs of spread Facial swelling means the tumour has breached the front of the sinuses; a palatal or alveolar ulcer or mass, downward spread; a middle ear effusion , nasopharynx, Eustachian tube or tensor veli palatini; anosmia, blurred vision, diplopia or trigeminal numbness, the skull base. In the orbit, epiphora means the nasolacrimal duct; diplopia, compression of nerves or muscles; proptosis, displacement or invasion of the globe. Visual loss is late, but an afferent pupillary defect, poor colour vision or field loss come earlier, and orbital spread may be silent."],["osec","th:op-106~s1-4","Imaging and preparation","Medial Maxillectomy · Operating note","CT and MRI are often both needed, especially for the skull base, orbit, pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae: coronal images for the orbital walls and cribriform plate, axial for the posterior maxillary wall and the fossae behind it, sagittal for the cribriform plate, planum and clivus. CT shows bone (destruction suggests malignancy; a widened or sclerotic infraorbital, vidian or trigeminal foramen suggests perineural spread ). MRI is better for the primary tumour, soft tissue, dura (thickening or enhancement), brain, carotid and nerves, and above all it separates tumour from retained secretions . Angiography is reserved for vascular tumours such as juvenile angiofibroma, where it allows embolisation. Figure 2 (A) Coronal CT: opacity of the right nose, maxillary and ethmoid sinuses with apparent destruction of the lateral wall and septum, unclear at the orbital floor and cribriform plate. (B) Coronal gadolinium T1 MRI of the same patient: tumour limited to the nose and ethmoids, retained secretions in the maxillary sinus, orbit and skull base not invaded. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 106.2 Map the tumour, examine the cranial nerves, and arrange an ophthalmological assessment. Most patients need a maxillofacial prosthodontist for impressions and an obturator or splint. Discuss neoadjuvant or adjuvant (chemo)radiotherapy at a multidisciplinary meeting. Routine bloods, coagulation, glucose, chest radiograph and ECG, with medical optimisation. Talk honestly with the patient and family about the disease, the risks, the stay, feeding, rehabilitation and adjuvant trea"],["osec","th:op-106~s2-1","Landmarks","Medial Maxillectomy · Anatomy you must see","The infraorbital nerve: the lateral limit of the resection. The nasolacrimal duct and sac , in the fossa between the anterior and posterior lacrimal crests. The frontoethmoidal suture: it marks the floor of the anterior cranial fossa and leads back to the anterior and posterior ethmoidal foramina . The anterior and posterior ethmoid arteries: the optic nerve lies 4 to 5 mm behind the posterior artery. The cribriform plate and fovea ethmoidalis , and the insertion of the middle turbinate. The opticocarotid recess in the sphenoid; sphenoid septa often insert into it."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-1","Protect the eye and mark the incision","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Place a temporary tarsorrhaphy. Mark the lateral rhinotomy by joining three points: halfway between the nasion and the medial canthus , the start of the alar crease , and the base of the columella . The basic incision is enough for a medial maxillectomy; a lip-splitting extension or a Lynch extension gives the exposure for a total maxillectomy. Figure 3 The lateral rhinotomy (points 1 to 3) and its extensions: a lip split (4) and a Lynch extension (5), with a temporary tarsorrhaphy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 106.3 What you should see A closed, protected eye and an incision joining the three marked points."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-2","Raise the cheek and release the medial canthus","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Elevate the cheek soft tissue subperiosteally over the maxilla and around the infraorbital nerve. Release the medial canthal tendon from the nasal bone. Elevate the periorbita over the medial wall to expose the lacrimal crest, lamina papyracea and frontoethmoidal suture. What you should see The anterior maxilla with the infraorbital nerve, and the medial orbital wall with the lacrimal crest and the frontoethmoidal suture."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-3","The ethmoid arteries","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Follow the frontoethmoidal suture back to the anterior and posterior ethmoidal foramina; bipolar, clip or tie the arteries and divide them. Remember the optic nerve 4 to 5 mm behind the posterior artery . Dissect the orbital floor as far laterally as the inferior orbital fissure. What you should see The anterior ethmoid artery about 24 mm behind the lacrimal crest and the posterior about 12 mm further, each sealed before it is divided. Orbital haematoma An ethmoid artery that retracts into the orbit unsecured bleeds inside it. Coagulate or clip the arteries before you divide them."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-4","The lacrimal sac","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Find the sac in its fossa. If the duct is divided, marsupialise the sac widely into the nose as a dacryocystorhinostomy, with silicone stents through the canaliculi into the nose, left for 3 to 6 months, to prevent stenosis and epiphora. Figure 4 Medial maxillectomy: (A) subperiosteal elevation around the infraorbital nerve and over the lacrimal crest to the sac; (B) the anterior ethmoid artery at the frontoethmoidal suture; (C) the sac marsupialised with silicone stents; (D) the osteotomies; (E) the healed lateral rhinotomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 106.4 What you should see The sac opened widely into the nose with stents through both canaliculi."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-5","The midfacial degloving alternative","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Make a complete transfixion incision and bilateral intercartilaginous incisions , and elevate over the dorsum to the nasal root. Add a gingivobuccal incision across the midline to both tuberosities. Deglove the nasal skeleton to the pyriform aperture, continue subperiosteally over both maxillae up to the infraorbital rims, and join the two by sharp dissection at the pyriform aperture. It needs familiarity with closed rhinoplasty incisions. What you should see The pyriform aperture and both maxillae exposed to the infraorbital rims with the nasal skin lifted in one piece."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-6","The osteotomies","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Cut vertically just medial to the infraorbital foramen , horizontally above the tooth roots into the pyriform aperture, and obliquely along the nasomaxillary suture , and remove the anterior maxillary wall medial to the nerve; lateral to the foramen the antrostomy can be widened to show the zygomatic recess. Begin the lateral wall resection with a cut along the nasal floor below the inferior turbinate , from the pyriform aperture back to the posterior maxillary wall. What you should see The antrum open, the infraorbital nerve preserved, and the lateral nasal wall free above and below."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-7","The ethmoids and the turbinate","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Retract and protect the orbit laterally with a malleable retractor and resect the lamina papyracea if needed. Perform a complete sphenoethmoidectomy , staying below the frontoethmoidal suture . Cut the superior attachment of the middle turbinate along the roof of the nose with scissors; never fracture or pull it. What you should see The orbit protected, the skull base intact above the suture line, and the middle turbinate cut, not pulled. The cribriform plate The commonest site of CSF leak is the cribriform plate at the insertion of the middle turbinate. Study the skull base on the scan and divide the turbinate sharply. The sphenoid Note the opticocarotid recess, any dehiscence of the carotid or optic canal, and where the septa insert. Septa inserting on the recess must not be fractured."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-8","Deliver and check","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Join the lateral wall cuts behind the turbinates with right-angled scissors and deliver the specimen; check the margins with frozen sections. Add septal cuts if the septum is involved. What you should see A wide cavity from the nasal floor to the skull base and back to the sphenoid face, the orbit intact behind its periorbita, the infraorbital nerve preserved laterally."],["osec","th:op-106~s3-9","Close","Medial Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Reattach the medial canthal tendon to the nasal bone in its anatomical position. Close the rhinotomy in several careful layers; it usually heals very well. Close sublabial incisions with absorbable sutures. Non-adherent packing may stay for 1 to 2 days. What you should see The medial canthus at the same level as the other side, and a rhinotomy closed without distorting the ala."],["osec","th:op-106~s4-1","Crisis drills","Medial Maxillectomy · When it goes wrong","CSF leak during the resection 1 Prevent study the cribriform plate cut the middle turbinate, do not pull it 2 Afterwards observe overnight for a CSF leak the risk is meningitis Bleeding around the eye 1 Cause an ethmoid artery not secured before division 2 Prevent bipolar or clip before cutting"],["osec","th:op-106~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Medial Maxillectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Not draining the lacrimal sac after dividing the duct. Not reattaching the medial canthal tendon (telecanthus). Excess traction on the infraorbital nerve (cheek numbness). An incomplete sphenoethmoidectomy, leaving poorly drained sinuses and recurrent sinusitis."],["osec","th:op-106~s5-1","After the operation","Medial Maxillectomy · Aftercare","Keep the patient overnight to watch for bleeding, CSF leak and orbital complications. Analgesia; head up 30 degrees; no strenuous activity; broad-spectrum antibiotics for 24 hours; remove any pack at 24 to 48 hours; generous saline irrigation against crusting."],["osec","th:op-106~s5-2","Complications","Medial Maxillectomy · Aftercare","Complications of medial maxillectomy Complication Notes Bleeding Postoperative haemorrhage Infraorbital nerve Cheek or dental numbness, often temporary from traction Corneal injury Tarsorrhaphy or shield Epiphora Duct injury or stenosis; marsupialise the sac Telecanthus Medial canthal tendon not reattached Orbital haematoma Ethmoid arteries Diplopia Swelling or injury of extraocular muscles Optic nerve injury Sphenoid and posterior ethmoid dissection CSF leak and meningitis Usually the cribriform plate"],["ev","th:op-106~s5-3","Medial Maxillectomy: current evidence","Medial Maxillectomy · Aftercare","Inverted papilloma. A meta-analysis of 14 cohort studies found that endoscopic resection reduced recurrence compared with external approaches (risk ratio 0.56) (Kim et al., Laryngoscope 2017); the key is identifying the attachment site and drilling the underlying bone. Recurrence also rises with higher Krouse stage. Endoscopic medial maxillectomy (with modified or prelacrimal approaches to reach the anterior and lateral walls) now replaces lateral rhinotomy for most benign tumours and selected malignant ones."],["osec","th:op-106~s5-3","Alternatives","Medial Maxillectomy · Aftercare","Depending on the disease: observation of benign, symptomless lesions; embolisation or coiling of vascular lesions with recurrent epistaxis; palliative radiotherapy or chemotherapy for malignancy."],["osec","th:op-106~s6-1","Endoscopic or open?","Medial Maxillectomy · For the examination","The lateral rhinotomy is the gold standard for safe, adequate resection of tumours of the nose, septum, lateral wall and sinuses, with lower recurrence than more limited approaches. In carefully selected cases centred on the medial maxillary wall, an entirely endonasal endoscopic medial maxillectomy gives equal oncological results. The principles are the same either way. The endoscopic operation was classically described for inverted papilloma , removing both the tumour and its bony attachment, and is now used for other benign tumours, access to the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae, and for recalcitrant maxillary sinusitis where mucociliary clearance has failed (cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia), because a middle meatal antrostomy depends on cilia to drain."],["osec","th:op-106~s6-2","Viva essentials","Medial Maxillectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for What medial maxillectomy removes, and its lateral limit. Indications and when it is not enough. Lateral rhinotomy markings, and the extensions for total maxillectomy. Midfacial degloving: incisions, advantages, limits. The frontoethmoidal suture, the ethmoid arteries, and the optic nerve 4 to 5 mm behind the posterior artery. The three osteotomies. Managing the lacrimal sac and the medial canthal tendon. CT versus MRI, and MRI's ability to separate tumour from secretions. A unilateral polyp is a tumour until proven otherwise."],["osec","th:op-106~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Medial Maxillectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Biopsying before imaging. Drilling above the frontoethmoidal suture. Fracturing sphenoid septa. Forgetting the prosthodontist when the palate may be involved."],["osec","th:op-107~s1-1","The case at a glance","Complete Maxillectomy · Operating note","Complete maxillectomy Summary Types Infrastructure (oral cavity tumours involving the sinus); suprastructure (facial skin tumours without palatal involvement, rarely lacrimal or orbital); medial (nasal tumours of the lateral wall, such as inverted papilloma); complete (tumours filling the sinus); complete with orbital exenteration ; extended (with facial skin, anterior skull base or infratemporal fossa) Indications Malignant tumours; symptomatic or growing benign tumours (no wide margins needed); symptom relief Contraindications Unfit for anaesthesia or major blood loss; unresectable tumour (unless for palliation); diffuse distant metastasis; lymphoma, plasmacytoma and other highly radio- or chemosensitive tumours Airway Usually oral intubation and extubation; awake or elective tracheostomy for severe trismus, large pharyngeal or soft palate resections, or free flap palate reconstruction Anaesthesia General; paralysis acceptable unless a parotidectomy or neck dissection is added; normal blood pressure, no planned hypotension; careful fluids; tube taped to the opposite side Position Table turned 180 degrees (or just past 90 toward the operated side); tarsorrhaphy; neck and thigh prepared; povidone-iodine around the eyes, washed out with balanced salt solution at the end Antibiotics First- or second-generation cephalosporin or a penicillin 30 minutes before incision and for 24 hours; clindamycin in allergy Tray Soft tissue and neck dissection sets; small bone saws; 1 mm side-cutting pencil drill; chisels; dental extraction set; oral retractors; monopolar and bipolar diathermy"],["osec","th:op-107~s1-2","Consent","Complete Maxillectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer together with the whole upper jaw on that side. The palate opening is closed with an obturator (a plate like a denture) made beforehand, or rebuilt with tissue from elsewhere. The eye: it may sink (hypoglobus) or see double; vision loss is rare. If the cancer involves the eye more than the scans show, its removal may be needed: tell us your wishes in advance. Numbness of the cheek (the infraorbital nerve is usually taken), changes in speech and swallowing, lip competence. Bleeding , wound problems, and failure of a free flap if one is used. A neck operation if nodes are found when the neck is opened for a free flap. Radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy usually follows for cancer. Jaw stiffness (trismus): exercises start early."],["osec","th:op-107~s1-3","Before you start","Complete Maxillectomy · Operating note","Patients present with nasal congestion, a palatal or alveolar lesion, epistaxis, facial numbness, tearing, diplopia or visual loss. Nodal metastasis is under 10% at presentation overall, but up to 35% for squamous cancer of the alveolus and hard palate (usually levels I and II and the facial nodes). Trismus, airway compromise, blindness, ophthalmoplegia and lower cranial nerve palsies mean advanced disease. What the examination tells you Loss of palatal sensation: perineural spread into the palatine canals or the pterygopalatine fossa. Trismus: infratemporal fossa invasion. Hyperglobus may be expansion, not invasion; severe muscle restriction and visual loss almost always mean intraconal invasion. Tearing: lacrimal invasion, or pterygopalatine invasion disrupting the vidian supply. Skin change: breach of the anterior wall, advanced disease with reconstructive consequences. Examine the teeth, palate, buccal mucosa and soft palate movement; cranial nerves III to VI, eye movements and infraorbital sensation; the neck. Endoscope the nasal floor, the lateral wall and the middle meatus for ethmoid involvement, and biopsy intranasal tumour."],["osec","th:op-107~s1-4","Imaging","Complete Maxillectomy · Operating note","CT and MRI are complementary and both essential. CT shows the bony boundaries and invasion of the palate, posterior wall and orbital floor; MRI shows the soft tissue, perineural spread, orbit, skull base, intracranial extension and facial skin. Stage the neck and chest with contrast CT or PET-CT. Angiography only for a suspected vascular malformation or carotid invasion. Figure 1 (A) Coronal CT: destruction of the medial maxillary wall and ethmoids, with little information on orbital or intracranial spread. (B) Coronal MRI of the same patient showing the full orbital and intracranial extent. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 107.1 Figure 2 Ohngren's line, from the medial canthus to the angle of the mandible: tumours below and in front of it do better than those above and behind. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 107.2"],["osec","th:op-107~s1-5","Preparation","Complete Maxillectomy · Operating note","Stage fully and present at a multidisciplinary meeting. Plan reconstruction: a prosthodontist for the obturator, a microvascular surgeon if a free flap is considered. Discuss the \"what ifs\" and record the patient's wishes (more orbital involvement than imaging shows, facial skin involvement, positive nodes found in the neck) so decisions in theatre can be made quickly. Check blood count, coagulation and biochemistry, and group and screen. Plan the airway with the anaesthetist. Embolisation is rarely needed, only for vascular tumours such as AVMs or angiofibromas: the internal maxillary artery is the main supply, with the inferolateral trunk of the carotid also feeding the infratemporal fossa. Carotid sacrifice has virtually no place in high-grade epithelial malignancy."],["osec","th:op-107~s2-1","The attachments to cut","Complete Maxillectomy · Anatomy you must see","The maxilla is attached at the nasal cavity (piriform aperture and medial wall), the orbit (rim and floor), the zygoma (malar process or arch, and the lateral buttress), the palate , and finally the pterygoid plates . Behind the plates lie the pterygoid muscles , the internal maxillary artery and, at the skull base, V3 ; V2 leaves through the infraorbital foramen and is followed for a neural margin."],["osec","th:op-107~s2-2","Margins","Complete Maxillectomy · Anatomy you must see","En bloc resection with clear margins is the principle, and bony margins should be at least 1 to 1.5 cm from tumour. It is hard to achieve along the periorbita, or when tumour reaches the orbital apex or the infratemporal fossa."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-1","Choose the incision","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Take the incisions down to bone with diathermy if the anterior soft tissue is not invaded: a sublabial incision alone; sublabial with partial or full degloving; a lateral rhinotomy , which the author places at the junction of the nasal dorsum and lateral wall rather than in the nasofacial crease (better appearance and less contact with the airway); or an extended lateral rhinotomy with a lip split (with a dart against notching) or a Weber-Ferguson periocular extension at the infraorbital rim in the elderly, or subciliary or transconjunctival in the young. Figure 3 Incisions: (A, C) the lateral rhinotomy at the dorsum-sidewall junction rather than the nasolabial crease; (B) with a lip split and dart; (D) the Weber-Ferguson extension. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 107.3 What you should see An incision that gives the whole anterior maxilla, with the lip marks aligned if it is split."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-2","Expose and take V2","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Expose the whole anterior face of the midface, the inferior and lateral orbit and the buccal space. Dissect out V2 and take neural margins. Incise along the piriform aperture into the nose and retract the nose to the other side. What you should see The anterior maxilla, the infraorbital rim and nerve, and the buccal fat laterally."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-3","The palate","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Make the palatal incisions and take intraoral margins. If the soft palate is kept, free it from the hard palate to open into the nasopharynx; control the palatine vessels with bipolar. What you should see Palatal cuts through mucosa to bone with a margin, and the soft palate freed if it is staying."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-4","The orbit","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Elevate the periorbita from the rim and floor, and examine them closely for invasion. What you should see Clean periorbita that lifts easily off the floor: no tumour through it."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-5","Anterior and orbital osteotomies","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","With a 1 mm side-cutting pencil drill and copious irrigation, cut from the upper piriform aperture through the orbital rim, protecting the eye with a metal retractor. Lift the globe and join the medial and lateral orbital rim cuts with a thin sharp osteotome, running back into the orbit to free the orbital floor. What you should see The cut through the rim and floor with the globe held up and protected."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-6","The lateral osteotomy","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","With the same drill, cut from the lateral orbit through the rim and across the malar process (occasionally the arch), back toward the pterygoid plates and through the lateral buttress . What you should see The cut across the malar buttress heading for the pterygomaxillary junction."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-7","The palatal osteotomy","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","With a thin reciprocating saw, cut the palate from back to front and continue into the front of the nose. What you should see The palate divided from back to front, between tooth sockets where possible."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-8","The pterygoid plates: from the side","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Now only the plates hold the maxilla. The traditional curved osteotome behind the plates, angled up into the infratemporal fossa, gives no proximal control of the internal maxillary artery and can fracture through the pterygopalatine fossa instead of behind the plates. So work from the lateral side : retract the mandible down and back, find the plates and muscles by blunt dissection, and feel (and if needed clamp) the internal maxillary artery . Feel the fused plates at the base of the middle cranial fossa, fracture them from the sphenoid with a sharp straight osteotome, and rock the maxilla medially and down. What you should see The plates divided under vision with the internal maxillary artery clipped, and the maxilla rocking free. The last cut Incomplete removal of the posterior wall and pterygoid plates is a common error; a fracture through the pterygopalatine fossa leaves tumour behind and opens a bleeding artery you cannot reach. Control the artery from the side."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-9","Free and deliver","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","With the infratemporal fossa open, control the internal maxillary artery fully and strip the pterygoid muscles from bone with bipolar. Find V3 and take skull base neural margins if needed. Cut the rest of the medial maxillary wall with large curved scissors, back from the medial orbital osteotomy, across the duct and down toward the torus tubarius, and remove the specimen. Send soft tissue margins for frozen section and secure haemostasis. What you should see A clean cavity from orbital floor to palate, the pterygoid stumps behind, the infratemporal fossa dry, and the orbit supported by its periorbita."],["osec","th:op-107~s3-10","Graft, pack and obturate","Complete Maxillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Lay a medium-thickness split skin graft from the thigh over the exposed buccal tissue and drape it back into the infratemporal defect. Pack with gauze and fix the temporary obturator with wires and screws. Close the face in layers, carefully realigning orbicularis oris and oculi. If a free flap with a bony palate is used, remove the coronoid process routinely so the mandible cannot fuse to the new palate. Figure 4 Healed defect and obturator: (A) five years after a Weber-Ferguson incision; (B) the defect with the obturator out; (C) the obturator; (D) in place. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 107.4 What you should see The graft lining the cavity, the obturator seated firmly, and the lip and lids realigned."],["osec","th:op-107~s4-1","Crisis drills","Complete Maxillectomy · When it goes wrong","Loss of vision or diplopia after surgery 1 Act emergency ophthalmology review pressures, acuity, fields, eye movements 2 Raised pressure (over 30) with falling acuity emergency lateral canthotomy and cantholysis 3 Then imaging for retrobulbar haematoma plan decompression Bleeding behind the plates 1 Prevent identify and control the internal maxillary artery from the lateral approach before the final fracture"],["osec","th:op-107~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Complete Maxillectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Bony margins that are too small or too superficial (less than 1 to 1.5 cm). Incomplete removal of the posterior wall and pterygoid plates. Underestimating the difficulty of clearing periorbital or ethmoid disease. Leaving the coronoid when a bony free flap rebuilds the palate."],["osec","th:op-107~s5-1","After the operation","Complete Maxillectomy · Aftercare","Fastidious wound and eye care. Clear fluids, or nil by mouth with a nasogastric tube after a free flap. Early mobilisation and routine DVT prophylaxis . Start trismus exercises early , because the muscles of mastication scar as they heal."],["ev","th:op-107~s5-2","Complete Maxillectomy: current evidence","Complete Maxillectomy · Aftercare","Reconstruction. The Brown classification describes maxillectomy defects by vertical and horizontal extent and guides the choice between an obturator and a free flap (Brown and Shaw, Lancet Oncol 2010); obturators suit small palatal defects, while larger defects and those involving the orbit are better reconstructed with a free flap such as a fibula or scapula. Orbit. Orbital exenteration is needed when tumour invades the periorbita into orbital fat, muscle or the apex; bone erosion alone with an intact periorbita usually allows the eye to be kept. Endoscopic resection gives comparable oncological results for selected tumours with less morbidity (Lund et al., Rhinology Suppl 2010)."],["osec","th:op-107~s5-2","Complications","Complete Maxillectomy · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Facial wound problems From the incisions Free flap failure When used Corneal ulceration Tarsorrhaphy and eye care Bleeding or haematoma Internal maxillary artery Vision loss or diplopia Emergency ophthalmology; canthotomy for raised pressure with falling acuity Facial numbness Beyond V2, which is expected Carotid injury Rare, in the parapharyngeal region"],["osec","th:op-107~s6-1","Do margins matter?","Complete Maxillectomy · For the examination","Complete removal is a central principle, but near the carotid and optic nerve it may not be possible: microscopic residual disease has been reported in 30% of open and 24% of endoscopic resections. Residual gross disease clearly causes recurrence and death; the effect of microscopic margins was long equivocal, but the International Collaborative Study Group found margin status a critical predictor of overall, recurrence-free and disease-specific survival, and Manjunath and colleagues found more regional and distant recurrence with positive margins. Margins must be taken in three dimensions, from bone, nerve and soft tissue, and discussed frankly with the patient."],["osec","th:op-107~s6-2","Viva essentials","Complete Maxillectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The types of maxillectomy and their indications. The commonest histology; why lymphoma and plasmacytoma are not operated on. CT and MRI together; Ohngren's line. Signs of perineural, infratemporal and intraconal spread. The order of osteotomies, and why the plates are freed from the side. The obturator, the skin graft, the coronoid. Managing visual loss after surgery."],["osec","th:op-107~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Complete Maxillectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not preparing the neck and thigh. Not discussing orbital exenteration as a possibility beforehand. Forgetting the prosthodontist. Starting trismus therapy late."],["osec","th:op-108~s1-1","The case at a glance","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Operating note","Endoscopic ethmoidectomy Summary Indications Symptomatic chronic rhinosinusitis, with or without polyps, with significant disease causing obstruction despite appropriate medical treatment; ethmoid mucocele; access to the frontal sinus (agger nasi and suprabullar cells) and the sphenoid; recurrent acute sinusitis; fungal disease; and, with advanced skills, tumours, CSF leaks, orbital decompression and skull base approaches Contraindications Unfit for general anaesthesia; uncontrolled asthma (control it first, with a chest physician); profuse bleeding that prevents a safe view is a reason to stop Anaesthesia General; total intravenous anaesthesia gives a drier field in severe polyposis; avoid beta-blockers alongside topical alpha agonists (unopposed alpha stimulation, cardiovascular collapse) Position Supine, head up 20 to 30 degrees and tilted 15 degrees toward the surgeon; surgeon, patient and screens in one line; a shoulder roll extends the head for frontal work Antibiotics Culture-directed if a culture is available (essential in cystic fibrosis); otherwise cefazolin 1 to 2 g by weight Monitoring Standard; a heart rate around 60 and mean arterial pressure around 60 mm Hg, where safe, give a better field Imaging Mandatory: a dedicated sinus CT, 1 mm or thinner, in a navigation protocol with coronal and sagittal reformats; MRI with contrast for tumours, to separate tumour from inflamed mucosa and secretions and show perineural spread Tray 0-, 30- and 45-degree telescopes; straight and 45-degree upturned through-cutting forceps (fine flat jaws, including 1 mm sizes); double-e"],["osec","th:op-108~s1-2","Consent","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: opening the small air cells between the eyes from inside the nose, removing diseased bone partitions while keeping the lining, so that the sinuses drain and rinses and sprays can reach them. What it is not: surgery does not cure chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps or cystic fibrosis; it is part of long-term treatment, and the rinses, sprays and follow-up afterwards are as important as the operation. Bleeding , occasionally needing packing or transfusion. Infection, scarring and recurrence , which may need further surgery. A worse or lost sense of smell. Leak of brain fluid (CSF) , needing repair; rarely meningitis. Eye injury: bruising, swelling, a bleed behind the eye, double vision, and, rarely, loss of sight. Watering eye from injury to the tear duct. Symptoms may not improve or may get worse."],["osec","th:op-108~s1-3","Before you start","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Which symptom troubles the patient most, and for how long: blockage, reduced smell, facial pressure, discharge or postnasal drip. This frames what surgery can achieve. What has been tried: systemic antibiotics and steroids, topical treatments, previous surgery. Asthma: common with sinus disease; surgery waits until it is controlled. Cystic fibrosis: plan with a chest physician; perioperative antibiotics are usually needed. Aspirin or anti-inflammatory sensitivity (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease). Anticoagulants, aspirin and supplements that increase bleeding. 1.3.2 The examination Endoscopy with 0- and 30-degree telescopes in three passes (floor and nasopharynx; middle meatus, bulla, uncinate and sphenoethmoidal recess; olfactory cleft), recording polyps, pus, septal deviation and masses. 1.3.3 Reading the CT Five things to find on the CT before every ethmoidectomy The ethmoid bulla and how close it lies to the medial orbital wall ; any erosion or old fracture of the lamina. The superior turbinate and how the posterior ethmoid relates to the skull base. The anterior and posterior ethmoid arteries : in bone, or hanging below the roof in a mesentery (more at risk). The height of the ethmoid compared with the maxillary sinus : a short ethmoid above a big antrum means the skull base is closer than it looks. Variants: Onodi cells (bringing the optic nerve and carotid close), suprabullar cells, Keros depth."],["osec","th:op-108~s2-1","The skull base","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The roof of the ethmoid slopes down from front to back and from lateral to medial ; it is thicker laterally and thinnest at the lateral lamella of the cribriform plate . Keros classification by depth of the olfactory fossa: type I 1 to 3 mm, type II 4 to 7 mm, type III 8 to 16 mm . The deeper the fossa, the longer and more exposed the thin lateral lamella, and the higher the risk of a CSF leak. Figure 1 Sagittal CT showing the slope of the skull base; posterior ethmoid cells marked P, the basal lamella of the middle turbinate marked with an asterisk. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.1 Figure 2 Keros types I, II and III by depth of the olfactory fossa. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.2 Figure 3 A short ethmoid above a well-pneumatised maxillary sinus: the skull base is closer than it appears. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.6"],["osec","th:op-108~s2-2","The arteries","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The anterior ethmoid artery is seen on coronal CT just behind the globe , at the notch between the superior oblique and medial rectus. Arteries running below the roof in a mesentery are more easily torn, and a torn artery can retract into the orbit and cause an orbital haematoma . Figure 4 Coronal CT: the left anterior ethmoid artery just behind the globe (arrow). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.4 Figure 5 Endoscopic view of the anterior (single arrow) and posterior (double arrows) ethmoid arteries, with a left maxillary antrostomy (asterisk). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.3"],["osec","th:op-108~s2-3","The four landmarks in order","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The lamina papyracea (medial orbital wall), seen once the bulla is removed. The basal lamella of the middle turbinate, which divides anterior from posterior ethmoid. The superior turbinate , seen through the opened basal lamella. The skull base in the posterior ethmoid, reached at the corner where the skull base, the medial orbital wall and the face of the sphenoid meet. Senior's pearl Once you have reached the corner, you can complete the ethmoidectomy safely by working forward along the roof. Until you have found it, do not touch the roof."],["osec","th:op-108~s3-1","Inject and decongest","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline endoscopically at the sphenopalatine region (about 1 cm behind the posterior end of the middle turbinate on the lateral wall), the axilla of the middle turbinate, the uncinate process and the medial surface of the middle turbinate. Place pledgets soaked in equal parts 0.05% oxymetazoline and 4% lidocaine (or 1:1000 adrenaline) for at least 10 minutes, one between the middle turbinate and lateral wall to gently medialise it and shrink the bulla region. (The uncinectomy and antrostomy are described in their own chapter.) Figure 6 The theatre set-up: surgeon on the patient's right, navigation above the head, the endoscopy screen facing the surgeon across the head, and the head raised and tilted toward the surgeon. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.5 Figure 7 Injection of the left lateral nasal wall, the axilla of the middle turbinate and the medial surface of the middle turbinate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.7 What you should see A pale, shrunken middle meatus with the uncinate, bulla and middle turbinate clearly seen."],["osec","th:op-108~s3-2","Remove the bulla and find the lamina","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Pass the J-curette behind the bulla with its curved end upward and bring it forward only , removing the whole bulla; never push it up (skull base) or sideways (orbit). Alternatively, enter the bulla with a sickle knife pointing down and remove it from the lamina with through-cutting forceps. Clear any partitions left on the lamina with a sphenoid punch or Kerrison rongeur. Figure 8 Removing the left ethmoid bulla with a J-curette. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.8 What you should see The lamina papyracea : a smooth, flat, yellow-white wall laterally, with no partitions left on it. The orbit Most orbital injuries happen when the lamina is not identified early, or when polyps and bleeding hide it. Find it before you go further back, and enter every new cell low and medial , away from the orbit and the skull base."],["osec","th:op-108~s3-3","Through the basal lamella","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Look at the basal lamella and the roof of the maxillary sinus (seen through the antrostomy) to set your line into the posterior ethmoid; the roof of the maxillary sinus lies at about the height of the floor of the posterior ethmoid. Pierce the vertical part of the basal lamella low and medially with the straight end of the J-curette or a sickle knife and remove it. Keep the horizontal part , which steadies the middle turbinate. What you should see The posterior ethmoid cells opening behind the basal lamella, and the superior turbinate medially. The floppy middle turbinate Removing too much of the basal lamella destabilises the middle turbinate, which then falls laterally after surgery and blocks the ostiomeatal complex and frontal drainage."],["osec","th:op-108~s3-4","Find the superior turbinate","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Clear the remaining soft tissue of the basal lamella with the microdebrider in a controlled sweep (from 6 to 9 o'clock on the left, 3 to 6 o'clock on the right) to show the front of the superior turbinate clearly. What you should see The head of the superior turbinate, with the posterior ethmoid cells lateral and above it."],["osec","th:op-108~s3-5","Reach the corner","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the posterior ethmoid partitions with 45-degree upturned through-cutting forceps (straight forceps for partitions running axially or sagittally) until you see the skull base at the back of the posterior ethmoid, and then the corner where it meets the medial orbital wall and the face of the sphenoid. What you should see The roof of the posterior ethmoid, smooth and slightly darker, meeting the lamina laterally and the sphenoid face behind. The Onodi cell A posterior ethmoid cell that extends above and lateral to the sphenoid can carry the optic nerve , and sometimes the carotid , in its wall. You will have seen it on the CT; work slowly here and do not mistake it for the sphenoid sinus."],["osec","th:op-108~s3-6","Clear the roof from back to front","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Change to a 30- or 45-degree telescope and remove the remaining partitions along the skull base from back to front , following its slope, with through-cutting instruments. Cut through mucosa, bone and mucosa cleanly; do not twist or strip. Identify the posterior and anterior ethmoid arteries , especially if they hang in a mesentery, and work around them. Figure 9 Removing the ethmoid partitions along the skull base with a through-cutting instrument. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 108.9 What you should see A smooth, lined ethmoid cavity: the lamina laterally, the roof above, the middle turbinate medially, and both arteries intact. Senior's pearl Through-cutting forceps are what make surgery \"functional\". Stripped mucosa heals with scar, osteitis and crusting; cleanly cut partitions leave a lined cavity that clears its own mucus. And never point the open window of a microdebrider at the orbit or skull base where the bone may be dehiscent."],["osec","th:op-108~s3-7","Finish","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Check for bleeding and for CSF, and keep the middle turbinate medial (a medialisation suture or controlled scarring to the septum if it is unstable). Packing is rarely needed; if the lamina was breached, avoid packing. What you should see The lamina papyracea intact from front to back, the skull base clean and dry along its length, and the middle turbinate stable in the midline. No clear fluid from the roof, and a soft eye on balloting the globe. Before the patient wakes Check both eyes for proptosis, a tense lid or a dilated pupil while the patient is still asleep. A retro-orbital haematoma found in recovery has already cost time. Senior's pearl Do the eye-press test at the end: press on the closed eye and watch the lamina. Movement of fat or lamina shows a breach you may not have noticed."],["osec","th:op-108~s4-1","Crisis drills","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · When it goes wrong","Orbital fat in the field 1 Recognise yellow fat that bulges when you press gently on the closed eye 2 Act stop working in that area do not pull or remove fat avoid packing 3 Then check the eye at the end and afterwards warn the patient and nurses about swelling and bruising Orbital haematoma 1 Recognise proptosis, a tense eye, chemosis, a dilated pupil, reduced vision, often after an ethmoid artery bleed 2 Act stop, remove any packs, call ophthalmology measure vision and pressure 3 Decompress lateral canthotomy and cantholysis without delay if vision or pupil reactions are threatened medical lowering of pressure (mannitol, acetazolamide, steroids) 4 Then endoscopic orbital decompression or ligation of the artery if bleeding continues CSF leak during surgery 1 Recognise clear fluid washing the blood away, a halo, or a visible defect in the roof or lateral lamella 2 Act repair it there and then define the defect, remove mucosa around it, place a graft (mucosa, fascia or fat) with sealant 3 Then bed rest, stool softeners, no nose blowing consider antibiotics per local policy Bleeding that hides the field 1 Act pledgets with vasoconstrictor, head up, ask for lower blood pressure (TIVA helps) 2 Escalate if you still cannot see safely, stop and come back another day"],["osec","th:op-108~s5-1","After the operation","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Aftercare","No nose blowing; sneeze with the mouth open. High-volume, low-pressure saline irrigation (about 240 ml split between the nostrils) three times a day, starting the day of or the day after surgery. Antibiotics at the surgeon's discretion. Polyposis: a course of oral steroid after surgery, for example methylprednisolone 32 mg daily for a week, then tapering over about three weeks. Review within a week for debridement of clot and crusts, then regularly until the cavity is lined. A long-term medical plan suited to the disease."],["osec","th:op-108~s5-2","Complications","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Aftercare","Complications of endoscopic sinus surgery Major Minor Orbital haemorrhage Periorbital bruising Loss of vision Periorbital emphysema Double vision Postoperative nosebleed CSF leak, meningitis, brain abscess Infection, crusting Massive bleeding needing transfusion Adhesions Intracranial bleeding, brain injury Stenosis of the maxillary or frontal opening Anosmia Hyposmia, worsened asthma, dental pain"],["ev","th:op-108~s5-3","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy: current evidence","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Aftercare","Who should have surgery. The international RAND appropriateness criteria recommend surgery for adults with uncomplicated chronic rhinosinusitis when CT shows disease (Lund–Mackay score at least 1), after at least eight weeks of nasal steroid and a short course of systemic steroid or antibiotic, with a SNOT-22 score of 20 or more (Rudmik et al., Int Forum Allergy Rhinol 2016). In the UK MACRO trial, endoscopic sinus surgery improved symptoms and quality of life over placebo, while clarithromycin did not (Philpott et al., Lancet 2025). Complications. Major complications (CSF leak, orbital injury, major bleeding) occur in well under 1% in large series. Image guidance is recommended for revision and complex cases, but has not been proven to reduce complications."],["osec","th:op-108~s5-3","Alternatives","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · Aftercare","Appropriate medical treatment comes first: culture-directed antibiotics for as long as the patient keeps improving (often 3 to 4 weeks), steroid sprays, a short course of oral steroid and sometimes topical antimicrobials. Exceptions where surgery comes sooner: impending orbital complications, a mucocele, and one-sided disease needing a diagnosis (always consider a dental source in one-sided maxillary disease). Whether to remove a diseased or unstable middle turbinate is still debated: a systematic review found no difference in quality of life, nasal resistance or frontal sinusitis, and somewhat better smell, with resection."],["osec","th:op-108~s6-1","Where the books differ","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra describes two techniques, Stammberger's anterior-to-posterior approach (uncinate to sphenoid, tailored to the disease) and Wigand's posterior-to-anterior approach (starting at the sphenoid, for extensive polyposis and revisions); gives the order uncinectomy, maxillary ostium, bullectomy, basal lamella pierced low and medial, posterior ethmoids, frontal recess and sphenoidotomy; removes the lateral lamella of a concha bullosa while preserving the medial for smell; packs when the septum is also operated on; continues antibiotics for 7 to 10 days and starts saline after a week; and lists major and minor complications. Lee gives the Keros types with type II as the commonest, the Onodi cell in about 30%, the slope of the fovea ethmoidalis of about 15 degrees, and the orbital rules: stop dissecting where fat is exposed, never remove fat, avoid packing, and treat chemosis and proptosis as an orbital compartment syndrome. Myers combines a forward start with the safe \"corner\" principle: find the lamina, the basal lamella, the superior turbinate and the skull base in that order, reach the junction of skull base, orbit and sphenoid, then clear the roof from back to front; it starts saline the same or next day and stresses the height of the ethmoid relative to the maxillary sinus over the Keros grade."],["osec","th:op-108~s6-2","Viva essentials","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The four landmarks and the corner . Keros types and why depth matters; the lateral lamella as the weakest point. The Onodi cell and what lies in its wall. Where the anterior ethmoid artery lies on CT and why a mesentery matters. Why the basal lamella is entered low and medial , and why its horizontal part is kept. Stammberger versus Wigand. Recognition and immediate management of an orbital haematoma (lateral canthotomy) and an intraoperative CSF leak . That surgery is an adjunct to medical treatment in chronic rhinosinusitis."],["osec","th:op-108~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Endoscopic Ethmoidectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating without studying the CT in all three planes. Leaving partitions on the lamina, which blocks the way back. Not identifying the superior turbinate. Taking too much basal lamella, leaving a floppy middle turbinate. Working on the roof before reaching the corner. Stripping mucosa instead of cutting it. Pointing the microdebrider window at a dehiscent orbit or skull base. Rocking the middle turbinate (lateral lamella fracture, CSF leak)."],["osec","th:op-109~s1-1","The case at a glance","External Ethmoidectomy · Operating note","External ethmoidectomy and ethmoidal artery ligation Summary Indications Ethmoidal artery ligation for epistaxis not controlled by sphenopalatine ligation or from the upper nose; subperiosteal orbital abscess; access for sinonasal tumours, orbital decompression or trauma; ethmoid disease where endoscopy is unavailable Contraindications Disease better treated endoscopically Anaesthesia General Position Supine, head up; protect the cornea (temporary tarsorrhaphy or shield) Tray Lynch incision set, Freer and Cottle elevators, malleable retractors, bipolar and clips, Kerrison rongeurs, sinus forceps, endoscope"],["osec","th:op-109~s1-2","Consent","External Ethmoidectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient A small scar beside the nose (webbing is uncommon with a straight incision). Double vision from medial rectus or trochlea injury, usually temporary. Watering eye if the lacrimal sac is injured. Vision loss, rare, from orbital haematoma or optic nerve injury. CSF leak if the skull base is breached."],["osec","th:op-109~s1-3","Before you start","External Ethmoidectomy · Operating note","Measure on CT the position of the ethmoidal foramina along the frontoethmoidal suture and their distance from the anterior lacrimal crest. The medial orbital wall in millimetres (average adult) From To Distance Anterior lacrimal crest Anterior ethmoidal foramen About 24 mm Anterior ethmoidal foramen Posterior ethmoidal foramen About 12 mm Posterior ethmoidal foramen Optic canal About 6 mm"],["osec","th:op-109~s2-1","Incise","External Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Inject the site. Make a straight incision halfway between the nasal dorsum and the medial canthus, from about 1 cm above to 1 cm below the canthus. Control the angular vessels with bipolar. Figure 1 The incision halfway between the nasal dorsum and the medial canthus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 109.1 What you should see Periosteum over the frontal process of the maxilla and nasal bones."],["osec","th:op-109~s2-2","Elevate the periorbita","External Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Incise and elevate the periosteum backwards off the medial orbital wall. Find the frontoethmoidal suture line, which marks the level of the skull base, and follow it back. Inferiorly elevate to the top of the lacrimal fossa and medial canthal tendon; keep the sac in the orbit. What you should see The lamina papyracea with the frontoethmoidal suture running backwards above it."],["osec","th:op-109~s2-3","Control the ethmoidal arteries","External Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Retract the orbital contents gently with a malleable retractor and find the anterior ethmoidal artery crossing from the orbit into its foramen on the suture line, about 24 mm behind the lacrimal crest. Coagulate it with bipolar (more secure than a clip) and divide it to continue. The posterior ethmoidal artery lies about 12 mm further back; the optic nerve about 6 mm behind that. Figure 2 The periorbita elevated from the lamina papyracea with the ethmoidal arteries in view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 109.2 What you should see The anterior ethmoidal artery tented between the periorbita and its foramen, then coagulated. Never beyond the posterior ethmoidal artery The optic nerve is only a few millimetres behind the posterior ethmoidal foramen. For epistaxis, ligating the anterior artery is usually enough; if the posterior must be controlled, coagulate it without dissecting past it."],["osec","th:op-109~s2-4","Open the ethmoid","External Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Fracture the lamina papyracea with an elevator below the suture line and widen the opening with Kerrison rongeurs up to the suture and forward to the posterior lacrimal crest. Remove the ethmoid cells medially and backwards to the basal lamella or the sphenoid face, keeping the middle turbinate as the medial limit and its skull base attachment intact. Figure 3 Removing the lamina papyracea to open the ethmoid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 109.3 What you should see The ethmoid cleared and communicating with the nose, the skull base above untouched. Senior's pearl The frontoethmoidal suture is the skull base. Stay below it when removing bone and you will not enter the anterior cranial fossa."],["osec","th:op-109~s2-5","Close","External Ethmoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Release the retractor, check the eye moves freely, and close periosteum and skin in layers. What you should see A soft orbit, normal pupil, and a fine straight wound."],["osec","th:op-109~s3-1","Crisis drills","External Ethmoidectomy · When it goes wrong","Retracted, bleeding anterior ethmoidal artery 1 Act bipolar the stump pressure do not pack the orbit blindly 2 Tight orbit afterwards lateral canthotomy and cantholysis ophthalmology Orbital fat herniates 1 Act shrink gently with bipolar do not pull on it"],["osec","th:op-109~s4-1","After surgery","External Ethmoidectomy · Aftercare","Eye observations (vision, pupils, proptosis, pain) for the first night. Ice packs. Remove skin sutures at about 5 days."],["ev","th:op-109~s4-2","External Ethmoidectomy: current evidence","External Ethmoidectomy · Aftercare","The 24-12-6 relationship (lacrimal crest to anterior ethmoidal foramen, to posterior foramen, to optic canal) is a standard guide to the medial orbital wall (Oxford Specialist Handbook of Otolaryngology). In refractory epistaxis without an obvious source, septal branches of the anterior ethmoidal artery may bleed and can be controlled endoscopically (Turri-Zanoni et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2018); the anterior ethmoidal artery can also be ligated by a transethmoidal endoscopic route after partial removal of the lamina (Araujo Filho et al., Braz J Otorhinolaryngol 2011)."],["osec","th:op-109~s4-2","Complications","External Ethmoidectomy · Aftercare","Orbital haematoma, diplopia, epiphora, scar webbing, CSF leak, vision loss."],["osec","th:op-109~s5-1","Where the books differ","External Ethmoidectomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers the external route for epistaxis, orbital infection, tumours and where endoscopy is unavailable, with bipolar control of the anterior ethmoidal artery and no dissection beyond the posterior one. Dhingra external ethmoidectomy for ethmoid disease and orbital abscess, and ethmoidal artery ligation in the epistaxis ladder. The literature endoscopic routes can reach the anterior ethmoidal artery but the external approach remains the reliable fallback."],["osec","th:op-109~s5-2","Viva essentials","External Ethmoidectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The incision and the plane. The 24-12-6 rule. The frontoethmoidal suture as the skull base. Ethmoidal artery ligation in epistaxis management."],["osec","th:op-109~s5-3","Common pitfalls","External Ethmoidectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Dissecting past the posterior ethmoidal artery. Removing bone above the frontoethmoidal suture. Injuring the lacrimal sac by elevating below the canthal tendon carelessly. Not protecting the cornea."],["osec","th:op-110~s1-1","The case at a glance","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Operating note","Endoscopic sphenoidotomy Summary Indications Chronic symptomatic sphenoid sinusitis after failure of maximal medical therapy; a neoplasm in the sphenoid, for biopsy or definitive treatment; CSF leak, with or without meningoencephalocele, for skull base repair; access for expanded endonasal approaches to the skull base or orbit Contraindications Not medically fit for surgery; a largely dehiscent carotid artery (relative) Routes Transnasal (transseptal) : through the sphenoethmoidal recess between septum and middle turbinate, for isolated sphenoid disease and sagittal-plane skull base access. Transethmoidal : lateral to the middle turbinate after ethmoidectomy, as part of functional sinus surgery for chronic sinusitis. Either may add a posterior septectomy for both sinuses. A transpterygoid extension reaches a pneumatised lateral recess Anaesthesia General; total intravenous anaesthesia may reduce bleeding; keep blood pressure and heart rate low, but keep the mean arterial pressure normal if the optic nerve is already compromised. Topical anaesthesia in the office for revision of a scarred ostium Position Supine, reverse Trendelenburg about 20 degrees, head turned slightly toward the surgeon Antibiotics First- or second-generation cephalosporin; clindamycin in penicillin allergy Tray Endoscopic sinus set (Cottle elevator, J curette, mushroom and Kerrison punches, straight through-cutting forceps); 0 degree 4 mm endoscope; microdebrider; image guidance with its probe; pledgets of 1:1000 adrenaline Imaging Thin-cut CT (under 1 to 1.25 mm) in all three planes, loaded on the navi"],["osec","th:op-110~s1-2","Consent","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to open the deepest sinus and let it drain, or to treat or sample a growth, or to repair a leak. Bleeding, sometimes from an artery in the back of the nose needing cautery. Leak of brain fluid and meningitis: rare. Damage to the eye or the nerve of sight , which can be permanent: rare but devastating. Injury to the main artery to the brain : very rare, life-threatening. Scarring that narrows the opening again, sometimes needing a further procedure. Reduced sense of smell: a small risk if part of the superior turbinate is removed. Nasal cleaning in clinic and saline rinses afterwards."],["osec","th:op-110~s1-3","Before you start","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Operating note","Ask about the cardinal symptoms of chronic rhinosinusitis: obstruction, postnasal drip or discharge, loss of smell and facial pain. Sphenoid sinusitis classically, but not reliably, causes pain at the vertex or deep behind the eyes. Look for features that suggest something other than inflammation: loss of vision or colour vision, double vision, dry eye, an orbital apex syndrome (reduced vision, ophthalmoplegia, proptosis) or a superior orbital fissure syndrome (ophthalmoplegia, proptosis), and neurological features: confusion, facial numbness, severe headache. In chronic sinusitis, record recent antibiotics and oral steroids, topical treatment and saline, the response, asthma, allergy, aspirin sensitivity and immunodeficiency. Tailor further questions: unilateral watery rhinorrhoea or meningitis for a meningocele; immunosuppression, diabetes and country of origin for chronic invasive fungal disease. Assess fitness for anaesthesia, and screen for systemic disease that presents in the nose: granulomatosis with polyangiitis, sarcoidosis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss), aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease and cystic fibrosis. Ask about previous sinus, nasal or skull base surgery and its complications: revision surgery proceeds through distorted anatomy. Ask about coagulopathy and atopy in the family, antiplatelet drugs, and herbal products such as garlic, ginseng and ginkgo. Examine the head and neck and perform diagnostic nasal endoscopy in every patient. After topical anaesthetic and decongestant, pass a 30 degree scope angled upward along t"],["osec","th:op-110~s1-4","Imaging","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Operating note","CT in axial, coronal and sagittal planes, ideally with cuts under 1 mm, defines the pneumatisation and bony walls: look for dehiscence of the optic canal, the carotid canal or the skull base , a deviated septum, ethmoid disease and a lateral recess. Read bone windows, and soft tissue windows when the sinus is opaque: heterogeneous opacification suggests a fungal ball, eosinophilic mucin or a tumour. MRI characterises an opaque sinus or a soft tissue lesion and shows spread outside the sinus: a suspected neoplasm, encephalocele or meningocele, or complicated sinusitis."],["osec","th:op-110~s1-5","Preparation","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Operating note","Consider oral steroids and antibiotics for 3 to 5 days beforehand to reduce inflammation and bleeding. Obtain medical clearance if needed and manage anticoagulants."],["osec","th:op-110~s2-1","The landmarks","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Anatomy you must see","The superior turbinate: the sphenoid ostium is always medial to it. Its anterior end is found by lateralising the middle turbinate (transnasal), or in the superior meatus just behind and medial to the basal lamella of the middle turbinate (transethmoidal). The sphenoid face (rostrum): in the unopened sinus it looks convex compared with the ethmoid skull base. The ostium lies in its inferior and medial quadrant, just lateral to where the septum joins the face. The choanal arch: the ostium is typically about 2 cm above the arch of the choana. The skull base: the ostium averages 11 mm below the skull base , about halfway between the floor of the sinus and the planum sphenoidale; it is closer to the skull base in a poorly pneumatised sinus. The orbital floor: after a maxillary antrostomy, the ostium lies below the level of the orbital floor, which is your upper limit when searching. Figure 1 The right sphenoid ostium medial to the superior turbinate, which is retracted laterally, after total ethmoidectomy and maxillary antrostomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 110.1"],["osec","th:op-110~s2-2","What surrounds the sinus","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Anatomy you must see","The planum sphenoidale: the roof, just in front of the thick tuberculum sellae. The optic nerve and lamina papyracea: the face of the sphenoid inserts laterally onto one or both. In an Onodi cell , a posterior ethmoid cell pneumatised behind the coronal plane of the sphenoid face, the optic nerve may be dehiscent in its wall. The internal carotid artery: dehiscent in the sinus in up to 22% . The intersinus septum inserts onto the carotid in 16% . The posterior septal branch of the sphenopalatine artery: runs 2 to 6 mm below the ostium in the mucosa above the choana; it is the pedicle of the nasoseptal flap, and is met when the ostium is widened downward. Olfactory epithelium: on the upper septum and the medial surfaces of the superior and middle turbinates, most concentrated in the posterior superior turbinate. The lateral recess: in some sinuses, pneumatised between the vidian and trigeminal nerves; reached by a transpterygoid extension. Figure 2 The right sphenoid sinus after sphenoidotomy: the pituitary (P) and the clival recess (C) on the posterior wall, and the impression of the optic nerve (asterisk) in the superolateral corner. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 110.3"],["osec","th:op-110~s3-1","Review the scan and prepare the nose","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Before the patient comes in, review thin-cut CT in all three planes and register it to the navigation system after intubation. For the transethmoidal route, note the ethmoid disease, the position of the anterior and posterior ethmoid arteries, and the shape and integrity of the orbit and skull base. Place pledgets of 1:1000 adrenaline on the mucosa medial to the middle turbinates. What you should see On the scan: the pneumatisation of the sphenoid, an Onodi cell if present, and the carotid and optic canals, with any dehiscence."],["osec","th:op-110~s3-2","Transnasal: lateralise the middle turbinate","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Place a Freer elevator on the lower part of the head of the middle turbinate and press it laterally, step by step from front to back, until its basal lamella is displaced and the anterior end of the superior turbinate comes into view. What you should see The narrow sphenoethmoidal recess between septum and turbinates, with the superior turbinate ahead and the sphenoid face behind it."],["osec","th:op-110~s3-3","Transethmoidal: through the ethmoid","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Perform a total ethmoidectomy (see the endoscopic ethmoidectomy chapter). Lower the basal lamella of the middle turbinate to see the lower part of the superior turbinate, and clear the posterior ethmoid cells of the superior meatus so the turbinate can be moved and trimmed. This route gives a wider corridor and shows the skull base and lamina papyracea early, before the ostium is opened. What you should see The lamina papyracea and skull base, and the lower part of the superior turbinate behind the opened basal lamella."],["osec","th:op-110~s3-4","Find the ostium","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Gently move the superior turbinate laterally with a straight navigation probe or a Cottle elevator and look medial to it for the ostium. In the transethmoidal route with an antrostomy, keep below the level of the orbital floor. What you should see The ostium medial to the superior turbinate, about 1 to 1.5 cm above the arch of the choana, close to the septum. Entering the wrong place Entering the sphenoid anywhere but its natural ostium, or mistaking an Onodi cell or posterior ethmoid cell for the sphenoid, is how the optic nerve and orbit are injured. Find the true ostium; confirm with navigation if in doubt."],["osec","th:op-110~s3-5","Trim the superior turbinate","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the lowest one fifth to one third of the superior turbinate, which may lie below the ostium and block its drainage, with straight through-cutting forceps or a powered instrument. In the transnasal route a limited posterior ethmoidectomy, taking cells within the superior meatus, gives more room and shows the lamina papyracea and ethmoid skull base early. Figure 3 The right sphenoid ostium in view after resecting the lower third of the superior turbinate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 110.2 What you should see The ostium fully exposed with the upper turbinate and its olfactory mucosa left in place."],["osec","th:op-110~s3-6","Enter and widen","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Enter the ostium bluntly with a J curette or Cottle elevator and enlarge it enough to pass a Kerrison or mushroom punch. Then widen it laterally toward the lamina papyracea and superiorly toward the planum sphenoidale , which gives lasting patency. Preserve mucosa, except in tumour surgery: stripped mucosa scars and closes the opening. What you should see A wide opening into the sinus, showing its posterior wall: the pituitary, the clival recess and the optic nerve impression (figure 2). Widening downward Going down toward the nasal floor meets the posterior septal branch of the sphenopalatine artery . If you open toward it, cauterise the mucosa over the sphenoid face and upper choana first. Remember that it is the pedicle of a nasoseptal flap you may need later. The intersinus septum and the back wall The intersinus septum may insert on the carotid, and the carotid may be dehiscent. Reduce or remove the septum only with sharp through-cutting instruments and great caution, and do not touch the posterior wall while you are looking for or widening the ostium. Stripping the face mucosa into the sinus Peeling the mucosa of the sphenoid face off the rostrum and pushing it into the sinus loses your landmarks and causes heavy bleeding inside the cavity."],["osec","th:op-110~s3-7","The midline approach","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · The operation, stage by stage","For bilateral access, or when oedema or polyps hide the ostium, disarticulate the posterior septum from the rostrum with a Cottle elevator and remove the rostral bone with rongeurs or a drill to open both sinuses, then enlarge the opening with Kerrison rongeurs. The face can be safely punctured with a Frazier suction inferomedial to the ostium , where the bone is usually thinnest. What you should see The keel of the rostrum, then both sinus cavities with the intersinus septum between them. Make it wide in osteitic disease In chronic inflammatory disease with osteitis, circumferential stenosis of a sphenoidotomy is common. A wide opening down to the floor of the sinus, or across the rostrum and intersinus septum, prevents it."],["osec","th:op-110~s4-1","Crisis drills","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · When it goes wrong","Arterial bleeding from the inferior edge 1 Recognise the posterior septal branch of the sphenopalatine artery, low on the sphenoid face 2 Control cauterise the mucosa over the sphenoid face and upper choana 3 Then continue, keeping further enlargement lateral and superior Suspected optic nerve or orbital injury 1 Causes imprecise lateral dissection of the face, an unrecognised Onodi cell containing the nerve, or entry away from the natural ostium 2 Act immediate ophthalmology referral for assessment of vision 3 Know it is devastating and difficult to correct prevention is precise entry through the natural ostium"],["osec","th:op-110~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Failing to find the true natural ostium; confusing an Onodi or posterior ethmoid cell with the sphenoid. Dissecting downward through the posterior septal artery. Too little blunt opening, so the punch will not pass. Touching the posterior wall during identification or dissection. Not preserving mucosa, outside tumour surgery. An opening too small, which restenoses. Stripping the face mucosa into the sinus."],["osec","th:op-110~s5-1","After the operation","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Aftercare","Give the standard instructions after sinus surgery, start saline irrigation on day 1 , and debride and clear the cavity in clinic at 7 to 14 days . Typical medicines are an antistaphylococcal antibiotic, oral prednisone and short-course analgesia. Survey endoscopically in clinic for persistent inflammation and restenosis."],["osec","th:op-110~s5-2","Complications","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Aftercare","Complications of sphenoidotomy Complication Notes Synechiae and stenosis Limited by early follow-up; symptomatic scar can be cut in clinic with through-cutting and powered instruments, and a circumferential stenosis enlarged under local anaesthesia with an elevator and mushroom punch Loss of smell From over-resection of the superior turbinate, which carries olfactory filaments Arterial bleeding Posterior septal branch of the sphenopalatine artery CSF leak Skull base breach Optic nerve or orbital injury Imprecise lateral dissection, an unrecognised Onodi cell, entry away from the natural ostium; immediate ophthalmology review Internal carotid artery injury A dehiscent carotid, or an intersinus septum inserting on it"],["ev","th:op-110~s5-3","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy: current evidence","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Aftercare","Safety. Bony dehiscence over the internal carotid artery and the optic nerve is common in CT studies, and Onodi (sphenoethmoidal) cells place the optic nerve in the posterior ethmoid; review the CT systematically before surgery (for example with the CLOSE checklist). Open the sphenoid inferiorly and medially, through the natural ostium or the posterior ethmoid, away from the lateral wall. Image guidance helps in revision and abnormal anatomy but does not replace a careful CT review."],["osec","th:op-110~s5-3","Alternatives","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · Aftercare","Balloon dilatation of the ostium, under local anaesthesia in the office or with other surgery in theatre, with image guidance to confirm entry; for chronic sinusitis without polyps or recurrent acute sinusitis. Continued medical treatment and observation , appropriate for most patients with inflammatory sphenoid disease."],["osec","th:op-110~s6-1","Does trimming the superior turbinate cost smell?","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · For the examination","The superior turbinate carries olfactory epithelium, most densely at its back, and loss of smell lowers quality of life and is dangerous (spoiled food, smoke, gas). Yet in a histological study of the inferior superior turbinate removed at sinus surgery, olfactory fibres were found in only 16% of specimens, and none of the 12% of patients who reported smell loss afterwards had olfactory neurons in their specimen. The evidence so far suggests that removing the inferior part of the superior turbinate carries a low risk to smell; over-resection is what to avoid."],["osec","th:op-110~s6-2","Viva essentials","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The ostium: medial to the superior turbinate , in the inferomedial quadrant of the face, about 2 cm above the choana , about 11 mm below the skull base . Transnasal versus transethmoidal routes: indications and advantages. The face inserts laterally on the lamina papyracea, sometimes the optic nerve . Onodi cell and the optic nerve. Carotid dehiscence (up to 22%) and the septum inserting on it (16%). The posterior septal artery below the ostium, and its role in the nasoseptal flap. Widen laterally and superiorly, not inferiorly. How you would manage bleeding, CSF and an orbital injury."],["osec","th:op-110~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Endoscopic Sphenoidotomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Searching too high or too lateral for the ostium. Opening the sinus through the posterior ethmoid without confirming you are in the sphenoid. Removing the intersinus septum without sharp instruments and great caution. Taking too much superior turbinate. A small opening in osteitic disease."],["osec","th:op-111~s1-1","The case at a glance","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","Frontal sinus trephination Summary Indications Acute frontal sinusitis with impending complications when endoscopic drainage is not possible; an aid to finding the frontal recess during endoscopic surgery (irrigation from above); combined above-and-below access to lateral frontal disease, mucoceles, fungal balls, osteomas or tumours Contraindications A small or absent frontal sinus (check CT: a hypoplastic sinus means the hole may enter the anterior cranial fossa); suspected tumour of the anterior table Site Medial brow, on a flat part of the anterior table or floor, medial to the supraorbital notch, guided by CT and navigation Anaesthesia General with the endoscopic procedure, or local Tray Trephine kit with soft tissue sleeve, depth stop and irrigation cannula (or a drill with a guard), 15 blade, syringe and tubing, culture swab, fine absorbable sutures"],["osec","th:op-111~s1-2","Consent","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","What to tell the patient A small scar in the eyebrow and temporary numbness of the forehead. Skin burn from the drill (rare). CSF leak if the back wall is breached; eye injury if irrigation reaches the orbit through a thin floor. Infection of the bone; a small dent if a larger hole is made."],["osec","th:op-111~s1-3","Before you start","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","Measure the frontal sinus on CT: depth from the anterior to the posterior table, height and lateral extent, and look for dehiscence of the orbital roof or posterior table. Plan the depth limit for the drill. Figure 1 Coronal CT: choosing the trephine site over a well-pneumatised sinus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 111.1"],["osec","th:op-111~s2-1","Mark and incise","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Confirm the site with navigation or palpable landmarks. Inject 1 ml of local anaesthetic with adrenaline. Make a stab incision in the medial brow, on the line of the supraorbital foramina, over flat frontal bone. The mobile brow skin lets you slide the hole upward onto the anterior table. Figure 2 The incision in the medial brow. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 111.3 What you should see Brow skin elevated, with the periosteum intact beneath."],["osec","th:op-111~s2-2","Drill the hole","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Dissect bluntly to bone. Seat the toothed soft tissue sleeve on the periosteum to protect the skin, set the depth stop from the CT, and drill slowly with irrigation into the sinus. Figure 3 Trephination of the frontal sinus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 111.2 What you should see A sudden loss of resistance into the sinus, with mucus or pus returning. Stop at the anterior table The posterior table is a few millimetres behind and often thin. Use the depth stop and never advance after the drill has entered the sinus."],["osec","th:op-111~s2-3","Cannulate and irrigate","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Pass the stylet and insert the fluted cannula so it grips the bone. Send pus for culture. Irrigate with saline (or fluorescein-tinted saline during endoscopic surgery) and watch for it in the nose to identify the frontal recess. What you should see Irrigant or dye appearing in the frontal recess on the endoscope, and none around the eye. Senior's pearl Watch the eye while you irrigate. Swelling of the upper lid means the fluid is tracking into the orbit through a dehiscent floor: stop at once."],["osec","th:op-111~s2-4","Finish","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the cannula, or leave a drain for repeated irrigation in acute sinusitis, and close in two layers. A larger trephine can admit a small endoscope and instruments for lateral disease. What you should see A neat brow wound, or a secured drain if irrigation will continue."],["osec","th:op-111~s3-1","Crisis drills","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · When it goes wrong","Clear fluid from the trephine 1 Act stop assess for posterior table breach repair endoscopically or through the trephine with fascia Skin burn 1 Act excise the burnt skin and close"],["osec","th:op-111~s4-1","After surgery","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","Remove sutures (if non-absorbable) at a week; manage the drain if left. Continue the treatment of the sinusitis."],["ev","th:op-111~s4-2","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus: current evidence","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","In patients with frontal sinus disease after bifrontal craniotomy, both endoscopic trephination and the osteoplastic flap were safe and effective, with trephination less invasive (Yu et al., Br J Neurosurg 2021). Combined endoscopic and mini-trephination (above and below) approaches are reported for isolated frontal fungal balls and lateral disease (Rinaldi et al., Rev Laryngol Otol Rhinol 2012)."],["osec","th:op-111~s4-2","Complications","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","Skin burn, scar, forehead numbness, osteitis, soft tissue depression into a large defect, CSF leak, orbital injury."],["osec","th:op-111~s5-1","Where the books differ","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers mini-trephination mainly as an adjunct to endoscopic frontal surgery, with a kit that protects the skin and limits depth. Dhingra trephination for acute frontal sinusitis not responding to treatment, through the floor of the sinus under the medial brow."],["osec","th:op-111~s5-2","Viva essentials","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications today versus in the past. Site and why the CT matters. Risks : posterior table, orbit, skin."],["osec","th:op-111~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Trephination of the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","Common pitfalls Trephining a hypoplastic sinus. Drilling beyond the anterior table. Irrigating without watching the eye."],["osec","th:op-112~s1-1","The case at a glance","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","Endoscopic frontal sinusotomy (Draf IIa) Summary Indications Chronic frontal sinusitis refractory to maximal medical therapy : eosinophilic or non-eosinophilic chronic rhinosinusitis with or without polyps; mechanical obstruction (anatomical variants of the recess, osteoma); mucocele. Acute frontal sinusitis that is severe and refractory to intravenous antibiotics or has intracranial or orbital complications. Limited tumours not needing an open approach Contraindications Uncomplicated acute frontal sinusitis; extensive tumours better treated open; comorbidity raising the risk of general anaesthesia; inadequate instruments or expertise Anaesthesia General, oral intubation, which protects the airway and stops unexpected movement; for a right-handed surgeon an oral RAE tube to the left of the tongue, taped to the left lower lip Position Supine as for any sinus surgery, head slightly toward the surgeon, head up 30 degrees ; the eyes left undraped to watch and palpate Antibiotics No evidence for intraoperative antibiotics; often a first-generation cephalosporin (clindamycin in allergy) at induction; a first- or second-generation cephalosporin for a week if packing is left Tray Basic sinus set and frontal sinus set; 0, 30, 45 and 70 degree telescopes (reverse-post angled scopes keep the light lead up and away); angled microdebrider blades or burs; navigation if used Skills Advanced endoscopic sinus surgery, fluent with 45 and 70 degree telescopes; turning axial, coronal and sagittal CT into a 3D picture"],["osec","th:op-112~s1-2","Consent","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to open the drainage channel of the frontal sinus so it can drain and be treated with rinses and sprays. Forehead pain: if pain is your main complaint, it may not improve even though the scan shows frontal disease. Bleeding, including from the artery at the back of the channel (anterior ethmoid artery). Leak of brain fluid and meningitis: rare. Injury to the eye socket: rare. Scarring that blocks the channel again, which may need further surgery; a stent is sometimes used. Saline rinses several times a day and clinic cleaning afterwards."],["osec","th:op-112~s1-3","Before you start","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","Patients are selected by the same criteria as for any endoscopic sinus surgery: significant symptoms of chronic sinusitis by the American Academy of Otolaryngology criteria despite maximal medical therapy. Ask about aspirin sensitivity, asthma, atopy and eosinophilia. New or continuing frontal problems after previous surgery suggest chronic inflammatory mucosal disease or scarring in the recess, for example synechiae between the middle turbinate and the lateral nasal wall or the face of a suprabullar cell. Frontal headache alone A patient whose main complaint is isolated or severe frontal pain should be told it may not improve, even when the scan shows frontal disease. Frontal headache on its own does not make a good candidate. On endoscopy look for inflammation and polyps in the middle meatus, scarring in the recess or anterior ethmoid after previous surgery, and where and what the secretions are. Examine the face: proptosis or orbital cellulitis means an orbital complication, and a soft swelling of the forehead suggests osteomyelitis with a subperiosteal abscess of the anterior table ( Pott's puffy tumour ). Every patient needs CT of the sinuses . Low-dose scanners give fine cuts of 0.6 to 1.0 mm, enough for multiplanar reconstruction and navigation, with little extra radiation. Review the scan with multiplanar reconstruction software, the three planes and crosshairs together as in theatre. MRI is not routine but helps with a suspected tumour or mucocele. Stop antiplatelet drugs; systemic steroids or antibiotics before surgery reduce bleeding and oedema in a space where a"],["osec","th:op-112~s2-1","How the uncinate decides the drainage","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Anatomy you must see","In most patients the uncinate rises from its attachments to the inferior turbinate and lacrimal bone and blends with the medial wall of the agger nasi cell . Then, in about 85% , the frontal sinus drains between the uncinate and the middle turbinate , that is medial and posterior to the agger nasi. In the rest the uncinate attaches to the middle turbinate or the skull base and the frontal sinus drains directly into the infundibulum . If the uncinate attaches both to the middle turbinate and to the agger nasi, the sinus drains posteriorly . Figure 1 The superior attachment of the uncinate: its three patterns of attachment, which decide where the frontal sinus drains. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 112.1"],["osec","th:op-112~s2-2","The walls of the recess","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Anatomy you must see","In front and laterally: the uncinate, the agger nasi and the Kuhn frontal cells; laterally, once the anterolateral cells are removed, the lamina papyracea . Behind: the ethmoid bulla, suprabullar cells and supraorbital ethmoid cells. Medially: the attachment of the middle turbinate and the thin lateral lamella of the cribriform plate . The axilla of the middle turbinate, where it joins the lateral nasal wall, marks the front and lower limit of the agger nasi region. The slope of the posterior table as it joins the ethmoid skull base decides the front-to-back depth you can achieve."],["osec","th:op-112~s2-3","Cells and the artery","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Anatomy you must see","Learn the Kuhn classification of frontoethmoid cells, and the newer systems (the European position paper and the International Frontal Sinus Anatomy Classification). Supraorbital ethmoid cells are suspected when the frontal sinus looks multiseptated on coronal CT. When present, the true frontal recess lies medial and anterior to the supraorbital cell's opening. The anterior ethmoid artery usually lies at the posterior margin of the recess , often at the back of a supraorbital cell. Find it on the scan. Figure 2 Navigation localising the anterior ethmoid artery (AEA) at the posterior border of the frontal sinus opening (FS) and the supraorbital ethmoid cell (SOE) in coronal, sagittal and axial planes, with the endoscopic view: the frontal recess lies medial and anterior to the supraorbital cell opening. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 112.2"],["osec","th:op-112~s2-4","What to plan at the scan","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Anatomy you must see","The most important part of planning is an accurate 3D picture of the size and arrangement of every cell around the recess. Look at the uncinate, the agger nasi, the size and depth of the frontal sinus, the anterior ethmoid artery, and supraorbital cells. Some surgeons build 3D models of the drainage pathway."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-1","Decongest and inject","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Spray 0.05% oxymetazoline every 10 minutes for 30 minutes in the holding area, or place oxymetazoline patties after induction while positioning. Inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline into the superior attachment of the middle turbinate, the lower uncinate, and the junction of the basal lamella with the palatine bone. A 25 to 27 G needle on a 3 mL syringe suits the turbinate and uncinate; a 22 G spinal needle bent 45 degrees 1.5 cm from the tip is stiff enough to reach the sphenopalatine branches as they leave the palatine bone. One author injects along the maxillary line with the bent needle, entering just above the inferior turbinate and advancing submucosally up toward the axilla, injecting as he withdraws: one puncture, low, away from the frontal recess. Place adrenaline or oxymetazoline patties in the middle meatus and over the septal swell body, and wait 5 minutes . What you should see Blanching at the axilla of the middle turbinate and along the uncinate, and a wide, dry middle meatus."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-2","Uncinectomy and nasofrontal bone","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the uncinate with a backbiter and 90 degree Blakesley forceps, a microdebrider or a sickle knife, as you prefer. Then take bone from the nasofrontal process between the front of the middle turbinate attachment and the lamina papyracea, the anterior wall of the agger nasi region, with a Hosemann or angled Stammberger mushroom punch. This lets you start with less angled telescopes, a simplified axillary flap approach without a flap. Figure 3 Removing bone from the nasofrontal process with a Hosemann punch. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 112.3 What you should see The agger nasi opened from the front, and the frontal process of the maxilla thinned so a less angled scope reaches the recess."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-3","Ethmoidectomy first","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Perform an ethmoidectomy to the extent of the disease before you enter the recess. It gives room to work along the ethmoid skull base from back to front. What you should see The lamina papyracea laterally and the ethmoid roof followed forward from the sphenoid face."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-4","Enter the recess","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Work from posterior to anterior and medial to lateral , staying in front of, lateral to and below the lateral lamella of the cribriform plate and the posterior table. Start with a 30 degree reverse-post telescope at the back and bottom of the recess and move to more angled scopes as you go forward and up. Identify the anterior ethmoid artery at the posterior margin, at the back of a supraorbital cell if one is present. What you should see The posterior table sloping up and forward from the skull base, the anterior ethmoid artery behind, and the dark drainage pathway medial to the agger nasi. The lateral lamella The medial wall of the recess is the thin lateral lamella of the cribriform plate, and the posterior table lies behind. Keep in front of, lateral to and below them; CSF leak is a recognised risk of this operation."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-5","Confirm the pathway","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","With navigation, gently slip a 70 or 90 degree seeker into the frontal sinus to confirm the drainage pathway, even before much dissection. It usually lies medial and posterior to the back wall of the agger nasi . Figure 4 Triplanar CT navigation (A) and the 45 degree endoscopic view (B): an angled seeker in the frontal sinus shows the outflow pathway before major dissection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 112.4 What you should see The seeker passing up into the sinus, the crosshairs confirming it in all three planes."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-6","Remove the partitions","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the medial and posterior walls of the agger nasi and any type I or II frontal cells, which are not attached to the skull base: either sharply with angled frontal punches, or by fracturing them from back to front with a 55 or 90 degree curette under a 45 or 70 degree telescope. Remove the fragments with angled non-cutting giraffe forceps or an angled microdebrider, taking care not to grab and strip the mucosa . Figure 5 An angled frontal sinus punch removing a bony partition. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 112.5 Figure 6 Angled non-cutting forceps removing a bone fragment. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 112.6 What you should see The true frontal ostium with intact mucosa all round, and the sinus interior on an angled scope. Change your view When the anatomy will not make sense, change the telescope (from 45 to 70 degrees, say). A new angle often shows you the anatomy in a new light. Angled scopes need not stay at 12 o'clock either: rotate the light post a little to turn the view."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-7","Enlarge the opening","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","With the agger nasi and frontal cells gone, enlarge the opening anteriorly and laterally with a Hosemann punch or an angled Kerrison (Bachert) punch. Then leave the frontal sinus itself alone. Figure 7 Enlarging the frontal sinus opening with a Hosemann punch. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 112.7 What you should see A wide, mucosa-lined opening from the recess into the sinus, the middle turbinate intact medially, the lamina papyracea laterally and the artery safe behind."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-8","Stent only when needed","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Stenting is rarely needed. Relative indications: An opening narrower than 5 mm . Extensive or circumferential mucosal trauma with bone exposed. Severe polyposis or mucosal hyperplasia. A destabilised or lateralised middle turbinate. Frontoethmoid mucoceles, a sign of long-standing disease or previous failed surgery. What you should see An opening you can pass a curved suction through freely, with mucosa preserved around at least part of its rim."],["osec","th:op-112~s3-9","Frontal sinus rescue","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","A common problem in revision surgery is a lateralised middle turbinate stump left when the lower turbinate was removed, scarred to the agger nasi, frontal cells or lamina papyracea. Kuhn's frontal sinus rescue deals with it: divide the synechiae and find and enter the recess; dissect the nasal mucosa off the medial side and the sinus mucosa off the lateral side of the bony remnant; resect the bone and nasal mucosa at the skull base; then rotate the flap of sinus mucosa up and medially to cover the roof of the nasal vault. What you should see A mucosal flap from the stump laid medially over the bare bone, and an open path into the frontal sinus."],["osec","th:op-112~s4-1","Crisis drills","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · When it goes wrong","Oozing fills the recess 1 Stop do not dissect blind through blood in the tight recess 2 Pack oxymetazoline or adrenaline patties for a few minutes 3 Resume when the view is clear keeping the head up 30 degrees reduces bleeding"],["osec","th:op-112~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Using grasping instead of through-cutting instruments, stripping mucosa. Letting a Hosemann punch clog: clean it regularly so it stays sharp and does not drop bone and mucosa into the field. Keeping angled scopes rigidly at 12 o'clock. Dissecting on through oozing instead of packing."],["osec","th:op-112~s5-1","After the operation","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","Usually a day case. Leave no packing, an absorbable or non-absorbable dressing, or a steroid-eluting stent, as you prefer; the authors use none or an absorbable dressing in the middle meatus and anterior ethmoid, below the recess, adding one especially if the middle turbinate was destabilised or early topical steroid is wanted. Start frequent low-pressure, high-volume (240 mL) saline irrigation several times a day from day 1 . Debride gently in clinic at about 1 and 2 weeks , dividing early synechiae and removing fibrinous debris from the recess."],["osec","th:op-112~s5-2","Complications","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","Complications of endoscopic frontal sinus surgery Complication Notes Haemorrhage Including the anterior ethmoid artery Synechiae More likely when mucosa is stripped; divided at early debridement CSF rhinorrhoea Unintended entry into the cranial cavity Orbital entry Through the lamina papyracea"],["ev","th:op-112~s5-3","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus: current evidence","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","Classification. The International Frontal Sinus Anatomy Classification (IFAC) describes frontal cells by their position (anterior, posterior, medial) and grades the extent of surgery from Draf I to III (EFSS) (Wormald et al., Int Forum Allergy Rhinol 2016). Draf III. In a meta-analysis of 1205 patients, the endoscopic modified Lothrop procedure gave symptom improvement in 87%, patency in 91% and revision in 13% (Shih et al., Laryngoscope 2018). Preserve mucosa around the frontal ostium to prevent stenosis; balloon dilatation suits limited disease without polyps."],["osec","th:op-112~s5-3","Alternatives","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","Balloon dilatation for limited frontal disease. Trephination for severe, complicated acute frontal sinusitis. Drill-out procedures (Draf IIb, Draf III) for more severe chronic disease. Obliteration or cranialisation in selected cases, now less often needed. Open surgery when the disease, the surgeon's expertise or the instruments demand it."],["osec","th:op-112~s6-1","Does image guidance help?","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","Navigation is widely used, and most single studies show no benefit. But a meta-analysis of 14 studies (Dalgorf and colleagues, 2013) found more major complications without it (inadvertent entry beyond the nose and sinuses, bleeding needing surgery or embolisation, abandoned procedures): relative risk 0.48 with guidance. Total complications were also higher without it (relative risk 0.66). So pooled data suggest guidance may reduce complications, though there is no evidence that it improves outcomes."],["osec","th:op-112~s6-2","The Draf classification","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","Draf I is an ethmoidectomy without touching the frontal recess. Draf IIa , this operation and the commonest frontal sinusotomy, removes the partitions of the recess between the middle turbinate and the orbit, keeping the turbinate. Draf IIb and III extend beyond the recess and remove part or all of the frontal sinus floor; there are few data on when they should be chosen over a IIa, even in severe polyposis."],["osec","th:op-112~s6-3","Viva essentials","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for How the uncinate attachment determines drainage; the 85% rule. The boundaries of the recess, and the lateral lamella as its weakest wall. The anterior ethmoid artery at the back of the recess; supraorbital cells and where the true recess lies. The order: uncinectomy, ethmoidectomy, then back to front and medial to lateral. Which cells you remove (not attached to the skull base) and how, preserving mucosa. Indications for stenting. The frontal sinus rescue for a lateralised turbinate stump. Draf I to III. Why acute sinusitis and frontal headache alone are poor indications."],["osec","th:op-112~s6-4","Common pitfalls","Endoscopic Approach to the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","Common pitfalls Starting without a 3D plan of the cells from the scan. Draping the eyes. Entering the recess before doing the ethmoidectomy. Mistaking a supraorbital cell for the frontal sinus."],["osec","th:op-113~s1-1","The case at a glance","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · Operating note","Draf III (endoscopic modified Lothrop) Summary Indications Recalcitrant frontal sinusitis after previous endoscopic frontal surgery; failed obliteration; mucociliary disease (cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia); frontal tumours (inverted papilloma, osteoma, fibrous dysplasia); selected trauma and posterior table CSF leaks; anterior access for skull base surgery Contraindications A narrow anteroposterior frontal dimension (under about 1 cm at the beak); unfitness; disease too lateral to reach (a supraorbital cell far out may need an external approach) Approaches Inside-out (from an identified frontal recess) or outside-in (from below, for scarred or lost recesses) Anaesthesia General, hypotensive Tray 0, 30, 45 and 70 degree endoscopes, navigation, angled drills with coarse diamond burrs, frontal instruments, optional frontal trephine for fluorescein irrigation"],["osec","th:op-113~s1-2","Consent","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · Operating note","What to tell the patient CSF leak (repaired at the time if recognised), pneumocephalus, meningitis. Orbital injury, retrobulbar haematoma, double vision. Bleeding. Reduced smell if the olfactory area is disturbed. Restenosis of the opening and revision surgery (about one in eight over time). Cosmetic change if too much bone is removed in front (rare)."],["osec","th:op-113~s1-3","Before you start","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · Operating note","On CT measure the anteroposterior diameter of the frontal beak, map the frontal cells, the anterior ethmoidal arteries, the skull base height and the olfactory fossa depth, and look for dehiscence of the posterior table or orbit. Load navigation. Figure 1 A completed Draf IIb on the right: the starting point for an inside-out Draf III. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 113.1"],["osec","th:op-113~s2-1","Inside-out: open one frontal sinus fully","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · The operation, stage by stage","Identify the frontal recess on the easier side (navigation, or fluorescein irrigated through a trephine). Remove the head of the middle turbinate up to the back of the ostium, then remove the frontal sinus floor from the ostium to the septum: a Draf IIb. What you should see One frontal sinus widely open to the nasal septum."],["osec","th:op-113~s2-2","Septal window","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · The operation, stage by stage","Make a superior septectomy: start opposite the attachment of the middle turbinate, angle the cut forwards to end 5 to 10 mm in front of the turbinate head, run the lower cut back to the turbinate, and the posterior cut up along the front edge of the turbinate to preserve olfactory mucosa. Figure 2 The septal window. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 113.2 Figure 3 The planned superior septectomy and the view of both middle turbinates after it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 113.5 and 113.6 and 113.7 What you should see Both middle turbinate heads and both frontal recess areas visible through one nostril."],["osec","th:op-113~s2-3","Find the first olfactory fibre","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · The operation, stage by stage","Elevate mucosa back along the remaining upper septum towards the olfactory cleft until the first olfactory fibre appears. It marks the posterior limit of drilling on each side. Figure 4 Identifying the first olfactory fibre. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 113.9 What you should see A nerve fibre entering the cribriform plate at the front of the olfactory cleft. The skull base is directly behind the first olfactory fibre Drill only in front of the first olfactory fibres and the posterior table of the frontal sinus. The skull base is thinnest here and a CSF leak is the commonest serious complication."],["osec","th:op-113~s2-4","Drill the floors and beak","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · The operation, stage by stage","With a 4 mm coarse diamond, drill in the midline from the septal window upwards and outwards, removing the nasofrontal beak, the frontal sinus floors and the frontal processes of the maxilla in a horseshoe shape, from one lamina papyracea to the other, as far forward as the skin of the glabella allows. Remove the intersinus septum. Figure 5 Drilling begins in the midline and extends laterally over the frontal process of the maxilla. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 113.10 and 113.11 What you should see A single wide horseshoe-shaped opening joining both frontal sinuses, with intact mucosa on the posterior table."],["osec","th:op-113~s2-5","Outside-in when no recess can be found","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · The operation, stage by stage","When scarring, new bone or tumour hides both recesses, do the ethmoidectomies to define the orbit and skull base, make the septal window first, and drill from below the frontal floor upwards in the midline (in front of the first olfactory fibres) until the sinuses are entered, then widen. Figure 6 The frontal floor exposed and the completed inside-out opening. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 113.3 and 113.4 What you should see Entry into the frontal sinuses from below without having needed to find a recess. Senior's pearl Make it as big as the bony limits allow: lamina to lamina and as far forward as safe. The opening narrows by a third or more as it heals, so a small Draf III becomes a stenosed Draf III. Preserve the mucosa on the posterior wall and lay free mucosal grafts on bare bone if you can."],["osec","th:op-113~s3-1","Crisis drills","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · When it goes wrong","CSF leak from the skull base 1 Act identify the defect repair at once with a multilayer graft or septal flap 2 After head up, no straining watch for pneumocephalus Orbital haematoma 1 Act stop, remove packing, lateral canthotomy and cantholysis ophthalmology"],["osec","th:op-113~s4-1","After surgery","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · Aftercare","High-volume saline irrigation and topical steroid irrigations; debridement in clinic over the first months to prevent scarring and crusting; treat infection. Long-term endoscopic review."],["ev","th:op-113~s4-2","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop: current evidence","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · Aftercare","In 229 patients with recalcitrant chronic rhinosinusitis (on average 3.8 previous operations), the Draf III succeeded without further surgery in 95% over about 4 years; allergic fungal sinusitis and recurrent Staphylococcus aureus infection predicted failure (Naidoo et al., Laryngoscope 2014). A meta-analysis of 1,205 patients found significant or complete symptom improvement in 87%, patency in 91% and revision in about 13%, with revisions rising after 2 years of follow-up (Shih et al., Laryngoscope 2018)."],["osec","th:op-113~s4-2","Complications","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · Aftercare","CSF leak, pneumocephalus, orbital injury, haemorrhage, anosmia, restenosis, cosmetic depression, saddling if too much septum is removed."],["osec","th:op-113~s5-1","Where the books differ","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers inside-out and outside-in techniques, the first olfactory fibre as the posterior landmark and a horseshoe opening from lamina to lamina. Dhingra and K. J. Lee the Draf classification (I, IIa, IIb, III) and the place of external procedures such as the osteoplastic flap. The literature patency about 90% and success in most recalcitrant cases, with revisions accumulating over time."],["osec","th:op-113~s5-2","Viva essentials","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The Draf classification. Indications and the minimum anteroposterior diameter. The boundaries : laminae, skull base and first olfactory fibre, glabellar skin. Inside-out versus outside-in. Stenosis and how to prevent it."],["osec","th:op-113~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Draf III, Endoscopic Modified Lothrop · For the examination","Common pitfalls Drilling behind the first olfactory fibre. Making the opening too small. Stripping mucosa off the posterior table. Over-resecting the septum and causing a saddle."],["osec","th:op-114~s1-1","The case at a glance","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","Osteoplastic flap with frontal sinus obliteration Summary Indications Recurrent chronic frontal sinusitis after failed endoscopic surgery (including failed Draf III); lateral frontal mucoceles out of endoscopic reach (lateral to the mid-pupil and high on the posterior table); severe frontal fractures involving the outflow tract or with CSF leak; tumours or osteomas that cannot be removed endoscopically Contraindications A tiny frontal sinus; when an endoscopic route will do Incision Bicoronal (standard); mid-forehead crease (balding men); upper brow \"gull wing\" (quick, but numbs the forehead) Outline Navigation, or a template cut from a 6-foot Caldwell radiograph Obliteration Abdominal fat (most common); pericranium or bone; or leave unobliterated if the outflow is restored Anaesthesia General Tray Bicoronal set, navigation, oscillating saw with a fine blade, osteotomes, drills with cutting and diamond burrs, plates, fat harvest set"],["osec","th:op-114~s1-2","Consent","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","What to tell the patient Forehead numbness (especially with a brow incision) and a scar ; hair loss along a bicoronal scar. CSF leak, dural injury, meningitis if the osteotomy enters the skull. Contour irregularity, bossing or a dent of the forehead. Mucocele forming years later inside an obliterated sinus (lifelong follow-up, MRI). Abdominal scar from the fat graft. Facial nerve (temporal branch) weakness, rare."],["osec","th:op-114~s1-3","Before you start","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · Operating note","Map the sinus on CT (navigation) or make the Caldwell template. MRI helps separate a mucocele from fat or tumour, and is the baseline for future surveillance of an obliterated sinus. Figure 1 A large lateral frontal mucocele on CT and MRI. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 114.1 and 114.2"],["osec","th:op-114~s2-1","Raise the scalp","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Make a bicoronal incision behind the hairline from helix to helix and raise the flap in the subgaleal plane down to the supraorbital rims, keeping the supraorbital and supratrochlear bundles in the flap. Stay deep to the temporoparietal fascia laterally to protect the frontal branch. Figure 2 Bicoronal or brow incisions to approach the frontal sinus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 114.3 What you should see Intact pericranium over the frontal bone and the supraorbital rims exposed."],["osec","th:op-114~s2-2","Incise the pericranium","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Cut the pericranium above the planned upper osteotomy so it will cover the bone cut when replaced, and leave it attached to the bone flap below as its blood supply. What you should see A pericranial hinge left on the lower part of the frontal bone."],["osec","th:op-114~s2-3","Mark and cut the bone flap","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the sinus outline with navigation or the template, then cut inside it with a fine saw angled (bevelled) towards the sinus. Cut the medial and lateral limbs, then the upper limb. Figure 3 The sinus outlined from the template and the bevelled osteotomies cut with the saw. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 114.4 and 114.5 What you should see Saw cuts entering the sinus cavity everywhere, never the cranium. Stay inside the outline An osteotomy outside the sinus margin goes straight into the anterior cranial fossa: dura, CSF and the superior sagittal sinus in the midline. Bevel the blade inwards and check with navigation."],["osec","th:op-114~s2-4","Fracture the flap down","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Lever the flap forwards with osteotomes through the upper cut and crack it along the sinus floor, hinging it down on its pericranium. Avoid fracturing the anterior table itself; plate it if it breaks. What you should see The whole sinus open, with the anterior wall hinged down on the brows."],["osec","th:op-114~s2-5","Clear the disease and every scrap of mucosa","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Treat the pathology (drain the mucocele, remove the osteoma or tumour). If obliterating, strip all mucosa from the sinus walls and the underside of the flap and drill the inner cortex with a diamond burr so no epithelium survives in the bony pits. Invert the frontal recess mucosa and plug the outflow tracts with fascia or muscle. What you should see Bare, freshly drilled bone throughout, with the ducts sealed. Senior's pearl A mucocele after obliteration grows from mucosa left behind, often in the recesses or on the back of the flap. Drill every surface; it is the only part of the operation that determines its long-term success."],["osec","th:op-114~s2-6","Obliterate and close","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · The operation, stage by stage","Harvest abdominal fat and pack the sinus with it, close the flap, fix it with plates, replace the pericranium, drain the scalp and close. What you should see A flap seated flush with the forehead and the sinus filled with fat."],["osec","th:op-114~s3-1","Crisis drills","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · When it goes wrong","Dura opened by an osteotomy 1 Act repair with fascia seal antibiotics 2 After watch for CSF leak and meningitis Anterior table fractures 1 Act reduce and fix with miniplates"],["osec","th:op-114~s4-1","After surgery","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","Scalp drain for a day or two, head dressing, antibiotics. MRI in the first year as a new baseline, then surveillance for mucocele by symptoms and imaging."],["ev","th:op-114~s4-2","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus: current evidence","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","In 40 patients followed for a mean of almost 7 years after fat obliteration, MRI suggested possible mucoceles in 6 and revision was needed in 7.5% (Hansen et al., Rhinology 2018), so lifelong surveillance is needed. Endoscopic frontal surgery, including the Draf III with about 90% patency, has replaced obliteration for most chronic sinusitis (Shih et al., Laryngoscope 2018)."],["osec","th:op-114~s4-2","Complications","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · Aftercare","CSF leak, dural injury, forehead numbness and contour deformity, alopecia along the scar, frontal branch palsy, infection, late mucocele, fat graft necrosis."],["osec","th:op-114~s5-1","Where the books differ","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers bicoronal osteoplastic flap with the pericranial hinge, bevelled osteotomies within the sinus outline, and obliteration with fat after removing all mucosa. Dhingra and K. J. Lee external frontal approaches (Lynch-Howarth frontoethmoidectomy, osteoplastic flap) as alternatives to endoscopic surgery for complicated frontal disease. The literature obliteration carries a late mucocele risk; endoscopic surgery is now first-line."],["osec","th:op-114~s5-2","Viva essentials","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications in the endoscopic era. How to outline the sinus and why the bevel matters. Why every trace of mucosa must go. Late mucocele and follow-up."],["osec","th:op-114~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Osteoplastic Obliteration of the Frontal Sinus · For the examination","Common pitfalls Cutting outside the sinus outline. Leaving mucosa in the recesses or on the flap. Losing the pericranial hinge. No long-term imaging follow-up."],["osec","th:op-116~s1-1","The case at a glance","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · Operating note","Endoscopic repair of a CSF leak Summary Causes Trauma (most heal within 7 to 10 days); surgery (sinus or skull base); spontaneous (often obese women with idiopathic intracranial hypertension, lateral sphenoid recess and cribriform plate); tumours; congenital encephaloceles Indications A confirmed leak that persists beyond about a week after trauma, any surgical leak recognised later, spontaneous leaks, encephaloceles, recurrent meningitis or pneumocephalus Confirm Beta-2 transferrin (or beta-trace protein) on the fluid; glucose tests are unreliable Localise Fine-cut CT in coronal and sagittal planes; CT cisternography (with a saline challenge) for intermittent leaks; MRI for meningocele or encephalocele; intrathecal fluorescein at surgery (with consent, low dose) Anaesthesia General Tray Endoscopes, navigation, sinus set, drills, graft materials (fascia lata, temporalis fascia, fat, cartilage, bone), nasoseptal or turbinate flap instruments, fibrin glue, Gelfoam, packing or a balloon"],["osec","th:op-116~s1-2","Consent","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · Operating note","What to tell the patient Success at the first attempt is about 90% ; a second repair succeeds in most of the rest. Meningitis, recurrence of the leak, headache, and pneumocephalus. Rare but serious: bleeding (including the carotid in sphenoid repairs), vision loss or double vision , brain injury. Smell may be reduced; graft donor site from the thigh or abdomen. Spontaneous leaks may need treatment of raised pressure (weight loss, acetazolamide, sometimes a shunt)."],["osec","th:op-116~s1-3","Before you start","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · Operating note","Confirm the fluid biochemically and localise the defect. Look for a large empty sella, arachnoid pits and multiple defects, which suggest raised intracranial pressure. Vaccinate against pneumococcus. Discuss with neurosurgery for large defects, encephaloceles with brain tissue, or frontal sinus posterior wall leaks that the endoscope cannot reach. Figure 1 CT cisternogram: a post-traumatic leak through the right cribriform plate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 116.1 Figure 2 MRI: a large meningoencephalocele of the anterior cranial base. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 116.3"],["osec","th:op-116~s2-1","Find the leak","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest. If consented, give low-dose intrathecal fluorescein through a lumbar drain beforehand and look for green fluid with a blue-light filter. Open the sinuses needed to reach the site with navigation: ethmoidectomy for the fovea and cribriform plate, a wide sphenoidotomy (or a transpterygoid approach for a lateral recess defect), a frontal approach for the recess. Figure 3 CT showing the limits of the sphenoid sinus and a lateral recess defect. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 116.4 What you should see The defect with CSF or fluorescein flowing through it, and its full margins."],["osec","th:op-116~s2-2","Prepare the bed","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · The operation, stage by stage","Strip the mucosa for several millimetres around the defect so grafts stick to bone, not to mucosa. Reduce any encephalocele with bipolar cautery flush with the skull base (herniated brain is non-functional). What you should see Bare bone all round the defect, with the dural edge visible. Encephalocele vessels Coagulate the encephalocele stalk with bipolar before cutting; a torn vessel retracts intracranially and bleeds where you cannot reach it. Check the CT and MRI for vessels and the carotid before working in the sphenoid."],["osec","th:op-116~s2-3","Inlay","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · The operation, stage by stage","Undermine the dural edge and tuck a fascia graft (or a fat \"bath plug\" for small holes) between dura and bone, larger than the defect. Never place mucosa as an inlay. What you should see A graft sitting snugly under the bony edges, with CSF flow stopped."],["osec","th:op-116~s2-4","Onlay and flap","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · The operation, stage by stage","Place a second graft or, for larger defects, a vascularised flap over the defect and the bare bone: a middle turbinate mucoperiosteal flap for small cribriform or fovea defects, or a nasoseptal flap pedicled on the posterior septal artery for large ones. In the sphenoid, a fat obliteration of the sinus is an alternative. What you should see The defect covered in two or more layers, the flap lying flat on bone with its pedicle untwisted. Senior's pearl Size decides the repair: small, low-flow defects close with free grafts; large or high-flow defects (skull base surgery, intracranial pressure) need a vascularised flap. Plan the nasoseptal flap at the start, before the septum or sphenopalatine area is damaged."],["osec","th:op-116~s2-5","Support","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · The operation, stage by stage","Lay Gelfoam over the repair so packing does not stick, then fibrin glue, then dissolvable packing or a Foley balloon or sponge packing, removed after 3 to 7 days. What you should see A dry field with the repair held in place."],["osec","th:op-116~s3-1","Crisis drills","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · When it goes wrong","Sudden vision loss or a tight, proptosed eye 1 Act remove packing lateral canthotomy and cantholysis at the bedside 2 Then return to theatre for medial orbital decompression usually anterior ethmoidal artery bleeding Carotid injury in the sphenoid 1 Act pack with pressure crushed muscle patch control blood pressure 2 Then urgent angiography for pseudoaneurysm interventional radiology Leak recurs 1 Check raised pressure missed second defect graft displacement 2 Act measure pressure treat it re-image and revise"],["osec","th:op-116~s4-1","After surgery","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · Aftercare","Bed rest with the head up for a day or two, stool softeners, no nose blowing, no straining or heavy lifting for 6 weeks, sneeze with the mouth open. Lumbar drains are used selectively, not routinely. Measure intracranial pressure in spontaneous leaks and treat it with weight loss, acetazolamide or, if needed, a shunt."],["ev","th:op-116~s4-2","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks: current evidence","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · Aftercare","A systematic review of 55 studies (1,778 repairs) found endoscopic repair successful in 90% at the first attempt and 97% after a second, with very few complications; evidence for routine lumbar drains and antibiotics was limited (Psaltis et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2012). Comparing 1,178 patients, open and endoscopic repairs had similar success (about 90%), but endoscopic repair had less meningitis (1.1% against 3.9%) and fewer wound infections (Komotar et al., J Neurol Surg A 2013). After extended endonasal skull base surgery, CSF leak rates have fallen markedly over two decades with multilayer and vascularised flap repair (Zamanipoor Najafabadi et al., Acta Neurochir 2021)."],["osec","th:op-116~s4-2","Complications","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · Aftercare","Recurrent leak, meningitis, pneumocephalus, headache, sinusitis, orbital injury, epistaxis, anosmia, carotid injury."],["osec","th:op-116~s5-1","Where the books differ","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers localise with high-resolution CT (and cisternography with a saline challenge), repair with inlay and onlay grafts or turbinate and nasoseptal flaps, obliterate the sphenoid with fat when needed. Dhingra the causes and diagnosis of CSF rhinorrhoea, including the \"reservoir sign\" and the glucose test (now superseded by beta-2 transferrin). The literature endoscopic repair is the standard, with about 90% primary success and fewer complications than open repair."],["osec","th:op-116~s5-2","Viva essentials","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Confirming CSF: beta-2 transferrin. Localising the leak: CT, cisternography, MRI, fluorescein. Spontaneous leaks and raised intracranial pressure. The multilayer repair and the nasoseptal flap. Post-operative care and the place of lumbar drains."],["osec","th:op-116~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Endoscopic Management of Cerebrospinal Fluid Leaks · For the examination","Common pitfalls Relying on a glucose test. Placing mucosa as an inlay graft. Missing raised intracranial pressure in a spontaneous leak. Cutting an encephalocele without coagulation."],["osec","th:op-117~s1-1","The case at a glance","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Operating note","Endoscopic endonasal approach to the sella Summary Indications Pituitary adenoma (functional tumours not controlled medically; macroadenomas with visual loss or growth); craniopharyngioma; Rathke's cleft or pituitary cysts with headache or visual loss; meningoceles and meningoencephaloceles; planum and tuberculum meningiomas; selected lesions of the medial or anterior cavernous sinus; medial optic nerve and orbital apex decompression; sphenoid inflammatory disease, sinonasal tumours and fibro-osseous lesions Never Cardiopulmonary disease prohibiting surgery; pathology on the far side of a nerve or vessel Caution Significant lateral extent; bleeding disorder; prolactinoma not yet tried on medical treatment ; vascular encasement Emergency Rapid headache and visual loss suggest pituitary apoplexy Anaesthesia General; short-acting relaxant only if nerves are monitored; arterial line and good access for fragile or vascular cases; no mask ventilation once the dura is open; orogastric tube; MAP above 80 with visual loss, plus steroid (methylprednisolone 100 mg or dexamethasone 10 mg); stress-dose steroids in hypopituitarism Position Supine, pins or horseshoe; bed turned for two surgeons; reverse Trendelenburg Antibiotics Ceftriaxone 2 g; clindamycin 600 mg or vancomycin 1 g in cephalosporin allergy Monitoring Somatosensory evoked potentials in all cases; cranial nerve EMG for cavernous sinus work Tray Oxymetazoline 0.05% or adrenaline 1:1000 pledgets (not once dura is open); 0 and 45 degree scopes; sinus and endoscopic neurosurgical instruments; 4 to 5 mm coarse diamond drill; tra"],["osec","th:op-117~s1-2","Consent","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the tumour through the nose with a camera, without cuts on the face or opening the skull. Leak of brain fluid and meningitis; it is repaired at once if it occurs. Hormone problems: temporary or permanent need for hormone tablets; diabetes insipidus (passing large volumes of urine). Vision: rarely worse, from injury to the optic nerve or its blood supply. Bleeding, including, rarely, from the carotid artery, which can be life-threatening. Nose: crusting, adhesions, a hole in the septum, sinusitis; regular cleaning in clinic for up to 3 months if a flap is used. Reduced smell. Nerve palsies of the eye muscles if the cavernous sinus is entered."],["osec","th:op-117~s1-3","Before you start","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Operating note","Ask about visual loss or double vision, endocrine symptoms (especially Cushing's syndrome and acromegaly ), headache, clear rhinorrhoea and polyuria. Record previous medical treatment and radiotherapy for the tumour; heart disease; intracranial hypertension (often undiagnosed in the obese); diabetes mellitus and insipidus; recurrent sinusitis, polyps with chest disease, cystic fibrosis, allergic fungal sinusitis, Samter's triad; previous sellar or nasal surgery; bleeding disorders and MEN 1 in the family; anticoagulants, dopamine agonists, diabetic drugs, steroids and decongestants (which blunt intraoperative decongestion); alcohol, tobacco and drugs ( cocaine damages the septum, scars the mucosa and blunts decongestants). A misprint about medical treatment The chapter lists previous medical treatment of pituitary tumours as \"dopamine antagonists\". Prolactinomas are treated with dopamine agonists (such as cabergoline and bromocriptine), as the chapter itself says under alternatives. Look for Cushing's features (fat distribution, buffalo hump, moon face, striae, pigmentation, bruising) and acromegaly (frontal bossing, macroglossia, prognathism, large hands and feet). Test fields, acuity and globe position; refer to ophthalmology if imaging shows optic nerve, chiasm or orbital involvement. Examine cranial nerves III to VI. Endoscope the nose: septal deviation or perforation, polyps, pus, masses, and after previous surgery whether a nasoseptal flap pedicle survives."],["osec","th:op-117~s1-4","Imaging and preparation","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Operating note","CT (with contrast, coronal and sagittal) for the sinuses, orbits and skull base: the slope of the skull base, the anterior ethmoid arteries, and Onodi cells ; CT angiography shows the carotid and intracranial vessels. MRI with gadolinium: tumour extent, the residual gland and stalk, cavernous sinus invasion, the carotid and the chiasm. Arrange otolaryngology, neurosurgery, endocrinology and (for visual loss) ophthalmology. Stop aspirin and anticoagulants. Treat acute bacterial sinusitis first."],["osec","th:op-117~s2-1","Landmarks","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Anatomy you must see","The sphenoid ostium: about a third of the way from the choanal arch to the skull base, behind the superior turbinate. The rostrum: the midline \"keel\" where the vomer attaches. The planum sphenoidale: the roof, continuous in front with the ethmoid roof and cribriform plate. Pneumatisation: conchal, presellar or sellar; it decides what you see first. The optic nerves , superolateral, converging over the sella; the canal may lie in an Onodi cell , often dehiscent there. The clival recess below the sella; the tubercular strut where sella meets planum, over the superior intercavernous sinus . The carotid: from the lacerum segment it runs superomedially as the paraclival segment, enters the cavernous sinus beside the sella, then forms the paraclinoid segment, the most medial and prominent part (the anterior protuberance). The intersinus septum often swings laterally onto the carotid's anterior protuberance. The lateral opticocarotid recess , the pneumatised optic strut between the carotid and the optic nerve; the medial recess is less distinct. The vidian nerve crosses the sinus floor toward the foramen lacerum, over the petrous carotid; V2 bulges superolateral to it in a well-pneumatised lateral recess. Figure 1 The sphenoid bone from the front; the sphenoidotomy extends between the foramen rotundum and the pterygoid canal and includes the lateral recess. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.1 Figure 2 Cadaver dissection of the right side: optic nerve (ON), carotid (ICA), lateral and medial opticocarotid recesses (lOCR, mOCR) and sella (S). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, "],["osec","th:op-117~s3-1","Decongest and make room","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest with pledgets and inspect with the 0 degree scope. Medialise the right middle turbinate, or resect it at the junction of its upper and middle thirds below the skull base for room for the scope and the neurosurgeon's left hand, carefully cauterising the turbinate branch of the sphenopalatine artery without injuring the main trunk. Its mucosa can be kept as a free graft. What you should see Both nasal cavities to the sphenoethmoidal recess, with room for two instruments and the scope."],["osec","th:op-117~s3-2","Decide about the flap now","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · The operation, stage by stage","Before any sphenoidotomy, decide whether a nasoseptal flap may be needed (dural opening or carotid exposure) and raise it on either side, or at least preserve its pedicle as a rescue flap . What you should see The posterior septal artery crossing the sphenoid face below the ostium, intact. The pedicle Opening the sphenoid too low cuts the posterior septal artery, the pedicle of the nasoseptal flap."],["osec","th:op-117~s3-3","Wide bilateral sphenoidotomy","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · The operation, stage by stage","Lateralise or partly resect the superior turbinates and open both sphenoids widely, exposing the planum and both orbits. Remove the intersinus septum conservatively with cutting instruments until the two-surgeon team can drill it flush. Perform a posterior septectomy : separate the vomer from the rostrum with a Cottle, remove the rostrum (rongeur or drill) and take the back of the septum with backbiters, sparing the olfactory mucosa in the upper 1 cm of the septum. Remove posterior ethmoid cells, more on the right to let the scope sit above the neurosurgeon's corridor; go further forward when access beyond the planum is needed. Figure 3 Both sphenoids opened widely with the intersinus septum removed; the right middle turbinate preserved; a rescue flap kept below the suction. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.4 Figure 4 Bone removed from the sphenoid face, posterior septum and sella for standard sellar surgery. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.3 What you should see One cavity: both lateral recesses, the planum above and the floor below, with septations drilled flush."],["osec","th:op-117~s3-4","Inferior exposure without losing the flap","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · The operation, stage by stage","Drill the rostrum and sphenoid floor for more inferior access. Sacrificing one posterior septal artery may help but is not always needed; alternatively, cut a window in the posterior septum and push the sphenoid face mucosa below the ostium downward to keep the pedicle. What you should see The sphenoid floor lowered toward the clivus with the flap pedicle still intact."],["osec","th:op-117~s3-5","Expose the sella","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · The operation, stage by stage","Confirm the sella, carotids and optic nerves with navigation, drill septations flush, and from now on work three-handed . Strip the mucosa off the sella with a Cottle and suction (and wider if the planum, lateral sphenoid or clivus must be drilled). Thin the sellar bone and lift it off with an elevator or a Kerrison used to elevate, not bite , near the carotid. Drill laterally to show the edge of the cavernous sinus dura on each side, the safest way to define the parasellar carotid. Figure 5 The sella after the partitions are drilled; suction in the clival recess; the left carotid siphon (ICA) and both optic nerves (ON). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.5 Figure 6 Sellar dura exposed, with the blue cavernous sinuses (CS) on each side. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.6 What you should see The sellar bulge in the midline, the carotid prominences on each side, the optic canals above and the opticocarotid recesses between them, then bare sellar dura from one cavernous sinus to the other."],["osec","th:op-117~s3-6","Suprasellar exposure","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · The operation, stage by stage","Drill the midline above under visual and navigational control of the carotid, which is most at risk at the medial opticocarotid recess . Thin the planum to eggshell and remove it from front to back; the tuberculum must be very thin before removal, to avoid a CSF leak or bleeding from the superior intercavernous sinus. Figure 7 The planum and sella removed in continuity for suprasellar extension. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.7 What you should see Eggshell bone over the planum and tuberculum, and the blue superior intercavernous sinus."],["osec","th:op-117~s3-7","The lateral recess: transpterygoid","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · The operation, stage by stage","A well-pneumatised lateral recess, bounded by the vidian nerve and V2, needs a transpterygoid approach; take the nasoseptal flap from the other side , since the sphenopalatine artery will be sacrificed. Feel the crista ethmoidalis, raise mucosa to the sphenopalatine foramen, cauterise and divide the artery, and remove the posterior maxillary wall from the foramen outward with a 1 mm then larger Kerrison. Displace the pterygopalatine contents laterally to find the vidian artery and nerve in the pterygoid canal; dividing the vidian nerve lets you retract further to see V2 at the foramen rotundum. Drill the sinus floor and pterygoid base as needed. The vidian nerve leads back to the foramen lacerum and the petrous carotid at its second genu. For more inferior access, add a medial maxillectomy (posterior two thirds of the inferior turbinate, antrostomy to the nasal floor), sparing the greater palatine bundle. Figure 8 Coronal CT of the lateral recess, its opening bounded by the vidian nerve and V2. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.8 Figure 9 The pterygoid canal (arrow), a reliable landmark for the petrous carotid at its second genu. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.9 What you should see The vidian canal medially and the foramen rotundum laterally at the back of the pterygopalatine fossa, with the lateral recess open between them. The vidian nerve finds the carotid The vidian nerve runs superolaterally over the petrous carotid to the foramen lacerum. Follow it, and drill below and lateral to it, and you will know where the artery is."],["osec","th:op-117~s4-1","Small defects","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Reconstruction","Pituitary surgery without significant vascular or arachnoid exposure can be closed with a free mucosal graft from the middle turbinate or nasal floor, its edges held with Surgicel and reinforced with Gelfoam; no packing is needed. With marked diaphragmatic herniation and possible benign intracranial hypertension, fat can support the defect, though it makes revision harder."],["osec","th:op-117~s4-2","Larger defects: the nasoseptal flap","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Reconstruction","With an exposed carotid, a significant dural defect or cavernous sinus dissection, use the nasoseptal flap . Lay it so its whole length touches dura and bone, choosing an oblique or midline arc to suit; put its thick distal end where the risk is greatest; if unsure which side is mucosa, lay it back in place and mark it; never twist the pedicle; hold the edges with Surgicel and Gelfoam. Figure 10 A right nasoseptal flap held with Surgicel over the sellar defect; the pedicle with the nasoseptal artery (asterisk) is visible. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 117.10"],["osec","th:op-117~s4-3","Layers and packing","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Reconstruction","Close dural defects in layers: a collagen underlay, then the graft or flap; consider three layers for high-flow or large leaks (fascia lata, acellular dermis, porcine intestine or similar as the middle layer). After dural repair or a flap, leave packing for 5 to 7 days : Merocel or a balloon for a leak, a risky repair or carotid exposure; with exposed optic nerves, Merocel placed gently and no balloon , with regular vision checks. Sew Doyle splints to the septum for 3 weeks after a flap; a free graft on the denuded septum speeds healing."],["osec","th:op-117~s5-1","Crisis drills","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · When it goes wrong","CSF leak after surgery 1 Recognise clear fluid increasing with straining or leaning forward test for beta-2 transferrin, but history counts 2 Act repair within 12 to 24 hours the authors favour emergency exploration and repair over lumbar drainage 3 Meningitis close the leak and give broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics Bleeding after surgery 1 Mild mucosal expectant 2 Brisk and immediate a sphenopalatine branch or the middle turbinate stump 3 Significant and delayed may be a sentinel bleed from a carotid pseudoaneurysm emergency angiography Arterial bleeding at the sella 1 Think the inferior hypophyseal artery coagulate it rather than avulse it, or it can mimic a carotid injury 2 Prevent carotid injury take carotid canal bone with care beware dissectors and powerful drills"],["osec","th:op-117~s5-2","Common errors in technique","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Too high a trajectory into the sphenoid, breaching the skull base. Opening the sphenoid too low, cutting the flap pedicle. Too little superolateral posterior ethmoidectomy, so the two surgeons fight for space. Leaving the rostrum, blocking the clival recess and lower sella. Too small a sellar opening, limiting the cavernous sinus and tumour margins."],["osec","th:op-117~s6-1","After the operation","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Aftercare","Check vision as soon as the patient cooperates. Oral antibiotics while packing remains. Test pituitary function if the gland was handled. Start saline irrigation once a leak is no longer a concern. Debride in clinic, often in the first month and for up to 3 months after a flap, without disturbing the repair."],["osec","th:op-117~s6-2","Complications","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes CSF leak, meningitis Repair early; antibiotics for meningitis Epistaxis Mucosal, sphenopalatine branch, turbinate stump; delayed: think pseudoaneurysm Synechiae Silastic splints; divide in clinic Septal perforation A tear in the opposite mucosa during flap harvest Iatrogenic sinusitis Medical treatment; occasionally further sinus surgery Diabetes insipidus, hypopituitarism Gland or stalk injury; expected when the stalk is sacrificed (craniopharyngioma) Optic nerve injury Direct, thermal (irrigate when drilling the canal) or vascular (superior hypophyseal or, rarely, ophthalmic artery) Cranial nerve injury Identify nerves; monitor Carotid injury Catastrophic; prevention is everything"],["ev","th:op-117~s6-3","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas: current evidence","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Aftercare","Endoscopic or microscopic. Meta-analyses show similar or slightly higher rates of gross total resection with the endoscopic endonasal approach, fewer nasal complications, and a shorter stay, with similar endocrine outcomes. CSF leak. Multilayer closure with a nasoseptal flap for high-flow leaks has reduced postoperative leaks to a few per cent, and a lumbar drain helps after high-risk defects (Zwagerman et al., J Neurosurg 2019). Prolactinomas are treated first with cabergoline; surgery is for intolerance or resistance, or as a first option for selected small adenomas (Petersenn et al., Nat Rev Endocrinol 2023). Sodium. Watch for diabetes insipidus in the first days and delayed hyponatraemia (SIADH) around day seven."],["osec","th:op-117~s6-3","Alternatives","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · Aftercare","Medical treatment (growth hormone receptor antagonists or somatostatin analogues; dopamine agonists) and stereotactic radiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-117~s7-1","Endoscopic or microscopic?","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · For the examination","There are no randomised trials. Meta-analyses of retrospective series favour the endoscopic endonasal approach for completeness of resection and septal perforation , and over the sublabial approach for CSF leak and epistaxis; meningitis, diabetes insipidus and endocrine dysfunction did not differ. ACTH-secreting tumours have similar biochemical cure rates; for growth hormone-secreting tumours, often larger and invading the cavernous sinus, endoscopy appears equal or superior, though comparison is muddied by stricter cure criteria adopted in 2010."],["osec","th:op-117~s7-2","Viva essentials","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications and contraindications, including medical treatment first for prolactinoma. Apoplexy as an emergency. The anaesthetic points: no mask ventilation, MAP above 80, steroids. Landmarks of the posterior sphenoid wall: optic nerves, carotid segments, opticocarotid recesses, Onodi cell. Deciding on the nasoseptal flap before the sphenoidotomy; the rescue flap. The vidian nerve as the guide to the petrous carotid. Reconstruction by defect size; packing and optic nerves. Managing a postoperative leak and a delayed bleed."],["osec","th:op-117~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Endonasal Approach to the Sella and Parasellar Areas · For the examination","Common pitfalls Resecting olfactory mucosa high on the septum. Biting sellar bone with a Kerrison near the carotid. Balloon packing over exposed optic nerves. Treating a postoperative leak with a lumbar drain alone and waiting."],["osec","th:op-118~s1-1","The case at a glance","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · Operating note","Anterior cranial base resection Summary Indications Benign and malignant tumours of the ethmoid, nasal cavity and cribriform plate reaching or crossing the anterior cranial base, after multidisciplinary planning Approaches Endoscopic endonasal transcribriform resection; open craniofacial resection (bicoronal frontal craniotomy plus lateral rhinotomy, midfacial degloving or endoscopy from below); combined Limits of endoscopy Extensive brain, orbital (beyond periorbita), skin or anterior frontal sinus wall involvement; lateral spread over the orbital roof Reconstruction Pericranial flap (open), nasoseptal flap or extracranial pericranial flap (endoscopic), multilayer fascia grafts Anaesthesia General; antiseizure drugs as needed; lumbar drain selectively Tray Craniotomy set, bicoronal set, endoscopes and navigation, drills with diamond burrs, bipolar, dural repair materials"],["osec","th:op-118~s1-2","Consent","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · Operating note","What to tell the patient Loss of smell is permanent when the olfactory bulbs are removed. CSF leak, meningitis, pneumocephalus, seizures, brain injury, stroke. Bleeding, rarely from the carotid; transfusion. Vision loss or double vision from orbital or ophthalmic artery injury. Open approach: scar, forehead numbness, frontal branch weakness, flap necrosis, cosmetic forehead changes. Radiotherapy is usually needed afterwards; recurrence is possible."],["osec","th:op-118~s1-3","Before you start","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · Operating note","Biopsy the tumour endoscopically. Stage it with CT (bone), MRI (dura, brain, orbit, perineural spread) and neck and chest imaging. Discuss in the skull base multidisciplinary team. Plan the reconstruction: measure the length the pericranial flap must reach, and preserve the nasoseptal flap pedicle if the septum is not involved."],["osec","th:op-118~s2-1","Bicoronal exposure and pericranial flap","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · The operation, stage by stage","Infiltrate and make a bicoronal incision at least 2 cm behind the hairline, extending into the preauricular creases. Raise the scalp in the subgaleal plane, leaving the pericranium on the bone, and stay deep to the temporoparietal fascia laterally to spare the frontal branch. Raise a long U-shaped pericranial flap based on the supraorbital and supratrochlear vessels, keep it moist, and free the supraorbital bundles from their foramina. Figure 1 The coronal incision, extendable in front of the ear. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 118.3 Figure 2 Raising the pericranial flap and freeing the supraorbital nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 118.4 and 118.5 What you should see A long, intact pericranial flap with its vascular pedicle at the brow."],["osec","th:op-118~s2-2","Frontal craniotomy and dissection","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · The operation, stage by stage","Remove a low frontal bone flap (with or without the supraorbital bar for low access). Elevate the dura off the anterior cranial fossa floor, or open it and remove involved dura and bulbs, and define the tumour's upper limit. Figure 3 Segments of frontal bone and orbital rim that can be removed for access. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 118.6 What you should see The cribriform plate and fovea ethmoidalis from above, with the tumour's intracranial margin."],["osec","th:op-118~s2-3","Facial or endoscopic resection from below","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · The operation, stage by stage","Through a lateral rhinotomy, midfacial degloving or the endoscope, free the tumour from the nasal and sinus walls and deliver it with the cribriform plate and ethmoid roof en bloc. Figure 4 The lateral rhinotomy incision. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 118.1 Figure 5 The midfacial degloving approach. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 118.2 What you should see The specimen with the skull base attached, and clear margins on frozen section."],["osec","th:op-118~s2-4","Endoscopic transcribriform resection","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · The operation, stage by stage","Debulk the nasal component to find its origin, complete bilateral ethmoidectomies, a Draf III and wide sphenoidotomies, and take the septum below the tumour. Ligate the anterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries. Drill a rectangular craniotomy: the posterior frontal table in front, the fovea ethmoidalis on each side to the laminae, and the planum in front of the optic canals behind. Open the dura, divide the falx in front of the crista galli (drilled thin), and remove dura, bulbs and tumour together; send olfactory nerve margins for frozen section. Figure 6 The endoscopic view of the anterior cranial base and the transcribriform resection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 118.9 What you should see A rectangular skull base and dural defect from the frontal sinus to the planum, with the tumour removed and margins clear. The ophthalmic and ethmoidal arteries, and the optic canals The posterior limit is the planum just in front of the optic canals; the lateral limits are the laminae. Ligate the ethmoidal arteries at the orbit before cutting, or they retract and bleed into the orbit."],["osec","th:op-118~s2-5","Reconstruct","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · The operation, stage by stage","Close in layers: an inlay graft under the dura, an onlay graft, and a vascularised flap over everything (the pericranial flap laid along the floor of the anterior fossa in open surgery; a nasoseptal flap, or a pericranial flap passed through a small glabellar opening, endoscopically). Cover burr holes and bone defects for cosmesis. Figure 7 The anterior cranial base defect closed with the pericranial flap, and burr-hole covers. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 118.7 and 118.8 What you should see A watertight, multilayer repair with a well-perfused flap covering the whole defect. Senior's pearl The reconstruction is planned before the first cut. A pericranial flap that is too short, or a nasoseptal flap whose pedicle was sacrificed during the approach, leaves a defect you cannot close."],["osec","th:op-118~s3-1","Crisis drills","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · When it goes wrong","Sudden fall in conscious level after surgery 1 Act CT for tension pneumocephalus or haematoma 2 Tension pneumocephalus needle aspiration or burr hole with neurosurgery check the repair CSF leak after surgery 1 Act head up consider lumbar drain 2 Persisting re-explore and reinforce the repair"],["osec","th:op-118~s4-1","After surgery","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · Aftercare","Neurosurgical observation, antiseizure drugs if used, no nose blowing or straining, saline douching once healed, endoscopic surveillance and MRI. Adjuvant radiotherapy is usual for malignancy."],["ev","th:op-118~s4-2","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base: current evidence","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · Aftercare","A pooled analysis of 226 patients with sinonasal malignancies (mostly olfactory neuroblastoma, adenocarcinoma and undifferentiated carcinoma) found no significant difference between endoscopic and craniofacial resection for low-stage tumours (5-year overall survival about 87% endoscopic against 77% open), while data for high-stage tumours were too sparse to compare (Higgins et al., Int Forum Allergy Rhinol 2011). CSF leak rates after extended endonasal surgery have fallen substantially with multilayer and vascularised flap repair (Zamanipoor Najafabadi et al., Acta Neurochir 2021)."],["osec","th:op-118~s4-2","Complications","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · Aftercare","CSF leak, meningitis, pneumocephalus, seizures, brain injury, haemorrhage, visual loss, flap necrosis, frontal branch palsy, anosmia, crusting."],["osec","th:op-118~s5-1","Where the books differ","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers the bicoronal approach with a pericranial flap, facial approaches from below, and the endoscopic transcribriform resection with its limits and multilayer reconstruction. K. J. Lee the tumours of the anterior skull base, staging (Kadish for olfactory neuroblastoma) and the indications for open versus endoscopic surgery. The literature equivalent outcomes for selected low-stage tumours; endoscopic surgery avoids facial incisions and craniotomy."],["osec","th:op-118~s5-2","Viva essentials","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications and the multidisciplinary work-up. Open versus endoscopic approaches and their limits. The boundaries of the transcribriform resection. Reconstruction : pericranial and nasoseptal flaps. Complications , especially CSF leak and pneumocephalus."],["osec","th:op-118~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Transcranial and Endonasal Approaches to the Anterior Cranial Base · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not planning the reconstruction before the approach. Cutting the supraorbital vessels and losing the pericranial flap. Missing skip lesions in the olfactory nerves. Leaving the ethmoidal arteries uncontrolled."],["osec","th:op-119~s1-1","The case at a glance","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Operating note","Endoscopic endonasal transpterygoid approaches Summary Reaches Meckel's cave and the trigeminal nerve and foramina; cavernous sinus; petrous and parasellar carotid; pterygopalatine fossa; infratemporal fossa; fossa of Rosenmüller Indications Lateral sphenoid recess CSF leaks and meningoencephaloceles (the first use); selected lesions of the pterygopalatine fossa, petrous apex, anterior Meckel's cave, cavernous sinus, infratemporal fossa and medial middle cranial fossa: meningioma, chordoma, chondrosarcoma, trigeminal schwannoma; selected sinonasal cancers, angiofibroma, aggressive pituitary macroadenoma, metastases Contraindications Comorbidity that would also bar an open approach; an approach that cannot give the resection the disease needs; active sinonasal bacterial infection before an intradural approach; a lesion attached to or behind the carotid or a functioning cranial nerve Carotid involvement Balloon occlusion testing ; hypoperfusion means bypass or stenting; elective stenting for benign tumours (needs 6 to 12 weeks of antiplatelets, so impractical for cancer); for encasement consider sacrifice, another approach or partial resection with adjuvant treatment Anaesthesia General (preferably intravenous), sedation or local Position Supine, head in a horseshoe, tilted slightly left and turned slightly right; three-pin fixation when EMG is used or the case is long Antibiotics Third- or fourth-generation cephalosporin with good CSF penetration, for 48 to 72 hours Monitoring SSEPs (the carotid is at risk), cranial nerve EMG, brainstem evoked responses as needed, EEG; navig"],["osec","th:op-119~s1-2","Consent","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to reach a tumour or leak at the side of the skull base through the nose and the back of the cheek sinus. The carotid artery lies close: injury is rare but can be catastrophic or fatal. Numbness of the cheek, teeth, palate or jaw (V2, V3); about 15% develop nerve pain needing medication such as gabapentin or carbamazepine. A dry eye from cutting the vidian nerve; usually unnoticed, but dangerous if the eye is already dry or numb. Double vision from irritation of the nerve to the eye muscles. Jaw stiffness after infratemporal fossa work; exercises start early. Leak of brain fluid (about 6%) and infection (under 2%). Ear fullness if the Eustachian tube is disturbed. Further treatment , such as radiotherapy, may follow."],["osec","th:op-119~s1-3","Before you start","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Operating note","Ask about mass effect or facial swelling, pain, trismus , double vision, dry eye or corneal ulcers, numbness and facial weakness; antiplatelets, herbal products, vitamin E and fish oil; previous surgery or radiotherapy. Examine the cranial nerves (especially I to VII), eye movements and function, mouth opening, the nose and nasopharynx with an endoscope, and smell."],["osec","th:op-119~s1-4","Imaging and planning","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Operating note","MRI and CT are complementary: MRI separates tumour from secretions and shows perineural spread and dural, brain or orbital involvement; CT shows bone. CT angiography maps the carotids and skull base for navigation. PET-CT stages malignancy. Figure 1 The left sphenoid bone from the front; the shaded area is the pterygoid process, which every transpterygoid approach passes through in part or whole. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.1 Figure 2 The foramen rotundum (R) and vidian canal (V) on the face of the pterygoid process. Vertical and horizontal lines through them divide the region into the sinonasal tract, pterygopalatine fossa, lateral sphenoid recess, middle cranial fossa, petrous carotid and infratemporal fossa. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.2 Plan on the two foramina A vertical line through the foramen rotundum divides lesions reachable after removing the posterior antral wall (medial) from those needing the lateral antral wall as well (lateral). A horizontal line through the vidian canal divides those reachable through a wide nasoantral window (above) from those needing a full medial maxillectomy (below). Figure 3 (A) Lesions medial to the line through the foramen rotundum (green) need only the posterior antral wall; lateral ones (pink) need the lateral wall too. (B) Lesions above the line through the vidian canal can be reached through a large nasoantral window (NAW); those below need a medial maxillectomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.3 Set goals honestly (cure, gross total resection, or decompression) and consider multimodality tre"],["osec","th:op-119~s2-1","The pterygoid process","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Anatomy you must see","Paired, with medial and lateral plates projecting backward from a common anterior wall and base, hanging down from the junction of the sphenoid body and greater wing. On its face: the foramen rotundum (V2) and the vidian canal , carrying preganglionic parasympathetic fibres to the sphenopalatine ganglion and postganglionic sympathetic fibres from the deep petrosal nerve."],["osec","th:op-119~s2-2","The pterygopalatine fossa","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Anatomy you must see","An inverted pyramid: an anterior vascular compartment (the internal maxillary artery and its terminal branches) and a posterior neural compartment (sphenopalatine ganglion, vidian nerve, greater and lesser palatine nerves, orbital branches, and a branch to the infraorbital nerve), wrapped in fat and a venous plexus. Figure 4 The inverted pyramid of the pterygopalatine fossa: a vascular compartment in front of a neural one. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.5 Figure 5 The right pterygopalatine fossa with its vascular compartment exposed (schematic and cadaver): internal maxillary, sphenopalatine and posterior nasal arteries, palatine bundle, pterygoid process, torus tubarius. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.6"],["osec","th:op-119~s2-3","Infratemporal fossa landmarks","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Anatomy you must see","V3 and the foramen ovale , just behind the base of the lateral pterygoid plate. The tensor veli palatini , in the interpterygoid fossa just in front of the Eustachian tube. The petrous carotid , behind the foramen ovale and the Eustachian tube. The lateral plate, foramen ovale and carotid canal lie in one sagittal line: the lateral plate is a proxy for the parapharyngeal carotid, but the artery can be tortuous, so confirm on imaging. Figure 6 Basal view: the lateral pterygoid plate, foramen ovale (V3) and carotid canal in line; the lateral plate approximates the parapharyngeal carotid, which must be confirmed on imaging. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.11 Figure 7 Basal view: the Eustachian tube (grey arrow) leads to the petrous carotid (red arrow); lateral plate shaded, foramen ovale circled. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.12"],["osec","th:op-119~s3-1","The five types","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Transpterygoid approaches Type What is removed Target A Part of the medial and/or lateral plates Passage for a temporoparietal fascia flap B Medial base of the pterygoid Lateral sphenoid recess above the vidian canal (CSF leaks) C Base of the plates along the vidian canal Petrous apex, Meckel's cave, cavernous sinus D Variable plates Infratemporal fossa E Medial plate or the whole process, plus the medial third of the Eustachian tube Fossa of Rosenmüller, lateral craniocervical junction What you should see On the scan: the target's position relative to the vidian canal and the foramen rotundum, and where the carotid lies."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-2","Prepare","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Prepare the periumbilical skin for fat and, when the carotid is near, the lateral thigh for muscle to patch the carotid . Decongest with 1:10,000 adrenaline cottonoids and inject 1% lidocaine with adrenaline into the septum and middle meatus. What you should see A decongested nose and prepared donor sites."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-3","The nasal corridor","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Use both nostrils for a four-handed technique and a cross-court view. The 0 degree scope with straight instruments does most of the work; angled scopes look round corners but need angled instruments. Out-fracture both inferior turbinates and the opposite middle and superior turbinates. Do an uncinectomy and enlarge the maxillary ostium back to the posterior wall, up to the orbital floor and medial wall, down to the inferior turbinate. Take part of the middle turbinate (a free graft) and clear the ethmoids, preserving the lamina papyracea and skull base. What you should see The posterior wall of the maxillary sinus and the sphenopalatine region, visible with a 0-degree scope through both nostrils."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-4","The flap from the other side","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Harvest the nasoseptal flap from the opposite side , because the approach endangers the ipsilateral pedicle; cover the donor septum with a reverse flap from the ipsilateral side, and protect both with silicone splints. Make wide bilateral sphenoidotomies to the roof and lateral wall, dissect the basopharyngeal fascia off the sphenoid floor to drill toward the clival recess and foramen lacerum, then cauterise and divide the ipsilateral sphenopalatine artery and its companions. What you should see The flap tucked into the nasopharynx on an intact pedicle, and both sphenoids open."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-5","The antral corridor","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Types A to C (targets above the vidian canal): a wide nasoantral window , from the nasolacrimal duct to the posterior wall and from the orbital floor to the top of the inferior turbinate, showing the upper half of the posterior wall. Types D and E (below it): a medial maxillectomy , from the duct to the posterior wall and from orbital floor to nasal floor. Far lateral targets: an endoscopic Denker (Sturman-Canfield) extension: remove the rest of the inferior turbinate and the front of the inferior meatus, cut onto the pyriform aperture just in front of the turbinate head, remove the aperture and ascending process of the maxilla (often only below the duct opening), divide the lacrimal duct sharply, and remove the anterior maxilla to expose the whole lateral antral wall. Figure 8 The endoscopic Denker (Sturman-Canfield) extension: pyriform aperture and anterior maxilla removed for a straight line of sight into the infratemporal fossa. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.4 What you should see The whole posterior antral wall, from the orbital floor to the level your target needs."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-6","Enter the pterygopalatine fossa","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Identify and clip or cauterise the sphenopalatine and posterior nasal arteries where they enter the nose; there are often more than two, through separate foramina. Enlarge the sphenopalatine foramen forward with a 1 to 2 mm Kerrison or Citelli. Remove the posterior antral wall as far as needed, out to the inferior orbital fissure , keeping the periosteum intact over the fossa. Find the infraorbital nerve as it leaves the fossa. Inferomedially, dissect carefully to free the descending palatine artery and greater palatine nerve in their canal down to the greater palatine foramen, which lets you mobilise the fossa laterally. Figure 9 The left pterygopalatine fossa: the elevator reflects mucoperiosteum over the palatine bone and medial plate to expose the Eustachian tube. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.7 Figure 10 The left fossa: the palatine neurovascular bundle (artery in front, greater and lesser palatine nerves behind). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.8 What you should see The fossa contents in their periosteal sac, the infraorbital nerve in the roof leading back to the foramen rotundum, and the clipped sphenopalatine branches."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-7","Uncover the pterygoid","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the orbital process and front of the perpendicular plate of the palatine bone, the walls of the greater palatine canal, then the sphenoid process of the palatine bone to expose the palatovaginal canal ; sacrifice its artery to lateralise the fossa. The fossa contents can be kept in a periosteal sac. The vidian bundle tethers the fossa and usually must be cauterised and divided (it can sometimes be kept for infrapetrous carotid and Meckel's cave approaches). Further lateral and superior dissection exposes the base of the pterygoid with the foramen rotundum and V2 . Figure 11 The left fossa: the palatine bundle displaced laterally to expose the pterygoid process, with the vidian and rotundum foramina (V2). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.9 What you should see The front of the pterygoid process bare, with the vidian canal at the junction of the medial plate and the sphenoid floor, and the foramen rotundum above and lateral to it."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-8","Type B: the lateral recess","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Extend the sphenoidotomy laterally, removing the front of the lateral recess (the pneumatised pterygoid base) above the vidian canal; if pneumatisation reaches the greater wing, the vidian bundle may need sacrificing. What you should see The lateral recess wide open between the vidian canal and V2."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-9","Type C: petrous apex and Meckel's cave","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Removing the medial pterygoid base is usually enough. Drill the sphenoid floor flush with the clival recess (4 mm hybrid or coarse diamond) and the medial plate (3 mm coarse diamond), and follow the vidian canal , drilling around it, to the foramen lacerum , identifying and protecting the horizontal petrous carotid. To keep the vidian nerve, thin its canal to eggshell and out-fracture the shell, keeping its periosteum to protect the nerve, then transpose it laterally. What you should see The vidian canal leading you back to the second genu of the carotid at the foramen lacerum; you stay below and medial to it. Follow the vidian canal back Drill circumferentially around the vidian canal, never above and medial to it blindly: the canal leads to the foramen lacerum and the second genu of the carotid. It is your fixed guide to the artery."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-10","Type D: the infratemporal fossa","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the internal maxillary artery at the pterygomaxillary fissure , and the vidian bundle and the ganglion's branch to V2, so the fossa contents fall down and out to show the whole height of the pterygoid. Drill into the lateral plate until it is level with the middle fossa and foramen ovale, as far as needed. Figure 12 The right pterygoid process after displacing the fossa contents (schematic and cadaver): vidian nerve, V2, V3, carotid, torus tubarius. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.10 What you should see The lateral pterygoid plate, with the foramen ovale and V3 behind its root."],["osec","th:op-119~s3-11","Type E: the whole pterygoid","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · The operation, stage by stage","For extensive infratemporal disease, take the whole pterygoid: control the internal maxillary artery, detach the lateral and medial pterygoid muscles, and remove the lateral plate, staying subperiosteal to avoid the pterygoid plexus. Removing the Eustachian tube and its bony canal, laterally and upward, leads to the carotid canal just behind. Figure 13 The right pterygoid process removed, showing tensor veli palatini just in front of the Eustachian tube, with V2, V3, the pterygoid muscles and temporalis. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.13 Figure 14 Tensor veli palatini removed, showing the Eustachian tube and V3 in line, and the horizontal, vertical and parapharyngeal carotid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 119.14 What you should see V2 at the rotundum above, the vidian canal leading back to the lacerum and the petrous carotid, V3 at the foramen ovale behind the lateral plate, the Eustachian tube with the carotid behind it."],["osec","th:op-119~s4-1","Crisis drills","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · When it goes wrong","Carotid injury 1 Control immediate pressure with a crushed muscle patch 2 Then endovascular stenting or occlusion 3 Prepare thigh prepared for muscle two suctions four hands Heavy bleeding in the fossa 1 Source internal maxillary artery or pterygoid venous plexus 2 Control bipolar diathermy is most effective stay subperiosteal to avoid the plexus Suspected CSF leak after surgery 1 Screen clear drainage or salty taste the reservoir sign (chin to chest for a minute) at least daily 2 Image CT head more intradural air than on the immediate scan suggests a fistula 3 Act explore and repair immediately beta-2 transferrin is slow and not always available"],["osec","th:op-119~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Inadequate exposure. Mistaking the superior orbital fissure for the foramen rotundum. Inadequate instruments."],["osec","th:op-119~s5-1","After the operation","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Aftercare","Intensive or step-down care for 24 hours; early mobilisation and chemical DVT prophylaxis unless contraindicated. Neurological checks after CSF loss or neurovascular manipulation; keep systolic pressure below 160 ; correct endocrine problems (diabetes insipidus needs hourly fluid balance and 6-hourly sodium, osmolality and urine specific gravity). Patients on CPAP may only have mask oxygen after transdural surgery, so monitor them. An immediate non-contrast CT catches tension pneumocephalus and haemorrhage; after tumour surgery, MRI within 24 hours shows the resection and the flap. Avoid bending, lifting, straining and squatting, take stool softeners, and cough and sneeze with the mouth open. Intravenous antibiotics for 24 to 48 hours, then oral while packing remains. Start trismus exercises early after infratemporal work. Saline sprays at once, lavage from day 3 to 5, debridement every 1 to 4 weeks; discharge usually after two or three nights."],["osec","th:op-119~s5-2","Complications","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Bleeding Internal maxillary artery, pterygoid plexus; bipolar Reduced tearing Vidian nerve or ganglion sacrificed; corneal ulceration if the eye is already dry or V1/V2 is numb Facial numbness V2 and V3; about 15% deafferentation pain (gabapentin, carbamazepine) Carotid injury Muscle patch, then endovascular stent or occlusion CSF leak Fewer since pedicled flaps; explore and repair at once Abducens palsy Prism glasses or strabismus surgery if permanent Trismus Early stretching exercises and devices A misprint about the nerve The chapter names this palsy \"CN V1\" and treats it with prisms or strabismus surgery. Prisms and squint surgery treat double vision from an eye muscle palsy, and the nerve vulnerable here is the abducens (VI) ; \"V1\" appears to be a misprint for VI."],["ev","th:op-119~s5-3","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa: current evidence","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Aftercare","Transpterygoid approach. Removing the medial pterygoid plate after identifying the vidian nerve and canal, which leads to the anterior genu of the petrous carotid, opens the lateral recess of the sphenoid and the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae. Series report effective repair of lateral recess CSF leaks and encephaloceles, and access to angiofibromas and schwannomas, with vidian nerve section causing dry eye in a proportion of patients. The vidian canal is the most reliable landmark to the petrous carotid at the foramen lacerum."],["osec","th:op-119~s5-3","Alternatives","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · Aftercare","Open approaches to the middle fossa (temporal, frontotemporal, subtemporal) and the transorbital approach; lateral (preauricular-subtemporal, postauricular-transtemporal) or anterior (Le Fort I, facial translocation, maxillary swing) approaches to the infratemporal fossa and nasopharynx; or combined multicorridor surgery."],["osec","th:op-119~s6-1","Viva essentials","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The pterygoid process and its two foramina; what passes through the vidian canal. Planning with the rotundum and vidian lines; nasoantral window versus medial maxillectomy versus Denker. Types A to E. The contralateral nasoseptal flap and the reverse flap. The compartments of the pterygopalatine fossa; keeping its periosteum. The vidian canal as the guide to the carotid at the foramen lacerum. Infratemporal landmarks: V3 behind the lateral plate, tensor veli palatini, the Eustachian tube in front of the carotid. Managing a carotid injury; the reservoir sign and pneumocephalus."],["osec","th:op-119~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Endoscopic Endonasal Transpterygoid Approaches to the Middle Cranial Fossa · For the examination","Common pitfalls Using the ipsilateral nasoseptal flap. Stripping the fossa periosteum and bleeding from its vessels. Trusting the lateral plate for the carotid without imaging. Delaying a leak repair."],["osec","th:op-12~s1-1","The case at a glance","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","Horizontal partial laryngectomy Summary Supraglottic Cancers of the supraglottis , and exophytic tongue base cancers confined to the vallecula; limited pre-epiglottic space invasion is not a contraindication Supracricoid T1 to T3 glottic cancer extending onto the supraglottis or involving both folds but sparing one arytenoid; T3 glottic or supraglottic cancer with paraglottic spread fixing the fold but not the cricoarytenoid joint; T2 supraglottic cancer reaching the true fold; T2 to T3 with under 10 mm anterior subglottic extension; T3 with suspected inner cortex thyroid cartilage invasion; failure of primary radiotherapy if all criteria are met Not fit Unable to undergo anaesthesia or withstand aspiration: cannot climb two flights of stairs , Karnofsky under 80 Tumour limits Interarytenoid or posterior commissure involvement; both arytenoids; the cricoarytenoid joint; the cricoid; subglottic extension over 10 mm ; massive inferior paraglottic invasion to the pyriform apex, or pyriform involvement; over 2 cm of tongue base ; extralaryngeal spread. Advanced age matters little Set-up General; supine, head at the very top of the table, tube taped to the left lower lip for laryngoscopy, shoulder roll for neck dissection; upper lip, mastoid, trapezius and clavicles visible Antibiotics Augmented penicillin for 24 hours; clindamycin in allergy Monitoring Nerve monitoring by preference (no evidence either way); probes can confirm the hypoglossal, superior and recurrent laryngeal and accessory nerves Tray Direct laryngoscopy set with Lewy arm; video tower with 30 and 70 degree tele"],["osec","th:op-12~s1-2","Consent","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer and part of the voice box while keeping your voice and your natural breathing route. You will aspirate after surgery: food and saliva will go the wrong way at first. Recovery needs intense swallowing therapy, coughing and expectoration. A temporary tracheostomy and feeding tube; most patients have the tube out within about two weeks, some later. Consent for total laryngectomy if the cancer is more extensive than expected. Chest infection, bleeding, wound infection, air under the skin , and the repair breaking down if you vomit or have reflux. Nerve injury to the tongue (hypoglossal) or larynx. Neck dissection on both sides for supraglottic cancer."],["osec","th:op-12~s1-3","Why conservation surgery came back","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","Early (T1, T2) cancers are treated equally well by radiotherapy or surgery. After the VA Laryngeal Cancer Study in the 1980s, organ preservation with radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy became popular for advanced disease, but Hoffman and colleagues later found that this shift coincided with falling overall survival , especially for T3 cancers. Some patients are better treated with surgery first, and that has renewed interest in conservation laryngectomy. Today supracricoid laryngectomy is the commonest horizontal operation because its results are reproducible; supratracheal laryngectomy has been described but is less reproducible."],["osec","th:op-12~s1-4","Before you start","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","Ask what the first symptom was (dysphonia, dysphagia, odynophagia) and how the larynx works now: breathlessness at rest, lying flat or on exertion; whether the patient is easy to understand; aspiration of liquids or solids, pneumonia in the past year , and loss of over 10 pounds. Record previous biopsy, laser, surgery, radiotherapy or chemotherapy; recurrent bronchitis, COPD, heart disease, cirrhosis with varices or GI bleeding; thyroidectomy or anterior cervical discectomy (possible superior laryngeal nerve injury) and other neck surgery; cancer in first-degree relatives; pack-years, attempts to stop, alcohol volume and withdrawal; occupation and reliance on the voice; inhalers, oxygen, heart failure drugs and antiplatelets. The Charlson Comorbidity Index or Karnofsky Performance Status helps select patients; can the patient climb two flights of stairs?"],["osec","th:op-12~s1-5","Examination","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","Flexible laryngoscopy is the most important part: the subsites involved, laryngeal function and second primaries. Disease reaching the anterior commissure raises suspicion of thyroid cartilage invasion and poorer cure by radiotherapy. Judge fold and cricoarytenoid mobility; an atrophic fold suggests involvement of the joint or the recurrent nerve. Subglottic extension is best judged in theatre with angled telescopes. Videostroboscopy for cancer on the true folds shows mucosal extent, helps plan margins and predicts arytenoid resection. Behaviour: a patient who will not do intense swallowing therapy will not overcome the aspiration and may need a gastrostomy or a long tracheostomy. Voice, breathing and aspiration; the mouth and oropharynx for second primaries and extension. The laryngotracheal complex: fixed to the front soft tissues means extralaryngeal spread and total laryngectomy; fixed to the spine means unresectable. The thyroid: extension needs total laryngectomy with lobectomy or thyroidectomy. Neck nodes: palpable disease may need comprehensive dissection; a clinically negative neck needs selective dissection."],["osec","th:op-12~s1-6","Imaging and preparation","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","Thin-cut (1 mm) axial CT of the larynx with reconstructions shows pre-epiglottic and paraglottic spread and thyroid cartilage invasion with about 75% accuracy, rising to 96% when gross radiographic signs of anterior commissure involvement are added. CT is more sensitive than examination for nodes (over 1 cm, round, hypodense or without a fatty hilum). Chest imaging looks for lung cancer, the commonest second primary. MRI shows tongue base extent, deciding between an extended partial and a total laryngectomy. Pulmonary function and blood gases describe COPD, but no threshold defines suitability; the stairs test is practical. Perform direct laryngoscopy with 30 and 70 degree telescopes, palpate the cricoarytenoid joints (fixation is a contraindication) and biopsy, before or immediately before surgery. Review outside pathology. Speech, swallowing and nutritional assessment beforehand help but can be done after surgery. After radiotherapy In an irradiated larynx the cancer's edges may be blurred by mucosal change, submucosal spread, scattered nests or deep extension. These patients may be better served by total laryngectomy, and if offered partial surgery must understand that a total laryngectomy may be needed."],["osec","th:op-12~s2-1","The pre-epiglottic space","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Anatomy you must see","Bounded in front by the thyrohyoid membrane and above by the hyoepiglottic ligament. Cancer confined to it does not rule out supraglottic laryngectomy, but spread in front of it must be recognised and excised. Figure 1 The pre-epiglottic space (shaded), bounded in front by the thyrohyoid membrane and above by the hyoepiglottic ligament. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 12.1"],["osec","th:op-12~s2-2","The paraglottic space","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Anatomy you must see","The submucosal tissue inside the thyroid cartilage. Supraglottic cancer can spread down it; if it extends below the plane of the true fold , a supraglottic laryngectomy would cut through it at the level of the fold and is contraindicated. Figure 2 The paraglottic space (shaded): cancer extending below the plane of the true fold contraindicates supraglottic laryngectomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 12.2"],["osec","th:op-12~s2-3","What must survive","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Anatomy you must see","The hyoid , at least one innervated cricoarytenoid unit , the cricoid , the recurrent laryngeal nerves and the internal branches of the superior laryngeal nerves . The ability to keep the epiglottis decides between CHEP and CHP. Landmarks in the operation are the epiglottis, vocal folds, tongue base, thyroid cartilage and pyriform sinuses."],["osec","th:op-12~s3-1","Tracheostomy, apron and neck","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Make the tracheostomy through a transverse incision that becomes part of the apron . Raise the apron flap and do the neck dissections: both sides for supraglottic cancer. What you should see Both necks cleared and the larynx and hyoid exposed beneath the apron."],["osec","th:op-12~s3-2","Choose the approach","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Transhyoid , when the lingual surface of the epiglottis is free of cancer: free the hyoid laterally and divide the infrahyoid straps; open the vallecula, cross the pre-epiglottic space and enter the pharynx in the midline onto the epiglottis. Lateral pharyngotomy , when the lingual surface is involved: divide the mylohyoid and the infrahyoid straps so the hyoid comes with the specimen , and enter the pharynx on the side opposite the cancer. What you should see The epiglottis delivered through the pharyngotomy and the tumour seen from above."],["osec","th:op-12~s3-3","Prepare the thyroid cartilage","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Incise and elevate the perichondrium along the upper edge of the thyroid cartilage. What you should see Perichondrium peeled down off the upper half of the ala, ready to be sewn up at closure."],["osec","th:op-12~s3-4","The endolaryngeal cuts","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Under direct vision, cut the aryepiglottic fold at the level of the ventricle , and posteriorly across the face of the arytenoids unless the cancer needs an extended procedure. Join the mucosal cut to the cartilage cut and divide the thyroid cartilage at the level of the ventricle . Figure 3 The mucosal cuts made under direct vision: through the ventricle at the level of the true fold below, and across the face of the arytenoids behind. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 12.3 What you should see The ventricle as the lower limit, with both true folds, both arytenoids and the anterior commissure intact below the cut. What you must not cut Do not cut the true vocal folds or the arytenoids. Do not disturb the attachment of the anterior commissure to the thyroid cartilage."],["osec","th:op-12~s3-5","Check the margins","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the specimen, examine the margins and take frozen sections as needed. Place the nasogastric tube into the pharynx. What you should see Clear margins at the tongue base, the ventricle and the aryepiglottic folds."],["osec","th:op-12~s3-6","Set the tongue base back","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Place three sutures from the external perichondrium of the cricoid to the fibroadipose (fibroaponeurotic) layer of the tongue base 12 to 15 mm deep to its mucosa , never through the tongue mucosa itself. Relieve the tension, tie them firmly, and close the lateral parts of the pharyngotomy mucosa separately. This sets the tongue base back as a roof over the glottis and reduces aspiration. Figure 4 The cricoid external perichondrium brought to the fibroaponeurotic layer of the tongue base, placing the tongue base back over the glottis. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 12.4 What you should see The tongue base brought down over the glottis like a roof, with no suture visible in its mucosa."],["osec","th:op-12~s3-7","Isolate the tracheostomy and close","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Irrigate and place suction drains. Suture the strap muscles to the platysma of the apron flap around the tracheostomy so that tracheal air and secretions cannot track under the flap into the dissected neck. Figure 5 (A) The tracheostomy isolated from the dead space of the flap and neck dissection. (B) Without isolation, contaminated air and secretions pass under the flap, causing surgical emphysema and wound infection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 12.5 What you should see The straps sealed to the flap around the tracheostomy."],["osec","th:op-12~s3-8","The supracricoid impaction","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","In supracricoid laryngectomy, the impaction sutures are tied tightly to bring the cricoid up to the hyoid, obliterating the space once filled by the thyroid cartilage and thyrohyoid membrane (see the supracricoid chapter). Figure 6 Impaction sutures tied tightly to bring the cricoid complex to the hyoid, obliterating the space of the thyroid cartilage and membrane. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 12.6 What you should see The cricoid tight against the hyoid."],["osec","th:op-12~s4-1","Crisis drills","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · When it goes wrong","Pexy ruptured 1 Who patients with postoperative nausea and vomiting or uncontrolled reflux 2 Act return to theatre and revise the closure 3 Prevent antiemetics, careful advancement of feeds Bleeding after surgery 1 Usual source poorly controlled tongue base muscle and mucosa 2 Vessel blowout from a salivary fistula soaking major vessels urgent surgery 3 Prevent meticulous haemostasis at the tongue base active suction drains early recognition of a fistula"],["osec","th:op-12~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Positive margins , usually from unrecognised tongue base, pyriform, pre-epiglottic or subglottic spread. Prevent with recent imaging, laryngoscopy immediately before surgery, a pharyngotomy planned to let you cut under direct vision, and frozen sections. Suturing tongue mucosa to the glottis instead of setting the tongue base back through its deep fibroadipose layer. Contralateral neck recurrence: bilateral selective dissection in the N0 neck lowers it from about 20% to 9%."],["osec","th:op-12~s5-1","The first two weeks","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Nurse on a monitored ward with staff skilled in fresh tracheostomies, with continuous oximetry. Nil by mouth with the nasogastric tube on low suction, then feeds and drugs through it once bowel sounds return. Avoid vomiting , which can disrupt the reconstruction: advance feeds cautiously and give antiemetics. Day 3: try deflating the cuff; aspiration shows the oedema is settling, and the cuff is often reinflated. Encourage expectoration. Day 5: flexible laryngoscopy with the cuff down; if oedema is better and aspiration not severe, change to an uncuffed No. 4 tube, otherwise back to cuffed. Once the uncuffed tube is tolerated lying down and overnight, cap it ; after 24 hours capped, check the airway by laryngoscopy and decannulate , usually on days 12 to 14 ."],["osec","th:op-12~s5-2","Swallowing","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Start therapy when the speaking valve or cap goes on: tongue base retraction, laryngeal elevation and adduction exercises, then the supraglottic swallow and the swallow-cough technique. Calorie counts start with oral feeding; about 2 to 3 days of therapy are needed to feed adequately. Aggressive inpatient dysphagia management achieves a safe swallow before discharge without the cost of a gastrostomy and home care."],["osec","th:op-12~s5-3","Complications","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Complications of supraglottic laryngectomy Complication Notes Aspiration pneumonia The commonest; everyone aspirates. Minimise with technique (tongue base set back, well-placed pexy sutures), selection, expectoration, pulmonary toilet, early walking, coughing and intense swallow therapy Medical Cardiac ischaemia, bleeding ulcer, delirium tremens, pneumothorax from ruptured blebs: avoided by selection and preparation Haemorrhage Tongue base; vessel blowout from a fistula Subcutaneous emphysema Straps not closed to the flap around the stoma; meticulous closure and pressure dressing Neck infection Prophylactic antibiotics; close the dead space under the apron Ruptured pexy Vomiting or reflux; revise Failure to decannulate Usually arytenoid oedema : steroids and time; or scarred mucosal flaps, which some excise with the CO2 laser Delayed swallowing Poor selection or surgery; older patients with prior dysphagia, the non-compliant, the irradiated, extended tongue base resections: consider early gastrostomy Regional recurrence Untreated necks: bilateral dissection for supraglottic disease, central (paratracheal) dissection with subglottic involvement Delayed decannulation Usually oedema after supracricoid surgery; discharged to rehabilitation with a nasogastric tube, most decannulated at the first clinic visit"],["ev","th:op-12~s5-4","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy: current evidence","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Place today. Open horizontal supraglottic laryngectomy has largely been replaced by transoral laser microsurgery and transoral robotic surgery, which give comparable oncological control with faster swallowing recovery and fewer tracheostomies in published series. Selection remains key: good lung function (to tolerate aspiration), no involvement of the anterior commissure, arytenoid fixation or thyroid cartilage, and a motivated patient. Postoperative radiotherapy is given for nodal disease or close margins."],["osec","th:op-12~s5-4","Alternatives","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Radiotherapy for early laryngeal cancer. Transoral partial laryngeal resection , which has replaced horizontal partial laryngectomy in most centres."],["osec","th:op-12~s6-1","The evidence","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · For the examination","In the VA study , 332 patients with advanced (mostly T3) cancer were randomised to chemoradiotherapy or surgery with radiotherapy: two-year survival was 68% in both, with salvage laryngectomy for failures, and 64% kept the larynx. Surgical series do better: Dufour and colleagues treated 118 T3 cancers by supracricoid laryngectomy with 3- and 5-year survival of 84% and 74% , local control over 91%, and 89.8% laryngeal preservation; Rizzotto and colleagues reported 5-year overall and disease-free survival of 95.6% and 90.9% in 399 patients."],["osec","th:op-12~s6-2","Viva essentials","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Supraglottic versus supracricoid laryngectomy: what each removes and leaves. The structures that must survive. The stairs test and Karnofsky under 80. Tumour contraindications, especially subglottic extension over 10 mm and tongue base over 2 cm. The pre-epiglottic and paraglottic spaces and their significance. The transhyoid versus lateral pharyngotomy approach. Cuts at the ventricle; protect the folds, arytenoids and anterior commissure. Setting the tongue base back 12 to 15 mm deep; isolating the tracheostomy. Bilateral neck dissection (20% to 9%). The cuff, uncuffed tube, capping and decannulation timetable."],["osec","th:op-12~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Horizontal Partial Laryngectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Offering open surgery when transoral resection would do. Selecting a patient with poor lungs or poor motivation. Leaving one side of the neck untreated in supraglottic cancer. Letting the patient vomit."],["osec","th:op-121~s1-1","The case at a glance","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Operating note","Infratemporal fossa approaches Summary Indications Benign tumours (schwannoma, pleomorphic adenoma, AVM, paraganglioma, meningioma); malignant tumours (rhabdomyosarcoma, salivary cancers, extension of oral, sinus or nasopharyngeal cancer); infection from teeth, sinuses or salivary glands not resolved by culture-directed antimicrobials; fibromatosis and soft tissue radionecrosis Contraindications High anaesthetic risk; carotid involvement with a failed balloon occlusion test ; extensive brain invasion by malignancy; disease not suited to surgery (lymphoma, plasmacytoma) Biopsy first Confirm benign or malignant before major surgery (open, transnasal endoscopic, or image-guided FNA for deep tumours); not for typical vascular lesions such as glomus tumours Approaches Preauricular subtemporal; postauricular transtemporal; Fisch types A to D; facial translocation; endoscopic endonasal Anaesthesia General; plan the airway: trismus or expected airway compromise may need fibreoptic intubation or tracheostomy; no paralysis if nerves are monitored Position Supine; head holder or Mayfield pins if intracranial work is expected; neck accessible for vascular control Antibiotics Skin and aerodigestive cover; good CNS penetration if intracranial (the authors use cefazolin and metronidazole) Monitoring Facial nerve EMG for open approaches; vagal monitoring (laryngeal EMG tube) as needed Tray Open: Raney clips, drill, saw, osteotome, midface and cranial plates, vascular set, head and neck set. Endoscopic: navigation, 0 and angled scopes, sinus set, endoscopic bipolar and suction monopolar, cl"],["osec","th:op-121~s1-2","Consent","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove a growth deep in the side of the head, below the skull base. Numbness of the face, teeth or tongue (trigeminal branches), sometimes permanent; a numb face can be bitten or cut without noticing. Facial weakness , even if your face works normally now, especially if the nerve is moved; eye protection may be needed. Jaw stiffness (trismus) and a change in bite; exercises are essential. Bleeding , and rarely injury to the carotid artery with stroke or death. Leak of brain fluid. Hollowing of the temple if the temporalis muscle is moved, which can be filled later. Swallowing and voice problems if the lower cranial nerves are involved; possibly a feeding tube or tracheostomy. Hearing loss or a blocked ear if the Eustachian tube or temporal bone is involved."],["osec","th:op-121~s1-3","Before you start","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Operating note","Ask about every cranial nerve. Trigeminal symptoms are common: numbness or dysaesthesia in V1, V2 or V3, chewing problems, malocclusion or jaw deviation from mass effect or masseteric weakness. Facial weakness means compression or invasion, but normal function does not guarantee the nerve will survive . Tumours near the jugular foramen affect IX to XII; temporal bone or posterior fossa invasion causes sensorineural loss and imbalance. Dysphagia and aspiration may worsen after surgery: consider a feeding tube or tracheostomy. A unilateral middle ear effusion with conductive loss suggests Eustachian tube compression. Ask about vision and diplopia, previous tumours and their treatment, general fitness and smoking. Look for subtle swelling of the temple above the arch, test all cranial nerves, check for trismus (pterygoid or joint involvement, and the airway plan), record visual acuity and audiometry, and endoscope the nose if an endonasal route is considered."],["osec","th:op-121~s1-4","Imaging and preparation","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Operating note","CT shows bone and widened foramina; MRI shows soft tissue planes and perineural spread; both are often needed. Define the carotid : MR angiography or angiography if in doubt, a balloon occlusion test if it may be manipulated, and a neurovascular surgeon if bypass is possible. Stage metastatic disease by histology. Plan navigation, have blood products ready (internal maxillary artery, carotid), consider embolising the internal maxillary artery (which may limit reconstructive options), consult the microvascular surgeon early for large defects (Allen test, vascular studies), and place a lumbar drain if significant intradural dissection is expected. Involve neurosurgery for skull base or intracranial spread."],["osec","th:op-121~c2","Anatomy you must see","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Anatomy you must see","Walls and contents. Boundaries of the infratemporal fossa Wall Bone and foramina Muscle Nerves and vessels Superior Temporal bone, greater wing of sphenoid; carotid canal, jugular foramen, foramina ovale, spinosum and lacerum — V3; carotid; jugular vein Medial Lateral pterygoid plate, pterygopalatine fissure Superior constrictor, pharyngobasilar fascia — Lateral Mandible, zygoma Masseter, temporalis — Contents — Medial and lateral pterygoids Internal maxillary artery; pterygoid plexus Posterior Articular tubercle, glenoid fossa, condyle, styloid; prevertebral fascia — IX to XI, sympathetic chain; carotid; jugular vein; parotid"],["osec","th:op-121~s2-1","Landmarks by approach","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Anatomy you must see","Preauricular: the frontal branch of the facial nerve in the superficial layer of the deep temporal fascia; the facial nerve trunk; the internal maxillary artery and carotid; IX to XII. Follow the lateral pterygoid plate back to the foramen ovale (V3), the foramen spinosum (middle meningeal artery) and the spine of the sphenoid (the carotid canal). Postauricular: the spine of Henle; the intratemporal facial nerve and the stylomastoid foramen; the bony-cartilaginous junction of the canal if it will be closed; the great vessels and X, XI and XII in the neck. Endoscopic: the maxillary antrostomy and orbital floor; the sphenopalatine and internal maxillary arteries and V2 behind the posterior antral wall."],["osec","th:op-121~s3-1","Indications","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · The operation: preauricular subtemporal","Suits many tumours of the fossa and intracranial tumours of the greater wing or anterior temporal bone; it cannot remove the whole temporal bone or control the infratemporal facial nerve or jugular bulb. What you should see On the scan: the tumour's relation to the carotid, the jugular bulb, the facial nerve and the middle fossa floor, which together choose the approach."],["osec","th:op-121~s3-2","Steps","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · The operation: preauricular subtemporal","A hemicoronal or bicoronal incision across the vertex, extended in front of the ear; carry it across the midline for more rotation. Go through galea and pericranium and raise the flap forward subpericranially , Raney clips on the edges, keeping the anterior branches of the superficial temporal artery that feed the flap. Over temporalis, from back to front, make an interfascial incision through the superficial layer of the deep temporal fascia and continue down and forward deep to the temporal fat pad , which protects the frontal branch ; reflect the fat pad and superficial fascia with the flap to the orbitozygomatic complex, exposed subperiosteally. Free the periosteum on both sides of the zygoma and the periorbita of the lateral orbit from roof to inferior orbital fissure. With a reciprocating saw, make bevelled cuts at the root of the zygoma, the zygomaticofrontal suture and the zygomaticomaxillary buttress to free the orbitozygomatic segment; pre-bend and drill the plates first, and keep the bone in saline. Find and protect the facial nerve trunk as in parotidectomy; divide other soft tissue in front of the temporal bone to rotate the flap further. Lift temporalis from its fossa on its inferior pedicle , protecting the deep temporal vessels from the internal maxillary artery near the coronoid; leave a fascial cuff above for reattachment. The fossa is now open. A subtemporal craniectomy helps find the foramina. To reach the petrous carotid , dislocate or resect the condyle to enter the glenoid fossa (the carotid lies medial to it), with a temporal craniotomy if needed. Fi"],["osec","th:op-121~s3-3","Close","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · The operation: preauricular subtemporal","Obliterate dead space and seal any communication with the aerodigestive tract; transpose all or the front of temporalis into the defect, any free part into the anterior temple for appearance. Replace the orbitozygomatic bone with midface plates. Use free tissue if temporalis is not viable or the defect is large (maxillectomy, orbital exenteration). Drain under the scalp flap. What you should see No dead space, no communication with the pharynx or sinuses left open, and bone plated back in position. In children The mandible sits closer to the skull base in children, so the infratemporal skull base can sometimes be reached through the neck alone, protecting the facial nerve."],["osec","th:op-121~s4-1","Steps","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · The operation: postauricular transtemporal","Suited to temporal bone lesions extending into the fossa. A C-shaped or question-mark postauricular incision from the temple, over the mastoid, into a mid-neck crease. Transect and close the ear canal if the middle ear will be sacrificed and there is a CSF leak risk; otherwise reflect the auricle forward with incisions around the tragus and conchal bowl, leaving the canal intact to avoid stenosis. Raise the cervicofacial flap below the platysma in the neck, above the SMAS over the parotid and on the deep layer of the deep temporal fascia over the skull, and reflect it forward. Find the facial nerve trunk and keep a cuff of soft tissue around it against traction. A mastoidectomy with dissection of the intratemporal nerve allows it to be mobilised for wider access to the fossa, jugular bulb and lower cranial nerves. Control the common, internal and external carotids and the jugular vein in the neck. Detach sternomastoid and posterior digastric from the mastoid; feel the styloid , divide stylohyoid and stylopharyngeus and remove it: IX may now be seen crossing the carotid. Add a temporal craniotomy and orbitozygomatic osteotomy as above, remove the tumour and reconstruct. Figure 2 The postauricular approach: (A) the cervicofacial flap reflected, showing temporalis, the orbitozygomatic bone, the closed ear canal, the facial nerve entering the parotid, the hypoglossal nerve and the great vessels; (B) temporalis reflected, the bone removed and the temporal craniotomy completed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 121.2 What you should see The facial nerve trunk at the stylomast"],["osec","th:op-121~s4-2","Fisch's lateral approaches","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · The operation: postauricular transtemporal","Ugo Fisch's transtemporal approaches drill through the temporal bone and reroute the facial nerve for subtemporal exposure. Type A suits glomus jugulare and middle ear cleft tumours. Type B reflects the arch and temporalis down, performs a subtotal petrosectomy and disarticulates the jaw joint to expose the carotid and foramen ovale. Type C adds the pterygoid plates for the Eustachian tube orifice, nasopharynx and posterior maxillary sinus. A later type D is essentially a preauricular approach without removing middle ear structures, rerouting the nerve or fully exposing the carotid. Figure 3 The approximate cranial base access of the Fisch infratemporal approaches. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 121.3 What you should see The landmarks that define each type: the rerouted facial nerve (A), the petrous carotid and foramen ovale (B), the pterygoid plates and Eustachian tube (C)."],["osec","th:op-121~s5-1","Steps","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · The operation: facial translocation","Reserved for very extensive tumours of the fossa, masticator space, pterygomaxillary fossa and paraclival region, and nasopharyngeal tumours reaching the fossa. A hemicoronal incision with preauricular extension, the scalp raised as before. A modified Weber-Ferguson incision to periosteum (a lip split only if needed), joined by a horizontal incision from the lateral canthus to the top of the ear. Identify, tag and divide the frontal branches (EMG helps) for repair at the end. Reflect the mid and lower face down; divide and tag the infraorbital nerve for repair if uninvolved. Raise the frontotemporal flap subpericranially and perform the orbitozygomatic osteotomies, plus a unilateral Le Fort I or II as needed to free the maxillary face with the complex. Reflect temporalis down; osteotomise the coronoid for more rotation, protecting the deep temporal arteries. The fossa is open; remove the pterygoid plates for the medial fossa or nasopharynx; add a temporal or subtemporal craniotomy for intracranial access. Reconstruct with transposed temporalis or free tissue. A temporary tarsorrhaphy for 10 to 14 days prevents ectropion; stent transected canaliculi with silicone into the nose. Close in layers. Figure 4 Facial translocation: (A) the incisions; (B) the craniofacial skeleton exposed; (C) the orbitozygomaticomaxillary osteotomies; (D) the view through the fossa to the nasopharynx; (E) added frontotemporal craniotomy; (F) temporalis transposed into the skull base defect. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 121.4 What you should see The maxilla swung on its cheek flap, the infr"],["osec","th:op-121~s6-1","Steps","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · The operation: endoscopic endonasal","For biopsy or resection of tumours of the medial fossa, with navigation on thin-cut CT. Prepare the face with the eyes lubricated but visible (pupils and globes), decongest, register navigation, endoscope both sides, inject, and correct a septal deviation if it blocks access. Make a generous maxillary antrostomy and remove the posterior wall to the pterygomaxillary fissure. Clip or cauterise the sphenopalatine artery and continue into the pterygopalatine fossa. Find V3 at the foramen ovale with navigation, and navigate to the lesion. For more lateral or three-dimensional access, add a Caldwell-Luc antrostomy; removing the back of the inferior turbinate or a medial maxillectomy adds room. Continue back to the pterygoid plates and the Eustachian tube. Figure 5 The endoscopic endonasal route to the right infratemporal fossa: (A) posterior antral wall and sphenopalatine foramen; (B) the fossa after removing the internal maxillary artery; (C, D) the pterygopalatine fossa and the distal internal maxillary branches; (E) the transected internal maxillary artery and the heads of lateral pterygoid; (F) V3 leaving the foramen ovale and dividing into lingual, inferior alveolar, buccal and pterygoid branches; (G, H) the pterygoid plates removed and the muscles reflected. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 121.5 What you should see The posterior antral wall removed, the internal maxillary artery and its branches clipped, and the pterygoid plates behind with the tumour medial to the lateral plate."],["osec","th:op-121~s7-1","Crisis drills","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · When it goes wrong","Carotid injury 1 Know ischaemic brain injury can occur even after a satisfactory balloon test 2 Prepare neck control, vascular set, neurovascular surgeon available The eye will not close 1 Risk exposure keratitis, even blindness 2 Act lubrication, humidity chamber, taping tarsorrhaphy or a gold or platinum weight Bleeding after an endoscopic approach 1 Think internal maxillary artery or its branches 2 Act evaluate carefully consider packing, which also steadies grafts"],["osec","th:op-121~s7-2","Common errors in technique","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Inadequate exposure. Elevating in the wrong plane and injuring nerve branches (the frontal branch above all). Sacrificing reconstructive tissue or its blood supply during exposure. Failing to restore tissue planes at closure. No vascular control."],["osec","th:op-121~s8-1","After the operation","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Aftercare","Intensive care at least the first night. Watch facial nerve function and protect the eye. After endoscopic approaches, watch for epistaxis. When the dura was opened, catch a CSF leak early: lumbar drainage or re-operation may be needed."],["osec","th:op-121~s8-2","Complications","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Trigeminal loss V2/V3 numbness risks biting and shaving injuries; V1 numbness risks corneal ulcer , managed aggressively; V3 motor loss causes malocclusion and jaw drift Facial nerve injury Transection or traction; protect the eye Trismus Pain early, pterygoid scarring late; regular jaw-opening exercises Vascular injury Bleeding; carotid injury and ischaemia CSF leak Immediate or delayed Temple hollowing From temporalis transposition; fill with a graft or implant"],["ev","th:op-121~s8-3","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa: current evidence","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Aftercare","Endoscopic or open. Endoscopic endonasal and transmaxillary (including prelacrimal and Denker) approaches now reach many infratemporal fossa lesions that once needed a Fisch infratemporal fossa or facial translocation approach, with less facial morbidity in published series; open approaches remain necessary for large, lateral or malignant tumours involving the skin, mandible or skull base widely. Morbidity. Trismus from pterygoid muscle resection, V3 numbness, Eustachian tube dysfunction and middle ear effusion are common after infratemporal surgery."],["osec","th:op-121~s8-3","Alternatives","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · Aftercare","External beam radiotherapy with or without chemotherapy for some tumours, decided at a multidisciplinary tumour board."],["osec","th:op-121~s9-1","Schwannomas: radical or function-sparing?","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · For the examination","Schwannomas are among the commonest tumours of the infratemporal and parapharyngeal spaces. Radical resection with the nerve of origin should lower recurrence but causes major morbidity for complex nerves such as the high vagus, facial or trigeminal. Netterville and Groom's function-sparing intracapsular enucleation in 43 cervical and parapharyngeal schwannomas used EMG mapping to spare the functional fibres, often on the tumour surface: most kept normal or near-normal voice with no recurrence at 36 months. Whether this extends to high infratemporal tumours is unclear, but the case for sparing function is compelling. The editor adds that there is no single best approach, combinations may give the best exposure with the least morbidity, and surgeons should enlist colleagues with complementary skills."],["osec","th:op-121~s9-2","Viva essentials","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The boundaries and contents of the infratemporal fossa. Why biopsy first, and when not to (glomus tumours); why not to operate on lymphoma or plasmacytoma. The carotid: angiography, balloon occlusion, bypass. Protecting the frontal branch: the interfascial plane deep to the fat pad. The orbitozygomatic osteotomies; reaching the petrous carotid through the glenoid fossa. Landmarks: lateral plate to foramen ovale, spinosum and the sphenoid spine. Fisch types A to D; facial translocation; the endoscopic route. Trismus and eye care afterwards."],["osec","th:op-121~s9-3","Common pitfalls","Surgical Approaches to the Infratemporal Fossa · For the examination","Common pitfalls Underestimating the airway in a patient with trismus. Promising that a working facial nerve will be preserved. Forgetting a lumbar drain for intradural work. Leaving the temple hollow."],["osec","th:op-122~s1-1","The case at a glance","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Operating note","Juvenile angiofibroma Summary Suspect it Unilateral nasal obstruction and recurrent epistaxis in an adolescent boy Diagnosis Imaging , without biopsy in most cases; biopsy only for atypical features suggesting sarcoma, under general anaesthesia with the airway controlled Indications Surgery is the treatment of choice for all angiofibromas, even with intracranial extension Contraindications Residual or recurrent tumour that is not growing (observe); refusal of blood products (relative, for advanced tumours with residual vascularity); medical conditions precluding surgery Embolisation Bilateral angiography and embolisation the day before (the authors prefer Onyx; coils in the proximal internal maxillary arteries); for small tumours, ligating the internal maxillary artery lateral to the tumour at the start is an alternative Approach by stage UPMC I and II: endoscopic endonasal; larger tumours with residual vascularity: add a transmaxillary approach; lateral extension to the middle fossa or infratemporal area: combine with a lateral (infratemporal) approach Anaesthesia General, oral intubation Position Supine; Mayfield pins if navigation and drilling at the skull base Antibiotics Second-generation cephalosporin; third-generation if the dura may be opened Monitoring SSEPs when the carotid will be dissected; EMG of III, IV, VI and V3 Tray Sinus set; maxillofacial instruments; Doppler for the carotid; nerve stimulator; endoscopic bipolar; endoscopic drill; harmonic scalpel; navigation Blood Cross-matched blood despite embolisation; directed donors, banked autologous blood in older"],["osec","th:op-122~s1-2","Consent","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Operating note","What to tell the patient and family Why: to remove a non-cancerous but very bloody growth at the back of the nose, which will keep growing and bleeding if left. Embolisation the day before to block its blood supply. Bleeding can still be heavy; a transfusion may be needed, and the operation may be done in stages. Numbness of the palate, cheek or teeth (the commonest problem), and of the lower face with jaw weakness if the tumour is large. A slightly dry eye from cutting the vidian nerve, usually unnoticed. Double vision or, rarely, visual loss if the tumour involves the orbit or optic canal. Jaw stiffness for a while; stretching exercises help. Leak of brain fluid (rare) and, very rarely, injury to the carotid artery. Leftover tumour is possible; small pieces that do not grow can be watched."],["osec","th:op-122~s1-3","Before you start","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Operating note","Ask about nasal obstruction (one or both sides), recurrent epistaxis, facial swelling, diplopia, V2 numbness, facial or sinus pain and hearing loss. On endoscopy, a reddish, smooth, lobulated mass fills the back of the nasal cavity; look for cheek or temporal swelling, proptosis or globe displacement, a middle ear effusion with conductive loss, and V2 hypoaesthesia."],["osec","th:op-122~s1-4","Imaging","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Operating note","CT with contrast first: it shows the vascular tumour and the bone, with the characteristic widening of the pterygopalatine space and bowing of the posterior maxillary wall ; obtain a CT angiogram on the skull base and navigation protocol to show the carotid. MRI shows the interface with orbit, masticator space and dura, and separates tumour from retained secretions; without it CT can suggest an aggressive tumour such as sarcoma. The carotids and tumour vessels appear as flow voids. Angiography confirms the hypervascular tumour and shows its supply, usually the internal maxillary and ascending pharyngeal branches of the external carotid, often bilateral in large tumours; advanced tumours also draw from the internal carotid through the vidian and cavernous branches. With skull base involvement near the carotid, angiography assesses the circle of Willis; a balloon occlusion test guides management if the carotid is encased (most young patients tolerate sacrifice if necessary). Figure 1 CT of an angiofibroma (UPMC stage V) with bone erosion and extension into the orbits and cranial cavity; CT is best for bone and for estimating vascularity. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 122.1 Figure 2 MRI of the same stage: the soft tissue interfaces, with the carotids (arrows) and tumour vessels as flow voids. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 122.2 Figure 3 Angiography after internal maxillary embolisation (coils in the proximal trunk) showing much residual supply from the internal carotid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 122.3"],["osec","th:op-122~s1-5","UPMC staging","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Operating note","Bleeding is the greatest risk in surgery, and the UPMC system is the only one that counts residual vascularity after embolisation ; it predicts blood loss and residual or recurrent tumour better than others. UPMC staging Stage Extent Residual vascularity I Nasal cavity, medial pterygopalatine fossa — II Paranasal sinuses, lateral pterygopalatine fossa None III Skull base erosion, orbit, infratemporal fossa None IV Skull base erosion, orbit, infratemporal fossa Present V Intracranial extension (M medial, L lateral) Present"],["osec","th:op-122~s2-1","Landmarks","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Anatomy you must see","The pterygoid canal and pterygoid base: the site of origin ; tumour infiltrates the spaces of the bone and must be drilled out. The canal, carrying the vidian nerve and artery, lies inferomedial to the lateral recess of the sphenoid, immediately behind the sphenopalatine foramen, and leads to the petrous carotid . The greater palatine nerve: descends in its canal just deep to the sphenopalatine foramen, often stretched over the tumour surface ; injury numbs the palate. The infraorbital nerve: often pushed up as the tumour erodes the orbital floor; injury numbs the cheek. The foramen rotundum (V2), superolateral to the lateral recess; the foramen ovale (V3), below it and behind the lateral pterygoid plate. Figure 4 Coronal CT showing the characteristic invasion of the pterygoid base around the pterygoid canal (circle); the normal canal and foramen rotundum are seen on the other side. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 122.4"],["osec","th:op-122~s3-1","Open the corridor around the tumour","Juvenile Angiofibroma · The operation: endoscopic endonasal","Decongest with 0.05% oxymetazoline and assess the tumour. Make a large middle meatal antrostomy on the tumour side, enlarge it back to the sphenopalatine foramen and remove the overlying (often thin, remodelled) bone with 1 to 2 mm Kerrisons to expose the pterygopalatine space, lateral to the tumour's edge and up toward the foramen rotundum. Perform an ethmoidectomy, take the lower middle turbinate, clear the posterior ethmoids to the roof and medial orbital wall, and open the sphenoid above the tumour if possible. Do not instrument the tumour ; its surface can be bluntly freed from the skull base and pushed down. Control surface bleeding with bipolar . What you should see The tumour's surface in the nasopharynx and nose, the pterygopalatine fossa opened lateral to it, and the sphenopalatine and internal maxillary supply clipped."],["osec","th:op-122~s3-2","Binarial access","Juvenile Angiofibroma · The operation: endoscopic endonasal","On the other side, cauterise and cut the posterior septal mucosa, remove the rostrum and perform a sphenoidotomy for access through both nostrils. Incise the nasopharyngeal mucosal margins with a needle-tip diathermy, which eases final delivery into the nasopharynx. What you should see The tumour approached through both nostrils, with the nasopharyngeal mucosa cut round its base."],["osec","th:op-122~s3-3","Free the lateral margin","Juvenile Angiofibroma · The operation: endoscopic endonasal","With access all round, dissect the pterygopalatine space to expose the tumour's lateral margin, pushing the fossa contents from medial to lateral to save the greater palatine nerve, often displaced forward. Stay on the tumour surface and deliver nodules from the masticator space and from behind the maxilla. What you should see The lateral edge of the tumour with normal fat and muscle beyond it, and the tumour surface intact. Early violation of the capsule Dissecting into the tumour before its margins are fully exposed causes troublesome bleeding. Expose first, then mobilise."],["osec","th:op-122~s3-4","The sphenoid and the pterygoid base","Juvenile Angiofibroma · The operation: endoscopic endonasal","Free the sphenoid component bluntly from the sinus walls with the optic canal and carotid clearly in view. The last attachment is the pterygoid base , where tumour fills the bony spaces and the vidian artery may feed it: dissect it bluntly off the bone and cauterise the vidian artery and nerve with bipolar. What you should see The optic canal and carotid prominence in the sphenoid, and the tumour's last stalk at the pterygoid base."],["osec","th:op-122~s3-5","Deliver","Juvenile Angiofibroma · The operation: endoscopic endonasal","Deliver the whole tumour through the mouth or in pieces; divide large tumours into vascular segments with the harmonic scalpel or coblation. What you should see The whole tumour out, with a bed that is not pulsating with fresh bleeding."],["osec","th:op-122~s3-6","Drill the pterygoid base","Juvenile Angiofibroma · The operation: endoscopic endonasal","Finally, drill the pterygoid circumferentially around the pterygoid canal to remove all remnants, down to the plane of the petrous carotid if necessary. Control bleeding with bipolar, gelatin powder and warm saline, and cover raw surfaces with fibrin glue. What you should see Clean, bleeding bone all round the pterygoid canal with no pale tumour in the cancellous spaces. Remnants in the pterygoid base Small remnants hide in the spaces of the pterygoid base and are the commonest source of recurrence. Drill it completely."],["osec","th:op-122~c4","The operation: transmaxillary","Juvenile Angiofibroma · The operation: transmaxillary","More room laterally. Perform an endoscopic medial maxillectomy : cauterise and cut the inferior turbinate, cauterise the posterior stump, resect the medial maxillary wall with backbiters and drill it to the nasal floor, preserving the nasolacrimal duct . For more lateral access, add an anteromedial maxillotomy : incise at the pyriform aperture, lift periosteum off the edge and the anterior wall, and remove 1 to 2 cm of the anteromedial maxilla, extending across the anterior wall; or use a Caldwell-Luc , which also lets bipolar forceps in. Remove posterolateral maxillary wall to reach the masticator space and the tumour's lateral limit, and dissect the tumour off the infraorbital nerve and orbital floor."],["osec","th:op-122~c5","The operation: infratemporal skull base","Juvenile Angiofibroma · The operation: infratemporal skull base","For lateral extension. Through a hemicoronal incision extended in front of the ear, raise the scalp above the deep temporal fascia, protecting the temporal branches of the facial nerve. Incise the periosteum along the lateral orbital rim and arch, free masseter from the arch, lift the periorbita from the lateral orbital wall and find the inferior orbital fissure. Cut the arch behind, the lateral orbital wall, and finally across the malar eminence to the inferior orbital fissure, freeing the orbitozygomatic segment. Reflect temporalis down, perform a subtemporal craniectomy for the middle fossa, preserving V3 at the foramen ovale, and dissect the infratemporal extension deep to temporalis and the pterygoids. Transpose the front half of temporalis to cover the carotid or dura if needed."],["osec","th:op-122~s6-1","Crisis drills","Juvenile Angiofibroma · When it goes wrong","Brisk bleeding (stages IV and V) 1 Use bipolar, not monopolar 2 Strategy take the vascular segments one at a time 3 Too much pack the cavity and stage the operation A hole in the carotid 1 Prevent never avulse tumour vessels near the artery keep the field clear 2 Small hole bipolar diathermy 3 Afterwards angiography to exclude a sentinel bleed if the carotid was injured Bleeding after surgery 1 Cause usually unembolised external carotid branches 2 Act nasal packing angiography if the carotid was injured during surgery"],["osec","th:op-122~s6-2","Common errors in technique","Juvenile Angiofibroma · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Inadequate exposure (use a medial maxillectomy, bilateral sphenoidotomies, a Caldwell-Luc for instruments). Entering the tumour before exposing its margins. No plane in the pterygopalatine space, so V2 branches are lost. Incomplete drilling of the pterygoid base. Missing nodules behind and below the maxilla (palpate through the mouth to push them into view)."],["osec","th:op-122~s7-1","After the operation","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Aftercare","Remove packing at 1 to 2 days if there is no bleeding, with antibiotics while it remains. Remove silastic septal splints at 1 week. Eat from day 1 (chlorhexidine rinse after a Caldwell-Luc). Saline sprays at once, rinses from a week. Clean crusts in clinic from 1 week. Gentle stretching for trismus."],["osec","th:op-122~s7-2","Complications","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Haemorrhage Stages IV and V; segment, bipolar, stage if needed Carotid injury Poor view from bleeding; avulsed vessels CSF leak Dural invasion is rare; inlay and onlay fascia with fat support V2 hypoaesthesia The commonest complication: palate and midface; find the greater palatine and infraorbital nerves early V3 injury Lower face numbness and masticatory weakness with large tumours Orbital injury, visual loss Infraorbital canal, inferior orbital fissure, optic canal; no diathermy near the nerve, irrigate when drilling Dry eye Vidian nerve sacrifice reduces emotional tearing Trismus Early inflammation; late scarring treated with stretching devices Residual tumour Cavernous sinus, middle fossa, masticator space; linked to stage, bleeding, exposure, multilobulated tumours, prior treatment"],["ev","th:op-122~s7-3","Juvenile Angiofibroma: current evidence","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Aftercare","Endoscopic surgery. A systematic review of 1047 cases found less blood loss and fewer recurrences with endoscopic resection than open surgery, although the recurrence difference disappeared after adjustment for stage (Boghani et al., Laryngoscope 2013). Staging. The UPMC system, which includes residual internal carotid vascularity after embolisation, predicts blood loss and recurrence (Snyderman et al., Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2010). Embolisation 24 to 72 hours before surgery reduces blood loss in larger tumours. Follow-up with contrast MRI for at least three years, since most recurrences appear within two years in the basisphenoid and vidian canal."],["osec","th:op-122~s7-3","Alternatives","Juvenile Angiofibroma · Aftercare","Surgery is preferred because the disease is benign and radiotherapy has long-term effects in the young; subtotal resection with observation of remnants is reasonable near critical structures. Radiotherapy is rarely indicated: for poor surgical candidates, those refusing surgery or blood with advanced tumours, or unresectable tumours (perhaps after subtotal resection to limit the field). Refer to experienced centres. Chemotherapy and hormones are not effective."],["osec","th:op-122~s8-1","Is endoscopic surgery better?","Juvenile Angiofibroma · For the examination","Boghani and colleagues' systematic review of 1047 cases compared endoscopic, endoscopic-assisted and open resection: endoscopic resection had less blood loss and fewer recurrences , but after adjusting for tumour stage (Radkowski or Sessions), recurrence rates did not differ . The editor stresses that bleeding, especially internal carotid supply left after embolisation, is the greatest challenge, best met by a two-surgeon team with one surgeon's hands free for dissection."],["osec","th:op-122~s8-2","Viva essentials","Juvenile Angiofibroma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The classic presentation; diagnosis by imaging; no biopsy in clinic. Imaging findings: widened pterygopalatine fossa, bowed posterior antral wall, flow voids. Blood supply, and internal carotid supply in advanced tumours. Embolisation the day before; UPMC staging and why residual vascularity matters. The site of origin and why the pterygoid base must be drilled. The order: expose, free the margins, the pterygoid base last. V2 branches and how to save them. Recurrence sites; the role of radiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-122~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Juvenile Angiofibroma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Biopsying a vascular mass in clinic. Operating without embolisation or cross-matched blood. Chasing the tumour piecemeal before exposing it. Leaving tumour in the pterygoid base."],["osec","th:op-124~s1-1","The case at a glance","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Operating note","Endoscopic skull base reconstruction Summary Indications A dural defect after tumour or encephalocele surgery; a CSF leak after sinus surgery; large extradural defects (to promote healing, protect vital structures and reduce intracranial infection, especially before radiotherapy) Contraindications Unacceptable anaesthetic risk (observation, medical treatment or radiotherapy instead); an unresectable tumour (medical management unless surgery is the last palliation) Workhorse Nasoseptal flap on the posterior nasal artery from the sphenopalatine artery Alternatives Free mucosal graft (nasal floor, middle turbinate, septum); extranasal flaps (pericranial, temporalis, temporoparietal fascia); free tissue (radial forearm); middle and inferior turbinate flaps (limited use) Anaesthesia General with a secured airway (irrigation, blood and CSF run into the pharynx); warn the anaesthetist when near the optic nerves or carotid so the patient cannot move Position Supine, turned 90 or 180 degrees, with access to the head, abdomen and thigh (iliac crest to patella) for fat or fascia; eyes protected but accessible Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin (clindamycin, with better bone penetration, in allergy) for extradural or sellar work without high-flow leak; third-generation cephalosporin (vancomycin in allergy) after significant intra-arachnoid dissection Tray Sinus set; guarded monopolar (used cautiously near the skull base); endoscopic bipolars; microsurgical dissectors (Rhoton); navigation; allograft or collagen matrix sized beforehand; thrombin-gelatin; fibrin or biological glue"],["osec","th:op-124~s1-2","Consent","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to seal the opening between the nose and the brain after the tumour is removed, to stop brain fluid leaking and infection. The repair may leak (now under about 1 in 10); a further operation may be needed. Air inside the skull is expected; rarely it builds up under pressure and needs urgent treatment. A lumbar drain may be used, with its own small risks. Packing for a few days, then cleaning in clinic. Crusting , a blocked nose, reduced smell, and rarely a mucocele (a trapped sinus cyst) later. Fat or fascia may be taken from the abdomen or thigh. Six weeks without heavy activity, straining or CPAP."],["osec","th:op-124~s1-3","Before you start","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Operating note","Sinonasal disease presents with obstruction, discharge or epistaxis; planar and perisellar disease with headache or eye signs (field cuts, ophthalmoplegia, ptosis). A postoperative leak usually appears within a week as clear, persistent, salty rhinorrhoea ; later leaks can come from encephaloceles. Ask about previous sinus, septal, head and neck, neurosurgical or facelift surgery and radiotherapy, which may have destroyed the vascular pedicles and planes a flap needs; known skull base disease and its treatment; antiplatelets, anticoagulants, herbal products and alcohol."],["osec","th:op-124~s1-4","Examination","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Operating note","Endoscopy before surgery shows the tumour and whether the septum or rostrum is involved, which decides the flap. After surgery, endoscopy watches for tumour and leaks: a positive reservoir sign and more rhinorrhoea on Valsalva suggest a leak; intermediate (2 to 6 weeks) and late (over 6 weeks) leaks are uncommon, usually intermittent and low-flow from a pinpoint dural hole, and can usually be seen. Record orbital and trigeminal function, and examine the neck (nodes, and the vessels if free tissue may be needed)."],["osec","th:op-124~s1-5","Imaging and preparation","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Operating note","CT shows the bone and sizes the craniectomy; MRI shows intracranial extent and neurovascular relations; contrast shows the vessels; format both for navigation. Angiography is not needed for reconstruction. Make sure the whole team agrees on the resection and repair, consent for a lumbar drain if one is planned, load the images, and stop antiplatelets. Figure 1 Types of endonasal defect: (A, B) a small left ethmoid encephalocele on CT and MRI; (C, D) a large pituitary adenoma; (E) after resection, a well-placed, enhancing nasoseptal flap with a Foley balloon; (F, G) a tuberculum and planum meningioma with the anterior communicating and A2 arteries along its upper edge; (H) the nasoseptal flap after resection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 124.1 Figure 2 A transclival defect after resection of a posterior fossa meningioma, the basilar artery visible through it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 124.2"],["osec","th:op-124~s2-1","The nasoseptal flap pedicle","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Anatomy you must see","Blood comes from the external carotid through the sphenopalatine artery to the posterior nasal (septal) branches , which cross above the choana. The pedicle always runs between the free edge of the choana and the sphenoid ostium . Do not dissect the lower front of the sphenoid until the flap is raised."],["osec","th:op-124~s2-2","The bony edge","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Anatomy you must see","Measure the defect and define its bony edges: inlay grafts must sit behind them; onlay grafts must cover them. Figure 3 The nasoseptal flap and its incisions (dotted): about 1.5 cm below the olfactory groove, extendable onto the nasal floor or forward to the mucocutaneous junction, on the posterior nasal artery (PNA) between the choana and the sphenoid ostium (SO). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 124.3"],["osec","th:op-124~s3-1","Decongest and open the view","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest with 0.05% oxymetazoline, given time to work (topical adrenaline is an option, beware rebound bleeding); the authors do not inject. Out-fracture the inferior and middle turbinates to see the whole septum from the olfactory sulcus to the floor. What you should see The whole septum from the olfactory cleft to the floor on the side you will harvest."],["osec","th:op-124~s3-2","A free mucosal graft","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Measure the defect first and mark the graft's mucosal side so it faces the nose. Middle turbinate: see it up to the axilla; cut from front to back just below the axilla (never upward into the skull base), divide the horizontal basal lamella, cauterise the turbinate branch without harming the main sphenopalatine trunk, and strip the mucosa on the back table, keeping the bone. Nasal floor: between the septum and the inferior meatus, extendable along the lateral wall and septum; not behind the hard palate (soft palate muscle); beware Hasner's valve and the mucocutaneous junction; raise subperiosteally. Keep grafts in saline. What you should see A graft larger than the defect, with its mucosal face marked."],["osec","th:op-124~s3-3","Choose the side of the flap","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Take the flap from the opposite side if the operation will clear the pterygoid recess or pterygopalatine fossa, or if tumour invades the rostrum or septal mucosa. For midline lesions, a sharp spur that risks perforation may decide; the right side is easier for a right-handed surgeon. What you should see A side with intact septal mucosa and an undamaged posterior septal pedicle."],["osec","th:op-124~s3-4","Harvest the nasoseptal flap","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Find and enter the sphenoid ostium on the harvest side. Inferior incision with needle-tip diathermy in one smooth line (suction at the nostril for smoke): from inferolateral to the ostium, below the sphenoid floor, across the choana, over the back of the septum curving down to the hard palate, then forward along the septum-floor junction as far as the defect needs. Include the floor for a wider flap. The whole septal lining can cover from the frontal sinus to the sella and orbit to orbit. Superior incision from the ostium across the sphenoid face, sloping up over the rostrum, then forward 10 to 15 mm below the top of the septum to spare olfactory epithelium. Join them with a vertical incision in front of the head of the inferior turbinate, or as far forward as the mucocutaneous junction for a longer flap. Overestimate the size and trim later. Complete every incision before elevating , using scissors where diathermy did not go through. Elevate from the front in the subperichondrial plane with a Cottle or suction dissector, and free it from the sphenoid face, preserving the posterolateral pedicle. Park the flap in the nasopharynx or the maxillary sinus through an antrostomy, away from the drill. What you should see The flap raised in the subperichondrial plane on a pedicle between the sphenoid ostium and the choana, pink, and stored in the nasopharynx or maxillary sinus. Losing the flap Pedicles are cut while widening the sphenoid ostium, making the inferior choanal cut, or freeing the flap from the sphenoid face; confirm the ostium before the inferior cut and keep the field "],["osec","th:op-124~s3-5","Prepare the defect","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","After the resection, remove all mucosa around the defect (trapped mucosa means poor healing and a mucocele) and make a planar surface , filling dead space with fat if needed; dermal fat grafts give a smooth outer face. What you should see Bare bone all round the defect, with no mucosa left under where the flap will lie."],["osec","th:op-124~s3-6","Layer it","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Subdural inlay of collagen matrix or allograft, extending 5 to 10 mm beyond the dural edges, which also fills intradural dead space. A second epidural inlay between bone and dura if needed (the bony ledges are sometimes too thin). Onlay of the free graft or nasoseptal flap over the bony edges, every edge resting on bone, mucosal side out. Never put sealant between the layers or under the flap : it prevents tissue contact. If the flap is torn, keep the tear off the dural defect, or plug it with a dermal graft. Figure 4 A right nasoseptal flap (asterisks) over a sellar and planar defect; the pedicle rotated so the flap lies above the clival recess (CR) on a flatter surface. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 124.4 What you should see Each layer flat with no CSF coming through, and the flap's edges on bone all round, pedicle untwisted."],["osec","th:op-124~s3-7","Pack under vision","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Bolster the flap edges with oxidised cellulose, separate the repair from the packing with a non-adherent layer (gelatin sponge or film) so removal does not drag it, and place a 12 Fr Foley balloon (7 to 10 mL saline, tip first in the nasopharynx) or 10 cm expanding sponges under direct endoscopic vision , without over-inflating. What you should see The flap unmoved as the balloon fills, seen on the endoscope. Packing moves the repair Packing can shift the inlay and onlay. Place and inflate it while watching the repair."],["osec","th:op-124~s4-1","Crisis drills","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · When it goes wrong","Postoperative leak 1 Recognise clear rhinorrhoea reservoir sign 2 Confirm endoscopy, beta-2 transferrin, topical fluorescein pledgets, or intrathecal fluorescein 3 Act explore and repair Pneumocephalus 1 Expected some air observe if asymptomatic 2 Increasing on serial CT a leak explore early and repair 3 Tension usually with positive pressure ventilation theatre to take down the repair and release the air Major vessel injury 1 Act pack to control temporarily 2 Then angiographic treatment a nasoseptal flap covers the injured vessel, sometimes as a delayed repair after packing is removed"],["osec","th:op-124~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls A graft over a non-planar surface or dead space. Mucosa left around the defect. A flap that does not cover all bony edges. Packing placed blind, moving the flap. Figure 5 A reconstruction displaced into a craniofacial defect after sinonasal cancer resection; though not leaking, it was revised to sit at the level of the skull base. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 124.5"],["osec","th:op-124~s5-1","After the operation","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Aftercare","Bed rest for 48 hours after a high-flow intraoperative leak. No routine lumbar drain. Head of bed 30 to 45 degrees . Stool softener . Remove packing or balloon at 3 to 5 days ; start saline rinses. First debridement at 2 weeks (gently, only non-adherent packing); second at 4 weeks (most of the remaining packing). No strenuous activity or CPAP for 6 weeks. How long the packing stays The sella chapter in this book leaves packing for 5 to 7 days after a dural repair or flap; this chapter removes it at 3 to 5 days. Both are presented as the authors' practice."],["osec","th:op-124~s5-2","Complications","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes CSF leak Confirm and repair Pneumocephalus Observe if mild; explore if increasing; release if tension Haemorrhage Pack, angiography, flap cover Mucocele Mucosa left under the repair or a blocked sinus outflow: strip mucosa, open neighbouring sinuses widely (sphenoidotomy, posterior septectomy, modified Lothrop); later endoscopic marsupialisation Flap failure Cut pedicle or perforation"],["ev","th:op-124~s5-3","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery: current evidence","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Aftercare","Nasoseptal flap. Described by Hadad and colleagues ( Laryngoscope 2006), the vascularised nasoseptal flap reduced postoperative CSF leak after extended endoscopic skull base surgery to about 5% in later series. Lumbar drains. In a randomised trial of high-risk defects, a lumbar drain for three days reduced postoperative leaks from 21% to 8% (Zwagerman et al., J Neurosurg 2019). Free grafts suffice for small, low-flow defects. Spontaneous leaks are linked to raised intracranial pressure; weight loss and acetazolamide reduce recurrence."],["osec","th:op-124~s5-3","Alternatives","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · Aftercare","Extranasal flaps (pericranial, temporalis, temporoparietal fascia) or free tissue (radial forearm) for large defects or when the nasoseptal flap is unavailable, usually needing an open approach for the pedicle. Lumbar drainage to unload the repair, now used less for small, low-flow leaks because of its complications."],["osec","th:op-124~s6-1","Lumbar drains for high-flow leaks?","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · For the examination","High-flow leaks come from opening a basal cistern or the third ventricle. Lumbar drains carry about 5% minor and 3% major complications , and Tien and colleagues found only non-randomised data, with no clear difference in leak rate when a drain is added to a multilayer vascularised repair. Decide case by case: consider a drain for intracranial hypertension, an early postoperative leak, previous radiotherapy , or the need for intrathecal fluorescein. The editor finds that large anterior and posterior fossa defects benefit from CSF diversion, while sellar and suprasellar defects do not."],["osec","th:op-124~s6-2","Vascularised flap or free graft?","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · For the examination","Early free-graft repairs leaked in 5 to 35% ; recent reviews report about 8.5% , largely thanks to vascularised flaps. Harvey and colleagues found leaks in 6.7% with vascularised repair against 15.6% with free grafts; Soudry and colleagues found 94% success with vascularised repair of high-flow leaks against 82% with free grafts."],["osec","th:op-124~s6-3","Viva essentials","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The nasoseptal flap's blood supply and where the pedicle runs. Incisions: the olfactory margin of 10 to 15 mm, the vertical cut, extensions. Which side to harvest, and why. Measuring, stripping mucosa, making it planar. Inlay, onlay, no sealant between layers. Packing under vision; balloon volume. Aftercare: head up, stool softener, no CPAP for 6 weeks. Leak, pneumocephalus and mucocele. The lumbar drain debate; flap versus free graft numbers."],["osec","th:op-124~s6-4","Common pitfalls","Reconstruction After Skull Base Surgery · For the examination","Common pitfalls Drilling the sphenoid floor before the flap is raised. Elevating the flap before completing the incisions. Leaving the flap in the path of the drill. Glue under the flap."],["osec","th:op-125~s1-1","The cases at a glance","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Operating note","Office procedures in otology Procedure Main indications Anaesthesia Key risk Wax and cavity cleaning Impacted cerumen, mastoid cavity debris None Canal trauma, vertigo from suction in a cavity Auricular haematoma drainage Haematoma of the pinna after shearing trauma Regional block of the pinna Recurrence, cauliflower ear, perichondritis Abscess drainage Furuncle, auricular abscess, perichondritis with collection Local or regional block Cartilage necrosis and rim deformity Canal biopsy Mass in the external canal Local canal block or none for a polyp Facial nerve (schwannoma), bleeding (glomus) Intratympanic gentamicin Ménière's disease failing conservative treatment Topical phenol Hearing loss, imbalance, perforation Intratympanic steroid Sudden sensorineural hearing loss; Ménière's disease Topical phenol Perforation, pain, transient vertigo Myringotomy and tube Effusion, complicated acute otitis media, Eustachian tube dysfunction, hyperbaric therapy Topical phenol Bleeding, vascular anomaly mistaken for effusion"],["osec","th:op-125~s1-2","When not to do it in the clinic","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Operating note","Children, anxious or uncooperative adults, narrow canals, and complex or long procedures need sedation or a general anaesthetic. A pulsatile canal or middle ear mass (glomus tumour) must not be biopsied in the clinic. Tell the patient beforehand how long it will take, how much it will hurt, how loud it will be and whether they may feel dizzy; expectations shape satisfaction."],["osec","th:op-125~s1-3","Consent","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Operating note","What to tell the patient Wax and cavity care: noise, brief dizziness with suction, minor bleeding. Haematoma drainage: recurrence, infection, a lasting change in the shape of the ear, and the need to wear a bolster for a week. Abscess drainage: the infection may take weeks to settle and may recur; damaged cartilage may leave a notch in the rim. Biopsy: bleeding, infection, the need for imaging or a formal operation depending on the result. Intratympanic gentamicin: about 90% control of vertigo; hearing loss in roughly 17% to 30% (lower with low-dose regimens), more tinnitus, imbalance for days or longer, persistent perforation, and the chance that the other ear later develops Ménière's disease. Intratympanic steroid: pain and a burning sensation, brief dizziness, a small risk of persistent perforation or infection."],["osec","th:op-125~s2-1","The tympanic membrane","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Anaesthesia in the clinic","The ear must be meticulously clean for any topical agent to work; a thickened drum in acute otitis media may not respond, and a perforated drum lets the agent reach the middle ear, which can cause transient facial palsy or vertigo. Phenol is the preferred agent: a controlled chemical burn that both anaesthetises and stops bleeding. Apply it sparingly with a phenol applicator (used like a quill pen), a wisp of cotton on a metal applicator or a sponge-tipped kit, to a dry drum: a line for a myringotomy, a dot for an injection. It stings for seconds and the drum blanches white at once. Excess causes scarring. Lidocaine 4% to 10% in saline or tetracaine 8% to 10% in alcohol fill the medial canal with the patient supine and head turned away, for about 15 minutes (lidocaine) or 60 minutes (tetracaine); a pledget or wick lets them walk about meanwhile. Remove all the solution and test before you start. EMLA (lidocaine and prilocaine 2.5% each) is safe on the drum but needs an hour and is not always enough. Figure 1 Phenol application. A: a prepackaged sponge-tipped kit. B: cotton on a metal applicator, and a phenol applicator. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 125.1"],["osec","th:op-125~s2-2","The pinna and canal","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Anaesthesia in the clinic","Topical EMLA or LET gel (lidocaine 4%, adrenaline 0.1%, tetracaine 0.5%) under an occlusive dressing for at least 30 minutes (LET) or 60 minutes (EMLA), then test with a pinprick. Local infiltration of 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline through a 25-gauge or finer needle on a 1 to 3 ml syringe; buffering with bicarbonate (1 part to 10) reduces the burning. Inject slowly through as few punctures as possible. Maximum dose 7 mg/kg with adrenaline (to 500 mg), 4.5 mg/kg without; onset 2 minutes, duration 4 to 6 hours."],["osec","th:op-125~s2-3","The nerves of the ear","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Anaesthesia in the clinic","The great auricular nerve (C2 and C3) supplies the lower pinna and the skin over the mastoid; the lesser occipital nerve the middle of the back of the pinna; the auriculotemporal nerve (mandibular division of the trigeminal) the upper pinna, tragus and front of the canal; the auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold's nerve) the concha and back of the canal; with contributions from the facial nerve. Figure 2 The sensory supply of the auricle: auriculotemporal, great auricular and lesser occipital nerves and the auricular branch of the vagus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 125.2"],["osec","th:op-125~s2-4","The regional blocks","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Anaesthesia in the clinic","Outer ear (ring) block for the whole pinna, through two entry points at the upper and lower attachment of the pinna to the head. With a 10 ml syringe of 1% lidocaine with adrenaline and a 25-gauge needle, advance subcutaneously, aspirate, inject 2 to 3 ml as you withdraw, redirect behind the pinna without leaving the skin and inject another 2 to 3 ml; repeat from the lower point. It does not numb the canal or drum. Four-quadrant canal block for the canal and drum but not the pinna: a 27-gauge needle on a 1 to 3 ml syringe enters at the bony-cartilaginous junction in four quadrants and advances a few millimetres along the bone; slight pressure with the speculum lateral to the site pushes the anaesthetic medially. Watch the medial canal blanch and stop before a bleb forms, which narrows the canal and can split the skin. Figure 3 Regional block of the pinna and external ear from two entry points above and below the attachment of the pinna. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 125.3"],["osec","th:op-125~s3-1","Wax","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Cleaning wax and cavities","Wax is the commonest cause of conductive hearing loss in adults. The canal cleans itself by outward migration of epithelium from the drum; narrow canals, hearing aids, cotton buds and previous radiotherapy interrupt this. Do not syringe an ear with a perforation, a retracted or atrophic drum, or a history of discharge or ear surgery. Under the microscope, a well-shaped plug in a wide canal can be lifted with Hartmann or small crocodile forceps. In a narrow or occluded canal, find the interface between wax and canal wall with a small right-angled pick and follow the contour of the canal medially behind the plug. Choose the instrument by consistency: suction (5 French as a rule, 3 French near the drum, 7 French only to open a fully blocked canal and then step down to reduce noise), picks, forceps or curettes. Docusate liquid in the canal for 15 minutes softens hard wax."],["osec","th:op-125~s3-2","Mastoid cavities","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Cleaning wax and cavities","Read the previous operation note first: the state of the drum and middle ear, the lateral semicircular canal, the facial nerve and the tegmen. Remove all squamous and ceruminous debris. Moisture beneath it can be dusted with boric acid powder to prevent infection. Suction in a cavity often causes vertigo by cooling or moving the lateral canal; mop with cotton on crocodile forceps or a metal applicator instead, and use the finest sucker you can."],["osec","th:op-125~s4-1","Understand the injury and block the ear","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: draining an auricular haematoma","A shearing force (wrestling, boxing, rugby) lifts the skin and perichondrium off the cartilage and blood collects between them. Cartilage depends on the perichondrium for its blood supply, so it starts to die; fibrosis, contracture and new cartilage then produce the cauliflower ear , and a thickened concha can even close the canal. Treat early. Ask how and when it happened, and whether the patient is an athlete who will return to contact sport at once, in which case a bulky dressing and needle aspiration are poor choices. Prepare the pinna and give an outer ear block . What you should see A tense, fluctuant swelling on the front of the pinna, obliterating the normal folds. Senior's pearl Incision and drainage recurs less often than needle aspiration and deals with loculated and intracartilaginous collections. Reserve aspiration for small, fresh haematomas in non-athletes."],["osec","th:op-125~s4-2","Incise and evacuate","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: draining an auricular haematoma","Make the incision along the helical or antihelical fold , where the scar hides. Express the blood and serum by gentle digital compression. Pass a fine haemostat into the cavity to break down loculations so that every pocket drains into the main one. A small sterile rubber band or Penrose drain may be left in. What you should see The pinna's folds returning as the collection empties, and no residual fluctuant area. Stripping the perichondrium Do not peel the perichondrium further off the cartilage than the haematoma already has: every millimetre lifted is cartilage without blood supply."],["osec","th:op-125~s4-3","Bolster","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: draining an auricular haematoma","Success depends on the compression. Cut a dental roll to the size of the haematoma for the front of the pinna and a similar or slightly smaller roll for the back, with Silastic sheeting (1 mm) for support if wished. Pass a 3-0 nylon or silk suture on a Keith needle through the front roll and the pinna from lateral to medial, through the back roll and back through it, then from medial to lateral through the pinna and the front roll, and tie it as a through-and-through mattress suture . Coat the rolls and incision with antibiotic ointment. Figure 4 A Penrose drain and Silastic bolsters held with a through-and-through mattress suture after drainage of an auricular haematoma. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 125.4 What you should see The bolster pressing the skin flat against the cartilage over the whole area of the haematoma, without blanching the skin edges."],["osec","th:op-125~s4-4","Aftercare","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: draining an auricular haematoma","Oral antibiotics while the bolster is in; remove it at 5 to 7 days . A bolster allows an immediate return to contact sport with a low recurrence rate. Watch for recurrence and infection; a recurrent haematoma may need open exploration. What you should see At removal of the bolster: skin lying flat on the cartilage with the folds of the pinna visible and no fluctuance."],["osec","th:op-125~s5-1","Select and prepare the patient","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: intratympanic injection","Gentamicin is an ablative treatment for vertigo in Ménière's disease when diet and diuretics have failed. Confirm the diagnosis and exclude vestibular migraine, benign positional vertigo and superior canal dehiscence by history, examination, audiometry, vestibular testing and imaging. Discuss the non-ablative and surgical alternatives (betahistine, pressure devices, endolymphatic sac surgery, labyrinthectomy, vestibular nerve section). Do not use gentamicin in an only hearing ear, and test the other ear's vestibular function first: a weak opposite labyrinth is a contraindication. Take care in the elderly, the visually impaired and those with peripheral neuropathy, who compensate poorly; start vestibular physiotherapy before if possible. Gentamicin is chosen because it is more vestibulotoxic than cochleotoxic; it controls vertigo in about 90%. Dexamethasone is used for sudden sensorineural hearing loss , as salvage after or with oral prednisolone, or as primary treatment when systemic steroids are unsafe; oral and intratympanic steroids give similar results in trials. Order a gadolinium MRI to exclude a vestibular schwannoma, but do not delay the first injection for it. Perform pure-tone audiometry before every injection . What you should see A current audiogram and, for gentamicin, a documented normal opposite labyrinth."],["osec","th:op-125~s5-2","Anaesthetise and set up","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: intratympanic injection","Lay the patient supine with the head turned 45 degrees away , the treated ear up. Apply phenol to two small spots on the drum, anterosuperior and posteroinferior . Draw up 0.6 ml in a tuberculin syringe with a 1.5-inch 25-gauge needle (a full syringe puts the plunger out of reach of your thumb) and bend the needle slightly so that it does not block your view down the speculum. Figure 5 Intratympanic injection. Left: the head turned 45 degrees away from the treated ear, held for 25 minutes. Right: a slight bend in the needle keeps the drum in view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 125.5 and 125.6 What you should see Two white phenol spots on the drum and a bent needle that leaves the drum in view. The posterosuperior quadrant The injection sites avoid the ossicles; never inject through the posterosuperior quadrant."],["osec","th:op-125~s5-3","Inject","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: intratympanic injection","Make a puncture in the anterosuperior drum first: it lets air escape from the middle ear during the injection. Then, through the posteroinferior puncture, aimed toward the round window niche, inject slowly, watching the air-fluid level rise behind the drum up to the anterosuperior vent; about 0.4 ml fills the middle ear. Stop when a little fluid wells into the canal. Gentamicin is buffered with bicarbonate to about 30 mg/ml; dexamethasone is 10 to 24 mg/ml (methylprednisolone hurts more). What you should see A rising fluid level reaching the anterosuperior puncture, and a little efflux into the canal. Senior's pearl The round window is the gate to the inner ear. A mucosal fold or false membrane over the niche blocks absorption, which is one reason injections sometimes fail."],["osec","th:op-125~s5-4","Keep the drug in the ear","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: intratympanic injection","The patient stays supine with the head turned for 25 minutes , spitting saliva into a basin rather than swallowing, which would open the Eustachian tube and drain the drug away. What you should see A fluid level still visible behind the drum at the end of the 25 minutes."],["osec","th:op-125~s5-5","Aftercare","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · The operation: intratympanic injection","Keep the ear dry. Gentamicin: review at 4 weeks; repeat the injection if vertigo persists; audiometry before each injection and 2 months after the last; continue diet and diuretics. Dexamethasone: review weekly with audiometry and repeat weekly for three weeks, or until hearing returns to baseline or plateaus. The punctures usually close within weeks. What you should see At review: closed punctures, a current audiogram and, after gentamicin, no further vertigo spells."],["osec","th:op-125~s6-1","Draining an abscess of the ear","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Other office procedures","A furuncle may settle with warm compresses and expression, but any acute, painful fluctuant swelling needs drainage. Early infection without a collection may respond to high-dose antibiotics with close review. Give antibiotic cover to the immunocompromised, diabetic, alcoholic, and those on chemotherapy or steroids. Plan incisions so they do not hinder later reconstruction when cartilage is likely to be lost. Infiltrate lateral to a furuncle and open it with a myringotomy knife in the canal or a size 11 blade on the pinna; for a larger auricular abscess, use a regional block, incise with a size 15 blade along the helical or antihelical fold, send pus for culture, break down every loculation, debride necrotic cartilage (staged debridement keeps the rim deformity small; extensive necrosis is better handled in theatre), irrigate with saline and leave the wound open, with a drain if needed. Auricular cartilage is relatively avascular, so systemic antibiotics reach it poorly. Warn that an auricular abscess can take weeks to clear and may recur; redrain at once if it does, and refer for rim reconstruction if a notch results."],["osec","th:op-125~s6-2","Biopsy of a canal mass","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Other office procedures","Masses in the canal may be benign (osteoma, exostosis, keratosis obturans, cholesteatoma, polyp, neurofibroma, paraganglioma or schwannoma, fibrous dysplasia, eosinophilic granuloma, temporomandibular joint herniation, vascular lesions) or malignant (squamous and basal cell carcinoma, ceruminous gland tumours, adenoid cystic carcinoma, melanoma, sarcoma, metastases). Osteomas and exostoses are diagnosed by their appearance and gentle palpation. A recently painful, discharging lesion should first have antibiotic-steroid drops. A polyp usually arises from a perforation or a cholesteatoma; but about 13% of facial nerve schwannomas present as a canal mass , so if a polyp arises from the posterior canal wall or its origin cannot be seen, obtain a CT before removing it. In a diabetic patient, granulation tissue at the bony-cartilaginous junction suggests necrotising otitis externa , but biopsy it to exclude carcinoma. A pulsatile mass (glomus tumour) must not be biopsied in the clinic. Have specula, a 5 French sucker, fine cup forceps, Bellucci scissors, 1:10,000 adrenaline or silver nitrate, a specimen pot and antibiotic ointment ready. Use antibiotic-steroid drops afterwards and review in a week with the histology."],["osec","th:op-125~s6-3","Myringotomy and tube under local anaesthetic","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · Other office procedures","Most adults and many older children tolerate a tube in the clinic. Examine with the microscope and pneumatic otoscopy, confirm the conductive loss with tuning forks and an audiogram, and examine the nasopharynx in any adult with a unilateral effusion . Exclude an aberrant carotid, dehiscent jugular bulb or glomus tumour, which can look like fluid. Use lower magnification for easier handling; apply phenol to the site and make a radial myringotomy anteroinferiorly or posteroinferiorly , avoiding the posterosuperior quadrant (an overhanging anterior wall can make the anterosuperior quadrant hard to reach). Grasp a bobbin tube by its lateral flange, seat part of the medial flange in the incision and tease the rest through with a fine pick; a stepped insertion puts less tension on the drum and hurts less. Small tubes suit short-term use, mid-sized tubes chronic effusion and larger lumens mucoid effusions; a T-tube may need sedation. A myringotomy alone closes in days, so it does not treat chronic effusion. Serous effusions need no drops; bleeding, infection or thick mucus warrant antibiotic drops with or without steroid for 5 to 7 days. Review at a month, then six-monthly until the tube extrudes; much more closely after a complicated acute otitis media."],["ev","th:op-125~s7-1","Office-Based Procedures in Otology: current evidence","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · When it goes wrong","Wax and clinic otology. The American guideline supports microsuction, irrigation or manual removal for symptomatic wax only, and advises against cotton buds and ear candling (Schwartz et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017); NICE (NG98) recommends that wax removal be available in primary or community care. Office procedures. Intratympanic steroid or gentamicin injection, adult grommet insertion and biopsy under local anaesthesia are well tolerated in clinic and avoid general anaesthesia, with outcomes similar to theatre in published series. Microsuction can cause transient vertigo and, rarely, noise-induced threshold shift; warn the patient and use the smallest suction needed."],["osec","th:op-125~s7-1","Crisis drills","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · When it goes wrong","Vertigo during cavity cleaning 1 Stop suctioning the lateral canal is being cooled or moved 2 Settle lie the patient flat, reassure, wait for it to pass 3 Change technique mop with cotton the smallest sucker consider a fistula if vertigo follows pressure The patient faints in the chair 1 Recognise vasovagal reaction, often from Arnold's nerve or pain 2 Act lie flat, legs raised, airway and pulse 3 Next time lie them down from the start better anaesthesia consider theatre Brisk bleeding from a canal biopsy 1 Pressure pledget with 1 10,000 adrenaline silver nitrate 2 Reassess was the mass pulsatile? suspect a paraganglioma or vascular anomaly 3 Escalate pack the canal, imaging, senior review"],["osec","th:op-125~s8-1","Where the books differ","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra treats auricular haematoma by aspiration under strict asepsis with a pressure dressing that packs every concavity, repeated if needed, and incision with dental-roll bolsters tied by through-and-through sutures when aspiration fails, with antibiotics for all. For wax it describes syringing with water at body temperature (too hot or cold causes caloric vertigo), instrument removal with a hook or Jobson-Horne probe passed beyond the plug, and softening drops (sodium bicarbonate in glycerine and water, hydrogen peroxide, oil). Lee gives intratympanic gentamicin as a titrated, low-dose ablation and intratympanic steroid as salvage for sudden hearing loss. Myers prefers incision and drainage over aspiration for haematoma, adds the regional nerve blocks in detail, and warns about the facial nerve schwannoma presenting as a canal polyp."],["osec","th:op-125~s8-2","Viva essentials","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Phenol on the drum: why it is preferred and why the drum must be dry. The nerve supply of the pinna and the two regional blocks; maximum lidocaine doses. Auricular haematoma: why it must be drained (perichondrial blood supply), and the bolster. Intratympanic gentamicin: indications, contraindications (only hearing ear, weak opposite labyrinth), outcomes (about 90% vertigo control, 17% to 30% hearing loss). Why the patient lies still and does not swallow. The canal polyp that may be a facial nerve schwannoma, and the pulsatile mass not to biopsy."],["osec","th:op-125~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Office-Based Procedures in Otology · For the examination","Common pitfalls Syringing an ear with a perforation or a cavity. Needle aspiration of a haematoma with no compression, in an athlete going straight back to the mat. A bleb from the canal block that splits the canal skin. Injecting gentamicin without checking the opposite labyrinth, or into an only hearing ear. Removing a posterior-wall polyp without imaging. Biopsying a pulsatile mass in the clinic."],["osec","th:op-126~s1-1","The case at a glance","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","Removal of a foreign body from the ear canal Summary Indications Any foreign body in the canal, presenting with pain, discharge, hearing loss or found on examination; a foreign object should not be left in the canal Urgency Disc batteries: remove at once. Live insects: urgent. Vegetable matter: soon, before it swells. Inert objects: elective, so the right instruments, light and anaesthesia can be arranged Anaesthesia None for most in the clinic; local canal infiltration for a painful canal; sedation or general anaesthesia for young children, anxious or uncooperative adults, a severely inflamed canal, an object wedged medially, or after failed attempts Position Seated or supine with the head stabilised on a headrest; insect cases supine with the affected ear up; avoid a papoose if possible Antibiotics Not needed for an atraumatic removal; topical drops for a traumatised or infected canal; systemic antibiotics for cellulitis or a diabetic patient Tray Binocular microscope, specula 3 to 6 mm, suction 3 to 12 French (a 10 or 12 French nasal sucker for round objects), small crocodile and Hartmann forceps, right-angled hook, wax curette and loop, cotton-tipped applicators, mineral oil, alcohol and lidocaine for insects, a strong magnet for batteries, lidocaine with adrenaline, silver nitrate, ear wick, irrigation kit (rarely used)"],["osec","th:op-126~s1-2","Consent","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","What to tell the patient or parent Most objects come out in the clinic in a few minutes; some need a short general anaesthetic, especially in young children. Bleeding and a graze of the canal skin , which can become infected. A hole in the eardrum or, rarely, injury to the hearing bones, with hearing loss or dizziness, if the object is deep or the patient moves. Narrowing of the canal later, after a severe injury. Rarely, a small cut at the entrance of the ear or behind the ear to reach an object that is wedged deep."],["osec","th:op-126~s1-3","Before you start","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","Ask when it happened and whether anyone saw it; what was within reach (beads, toys, food, batteries in the house, hearing aids); how long it has been there; pain, discharge, smell, bleeding and hearing loss; and, crucially, how many attempts have already been made and by whom . A history of previous foreign bodies, infection or discharge suggests a canal that may be stenosed, friable and hypervascular, or an object that has been there for weeks. Ask about diabetes, bleeding tendency and anticoagulants, and previous ear surgery: in a canal-wall-down cavity or a congenital anomaly the facial nerve, the lateral semicircular canal and the windows may be exposed. Examine both ears with the microscope, and the nose (a child who puts something in one orifice may have put something in another). Listen to the breathing and the chest: if there is any suggestion of an inhaled object, flexible laryngoscopy and a chest radiograph come first. Imaging is not routine: CT if the object's medial extent is in doubt or the ear has had extensive surgery or disease, MRI if an encephalocele or CSF leak is suspected. Obtain an audiogram before any removal under sedation or general anaesthesia, and an audiogram and CT if vertigo or hearing loss suggests the drum or inner ear has been breached. Who should go straight to the ENT clinic Spherical or sharp objects, disc batteries, vegetable matter, objects next to the drum, objects present for more than 24 hours, children under four, a hidden object or an agitated child, and any ear where removal has already been tried."],["osec","th:op-126~s2-1","The canal","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Anatomy you must see","The adult canal is about 24 mm long. Its lateral third is cartilaginous , hair-bearing and mobile, lined by thick skin; its medial two thirds are bony , lined by thin skin directly on periosteum, which bleeds and hurts at the lightest touch. The canal narrows at the bony-cartilaginous junction and again at the isthmus in the bony part; a round object pushed past the isthmus is trapped. Medially the floor dips into the anteroinferior recess between the drum and the anterior canal wall, where small objects hide. In a child the bony canal is short and the drum lies more horizontally."],["osec","th:op-126~s2-2","The nerves you will provoke","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Anatomy you must see","The canal and concha are supplied by the auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold's nerve), which can trigger coughing, vomiting or a vasovagal faint, and by the auriculotemporal nerve and the cervical plexus. Facial grimacing moves the cartilaginous canal, so a calm patient matters."],["osec","th:op-126~s2-3","The drum and beyond","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Anatomy you must see","Keep the drum, the handle of the malleus and the posterosuperior quadrant in view. In an operated ear, know where the facial nerve and lateral canal lie before you put an instrument in the cavity."],["osec","th:op-126~s3-1","Prepare the patient and the room","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Plan before you touch: the right speculum sizes, the suction, a hook and forceps on the tray, the microscope set up, and an assistant to steady a child's head. Explain each step and the noise of the suction. A calm, still patient is the single most important factor; a child who has already been hurt may need a general anaesthetic, and it is better to decide that now than after another failed attempt. What you should see The object itself, its shape, consistency and how far in it lies, and whether any canal wall or drum is visible beyond it. Senior's pearl You may have only one chance with a child. If you cannot see past the object, or the child will not keep still, stop and book a short general anaesthetic."],["osec","th:op-126~s3-2","Try suction first for smooth, round objects","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Beads and other hard, smooth objects cannot be grasped: forceps simply push them further in. Choose a large suction (10 or 12 French) , bring the tip into contact with the object to make a seal, and draw it out along the canal. Soft, wet material behaves like wax and can be aspirated with a 5 French sucker. Figure 1 A large-bore sucker sealed against the object draws most objects out; take care not to push the object further in. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 126.1 What you should see The object coming out on the sucker tip without the canal wall bleeding. Pushing it deeper Approaching a round object directly from the front pushes it past the isthmus onto the drum. If suction will not seal, change method rather than pushing harder."],["osec","th:op-126~s3-3","Get behind it with a right-angled hook","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Pass a right-angled hook flat along the canal wall, parallel to the object and through the gap between object and wall, past its medial side. Rotate the tip 90 degrees so it lies behind the object and draw it laterally. Pass along the superior or anterior wall where there is room; instruments slid medially along the posterior wall can strike the drum. Figure 2 A right-angled hook passed beyond the object and turned behind it, then drawn laterally. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 126.2 What you should see The tip of the hook beyond the object before you turn it, and the object rolling outward. Senior's pearl Irregular, soft objects (paper, tissue, foam, a cotton bud tip) are the ones for the crocodile forceps. Hard, smooth ones are for the hook and the sucker."],["osec","th:op-126~s3-4","Clear and inspect","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","After removal, look again at the whole canal and the drum. Remove fragments and wax, check the canal skin for lacerations and replace any flap of lacerated skin so that epithelium is not trapped to form a canal cholesteatoma. Check the drum for perforation. Remove any second object. What you should see An intact drum with a visible handle of malleus and a canal without trapped skin flaps. Missed injury Failing to reposition a lacerated canal skin flap can trap epithelium and produce a canal cholesteatoma months later."],["osec","th:op-126~s4-1","Disc batteries","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Special objects","Remove a battery at once . Moisture lets current flow and causes liquefactive necrosis of the canal and drum within hours. Do not irrigate , and avoid lacerating the canal: water and blood both let current flow. Combine forceps and a right-angled hook; most disc batteries are magnetic, so a strong magnet may lift it out atraumatically. Inspect carefully afterwards for burns and arrange early review."],["osec","th:op-126~s4-2","Live insects","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Special objects","A live insect causes terrible noise and pain, and trying to grasp it makes both worse and risks the drum. Lay the patient supine with the ear up and drown or paralyse it first by filling the canal with mineral oil, alcohol (ether in older practice), or topical lidocaine or tetracaine; expect a brief flurry of activity as it drowns. Infiltrate the canal with local anaesthetic if the pain is severe. Then remove the insect and the fluid with suction, pick out wings and legs with forceps or a fine cotton applicator, and inspect the canal and drum. Irrigation is acceptable once the insect is dead and the drum is known to be intact."],["osec","th:op-126~s4-3","Vegetable matter","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Special objects","Peas, beans, nuts, seeds and corn swell when they absorb water or canal secretions, becoming tightly impacted and painful. Remove them dry: suction if intact, or a right-angled hook passed medial to the object to roll it out. Avoid irrigation , which makes them swell and disintegrate. If the material is soft and crumbling and cannot be suctioned, gentle irrigation may be needed after all."],["osec","th:op-126~s4-4","Irrigation, when it is right","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Special objects","Irrigation rarely flushes a foreign body out and often macerates the skin; reserve it for soft, friable material and dead insects in an ear with an intact drum. Use warm water (water at body temperature avoids caloric vertigo), half-strength hydrogen peroxide, a proper irrigator or a wide cannula on a syringe, and direct the jet along the canal wall, not at the drum. Never irrigate an ear with a perforation, an atrophic or retracted drum, a battery or vegetable matter."],["osec","th:op-126~s5-1","Widening the meatus","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · When it is wedged","A wedged object that completely fills the canal leaves no room for an instrument. After local infiltration, often best done in theatre, a relaxing incision at the incisura (the 12 o'clock position between the tragus and the helix) widens the meatus enough to pass a hook medial to the object. Figure 3 An incision at the incisura (12 o'clock) widens the meatus so that an instrument can pass medial to the object. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 126.3"],["osec","th:op-126~s5-2","The postauricular approach","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · When it is wedged","Rarely, an object impacted at or beyond the bony isthmus, or pushed into the middle ear, needs a postauricular incision under sedation or general anaesthesia. Elevate the canal skin, reflect the ear forwards, and, if needed, drill away a prominent spine of Henle or the tympanomastoid suture to recontour the canal and deliver the object from behind. Figure 4 The ear reflected forwards through a postauricular incision to expose an object lodged in the medial canal. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 126.4"],["osec","th:op-126~s6-1","Crisis drills","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · When it goes wrong","The object goes deeper 1 Stop do not push again 2 Reassess can you see beyond it? is the drum intact? is the patient still? 3 Escalate microscope and general anaesthetic meatal or postauricular access if wedged at the isthmus Vertigo, hearing loss or blood from behind the drum 1 Suspect perforation, ossicular dislocation, stapes fracture or perilymph fistula 2 Act stop instrumentation audiogram and CT early senior review avoid irrigation 3 Plan exploratory tympanotomy for a suspected fistula or ossicular injury Faint during removal 1 Recognise vagal stimulation from Arnold's nerve 2 Act stop, lay flat, raise the legs recover before trying again consider infiltration or a general anaesthetic"],["osec","th:op-126~s7-1","After removal","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","An atraumatic removal from a healthy canal needs no treatment. If the canal was macerated, infected or lacerated, give antibiotic-steroid drops twice daily; if it is oedematous, place an ear wick and remove it after about three days (a week for a severe laceration), continuing the drops for a few more days. Keep the ear dry for one to two weeks (cotton wool with petroleum jelly in the shower). Review within a week any patient with infection, a difficult removal or a complication, and early for any drum or middle ear injury."],["ev","th:op-126~s7-2","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal: current evidence","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","First attempt matters. In a meta-analysis of 13 studies (3891 children), removal by ENT surgeons without a previous attempt succeeded in about 93%, compared with 64% in emergency departments; after a failed attempt elsewhere, ENT success fell to 64% (White et al., Ann Otol Rhinol Laryngol 2023). Refer early if the object is round, deep, or the child is uncooperative, and avoid repeated attempts. Button batteries in the ear cause rapid tissue necrosis and need same-day removal; never irrigate them, or vegetable matter, which swells."],["osec","th:op-126~s7-2","Complications","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","Complications Complication Recognition Management Canal laceration and bleeding Blood in the canal, a raised skin flap Replace the flap, topical adrenaline or silver nitrate, drops Otitis externa and cellulitis Pain, swelling, discharge, spread to the concha Drops and wick; oral antibiotics (ciprofloxacin covers Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas) if cellulitis or diabetes Drum perforation Seen on inspection; conductive loss Keep dry; most heal; myringoplasty if persistent Ossicular injury, perilymph fistula Conductive or sensorineural loss, vertigo Audiogram, CT, exploration Canal stenosis Late narrowing after severe trauma or infection Stenting early; canalplasty if established Canal cholesteatoma Late keratin collection from trapped skin Removal; prevent by replacing flaps Battery burn Necrosis of canal or drum Early review, debridement, repair later"],["osec","th:op-126~s8-1","Where the books differ","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra lists forceps, syringing, suction, microscopic instruments and the postaural approach; recommends syringing for most seed grains and smooth objects, warns that smooth hard objects such as ball bearings should not be grasped with forceps, and advises general anaesthesia and the microscope for impacted objects or after earlier attempts. For live insects it advises killing the insect with oil before removal. Lee emphasises that foreign bodies in children are often multiple and that batteries are an emergency. Myers argues against irrigation for most objects (it rarely flushes them out and macerates the canal, and makes vegetable matter swell), favouring large-bore suction and the right-angled hook, and adds the magnet for batteries and the incisural incision."],["osec","th:op-126~s8-2","Viva essentials","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Choose by the object: soft and irregular, forceps; hard and round, suction or a hook behind it . The disc battery : remove now, no irrigation, no bleeding, try a magnet. The insect : drown it first. Vegetable matter : no water. Ease of removal falls with every failed attempt; know when to stop and book a general anaesthetic. Anatomy: the isthmus and the anteroinferior recess; Arnold's nerve."],["osec","th:op-126~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Foreign Bodies of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","Common pitfalls Grasping a bead with forceps and pushing it onto the drum. Irrigating a battery or a pea. Grabbing a live insect. Instruments slid along the posterior canal wall onto the drum. Leaving a lacerated skin flap displaced. Repeated attempts on a frightened child."],["osec","th:op-127~s1-1","The case at a glance","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","Canalplasty for exostoses and osteoma Summary Indications Complete or near-complete occlusion with trapped debris that cannot be cleaned in clinic; conductive loss from occlusion or impaction; recurrent otitis externa despite medical treatment; frequent impactions needing repeated visits Contraindications Active otitis externa (treat first); unfitness for general anaesthesia (a narrow-pedicled osteoma can be removed under local) Anaesthesia General for exostoses and the postauricular route; local, with or without sedation, for a lateral, narrow-pedicled osteoma Position Supine, head on a ring turned away; protect the other ear Antibiotics None unless the canal is infected (then an antipseudomonal agent) Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring strongly advised Tray Microscope, ear set, high-speed drill with 3, 2 and 1 mm coarse and fine diamond burrs, 1 mm osteotome, suction irrigation, Silastic sheet or foil from a suture pack, Gelfoam, rosebud packing"],["osec","th:op-127~s1-2","Consent","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","What to tell the patient Hearing: a small risk of conductive loss (drum or ossicle injury) and of sensorineural loss from drill noise or contact with the malleus. Perforation of the eardrum, usually small and repaired at the time. Narrowing of the canal as it heals, which may need further treatment. Facial weakness, rare. Jaw pain if the joint capsule is breached. Recurrence of exostoses with continued cold-water exposure: wear earplugs."],["osec","th:op-127~s1-3","Before you start","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","Ask about wax impaction, otitis externa and hearing, how often the patient needs clinic cleaning, and whether they surf, swim or kayak in cold water. Examine both ears under the microscope and estimate how much of the canal's cross-section is blocked; an endoscope can show the medial canal. Record an audiogram with tympanometry and reflexes: a pre-existing high-frequency sensorineural loss must be documented before drilling. Obtain a high-resolution temporal bone CT for an osteoma that hides the medial canal, or for near-complete exostoses, to look for cholesteatoma trapped behind the obstruction, to define the pedicle and to see how close bone lies to the drum. Treat active otitis externa with drops before the operation, and stop anticoagulants if safe. Figure 1 Broad-based medial exostoses (left) and a posteriorly based osteoma hiding the medial canal (right). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 127.1 and 127.3 Figure 2 CT confirms a pedunculated attachment to the lateral posterior canal wall, with a normal medial canal and middle ear. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 127.4"],["osec","th:op-127~s2-1","Telling them apart","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · Know the disease","Exostoses and osteoma compared Exostoses Osteoma Number and side Multiple, usually bilateral Single, unilateral Site Medial bony canal, near the drum Lateral bony canal, at the tympanosquamous or tympanomastoid suture Base Broad, sessile, anterior and posterior Narrow pedicle Nature Reactive lamellar bone after cold-water exposure True benign neoplasm of bone Typical patient Surfer, swimmer, kayaker, male Any adult Usual surgery Postauricular drill or osteotome canalplasty Transcanal removal with a curette or osteotome"],["osec","th:op-127~s2-2","When to operate","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · Know the disease","Most lesions are found incidentally. Under about 80% occlusion, regular cleaning and earplugs in cold water are usually enough. Operate for symptoms, for impractical repeated cleaning, or for complete occlusion, which risks cholesteatoma behind the bone."],["osec","th:op-127~s3-1","Prepare and choose the approach","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Place the facial nerve electrodes, prepare and drape as for any ear operation, and inject all four quadrants of the canal with lidocaine and adrenaline. Choose the route: transcanal or endaural for a single lateral osteoma; postauricular for extensive medial exostoses, where a transcanal view is too restricted. What you should see Blanched canal skin after the four-quadrant injection, and a working facial nerve monitor."],["osec","th:op-127~s3-2","Raise the canal flap and open behind the ear","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Make a vertical incision in the posterior lateral bony canal with releasing cuts at 6 and 12 o'clock to create a laterally based (Koerner's) flap. Make the postauricular incision, join the two, retract the pinna forward with tape and place self-retaining retractors. Figure 3 The laterally based Koerner's flap is cut through the meatus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 127.5 Figure 4 The postauricular incision joined to the canal incision, with the pinna retracted forward. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 127.6 What you should see The full length of the bony canal from behind, with the exostoses in view."],["osec","th:op-127~s3-3","Preserve the skin","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","For each exostosis make a circumferential incision lateral to it with vertical releasing cuts, and elevate a medially based skin flap over the bone towards the drum. Fold the flaps over the drum and protect them, and the drum, with Silastic sheeting or foil from a suture pack. Figure 5 The posterior canal skin is elevated medially and protected under foil before drilling. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 127.7 What you should see Intact medially based flaps tucked safely over the drum, and bare bone of each exostosis exposed. Senior's pearl Skin is the operation. Every millimetre of canal skin you keep is a millimetre of canal that will not scar down. Elevate patiently and protect the flaps before the drill comes near."],["osec","th:op-127~s3-4","Remove the bone","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Drill from lateral to medial with coarse diamond burrs of decreasing size (3, 2, 1 mm) and copious irrigation, then polish with a fine diamond. Near the annulus, thin the bone and lift off the last lip with a 1 mm osteotome, or undermine it and fracture it gently away from the drum. Aim for a smooth, straight canal in which the whole annulus can be seen from the meatus. What you should see A widened, smooth canal with the anterior annulus visible and no remaining medial lip of bone. Five structures in the way The drum and annulus: the drill can tear the drum and the annulus can be avulsed. The malleus: touching the short process with the drill conducts noise to the cochlea and can disrupt the chain. The attic: drilling too far up the scutum opens the epitympanum. The jaw joint: anteriorly, halfway along the bony canal; stop if you see glenoid periosteum. The facial nerve: in about 70% of people its vertical segment lies lateral to the annulus in the posteroinferior canal, 2 to 5 mm away, with no surface landmark."],["osec","th:op-127~s3-5","Replace the skin and pack","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Lay the flaps back in position. Cover bare areas with a thin free skin graft from behind the ear if needed. Pack with Gelfoam soaked in antibiotic drops if no grafts were used; if grafts were used, place medial and lateral rosebud packs to hold them against the bone. Figure 6 Bare areas are grafted, and double rosebud packing holds the skin in place. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 127.8 and 127.9 What you should see Skin lining the whole canal, held flat against the bone, with the drum intact."],["osec","th:op-127~s3-6","Osteoma by the transcanal route","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","A narrow-pedicled osteoma can often be removed through the meatus with a curette or osteotome, sometimes after a small incision in the incisura to widen the meatus. For a broad-based osteoma, raise and preserve a skin flap as for exostoses and drill the base flush with the surrounding canal. A small bare area heals by secondary intention. Figure 7 An incision in the incisura widens the meatus for transcanal removal. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 127.10 Figure 8 A broad-based osteoma: skin flap, flush drilling of the base, and replacement of the skin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 127.11 What you should see The osteoma removed whole with its pedicle, and smooth bone where it was attached."],["osec","th:op-127~s4-1","Crisis drills","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · When it goes wrong","Drum torn during drilling 1 Small tear support medially with Gelfoam it usually heals 2 Large tear repair with fascia or perichondrium at the same operation Glenoid periosteum or jaw joint exposed 1 Stop drilling there leave a thin layer of bone do not chase exostosis further forward 2 After warn of jaw pain soft diet Facial monitor fires in the posteroinferior canal 1 Stop irrigate reassess the depth 2 Proceed change to a smaller diamond burr thin rather than remove the last bone there"],["osec","th:op-127~s5-1","On the ward and in clinic","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","Start antibiotic drops twice daily. Keep the postauricular wound dry for a week, then protect the canal with Vaseline-coated cotton wool when washing. Remove packs at 7 to 10 days, or at 14 days if free grafts were used. Treat granulations with silver nitrate and hypertrophic scar with steroid injection, and keep a stent in the canal with weekly review until it is fully epithelialised. Repeat the audiogram after healing. Return to water sports only once healed, and always with earplugs."],["ev","th:op-127~s5-2","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal: current evidence","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","Drill or osteotome. A systematic review and meta-analysis of 15 studies (1,788 ears) found drum perforation in 5.3% with the osteotome and 3.8% with the drill, but sensorineural loss in 0.7% against 4.3% and canal stenosis in 1.1% against 4.1%: the osteotome may risk more perforations, the drill more inner ear injury and stenosis (Swisher et al., Ann Otol Rhinol Laryngol 2023). Endoscopic transcanal osteotome removal is an increasingly used, less invasive option (Kozin et al., Am J Otolaryngol 2015). Osteoma pedicle. In a meta-analysis of 63 osteomas, drilling the stalk site after removal did not reduce recurrence (about 9% with drilling against 5% without; overall about 7%); avoid it unless a definite pedicle still projects into the canal (Argaman et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2024). Who gets them. Exostoses relate to cumulative cold-water exposure: in 207 English surfers, 53% had exostoses, with severity rising with years surfed (Alexander et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2015)."],["osec","th:op-127~s5-2","Complications","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","Incomplete removal and early recurrence; soft tissue stenosis; drum perforation; conductive loss from ossicular injury; sensorineural loss from drill noise or malleus contact; a mastoid fistula from drilling too deep posteriorly; jaw pain; facial palsy."],["osec","th:op-127~s6-1","Where the books differ","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers postauricular drill canalplasty with medially based skin flaps for exostoses; transcanal curette or osteotome removal for pedicled osteomas; facial nerve monitoring strongly advised. Dhingra and K. J. Lee describe the clinical difference between exostoses and osteoma and advise surgery only for obstruction, infection or hearing loss. The literature the osteotome trades a slightly higher perforation rate for less sensorineural loss and stenosis than the drill."],["osec","th:op-127~s6-2","Viva essentials","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Exostosis versus osteoma: number, site, base, cause. Indications for surgery and why complete occlusion is dangerous (trapped cholesteatoma). The five structures at risk , especially the facial nerve lateral to the annulus. Skin preservation with medially based flaps. Drill or osteotome: the trade-off in complications."],["osec","th:op-127~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Osteomas and Exostoses of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","Common pitfalls Attempting extensive medial exostoses through the canal. Drilling without protecting the drum and skin flaps. Touching the short process of the malleus with the burr. Drilling anteriorly into the jaw joint. Letting a patient return to cold water without earplugs."],["osec","th:op-129~s1-1","The case at a glance","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Operating note","Myringotomy and ventilation tube Summary Indications Bilateral otitis media with effusion for 3 months or more with documented hearing loss (children 6 months to 12 years); recurrent acute otitis media with effusion present; acute otitis media failing antibiotics; acute otitis media with a complication (mastoiditis, meningitis, intracranial abscess, sigmoid sinus thrombosis, facial palsy); otitis media with effusion in children at risk (sensory, physical, cognitive or behavioural problems); retraction and non-adherent atelectasis; eosinophilic otitis media; barotrauma and hyperbaric oxygen therapy Contraindications Clear fluid at myringotomy (possible CSF: do not insert a tube); a suspected high jugular bulb or aberrant carotid artery (stop and image); unfitness for anaesthesia Anaesthesia General by mask or laryngeal mask in children and anxious adults; in adults topical phenol or trichloroacetic acid on the drum, local injection of the canal (1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline) or sedation Position Supine, head turned away from the operated ear; semi-recumbent or sitting in the office Antibiotics Not recommended; clean-contaminated Tray Operating microscope or 0-degree endoscope (4 mm or 2.7 mm), specula of several sizes, wax loops, curettes and cup forceps, myringotomy knife, serrated crocodile (alligator) forceps, Rosen needle or curved pick and right-angled hook, 3, 5 and 7 French suction, the chosen tubes, cotton pledgets with 1:1000 adrenaline, antibiotic-steroid drops; Alden-Senturia trap or tuberculin syringe with an 18-gauge needle for culture Time 5 to 10 min"],["osec","th:op-129~s1-2","Consent","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Operating note","What to tell the parents Why: to let air into the middle ear and drain the fluid, so that hearing returns and infections are fewer. Tubes fall out on their own, usually within 6 to 12 months for short-term tubes. Discharge (otorrhoea): in more than 1 in 5 children at some point; treated with drops. Blockage of the tube (about 7%), granulation tissue (about 4%) and early extrusion (about 4%). A hole left in the drum after the tube falls out (1% to 6%); most close, a few need repair. The tube falling inwards into the middle ear (about 0.5%), rarely needing an operation to retrieve it. Scarring of the drum (myringosclerosis) and thinning, usually harmless. Need for further tubes if the fluid returns once they extrude. The risks of a general anaesthetic."],["osec","th:op-129~s1-3","Before you start","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Operating note","Take a focused history: fevers, otalgia, discharge, ear tugging and sleepless nights in a small child; hearing, speech, school performance and balance; how long effusion has been documented and how many acute episodes there have been. Note previous treatment (antibiotics, nasal steroids, antihistamines, decongestants) and conditions that make otitis media more likely or more serious: adenoid hypertrophy, rhinosinusitis, reflux, cleft palate and craniofacial syndromes, Down syndrome, ciliary dysfunction, smoke exposure, a family history of tubes, and previous tubes or ear surgery. Examine both drums with pneumatic otoscopy or the microscope, the gold standard: opacity, bulging, fluid levels, retraction pockets and their depth, atelectasis, cholesteatoma and mobility. A flexible or rigid nasendoscopy shows the adenoid and the Eustachian tube orifice in older children and adults. A tympanogram (type B or C) and an age-appropriate hearing test document the indication. CT is needed only when cholesteatoma, granulation, acute mastoiditis, a tumour, a tegmen defect or encephalocele, or a vascular anomaly is suspected. Definitions the guideline uses Recurrent acute otitis media: three episodes in 6 months, or four in 12 months with one in the last 6. Persistent acute otitis media: failure to resolve on appropriate antibiotics, or two episodes within a month. Chronic otitis media with effusion: fluid for more than 3 months."],["osec","th:op-129~s1-4","Choosing the tube","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Operating note","Short-term tubes are grommet-shaped, either an hourglass (Shepard type) or with bevelled inner flanges (Armstrong type), which stays longer; they are retained 6 to 12 months. Long-term tubes are T-tubes with long inner flanges, retained more than 12 months, for children with early extrusion or a long need for ventilation, such as cleft palate; the flanges can be trimmed. Plastic and silicone are standard; metal and hydroxyapatite tubes are still used. Figure 1 Ventilation tubes. Left: a short-term bevelled grommet. Right: a long-term modified T-tube. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 129.3"],["osec","th:op-129~s2-1","The drum through the microscope","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Anatomy you must see","Find the handle of the malleus , running down and back from the lateral process to the umbo at the centre of the drum. Draw an imaginary line along the handle and a second line at right angles through the umbo: these divide the pars tensa into four quadrants. The pars flaccida lies above the lateral process. The light reflex fans anteroinferiorly from the umbo. The annulus is the thickened rim of the pars tensa, seated in the tympanic sulcus. Figure 2 A right tympanic membrane through the endoscope. The posterosuperior quadrant (X), behind which lie the incus, stapes and chorda tympani, is avoided. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 129.4"],["osec","th:op-129~s2-2","What lies behind each quadrant","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Anatomy you must see","Posterosuperior: the long process of the incus, the stapes and its footplate in the oval window, and the chorda tympani crossing medial to the drum. This quadrant is never incised. Posteroinferior: the round window niche and, in some, a high jugular bulb . Anteroinferior: the hypotympanum and the Eustachian tube orifice anteriorly; the usual site for a tube, and the least prone to early extrusion in many hands. Anterosuperior: the tensor tympani and the protympanum; a safe site but more awkward through a narrow canal. Anterior wall of the middle ear: in rare cases a dehiscent or aberrant internal carotid artery ; a pulsatile red mass behind the drum is a reason to stop."],["osec","th:op-129~s2-3","The Eustachian tube","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Anatomy you must see","The tube runs from the protympanum down, forward and medially to the torus tubarius in the nasopharynx, posterior and slightly below the back of the inferior turbinate. Its lateral third is bony, its medial two thirds cartilaginous; the narrowest point, the isthmus , is the bony-cartilaginous junction. The middle 8 to 12 mm of the cartilaginous part acts as a valve, closed at rest and opened by the tensor veli palatini on swallowing and yawning; in infants it is shorter and more horizontal, which is why they get otitis media."],["osec","th:op-129~s3-1","See the drum and clean the canal","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Choose the largest speculum the canal will take, and the microscope or a 0-degree endoscope. Remove wax and squamous debris with a loop, curette or fine suction, touching the canal wall as little as possible: the thin medial canal skin bleeds at a touch, and blood obscures the drum and blocks the tube. If you plan a culture, sterilise the canal first with alcohol or povidone-iodine irrigation. What you should see The whole pars tensa with the annulus, the handle of the malleus and the umbo. The colour of the drum and of the fluid behind it, and any retraction pocket. The canal wall A haematoma raised by a speculum pushed too deep or a loop scraping the canal ruins the view and starts the bleeding that blocks tubes. Senior's pearl Look at the pars flaccida and the posterosuperior quadrant before you cut. A retraction pocket with keratin there is cholesteatoma, which changes the operation."],["osec","th:op-129~s3-2","Make the myringotomy","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","With the myringotomy knife make an incision of about 2 mm in the anteroinferior quadrant (or anterosuperior), radial or circumferential according to preference, through the full thickness of the drum. Keep away from the posterosuperior quadrant, do not touch the handle of the malleus and do not extend to the annulus. An incision that is too small makes insertion difficult; it can be widened with the crocodile forceps. What you should see A clean slit with fluid (serous, mucoid or pus) welling through it, or the mucosa of the promontory if the ear is dry. Clear fluid and pulsation Clear fluid that keeps coming may be CSF: do not insert a tube; seek senior advice and image. A blue or red mass behind the drum, or pulsatile bleeding, means a jugular bulb or carotid artery; stop."],["osec","th:op-129~s3-3","Clear the middle ear","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Aspirate the effusion through the myringotomy with a 5 French suction, or a 3 French if the drum is thin and atrophic, and a 7 French with saline irrigation if the fluid is thick glue. Send fluid for culture when the patient is unwell, immunocompromised or has a complication. What you should see The middle ear emptied, with the drum falling back from the promontory. Senior's pearl Do not chase every drop of glue. Suction long and hard at the promontory traumatises the mucosa, and the tube will do the rest of the draining."],["osec","th:op-129~s3-4","Insert the tube","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Load the tube on serrated crocodile forceps at the edge of its outer flange, angled so the leading inner flange will enter the incision first. Pass it down the speculum without touching the canal, engage the inner flange through the incision and push or tease it home with a curved pick or Rosen needle until both flanges sit either side of the drum. A T-tube is inserted by folding the long flanges together. What you should see The tube seated perpendicular to the drum with its lumen open, and middle ear mucosa visible through it. The tube that falls in Pushing too hard through a large incision can drop the tube into the middle ear. Retrieve it through the incision with a hook; if you cannot, place a new tube and deal with the medialised tube as a planned procedure."],["osec","th:op-129~s3-5","Finish","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · The operation, stage by stage","Stop bleeding with a pledget soaked in 1:1000 adrenaline; a dry tube is a tube that stays open. Instil antibiotic-steroid drops if there was effusion, pus or bleeding. Record the side, the site, the fluid (serous, mucoid, purulent or dry) and the tube type. What you should see A dry canal and a patent tube."],["osec","th:op-129~s4-1","The atelectatic drum","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Variations and difficulties","When the drum lies on the promontory there is nowhere to insert a tube. Inject 0.1 to 0.2 ml of air through the drum with a 27-gauge needle to lift it off the promontory and create a space. In adherent atelectasis a tube may be impossible and tympanoplasty with a tube is the alternative; in some, observation is right. Figure 3 Severe atelectasis, with the drum draped over the stapes and promontory as a natural type II or III tympanoplasty. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 129.2"],["osec","th:op-129~s4-2","The narrow canal","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Variations and difficulties","Dilate a stenotic meatus with serially larger specula. When only a small speculum will pass, carry the tube into the medial canal beside the speculum and then manoeuvre it into the incision."],["osec","th:op-129~s4-3","Myringotomy for culture and tympanocentesis","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Variations and difficulties","For a culture, follow the first two stages, then swab the middle ear or aspirate with a 3 or 5 French suction attached to a small syringe. For tympanocentesis , follow the first stage and aspirate through the drum with an 18-gauge Rosen needle on a tuberculin syringe or an Alden-Senturia trap , again avoiding the posterosuperior quadrant. Both are used in acute otitis media with high fever, sepsis or a complication; myringotomy alone for pain is now uncommon, and a tube is usually inserted in a complicated case."],["osec","th:op-129~s5-1","Who and how","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Balloon Eustachian tuboplasty","Balloon dilation of the cartilaginous Eustachian tube is offered to adults, and to children from about seven years when the tube approaches adult length, with dilatory Eustachian tube dysfunction: aural fullness, popping and pain with pressure change, type B or C tympanograms, persistent effusion or non-adherent atelectasis, and those who previously improved with tubes. It is contraindicated by a dehiscent carotid canal in the bony tube, so obtain a temporal bone CT beforehand and look at the sinuses too. It is done under general anaesthesia via a laryngeal mask, supine, the table turned 90 degrees and the head turned slightly toward the surgeon, with cardiac monitoring. 5.1.1 The steps Decongest the nose with pledgets of oxymetazoline or 1:1000 adrenaline, then inspect with a 30- or 45-degree endoscope. Bend the catheter tip to about 55 to 70 degrees and guide it to the back of the nose; some systems use a 70-degree guide catheter. Out-fracture the inferior turbinate with a Freer elevator, or dilate between septum and turbinate, if the passage is tight; if the scope and catheter do not fit one side, view from the other. Identify the torus tubarius , rotate the catheter laterally into the orifice, and use it to roll the posterior cushion medially to see the lumen. Advance gently; stop at resistance , which is the isthmus. A balloon no longer than about 18 mm treats the cartilaginous valve without entering the bony tube. Inflate for 2 minutes , deflate and withdraw, then inspect the lumen for tears or a false passage. A pledget soaked in dexamethasone can be placed in the or"],["osec","th:op-129~s6-1","Crisis drills","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · When it goes wrong","Bleeding during myringotomy 1 Minor bleeding pledget with 1 1000 adrenaline avoid further canal trauma 2 Brisk bleeding fill the canal with thrombin-soaked gelatin sponge raise the head treat hypertension 3 Profuse or pulsatile bleeding pack the canal tightly consider a jugular bulb or carotid injury senior help and transfer to a tertiary centre imaging Clear fluid through the myringotomy 1 Stop do not insert a tube 2 Think CSF leak through a tegmen defect or inner ear malformation 3 Act senior review, CT of the temporal bones, manage as a CSF leak Arrhythmia during balloon dilation 1 Deflate at once 2 Abort withdraw the catheter 3 Monitor treat hypertension, observe the rhythm"],["osec","th:op-129~s7-1","After a tube","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Aftercare","No drops are needed for a dry ear. For serous fluid or bleeding, steroid-fluoroquinolone (or fluoroquinolone) drops for 3 days; for mucoid or purulent fluid, up to 10 days, with review at 1 to 2 weeks. Otherwise review at about 4 weeks with an audiogram and tympanogram, then every 3 to 6 months until the tubes extrude and the drums heal. Routine water precautions are not supported by the evidence; advise them for lake, river or sea water, shallow diving, and children who get pain or discharge after swimming."],["osec","th:op-129~s7-2","Complications","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Aftercare","Complications of ventilation tubes Complication Frequency Management Otorrhoea More than 20% Antibiotic or antibiotic-steroid drops and aural toilet; culture-directed treatment if persistent; look for cholesteatoma or mastoid disease if it will not settle Tube blockage About 7% Drops or hydrogen peroxide; replace if needed Granulation tissue About 4% Antibiotic-steroid drops; remove the tube if persistent (foreign-body reaction) Early extrusion About 4% Seen in some on CPAP; lower the pressure; replace if still indicated Perforation after extrusion 1% to 6% Observe a small one (it works as a ventilation tube); myringoplasty or tympanoplasty if persistent Medialised tube About 0.5% Retrieve through the incision; exploratory tympanotomy if there is inflammation and it cannot be retrieved Retention beyond 2 to 2.5 years Uncommon with short-term tubes Remove, with or without myringoplasty, or replace Myringosclerosis, atrophy, atelectasis Common Generally irreversible and usually harmless"],["ev","th:op-129~s7-3","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation: current evidence","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Aftercare","Glue ear. A Cochrane review found that grommets improve hearing modestly in the first six months, with the benefit fading by one to two years (Browning et al., 2010). NICE (NG233, 2023) recommends active observation for three months, then grommets or hearing aids for persistent bilateral loss affecting the child. Recurrent acute otitis media. A Cochrane review found that grommets may reduce the number of episodes in the first six months, with low-certainty evidence (Venekamp et al., 2018). The American guideline advises against grommets for recurrent acute otitis media without effusion at assessment (Rosenfeld et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2022). Tube otorrhoea. Antibiotic–steroid drops beat oral antibiotics (van Dongen et al., NEJM 2014). Water precautions are not needed for routine swimming."],["osec","th:op-129~s7-3","Alternatives","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · Aftercare","Culture-directed antibiotics (with infectious disease advice) for recurrent or persistent acute otitis media; treatment of reflux, rhinosinusitis and Eustachian tube inflammation with nasal or oral steroids; adenoidectomy with or instead of tubes when the adenoid is large and in children over about four with recurrent effusion; tubes and balloon dilation as alternatives to each other in adults; microdebrider reduction of a hypertrophic posterior cushion."],["osec","th:op-129~s8-1","Where the books differ","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra for acute suppurative otitis media, a circumferential incision in the posteroinferior quadrant midway between the malleus handle and the annulus, avoiding the incudostapedial joint; for effusion, a small radial incision in the posteroinferior or anteroinferior quadrant; a grommet preferably in the anterosuperior quadrant for longer retention. It warns of an incision that stays in the superficial layers of a thick drum, of cutting the inflamed posterior canal wall, and of a high jugular bulb, aberrant carotid or glomus tympanicum; it adds intratympanic steroid injections and aero-otitis as indications. Lee uses the AAO-HNS guideline indications, emphasises adenoidectomy with a second set of tubes, and gives the otorrhoea, blockage and perforation rates. Myers adds balloon Eustachian tuboplasty, practical fixes for the atelectatic drum and the narrow canal, and the warning about clear fluid and vascular anomalies."],["osec","th:op-129~s8-2","Viva essentials","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Guideline indications: 3 months of bilateral effusion with hearing loss; recurrent acute otitis media with effusion; the at-risk child. Which quadrant and why the posterosuperior quadrant is avoided (incus, stapes, chorda tympani). Short-term versus long-term tubes and who gets a T-tube. Complication rates: otorrhoea more than 20%, perforation 1% to 6%. Clear fluid and the vascular anomaly: what you do. Balloon dilation: the isthmus, the carotid canal and the arrhythmia."],["osec","th:op-129~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Otitis Media, Myringotomy, Tympanostomy Tube, and Balloon Dilation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Incising the posterosuperior quadrant or touching the malleus handle. Raising a canal-wall haematoma with an oversized speculum or rough cleaning. A myringotomy too small for the tube, or so large that the tube falls in. Missing an attic retraction or cholesteatoma before inserting a tube. Leaving blood in the tube at the end. Advancing a balloon past resistance at the isthmus."],["osec","th:op-131~s1-1","The case at a glance","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Operating note","Myringoplasty and tympanoplasty Summary Indications A perforation that restricts water activities; recurrent discharge or recurrent ear infections; to improve hearing Contraindications An only hearing ear (prefer a hearing aid unless active disease persists despite maximal treatment); active vestibular disease; active discharge not yet settled (dry the ear first) Anaesthesia General for most; intravenous sedation with local anaesthetic for a limited, transcanal repair in a cooperative patient Position Supine, bed turned 180 degrees with the feet toward the anaesthetist, head turned away Antibiotics Not routinely needed; a single dose of cefazolin (clindamycin if allergic) for an infected ear or when a prosthesis is placed Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring optional; not the standard of care for this operation Imaging CT only if cholesteatoma or mastoid disease is suspected; MRI for imbalance, vertigo or asymmetric sensorineural loss Tray Middle ear instrument set; round knives, picks, cup forceps, Freer elevator; Lempert speculum; high-speed drill with cutting and diamond burs for canaloplasty or mastoidectomy; ossicular prostheses if reconstruction is planned; Gelfoam, Silastic sheet Before theatre Dry the ear with drops or systemic antibiotics; read every previous operation note (local graft tissue may be used up); confirm the patient will come back for follow-up"],["osec","th:op-131~s1-2","Consent","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it does: closes the hole in the eardrum, and checks and repairs the middle ear behind it, aiming for a dry ear that can get wet and, if possible, better hearing. The graft may not take , leaving or creating a hole that needs another operation; more likely after previous surgery or with a poorly working Eustachian tube. Hearing may not improve , may get worse, and very rarely is lost completely. Taste change on the side of the tongue if the taste nerve is stretched or cut; usually temporary. Dizziness , usually short-lived; tinnitus . Facial weakness: very rare. Numbness of the top of the ear after an incision behind it. Late problems: the drum healing away from the bone (lateralisation), scarring in the front angle (blunting), and small skin cysts. Water precautions for several weeks to months."],["osec","th:op-131~s1-3","Before you start","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Hearing loss: sudden or gradual. Discharge: chronic or recurrent. Trauma , such as a slap, a cotton bud, barotrauma, diving or forceful syringing. Pain is not typical of a simple perforation: look for another cause in the head and neck. Vertigo must be investigated before surgery; do not operate on an ear with an active vestibular problem. Previous ear surgery and grommets; cleft palate repair (tube dysfunction); severe seasonal allergy; anticoagulants; smoking. 1.3.2 The examination Use the microscope. The perforation: size (small, subtotal, total); site (central or marginal ); a \"trail\" of keratin running from a marginal edge means squamous epithelium in the middle ear. Wet or dry; the middle ear mucosa; cholesteatoma, hypertrophic mucosa or granulation suggests a mastoidectomy may also be needed. The other ear: retraction, atelectasis, effusion and tympanosclerosis point to poor tube function, which predicts a poorer result, especially in children. 1.3.3 The audiogram A small perforation can have normal hearing, and a near-total one causes about 35 to 40 dB of conductive loss. A bigger gap than the hole explains points to an ossicular break or fixation, which you must plan for. In mixed loss, closing the gap may still leave the patient needing a hearing aid; but if a severe mixed loss is too much for a comfortable aid, correcting the conductive part can help rehabilitation. Who is less likely to succeed Multiple failed previous repairs, persistent Eustachian tube dysfunction, severe disease in the other ear, and palatal problems. Tell these patients bef"],["osec","th:op-131~s2-1","The tympanic membrane","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Anatomy you must see","Pars tensa and pars flaccida ; three layers: outer squamous epithelium, middle fibrous layer, inner mucosa. The fibrous annulus sits in the bony sulcus of the tympanic ring. Central perforations have a rim of drum all round; marginal ones reach the annulus."],["osec","th:op-131~s2-2","The canal","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Anatomy you must see","The bony-cartilaginous junction is where the canal block is injected. The tympanomastoid and tympanosquamous suture lines guide the vascular strip incisions. The anterior canal bulge often hides the anterior annulus; it must be drilled for lateral grafting. The linea temporalis marks the upper limit of the periosteal flap; the temporomandibular joint lies in front of the anterior canal wall."],["osec","th:op-131~s2-3","The middle ear","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Anatomy you must see","Ossicles: malleus (head, neck, handle, lateral and anterior processes), incus (body, short and long processes), stapes (head, crura, footplate). The chorda tympani crosses medial to the drum, near the posterior annulus, under the tympanomeatal flap. The facial nerve: its tympanic segment above the oval window and its mastoid segment behind the posterior canal wall. Tympanosclerosis: chalky plaques in the drum, on the ossicles and the medial wall; remove plaque that stiffens the drum."],["osec","th:op-131~s3-1","Anaesthesia of the canal","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: myringoplasty","Inject 1% to 2% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline in four quadrants, just lateral to the bony-cartilaginous junction, through a Lempert speculum, until the skin blanches. Inject behind the ear too if you will take fascia or use a postauricular approach. What you should see The canal skin blanching all round, down to the annulus, without a bleb lifting the skin. Blebs and the facial nerve Injecting fast raises blisters that tear the flap you are about to lift. Anaesthetic that tracks deep to the stylomastoid foramen causes a temporary facial palsy; warn the patient and wait. Senior's pearl Inject slowly and wait ten minutes. A dry canal is made at this step, not later with adrenaline pledgets."],["osec","th:op-131~s3-2","The fresh traumatic tear","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: myringoplasty","Fold the edges out from the medial surface so no squamous epithelium is left inside, then lay them back together. Support them with Gelfoam soaked in ciprofloxacin (with or without dexamethasone) medially, and a cigarette paper, Steri-Strip or silk patch on the outer surface. What you should see Everted edges lying flat and touching, with no skin curled into the middle ear and the handle of the malleus in its normal position. Vertigo, sensorineural loss or facial weakness These mean the injury went deeper than the drum (stapes subluxation, perilymph fistula): this is no longer a clinic patch."],["osec","th:op-131~s3-3","The small chronic perforation","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: myringoplasty","Touch the edges with phenol or trichloroacetic acid, then freshen the margin all round with a sharp pick , removing epithelium that has grown under the medial surface and making the edge bleed; then patch the outer surface. Skin left inside Squamous epithelium left on the medial surface of the edge, or trapped under any graft, grows into a cholesteatoma. Rimming the perforation properly is the single most important step of every technique. What you should see A bleeding raw rim all the way round, and healthy, dry middle ear mucosa through the hole."],["osec","th:op-131~s3-4","Fat or areolar tissue graft","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: myringoplasty","If patching fails, prepare the edges the same way, fill the middle ear behind the hole with Gelfoam, and pass a dumbbell of fat from the ear lobe through the perforation, or place compressed areolar tissue or fascia medial to it, held by Gelfoam on both sides. What you should see The fat plug bulging on both sides of the drum like a collar stud, filling the hole with no gap at its edge. Senior's pearl Fat plugs suit small central perforations, under about a quarter of the drum, in a dry ear. A larger hole needs a formal graft."],["osec","th:op-131~c4","The operation: cartilage butterfly (inlay)","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: cartilage butterfly (inlay)","Eavey's butterfly graft suits small to medium perforations whose whole edge can be seen through the canal . With no canal incisions and no packing, there is less pain, the hearing improves at once, and the operation is short. (Because no tympanomeatal flap is raised, some call it a myringoplasty.)"],["osec","th:op-131~s4-1","Rim and measure","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: cartilage butterfly (inlay)","Inject the canal and the tragus. Rim the perforation with a curved pick, without enlarging it beyond what you can see through the canal. Measure it with a right-angled hook or round knife. Figure 1 Measuring the perforation with a right-angled hook; the exposed malleus and tympanosclerosis help lock the graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.6 What you should see A freshened rim with no epithelium turned in, and the whole margin of the perforation visible through the canal: if the anterior edge is hidden, this technique is the wrong one. Enlarging the hole Do not tear the remnant while rimming; the cartilage is cut to the measurement you take now."],["osec","th:op-131~s4-2","Harvest and shape the cartilage","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: cartilage butterfly (inlay)","Incise the back (posteromedial) surface of the tragus through skin and cartilage in one stroke with a size 15 blade, keeping the dome of the tragus for cosmesis; free the cartilage, keeping perichondrium on one or both sides, and close with one or two plain gut stitches. Cut the cartilage to the exact shape of the perforation but 1 to 2 mm larger . With a blade, cut a groove about 2 mm deep all round its edge, keeping the two flanges equal, so it opens like butterfly wings. Figure 2 The tragal cartilage shaped 1 to 2 mm larger than the perforation, with a groove cut all round its edge so it opens like butterfly wings. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.7 What you should see A disc 1 to 2 mm larger than the hole with an even groove round its edge and perichondrium intact on the lateral face. An uneven groove If one flange is thinner than the other, the graft tilts or falls into the middle ear. Recut it if the wings are unequal."],["osec","th:op-131~s4-3","Snap it in","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: cartilage butterfly (inlay)","Pass the graft down the canal and seat it so one flange lies medial to the edge of the perforation and the other, with perichondrium, lateral . Check every point of the rim, then press gently on the centre: the whole drum should move with it. Figure 3 The butterfly graft in place, one edge lifted to show the two flanges. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.8 What you should see A graft that sits flush in the hole all round, with the medial flange under the drum remnant at every point. Senior's pearl Sizing makes or breaks this graft: too large and it will not snap in, too small and it falls into the middle ear. Use the same instrument to measure the hole and to mark the cartilage."],["osec","th:op-131~c5","The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","The lateral graft suits total and near-total perforations , revisions, and drums thick with plaque or scarred remnants that need removing."],["osec","th:op-131~s5-1","Vascular strip and postauricular approach","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","Under the microscope, rinse the canal and make vascular strip incisions along the tympanomastoid and tympanosquamous suture lines (a semilunar incision joining them just lateral to the annulus is optional; leaving it out lets the strip lift the outer layer of the drum remnant with it). Make a postauricular incision a few millimetres behind the crease, ending just below the lobule and never beyond the mastoid tip , where the facial nerve becomes superficial in children. Dissect forward in the loose areolar plane over the temporalis fascia until the posterior canal skin is a few millimetres thick, and place a self-retaining retractor. Figure 4 The vascular strip incisions along the tympanomastoid and tympanosquamous suture lines. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.9 What you should see The loose areolar plane over the temporalis fascia, the posterior canal skin thinned from behind, and the spine of Henle at the back of the meatus. The facial nerve in children Under about two years the mastoid tip is undeveloped and the nerve leaves the skull superficially. Keep the incision high and off the tip."],["osec","th:op-131~s5-2","Harvest the fascia","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","Incise the true temporalis fascia (not the muscle) for 2 cm at its lower end, lift it off the muscle with a Freer, and cut a 2 by 2 cm piece; the loose areolar layer over it can be used if it is thick enough. Clean off muscle and fat on a block, press it flat and let it dry under a lamp. Make a 7- or T-shaped periosteal incision along the linea temporalis and down the skin incision, and lift the periosteal flap into the retractor with the ear. What you should see True temporalis fascia: white and glistening with the red muscle visible beneath once it is lifted. A flat, dry, parchment-like graft with no fat or muscle on it. Senior's pearl Take more than you think you need, and take it first: it dries while you work, and a dry graft handles like paper; a wet one curls."],["osec","th:op-131~s5-3","Lift the canal skin","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","Complete the elevation of the vascular strip and fold it out of the canal into the retractor. With a large round knife, lift the rest of the canal skin and periosteum from the annulus outward to the bony-cartilaginous junction, leaving it attached there and folding it out. If the outer layer of the drum remnant stays behind, remove it separately. Figure 5 The periosteum and canal skin lifted from the annulus outward, toward the cartilaginous canal. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.10 What you should see Bare bone of the canal all round with the tympanosquamous and tympanomastoid sutures, and the canal skin hinged laterally in one piece. Tearing the skin The anterior canal skin is thin and stuck at the suture lines; divide the fibres there sharply. A torn or lost flap leaves bare bone that granulates and stenoses."],["osec","th:op-131~s5-4","Drill the canal","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","Widen the bony canal with a size 3 cutting bur then a size 2 diamond with continuous suction irrigation, removing the anterior and inferior bulges until you can see the whole annulus at once and the anterior angle is opened. Keep away from the facial nerve posteriorly and the temporomandibular joint anteriorly. Figure 6 Drilling away the anterior and inferior canal bulges with suction irrigation. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.11 Blunting A canal left narrow, or a graft that rides up onto the anterior or inferior canal wall instead of lying on the annulus, scars across the anterior angle, which blunts and thickens the drum and costs hearing. Drill until the whole annulus is in view, and keep the graft off the bone. What you should see The whole annulus in one view of the microscope, including the anterior angle, without moving the patient's head. Senior's pearl Leave a thin shell over the temporomandibular joint: if the bone turns pink or blue, stop. Keep the skin in the retractor moist while you drill."],["osec","th:op-131~s5-5","De-epithelialise the remnant","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","Starting in front with a round knife along the bony annulus, scrape the squamous layer off the drum remnant all round, leaving the annulus and fibrous layer intact, and clear the rim of the perforation with cup forceps. For a total perforation or a thin graft, fill the middle ear with small Gelfoam pledgets. Pearls and cholesteatoma Any squamous epithelium left under a lateral graft becomes an intratympanic cyst or cholesteatoma. Take your time over this step. What you should see A dull, matt fibrous layer all round with no shiny epithelium, and the bare bony annulus anteriorly."],["osec","th:op-131~s5-6","Place the graft","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","Trim the dried fascia to an oval about 1.3 by 1.5 cm and cut a slit at 12 o'clock. Wet it, and lay it lateral to the annulus and the cleaned remnant, with the slit tucked medial to the malleus handle (in contact with the tensor tendon, in Sheehy's description). Overlap the two edges of the slit, or fold the anterior flap back over the handle. Smooth the graft onto the annulus in front and below, keeping it off the bony canal , and bring any excess onto the posterior wall. If the malleus is missing, cut two slits to make a tongue that tucks under the scutum into the attic, then overlap the side flaps over it. Figure 7 The dried fascia trimmed to an oval with a slit at 12 o'clock for the malleus handle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.12 Figure 8 The graft lateral to the annulus with its slit medial to the handle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.13 Figure 9 The anterior flap folded back over the exposed handle, or both flaps overlapped leaving only the umbo exposed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.14 Figure 10 With no malleus, two cuts create a tongue to tuck into the attic. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.15 Figure 11 The tongue tucked into the attic and the flaps overlapped to seal the top of the graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.16 Lateralisation A graft not placed medial to the malleus handle drifts outward from the ossicles over 6 to 12 months, and the hearing falls. The handle anchors the graft. What you should see The graft flat on the remnant and annulus, tucked under the malleus handle, with its anterior edge sto"],["osec","th:op-131~s5-7","Replace the skin and pack","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: lateral (overlay) graft","Lay the canal skin back on the bone, overlapping the graft by about 1 mm, without folding its edges under (folded edges form canal cysts). Roll a dry cigar of Gelfoam into the anterior sulcus, or place a piece of thick Silastic over the graft, then Gelfoam on top. Release the retractors, return the vascular strip, close the periosteal flap with one stitch to steady the ear, check through the speculum that the strip is in place with its edges everted, fill the canal with Gelfoam and place a cotton ball. Close the periosteum and skin in layers with absorbable sutures and glue, and apply a mastoid dressing for 24 hours. Figure 12 The canal skin returned over the bone, overlapping the fascia by about 1 mm. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.17 What you should see The graft flat on the annulus all round, medial to the handle, clear of the anterior canal wall, and the canal skin lying flat with no edges turned under."],["osec","th:op-131~c6","The operation: medial (underlay) graft","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: medial (underlay) graft","The medial graft suits most central perforations with an adequate rim, infrequent discharge and little middle ear disease. If the whole perforation is visible through the canal, the operation can be transcanal, with a small postauricular incision just for the fascia."],["osec","th:op-131~s6-1","Flap and middle ear","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: medial (underlay) graft","Inject, rim the perforation, make the vascular strip incisions and join them with a semilunar incision about 4 mm lateral to the annulus . Harvest fascia as above. Lift the tympanomeatal flap with the annulus out of its sulcus with a round knife until you enter the middle ear, finding and preserving the chorda tympani . Inspect and feel the ossicles: if the chain is stiff, separate the incudostapedial joint to tell whether the stapes or the malleus and incus are fixed. Clear granulation, hyperplastic mucosa, cholesteatoma or plaque that limits movement. What you should see The annulus lifting out of its sulcus, the chorda tympani crossing under the flap, then the incudostapedial joint, the round window niche and the promontory. A round window reflex when you move the malleus: the chain is intact and mobile. The chorda and the incus Enter the middle ear posteroinferiorly, under the annulus, and work upward; entering high tears the chorda and can dislocate the incus. Senior's pearl Always test the chain before you graft. A perfect drum over a fixed or broken chain does not hear."],["osec","th:op-131~s6-2","Place the graft","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: medial (underlay) graft","Fill the middle ear with Gelfoam as a bed, wet the graft, and place it against the medial surface of the perforation, tucked under the remnant all round. Lay the flap back and hold everything with Gelfoam lateral to the drum. Figure 13 The medial graft: after freshening the edges and raising the tympanomeatal flap, the graft is placed medial to the perforation on a bed of Gelfoam. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.18 What you should see Graft visible behind the whole rim with no gap, especially anteriorly, once the flap is laid back. The anterior edge A medial graft fails where it has least support: anteriorly. If the anterior remnant is narrow, pull the graft through a small anterior canal-skin tunnel or pack the anterior mesotympanum firmly with Gelfoam."],["osec","th:op-131~s6-3","The over-under graft","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · The operation: medial (underlay) graft","When the perforation reaches the malleus handle, scrape the periosteum and any drum remnant off the handle (incise its periosteum with a sickle knife and peel from the umbo up to the lateral process), then place the graft lateral to (\"over\") the handle and medial to (\"under\") the annulus and remnant . Put Gelfoam over the handle to press the graft onto the bone. What you should see A graft lying under the remnant all round, supported by Gelfoam, with the flap returned exactly and the chorda intact. Senior's pearl Underlay grafts fail anteriorly, where the remnant is thin and the canal bulge hides the edge. The over-under technique, a little more Gelfoam anteriorly, or a cartilage graft for a large or high-risk anterior perforation, all reduce this."],["osec","th:op-131~s7-1","Crisis drills","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · When it goes wrong","The face is weak in recovery 1 First wait for the local anaesthetic to wear off before acting 2 If still weak remove the packing examine the nerve's course steroids imaging and exploration if no improvement or the nerve was not seen The canal is narrowing after lateral grafting 1 Recognise soft-tissue stenosis across the mid canal, sometimes looking like lateralisation 2 Act early debridement and stenting in clinic silver nitrate to granulation keep or replace the Silastic stent A small white cyst appears on the graft or canal 1 Recognise an epithelial pearl from trapped or folded-in skin 2 Act open and marsupialise it in clinic while small The hearing drops months after a lateral graft 1 Suspect lateralisation (graft lifted off the malleus) or anterior blunting 2 Act microscopy and audiogram revision surgery if hearing is significantly affected"],["osec","th:op-131~s8-1","After the operation","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Aftercare","Paper patch: keep the ear dry and review until the patch migrates onto the canal wall, usually 6 to 12 weeks. Packed myringoplasty: remove the packing at 7 to 10 days. Cartilage butterfly: no drops needed; review at about 3 weeks; keep dry until healed, usually 3 to 4 weeks. Lateral and medial grafts: remove the mastoid dressing the next day; the wound can get wet after 24 hours with glue, but keep the canal dry with a Vaseline-coated cotton ball. Start drops twice a day a week before the review at about 3 weeks; they soften the Gelfoam so it can be suctioned out. Silastic after lateral grafting can be removed at the first review, or left as a stent for 6 to 8 weeks if there is concern about stenosis, blunting or lateralisation. The drum should be intact by 3 weeks ; check mobility with pneumatic otoscopy and do tuning forks; audiogram at 2 to 3 months . Water restrictions lift at about 2 months after medial grafting and 3 to 4 months after lateral grafting."],["osec","th:op-131~s8-2","Complications","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Aftercare","Complications of myringoplasty and tympanoplasty Complication Cause or prevention Graft failure, persistent perforation Poor rimming; tube dysfunction; previous surgery; mucosal disease Lateralisation Lateral graft not placed medial to the malleus; 6 to 12 months later Anterior blunting Narrow canal or graft on the anterior canal wall Epithelial pearls and cholesteatoma Skin left medial to the graft or folded under Canal stenosis Healing of the canal after lateral grafting Taste disturbance Chorda tympani stretched or cut Sensorineural loss, vertigo, tinnitus Manipulation of the ossicles, drilling on the chain Facial weakness Local anaesthetic (wait), or injury Wound infection Uncommon"],["osec","th:op-131~s8-3","Choosing a technique and material","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Aftercare","Techniques compared Technique Best for Watch for Paper patch Small fresh traumatic or small chronic perforation Repeated application may be needed Fat plug Small central perforation after a failed patch Small holes only Cartilage butterfly Small to medium perforations visible through the canal Exact sizing Medial (underlay) Most central perforations with a rim Anterior failure, narrowed middle ear space Lateral (overlay) Total and subtotal, revisions, diseased remnants Blunting, lateralisation, pearls; harder to learn Success exceeds 90% with any technique in skilled hands, and some studies give 97% or more for button, overlay and underlay grafts alike; one large series slightly favoured lateral grafts. Graft materials give similar hearing, but cartilage gives a better-looking, more durable drum and is preferred for high-risk ears (atelectasis, cholesteatoma, revisions). Routine mastoidectomy with tympanoplasty has not clearly improved closure, though it may reduce revisions; add it for severe mucosal disease or cholesteatoma."],["ev","th:op-131~s8-4","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty: current evidence","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Aftercare","Graft choice. A meta-analysis of 37 studies found graft take of 92% with cartilage and 82% with temporalis fascia, with similar hearing results (Jalali et al., Laryngoscope 2017); cartilage is preferred for subtotal, revision and high-risk perforations and for retraction. Endoscopic approach. Transcanal endoscopic myringoplasty gives graft take similar to microscopic surgery, with less need for postauricular incisions and canalplasty, in meta-analyses of mostly non-randomised studies. Outcome. Overall closure rates of 85 to 95% are reported; smoking, an active discharging ear and anterior or large perforations predict failure."],["osec","th:op-131~s8-4","Alternatives","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · Aftercare","Observation, or a hearing aid with water precautions, for any patient. A conductive loss over 30 to 35 dB usually limits function enough that observation alone is not reasonable, though an aid avoids surgical risk at the cost of batteries, breakdowns, background noise and feedback. A staged operation with Silastic in the middle ear may be needed to rebuild an aerated space first."],["osec","th:op-131~s9-1","Where the books differ","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra separates myringoplasty (closing the drum without inspecting the middle ear) from tympanoplasty (clearing disease and rebuilding the hearing mechanism) and gives the Wullstein types; lists contraindications to myringoplasty (discharge, uncontrolled nasal allergy, otitis externa, squamous ingrowth, an only hearing ear, and young age); compares underlay grafting (narrower middle ear, anterior reperforation) with overlay (blunting, pearls, lateralisation); and uses a paper patch with cautery of the rim, or a fat graft, for small holes. Lee covers tympanoplasty with ossicular reconstruction, noting that more patients reach a gap under 20 dB with a partial than a total prosthesis, and that graft material matters less than technique. Myers describes three techniques in operative detail: the cartilage butterfly with exact sizing, Sheehy's lateral graft with vascular strip, canaloplasty and de-epithelialisation, and the medial graft with the over-under modification; it stresses rimming the perforation as the essential step in all of them, and the specific timetable for drops, reviews and lifting water restrictions."],["osec","th:op-131~s9-2","Viva essentials","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Myringoplasty versus tympanoplasty , and the Wullstein types. Why a gap larger than the hole explains means ossicular disease. Rimming the perforation, and why. Underlay versus overlay : indications and each one's complications. Lateralisation and blunting : causes and prevention. The cartilage butterfly : indications and the importance of sizing. Graft materials and when to choose cartilage . Prognostic factors : the other ear, tube function, previous surgery, palate."],["osec","th:op-131~s9-3","Common pitfalls","Myringoplasty and Tympanoplasty · For the examination","Common pitfalls Inadequate rimming. Not checking the other ear and tube function. Missing an ossicular problem the audiogram predicted. A narrow canal or a graft on the anterior canal wall. Leaving skin under the graft or folding canal skin edges under. A lateral graft not medial to the malleus handle. Extending the postauricular incision below the mastoid tip in a child. Wrongly sized cartilage."],["osec","th:op-132~s1-1","The case at a glance","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Operating note","Ossicular chain reconstruction Summary Indications Conductive loss of known or suspected ossicular cause (erosion, fixation, dislocation, fracture, after trauma, chronic otitis media, cholesteatoma, tympanosclerosis, congenital anomaly, middle ear tumour); a chain that must be disarticulated to reach other disease; an extruding prosthesis from previous surgery; a large mixed loss, to bring hearing within reach of an aid Contraindications An only hearing ear ; conductive loss from tumour or infection involving the dura or brain; a contracted, poorly aerated middle ear where reconstruction will not help; a small air-bone gap in revision surgery; medical comorbidity Anaesthesia Usually general; monitored anaesthesia care for a compliant patient with an experienced team, and for minor adjustment or replacement of a prosthesis Position Supine, head turned away; table and microscope set so you sit comfortably with relaxed, not outstretched, arms Antibiotics Routine: cefazolin; clindamycin in penicillin allergy Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring not routine, unless combined with mastoidectomy or atresia repair Tray Binocular microscope; complete middle ear and mastoid set; monopolar and bipolar diathermy; micro drill; a full stock of prostheses and a sizing set Skills Microscopic otoscopy; temporal bone anatomy; tympanoplasty; stapedectomy and stapedotomy"],["osec","th:op-132~s1-2","Consent","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to rebuild the chain of hearing bones so that sound reaches the inner ear. Hearing may not improve, may worsen, and rarely the ear may lose its hearing; tinnitus and dizziness. A second operation may be planned (staging), or needed later for a prosthesis that moves or extrudes. Taste change on that side of the tongue. Facial weakness: rare. A hole in the drum, infection, a leak of fluid from the inner ear or of brain fluid, narrowing of the ear canal. Success still allows a small residual gap; a hearing aid may still be wanted."],["osec","th:op-132~s1-3","Before you start","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Operating note","Ask about risk factors: previous surgery, Eustachian tube dysfunction, trauma, inflammatory or granulomatous disease, congenital anomaly, cholesteatoma, middle ear tumour and tympanosclerosis. Characterise the loss: sudden or gradual and in what circumstances, stable or worsening, constant or fluctuating, and its type and degree. Ask about tinnitus, fullness, distortion, pain, discharge, infection, vertigo and imbalance, and about autophony and the Tullio phenomenon . Take the otological past (perforation, grommets, otitis externa or media, cholesteatoma), autoimmune or granulomatous disease, nasal disease that impairs the tube, previous operations (get the notes if you can; with past cholesteatoma think of recurrence; a previous canal wall down operation can leave the facial nerve exposed), the family history and smoking, which impairs ventilation and healing. Examine both ears with the binocular microscope: the opposite ear predicts how well this one will do when the Eustachian tube is poor. Use pneumatic otoscopy on both drums; reduced movement of the malleus suggests lateral chain fixation, though it is usually found at exploration. Test with 256, 512 and sometimes 1024 Hz forks. Obtain bilateral air and bone conduction audiometry with speech reception and recorded word discrimination. Not every conductive loss is ossicular A conductive loss can arise in the inner ear. A third window , such as superior canal dehiscence, lets the inner ear fluid wave escape, giving a conductive loss with the acoustic reflexes still present. Ear canal obstruction and early Ménière's disea"],["osec","th:op-132~s2-1","Landmarks","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Anatomy you must see","The bony-cartilaginous junction of the canal; the annulus; the pars tensa and pars flaccida; the chorda tympani; the ossicles or any existing prosthesis; the horizontal facial nerve; the round and oval windows; the jugular bulb (beware a high or dehiscent one); the pyramidal eminence; and the cochleariform process."],["osec","th:op-132~s2-2","The Wullstein classification","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Anatomy you must see","Figure 1 Wullstein's five types of tympanoplasty. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.1 Each type is named by the most lateral intact structure still connected to the inner ear on which the drum or chain is rebuilt. Type I: all three ossicles present and mobile; no reconstruction. Type II: drum grafted onto an intact incus and stapes; rarely needed. Type III: drum or graft directly on a mobile stapes superstructure. Type IV: drum or graft on a mobile footplate, the superstructure gone. Type V: fenestration of the horizontal canal; no longer used."],["osec","th:op-132~s2-3","Farrior's modification","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Anatomy you must see","Farrior based the type on the anatomy at the end of surgery. Type III restores continuity from the stapes to the drum or malleus by interposing a sculpted ossicle, bone or a synthetic material. Type IV reconstructs from a mobile footplate to the malleus, drum, graft or, rarely, incus. Type Va is a true fenestration; type Vb means a fixed or absent footplate, the vestibule sealed with tissue and continuity restored from the oval window. Stapedectomy is technically a Vb, though the term is not used for otosclerosis."],["osec","th:op-132~s2-4","Partial and total prostheses","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Anatomy you must see","PORP (partial ossicular replacement prosthesis): from the stapes superstructure to the drum, graft or malleus; also called an incus replacement prosthesis. TORP (total ossicular replacement prosthesis): from the footplate or a grafted oval window to the malleus, drum or graft; also called an incus-stapes replacement prosthesis. Incus interposition with the patient's own incus is the commonest type III. Stapes augmentation raises the stapes above the fallopian canal in a canal wall down cavity without a malleus."],["osec","th:op-132~s2-5","The rules of a working construct","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Anatomy you must see","Slight tension: the prosthesis should not only touch the drum but tent it very slightly. Too short and it falls or moves; too long, or with a poor tube, and it extrudes. No contact with bone: it must not touch the tympanic ring or the bony canal, nor rest on the promontory. Contact damps vibration. A precise connection from the drum, graft or malleus to the stapes, footplate or vestibule. Anticipate the drum's final resting place, and that the patient will be upright: you build lying flat with the ear facing the ceiling. Figure 2 A hydroxylapatite partial prosthesis touching the drum, which is very slightly tented over it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.2"],["osec","th:op-132~s3-1","Raise a flap that shows everything","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Graft the drum by your usual technique (see the tympanoplasty chapter). Because the state of the chain is often unknown beforehand, make the tympanomeatal flap with its superior limb above the pars flaccida, about 5 mm from the posterosuperior annulus . That lets you remove bone to expose the neck of the malleus, the body of the incus and the whole footplate, and to extract the incus if you must. What you should see The neck of the malleus, the incus body and long process, the stapes and its footplate, the chorda tympani and the round window."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-2","Palpate every link","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Inspect and gently palpate the drum and every ossicle. Myringosclerosis and tympanosclerosis, alone or together, can cause the loss; lateral chain fixation is easily missed. What you should see Each link moving when you touch the one before it: malleus, incus, then the stapes, with a round window reflex at the end. Lateral chain fixation Normal mobility of the malleus and incus must be confirmed by palpation: an inexperienced surgeon misses lateral chain fixation and reconstructs the wrong link."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-3","A fixed malleus","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Fixation of the malleus and incus, the lateral chain, is uncommon: the malleus head and incus body ankylosed to the scutum, a stiff incudomalleolar joint or a calcified anterior malleolar ligament. Elevate the flap in front of the neck of the malleus and palpate to confirm it is immobile. Separate the incudostapedial joint, palpate the stapes again to exclude otosclerosis, then disarticulate the incudomalleolar joint with a curved pick and remove the incus. If the malleus is still fixed, divide its head from the handle at the neck with a 1 mm micro drill: the handle becomes mobile, held by the drum and the tensor tendon. Then interpose the sculpted incus between stapes head and malleus handle, or place a PORP from the stapes to the handle or the drum. Figure 3 The incus body disarticulated from the malleus head and removed with cup forceps. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.4 What you should see The malleus handle rigid to the needle while the freed stapes still moves."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-4","An absent or short malleus handle","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Decide whether enough handle remains. If it is shortened beyond a third of its length , closure of the gap suffers unless the prosthesis touches the drum directly. An interposed incus must be tall enough to connect the stapes to the drum, and turning its short process toward the centre of the drum improves transmission. Synthetic prostheses are commoner here: a PORP if the superstructure is present, a TORP if only a mobile footplate remains. Figure 4 Type III tympanoplasty with a partial prosthesis when the malleus is absent: stapes to the drum or graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.5 What you should see How much handle is left measured against its normal length, and whether the stapes superstructure is present."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-5","A total perforation and missing ossicles","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Graft the drum and place a TORP from the footplate to the graft. The difficulty is that you do not know where the graft will settle: it may medialise, or less often lateralise, so the right length is uncertain. Consider staging : graft the drum with thick (0.04 inch) Silastic sheeting in the middle ear, and reconstruct about 6 months later. What you should see A mobile footplate in a clean, dry niche, and a middle ear with healthy mucosa."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-6","A fractured malleus handle","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Rare. Pneumatic otoscopy may show a hypermobile drum and malleus. Reconstruct with incus interposition or a PORP. What you should see A handle that moves separately from the malleus neck when touched."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-7","An eroded incus: incus interposition","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","The incus is the commonest failed link, usually its long and lenticular processes eroded or joined only by fibrous tissue. With a mobile malleus and stapes, interpose the incus. Note the mucosa and how the malleus relates to the stapes: directly lateral, or anterior to its vertical plane. Note the height of the stapes above the promontory and facial nerve, especially in a canal wall down cavity. No cartilage is needed between an incus and the drum. Figure 5 Where the malleus lies relative to the stapes decides how the incus is sculpted: left, the malleus lateral to the stapes; right, both height and anterior span are needed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.6 What you should see A gap or a fibrous band between the long process and the stapes head, with the malleus and stapes each moving freely."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-8","Remove the incus","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Remove more posterosuperior canal bone if needed. Free remaining attachments to the malleus head with a right-angled hook or curved needle, grasp the proximal long process with cup forceps, and extract it from the attic with a rotating, downward movement , without displacing the stapes or injuring the chorda. What you should see The incus out whole, the stapes undisturbed and upright, the chorda intact."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-9","Sculpt the incus","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Hold it in a fine haemostat or ossicle-holding forceps and shape it with the mastoid drill or a battery micro drill. Stapes and malleus nearly in one vertical plane: height is needed. Drill an acetabulum for the stapes head into the short process , and a groove for the malleus handle in the articular surface of the body. Malleus anterior to the stapes: height and horizontal span are needed. Drill the acetabulum into the body near the long process ; place it under the handle, then lift and roll the malleus-incus complex onto the stapes. An oval socket can fit over the shoulders of the crura. A large height to bridge: a third configuration gains mostly height and some span. Figure 6 Sculpting the incus: (A) for vertical height, (B) some height but mostly horizontal span, (C) mostly height with some span. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.7 Figure 7 (A) Interposition gaining some height and mostly span. (B) The sculpted incus with an oval socket that fits snugly over the stapes (arrow). (C) In a left ear, the incus lying over the stapes and medial to the malleus handle, supported by the chorda (arrow). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.8 What you should see A cup that fits the stapes head and a groove that fits the handle, with no thin edge left to fracture."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-10","Set the incus in place","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Make sure the undersurface of the short process does not rest on the promontory. Placing the incus above or below the chorda tympani gives it extra support. What you should see The incus locked between stapes head and malleus handle, clear of the promontory and the canal wall, and the whole assembly moving when the malleus is touched."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-11","Erosion only at the joint","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","When only the incudostapedial joint is eroded, you need not remove the incus: an Applebaum hydroxylapatite prosthesis sits on the stapes and supports the remaining long process, or hydroxylapatite bone cement can reattach the long process to the stapes. Figure 8 An Applebaum prosthesis between the stapes and an eroded long process of the incus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.9 What you should see The long process still solid and the gap to the stapes head short enough to bridge."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-12","A synthetic PORP","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Use a PORP when the incus is unusable, badly eroded or infiltrated by cholesteatoma. Hydroxylapatite heads against the drum and titanium against the stapes are well tolerated. A dense hydroxylapatite head needs no cartilage between it and the drum; a titanium head does . Adjustable PORPs can be shortened to fit. What you should see The prosthesis cup seated squarely on the stapes head, its head flat under the drum or handle, standing upright without tension."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-13","The canal wall down cavity","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","With the malleus head removed, the stapes must still be connected to the handle, by interposition or prosthesis. The membrane can be grafted directly onto the stapes as Wullstein described, but a prosthesis gives more surface contact. To augment the stapes , drill a dome for the superstructure from the incus body, the malleus head, or a small cortical graft from the root of the zygoma or the squamous temporal bone; the concave face of a conchal cartilage graft also works. A PORP or a hydroxylapatite mushroom cap raises the stapes head and broadens its contact with the drum. Figure 9 Reconstruction in a canal wall down cavity: type III tympanoplasty with stapes augmentation, giving the stapes a larger area of contact with the drum or graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.10 What you should see The stapes head as the highest point in the cavity under the graft, or a prosthesis sitting on it with cartilage over the head."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-14","A partly eroded stapes: go to the footplate","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","A construct on a partly eroded arch or one crus will probably fail. Reconstruct from the undersurface of the malleus to the mobile footplate instead. Measure the distance before you sculpt. An incus may be long enough, using its short or long process to reach the footplate, with a groove in its articular surface to lock onto the medial malleus. With the long process kept, remove the lenticular process and drill the groove on the upper surface. Place it on the footplate between the remaining crura, rotate it into position while lifting the handle, and use the chorda to steady it. Figure 10 Sculpting the incus for a type IV tympanoplasty: (A) the short process flattened, the long process removed and a groove drilled for the malleus handle; (B) the long process flattened, with the notch for the handle on the upper surface. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.11 What you should see A mobile footplate with a round window reflex, and only a remnant of the arch."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-15","A TORP to the footplate","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","A deep, narrow niche often will not take an incus between promontory and facial nerve, so a TORP is usually needed; the authors use a dense hydroxylapatite head on an adjustable titanium shaft. Find the length with a sizing set . Steady the shaft with wet Gelfoam up to the level of the facial nerve if the handle is present, or around the whole prosthesis up to the drum when it touches the drum directly. Fascia or a footplate shoe can also support the base. Figure 11 Type IV tympanoplasty from the footplate to the malleus with an incus-stapes replacement (total) prosthesis. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.12 What you should see The shaft centred on the footplate and perpendicular to it, clear of the facial nerve and promontory, the head under the handle or drum with cartilage over it."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-16","A fixed footplate","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","The commonest cause is otosclerosis (see the otosclerosis chapter). If you find a fixed footplate while repairing a perforation, stage it : do not open the vestibule into a possibly contaminated ear. Tympanosclerosis of the footplate is managed the same way when there is a perforation or chronic otitis media: confirm fixation by gently palpating the stapes and watching for a round window reflex , repair the drum, and return in 4 to 6 months . Options then are an aid, mobilisation, removal of the plaque by microdissection, or partial stapedectomy with a prosthesis. If the disease is stable, hearing after mobilisation is similar in the short and long term, so refixation seems unlikely; stapedectomy in a dry, sterile ear is also safe and effective. What you should see No movement of the stapes and no round window reflex on gentle palpation. The tympanosclerotic stapes Excessive manipulation of a stapes encased in tympanosclerosis can fracture it, pull it out or open the footplate."],["osec","th:op-132~s3-17","An open vestibule","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · The operation, stage by stage","Cholesteatoma can erode the footplate, and if the vestibule is open, by disease or by accident, reconstruct now. An intact incus is rare with such disease, so reconstruct from the vestibule to the malleus, drum or graft (type Vb). Cover the vestibule with a thin but strong graft: true temporalis fascia, air-dried and cut to about 3 to 4 mm. Seat the shaft in the centre of the graft and the head under the handle or against the grafted drum. A grafted drum may settle more medially under negative middle ear pressure, putting more load on the oval window graft; if the prosthesis might sink into the vestibule, use perichondrium from the back of the concha or the tragus instead. Figure 12 Type Vb tympanoplasty with a total prosthesis: the shaft embedded in a graft over the vestibule, the head tenting the grafted drum, with no malleus handle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 132.13 What you should see The vestibule covered edge to edge by the graft, with no perilymph welling round it, and the shaft resting on the graft's centre."],["osec","th:op-132~s4-1","Crisis drills","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · When it goes wrong","Unexpected findings once the flap is up 1 Fixed footplate with a perforation repair the drum and stage the footplate at 4 to 6 months 2 Unknown final graft position stage with Silastic sheeting and reconstruct at about 6 months 3 Partly eroded stapes build to the footplate, not to the remaining crus 4 Vestibule opened by disease or accident seal with fascia or perichondrium and reconstruct now"],["osec","th:op-132~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Missing lateral chain fixation because the malleus and incus were not palpated. A PORP on a partly eroded stapes, which cannot support it. Rough handling of a tympanosclerotic stapes. Too little tension between the prosthesis and the drum or cartilage, so it moves. Removing too much scutum without a cartilage block, inviting attic retraction and cholesteatoma. A prosthesis resting on the promontory or touching the canal wall."],["osec","th:op-132~s5-1","After the operation","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Aftercare","Keep the ear dry. No nose blowing or Valsalva, and no flying or other large pressure changes for several weeks. Limit vigorous head movement; keep children from gymnastics and similar activity. After a type V reconstruction to the vestibule, no heavy lifting."],["osec","th:op-132~s5-2","Packing and review","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Aftercare","With an intact drum, pack with Gelfoam or a silk sleeve held by small cotton balls with ointment, removed at 7 to 10 days . After a lateral graft tympanoplasty, an outer and an inner rosebud pack: remove the outer at 1 week and start drops, the inner a week later, and continue drops twice daily for another week. Review a few weeks later. When the drum is thin, mobile and no longer inflamed, test the hearing. In a type III or IV tympanoplasty, a residual conductive loss of 15 to 20 dB still counts as success ."],["osec","th:op-132~s5-3","Complications","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Hearing loss Conductive or sensorineural, temporary or permanent; failure to improve Ossicular trauma Dislocation, a fractured stapes, unresolved conductive loss Inner ear trauma Perilymphatic fistula, sensorineural loss, tinnitus, vertigo and imbalance Prosthesis problems Displacement or extrusion (too long, or a poor Eustachian tube) Drum and canal Perforation, canal stenosis, middle ear infection, cholesteatoma Nerves Altered or lost taste; facial weakness Other Bleeding, infection, pain, CSF leak, revision surgery"],["ev","th:op-132~s5-4","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction: current evidence","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Aftercare","Predicting results. The Austin–Kartush classification and the Middle Ear Risk Index predict hearing outcome; a mobile stapes superstructure is the strongest predictor of success. Prostheses. Series show better results with a partial prosthesis (PORP) on the stapes than a total prosthesis (TORP) on the footplate, with titanium and hydroxyapatite giving similar results; a cartilage cap between prosthesis and drum reduces extrusion to a few per cent. Bone cement bridges small incus defects with results comparable to prostheses. Staging reconstruction at a second-look operation is reasonable when disease or mucosa is poor."],["osec","th:op-132~s5-4","Alternatives","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · Aftercare","Regular aural toilet for chronic otitis media with a perforation. A conventional hearing aid or a bone-anchored hearing aid."],["osec","th:op-132~s6-1","The evidence","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · For the examination","In a retrospective review of 110 patients, success correlated with the state of the middle ear , not with the type of prosthesis or technique. Staging to let the drum settle and the middle ear heal has become more important in chronic otitis media and cholesteatoma, and Eustachian tube function governs the long-term result."],["osec","th:op-132~s6-2","Viva essentials","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The Wullstein types and Farrior's modification. PORP versus TORP, and incus interposition. The rules: slight tension, no bony contact, anticipate the drum's final position. The flap that exposes the whole chain. How to sculpt the incus for each malleus-stapes relationship. Why a partly eroded stapes is bypassed to the footplate. When to stage , and how long to wait (about 6 months; 4 to 6 months after drum repair). Cartilage under a titanium head, not under hydroxylapatite. Success: a residual gap of 15 to 20 dB in types III and IV. Superior canal dehiscence : a conductive loss with the reflexes present."],["osec","th:op-132~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Ossicular Chain Reconstruction · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not checking the other ear and the Eustachian tube. Not having the right prostheses in theatre. Opening the vestibule in a wet or contaminated ear. Building for a drum position that will change."],["osec","th:op-133~s1-1","The case at a glance","Otosclerosis · Operating note","Stapedotomy and stapedectomy for otosclerosis Summary Indications Progressive conductive loss with absent stapedial reflexes ; an air-bone gap over 20 dB in at least three frequencies ; tympanosclerotic footplate fixation; traumatic fracture or subluxation of the footplate Contraindications A perforation (and grommets, which must be removed and the drum healed first); a gap under 20 dB; Ménière's disease (relative: more vertigo and sensorineural loss); active external or middle ear disease; the only or better hearing ear Anaesthesia Local with sedation (you can test the hearing and hear about vertigo, but the patient may startle and move) or general (easier positioning, no unexpected movement, but no hearing check); decided by mobility and comorbidity Position Supine, head turned away; table tilt, a horseshoe headrest or a bump under the torso and hip when the neck will not turn; pad arms and knees; prepare the other hand's dorsum if a vein graft is planned Antibiotics Not indicated Monitoring No facial monitor; under general anaesthesia avoid long-acting relaxants so the nerve can be stimulated if needed Tray Otological set; stapes curettes, measuring rod, footplate picks; laser and micro drill (such as a Skeeter); crimper; microscope; stapes prostheses Success Air-bone gap closed to under 10 dB (Committee on Hearing and Equilibrium)"],["osec","th:op-133~s1-2","Consent","Otosclerosis · Operating note","What to tell the patient Hearing: surgery may fail to help, and rarely hearing gets worse. In about 1% the operated ear suffers a severe loss that cannot be corrected. Mixed loss: improvement stops at the level of your nerve hearing. Tinnitus may be louder afterwards, usually when the hearing is worse; surgery does not usually cure it. Dizziness from irritation of the inner ear; some unsteadiness for weeks; rarely severe or prolonged. Taste disturbance and a dry mouth are common for a few weeks, prolonged or permanent in about 5% . Facial weakness: rare and usually temporary. The prosthesis may be rejected or move, needing another operation. A hole in the drum , usually healing by itself. Infection , rarely spreading to cause meningitis; a leak of brain fluid ; meningitis, brain abscess and stroke are now very rare. Afterwards: no flying until healed, no heavy lifting or nose blowing; plan time off work if your job involves flying or lifting. The alternative is a hearing aid, which you can try first."],["osec","th:op-133~s1-3","Before you start","Otosclerosis · Operating note","Otosclerosis is a disease of the otic capsule with resorption, new bone and new vessels; it fixes the ossicles, usually at the footplate or the incudostapedial joint. Most cases are familial, autosomal dominant with variable penetrance, twice as common in women, with a prevalence of 0.2 to 0.4% and onset usually in the third decade (teens to sixties). It is commonest in white people of European descent, increasingly diagnosed but milder in Japanese and Chinese patients, and found histologically ten times as often as it causes symptoms. Pregnancy can bring it on. The loss is usually conductive, unilateral or bilateral, rarely mixed or purely sensorineural from cochlear disease, often with tinnitus, and it always progresses . Ask about vertigo, especially suggesting Ménière's disease or superior canal dehiscence (vertigo with loud sound, the Tullio phenomenon); fitness for anaesthesia; alcohol or substance misuse; previous ear surgery (previous stapes surgery worsens hearing outcomes and raises the risk of vertigo); relatives with hearing loss starting in middle age or treated by surgery; anticoagulants (stop about 7 days before); seasonal allergies (time the surgery and treat them); ability to give consent; and a job involving frequent flying or heavy lifting. The ear usually looks normal. Examine both under the microscope to confirm normal canals and healthy drums and exclude other causes. A faint redness on the promontory ( Schwartze sign ) reflects active, vascular otospongiosis. With 256 and 512 Hz forks, a negative Rinne and a Weber to the worse ear imply a gap of at le"],["osec","th:op-133~s1-4","Audiometry and imaging","Otosclerosis · Operating note","Air-bone gap: at least 20 dB before you offer surgery; less, and you manage it conservatively and review later. Carhart notch: a dip in bone conduction at 2000 Hz, thought to be from loss of the ossicular chain's resonance. It is suggestive, not diagnostic: effusion and malleus fixation can mimic it. Imaging is not needed routinely. CT is indicated when the reflexes are present, to exclude dehiscence. CT may show lucency of the capsule near the oval or round window in active disease, and extensive cochlear demineralisation as the double ring (halo) sign , often with a mixed loss. Gadolinium MRI may enhance active foci. Figure 1 Right conductive loss with a dip in bone conduction at 2000 Hz: the Carhart notch. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.1 Figure 2 Extensive cochlear demineralisation giving the double ring or halo sign. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.2"],["osec","th:op-133~s1-5","Preparation","Otosclerosis · Operating note","Check neck mobility , which you need to position the head. Check the canal: one that takes a 5 mm speculum is enough; a narrower canal or a big anterior bulge may need a postauricular approach or a releasing incision at 12 o'clock in the incisura. Always operate on the poorer hearing ear , and never on the only hearing ear."],["osec","th:op-133~s2-1","The five landmarks","Otosclerosis · Anatomy you must see","The horizontal segment of the facial nerve . The pyramidal eminence . The promontory . The malleus and the cochleariform process . The round window , if it can be seen. With all five in view, the stapes and the lenticular process of the incus are well enough exposed. Keep the chorda tympani in sight throughout. Figure 3 Adequate middle ear exposure: the malleus handle, the facial nerve, the pyramidal process and the round window niche. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.5"],["osec","th:op-133~s3-1","Inject the canal","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline, or 2% with 1:50,000, through a 27 G needle, slowly so the skin does not balloon, circumferentially and finishing with the anterior canal so blood does not run into the field. Under the microscope you should see the blanch run down onto the drum. Inject even under general anaesthesia, for the vasoconstriction. What you should see The canal skin blanching down onto the drum without ballooning."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-2","Make a long enough flap","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Incise at 12 and 6 o'clock and join the incisions in the posterolateral canal. The flap must be long enough to cover the bone you will remove from the scutum. Elevate it, and lift the annulus inferiorly so you do not injure the chorda; identify the chorda. Figure 4 The correct canal incision, which allows enough bone removal and exposure of the ossicular chain. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.3 What you should see A flap that will cover the scutum once bone is removed, the annulus lifted inferiorly, and the chorda tympani. A short flap If the flap is too short it cannot cover the posterior canal wall once you have curetted the scutum."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-3","Curette the scutum","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Curette the posterosuperior canal wall until the five landmarks are in view, protecting the chorda: leaving a thin lip of bone beside it until the end helps. Figure 5 Curetting the posterosuperior bony canal wall, preserving the chorda tympani. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.4 What you should see The five landmarks: the horizontal facial nerve, the pyramidal eminence, the promontory, the malleus or cochleariform process and the round window niche."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-4","Palpate the chain","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Lift the malleus gently laterally and watch the incus; then press the distal incus and look for compression at the incudostapedial joint or movement of the stapes. What you should see The malleus and incus moving freely; the stapes fixed, with no round window reflex."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-5","Separate the incudostapedial joint","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","With a joint knife or angled pick, separate the joint from posterior to anterior , so that the stapedius tendon braces the stapes against your pull. Then palpate again to confirm that it is the stapes that is fixed and that the lateral chain moves. Figure 6 Separating the incudostapedial joint while preserving the lenticular process of the incus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.6 What you should see A clean joint space with the lenticular process intact, and a lateral chain that now moves freely. Cutting the lenticular process Feel for the joint space before you cut. The novice assumes the joint is more lateral than it is and cuts through the lenticular process, which matters most when a bucket prosthesis will hang on it."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-6","Harvest a graft if needed","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","For a large stapedotomy or a stapedectomy, take soft tissue or a vein graft and let it dry. Mark and inject a straight, unbranched vein on the back of the opposite hand, cut through the dermis across its line, spread bluntly with tenotomy scissors along the vein, and tie it off with 4-0 Vicryl or chromic; 1 cm gives room to spare. Clean off fat and adventitia, thread it over one blade of the scissors, open the lumen and lay it out to dry. Figure 7 Vein graft: (A) a 2 cm transverse incision on the back of the hand; (B) removing connective tissue; (C) opening the vein. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.7 What you should see A flat, clean sheet of vein laid out to dry."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-7","Test the laser and make a control hole","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Test the laser on a tongue depressor: it should be focused, aligned with the aiming beam and burning well. Make a small control hole in the footplate. It shows you a gusher early, and it gives a hook or pick a way to retrieve the footplate if it floats. Figure 8 Fenestrating the superior central footplate with the laser. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.8 What you should see A small dark hole in the footplate with perilymph level in it, not welling out. Why the control hole comes first A floating footplate follows down-fracture of the arch when the remaining annular ligament gives way. A hole made before the fracture lets you hook the footplate out with a right-angled pick."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-8","Divide the tendon and the posterior crus","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the stapedius tendon with the laser or Bellucci scissors, then vaporise the posterior crus with the laser. Figure 9 Dividing the stapedius tendon. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.9 Figure 10 Vaporising the posterior crus with the laser. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.10 What you should see The tendon divided and the posterior crus gone, with the footplate unmoved."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-9","Down-fracture the superstructure","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","With a Rosen pick, fracture the superstructure inferiorly toward the promontory , push it down onto the promontory and remove it with cup or alligator forceps. Figure 11 Down-fracturing the stapes superstructure toward the promontory: (A) the surgeon's view; (B) in relation to the facial nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.11 What you should see The superstructure lifted out in one piece and a still, intact footplate."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-10","Measure","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","For the classic bucket-handle prosthesis, measure from the vestibule to the medial edge of the incus ; if you measure before the stapedotomy, measure from the footplate to the middle of the incus's thickness . Figure 12 Measuring from the vestibule to the undersurface of the incus for the Robinson prosthesis. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.12 What you should see The same measurement taken twice."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-11","Make the stapedotomy","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Enlarge the control hole by vaporising its edges in a rosette , which avoids loose footplate fragments; remove fragments or widen the opening with a footplate pick or rasp. Figure 13 Removing the central area of the footplate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.13 What you should see A round, clean opening with perilymph level with its rim and no loose fragments. Never suck over the vestibule Even with blood in the niche, point the suction away from the open vestibule. Suction over the vestibule raises the risk of vertigo and sensorineural hearing loss."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-12","Lay the vein graft","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Trim the graft to clean edges, about 5 × 4 mm , so it covers the opening completely and drapes onto the facial nerve and promontory. Press its centre down over the hole, to show you where the prosthesis must go. Figure 14 The vein graft over the stapedotomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.14 What you should see The graft covering the whole opening and draped onto the facial canal and promontory, with a dimple over the hole."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-13","Seat the prosthesis","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Hold the posterior lip of the bucket in alligator forceps with the handle below, and set the piston into the dimple in the graft. Rest the bucket against the incus and let go. Steady the incus with a right-angled hook, put a Rosen needle in the well of the bucket and push gently medially, so that the bucket slips under the lenticular process. Lift the handle over the distal incus; if the bail is loose, a small piece of vein over the handle secures it. Figure 15 Placing the Robinson prosthesis two-handed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.15 What you should see The shaft upright in the dimple and the bucket squarely under the lenticular process, handle over the incus."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-14","Check and close","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Check that the prosthesis moves: look for a round window reflex . If the patient is awake, test the hearing. Replace the flap carefully with no curled edges, and lay Gelfoam soaked in ofloxacin over the incision. What you should see A prosthesis seated in the graft and under the lenticular process, moving with the incus, and a round window reflex when you touch the malleus."],["osec","th:op-133~s3-15","A crimped wire piston instead","Otosclerosis · The operation, stage by stage","Steps up to the measurement are the same. Make the opening 0.1 mm larger than the piston , set the piston in the fenestra, and crimp the wire or ribbon over the incus with a crimper; a Nitinol piston is tightened by heating its hook with the laser so it curls around the incus. Some surgeons seal the fenestra with a drop of fresh blood or Gelfoam. Check and close as above. What you should see The piston centred in the fenestra and the loop closed on the long process: no rattle, but still able to rotate. Crimping A loose crimp lets the prosthesis move and erode the long process; an overtight crimp causes necrosis of the long process. Figure 16 A left ear: a crimp-on prosthesis that has eroded the long process of the incus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.16"],["osec","th:op-133~s4-1","Crisis drills","Otosclerosis · When it goes wrong","Floating footplate 1 Prevent a control hole before down-fracture 2 Retrieve a right-angled hook through the control hole without one, drill a small rescue hole in the promontory edge to engage a large fragment 3 Small fragments leave them they rarely affect hearing, though they may add positional vertigo 4 Do not make repeated attempts or suck over the vestibule 5 Cannot remove it lay the graft and prosthesis and finish Figure 17 (A) A small rescue hole for a floating footplate; (B) a fine right-angled pick through it to retrieve the footplate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.17 Perilymph gusher 1 Cause a widely patent cochlear aqueduct 2 Reduce head up (reverse Trendelenburg) some suck until the reservoir in the cerebellopontine cistern is exhausted 3 Seal fascia, fat or vein over the oval window, held by the prosthesis 4 Rarely a lumbar drain afterwards Figure 18 (A) A gusher of perilymph through the fenestra; (B) head up to relieve it; (C) a piston with an adipose graft sealing it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 133.18 Perforation of the drum during surgery 1 Small medial graft tympanoplasty now, and continue 2 Large repair the drum only, and come back for the stapes later, to avoid contaminating the vestibule Chorda tympani in the way or injured 1 Tortuous and blocking the view consider dividing it completely 2 Divided or injured lay the ends together at the end most patients have temporary taste change for some months"],["osec","th:op-133~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Otosclerosis · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls A flap too short to cover the curetted canal wall. Starting without all the landmarks in view. Misidentifying the joint and cutting the lenticular process. Moving the incus too much, which dislocates it. Too loose or too tight a crimp. Suction near the vestibule."],["osec","th:op-133~s5-1","After the operation","Otosclerosis · Aftercare","Usually a day case; keep frail or immobile older patients overnight. Avoid brisk head movement, straining, heavy lifting and nose blowing, and do not fly until the ear has healed. Remove the packing at 1 to 2 weeks . Test the hearing at 1 to 3 months ; it may keep improving a little after that. Tell the patient what prosthesis they have and whether it is MRI-safe . Low-dose paracetamol with an opioid if needed; no antibiotics."],["osec","th:op-133~s5-2","Complications","Otosclerosis · Aftercare","Complications of stapes surgery Complication Notes Drum perforation Small: graft now; large: repair and stage the stapes Chorda tympani injury Temporary dysgeusia for months; avoid by careful flap, annulus and entry Facial nerve injury Rare; a dehiscent or overhanging nerve can still be managed with careful technique Ossicular dislocation Rough flap elevation or palpation; most often the incus pushed laterally while placing the prosthesis Vertigo and sensorineural loss Rough handling of the stapes, a misplaced prosthesis, or loss of perilymph Floating footplate Prevented by the control hole; see the drill Gusher See the drill Reparative granuloma About 0.1%, at 1 to 6 weeks, with vertigo, sensorineural loss and roaring tinnitus; steroids and antibiotics, or exploration with removal of the granulation, prosthesis and graft Prosthesis erosion or extrusion Crimp problems; rejection"],["ev","th:op-133~s5-3","Otosclerosis: current evidence","Otosclerosis · Aftercare","Results. Small-fenestra stapedotomy closes the air–bone gap to within 10 dB in about 90% of primary cases in experienced hands, with total sensorineural loss in under 1%; revision surgery has lower success and higher risk. Laser and microdrill fenestration give similar results in comparative series. Far-advanced disease. A review-based strategy recommends stapedotomy with a hearing aid first when aided speech discrimination is reasonable, and cochlear implantation otherwise (Merkus et al., Laryngoscope 2011). Fluoride for cochlear otosclerosis has limited, low-quality evidence (Hentschel et al., Otol Neurotol 2014). Hearing aid should always be offered as the alternative."],["osec","th:op-133~s5-3","Alternatives","Otosclerosis · Aftercare","A hearing aid , which also lets an undecided patient function while deciding. Sodium fluoride , and rarely bisphosphonates, for cochlear or progressive disease: without good level 1 evidence, but worth discussing."],["osec","th:op-133~s6-1","Stapedotomy, lasers, grafts and prostheses","Otosclerosis · For the examination","Stapedectomy strictly means removing the whole footplate; most surgeons now perform a stapedotomy , a small fenestra leaving most of it. The authors use a CO2 laser : entering the vestibule this way seems less traumatic and less likely to mobilise the footplate than a drill or a pick, and CO2 energy is absorbed by water so it does not penetrate deeply into the vestibule. Systematic reviews suggest lasers bring less sensorineural loss and fewer footplate fractures, though the studies are biased and varied, and no laser type has been shown better than another (Er:YAG and CO2 were similar). Teflon-wire pistons and stainless steel buckets gave similar hearing; a non-randomised study favoured vein grafts, but technique differed between the groups; fat, fascia and vein did equally well. No method or prosthesis is clearly superior ; the surgeon's familiarity with a technique probably matters most."],["osec","th:op-133~s6-2","Viva essentials","Otosclerosis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Pathology, genetics and epidemiology; Schwartze sign; the Carhart notch and why it is not diagnostic. Indications (gap over 20 dB at three frequencies, absent reflexes) and contraindications (perforation, only or better hearing ear, Ménière's, active disease). Reflexes present: exclude superior canal dehiscence with CT. No imaging is required routinely. The five landmarks ; the Eustachian tube is not one of them. Joint separation from back to front, and why. The control hole; the floating footplate; the gusher. Never suction over the vestibule. Success: gap under 10 dB."],["osec","th:op-133~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Otosclerosis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on the better or only hearing ear. Missing a third window because the reflexes were not checked. Operating with a perforation or a grommet in place. Forgetting to warn a mixed-loss patient of the limits. Chasing small footplate fragments."],["osec","th:op-134~s1-1","The case at a glance","Mastoid Surgery · Operating note","Mastoidectomy Summary Indications Cholesteatoma of the attic or mastoid; chronic suppurative mastoiditis without cholesteatoma; acute coalescent mastoiditis, with or without temporal bone or intracranial complications; access for cochlear implantation, facial nerve surgery (decompression, tumour, grafting), endolymphatic sac surgery, labyrinthectomy, temporal bone resection and approaches to the posterior fossa and petrous apex Canal wall up (intact) Drainage of acute mastoiditis; cholesteatoma that can be fully removed or will have a planned second look; implant and facial nerve surgery Canal wall down Small, sclerotic mastoid with limited exposure; low tegmen or forward sigmoid sinus; disease that cannot be fully removed and must be exteriorised; labyrinthine fistula; matrix on an eroded footplate or a widely dehiscent facial nerve; an only hearing ear; unreliable follow-up; revision surgery with higher risk Contraindications Unfit for general anaesthesia; poor wound healing; cholesteatoma extending beyond the temporal bone (mastoidectomy alone is inadequate). An auto-atticotomy or auto-mastoidectomy cavity may be managed by cleaning in clinic Anaesthesia General, without muscle relaxation after induction, whether or not the facial nerve is monitored; controlled hypotension helps Position Supine, head turned away, neck neutral or slightly flexed; strapped to the table at several points because the table will be rotated; facial nerve electrodes placed and tested Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin; clindamycin or vancomycin in penicillin allergy Tray Tympanoplasty a"],["osec","th:op-134~s1-2","Consent","Mastoid Surgery · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove infection or skin trapped in the ear (cholesteatoma) and make the ear safe and dry. Hearing is a secondary aim and may not improve; it may be worse. Further surgery: a planned second look after 9 to 12 months, a staged hearing reconstruction, or revision for recurrence. A mastoid cavity if the canal wall is taken down: a wider ear opening, lifelong cleaning in clinic, care with water, and sometimes dizziness with cold air or water. Hearing loss , including a small risk of total loss in that ear, and tinnitus . Dizziness , usually temporary. Facial weakness: under 1% in a first operation, about 4% to 10% in revisions; usually temporary when it occurs. Altered taste on that side of the tongue, often recovering over months. Leak of brain fluid, meningitis or bleeding from the large vein behind the ear: rare. Wound problems , numbness of the ear, and a change in the position of the pinna after meatoplasty. A hearing aid may still be needed."],["osec","th:op-134~s1-3","Before you start","Mastoid Surgery · Operating note","Ask about the duration and pattern of discharge, hearing loss, otalgia and dizziness; trauma, water exposure and barotrauma; response to treatment; and red flags: rapid progression, headache or altered mental state (intracranial complication), facial weakness and vertigo. Take the otological past: childhood infections and surgery, previous mastoid surgery or implants, noise, ototoxic drugs, radiotherapy and hearing aid use. The other ear decides a great deal: bilateral cholesteatoma, a contralateral perforation or hearing loss change how conservative you must be. Assess the patient's ability to attend for cavity care and second-stage surgery. Examine both ears under the microscope after cleaning, and again after treating infection: an attic retraction pocket, keratin, a polyp, erosion of the scutum, the state of the pars tensa and a narrow canal that will need canalplasty. Record Rinne and Weber. Examine the nasopharynx, the cranial nerves and the fundi. A postauricular swelling, an acute facial palsy or papilloedema needs urgent action. Perform a fistula test when vertigo is reported. Obtain bilateral audiometry (auditory brainstem responses in a child who cannot be tested), but never delay emergency surgery for it. CT is the anatomical map: on axial cuts, the pneumatisation and the position of the sigmoid sinus; on coronal cuts, the level of the tegmen; and erosion of the lateral semicircular canal, tegmen, facial canal and ossicles. CT cannot reliably show the stapes or tell cholesteatoma from fluid or mucosa unless bone is eroded. MRI shows encephalocele (coronal T2), i"],["osec","th:op-134~s2-1","On the surface of the bone","Mastoid Surgery · Anatomy you must see","The linea temporalis (supramastoid crest): the ridge running back from the root of the zygoma at the lower edge of the temporalis; it marks roughly the level of the middle fossa dura . The spine of Henle: the small spur at the posterosuperior margin of the bony meatus. MacEwen's (suprameatal) triangle, with the cribriform area of small vessel holes: bounded by the linea temporalis above, the posterosuperior meatal wall in front and a tangent to the back of the canal behind. The antrum lies deep to it, about 12 to 15 mm in the adult and only a few millimetres in an infant. The mastoid tip, with the digastric groove on its inner side."],["osec","th:op-134~s2-2","In the cavity","Mastoid Surgery · Anatomy you must see","The tegmen mastoideum, the plate of bone under the middle fossa dura: it shows as a pink blush through thin bone, sometimes with small veins. The sigmoid sinus, behind and medial: a blue blush through its bony plate. Between them is the sinodural angle . Koerner's septum (the petrosquamous lamina), a plate of bone that can be mistaken for the medial wall of the antrum; the true antrum is deep to it. The lateral (horizontal) semicircular canal: a smooth, dense, ivory-coloured dome in the floor of the antrum. It is the most reliable landmark in the mastoid. The short process of the incus in the fossa incudis , seen in front of the canal through the aditus. The digastric ridge at the tip: its front end leads to the stylomastoid foramen."],["osec","th:op-134~s2-3","The facial nerve","Mastoid Surgery · Anatomy you must see","The tympanic segment runs back above the oval window, below the lateral canal. The second genu lies just below and medial to the dome of the lateral canal, at the posterior edge of the oval window and medial to the short process of the incus, which points to it; this is the segment injured most often. The mastoid (vertical) segment descends from there to the stylomastoid foramen at the front of the digastric ridge, lying deeper above and more lateral as it descends. The chorda tympani leaves it a few millimetres above the foramen and runs up and forward to the annulus. The facial recess is the triangle between the facial nerve medially, the chorda laterally and the incus buttress above."],["osec","th:op-134~s3-1","Set up, inject and incise","Mastoid Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Prepare the field, check the monitor, and examine and clean the ear under the microscope to confirm the findings. If a tympanomeatal flap will be raised, inject lidocaine with adrenaline in four quadrants at the bony-cartilaginous junction (the medial limit of the hair-bearing skin), bevel to the bone and subperiosteal, until the skin blanches down to the drum; then make canal incisions at 12 and 6 o'clock joined by a vertical cut halfway between the annulus and the junction, forming a laterally based vascular strip and a medially based tympanomeatal flap. Make the postauricular incision a few millimetres behind the crease, from above the root of the helix to the tip, down to the loose areolar tissue over the temporalis fascia. In children, keep the lower limb further back and off the tip. Figure 1 The H-shaped canal incisions: a laterally based vascular strip and a medially based tympanomeatal flap. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.1 What you should see Canal skin blanched to the drum, and the glistening temporalis fascia with loose areolar tissue over it. The child's facial nerve The mastoid tip is undeveloped in small children and the nerve leaves the skull superficially. Curve the incision back and stop short of the tip."],["osec","th:op-134~s3-2","Expose the cortex and the landmarks","Mastoid Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the periosteum along the linea temporalis with a vertical limb to the tip (a \"T\" or \"7\"), elevate it forward to expose the posterior bony canal, lift the vascular strip, and retract the pinna forward with a tape or Penrose passed through the canal. Harvest temporalis fascia now and set it to dry. Identify the linea temporalis, the spine of Henle and the cribriform area. What you should see The whole mastoid cortex from the root of the zygoma to the tip, the posterior canal wall, and MacEwen's triangle with its small pits. Senior's pearl Exposure decides the operation. If you cannot see the zygomatic root and the tip, you will drill a deep narrow hole, which is how facial nerves are injured."],["osec","th:op-134~s3-3","Make the first cuts and saucerise","Mastoid Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","With the largest cutting bur that fits and continuous suction irrigation, make the first cut along the linea temporalis , the second parallel to the posterior canal wall , and join them behind along the line of the sigmoid sinus , forming a triangle. Remove the cortex and the air cells within it in long strokes parallel to the structure you are approaching, bevelling (saucerising) the edges widely so light and instruments reach the depth. Figure 2 The first bone cuts on the cortex outline the linea temporalis, the posterior canal wall and the sigmoid sinus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.2 What you should see A wide, shallow, kidney-shaped cavity with sloping edges, deepest at its front and top. A deep hole Keep the deepest point over the antrum , behind the spine of Henle. Beginners drift down and back, away from the antrum and into the plane of the facial nerve and the sigmoid sinus. If you cannot find the antrum, go forward and up, never down."],["osec","th:op-134~s3-4","Define the tegmen and the sinus","Mastoid Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Thin the bone above until the tegmen shows as a pink plate, and behind until the sigmoid sinus shows blue through its plate, leaving a thin shell on each. Open the sinodural angle between them. These two plates are the upper and posterior walls of your cavity and are followed forward to the antrum. What you should see A smooth pink tegmen above, a blue sinus plate behind, and the posterior canal wall thinned in front. Dura and sinus A cutting bur run across exposed dura or the sinus tears it. Change to a diamond bur as you approach them, and stroke along them, not into them."],["osec","th:op-134~s3-5","Enter the antrum","Mastoid Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Using the tegmen as the roof and the canal wall as the front, remove Koerner's septum and enter the antrum. Confirm it by its size and by what lies in its floor: the dense, pale dome of the lateral semicircular canal . What you should see A large air space or sac of disease, and in its floor the smooth ivory bulge of the lateral canal. Senior's pearl If you think you are in the antrum but cannot see the lateral canal, you are probably still lateral to Koerner's septum. The antrum is deeper than you expect."],["osec","th:op-134~s3-6","Open the aditus and find the incus","Mastoid Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Change to a smaller bur and drill forward toward the root of the zygoma, between the tegmen and the canal wall, keeping the bur turning away from the ossicles, until the short process of the incus appears in the fossa incudis. Flood the cavity: the incus is often first seen as a refraction under the water. Remove remaining cells from the tip, exposing the digastric ridge , and from the sinodural angle, as the disease requires. Figure 3 A completed intact canal wall mastoidectomy: the aditus, the short process of the incus, the lateral semicircular canal and the digastric ridge. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.3 What you should see The incus in its fossa, the lateral canal behind and below it, the digastric ridge at the tip, and an intact thin posterior canal wall. Touching the incus A rotating bur touching the incus transmits its energy to the inner ear and causes high-tone sensorineural loss. Leave a sliver of bone over the short process and remove it with a pick or curette."],["osec","th:op-134~s3-7","Close","Mastoid Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Replace the vascular strip and pack the canal with Gelfoam or Telfa. Close the periosteum (which stops the scar retracting into the cavity), then the deep dermis with 3-0 or 4-0 absorbable sutures and the skin with a fine absorbable suture. Apply a mastoid dressing for 24 hours. Count the pledgets. What you should see The periosteum closed over the cavity, the vascular strip back in the canal and every pledget counted."],["osec","th:op-134~s4-1","Thin the canal wall and set the landmarks","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)","The recess is bounded by the incus buttress above, the facial nerve medially and behind, and the chorda tympani laterally and in front. Before entering it, have the lateral canal, the fossa incudis and the digastric ridge in view, and thin the posterior canal wall well: a thick wall hides the recess and forces you medially toward the nerve. What you should see The short process of the incus pointing to the recess, and a canal wall thin enough to see instruments through."],["osec","th:op-134~s4-2","Find the nerve and open the recess","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)","With a small diamond bur and copious irrigation, drill just below the incus buttress in strokes parallel to the course of the facial nerve , never across it. Identify the nerve through a thin covering of bone as a pink-white cord with its fine vessels, and the chorda coming off it laterally. Enter the middle ear at the upper end of the recess, where it is widest. What you should see The facial nerve seen through bone medially, the chorda laterally, and a dark slit between them opening into the middle ear. Heat and the nerve A diamond bur generates heat quickly; without irrigation it burns the nerve through intact bone. Keep the water running and leave a shell of bone on the nerve."],["osec","th:op-134~s4-3","Widen and inspect","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: facial recess (posterior tympanotomy)","Widen the opening by removing bone medial to the chorda. Through it you see the incudostapedial joint, the stapes and stapedius tendon, the promontory and the round window niche. In an extended facial recess , the chorda is sacrificed and bone removed down to the inferior annulus to expose the hypotympanum and the medial surface of the drum. Figure 4 The middle ear through the completed facial recess: chorda tympani, incudostapedial joint, stapes, promontory and round window niche. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.4 What you should see The incudostapedial joint and round window niche, with the sinus tympani hidden medial to the nerve (use an angled endoscope for it). Senior's pearl Dissect disease off the stapes from back to front, in the direction of the stapedius tendon, which braces the stapes against your pull."],["osec","th:op-134~s5-1","Decide and deal with the incus","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: canal wall down","Complete the cortical mastoidectomy first. If the incus is diseased or the chain must be interrupted, separate the incudostapedial joint before taking the wall down, so that drilling on the bridge cannot be transmitted to the stapes. If the incus can stay, leave a thin bridge of bone over its short process and remove it with a pick or curette. What you should see A divided incudostapedial joint, or a protected incus under a thin bony bridge."],["osec","th:op-134~s5-2","Take down the wall","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: canal wall down","Remove the posterior canal wall with a 4 mm cutting bur. Think of it as three parts: the anterior buttress (at the tympanosquamous suture and the lateral attic wall), the posterior buttress (which becomes the facial ridge), and the bridge between them over the aditus. Remove the bridge, then lower both buttresses so the attic roof runs smoothly into the tegmen and the anterior attic is open. Figure 5 A completed canal wall down cavity. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 134.5 What you should see The attic, antrum and mastoid as one cavity with the canal, with no overhang at the anterior attic."],["osec","th:op-134~s5-3","Lower the facial ridge","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: canal wall down","Thin the bone over the second genu and vertical segment with a diamond bur, in strokes parallel to the nerve, until the ridge is level with the floor of the canal and the nerve is covered by only a thin plate. A high facial ridge leaves a sump behind it that collects debris and discharges for life. What you should see A low, smooth ridge with the nerve's course visible through bone, the lateral canal dome above it, and a round rather than kidney-shaped cavity. The second genu This is where the nerve is injured: just below the lateral canal, at the level of the fossa incudis. Above the canal you are safe; at and below it, use the diamond bur and the monitor."],["osec","th:op-134~s5-4","Saucerise and remove the disease","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: canal wall down","Bevel the upper and posterior edges of the cavity widely so the soft tissues fall in and reduce its volume, and remove the tip cells or amputate the tip so the cavity has no dependent pocket. Remove the cholesteatoma matrix in continuity from the mastoid forward. Over a fistula of the lateral canal , leave the matrix until the end, then either leave it in place (exteriorised) or lift it under irrigation and cover the fistula at once with fascia or bone pate. What you should see A shallow, smooth bowl with no overhanging edges and no cells left full of disease. The open labyrinth Suction on an open fistula, or peeling matrix off it carelessly, causes a dead ear. Never suction directly on it."],["osec","th:op-134~s5-5","Reconstruct and graft","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: canal wall down","Pack the middle ear with Gelfoam. Lay the dried fascia as an underlay graft, draped onto the tympanic facial nerve above and the facial ridge behind, to close off a shallow middle ear space, with ossicular reconstruction now or at a second stage. Pack lateral to the graft and drum remnant. What you should see The graft draped from the facial ridge to the tympanic facial nerve, closing a shallow middle ear under the drum remnant."],["osec","th:op-134~s5-6","Meatoplasty","Mastoid Surgery · The operation: canal wall down","Always perform a meatoplasty when the wall is taken down: the opening must let you see and clean the upper, posterior and lower parts of the bowl, and it will contract as it heals, so make it generous. Either extend the upper canal incision into the incisura and suture the conchal bowl back to the postauricular tissues with 3-0 absorbable sutures (too aggressive and the ear cups forward), or remove a crescent of conchal cartilage from behind, leaving the anterior skin, and suture the meatal skin back to the periosteum behind the cavity. Stent the meatus with rolled Telfa, an expanding wick or antibiotic gauze for 10 to 14 days. What you should see A meatus that admits a fingertip and shows the whole cavity with the microscope."],["osec","th:op-134~s6-1","Bondy modified radical mastoidectomy","Mastoid Surgery · Other operations","For an attic cholesteatoma that has grown lateral to the ossicles with an intact pars tensa and middle ear, often in an only or better hearing ear: the disease is exteriorised through an endaural approach, the anterior buttress removed and the superior canal wall lowered to the level of the incus, without entering the middle ear. The matrix is left as the lining of the exteriorised attic."],["osec","th:op-134~s6-2","Radical mastoidectomy","Mastoid Surgery · Other operations","For irreversible middle ear disease that a canal wall down procedure cannot control. After a canal wall down mastoidectomy, the drum remnant, malleus and incus are removed (the stapes is kept), the mucosa is removed, the Eustachian tube orifice is obliterated with muscle, fat or fascia, and no reconstruction is attempted: the whole middle ear is exteriorised into the cavity."],["osec","th:op-134~s6-3","Reconstruction and obliteration","Mastoid Surgery · Other operations","Rebuilding the canal wall gives the exposure of an open operation without a cavity, at the cost of foreign material in a contaminated field, and needs a second look. Obliterating the cavity makes long-term care easier but can bury matrix."],["osec","th:op-134~s7-1","Crisis drills","Mastoid Surgery · When it goes wrong","Bleeding from the sigmoid sinus or jugular bulb 1 Do not panic or pack blindly into the lumen take the bur away 2 Control a large piece of Surgicel or adrenaline-soaked Gelfoam over the hole with a flat pledget and gentle pressure lower the head slightly if air embolism is a concern 3 Continue leave the patch in place and work elsewhere bone wax for emissary vein bleeding Dura exposed or torn, CSF in the field 1 Exposed dura healthy exposed dura under 1 cm needs nothing 2 Defect over 1 cm or any tear repair now fascia under and over the defect, cartilage or bone for a large gap, tissue glue 3 Afterwards head up, no straining watch for meningitis Labyrinth opened (snail-track, endosteum or fluid seen) 1 Stop no suction on it 2 Seal cover at once with fascia, bone wax or bone pate 3 Afterwards steroids and antibiotics test hearing and document Facial weakness in recovery 1 Immediate, complete wait for local anaesthetic to wear off (a few hours) if it persists and you did not positively see an intact nerve, re-explore early 2 Complete, but you are sure the nerve is intact steroids, eye care, electroneuronography after 3 days explore if degeneration exceeds 90% 3 Integrity in doubt electromyography and CT re-explore, decompress, or graft 4 Delayed or partial loosen the packing, steroids, eye care recovery is the rule"],["osec","th:op-134~s8-1","After the operation","Mastoid Surgery · Aftercare","In recovery, check the face , a tuning fork (Weber should go to the operated ear) and for nystagmus . Most patients go home the same or next day with restricted activity, keeping the ear dry. Remove the meatal packing at 10 to 14 days and start antibiotic-steroid drops or rinses. An open cavity needs regular cleaning in clinic until it is fully epithelialised (often 6 to 12 weeks) and then at intervals for life. After an intact canal wall operation for cholesteatoma, plan a second look at 9 to 12 months or follow with diffusion-weighted MRI."],["osec","th:op-134~s8-2","Complications","Mastoid Surgery · Aftercare","Complications of mastoid surgery Complication Notes Bleeding, haematoma Usually the sigmoid sinus, jugular bulb or emissary vein; pressure with Surgicel or Gelfoam Facial paresis or palsy Under 1% in primary and 4% to 10% in revision surgery; the second genu is the commonest site; mastoid surgery accounts for most otological facial nerve injuries Conductive hearing loss Ossicular or drum injury; reconstruct Sensorineural loss, vertigo Drill on the incus, stapes subluxation, fistula of the lateral canal or opened labyrinth Dural injury, CSF leak, encephalocele Repair defects over 1 cm and all tears Altered taste Chorda stretched or cut; often recovers in months Wound infection, perichondritis Antibiotics; drain a collection Discharging cavity High facial ridge, inadequate saucerisation, small meatus, residual cells, tubal reflux Residual or recurrent cholesteatoma More frequent after intact canal wall surgery; second look or MRI Meatal stenosis, cupped ear Too small or too aggressive a meatoplasty"],["ev","th:op-134~s8-3","Mastoid Surgery: current evidence","Mastoid Surgery · Aftercare","Canal wall up or down. In a meta-analysis of 12 studies (2379 patients), canal wall down mastoidectomy with obliteration had lower recurrence than canal wall up, with better control of discharge than an open cavity (Salem et al., Am J Otolaryngol 2023); in children, a meta-analysis found no significant difference in recurrence or residual disease between canal wall up and down, with better hearing after canal wall up (Solis-Pazmino et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2023). Second look. Non-echo-planar diffusion-weighted MRI detects residual or recurrent cholesteatoma with about 94% sensitivity (Li et al., Laryngoscope 2013) and has replaced many planned second-look operations. Staging. The EAONO/JOS system stages cholesteatoma by the sites involved and complications. Facial nerve monitoring is widely used, especially in revision surgery, though it does not replace anatomical knowledge."],["osec","th:op-134~s8-3","Alternatives","Mastoid Surgery · Aftercare","If surgery for cholesteatoma is deferred (frail patient, only hearing ear), regular microsuction, home irrigation and intermittent topical or systemic antibiotics may control the disease."],["osec","th:op-134~s9-1","Where the books differ","Mastoid Surgery · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra describes three classical operations. Cortical (Schwartze) mastoidectomy exenterates the mastoid cells without disturbing the canal wall or middle ear, for acute coalescent mastoiditis and as an approach. Radical mastoidectomy makes the mastoid, middle ear, attic and antrum one cavity, removing the drum remnant, malleus and incus and closing the Eustachian tube. Modified radical mastoidectomy removes the disease and the canal wall but preserves as much of the drum, ossicles and hearing as possible. It gives the antrum's depth as about 12 to 15 mm beneath MacEwen's triangle, and stresses lowering the facial ridge, saucerisation and an adequate meatoplasty. Lee frames the choice as canal wall up versus canal wall down, with higher residual and recurrent disease after canal wall up surgery balanced against the lifelong cavity of the open technique. Myers gives the landmark-by-landmark sequence, the facial recess, and the evidence on facial nerve injury and monitoring."],["osec","th:op-134~s9-2","Viva essentials","Mastoid Surgery · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for MacEwen's triangle and the depth of the antrum. The landmarks in order: tegmen, sinus, Koerner's septum, antrum, lateral canal , incus, digastric ridge. The facial nerve landmarks, and where it is injured (second genu). Boundaries of the facial recess . Canal wall up versus down: indications and trade-offs. Why cavities discharge: high ridge, poor saucerisation, small meatus. Management of the lateral canal fistula and postoperative facial palsy."],["osec","th:op-134~s9-3","Common pitfalls","Mastoid Surgery · For the examination","Common pitfalls Drilling a deep, narrow hole instead of saucerising. Drifting down and back in search of the antrum. Mistaking Koerner's septum for the medial wall of the antrum. Touching the incus with the bur. A small bur, or a cutting bur, deep at the tip; a diamond bur without irrigation over the nerve. A high facial ridge and an inadequate meatoplasty. Using muscle relaxant with the nerve monitor on."],["osec","th:op-135~s1-1","The case at a glance","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · Operating note","Bone-anchored hearing device Summary Indications Conductive or mixed loss with a bone-conduction average of about 45 dB or better (55 dB for the most powerful processors) where a conventional aid or middle ear surgery is unsuitable: atresia, chronic discharge or a mastoid cavity, a closed canal after temporal bone resection, mould allergy. Single-sided deafness with a normal other ear (air-conduction average 20 dB or better) Contraindications Bone too thin or poor quality; inability to care for the site; unrealistic expectations; MRI needs (magnet devices) Trial Test the device on a softband or headband before surgery Anaesthesia Local or general (general in children) Position Supine, head turned, hair shaved around the site Choice Percutaneous abutment (stronger sound, skin care for life) or transcutaneous magnet (passive or active; intact skin, weaker passive transmission) Tray Device drill system (guide drill, countersink, fixture inserter), templates, soft tissue gauge, otology drill, bone wax, methylene blue"],["osec","th:op-135~s1-2","Consent","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · Operating note","What to tell the patient Skin reactions around a percutaneous abutment, from redness to overgrowth, are the commonest problem. Failure of osseointegration or later loss of the implant, needing revision or a new site. Numbness of the scalp, especially with a transcutaneous flap; pain or pressure under a magnet. Bleeding, rarely from the dura or sigmoid sinus; very rarely a CSF leak. MRI: magnets may need removal or cause artefact. The processor is fitted after healing: weeks for modern fixtures."],["osec","th:op-135~s1-3","Before you start","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · Operating note","Confirm the indication with pure tone and speech audiometry, and a trial on a softband: the patient should hear the difference before agreeing to an operation. In children, the skull must be thick enough (traditionally from about 5 years, staged under that). Choose the site 50 to 70 mm behind the canal, level with the top of the auricle, away from glasses, previous scars and where a future microtia reconstruction will go. Figure 1 For a transcutaneous device the site is marked and a C-shaped incision planned around it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 135.1"],["osec","th:op-135~s2-1","Mark and incise","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the site with the template and tattoo the bone through the skin with methylene blue on a fine needle. Inject local anaesthetic with adrenaline. For a transcutaneous magnet make a C-shaped incision at least 15 mm from the magnet, based posteriorly, and elevate above the periosteum until the dye is found. For a percutaneous abutment use a short linear incision beside the site, or a punch through the skin (the minimally invasive technique). Figure 2 For a percutaneous abutment a 2 to 3 cm incision exposes the bone beside the planned site. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 135.4 What you should see The blue mark on intact periosteum, on a flat area of squamous temporal bone."],["osec","th:op-135~s2-2","Choose the bone","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Make a cruciate incision in the periosteum and inspect the bone. Move a few millimetres if the surface dips: place the fixture on a \"mountain\", not in a \"valley\", so that a magnet can vibrate without touching bone. What you should see A flat, solid bone surface large enough for the fixture and its magnet."],["osec","th:op-135~s2-3","Drill the guide hole","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Use the guide drill with its spacer at about 2000 rpm under copious irrigation, perpendicular to the bone . Probe the hole: bone at the bottom is needed for integration. If 3 mm of bone is present, remove the spacer and drill to 4 mm; the 4 mm fixture is preferred in adults. Figure 3 The guide drill, held perpendicular, with copious irrigation. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 135.2 What you should see A clean hole with bone, not dura or sinus, at its base. The tilted fixture An angled guide hole tilts everything that follows: the countersink, the fixture and the magnet, which then catches on bone. Check the angle from two directions before you drill."],["osec","th:op-135~s2-4","Countersink and insert the fixture","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Widen the hole with the countersink at 2000 rpm, irrigating, until a shallow 360 degree ring appears. Load the fixture from its ampoule without touching it, irrigate away all bone dust, then screw it in at low speed (torque about 20 to 30 Ncm in soft bone, 40 to 50 Ncm in hard bone) with no irrigation until the first threads have engaged. The drill stops at the set torque. What you should see The fixture flange sitting flush in the countersink ring, firm in the bone. Senior's pearl Titanium integrates only with bone that has not been burnt or contaminated. Keep the drill cool, keep your fingers off the fixture, and never force it."],["osec","th:op-135~s2-5","Attach the magnet or abutment","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Transcutaneous: rotate the bone bed indicator around the fixture and drill away any bone it catches; screw on the magnet (arrow up) to 25 Ncm; measure the flap and thin it evenly to 6 mm or less; place tacking sutures between magnet and incision to close the dead space, never over the magnet. Percutaneous: choose an abutment length for the skin thickness and keep the soft tissue (modern practice no longer thins it); bring the abutment through a punch hole or the incision. Figure 4 Tacking sutures close the dead space around the magnet, away from it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 135.3 What you should see A magnet that sits free of bone under a uniformly thin flap, or an abutment standing clear of the skin."],["osec","th:op-135~s2-6","Close and dress","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Close in layers. Dress a percutaneous abutment with a healing cap and folded antiseptic gauze under it; apply a light pressure dressing over a magnet flap. Figure 5 Folded iodine-impregnated gauze between the skin and the healing cap. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 135.5 What you should see A dry wound, the abutment upright, and no tension on the skin."],["osec","th:op-135~s3-1","Crisis drills","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · When it goes wrong","Dura or sigmoid sinus exposed in the guide hole 1 Bleeding bone wax and gentle pressure 2 Next choose another site with adequate bone a 3 mm fixture if bone is thin everywhere Skin reaction around an abutment (Holgers grade) 1 Grade 1 to 2 cleaning, topical antibiotic or steroid cream, silver nitrate 2 Grade 3 oral antibiotics by culture triamcinolone injection 3 Overgrowth or grade 4 longer abutment or surgical revision remove if it will not settle Implant loose or lost 1 Early failure of integration remove and re-site after healing 2 Late trauma or infection revise at a new site"],["osec","th:op-135~s4-1","After surgery","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · Aftercare","Remove the dressing at about a week. Clean around a percutaneous abutment daily with a soft brush. Fit the processor once the fixture is stable (commonly 3 to 6 weeks with modern fixtures). Review the skin regularly and teach the patient what a reaction looks like. Sound processors are upgraded over the years without further surgery."],["ev","th:op-135~s4-2","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices: current evidence","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · Aftercare","Complications. A meta-analysis of 2,134 patients and 2,310 implants found Holgers grade 2 to 4 skin reactions in 2.4% to 38%, failure of osseointegration in 0% to 18% of adults (0% to 14% of children), revision in 1.7% to 34.5% and implant loss in 1.6% to 17.4%; major complications were rare (Kiringoda and Lustig, Otol Neurotol 2013). Keep the skin. A systematic review of 381 implants placed without skin thinning found grade 3 reactions in 2.5% (punch) and 5.9% (linear incision), with shorter surgery and faster healing than skin-thinning techniques (Verheij et al., Otol Neurotol 2016). In a multicentre randomised trial, the minimally invasive punch technique matched the linear incision for inflammation and gave less numbness, better cosmesis and shorter surgery (Calon et al., Otol Neurotol 2018). Atresia. Bone conduction implants give larger, more predictable hearing gains than atresiaplasty (Nadaraja et al., Otol Neurotol 2013)."],["osec","th:op-135~s4-2","Complications","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · Aftercare","Skin reactions and overgrowth; failure of osseointegration and implant loss; revision surgery; numbness, flap thinning or magnet exposure; pain under the magnet; haematoma; dural or sinus injury."],["osec","th:op-135~s5-1","Where the books differ","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers detailed technique for the transcutaneous magnet and the percutaneous abutment, with the move from skin thinning to tissue preservation. K. J. Lee and the Oxford books: indications by bone-conduction thresholds, single-sided deafness, and the softband trial before surgery. The literature: tissue preservation and the punch technique reduce skin problems and operating time."],["osec","th:op-135~s5-2","Viva essentials","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The two indications and the threshold limits. How it helps single-sided deafness (transfer to the good cochlea) and its limits. Osseointegration and how to protect it. Percutaneous versus transcutaneous devices. The Holgers grading of skin reactions and their management."],["osec","th:op-135~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Bone-Anchored Hearing Devices · For the examination","Common pitfalls Implanting without a softband trial. Drilling at an angle. Burning the bone or touching the fixture. Thinning only the skin over the magnet, leaving a step. Placing the device where a future microtia reconstruction will go."],["osec","th:op-136~s1-1","The case at a glance","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · Operating note","Active middle ear implants Summary Devices Vibrant Soundbridge : an external processor drives an implanted floating mass transducer (FMT) clipped to the incus or coupled to the stapes or round window. Esteem : fully implanted sensor on the incus body and driver on the stapes, with the incus long process removed. Maxum : a magnet on the incudostapedial joint driven by a deep canal processor Indications Adults with stable moderate to severe sensorineural loss (roughly 40 to 90 dB) and word recognition of at least about 40%, who cannot use or benefit from hearing aids; conductive or mixed loss (FMT on the stapes or round window) when conventional surgery or aids have failed Contraindications Active middle ear disease, retrocochlear loss, fluctuating hearing, Eustachian tube failure (Esteem), need for MRI with non-compatible devices, unrealistic expectations Anaesthesia General Position Supine, head turned Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring Tray Mastoid set, otology drill, middle ear instruments, device templates and crimping instruments, diode laser (Esteem), cement"],["osec","th:op-136~s1-2","Consent","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · Operating note","What to tell the patient Facial nerve risk from the facial recess, as with cochlear implantation. Taste change if the chorda tympani is cut (often needed for the Esteem). Loss of residual hearing or of the device's benefit; revision if the transducer moves. Drum perforation. MRI restrictions for some devices; battery replacement surgery every few years for a fully implanted device."],["osec","th:op-136~s1-3","Before you start","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · Operating note","Document air and bone thresholds, speech recognition and a trial of the best possible hearing aids; the implant must offer something the aids cannot. Check the middle ear is healthy and aerated. Explain the external processor (or not) and the MRI implications of the chosen device. Figure 1 The fully implanted Esteem system. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 136.1 Figure 2 Placement of the Vibrant Soundbridge, with the floating mass transducer. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 136.6"],["osec","th:op-136~s2-1","Plan and open","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the processor or receiver site on flat bone behind and above the mastoid with the template, and a postauricular incision well clear of it. Raise flaps, elevate periosteum to the spine of Henle, and drill a bed for the receiver with a shallow trough for the lead. What you should see A seated receiver bed behind the planned mastoid cavity, the skin over it of healthy thickness."],["osec","th:op-136~s2-2","Cortical mastoidectomy and posterior tympanotomy","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Perform a cortical mastoidectomy, thin the posterior canal wall, identify the short process of the incus, the lateral canal and the facial nerve, and open a wide facial recess between the facial nerve and the chorda tympani. What you should see The long process of the incus, the stapes and the round window niche through the facial recess. The facial nerve in a wide recess The floating mass transducer needs a generous recess, so the drilling goes closer to the nerve than in a routine tympanotomy. Keep a thin bone cover, use diamond burrs, irrigate, and keep the monitor on."],["osec","th:op-136~s2-3","Couple the transducer","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Pass the FMT through the recess and crimp its clip around the long process of the incus so the transducer's axis points towards the stapes, sitting free of bone and the promontory. For conductive or mixed loss, couple it instead to the stapes head, the footplate or the round window membrane (with a coupler or fascia), according to what remains of the chain. Figure 3 The floating mass transducer clipped to the long process of the incus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 136.7 What you should see A transducer firmly crimped, moving with the chain, touching nothing else. Senior's pearl Coupling is everything. A loose crimp or a transducer that touches bone gives poor gain and early revision. Check it by gently moving the malleus and watching the FMT move with the chain."],["osec","th:op-136~s2-4","Secure the lead and close","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Fix the receiver in its bed, lay the lead in its trough without tension, cover the recess with fascia or bone pâté, and close in layers. Activate and fit the processor after a few weeks. What you should see A receiver flat to the skull, the lead lying slack, and the transducer still in position after closure."],["osec","th:op-136~s2-5","The Esteem in outline","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · The operation, stage by stage","Through a wide mastoidectomy and epitympanotomy, the incudostapedial joint is separated and part of the incus long process removed (diode laser), the sensor is cemented to the incus body, the driver to the stapes head, and the battery-powered processor is buried behind. No external part is worn; the battery is replaced every few years. The incus separation is permanent. Figure 4 Positioning of the Esteem driver and sensor. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 136.4 What you should see Sensor and driver cemented in line with their ossicles, with no contact between the two."],["osec","th:op-136~s3-1","Crisis drills","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · When it goes wrong","Poor gain at fitting 1 Check processor fitting and coupling imaging if needed 2 Then revise the coupling (the commonest reason for revision is round window coupling without a coupler) Facial monitor fires during the recess 1 Stop irrigate, find the nerve with a diamond burr 2 Proceed accept a narrower recess and couple to the incus from a safe angle"],["osec","th:op-136~s4-1","After surgery","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · Aftercare","Head dressing for a day, wound care, and activation at about 4 to 6 weeks. Expert programming is essential, especially for the Esteem's feedback. Test aided thresholds and speech in quiet and noise."],["ev","th:op-136~s4-2","Implantable Middle Ear Devices: current evidence","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · Aftercare","Systematic reviews of the Vibrant Soundbridge found it safe and effective for conductive and mixed loss with its different couplings (Ernst et al., Laryngoscope 2016), and for mild to severe sensorineural loss in patients who cannot use hearing aids, with good satisfaction (Bruchhage et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2017). In 83 patients followed for a median of 27 months, 15.6% needed revision to improve performance, most often after round window coupling without a coupler, with a peak around 3 years (Schraven et al., Otol Neurotol 2016)."],["osec","th:op-136~s4-2","Complications","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · Aftercare","Facial nerve injury, chorda tympani injury, drum perforation, loss of residual hearing, device extrusion or failure, transducer displacement needing revision, wound infection."],["osec","th:op-136~s5-1","Where the books differ","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers describes the fully implanted Esteem in detail, the Vibrant Soundbridge with incus coupling, and the Maxum, with the need for a wide facial recess and careful crimping. K. J. Lee and the Oxford books: place middle ear implants between hearing aids and cochlear implants, with bone conduction devices as the alternative for conductive loss. The literature: the Soundbridge is effective across conductive, mixed and sensorineural losses, but coupling determines durability."],["osec","th:op-136~s5-2","Viva essentials","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Who benefits, and why a hearing aid fails them. Types of device and how each couples to the ear. The surgical steps : posterior tympanotomy and coupling. Middle ear implant, bone conduction device or cochlear implant: choosing by the loss. MRI and battery issues."],["osec","th:op-136~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Implantable Middle Ear Devices · For the examination","Common pitfalls Implanting without a proper hearing aid trial. A narrow facial recess that forces the transducer against bone. A loose crimp on the incus. Forgetting the device's MRI status."],["osec","th:op-137~s1-1","The case at a glance","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Operating note","Cochlear implantation in adults Summary Adults (postlingual) Bilateral moderate to profound sensorineural loss no longer helped by aids; open-set sentence scores under 60% best-aided, under 50% in the better ear (non-Medicare criteria) Hybrid implant Normal to moderate low-frequency hearing with severe to profound mid and high frequency loss; CNC word score 10 to 60% in the ear to be implanted (aided), the other ear equal or better but not above 80% Children Prelingual deafness; 1 to 2 years: PTA over 90 dB bilaterally; over 2 years: a bilateral threshold printed incompletely in the chapter (see the note at the end), or failure to progress with aids Before A suitable hearing aid trial if not already aided Contraindications Cochlear nerve aplasia ; cochlear aplasia ; active middle ear infection Preparation Chlorhexidine wash in the 24 hours before; pneumococcal vaccination (Pneumovax 23 and Prevnar 13); stop anticoagulants Anaesthesia and position General; supine, head turned away Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin Monitoring Facial nerve monitor Tray Microscope; mastoid set; implant insertion instruments; smooth jeweller's forceps; 5910 Beaver blade for the round window Skills Complete mastoidectomy; fine motor control for insertion"],["osec","th:op-137~s1-2","Consent","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to place a device that lets you hear by stimulating the hearing nerve directly; hearing will be different from natural hearing and improves with programming and practice. Remaining hearing in the implanted ear may be lost. Taste change on that side (chorda tympani), usually resolving over months. Facial weakness: rare. Infection around the device, a collection of blood, and rarely removal of the device. Meningitis: slightly more likely than in the general population; vaccination lowers the risk. A leak of brain fluid if the bone above the mastoid is thin."],["osec","th:op-137~s1-3","Before you start","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Operating note","Ask when the loss began and how it progressed (gradual, rapid or sudden), its cause (hereditary, noise, ototoxic drugs, meningitis ), which ear is better, how long each ear has been aided and when aids were abandoned, lip-reading ability, difficult environments and social isolation. Record sudden loss, chronic ear disease, infections, radiotherapy; fitness for 3 hours of general anaesthesia; previous implants, stapes or chronic ear surgery or craniotomy; anticoagulants and NSAIDs; and opioid-paracetamol misuse. Assess communication in clinic, eye contact and syndromic features. Examine the postauricular skin and scars, the canal and drum (intact and dry), nystagmus and gait, facial nerve function and tuning forks."],["osec","th:op-137~s1-4","Imaging","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Operating note","CT of the temporal bones: cochlear morphology and ossification after meningitis , an enlarged vestibular aqueduct , and the facial nerve, sigmoid sinus and tegmen. MRI when cochlear nerve aplasia is suspected (the nerve in the internal auditory canal), and for cochlear patency."],["osec","th:op-137~s2-1","Landmarks","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Anatomy you must see","The facial recess: the posterior tympanotomy between the facial nerve and the chorda tympani . The stapedius tendon , often the first thing seen in the middle ear, runs from the pyramidal process to the stapes in the oval window. The round window niche , directly below the oval window , about 2 mm inferior to the incudostapedial joint , over the scala tympani of the basal turn. The cochleostomy site , anteroinferior to the round window. Figure 1 The right facial recess: lateral process of the incus (orange), lateral semicircular canal (blue), incudostapedial joint (white), round window niche (black), and the cochleostomy site anteroinferior to it (asterisk). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 137.2"],["osec","th:op-137~s3-1","Incision and flap","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · The operation, stage by stage","Confirm the side at the timeout. Mark the incision 1 cm behind the postauricular crease , from above the mastoid tip to the superior temporal line, and inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline. Go down to periosteum, undercutting the edges toward the mastoid tip and zygomatic root. Raise an anteriorly based periosteal flap to the spine of Henle, identifying the posterior canal wall without lifting the soft tissue along the canal. Figure 2 The incision for a right cochlear implant. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 137.1 What you should see The mastoid cortex, the spine of Henle and the posterior canal wall, with canal skin intact."],["osec","th:op-137~s3-2","Mastoidectomy and facial recess","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · The operation, stage by stage","Perform a complete mastoidectomy, thin the posterior canal wall, and open the facial recess wide enough to see the incudostapedial joint and the round window niche below it. What you should see Through the recess: the incudostapedial joint above and the round window niche below, with the facial nerve under a shell of bone. The facial nerve and the drill shaft Heat from the drill shaft while opening the recess can injure the facial nerve, as can direct contact with its mastoid segment. Irrigate, and keep the shaft off the nerve."],["osec","th:op-137~s3-3","Open the round window","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · The operation, stage by stage","Drill away the bony lip above the round window and remove any fibrous tissue covering the membrane until it is clearly seen. Alternatively, make a cochleostomy anteroinferior to the round window, thinning the bone to eggshell and removing the last layer with a pick or rasp to spare the inner ear. A lubricant such as sodium hyaluronate can be placed over the opening. Figure 3 Magnified view of the right facial recess: the incudostapedial joint (white), the round window opened (black), the cochleostomy site (asterisk). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 137.3 What you should see The round window membrane itself: a grey, shimmering membrane lying almost horizontally under its bony overhang, moving when the stapes is touched. The wrong hole The array can be pushed into a hypotympanic air cell and miss the cochlea entirely. Prevent it with enough exposure through the recess, removal of the round window lip and clearance of fibrous tissue."],["osec","th:op-137~s3-4","The receiver-stimulator well","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · The operation, stage by stage","Raise a subperiosteal pocket posterosuperior to the mastoidectomy, checking its size with the mock-up; the device usually lies 30 to 45 degrees up from the temporal line. Drill the well at least 2 cm behind the mastoidectomy so the device does not abut the external processor, and a channel from the well to the mastoid for the array. Check the fit with the silicone mock-up. Irrigate away bone dust and take the monopolar off the field . What you should see A tight pocket and a well that the mock-up drops into without rocking."],["osec","th:op-137~s3-5","Insert the electrode","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · The operation, stage by stage","Place the device in the pocket and well, with the self-retaining retractor already in place to avoid damaging it. Under the microscope, orient the contacts upward, toward the modiolus . Grasp the array just proximal to the contacts with smooth forceps and insert it slowly . Technique depends on the design: with the advance-off-stylet technique, insert partway with the stylet, then advance the array off the stationary stylet, and withdraw the stylet once fully in. If insertion is difficult, rotate gently or back out slightly and readvance. Insert to the manufacturer's marker rings. Figure 4 Insertion in a right ear: the array at the round window with its contacts facing up toward the modiolus (white); the incus (green). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 137.4 What you should see The array sliding in without buckling or resistance to its marker, heading forward and down along the basal turn."],["osec","th:op-137~s3-6","Seal and close","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · The operation, stage by stage","Pack a small piece of fascia or periosteum around the array at the opening to seal it, and another in the facial recess. Coil any excess array in the mastoid so it does not pull out. Place the ground electrode, if present, under temporalis. Close the periosteum over the array and the soft tissue in three layers; apply a mastoid dressing. What you should see The opening sealed around the array, the lead coiled in the mastoid without tension, and good impedances and responses on testing."],["osec","th:op-137~s4-1","Crisis drills","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · When it goes wrong","Facial palsy on waking 1 Think heat from the drill shaft at the recess, or direct injury to the mastoid segment 2 Concern for direct injury return to theatre and explore 3 Delayed or partial conservative management Wound infection 1 Signs pus, swelling or redness over the device 2 Act antibiotics promptly if not rapidly better, a limited incision and drainage, often without removing the device Swelling or bleeding from the wound 1 Act a haematoma goes back to theatre for evacuation and haemostasis"],["osec","th:op-137~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls A narrow facial recess: the round window is hard to find and the array hard to release from the forceps. A receiver-stimulator placed too far forward to keep the incision short. Rapid insertion, which damages the cochlea."],["osec","th:op-137~s5-1","After the operation","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Aftercare","Mastoid dressing for 24 hours; showering without scrubbing the wound after 48 hours. Mild pain is expected. Anticoagulants can restart on day 1."],["osec","th:op-137~s5-2","Complications","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Misplaced array Hypotympanic cell; prevent with exposure Facial nerve injury Explore if direct injury is suspected Chorda tympani injury Metallic taste for months, then gradual recovery Annulus breach Entry into the medial ear canal Tegmen injury CSF leak with a low tegmen Haematoma Theatre Infection Antibiotics; drainage often without removing the device Meningitis Slightly increased risk; vaccinate"],["ev","th:op-137~s5-3","Cochlear Implantation in Adults: current evidence","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Aftercare","Candidacy. NICE (TA566, 2019) recommends implants for severe to profound loss (80 dB HL or worse at two or more frequencies) with inadequate benefit from aids (Arthur Boothroyd word score 50% or less), and bilateral simultaneous implants for children. Earlier implantation (before about 12 to 18 months) gives better spoken language. Hearing preservation. Soft surgery (round window insertion, slow insertion, steroids) preserves residual low-frequency hearing in many patients, allowing electro-acoustic stimulation. Meningitis. Implanted patients have a higher risk of pneumococcal meningitis and should receive pneumococcal vaccination. Cognition. In the ACHIEVE trial, hearing intervention slowed cognitive decline in older adults at higher dementia risk (Lin et al., Lancet 2023)."],["osec","th:op-137~s5-3","Alternatives","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · Aftercare","Hearing aids; sign language and lip-reading."],["osec","th:op-137~s6-1","Does it work?","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · For the examination","Yes: one study showed open-set word recognition rising from a mean of 16% (preoperative HINT) to 61% (CNC) in the implanted ear. The editor notes that candidacy now includes people with good low-frequency hearing (hybrid or electroacoustic devices, which still beat aids if that hearing is lost), that testing has moved to harder sentence tests in noise (AzBio at +5 dB signal-to-noise ratio), and that bilateral implants improve localisation and hearing in noise, though most adults are content with one implant and an aid in the other ear. A misprint in the paediatric criteria The chapter gives the threshold for children over 2 years as a pure tone average \"over 0 dB\", which cannot be right; a digit appears to be missing. The intended criterion is severe to profound loss, or failure to progress with aids. Check current national candidacy criteria."],["osec","th:op-137~s6-2","Viva essentials","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Candidacy for adults, hybrid devices and children. Contraindications: cochlear or nerve aplasia, active infection. Pneumococcal vaccination and why. CT findings that matter (ossification, enlarged vestibular aqueduct, facial nerve, sigmoid, tegmen); MRI for the nerve. The facial recess, the round window 2 mm below the incudostapedial joint, scala tympani. Round window versus cochleostomy. Contacts toward the modiolus; slow insertion; advance off stylet. The well 2 cm behind the mastoid; monopolar off the field. Managing infection and facial palsy."],["osec","th:op-137~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Cochlear Implantation in Adults · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing an ossified cochlea after meningitis. Implanting with an active middle ear infection. Leaving fibrous tissue over the round window. Using monopolar diathermy with the device in the field."],["osec","th:op-138~s1-1","The case at a glance","Facial Nerve Decompression · Operating note","Facial nerve decompression Summary Bell's palsy 90% or more CMAP reduction on evoked EMG and no voluntary motor units on EMG, within 14 days of onset; through the middle fossa to reach the meatal foramen Chronic otitis media Paralysis with chronic suppuration or cholesteatoma : operate without delay , whatever the degree or duration, to remove disease and explore the nerve (transmastoid) Iatrogenic injury Immediate complete palsy when the nerve's integrity is unknown or it was roughly handled: explore; or, if observed, EEMG under 10% of normal within 5 days with no voluntary units: explore Contraindications Ramsay Hunt syndrome (role of surgery limited); acute otitis media (myringotomy, tube, antibiotics instead); paralysis in an only hearing ear ; unfit for general anaesthesia Anaesthesia General, no relaxants beyond induction; dexamethasone; mannitol at incision; hyperventilation to PaCO2 about 25 mmHg Position Supine, head turned, ear up; surgeon at the head; Mayfield pins if image guidance is used Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin Monitoring Somatosensory evoked potentials; facial and cochlear nerve monitoring Tray Microscope; otological drill and burs; fine otological instruments; craniotomy set; middle fossa retractor ; facial nerve monitor and stimulator"],["osec","th:op-138~s1-2","Consent","Facial Nerve Decompression · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the bone squeezing the swollen facial nerve, to improve the chance of good recovery. The face may still not recover fully. Hearing loss , sometimes total, and dizziness , because the inner ear lies right beside the nerve. Leak of brain fluid , brain swelling or bruising , bleeding over the brain needing another operation, and stroke : uncommon but serious, as this is an operation through the skull. A night in intensive care and a stay of 3 to 6 days."],["osec","th:op-138~s1-3","Before you start","Facial Nerve Decompression · Operating note","Ask how long the paralysis has lasted; whether it came on suddenly or gradually; previous episodes on either side; hearing loss, ear pain or discharge, dizziness, head injury; chronic ear disease or previous Bell's palsy; previous ear surgery; anticoagulants. Examine which branches are affected and how much (House-Brackmann grade), other cranial nerves, a vesicular rash, discharge or cholesteatoma, and injury to the canal or drum. House-Brackmann grading Grade Description I Normal II Mild: slight weakness on close inspection III Moderate: obvious but not disfiguring; complete eye closure with effort; mild synkinesis IV Moderately severe: obvious weakness or disfiguring asymmetry; incomplete eye closure ; normal tone at rest V Severe: barely perceptible movement; asymmetry at rest VI Total paralysis When to image Imaging adds little in Bell's palsy, though CT helps plan a middle fossa approach or image guidance. Paralysis with suppurative ear disease needs imaging. Paralysis lasting over 6 months, facial twitching, slow progression over weeks or months, or recurrence demand a search for a tumour. A forehead that is spared suggests a central cause. Not every sudden palsy is Bell's: an acute infection on a cholesteatoma can cause it."],["osec","th:op-138~s1-4","The nerve and its injuries","Facial Nerve Decompression · Operating note","The nerve has about 10,000 fibres, 70% motor, the rest secretomotor, sensory and taste. Each fibre is wrapped in endoneurium , fascicles in perineurium , and the whole nerve in epineurium , which carries its vessels. Figure 1 The nerve in cross-section: endoneurium, perineurium and epineurium. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 138.1 Sunderland's degrees of injury Degree Injury Recovery 1st (neurapraxia) Conduction block from oedema, structure intact Normal once compression is relieved 2nd (axonotmesis) Axons injured, endoneurial sheaths intact Complete if the cause is relieved 3rd Endoneurial tubes disrupted Incomplete, with synkinesis from misdirected regrowth 4th Perineurium disrupted too Very poor 5th Complete transection None without repair Neurotmesis covers the third to fifth degrees. Figure 2 Sunderland's degrees of nerve injury, from compression (first) to complete transection (fifth). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 138.2"],["osec","th:op-138~s1-5","Electrical testing","Facial Nerve Decompression · Operating note","Testing is needed only when the paralysis is complete ; incomplete paralysis recovers well. Evoked EMG (electroneuronography, EEMG): stimulate at the stylomastoid foramen and record at the nasolabial fold; the injured side's CMAP as a percentage of the normal side estimates the excitable fibres. A transected nerve can still give a normal response for 48 to 72 hours until Wallerian degeneration passes the stimulation point, so test after that, then every other day . It becomes unreliable after 2 to 3 weeks, when recovering fibres fire out of step. Needle EMG: records voluntary activity and can find voluntary motor units when no movement is visible, proving the nerve is intact. Their absence is not specific: neurapraxia can look identical. Fibrillation potentials at 2 to 3 weeks mean denervation; polyphasic potentials mean reinnervation. Why both: when EEMG reaches the threshold for surgery, check EMG for early voluntary units, which predict good recovery without surgery: early recovery can desynchronise firing so the CMAP vanishes, a false positive on EEMG. Figure 3 Evoked EMG on days 3, 4 and 5 showing a progressively falling response on the left from axonal degeneration; a masseter artefact with very short latency can mimic a small response. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 138.3 An audiogram helps the diagnosis and the choice of approach: in Bell's palsy hearing is symmetrical, with an absent stapedial reflex on the affected side."],["osec","th:op-138~s2-1","Where Bell's palsy blocks the nerve","Facial Nerve Decompression · Anatomy you must see","The meatal foramen , where the nerve enters the fallopian canal from the fundus of the internal auditory canal to become the labyrinthine segment, is the narrowest part of the canal, about 0.68 mm . Bill's bar and the falciform (transverse) crest meet there in dense bone; the nerve has no epineurium, only a periosteal band sealing the entrance, so a swollen nerve is trapped. Figure 4 The lateral internal auditory canal, meatal foramen and first genu, left ear from above. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 138.4"],["osec","th:op-138~s2-2","Middle fossa landmarks","Facial Nerve Decompression · Anatomy you must see","The arcuate eminence and superior semicircular canal: usually the first landmark; the labyrinthine segment runs just in front of the ampullated end of the superior canal. The eminence can mislead: it may not lie over the canal. The greater superficial petrosal nerve: runs forward across the floor of the middle fossa; confirm it by stimulating the facial nerve backwards. The angle between it and the superior canal is about 120 degrees , bisected by the internal auditory canal, which lies about 60 degrees in front of the superior canal. The middle ear: the malleus head and incus body lead to the tympanic segment, which can be followed back to the geniculate ganglion and labyrinthine segment. The middle meningeal artery marks the front of the dissection; the superior petrosal sinus runs along the petrous ridge."],["osec","th:op-138~s3-1","Prepare the brain","Facial Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Give dexamethasone and mannitol at incision, and hyperventilate to a PaCO2 of about 25 mmHg, to relax the temporal lobe. The mannitol dose The chapter prints the mannitol dose as 0.5 mg/kg. Mannitol for brain relaxation is normally dosed in grams per kilogram (for example 0.5 g/kg), so the printed unit appears to be a typographical error. Agree the dose with your anaesthetist. What you should see A slack brain on the monitor numbers before the bone is opened."],["osec","th:op-138~s3-2","Incision and muscle flap","Facial Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Incise in front of the tragus, up past the root of the helix, curving back then forward, about 10 to 12 cm . Raise an anteriorly based U-shaped temporalis flap that keeps its nerve and blood supply; keep skin and muscle moist under sponges and retract them. Figure 5 The incision for middle fossa exposure, from just in front of the tragus upward 10 to 12 cm. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 138.5 What you should see The temporalis flap turned forward on its supply, and the squamous temporal bone with the root of the zygoma."],["osec","th:op-138~s3-3","The craniotomy","Facial Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Cut a window about 5 cm front to back and 4 cm high , with two thirds in front of the ear canal (the petrous pyramid angles forward), its lower edge flush with the root of the zygoma , the level of the middle fossa floor, to limit retraction. Make the vertical cuts parallel so the retractor fits; too wide and the retractor cannot span it. Lift the dura off the bone flap and keep the flap for closure; control middle meningeal branches with irrigating bipolar. Lower the inferior edge with a Kerrison or drill if needed. Figure 6 (A) Cutting the craniotomy; (B) freeing the bone flap from the dura; (C) lowering the inferior margin to the floor of the middle fossa; (D) elevating the dura from back to front. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 138.6 What you should see A bone window flush with the middle fossa floor, two thirds in front of the canal, and intact dura."],["osec","th:op-138~s3-4","Elevate the dura","Facial Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Lift the dura from back to front , so you do not lift the greater superficial petrosal nerve and tear a dehiscent geniculate ganglion. The middle meningeal artery marks the front; bleeding from the pterygoid plexus there is controlled with oxidised cellulose. Identify the superior petrosal sinus and seat the retractor atraumatically. What you should see The floor of the middle fossa: the middle meningeal artery in front, the arcuate eminence, the greater superficial petrosal nerve and the petrous ridge with its sinus."],["osec","th:op-138~s3-5","Find the superior canal","Facial Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","The superior canal runs perpendicular to the petrous ridge. If the arcuate eminence is unclear, remove bone layer by layer with a large diamond bur and copious irrigation in the expected area, guided by the dense yellow otic capsule bone , until the canal shows blue. Estimate the internal auditory canal 60 degrees in front of it. What you should see Yellow otic capsule bone, then the blue line of the superior canal at right angles to the ridge."],["osec","th:op-138~s3-6","Find the nerve","Facial Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Either blue-line the internal auditory canal medially near the porus , where there are no hazards, then follow it laterally to Bill's bar with the labyrinthine segment in front of it; or open the tegmen tympani , find the nerve at the first genu and follow it to the geniculate ganglion. Figure 7 (A) Finding the nerve at the porus of the internal auditory canal, about 60 degrees in front of the superior canal; (B) the alternative: opening the tegmen, finding the tympanic segment and following it back to the geniculate ganglion and labyrinthine segment (arrow: the meatal foramen). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 138.7 What you should see The blue-grey dura of the internal canal, Bill's bar at its lateral end, and the facial nerve in front of the bar."],["osec","th:op-138~s3-7","Decompress the meatal foramen","Facial Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Decompress with a 1 mm diamond bur and copious irrigation (heat injures the nerve). Watch for a blue line in front of the nerve (the cochlea ) and behind it (the superior canal ). Remove the last shell over the labyrinthine segment and geniculate ganglion with fine picks, and open the periosteal sheath sharply to relieve the constriction at the foramen. What you should see The labyrinthine segment and geniculate ganglion bare, with the nerve bulging once its sheath is opened, and no blue line breached in front or behind. The cochlea and the canal Hearing is normal in Bell's palsy, yet the cochlea lies just in front of the labyrinthine segment and the superior canal just behind. Stray off the nerve and the ear is lost. If you open either, seal it at once with bone wax."],["osec","th:op-138~s3-8","Close","Facial Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Wax opened petrous apex cells, lay temporalis muscle over the internal auditory canal and fascia over the middle fossa floor, remove the retractor, close the temporalis flap, replace and fix the bone flap, and close the skin in layers, watertight, with absorbable deep and nylon skin sutures. What you should see No CSF welling, the bone flap fixed, and a face that moves no worse on waking."],["osec","th:op-138~s4-1","Crisis drills","Facial Nerve Decompression · When it goes wrong","Inner ear opened 1 Act seal the opening immediately with bone wax 2 Expect sensorineural loss and vertigo the drill touching the ossicles also causes sensorineural loss Dura torn 1 Act repair with fascia, muscle or homograft dura or pericardium 2 Prevent the right size bur, watch all its edges, diamond burs in tight places and near dura Confusion or neurological change after surgery 1 Suspect temporal lobe oedema or contusion, or epidural haematoma 2 Act CT of the head and a neurosurgeon urgent craniotomy for an epidural haematoma Complete palsy on waking after ear surgery 1 First wait for local anaesthetic to wear off remove tight packing 2 Nerve seen and intact observe 3 Status unknown or roughly handled explore 4 Observing EEMG after 48 to 72 hours under 10% of normal within 5 days and no voluntary units on EMG: explore 5 Timing results fall sharply beyond 30 days and are uniformly poor beyond a year delayed-onset palsy usually recovers well"],["osec","th:op-138~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Facial Nerve Decompression · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls A craniotomy in the wrong place or of the wrong size. Not lowering the lower edge to the floor of the middle fossa. Injuring an exposed greater superficial petrosal nerve or geniculate ganglion. Opening the cochlea or superior canal. Missing a dural tear. Poor haemostasis, leading to a haematoma."],["osec","th:op-138~s5-1","After the operation","Facial Nerve Decompression · Aftercare","Observe overnight in intensive care; use opioids sparingly, as they can mask intracranial complications. Next day move to the ward, mobilise and eat. Remove the drain when under 20 mL. Discharge usually at 3 to 6 days ."],["osec","th:op-138~s5-2","Complications","Facial Nerve Decompression · Aftercare","Complications of middle fossa decompression Complication Notes Sensorineural hearing loss and vertigo Opening the cochlea or labyrinth; drill on the ossicles CSF leak Dural injury; repair Temporal lobe oedema or contusion CT and neurosurgical advice Epidural haematoma Urgent evacuation Persisting facial weakness Despite decompression Stroke Rare"],["osec","th:op-138~s5-3","Alternatives","Facial Nerve Decompression · Aftercare","Transmastoid decompression when the injury is limited to the tympanic and mastoid segments. Translabyrinthine decompression , reaching the whole nerve, when hearing is not to be preserved."],["ev","th:op-138~s5-4","Facial Nerve Decompression: current evidence","Facial Nerve Decompression · Aftercare","Decompression for Bell's palsy. In a non-randomised study, middle fossa decompression within 14 days of onset in patients with more than 90% degeneration on ENoG and no voluntary EMG activity improved recovery (Gantz et al., Laryngoscope 1999); the American guideline makes no recommendation for or against it because the evidence is limited and the operation carries risk (Baugh et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2013). Trauma. Decompression is considered for immediate complete palsy with ENoG degeneration over 90%. Reanimation. Within about 18 months with viable muscle, a masseteric or hypoglossal nerve transfer gives tone and movement; for long-standing palsy, free gracilis transfer is needed. Eye protection comes first: gold or platinum weight, lateral tarsal strip or tarsorrhaphy."],["osec","th:op-138~s5-4","Other causes","Facial Nerve Decompression · Aftercare","Ramsay Hunt syndrome: varicella zoster reactivated in the geniculate ganglion; complete paralysis is commoner and recovery poorer, worse with auditory and vestibular symptoms; electrical tests are unreliable; give steroids and antivirals early ; surgery has a limited role. Acute otitis media: now rare and almost only in children; urgent myringotomy with a tube and topical and systemic antibiotics; mastoidectomy for coalescent mastoiditis or intracranial spread; decompression is not recommended. Bell's palsy medicine: guidelines support prednisone 1 mg/kg/day with a taper plus valaciclovir 500 mg three times daily for 10 days within 3 days of onset; common practice extends this to 7 days."],["osec","th:op-138~s6-1","Does decompression help Bell's palsy?","Facial Nerve Decompression · For the examination","Bell's palsy makes up 75% of acute facial palsies (30 to 40 per 100,000 a year); over 80% recover satisfactorily and 85% improve within 3 weeks; the timing of recovery and completeness of the palsy are the main prognostic factors. In Gantz and colleagues' prospective study, patients with 90% degeneration on ENoG and no voluntary units within 14 days were offered middle fossa decompression: of 19 operated, 95% reached House-Brackmann I or II (half normal), against a poor outcome in 66% of 11 controls on steroids, none of whom became normal. Decompression after 14 days gave results no better than controls. Patients meeting these criteria have at least a 50% chance of poor recovery without surgery. If 90% degeneration has not occurred within 2 weeks, further testing is unnecessary and the outlook is good."],["osec","th:op-138~s6-2","Viva essentials","Facial Nerve Decompression · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Sunderland's degrees and synkinesis. House-Brackmann grading. EEMG and EMG: timing, the 48 to 72 hour lag, every-other-day testing, the false positive, voluntary units. The criteria for decompression in Bell's palsy, and the 14-day window. The meatal foramen: why it traps the nerve, 0.68 mm. Middle fossa landmarks: arcuate eminence, GSPN, the 60 and 120 degree angles, Bill's bar. Urgent surgery for cholesteatoma palsy; no decompression for acute otitis media; limited role in Ramsay Hunt. Managing a palsy after ear surgery."],["osec","th:op-138~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Facial Nerve Decompression · For the examination","Common pitfalls Testing too early, within 72 hours, and being falsely reassured. Operating on EEMG alone without checking EMG for voluntary units. Calling every sudden palsy Bell's palsy. Trusting the arcuate eminence blindly."],["osec","th:op-139~s1-1","The case at a glance","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · Operating note","Surgery for facial nerve tumours Summary Tumours Schwannoma (commonest, any segment, often the geniculate); haemangioma (geniculate, causes palsy while small); also neurofibroma, malignant invasion from parotid or skin cancer Indications Facial function of House-Brackmann grade IV or worse, progressive weakness, rapid growth, brainstem compression, or doubt about the diagnosis Options Observation with serial MRI; bony decompression; stereotactic radiosurgery; debulking; resection with nerve grafting Approach Transmastoid (tympanic and mastoid segments); middle fossa (internal auditory canal, labyrinthine, geniculate) with hearing; translabyrinthine without hearing; parotidectomy for extratemporal tumours; combined as needed Anaesthesia General; mannitol, furosemide and steroids for the middle fossa Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring Tray Mastoid and middle fossa sets, drill and diamond burrs, microsurgical instruments, nerve graft harvest set, fibrin glue"],["osec","th:op-139~s1-2","Consent","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · Operating note","What to tell the patient If the nerve has to be cut and grafted, the face will be weak for 6 to 12 months and recover at best to a grade III (good tone, movement with some synkinesis). Decompression and radiosurgery aim to keep current function longer, not to remove the tumour. Hearing loss and imbalance depending on the route; total deafness with the translabyrinthine approach. CSF leak, meningitis, and the risks of a craniotomy. Eye care for life if eye closure is poor: drops, taping, perhaps a gold weight."],["osec","th:op-139~s1-3","Before you start","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · Operating note","Document facial function (House-Brackmann), hearing and balance. Get MRI with gadolinium for the extent along the nerve (a \"tubular\" enhancing lesion following the canal, or a dumbbell through the facial hiatus), and CT for the bony canal and middle ear. Electrophysiology helps when the face looks normal. Discuss the options openly: in many, observation is right. Figure 1 Segments of the facial nerve involved by schwannomas and their frequency. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 139.1"],["osec","th:op-139~s2-1","Choose the strategy","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · The operation, stage by stage","Decompression removes the bone of the Fallopian canal over the tumour to give it room and delay nerve failure. Debulking removes the part of the tumour outside the nerve's fibres, accepting regrowth. Resection removes the tumour with the involved nerve and grafts the gap. Size, site, hearing and the face decide the route. What you should see A plan that reaches the whole involved segment with healthy nerve at both ends."],["osec","th:op-139~s2-2","Transmastoid exposure","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · The operation, stage by stage","Through a postauricular incision, perform a wide cortical mastoidectomy, identify the lateral canal and sinodural angle, thin the posterior canal wall and open the facial recess widely. Remove the incus (and the malleus head if the proximal tympanic segment is involved) to expose the tympanic segment. Sacrifice the chorda if the tumour extends to the lower vertical segment. Figure 2 A complete mastoidectomy with facial recess exposes the tympanic and mastoid segments. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 139.2 What you should see The nerve skeletonised to translucent bone over the whole tumour."],["osec","th:op-139~s2-3","Middle fossa exposure","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · The operation, stage by stage","Turn a 5 by 5 cm craniotomy two-thirds in front of and one-third behind the ear canal, as low as possible. Raise the dura from behind forwards, identify the middle meningeal artery, the greater superficial petrosal nerve and the arcuate eminence, place the retractor, blue-line the superior canal and open the internal auditory canal, labyrinthine segment and geniculate ganglion. Figure 3 Middle fossa approach to a tumour at the geniculate ganglion. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 139.3 Figure 4 Incision for a combined middle fossa and transmastoid approach. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 139.5 What you should see The nerve from the internal auditory canal to the geniculate, with the cochlea and superior canal untouched."],["osec","th:op-139~s2-4","Translabyrinthine exposure","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · The operation, stage by stage","When the ear does not hear usefully, a translabyrinthine approach reaches the nerve from the cerebellopontine angle to the stylomastoid foramen without brain retraction, allowing the horizontal and vertical segments to be mobilised for a longer reach. Figure 5 Translabyrinthine exposure and mobilisation of the horizontal and vertical segments. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 139.6 and 139.7 What you should see Healthy nerve proximal to the tumour, usually in the internal auditory canal or cerebellopontine angle."],["osec","th:op-139~s2-5","Remove and test the margins","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · The operation, stage by stage","Try to separate the tumour from the nerve; if it cannot be separated, transect sharply proximal and distal to it, remove it, and send the nerve ends for frozen section to confirm clear margins. What you should see Two clean, fascicular nerve ends with tumour-free frozen sections. Cutting the nerve is final Once the nerve is transected the face will be paralysed for months and will never be normal. Make that decision before the operation with the patient, not on the table, unless the patient has agreed to it."],["osec","th:op-139~s2-6","Graft the gap","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · The operation, stage by stage","Harvest the greater auricular nerve (it runs across the sternomastoid, halfway between the mastoid tip and the angle of the jaw, parallel to the external jugular vein) or the sural nerve for a long graft. Lay it between the ends without tension and hold it with fascia or fibrin glue rather than sutures in the bony canal. Reconstruct the ossicles if the chain was disrupted. Figure 6 Interposition cable graft from the internal auditory canal. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 139.4 Figure 7 Greater auricular nerve graft and how to find the nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 139.8 and 139.9 What you should see A graft lying in the bony canal touching both ends, without tension or twisting. Senior's pearl Tension is what kills a nerve repair. A slightly long graft lying loose does better than a short one pulled tight. Fibrin glue in the fallopian canal is enough; avoid crowding fascicles with sutures."],["osec","th:op-139~s3-1","Crisis drills","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · When it goes wrong","Clear fluid from the nose or wound 1 Act head up, avoid straining, consider a lumbar drain 2 Persisting re-explore and seal watch for meningitis Poor eye closure after surgery 1 Act lubricants day and night, taping, moisture chamber 2 Longer term gold or platinum eyelid weight ophthalmology review"],["osec","th:op-139~s4-1","After surgery","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · Aftercare","Protect the cornea. Expect no movement for 6 to 12 months after grafting, then gradual return of tone and movement with synkinesis; facial physiotherapy helps. Follow with MRI for regrowth after decompression, debulking or radiosurgery."],["ev","th:op-139~s4-2","Tumors of the Facial Nerve: current evidence","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · Aftercare","Management has shifted away from routine resection. In 79 patients over 30 years, resection fell from 85% of cases before 1995 to 27% afterwards, replaced by decompression, observation and radiotherapy; facial grade was maintained or improved in 79% after decompression and 100% with observation or radiation, against 55% after resection and grafting (Wilkinson et al., Laryngoscope 2011). Surgery or radiosurgery. A meta-analysis of 519 patients with intracranial facial schwannoma found facial function worsened in 30% after surgery against 9% after stereotactic radiosurgery, and stayed stable in 41% against 66%; radiosurgery is recommended when treatment is needed and surgery kept for other indications such as brainstem compression (Rotter et al., J Neurosurg 2021)."],["osec","th:op-139~s4-2","Complications","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · Aftercare","Facial paralysis, CSF leak, meningitis, hearing loss, vestibular loss, intracranial bleeding, corneal exposure."],["osec","th:op-139~s5-1","Where the books differ","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers treat only progressive tumours or failing nerves; decompression, debulking or resection with grafting through transmastoid, middle fossa, translabyrinthine or combined routes. K. J. Lee the imaging (tubular enhancement, scalloped canal, dumbbell at the geniculate) and the haemangioma at the geniculate that causes early palsy. The literature observation, decompression and radiosurgery preserve facial function better than resection for most schwannomas."],["osec","th:op-139~s5-2","Viva essentials","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for When to treat and why watching is often right. The options and what each achieves. Approaches by segment and hearing. Nerve grafting: donors, technique, expected outcome (grade III at best). Eye protection."],["osec","th:op-139~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Tumors of the Facial Nerve · For the examination","Common pitfalls Resecting a tumour in a patient with a normal face who was not warned of paralysis. Grafting under tension. Forgetting frozen sections of the margins. Neglecting the cornea."],["osec","th:op-14~s1-1","The case at a glance","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","Supracricoid partial laryngectomy Summary Indications T1, T2 and selected T3 , N0 to N1 glottic and supraglottic squamous cell carcinoma, especially involving the anterior commissure or the petiole of the epiglottis; recurrent T1 to T2 (selected T3) disease after narrow field radiotherapy; a motivated patient able to take part in swallowing rehabilitation Reconstruction CHEP for glottic cancer with limited supraglottic extension; CHP when the epiglottis must be resected Tumour limits Vocal fold immobility with arytenoid fixation ; subglottic extension; invasion of the cricoid or the hyoid ; extralaryngeal spread Not suitable Comorbidity raising the risk of anaesthesia; poor pulmonary reserve ; previous wide field radiotherapy; dysphagia from earlier treatment or illness; expected need for postoperative (chemo)radiotherapy; active anxiety, depression or substance abuse; lack of motivation or support Still suitable Vocal fold fixation alone; limited pre-epiglottic space invasion; previous laser surgery Anaesthesia General; position supine Antibiotics Cefazolin and metronidazole; clindamycin in penicillin allergy; continued for 24 hours Tray Standard laryngectomy and tracheostomy sets Skills Expertise in total laryngectomy and thyroid surgery"],["osec","th:op-14~s1-2","Consent","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer and much of the voice box while keeping your ability to speak and swallow without a permanent hole in the neck. Consent for a total laryngectomy is required, in case the cancer is found to be more extensive during the operation. A tracheostomy for several weeks , with copious secretions that can get into the lungs and cause pneumonia. Swallowing will be difficult at first; you will need a feeding tube for some weeks and swallowing therapy. Most people do not need a long-term feeding tube. Your voice will be understandable but will not sound like your old voice. Breakdown of the repair , narrowing of the airway, wound infection, a fracture of the cricoid, which may lead to total laryngectomy. Injury to nerves of the larynx and tongue. The alternatives: total laryngectomy, whose recovery is usually far easier and quicker, or radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-14~s1-3","Before you start","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","Ask about hoarseness, stridor, breathlessness, a neck lump, weight loss and pneumonia ; previous treatment (especially radiotherapy and its field) and the original stage; medical illness, functional status and exercise tolerance; smoking, alcohol and other substances; antiplatelet drugs and thyroxine. Look at the whole patient: vitality, frailty, cooperation and behaviour, and lung function , because the patient must cope with some aspiration. Note voice, articulation and stridor. Exclude second primaries in the mouth and assess tongue movement; exclude tonsil and tongue base involvement; examine the neck for nodes. Laryngoscopy is mandatory: the movement of the vocal folds and of the arytenoids , supraglottic and subglottic extension, and arytenoid involvement. Fold fixed, arytenoid mobile Paraglottic invasion usually fixes the vocal fold while the arytenoid still moves, and resecting that arytenoid usually gives a clear margin. A fold fixed with a fixed arytenoid usually means the cricoarytenoid joint is invaded, and resection rarely clears it. After radiotherapy the joint may be fixed without tumour in it."],["osec","th:op-14~s1-4","Imaging and preparation","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Operating note","CT with contrast or MRI with gadolinium is limited for mucosal extent but reliably shows contraindications such as extralaryngeal spread. PET-CT is most useful in restaging recurrence after radiotherapy, for nodes and distant disease. Arrange medical and oncology review, review the imaging, obtain consent including for total laryngectomy , involve speech and swallowing therapy, and plan an examination with mapping biopsies under anaesthesia: it may show subglottic extension missed on imaging, a contraindication. Confirm outside pathology. Pan-endoscopy may be done as a separate stage or just before the open operation. Choose total laryngectomy for some For a patient with active mental illness, substance abuse or poor motivation, recovery from supracricoid laryngectomy is too demanding. Total laryngectomy usually lets them eat within weeks, and some patients prefer a free diet to a lung-powered voice."],["osec","th:op-14~s2-1","The cricoarytenoid unit","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Anatomy you must see","The residual larynx works only if at least one cricoarytenoid unit survives: the arytenoid, its joint and its recurrent laryngeal nerve supply. It swings forward against the epiglottis or tongue base when the patient swallows, closing the airway. Resecting both arytenoids means total laryngectomy."],["osec","th:op-14~s2-2","The recurrent laryngeal nerve and the cricothyroid joint","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Anatomy you must see","The recurrent laryngeal nerve lies immediately posterior to the cricothyroid articulation , which cannot be seen until the cricopharyngeus and inferior constrictor are released from the posterior thyroid lamina and inferior cornu. It is at risk when the joint is disarticulated. Figure 1 The right side of the thyroid cartilage and trachea: the inferior constrictor and cricopharyngeus must be released from the posterior lamina and inferior cornu to expose the cricothyroid joint, with the recurrent laryngeal nerve just behind it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 14.1"],["osec","th:op-14~s2-3","The superior laryngeal nerve","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Anatomy you must see","The external and internal branches relate to the thyrohyoid membrane . For glottic cancer, entering the larynx through the membrane below the internal branch preserves sensation to the remaining epiglottis and helps swallowing."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-1","Map, then open","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","After pan-endoscopy and mapping, make an apron incision centred on the cricoid and raise subplatysmal flaps to 1 cm above the hyoid. Do neck dissections as indicated. Separate the straps in the midline; divide sternohyoid and omohyoid at the upper edge of the thyroid laminae and sternothyroid over the cartilage, and retract the strap flaps laterally. Keep the straps : they reinforce the pexy and separate the tracheostomy from the neck. What you should see The thyroid cartilage, cricoid and hyoid exposed, with the strap flaps preserved."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-2","Free the thyroid cartilage","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the thyroid isthmus and dissect the lobes off the trachea, keeping the superior thyroid pedicle. Finger-dissect the pretracheal plane down to the carina . Release the inferior constrictors from the posterior thyroid cartilage and divide the superior cornua at their bases to help delivery. Mobilise the pyriform mucosa from the inner laminae. What you should see The trachea free down to the carina, and the posterior borders of the thyroid laminae cleared."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-3","Disarticulate the cricothyroid joint","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the joint sharply with a No. 15 blade angled medially and superiorly, just under the inferior cornu , to keep the recurrent laryngeal supply to the cricoarytenoid units. What you should see The joint open with the recurrent nerve untouched behind it. The recurrent laryngeal nerve It sits just behind the joint. A blade angled outward or downward cuts it, and with it the function of the unit you came to save."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-4","Inspect the subglottis","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the cricothyroid membrane along the cricoid and look below. Cancer may reach the level of the cricoid but not below it ; subglottic extension rules out the operation. Frozen section margins must be clear. Pass the tube through the cricothyrotomy. What you should see A subglottis free of tumour at and below the upper border of the cricoid."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-5","Entry for CHEP: through the pre-epiglottic space","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","For glottic cancer with little supraglottic extension, start an incision just above the thyroid notch and carry it through the pre-epiglottic space ; a Deaver retractor in the mouth against the infrahyoid epiglottis keeps you oriented. Cut through the infrahyoid epiglottic cartilage and enter the airway through the petiole , then extend the opening laterally on the less involved side behind the top of the thyroid cartilage. Figure 2 For carcinoma with minimal supraglottic involvement, entry through the pre-epiglottic space: an incision from just above the thyroid notch, through the infrahyoid epiglottis, into the larynx with the glottis in view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 14.2 What you should see The laryngeal lumen from above with the tumour in view, entered on the less involved side."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-6","Resect","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Carry the cut down to the cricothyrotomy through the aryepiglottic fold and the back of the false and true folds, just in front of the arytenoid . Once the upper and lower openings meet, fracture the thyroid cartilage vertically at the notch and open the larynx like a book; make the final cuts under direct vision and resect the paraglottic space. The opposite arytenoid may be taken if necessary, but keep the mucosa over the posterior commissure and interarytenoid region, or the arytenoid fixes and the glottis stenoses. Figure 3 View from the head of the table after dividing the infrahyoid epiglottis: cuts just in front of the arytenoid, through the aryepiglottic fold and false and true folds, with cancer at the anterior commissure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 14.3 What you should see The larynx opened like a book, the tumour and paraglottic space in the specimen, and a mobile arytenoid with intact mucosa left behind. Dislocating the arytenoid An arytenoid dislocated during the resection cannot be saved. Cut in front of it and handle it gently."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-7","Reposition the arytenoids","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","With clear frozen sections, pull the arytenoids forward with 4-0 Vicryl. The arytenoid mucosa usually does not reach the anterior cricoid arch, so the sutures are tied as air knots. What you should see The arytenoids tilted forward, not falling back into the pharynx."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-8","The pexy","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Impact the cricoid against the hyoid with three submucosal No. 1 Prolene sutures on a TP-1 needle. Pass the first in the midline, submucosally under the cricoid, around the hyoid, under the epiglottis and the tongue base mucosa; place two more 1 cm either side of the midline , avoiding the hypoglossal nerves, and tie them securely, snug enough to hold but not so tight as to crack the cricoid, which is fragile, especially after radiotherapy. Figure 4 Reconstruction: cricohyoidopexy with three submucosal sutures around the hyoid and cricoid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 14.4 What you should see The cricoid snug against the hyoid in the midline, with no gap and no crack. The pexy sutures Misplaced or wrongly tensioned sutures cause dehiscence or a cricoid fracture; a damaged cricoid leads to infection, rupture of the pexy and usually total laryngectomy."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-9","Supraglottic cancer: CHP","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","When the epiglottis and pre-epiglottic space must go, enter the vallecula by dissecting immediately below the hyoid, with a Deaver in the vallecula to guide you, instead of entering through the pre-epiglottic space. Make the epiglottic and lower cuts across the aryepiglottic fold and posterior false and true folds as before, removing the pre-epiglottic space with the specimen. The pexy impacts the cricoid and arytenoids against the tongue base . Figure 5 View from the head of the table for a CHP: the infrahyoid epiglottis is removed with the specimen, the cuts made just in front of the arytenoid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 14.5 Figure 6 Infrahyoid dissection with resection of the supraglottis and glottis together with the paraglottic and pre-epiglottic spaces, to be reconstructed by cricohyoidopexy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 14.6 What you should see The vallecula entered below the hyoid, and the pre-epiglottic fat going with the specimen."],["osec","th:op-14~s3-10","Tracheostomy and closure","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Only now make a low tracheostomy at the level of the neck incision, where it will not pull on the pexy. Advance and secure the skin flaps over the stoma to isolate the neck from the tracheostoma . Draw the pyriform mucosa forward with 4-0 Vicryl into a more natural position. No cricopharyngeal myotomy. Reinforce the pexy with the strap muscles and close in layers over suction drains. What you should see A stoma walled off from the neck wound, and straps laid over the pexy."],["osec","th:op-14~s4-1","Crisis drills","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · When it goes wrong","Margins positive or both arytenoids involved 1 Recognise a positive frozen section that cannot be cleared, or both arytenoids needing resection 2 Act convert to total laryngectomy, as consented Tracheostomy tube dislodged in the first days 1 Recognise an emergency reintubation from above may be very hard (swelling, secretions, a neck that will not extend) 2 Prevent suture and tie the tube for 5 days before the first change"],["osec","th:op-14~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Not saving the strap muscles for the reconstruction. Not saving at least one cricoarytenoid unit. Poorly placed pexy sutures, or the wrong tension on them. A tracheostomy placed where it pulls on the impaction. Not separating the neck from the tracheostoma."],["osec","th:op-14~s5-1","After the operation","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Antibiotics for 24 hours, meticulous tracheostomy care, coughing, deep breathing and walking, and speech rehabilitation. Feed by nasogastric tube or temporary gastrostomy. Deflate the cuff the next day and change to an uncuffed tube at the first tube change . Falling secretions mean swallowing is returning; once the patient can swallow saliva, start thickened liquids and a swallowing programme. Patients are usually decannulated before the feeding tube comes out."],["osec","th:op-14~s5-2","Complications","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Complications of supracricoid partial laryngectomy Complication Notes Subcutaneous air and wound infection Tracheostomy not sutured to the muscle and skin flaps, letting secretions into the neck Dehiscence of the pexy Poorly placed sutures; may lead to stenosis Aspiration pneumonia Dysphagia is expected; poor pulmonary reserve predisposes; select carefully Tracheostomy dislodgement An emergency; secure the tube for 5 days Laryngeal stenosis After dehiscence or extended arytenoid resection Cricoid fracture Infection and pexy rupture, usually ending in total laryngectomy Late Neck fibrosis and variable dysphagia; most do not need a long-term gastrostomy"],["ev","th:op-14~s5-3","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy: current evidence","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Outcomes. In a systematic review of supracricoid partial laryngectomy in the United States, five-year disease-specific survival was 88% after primary surgery, local recurrence was about 2% after primary and 10% after salvage surgery, and over 90% of patients were decannulated (Saturno et al., Laryngoscope 2024). Selection. Good pulmonary function, at least one mobile arytenoid, and no subglottic extension beyond about 1 cm anteriorly or cricoid involvement. Swallowing rehabilitation takes weeks, and aspiration pneumonia is the main functional risk; fewer surgeons now perform it as transoral and non-surgical options have grown."],["osec","th:op-14~s5-3","Alternatives","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Total laryngectomy. Radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-14~s6-1","Outcomes","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · For the examination","Local control after supracricoid laryngectomy is extremely favourable compared with radiotherapy alone, and indications have widened to selected T3 and to recurrence after radiotherapy, with good local control and disease-specific survival in large series. Salvage: it works after narrow field radiotherapy, but after definitive wide field radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy swallowing is poor and total laryngectomy is recommended. The editor reminds us that total laryngectomy, though it preserves life, carries a permanent stoma and the cost of valves, so conservation surgery should always be considered for suitable patients."],["osec","th:op-14~s6-2","Viva essentials","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for What is removed and what remains; CHEP versus CHP . The cricoarytenoid unit, and why one must survive. Contraindications: fixed arytenoid, subglottic extension, cricoid or hyoid invasion, extralaryngeal spread, wide field radiotherapy, poor lungs. The recurrent laryngeal nerve behind the cricothyroid joint, and how the joint is divided. Entering below the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. Three Prolene pexy sutures; the low tracheostomy; no cricopharyngeal myotomy. Consent for total laryngectomy. The recovery: cuff down, uncuffed tube, thickened fluids, decannulation before the feeding tube."],["osec","th:op-14~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Supracricoid Partial Laryngectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Offering it to a patient with poor lungs or poor motivation. Missing subglottic extension by not inspecting through the cricothyrotomy. Stripping the posterior commissure mucosa. Overtightening the pexy on an irradiated cricoid."],["osec","th:op-140~s1-1","The case at a glance","Acoustic Neuroma · Operating note","Acoustic neuroma Summary Strategies Observation (\"watch and scan\"); stereotactic radiation; microsurgical resection (the only cure) Translabyrinthine No serviceable hearing, or hearing preservation unlikely; little or no brain retraction, no intradural drilling, the facial nerve found reliably at the meatal foramen Retrosigmoid Hearing preservation with limited lateral canal extension, including tumours extending more than 0.5 cm into the angle ; when hearing is not to be saved, its choice over translabyrinthine for medium and large tumours is the surgeon's (the authors prefer translabyrinthine) Middle fossa Hearing preservation for small, laterally based tumours; poor posterior fossa access, needs temporal lobe retraction; caution in the elderly Hearing worth saving SRT (or PTA) of at least 50 dB and discrimination of at least 50% Contraindications Medical unfitness (observe, and radiate if it grows, with CSF diversion for hydrocephalus); brainstem or cerebellar compression contraindicates radiation (tumours swell for about 18 months afterwards), so offer surgery Anaesthesia General Position Translabyrinthine: supine, head on a foam ring. Middle fossa: supine in pins, the retractor fixed to the pins so head and retractor move together. Retrosigmoid: lateral or prone in pins Medication Cefazolin before incision, and in drill and wound irrigation; dexamethasone 10 mg; famciclovir 500 mg three times daily for 3 days before and 4 days after surgery against delayed facial palsy Monitoring Facial nerve; ipsilateral ABR for hearing preservation; for large tumours contralateral AB"],["osec","th:op-140~s1-2","Consent","Acoustic Neuroma · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove a benign tumour on the balance nerve; the alternatives are watching it with scans or treating it with focused radiation. Hearing: lost completely with the translabyrinthine approach; may be lost even when we try to save it. Facial weakness , immediate or delayed, usually recovering; rarely permanent; eye protection meanwhile. Imbalance , improving with vestibular therapy. Leak of brain fluid , meningitis, bleeding inside the skull, stroke. Headache , occasionally long-lasting, especially after the retrosigmoid approach. Rarely: tracheostomy, feeding tube, locked-in syndrome or death. Radiation's risks are similar but can appear years later, and include hydrocephalus and, very rarely, a radiation-induced cancer."],["osec","th:op-140~s1-3","Before you start","Acoustic Neuroma · Operating note","Hearing loss is usually gradual; 5% present with sudden loss ; asymmetric hearing loss is an acoustic neuroma until proven otherwise . Vestibular symptoms are usually motion intolerance rather than vertigo. Facial weakness is rarely a presenting sign. Large tumours cause trigeminal numbness, diplopia (VI), dysphagia, hoarseness and aspiration (IX, X), and raised pressure with headache and papilloedema. A previous schwannoma or meningioma, an opposite-side tumour or a family history suggests NF2 , which is managed differently. Examine the ears under the microscope (a perforation raises the risk of CSF otorrhoea; an inflamed mastoid affects the choice of transtemporal route), all cranial nerves (VII especially), and look for Bruns' nystagmus in large tumours: coarse, slow nystagmus looking toward the tumour (brainstem) and fine, fast nystagmus looking away (vestibular). Test gait, Fukuda stepping and head thrust."],["osec","th:op-140~s1-4","Tests and imaging","Acoustic Neuroma · Operating note","Audiometry (air, bone, speech). Vestibular battery: rotational chair, VEMP and VNG as a baseline and a guide to therapy. ENoG for subclinical facial compromise. ABR in large tumours (baseline for contralateral brainstem monitoring) and to judge the cochlear nerve before hearing preservation. MRI with gadolinium is the gold standard, near 100% sensitive (false positives occur), finding tumours of 1 to 2 mm; T2 shows the nerve of origin and whether a CSF cap at the fundus may help hearing preservation. Where MRI is impossible, ABR with CT (ABR sensitivity 82%, 95.6% for tumours over 1 cm). Non-contrast CT for planning: mastoid pneumatisation, sigmoid-to-canal distance and jugular bulb height (translabyrinthine); arcuate eminence, superior canal and pneumatisation over the canal (middle fossa); petrous pneumatisation, sigmoid, bulb and canal height (retrosigmoid)."],["osec","th:op-140~s1-5","Choosing the strategy","Acoustic Neuroma · Operating note","Observation: avoid treatment that adds more harm than the natural course. In the Danish series, 17% of intrameatal and 28.9% of extrameatal tumours grew within 5 years, and 45% lost class A hearing regardless of growth. Growth cannot be predicted, so follow-up must be close. Stereotactic radiation: single-dose radiosurgery or 3 to 5 fraction radiotherapy, now at lower doses (12 Gy single, 18 Gy fractionated) with similar control and fewer neuropathies. Control is 90 to 95% overall but 80 to 85% in growing tumours ; useful hearing falls to about 20% at 5 years (Mayo); there is no specimen, and eight radiation-induced malignancies have been reported. The authors radiate only tumours that have grown, and rarely the young. Microsurgery cures, controls mass effect and gives a diagnosis; results are best in high-volume centres."],["osec","th:op-140~s2-1","Landmarks","Acoustic Neuroma · Anatomy you must see","The facial nerve , intratemporal and intracranial, preserved throughout. The labyrinth: the lateral canal for translabyrinthine orientation, the superior canal for the middle fossa. The fundus of the canal: the transverse crest separates superior and inferior vestibular nerves; the vertical crest (Bill's bar) separates the superior vestibular nerve behind from the facial nerve in front . Figure 1 The vestibular nerve and tumour separated from the facial nerve in the canal and dissected toward the porus and angle; Bill's bar, the transverse crest, and the cochlear, facial and vestibular nerves. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 140.2"],["osec","th:op-140~s3-1","Expose the temporal bone","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: translabyrinthine","A C-shaped postauricular incision with staggered periosteal incisions. Perform a complete mastoidectomy, uncovering the middle and posterior fossa dura and the sigmoid sinus. What you should see Middle and posterior fossa dura and the sigmoid sinus under a thin shell or bare, with the sinodural angle open."],["osec","th:op-140~s3-2","Labyrinthectomy and the facial nerve","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: translabyrinthine","Remove the labyrinth and identify the facial nerve in its mastoid segment. What you should see The vestibule opened after the three canals, and the facial nerve under bone from the second genu down."],["osec","th:op-140~s3-3","Open the canal","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: translabyrinthine","Drill an inferior trough between the canal and the jugular bulb, with the cochlear aqueduct as the anteroinferior limit, and a superior trough between the middle fossa dura and the canal, exposing 270 degrees of the canal dura. Figure 2 The canal skeletonised to 180 to 270 degrees; the facial nerve found as it leaves the canal at Bill's bar. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 140.1 What you should see The grey dura of the internal canal bared for three quarters of its circumference, with the jugular bulb below and the transverse crest and Bill's bar at the fundus."],["osec","th:op-140~s3-4","Obliterate the Eustachian tube","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: translabyrinthine","Remove the incus, cut the tensor tendon and pack the tube with alternating temporalis muscle and fibrillar Surgicel. What you should see The tube orifice packed solid, with the stapes left in place."],["osec","th:op-140~s3-5","Find the facial nerve in the canal","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: translabyrinthine","Divide the superior vestibular nerve at Bill's bar to show the facial nerve in front of it, and dissect the tumour off the nerve medially until the plane is lost at the porus. What you should see The transverse crest dividing the vestibular nerves, Bill's bar, and the facial nerve anterior to the divided superior vestibular nerve."],["osec","th:op-140~s3-6","Debulk and remove","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: translabyrinthine","Debulk large tumours fast with two surgeons and four hands. Remove the last tumour and capsule under continuous monitoring; if total removal would sacrifice the nerve, leave a little on it ( near-total removal , low recurrence risk). Figure 3 The two-surgeon, four-handed technique: surgeons face each other, both with instruments in the field. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 140.3 What you should see The facial nerve on the front of the tumour, found first at the fundus by Bill's bar or at the brainstem, stimulating at a low threshold. A firing facial nerve When the monitor shows the nerve firing from irritation, stop: you cannot read changes while it fires, and pressing on harms it."],["osec","th:op-140~s3-7","Close watertight","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: translabyrinthine","Fill the dural defect with abdominal fat (left lower quadrant) or acellular dermis and reconstruct with hydroxyapatite cement for a watertight seal. Close the musculoperiosteal flaps, then the skin in two layers (2-0 Vicryl, then running locked 3-0 Vicryl Rapide); Steri-Strips and a cup dressing. What you should see No CSF through the fat, and the aditus and tube sealed."],["osec","th:op-140~s4-1","Craniotomy","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: retrosigmoid","A retromastoid incision to the nuchal muscles; musculoperiosteal flaps expose the retrosigmoid bone. Make a craniotomy bounded by the sigmoid in front and the transverse sinus above, and wax the mastoid cells. What you should see The blue edge of the sigmoid in front and the transverse sinus above at the corner of the bone window."],["osec","th:op-140~s4-2","Relax the cerebellum","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: retrosigmoid","Open the dura over the cerebellum, support it with a Telfa strip and suction, and open the cisterna magna , releasing CSF patiently so the cerebellum relaxes without injury. What you should see CSF draining and the cerebellum falling away from the petrous bone without a retractor's force."],["osec","th:op-140~s4-3","Drill the canal","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: retrosigmoid","Debulk the angle tumour first if it blocks drilling. Reflect the canal dura down as a semicircular flap and drill 270 degrees of the canal. In hearing preservation expose only the medial two thirds : further laterally you enter the inner ear. Figure 4 After opening the dura, the canal exposed about 180 degrees by drilling troughs on either side. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 140.4 What you should see The canal dura and its contents, with no blue line of the posterior canal or vestibule opened laterally."],["osec","th:op-140~s4-4","Remove the tumour and seal","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: retrosigmoid","Remove the tumour as in the translabyrinthine route. Wax the canal's air cells and plug the canal with muscle. What you should see The seventh and eighth nerves at the brainstem, the lower cranial nerves below, and every opened air cell waxed. Opened air cells Unsealed petrous air cells around the canal let CSF escape to the middle ear and nose. Wax every opened cell."],["osec","th:op-140~s4-5","Close","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: retrosigmoid","Fill the subarachnoid space with saline, close the dura primarily or with a graft, reconstruct with hydroxyapatite cement, and close as before. What you should see A watertight dural closure under saline."],["osec","th:op-140~s5-1","Craniotomy on the zygomatic root","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: middle fossa","A vertical preauricular incision to temporalis; divide it to expose the squama and find the zygomatic root , the level of the middle fossa floor. Make a 5 × 5 cm craniotomy centred on it, flush with the floor. What you should see A window flush with the middle fossa floor at the zygomatic root."],["osec","th:op-140~s5-2","Elevate the dura","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: middle fossa","Raise the dura from back to front to protect the geniculate ganglion and facial nerve, dehiscent in up to 15% , forward to the foramen spinosum and medially to the superior petrosal sinus and petrous ridge. Fix the retractor (two spatulas on gooseneck arms) to the head frame. What you should see The middle meningeal artery in front, the greater superficial petrosal nerve, the arcuate eminence and the petrous ridge."],["osec","th:op-140~s5-3","Find the landmarks","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: middle fossa","Find the geniculate ganglion by antidromic stimulation of the greater superficial petrosal nerve . Find the superior canal directly, or through the tegmen cells; blue-line it, and occlude it with wax if more room is needed. What you should see The blue line of the superior canal, with the internal canal at about 60 degrees in front of it."],["osec","th:op-140~s5-4","Drill the canal","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: middle fossa","Drill from medial to lateral ; laterally the cochlea lies close in front and limits exposure. What you should see The canal dura bared medially first, with Bill's bar separating the facial from the superior vestibular nerve laterally."],["osec","th:op-140~s5-5","Remove the tumour","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: middle fossa","Open the canal dura and separate the facial nerve, which lies anteriorly , from the tumour first; dissect medial to lateral to protect the cochlear fibres at the habenula perforata. Figure 5 An intracanalicular tumour dissected from the facial and cochlear nerves through the middle fossa. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 140.5 What you should see The facial nerve lying in front and above, stimulating, separated before the tumour is moved."],["osec","th:op-140~s5-6","Close","Acoustic Neuroma · The operation: middle fossa","Cover the bony defect with fat or acellular dermis, remove the retractors, replate the bone flap, and close the fascia and skin. What you should see A dry field, the bone flap fixed and no CSF from the wound."],["osec","th:op-140~s6-1","Crisis drills","Acoustic Neuroma · When it goes wrong","Facial weakness after surgery 1 Nerve stimulates at low current at the end good outlook delayed palsy also does well 2 Eye drops, night ointment, moisture chamber platinum lid weight if stimulation was poor or the palsy lasts over a month 3 Nerve divided immediate direct repair otherwise nerve substitution (V to VII, XII to VII) CSF leak 1 From the wound extra sutures and a tight mastoid dressing lumbar drain if it persists 2 From the nose lumbar drain first then surgery reopen and wax air cells after middle fossa or retrosigmoid surgery if hearing is gone, obliterate the Eustachian tube with muscle, Surgicel or cement 3 Recalcitrant close the ear canal, remove all canal and drum skin, obliterate the tube, middle ear and mastoid"],["osec","th:op-140~s6-2","Common errors in technique","Acoustic Neuroma · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Too little bone removed in translabyrinthine surgery. A misplaced middle fossa or retrosigmoid craniotomy, limiting medial exposure. Stimulating or dissecting a firing facial nerve."],["osec","th:op-140~s7-1","After the operation","Acoustic Neuroma · Aftercare","Intensive care for 1 to 2 days. CT of the head and temporal bones next morning; if there is no bleeding, start subcutaneous heparin. Blood count and chemistry. Walk early: early vestibular physiotherapy has cut the authors' average stay to 3.5 days."],["ev","th:op-140~s7-2","Acoustic Neuroma: current evidence","Acoustic Neuroma · Aftercare","Wait, irradiate or operate. In the V-REX trial of small and medium tumours, upfront radiosurgery reduced tumour volume more than wait and scan, but hearing, balance and quality of life did not differ at four years (Dhayalan et al., JAMA 2023). The European (EANO) guideline recommends observation for small asymptomatic tumours, radiosurgery for small to medium tumours that grow, and surgery for large tumours with mass effect (Goldbrunner et al., Neuro Oncol 2020). Approach. The Congress of Neurological Surgeons guidelines find no approach superior for facial nerve outcome (Olson et al., Neurosurgery 2018); near-total removal followed by radiosurgery to the remnant protects the facial nerve in large tumours."],["osec","th:op-140~s7-2","Complications","Acoustic Neuroma · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Facial paralysis Immediate or delayed even with an intact nerve; eye care; repair or substitution if divided CSF leak Wound or nose; cement has reduced it; see the drill Intracranial haemorrhage Managed by site and size Hearing loss Can occur even with good ABR at the end; CROS aids, bone-conduction implants Headache Limit bone dust spread and muscle-dura adhesion; worst after retrosigmoid surgery Stroke, meningitis, wound infection Uncommon"],["osec","th:op-140~s8-1","The evidence","Acoustic Neuroma · For the examination","Near-total versus total removal: leaving small remnants on critical structures may reduce morbidity; prospective long-term trials are under way. Delayed facial palsy: a retrospective House Clinic report suggests steroids and antivirals beforehand, as for Bell's palsy, prevent it. Trends: in SEER (8330 patients, 2004 to 2011) 48% had surgery, 24% radiation alone and 29% observation; surgery fell and observation rose; younger patients and larger tumours were operated on. The senior author's 1709 tumours: observation 47%, translabyrinthine 32%, middle fossa 11%, retrosigmoid 7%, radiation 3%."],["osec","th:op-140~s8-2","Viva essentials","Acoustic Neuroma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Asymmetric loss is a schwannoma until proven otherwise; MRI is the gold standard. Bruns' nystagmus. The 50/50 rule for hearing worth saving. The three strategies and their outcomes; why compression rules out radiation. The three approaches: indications, advantages, limits. Fundus anatomy: transverse crest, Bill's bar, the facial nerve in front. Eustachian tube obliteration; fat and cement closure. Managing a CSF leak and facial palsy."],["osec","th:op-140~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Acoustic Neuroma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing NF2. Radiating a tumour that compresses the brainstem. Fixing the middle fossa retractor to the table instead of the pins. Drilling the lateral third of the canal in hearing preservation retrosigmoid surgery."],["osec","th:op-141~s1-1","The case at a glance","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Operating note","Temporal bone resection Summary Suspect it Long-standing otorrhoea, deep unrelenting pain, bloody discharge, facial palsy, a mass or ulcer in the canal, an ear canal condition not responding to topical or systemic antibiotics Sleeve resection Very limited lesions of the meatus and lateral canal (but see the common errors) Lateral resection Cancer confined to the canal: T1, T2 Subtotal resection Cancer in the middle ear and mastoid: T3, T4 Total resection Cancer in the petrous apex or the Eustachian tube Extensions Jaw joint, neck, dura, infratemporal fossa as required Contraindications Cavernous sinus or brain invasion (cure unlikely); unfitness for long anaesthesia and recovery; carotid canal involvement is a problem but not an absolute bar Biopsy By direct incision ; never a staging mastoidectomy (it spoils imaging and margins) Anaesthesia General oral intubation; arterial line beyond a lateral resection; urinary catheter; no neuromuscular block ; furosemide and mannitol 0.25 to 0.5 g/kg 30 minutes before intracranial dissection Position Supine, ear up; Mayfield clamp for intracranial or extensive work; taped to the table at shoulders, hips and thighs; compression stockings; prepare neck and graft sites Antibiotics Third-generation cephalosporin with CSF penetration (ceftriaxone) Monitoring Facial nerve (orbicularis oris and oculi); IX and X in some cases Tray Otological and plastic microscopes; otological drill; mastoid and middle ear set; head and neck set; diathermy for both teams; Prass or Kartush probe; fine straight and curved osteotomes with mallet; osseointegrated"],["osec","th:op-141~s1-2","Consent","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer with the bone of the ear around it; this is the only treatment that can cure it. Hearing on that side will be reduced or lost; a bone-conduction device can help later. The outer ear may need to be removed; a prosthetic ear can be fitted later. Glasses may be hard to wear if the upper ear goes. Facial weakness , sometimes from removing the nerve; eye protection. Dizziness after larger resections. Swallowing and voice problems if the lower cranial nerves are removed; tracheostomy and vocal cord surgery may be needed. Leak of brain fluid , meningitis, bleeding, stroke or brain swelling. Radiotherapy about 6 weeks afterwards for most cancers."],["osec","th:op-141~s1-3","Before you start","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Operating note","Cancer can arise in the canal, middle ear, mastoid, and rarely the jugular foramen and petrous apex; it spreads into the bone from the concha, pinna, parotid and dura; metastases favour the petrous apex . It mimics chronic or malignant otitis externa, canal cholesteatoma, osteonecrosis and otitis media. Symptoms are deep, persistent pain, bloody otorrhoea, hearing loss, facial weakness, other cranial nerve palsies and a swollen or narrowed canal. Younger patients tend to have more aggressive disease. Look for a friable, necrotic, bleeding broad-based mass, polyp or ulcer, or diffuse thickening or stenosis of the canal; define cartilaginous and bony involvement under the microscope; test the facial and other cranial nerves; look for drum or annulus involvement, a middle ear mass or effusion, neck masses and trismus (the jaw joint). Obtain an audiogram."],["osec","th:op-141~s1-4","Imaging and staging","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Operating note","Both CT and MRI are usually needed: thin-cut temporal bone CT, skull base MRI with and without contrast, and neck CT or MRI. CT angiography, or angiography with balloon occlusion , if carotid resection is possible; venography for the dural sinuses. Chest radiograph and liver tests; for cancers prone to spread, such as melanoma, CT of chest, abdomen and pelvis and a bone scan; PET may help. Imaging tends to underestimate spread, especially backward, upward and medially. Modified Pittsburgh staging T Extent T1 Limited to the canal, no bone erosion or soft tissue extension T2 Limited canal bone erosion, or under 0.5 cm of soft tissue T3 Full-thickness canal bone erosion with under 0.5 cm soft tissue, or tumour in the middle ear or mastoid T4 Cochlea, petrous apex, medial middle ear wall, carotid canal, jugular foramen or dura; over 0.5 cm soft tissue; or facial nerve paresis"],["osec","th:op-141~s1-5","Preparation","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Operating note","Assemble the team: neurotologist, head and neck and reconstructive surgeons, neurosurgeon, pathologist, radiation oncologist, intensivist, experienced nurses. Decide whether to place osseointegrated implants for a hearing device or prosthetic ear now or later. Optimise heart, lung, diabetes, bleeding disorders and nutrition."],["osec","th:op-141~s2-1","Pathways","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Anatomy you must see","The cartilaginous canal is a poor barrier: cancer erodes into the parotid in front and the concha and postauricular sulcus behind, and through the fissures of Santorini straight into periparotid tissue. The bony canal resists, so cancer tracks medially through the drum into the middle ear and mastoid. The foramen of Huschke , a defect in the anteroinferior tympanic ring that usually closes by age 5, links the bony canal with the parotid and jaw joint. From the middle ear, along the Eustachian tube to the carotid, Meckel's cave and cavernous sinus; upward through the tegmen to the middle fossa dura and temporal lobe. The otic capsule resists, but the round and oval windows let cancer into the vestibule, internal auditory canal and posterior fossa. Along the facial nerve intracranially, into the neck through the stylomastoid foramen, or to the infratemporal fossa via the greater superficial petrosal nerve. Backward to the posterior fossa plate, dura and sigmoid; downward to the jugular foramen and neck. Lymphatics of the canal drain forward to parotid and periparotid nodes, down to the internal jugular chain, back to the mastoid nodes; middle ear and mastoid disease rarely spreads to nodes. Figure 1 Axial (A) and coronal (B) pathways of spread: (1) forward through the cartilaginous canal to the parotid, (2) through the concha, (3) through the drum, (4) into the mastoid, (5) to the carotid and Eustachian tube, (6) through the windows to the otic capsule, (7) along the facial nerve, (8) through the mastoid to the posterior fossa and sigmoid, (9) down to the jugular foramen and "],["osec","th:op-141~s3-1","What comes out","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: lateral temporal bone resection","For cancer limited to the canal without medial spread through the drum. The canal, drum and malleus are removed en bloc, bounded by the middle ear medially, the jaw joint capsule in front, the zygomatic root above, the mastoid behind and the infratemporal fossa below, with concha and pinna laterally as needed. What you should see On examination and scan: tumour confined to the canal with an intact drum."],["osec","th:op-141~s3-2","Incisions","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: lateral temporal bone resection","Inject 2% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline. Cut around the concha for a wide skin margin; keeping the upper third of the pinna helps those who wear glasses, otherwise saving fragments has little value. Make a wide incision 3 cm behind the postauricular sulcus , into the neck if a neck dissection is planned. Figure 3 A typical incision for a canal cancer: circumferential through the cartilaginous canal and a wide postauricular incision. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 141.3 What you should see An incision clear of the tumour by a wide margin all round the concha."],["osec","th:op-141~s3-3","Free the soft tissue and seal the canal","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: lateral temporal bone resection","Elevate the skin a little forward, then go medially through the fibroperiosteal layer to the mastoid cortex. Raise the subperiosteal flap and join the conchal and postauricular incisions lateral to the temporalis and parotid fascia, continuing forward to masseter. Send the skin margin for frozen section. Oversew the lateral canal to prevent spill. What you should see The canal closed off like a purse, with the tumour sealed inside the specimen."],["osec","th:op-141~s3-4","Mastoid and extended facial recess","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: lateral temporal bone resection","Perform a complete mastoidectomy without thinning the canal enough to expose the cancer . Open the facial recess and extend it by sacrificing the chorda tympani; inspect the middle ear to confirm the drum is not breached. Follow the facial nerve to the stylomastoid foramen. Separate the incudostapedial joint, remove the incus and cut the tensor tendon. What you should see A healthy middle ear through the extended facial recess, the facial nerve followed to the stylomastoid foramen, and the incus out."],["osec","th:op-141~s3-5","Free the canal below and above","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: lateral temporal bone resection","Inferiorly, drill between the facial nerve and the annulus toward the front of the canal until you reach the jaw joint capsule and the periosteum of the anterior tympanic ring. Superiorly, drill through the zygomatic root cells between the tegmen and the epitympanum to the endosteum of the glenoid fossa. Now only the thin tympanic plate holds the canal in front; extend the resection into the joint or infratemporal fossa if the cancer goes there. What you should see The canal attached only in front, with the jaw joint capsule below and the glenoid fossa above."],["osec","th:op-141~s3-6","The osteotomy","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: lateral temporal bone resection","Pass a fine osteotome through the extended facial recess to the bone just lateral to the Eustachian tube orifice ; a few gentle taps complete the cut, and the canal comes off the joint capsule. Pack the Eustachian tube. What you should see The osteotome's tip lateral to the tube orifice before each tap, then the canal block rocking free. The carotid in front of the tube Stay oriented to the Eustachian tube orifice during the osteotomy: the carotid lies just medial and anterior."],["osec","th:op-141~s3-7","Parotid, specimen and closure","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: lateral temporal bone resection","Perform a superficial parotidectomy with facial nerve dissection and remove the canal with the parotid; orient the specimen yourself for the pathologist. Do the neck dissection as a separate specimen. Fill the defect with an inferiorly based temporalis flap , helped by removing the zygomatic root, and close the conchal skin directly or with a split skin graft. What you should see The canal, drum and parotid as one oriented specimen, a stimulating facial nerve, and the defect filled."],["osec","th:op-141~s4-1","What comes out","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: subtotal and total resection","For cancer through the drum into the middle ear, hypotympanum, otic capsule, facial nerve or mastoid. The main specimen usually includes the canal, drum, superficial or total parotid, mandibular condyle and facial nerve; the otic capsule, tegmen, posterior fossa plate and jugular bulb are usually drilled piecemeal (or taken en bloc), with dura, temporal lobe and infratemporal contents as needed. Margins: middle fossa dura above, posterior fossa dura and sigmoid behind, internal auditory canal anteromedially, skull base and jugular bulb below, petrous apex medially. What you should see On the scan: which structures medial to the drum are involved, to define the block."],["osec","th:op-141~s4-2","Neck control","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: subtotal and total resection","Complete a lateral resection. Expose and control the great vessels and lower cranial nerves in the neck and dissect the neck as the nodes require. Detach sternomastoid from the mastoid tip to show digastric and the transverse process of the atlas, exposing the jugular vein, carotid and nerves at the skull base. What you should see The jugular vein, internal carotid and cranial nerves IX to XII controlled at the skull base."],["osec","th:op-141~s4-3","The temporal bone","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: subtotal and total resection","Thin and resect the tegmen and posterior fossa plate to expose the jugular bulb. Decompress the whole facial nerve if keeping it. Open the internal auditory canal (translabyrinthine or middle fossa), divide the cochlear and vestibular nerves, and mobilise or divide the facial nerve. If cancer reaches the protympanum, resect the condyle and dissect the infratemporal fossa with total parotidectomy, carotid transposition, Eustachian tube resection and V3 as needed. Drill out the rest of the otic capsule and the vaginal process between bulb and carotid, leaving only the anterior petrous apex, and expose the vertical and horizontal carotid. What you should see Middle and posterior fossa dura, the sigmoid and bulb exposed, and the petrous carotid found before bone is removed around it."],["osec","th:op-141~s4-4","Sinus, bulb and dura","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: subtotal and total resection","If cancer is on the lateral wall of the sigmoid or bulb, ligate the jugular vein and sigmoid, open the bulb and pack it against inferior petrosal bleeding. Cancer on the medial bulb wall means entering the pars nervosa and taking IX, X and XI. Resect involved dura with margins and repair with fascia or pericranium; limited temporal lobe invasion can be resected, with little chance of cure. Cover the internal auditory canal with fascia and fibrin glue. What you should see The sinus tied above and the vein below before the bulb is opened; clean dural margins. The vein of Labbé Injury causes temporal lobe infarction, with aphasia on the dominant side and seizures. When packing or ligating the sigmoid or transverse sinus, stay well forward of where the vein enters the transverse sinus."],["osec","th:op-141~s4-5","Total resection","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: subtotal and total resection","For the Eustachian tube or petrous apex: after a subtotal resection, gain proximal and distal control of the intrapetrous carotid, mobilise it from the skull base, drill the remaining apex and resect or obliterate the tube. What you should see The petrous carotid freed and controlled at both ends."],["osec","th:op-141~s4-6","Reconstruct","Resection of the Temporal Bone · The operation: subtotal and total resection","Close the dura watertight , obliterate dead space, and use a microvascular free flap , the most flexible option for large defects. Avoid suction drains near the dural repair. Close the Eustachian tube completely. If a tracheostomy is made, keep it separate from the neck dissection, so CSF cannot track from the neck to the stoma. What you should see Watertight dura, a closed Eustachian tube, and a flap filling the cavity."],["osec","th:op-141~s5-1","Crisis drills","Resection of the Temporal Bone · When it goes wrong","Carotid laceration 1 Options primary repair patch saphenous vein bypass sacrifice without reconstruction 2 Know beforehand whether sacrifice is tolerated, from a balloon occlusion test Acute intracranial haemorrhage after surgery 1 Recognise altered consciousness focal signs, a fixed dilated pupil, bradycardia and hypertension 2 Act non-contrast CT mannitol 0.25 to 0.5 g/kg and hyperventilation if intubated 3 Unstable or herniating emergency exploration ventriculostomy for rapid decompression reopen, evacuate, find the source CSF leak 1 Through the incision horizontal mattress sutures and a pressure dressing 2 Over 24 hours lumbar drain 3 Still leaking at 3 to 5 days explore Flap in trouble 1 Detect pinprick, Doppler 2 Act back to theatre for revision or another flap dehiscence is commonest at the distal edge of a pedicled flap"],["osec","th:op-141~s5-2","Common errors in technique","Resection of the Temporal Bone · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Lesser operations (sleeve resection, cartilaginous canal only, radical mastoidectomy) for canal cancer, which heal poorly with collapse, cholesteatoma and impaction: a lateral temporal bone resection is the minimum . Breaching the bony canal or annulus during a lateral resection, exposing the field to cancer. Losing orientation to the Eustachian tube during the osteotomy. Poorly labelled frozen sections; not orienting the specimen yourself. Not being prepared to extend the resection, especially for adenocarcinoma and adenoid cystic carcinoma."],["osec","th:op-141~s6-1","After the operation","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Aftercare","After intracranial surgery: neurointensive care, frequent neurological checks and CT next morning. Monitor heart, lungs and fluid balance; head up, stool softeners, no straining; diet as tolerated; antibiotics for 24 hours; compression stockings. Mastoid dressing for 3 to 5 days (check the wound at days 1 to 2); bolsters off at day 5; leave flaps uncovered for monitoring; watch for CSF leak and congestion of the remaining pinna or flap."],["osec","th:op-141~s6-2","Eye and nerves","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Aftercare","For facial palsy: saline drops every 1 to 2 hours by day, lubricant and taping at night, tape slings for the lids, a moisture chamber. If recovery will be slow or absent, a gold or platinum lid weight and a lateral tarsal strip later. If working lower cranial nerves were divided, perform a tracheostomy and consider a type I thyroplasty . Gait and vestibular therapy after extensive resections."],["osec","th:op-141~s6-3","Later","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Aftercare","Radiotherapy at about 6 weeks or when healed. Imaging at 3 months, then every 6 months (PET helps see beneath flaps). A prosthetic ear by adhesive or osseointegrated implants after radiotherapy and healing; a bone-conduction device or CROS aid for hearing."],["osec","th:op-141~s6-4","Complications","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Aftercare","Aspiration pneumonia, meningitis, CSF leak, intracranial haemorrhage, wound and flap problems, deformity, hearing loss, vertigo, facial and lower cranial nerve palsies, temporal lobe oedema or infarction, cerebral ischaemia."],["ev","th:op-141~s6-5","Resection of the Temporal Bone: current evidence","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Aftercare","Staging. The modified Pittsburgh staging system is used for squamous carcinoma of the ear canal (Moody et al., Am J Otol 2000). Treatment. Lateral temporal bone resection for T1 to T2 tumours, subtotal resection for T3 to T4, with superficial parotidectomy and neck dissection as indicated, and postoperative radiotherapy for all but small T1 tumours with clear margins. Five-year survival is about 80 to 90% for early tumours and 30 to 50% for advanced ones in published series. Chronic otitis externa that does not respond, bleeds or causes facial weakness needs a biopsy."],["osec","th:op-141~s6-5","Adjuvant treatment","Resection of the Temporal Bone · Aftercare","Postoperative radiotherapy for T3 and T4 , strongly considered for T2 , and for perineural invasion, close margins, nodal disease or extracapsular spread; surgery alone cures T1 with adequate margins. Radiotherapy alone gives poor survival. Chemotherapy is promising but unproven."],["osec","th:op-141~s7-1","What we do not know","Resection of the Temporal Bone · For the examination","The roles of elective neck dissection, parotidectomy, primary radiotherapy and chemotherapy, and the limits of resectability (total and piecemeal resection with dural or brain involvement), remain unsettled. The disease is rare, series are small and heterogeneous, staging and treatment vary, and there is no AJCC-endorsed staging system. The editor stresses early recognition, a common staging system and long-term clinical and radiological surveillance."],["osec","th:op-141~s7-2","Viva essentials","Resection of the Temporal Bone · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for When to suspect cancer in a chronic ear. The modified Pittsburgh staging and the operation for each stage. Why biopsy is incisional and never a mastoidectomy. The pathways: fissures of Santorini, foramen of Huschke, the drum, Eustachian tube, tegmen, windows, facial nerve. The steps and boundaries of a lateral temporal bone resection; the osteotomy beside the Eustachian tube. Subtotal and total resection: the carotid, the jugular bulb, the vein of Labbé. Reconstruction, eye care, tracheostomy with lower cranial nerve loss. Adjuvant radiotherapy by stage."],["osec","th:op-141~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Resection of the Temporal Bone · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating a bleeding, painful canal mass as otitis externa for months. A sleeve resection for canal cancer. Joining a tracheostomy to the neck dissection. Active suction near the dural repair."],["osec","th:op-142~s1-1","The case at a glance","Glomus Tumors · Operating note","Glomus tumours of the temporal bone Summary Aim Halt troublesome symptoms (pulsatile tinnitus, hearing loss, dizziness, dysphagia, hoarseness, shoulder weakness, catecholamine effects) without adding new ones Tympanicum Most symptomatic tumours removed with little risk: transcanal for small tumours, postauricular with mastoidectomy and extended facial recess for larger ones Jugulare Many removable completely with modest risk unless the pars nervosa, significant carotid or intracranial extension is involved; then consider observation, subtotal resection with observation or radiation, or primary radiation Small to medium tumours Observation with serial MRI or primary radiation are options Contraindications Age and comorbidity making anaesthesia too risky; existing dysphagia (from stroke or other disease) that would not tolerate further lower cranial nerve injury Preparation Alpha and beta blockade for catecholamine excess; embolisation 1 to 2 days before most jugulare surgery; balloon occlusion with xenon perfusion (or a protective stent) if the carotid is at risk; cross-match 2 to 4 units for jugulare tumours Anaesthesia and position General; supine, head turned away Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin; clindamycin in allergy Monitoring Facial nerve; IX to XII for extensive jugulare tumours; somatosensory potentials if the carotid is involved Tray Microscope; ear set; otological drill; neurosurgical set and retractors for intracranial extension; haemostatic agents (Surgicel, Avitene, Surgifoam)"],["osec","th:op-142~s1-2","Consent","Glomus Tumors · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove a benign but growing, very vascular tumour of the ear and skull base, or to control it. Alternatives: watching it with scans, or focused radiation, which stops growth in most cases but does not remove it. Bleeding , sometimes heavy; transfusion may be needed. Hearing loss and dizziness. Facial weakness , especially if the facial nerve has to be moved. Swallowing, voice, shoulder and tongue weakness if the lower cranial nerves are injured; a feeding tube or tracheostomy and voice surgery may be needed. Leaving tumour on these nerves reduces this risk. Leak of brain fluid , stroke and carotid injury in large tumours. Family screening if the tumour is of the inherited type."],["osec","th:op-142~s1-3","Before you start","Glomus Tumors · Operating note","About 20% are familial; these are more often multicentric and bilateral and present younger in successive generations. Ask about pulsatile tinnitus , hearing loss, vertigo or imbalance, otalgia, facial weakness or twitching, dysphagia, hoarseness, shoulder weakness or pain, dysarthria and tongue weakness, and labile hypertension, tachycardia, headache, sweating, pallor and nausea , which suggest catecholamine release needing medical treatment. Record previous surgery or radiation, heart and lung disease, bleeding disorders and a family history of vascular or endocrine tumours. Look at the ear with the microscope or endoscope: a vascular middle ear mass, drum involvement, canal extension, and Brown's sign (the mass blanches when the drum is pressurised). Test with forks, gait, tandem gait and Romberg, the facial and lower cranial nerves, and feel for a mass in the upper neck. Obtain pure tones, speech discrimination and a tympanogram."],["osec","th:op-142~s1-4","Imaging","Glomus Tumors · Operating note","CT of the temporal bone at 0.6 mm: soft tissue in the middle ear, attic or mastoid; erosion along the jugular bulb and foramen ; carotid involvement; posterior petrous erosion (posterior fossa extension); otic capsule erosion. MRI with gadolinium of brain, internal auditory canal and neck: the enhancing tumour's extent, flow voids in the sigmoid, bulb and jugular vein, and synchronous tumours. Angiography (four-vessel), usually at embolisation: tumour blush, feeders, other vascular tumours. MR or CT angiography is a less sensitive alternative. Figure 1 Bone-window CT: a left hypotympanic mass (white arrow) eroding bone between the carotid (black arrowhead), the jugular vein (large arrow) and the facial nerve (small arrow); the pars nervosa is spared (white arrowhead). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 142.1"],["osec","th:op-142~s1-5","Classification","Glomus Tumors · Operating note","Fisch classification Type Extent A Middle ear cleft (promontory) B Middle ear, hypotympanum and mastoid C1 Eroding the jugular bulb and carotid foramen, not invading the carotid C2 Infralabyrinthine and apical temporal bone, eroding the vertical carotid C3 As C2, eroding the horizontal carotid C4 Reaching the foramen lacerum and cavernous sinus D1 Intracranial under 2 cm D2 Intracranial over 2 cm (e extradural, i intradural) Di3 Unresectable intracranial extension Glasscock-Jackson classification Type Tympanicum Jugulare I Small mass on the promontory Small tumour of the bulb, middle ear and mastoid II Filling the middle ear Extending under the internal auditory canal, may be intracranial III Middle ear and mastoid Into the petrous apex, may be intracranial IV Through the drum into the canal, may extend in front of the carotid Beyond the apex to the clivus or infratemporal fossa, may be intracranial"],["osec","th:op-142~s2-1","Landmarks","Glomus Tumors · Anatomy you must see","The facial nerve in its horizontal and vertical segments, found from the oval window niche, lateral canal and stylomastoid foramen. The lower cranial nerves leaving the jugular foramen: IX medial to the jugular vein ; X between the jugular vein and carotid ; XI crossing lateral to the jugular vein toward the posterior neck; XII lateral to the carotid , running forward deep to the digastric. The petrous carotid , medial to the Eustachian tube, foramen ovale and foramen spinosum. The medial wall of the jugular bulb , behind which lies the pars nervosa: leave it intact and avoid pressing on it to keep the nerves."],["osec","th:op-142~s3-1","Choose the route","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus tympanicum","Small tumours of the meso- and hypotympanum without bulb involvement come out through the canal , if the canal anatomy allows. Larger ones need a postauricular approach, perhaps with mastoidectomy and facial recess, and a canaloplasty if the inferior annulus cannot be seen because of a tortuous canal or hypotympanic extension. What you should see A red, pulsatile mass behind the drum, blanching with pneumatic pressure; and on CT, whether bone separates it from the jugular bulb (if its whole edge is seen, it is tympanic)."],["osec","th:op-142~s3-2","The tympanomeatal flap","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus tympanicum","Inject the canal and postauricular sulcus. For the postauricular route, make a lateral and posterior canal skin flap with superior, inferior and connecting incisions halfway down the bony canal; incise 3 mm behind the sulcus, join the incisions, retract the meatal flap with a Penrose and the pinna with a Weitlaner. Raise the tympanomeatal flap and enter the middle ear medial to the fibrous annulus, lifting it as far forward as the tumour requires, without cutting into the tumour . Figure 2 The transtympanic approach to a glomus tumour confined to the middle ear. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 142.2 What you should see The whole circumference of the tumour within the middle ear, including its lower edge."],["osec","th:op-142~s3-3","Find and divide the pedicle","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus tympanicum","For small tumours the vascular pedicle is usually on the promontory : manipulate the tumour gently until it shows, divide it, and control bleeding with bone wax or Surgicel. Use microbipolar sparingly: too much risks the cochlea. What you should see A vessel entering the tumour from the promontory (the tympanic plexus), then a tumour that shrinks and pales."],["osec","th:op-142~s3-4","Larger tumours","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus tympanicum","Larger tumours may have pedicles in the hypotympanum and protympanum that cannot be reached before debulking: shrink with bipolar, debulk, then find and divide them; adrenaline-soaked pledgets help. If the tumour reaches the attic or mastoid, add a canal wall up mastoidectomy . Remove the middle ear, hypotympanic and protympanic tumour first to take its blood supply. What you should see Feeding vessels from the hypotympanum and protympanum as the bulk comes down."],["osec","th:op-142~s3-5","The ossicles and the footplate","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus tympanicum","The incus and malleus head may need removing (reconstruct afterwards), or only a temporary separation of the incudostapedial joint. Tumour in the obturator foramen or on the footplate can be vaporised with a diode or argon laser (0.2 mm fibre, 1 to 2 W, 0.2 s single pulses) or a CO2 laser at 2 to 5 W, sparing the footplate and horizontal facial nerve. What you should see The stapes, footplate and horizontal facial nerve clear of tumour, intact."],["osec","th:op-142~s3-6","Toward the bulb","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus tympanicum","Rarely the tumour extends below the hypotympanum toward the bulb (usually without invading its wall, so the sigmoid need not be opened): extend the infratympanic facial recess , and if needed skeletonise the distal vertical facial nerve as a fallopian bridge . Figure 3 (A) The extended facial recess approach to the middle ear and hypotympanum, with retrofacial infralabyrinthine cells opened to show the tumour's back edge; (B) the operative view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 142.3 What you should see The jugular bulb's dome beneath the hypotympanum, with the vertical facial nerve bridged in bone."],["osec","th:op-142~s3-7","Close","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus tympanicum","Repair any drum perforation with a medial graft, pack the middle ear with Gelfoam or Surgicel and the canal with Gelfoam, close in layers and apply a mastoid dressing. What you should see A dry middle ear, an intact or grafted drum."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-1","Incision and neck control","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","Most involve the middle ear, bulb and hypotympanic cells without intracranial spread (Fisch C1 to C2, Glasscock-Jackson jugulare I and II). Make a postauricular incision 3 cm behind the pinna , over the mastoid tip and into an upper neck crease toward the greater horn of the hyoid, through platysma, preserving the great auricular nerve. Without cervical tumour, the neck dissection is for inferior control of the jugular vein ; keep it separate from the mastoid wound so CSF cannot leak into the neck if the dura is opened. Figure 4 The postauricular incision carried into the neck: carotid, jugular vein and lower cranial nerves isolated, sternomastoid and digastric retracted. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 142.4 What you should see The internal jugular vein, the carotids and nerves IX to XII in the upper neck, with a loop round the vein."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-2","Temporal bone and fallopian bridge","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","Perform the mastoid dissection described above. Open the facial recess, sacrificing the chorda, as an extended facial recess ; remove the retrofacial and infralabyrinthine cells to the stylomastoid foramen, skeletonising the nerve to eggshell as a fallopian bridge . What you should see The facial nerve as a bridge of eggshell bone, with the hypotympanum open in front of it and the retrofacial cells behind."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-3","Expose the sigmoid and bulb","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","Outline the digastric ridge and continue forward to the stylomastoid foramen, removing the mastoid tip if needed. Expose the posterior fossa dura in front of and behind the sigmoid and decompress the sinus, leaving a shelf of bone over its proximal part near the sinodural angle (above Donaldson's line), and free the sinus from its bony roof. Follow it down to the bulb, removing all bone. Figure 5 A canal wall down dissection isolating the sigmoid sinus, the vertical facial nerve along the extended recess, the digastric ridge, the neck vessels and the lower cranial nerves. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 142.5 What you should see The sigmoid bare from the shelf above down to the bulb."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-4","Control the sigmoid above and the jugular below","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","Before opening the sigmoid, gain control: pack Surgicel extraluminally under the bony shelf over the proximal sinus, or, if that fails, ligate it: make small dural openings in front of and behind the sinus, pass an aneurysm needle from back to front hugging its deep surface (away from the cerebellum), draw through a long 2-0 silk, cut it into two ligatures, and tie both over a small piece of muscle. Doubly ligate the jugular vein in the neck. If tumour enters the cervical jugular vein, dissect its proximal stump up to the jugular fossa, passing it medial to the accessory nerve , which must be preserved; otherwise leave the tied vein. Keep the distal extratemporal carotid in view; remove the styloid and its muscles if needed. Figure 6 The extended facial recess exposing tumour in the hypotympanum, the vertical facial nerve left in place; the proximal sigmoid controlled by extraluminal packing or suture ligation. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 142.6 Figure 7 The proximal sigmoid controlled; the jugular vein divided and its proximal segment dissected into the jugular fossa, the carotid marking the anterior plane. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 142.7 What you should see A collapsed sinus below the packing or ligatures, and the jugular vein tied in the neck."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-5","Remove the tumour","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","Remove tumour piecemeal down to the remnant around the sigmoid and bulb, with bipolar and adrenaline 1:1000 pledgets. Open the sigmoid below the ligatures, incising its inferior wall lengthwise toward the bulb; its medial wall is usually free unless there is intracranial spread. Free the tumour from back to front, separating the superior, lateral and inferior walls from the medial wall, and dissect the lateral and upper bulb. What you should see The tumour peeling off the intact medial wall of the bulb."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-6","The inferior petrosal sinus","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","Once the tumour comes out of the bulb, brisk bleeding comes from the openings of the inferior petrosal sinus and condylar vein: pack the medial bulb with oxidised cellulose. This is done deep to the vertical facial nerve: do not press on its deep surface. Remove the remaining hypotympanic and middle ear tumour from both sides of the nerve. What you should see Dark venous bleeding from the openings on the medial wall, stopping with light packing."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-7","Leave the pars nervosa","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","Dissection medial to the medial wall of the bulb means dissecting the lower cranial nerves. The current trend is not to remove tumour there unless absolutely necessary, and to observe or irradiate it. What you should see The medial wall of the bulb left in place, covering nerves IX to XI. The medial wall of the bulb Overaggressive dissection of the pars nervosa leaves lasting dysphagia, hoarseness and shoulder weakness. Leave the medial wall, and do not compress it with packing."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-8","When to go canal wall down","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","For more exposure of tumour invading the carotid, or when the retrofacial and infralabyrinthine space is too tight (which may also mean transposing the facial nerve ); the drum and ossicles can still be reconstructed. What you should see The carotid canal in front of the tumour, and whether the facial nerve blocks access to it."],["osec","th:op-142~s4-9","Close","Glomus Tumors · The operation: glomus jugulare","CSF is met only if the medial bulb, sigmoid or posterior fossa dura is breached; then partly obliterate dead space with abdominal fat, otherwise leave it aerated. Pack the canal as before. Use a Penrose under the neck flaps after significant neck dissection (a suction drain should be turned off after a day, as it sucks air through the Eustachian tube). Close in three layers. What you should see No CSF, or a defect filled with fat; a closed wound."],["osec","th:op-142~s5-1","Exposure","Glomus Tumors · The operation: large jugulare tumours","Tumours of the petrous apex, horizontal carotid or cavernous sinus (Fisch C2 and beyond) add a postauricular infratemporal exposure to the temporal bone dissection. The canal is usually removed and the meatus closed; after significant dural resection, close the Eustachian tube and obliterate the defect with abdominal fat. What you should see The petrous carotid, with the tumour's anterior limit beyond it."],["osec","th:op-142~s5-2","Facial nerve transposition","Glomus Tumors · The operation: large jugulare tumours","When the vertical facial nerve blocks access to a tumour filling the jugular fossa and invading the carotid, decompress it from the geniculate ganglion to the stylomastoid foramen (cutting then diamond burs; favourable anatomy may allow mobilisation from the second genu only). Divide the remaining tendon and posterior digastric within the mastoid next to the stylomastoid foramen, lift the muscle with the foramen's contents from back to front, free the nerve from the fallopian canal, and suture its fibrous cuff forward to the periparotid fascia . Figure 8 The facial nerve mobilised forward, exposing tumour in the mastoid, middle ear and bulb; the styloid tendons divided and the mandible retracted to isolate the carotid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 142.8 What you should see The nerve lying forward in a groove in the parotid without tension, on its sheath of periosteum and digastric tissue at the foramen."],["osec","th:op-142~s6-1","Crisis drills","Glomus Tumors · When it goes wrong","Brisk bleeding from the bulb 1 Source inferior petrosal sinus and condylar vein openings 2 Act pack the medial bulb with oxidised cellulose avoid pressure on the facial nerve and pars nervosa CSF leak 1 Act lumbar drainage 3 to 5 days pressure mastoid and neck dressings surgical repair if needed 2 Prevent keep the neck wound separate fat obliteration Eustachian tube closure when the dura was resected New dysphagia or hoarseness 1 Assess barium swallow or FEESST 2 Manage diet to suit nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding while nil by mouth medialisation laryngoplasty, with or without cricopharyngeal myotomy"],["osec","th:op-142~s6-2","Common errors in technique","Glomus Tumors · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Too little infratemporal exposure from incomplete planning. Not being ready to control a CSF leak. Overaggressive pars nervosa dissection. No plan for the carotid, inviting catastrophic bleeding or stroke."],["osec","th:op-142~s7-1","After the operation","Glomus Tumors · Aftercare","Check the haematocrit after heavy bleeding. Watch for CSF rhinorrhoea, otorrhoea or a wound leak. Assess swallowing, voice and vocal fold movement, shoulder and tongue. Physiotherapy for shoulder weakness."],["osec","th:op-142~s7-2","Complications","Glomus Tumors · Aftercare","Uncontrolled haemorrhage; facial and lower cranial nerve injury; cochleovestibular damage; CSF leak; carotid injury; arterial or venous stroke."],["ev","th:op-142~s7-3","Glomus Tumors: current evidence","Glomus Tumors · Aftercare","Genetics. About a third of head and neck paragangliomas are hereditary, so guidelines recommend testing every patient for SDHx and related mutations. Radiosurgery. A meta-analysis found tumour control in about 95% of jugular paragangliomas after radiosurgery, with fewer new cranial neuropathies than surgery (Guss et al., Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys 2011). Current approach. Small tympanic tumours (Fisch A, Glasscock–Jackson tympanic I to II) are removed transcanally or via the mastoid; large jugular tumours in older patients are increasingly observed or irradiated, because surgery often costs lower cranial nerves. Preoperative embolisation reduces blood loss for larger tumours."],["osec","th:op-142~s7-3","Alternatives","Glomus Tumors · Aftercare","Observation with serial imaging; radiation: single-fraction radiosurgery, fractionated stereotactic radiotherapy or external beam, as primary treatment, after recurrence or after planned subtotal resection."],["osec","th:op-142~s8-1","How well are they controlled?","Glomus Tumors · For the examination","Gross total resection rarely recurs, especially for tympanicum tumours. For jugulare tumours, a meta-analysis found control of 69% after subtotal resection, 86% after gross total resection, 71% after subtotal resection with radiosurgery and 95% with radiosurgery alone, though groups differed. Lower cranial nerve function is usually good if the pars nervosa is not dissected and often poor if it is. After radiosurgery (Martin and colleagues), existing neuropathies improved in 20% and were stable in 77%, intact nerves were all preserved, and 5- and 10-year control was 97% and 94%."],["osec","th:op-142~s8-2","Genetics","Glomus Tumors · For the examination","About 20% are familial: four paraganglioma loci (PGL1 to 4), with SDH gene mutations inherited as autosomal dominant traits; finding one means searching for other tumours and screening relatives."],["osec","th:op-142~s8-3","Viva essentials","Glomus Tumors · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Tympanicum versus jugulare: origin, nerves at risk, treatment. Symptoms, Brown's sign, catecholamine excess and blockade. CT, MRI and angiography findings; embolisation 1 to 2 days before. Fisch and Glasscock-Jackson classifications. The nerves at the foramen and their relation to the jugular vein and carotid. The extended facial recess, the fallopian bridge, transposition. Controlling the sigmoid and jugular; the inferior petrosal sinus. Leaving the medial bulb wall; the function-preserving trend."],["osec","th:op-142~s8-4","Common pitfalls","Glomus Tumors · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating without screening for catecholamine excess. Cutting into a tympanicum tumour before finding the pedicle. Opening the sigmoid before controlling it. Joining the neck and mastoid wounds."],["osec","th:op-145~s1-1","The case at a glance","Temporal Bone Trauma · Operating note","Facial nerve decompression after temporal bone fracture Summary Indications Immediate-onset complete paralysis (or palsy progressing to paralysis) with more than 90 to 95% degeneration on electroneurography and no voluntary motor units on EMG, in a patient fit for surgery Timing As early as possible, within about 12 to 14 days of injury Route Hearing present: transmastoid plus middle fossa craniotomy. No useful hearing: translabyrinthine Contraindications Unfit or unconscious patient through the window; incomplete or delayed-onset palsy (good prognosis) Anaesthesia General, with neurosurgical support for the middle fossa Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin (clindamycin or vancomycin if allergic) Tray Otology drill and diamond burrs, ear set, middle fossa retractor, craniotomy set, nerve graft instruments, fat graft"],["osec","th:op-145~s1-2","Consent","Temporal Bone Trauma · Operating note","What to tell the patient The palsy may recover partly or fully either way ; the evidence for decompression is limited and comes from case series. Hearing: the middle fossa route aims to keep it; the translabyrinthine route leaves the ear permanently deaf. Middle fossa risks: bleeding inside the skull, seizures and speech disturbance from temporal lobe retraction. CSF leak and meningitis. Dizziness, usually temporary. A nerve graft may be needed if the nerve is torn, with slower and less complete recovery."],["osec","th:op-145~s1-3","Before you start","Temporal Bone Trauma · Operating note","Resuscitate under trauma protocols and image the brain. In the ear, look for blood behind the drum, a canal laceration or step, CSF otorrhoea (or rhinorrhoea through the Eustachian tube), Battle's sign, nystagmus, and test the facial nerve as early as possible: was the palsy immediate or delayed, complete or incomplete? Document it before sedation if you can. Test hearing with tuning forks at the bedside and audiometry when able. Obtain fine-cut temporal bone CT; add CT angiography if the fracture crosses the carotid canal. Reading the fracture Pattern Typical injury Otic capsule sparing (most fractures; often \"longitudinal\") Conductive loss from haemotympanum or ossicular disruption; facial palsy in a minority, usually near the geniculate and often delayed and incomplete Otic capsule violating (less common; often \"transverse\") Sensorineural deafness and vertigo; higher rates of facial palsy and CSF leak Figure 1 An otic capsule sparing fracture on CT. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 145.4 What the facial nerve tells you Finding Meaning Delayed onset or incomplete palsy Excellent prognosis: steroids if appropriate, observe Immediate complete palsy Possible transection or severe injury: electrical testing from day 3 ENoG over 90 to 95% degeneration within 14 days, no voluntary EMG Poor prognosis: consider decompression"],["osec","th:op-145~s2-1","Choose the route","Temporal Bone Trauma · The operation, stage by stage","Usable hearing and a fit patient: combined transmastoid and middle fossa approach, which preserves hearing. No useful hearing and a normal opposite labyrinth: translabyrinthine approach, which avoids brain retraction but leaves the ear dead. What you should see A CT that shows where the fracture crosses the facial canal, usually near the geniculate ganglion."],["osec","th:op-145~s2-2","Transmastoid: vertical and tympanic segments","Temporal Bone Trauma · The operation, stage by stage","Perform a cortical mastoidectomy and open the facial recess. Separate the incudostapedial joint and dislocate or remove the incus if needed for access. Skeletonise the nerve from the second genu to the stylomastoid foramen, leaving a thin eggshell of bone, then flake it off with a curved pick. Do not open the epineurium. Figure 2 The transmastoid approach exposes the tympanic and mastoid segments. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 145.1 What you should see The nerve bared over its vertical and tympanic course, with any bony spicule or haematoma removed."],["osec","th:op-145~s2-3","Middle fossa: geniculate, labyrinthine and meatal segments","Temporal Bone Trauma · The operation, stage by stage","Turn a temporal craniotomy flap and elevate the dura from posterior to anterior up to the arcuate eminence and the meatal plane, so the greater superficial petrosal nerve is not pulled with it. Identify the superior semicircular canal (blue-line it if unsure) and the greater superficial petrosal nerve. Drill medial to them down to the internal auditory canal and follow it laterally to the meatal foramen, labyrinthine segment and geniculate ganglion with diamond burrs and copious irrigation. Open the tegmen to join the tympanic segment. Replace the incus if it was moved, fix the bone flap and close. Figure 3 The combined middle fossa and transmastoid approach to decompress the whole intratemporal nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 145.2 What you should see The nerve exposed continuously from the internal auditory canal to the stylomastoid foramen. The labyrinthine segment and the cochlea The labyrinthine segment is the narrowest part of the canal and lies immediately lateral to the basal turn of the cochlea. Drill with small diamonds, cool constantly and never let the burr wander medially. Senior's pearl Raise the middle fossa dura from behind forwards. Lifting it front to back can drag the greater superficial petrosal nerve and tear the geniculate ganglion, injuring the very nerve you are trying to save."],["osec","th:op-145~s2-4","Translabyrinthine route","Temporal Bone Trauma · The operation, stage by stage","When the ear is dead, complete a labyrinthectomy and find the labyrinthine segment just medial to the ampulla of the superior canal; decompress the meatal, labyrinthine, geniculate, tympanic and mastoid segments without brain retraction. Avoid opening the internal auditory canal dura; close with a muscle plug in the aditus, wax the cells and fill the cavity with fat. Figure 4 The translabyrinthine route to total decompression when there is no useful hearing. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 145.3 What you should see The full nerve exposed with the internal auditory canal dura intact."],["osec","th:op-145~s2-5","If the nerve is torn","Temporal Bone Trauma · The operation, stage by stage","Excise the fibrosed or crushed segment and repair tension-free: a direct end-to-end repair if the ends meet, otherwise an interposition graft, usually the greater auricular nerve (the sural nerve if a long graft is needed). What you should see Coaptation without tension, the graft lying in the bony canal."],["osec","th:op-145~s3-1","Crisis drills","Temporal Bone Trauma · When it goes wrong","Deterioration after a middle fossa craniotomy 1 Act urgent CT 2 Haematoma return to theatre or ventricular drain with neurosurgery CSF leak after injury or surgery 1 First head up, avoid straining, stool softeners most stop within 7 to 10 days 2 Persisting lumbar drain for about 3 days 3 Still leaking surgical repair consider meningitis risk and vaccination"],["osec","th:op-145~s4-1","After surgery","Temporal Bone Trauma · Aftercare","Protect the eye with lubricants, taping and a moisture chamber until blinking returns. Expect slow recovery over months; record House-Brackmann grades. Treat vertigo with short-term vestibular sedatives and then vestibular rehabilitation. Reassess conductive loss at about 3 months, when blood has cleared, and reconstruct the ossicles if a gap persists; offer aids, CROS or a bone conduction device for sensorineural loss."],["ev","th:op-145~s4-2","Temporal Bone Trauma: current evidence","Temporal Bone Trauma · Aftercare","The evidence for decompression is weak and comes from small case series. In 24 patients, decompression within the first two months was associated with a better outcome than later surgery (Abbaszadeh-Kasbi et al., Craniomaxillofac Trauma Reconstr 2019), and in 13 patients with House-Brackmann V to VI palsy, delayed transmastoid decompression gave grade III or better in 12 (Gür et al., J Int Adv Otol 2021). Reviews agree on the order of repair: direct coaptation first, then an interposition graft, then nerve transfer while the motor end plates survive, and muscle transfer once they have atrophied (Gordin et al., Craniomaxillofac Trauma Reconstr 2015). Classifying fractures as otic capsule sparing or violating predicts sensorineural loss, facial palsy and CSF leak better than the old longitudinal and transverse system."],["osec","th:op-145~s4-2","Complications","Temporal Bone Trauma · Aftercare","Intracranial haemorrhage, seizures, dysphasia, CSF leak, meningitis, sensorineural and conductive hearing loss, dizziness, and failure of the nerve to recover."],["osec","th:op-145~s5-1","Where the books differ","Temporal Bone Trauma · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers decompress for immediate complete paralysis with more than 95% degeneration on ENoG and no voluntary EMG within 12 to 14 days, by the middle fossa plus transmastoid route if hearing is present. Dhingra teaches the longitudinal and transverse fractures and their typical injuries. The literature otic capsule sparing or violating is the more useful classification, and outcome data for decompression are limited."],["osec","th:op-145~s5-2","Viva essentials","Temporal Bone Trauma · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Resuscitation first; the ear comes later. Fracture types and the injuries each causes. Immediate versus delayed palsy and why it matters. Electroneurography timing and the 90 to 95% threshold. Choosing the route by hearing; the segments each approach reaches. Management of CSF leak and of conductive loss."],["osec","th:op-145~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Temporal Bone Trauma · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not documenting facial function before sedation. Operating on a delayed-onset or incomplete palsy. Testing ENoG before day 3, when it is still normal. Raising the middle fossa dura from front to back. Repairing the ossicles before the haemotympanum has cleared."],["osec","th:op-146~s1-1","The case at a glance","Surgery for Vertigo · Operating note","Surgery for vertigo Summary Principle Only for disabling vestibular symptoms after failed maximal medical treatment and rehabilitation, with the correct diagnosis and side Canal plugging Intractable posterior canal BPPV after repositioning by trained therapists or doctors; under 1% need surgery; success over 90%; not in the only hearing ear; caution in the elderly (vestibulitis and disequilibrium worse than the BPPV) Sac surgery Ménière's disease after maximal medical treatment including intratympanic steroid; spares balance and usually hearing; vertigo relieved in about 60% Nerve section Ménière's or uncompensated unilateral loss with aidable hearing ; vertigo control over 95%, hearing preserved over 95%; retrolabyrinthine or retrosigmoid preferred; middle fossa an option Labyrinthectomy Ménière's or uncompensated loss with severe to profound hearing loss; vertigo control over 95%; destroys hearing (can be combined with a cochlear implant) No ablation if Bilateral peripheral or central vestibulopathy (oscillopsia) Anaesthesia General, no paralysis (facial and other nerve monitoring) Position Turned 180 degrees, surgeon on the operated side; pins and Mayfield for retrosigmoid, perhaps with shoulder and hip bolsters Medication Cefazolin (clindamycin in allergy); dexamethasone 10 mg against postoperative vertigo and nausea Monitoring Facial nerve; cochlear nerve for hearing-preservation surgery; SSEPs for intracranial procedures Tray Microscope; mastoid set; otological drill; neurosurgical instruments; facial nerve dissector and stimulator"],["osec","th:op-146~s1-2","Consent","Surgery for Vertigo · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to stop disabling attacks of vertigo after other treatment has failed. The chance of success depends on the operation: about 60% for sac surgery, over 90% for canal plugging, over 95% for nerve section or labyrinthectomy. Hearing: may be partly or totally lost; labyrinthectomy removes it deliberately. Imbalance afterwards, sometimes permanent; you may struggle with heights, ladders and tasks needing fine balance, especially when older; rehabilitation is essential. Facial weakness , taste change, tinnitus. For operations inside the skull: leak of brain fluid, meningitis, bleeding, air inside the skull, headache, seizure, stroke, death. Ménière's symptoms such as fluctuating hearing, fullness and tinnitus may continue after sac surgery."],["osec","th:op-146~s1-3","Before you start","Surgery for Vertigo · Operating note","Establish the frequency, duration, side and character of attacks and how long they have gone on; migraine, motion intolerance, visual symptoms and family history; seizures and cardiovascular disease; trauma; past and present drugs. Tinnitus matters mainly when pulsatile : think of glomus tumours, dural arteriovenous malformations, meningoceles, superior canal dehiscence, aneurysms, vascular loops and a dehiscent high jugular bulb. Office testing can identify BPPV ( Dix-Hallpike ), hypofunction, vestibulo-ocular reflex abnormalities, fistula and acute attacks. Exclude postural hypotension. Pneumatic otoscopy should not provoke vertigo or nystagmus; if it does, consider perilymph fistula or dehiscence."],["osec","th:op-146~s1-4","Tests","Surgery for Vertigo · Operating note","Audiogram: early Ménière's gives a low-frequency sensorineural loss, later a flat loss; dehiscence may give a conductive loss (third window) and the Tullio phenomenon . Labyrinthectomy suits severe to profound loss. VNG identifies the weak side, or bilateral weakness , which rules out ablation; rotary chair if available. VEMP helps identify dehiscence or the side. MRI with gadolinium for central lesions and nerve inflammation; coronal CT for superior canal dehiscence. Consult cardiology (Holter, tilt table), neurology (headache, seizures, TIAs) and psychiatry (depression and anxiety are common). Gentamicin and hearing Up to 30% lose further hearing after intratympanic gentamicin; counsel patients with useful hearing carefully. The editor advises avoiding it in chronic ongoing vestibulopathy, which it may aggravate."],["osec","th:op-146~s2-1","Landmarks","Surgery for Vertigo · Anatomy you must see","The lateral semicircular canal , the key landmark; the posterior canal lies directly behind and perpendicular to it; the facial nerve and sigmoid sinus beside them. The endolymphatic sac on the posterior fossa dura between the posterior canal and the sigmoid, always below Donaldson's line (a line extended backward along the plane of the lateral canal). The five vestibular end organs: the three ampullae, utricle and saccule; the posterior ampulla lies medial to the vertical facial nerve . In the angle , the vestibular part of VIII is superior to the cochlear part; in the internal auditory canal the cochlear nerve lies anterior and inferior to the facial nerve and the superior and inferior vestibular nerves lie behind. Bill's bar separates the facial nerve in front from the superior vestibular nerve behind."],["osec","th:op-146~s3-1","Expose the canal","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: posterior canal plugging","Confirm the affected canal. Through a postauricular incision, perform a cortical mastoidectomy, finding the lateral canal, incus, tegmen and sigmoid; a saline Gelfoam pledget in the antrum keeps blood and dust out of the middle ear. What you should see The lateral canal, the incus and the outline of the posterior canal behind and below it."],["osec","th:op-146~s3-2","Fenestrate and plug","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: posterior canal plugging","With a diamond bur, skeletonise and blue-line the posterior canal and open a 1 × 3 mm window over its mid-portion, midway between the ampulla and the common crus , exposing but not breaching the membranous labyrinth. Plug the canal with bone wax, bone paste or connective tissue with a duckbill elevator, compressing and occluding the membranous duct, and cover it with fascia. If the membrane is breached, plug it anyway. Figure 1 Posterior canal occlusion: (A) skeletonising the mid-portion with a small diamond bur; (B) fenestrating without breaching the membranous labyrinth; (C) packing with bone wax, paste or soft tissue; (D) the membranous labyrinth compressed and occluded. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.1 What you should see A blue line, then a window showing the membranous canal intact, and the plug filling the lumen. No suction Suction near an opened or dehiscent canal can disrupt the membranous labyrinth and cause sensorineural loss."],["osec","th:op-146~s4-1","Find the sac","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: endolymphatic sac decompression","Through a cortical mastoidectomy (Gelfoam in the aditus), find the lateral canal, incus, tegmen and sigmoid, and the vertical facial nerve. Skeletonise and decompress the sigmoid as needed. What you should see The sigmoid, the lateral and posterior canals and the vertical facial nerve."],["osec","th:op-146~s4-2","Decompress","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: endolymphatic sac decompression","Remove the posterior fossa plate between the posterior canal and the sigmoid, following it up to and medial to the facial nerve as needed. The sac is a fan-shaped thickening of dura ; follow it forward under the posterior canal to the operculum and duct. Decompression is then complete; Surgicel above and below can keep the sac tented off the bone. Figure 2 The posterior fossa dura widely exposed, the endolymphatic sac a thickening fanning out from the posterior canal toward the sigmoid sinus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.2 What you should see Thick, white, fan-shaped dura between the posterior canal and the sigmoid, below a line drawn back along the lateral canal (Donaldson's line)."],["osec","th:op-146~s4-3","Shunt, if used","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: endolymphatic sac decompression","Open the sac with a Beaver blade, separate its walls and place a narrow strip of 0.005 inch Silastic sheeting. What you should see The lumen of the sac with its glistening lining, and the Silastic lying inside it."],["osec","th:op-146~s5-1","Open the canals in order","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: labyrinthectomy","After a mastoidectomy, remove the perilabyrinthine cells and identify the horizontal and vertical facial nerve. Open the lateral canal first, from above downward and back to the posterior canal, leaving its inferior wall to protect the facial nerve . Open the posterior canal , following it forward and down while protecting the vertical facial nerve medially; keeping its inferior wall protects a high jugular bulb. Follow it up to the common crus, then the superior canal up and forward, leaving its superior wall to protect the tegmen . Figure 3 The superior wall of the lateral canal removed, the inferior wall left to protect the facial nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.3 What you should see Each canal opened in turn, with the facial nerve under intact bone below the lateral canal."],["osec","th:op-146~s5-2","Clear the vestibule","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: labyrinthectomy","Open the vestibule, exposing the superior and lateral ampullae, and remove the neuroepithelium of all five end organs with a round knife or microcurette. The posterior ampulla lies medial to the vertical facial nerve and needs a deliberate effort. Leave the medial vestibular wall to protect the internal auditory canal and avoid a CSF leak. Figure 4 The five areas of neuroepithelium exposed and removed systematically. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.4 What you should see The spherical and elliptical recesses empty, and all five patches of neuroepithelium removed. The posterior ampulla Leaving neuroepithelium in the posterior ampulla is the commonest cause of persisting vertigo after labyrinthectomy."],["osec","th:op-146~s6-1","Exposure","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: retrolabyrinthine nerve section","Incise 2 to 3 cm behind the ear crease from above the temporal line to the mastoid tip; raise an anterior skin flap and a large Palva or T-based flap. Perform a complete mastoidectomy exposing the canals, incus, tegmen and sigmoid; remove bone over the posterior fossa dura, sinodural angle and tegmen. Preserve the endolymphatic sac. What you should see Posterior fossa dura between the sigmoid and the labyrinth, with the sac preserved."],["osec","th:op-146~s6-2","Into the angle","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: retrolabyrinthine nerve section","Open the dura between the sigmoid and the posterior canal as a C or U flap. Protect the cerebellum with a ½ × 3 cm patty and open the cerebellopontine cistern to drain CSF; the cerebellum falls away to show the VII-VIII complex. Confirm the facial nerve by stimulation. Figure 5 Retrolabyrinthine exposure of the posterior fossa: V, VII, VIII and IX to XI. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.5 What you should see The seventh and eighth nerves crossing the angle, with the flocculus and the lower nerves below."],["osec","th:op-146~s6-3","Find the plane and cut","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: retrolabyrinthine nerve section","Find the cleavage plane between the cochlear and vestibular parts of VIII, marked by a small vessel or a slight groove, the cochlear part being whiter . Separate them with a ball dissector or Rhoton, divide the vestibular part with curved bayonet scissors or a nerve knife from the superodorsal surface, exposing the nervus intermedius, and remove a short segment so the ends cannot rejoin. Figure 6 The perineural plane between cochlear and vestibular nerves, often marked by a small vessel or groove on the nerve's dorsal surface. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.6 Figure 7 Separating the cochlear and vestibular nerves. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.7 Figure 8 Dividing the vestibular nerve, starting from the superodorsal surface of VIII. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.8 What you should see A fine vessel or groove between the greyer vestibular half (toward the tentorium) and the whiter cochlear half, with the facial nerve in front."],["osec","th:op-146~s6-4","Close","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: retrolabyrinthine nerve section","Secure haemostasis, remove the patty, close the dura (or patch with homograft) and seal with fibrin glue; seal the antrum with fascia and pack the mastoid with abdominal fat. Admit until the severe vertigo settles. What you should see A sealed dura and a filled mastoid."],["osec","th:op-146~s7-1","Exposure","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: retrosigmoid nerve section","Supine in pins, the head flexed about 45 degrees and turned away with a soft shoulder roll; some give mannitol. Make an oblique incision from posterosuperior toward the mastoid tip over the sigmoid (navigation helps), raise anterior and posterior flaps, and occlude the mastoid emissary vein. Figure 9 The skin incision for a retrosigmoid vestibular nerve section. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.9 Figure 10 Surface anatomy: the asterion, squamosal, lambdoid and occipitomastoid sutures and the estimated course of the transverse and sigmoid sinuses. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.10 What you should see The junction of the transverse and sigmoid sinuses marked on the skin."],["osec","th:op-146~s7-2","Craniotomy and section","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: retrosigmoid nerve section","Make a 1.5 to 2 cm craniotomy, sometimes exposing the back of the sigmoid; wax the cells, open a C or U dural flap, then proceed as for the retrolabyrinthine section. What you should see The eighth nerve complex at the brainstem."],["osec","th:op-146~s7-3","Close against headache","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: retrosigmoid nerve section","Fill the craniotomy with bone dust and chips wrapped in Surgicel or replace the bone flap (plates or hydroxyapatite cement are alternatives) to reduce headache. What you should see A filled bone defect under the muscle closure."],["osec","th:op-146~s8-1","Middle fossa","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: middle fossa and translabyrinthine section","Its advantage is identifying the superior, inferior and singular nerves for complete section; its disadvantages are temporal lobe retraction, greater risk to hearing and facial nerve, and headache. Make a 4 × 5 cm craniotomy, two thirds in front of the ear canal, and keep the bone flap in antibiotic solution. Elevate the dura to see the middle meningeal artery in front, the petrous ridge medially and the arcuate eminence behind, without stretching the greater superficial petrosal nerve , the commonest cause of facial injury here. Find the canal by bisecting the angle between the GSPN and the arcuate eminence (or with navigation), drill from medial to lateral sparing the basal turn, and expose Bill's bar. Open the canal dura, section the superior vestibular nerve and reflect it medially to reach the inferior vestibular and singular nerves, and remove Scarpa's ganglion . Close the dura, plug with muscle, let the lobe re-expand and plate the flap. Figure 11 Middle fossa craniotomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.11 Figure 12 The floor of the middle fossa: middle meningeal artery, GSPN, facial, cochlear and vestibular nerves. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.12 Figure 13 Exposure of the internal auditory canal from the middle fossa: GSPN, geniculate ganglion, arcuate eminence. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.13 What you should see Bill's bar and the transverse crest at the fundus, separating the four nerves."],["osec","th:op-146~s8-2","Translabyrinthine","Surgery for Vertigo · The operation: middle fossa and translabyrinthine section","Very rare, after a failed labyrinthectomy, to remove Scarpa's ganglion. Begin as a labyrinthectomy, drill 270 degrees of the lateral canal up to the superior ampulla, identify the transverse crest, open the dura and section the nerves laterally to medially, separating the superior nerve from the facial nerve and removing segments of it and the inferior and singular nerves. Remove the incus, isolate the epitympanum from the antrum with fascia, and pack the mastoid with fat. It adds risk to the facial nerve and of CSF leak. Figure 14 The fundus of the canal: the transverse crest and Bill's bar, with the facial, superior and inferior vestibular and singular nerves. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 146.14 What you should see The nerves in the canal after the dura is opened, with the facial nerve in front and above."],["osec","th:op-146~s9-1","Crisis drills","Surgery for Vertigo · When it goes wrong","Facial paralysis 1 Usual cause stretching sometimes direct injury 2 Act high-dose steroids for 7 to 10 days repair a cut nerve at once reanimation if it does not recover CSF leak 1 Early lumbar drain and pressure dressing 2 Persistent reoperate to seal the defect Intracranial problems 1 Bleeding neurosurgery evacuate and control 2 Pneumocephalus headache observe as it resolves, or evacuate with neurosurgical advice"],["osec","th:op-146~s9-2","Common errors in technique","Surgery for Vertigo · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Operating on the wrong side or for the wrong diagnosis. Ablating a patient with bilateral vestibular loss. Fenestrating the posterior canal near the ampulla or common crus instead of its middle. Suction near an open canal. Leaving the posterior ampulla's neuroepithelium."],["osec","th:op-146~s10-1","After the operation","Surgery for Vertigo · Aftercare","Discharge extracranial cases when the patient walks safely and eats; after intracranial procedures, neurosurgical intensive care until stable. Treat vertigo with promethazine, diazepam or lorazepam (ondansetron and meclizine seem less effective). Antibiotics for 7 days or perioperatively only, by preference. Vestibular rehabilitation for every ablative procedure; some need short-term disability leave."],["osec","th:op-146~s10-2","Complications","Surgery for Vertigo · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Vertigo and disequilibrium Common early; rehabilitation; lorazepam for acute vertigo CSF leak Lumbar drain, pressure, reoperation Meningitis Intravenous antibiotics Hearing loss Aids, osseointegrated or cochlear implants Taste change Chorda stretched or cut; recovers over 1 to 9 months, persists in about 2% Facial paralysis Steroids, repair, reanimation Intracranial bleeding, pneumocephalus, headache Neurosurgery; analgesia; pain or neurology services for chronic headache"],["ev","th:op-146~s10-3","Surgery for Vertigo: current evidence","Surgery for Vertigo · Aftercare","Ménière's disease. The American guideline recommends a stepped approach: lifestyle measures, then non-destructive options (intratympanic steroids, endolymphatic sac surgery), then destructive options (intratympanic gentamicin, labyrinthectomy, vestibular neurectomy) (Basura et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020). A meta-analysis found endolymphatic sac surgery controlled vertigo in at least 75% of patients who had failed medical treatment, with similar results for decompression and shunting (Sood et al., Otol Neurotol 2014). Labyrinthectomy and vestibular neurectomy control vertigo in over 90%. BPPV. Posterior canal occlusion is highly effective for intractable BPPV that fails repositioning, with a small risk to hearing."],["osec","th:op-146~s10-3","Alternatives","Surgery for Vertigo · Aftercare","A transcanal labyrinthectomy is quicker for those who need a short anaesthetic. For those unfit or unwilling, vestibular suppressants, intratympanic steroid or gentamicin, and physiotherapy for safe walking."],["osec","th:op-146~s11-1","A ladder for Ménière's disease","Surgery for Vertigo · For the examination","\"Do no harm\": choose the most likely success with the fewest complications. Intratympanic steroid controls about 90% at a year in a recent English study, a little better than gentamicin, which risks hearing (up to 30%). When steroid fails, weigh sac surgery (no significant risk to hearing or balance, success quoted at about 65% here and 60% earlier in the chapter) against office gentamicin . If sac surgery fails and hearing is to be kept, vestibular nerve section (under 5% risk of significant hearing loss, but intracranial) may be better than gentamicin. Labyrinthectomy comes last, destroying hearing and balance. The editor adds that canal occlusion has replaced singular neurectomy for BPPV, that Epley manoeuvres rarely fail, that the opposite ear's function must be known before ablation, and that the nerve orientation in the canal must be mastered."],["osec","th:op-146~s11-2","Viva essentials","Surgery for Vertigo · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Selection: failed medical treatment, correct side and diagnosis, the other ear working. Bilateral loss and oscillopsia. Posterior canal occlusion: where to open it and why; no suction. The endolymphatic sac and Donaldson's line. Labyrinthectomy order; the walls left to protect the facial nerve, bulb and tegmen; the posterior ampulla. Nerve section: the cochleovestibular plane (vessel, colour), nerve orientation in the angle and canal. Middle fossa: finding the canal from the GSPN and arcuate eminence; Scarpa's ganglion. Success rates and the Ménière's ladder."],["osec","th:op-146~s11-3","Common pitfalls","Surgery for Vertigo · For the examination","Common pitfalls Plugging the wrong canal. Gentamicin in a good-hearing ear without counselling. Stretching the GSPN in middle fossa surgery. Forgetting rehabilitation."],["osec","th:op-147~s1-1","The case at a glance","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · Operating note","Repair of superior canal dehiscence Summary Indications Disabling symptoms of the syndrome (sound- or pressure-induced vertigo, autophony, pulsatile tinnitus, oscillopsia) with matching CT and physiological tests; the patient chooses when Contraindications A dehiscence on CT without symptoms or test evidence; an only-hearing ear (relative); bilateral disease without clear lateralisation Approaches Middle fossa craniotomy (direct view, the classic route) or transmastoid (no brain retraction) Repair Plugging the canal lumen, resurfacing (capping) the defect, or both Anaesthesia General Position Supine, head turned; head clamp if navigation is used Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring; navigation optional Tray Otology drill and diamond burrs, middle fossa retractor, small suction-irrigator, warmed bone wax, temporalis fascia, bone chips or pâté, haemostatics"],["osec","th:op-147~s1-2","Consent","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · Operating note","What to tell the patient Vertigo and sound or pressure sensitivity improve in most; hearing is not usually improved , though the air-bone gap often closes. Sensorineural hearing loss , rarely total, and temporary imbalance afterwards. Facial weakness (rare): the geniculate ganglion is often uncovered in these patients. From the middle fossa route: epidural haematoma, CSF leak, seizures or speech disturbance. Persistent symptoms if the plug is incomplete, needing revision."],["osec","th:op-147~s1-3","Before you start","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · Operating note","Confirm the syndrome, not just the CT: a pseudo-conductive low-frequency gap with preserved acoustic reflexes , bone conduction thresholds better than 0 dB, and lowered cervical VEMP thresholds or raised ocular VEMP amplitudes. Look for Tullio and Hennebert signs and pressure- or sound-evoked nystagmus in the plane of the superior canal. Obtain thin-slice CT reformatted in the plane of the canal (Pöschl) and perpendicular to it (Stenvers): the standard axial and coronal cuts over-call dehiscence. Figure 1 The audiogram of dehiscence: a low-frequency air-bone gap with bone thresholds better than normal. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 147.1 Figure 2 CT in axial, coronal, Pöschl and Stenvers planes showing the uncovered superior canal. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 147.2"],["osec","th:op-147~s2-1","Open the middle fossa","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · The operation, stage by stage","Make a preauricular incision extending into the temporal region, reflect the temporalis muscle forward towards the zygoma and leave a cuff of fascia for closure. Cut a craniotomy of about 4 by 5 cm centred over, or slightly in front of, the ear canal, flush with the middle fossa floor; take the inner cortex with a diamond burr so the dura stays intact. Figure 3 Skin incision and the craniotomy centred over the ear canal. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 147.3 What you should see A bone flap lifted off intact dura, its lower edge at the floor of the middle fossa."],["osec","th:op-147~s2-2","Elevate the dura and find the dehiscence","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · The operation, stage by stage","Elevate the temporal lobe dura from the floor, sweeping posterior to anterior , and control venous ooze with haemostatic packing. Work medially to the arcuate eminence near the petrous ridge. The greater superficial petrosal nerve anteriorly, and navigation, help orientation if there are several small tegmen defects. What you should see The blue line or open lumen of the superior canal on the arcuate eminence, just behind the internal auditory canal. The uncovered geniculate ganglion These patients often have a thin or absent bony cover over the geniculate ganglion. Raising the dura from front to back can stretch the greater superficial petrosal nerve and injure the facial nerve. Check the CT and sweep from behind forwards."],["osec","th:op-147~s2-3","Protect the labyrinth","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · The operation, stage by stage","Once the dehiscence is open, never suction over it. Use a small, irrigating suction tip held away from the canal to keep the field moist and clear without drawing out perilymph or tearing the membranous labyrinth. Senior's pearl The membranous labyrinth sits millimetres below your suction. Irrigate and let the fluid run off; touch the dehiscence only with the plug."],["osec","th:op-147~s2-4","Plug and resurface","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · The operation, stage by stage","Press a small pledget of warmed bone wax into the canal lumen on both sides of the dehiscence to occlude it, then cover the defect with fascia and a bone chip or bone pâté. Some surgeons resurface (cap) alone, but plugging gives a more durable seal. Figure 4 Plugging the dehiscent superior canal through the middle fossa. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 147.4 What you should see The canal lumen filled on both sides of the defect, covered with fascia and bone."],["osec","th:op-147~s2-5","Close","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · The operation, stage by stage","Let the dura settle, replace the bone flap, resuspend the temporalis and close. A transmastoid alternative opens the canal from below in a pneumatised mastoid: the canal is blue-lined, opened either side of the dehiscence and plugged with fascia and bone pâté, without lifting the brain. What you should see A dry field, the flap in place, and no CSF."],["osec","th:op-147~s3-1","Crisis drills","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · When it goes wrong","Sudden hearing drop after surgery 1 Act audiogram high-dose steroids 2 Then vestibular rehabilitation for imbalance hearing rehabilitation if it does not recover Headache, drowsiness or focal signs after craniotomy 1 Act urgent CT 2 Epidural haematoma return to theatre for evacuation Symptoms persist 1 Check repeat CT and VEMP for an unplugged lumen or a second dehiscence 2 Then consider revision, often transmastoid"],["osec","th:op-147~s4-1","On the ward and after","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · Aftercare","Observe neurologically after a craniotomy. Expect temporary imbalance as the canal is now blocked; mobilise early and start vestibular rehabilitation. Check hearing at a few weeks. Avoid straining and heavy lifting while the wound heals."],["ev","th:op-147~s4-2","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence: current evidence","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · Aftercare","A systematic review of 20 studies (150 procedures) found symptomatic success in about 94%, with no significant difference between plugging, capping, resurfacing or combined repair, or between the middle fossa and transmastoid approaches (Gioacchini et al., Laryngoscope 2016). Another systematic review found surgery effective mainly for the vestibular symptoms, with no evidence that it improves hearing, and favoured transmastoid plugging for its lower complication and revision rates and shorter stay (Ziylan et al., Otol Neurotol 2017)."],["osec","th:op-147~s4-2","Complications","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · Aftercare","Sensorineural hearing loss, temporary or persistent imbalance, facial palsy, epidural haematoma, CSF leak, and persistent or recurrent symptoms."],["osec","th:op-147~s5-1","Where the books differ","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers the middle fossa plugging technique, with dural elevation from behind forwards to protect a dehiscent geniculate ganglion, and the patient deciding when to intervene. K. J. Lee and the Oxford books: the third-window physiology, the audiometric and VEMP findings, and CT in the Pöschl and Stenvers planes. The literature: success rates are similar across repair types and approaches; transmastoid plugging may have fewer complications."],["osec","th:op-147~s5-2","Viva essentials","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The third window and how it produces each symptom. The audiogram : low-frequency gap, supranormal bone conduction, normal reflexes (unlike otosclerosis). VEMPs and CT planes for diagnosis. Middle fossa versus transmastoid , plugging versus resurfacing. Why the geniculate ganglion is at risk."],["osec","th:op-147~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Superior Semicircular Canal Dehiscence · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a CT finding without the syndrome. Calling it otosclerosis and doing a stapedotomy (reflexes are present in dehiscence). Suctioning over the open canal. Raising the dura from front to back."],["osec","th:op-149~s1-1","The case at a glance","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Operating note","Dacryocystorhinostomy Summary Indications Acquired obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct or lacrimal sac with tearing; acute dacryocystitis with an abscess that does not settle on antibiotics; chronic dacryocystitis; a dacryolith; external DCR with biopsy whenever a lacrimal sac mass is suspected Contraindications A malignancy of the sac (needs dacryocystectomy with margins, and no opening into the nose); an upper system block (punctum, canaliculi) that is not treated at the same time (DCR alone will fail); untreated acute infection where avoidable Anaesthesia General (preferred, for comfort and airway); local with sedation is possible; topical cocaine is an excellent decongestant and anaesthetic Position Supine Antibiotics Cephalexin 2 g IV, or clindamycin 600 mg IV if allergic Monitoring Standard anaesthetic Imaging Not needed in a straightforward case; CT of the orbits and sinuses after facial trauma or if a mass is suspected; MRI with gadolinium in a child whose tearing began after birth with normal lids (a mass until proven otherwise); dacryoscintigraphy occasionally Tray 8 French sucker; neurosurgical cottonoids; decongestant; skin rakes; size 15 blade; Cottle elevator; small and medium up-biting Kerrison punches; small osteotome; silicone intubation set (Crawford or similar) with probes and retrieval hook; Bowman probes 0 and 00; bipolar; fine needle holders; 6-0 Vicryl, 6-0 silk, 7-0 chromic, 7-0 or 8-0 skin suture; 12 French silicone catheter; 5-0 nylon Before theatre Stop anticoagulants where possible; treat acute dacryocystitis first; ophthalmology review for ocul"],["osec","th:op-149~s1-2","Consent","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it does: makes a new drainage channel from the tear sac into the nose so tears and discharge can drain. Success is around nine in ten; the eye may still water if the channel scars, or if part of the watering comes from the eye itself. A small scar beside the nose, usually hidden in a natural crease (external approach). Bleeding , including nosebleeds for a few days. Infection. Tubes are left in the tear ducts for about two months and removed in clinic; they can occasionally slip or wear the tear opening if too tight. Blinking may be slightly affected if small branches of the facial nerve are disturbed. Rare: a leak of brain fluid if the bone near the skull base fractures. If a growth is found, it will be sampled, and a different operation may be needed."],["osec","th:op-149~s1-3","Before you start","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Operating note","1.3.1 Why is the eye watering? Three causes of a watering eye Cause Clues Action Hypersecretion Burning, grittiness, foreign body feeling; allergy, dry eye with reflex tearing (for example Graves disease); lashes rubbing the cornea; poor lid closure Ophthalmology: treat the ocular surface or lid first Impaired drainage Tearing without irritation; mucus or pus; swelling over the sac Find the level of the block Both Mixed features Treat the surface first, then reassess Fluorescein with a blue light shows corneal epithelial defects in a dry eye. Eyelid position: an everted or lax lower lid, or a weak medial canthal pump, causes functional obstruction. Rhinitis can rarely cause functional obstruction; treat it first. 1.3.2 Where is the block? Congenital: in infants the block is usually at the valve of Hasner at the lower end of the duct; most open by 6 to 12 months, so probing waits until the end of the first year, when it usually cures. Nose: examine endoscopically under the inferior turbinate for an obstruction at the duct opening, and note septal deviation (important for an endonasal approach; a septoplasty may be needed). Irrigation in clinic: after topical anaesthesia, inject saline gently through a canaliculus with a 22 gauge or smaller cannula and note the flow, the resistance and any reflux. Reading lacrimal irrigation Finding Meaning Saline passes easily to the nose System open; reconsider the diagnosis (unless a dacryolith lets fluid pass around it) Passes only with force, with resistance Functional obstruction Reflux from the same canaliculus, or resistance to a fine"],["osec","th:op-149~s2-1","The lacrimal drainage system","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Anatomy you must see","Tears enter the upper and lower puncta , run through the canaliculi (a vertical part of about 2 mm, then a horizontal part of about 8 mm) into the common canaliculus , then the lacrimal sac and the nasolacrimal duct , which opens under the inferior turbinate in the front of the inferior meatus. The sac sits in the lacrimal fossa between the anterior and posterior lacrimal crests, behind the medial canthal tendon and wrapped by the deep fibres of orbicularis (Horner's muscle), which drive the lacrimal pump. Figure 1 The lacrimal drainage system: canaliculi, common canaliculus, sac and the muscles and tendon around them. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.1"],["osec","th:op-149~s2-2","The landmarks for the operation","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Anatomy you must see","The anterior lacrimal crest , the front edge of the fossa on the frontal process of the maxilla, is where you incise and begin. A thin seam between the maxillary and lacrimal bones often gives a natural entry for the Kerrison punch. The nasal opening is made just in front of the tip of the middle turbinate . Bone removal must stay no more than about 2 mm above the medial canthal tendon , below the skull base. Figure 2 The anterior lacrimal crest (arrow); the incision will lie in the lower eyelid crease. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.2"],["osec","th:op-149~s3-1","Prepare the nose and the skin","Dacryocystorhinostomy · The operation: external DCR","Decongest the nose with pledgets (cocaine or another vasoconstrictor) before or after induction. With a size 15 blade, make an incision about 1.5 cm long over the anterior lacrimal crest, in or near the lower lid crease, in one stroke down to periosteum , which keeps the tissue planes clean. What you should see White periosteum over the anterior lacrimal crest at the base of a clean, dry incision."],["osec","th:op-149~s3-2","Lift the periosteum and the sac","Dacryocystorhinostomy · The operation: external DCR","With a Cottle elevator, lift the periosteum off the maxilla and continue backward and upward into the fossa, carrying the lacrimal sac , fused to the periosteum, out of its fossa. Figure 3 The lacrimal sac lifted out of its fossa with the periosteum. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.3 What you should see The empty bony fossa, with the sac reflected laterally inside its periosteal envelope."],["osec","th:op-149~s3-3","Remove the bone","Dacryocystorhinostomy · The operation: external DCR","Start the bone opening at the seam between the maxillary and lacrimal bones with a Kerrison punch (or a small osteotome if there is no seam; a drill is rarely needed, more often in inflammatory disease such as sarcoid or granulomatosis with polyangiitis). Remove part of the anterior lacrimal crest if it blocks your view. Make the opening wide , which makes the work in the nose easier and is the best protection against failure. Figure 4 The sac reflected and bone partly removed from the fossa. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.4 The skull base Twisting the punch while working upward can send a fracture into the skull base and cause a CSF leak. Bite cleanly, do not twist, and stay within about 2 mm above the medial canthal tendon. What you should see An opening from the medial canthal tendon above to the start of the nasolacrimal canal below, with the anterior crest removed in front, and intact nasal mucosa bulging through it. Senior's pearl The commonest cause of failure is a bony window that is too small or too low, leaving a sump. Take bone until the whole medial wall of the sac is free, including the common canaliculus opening."],["osec","th:op-149~s3-4","Open the sac over a probe","Dacryocystorhinostomy · The operation: external DCR","Gently dilate the upper punctum and pass a 0 or 00 Bowman probe. Turn it medially at once while pulling the lid laterally, which straightens the canaliculus. When the tip is in the sac, swing the probe up over the brow so that it tents the sac, visible in the wound. Open the sac along the probe with a crescent blade, fine needle diathermy or radiofrequency, as far up and down as possible, extending the cut forward at each end so you can see inside. Remove any dacryolith or foreign body, and biopsy anything suspicious. Figure 5 The probe, passed through the punctum, seen in the wound inside the opened sac. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.5 What you should see The probe tip shining inside a widely opened sac, with its lining healthy and no mass. The canaliculus Passing a probe without pulling the lid out to straighten the canaliculus tears its lining, and a damaged canaliculus is much harder to treat than the original block. Be gentle at every pass. Senior's pearl Keep a low threshold for biopsy. If frozen section shows a benign lesion, you can finish the DCR; if it shows malignancy, remove the sac with margins and do not open it into the nose."],["osec","th:op-149~s3-5","Open the nose and intubate","Dacryocystorhinostomy · The operation: external DCR","Remove the nasal pledgets. With needle diathermy, cut a vertical straight or oval opening in the nasal mucosa through the bony window. Pass one probe of a silicone intubation set through the upper canaliculus into the sac and out through the new opening, and retrieve it in the nose; do the same through the lower canaliculus . Hook both tubes up through the wound, tie them together with a single square knot of 6-0 silk at about the level of the skin incision (a single knot can later come out through the punctum), trim the ends to 3 mm, and check the tension: the loop at the inner corner should pull out no further than the medial edge of the cornea . Figure 6 The silicone loop should be loose enough to prolapse only as far as the medial corneal limbus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.6 Tubes too tight If the knot sits too deep and the loop is tight, the tubes cheese-wire through and erode the puncta. What you should see Both probes meeting metal in the nose and coming out under the middle turbinate, and a loose loop of silicone at the medial canthus."],["osec","th:op-149~s3-6","Stent or flaps","Dacryocystorhinostomy · The operation: external DCR","Cut about 4 cm of a 12 French straight silicone catheter (bevel one end), pass it over both wire probes into the nose and up into the wound, and grasp it there. Pass a double-armed 7-0 chromic suture across its lumen about 4 mm from the end (taking care not to pierce the silicone tubes), pull the catheter back into the nose, bring both needles out through the front wall of the sac, draw the catheter up into the sac and tie it there. Irrigate with antibiotic solution. Cut all three tubes together about 1 cm inside the nostril and anchor the catheter loosely to the nasal mucosa with a 5-0 nylon stitch, turning the ends into the lumen. Alternatively , suture at least the posterior flap of the sac to a posterior flap of nasal mucosa (and the anterior flaps over the tubes if possible) with 6-0 Vicryl. Figure 7 The catheter over the silicone tubes, with the chromic suture passed across its lumen to fix it in the sac. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.7 Figure 8 The catheter sewn into the front of the sac remnant, resting in the new opening. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.8 What you should see Silicone tubes running from both puncta through the sac and the new opening into the nose, with the stent secure in the sac."],["osec","th:op-149~s3-7","Close","Dacryocystorhinostomy · The operation: external DCR","Close the skin with a running 8-0 nylon or interrupted 7-0 chromic suture, apply antibiotic-steroid ointment, and add a pressure dressing if a haematoma is likely. Figure 9 The closed incision, hidden in the lower lid crease. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 149.9 What you should see The canthus in its normal position with no bowstringing of the scar, and the tubes sitting quietly in the puncta. The angular vein and the canthal tendon Bleeding from the angular vein obscures everything else and causes the postoperative haematoma; tie it. Do not detach the medial canthal tendon without reattaching it, or the canthus drifts."],["osec","th:op-149~s4-1","Crisis drills","Dacryocystorhinostomy · When it goes wrong","Clear fluid from the nose after DCR 1 Suspect CSF leak from a fracture extending to the skull base 2 Act test for beta-2 transferrin CT neurosurgical or skull base review most small leaks settle with conservative care A mass is found in the sac 1 Act biopsy and frozen section 2 Benign complete the DCR 3 Malignant dacryocystectomy with margins no opening into the nose plan further treatment Tearing returns after DCR 1 Find out irrigate and examine the nose endoscopically 2 Common causes bony opening too small, sac not opened properly, scarring of the mucosal opening, an upper system block 3 Act revise, ideally with a wider opening treat any canalicular block The silicone loop is pulling at the punctum 1 Act review the tension remove or reposition the tubes early if the punctum is eroding"],["osec","th:op-149~s5-1","After the operation","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Aftercare","Oral antibiotic while the catheter is in, or if there was infection. Antibiotic-steroid eye drops four times a day and a nasal steroid spray . Ointment on the skin sutures; sutures out at 5 to 7 days . No nose blowing or picking for 3 weeks. At 10 days to 2 weeks , cut the nylon stitch in the nose and pull the catheter out through the nostril (the chromic suture has dissolved); taper the drops to twice a day, then stop a week later. Continue the nasal steroid until the silicone tubes come out at 2 months : either cut at the inner corner and pull out through the nose, or rotate the loop until the silk knot appears at a punctum, cut between and draw the tubes out."],["osec","th:op-149~s5-2","Complications","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Aftercare","Complications of dacryocystorhinostomy Complication Cause or prevention Persistent tearing Small bony opening, poor marsupialisation, mucosal opening scarring, missed upper block, or ocular surface disease Punctal erosion Silicone tubes too tight Canalicular injury Probing without straightening the canaliculus Bleeding Nasal mucosa and bone edges; usually settles Infection Antibiotic cover Scar Hidden in the lid crease Weak blink Fibres of the zygomatic branch of the facial nerve CSF leak Fracture extending to the skull base"],["ev","th:op-149~s5-3","Dacryocystorhinostomy: current evidence","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Aftercare","Endonasal or external. Modern endonasal dacryocystorhinostomy with full exposure of the sac and mucosal flaps gives success rates (about 85 to 95%) close to external DCR, without a skin scar; randomised comparisons are few. Adjuncts. Mitomycin C improved success in meta-analyses (Xue et al., Orbit 2014); silicone intubation is commonly used but has not been shown to improve primary success consistently. Selection. Patients with a functional block (patent on syringing) do less well than those with a complete nasolacrimal duct obstruction."],["osec","th:op-149~s5-3","Evidence and alternatives","Dacryocystorhinostomy · Aftercare","In one analysis of 100 failed DCRs (about three-quarters external), the commonest anatomical reasons were a small bony opening, poor opening of the sac, and closure of the mucosal opening, the same for both approaches. A single surgeon's series of over 1000 endoscopic DCRs with stents reported 92.7% success; a meta-analysis of over 2000 cases found the same 87% for external and endoscopic DCR, and recent studies give 91% to 100% and 91% to 97% respectively. Upper system blocks need their own procedures. Dacryocystectomy may be better for recurrent infection in a dry eye, and in inflammatory disease such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis. In children, work with their own anatomy first: probing, sometimes repeated, then silicone intubation, before any bypass. External versus endoscopic DCR External Endoscopic Success About 87% to 100% About 87% to 97% Scar Small, in the lid crease None Best for Acute infection, suspected mass, dacryolith, sac diverticulum, previous trauma Uncomplicated duct obstruction; patients avoiding a skin scar; concurrent nasal surgery Watch for Facial branch fibres, skull base Septal deviation, the middle turbinate, the axilla"],["osec","th:op-149~s6-1","Where the books differ","Dacryocystorhinostomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra lists dacryocystorhinostomy among advanced endoscopic nasal techniques, and septoplasty as a step to gain access for it when a deviated septum blocks the way. Lee describes congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction from failed canalisation at the lower end of the duct (90% one-sided, presenting at 1 to 3 weeks, often resolving, otherwise marsupialisation or dilatation), the lengths of the vertical and horizontal parts of the canaliculi, silicone intubation for canalicular lacerations, and the commonest acquired causes, including dacryoliths, trauma, punctal plugs and idiopathic stenosis in postmenopausal women; and DCR with a stent if the duct is cut during a maxillectomy. Myers gives the full external operation: the clinic irrigation that locates the block, the indications and the upper system rule, the one-stroke incision, bone removal with its upper limit, opening the sac over a probe, silicone intubation with its tension check, the catheter stent or mucosal flaps, and a detailed timetable for removing the stents."],["osec","th:op-149~s6-2","Viva essentials","Dacryocystorhinostomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three causes of a watering eye , and why surface disease is treated first. Interpreting lacrimal irrigation : same-side reflux versus reflux from the opposite punctum. Why probing adults is wrong, and when to probe infants (end of the first year). The upper system rule and what to do for canalicular block (Jones tube). Landmarks : anterior lacrimal crest, front of the middle turbinate, 2 mm above the medial canthal tendon. Acute dacryocystitis : management. Red flags for a sac tumour and what to do. External versus endoscopic DCR and the reasons for failure."],["osec","th:op-149~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Dacryocystorhinostomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on reflex tearing from ocular surface disease. Missing an upper system block. Probing an adult, or probing without straightening the canaliculus. Too small a bony opening. Twisting the punch while working upward. Not opening the sac fully, or not biopsying a suspicious sac. Tying the silicone tubes too tight. Opening a malignant sac into the nose."],["osec","th:op-15~s1-1","The case at a glance","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Operating note","Supraglottic transoral robotic surgery Summary Indications T1 and T2 squamous carcinoma of the supraglottis; selected recurrent supraglottic or hypopharyngeal cancer; glottic cancer only in small series Contraindications Inadequate exposure; vocal fold fixation ; extralaryngeal soft tissue spread; cartilage destruction; bilateral glottic involvement; major tongue base invasion; arytenoid fixation ; poor cardiopulmonary reserve; advanced neck disease needing concurrent chemoradiotherapy afterwards (relative) Consider open surgery Significant pre-epiglottic or glottic involvement, where framework must come as a margin and the arytenoids repositioned Adjuvant treatment 51% needed it in a large European series; N2/N3 or obvious extracapsular spread on imaging may be better treated with primary chemoradiotherapy Classification Remacle's European Laryngological Society types for endoscopic supraglottic laryngectomy apply Anaesthesia Airway plan agreed beforehand; a small tube; nasal intubation can help exposure; fibreoptic or otolaryngologist intubation for bulky tumours; paralysis helps exposure; arterial line if comorbid Position Supine, bed turned 180 degrees, arms tucked; 0 silk through the junction of the anterior and middle thirds of the tongue for traction Antibiotics Not routinely Tray Feyh-Kastenbauer (FK) or Crowe-Davis retractor; 8 mm 0 degree scope (or 30 degree) with 5 mm instruments: Maryland retractor, monopolar cautery, or needle driver with a CO2 laser fibre; bedside paediatric Yankauer and laryngeal suctions, bipolar, endoscopic clip applier"],["osec","th:op-15~s1-2","Consent","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer from the upper voice box through the mouth with robotic instruments, keeping the voice box. Swallowing will be difficult at first; food may go the wrong way; a nasogastric tube for up to 2 to 3 weeks, rarely a gastrostomy (about 1 in 10). Bleeding from the voice box artery, which can block the airway and needs urgent return to theatre. Airway swelling , sometimes delayed; rarely a tube or tracheostomy; seek help at once if breathing changes. Numb or tingling tongue from pressure, which recovers; damage to teeth . Narrowing of the airway later. Neck dissection at the same time, and possibly radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy afterwards."],["osec","th:op-15~s1-3","Before you start","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Operating note","Ask about pain, breathlessness, voice change, stridor, dysphagia and aspiration, how fast symptoms are progressing (impending airway compromise), nutrition and weight loss, and previous treatment. Record previous head and neck or lung cancer, COPD or asthma, heart disease, diabetes and renal disease, previous neck surgery (carotid endarterectomy, tracheostomy), aspirin, anticoagulants, nebulisers and steroids, and tobacco, cannabis and alcohol."],["osec","th:op-15~s1-4","Will it be exposed?","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Operating note","Examine walking and breathing; in the mouth, trismus , Mallampati grade and tongue size, the teeth and micrognathia; in the neck, nodes, scars, the hyomental distance and neck extension; listen for stridor, wheeze and hoarseness. On flexible laryngoscopy map the tumour (suprahyoid or infrahyoid epiglottis, false fold, glottis, hypopharynx, tongue base and vallecula), check vocal fold movement (fixation is a contraindication), and look for second primaries."],["osec","th:op-15~s1-5","Imaging and preparation","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Operating note","Contrast CT of the neck (pre-epiglottic and extralaryngeal spread, nodes); CT of the chest (metastases, lung primary, COPD, and a high brachiocephalic artery if a tracheostomy is planned), or PET-CT for stage III and IV; MRI if the tongue base may be involved. Assess swallowing: significant dysphagia beforehand predicts poor swallowing afterwards , and teaching the supraglottic swallow before surgery reduces it. Arrange dental review if adjuvant treatment is likely, stop aspirin and anticoagulants a week before, and continue COPD medication."],["osec","th:op-15~s2-1","Boundaries and barriers","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Anatomy you must see","The supraglottis runs from the ventricle up to the tip of the epiglottis: the suprahyoid and infrahyoid epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, arytenoid, corniculate and cuneiform cartilages, and false folds. The quadrangular membrane, epiglottic cartilage and hyoepiglottic ligament resist spread, but tumours reach the glottis quickly through the paraglottic space , continuous with the pre-epiglottic space. Cancer through the epiglottic cartilage reaches the vallecula and tongue base; with aryepiglottic fold involvement, check the pyriform sinus."],["osec","th:op-15~s2-2","The superior laryngeal artery","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Anatomy you must see","From the transoral view it runs deep to the nerve , beneath the mucosa near the pharyngoepiglottic fold in the pre-epiglottic space. It can be large and must be controlled definitively with clips or bipolar . Preserve the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve for sensation unless the tumour requires it."],["osec","th:op-15~s3-1","Protect and map","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Protect the upper teeth (a thermoplastic splint moulded to them is thinner and tougher than standard guards), and the lower teeth too if the tongue is retracted with a suture, to avoid lacerating its underside. Map the cancer by direct laryngoscopy. What you should see Guards on the teeth, and the tumour's edges on the epiglottis, folds and vallecula mapped."],["osec","th:op-15~s3-2","Expose and dock","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Place the FK retractor with the laryngeal or flat tongue base blade in the vallecula (or a Crowe-Davis), using the 0 or 30 degree scope as the anatomy suits. Bring the robot in at 30 to 45 degrees to the table, with the Maryland retractor and monopolar cautery (or laser) on either side of the camera in an isosceles triangle of 30 to 45 degrees, the black pivot marks on the ports near the oral commissures. Figure 1 A supraglottic carcinoma of the midline suprahyoid and infrahyoid epiglottis (asterisk: its centre). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 15.1 Docking angle The chapter describes docking the robot at 30 degrees to the head of the bed in one place, and bringing its base in at 30 to 45 degrees to the foot of the table in another. Position it so the arms do not collide. What you should see The whole tumour in the camera view with both instruments reaching beyond its edges without colliding."],["osec","th:op-15~s3-3","Mucosal incisions","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the mucosa 5 mm to 1 cm from the tumour, through the aryepiglottic and pharyngoepiglottic folds. Figure 2 Monopolar incision of the right aryepiglottic and pharyngoepiglottic folds at a suitable distance from the tumour. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 15.2 What you should see A mucosal cut 5 to 10 mm clear of visible tumour all round."],["osec","th:op-15~s3-4","Into the vallecula","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Carry the incision into the vallecula to mobilise the tumour and, if needed, take the pre-epiglottic space down to the hyoid. Figure 3 The incision continued into the vallecula, resecting the pre-epiglottic space to the hyoid if necessary. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 15.3 What you should see The vallecula open and pre-epiglottic fat exposed, with the hyoid as the anterior limit."],["osec","th:op-15~s3-5","Rotate, do not regrasp","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Rotate the epiglottis back and forth to make the circumferential mucosal and deep cuts without bisecting the specimen, or remove large cancers in carefully oriented pieces. Avoid repeatedly regrasping the epiglottis, which tears mucosa and cartilage. Figure 4 The epiglottis grasped and rotated to continue the dissection with the mucosal surfaces in view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 15.4 What you should see The specimen turning as one piece, with intact mucosa on its surface."],["osec","th:op-15~s3-6","Control the superior laryngeal artery","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Laterally, positively identify the superior laryngeal vessels and control them with bipolar or clips. What you should see The superior laryngeal pedicle entering through the thyrohyoid membrane in the lateral cut, clipped before division. The superior laryngeal artery Failing to identify and control it leads to brisk bleeding in theatre or, worse, afterwards, with rapid loss of the airway."],["osec","th:op-15~s3-7","The inferior cut","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","With the anterior and ipsilateral incisions made, the whole tumour can be seen. When the specimen is free above and laterally, make the inferior incision. Keep talking with the bedside assistant to stay oriented, especially with small specimens. Figure 5 With the epiglottis freed by right-sided and vallecular incisions, the extent of the left-sided tumour (asterisk) is visible. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 15.5 Figure 6 The contralateral inferior mucosal incision mobilising the tumour. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 15.6 What you should see The ventricle and the true folds below the inferior cut, untouched."],["osec","th:op-15~s3-8","Margins and haemostasis","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Take circumferential frozen sections while the patient is still asleep. Irrigate, inspect after a Valsalva , and apply a haemostatic agent if needed. Figure 7 The defect after supraglottic partial laryngectomy of the suprahyoid and infrahyoid epiglottis down to the ventricles. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 15.7 What you should see A dry bed on Valsalva and clear margins all round."],["osec","th:op-15~s4-1","Crisis drills","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · When it goes wrong","Bleeding into the airway after surgery 1 Act return to theatre for intubation and operative control 2 Prevent deliberate control of the superior laryngeal artery at the time of resection Delayed airway swelling 1 Prevent perioperative steroids, especially when the false folds are resected 2 Act intubation tracheostomy if needed"],["osec","th:op-15~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls An endotracheal tube not secured, dislodged by the robotic arms. Poor docking, causing arm collisions. Not identifying and controlling the superior laryngeal artery. Inadequate exposure, leading to incomplete resection and positive margins. Sacrificing the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve unnecessarily."],["osec","th:op-15~s5-1","After the operation","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Aftercare","A single perioperative steroid dose may suffice for an epiglottectomy; give 24 hours if oedema is a concern. Antibiotics are not routine. Most patients stay for observation and a swallowing assessment: if secretions are managed the next day, the speech therapist assesses at the bedside, by modified barium swallow or FEES. If not, or intake is inadequate, place a nasogastric tube until review. A reliable patient after a limited resection without significant comorbidity may go home after recovery. Warn every patient that delayed laryngeal swelling can occur and needs prompt attention."],["osec","th:op-15~s5-2","Complications","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Airway bleeding Return to theatre, intubate, control Delayed airway oedema Intubation or tracheostomy Aspiration, prolonged dysphagia Nasogastric feeding for up to 2 to 3 weeks; gastrostomy if prolonged Airway stenosis Late Dental injury Guards Lingual nerve neurapraxia Temporary"],["ev","th:op-15~s5-3","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery: current evidence","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Aftercare","Transoral laser microsurgery. Large series show local control and larynx preservation for T1 to T2 supraglottic and glottic cancer comparable to radiotherapy, with the option of radiotherapy kept for recurrence. Cordectomy classification. The European Laryngological Society classification (types I to VI) standardises the extent of endoscopic cordectomy and predicts voice results (Remacle et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2000). Voice. After type I to II cordectomy voice is usually close to that after radiotherapy; deeper resections leave a weaker voice. Margins are narrow by design; positive margins need re-resection or radiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-15~s5-3","Alternatives","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · Aftercare","Transoral laser microsurgery, open partial laryngectomy, total laryngectomy, or (chemo)radiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-15~s6-1","Outcomes","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · For the examination","In Arshad and colleagues' SEER study of over 2600 patients, surgically treated T1 to T2 N0 supraglottic cancer had 81 to 86% disease-specific survival. No prospective trial compares robotic, laser and open supraglottic laryngectomy, but retrospective outcomes are equivalent. When a tracheostomy is made, decannulation is usually possible within 2 weeks ; feeding is usually nasogastric, with 9.5% needing gastrostomy in the largest European series. Complications range from 0 to 28% (bleeding, delayed airway compromise, aspiration pneumonia). The editor notes the advantage over laser surgery when anatomy blocks the microscope's view, but also the high cost of the robot."],["osec","th:op-15~s6-2","Viva essentials","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Selection: T1 and T2, mobile folds and arytenoids, adequate exposure, adequate lungs. Contraindications, and when to choose open surgery or chemoradiotherapy. Spread through the pre-epiglottic and paraglottic spaces. The superior laryngeal artery: where, and how to control it. Setting up: retractor, robot angle, instrument triangle. The resection sequence and frozen sections. Steroids, swallowing assessment and delayed oedema."],["osec","th:op-15~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Supraglottic Transoral Robotic Surgery · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a patient who already aspirates badly. Bisecting the specimen through repeated regrasping. Discharging without warning about delayed swelling."],["osec","th:op-150~s1-1","The case at a glance","Optic Nerve Decompression · Operating note","Endoscopic optic nerve decompression Summary Indications Progressive compressive optic neuropathy from fibrous dysplasia, benign tumours (meningioma), mucocele or other bony compression; selected traumatic optic neuropathy with residual vision, especially with a canal fracture; part of skull base tumour surgery Uncertain Traumatic optic neuropathy without compression; complete blindness from the moment of injury Assessment Visual acuity, colour vision, fields, relative afferent pupillary defect, fundoscopy; fine-cut CT of the canal; MRI for tumours Anaesthesia General Tray Endoscopes (0 and 45 degree), navigation, sinus set, self-irrigating drill with 2 mm diamond burrs, fine dissectors, fibrin glue"],["osec","th:op-150~s1-2","Consent","Optic Nerve Decompression · Operating note","What to tell the patient Vision may improve, stay the same or worsen; the nerve can be injured by heat or handling. Bleeding, including catastrophic bleeding from the internal carotid artery beside the nerve. CSF leak if the sheath or dura is opened. Orbital injury, double vision."],["osec","th:op-150~s1-3","Before you start","Optic Nerve Decompression · Operating note","Document vision carefully. On CT, follow the canal, the carotid and its dehiscence, the anterior clinoid, the optic strut and any Onodi cell (the nerve may run in a posterior ethmoid cell rather than the sphenoid). Figure 1 CT before and after decompression for fibrous dysplasia. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 150.1"],["osec","th:op-150~s2-1","Wide exposure","Optic Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Through one nostril (both for bilateral or tumour work), medialise or resect the middle turbinate, complete the ethmoidectomy along the medial orbital wall and skull base, and open the sphenoid widely from the planum to the floor, flush laterally with the orbital apex. What you should see The medial orbital wall continuous with the lateral sphenoid wall."],["osec","th:op-150~s2-2","Identify the landmarks","Optic Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","In the lateral sphenoid wall find the optic prominence above, the carotid prominence below and behind it, and the opticocarotid recess between them; use navigation when anatomy is distorted. Figure 2 The optic and carotid prominences and the opticocarotid recess in the right sphenoid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 150.2 What you should see The optic canal running back from the orbital apex to the tuberculum sellae. The carotid is next door The internal carotid artery lies directly below and behind the optic canal, sometimes without a bony cover. Drill only where you have identified the nerve, on diamonds, and never use a cutting burr here."],["osec","th:op-150~s2-3","Decompress the posterior medial orbit","Optic Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Fracture and remove the thin posterior lamina papyracea, keeping the periorbita intact so fat does not fall into the view (shrink small herniations with bipolar). What you should see The orbital apex with intact periorbita."],["osec","th:op-150~s2-4","Drill the canal","Optic Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Thin the thick bone of the apex and canal with a self-irrigating 2 mm diamond, then lift the eggshell bone off with a fine dissector, from the apex back to the tuberculum, optic strut and paraclinoid carotid. Aim for at least 180 degrees of the circumference (often 270 when the clinoid is pneumatised). Do not open the sheath. Figure 3 Drilling the apex, the decompressed apex, and over 180 degrees of decompression on a 45 degree view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 150.3 and 150.4 and 150.5 What you should see The nerve sheath exposed along the length of the canal. Senior's pearl Heat kills the nerve as surely as a burr. Irrigate constantly, thin the bone without touching the nerve, and finish with a dissector rather than the drill."],["osec","th:op-150~s2-5","Finish","Optic Nerve Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Cover the nerve with fibrin glue if wished; avoid packing, which can compress the nerve. What you should see A dry field and no pressure on the decompressed nerve."],["osec","th:op-150~s3-1","Crisis drills","Optic Nerve Decompression · When it goes wrong","Carotid bleeding 1 Act pack firmly with pressure hypotension avoided two suckers and a crushed muscle patch 2 Then urgent angiography and endovascular treatment CSF from the sheath 1 Act seal with a graft and fibrin glue no packing on the nerve"],["osec","th:op-150~s4-1","After surgery","Optic Nerve Decompression · Aftercare","Check vision and pupils frequently; continue steroids if part of the protocol; no nose blowing; saline douching."],["ev","th:op-150~s4-2","Optic Nerve Decompression: current evidence","Optic Nerve Decompression · Aftercare","For traumatic optic neuropathy, a meta-analysis of 42 studies (2,859 patients) found visual improvement in about 26% after endoscopic optic canal decompression, with no difference between decompression with or without steroid pulses; benefit was greatest with residual vision and early surgery, but the evidence is mostly observational (Alomari et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2026). A systematic review of 16 studies of endoscopic decompression reported a mean visual recovery rate of 58% (Ganjeifar et al., Rom J Ophthalmol 2026); the difference reflects selection."],["osec","th:op-150~s4-2","Complications","Optic Nerve Decompression · Aftercare","Worse vision, carotid injury, CSF leak, orbital haematoma, diplopia, sinusitis."],["osec","th:op-150~s5-1","Where the books differ","Optic Nerve Decompression · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers wide sphenoidotomy, identification of the optic and carotid prominences, irrigated drilling and at least 180 degrees of decompression without opening the sheath. K. J. Lee optic nerve decompression among the endoscopic orbital procedures, with the uncertain role in trauma. The literature modest and variable benefit in traumatic optic neuropathy."],["osec","th:op-150~s5-2","Viva essentials","Optic Nerve Decompression · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications and the evidence in trauma. Landmarks in the sphenoid, including the Onodi cell. Technique : irrigation, eggshell, 180 degrees, sheath left closed. Carotid injury management."],["osec","th:op-150~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Optic Nerve Decompression · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing an Onodi cell carrying the nerve. Drilling without irrigation. Packing over the decompressed nerve. Opening the sheath unnecessarily."],["osec","th:op-151~s1-1","The case at a glance","Orbital Decompression · Operating note","Endoscopic orbital decompression Summary Urgent indications (active disease) Compressive optic neuropathy not responding to intravenous steroids; corneal exposure and ulceration Elective indications (inactive disease, stable for about 6 months) Disfiguring proptosis, pressure or pain, dry exposed eyes, globe subluxation Approaches Endoscopic medial wall and medial floor; lateral wall through a lateral canthal or lid incision; fat decompression; combinations (\"balanced\" medial and lateral) Sequence of rehabilitation Decompression first, then squint surgery, then eyelid surgery Anaesthesia General, hypotensive Tray Endoscopes, navigation, sinus set, sickle knife or beaver blade for the periorbita, Kerrison rongeurs, drill"],["osec","th:op-151~s1-2","Consent","Orbital Decompression · Operating note","What to tell the patient Double vision may appear or worsen (especially after medial decompression), sometimes needing squint surgery. Numbness of the cheek or teeth (infraorbital nerve) if the floor is taken. Sinusitis from obstructed drainage. CSF leak, bleeding, orbital haematoma, and rarely loss of vision. The eye may still look different; eyelid surgery may follow."],["osec","th:op-151~s1-3","Before you start","Orbital Decompression · Operating note","Work with the ophthalmologist: activity score, visual acuity, colour vision, fields, pupils, Hertel exophthalmometry and eye movements. Review CT for the sinuses, skull base, the ethmoidal arteries and the bulk of the apex. Treat active disease medically first unless sight is threatened. Figure 1 A patient with Graves orbitopathy before and after balanced decompression of both eyes. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 151.1 and 151.2"],["osec","th:op-151~s2-1","Open the sinuses widely","Orbital Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest; do a septoplasty first if needed. Trim the lower part of the middle turbinate for room, remove the uncinate and enlarge the maxillary antrostomy backwards and upwards to show the junction of the medial wall and floor. Complete the ethmoidectomy front to back and open the sphenoid widely; expose, but do not dissect, the frontal recess. What you should see The whole lamina papyracea from the frontal recess to the sphenoid face, and the orbital floor from the antrum."],["osec","th:op-151~s2-2","Remove the lamina papyracea","Orbital Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Fracture the lamina anteriorly with an elevator and lift the fragments off the periorbita, removing bone back to the sphenoid face, up to the skull base and down to the junction with the floor. What you should see Intact periorbita bulging into the ethmoid along the whole medial wall. Preserve a strut if you can Removing the bone at the junction of the medial wall and floor (the inferomedial strut) lets the globe drop and increases diplopia. Many surgeons keep it unless maximum decompression is needed for optic neuropathy."],["osec","th:op-151~s2-3","Take the medial floor","Orbital Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","With angled rongeurs remove the floor medial to the infraorbital nerve; drill the thick bone at the junction if it is being removed. Going lateral to the nerve adds little and risks numbness and a sunken eye. What you should see The orbital floor removed up to, but not beyond, the infraorbital canal."],["osec","th:op-151~s2-4","Open the periorbita","Orbital Decompression · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the periorbita in parallel strips from back to front, starting superiorly so herniating fat does not obscure the view, with a sickle knife, keeping the cuts shallow over the medial rectus. Massage the globe gently to help fat prolapse into the ethmoid. Figure 2 Optic neuropathy before decompression, and the medial wall and floor removed on CT afterwards. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 151.3 and 151.4 and 151.5 and 151.6 What you should see Orbital fat prolapsing into the ethmoid, and a softer, less proptotic eye. Senior's pearl For compressive optic neuropathy the apex matters most: take bone right back to the sphenoid face and open the periorbita posteriorly. For cosmetic proptosis, a balanced medial and lateral decompression gives the reduction with less risk of new double vision."],["osec","th:op-151~s3-1","Crisis drills","Orbital Decompression · When it goes wrong","Bleeding obscures the field 1 Act identify and coagulate the anterior ethmoidal or sphenopalatine artery head up, controlled hypotension Vision falls after surgery 1 Act remove any packing assess for haematoma canthotomy if tight urgent ophthalmology"],["osec","th:op-151~s4-1","After surgery","Orbital Decompression · Aftercare","No nose blowing for 2 weeks; saline douching; antibiotics if indicated; check vision and eye movements. Squint and lid surgery wait until the decompression has settled, usually months."],["ev","th:op-151~s4-2","Orbital Decompression: current evidence","Orbital Decompression · Aftercare","A systematic review found only limited randomised evidence comparing decompression techniques, with outcomes and complications varying by approach (Boboridis et al., Adv Ther 2015). In 247 patients, new diplopia in primary gaze followed decompression in 22%; preoperative diplopia in side gaze was the main risk factor, and medial-plus-lateral decompression gave more proptosis reduction than lateral alone (Rocchi et al., Thyroid 2012). Balanced endoscopic medial and transcutaneous lateral decompression improved compressive optic neuropathy in all cases in one series (Hernández-García et al., Acta Otolaryngol 2017)."],["osec","th:op-151~s4-2","Complications","Orbital Decompression · Aftercare","New or worse diplopia, infraorbital numbness, sinusitis, CSF leak, orbital haematoma, vision loss, hypoglobus."],["osec","th:op-151~s5-1","Where the books differ","Orbital Decompression · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers endoscopic medial wall and medial floor decompression with periorbital incisions, combined with lateral decompression as needed, urgently for optic neuropathy and electively for proptosis. K. J. Lee the walls that can be removed and the balanced approach to reduce diplopia. The literature diplopia after decompression is common and predicted by pre-existing restriction."],["osec","th:op-151~s5-2","Viva essentials","Orbital Decompression · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications : urgent versus elective. Which walls and why balance matters. The steps and the periorbital incisions. Complications , especially diplopia. The order of rehabilitative surgery."],["osec","th:op-151~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Orbital Decompression · For the examination","Common pitfalls Decompressing in active disease without a sight-threatening reason. Opening the periorbita inferiorly first, so fat hides the field. Cutting deep over the medial rectus. Obstructing the frontal recess."],["osec","th:op-152~s1-1","The case at a glance","Orbital Abscess · Operating note","Drainage of a subperiosteal orbital abscess Summary Chandler stages I preseptal cellulitis; II orbital cellulitis; III subperiosteal abscess; IV orbital abscess; V cavernous sinus thrombosis Urgent surgery Reduced vision or signs of optic neuropathy (colour vision, relative afferent pupillary defect); a large abscess; an abscess not in the medial position (superior, lateral); frontal sinusitis; intracranial spread; suspected anaerobic infection; older children and adults Trial of antibiotics Small medial abscess with normal vision in a young child (under about 9 years), with frequent eye checks; drain if no improvement in 24 to 48 hours Approach Endoscopic ethmoidectomy with removal of the lamina papyracea (medial abscess); external (Lynch or transcaruncular) if endoscopic access is poor; sub-brow or lid crease for superior abscesses Imaging Contrast CT of sinuses and orbits; MRI if intracranial spread is suspected Anaesthesia General Tray Endoscopes, paediatric sinus set, culture swabs, external approach set, corneal protectors"],["osec","th:op-152~s1-2","Consent","Orbital Abscess · Operating note","What to tell the family The aim is to save sight and clear the infection. Bleeding, orbital injury, double vision, rarely loss of vision. CSF leak (rare). A small scar if an external approach is needed; eyelid or eye movement problems. Antibiotics continue afterwards; the abscess can recur."],["osec","th:op-152~s1-3","Before you start","Orbital Abscess · Operating note","Examine vision, colour vision, pupils, eye movements and proptosis, with ophthalmology, and record them repeatedly. Read the CT for the abscess size, site and the sinuses involved. Start intravenous antibiotics covering streptococci, staphylococci and anaerobes. Figure 1 A child with a subperiosteal orbital abscess. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 152.2 Figure 2 Coronal and axial contrast CT of a right medial subperiosteal abscess. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 152.3 and 152.4 and 152.5"],["osec","th:op-152~s2-1","Check the eye under anaesthesia","Orbital Abscess · The operation, stage by stage","After intubation, test forced ductions in all directions and the pupils if the child could not cooperate before. Protect the cornea. What you should see A baseline for comparison at the end of the operation."],["osec","th:op-152~s2-2","Open the ethmoid","Orbital Abscess · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest the swollen mucosa well (expect bleeding), remove the uncinate, open the bulla and the anterior ethmoid cells, and identify the lamina papyracea, using navigation if available. Open the maxillary ostium and frontal recess if involved. Figure 3 Medial orbital landmarks: lacrimal crests, lacrimal fossa and ethmoidal foramina. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 152.1 What you should see The lamina papyracea, bulging or discoloured over the abscess."],["osec","th:op-152~s2-3","Drain the abscess","Orbital Abscess · The operation, stage by stage","Fracture and remove a window of lamina papyracea over the abscess with an elevator, keeping the periorbita intact. Pus flows out; send it for culture. Widen the window enough for continued drainage. What you should see Pus draining into the ethmoid and the orbit softening. Stay outside the periorbita The abscess is outside the periorbita. Do not incise it unless there is a true orbital abscess within the cone: orbital fat and the medial rectus lie directly behind it."],["osec","th:op-152~s2-4","External or superior approaches","Orbital Abscess · The operation, stage by stage","When endoscopic access is not possible, drain through a Lynch incision or transcaruncular conjunctival incision along the medial wall. For a superior abscess, use a sub-brow or upper lid crease incision, mark and protect the supraorbital bundle, incise periosteum along the rim and elevate it off the orbital roof to the abscess. Protect the levator and trochlea. Figure 4 The supraorbital notch, the sub-brow incision and the approach to the superior orbit. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 152.6 and 152.7 and 152.8 What you should see The abscess cavity opened and drained, and a free-moving globe on forced duction. Senior's pearl Re-examine the eye after drainage and every few hours afterwards. Improvement in vision and proptosis should be quick; if it is not, re-image for an undrained collection or an orbital abscess within the cone."],["osec","th:op-152~s3-1","Crisis drills","Orbital Abscess · When it goes wrong","Vision worsens on medical treatment 1 Act drain now ophthalmology consider steroids only with specialist advice Headache, drowsiness, seizures or bilateral eye signs 1 Act MRI for intracranial abscess or cavernous sinus thrombosis neurosurgery broaden antibiotics"],["osec","th:op-152~s4-1","After surgery","Orbital Abscess · Aftercare","Intravenous antibiotics guided by culture, then oral to complete the course; saline douching; regular checks of vision and pupils; review of the sinuses."],["ev","th:op-152~s4-2","Orbital Abscess: current evidence","Orbital Abscess · Aftercare","Abscess size helps predict who needs surgery: in children with subperiosteal abscess, larger CT volumes and dimensions were associated with surgical management, supporting medical treatment of small medial abscesses with normal vision (Todman and Enzer, Ophthalmic Plast Reconstr Surg 2011; Gavriel et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2011). In a ten-year paediatric series, CT was reserved for proptosis or failure to improve within 48 hours, and surgical drainage was needed in about 16% (Sciarretta et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2017)."],["osec","th:op-152~s4-2","Complications","Orbital Abscess · Aftercare","Persistent or recurrent abscess, orbital injury, diplopia, vision loss, CSF leak, intracranial spread."],["osec","th:op-152~s5-1","Where the books differ","Orbital Abscess · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers criteria for immediate surgery (vision, size, non-medial site, frontal sinusitis, intracranial spread, age 9 or over) and endoscopic or external drainage. Dhingra and K. J. Lee the Chandler classification and the spread of ethmoid infection through the lamina papyracea. The literature small medial abscesses in young children with normal vision often resolve with antibiotics."],["osec","th:op-152~s5-2","Viva essentials","Orbital Abscess · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Chandler stages. Indications for surgery versus antibiotics. Eye examination and how often. Endoscopic drainage steps and the periorbita. Intracranial complications."],["osec","th:op-152~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Orbital Abscess · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating with antibiotics while vision is falling. Incising the periorbita for a subperiosteal collection. Missing a superior or lateral abscess that the endoscope cannot reach. Infrequent eye checks."],["osec","th:op-153~s1-1","The case at a glance","Lateral Canthotomy · Operating note","Lateral canthotomy and inferior cantholysis Summary Emergency indication Orbital compartment syndrome: proptosis with a tight orbit plus any of reduced vision, a relative afferent pupillary defect, raised intraocular pressure (over about 40 mmHg), ophthalmoplegia or severe pain; usually retrobulbar haemorrhage after sinus or orbital surgery, trauma or anticoagulation Elective uses First step of lateral or inferior orbitotomy, eyelid tightening and lid reconstruction Do not wait for CT, an ophthalmologist or theatre when vision is threatened; medical measures (mannitol, acetazolamide, steroids) must not delay it Anaesthesia Local infiltration (lidocaine with adrenaline) at the bedside, or general Tray Haemostat, straight sharp scissors (iris or Stevens), toothed forceps, gauze; sterile skin preparation"],["osec","th:op-153~s1-2","Consent","Lateral Canthotomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient (often in an emergency) The aim is to save sight ; vision may not recover if damage has already occurred. Bleeding and a small scar at the outer corner of the eye. Eyelid malposition (a lax lower lid or rounded outer corner), repairable later. Globe injury, rare with care."],["osec","th:op-153~s1-3","Before you start","Lateral Canthotomy · Operating note","Recognise it: a hard, proptosed eye that will not retropulse, a dilated or sluggish pupil, falling vision, and pain. After sinus surgery, remove any nasal packing first. Check vision and pupils on both sides; measure intraocular pressure if a tonometer is to hand, but do not delay. Figure 1 The lateral orbital rim and the superior and inferior crura of the lateral canthal tendon. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 153.1"],["osec","th:op-153~s2-1","Infiltrate and crush","Lateral Canthotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Clean the skin and inject a little local anaesthetic with adrenaline at the lateral canthus. Clamp the lateral canthus horizontally from the outer corner to the orbital rim with a haemostat for about a minute to crush the tissue and reduce bleeding. What you should see A crushed, blanched line from the lateral canthus to the rim."],["osec","th:op-153~s2-2","Canthotomy","Lateral Canthotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Cut along the crushed line with straight scissors, about 1 cm, from the canthus to the rim, dividing skin, orbicularis and conjunctiva. Figure 2 Scissors placed for the canthotomy, and the incision. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 153.2 and 153.3 What you should see The lateral canthus divided into upper and lower limbs down to the orbital rim."],["osec","th:op-153~s2-3","Inferior cantholysis","Lateral Canthotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Grasp the lower lid with toothed forceps and pull it forwards and away from the globe. Point the scissors down and back towards the chin, and \"strum\" across the tissue between lid and rim: you can feel the inferior crus of the lateral canthal tendon as a tight band. Cut it. Figure 3 Strumming the scissors to find and cut the inferior crus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 153.4 What you should see The lower lid swinging away freely from the globe. The canthotomy alone does nothing Cutting the skin and canthus does not decompress the orbit. The pressure falls only when the inferior crus of the tendon is cut and the lower lid is free. If the lid is still tethered, you have not finished."],["osec","th:op-153~s2-4","Reassess","Lateral Canthotomy · The operation, stage by stage","Check the eye: it should soften, the pressure fall and the pupil and vision improve. If the orbit remains tight, cut the superior crus as well. Figure 4 Laxity of the lower eyelid after successful cantholysis. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 153.5 What you should see A softer orbit, falling intraocular pressure and improving pupil response. Senior's pearl Retropulse the eye before and after. If you cannot push the globe back before the cut and you can after it, you have decompressed the orbit. Then find the cause: a bleeding anterior ethmoidal artery after sinus surgery may need medial orbital decompression in theatre."],["osec","th:op-153~s3-1","Crisis drills","Lateral Canthotomy · When it goes wrong","Eye still tight after canthotomy and cantholysis 1 Act cut the superior crus release the inferior septum 2 Then theatre for orbital decompression (endoscopic medial wall or external) ophthalmology Retrobulbar haematoma after endoscopic sinus surgery 1 Act remove packs canthotomy and cantholysis at once 2 Then find and coagulate the anterior ethmoidal artery consider medial orbital decompression"],["osec","th:op-153~s4-1","After surgery","Lateral Canthotomy · Aftercare","Leave the wound open to heal; it usually closes acceptably. Lubricate the eye. Check vision regularly. Repair any lasting lid malposition electively."],["ev","th:op-153~s4-2","Lateral Canthotomy: current evidence","Lateral Canthotomy · Aftercare","Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis can be done at the bedside with simple instruments and can prevent irreversible blindness when performed promptly for orbital compartment syndrome (Rowh et al., J Emerg Med 2015). In a cadaver model, adding release of the inferior orbital septum lowered orbital pressure further than canthotomy and cantholysis alone (Oester et al., Ophthalmic Plast Reconstr Surg 2012)."],["osec","th:op-153~s4-2","Complications","Lateral Canthotomy · Aftercare","Bleeding, incomplete release, eyelid malposition (ectropion, rounded canthus), globe injury (rare), infection."],["osec","th:op-153~s5-1","Where the books differ","Lateral Canthotomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers canthotomy with inferior cantholysis as the emergency treatment, with medical measures never delaying it. Dhingra and K. J. Lee orbital haematoma as a complication of endoscopic sinus surgery and the role of canthotomy. The literature bedside decompression saves sight when done early; septal release adds further decompression."],["osec","th:op-153~s5-2","Viva essentials","Lateral Canthotomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Recognising orbital compartment syndrome and the time window. The steps , especially the inferior cantholysis. Causes after ENT surgery (anterior ethmoidal artery). What to do if the eye remains tight."],["osec","th:op-153~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Lateral Canthotomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Waiting for imaging or a specialist. Doing only the canthotomy. Forgetting to remove nasal packing. Pointing the scissors towards the globe."],["osec","th:op-155~s1-1","The case at a glance","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Operating note","Rhinoplasty and nasal valve surgery Summary Indications Improving the appearance of the nose; nasal obstruction from static or dynamic collapse of the internal or external valve ; reconstruction after trauma or cancer resection Functional add-ons Patients with obstruction often do best with a septoplasty and inferior turbinate reduction at the same time Contraindications Comorbidity raising anaesthetic or bleeding risk; unrealistic expectations or psychological disorders predicting dissatisfaction; active rhinitis medicamentosa (bleeding, and vasoconstrictors less effective) Anaesthesia General preferred (comfort, airway control if bleeding is heavy); local or sedation possible Position Supine, arms tucked, head of bed turned 90 degrees; tube secured so it does not distort the lip or upper face; whole face visible, prepared with ophthalmic povidone-iodine Antibiotics One preincision dose covering skin flora (weight-based cefazolin) Tray Septorhinoplasty set; straight, curved and guarded osteotomes (including a Rubin); rasps; Freer elevator; Converse scissors Skills Septoplasty"],["osec","th:op-155~s1-2","Consent","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to change the shape of your nose, to improve breathing, or both. The result: you will see improvement, but small asymmetries or irregularities you can feel but not see are usual. Swelling takes months to settle. You may be unhappy with the appearance; revision surgery is sometimes needed. Breathing: the nose can collapse if its support is weakened; scarring can block the airway. Bleeding after surgery, a hole in the septum , loss of smell, and rarely a leak of brain fluid. Splints for a week, saline sprays, no nose blowing for 2 weeks, limited activity. Stop blood thinners, fish oil and similar supplements 2 weeks before, if medically safe."],["osec","th:op-155~s1-3","Before you start","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Operating note","Explore the motivation for surgery: a realistic patient with sensible expectations is the foundation of a good outcome, and unrealistic or unachievable goals must be discussed beforehand. Ask about previous nasal surgery or trauma; obstruction, at rest or on breathing in ( static or dynamic collapse ); anosmia; easy bleeding or bruising; and screen for psychiatric disorders at your discretion. Stop antiplatelets and anticoagulants 2 weeks beforehand where medically appropriate, and over-the-counter and herbal products that increase bleeding, such as high-dose fish oil."],["osec","th:op-155~s1-4","Analysis","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Operating note","Examine the whole face and the external nose: The height of the nasion and rhinion , and the length of the nasal bones relative to the cartilaginous midvault. The nasal bones, palpated for irregularity and old fractures. Skin thickness. The shape and support of the medial and lateral crura , the columellar relationship, tip projection. A bulbous tip: thick skin, or the shape of the cartilages? The base: nostril shape and symmetry. Endoscopy for obstruction: septal deviation and turbinate hypertrophy. Watch the valves during quiet and slightly deeper inspiration for static and dynamic collapse of the internal and external valves. Imaging is not usually needed; review any existing scans for the bones and old trauma. Take the full set of photographs: frontal, oblique, basal, lateral and lateral smiling. Counsel about aftercare, activity, rinses and splints."],["osec","th:op-155~s2-1","Landmarks","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Anatomy you must see","Glabella; nasion and radix; rhinion; supratip; tip; infratip lobule; the anterior and posterior septal angles ; the head of the inferior turbinate; the cartilaginous and bony septum; the nasal bones; the upper and lower lateral cartilages ; the maxillary spine; the nasolabial (nasal-columellar) angle."],["osec","th:op-155~s2-2","The tip tripod","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Anatomy you must see","The tip behaves like a tripod: the paired medial crura form the lower leg, each lateral crus an upper leg. Shortening the lateral crura increases rotation ; shortening the medial crura decreases rotation . The lower laterals are often asymmetric, irregular at the domes and variably oriented, even in a symmetrical nose, so tip manoeuvres must be gentle and considered; the commonest functional penalty is external valve collapse from lost support. Tip support Major mechanisms Minor mechanisms Strength of the lower lateral cartilages Dorsal septum Attachment of the medial crura to the septum Interdomal ligaments Attachment of the upper to the lower lateral cartilages Membranous septum; nasal spine; skin and soft tissue; alar sidewalls"],["osec","th:op-155~s3-1","Inject","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Inject a 1:1 mixture of 1% lidocaine and 0.5% bupivacaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline into the columellar and marginal incision lines, the subcutaneous plane of the whole skin envelope and the osteotomy sites, and the septal mucosa if a septoplasty is planned. What you should see The whole envelope blanched, with the tip and dorsum not distorted by the volume."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-2","Marginal incisions","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Incise along the caudal margin of each lateral crus , then dissect with Converse scissors over the crura toward the tip to make pockets. Figure 1 The marginal incision just caudal to the lateral crus, with dissection over its superficial surface. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.1 What you should see The caudal edge of each lateral crus, with the cut on its margin, not through the cartilage."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-3","The transcolumellar incision","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Make a gull-wing (five-pointed) incision through skin only , sparing the medial crura, extending horizontally past their flare and then vertically toward the dome along the caudal margin of the lower laterals. Tunnel with Converse scissors subcutaneously from one vertical limb to the other, then complete the deep cut, cauterising the columellar artery. Figure 2 (A) The gull-wing transcolumellar design; (B) its lateral limb. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.2 Figure 3 Completing the transcolumellar incision with scissors, staying superficial to the medial crura. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.3 What you should see Intact medial crura beneath the columellar skin cut, and the columellar arteries sealed."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-4","Lift the envelope","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Follow the caudal margins of the medial crura to the tip and join the lateral pockets. Continue up toward the nasion superficial to the upper laterals , then subperiosteally over the nasal bones to the nasion. Bleeding is minor unless you stray laterally into the angular artery anastomosis. Figure 4 (A) Sharp elevation of the skin envelope off the tip cartilages; (B) retracting the tip cartilages down to dissect toward the nasion. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.4 What you should see The white lower lateral cartilages, the upper laterals and the bare nasal bones to the nasion, with the envelope lifted in one clean plane."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-5","The septum","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Separate the medial crura to reach the caudal septum and raise subperichondrial flaps on both sides beyond the bony-cartilaginous junction: ideal for severe caudal deviation and vestibular stenosis. For lesser deviations a hemitransfixion incision may be enough. Divide the upper laterals from the septum submucosally, protecting the lining. The septal cartilage is now available for grafts. What you should see The caudal and dorsal septum from above, both flaps intact, and an L-strut of at least 1 to 1.5 cm marked."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-6","Upper third","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Low radix: augment the nasion with autologous cartilage, with or without fascia or perichondrium, to balance a nose that looks short. Dorsal hump: resect the cartilaginous hump sharply (scalpel or angled Fomon scissors) and the bony hump with an osteotome (a Rubin), keeping the plane so that the nasal bones are left of equal height. Figure 5 Resecting the dorsum with angled Fomon scissors. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.5 Figure 6 Curved left and right osteotomes and the straight Rubin osteotome. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.6 Figure 7 The dorsal osteotomy with a Rubin osteotome. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.7 What you should see A smooth, straight profile with nasal bones of equal height and an open roof."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-7","Lateral osteotomies: high-low-high","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Removing the hump leaves an open roof , which must be closed by medialising the bones with lateral osteotomies , usually endonasal (percutaneous for severe deformity or old fractures). Start high on the pyriform aperture above the attachment of the inferior turbinate , keeping a triangle of maxilla that preserves the lateral suspensory ligament and stops the valve's lateral wall from medialising. Go low along the ascending process of the maxilla at the nasofacial junction, where thick skin hides it and the bone is strong. Curve high again toward the apex for a predictable back fracture, stopping at the level of the medial canthus to avoid a rocker deformity. Marking the line on the skin helps the inexperienced keep both sides symmetrical. Rasp the medialised bones smooth, because the skin here is thin. Figure 8 The high-low-high lateral osteotomy: position 1, the first segment; position 2, angled up along the nasofacial junction. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.8 Figure 9 Before (left) and after (right) a dorsal reduction and deprojection: (A) lateral; (B) base. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.9 What you should see The nasal bones moving in to close the roof, with no step on the side wall and no rocking of the bone."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-8","Middle third","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","The midvault is the upper laterals and their septal attachment. Contour the dorsal septum carefully, as over-resection causes functional and cosmetic problems, and correct any twist. Spreader grafts (long, flat strips of septal or auricular cartilage beside the dorsal septum) support the midvault, open the internal valve , and prevent an inverted V ; placed asymmetrically, they straighten a deviated dorsum. Fix them with three interrupted horizontal mattress sutures, then sew the upper laterals back to them. Turn-in flaps of the upper laterals that would otherwise be resected, scored and folded under, give similar support without harvesting cartilage. What you should see Spreader grafts level with the dorsal septum, a straight dorsum, and a wider angle at the internal valve."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-9","Lower third: the tip","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Cartilage-sparing first: change the relative crural lengths. In lateral crural steal , horizontal mattress sutures recruit lateral crus into the tip, lengthening the medial crura and increasing projection ; recruiting medial into lateral does the opposite. Cephalic trim: remove the cephalic margins of the lateral crura to reduce bulbosity and increase rotation, measuring what remains with calipers to avoid over-resection. Domal sutures: transdomal horizontal mattress sutures narrow the tip. Vertical dome division with interdomal sutures and reattachment narrows a boxy tip. Lateral crural overlay: divide, overlap and suture the lateral crura to shorten them without removing cartilage; with medial crural overlay too, rotation and projection are controlled together. Do not destabilise the tip excessively. Lateral crural strut grafts under the lateral crura correct a boxy tip, malpositioned or cephalic crura, rim retraction and concave crura, and support a collapsing external valve . Tip grafts under the skin sculpt the tip and infratip and hide irregularities. Figure 10 A conservative cephalic trim (red). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.10 Figure 11 Transdomal horizontal mattress sutures to narrow the tip. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.11 Figure 12 Vertical dome division with cephalic trim, interdomal sutures and reattachment of the lateral crura, narrowing a boxy tip. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.12 Figure 13 Lateral crural overlay (red: the overlap). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.13 What you should see Symmetrical domes from the"],["osec","th:op-155~s3-10","Septal extension and caudal strut grafts","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","The open approach divides tip support, and scarring de-projects the tip over time. A septal extension graft overlaps the caudal septum and extends between the medial crura; fixing the lower laterals to it controls projection and rotation almost at will, and placed on one side it can also pull a deviated caudal septum to the midline. A caudal strut graft joins end to end with the caudal septum: thinner, but less stable, useful for irregular medial crura. What you should see A tip that springs back when pressed, in the midline, at the planned projection and rotation. Restore what you remove Failing to restore tip support after interrupting it, or over-resecting dorsal or tip cartilage, leads to collapse of the valves and secondary deformity."],["osec","th:op-155~s3-11","Close","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · The operation, stage by stage","Re-drape the skin envelope. Close the columella first at its midline, then at the apices of the gull-wing, with 6-0 permanent monofilament, then interrupted sutures between; close the marginal incisions with 6-0 fast-absorbing gut. Place silicone splints if synechiae are a concern. Suck out the nasopharynx and pass an orogastric tube before waking. What you should see The columellar incision edges level with no notch, and a symmetrical tip once the skin is back."],["osec","th:op-155~s4-1","Crisis drills","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · When it goes wrong","Bleeding after surgery 1 First topical vasoconstrictor such as oxymetazoline 2 If it persists packing or absorbable haemostatic agents (infrequently needed)"],["osec","th:op-155~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Poor exposure; poor haemostasis. Elevating in the wrong plane. Over-resecting the dorsum or tip cartilage. Uneven or incomplete osteotomies. Not restoring tip support."],["osec","th:op-155~s5-1","After the operation","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Aftercare","External splint for 1 week ; internal silicone splints for 1 week with saline spray every 2 to 4 hours from the day of surgery to prevent crusting. Paracetamol and opioids for the first few days. No nose blowing for 2 weeks ; saline spray instead. At the 1-week visit remove splints and tape, examine and clear crusts."],["osec","th:op-155~s5-2","Deformities and complications","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Aftercare","Classic deformities Deformity Cause and treatment Inverted V Midvault and internal valve collapse after dorsal resection without support; spreader grafts Polybeak Supratip fullness from over-resected nasal bones, under-resected cartilaginous septum or supratip scarring; steroid for scar, revision for anatomy Open roof Wide, often asymmetric bony dorsum: greenstick lateral osteotomies that spring back, bones not medialised, aggressive packing, uncorrected deviated perpendicular plate Bossae Tip cartilage irregularities; revision directed at the cause Valve collapse Loss of support at the internal or external valve Figure 14 The inverted V deformity. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 155.14 Other risks: dissatisfaction, obstructive scarring, epistaxis, septal perforation, anosmia and CSF leak."],["ev","th:op-155~s5-3","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve: current evidence","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Aftercare","Assessment. Screen for body dysmorphic disorder, which is found in roughly 10 to 15% of patients seeking cosmetic rhinoplasty and predicts dissatisfaction; record a patient-reported outcome such as SCHNOS or NOSE and standard photographs. Function. The American consensus statement on nasal valve compromise supports spreader grafts, alar batten grafts and lateral crural strut grafts for valve collapse (Rhee et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2010). Preservation rhinoplasty lowers the dorsum without resecting it, avoiding the inverted V deformity, in selected noses; long-term comparative data are still limited. Piezoelectric osteotomies cause less bruising and oedema than chisels in randomised trials."],["osec","th:op-155~s5-3","Alternatives","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · Aftercare","Hyaluronic acid fillers are used by some as a \"non-surgical rhinoplasty\" to correct asymmetry and augment the dorsum; this is controversial because of reported vascular complications from embolised filler, but increasingly common."],["osec","th:op-155~s6-1","Do measurements predict beauty?","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · For the examination","Many ideals of nasal proportion exist. In a study of 300 lateral facial views, the Goode, Simons, Baum, Powell and Crumley measures of tip projection correlated neither with one another nor with attractiveness judged by 78 viewers: no single measurement should outweigh assessment of the whole face. The commonest is Goode's ratio of tip projection to nasal length, normally 0.55 to 0.60 , but experienced surgeons mostly judge projection by eye."],["osec","th:op-155~s6-2","Viva essentials","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Patient selection and expectations. Static versus dynamic collapse; internal versus external valve, and how to examine them. The open approach: marginal and gull-wing columellar incisions, the planes. The thirds of the nose and the main manoeuvres in each. High-low-high lateral osteotomies and why. Spreader grafts and the inverted V. The tripod; rotation and projection; major and minor tip support. Septal extension and caudal strut grafts. Polybeak, open roof and inverted V: causes and treatment."],["osec","th:op-155~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Rhinoplasty and Surgery of the Nasal Valve · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on unrealistic expectations. A dorsal hump removal without lateral osteotomies. A cephalic trim without measuring the remaining cartilage. Forgetting the septum and turbinates in a patient who cannot breathe."],["osec","th:op-16~s1-1","The case at a glance","Total Laryngectomy · Operating note","Total laryngectomy Summary Absolute indications Most T4 tumours; invasion of the thyroid cartilage ; extension into the soft tissues of the neck; salvage after failed non-surgical treatment; chondroradionecrosis not responding to conservative care Relative indications T3 cancer (laryngectomy versus chemoradiotherapy is debated); unfit for chemoradiotherapy; life-threatening aspiration from neurological disease; severe COPD ruling out conservation surgery Contraindications A tumour suitable for conservation surgery, radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy; comorbidity ruling out general anaesthesia; the patient's refusal Anaesthesia General; oral intubation, awake fibreoptic intubation or awake tracheostomy under local, agreed with the anaesthetist according to the tumour Position Supine, shoulder roll, arms tucked; bed turned 90 or 180 degrees Antibiotics First dose before incision: clindamycin 900 mg with gentamicin 5 mg/kg once, then clindamycin 900 mg 8-hourly for 3 doses (especially in penicillin allergy); or ampicillin-sulbactam 3 g 6-hourly for 4 doses Tray Head and neck set with retractors; monopolar and bipolar diathermy; 14 Fr red rubber catheter for a primary puncture; 1% lidocaine to inject into the carotid bulb adventitia for profound bradycardia Landmarks Thyroid notch, cricoid, sternal notch, carotid sheath, oesophagus"],["osec","th:op-16~s1-2","Consent","Total Laryngectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the voice box and the cancer, sometimes with the lymph nodes of the neck. You will breathe through a permanent opening in the neck (stoma) for the rest of your life; the nose and mouth no longer carry air, so the sense of smell is reduced. Your voice: the natural voice is lost. You can speak again with a valve in a puncture between windpipe and gullet, an electrolarynx, or oesophageal speech; the speech therapist will meet you before surgery. Swallowing changes, and narrowing of the gullet can occur. A leak from the throat to the skin (fistula), especially after radiotherapy (about 30% in salvage surgery), usually healing with tube feeding. Bleeding, infection, chest infections, blood clots. Damage to nerves of the tongue (hypoglossal) or to the parathyroid glands, affecting calcium. Further treatment with radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy may follow."],["osec","th:op-16~s1-3","Before you start","Total Laryngectomy · Operating note","Laryngeal cancer is commonest in the glottis, then the supraglottis, rarest in the subglottis; it is more frequent in men and mostly squamous. Tobacco is the main risk, with alcohol, HPV and asbestos as others. Hoarseness is the commonest complaint; ask about throat pain, odynophagia, dysphagia, otalgia and weight loss, and in a treated patient the return of the original symptoms. Record previous conservation surgery, radiotherapy or organ preservation; COPD, heart disease and previous DVT; the family history; tobacco, alcohol, asbestos and cannabis; antiplatelet drugs and warfarin; allergies. Listen to the voice and look for breathlessness and stridor. Examine the larynx with a mirror or, better, a flexible scope (useful for teaching and follow-up): note the tumour's site, vocal fold movement, pooling and aspiration. Examine the whole head and neck for a second primary, the neck for nodes and skin involvement over the larynx, and the nutritional state."],["osec","th:op-16~s1-4","Imaging and work-up","Total Laryngectomy · Operating note","Contrast CT of the neck in selected cases shows the pre-epiglottic space , thyroid cartilage and nodes; chest radiograph or CT looks for lung metastases, and some use PET-CT for distant disease, the primary and the nodes. Then: Endoscopy under general anaesthesia to map the tumour, biopsy it and exclude a second primary. Review outside slides at your own institution. A baseline PET-CT if not already done. Medical and radiation oncology review and a multidisciplinary tumour board . Referral to dental oncology, nutrition, speech therapy, social work, patient advocacy, and spiritual or psychological support if wanted. See the speech therapist first Counselling by the speech and language therapist before surgery is part of optimising the speech outcome, not an afterthought."],["osec","th:op-16~s2-1","Landmarks","Total Laryngectomy · Anatomy you must see","The thyroid notch, cricoid and sternal notch for the incision and the stoma. The carotid sheath , retracted laterally with the sternomastoid. The hypoglossal nerves , which must be seen on both sides above the hyoid. The superior laryngeal neurovascular bundle and superior thyroid artery , ligated. The pyriform sinus mucosa , peeled from the inner thyroid cartilage and kept when uninvolved. The party wall , the common wall between the back of the trachea and the front of the oesophagus, which must not be separated if a puncture is planned. The oesophagus."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-1","Secure the airway","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Agree the airway plan with the anaesthetist. An obstructing tumour may need a tracheostomy beforehand; otherwise direct laryngoscopy or fibreoptic intubation usually works. What you should see A secured airway and the tumour's extent confirmed at laryngoscopy."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-2","The apron incision","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Mark a U-shaped apron from the level of the greater horn of the hyoid on each side down along the anterior border of each sternomastoid to about 2 cm above the sternal notch , taking in any existing tracheostomy. Infiltrating 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline is optional. Divide the platysma and raise a subplatysmal flap up above the hyoid and down to the clavicles and sternal notch. Do any neck dissection now, before the laryngectomy. What you should see The subplatysmal flap up above the hyoid with the anterior jugular veins left on the straps."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-3","Separate the larynx from the lateral neck","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the fascia along the whole anterior border of each sternomastoid and retract the muscle laterally to show the carotid sheath . Dissect the tissue in front of and medial to the sheath down to the prevertebral fascia , freeing the laryngotracheal complex from the lateral neck along the full length of the muscle. Clamp, tie and divide the omohyoid, the superior thyroid artery and the superior laryngeal neurovascular bundle . Figure 1 The fascia along the whole sternomastoid incised and the muscle retracted laterally. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.1 Figure 2 The sternomastoid retracted laterally and the straps medially with the laryngotracheal complex: the carotid sheath, hypoglossal nerve, part of the ansa cervicalis and the superior laryngeal nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.2 What you should see The carotid sheath laterally, the prevertebral fascia behind, and the superior laryngeal pedicle tied. Bradycardia at the carotid Handling the carotid can cause profound bradycardia. Stop, and inject 1% lidocaine into the adventitia of the carotid bulb."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-4","Straps and thyroid","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the infrahyoid strap muscles low, at the level of the stoma, and lift them upward to expose the thyroid lobes, and divide the isthmus. To keep a lobe , dissect it off the side of the trachea with diathermy; to remove a lobe with the specimen, divide the fascia over its capsule and dissect posteriorly and laterally so as to keep the blood supply of the parathyroids. Figure 3 The straps divided low and lifted, exposing the thyroid gland. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.3 What you should see The trachea bare in front below the cricoid, and any preserved lobe pink on its lateral pedicle."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-5","Free the hyoid","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Identify both hypoglossal nerves . Divide the suprahyoid muscles off the body of the hyoid until you reach the mucosa of the vallecula, and free the greater horns, steadying the hyoid with a towel clip or Allis clamp. Figure 4 The hyoid skeletonised and freed, held in an Allis clamp. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.4 What you should see Both hypoglossal nerves lying on the hyoglossus close to the greater horns, then a bare hyoid. The hypoglossal nerves They lie close to the greater horns of the hyoid. See them on both sides before you free the hyoid."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-6","Release the constrictors and the pyriform mucosa","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Rotate the larynx with double hooks and divide the middle and inferior constrictors off the posterolateral thyroid cartilage. With a Cottle elevator, lift the pyriform sinus mucosa off the inside of the cartilage and keep it, unless it is involved by tumour. Divide the thyrohyoid ligament to free the superior cornu. What you should see The posterior border of the thyroid cartilage, and pyriform mucosa peeling off its inner surface intact. Every millimetre of mucosa counts Removing too much pyriform mucosa narrows the neopharynx and risks stricture or a tight closure."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-7","Divide the trachea and make the stoma","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Mobilise the distal trachea bluntly along its front so it can be fixed to the clavicular heads later. Cut through the trachea between rings below the tumour , bevelling its lateral sides with heavy Mayo scissors to widen the stoma. Secure the stoma to the skin, place a reinforced tube in the distal trachea and connect it to the circuit sterilely. Divide the posterior membranous trachea up to the party wall, without separating the party wall below, and lift the proximal trachea bluntly to the cricoid. Figure 5 After the tracheal incision, dissection in the plane of the party wall up to the back of the cricoid; the party wall and the remaining thyroid lobe are seen. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.5 What you should see The tracheal lumen below the tumour, a bevelled stump, and the oesophageal muscle of the party wall behind it."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-8","Enter the pharynx","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Move to the head of the table with a headlight. Enter the pharynx through the vallecula , the pyriform sinus or the postcricoid mucosa, according to the tumour and your preference. To enter the vallecula, divide the hyoepiglottic ligament, which leads to the epiglottis, or press a suction tip into the vallecular mucosa and cut onto it. Grasp the tip of the epiglottis with an Allis clamp. Through the pyriform sinus, cut down onto a suction tip in the sinus and open the larynx like a book. Figure 6 After entering the vallecula, the epiglottis is grasped and the tumour incisions are made under direct vision. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.6 What you should see The tip of the epiglottis, then the tumour from above, before you cut round it."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-9","Resect with a margin","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","With the tumour in view, cut the mucosa with scissors including a 1 cm margin , keeping uninvolved pyriform mucosa. Open the specimen, fracturing it through the posterior cricoid, to check the margins, and take more where needed. Irrigate, secure haemostasis, then change gowns, gloves and instruments for the reconstruction. What you should see The specimen opened with a clear margin all round, and an open pharyngeal defect from base of tongue to oesophageal inlet."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-10","Nasogastric tube and stoma","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Pass an 18 Fr nasogastric tube through the nose and pharynx into the oesophagus, secured with a suture through the columella. Fix the trachea to the clavicular heads on both sides with 2-0 Vicryl through its lateral wall 2 to 3 rings below the cut edge, so the stoma does not drop into the chest. Sew the front of the stoma to the skin with half vertical mattress sutures of 2-0 Vicryl that cover the cartilage edge. The stoma should be about 3 cm across. What you should see A wide, bevelled stoma with skin covering the cartilage edges and no tension. Microstoma A small stoma stenoses. Bevel the trachea, cover the cartilage, and aim for 3 cm."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-11","A primary tracheoesophageal puncture","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Buttress the party wall to the back of the membranous trachea at its cut edge with 4-0 Vicryl. A separated party wall is an absolute contraindication to a primary puncture. Constrictor myotomy: with a finger in the pharyngeal defect, cut the middle and inferior constrictors and the upper oesophageal muscle vertically from the oesophageal inlet to the base of tongue down to the mucosa, to prevent spastic speech. Close any accidental pharyngotomy with inverting sutures. The puncture: place a right-angled clamp through the defect against the posterior membranous trachea 1 to 1.5 cm below its cut edge , cut onto its tip with a No. 15 blade, push the tips through, grasp a Foley or red rubber catheter, pull it into the pharynx and direct it down the oesophagus. Fix it with a 2-0 silk stitch; the patient can be fed through it instead of a nasogastric tube. A voice prosthesis can be placed at once from a kit. A flap reconstruction does not rule out a primary puncture, provided the party wall is intact. Figure 7 The front of the stoma secured with half vertical mattress sutures and the constrictor myotomy done; the puncture is made 1 to 1.5 cm below the cut edge of the membranous trachea. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.7 Figure 8 (A) A No. 15 blade cutting horizontally onto a right-angled clamp pushed through the posterior wall; (B, C) a catheter pulled through the puncture into the pharynx and directed down the oesophagus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.8 What you should see The puncture in the midline about 1 to 1.5 cm below the mucocutaneous edge, with the cat"],["osec","th:op-16~s3-12","Close the pharynx","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Close the mucosa with a running inverted 3-0 Vicryl suture, vertically, horizontally or as a T, depending on preference and on how much mucosa remains. Make any puncture before closing. Figure 9 The remaining pharyngeal mucosa closed watertight with inverted edges to prevent a fistula. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 16.9 What you should see Mucosal edges turned in along the whole suture line, with enough width left to take your finger. The editor's view of the closure The editor, Johan Fagan, advises a capacious, relaxed pharynx: a cricopharyngeal myotomy, avoiding a vertical closure , a low threshold for augmenting a narrow pharynx with a pectoralis major flap, a correctly sited puncture, and dividing the sternal heads of the sternomastoids for a flat stoma. The chapter itself leaves the orientation of the closure to the surgeon."],["osec","th:op-16~s3-13","Test, drain and close","Total Laryngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Fill the pharynx from the mouth with half-strength hydrogen peroxide in a bulb syringe: bubbles along the suture line mark a leak. Place 10 mm suction drains on each side. Return the upper flap, sew the back of the stoma to the skin with interrupted 2-0 Vicryl, close the platysma and staple the skin. Place a laryngectomy tube if needed to keep the stoma open, and extubate in theatre if stable. What you should see No bubbles along the suture line, and flaps lying flat over the drains."],["osec","th:op-16~s4-1","Crisis drills","Total Laryngectomy · When it goes wrong","Profound bradycardia 1 Recognise heart rate falling as you handle the carotid 2 Act stop handling it inject 1% lidocaine into the adventitia of the carotid bulb A leak on the peroxide test 1 See bubbles along the suture line 2 Repair reinforce the closure where it leaks and test again 3 Consider flap augmentation if the closure is tight or the tissues irradiated Fistula after surgery 1 Recognise saliva in the wound or drain, or a leak on barium swallow at 5 to 7 days 2 Manage continue tube feeding and give antibiotics"],["osec","th:op-16~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Total Laryngectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Inadequate perioperative antibiotic cover. Understaging the cancer. Removing too much pyriform sinus mucosa. A poor pharyngeal closure, giving a fistula or stenosis. Too small a stoma. Separating the party wall when making a puncture."],["osec","th:op-16~s5-1","After the operation","Total Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Admit to a step-down or intensive care unit. Antibiotics for 24 hours. Humidified air or oxygen to the stoma by tracheostomy collar, regular tracheal suction and cleaning of crusts; a laryngectomy tube if needed. Antiemetics; analgesia by PCA or scheduled opioid, then oral once feeds and pain allow. Start tube feeds by protocol. Physiotherapy from day 1, but no neck extension for 10 days . Teach stoma care to the patient and family from day 1, and start electrolarynx teaching before discharge if indicated. A barium swallow at 5 to 7 days looks for a small fistula: if none, start liquids and advance; if one, continue tube feeds and start antibiotics."],["osec","th:op-16~s5-2","Complications","Total Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Complications of total laryngectomy Complication Notes Haematoma and seroma Drains on both sides Wound infection Adequate antibiotic cover Pharyngocutaneous fistula About 30% after salvage surgery; tube feeds and antibiotics Dehiscence of stoma or neck wound Covered cartilage edge, tension-free stoma Pharyngeal stricture Too little mucosa, a tight closure Deep vein thrombosis, chest infection General risks Puncture complications Party wall separation, misplacement Hypoglossal nerve injury, bradycardia, carotid sheath injury, positive margins, bleeding Intraoperative risks"],["ev","th:op-16~s5-3","Total Laryngectomy: current evidence","Total Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Larynx preservation versus laryngectomy. In the VA larynx trial, induction chemotherapy followed by radiotherapy in responders preserved the larynx in about two-thirds without compromising survival ( NEJM 1991), and in RTOG 91-11 concurrent chemoradiation gave the best larynx preservation (Forastiere et al., NEJM 2003; J Clin Oncol 2013). For T4a disease with cartilage invasion, primary total laryngectomy gives better survival in database studies. Salvage surgery. Fistula complicates about a third of salvage laryngectomies; a systematic review found that a pectoralis major onlay flap reduced fistula risk (Guimarães et al., Head Neck 2016). Voice. Primary tracheoesophageal puncture with a voice prosthesis gives the best early voice."],["osec","th:op-16~s5-3","Alternatives","Total Laryngectomy · Aftercare","Primary radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy. Palliative care for those who decline treatment."],["osec","th:op-16~s6-1","Does a pectoralis flap prevent fistula after salvage laryngectomy?","Total Laryngectomy · For the examination","Yes. Pharyngocutaneous fistula follows about 30% of salvage laryngectomies; in a recent study, a pectoralis major myofascial flap reduced the risk by 22% compared with controls, and the fistula rate did not differ between laryngectomy and pharyngolaryngectomy."],["osec","th:op-16~s6-2","Viva essentials","Total Laryngectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Absolute and relative indications; T3 is debated. The airway plan, including awake tracheostomy. The apron incision and the order of release from below upward. Seeing both hypoglossal nerves . Saving uninvolved pyriform mucosa. The stoma: bevelled, 3 cm, cartilage covered with half vertical mattress sutures, fixed to the clavicular heads. Primary puncture: intact party wall, 1 to 1.5 cm below the cut edge, constrictor myotomy against spasm. The watertight inverted closure and the peroxide test. Barium swallow at 5 to 7 days; fistula rates and the pectoralis flap. Carotid bradycardia and its treatment."],["osec","th:op-16~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Total Laryngectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Dividing the trachea without a clear margin below the tumour. Forgetting the constrictor myotomy. Extending the neck in the first 10 days."],["osec","th:op-161~s1-1","The case at a glance","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · Operating note","Excision of head and neck skin cancer Summary Basal cell carcinoma Low-risk, well-defined, under 2 cm: about 4 mm clinical margin. High-risk (morphoeic, infiltrative, micronodular, recurrent, over 2 cm, central face): wider margins or margin control Squamous cell carcinoma Low-risk (under 1 cm, thin, well differentiated): about 4 mm; moderate risk: about 6 mm; high-risk (2 cm or more, over 6 mm thick, invading fat, perineural invasion, temple, lip, scalp, ear): about 9 mm or Mohs; assess the parotid and neck Melanoma Excision margin by Breslow thickness (in situ about 5 mm to 1 cm; thicker lesions 1 to 2 cm), sentinel node biopsy from about 0.8 to 1 mm or with ulceration; Mohs or staged excision for lentigo maligna Margin control Mohs surgery or frozen sections for high-risk sites and recurrent tumours; delay reconstruction until margins are clear Anaesthesia Local for most; general for large or nodal surgery Anticoagulants Usually continued for small excisions Tray Skin set, marking pen, fine instruments, bipolar, sutures; flap and graft instruments"],["osec","th:op-161~s1-2","Consent","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · Operating note","What to tell the patient A scar, and the possibility of a flap or skin graft. Incomplete excision needing further surgery, and recurrence. Bleeding, infection, wound breakdown, partial flap or graft loss. Nerve injury: temporal and marginal mandibular branches of the facial nerve, greater auricular, accessory and sensory nerves of the forehead. For squamous carcinoma or melanoma: possible parotidectomy, neck dissection or radiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-161~s1-3","Before you start","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · Operating note","Biopsy anything uncertain. Examine with good light and dermoscopy for the true edge. Palpate the parotid and neck. Ask about previous treatment (recurrence means the margins were not clear), immunosuppression (transplant recipients get aggressive squamous carcinomas) and syndromes (basal cell naevus syndrome, xeroderma pigmentosum). Image the parotid and neck for high-risk squamous carcinoma or perineural symptoms. Figure 1 A nodular basal cell carcinoma at the inner canthus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 161.1 Figure 2 Squamous cell carcinoma of the auricle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 161.2 Figure 3 Locally recurrent melanoma on the temple. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 161.3"],["osec","th:op-161~s2-1","Mark the margins","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the visible tumour edge, then the margin around it, and orient the ellipse along the relaxed skin tension lines (about three times as long as wide, with 30 degree points). What you should see A margin of the planned width all round, lying in a natural crease."],["osec","th:op-161~s2-2","Excise at the right depth","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Infiltrate local anaesthetic with adrenaline, incise vertically, and excise to a plane deep to the tumour: subcutaneous fat for most, including fascia or perichondrium where the tumour is deep. Orient the specimen with sutures for the pathologist. What you should see A specimen of even thickness with a clean deep plane, marked for orientation."],["osec","th:op-161~s2-3","Check the margins before reconstructing","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","For high-risk tumours, use Mohs surgery (complete margin examination by horizontal sections, stage by stage) or frozen sections. If margins are uncertain, close simply or dress the wound and reconstruct when the margins are confirmed clear. What you should see Clear margins reported before tissue is moved. Never bury an unclear margin Rotating a flap over a defect with tumour still at its edge spreads the tumour under the flap, where recurrence is found late and deep. Confirm clearance first."],["osec","th:op-161~s2-4","Close","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Undermine in the right plane (above the SMAS on the face, subgaleal on the scalp) and close in layers without tension. Choose secondary intention for concave sites (medial canthus, conchal bowl, temple), a local flap for convex sites with laxity nearby, and a full-thickness skin graft where flaps would distort (nasal tip, eyelids). Figure 4 A helical rim advancement flap after excision on the ear. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 161.4 What you should see A tension-free closure that does not pull on the eyelid, lip or nasal ala. Senior's pearl Watch the free margins. A closure that pulls the lower eyelid, the lip or the alar rim will distort them as it contracts; choose the vector of closure so that tension runs parallel to the free edge."],["osec","th:op-161~s2-5","Regional disease","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","For squamous carcinoma with parotid or neck nodes, add parotidectomy and neck dissection, with adjuvant radiotherapy for high-risk features. For melanoma, sentinel node biopsy guides nodal treatment, and systemic therapy is discussed in the multidisciplinary team. What you should see Staging of the parotid and neck completed and planned with the team."],["osec","th:op-161~s3-1","Crisis drills","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · When it goes wrong","Margin involved on final histology 1 Act re-excise, ideally with margin control or radiotherapy if surgery is unsuitable Expanding haematoma under a flap 1 Act open the wound, evacuate, find the vessel a flap under a haematoma will die Dark flap at a week 1 Act wait much dusky tissue survives debride only when necrosis has declared itself"],["osec","th:op-161~s4-1","After surgery","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · Aftercare","Wound care, suture removal at 5 to 7 days on the face, sun protection, and regular skin surveillance (new primaries are common); nodal follow-up for squamous carcinoma and melanoma."],["ev","th:op-161~s4-2","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck: current evidence","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · Aftercare","In a randomised trial of high-risk facial basal cell carcinomas with 10-year follow-up, recurrence after Mohs surgery was 4.4% against 12.2% after standard excision for primary tumours, and 3.9% against 13.5% for recurrent tumours, where the difference was significant; over half of recurrences of primary tumours appeared after 5 years (van Loo et al., Eur J Cancer 2014)."],["osec","th:op-161~s4-2","Complications","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · Aftercare","Bleeding, haematoma, infection, flap or graft necrosis, scarring, distortion of free margins, nerve injury, incomplete excision, recurrence."],["osec","th:op-161~s5-1","Where the books differ","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers margins by tumour type and risk (about 4 mm for low-risk basal cell carcinoma, 4, 6 and 9 mm for low, moderate and high-risk squamous carcinoma), Mohs surgery for high-risk and recurrent tumours and for head and neck melanoma, and reconstruction after clearance. K. J. Lee risk factors, staging (including the AJCC 8 cutaneous squamous carcinoma staging), sentinel node biopsy and the parotid as the first echelon. The literature Mohs surgery halves recurrence in high-risk facial basal cell carcinoma over 10 years."],["osec","th:op-161~s5-2","Viva essentials","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Margins for basal cell carcinoma, squamous carcinoma and melanoma. High-risk features and sites. Mohs surgery : what it is and when to use it. Reconstructive ladder and the free margins. Regional disease : parotid, neck, sentinel node."],["osec","th:op-161~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Management of Skin Cancer of the Head and Neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls Moving a flap before margins are clear. Ignoring perineural symptoms or parotid nodes. Closures that distort the eyelid or lip. Debriding a dusky flap too early."],["osec","th:op-162~s1-1","The case at a glance","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Operating note","Microvascular reconstruction of the head and neck Summary Indications Mandibular defects (especially anterior); large glossectomy and base of tongue defects; total or near-total pharyngeal defects; maxillectomy and midface defects; skull base defects beyond local flaps Contraindications Expected poor quality of life or very limited survival (which may change the recommendation); severe peripheral vascular disease; no recipient vessels left in the neck after previous surgery Anaesthesia General Position Supine with the bed turned 180 degrees so the resecting and reconstructive teams can work at once; compression stockings on the opposite leg for a fibula, on both legs for a thigh flap; lateral decubitus for scapular and latissimus flaps Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin for skin defects not open to the mouth or pharynx; ampicillin-sulbactam (or clindamycin in penicillin allergy) for oral and pharyngeal defects Tray Microvascular set and operating microscope; Doppler for perforators and monitoring; craniofacial and mandibular plates; venous coupler; implantable Doppler (optional) Recipient vessels Arteries: facial, superior thyroid, transverse cervical, external carotid. Veins: in zones I and V of the neck, and the external jugular Skills Advanced microvascular training (head and neck or plastic surgery fellowship) Flap failure Should be well under 5% in experienced hands"],["osec","th:op-162~s1-2","Consent","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to rebuild the part removed with tissue moved from elsewhere, with its blood vessels joined under the microscope. The flap can fail (under 5% in experienced hands), usually from a clot at the join; a return to theatre may save it, and another flap may be needed. The donor site: a scar, a skin graft that may not take, exposed tendon, swelling, weakness or stiffness of the wrist or ankle, a hernia after an abdominal flap, a blood clot in the leg. A leak of saliva to the skin (fistula), deep neck infection, narrowing of the throat after pharyngeal reconstruction. Close monitoring for several days, with frequent checks of the flap. Plates and screws for bone reconstruction, which can later be exposed or break."],["osec","th:op-162~s1-3","Before you start","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Operating note","Reconstruction begins with planning the resection from the TNM stage , and thinking about what both resection and reconstruction will do to the patient's quality of life. Prognosis, dental rehabilitation, the need for radiotherapy and the planned neck dissection affect the choice of flap and recipient vessels. Cardiac, vascular and pulmonary disease and alcoholism are common in these patients, and may shorten survival and narrow the choices. Previous radiotherapy causes fibrosis, especially around vessels, which favours a pedicled or free flap over a skin graft. Previous surgery (an open abdomen, fixation of a radius or tibia) may rule out a donor site. Before you raise each flap Flap Check Radial forearm Allen test , to make sure the hand will survive Rectus, iliac crest, jejunum An existing abdominal wall hernia Fibula Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses Anterolateral thigh The thickness of the thigh, to judge suitability Image the tumour with CT and/or MRI. Pre-bent plates and CT-guided cutting templates can be made before surgery for bony flaps. Whether the leg vessels must be imaged before a fibula is controversial : large series without imaging report no harm, but peripheral vascular disease is common in these patients; ankle-brachial index and colour Doppler are cheaper and quicker than angiography or MR angiography."],["osec","th:op-162~s1-4","Preparation","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Operating note","Plan for a second or back-up flap , free or pedicled, and protect a limb from venepuncture. Make sure the margins are clear before finalising the flap design. Decide on the likely recipient vessels; if the neck has none, be ready for vein grafts from a limb."],["osec","th:op-162~s2-1","The mandible","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Anatomy you must see","How much a mandibular resection disables the patient depends on the mouth before surgery (and whether useful teeth remain), how much bone goes, whether the defect is anterior or lateral , and the soft tissue removed. With a large soft tissue loss the bone may be \"incidental\": closing the soft tissue and covering the reconstruction come first. Lateral defects: some patients function without continuity, but the face loses its lower contour and the opposite muscles pull the remaining mandible across, causing malocclusion in the dentate. Most surgeons therefore offer primary reconstruction with an osseous free flap (fibula or osteocutaneous radial forearm). A plate with soft tissue cover (pectoralis or a soft tissue free flap) has been used too, but delayed failure from plate exposure or fracture is 7 to 30% with pectoralis cover alone, and would be higher if more patients lived beyond three years (three-year survival in that group was 11%). Anterior defects: without reconstruction the segments collapse to the midline, giving the Andy Gump deformity with drooling and disfigurement. A composite osteocutaneous free flap is the method of choice. Figure 1 Osteocutaneous radial forearm flap for a lateral mandibular defect: (A) lateral mandible and floor of mouth defect after resection of a T4 carcinoma; (B) design with the radius, skin paddle, cephalic vein and radial artery marked. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 162.1 Figure 2 Scapular fasciocutaneous flap: (A) composite resection of the lateral mandible, floor of mouth and cheek skin; (B) continuity restored with a plate; ("],["osec","th:op-162~s2-2","The tongue","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Anatomy you must see","A reinnervated, unidirectional muscle such as the rectus cannot imitate the tongue. The realistic goals are to keep the remaining tongue mobile and to restore bulk so the neotongue meets the palate for speech and swallowing. The mobile tongue (articulation, chewing, the oral phase) needs thin, pliable, possibly sensate tissue: radial forearm or anterolateral thigh. The base of tongue (the pharyngeal phase) needs volume so it can meet the pharyngeal wall and drive the bolus. A segmental mandibulectomy with a glossectomy may need two flaps, such as a thigh (or forearm, rectus or pectoralis) flap with a fibula. Figure 3 Near-total mandibulectomy and total glossectomy: (A) the defect; (B) fibula design with skin perforators marked; (C, D) a pectoralis flap for the posterior tongue; (E) closing wedge osteotomies; (F) fibula inset; (G) a saphenous vein graft into the internal jugular stump, both internal jugulars having been ligated; (H, I) final inset. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 162.3"],["osec","th:op-162~s2-3","The pharynx","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Anatomy you must see","The aim is to restore swallowing, with a flap not too bulky to obstruct. The main options are the anterolateral thigh, the radial forearm and the jejunum. Keeping the larynx with a large pharyngeal defect is especially hard because aspiration is inevitable, and whether aspiration can be minimised may decide whether a laryngectomy is needed; in most advanced cancers the larynx is removed. If the oesophageal resection goes below the thoracic inlet, a gastric pull-up remains the most reliable way to avoid a salivary leak in the chest. Figure 4 Anterolateral thigh flap: (A) design over the perforator; (B) total pharyngeal reconstruction, with fascia lata wrapped around it as an extra layer. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 162.4 Figure 5 Total pharyngeal reconstruction with a radial forearm flap harvested with a monitoring paddle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 162.5"],["osec","th:op-162~s2-4","Midface and skull base","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Anatomy you must see","Midface reconstruction may need the palate, orbit, cheek and bony midface rebuilt separately. In the Cordeiro and Santamaria algorithm, palatal defects were closed with soft tissue, and the zygoma, maxilla and orbital floor rebuilt with non-vascularised (rib, calvarium) or vascularised bone (osteocutaneous radial forearm), using temporalis and soft tissue free flaps for bulk. A fibula in the midface allows osseointegrated implants; the thigh flap can carry several paddles on separate perforators; an Abbe flap can restore oral competence when the lip is resected too. At the skull base, small defects should be closed with local flaps (pericranial, temporoparietal fascia), but many need a free flap (rectus, anterolateral thigh, latissimus, serratus) to separate the cranial cavity from the upper aerodigestive tract, fill dead space, cover vital structures, prevent brain herniation and CSF leak, and restore appearance."],["osec","th:op-162~s3-1","Radial forearm flap","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","A fasciocutaneous flap on branches of the radial artery and its venae comitantes in the septum between brachioradialis and flexor carpi radialis. Its strength is a large area of thin skin for lining and external skin. The antebrachial cutaneous nerves can be included to make it sensate. A segment of radius up to about 10 cm , between the insertions of pronator teres and brachioradialis, can come with it, supplied through the lateral septum and perforators through flexor pollicis longus. Use a boat-shaped osteotomy rather than right-angled cuts, and keep the periosteum on the volar and radial surfaces; one or two osteotomies from the medullary side can shape the bone for a symphyseal or parasymphyseal defect. It restores the forward projection of the anterior mandible but not its height, and the bone is usually too thin for implants. What you should see A normal Allen's test before you start; then the radial vessels in the septum between brachioradialis and flexor carpi radialis, the cephalic vein, and the superficial radial nerve kept on the forearm with paratenon left on the tendons. Fracture of the radius The text advises taking no more than 30% of the cross-section of the radius; the list of errors warns that taking more than 40% risks a pathological fracture and advises prophylactic plating. Take as little as the defect needs."],["osec","th:op-162~s3-2","Anterolateral thigh flap","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","The commonest flap in head and neck reconstruction: a perforator flap from the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery . It has a long pedicle (8 to 16 cm) of good calibre, can include vastus lateralis, rectus femoris or tensor fasciae latae, or be made sensate with the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh, and can be raised while the tumour is being removed. Widen or lengthen it with several perforators, or supercharge it with vessels from the medial, superior or posterolateral thigh. Other thigh flaps (medial thigh, tensor fasciae latae) are alternatives. What you should see Perforators marked by Doppler near the midpoint of the line from the anterior superior iliac spine to the superolateral patella, then the descending branch in the groove between rectus femoris and vastus lateralis."],["osec","th:op-162~s3-3","Raising the thigh flap","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the midpoint of a line from the anterior superior iliac spine to the superolateral corner of the patella , and draw a circle of 3 cm radius around it: most perforators lie in its inferolateral quadrant . Find them with a pencil Doppler and centre the flap over them with its long axis along the thigh. Raise skin and fat until the perforator is seen, then follow it to the main pedicle: through vastus lateralis if it is musculocutaneous (most patients), or between vastus lateralis and rectus femoris if septocutaneous. A donor defect under 9 cm wide closes primarily without reported compartment syndrome; wider needs a graft. In heavier patients it is thick, but it can be thinned to the subdermal fat (about 4 mm). Quadriceps function is near normal even when vastus lateralis is taken. What you should see A perforator traced to the descending branch, with the flap centred over the inferolateral quadrant of the circle."],["osec","th:op-162~s3-4","Scapular and parascapular flaps","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","The circumflex scapular artery gives a horizontal branch (scapular flap) and a descending branch (parascapular flap). Up to 14 cm of the lateral border of the scapula can be taken on its periosteal branches, and, uniquely, the bone and two skin paddles can be oriented independently for complex three-dimensional defects. Latissimus dorsi and serratus anterior can be added on the common subscapular pedicle. Drawbacks: the lateral position, thin bone that may not hold implants, and a segmental periosteal supply that multiple osteotomies for an anterior defect may compromise. What you should see The triangular space between teres minor, teres major and the long head of triceps, with the circumflex scapular pedicle emerging from it."],["osec","th:op-162~s3-5","Rectus abdominis and DIEP flaps","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","The rectus musculocutaneous flap is based on periumbilical perforators from the deep inferior epigastric vessels, so the paddle can point almost any way from the midline. After finding the perforators, open the anterior sheath medial to the linea semilunaris and lateral to the linea alba; do not take anterior sheath below the arcuate line , to keep the abdominal wall strong. Free the muscle from the posterior sheath, and follow the pedicle (up to 15 cm) to the external iliac vessels. A DIEP flap takes only the skin and its perforator, dissected through the muscle, preserving the abdominal wall. Close the fascia directly. Losing one rectus matters little except to the very active. What you should see Perforators around the umbilicus entering the paddle, the deep inferior epigastric pedicle on the back of the muscle, and intact sheath below the arcuate line. A short pedicle The rectus pedicle may not reach the neck, especially when the mandible is intact. Plan the paddle carefully and dissect the pedicle out of the muscle for length."],["osec","th:op-162~s3-6","Fibula flap","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","The first choice for the mandible , as bone alone, with skin or with muscle. It gives up to 26 cm of bone on the peroneal artery and its two venae comitantes, with both endosteal and periosteal supply, so it survives multiple osteotomies, and its cross-section suits osseointegrated implants . The lateral calf skin is supplied by septocutaneous perforators in the posterior crural septum (between soleus and gastrocnemius behind and the peronei in front), and sometimes by musculocutaneous perforators through flexor hallucis longus and soleus. Its weaknesses are a height mismatch with the native mandible, which a double-barrel fold can correct, and little soft tissue for large glossectomy or through-and-through defects, which a second flap (thigh or pectoralis) supplies. Most patients lose a little ankle movement and strength; taking flexor hallucis longus loses great toe movement; daily activities are rarely affected. What you should see Septal perforators to the skin paddle, the peroneal vessels on the back of the bone, the common peroneal nerve protected at the fibular neck, and 6 to 7 cm of bone left at each end. Peripheral vascular disease Even with three-vessel run-off, a patient with an ankle-brachial index under 1.0 may heal the fibula donor site slowly."],["osec","th:op-162~s3-7","Jejunal flap","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","The first free flap reported, in 1959, now partly replaced by fasciocutaneous flaps. Used as a tube or a patch, it matches the oesophagus in diameter, may show peristalsis and secretes. Drawbacks are abdominal complications in up to 5.8% and wetter, less distinct tracheo-oesophageal speech. General surgeons usually harvest it, sometimes laparoscopically and at the same time as the resection; no bowel preparation is needed. A single jejunal vessel from the superior mesenteric artery feeding several arcades can supply a long segment. If the base of tongue end is too wide, open the antimesenteric border. Inset the base of tongue end first , then the anastomosis; once perfused the jejunum expands, so trim the redundancy before joining it to the oesophagus, or it folds and blocks the bolus. What you should see A segment of jejunum with a single arcade pedicle, pink and peristalsing, marked so it is inset in the direction of peristalsis. Ischaemia of the jejunum The jejunum suffers irreversible ischaemia within about 2 hours . Divide its vessels only when the defect and recipient vessels are ready for the anastomosis."],["osec","th:op-162~s4-1","Crisis drills","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · When it goes wrong","The flap looks wrong 1 Venous congestion bluish, warm, swollen, quick dark bleeding on pinprick 2 Arterial compromise pale, cool, slow or no bright red bleeding on pinprick 3 Either back to theatre at once to explore the anastomoses and pedicle 4 Leeches only after the anastomoses and pedicle have been explored and found sound Figure 6 Venous outflow obstruction on day 1 in the patient of figure 3: (A) congested fibula paddle with bluish blood on pinprick; (B) clotted saphenous graft and internal jugular; (C) clots in the venae comitantes cleared with a No. 3 Fogarty catheter. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 162.6"],["osec","th:op-162~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls A rectus pedicle too short for the neck. Taking too much radius (over 40%) without prophylactic plating. Dividing the jejunal vessels before the recipient site is ready. Missing venous congestion, which ends in total flap loss. Using leeches before exploring the anastomosis."],["osec","th:op-162~s5-1","Monitoring","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Aftercare","Clinical examination remains the most reliable monitor: the authors have nurses check every flap, with a Doppler on the pedicle, every hour for 72 hours , and a doctor every 6 hours. Look at colour, turgor, temperature, swelling, the amount and colour of bleeding on pinprick, and the Doppler sound: the arterial signal should strengthen over the following days and the venous signal should augment . A buried flap, as in the pharynx, can carry a small monitor paddle in the neck skin closure, removed later under local anaesthesia. Implantable Dopplers are also available."],["ev","th:op-162~s5-2","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck: current evidence","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Aftercare","Reliability. Free flap success is about 95 to 99% in experienced units; most failures are venous and occur in the first 48 to 72 hours, so frequent flap monitoring and early return to theatre are the keys to salvage. Choice of flap. The anterolateral thigh flap became a workhorse after a series of 672 flaps showed it to be reliable and versatile with low donor morbidity (Wei et al., Plast Reconstr Surg 2002); the fibula is the standard for mandibular reconstruction. Virtual surgical planning with cutting guides improves accuracy of bony reconstruction and shortens ischaemia time in comparative studies. Enhanced recovery (early mobilisation and feeding, judicious fluids) reduces complications and stay."],["osec","th:op-162~s5-2","Complications","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Flap failure Thrombosis at an anastomosis, a kinked vessel, or a design beyond the perforator's territory (partial skin necrosis) Fistula and deep neck infection Oro- or pharyngocutaneous Pharyngeal stenosis After pharyngeal reconstruction Donor site Exposed tendon, failed skin graft, hernia, limb swelling, DVT, reduced wrist or ankle movement Plate failure Exposure or fracture with soft tissue cover alone"],["osec","th:op-162~s6-1","Partial or circumferential pharyngeal defect?","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · For the examination","When a strip of hypopharyngeal mucosa remains after total laryngopharyngectomy, keeping it may reduce stenosis, but the extra suture lines may raise the fistula risk compared with a tubed flap. For circumferential defects the jejunum has been the most reliable: it needs no tubing, so one less suture line, and most studies show stenosis and fistula rates lower than or equal to tubed skin flaps. The main alternative, the tubed radial forearm , avoids the abdomen, gives better tracheo-oesophageal speech and can provide separate neck skin paddles; a Montgomery stent may lower fistula and stenosis. The thigh flap can add a fascia lata layer around the neopharynx."],["osec","th:op-162~s6-2","Viva essentials","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Like with like: epithelium for lining, muscle for bulk, bone for bone. The six workhorse flaps, their pedicles and lengths: radius about 10 cm, scapula 14 cm, fibula 26 cm; thigh pedicle 8 to 16 cm, rectus up to 15 cm. Fibula first for the mandible ; why the anterior mandible must be rebuilt (Andy Gump). Locating thigh perforators: the ASIS-patella midpoint, 3 cm circle, inferolateral quadrant. Tongue goals: mobility for the front, bulk for the base. The pre-harvest checks: Allen test, foot pulses, hernia, thigh thickness. Signs of venous versus arterial failure, and why you return to theatre. A perforator flap: the anterolateral thigh and the DIEP."],["osec","th:op-162~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Microvascular Reconstruction of the Head and Neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls Designing the flap before the margins are clear. Forgetting a back-up flap and a protected limb. A flap too bulky for the pharynx. Infrequent monitoring."],["osec","th:op-165~s1-1","The case at a glance","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Operating note","Paramedian forehead flap Summary Indications Cutaneous nasal defects; periorbital and cheek defects when other local flaps are not available Contraindications Unfit even for local anaesthesia with sedation; psychiatrically or emotionally unstable (especially for several stages); scarring, radiotherapy or previous surgery to the forehead (such as a bicoronal incision) that may have cut the blood supply Caution Smoking is not an absolute contraindication but raises flap ischaemia and donor site problems; a delay (incising the outline without raising it) may be advised Pedicle One supratrochlear artery , about 1.25 to 1.5 cm from the midline at the orbital rim Base width 1.0 to 1.5 cm : wide enough to carry the artery, narrow enough to rotate without losing length Anaesthesia General preferred; local (lidocaine with or without bupivacaine) with intravenous benzodiazepine sedation if needed Position Supine, bed turned 90 degrees so both sides of the face, forehead, scalp, nose, ears and neck are available (symmetry, cartilage grafts) Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin; clindamycin in allergy Tray Plastic soft tissue tray (No. 15C blades, small double hooks, iris scissors, 0.5 toothed forceps); needle-tip and bipolar diathermy; sterile Doppler; rhinoplasty set Skills Soft tissue handling, advancement-rotation flaps, pedicled flaps, functional rhinoplasty"],["osec","th:op-165~s1-2","Consent","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to rebuild the nose with skin moved down from the forehead on its own blood supply. Stages: the flap stays attached to the forehead for about three weeks, with a bridge of skin you can see, before a second operation divides it; sometimes there is a middle stage to thin it. A scar down the forehead, closed directly or left to heal, which usually looks very acceptable. The flap may partly die , especially if you smoke. Bleeding, infection, numbness of the scalp, and a change in the position of the brow or hairline. Further small procedures (dermabrasion, thinning) at 6 to 12 months to refine the result. Hair may be carried onto the nose if the forehead is short."],["osec","th:op-165~s1-3","Before you start","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Operating note","Forehead flaps mostly rebuild the nose after cancer excision, trauma, infection or congenital loss. Ask about previous forehead injuries or excisions that may have disrupted the blood supply, age and comorbidity (anaesthetic risk), psychiatric stability and readiness for a staged operation, anticoagulants (weigh the need against stopping or bridging) and smoking . Characterise the defect: Size: most suit a paramedian flap; a median flap gives more surface for a near-total nose. Shape: a large three-dimensional defect needs a template of the nasal subunits drawn on the forehead. Location: to reach low defects such as the columella, the flap may need to run into the hairline; to avoid that, lengthen it by extending the base into the brow or angling it slightly. Missing layers: the flap replaces skin; cartilage and lining must be reconstructed by other means. Examine the forehead for scars and actinic damage, note the hairline , measure the distance from the base to the defect carefully, and consider tissue expansion for very wide defects. Imaging is not needed. Figure 1 A paramedian forehead flap based on the right supratrochlear artery. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 165.1 Figure 2 Reconstruction of the tip, alae and columella with an expanded forehead flap: (A) expansion complete, the nasal template outlined; (B) expander out, flap ready to rotate; (C) the nose rebuilt and the forehead closed primarily. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 165.2"],["osec","th:op-165~s1-4","Preparation","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Operating note","Decide with the patient among the alternatives (secondary healing, primary closure, grafts, other local flaps). Timing: after Mohs surgery, the defect is known and the plan definite; at the time of excision, counsel for several options, and delayed reconstruction pending final margins is often better. Obtain medical clearance, stop anticoagulants and smoking if possible, and photograph the defect."],["osec","th:op-165~s2-1","From the internal carotid (ophthalmic artery)","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Anatomy you must see","Supraorbital artery: leaves the supraorbital foramen or notch above the mid-orbit to supply the forehead above. Supratrochlear artery: leaves the superomedial orbit above the medial canthus and runs up the forehead. It runs superficial to corrugator and deep to orbicularis oculi and frontalis , then pierces frontalis to become subcutaneous 1 to 2.5 cm above the orbital rim . Dorsal nasal artery: runs down along the medial canthus to the upper nasal sidewall; where it meets the angular artery, a central artery often runs up to supply the glabella and lower central forehead."],["osec","th:op-165~s2-2","From the external carotid","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Anatomy you must see","Facial artery: gives the labial arteries and ends as the angular artery , which gives the lateral nasal branch, passes the medial canthus and joins the dorsal nasal and sometimes the supratrochlear artery, contributing to the central artery. Superficial temporal artery: its frontal branch supplies the lateral forehead and joins the supraorbital. Figure 3 The periorbital vessels (supratrochlear, supraorbital, angular) and their relation to frontalis, corrugator and orbicularis oculi. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 165.3"],["osec","th:op-165~s3-1","Choose the flap","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · The operation, stage by stage","Paramedian: on one supratrochlear artery, centred over it. The usual choice. Midline: on the supratrochlear artery with the flap in the centre of the forehead and the artery at its edge; it can be based on the central artery , lower and more medially along the rim, gaining length before the hairline. Median: on both supratrochlear arteries, broader based, with a smaller arc of rotation. Laterally based , on one or both superficial temporal arteries, for orbital or cheek defects when other local flaps and the midline supply are unavailable; its donor site can be conspicuous. What you should see A forehead with enough height below the hairline for the flap you have chosen, and no scars across its base."],["osec","th:op-165~s3-2","Find the artery","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · The operation, stage by stage","A Doppler can mark it, but landmarks are reliable: about 1.25 to 1.5 cm lateral to the midline at the orbital rim. What you should see A Doppler signal or the landmark 1.25 to 1.5 cm from the midline at the rim, usually at the medial end of the brow."],["osec","th:op-165~s3-3","Design with a template","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · The operation, stage by stage","Base the flap above the medial canthus, centred 1.25 to 1.5 cm from the midline, 1.0 to 1.5 cm wide . Widen the distal paddle if the defect needs more skin. Before cutting, make a template (a piece of towel, for example), hold its base in place and rotate it to the defect to check length and shape. Keep the flap below the hairline where possible. What you should see The template reaching the far edge of the defect without tension when pivoted at the base. Too short Measure and re-measure. Rotation shortens the flap, and the inset must be tension-free . A flap that falls short cannot be rescued."],["osec","th:op-165~s3-4","Raise and inset","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · The operation, stage by stage","In a multistage nasal reconstruction, include full-thickness frontalis in the distal flap to limit contraction; it can be thinned later. Inset with 5-0 nylon and a few deep dermal sutures. Skin-graft the raw underside of the bridge over intact nasal skin, or cover it with an acellular dermal matrix, to reduce bleeding. What you should see A pink flap reaching the defect without tension, its pedicle untwisted, the bridge spanning the intact nose."],["osec","th:op-165~s3-5","The stages","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · The operation, stage by stage","First operation: sew the flap in with its middle bridging the intact skin, where you can watch it. If the pedicle is twisted and the flap is poorly perfused, detach it and return it to the forehead; re-rotate it 5 to 7 days later, when the delay phenomenon has improved its blood supply. Second operation (optional): raise the flap from the defect, thin it to match, and place or thin cartilage grafts. Third operation: divide the pedicle and inset the proximal edge to complete the closure. A single-stage island flap , its pedicle de-epithelialised and tunnelled under the skin, avoids staging but risks compressing the artery and vein in the tunnel. What you should see A pink paddle with brisk capillary refill at each stage, and bleeding from its edge when it is thinned."],["osec","th:op-165~s3-6","The donor site","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · The operation, stage by stage","Close it primarily if the flap is narrow or was expanded. Otherwise let it heal by secondary intention , kept moist with antibiotic ointment or petroleum jelly, which usually leaves a very acceptable scar. Skin grafts are not routinely used. What you should see A forehead closed without lifting the brow, or a clean, moist wound left to granulate."],["osec","th:op-165~s4-1","Crisis drills","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · When it goes wrong","Poor perfusion or heavy congestion 1 Look many flaps look slightly blue after rotation this settles in 2 to 3 days 2 Excessive congestion or doubtful perfusion back to theatre return the flap to its native position 3 Later re-rotate after 5 to 7 days of delay"],["osec","th:op-165~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls A flap too short: measure, re-measure, allow for rotation. An inset under tension. Carrying frontal hair onto the nose."],["osec","th:op-165~s5-1","After the operation","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Aftercare","Usually a day case; an overnight stay for oozing under the bridge, pain or comorbidity. Divide the pedicle only after neovascularisation , usually safely after 3 weeks . Refinements such as dermabrasion or debulking come after final healing at 6 to 12 months ."],["ev","th:op-165~s5-2","Paramedian Forehead Flaps: current evidence","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Aftercare","The paramedian forehead flap remains the standard for large, full-thickness nasal defects, based on the supratrochlear vessels; a three-stage technique with an intermediate thinning stage gives better contour than two stages. Results depend on reconstructing all three layers: lining (local flaps, a folded forehead flap or a free flap), structural cartilage grafts, and skin. Evidence comes from large case series; complication rates (flap necrosis, infection) are low, higher in smokers."],["osec","th:op-165~s5-2","Complications","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Partial flap loss Arterial insufficiency or venous congestion Haematoma, infection Early Poor cosmesis Scarring, notching, colour mismatch, brow malposition, hair on the nose Donor site Slow healing, scalp numbness, hairline distortion"],["osec","th:op-165~s6-1","When to divide the pedicle","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · For the examination","The accepted standard is at least 3 weeks , longer in smokers and in hypertension or diabetes, while the flap gains new vessels from the recipient bed. Indocyanine green angiography studies suggest neovascularisation by 1 to 2 weeks and support earlier division to reduce deformity. In clinic, gently compress the pedicle: if the distal flap stays pink, proceed; if capillary refill is doubtful, wait. The editor adds that tissue expansion widens and lengthens the flap but expanded skin may contract, and expansion can also reduce wide donor site scars later."],["osec","th:op-165~s6-2","Viva essentials","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The history of the forehead flap. The supratrochlear artery: position, depth, where it becomes subcutaneous. Paramedian, midline, median and laterally based flaps. Base width 1.0 to 1.5 cm; the template; allowing for rotation. Frontalis in the distal flap for multistage repairs. The three stages, the island flap, and the delay phenomenon. Donor site closure or secondary healing. Timing of pedicle division and how to test it."],["osec","th:op-165~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Paramedian Forehead Flaps · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not checking for old forehead scars or radiotherapy. Reconstructing only skin when lining and cartilage are missing. Dividing the pedicle early in a smoker."],["osec","th:op-170~s1-1","The case at a glance","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Operating note","Pectoralis major myocutaneous flap Summary Indications Carotid exposure or blowout from infection or radiotherapy; complications of carotid endarterectomy or stenting; neck skin defects with vessels exposed after neck dissection, pharyngeal or oesophageal fistula, or a failed free flap; aerodigestive reconstruction in a vessel-depleted, multiply operated neck; comorbidity or poor physical state that rules out microvascular transfer Contraindications Absolute: anatomical compromise of the muscle or its blood supply (a subpectoral pacemaker, surgery near the pedicle, congenital absence as in Poland syndrome ). Relative: a non-functioning latissimus dorsi on that side (shoulder dysfunction must be weighed against a critical defect); cosmetic concerns in women and deformity in men; too short an arc or too small a flap for the defect Before Negative tumour margins and an uninfected wound Anaesthesia General Position Supine, the bed turned 90 or 180 degrees from the anaesthetist Antibiotics A first-generation cephalosporin; nothing extra for the harvest when combined with the resection Monitoring Standard; blood gases and haemoglobin when indicated Tray Head and neck set with retractors; monopolar and bipolar diathermy; lighted retractors for minimal-access harvest; laparoscopic harmonic scalpel"],["osec","th:op-170~s1-2","Consent","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to bring healthy muscle and skin from the chest up into the neck, to cover vital structures or close a leak. Chest scar and change in shape of the chest or breast; in women, the paddle is placed at the fold under the breast where possible. Weakness of the shoulder , especially if the back muscle on that side does not work. Partial loss of the flap skin , a leak of saliva or wound breakdown, which may need further surgery or another flap. Bleeding , and rarely injury to the lung lining (pneumothorax). Bulk in the neck at first, which shrinks as the denervated muscle wastes; the skin paddle may later be trimmed under local anaesthetic."],["osec","th:op-170~s1-3","Before you start","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Operating note","Ask about anything that may have damaged the muscle or its pedicle: a subpectoral pacemaker , operations near the pedicle, or congenital absence of the muscle ( Poland syndrome ). Check the latissimus dorsi on the same side: if it is paralysed, losing pectoralis will impair the shoulder. Assess and correct, where you can, conditions affecting survival (cardiac and respiratory disease, alcoholism) and healing (diabetes, poor nutrition, smoking, thyroid dysfunction). Examine the function and integrity of pectoralis major and the latissimus dorsi. Judge the fat and breast tissue over the muscle to decide between a myocutaneous and a myofascial flap. Imaging is usually unnecessary; if needed, the authors favour surgeon-performed duplex ultrasound."],["osec","th:op-170~s2-1","The muscle","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Anatomy you must see","Pectoralis major lies below the clavicle and deltoid and above the rectus abdominis and external oblique. It arises from the medial half of the clavicle, the front of the sternum, the costal cartilages of the upper seven ribs and the external oblique aponeurosis, and inserts into the lateral lip of the bicipital groove."],["osec","th:op-170~s2-2","The pedicle","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Anatomy you must see","The dominant pedicle is the pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial artery with its venae comitantes, from the subclavian vessels. It emerges through the clavipectoral fascia between subclavius and pectoralis major, at a point just medial to the deltopectoral triangle (Mohrenheim's fossa, bounded by deltoid, pectoralis major and clavicle), and runs on the deep surface of the muscle along a line from that point to the xiphoid . Minor pedicle: the pectoral branch of the lateral thoracic artery, entering the upper border 3 to 4 cm lateral to the thoracoacromial branches; in a few patients it is the main supply. Segmental: internal mammary perforators through the first to sixth intercostal spaces, entering within 1.5 to 2 cm of the sternum; and branches of the fifth to seventh intercostal arteries entering 4 to 6 cm from the sternal origin. The acromial and deltoid branches of the thoracoacromial trunk must be ligated during the dissection."],["osec","th:op-170~s2-3","The nerves","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Anatomy you must see","The lateral pectoral nerve (lateral cord) enters the deep surface near the middle of the muscle; the medial pectoral nerve (medial cord) enters laterally after passing through pectoralis minor. Sensation comes segmentally from the second to seventh intercostal nerves."],["osec","th:op-170~s3-1","Design the paddle","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the thoracoacromial vessels and the territory of the deltopectoral flap , which you may need one day. Design the skin paddle next to the inferior border of the muscle , or at the inframammary fold in women: the more distal the paddle, the better the arc of rotation. Keep the paddle at least partly over the muscle . Duplex ultrasound can confirm the vessels. Figure 1 Exposure of spinal hardware through the posterior pharynx and cervical oesophagus after chemoradiotherapy for tonsil cancer: (A) the flap raised through the perimeter of its paddle and inset, with a stretched 1.5:1 meshed graft to offset the tension of radiated skin, and the tracheostomy flange trimmed and sutured; (B) later, graft contracture reduces the deformity, the muscle atrophies and the paddle is excised under local anaesthetic. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 170.1 What you should see The pedicle line drawn from the acromion to the xiphoid, meeting a perpendicular dropped from the midpoint of the clavicle, and a paddle medial to the nipple lying over muscle."],["osec","th:op-170~s3-2","Raise the paddle","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Start with the skin paddle; its perimeter is often enough exposure for the whole harvest. If part of the paddle lies beyond the muscle (a random portion), include the deep fascia just above the intercostal muscles to keep that part alive. What you should see The paddle attached to muscle with no shearing, and the lower and lateral borders of pectoralis major. Shearing the paddle The skin can shear off the muscle. Handle the paddle gently and bevel outward as you dissect a small paddle, which maximises its viability."],["osec","th:op-170~s3-3","Raise the muscle on its pedicle","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Dissect a narrow central strip of muscle containing the pedicle toward the clavicle, using lighted breast retractors or a headlight; a laparoscopic harmonic scalpel gives secure haemostasis. Keeping the lateral remnant preserves the anterior axillary fold. Take the whole muscle when you need it to cover vessels, a pharyngeal suture line or a large defect. Keep the pedicle on the deep surface in view , and feel its pulse. Figure 2 Harvest in a woman during salvage surgery: the paddle from the inframammary fold on a narrow, denervated strip of muscle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 170.3 What you should see The pedicle pulsing on the deep surface of the muscle strip, all the way up to the clavicle."],["osec","th:op-170~s3-4","Ligate branches and divide the nerves","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","At the clavicle, divide and ligate the deltoid and acromial branches of the thoracoacromial trunk: this lengthens the arc and prevents postoperative bleeding. Identify and divide the pectoral nerves so the muscle atrophies and does not twitch in the neck. Figure 3 Pharyngeal leak after salvage laryngectomy and neck dissection for failed chemoradiotherapy: (A) bevelled dissection of a small paddle; (B) the whole muscle used to cover pharynx and neck; (C) radiated neck and chest skin divided completely to keep pressure off the pedicle, the exposed muscle grafted with sheet skin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 170.2 What you should see The pedicle on the deep surface of the muscle in its fascia, with the branches tied and the nerves cut."],["osec","th:op-170~s3-5","Make the tunnel","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Tunnel subcutaneously from the chest to the neck, wide enough to pass four fingers . The clavipectoral fascia, once detached from the clavicle, forms a band that must be released or it will compress the pedicle. Radiated neck skin does not stretch: divide it and skin-graft the muscle rather than force the flap through a tight tunnel. What you should see A tunnel that takes four fingers, with no band across it over the clavicle. A tight tunnel A tight tunnel, a fascial band or stiff radiated skin compresses the pedicle and kills the flap. Make it generous and release what constricts it."],["osec","th:op-170~s3-6","Inset without twisting","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Pass the flap into the neck without twisting the pedicle. Anchor the deep parts to the prevertebral fascia or to structures such as the mandible so that gravity does not pull the flap down and open the suture lines. Avoid excessive traction on the pedicle: design, dissection, inset of the paddle and suture stabilisation of the muscle all help. What you should see A pink paddle with bleeding edges, lying without tension, and a pedicle that is neither twisted nor kinked."],["osec","th:op-170~s3-7","Preserve the next flap and close","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · The operation, stage by stage","Preserve the internal mammary perforators 2, 3 and 4 , to keep a future deltopectoral or internal mammary perforator flap alive. Close most donor sites directly; for larger defects, use negative pressure or staged closure devices. Skin grafts are rarely needed. What you should see The parasternal skin and its perforators untouched, and a chest closed over a drain. Nothing around the neck No ties around the neck after surgery. Cut off the tie openings on the tracheostomy flange and suture it to the neck skin, and post a \"Nothing around neck\" sign over the bed. This applies to free flaps too."],["osec","th:op-170~s4-1","Crisis drills","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · When it goes wrong","Salivary leak or dehiscence after inset 1 Recognise saliva in the drain or wound, breakdown of the suture line 2 Act now drain the saliva and debride 3 Carotid exposed further flap cover is urgent 4 Prevent anchor the flap to prevertebral fascia or mandible avoid excess bulk The paddle is failing 1 Look for compression tunnel, fascial band, radiated skin or a tracheostomy tie 2 Release open the tunnel, remove ties, graft the muscle if the skin will not close 3 Expect partial necrosis mainly in a random part of the paddle beyond the muscle"],["osec","th:op-170~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Too much bulk, so the neck or defect will not close. A tight tunnel compressing the pedicle. A tracheostomy tie compressing the pedicle. Too short a pedicle or arc: measure before you cut, and remember body habitus limits reach. Excess traction on the pedicle. A paddle lying off the muscle, which is lost. Dehiscence from a flap pulled down by gravity."],["osec","th:op-170~s5-1","After the operation","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Aftercare","Control pain, keep the room warm and the patient well hydrated to avoid vasospasm. Look after the tracheostomy so nothing compresses the pedicle. Give perioperative antibiotics, and refer to physiotherapy for the shoulder."],["osec","th:op-170~s5-2","Complications","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Dehiscence and fistula From bulk and tethering by the pedicle; drain and debride; further flaps urgently if the carotid is exposed Partial flap necrosis Usually the random portion of the paddle Shoulder and neck dysfunction Worse if latissimus is weak Bleeding Unligated deltoid and acromial branches Haemopneumothorax Rare Pedicle damage During dissection"],["ev","th:op-170~s5-3","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery: current evidence","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Aftercare","Still useful. The pectoralis major myocutaneous flap remains a reliable option for salvage surgery, patients unfit for free tissue transfer, and to cover the great vessels; partial flap necrosis occurs in about 10 to 15% in series, more in women and the obese. Salvage laryngectomy. A systematic review found that a pectoralis major onlay flap reduced pharyngocutaneous fistula after salvage total laryngectomy (Guimarães et al., Head Neck 2016). Bulk can be a disadvantage in the oral cavity; a muscle-only flap with skin grafting reduces bulk."],["osec","th:op-170~s5-3","Alternatives","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · Aftercare","Free flap reconstruction. Other regional flaps."],["osec","th:op-170~s6-1","Pectoralis or free flap?","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · For the examination","The pectoralis flap is reliable and quicker to raise, but in the comparisons quoted it brings longer hospital stays and higher costs (about 41,000 dollars against 28,500 for a free flap), probably from more partial necrosis, salivary fistula, wound infection and delayed healing: its bulk, its tethering pedicle and a donor site that communicates with the reconstruction all play a part. Delayed healing matters, because radiotherapy should start within 6 weeks and all treatment finish within 100 days . There is a physiological reason too: the pectoralis skin paddle lies at least one angiosome away from its pedicle and is supplied mainly by internal mammary perforators (Taylor and Palmer), whereas a free flap's skin lies in its own pedicle's angiosome."],["osec","th:op-170~s6-2","Viva essentials","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Today's indications: salvage, the vessel-depleted neck, the unfit patient, carotid cover. The pedicle: pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial trunk, its surface marking (Mohrenheim's fossa to xiphoid) and the minor pedicles. Contraindications: Poland syndrome, pacemaker, a paralysed latissimus. Denervate the muscle; ligate the deltoid and acromial branches. A four-finger tunnel; release the clavipectoral band. Keep the paddle over the muscle; distal for reach. Preserve internal mammary perforators 2 to 4 for a deltopectoral flap. Why free flaps are now preferred."],["osec","th:op-170~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Pectoralis Major Musculocutaneous Flaps in Head and Neck Surgery · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not checking for a pacemaker or the state of latissimus. Losing sight of the pedicle. Tracheostomy ties around the neck. Forcing the flap through radiated skin."],["osec","th:op-171~s1-1","The case at a glance","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Operating note","Cervicofacial region flaps Summary Flap types Random pattern: cervicofacial. Axial pattern: cervicopectoral, supraclavicular, deltopectoral, submental Cervicofacial Cheek and lateral face defects, especially thin superficial ones; not if its blood supply is doubtful (size, base, radiotherapy, surgery, vascular disease) or the patient cannot accept a revision Supraclavicular Neck, lateral skull base, floor of mouth and lower face; pharyngectomy defects; caution after level IV or supraclavicular neck dissection (the perforator may be gone) Submental island Facial defects needing colour match and some bulk; oral cavity, oropharynx and laryngectomy defects; lateral and anterior skull base and orbit; not after neck surgery that divided the submental perforators, nor with bulky level Ib nodes or possible extracapsular spread Anaesthesia General; paralysis at the surgeon's discretion depending on the nerves at risk; facial nerve monitoring when branches are at risk Position Supine, shoulder roll or neck turned away during elevation; remove rolls and return to neutral if the closure is tight Antibiotics Depending on the defect and risk factors Tray Head and neck set; bipolar; sterile Doppler; fluorescent angiography (SPY) to predict viability"],["osec","th:op-171~s1-2","Consent","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to close the defect with nearby skin of matching colour and texture, carried on its own blood supply. Part of the flap may fail , especially its tip; if you smoke, stop for at least 2 weeks beforehand. A fold of skin (dog ear) may be left and trimmed at a later small operation. A collection of blood or fluid under the flap, which may need draining. Facial weakness (lower lip or face) if a facial nerve branch is injured; shoulder weakness if the accessory nerve is injured during a muscle flap. Infection , especially from a saliva leak. Scars on the neck, chest or shoulder."],["osec","th:op-171~s1-3","Before you start","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Operating note","Define the defect and its tissues: replace like with like, skin with skin of similar colour, thickness and texture, multiple layers with a flap carrying the same layers. Assess swallowing and breathing, and the possible need for a tracheostomy and nasogastric feeding. Ask about previous head and neck surgery and radiotherapy, diabetes (especially if poorly controlled), peripheral vascular disease, coagulopathy (also in the family), drugs that increase bleeding (a haematoma threatens the flap), and nicotine , which constricts arteries and must stop around surgery. Examine the face and neck for scars or earlier surgery that may have cut the blood supply, and measure the defect. For a submental flap , do the pinch test with the neck slightly extended to see how much skin can be taken while still closing the donor site without limiting head extension. For a supraclavicular flap , a Doppler must trace the pedicle from the supraclavicular fossa over the clavicle to the deltoid; mark the signals. Contrast CT can show whether pedicle vessels survive after previous surgery; CT angiography or angiography is rarely needed."],["osec","th:op-171~s2-1","Cervicofacial flap","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Anatomy you must see","A random-pattern fasciocutaneous flap of facial and cervical skin, better called a local than a regional flap. It moves by rotation as well as advancement , needs a broad base to recruit enough vessels, and is weakest at its distal edges."],["osec","th:op-171~s2-2","Supraclavicular flap","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Anatomy you must see","Its cutaneous perforator, from the transverse cervical artery, lies in the supraclavicular triangle , bounded by the anterior border of trapezius behind, the posterior border of sternomastoid in front and the clavicle below. The perforator is the fulcrum of rotation; beyond the deltoid tip the supply becomes random. The cephalic vein lies on its deep surface."],["osec","th:op-171~s2-3","Submental island flap","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Anatomy you must see","The submental artery arises from the facial artery deep to or within the submandibular gland, runs forward over the gland on the superficial surface of mylohyoid , and sends perforators up on both sides of the anterior belly of digastric . The submental vein is more variable: usually a branch of the facial vein, less often draining to the external jugular, and always superficial and lateral to the artery . Losing the vein during exposure can doom the flap. The marginal mandibular nerve lies over the facial vessels and gland."],["osec","th:op-171~s3-1","Design","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · The operation: cervicofacial flap","Start the design at the posterior edge of the defect . Carry the upper limb back to the preauricular region and around the lobule into the postauricular tissue (to increase the arc), down to the mastoid tip, then down the neck about 2 cm behind the anterior edge of trapezius , turning forward in a natural crease at the level of the clavicle. Size the flap to the defect, reassessing during surgery until it rotates without tension. Figure 1 A right anterior cheek defect after skin cancer excision: (A) the design, continued around the lobule and down to the clavicle; (B) the incisions; (C) elevation in the subcutaneous plane to avoid the facial nerve; (D) the result, with the postauricular limb and the dog ear excised and a drain in place. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 171.1 What you should see A flap outline that begins at the defect's posterior edge and reaches it when rotated on paper."],["osec","th:op-171~s3-2","Raise and rotate","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · The operation: cervicofacial flap","Raise the flap below the platysma in the neck and above the SMAS in the face , protecting the facial nerve branches. Rotate and advance it into the defect. What you should see The flap in the defect without tension or blanching at its tip, facial nerve branches untouched beneath the SMAS. The standing cone Rotation leaves a standing cone at the base. If you have any doubt about the flap's blood supply, leave it , and remove it at a later stage once the wound edges have revascularised the flap: excising it now can cut the supply to the distal flap."],["osec","th:op-171~s3-3","Drain and dress","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · The operation: cervicofacial flap","Almost always place a drain; a haematoma under the flap compromises it. A light compressive dressing keeps the flap on its bed, especially without a drain. Figure 2 The final appearance after a cervicofacial rotation-advancement flap: an excellent colour and texture match. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 171.2 What you should see The flap lying flat on its bed with the drain working."],["osec","th:op-171~s4-1","Design","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · The operation: supraclavicular flap","Design an elongated ellipse over the supraclavicular region toward the deltoid tip , around the Doppler-mapped pedicle. A width of 6 to 8 cm usually allows primary closure; 10 to 16 cm can be raised reliably but the donor site will need a skin graft. It can be up to 26 cm long, but extending beyond the deltoid tip makes the supply random: extend no more than 5 cm beyond the last Doppler signal . Figure 3 A right supraclavicular flap for a floor of mouth defect: (A) the pedicle mapped by Doppler from the triangle between sternomastoid, clavicle and trapezius over the shoulder to the deltoid tip; (B) the flap raised, the donor edges held with towel clips for primary closure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 171.3 What you should see A Doppler signal in the triangle between the sternocleidomastoid, the clavicle and the external jugular vein, with the paddle axis running from it to the deltoid."],["osec","th:op-171~s4-2","Raise","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · The operation: supraclavicular flap","Raise from distal to proximal in the subfascial plane, keeping a skin base or making it an island on the perforator for maximum arc. Protect the cephalic vein. Diathermy is fine distally, but release the fibrous attachments to the clavicle and trapezius sharply . Close the donor site primarily after undermining in most cases, and drain it. What you should see The pedicle entering the flap's deep surface proximally, and a flap that reaches the defect without stretching."],["osec","th:op-171~s5-1","Design","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · The operation: submental island flap","Choose fasciocutaneous, myocutaneous (including ipsilateral mylohyoid and anterior digastric, which simplifies the dissection and protects the pedicle on the mylohyoid), more bulk (adding the opposite mylohyoid and digastric), or osteomyocutaneous (with the lower mentum on midline bony perforators, useful for small maxillary bony defects). Find the dominant perforators by Doppler near the digastric insertions. Place the upper incision over the lower border of the mandible , not below it (which endangers the perforators), extending as far as needed (it can run angle to angle). Use the pinch test to set the lower limit. Paddles up to about 17 × 9 cm survive on one pedicle; the average pedicle length of 8 cm reaches from the zygoma to the clavicle. What you should see A paddle whose lower edge can be pinched closed, with a Doppler signal beneath it."],["osec","th:op-171~s5-2","Raise","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · The operation: submental island flap","Open the medial upper incision onto the mandible in the midline; free the flap subperiosteally with a Cottle elevator and release the anterior digastric(s) from the mandible. Open the ipsilateral incision to the lateral limit, down to platysma; divide the posterior platysma to expose the submandibular gland. Find the marginal mandibular nerve over the facial vessels and gland, and keeping it in view divide the platysma above to join the two dissections; sweep the nerve up off the gland fascia and out of the field. Find the pedicle at the deep posterior part of the gland, the vein superficial and lateral to the artery, draining to the common facial or external jugular vein. Trace the vessels through the gland, dividing the branches to the gland, keeping a broad fascial cuff from pedicle to skin that carries the perforators. Keep or remove the gland (if removing it, free it from the hypoglossal and lingual nerves and divide the duct). Follow the pedicle over mylohyoid, clearing level I nodes and fat in cancer surgery; if level I clearance is not needed, leave the fat around the pedicle. To include mylohyoid, release it from the mylohyoid line and the hyoid. Release the digastric from the hyoid and free the opposite side, above the platysma if no bulk is needed (protecting that side's nerves), or including platysma, mylohyoid and digastric for bulk. Inset; ligate the facial artery beyond the submental origin to raise perfusion pressure. Drain the submental area, where seromas are common. Figure 4 A submental island flap for a lateral temporal bone defect: (A) the tumour and th"],["osec","th:op-171~s6-1","Crisis drills","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · When it goes wrong","Arterial compromise 1 Cause compression from poor vessel geometry, a stretched pedicle or a haematoma 2 Act return to theatre to explore the pedicle and restore flow Venous compromise (commoner) 1 Cause kinking or compression from geometry or haematoma 2 Act return to theatre to explore and restore outflow 3 If flow cannot be restored leeches and heparin scrub may salvage the flap Haematoma or seroma 1 Haematoma find and evacuate it quickly, and secure haemostasis 2 Seroma usually needle aspiration in clinic an undrained seroma can cause infection and flap loss"],["osec","th:op-171~s6-2","Common errors in technique","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Excising the standing cone when the flap's supply is in doubt. An upper submental incision placed below the mandible. Thinning the fascia over the pedicle. Doing the neck dissection before raising the submental flap. Extending a supraclavicular flap more than 5 cm beyond the Doppler signal."],["osec","th:op-171~s7-1","After the operation","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Aftercare","Close monitoring of pedicled flaps is debatable, since pedicle compromise is unlikely; check Doppler, capillary refill, colour and warmth as you judge. Keep drains until under 30 mL in 24 hours and clear. No activity limits; analgesia as needed."],["osec","th:op-171~s7-2","Complications","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Arterial or venous compromise Venous is commoner; explore early Infection Usually late (after day 5), from a salivary leak; nil by mouth and oral care help Poor microcirculation Obesity, diabetes, vascular disease, radiotherapy Haematoma Evacuate quickly Seroma Aspirate Nerve injury Facial branches (cervicofacial, submental), accessory nerve (sternomastoid flap)"],["ev","th:op-171~s7-3","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps: current evidence","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Aftercare","Regional flaps revisited. The submental island, supraclavicular artery island and facial artery musculomucosal flaps give results comparable to free flaps for selected small to medium defects in published series, with shorter operating time and fewer medical complications in older or frail patients. Oncological caution. A submental flap carries the theoretical risk of moving level I nodal disease into the defect, so it is avoided when level I nodes are involved. Evidence is from retrospective comparative series."],["osec","th:op-171~s7-3","Alternatives","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · Aftercare","Secondary healing; skin grafts; other regional flaps (pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi); free flaps for larger, complex defects, at the cost of longer operations."],["osec","th:op-171~s8-1","Regional flap or free flap?","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · For the examination","Comparisons of the submental flap with radial forearm and anterolateral thigh free flaps for glossectomy and lateral skull base defects found shorter operations and hospital stays with the submental flap, with no difference in function or flap survival. The supraclavicular flap has likewise been compared with free flaps for hemiglossectomy and lateral skull base defects. The editor notes that these flaps serve especially well in vessel-depleted necks and patients who cannot tolerate long anaesthetics."],["osec","th:op-171~s8-2","Viva essentials","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Random versus axial pattern; examples of each. The cervicofacial flap's design, planes and the standing cone. The supraclavicular triangle; flap width and the 5 cm Doppler rule. The submental pedicle: artery on mylohyoid, vein superficial and lateral; the marginal nerve. Why the submental flap is raised before the neck dissection. Managing arterial and venous compromise; leeches."],["osec","th:op-171~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Cervicofacial Transposition Flaps · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a smoker without a nicotine-free fortnight. Missing the effect of previous level IV or level Ib surgery on the pedicle. Closing a donor site under tension instead of grafting it."],["osec","th:op-173~s1-1","The case at a glance","Skin Grafting · Operating note","Skin grafting in the head and neck Summary Indications Defects where direct closure or a flap would distort a free margin (eyelid, ala, lip), cause donor morbidity, or be impossible; oral, oropharyngeal and maxillectomy lining; after Mohs excision; patients unfit for long operations Full-thickness (FTSG) Face (nose, eyelids, ear); donors: postauricular, pre-auricular, supraclavicular, upper eyelid, Burow's triangle from a flap Split-thickness (STSG) Large defects, scalp, mucosal lining, mastoid cavities, muscle flaps; donor: thigh, buttock or arm, by dermatome (about 0.3 to 0.45 mm) Will not take on Bare bone without periosteum, cartilage without perichondrium, tendon without paratenon, irradiated or infected beds (use a flap) Anaesthesia Local or general Tray Dermatome (with blades, mineral oil), mesher, scalpel, scissors, bolster materials or negative-pressure dressing, sutures"],["osec","th:op-173~s1-2","Consent","Skin Grafting · Operating note","What to tell the patient Graft failure in part or whole, needing dressings or regrafting. Colour and texture mismatch; split grafts can look shiny or pale. Contraction, especially of split grafts. Donor site pain and healing over 2 to 3 weeks (split) or a linear scar (full thickness). Infection, haematoma."],["osec","th:op-173~s1-3","Before you start","Skin Grafting · Operating note","Choose the graft by the site and the bed. Match colour and texture for the face (postauricular skin for the nose tip, upper eyelid skin for the lower lid). Make sure the bed bleeds and is free of infection."],["osec","th:op-173~s2-1","Prepare the bed","Skin Grafting · The operation, stage by stage","Achieve haemostasis with care (no char), excise granulation if heavy, and make sure perichondrium, periosteum or paratenon covers exposed cartilage or bone. Make a template of the defect. What you should see A healthy, bleeding, flat bed without exposed avascular tissue."],["osec","th:op-173~s2-2","Harvest a full-thickness graft","Skin Grafting · The operation, stage by stage","Cut the template shape from the donor site, defat the underside completely with curved scissors until the dermis is white and shiny, and close the donor site directly. What you should see A thin graft with no fat on its underside."],["osec","th:op-173~s2-3","Harvest a split-thickness graft","Skin Grafting · The operation, stage by stage","Lubricate the donor skin with mineral oil and keep it taut. Hold the dermatome at about 45 degrees, set the thickness and width, and advance it steadily with even pressure. Mesh the graft if it must cover a large or uneven area or let fluid escape. Figure 1 The air-powered dermatome. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 173.1 Figure 2 Harvesting from a lubricated, tensioned donor site. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 173.2 Figure 3 Meshing the graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 173.3 What you should see Fine pinpoint bleeding on the donor site (correct depth) and a translucent, even graft. Upside down A graft placed epidermis down will not take. The dermal side is shiny and curls inwards; check before you lay it on the bed."],["osec","th:op-173~s2-4","Inset","Skin Grafting · The operation, stage by stage","Lay the graft dermal side down, trim it to fit, and suture it edge to edge without stretching (a tented graft does not touch its bed). Make small pie-crust holes in a sheet graft to let blood escape. What you should see Graft edges matching the wound edges, lying flat on the bed throughout."],["osec","th:op-173~s2-5","Immobilise","Skin Grafting · The operation, stage by stage","Apply a tie-over bolster (cotton or foam with long sutures tied over it) or a negative-pressure dressing for irregular or mobile sites, kept for about 5 to 7 days. Dress the donor site with a moist, non-adherent dressing. What you should see The graft pressed evenly onto the bed with no movement. Senior's pearl Shear and fluid are what kill grafts. If a graft fails, look for movement or a haematoma before blaming the bed."],["osec","th:op-173~s3-1","Crisis drills","Skin Grafting · When it goes wrong","Graft dusky or lifted at the first dressing 1 Act evacuate any haematoma or seroma through a small cut re-bolster 2 Lost dressings until granulation regraft or flap"],["osec","th:op-173~s4-1","After surgery","Skin Grafting · Aftercare","Remove the bolster at 5 to 7 days; moisturise and protect from sun; massage once healed. Donor sites heal in about 2 weeks."],["ev","th:op-173~s4-2","Skin Grafting: current evidence","Skin Grafting · Aftercare","A meta-analysis of 12 studies (653 patients) found negative-pressure wound therapy increased split-thickness graft take and reduced reoperation compared with conventional dressings, without a significant difference in infection (Yin et al., Int J Surg 2018). In irregular, mobile areas, a randomised trial found better take with negative pressure than with tie-over bolsters (Cao et al., Plast Reconstr Surg 2022)."],["osec","th:op-173~s4-2","Complications","Skin Grafting · Aftercare","Graft loss (haematoma, seroma, shear, infection, poor bed), contraction, colour mismatch, hypertrophic scarring, donor site pain or infection. Figure 4 Recipient and donor sites at 4 weeks, and a healed graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 173.4 and 173.5"],["osec","th:op-173~s5-1","Where the books differ","Skin Grafting · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers full- and split-thickness grafts, dermatome technique, meshing, bolsters, and grafts combined with flaps (Burow grafts) to avoid distortion. K. J. Lee the stages of take (imbibition, inosculation, revascularisation) and primary versus secondary contraction. The literature negative-pressure dressings improve take, especially on irregular areas."],["osec","th:op-173~s5-2","Viva essentials","Skin Grafting · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Full versus split thickness : when and why. Phases of graft take. Primary and secondary contraction. Causes of failure. Beds that will not accept a graft."],["osec","th:op-173~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Skin Grafting · For the examination","Common pitfalls Grafting onto bare cartilage or bone. Leaving fat on a full-thickness graft. Placing the graft upside down. Inadequate immobilisation."],["osec","th:op-18~s1-1","The case at a glance","Bronchoscopy · Operating note","Bronchoscopy Summary Indications: diagnostic Unexplained cough, wheeze or haemoptysis; suspected malignancy (endobronchial biopsy, endobronchial ultrasound needle aspiration of mediastinal and hilar nodes, navigational biopsy of peripheral nodules); localised collapse, persistent opacity or obstructive emphysema on chest radiograph; vocal cord palsy; washings and biopsies in interstitial disease or the immunocompromised; staging of head and neck cancer Indications: therapeutic Removal of foreign bodies; clearing clot in haemoptysis, isolating the bleeding lung with a balloon, laser to a bleeding tumour; debriding obstructing tumour and placing stents; clearing retained secretions; draining a lung abscess; brachytherapy catheters; guiding difficult intubations and confirming double-lumen tubes Contraindications For rigid bronchoscopy: unfit for general anaesthesia, immobile neck. For flexible bronchoscopy: massive haemoptysis (the channel cannot clear the blood); high-grade tracheal or bilateral main-bronchus obstruction or a large mediastinal mass, where mask ventilation after induction may fail. Laser in a patient needing high inspired oxygen (fire) Anaesthesia Flexible: topical with or without sedation, or general through a tube. Rigid: general, with spontaneous ventilation in critical obstruction and inhaled foreign bodies, or jet, intermittent or continuous ventilation through the scope Position Supine on a head ring, neck extended so the chin points to the ceiling; start sitting up in a patient in severe distress Antibiotics Not required Monitoring Continuous oximetry "],["osec","th:op-18~s1-2","Consent","Bronchoscopy · Operating note","What to tell the patient or parents Sore throat and a hoarse voice for a day or two. Injury to teeth, lips, tongue or throat ; rarely to the vocal cords or an arytenoid. Swelling of the airway after the procedure, especially in small children, which may need steroids, a period of ventilation or, rarely, a tracheostomy. Bleeding from biopsy sites, occasionally heavy. A tear in the airway with air in the chest (pneumothorax or pneumomediastinum), especially with dilatation. Low oxygen levels and, rarely, cardiac arrest. For a foreign body: it may break up or move, and more than one procedure may be needed. The possibility of intensive care and ventilation afterwards in serious airway cases."],["osec","th:op-18~s1-3","Before you start","Bronchoscopy · Operating note","Haemoptysis: how much, bright or dark, preceding episodes, infection or excess sputum, anticoagulants, smoking and lung cancer risk. Airway obstruction: breathlessness at rest or on exertion, positional symptoms, onset and progression, previous intubation (how long) or tracheostomy, granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Foreign body: a choking episode, witnessed or not, followed by cough, wheeze or unexplained pneumonia; in a toddler the history of choking is the most reliable clue. Examine the cardiorespiratory state, the body habitus and how far the neck extends, stridor (inspiratory or expiratory) and wheeze (unilateral wheeze or reduced air entry suggests a bronchial foreign body), neck nodes, and loose teeth. A chest radiograph shows tracheal narrowing or deviation, collapse, consolidation, masses and, in an inhaled foreign body, obstructive emphysema (hyperinflation on expiration films or lateral decubitus films) or collapse; most inhaled foreign bodies (nuts, food) are radiolucent. CT shows the airway calibre, intrinsic and extrinsic lesions, foreign bodies, lymph nodes and, with contrast, the bleeding vessel in haemoptysis. Resuscitate and stabilise, correct clotting with vitamin K and blood products, have the imaging on screen in theatre, and make sure the patient and family understand the gravity, including the possibility of postoperative ventilation."],["osec","th:op-18~s2-1","The route","Bronchoscopy · Anatomy you must see","The scope passes behind the tongue in the midline, under the epiglottis , through the glottis (a vertical slit seen end-on), the subglottis (narrowest part of a child's airway, the cricoid ring), and into the trachea , about 10 to 12 cm long in adults, with the carina at the level of the sternal angle (about 25 cm from the incisors in an adult). The right main bronchus is shorter, wider and more vertical, which is why inhaled foreign bodies favour it in adults and older children; its upper lobe bronchus leaves almost at once. The left main bronchus is longer and more horizontal, passing under the aortic arch."],["osec","th:op-18~s2-2","Matching the scope to the axis","Bronchoscopy · Anatomy you must see","To look down the right bronchial tree, turn the patient's head to the left , and vice versa, so the scope's axis lies along the bronchus. Know each lobar and segmental orifice and examine them in order on both sides."],["osec","th:op-18~s2-3","Paediatric sizes","Bronchoscopy · Anatomy you must see","Choose the bronchoscope by age (as a rough guide to the internal diameter: 3.0 mm for a term newborn, 3.5 to 3.7 mm up to a year, 4.0 mm at one to two years, 5.0 mm at three to four years and 6.0 mm from about five to seven years; check your own set's chart); the scope must pass the cricoid without force. Keep the procedure short in infants (Dhingra advises not beyond about 20 minutes) to avoid subglottic oedema."],["osec","th:op-18~s3-1","The four ways to ventilate through a rigid scope","Bronchoscopy · Ventilating a shared airway","Spontaneous ventilation: anaesthesia light enough for the patient to breathe until the scope is in and the airway secure. Safest in high-grade obstruction, large mediastinal masses and inhaled foreign bodies (positive pressure can push a foreign body further down), but without relaxation the passage may be harder. Intermittent ventilation: the circuit is attached to the scope between looks; the mouth and nose may need occluding or the pharynx packing to stop the leak. You cannot look or work while ventilating. Continuous ventilation: a glass window closes the viewing end and the circuit attaches to a side port, so you can look while ventilating, but opening the port to pass an instrument breaks the circuit. High-frequency jet ventilation: the preferred and commonest technique; the jet attaches to a Luer connector, needs no closed circuit and allows continuous work. Typical settings: 100 to 120 breaths per minute, driving pressure 15 to 25 psi, inspiratory time about 40%. Figure 1 Left: the rigid scope connected to the ventilating circuit with an occluding window. Centre and right: a jet ventilator attached to the scope, and its controls (rate, driving pressure, inspiratory time). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 18.4 and 18.5 and 18.6 Leaving the scope in If the airway is still too narrow for a tube at the end, a proximal stent has been placed, or the larynx may obstruct, keep the patient ventilated through the rigid scope until the anaesthetic has worn off and they breathe spontaneously, then remove it."],["osec","th:op-18~s4-1","Prepare and position","Bronchoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Protect the eyes and place a rubber guard over the upper teeth or gums. Put the head on a ring and extend the neck so the chin points vertically . Suction saliva from the pharynx with a Yankauer so the epiglottis and cords are clear. Check the light, the telescope fits, and the jet or circuit connections before you start. Sit on an adjustable stool. Figure 2 A rigid bronchoscope with its light source, and small and large bronchoscopes. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 18.1 and 18.7 What you should see A clear pharynx, the guard in place, and the scope, telescope and ventilation connections tested."],["osec","th:op-18~s4-2","Enter behind the tongue and find the epiglottis","Bronchoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Hold the scope vertically and look through it. Place your left thumb over the upper teeth or gums to guide the scope and protect them. Advance in the midline behind the tongue, then drop the proximal end of the scope downward to lift the tongue and bring the epiglottis into view. Figure 3 Passing the rigid scope through the mouth, the left thumb protecting the upper teeth. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 18.8 What you should see The tip of the epiglottis in the midline. The teeth Never lever the scope on the upper teeth; the thumb is the fulcrum. This is the commonest error."],["osec","th:op-18~s4-3","Expose the glottis","Bronchoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Pass the tip behind the epiglottis and lift the scope anteriorly to show the arytenoids, the cords and the glottic opening; by now the scope lies nearly horizontal. External pressure on the larynx helps an anterior larynx. Alternatively, expose the glottis with a laryngoscope in your left hand and pass the bronchoscope through it with your right (useful in infants and in adults with a short neck or bulky tongue), then withdraw the laryngoscope. What you should see The arytenoids, both cords and the dark glottic opening."],["osec","th:op-18~s4-4","Rotate and pass the cords","Bronchoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Advance toward the glottis, then rotate the scope 90 degrees so its bevelled tip lies in the line of the vertical glottic slit, pass between the cords into the trachea, and rotate back. Never force a closed glottis. Adjust the table and stool so you are comfortable, and start ventilation through the scope. What you should see Tracheal rings ahead, and the chest moving with ventilation. The cords and the subglottis Forcing an oversized scope through the glottis or a child's cricoid causes oedema, arytenoid dislocation and subglottic stenosis. Choose the size before you start; repeated insertions make it worse."],["osec","th:op-18~s4-5","Examine the tree and act","Bronchoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Advance along the trachea to the carina, then into each main bronchus, turning the head away from the side you examine. Examine every lobar and segmental orifice in turn, with a rod-lens telescope (0, 30 or 90 degrees) through the scope, or pass a flexible bronchoscope through it for the distal airways. Take washings and biopsies, suction secretions and clot, and treat as planned. Draw the scope back slowly at the end, examining the trachea, larynx and pharynx. What you should see Every segmental orifice on both sides, and the lesion's site and length recorded."],["osec","th:op-18~s5-1","Technique","Bronchoscopy · Retrieving a foreign body","Keep the child breathing spontaneously under deep inhalational anaesthesia with topical lidocaine to the cords, and ventilate through the side arm. Locate the object with the telescope, clear secretions around it, then use optical (telescopic) forceps , which let you see as you grasp, or a basket for round objects; a Fogarty balloon passed beyond it can draw a smooth object back. Pause ventilation while grasping so it does not move. Objects smaller than the scope's lumen come out through it. Larger ones are drawn up against the tip and the scope, forceps and object are withdrawn together as one unit, slowly through the cords; if it is lost in the subglottis, push it back into a main bronchus to restore the airway and try again. Afterwards, re-examine the whole tree for fragments and a second object. Losing it in the subglottis A foreign body stripped off at the cords can impact in the subglottis and obstruct the airway completely. Push it down into a bronchus to ventilate the other lung, then retrieve it again. Senior's pearl Vegetable foreign bodies (peanuts) swell and fragment, and cause a chemical bronchitis; take every piece. If the history is convincing, bronchoscope even when the chest radiograph is normal."],["osec","th:op-18~s6-1","Flexible bronchoscopy","Bronchoscopy · Flexible bronchoscopy and newer techniques","Done under topical anaesthesia (nebulised lidocaine, a spray to the oropharynx, lidocaine dropped on the cords and into the bronchi through the scope, or a transtracheal injection) with or without sedation, or through an endotracheal tube or tracheostomy under general anaesthesia. It reaches segmental and subsegmental bronchi and suits patients with stiff necks or jaw problems and the critically ill at the bedside, but its working channel is small, so heavy bleeding can overwhelm it. In severe obstruction, examine with topical anaesthetic alone and no sedation."],["osec","th:op-18~s6-2","Endobronchial ultrasound and navigation","Bronchoscopy · Flexible bronchoscopy and newer techniques","Endobronchial ultrasound shows mediastinal and hilar nodes in real time for transbronchial needle aspiration (lung cancer staging, sarcoidosis, lymphoma, infection); you must know their relation to the great vessels on ultrasound. Electromagnetic navigational bronchoscopy uses the patient's CT as a virtual map to steer a catheter to peripheral nodules for biopsy, with a risk of pneumothorax. Both are usually done under general anaesthesia so the patient does not move or cough. Figure 4 Left: flexible bronchoscopes, from top: endobronchial ultrasound, therapeutic, adult and paediatric. Right: the ultrasound probe at the tip of an endobronchial ultrasound scope. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 18.2 and 18.3"],["osec","th:op-18~s7-1","Crisis drills","Bronchoscopy · When it goes wrong","Massive haemoptysis 1 Position bleeding side down to protect the good lung 2 Rigid, not flexible rigid scope for suction capacity clear the clot 3 Isolate balloon catheter into the bleeding bronchus, or intubate the good main bronchus 4 Treat laser or topical adrenaline for a visible source interventional radiology (bronchial artery embolisation) or surgery Cannot ventilate at induction in critical obstruction 1 Prevent keep spontaneous breathing rigid scope ready and opened surgeon in the room at induction 2 Act pass the rigid scope immediately through the obstruction and ventilate through it 3 Last resort in very severe obstruction, establish extracorporeal support (veno-venous ECMO or bypass) before any anaesthesia Stridor after bronchoscopy in a child 1 Recognise subglottic oedema inspiratory stridor and suprasternal recession 2 Act humidified oxygen, nebulised adrenaline, intravenous dexamethasone 3 Escalate reintubate with a smaller tube tracheostomy if it persists Pneumothorax or surgical emphysema 1 Recognise rising airway pressures, falling saturation, reduced breath sounds, crepitus 2 Act chest radiograph needle decompression and chest drain for tension"],["osec","th:op-18~s8-1","After the procedure","Bronchoscopy · Aftercare","Recover the patient in the recovery room or intensive care, watching for airway compromise from laryngeal oedema or injury to the tongue and pharynx. Humidified air helps. Obtain a chest radiograph after rigid bronchoscopy to exclude pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum, especially after dilatation or foreign body removal."],["ev","th:op-18~s8-2","Bronchoscopy: current evidence","Bronchoscopy · Aftercare","Guideline. The British Thoracic Society guideline for diagnostic flexible bronchoscopy recommends sedation with titration, monitoring, topical lidocaine up to 8.2 mg/kg in adults, and caution with sedation in patients with severe lung disease (Du Rand et al., Thorax 2013). Airway foreign bodies in adults are often retrieved flexibly; rigid bronchoscopy is kept for large, sharp or impacted objects and for children. Endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) sampling of mediastinal nodes has replaced many mediastinoscopies for staging."],["osec","th:op-18~s8-2","Complications","Bronchoscopy · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Injury to teeth and lips Levering the scope Pharyngeal and tongue lacerations, contusion, oedema Rigid scope passage Vocal cord injury, arytenoid dislocation Forced passage Laryngeal or subglottic oedema Children, oversized scope, repeated insertions, long procedures Haemorrhage overwhelming suction, hypoxaemia Especially with flexible bronchoscopy Pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, surgical emphysema Airway injury, dilatation, transbronchial biopsy Hypoxia, arrhythmia, cardiac arrest Shared airway, inadequate ventilation"],["osec","th:op-18~s9-1","Where the books differ","Bronchoscopy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra lists diagnostic indications including cough or wheeze for more than four weeks, atelectasis, localised opacity, obstructive emphysema and vocal cord palsy; prefers general anaesthesia without an endotracheal tube; describes direct passage and passage through a laryngoscope (for infants and short necks), rotating the scope 90 degrees clockwise at the glottis, turning the head to the opposite side to enter each bronchus, and warns not to exceed about 20 minutes in children, not to force a closed glottis, and to avoid repeated insertions. Lee concentrates on paediatric foreign bodies: the history, radiographic signs, optical forceps and the ventilating bronchoscope. Myers covers the modern repertoire: jet ventilation, rigid bronchoscopy for haemoptysis and obstruction, endobronchial ultrasound and navigational bronchoscopy."],["osec","th:op-18~s9-2","Viva essentials","Bronchoscopy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Rigid versus flexible: what each does best, and why rigid for massive haemoptysis . The four ventilation options through a rigid scope and why spontaneous breathing for a foreign body. The passage: chin vertical, thumb on the teeth, lift the epiglottis, rotate 90 degrees at the glottis. Why foreign bodies go to the right , and the radiograph signs (obstructive emphysema). Withdrawing a large foreign body with the scope as one unit; losing it in the subglottis. Post-bronchoscopy stridor in a child."],["osec","th:op-18~s9-3","Common pitfalls","Bronchoscopy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Levering on the upper teeth. Inadequate topical anaesthesia or depth before passing the scope. An oversized scope forced through a child's cricoid. Using a flexible scope in massive haemoptysis. Paralysing and positive-pressure ventilating a child with a ball-valve foreign body. No chest radiograph after rigid bronchoscopy."],["osec","th:op-180~s1-1","The case at a glance","Facial Reanimation · Operating note","Facial reanimation Summary Under about 12 to 24 months, muscles alive (fibrillations on EMG), proximal nerve available Primary repair or interposition graft (greater auricular, sural) Muscles alive, no proximal nerve (after cerebellopontine angle surgery, trauma) Nerve transfer: hypoglossal-facial (full, split or jump graft) or masseteric-facial; cross-facial nerve graft for spontaneity Muscles atrophied (long-standing palsy, congenital) Muscle transfer: gracilis free flap (masseteric or cross-facial innervation) or temporalis transposition (lengthening myoplasty) Any stage, or as an adjunct Static support: fascia lata sling for the mouth corner, alar suspension for the nasal airway, brow lift, eyelid weight and lower lid tightening Lower lip only Digastric transfer, or weakening the opposite depressor Anaesthesia General"],["osec","th:op-180~s1-2","Consent","Facial Reanimation · Operating note","What to tell the patient Reinnervation takes 4 to 12 months to show movement; free muscle transfer takes longer. No technique gives a normal face: expect synkinesis after nerve repair and a smile that may need effort (masseteric) rather than being spontaneous. Hypoglossal transfer: weakness and wasting of one side of the tongue (less with the split or jump graft techniques). Masseteric transfer: smiling by clenching the teeth, which most learn to do automatically. Static slings stretch and may need tightening; synthetic materials can become infected or extrude. Donor site scars and numbness (neck, leg, thigh)."],["osec","th:op-180~s1-3","Before you start","Facial Reanimation · Operating note","Establish the cause, the duration, the status of the proximal nerve, and whether the muscles are alive (needle EMG fibrillations or voluntary potentials). Grade the face (House-Brackmann or Sunnybrook) region by region, photograph and video it. Examine the eye: closure, Bell's phenomenon, corneal sensation, lower lid laxity. Check the nose for valve collapse. Check the donor nerves (tongue, masseter bulk on clenching). Figure 1 A static fascia lata suspension of the mouth corner and alar base. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 180.1"],["osec","th:op-180~s2-1","Protect the eye first","Facial Reanimation · The operation, stage by stage","Lubricants and taping at night; a gold or platinum weight in the upper lid for lagophthalmos; lateral canthal tightening for an everted lower lid. These come before or with any reanimation. What you should see A cornea covered on gentle closure, without exposure keratitis."],["osec","th:op-180~s2-2","Hypoglossal-facial transfer","Facial Reanimation · The operation, stage by stage","Through a modified Blair (parotidectomy) incision, find the facial nerve trunk at the stylomastoid foramen and follow it to just before the pes anserinus; divide it there, keeping a strip of deep epineurium to stop it retracting. Find the hypoglossal nerve between the internal jugular vein and carotid deep to the digastric, divide it just beyond the descendens hypoglossi, bring the proximal end medial to the digastric for length, match the fascicles and repair with fine perineurial sutures without tension. Figure 2 Before and after hypoglossal-facial transposition. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 180.3 What you should see A tension-free coaptation of matched nerve ends under the microscope. Senior's pearl To spare the tongue, use a partial (split) hypoglossal or an interposition \"jump\" graft from a nick in the hypoglossal to the facial nerve, or use the masseteric nerve instead. The masseteric nerve, found in the subzygomatic triangle, reaches the facial nerve branches directly and gives a strong smile."],["osec","th:op-180~s2-3","Interposition and cross-facial grafts","Facial Reanimation · The operation, stage by stage","For a gap, harvest the greater auricular nerve (across sternomastoid, parallel to the external jugular vein) or the sural nerve for longer grafts. A cross-facial nerve graft tunnels a sural nerve from a redundant buccal branch on the normal side across the upper lip, to power the paralysed side or a later muscle flap, giving a spontaneous but weaker smile. What you should see Grafts lying loosely in their beds with matched fascicles at both ends."],["osec","th:op-180~s2-4","Free gracilis transfer","Facial Reanimation · The operation, stage by stage","Harvest a segment of gracilis with its pedicle (from the profunda femoris vessels) and its branch of the obturator nerve. Inset it in the cheek from the modiolus and lip to the zygoma and temporal fascia along the vector of the normal smile, anastomose the vessels (usually to the facial vessels) and the nerve to the masseteric branch, a cross-facial graft, or both. Figure 3 Muscle transposition for lip depressor paralysis (digastric). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 180.4 What you should see A muscle under appropriate tension, perfused, with its nerve coapted."],["osec","th:op-180~s2-5","Static sling for the mouth corner","Facial Reanimation · The operation, stage by stage","Measure from the zygomatic arch to the modiolus and harvest a 2.5 cm strip of fascia lata. Through a nasolabial or vermilion incision and a temporal hairline incision, tunnel between the subcutaneous fat and the SMAS. Split the distal end and fix it to the upper and lower lip and the modiolus with permanent sutures, then pull the sling up and out along the vector of the normal smile, overcorrect, and fix it around the zygomatic arch or to temporal fascia. What you should see A slightly overcorrected mouth corner, the sling invisible under the skin. Wrong plane, wrong tension Too superficial a sling shows as a ridge (the \"sabre\" sign); too deep and it dampens any residual movement. Wrapping the lips circumferentially narrows the mouth opening. Undercorrection is the commonest failure because the graft relaxes in the first weeks."],["osec","th:op-180~s2-6","Alar suspension","Facial Reanimation · The operation, stage by stage","For nasal obstruction from a collapsed ala, incise in the alar crease, expose the anterior maxilla (stay 1 cm below the orbital rim to protect the infraorbital nerve), and suspend the ala laterally with fascia or a bone anchor. Figure 4 Lateral suspension of the nasal ala with a bone anchor. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 180.2 What you should see An open nasal vestibule on the paralysed side at rest and on inspiration."],["osec","th:op-180~s3-1","Crisis drills","Facial Reanimation · When it goes wrong","Free gracilis flap looks congested or pale 1 Act check the pedicle at once return to theatre for anastomosis revision 2 Lost debride plan a second free flap or a temporalis transfer Sling infected or extruding 1 Act antibiotics remove synthetic material 2 Later replace with autologous fascia"],["osec","th:op-180~s4-1","After surgery","Facial Reanimation · Aftercare","Facial physiotherapy with mirror and biofeedback training is essential after nerve transfers and muscle transfers (patients learn to smile by pushing the tongue or clenching). Botulinum toxin treats synkinesis and balances the normal side. Revision of tension is common after slings and free muscle transfer."],["ev","th:op-180~s4-2","Facial Reanimation: current evidence","Facial Reanimation · Aftercare","Free gracilis. A meta-analysis of 31 studies (1,739 gracilis flaps) found flap failure in about 3% and larger smile excursion with masseteric nerve coaptation (about 10 mm) than with cross-facial nerve grafts (about 6.8 mm) (Roy et al., J Plast Reconstr Aesthet Surg 2019). A second meta-analysis confirmed greater commissure excursion with the masseteric nerve, while cross-facial grafts may give a more spontaneous smile (Vila et al., JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020). Temporalis or gracilis. For long-standing palsy, both lengthening temporalis myoplasty and the free gracilis improve symmetry; gracilis outcomes depend on the donor nerve, and dual innervation gave results similar to masseteric alone (Bos et al., J Craniomaxillofac Surg 2016)."],["osec","th:op-180~s4-2","Complications","Facial Reanimation · Aftercare","Flap failure, haematoma, infection, synkinesis, tongue weakness, sling stretch or extrusion, donor site morbidity, asymmetry."],["osec","th:op-180~s5-1","Where the books differ","Facial Reanimation · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers static suspension, alar lateralisation, hypoglossal-facial transposition and grafting (with the most consistent results), masseteric transfer, digastric transfer and the gracilis free flap. K. J. Lee the reanimation ladder by duration of palsy and the eye procedures. The literature masseteric-powered gracilis gives more excursion; cross-facial grafts give more spontaneity."],["osec","th:op-180~s5-2","Viva essentials","Facial Reanimation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The decision tree : nerve available, muscles alive, muscles dead. Timing: muscle survival of about 18 to 24 months. Donor nerves: hypoglossal, masseteric, cross-facial; their trade-offs. Static versus dynamic procedures. Eye protection as the first priority."],["osec","th:op-180~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Facial Reanimation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Forgetting the eye. Offering nerve transfer after the muscles have atrophied. Undercorrecting a static sling. Repairing nerves under tension."],["osec","th:op-182~s1-1","The case at a glance","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Operating note","Segmental mandibulectomy Summary Definitions Mandibulotomy: a cut for access, no bone removed. Marginal: alveolus or lingual plate removed, continuity kept. Segmental: a segment removed (ramus, angle, body or parasymphysis), continuity broken Indications Cancer invading the medullary space (alveolar primary or from the floor of mouth); a hypoplastic, edentulous or irradiated mandible that rules out a safe marginal; benign tumours whose removal would leave an unstable bone; advanced osteoradionecrosis, bisphosphonate osteonecrosis or osteomyelitis; severe trauma with unstable or dead bone Contraindications Unfit for anaesthesia (cardiac, haemodynamic, irreversible coagulopathy); tumour too extensive to remove; distant metastasis (palliative resection only in highly selected patients) Airway Oral intubation converted to tracheostomy in most; nasal intubation for small lesions confined to the bone Anaesthesia Paralysis only after the marginal mandibular and accessory nerves are found; arterial line for free flaps; discuss fluids and vasopressors, which can harm a flap Position Head turned 180 degrees from the anaesthetist for two teams; reverse Trendelenburg about 20 degrees Antibiotics Within 60 minutes of incision, redosed after two half-lives, covering skin, aerobes and anaerobes (for example clindamycin 600 mg then 4 doses 6-hourly; ampicillin-sulbactam 6-hourly; cefazolin 2 g then 3 doses 8-hourly); no benefit beyond 24 to 48 hours Tray Soft tissue and head and neck sets; oral surgery and extraction set; nerve monitor if needed; bipolar; reciprocating and oscillating saws"],["osec","th:op-182~s1-2","Consent","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer together with the part of the jawbone it has entered. The lower lip will be numb for good on that side, because the nerve in the bone is cut. Reconstruction , usually with bone from the leg (fibula) and its skin; or a plate. A tracheostomy and a feeding tube for some days; possibly a gastrostomy. Speech, swallowing and chewing will change; aspiration is a risk; dental rehabilitation may need implants. Bite and face shape may change; the jaw joint may ache. Infection, a leak of saliva, removal of plates, failure of the flap or the bone to heal , chronic pain. Another operation if the margins are not clear. Radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy usually follows."],["osec","th:op-182~s1-3","Before you start","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Operating note","Look for comorbidity and habits that impair healing, and plan swallowing, speech and dental rehabilitation. About 25% of head and neck cancers and 60% of oropharyngeal cancers are HPV-positive, which may change adjuvant treatment. Bone invasion has been variably associated with inferior alveolar paraesthesia, loose teeth, age, sex, alcohol and tobacco chewing, the type of lesion, skin fixation, dental state and recurrence after radiotherapy. Examine the head and neck, and use bimanual palpation to judge whether the cancer is fixed to periosteum or bone. Test lower lip sensation for mental nerve involvement. What protects the bone The periosteum and cortex resist invasion, but less so after radiotherapy, after previous surgery that stripped or removed periosteum, and with poor periodontal health. Cancers usually enter at the occlusal surface , but also at muscle attachments or along the mental or inferior alveolar nerve."],["osec","th:op-182~s1-4","Imaging","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Operating note","Clinical examination is sensitive, but best combined with imaging, and no technique is 100% accurate: 60 to 70% of the bone mineral must be lost before radiographs change. CT, MRI and bone scintigraphy have sensitivity and specificity of about 85 to 95%. CT shows cortical erosion beside the tumour, aggressive periosteal reaction, abnormal marrow attenuation and pathological fracture, though dental artefact degrades the alveolar crest. MRI is more sensitive for marrow and the inferior alveolar canal but overestimates invasion because of oedema. FDG-PET may also help. Before a free flap, conventional or MR angiography is recommended in patients over 50 and those with diabetes, hypertension, trauma, arteriopathy or doubtful pulses. 3D planning and models improve preparation. Figure 1 (A) CT of carcinoma eroding the ramus; (B) 3D reconstruction for virtual planning; (C) a 3D model; (D) the virtual plan with fibula; (E) fibula resection plan; (F) postoperative CT after fibula reconstruction. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 182.1"],["osec","th:op-182~s1-5","Planning with the team","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Operating note","Agree expectations, outcomes and complications (especially after radiotherapy), incisions, tracheostomy and possible gastrostomy. Involve dentistry and maxillofacial surgery (extractions, primary implants), the reconstructive team (setup, instruments, plan, grafts or pedicled flaps), general surgery (gastrostomy), radiation and medical oncology and speech therapy. Finalise the reconstruction before the resection. Ways of restoring continuity Method Notes Soft tissue only Regional pedicled flap, skin graft Reconstruction plate Alone, or with a regional pedicled flap THORP A plate with bone-anchored hollow screws Vascularised bone free flap Preferred : radius, fibula, scapula, iliac crest"],["osec","th:op-182~s2-1","The mandible and its functions","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Anatomy you must see","The mandible forms the outer boundary of the floor of mouth and the lower third of the face, and serves speech, swallowing, chewing and breathing. The mental foramen divides defects into anterior and lateral . Anterior defects remove the genioglossus and geniohyoid attachments: tongue control, chewing, oral competence, swallowing and the airway fail, and drooling follows. Symphyseal defects are the worst and must be reconstructed . Lateral defects are more forgiving if muscle attachments survive, but the bite is unbalanced and the posterior face loses contour; the remaining mandible swings toward the defect, pulled down by the suprahyoid muscles and rotating around the remaining condyle. The inferior alveolar nerve is cut with any segment, leaving the lip numb; nerve grafts (sural, lateral femoral cutaneous) have had variable success. Preserve the opposite mental nerve . The neck: over 30% of oral squamous cancers have nodal metastasis; histological nodal disease is the strongest prognostic factor."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-1","Airway and reassessment","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Perform the tracheostomy for exposure and the postoperative airway (nasal intubation may do for small resections). Re-examine the tumour under anaesthesia, confirm the resection and reconstruction plan, and mark mucosal margins 1 to 2 cm from the cancer. What you should see The tumour's limits by sight and bimanual palpation, and mucosal marks 1 to 2 cm clear of them."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-2","The incision","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","The authors prefer a lip split carried down to about the hyoid and back to the mastoid tip, a modified half-H that gives excellent exposure of the mandible and neck and spares the opposite mental nerve; a submental trifurcation allows bilateral neck dissection. Align the vermilion carefully before cutting. Keep the neck limb at least 2 cm below the angle , or two fingerbreadths below the lower border, to protect the marginal mandibular nerve, and do not let it communicate with the tracheostomy incision. A visor flap with intraoral gingivobuccal incisions looks better, but compromises the opposite mental nerve and gives poor views of the posterior and middle tongue and retromolar trigone. Figure 2 The lip-splitting modified half-H incision, with a submental trifurcation for bilateral neck dissection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 182.2 Figure 3 (A) A pre-contoured reconstruction plate; (B) harvesting a fibula osteocutaneous flap; (C) a visor incision with the fibula inset; (D) sagittal view of the inset with good projection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 182.3 What you should see Vermilion marks aligned on each side of the split, and a neck limb two fingerbreadths below the mandible."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-3","Raise the cheek flap","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Incise along the anterolateral gingivolabial and buccal sulcus to mobilise the lip. The mental nerve is sacrificed on the side of the resection, adding no morbidity since the inferior alveolar canal goes with the specimen. Dissect over the periosteum of the buccal plate in front of masseter, lifting the marginal mandibular nerve with the flap. If tumour extends lateral to the mandible, the facial artery and marginal branch may have to go. Figure 4 A lip split extended along the buccogingival sulcus so the cheek flap can be retracted, without compromising the lateral margin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 182.4 Figure 5 Reflecting the cheek flap gives an excellent view of the cancer. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 182.5 Figure 6 Dissecting the periosteum and masseter lateral to the mandible, without exposing the cancer by dissecting too high. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 182.6 What you should see The outer mandible exposed to the masseter with the marginal nerve up in the flap, and the tumour's lateral extent."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-4","Pre-plate the mandible","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","With the resection exposed, the reconstructive team may pre-bend a plate to the mandible and drill screw holes in the bone that will remain at each end, then remove and sterilise it for later. What you should see A plate lying flush on the bone with holes drilled in the segments that will remain."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-5","The osteotomies","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the cuts with a margin of at least 2 cm , extending forward to include the mental foramen. Extract teeth at the osteotomy sites, and any poor teeth or teeth left without bone support, since extraction later is difficult. Make the anterior cut with a reciprocating saw , retracting and protecting the floor of mouth. Section the ramus with an oscillating saw ; a cut above the sigmoid notch takes the coronoid and requires dividing the temporalis tendon, and this placement reduces bleeding from the inferior alveolar artery. If reconstruction is planned and clearance allows, a vertical ramus cut leaves more bone for fixation. Figure 7 Osteotomy of the condylar neck with an oscillating saw; it can also be made through the mid-ramus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 182.7 What you should see Clean cuts at least 2 cm from tumour, with the floor of mouth and the tongue protected behind the saw. The saw and the nerves A misaligned reciprocating saw damages surrounding structures. When the cheek pushes the saw backward, the inferior alveolar and lingual nerves are at risk: make the posterior cut through an open approach, under direct vision."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-6","Complete the resection","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Make the remaining mucosal cuts, helped by traction sutures on the tongue or the uvula, and mark the specimen with a suture for orientation. Control bleeding from the lingual, buccal and facial vessels with diathermy and ties; if the neck is dissected at the same time, the vessels are already controlled proximally. Smooth the bone edges with a drill. Figure 8 Retracting the soft palate with a suture through the uvula for precise mucosal cuts. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 182.8 What you should see The specimen out in one piece with a suture marking its orientation, and the lingual and facial vessels tied."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-7","Margins on bone","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Frozen section cannot assess bone, which needs decalcification, so send the proximal and distal ends of the inferior alveolar neurovascular bundle as surrogate margins, and talk the specimen through with the pathologist so it is correctly labelled. What you should see The cut ends of the inferior alveolar bundle at each osteotomy, sent separately and labelled."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-8","The neck and the duct","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Perform the neck dissection, often as a separate specimen if there is no direct spread to the perifacial nodes: a supraomohyoid dissection (levels Ia, Ib, II, III), with level IV still debated; bilateral for advanced tongue, floor of mouth, lip and mandibular tumours crossing the midline, or with tongue base involvement or major ipsilateral nodes. If the submandibular gland is kept, reroute its duct by sialodochoplasty to the floor of mouth. What you should see The neck levels cleared, and saliva from the rerouted duct if the gland is kept."],["osec","th:op-182~s3-9","Reconstruct","Segmental Mandibulectomy · The operation, stage by stage","The goals are to restore the lower facial contour, tongue volume and floor of mouth for speech and swallowing, and mandibular continuity with proper occlusion and a base for implants. The authors' commonest choice is the fibula osteocutaneous flap . What you should see The remaining teeth meeting in the preoperative occlusion with the plate fixed, and the condyle seated."],["osec","th:op-182~s4-1","Crisis drills","Segmental Mandibulectomy · When it goes wrong","Heavy bleeding during the resection 1 Sources lingual, buccal, facial vessels branches of the external carotid and internal maxillary arteries 2 Prepare identify the vessels proximally during the neck dissection Positive margin on final pathology 1 Act return to theatre for further resection"],["osec","th:op-182~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Segmental Mandibulectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Poor access: you must see the osteotomy and the structures you are protecting. A misaligned saw; a posterior cut made blind. Forgetting the submandibular duct when the gland stays. Being unprepared for bleeding from the lingual, buccal and facial vessels."],["osec","th:op-182~s5-1","After the operation","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Aftercare","Intensive care for 24 to 48 hours: flap checks by Doppler, capillary refill, colour and turgor (a suture marks the pedicle site for everyone checking), fluid balance, tracheal suction and drains. Remove any bolster at about a week (with antibiotic cover while it is in), then consider decannulation. Oral care and rinses. Feed by nasogastric tube at least until the bolster is out and the patient decannulated. Reflux precautions with a proton pump inhibitor."],["osec","th:op-182~s5-2","Complications","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Infection, fistula May require removal of hardware Flap failure Monitor closely in the first 48 hours Osteomyelitis, non-union Of the bony reconstruction Malocclusion In the dentate after segmental resection Jaw joint and chronic pain After resection and reconstruction Positive margins Further resection Dysphagia, dysarthria, aspiration Functional consequences Lip numbness Inferior alveolar nerve cut"],["ev","th:op-182~s5-3","Segmental Mandibulectomy: current evidence","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Aftercare","Classification. The Brown classification describes mandibular defects by which corners (angle, canine region) are removed, and guides the number of bony segments and the choice of flap (Brown et al., Lancet Oncol 2016). Reconstruction. The fibula free flap is the standard for segmental defects; dental implants can be placed primarily or later. Virtual surgical planning with cutting guides and prebent plates improves accuracy. Marginal or segmental. Marginal mandibulectomy is enough when tumour abuts the periosteum or cortex without medullary invasion; segmental resection is needed for medullary invasion, an edentulous atrophic mandible, or after radiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-182~s5-3","Alternatives","Segmental Mandibulectomy · Aftercare","For patients who cannot have surgery: definitive radiotherapy (66 to 74 Gy, 2 Gy daily over 7 weeks) with concurrent chemotherapy, or induction chemotherapy to shrink the tumour; platinum-based chemotherapy for recurrent or metastatic cancer."],["osec","th:op-182~s6-1","Adjuvant treatment after bone invasion","Segmental Mandibulectomy · For the examination","Bone invasion makes the tumour T4 , and many patients also have nodal disease, so adjuvant chemoradiotherapy appears beneficial. The MACH-NC meta-analysis (Bourhis and colleagues, 93 randomised trials) established concurrent chemoradiotherapy as standard for locally advanced head and neck squamous cancer: compared with radiotherapy alone, a 6.5% absolute survival gain at 5 years, with 12 to 13% better locoregional control and about 3% fewer distant metastases."],["osec","th:op-182~s6-2","Viva essentials","Segmental Mandibulectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Mandibulotomy, marginal and segmental mandibulectomy defined. Why the composite resection is no longer routine (Marchetta). How tumour enters the mandible and what weakens its defences. The limits of imaging: 60 to 70% mineral loss; MRI overestimates. Anterior versus lateral defects, and why anterior ones must be rebuilt. Incision choices and the nerves they risk. Bone margins of 2 cm; nerve ends as surrogate frozen sections. Reconstruction options; the fibula as workhorse."],["osec","th:op-182~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Segmental Mandibulectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Deciding the reconstruction after the resection. Removing teeth too sparingly at the osteotomy. A neck incision too close to the mandible. No angiography before a fibula in an older or vascular patient."],["osec","th:op-183~s1-1","The case at a glance","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Operating note","Mandibulotomy Summary Types Median (between the central incisors, often needing an extraction with the cut through the middle of the socket); paramedian (between the lateral incisor and canine, the wider gap, usually without extraction) Indications Tumours of the posterior mouth and oropharynx (soft palate, tonsil, tongue base, retromolar trigone) when the transoral route cannot give clear margins and the periosteum is not infiltrated; larger, infiltrative cancers; intact dentition or severe trismus blocking transoral access; access to the parapharyngeal space and skull base Not appropriate Cancer invading the mandible (segmental mandibulectomy); periosteum involved with an intact cortex (marginal mandibulectomy); never combine a marginal mandibulectomy with an anterior mandibulotomy (pathological fracture) Alternative Transoral robotic surgery when it can give clear margins; for tongue base, tonsil, soft palate and retromolar trigone the choice often rests on the surgeon's experience Airway Oral intubation (fibreoptic nasal if trismus), surgeon in the room; then tracheostomy and remove the oral tube; awake tracheostomy for severe airway compromise Position Supine, shoulder roll Antibiotics Clindamycin 600 mg then 4 doses 6-hourly; or ampicillin-sulbactam; or cefazolin 2 g then 3 doses 8-hourly Tray Head and neck set; extra-thin reciprocating and oscillating saws; mandibular plates and screws; high-speed drill"],["osec","th:op-183~s1-2","Consent","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to divide the lower lip and jawbone temporarily to reach a cancer at the back of the mouth or throat, then put the bone back with plates. A scar down the middle of the lip and chin; rarely a notch in the lip. A tracheostomy for a short time. The bone may heal slowly or not unite (under 3% now), may need a further operation, or the plates may need removing if they become infected. Teeth beside the cut may be injured or lost (uncommon). Numbness of the lip or chin if the mental nerve is stretched. Soft food for about 6 weeks. Radiotherapy can be given safely over the plates."],["osec","th:op-183~s1-3","Before you start","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Operating note","Ask where the tumour is (tongue, tonsil, gum, soft palate), how long it has been there, and about dysphagia, trismus, breathlessness, dysphonia, aspiration, weight loss and a neck lump (a major prognostic factor; mandibulotomy is often combined with neck dissection). Ask about other cancers (lung and oesophagus share the risk factors), dental disease (which raises the risk of infection, malunion and non-union, and of osteoradionecrosis after radiotherapy: get an oral and maxillofacial opinion and treat it beforehand), craniofacial anomalies, previous radiotherapy, diabetes, previous facial trauma or orthognathic surgery (plates may change the plan), scars of the lip, vermilion and chin (document them), a previous tracheostomy or tracheal surgery (and how it was done), and tobacco and alcohol. Inspect for scars and radiation skin change. Assess the tumour's size, site, infiltration and cranial nerves by inspection and palpation; with severe trismus, rely more on imaging and use transnasal laryngoscopy for spread to the hypopharynx and larynx. Palpate the neck. Look at the teeth and gums: swollen, red, tender gums suggest periodontal disease."],["osec","th:op-183~s1-4","Imaging and the bone decision","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Operating note","Orthopantomogram: the site of the cut, the angle and spacing of the roots, and (for a stair-step cut) the distance from the root apices to the lower border. CT: cortical invasion, which rules out a mandibulotomy. MRI: spread through the marrow. Imaging can miss subtle bone involvement and cannot always separate subperiosteal reaction from invasion; if a rim of normal tissue separates tumour from bone, the bone can be kept. The best examination is under anaesthesia; frozen section of the periosteum helps when tumour is close. Decide the approach beforehand (transoral, mandibulotomy or mandibulectomy), anticipate possible changes, and explain them to the patient."],["osec","th:op-183~s2-1","The lip","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Anatomy you must see","A composite of skin, orbicularis oris and mucosa. The vermilion has two lines to mark for an exact repair: the anterior vermilion line (the mucocutaneous junction with the skin) and the posterior vermilion line (the wet-dry line with the mucosa, where the lips meet when closed). The inferior labial artery , from the facial, crosses the lip between muscle and mucosa at about the level of the anterior vermilion line. Figure 1 Cadaver dissection: (A) the lip and chin incision with the vermilion lines marked; (B) the split mandible showing the floor of mouth. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 183.1"],["osec","th:op-183~s2-2","The mandible","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Anatomy you must see","The inferior alveolar nerve enters the mandibular foramen on the medial ramus, runs obliquely down and forward in its canal, then horizontally in the body, and leaves as the mental nerve through the mental foramen, usually below the gap between the first and second premolars, supplying the lip and chin; the inferior alveolar artery, from the maxillary, runs with it. On the inner symphysis, the anterior belly of digastric arises from the lower border, mylohyoid from the mylohyoid line (symphysis to third molar), geniohyoid from the inferior mental spine and genioglossus from the superior: all serve chewing, swallowing and speech. Figure 2 The mandible: (A) lateral view with the coronoid, condyle, canal and mental foramen; (B) posterior view with the mental spines, digastric fossa, mylohyoid line and mandibular foramen. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 183.2 Cut in front of the mental foramen A lateral mandibulotomy through the body has been largely abandoned: it risks the inferior alveolar nerve and, especially after or before radiotherapy, ischaemic necrosis. An osteotomy anterior to the mental foramen keeps the blood supply and heals best."],["osec","th:op-183~s3-1","Secure the airway","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · The operation, stage by stage","Intubate (surgeon present), perform the tracheostomy and take the tube out of the mouth. What you should see The tube out of the mouth and the airway on the tracheostomy."],["osec","th:op-183~s3-2","Mark the lip","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · The operation, stage by stage","Mark a vertical midline skin incision. Mark the anterior vermilion line with the back of the blade tip, and tattoo the wet-dry line with a needle and methylene blue, keeping the marks until closure. What you should see Tattoo marks on both sides of the planned split at the vermilion border and the wet-dry line."],["osec","th:op-183~s3-3","Split the lip and chin","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · The operation, stage by stage","Divide skin, orbicularis and mucosa, cauterising terminal branches of the inferior labial artery. Extend the incision down the chin and submental region to the hyoid, and in the midline down to periosteum. Incise the gingivobuccal sulcus laterally, leaving a 5 to 8 mm cuff of vestibular mucosa on the bone for closure. Incise the periosteum sharply and elevate it for about 2 cm either side of the planned cut, exposing the front of the mandible, its lower border and the digastric origin. Avoid exposing the mental nerve if you can; if you must, free it sharply from the periosteum. What you should see Bare mandible for 2 cm either side of the planned cut, a cuff of mucosa left on the bone, and both mental nerves safe."],["osec","th:op-183~s3-4","Pre-plate","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · The operation, stage by stage","Mark a stair-step osteotomy beginning between the lateral incisor and canine . Bend two four-hole plates to match the bone exactly and drill the holes, two each side: monocortical screws for the upper plate and bicortical (measured with a depth gauge) for the lower plate near the border. What you should see Two plates sitting flush across the planned stair-step cut, with holes drilled before the bone is cut. A poorly bent plate Take time to make the plate fit perfectly. A poor fit leads to unstable fixation and painful non-union."],["osec","th:op-183~s3-5","Cut the bone","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the gingiva between the lateral incisor and canine down to periosteum. With an extra-thin oscillating saw held perpendicular to the bone, cut vertically between the teeth; once past the roots (measured on the orthopantomogram or sagittal CT), turn 90 degrees laterally for 0.5 to 1 cm , then 90 degrees down to the lower border. Complete every cut with the saw , never by fracturing. What you should see A cut passing between intact roots, completed with the saw to the lower border. The roots and the gap An angled alveolar cut injures a root and loses the tooth: cut straight down between the roots. A thick blade leaves a wide gap and invites malunion or non-union; use an extra-thin (about 0.5 mm) blade."],["osec","th:op-183~s3-6","Open the corridor","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the floor of mouth mucosa and swing the mandible laterally. Divide mylohyoid and retract the submandibular gland and duct medially with the tongue to open the posterior mouth and oropharynx. For the parapharyngeal space or skull base, extend the cut back to the anterior tonsillar pillar and soft palate. Ligate the lingual artery , which can bleed dangerously after surgery; control inferior alveolar bleeding at the cut with bone wax or diathermy. Figure 3 A T3 floor of mouth cancer: (A) a paramedian stair-step mandibulotomy giving access, after resection and a selective neck dissection; (B) after reconstruction with a radial forearm flap, the pre-bent plates fixing well-aligned segments. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 183.3 What you should see The mandible swung laterally, the tongue medial with the gland and duct, the lingual and hypoglossal nerves in view, and the tumour exposed."],["osec","th:op-183~s3-7","Reconstruct and close","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · The operation, stage by stage","Return the mandible to position. Close the floor of mouth mucosa from back to front with absorbable sutures, repair mylohyoid, then fix the segments through the pre-drilled holes . Use a free flap for a large defect. Close soft tissue watertight over the bone, and close the lip in three layers (mucosa, orbicularis, skin) to the marked vermilion lines. Drain as needed, and apply a bulky supportive dressing to reduce swelling and immobilise. What you should see The teeth meeting as before with screws in the original holes, and the vermilion marks meeting exactly."],["osec","th:op-183~s4-1","Crisis drills","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · When it goes wrong","Unstable fixation 1 Recognise movement at the osteotomy, pain 2 Act return to theatre for proper fixation 3 After radiotherapy non-union may become osteoradionecrosis with sequestrum debridement, bone graft and fixation Persistent drainage over the plate 1 Cause saliva contaminating the osteotomy through poor cover or a leaking closure 2 Beyond 2 months a small procedure opening the labial sulcus to remove the plate"],["osec","th:op-183~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Not marking the anterior and posterior vermilion lines. Too small a cuff of vestibular mucosa (5 to 8 mm is enough). A poorly bent plate. A thick saw blade. Finishing the cut by fracture rather than with the saw. An osteotomy behind the mental foramen. An angled alveolar cut damaging a root. Poor soft tissue cover and a leaking closure. Combining a marginal mandibulectomy with a mandibulotomy."],["osec","th:op-183~s5-1","After the operation","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Aftercare","Liquids or soft diet once the soft tissues heal; no intermaxillary fixation; no chewing of solids until about the sixth week . Check the osteotomy clinically. Remove the tracheostomy as soon as swelling settles and oropharyngeal function returns. Radiotherapy follows the usual criteria and can be given safely: the plate barely affects dose (slight shadowing and hot spots, no adjustment needed), though previously irradiated patients carry more risk of osteonecrosis."],["osec","th:op-183~s5-2","Complications","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Aftercare","Complications of mandibulotomy Complication Notes Overall Fell from about 30% to 10% in recent series; minor or severe enough to delay radiotherapy Lip Notching, sulcus stenosis, fistula, loss of chin pad contour, numbness, reduced mobility Teeth Loss of one or both adjacent teeth; uncommon Fixation Malunion, non-union, malocclusion, delayed union; now under 3% Plate infection Removal if drainage persists beyond 2 months Bleeding Inferior alveolar branches; lingual artery (life-threatening postoperatively)"],["ev","th:op-183~s5-3","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches: current evidence","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Aftercare","Access. A paramedian lip-split mandibulotomy (between the lateral incisor and canine, or anterior to the mental foramen) gives wide access to the oropharynx and parapharyngeal space while preserving the mental nerve and genioglossus. Non-union and malunion occur in about 5 to 10% in series, more after radiotherapy; precise plating with pre-drilling before the osteotomy restores occlusion. Alternatives. Transoral robotic and laser surgery now avoid mandibulotomy for many tongue base and tonsil tumours (Ferris et al., J Clin Oncol 2022)."],["osec","th:op-183~s5-3","Alternatives","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · Aftercare","Transoral robotic surgery , preferable whenever it can give clear margins, avoiding a split lip and mandible. Radiotherapy and chemotherapy for inoperable cancer."],["osec","th:op-183~s6-1","Which osteotomy?","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · For the examination","In the edentulous mandible the site matters little. With teeth, an extraction and a cut through the middle of the socket preserves bone around the neighbouring teeth, but the trend is now to avoid extraction : keeping the teeth stabilises the occlusion, and a poorly healing extraction socket can expose bone and lead to malunion, osteomyelitis or osteoradionecrosis. Dental complications without extraction are uncommon with extra-thin (0.5 mm) saws. Because the gap between the lateral incisor and canine is wider, the paramedian osteotomy is preferred to the median."],["osec","th:op-183~s6-2","Viva essentials","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for When to split, when to resect a margin or a segment. The role of transoral robotic surgery. Median versus paramedian; stair-step and why. Cutting anterior to the mental foramen. Marking the vermilion lines; three-layer lip closure. Pre-plating; monocortical above, bicortical below. The extra-thin blade; completing cuts with the saw. Radiotherapy over plates. Complication rates (30% to 10%; fixation under 3%)."],["osec","th:op-183~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Mandibular Osteotomy Approaches · For the examination","Common pitfalls Splitting a mandible the tumour has reached. Ignoring dental disease before radiotherapy. Forgetting to ligate the lingual artery. Letting the patient chew before six weeks."],["osec","th:op-186~s1-1","The case at a glance","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · Operating note","Repair of orbital and midface fractures Summary Indications Displacement causing deformity (flattened cheek, widened face, telecanthus) or function loss: diplopia, muscle entrapment, enophthalmos, malocclusion, trismus from an arch impinging on the coronoid, blocked nasolacrimal duct Urgent Trapdoor (white-eyed) orbital floor fracture with entrapment in a child (within 24 to 48 hours); retrobulbar haematoma (canthotomy now); frontal sinus or skull base fracture with CSF leak (with neurosurgery) Timing otherwise Within about 2 weeks, when swelling has settled; late repair means remodelling and harder reduction Assessment Trauma survey (ATLS), eye examination by ophthalmology (acuity, pupils, movements, globe), CT with coronal and sagittal reformats, dental occlusion Approaches Coronal; brow or upper lid crease; transconjunctival (with lateral cantholysis if needed), subciliary or mid-lid; transcaruncular for the medial wall; upper buccal sulcus; Gillies temporal approach for the arch Fixation Low-profile plates and screws at the frontozygomatic suture, infraorbital rim and zygomaticomaxillary buttress; orbital implants (titanium mesh, preformed or custom plates, porous polyethylene); maxillomandibular fixation for Le Fort fractures Tray Maxillofacial plating set, orbital implants, disimpaction forceps, Rowe's elevator, Gillies elevator, arch bars or MMF screws, corneal shields, navigation or intraoperative CT if available"],["osec","th:op-186~s1-2","Consent","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · Operating note","What to tell the patient Scars (hidden in hairline, eyelid or mouth) and lower eyelid malposition (ectropion, entropion). Double vision may persist or appear; sunken eye (enophthalmos). Blindness (rare) from bleeding behind the eye. Numbness of the cheek and upper lip (infraorbital nerve). Bite problems , further surgery, plate infection or removal. Facial nerve weakness of the forehead (coronal and arch approaches)."],["osec","th:op-186~s1-3","Before you start","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · Operating note","Clear the cervical spine and head injury with the trauma team. Get an ophthalmology examination before surgery. Read the CT for each buttress, the orbital floor and medial wall (especially the posterior bulge), the naso-orbito-ethmoid region and the frontal sinus. Record the pre-injury bite (old photographs, wear facets). Plan intubation with the anaesthetist: oral, nasal or submental, avoiding nasal tubes in skull base fractures."],["osec","th:op-186~s2-1","Prepare and approach","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · The operation, stage by stage","Supine with a shoulder roll; corneal shields; prepare the face and wash the mouth with chlorhexidine; throat pack announced in and out. Inject the incisions. Choose the approach for each fracture: a vestibular incision 5 to 7 mm above the mucogingival junction for the buttresses and maxilla; transconjunctival or lid incisions for the floor and rim; a brow or lid crease incision for the frontozygomatic suture; a coronal flap for the frontal sinus, NOE complex, arch and upper face. Figure 1 The coronal flap, dissected deep to the temporoparietal fascia above the arch to protect the frontal branch. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 186.1 Figure 2 Lower eyelid approaches: transconjunctival, subciliary, mid-lid and infraorbital, and the transcaruncular approach. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 186.2 What you should see Each fracture exposed in the subperiosteal plane, with the infraorbital nerve and the frontal branch protected."],["osec","th:op-186~s2-2","Zygoma","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · The operation, stage by stage","The zygoma is a tetrapod, not a tripod: it joins the frontal bone, the maxilla at the infraorbital rim and buttress, the temporal bone at the arch and the sphenoid wing on the lateral orbital wall. Reduce it (Rowe's elevator or a hook under the body), check alignment at the sphenoid wing (the best guide to rotation), and fix it at one to three points. Isolated arch fractures are elevated through the Gillies approach (an incision in the temporal hair, through the deep temporal fascia, an elevator passed under the arch between fascia and muscle) and usually stay without fixation. Figure 3 A zygoma fracture with medial displacement, needing three-dimensional repositioning through several incisions. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 186.4 Figure 4 A comminuted arch, repaired as a flat bar rather than a curved arch. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 186.5 What you should see Smooth contours at the frontozygomatic suture, the infraorbital rim, the buttress and the sphenoid wing."],["osec","th:op-186~s2-3","Orbit","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · The operation, stage by stage","Repair the rim first, then the walls. Dissect subperiosteally beyond the edges of the defect on all sides until stable bone ledges are found, free the herniated orbital contents from the fracture, and place an implant that restores the posterior floor and medial bulge. Check forced duction before and after placing the implant. Repair a lateral cantholysis to the periosteum; a Frost suture can support the lower lid. Figure 5 A trapdoor fracture in a child with an entrapped inferior rectus, repaired with an implant positioned by navigation. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 186.6 What you should see Free globe movement on forced duction, and the implant sitting on the posterior ledge without impinging on the optic canal. The posterior ledge and the optic nerve Orbital implants must rest on intact bone at the back of the defect; an implant left short causes enophthalmos and one driven too far threatens the optic nerve. Check pupils after the case and image if doubtful."],["osec","th:op-186~s2-4","Naso-orbito-ethmoid and frontal sinus","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · The operation, stage by stage","Through a coronal flap, find the medial canthal tendon and its bone fragment, reduce the central fragment, and re-attach it by transnasal wiring to prevent telecanthus. Reduce anterior table fractures; cranialise the sinus (after removing all mucosa) when the posterior table is badly fractured with outflow tract injury, with dural repair by neurosurgery. Figure 6 Combined cranial, NOE, orbital and Le Fort injuries reconstructed with cranial bone grafts and transnasal canthal wiring. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 186.3 What you should see A normal intercanthal distance and a sealed anterior skull base."],["osec","th:op-186~s2-5","Le Fort fractures","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · The operation, stage by stage","Disimpact the maxilla with disimpaction forceps, place the patient into maxillomandibular fixation in the pre-injury occlusion, then plate the buttresses (nasomaxillary and zygomaticomaxillary) through an upper vestibular incision; avoid palatal incisions, which carry the blood supply. Work from stable bone: fix the mandible first if it is also broken, and use the upper face for height and projection. Figure 7 The Le Fort I, II and III patterns. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 186.7 What you should see The pre-injury bite with the jaws released, and plated buttresses restoring facial height. Senior's pearl Width, height, projection, in that order. A zygoma or arch reduced flat widens the face; a maxilla fixed in the wrong bite will never look right. Rebuild the outer frame first, then the orbit inside it."],["osec","th:op-186~s3-1","Crisis drills","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · When it goes wrong","Retrobulbar haematoma (pain, proptosis, tense orbit, falling vision) 1 Act lateral canthotomy and inferior cantholysis now then medical treatment (mannitol, acetazolamide, steroids) evacuate in theatre Child with entrapment, bradycardia and vomiting on upgaze 1 Act urgent release of the entrapped muscle (within 24 to 48 hours) the oculocardiac reflex resolves when the muscle is freed Malocclusion after surgery 1 Act elastics for minor discrepancy return to theatre to re-plate if the segment is malpositioned"],["osec","th:op-186~s4-1","After surgery","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · Aftercare","Check vision and pupils hourly at first after orbital surgery. Head up, ice, no nose-blowing, antibiotics as per local practice. Soft diet after Le Fort fixation. Orthoptic review for diplopia; postoperative CT for complex orbital repairs."],["ev","th:op-186~s4-2","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton: current evidence","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · Aftercare","A meta-analysis of 7 studies (1,283 patients) found that orbital fracture repair within 14 days reduced diplopia (OR 0.53) and enophthalmos (OR 0.32) compared with later repair, and that the transconjunctival approach caused less ectropion than the subciliary approach (OR 0.20) (Zhang et al., Ann Transl Med 2022). A 2026 meta-analysis of 15 cohort studies (2,666 patients) confirmed better recovery of diplopia and motility with repair within 7 to 14 days, especially with entrapment (Charmila et al., J Maxillofac Oral Surg 2026). One-point fixation gives stable results with few complications in non-comminuted zygomaticomaxillary fractures (Neto et al., J Maxillofac Oral Surg 2023)."],["osec","th:op-186~s4-2","Complications","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · Aftercare","Bleeding, retrobulbar haematoma, blindness, CSF leak, persistent diplopia, enophthalmos, ectropion or entropion, infraorbital numbness, temporal hollowing after a coronal flap, frontal branch injury, malocclusion, malunion, plate infection, frontal sinus mucocele."],["osec","th:op-186~s5-1","Where the books differ","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers approaches by fracture, reduction at every articulation of the zygoma, orbital reconstruction with shaped implants and navigation, and the occlusion as the guide for Le Fort fractures. K. J. Lee the classifications (Le Fort, zygoma, NOE, frontal sinus), the white-eyed fracture in children, and the Gillies and Keen approaches to the arch. The literature orbital repair within 14 days for better function, and transconjunctival access for fewer lid complications."],["osec","th:op-186~s5-2","Viva essentials","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Buttresses of the midface and the Le Fort patterns. Zygoma articulations and points of fixation. Indications and timing for orbital floor repair, including the white-eyed fracture. Retrobulbar haematoma recognition and canthotomy. Approaches and their complications."],["osec","th:op-186~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Fractures of the Cranio-Orbital and Midfacial Skeleton · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing entrapment in a child with a quiet-looking eye. Fixing the zygoma without checking the sphenoid wing. An orbital implant short of the posterior ledge. Plating the maxilla in the wrong bite."],["osec","th:op-189~s1-1","The case at a glance","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · Operating note","Choanal atresia and pyriform aperture stenosis repair Summary Indications Bilateral atresia: respiratory distress or failure to feed in the newborn, prolonged ventilation. Unilateral: persistent one-sided obstruction and discharge (repair electively in childhood). Pyriform aperture stenosis: distress or feeding problems not controlled conservatively Diagnosis Failure to pass a 6 French catheter; endoscopy; CT after suction and decongestion (atretic plate, thick vomer, narrow posterior cavity; pyriform aperture width under about 11 mm in a term baby) Associations CHARGE syndrome (about half of bilateral cases); for pyriform stenosis, a single central incisor and holoprosencephaly (check pituitary and brain) First aid Oral airway or McGovern nipple, orogastric feeding; intubate if needed Approach Endoscopic transnasal (standard); transpalatal for very thick bone or very narrow noses (older children) Anaesthesia General Tray 0 and 120 degree endoscopes (2.7 mm), urethral sounds or dilators, curved microdebrider drill, backbiters, sickle knife, bipolar, cut endotracheal tubes if stenting"],["osec","th:op-189~s1-2","Consent","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · Operating note","What to tell the parents Restenosis is common, especially in bilateral, bony atresia, syndromic babies and those operated very young; repeat dilatation or revision is often needed. Bleeding from the sphenopalatine or posterior septal artery. If stents are used: nostril and columella injury, blockage needing urgent care. Skull base injury and CSF leak (rare) if instruments are misdirected. The baby may still need breathing support, especially with CHARGE."],["osec","th:op-189~s1-3","Before you start","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · Operating note","Stabilise the airway and feeding. Screen for CHARGE (heart, eyes, ears, genitalia, growth). Read the CT for the plate thickness, the vomer, the skull base height and the sphenoid. Figure 1 Axial CT of choanal atresia: the atretic plate and thickened vomer. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 189.1"],["osec","th:op-189~s2-1","Prepare and view","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest with pledgets and inject the posterior septum, lateral wall and plate. Open the mouth with a Dingman retractor and place a 120 degree endoscope behind the palate to view the atresia from the nasopharynx; pass a 0 degree endoscope through the nose to the plate. Figure 2 The atretic plate seen from the nasopharynx and from the nose. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 189.2 What you should see The plate from both sides, with its thinnest point inferomedially."],["osec","th:op-189~s2-2","Raise mucosal flaps","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the mucosa over the plate with a sickle knife and elevate flaps to cover bone at the end (a laterally based flap or a small posterior septal flap where space allows). Figure 3 Endonasal mucosal flap creation. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 189.3 What you should see Mucosa lifted off the bony plate and preserved."],["osec","th:op-189~s2-3","Perforate the plate","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Puncture the plate with a urethral sound (start at 8 to 10 French), directed inferomedially along the floor, under endoscopic view from behind. If the bone is too thick, start the opening with a curved diamond drill in the same inferomedial spot. Enlarge with progressively larger sounds. Figure 4 Drilling the atretic plate under direct endoscopic view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 189.4 What you should see The instrument tip appearing in the nasopharynx on the 120 degree view, low and medial. Up and back is the skull base Sounds or drills directed upwards follow the sloping skull base towards the sphenoid and brain. Keep low, medial and along the floor, with the tip seen from behind."],["osec","th:op-189~s2-4","Remove the vomer and widen","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","With a 2.7 mm endoscope, remove the posterior vomer with a backbiter and drill, creating one wide \"neo-choana\" across both sides; drill the lateral pterygoid and posterolateral wall if needed. Figure 5 Removing the posterior vomer. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 189.5 What you should see A single wide posterior opening, larger than the anterior nasal cavity will allow you to pass. Senior's pearl Width beats stents. Removing the posterior vomer to make a common choana and covering raw bone with flaps prevent restenosis better than any tube."],["osec","th:op-189~s2-5","Finish","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Lay the flaps over exposed bone, coagulate bleeding, irrigate and place oxymetazoline. Stents (cut 3.5 mm tubes sutured round the columella) are optional and used less often. What you should see Flaps covering the bone, a clear airway on both sides, no active bleeding."],["osec","th:op-189~s2-6","Transpalatal and pyriform repairs","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Transpalatal: raise a U-shaped palatal flap on both greater palatine arteries, divide the soft palate aponeurosis, remove the back third of the hard palate, the plate and the posterior vomer, then close; it risks palatal growth problems, so it is kept for exceptional cases. Pyriform aperture stenosis: through a sublabial incision, drill the bony aperture laterally and inferiorly, protecting the tooth buds and nasolacrimal ducts, and stent briefly. What you should see A widened bony inlet admitting an age-appropriate tube."],["osec","th:op-189~s3-1","Crisis drills","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · When it goes wrong","Stent blocked or dislodged in a newborn 1 Act suction if obstructed and the baby desaturates, remove the stent and insert an oral airway Restenosis 1 Act endoscopic dilatation and revision widen the vomer resection 2 Recurrent consider topical mitomycin, steroid, or a stent for a short period"],["osec","th:op-189~s4-1","After surgery","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · Aftercare","Neonatal intensive care monitoring; saline drops and suction; topical steroid drops; stent care if used (suction several times a day); endoscopic review and dilatation under anaesthesia as needed."],["ev","th:op-189~s4-2","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis: current evidence","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · Aftercare","To stent or not. A meta-analysis of bilateral atresia repair found similar success with stents (65%) and without (64%), with stent complications including alar injury, vestibular stenosis, columellar tears and blockage (Strychowsky et al., Laryngoscope 2016). In a comparison of 20 bilateral repairs, narrowing was commoner with stents (40% against 20%) and granulation was more frequent (Saafan, Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2013). Endoscopic choanoplasty with a mucoperiosteal flap, wide vomer resection and no stent gives good patency (Ibrahim et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2010)."],["osec","th:op-189~s4-2","Complications","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · Aftercare","Restenosis (the commonest), bleeding, stent injury to the nostril or columella, failed extubation, skull base injury, palatal fistula or growth problems after the transpalatal route."],["osec","th:op-189~s5-1","Where the books differ","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers endoscopic repair with sounds, drill and vomer resection, mucosal flaps, optional stents, and the transpalatal route for thick bone; restenosis risk factors listed. Dhingra the emergency airway (McGovern nipple), the diagnostic tests and the older transpalatal repair. The literature stenting does not improve success and adds complications."],["osec","th:op-189~s5-2","Viva essentials","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Presentation and first aid in the newborn. CHARGE and pyriform aperture stenosis with the single incisor. The steps of endoscopic repair, including vomer resection. Stents: the evidence. Restenosis and its risk factors."],["osec","th:op-189~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Choanal Atresia/Pyriform Aperture Stenosis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Directing sounds upwards towards the skull base. Leaving the posterior vomer. Stripping all mucosa, leaving circumferential raw bone. Missing pyriform aperture stenosis as the cause."],["osec","th:op-19~s1-1","The case at a glance","Open Tracheostomy · Operating note","Open tracheostomy Summary Indications Upper airway obstruction, acute or chronic; prolonged mechanical ventilation; access for tracheobronchial toilet; a temporary airway for major oral, pharyngeal or laryngeal surgery; selected sleep apnoea, chronic lung disease and central hypoventilation Contraindications Almost none absolute. Uncorrectable coagulopathy (end-stage liver failure) and very high ventilating pressures, where losing the circuit for even a minute can cause flash pulmonary oedema. When seconds count, a cricothyroidotomy is the operation, not a tracheostomy Anaesthesia General through an endotracheal tube in most; local infiltration without sedation in the patient with a threatened airway; general in children Position Supine, shoulder roll, neck extended, head ring; sitting up at about 45 degrees if the patient cannot lie flat; no extension if the cervical spine is in doubt Antibiotics At induction if not already on them and for at least 24 hours (clindamycin, co-amoxiclav, or a cephalosporin with metronidazole; guided by cultures in the ICU patient) Tray Tracheostomy set with a tracheal hook, small rake retractors, cricoid hook, Kerrison or heavy scissors for a calcified ring, ligature carrier, bipolar and monopolar diathermy, the chosen tube with its cuff tested then deflated, one size smaller ready, suction, tapes or Velcro holder Team Anaesthetist at the head with a plan for the moment the trachea is opened; scrub nurse who knows the tube; face shields for everyone Time 20 to 40 minutes in a straightforward adult neck"],["osec","th:op-19~s1-2","Consent","Open Tracheostomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient or family Why: to secure breathing or allow ventilation to be weaned; most tubes are temporary and the hole closes once the tube is out. Voice: they will not be able to speak with a cuffed tube inflated; a speaking valve or a cuff-down trial usually restores speech later. Bleeding , usually minor; rarely a return to theatre. Infection of the wound and chest; the wound is always colonised within two days. Air leaks: surgical emphysema, pneumothorax or pneumomediastinum, commoner in children. Tube problems: blockage or displacement, the most dangerous early events; this is why the first week is spent on a ward that knows tracheostomies. Late problems: granulation tissue, tracheal narrowing, a persistent hole after removal (tracheocutaneous fistula) or a tethered scar, which may need further surgery. Rare but serious: damage to the oesophagus, fire in the airway, and a late bleed from the innominate artery, which can be fatal. In a large multi-institutional series about 1.4% of patients had an intraoperative complication, 5.6% a complication in the first week and 7.1% a late complication: the risk lies mainly after the operation, not during it."],["osec","th:op-19~s1-3","Before you start","Open Tracheostomy · Operating note","Ask the questions that decide the operation. Why does this patient need a tracheostomy, how soon, and for how long? Will they be ventilated, and for how long? Is there an alternative, such as continued intubation, a short course of steroids or observation? Who will look after the tube on the ward? In selected terminal cases, a decision not to intervene on the airway is right. Then the history and examination that change the conduct. Has the neck been operated on or a tracheostomy done before? Can the patient tolerate a general anaesthetic? Are they anticoagulated, and can it be stopped; are there signs of a bleeding tendency at puncture sites or the gums? If ventilated, what are the peak pressures and the oxygen requirement? Feel the neck: where are the cricoid and the trachea, how far above the sternum, is there a mass or open wound in the way, and can the neck extend? Imaging is not routinely needed. Choose the tube before you start. It must give an adequate airway, allow positive-pressure ventilation if needed, seal the trachea against aspiration and allow suction. Adults usually get a cuffed tube with an inner cannula , which can be removed at once if it blocks while the outer tube keeps the stoma. A deep neck needs an extended-length tube or one with an adjustable flange. Children's tubes (Shiley size 3 and smaller) have no inner cannula. Figure 1 Tracheostomy tubes. A: an uncuffed size 4, a cuffed size 6 and a cuffed size 8 percutaneous tube with a tapered tip; smaller tubes are also shorter. B: an uncuffed extra-length tube with the extra length proximal, for a deep "],["osec","th:op-19~s2-1","The landmarks you feel","Open Tracheostomy · Anatomy you must see","Before you scrub, feel three structures and mark them: the thyroid notch , the cricoid cartilage (the firm ring below the cricothyroid membrane, the last complete ring of the airway) and the sternal notch . The trachea lies in the midline between the cricoid and the sternum; in an extended young neck there are six or seven rings above the sternum, in an elderly kyphotic neck the cricoid may sit almost at the notch."],["osec","th:op-19~s2-2","The layers you cross","Open Tracheostomy · Anatomy you must see","From the skin inwards: subcutaneous fat and platysma (thin or absent in the midline), the anterior jugular veins running vertically either side of the midline, the investing fascia joining in the midline raphe between the strap muscles, the sternohyoid and sternothyroid muscles, the pretracheal fascia and the thyroid isthmus , which crosses the second to fourth rings, and then the trachea. Figure 2 The anterior jugular veins lie either side of the midline under the skin; they can be retracted and need not be divided unless injured. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 19.2"],["osec","th:op-19~s2-3","The structures at risk","Open Tracheostomy · Anatomy you must see","The innominate (brachiocephalic) artery crosses the trachea obliquely at or below the sternal notch; it can be higher in children, in a fully extended neck and in some adults. A stoma placed too low lets the tube's curve rest on it. The cricoid and first ring : incising them risks subglottic stenosis. The oesophagus lies directly behind the membranous posterior tracheal wall; an over-deep stab into a collapsed or mobile trachea goes straight through. The pleural domes , which rise into the lower neck in children. Thyroid isthmus and the inferior thyroid veins , the usual source of bleeding. Recurrent laryngeal nerves in the tracheo-oesophageal grooves, safe if you stay in front of the trachea."],["osec","th:op-19~c3","The operation, stage by stage","Open Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Six stages, each with what you should see before you move on."],["osec","th:op-19~s3-1","Prepare, mark and infiltrate","Open Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Position with the shoulder roll and the neck extended, then feel again: extension lifts the trachea out of the chest. Mark the cricoid and the notch. Inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline into the skin and subcutaneous tissue along the incision. Prepare the neck, face and upper chest and drape so that the anaesthetist still has the mouth and the tube. What you should see The cricoid and the sternal notch marked, with the incision line between them. A dry skin preparation: alcohol-based solutions pooling in the neck are fuel. Fire If you used an alcohol-based preparation, let it dry fully before any diathermy, especially in a hairy neck where the solution lingers."],["osec","th:op-19~s3-2","Incise and divide the subcutaneous layer","Open Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Make a horizontal incision about 3 cm long in a skin crease about 1 cm above the sternal notch or 2 cm below the cricoid , roughly over the second to third rings. Divide the subcutaneous fat sharply down to the investing fascia and place small rake retractors on each side. Identify the anterior jugular veins and retract them laterally; tie them only if they are cut. What you should see The two anterior jugular veins lying either side of the midline. The pale vertical midline raphe between the strap muscles. Senior's pearl Feel the trachea again with a fingertip every few minutes; the midline is a structure you find by touch, not by sight. Repeated palpation keeps you out of the carotid sheath."],["osec","th:op-19~s3-3","Split the strap muscles","Open Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Open the raphe vertically and separate the sternohyoid and then the sternothyroid muscles from each other in the midline, retracting them laterally with the rakes. Work layer by layer with good light and an assistant holding the retractors evenly on both sides, so the midline does not drift. Figure 3 A: the strap muscles split in the midline and retracted laterally. B: the thyroid isthmus and trachea identified. C: the trachea entered between the second and third rings, with the isthmus retracted upwards or divided. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 19.3 What you should see The thyroid isthmus crossing the upper trachea, and the tracheal rings above or below it. Off the midline If you see the carotid pulsation or the internal jugular vein, you are lateral. Stop, release the retractors, feel for the trachea and come back to it."],["osec","th:op-19~s3-4","Deal with the thyroid isthmus","Open Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","You can retract the isthmus upwards or divide it. To divide it, incise the pretracheal fascia at the lower border of the cricoid to expose the trachea, pass a curved haemostat beneath the isthmus in the plane on the front of the trachea, clamp on each side, divide and suture-ligate each end. In children the isthmus is always divided so that it cannot fall over the tract if the tube comes out. Once the front of the trachea is clean, place a tracheal (cricoid) hook under the cricoid or the second ring and give it to the assistant: the trachea is now lifted and held. What you should see The rings of the trachea, white and ridged, with the cricoid above. A dry field: every bleeding point is dealt with now, before the airway is opened. Senior's pearl Count the rings with the tip of the haemostat, from the cricoid down. Saying \"cricoid, one, two, three\" aloud prevents both a high stoma through the first ring and a low one onto the innominate artery."],["osec","th:op-19~s3-5","Open the trachea","Open Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Tell the anaesthetist you are about to enter the airway, ask for the oxygen to be lowered if they can, and change to cold steel. In adults open the trachea transversely between the second and third, or third and fourth rings ; excising the anterior part of one ring with heavy scissors or a Kerrison rongeur makes insertion easier, especially when the rings are calcified. Some surgeons fashion an inferiorly based flap (Björk flap) and suture it to the skin. Place stay (traction) sutures : in adults, in the midline around the ring above and the ring below the opening; in children, vertically either side of a vertical incision between the second and fourth rings, removing no cartilage, and label them left and right. Figure 4 Stay sutures. In children (first image) they lie on each side of a vertical incision and no cartilage is removed; in adults (second image) they pass around the rings above and below the stoma. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 19.4 and 19.5 What you should see The endotracheal tube cuff through the opening; ask for it to be deflated before you cut near it. Clear tracheal mucosa, and the stay sutures lifting the stoma up into the wound. Airway fire and the posterior wall Never enter the airway with diathermy, especially when the inspired oxygen is high: the endotracheal tube is the fuel. Cut through the front wall only; the membranous posterior wall and the oesophagus lie just behind."],["osec","th:op-19~s3-6","Insert and secure the tube","Open Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Ask the anaesthetist to withdraw the endotracheal tube slowly until its tip sits just above the stoma, but not out of the larynx. Pull up on the stay sutures to open the stoma, introduce the tube with its obturator held at right angles to the trachea , then rotate it downwards into the line of the trachea as it goes in. Remove the obturator, insert the inner cannula, inflate the cuff and connect. Confirm a capnograph trace , bilateral chest movement and air entry; only then is the endotracheal tube removed. Suture the neck plate to the skin and tie the tapes with the neck flexed so that one finger fits beneath them. Leave the wound open, without packing or tight sutures, and tape the stay sutures to the chest with a label. Figure 5 A: the tube introduced into the trachea. B: the neck plate sutured to the skin; tapes are optional. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 19.6 What you should see A sustained end-tidal carbon dioxide trace, the chest rising and the cuff sealing. Do not let go of the old airway too soon Withdrawing the endotracheal tube fully before the new tube is proven leaves the patient with no airway. Keep it in the larynx, ready to advance, until the capnograph confirms the tracheostomy. Senior's pearl In head and neck surgery, do not run tapes across a neck incision or over the pedicle of a flap; suture the flange instead."],["osec","th:op-19~s4-1","Crisis drills","Open Tracheostomy · When it goes wrong","Bleeding during the dissection 1 Stay calm and get control pressure with a swab better light and suction ask for retraction on both sides 2 Find the source anterior jugular vein tie thyroid isthmus or inferior thyroid veins clamp and tie a high innominate artery pressure and senior help 3 Prevent it stay in the midline, layer by layer, and make the field dry before the trachea is opened Fire in the airway 1 Stop the fire disconnect the oxygen remove the burning endotracheal tube flood the field with saline or water 2 Re-establish the airway reintubate or place a tracheostomy tube at once 3 Assess bronchoscopy for the extent of injury antibiotics and steroids observe in intensive care No capnograph trace after insertion 1 Assume the tube is not in the trachea do not keep ventilating through it 2 Restore oxygenation remove the tube advance the endotracheal tube back past the stoma 3 Re-site lift the stay sutures, look into the stoma with a good light and reinsert under vision Collapse once the obstruction is relieved 1 Expect it in long-standing obstruction with high carbon dioxide, relief can be followed by apnoea or cardiac arrest 2 Pulmonary oedema sudden loss of the obstruction's own positive end-expiratory pressure can cause flash pulmonary oedema ventilate with positive end-expiratory pressure 3 Prepare warn the anaesthetist and have resuscitation ready before the airway is opened"],["osec","th:op-19~s5-1","The obese neck","Open Tracheostomy · Variations","Obesity and obstructive airway disease often travel together, which raises the risks at the operation (flash pulmonary oedema) and at decannulation (poor tolerance of capping because of sleep apnoea). Remove the excess subcutaneous fat to shorten the track between skin and trachea, mature the stoma with half-mattress sutures between the skin and the tracheal opening so that a dislodged tube cannot enter a false passage, and use an extended-length tube. Warn that a matured stoma will later need surgical closure."],["osec","th:op-19~s5-2","The child","Open Tracheostomy · Variations","Airway misadventures in children are catastrophic and their tubes have no inner cannula, so the technique changes. Remove the naso- or orogastric tube first so it is not felt as the trachea. A vertical skin incision is often preferred for its extensibility. Palpate repeatedly. Always divide the isthmus. Place non-absorbable monofilament stay sutures lateral to a vertical tracheal incision, label them LEFT and RIGHT, tape them to the chest and keep them until the first tube change. The stoma may be matured with absorbable half-mattress sutures. Tie the flanges around the neck with shoestring ties and do not suture the flange to the skin, so that the tube can be changed quickly. Figure 6 A large-volume papilloma obstructing a child's airway, one of the obstructive indications for tracheostomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 19.1"],["osec","th:op-19~s5-3","The emergency","Open Tracheostomy · Variations","When the patient cannot be intubated or oxygenated, the operation is a cricothyroidotomy through the membrane between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages: it is faster, more superficial and avoids the isthmus, though it is not bloodless. Convert it to a formal tracheostomy once the airway is stable. Awake tracheostomy under local anaesthetic is the choice when intubation is impossible but there is time, for example in a large laryngeal tumour or severe laryngeal trauma; give extra plain lidocaine into the pretracheal tissues as you deepen, and avoid an early injection into the airway, which can provoke coughing, panic and obstruction."],["osec","th:op-19~s6-1","The first week on the ward","Open Tracheostomy · Aftercare","The patient goes to a ward whose nurses know fresh tracheostomies, with the tube size, a spare tube of the same size and one smaller, a tracheal dilator, suction and the stay sutures labelled at the bedside. Sit the patient semi-upright to help coughing and suction. Watch the observations: rising pulse, respiratory rate or blood pressure, agitation, anxiety and restlessness all mean hypoxia until proven otherwise, and may mean a blocked or displaced tube. Suction as needed, humidify always (by a tracheostomy mask; a T-piece levers the tube), remove and clean the inner cannula often, clean the stoma at least three or four times a day and keep the skin under the flange dry with a thin non-adherent dressing. Check cuff pressures to prevent mucosal necrosis."],["osec","th:op-19~s6-2","The first tube change","Open Tracheostomy · Aftercare","Do not change the tube in the first five to seven days unless the cuff fails or the patient must change to an uncuffed tube: until a track forms, the tissues fall together as soon as the tube is out. If it must be done early, do it with optimal positioning, an assistant, good light, a tracheal hook and suction, using the stay sutures, and consider a tube exchanger. Regular changes every two to three weeks thereafter reduce granulation tissue and biofilm."],["osec","th:op-19~s6-3","Decannulation","Open Tracheostomy · Aftercare","Check with a flexible endoscope that the upper airway is adequate and the larynx competent. Downsize the tube and plug it during waking hours, teaching the patient to remove the plug if breathless. If the plug is tolerated for 24 hours, remove the tube and cover the stoma with an occlusive dressing; it usually closes within days. If the plug is not tolerated, find the obstruction. Children are endoscoped before decannulation; a granuloma at the stoma is common and removing it helps."],["ev","th:op-19~s6-4","Open Tracheostomy: current evidence","Open Tracheostomy · Aftercare","Safety. The National Tracheostomy Safety Project algorithms for the blocked or displaced tube (McGrath et al., Anaesthesia 2012), bedhead signs, capnography and trained staff are recommended after the NCEPOD review On the Right Trach? (2014). Timing. In TracMan, early tracheostomy (within four days) did not reduce 30-day mortality in ventilated adults compared with tracheostomy at ten days or later (Young et al., JAMA 2013). Open or percutaneous. A Cochrane review found that percutaneous tracheostomy reduces wound infection and unfavourable scarring compared with open surgery in selected ICU patients (Brass et al., 2016); open tracheostomy remains the choice for difficult anatomy, children, and emergencies."],["osec","th:op-19~s6-4","Complications","Open Tracheostomy · Aftercare","Complications after tracheostomy Complication When and why Recognition Management Tube obstruction Early; thick or dried secretions, clot Distress, no air through the tube, suction catheter will not pass Remove the inner cannula; suction; if not cleared, change the whole tube Displacement Any time; worst before the track forms. Obesity, coughing, agitation, too short a tube, too low a stoma, loose tapes, bulky dressings Sudden distress or a patient who can suddenly speak Do not ventilate through a displaced tube (pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum kill in minutes). Lift the stay sutures and reinsert under vision; if it fails, intubate from above; or find the opening with a flexible scope or a suction catheter and railroad the tube over it False passage Tube placed between the trachea and the sternum High pressures, no capnograph trace, surgical emphysema Remove, oxygenate from above, re-site under vision Bleeding Early oozing or a vessel Blood in the wound or the tube Correct clotting, haemostatic packing for ooze, return to theatre for significant bleeding; do not pack an actively bleeding stoma Surgical emphysema Coughing, an uncuffed tube, tight sutures, packing Crepitus spreading from the wound Remove packing and sutures; mild cases resolve Pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum Pleural injury (children), false passage, air drawn in during struggling Chest radiograph after every tracheostomy Drain a significant pneumothorax; pneumomediastinum usually needs nothing Infection Colonisation within 24 to 48 hours by Pseudomonas, E. coli and staphylococci Cellulitis, purulent sec"],["osec","th:op-19~s7-1","Where the books differ","Open Tracheostomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra classifies tracheostomy by the level of the stoma: high (through the first ring, now avoided except in laryngeal cancer, because it risks perichondritis and subglottic stenosis), mid (second and third rings, the standard, usually dividing the isthmus) and low (below the isthmus, avoided because the trachea is deep there, close to the great vessels, and the tube impinges on the suprasternal notch). It advises intubation before tracheostomy whenever possible, especially in infants and children. It also lists the old metal (Jackson) tubes alongside plastic ones. Lee emphasises the Björk flap and stoma maturation, early tracheostomy in the ventilated patient, and the comparison with percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy, which suits the ICU patient with a palpable, normal neck. Myers stresses that the danger lies in the postoperative week rather than on the table, that an emergency airway is a cricothyroidotomy, and gives detailed paediatric and obese-neck modifications."],["osec","th:op-19~s7-2","Viva essentials","Open Tracheostomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications, and the one-line distinction between cricothyroidotomy (seconds count) and tracheostomy . The layers and the level: second to fourth rings , never the first ring or cricoid, never so low as to sit on the innominate artery. Adult versus child technique: incision, isthmus, stay sutures, flange fixing, no inner cannula. Fire triangle and the airway fire drill. The displaced tube drill and why you never bag a displaced tube. Sentinel bleed and the innominate artery manoeuvre. Decannulation steps."],["osec","th:op-19~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Open Tracheostomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Delegating the \"simple\" tracheostomy to an unsupervised junior without light, suction and help. A stoma through the first ring or cricoid, or a low stoma above the sternum. Using diathermy to enter the trachea with high inspired oxygen. Removing the endotracheal tube before the capnograph confirms the new tube. Packing or tightly suturing the wound around the tube. Changing a fresh tube without stay sutures, light and a spare tube, or ventilating a tube that may be in a false passage."],["osec","th:op-190~s1-1","The case at a glance","Midline Nasal Masses · Operating note","Surgery for congenital midline nasal masses Summary Lesions Nasal dermoid sinus cyst (commonest; pit, hair, infection); nasal glioma (glial heterotopia; firm, non-pulsatile); encephalocele (soft, pulsatile, enlarges with crying, Furstenberg positive); nasolacrimal duct cyst (newborn nasal obstruction, often with dacryocystocele) Indications All midline masses (deformity, infection, meningitis risk); nasolacrimal duct cysts once diagnosed Imaging MRI for intracranial extension; fine-cut CT for the foramen caecum, a bifid crista galli and bone defects Approaches External (vertical midline ellipse, open rhinoplasty, or brow incision); endoscopic intranasal; combined with frontal craniotomy (bicoronal) or endoscopic skull base repair when the tract reaches the dura Anaesthesia General Tray Lacrimal probes, open rhinoplasty set, osteotomes, endoscopes and microdebrider, neurosurgical set if intracranial, dural repair materials"],["osec","th:op-190~s1-2","Consent","Midline Nasal Masses · Operating note","What to tell the parents Recurrence if any of the cyst or tract is left (about 1 in 12 dermoids). Scar on the nose, and possible changes to nasal shape. If the tract enters the skull: CSF leak, meningitis, and the risks of craniotomy. Infection, bleeding, loss of smell. For a nasolacrimal duct cyst: recurrence and watering eye."],["osec","th:op-190~s1-3","Before you start","Midline Nasal Masses · Operating note","Examine the nose inside and out, look for a pit, hair, widening of the bridge (telecanthus) and pulsation, and test the Furstenberg sign. Never needle or biopsy the mass before imaging. Plan the approach on MRI and CT with neurosurgery if needed. Figure 1 A child with drainage from a midline nasal sinus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 190.2 Figure 2 CT of the skull base, the prenasal space, and MRI showing the extent of a tract. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 190.3 and 190.4 and 190.5"],["osec","th:op-190~s2-1","Nasolacrimal duct cyst","Midline Nasal Masses · The operation, stage by stage","Decongest, examine both sides endoscopically (cysts are often bilateral), and marsupialise the cyst under the inferior turbinate with fine forceps or a small microdebrider blade. The ophthalmologist probes and irrigates the lacrimal system; a stent may be placed. Figure 3 A large nasolacrimal duct cyst under the inferior turbinate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 190.1 Figure 4 A stent placed after removal of the cyst. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 190.7 What you should see A wide opening of the cyst into the inferior meatus and free passage of the probe."],["osec","th:op-190~s2-2","Dermoid: excise the pit and follow the tract","Midline Nasal Masses · The operation, stage by stage","Excise the pit with a vertical ellipse (or use an open rhinoplasty approach for a deep cyst without a pit), cannulate the tract and follow it down through the prenasal space under the nasal bones, removing it intact with a cuff of tissue. Mobilise or osteotomise the nasal bones if needed to follow it upwards. Figure 5 A dermoid with its tract, marked with a suture, and the bony defect on CT. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 190.6 What you should see The cyst and its tract removed whole, ending blind or reaching the skull base. Do not cut the tract short A tract that continues through the foramen caecum must be followed to the dura. Cutting it at the skull base leaves epithelium to grow into an intracranial dermoid or cause meningitis. If imaging showed an intracranial component, do this as a combined procedure."],["osec","th:op-190~s2-3","Intracranial extension","Midline Nasal Masses · The operation, stage by stage","For a tract to the dura, combine the external excision with a frontal craniotomy through a bicoronal incision (or an endoscopic endonasal skull base approach), remove the intradural or extradural part, and repair the dura and skull base. Figure 6 The bicoronal incision and the intracranial part of the dissection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 190.8 and 190.9 What you should see The tract removed in continuity, with a watertight dural repair."],["osec","th:op-190~s2-4","Glioma and encephalocele","Midline Nasal Masses · The operation, stage by stage","Extranasal gliomas are excised through an external incision; intranasal ones endoscopically, coagulating and dividing any fibrous stalk to the skull base and repairing the defect. Encephaloceles are reduced or amputated flush with the skull base after coagulation and the defect is closed in layers, endoscopically or with neurosurgery. What you should see A sealed skull base with no CSF leak. Senior's pearl A bifid crista galli or a widened foramen caecum on CT means the tract probably goes intracranially. That is the moment to bring in neurosurgery, not halfway through the operation."],["osec","th:op-190~s3-1","Crisis drills","Midline Nasal Masses · When it goes wrong","CSF leak during excision 1 Act identify the dural defect repair in layers (fascia, fat, flap) 2 After antibiotics per local protocol watch for meningitis Recurrence of a dermoid 1 Act re-image with MRI excise the residual tract completely, including any intracranial part"],["osec","th:op-190~s4-1","After surgery","Midline Nasal Masses · Aftercare","Wound care, antibiotics if infected before, and long follow-up: dermoid recurrences appear years later (mean about 3 years)."],["ev","th:op-190~s4-2","Midline Nasal Masses: current evidence","Midline Nasal Masses · Aftercare","In 96 children with nasal dermoids over 44 years, 96% had preoperative imaging, 82% had no intracranial extension, 89% were excised extracranially and 11% needed a combined intracranial approach; recurrence occurred in 8%, on average 3.3 years later (Herrington et al., Laryngoscope 2016). Gliomas benefit from early multidisciplinary planning, sometimes from prenatal diagnosis (Ajose-Popoola et al., Skull Base Rep 2011)."],["osec","th:op-190~s4-2","Complications","Midline Nasal Masses · Aftercare","Recurrence, scarring and nasal deformity, infection, CSF leak, meningitis, anosmia, and the risks of craniotomy."],["osec","th:op-190~s5-1","Where the books differ","Midline Nasal Masses · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers imaging first, complete excision by external, endoscopic or combined intracranial approaches, and endoscopic marsupialisation of nasolacrimal duct cysts. Dhingra the embryology (foramen caecum, fonticulus frontalis) and the clinical differences between dermoid, glioma and encephalocele. The literature most dermoids can be removed extracranially, but imaging must exclude intracranial extension."],["osec","th:op-190~s5-2","Viva essentials","Midline Nasal Masses · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Embryology of the prenasal space. Dermoid, glioma, encephalocele: clinical differences and the Furstenberg sign. Why never to biopsy first. Imaging and the bifid crista galli. Complete excision and when to involve neurosurgery."],["osec","th:op-190~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Midline Nasal Masses · For the examination","Common pitfalls Biopsying or excising a midline mass without imaging. Leaving the deep tract. Taking an intranasal glioma for a polyp. Missing a contralateral nasolacrimal duct cyst."],["osec","th:op-191~s1-1","The case at a glance","Adenoidectomy · Operating note","Adenoidectomy Summary Indications Obstructive sleep apnoea or sleep-disordered breathing from adenotonsillar hypertrophy; chronic nasal obstruction, mouth breathing and snoring; otitis media with effusion with the second set of tubes, or with the first set if the adenoid also causes symptoms; chronic or recurrent rhinosinusitis in children failing medical treatment (first-line surgery, especially under six); chronic adenoiditis with postnasal discharge Contraindications Cleft palate, submucous cleft or existing velopharyngeal insufficiency (risk of hypernasal speech; superior-pole partial adenoidectomy if essential); uncorrected coagulopathy; acute upper respiratory infection within about two weeks; unstable atlantoaxial joint Anaesthesia General, oral endotracheal tube secured in the midline; no sedative premedication in sleep apnoea Position Supine, shoulder roll, neck extended (Rose position), head ring, eyes lubricated and taped Antibiotics Not routine; endocarditis prophylaxis when indicated Tray Boyle-Davis or McIvor gag with a slotted blade and Draffin rods or a Mayo stand suspension, 6 or 8 French rubber catheters, nasopharyngeal mirror, adenoid curettes of several widths (with and without a guard), St Clair Thompson forceps, suction diathermy, gauze roll throat pack, oxymetazoline 0.025%, postnasal packs; optional microdebrider, coblation wand, 0- and 70-degree telescopes Time 10 to 20 minutes"],["osec","th:op-191~s1-2","Consent","Adenoidectomy · Operating note","What to tell the parents Why: to relieve blocked breathing and snoring, reduce glue ear or sinus infections. Bleeding: usually at the time and controlled in theatre; occasionally a pack in the back of the nose overnight, rarely a return to theatre. Damage to teeth, lips and gums from the mouth gag; loose milk teeth may come out. A nasal, hypernasal voice for a few weeks; very rarely persistent velopharyngeal insufficiency needing speech therapy or surgery. Neck pain and stiffness , usually muscle spasm; rarely an inflammatory loosening of the top joint of the neck (Grisel syndrome). Regrowth of adenoid needing further surgery in about 1% to 3%. Narrowing of the back of the nose by scarring, rare. The risks of anaesthesia, and an overnight stay for children at higher risk."],["osec","th:op-191~s1-3","Before you start","Adenoidectomy · Operating note","Ask about mouth breathing, snoring and witnessed apnoeas, restless sleep, daytime sleepiness, irritability or hyperactivity and bedwetting; hearing loss and speech delay; persistent effusion; chronic rhinorrhoea, cough and nasal obstruction. Ask about allergic rhinitis and asthma, a personal or family history of abnormal bleeding (and screen for von Willebrand disease if present), anticoagulants and nasal steroids, previous surgery, and nasal regurgitation of fluids or hypernasal speech , which suggest velopharyngeal insufficiency. Examine for adenoid facies (open mouth, lack of lip seal, high-arched palate, malocclusion, gummy smile), hyponasal speech, effusions and drum retraction, and look at the palate for a bifid uvula . The adenoid can be seen on nasendoscopy (or anterior rhinoscopy after decongestion); a lateral soft-tissue radiograph gives the adenoid-nasopharyngeal ratio (normal about 0.63 to 0.73). Postnasal discharge suggests adenoiditis; pus in the middle meatus suggests sinusitis. For sleep apnoea, polysomnography grades severity in children with severe symptoms, comorbidity or uncertain diagnosis; look for cardiac complications (pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular hypertrophy) with an echocardiogram when severe. Children with Down syndrome need assessment of the cervical spine, because the operation is done with the neck extended. Postpone surgery for two weeks after an upper respiratory infection. Figure 1 A hypertrophic adenoid seen endoscopically, protruding through the choana. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 191.1"],["osec","th:op-191~s2-1","The nasopharyngeal box","Adenoidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The nasopharynx lies behind the choanae , framed by the posterior edge of the vomer in the midline and the posterior ends of the inferior and middle turbinates laterally. Its roof and back wall slope down over the body of the sphenoid, the basiocciput, and the anterior arch of the atlas with the longus capitis and colli muscles. The adenoid (nasopharyngeal tonsil) sits on the roof and posterior wall, in the midline, with lateral bands extending toward the tubes."],["osec","th:op-191~s2-2","The lateral walls","Adenoidectomy · Anatomy you must see","On each lateral wall, the torus tubarius is the cartilaginous cushion around the pharyngeal opening of the Eustachian tube , at the level of the inferior turbinate; behind it is the fossa of Rosenmüller . Lymphoid tissue on and around the torus (Gerlach's tonsil) is left alone: the curette's edges must stay medial to both tori."],["osec","th:op-191~s2-3","The palate that must still close","Adenoidectomy · Anatomy you must see","At speech and swallowing, the soft palate lifts and meets the posterior pharyngeal wall, often on the adenoid pad itself (the adenoid \"pad\" helps closure in some children). If the palate is short, scarred or clefted, removing the adenoid opens a gap that air and fluid escape through. A submucous cleft is recognised by the triad of a bifid uvula, a notch in the posterior edge of the hard palate felt with a finger, and a translucent midline zone (zona pellucida) where the muscle is deficient."],["osec","th:op-191~s2-4","Structures at risk","Adenoidectomy · Anatomy you must see","Eustachian tube cushions , injured by a curette swept too laterally; scarring causes tube dysfunction. Prevertebral muscles, the atlas and the atlantoaxial joint , injured by a curette pressed too deep or a hyperextended neck. Posterior pharyngeal wall mucosa , torn by deep curettage. The palate , which must close after surgery."],["osec","th:op-191~s3-1","Position, gag and check the tube","Adenoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","After intubation, the anaesthetist secures the tube in the midline. Place the shoulder roll and head ring; avoid hyperextension. Tape the eyes, and drape so that no clip or tube rests on them. Insert the gag with a slotted blade over the tube and open it, protecting the lips and teeth, and suspend it. Ask the anaesthetist to recheck the airway pressures and tube position after the neck is extended and again after the gag is opened. Figure 2 Positioning with the gag suspended and the soft palate retracted by catheters through the nose. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 191.6 What you should see The tube held centrally in the blade groove, the uvula and palate in view, steady ventilation. The gag A gag can kink or push the tube into a bronchus, and can break a loose tooth or catch the lip. Look at the lips and teeth every time you open it."],["osec","th:op-191~s3-2","Feel the palate","Adenoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Before you remove anything, palpate the hard and soft palate with a finger for a notch in the posterior edge of the hard palate, and look at the uvula. A bifid uvula, a notch or a translucent midline means a submucous cleft: stop and reconsider (a superior-pole partial adenoidectomy or no adenoidectomy). Many surgeons also feel the nasopharynx to judge the size of the adenoid and sweep lateral tissue medially. Figure 3 Palpating the palate to exclude a submucous cleft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 191.5 What you should see and feel A single uvula, a smooth posterior border to the hard palate, and muscle across the soft palate. Senior's pearl The palate check takes ten seconds and prevents the most regretted complication of this operation: a child who speaks through the nose for life."],["osec","th:op-191~s3-3","Retract the palate and see the adenoid","Adenoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Place a throat pack of rolled gauze in the oropharynx to keep blood out of the larynx, and put a few drops of 0.025% oxymetazoline into each nostril. Pass a 6 or 8 French rubber catheter through each nostril, retrieve the tip from the mouth and clamp both ends together over the drape to pull the soft palate forward. With the nasopharyngeal mirror (warmed to prevent fogging) in your left hand, look up behind the palate and identify both tori and the adenoid between them. Figure 4 The soft palate retracted by catheters; the adenoid (A) between the two tori (T). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 191.7 What you should see The adenoid mass, both tori tubarii on either side, and the choanae above if the adenoid is small."],["osec","th:op-191~s3-4","Curette the adenoid","Adenoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Choose the widest curette that fits between the tori. Holding the mirror in your left hand, introduce the curette with your right, using your right thumb as a fulcrum, place it high in the nasopharynx against the posterior edge of the vomer, press it gently back onto the adenoid and sweep downward in one smooth stroke , flexing the head slightly as you pass the atlas. Most of the adenoid comes away in the first one or two sweeps; remove remnants with a narrower curette, the St Clair Thompson forceps or suction diathermy. With a guarded curette, many surgeons work by feel, with the guard against the septum. Figure 5 Adenoid curettes of different sizes, and the St Clair Thompson forceps for grasping and cutting remnants. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 191.3 and 191.4 What you should see The adenoid mass removed in one piece and a smooth, flat bed down to the prevertebral fascia, not deeper. Too deep or too lateral Deep curettage tears the prevertebral muscles and bleeds heavily; lateral sweeps scar the Eustachian tube openings. Do not cut blindly into a field full of blood."],["osec","th:op-191~s3-5","Control the bleeding","Adenoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Pack the nasopharynx with a gauze pack soaked in oxymetazoline or saline and wait a few minutes (or proceed to the tonsils, which are removed after the adenoid). Irrigate saline through the nose, suction it from the oropharynx and look with the mirror. Treat persistent bleeding points with suction diathermy under vision. If bleeding still continues, leave a postnasal pack for 24 hours. What you should see A dry nasopharyngeal bed and a complete view of both choanae , with no tags left in the choanae or on the tori. Senior's pearl Most bleeding from the adenoid bed is from the raw surface, not a vessel. Time, pressure and vasoconstrictor stop most of it; suction diathermy and a pack deal with the rest."],["osec","th:op-191~s3-6","Remove the packs and finish","Adenoidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Remove every pack (count them with the scrub nurse), suction the nasopharynx and oropharynx, remove the catheters and the gag, and check the teeth and lips. Figure 6 The removed adenoid mass. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 191.8 What you should see A clean pharynx, no clot in the nasopharynx or behind the palate, and the throat pack accounted for. The forgotten pack A throat or postnasal pack left in place obstructs the airway on extubation. State its removal aloud."],["osec","th:op-191~s4-1","Suction diathermy, microdebrider and coblation","Adenoidectomy · Other techniques","Suction diathermy adenoidectomy under mirror or endoscopic view ablates the adenoid layer by layer with less blood loss than curettage and good control near the tori. The microdebrider under a 70-degree telescope (or mirror) shaves the adenoid precisely, including tissue in the choanae. Coblation removes tissue with a radiofrequency plasma field at lower temperatures. Revision rates are similar for all (around 1% to 2%); curettage is quickest but bloodiest. Laser adenoidectomy has been associated with nasopharyngeal stenosis."],["osec","th:op-191~s5-1","Crisis drills","Adenoidectomy · When it goes wrong","Brisk bleeding from the nasopharynx 1 Pack oxymetazoline-soaked gauze pack wait 2 Look irrigate, mirror or endoscope remove tags left behind (residual tissue bleeds) 3 Coagulate suction diathermy under vision 4 Escalate postnasal pack for 24 hours check clotting Desaturation at induction or pulmonary oedema 1 Recognise a child with long-standing obstruction airway collapse at induction, or pink frothy secretions when the obstruction is relieved 2 Act intubate ventilate with positive end-expiratory pressure diuretic on anaesthetic advice 3 Prevent no sedative premedication warn the anaesthetist in severe sleep apnoea or pulmonary hypertension Rising pulse and dark vomit after surgery 1 Recognise swallowed blood from postoperative bleeding 2 Act resuscitate, examine the nasopharynx and oropharynx nasal vasoconstrictor 3 Escalate return to theatre for haemostasis and a postnasal pack under general anaesthesia"],["osec","th:op-191~s6-1","On the ward","Adenoidectomy · Aftercare","Healthy children from three years of age without bleeding history can go home the same day. Admit and monitor overnight the higher-risk children: those under three, severe sleep apnoea on polysomnography, cardiac complications of sleep apnoea, craniofacial or neuromuscular disorders, obesity and failure to thrive. Nurse in the semiprone (tonsil) position with continuous oxygen saturation monitoring, watching for residual sedation. Look in the oropharynx periodically for blood trickling down from the nasopharynx; vasoconstrictor drops treat minor ooze. Paracetamol is usually enough; codeine adds nothing and is no longer used in children. Oral intake is easy after adenoidectomy alone."],["osec","th:op-191~s6-2","Complications","Adenoidectomy · Aftercare","Complications of adenoidectomy Complication When Notes Injury to eyes, lips, teeth, oral mucosa Perioperative Tape the eyes; care with the gag Tube displacement or kinking Perioperative Recheck after extension and gag Airway obstruction, pulmonary oedema Induction and recovery Severe sleep apnoea and pulmonary hypertension Primary haemorrhage First 24 hours Pack, diathermy, postnasal pack; a rising pulse and dark vomit signal hidden bleeding Eustachian tube injury Perioperative Keep medial to the tori Velopharyngeal insufficiency Early, usually transient Prevent by the palate check; speech therapy; rarely surgery Regrowth Months to years Symptomatic regrowth under 1% to 3% Nasopharyngeal stenosis 3 to 10 weeks Rare; from scarring of the raw surface Grisel syndrome Within a week Painful torticollis from inflammatory atlantoaxial subluxation; imaging, neurosurgical opinion, anti-inflammatories, antibiotics, immobilisation"],["ev","th:op-191~s6-3","Adenoidectomy: current evidence","Adenoidectomy · Aftercare","Otitis media. An individual patient data meta-analysis found that adenoidectomy, with or without grommets, gives a small benefit in children aged two and over with recurrent acute otitis media or glue ear, and little in younger children (Boonacker et al., Health Technol Assess 2014); NICE (NG233, 2023) now advises considering adenoidectomy alongside grommets for glue ear, unless there is a palatal abnormality, because it reduces the need for repeat surgery. Sleep-disordered breathing. Adenotonsillectomy is first line; adenoidectomy alone suits children with small tonsils. Technique. Endoscopic-guided powered or coblation adenoidectomy removes more tissue around the tubal orifices than blind curettage; check for a submucous cleft first to avoid velopharyngeal insufficiency."],["osec","th:op-191~s6-3","Alternatives","Adenoidectomy · Aftercare","Antibiotics for adenoiditis, intranasal steroids, and montelukast for mild sleep-disordered breathing."],["osec","th:op-191~s7-1","Where the books differ","Adenoidectomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra uses the Boyle-Davis gag, examines the nasopharynx by palpation and mirror, and uses a guarded adenoid curette introduced until its edge touches the back of the septum, swept down with the head slightly flexed to protect the odontoid; it lists dental malocclusion and recurrent discharge in mucosal chronic otitis media as indications, warns that 10% to 20% of children with Down syndrome have atlantoaxial instability (flexion and extension radiographs), and that hyperextension should always be avoided. Lee emphasises the AAO-HNS indications, adenoidectomy as first-line surgery for paediatric chronic rhinosinusitis, and the polysomnography criteria in sleep apnoea. Myers retracts the palate with catheters and curettes under mirror vision, and gives the guideline criteria for overnight admission and the revision rates for each technique."],["osec","th:op-191~s7-2","Viva essentials","Adenoidectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications, and adenoidectomy with the second set of tubes for effusion. Contraindications : cleft and submucous cleft (the triad), bleeding disorders, recent infection. The tori as lateral limits; the atlas behind. How you control bleeding from the bed. Grisel syndrome and velopharyngeal insufficiency. Who stays overnight."],["osec","th:op-191~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Adenoidectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Skipping the palate palpation. Curetting blindly in a field full of blood. Sweeping lateral to the tori. Hyperextending the neck, especially in Down syndrome. Leaving tags in the choanae (they cause bleeding and persistent obstruction). Forgetting the throat or postnasal pack."],["osec","th:op-192~s1-1","The case at a glance","Tonsillectomy · Operating note","Tonsillectomy Summary Indications: airway Sleep-disordered breathing and obstructive sleep apnoea from adenotonsillar hypertrophy (the commonest indication in children); acute airway obstruction (often infectious mononucleosis); dysphagia from large tonsils Indications: infection Recurrent tonsillitis meeting the Paradise criteria; recurrent peritonsillar abscess (usually after two) or quinsy tonsillectomy in selected acute cases; Lemierre syndrome after acute treatment; PFAPA syndrome Indications: other Asymmetry with features suggesting lymphoma (excision biopsy); tonsilloliths failing conservative care; severe halitosis; febrile seizures with tonsillitis; multiple antibiotic allergies; guttate psoriasis; as part of uvulopalatopharyngoplasty, glossopharyngeal neurectomy or styloidectomy Contraindications No absolute ones; bleeding disorders need haematology input and a clear balance of benefit; acute infection (operate after it settles, except quinsy tonsillectomy); overt or submucous cleft palate makes velopharyngeal insufficiency more likely; uncontrolled systemic disease Anaesthesia General, oral tube centred on the tongue base and taped to the chin in the midline; inspired oxygen below 30% when diathermy is used; intravenous dexamethasone (0.15 to 1 mg/kg, single dose) reduces vomiting and pain Position Supine, the table turned so the surgeon sits at the head, shoulder roll (not in Down syndrome), head ring, eyes taped and covered Antibiotics Not recommended, before or after Tray McIvor, Boyle-Davis or Crowe-Davis gag with blades of several sizes, Draffin rods or Mayo"],["osec","th:op-192~s1-2","Consent","Tonsillectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient or parents Pain for one to two weeks, often worst around days 3 to 6, and pain referred to the ears; regular painkillers matter. Plan for one to two weeks off school or work. Bleeding: within 24 hours in about 0.2% to 2.2% and later (peaking at 7 to 10 days) in about 0.1% to 3%; higher after tonsillectomy for infection (about 3.7%) than for sleep apnoea (about 1.4%), highest after quinsy tonsillectomy (about 5.4%). Some need a return to theatre and, rarely, a transfusion. Any bleeding at home means coming straight back to hospital. Nausea, vomiting and dehydration , the commonest reasons for readmission. Damage to teeth, lips, gums and tongue ; jaw joint pain; a temporary change in taste or numbness of the tongue. Voice change (often a clearer, sometimes slightly nasal voice). Regrowth after a partial (intracapsular) tonsillectomy in about 6%. Death is exceptionally rare, about 1 in 16,000 to 35,000, from haemorrhage, aspiration or cardiopulmonary failure."],["osec","th:op-192~s1-3","Before you start","Tonsillectomy · Operating note","For sleep-disordered breathing, ask about snoring, witnessed apnoeas, gasping, restless sleep, mouth breathing, enuresis, daytime sleepiness, behaviour and school performance, and growth. For infection, document each episode: sore throat with a temperature above 38.3 degrees, tender or enlarged (over 2 cm) neck nodes, tonsillar exudate or a positive streptococcal culture, and antibiotic treatment. Ask about peritonsillar abscess, bleeding problems in the patient or family (and investigate with clotting tests and a haematology opinion if suggestive; routine blood tests are not cost-effective), recent illness, loose teeth, and syndromes: Down syndrome (atlantoaxial instability: avoid a shoulder roll; some obtain flexion-extension films or MRI), 22q11.2 deletion (medially placed carotid arteries; consider CT angiography) and craniofacial disorders. Grade the tonsils on the Brodsky scale : 0, in the fossa (removed); 1, less than 25% of the oropharyngeal width; 2, 26% to 50%; 3, 51% to 75%; 4, more than 75%. Look at the palate for a bifid uvula and feel for a submucous cleft; check the adenoid (endoscopy or lateral radiograph) if adenoidectomy is being considered. Polysomnography is the gold standard for sleep apnoea (in children, an apnoea-hypopnea index of 1 to 5 is mild, 5 to 15 moderate and above 15 severe; saturation below 92% is abnormal), but about 90% of children go to surgery on clinical grounds. Request it when the history and the tonsil size do not match, and in complex children: Down syndrome, obesity, craniofacial abnormalities, sickle cell disease, neuromuscular di"],["osec","th:op-192~s2-1","The tonsil and its bed","Tonsillectomy · Anatomy you must see","The palatine tonsil sits in the tonsillar fossa between the anterior pillar (palatoglossus muscle and mucosa) and the posterior pillar (palatopharyngeus). Its lateral surface is covered by a fibrous capsule ; lateral to the capsule is a layer of loose areolar tissue (the potential peritonsillar space ), and lateral to that the superior constrictor muscle, which forms the bed. The plica triangularis is a mucosal fold over the lower anterior tonsil. Inferiorly the palatine tonsil blends with the lingual tonsil at the tongue base without a clear boundary; superiorly a crypt (the intratonsillar cleft) extends into the upper pole. Figure 2 The incision through the anterior pillar mucosa, preserving the palatoglossus muscle and exposing the capsule. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 192.4"],["osec","th:op-192~s2-2","Blood supply","Tonsillectomy · Anatomy you must see","All from the external carotid system, mostly entering at the lower pole : the tonsillar branch of the facial artery (the main supply), the ascending palatine branch of the facial artery, the dorsal lingual branches of the lingual artery, the ascending pharyngeal artery, and the descending (lesser) palatine branch of the maxillary artery at the upper pole. Venous drainage is to the pharyngeal plexus and facial vein; the paratonsillar (external palatine) vein descends from the soft palate across the lateral surface of the capsule and is a common source of bleeding."],["osec","th:op-192~s2-3","What lies beyond the bed","Tonsillectomy · Anatomy you must see","The internal carotid artery lies about 2 to 2.5 cm posterolateral to the tonsil in the poststyloid parapharyngeal space, closer in children and sometimes much closer when tortuous or medialised (22q11.2 deletion). The glossopharyngeal nerve runs deep to the lower tonsillar bed toward the tongue base; injury causes altered taste and tongue numbness. Sensation from the fossa runs in the glossopharyngeal and lesser palatine nerves (hence referred otalgia through the tympanic branch of the ninth nerve). Lymph drains to level II (the jugulodigastric node) and the retropharyngeal nodes."],["osec","th:op-192~s3-1","Position and open the mouth","Tonsillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Confirm the tube is taped in the midline to the chin. Turn the table, place the shoulder roll (none in Down syndrome) and head ring, and tape and cover the eyes; a throat pack is best avoided, to remove the risk of a retained airway foreign body. Insert a gag with a blade long enough to cover most of the tongue base, the tube lying in the blade's midline groove; hook the upper bar on the upper incisors or canines, not the lip, mindful of loose teeth; open it and suspend it from the Mayo stand or Draffin rods. Protect the lips with a lip and cheek retractor or moist gauze. Ask the anaesthetist to confirm ventilation after the gag is opened. Figure 3 The McIvor gag positioned and suspended, with the tongue and tube displaced down and both tonsils and their lower poles in view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 192.2 What you should see Both tonsils with their lower poles, the uvula, and no tongue bulging around the edge of the blade. The tube and the jaw Opening the gag can kink or dislodge the tube, dislocate the temporomandibular joint or fracture a condyle if forced. Tongue bulging around the blade means the blade is too short."],["osec","th:op-192~s3-2","Grasp and retract the tonsil","Tonsillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Take a generous bite of the tonsil with curved Allis or tonsil-holding forceps in the hand opposite the tonsil (left hand for the right tonsil) and pull it medially and inferiorly . By increasing and relaxing the pull, feel how deep the tonsil lies and where the anterior pillar reflects off it. Figure 4 The tonsil grasped and retracted inferomedially to expose the mucosal reflection on the anterior pillar. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 192.3 What you should see A crease where the anterior pillar mucosa reflects onto the tonsil, tightening as you pull. Senior's pearl Take a large bite. A small bite tears out, bleeds and loses you the plane every time you regrasp."],["osec","th:op-192~s3-3","Incise the anterior pillar and find the capsule","Tonsillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the mucosa at its reflection from the tonsil onto the anterior pillar, starting superolaterally near the upper pole, keeping the cut right against the tonsil so you remove no extra mucosa. Preserve the palatoglossus muscle (losing it can make the palate floppier and worsen sleep apnoea). With inferomedial traction, spread gently with scissors or a dissector at the upper pole: the white, glistening capsule appears just deep to the incision. The incision can be carried over the upper pole to the posterior pillar. What you should see The pale capsule with loose areolar tissue lateral to it, and the fibres of the superior constrictor further out. Wrong plane If you see red muscle fibres running horizontally, you are in the constrictor: come back medially onto the capsule. Muscle in the bed bleeds more and hurts more."],["osec","th:op-192~s3-4","Dissect the tonsil out from above down","Tonsillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Move the forceps to the freed upper pole and pull downward and medially . Dissect from above downward along a broad front on the capsule, rolling the tonsil medially and toward the posterior pillar, with the Hurd dissector, scissors or a gauze-covered finger; an assistant's sucker provides counter-traction on the pillar. Leave the loose areolar tissue on the muscle. As each vessel crossing the plane appears, coagulate it with bipolar or tie or clip it before it bleeds. Free the posterior pillar mucosa along the tonsil's edge. Figure 5 Dissection along the capsular plane, coagulating vessels as they are met; the lower pole is divided at an arbitrary point. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 192.5 What you should see Vessels crossing from the bed to the capsule, sealed before they are divided; a clean constrictor bed behind. Senior's pearl Use the palate's own tension as counter-traction and keep the dissection on a broad front. Tunnelling down one side of the tonsil loses orientation and finds vessels the hard way."],["osec","th:op-192~s3-5","Divide the lower pole","Tonsillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","The lower pole has no clear end: the palatine tonsil merges into the lingual tonsil. Choose an arbitrary point at the junction and divide there, with a snare, scissors or the dissecting instrument, rather than chasing lymphoid tissue onto the tongue base. The lower pole carries the largest vessels: coagulate them carefully while they are stretched, at the moment of division, and remove the plica triangularis with the specimen. What you should see A complete tonsil in the forceps with its capsule intact, and the lower pole pedicle sealed. Chasing the lower pole Following tonsil tissue into the tongue base causes troublesome bleeding now and later, and injures the glossopharyngeal nerve."],["osec","th:op-192~s3-6","Haemostasis, the other side, and the final check","Tonsillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Press a swab into the fossa for a few minutes, then seal each remaining bleeder with bipolar or a tie (a figure-of-eight absorbable suture for a stubborn point in the bed). Repeat on the other side. Irrigate the pharynx with saline and suction the stomach with an orogastric tube (swallowed blood causes vomiting, and retching causes bleeding). Close the gag, wait a few moments, and reopen it : the gag's compression of the tongue can hide bleeding. Look at both fossae and the nasopharynx. Keep the suction and diathermy connected until the patient has left the theatre. What you should see Both fossae dry with the gag released and reopened, the stomach emptied and the teeth intact."],["osec","th:op-192~s3-7","Extubation","Tonsillectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Coughing, bucking or laryngospasm from blood on the cords at emergence can start bleeding or cause an airway catastrophe. Stay in the theatre until the patient is extubated and breathing comfortably. If an oral or nasal airway is needed, place it yourself, between the fresh beds. Send the tonsils for gross examination if that is your local practice, and send any asymmetrical or suspicious tonsil fresh for histology : lymphoma is the commonest occult malignancy in children's tonsils. What you should see A patient breathing quietly with a clear pharynx, both fossae dry, before the tube comes out."],["osec","th:op-192~s4-1","Choosing an instrument","Tonsillectomy · Other techniques","Methods of tonsillectomy and tonsillotomy Method Advantages Disadvantages Cold steel with ties or bipolar Cheap, lowest late bleeding rate, less pain More intraoperative bleeding, longer Monopolar diathermy Cheap, quick, little blood loss More pain, more late bleeding Bipolar scissors Easy, quick More pain Coblation Less pain Costly; possibly more late bleeding Harmonic scalpel, LigaSure Less intraoperative bleeding and pain Costly Diode laser Quick, little blood loss Costly, more late bleeding Tonsillotomy (coblation, microdebrider, laser, radiofrequency) Less pain and bleeding, quicker recovery, fewer readmissions for dehydration Regrowth about 6%; not for recurrent tonsillitis \"Hot\" techniques generally have higher secondary bleeding rates than cold steel with packing or ties, probably because thermal injury and eschar expose vessels later."],["osec","th:op-192~s4-2","Intracapsular tonsillectomy (tonsillotomy)","Tonsillectomy · Other techniques","Introduced in Sweden for sleep-disordered breathing, it removes the tonsil medial to the capsule , leaving a thin layer of tonsil on the capsule so the constrictor is never exposed. With the same set-up, the tonsil is retracted medially and reduced with a microdebrider, coblation, laser or diathermy scissors, stopping short of the capsule. It relieves obstruction as well as tonsillectomy with less pain and bleeding, but leaves tissue that can regrow (about 6%) and is generally not used for recurrent tonsillitis. Regrowth is observed if sleep apnoea is absent, or removed by tonsillectomy."],["osec","th:op-192~s5-1","Crisis drills","Tonsillectomy · When it goes wrong","Post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage in the emergency department 1 Airway first treat active oropharyngeal bleeding as an airway emergency sit the patient up leaning forward over a basin consider early intubation 2 Resuscitate two large intravenous lines haemoglobin, clotting, group and crossmatch fluids and blood as needed tranexamic acid by local protocol 3 Assess a cooperative older patient with a clot or minor ooze look with a headlight silver nitrate, or hydrogen peroxide gargles a clot in the fossa means admission for at least 24 hours 4 Decide active bleeding in a child, or any bleeding that does not stop: theatre now Taking a bleeding tonsil back to theatre 1 Prepare the room fully set up, two working suctions, gowned and gloved with a headlight during induction 2 Induction rapid sequence (the stomach is full of blood) be ready for a difficult view 3 Find it position as for tonsillectomy irrigate away clot press with a tonsil swab with vasoconstrictor while you plan 4 Control suction diathermy or bipolar figure-of-eight absorbable suture for a bed bleeder \"rough up\" both fossae with a dissector to find other sites 5 Finish empty the stomach thoroughly (clots block the tube reposition and irrigate) extubate awake 6 Last resort oversew the pillars over a pack, or interventional radiology external carotid ligation only in extremis Airway fire 1 Stop disconnect oxygen remove the burning tube flood the pharynx with saline 2 Restore reintubate bronchoscopy for the extent of injury 3 Prevent inspired oxygen below 30% with diathermy a cuffed tube moist swabs Pulmonary oedema afte"],["osec","th:op-192~s6-1","Who stays in hospital","Tonsillectomy · Aftercare","Admit overnight children with severe sleep apnoea (apnoea-hypopnea index over 10 or oxygen nadir below 80%), those under three, the obese, those with cardiopulmonary disease, neuromuscular disorders, failure to thrive, craniofacial abnormalities or a recent illness, and anyone with poor oral intake or desaturation in recovery. Others can go home the same day after a period of observation."],["osec","th:op-192~s6-2","Pain and eating","Tonsillectomy · Aftercare","Pain causes dehydration, nausea and readmission, so give analgesia regularly , not as needed: in children, alternating paracetamol (15 mg/kg every 4 to 6 hours, within the daily maximum) and ibuprofen (10 mg/kg every 6 to 8 hours). Codeine is contraindicated in children (unpredictable metabolism and deaths in rapid metabolisers). NSAIDs other than ketorolac have not been shown to increase bleeding after tonsillectomy. Rectal paracetamol helps a child refusing to swallow; a short course of oral steroids may reduce pain and speed healing. Routine postoperative antibiotics are not recommended. Encourage fluids and a normal diet; the fossae look white with slough for the first week, which is normal and not infection."],["osec","th:op-192~s6-3","Complications","Tonsillectomy · Aftercare","Complications of tonsillectomy Complication Notes Pain, nausea, vomiting, dehydration The commonest; mostly from poor pain control Primary haemorrhage (first 24 hours) 0.2% to 2.2%; usually inadequate haemostasis Secondary haemorrhage (after 24 hours) 0.1% to 3%; peaks at 7 to 10 days as the slough separates; higher with hot techniques and in infection or quinsy Airway: laryngospasm, obstruction, pulmonary oedema At emergence; highest risk in severe sleep apnoea Airway fire Diathermy in high oxygen Trauma to teeth, lips, gums, palate, pharyngeal wall Gag and instruments Temporomandibular dysfunction, condylar fracture Forced gag opening Taste disturbance, tongue numbness Glossopharyngeal nerve, gag compression of the tongue Velopharyngeal insufficiency, nasal speech Usually transient; more likely with a submucous cleft Surgical emphysema, internal jugular thrombosis, vascular injury Rare; deep dissection through the constrictor Grisel syndrome Inflammatory atlantoaxial subluxation Death 1 in 16,000 to 35,000"],["ev","th:op-192~s6-4","Tonsillectomy: current evidence","Tonsillectomy · Aftercare","Adults with recurrent tonsillitis. In the NATTINA trial, tonsillectomy roughly halved the number of sore throat days over two years compared with conservative management (Wilson et al., Lancet 2023). Children. The American guideline recommends watchful waiting unless the Paradise criteria are met (seven episodes in a year, five a year for two years, or three a year for three years), tonsillectomy for obstructive sleep-disordered breathing, a single intraoperative dose of dexamethasone, and ibuprofen with paracetamol for pain (Mitchell et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019). Technique. The UK National Prospective Tonsillectomy Audit found more returns to theatre for bleeding after hot techniques (diathermy, coblation) than after cold steel with ties or packs. Intracapsular tonsillotomy for sleep-disordered breathing gives less pain and bleeding, with a small risk of regrowth (Cochrane, Blackshaw et al., 2020)."],["osec","th:op-192~s6-4","Alternatives","Tonsillectomy · Aftercare","CPAP or watchful waiting for sleep-disordered breathing; observation for recurrent tonsillitis that does not meet the criteria. For apnoea persisting after adenotonsillectomy: lingual tonsillectomy, lateral pharyngoplasty, supraglottoplasty, tongue base and jaw procedures, nasal surgery and, rarely, tracheostomy."],["osec","th:op-192~s7-1","Where the books differ","Tonsillectomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra divides indications into absolute (recurrent infection by the 7-5-3 rule or two weeks' school loss a year, peritonsillar abscess, febrile seizures, obstructive hypertrophy, suspected malignancy) and relative (diphtheria and streptococcal carriers, chronic tonsillitis with halitosis, valvular heart disease); lists contraindications including haemoglobin below 10 g/dl, children under three, menstruation and polio epidemics; describes dissection and snare with silk ties in Rose's position with a Boyle-Davis gag; and separates reactionary haemorrhage (within 24 hours, a clot preventing the constrictor from clamping the vessels) from secondary haemorrhage (days 5 to 10, from infection). Lee gives the AAO-HNS guideline, Brodsky grading, admission criteria and the evidence for adenotonsillectomy in childhood sleep apnoea. Myers follows the 2011 guideline (dexamethasone yes, antibiotics no, codeine no, ibuprofen safe), preserves palatoglossus, divides the lower pole at an arbitrary point, and sets out the theatre drill for the bleeding tonsil."],["osec","th:op-192~s7-2","Viva essentials","Tonsillectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Paradise criteria and the guideline indications for sleep-disordered breathing. The capsule , the bed (superior constrictor), and the internal carotid 2 to 2.5 cm posterolateral . Blood supply; the paratonsillar vein ; why the lower pole bleeds. Primary, reactionary and secondary haemorrhage: timing, cause and management. Who stays overnight; why no codeine in children. Tonsillotomy versus tonsillectomy: who gets which."],["osec","th:op-192~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Tonsillectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls A small bite of tonsil that tears. Dissecting in the constrictor instead of on the capsule. Excessive mucosal removal (more pain). Chasing the lower pole into the tongue base. Not releasing and reopening the gag before finishing. Leaving theatre before the patient is safely extubated. Sending a child home on codeine."],["osec","th:op-194~s1-1","The case at a glance","Subglottic Stenosis · Operating note","Paediatric subglottic stenosis Summary Myer-Cotton grade I: 0 to 50% obstruction; II: 51 to 70%; III: 71 to 99%; IV: no lumen (measured with endotracheal tube sizes) Endoscopic Balloon dilatation (with radial incisions, steroid injection) for acute or grade I to II, soft stenosis Anterior cricoid split Neonates failing extubation because of subglottic narrowing, with good lungs Laryngotracheal reconstruction Grade II to III: anterior and/or posterior costal cartilage grafts; single-stage (endotracheal tube as stent) or double-stage (tracheostomy and stent) Cricotracheal resection Grade III to IV, especially with a margin of 3 to 4 mm below the cords Work-up Microlaryngoscopy and bronchoscopy with sizing; look for other airway lesions; MRSA swabs; lung function; swallow assessment; oesophagoscopy and biopsy (reflux, eosinophilic oesophagitis) Anaesthesia General, spontaneous or tubeless ventilation for endoscopy Tray Laryngoscopes, bronchoscopes, high-pressure airway balloons, sickle knife, steroid, rib cartilage harvest set, stents, neck set"],["osec","th:op-194~s1-2","Consent","Subglottic Stenosis · Operating note","What to tell the parents More than one procedure is common; repeat endoscopies and dilatations are expected. Restenosis, granulation, failure to decannulate. Voice change, swallowing difficulty and aspiration (especially after posterior grafts). Airway loss around the time of extubation; possible tracheostomy. Rib harvest: pneumothorax, pain, scar. Cricotracheal resection: anastomotic breakdown, recurrent laryngeal nerve injury."],["osec","th:op-194~s1-3","Before you start","Subglottic Stenosis · Operating note","Size the stenosis endoscopically and grade it. Look for laryngomalacia, vocal cord palsy, tracheomalacia and other problems that change the plan. Treat reflux and eosinophilic oesophagitis and optimise the lungs."],["osec","th:op-194~s2-1","Endoscopic balloon dilatation","Subglottic Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Suspend the larynx with an appropriate laryngoscope. Inject triamcinolone into the stenosis if wished, make radial incisions in the scar (avoiding the posterior party wall), and inflate a balloon of appropriate size for about a minute or until desaturation, repeating as needed. Re-scope at 1 to 4 weeks. What you should see A widened, round subglottis with split scar and no deep laceration."],["osec","th:op-194~s2-2","Anterior cricoid split","Subglottic Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Endoscopically, divide the anterior cricoid ring in the midline with a sickle knife and balloon-dilate; or through a small neck incision, divide the anterior cricoid and first two tracheal rings and let them spring apart. Intubate with a tube half a size larger for about 7 days as a stent, then extubate and check. What you should see The cut cricoid edges separating, with a tube passing easily through the subglottis."],["osec","th:op-194~s2-3","Harvest cartilage","Subglottic Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Take costal cartilage (fifth or sixth rib) through an inframammary or overlying incision, keeping perichondrium on one side and the pleura intact (test under saline). A thyroid ala graft is an alternative for small anterior grafts. Figure 1 A thyroid ala graft, and costal cartilage harvest. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 194.1 and 194.2 What you should see A graft carved with a flange (boat shape), perichondrium facing the lumen."],["osec","th:op-194~s2-4","Laryngotracheal reconstruction","Subglottic Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","Through a midline neck incision divide the cricoid anteriorly (and the upper trachea and lower thyroid cartilage as needed). For posterior stenosis divide the posterior cricoid plate down to the oesophageal muscle and wedge a posterior graft in. Sew the anterior graft into the anterior split with the flange inside and perichondrium facing the lumen (\"parachuting\" it in). Stent with an endotracheal tube (single-stage) or a laryngeal stent with a tracheostomy (double-stage). Figure 2 Anterior and posterior cartilage grafts. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 194.3 and 194.4 Figure 3 Suturing and parachuting the anterior graft, and placing the posterior graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 194.5 and 194.6 and 194.7 Figure 4 A laryngeal stent for double-stage reconstruction. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 194.8 What you should see An expanded cricoid ring held open by grafts that do not protrude into the lumen. The posterior split and the oesophagus Divide the posterior cricoid plate exactly in the midline down to, but not through, the oesophageal muscle. Too deep and you create a fistula; off-centre and you injure the arytenoid joint or the recurrent nerve."],["osec","th:op-194~s2-5","Cricotracheal resection","Subglottic Stenosis · The operation, stage by stage","For severe stenosis with a margin below the cords, resect the anterior cricoid arch and the stenosed tracheal segment, keeping the posterior cricoid plate (with its mucosa removed) to protect the recurrent nerves, and anastomose the healthy trachea to the thyroid cartilage and posterior cricoid. Release tension with chin-to-chest sutures or a laryngeal release. Figure 5 Retaining sutures in the distal trachea and the cricotracheal anastomosis. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 194.9 and 194.10 Figure 6 An inferior thyrotomy with a tracheal wedge for size mismatch. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 194.11 What you should see A tension-free, airtight anastomosis with mobile vocal cords. Senior's pearl Choose the operation by the scar, not just the grade: soft, recent stenosis responds to dilatation; firm, mature or complete stenosis needs open expansion or resection. Fix reflux and eosinophilic disease first, or the best graft fails."],["osec","th:op-194~s3-1","Crisis drills","Subglottic Stenosis · When it goes wrong","Failed extubation after single-stage reconstruction 1 Act reintubate (smaller tube) endoscopy for granulation, oedema or graft prolapse 2 Then steroids, treat granulation tracheostomy if repeated failure Air leak or surgical emphysema after cricotracheal resection 1 Act keep the neck flexed check the anastomosis endoscopically drain revise if dehisced"],["osec","th:op-194~s4-1","After surgery","Subglottic Stenosis · Aftercare","Intensive care with sedation for single-stage repairs; antibiotics (cover MRSA if carried); anti-reflux treatment; planned endoscopies to remove granulation and assess the lumen; speech and swallowing therapy; decannulation only after a stable, adequate airway is confirmed."],["ev","th:op-194~s4-2","Subglottic Stenosis: current evidence","Subglottic Stenosis · Aftercare","A meta-analysis of paediatric balloon dilatation (150 children) found that 65% avoided tracheostomy or open reconstruction in the short term, with failure more likely at higher grades (Lang and Brietzke, Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2014). Balloon laryngoplasty succeeded in all acute and 39% of chronic stenoses in 48 children, with success linked to recent onset, lower grade and younger age (Avelino et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2015). Endoscopic approaches have reduced the need for open surgery as neonatal intubation practice has improved (Myers, editorial comment)."],["osec","th:op-194~s4-2","Complications","Subglottic Stenosis · Aftercare","Restenosis, granulation, graft failure, aspiration, voice change, failed extubation or decannulation, pneumothorax, wound infection, anastomotic breakdown, recurrent nerve palsy."],["osec","th:op-194~s5-1","Where the books differ","Subglottic Stenosis · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers the full ladder from balloon dilatation and cricoid split to single- and double-stage reconstruction and cricotracheal resection, with a thorough pre-operative work-up. Dhingra and K. J. Lee congenital versus acquired stenosis, the cricoid as a complete ring, and the Myer-Cotton grading. The literature balloon dilatation as first line for soft and acute stenosis."],["osec","th:op-194~s5-2","Viva essentials","Subglottic Stenosis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes , why the subglottis is vulnerable. Myer-Cotton grading. The treatment ladder and choosing a rung. Grafts : harvest, carving, placement. Pre-operative factors (lungs, reflux, eosinophilic oesophagitis, MRSA)."],["osec","th:op-194~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Subglottic Stenosis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Dilating a mature, firm scar again and again. Ignoring reflux and eosinophilic disease. A graft protruding into the lumen. Missing a second airway lesion."],["osec","th:op-195~s1-1","The case at a glance","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · Operating note","Surgery for aspiration in children Summary Injection Symptomatic type 1 cleft or deep interarytenoid notch not helped by feeding therapy and texture changes; also chronic aspiration with normal anatomy (50% improved in one study); a temporary material can test the diagnosis Endoscopic repair Symptomatic type 1 and 2 clefts; some type 3 and 4 if exposure allows; a deep notch that improved after injection and relapsed when it dissolved Open repair Type 3 and 4 clefts not exposable endoscopically; types 1 and 2 after failed endoscopic repair Laryngotracheal separation Intractable aspiration despite maximal management (texture changes, tube or parenteral feeding, reflux treatment, fundoplication, treating comorbidities) Contraindications Injection or endoscopic repair: concomitant upper airway obstruction (subglottic stenosis, laryngomalacia), which may worsen; an asymptomatic type 1 cleft. Open repair: use endoscopic repair first when feasible Team Neurology, respiratory medicine, gastroenterology, speech and swallowing, otolaryngology"],["osec","th:op-195~s1-2","Consent","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · Operating note","What to tell the parents Why: to close a gap at the back of the voice box that lets food and drink into the lungs, or, in the last resort, to separate the windpipe from the voice box so nothing can be breathed in. Injection may need repeating as the material dissolves; a lasting repair may follow. Airway swelling after surgery: a night in intensive care, perhaps a breathing tube for a day or several days after open repair. The repair may break down and aspiration continue, especially if the child also has a neurological swallowing problem. Open repair: a neck incision, a possible fistula, scar tissue, a change in voice. Laryngotracheal separation: the child will breathe only through a stoma in the neck and cannot be intubated through the mouth; voice is lost; it is a last resort."],["osec","th:op-195~s1-3","Before you start","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · Operating note","Ask about the diet, coughing or choking and with which textures, what modifications have been tried, noisy or difficult breathing with feeds, weight gain, reflux (spitting up, vomiting, night cough); cyanosis or apnoea, pneumonia, bronchiolitis, chronic cough or recurrent croup, stridor; prematurity, NICU care and respiratory support; cardiac and pulmonary disease, previous intubations and cardiac procedures; reflux medication. Look for syndromes: Opitz (laryngeal malformations, ear anomalies, cleft lip and palate, hypertelorism, genitourinary and ventral midline defects), Pallister-Hall (laryngeal cleft, bifid epiglottis, genitourinary and neurological malformations, syndactyly), CHARGE and VACTERL . Look for failure to thrive, neck pits or masses, cleft or submucous cleft palate, respiratory distress and stridor."],["osec","th:op-195~s1-4","Assessment","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · Operating note","Flexible laryngoscopy: choanal patency, reflux changes or laryngomalacia; a cleft is hard to see this way. FEES: anatomy, cord movement, premature spillage, residue, penetration and aspiration; but some discomfort, poor cooperation, white-out hiding the pharyngeal phase, and rarely laryngospasm or a vasovagal reaction. Modified barium swallow: well tolerated and shows the pharyngeal phase, at the cost of radiation; a posterior pattern of aspiration suggests a cleft. Chest radiograph for aspiration pneumonia. Direct laryngoscopy and bronchoscopy: palpate the interarytenoid area to find and grade the cleft. Figure 1 (A) Type 1 cleft, down to but not beyond the vocal cords; (B) type 2, into but not through the cricoid; (C) type 3, through the whole cricoid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 195.1"],["osec","th:op-195~s2-1","Set up","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: injection","Spontaneous ventilation is ideal (apnoeic technique if not); no antibiotics; consider steroid. Suspension microlaryngoscopy with a shoulder roll if needed; an endoscopic needle; Radiesse, Prolaryn, Gelfoam or similar; microscope or 0 degree telescope. What you should see The interarytenoid notch held open and seen to its depth, with the child breathing."],["osec","th:op-195~s2-2","Inject","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: injection","Prime the needle and, under direct vision, insert it into the apex of the cleft with the bevel facing posteriorly . Inject until the notch visibly fills, usually about 0.2 mL ; consider about 0.1 mL into the medial surface of each arytenoid around the corniculate. Figure 2 Injecting a laryngeal cleft: in suspension, the needle bevel facing back, about 0.2 mL until the notch fills. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 195.2 What you should see The notch filling and rising to the level of the vocal folds, with the airway behind the folds unchanged. Overinjection Material spreading into the subglottis or the cricopharyngeal surface can obstruct the airway or cause dysphagia. Keep the bevel posterior and watch the airway as you inject."],["osec","th:op-195~s2-3","Afterwards","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: injection","Admit overnight for airway monitoring; steroids if concerned; a speech assessment of swallowing before feeding resumes; follow-up with a repeat modified barium swallow. What you should see Quiet breathing overnight without stridor."],["osec","th:op-195~s3-1","Set up","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: endoscopic repair","Spontaneous ventilation with carefully titrated intravenous anaesthesia for 30 to 40 minutes ; room air while the laser is used; no antibiotics; intraoperative steroids. Suspension with a vocal cord spreader to see the whole depth of the cleft, endoscopic needle driver and knot pusher, several 4-0 PDS on P2 needles, CO2 laser if used. What you should see The full depth of the cleft between spread arytenoids."],["osec","th:op-195~s3-2","Create the flaps","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: endoscopic repair","Incise along the cleft with the laser or a sickle knife (oxymetazoline pledgets first if using a knife) to create a laryngeal flap in front and an oesophageal flap behind, deepening with endoscopic scissors. What you should see Two separate raw edges on each side: one facing the airway, one facing the oesophagus."],["osec","th:op-195~s3-3","Close in two layers","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: endoscopic repair","Suture the left oesophageal flap to the right with 4-0 PDS, using the spreader to see; then the left laryngeal flap to the right, with the knots buried away from the laryngeal mucosa, which they irritate. Deep clefts need several sutures on each side. Figure 3 Endoscopic repair: (A) the laser incision creating anterior laryngeal and posterior oesophageal flaps; (B) suturing with a vocal cord spreader; (C) the completed repair. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 195.3 What you should see The cleft closed up to the level of the arytenoids, with no knot on the laryngeal surface."],["osec","th:op-195~s3-4","Afterwards","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: endoscopic repair","Intensive care overnight; steroids for 24 hours; intubate overnight only for significant oedema, and then no nasal or oral feeding tube (pressure on both sides of the repair); a swallowing assessment before oral feeds; home on day 1 if breathing is easy and feeding goes well; repeat swallow study. What you should see A child breathing easily with no tube lying across the repair. Breakdown of the repair Avoid an oesophageal tube while intubated and exposed suture tails on the laryngeal side; cough and irritation contribute to breakdown. A persistent posterior aspiration pattern on follow-up suggests breakdown, though neurological dysphagia may also persist."],["osec","th:op-195~s4-1","Exposure","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: open repair","General anaesthesia, intubated; broad-spectrum antibiotics; shoulder roll. Through a horizontal neck incision, divide the straps in the midline (and the isthmus if needed) and expose the cricoid and thyroid cartilages. Open the airway vertically in the midline through the cricoid and thyroid cartilage, extending down through tracheal rings as needed and up to but not through the anterior commissure , dividing it only if exposure demands. Retract the halves to show the cleft. Figure 4 Open repair: (A) the trachea, cricoid and lower thyroid cartilage opened; (B) the cleft divided into an anterior layer (future posterior tracheal wall) and a posterior layer (future anterior oesophageal wall); (C) closure of both layers with tracheal knots facing away from the airway; (D) an interposed cartilage graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 195.4 What you should see The cleft from the front through the opened cricoid, with the anterior commissure intact."],["osec","th:op-195~s4-2","Two layers and a graft","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: open repair","Incise both edges of the cleft from the lower arytenoids to its apex and separate an anterior laryngotracheal mucosal layer from a posterior pharyngo-oesophageal layer with a round knife or iris scissors. Close the posterior layer with interrupted sutures from the apex upward, without tension. An interposition graft of costal cartilage may be placed, notched to sit between the cricoid remnants and reaching up to the aryepiglottic folds. Close the anterior layer from the apex upward with buried knots , then the laryngeal incision, straps and skin over a Penrose drain. What you should see Two suture lines that do not lie directly over each other, with the graft between them."],["osec","th:op-195~s4-3","Afterwards","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: open repair","Intensive care; extubate in theatre for mild clefts, or keep intubated 2 to 5 days if oedema threatens the airway (steroids around extubation, reflux treatment meanwhile). Drain out at 24 to 48 hours; antibiotics. Feed by gastrostomy if present (oral trial at a week) or a small feeding tube; then a feeding trial or swallow study. Endoscopy at 10 to 14 days to check the repair and look for a residual fistula. What you should see An intact repair at endoscopy at 10 to 14 days."],["osec","th:op-195~s5-1","Divide the trachea","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: laryngotracheal separation","General anaesthesia through the tube or existing tracheostomy; broad-spectrum antibiotics; prepare as for a tracheostomy. Make a horizontal incision that includes any stoma, separate the straps, divide the isthmus, and choose the level, usually between the third and fourth rings or at the stoma. Expose the trachea hugging its wall to protect the recurrent nerves, transect it, intubate the distal trachea, and divide the posterior wall without entering the oesophagus; free the trachea above and below. What you should see The trachea divided with both recurrent nerves safe in the grooves and the oesophagus intact behind."],["osec","th:op-195~s5-2","Close the upper stump","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: laryngotracheal separation","Expose and remove the cartilage of the lowest ring of the upper segment, keeping its mucosa ; divide the next ring anteriorly so it folds and relieves tension; close the mucosa in a running inverting layer like a laryngectomy pharyngeal closure, reinforced with interrupted sutures through the divided ring. What you should see A blind upper pouch closed in two layers, airtight."],["osec","th:op-195~s5-3","Make the stoma","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: laryngotracheal separation","Bring the distal trachea to the skin and mature a laryngectomy-style stoma with half-mattress sutures. Close the wound over a Penrose drain and replace the tube with a tracheostomy tube. Figure 5 Laryngotracheal separation: (A) the lowest ring of the upper segment removed to leave mucosa for closure; (B) the mucosa closed in a running inverting layer; (C) the stoma; (D) the upper trachea closed as a blind pouch and the lower brought to the skin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 195.5 What you should see An end stoma sewn to skin without tension."],["osec","th:op-195~s5-4","Afterwards","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · The operation: laryngotracheal separation","Intensive care, sedated for the first days; antibiotics for 5 to 7 days ; drain out at 24 to 48 hours; oral feeding from about day 3 if tolerated (many still need tube feeds); daily stoma care. Tell everyone the child is now a total neck breather and cannot be intubated through the mouth: a sign over the bed. What you should see A sign over the bed, and a stoma that is clean and open."],["osec","th:op-195~s6-1","Crisis drills","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · When it goes wrong","Airway compromise after cleft surgery 1 Cause oedema from manipulation or injection spreading into the subglottis commoner after longer endoscopic repairs 2 Prevent short operations routine steroids posterior bevel 3 Act steroids overnight intubation if needed After laryngotracheal separation 1 Fistula from the stump inadequate mucosal closure 2 Bleeding from the stoma think of a tracheo-innominate fistula, often fatal"],["osec","th:op-195~s6-2","Common errors in technique","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Poor exposure of the interarytenoid area; overinjection. Too shallow an incision; too much handling and oedema; loose sutures. Inadequate exposure or closure in open repair; suture exposed in the airway. Inadequate stump closure, or dissecting too far laterally and injuring the recurrent nerves."],["ev","th:op-195~s7-1","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation: current evidence","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · Aftercare","Assessment. Videofluoroscopy and fibreoptic endoscopic evaluation of swallowing (FEES) are complementary in children; FEES can be done at the bedside without radiation. Laryngeal clefts. Type 1 clefts that cause aspiration can be treated with injection augmentation or endoscopic repair, with improved swallowing in most children in published series. Laryngomalacia with feeding difficulty and failure to thrive is an indication for supraglottoplasty (Carter et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2016). Multidisciplinary teams (ENT, speech and language therapy, gastroenterology, dietetics) improve outcomes."],["osec","th:op-195~s7-1","Complications","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · Aftercare","Complications Operation Complications Injection Airway compromise; persistent aspiration Endoscopic repair Airway compromise; breakdown; persistent aspiration; posterior glottic stenosis Open repair Tracheo-oesophageal fistula; breakdown with residual cleft (revise with sternomastoid muscle flap or temporalis fascia); granuloma (knots on the oesophageal side); blunting of the anterior commissure Separation Tracheocutaneous fistula from the stump; dysphagia (or the underlying neurological disease); tracheo-innominate fistula, often fatal"],["osec","th:op-195~s8-1","Type 1 cleft or deep notch?","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · For the examination","Type 1 was classically an absent or hypoplastic interarytenoid muscle with a cleft reaching the vocal cords, but no upper limit was ever defined, so \"deep interarytenoid notch\" and \"type 1 cleft\" are now used interchangeably for a cleft deeper than normal. Even subtle clefts often respond to injection, so most paediatric otolaryngologists treat them alike when symptomatic, and injection has helped children with chronic aspiration and normal anatomy, suggesting functional rather than anatomical clefts. The editor notes the rising, beneficial use of injection, both to treat and to establish the diagnosis."],["osec","th:op-195~s8-2","Viva essentials","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The Benjamin-Inglis classification and how each type presents. Associated syndromes. FEES versus modified barium swallow; the posterior aspiration pattern. Diagnosis by palpation at direct laryngoscopy. The ladder: injection, endoscopic, open, separation, with indications. Upper airway obstruction as a contraindication to injection. Two-layer closure; buried knots; no feeding tube against an endoscopic repair. Laryngotracheal separation and the \"neck breather\" warning."],["osec","th:op-195~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Surgery for Aspiration: Laryngeal Cleft Repair and Laryngotracheal Separation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Missing a cleft by looking without palpating. Injecting a child who also has subglottic stenosis. Ignoring the neurological cause of persistent aspiration."],["osec","th:op-197~s1-1","The case at a glance","Exit Procedure · Operating note","The EXIT procedure Summary Indications Prenatal imaging (ultrasound, fetal MRI) showing airway obstruction incompatible with life after delivery: large neck masses (teratoma, lymphatic malformation, rarer goitre or neuroblastoma), CHAOS (laryngeal atresia or web, subglottic stenosis), severe micrognathia; also EXIT-to-resection or EXIT-to-ECMO in selected cases Selection signs Tracheal deviation or compression, polyhydramnios (the fetus cannot swallow), absent fluid-filled stomach, large echogenic lungs with flattened diaphragms in CHAOS Timing Planned at about 35 to 38 weeks, or earlier if labour, polyhydramnios or fetal compromise intervene Team Obstetricians, maternal and fetal anaesthetists, paediatric otolaryngologist and surgeons, neonatologists, scrub teams, a rehearsed plan Anaesthesia Usually deep maternal general anaesthesia with high-dose volatile agent for uterine relaxation (regional techniques described); fetal analgesia and paralysis by intramuscular injection Time available Usually 30 to 60 minutes of placental support; reported up to about 2.5 hours Tray Neonatal laryngoscopes, 2.7 mm Hopkins telescopes (0 degree), ventilating bronchoscopes, endotracheal tubes (2.5 to 3.5, armoured), neonatal tracheostomy set and tubes, Mapleson circuit, fetal pulse oximeter, sterile ultrasound and echocardiography"],["osec","th:op-197~s1-2","Consent","Exit Procedure · Operating note","What to tell the parents The aim is to secure the baby's airway before the placenta is separated. The airway may not be achievable by any route, and the baby may die. Tracheostomy may be needed, with long-term care at home. For the mother: bleeding from a relaxed uterus, possible hysterectomy , wound infection, and a classical or high uterine incision that affects future pregnancies (caesarean delivery advised). An alternative is caesarean section with the airway team on the back table (lower risk for the mother, higher for the baby), or a decision not to resuscitate."],["osec","th:op-197~s1-3","Before you start","Exit Procedure · Operating note","Review the fetal MRI with all teams: where the airway is, how far the mass displaces the larynx and trachea, and whether a tracheostomy site is accessible. Agree the airway ladder (laryngoscopy, then rigid bronchoscopy, then tracheostomy, then resection or ECMO) and who does each step. Rehearse; lay out two setups so that the airway can be attempted on a back table if placental support fails. Figure 1 A large cervical teratoma with a tracheostomy secured during an EXIT procedure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 197.1"],["osec","th:op-197~s2-1","Uterine relaxation and exposure","Exit Procedure · The operation, stage by stage","The obstetric team performs a wide low transverse laparotomy, maps the placental edge with sterile ultrasound, and opens the uterus at least 6 cm from it with a haemostatic stapling device. Warm crystalloid is infused into the uterus to keep amniotic volume and the baby warm. Figure 2 The layout of the room for an EXIT procedure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 197.3 What you should see A soft, relaxed uterus and a hysterotomy away from the placenta."],["osec","th:op-197~s2-2","Partial delivery and monitoring","Exit Procedure · The operation, stage by stage","Deliver only the head, neck and an arm; full delivery triggers uterine contraction and placental separation. Place a fetal pulse oximeter on the hand, an intravenous line if possible, and run continuous sterile fetal echocardiography. Give fetal intramuscular analgesia and paralysis. What you should see Fetal saturations around 50 to 70% (normal on the placenta) and a steady fetal heart rate. Watch the cord and the mother Kinking or compression of the cord, loss of amniotic volume or placental separation causes sudden fetal bradycardia. Maternal hypotension from deep anaesthesia reduces placental flow. Fetal heart rate is the alarm: tell the team the moment it falls."],["osec","th:op-197~s2-3","Laryngoscopy and intubation","Exit Procedure · The operation, stage by stage","Attempt direct laryngoscopy and oral intubation first. If the larynx cannot be seen, use a 0 degree telescope or a ventilating bronchoscope to define the anatomy; a 3.0 tube loaded over a 2.7 mm telescope can be passed under vision (Seldinger-style). No ventilation is needed while the placenta supports the baby. Confirm position, secure the tube with great care, and ask for surfactant if the lungs are immature. Figure 3 A cervicofacial lymphatic malformation intubated orally during an EXIT procedure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 197.2 Figure 4 Direct laryngoscopy and intubation on placental support. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 197.4 What you should see A tube through the cords, confirmed by telescope or end-tidal carbon dioxide once ventilated."],["osec","th:op-197~s2-4","Tracheostomy if intubation fails","Exit Procedure · The operation, stage by stage","Make a transverse incision a finger's breadth or two above the sternal notch, expose the upper rings, confirm the cricoid and thyroid cartilages above, place vertical traction sutures either side of the midline, and incise the second and third rings vertically with the blade turned outwards to protect the back wall. Insert a neonatal tracheostomy tube, tie it, and test bag ventilation before the cord is touched. Figure 5 Tracheostomy on placental support for a large teratoma. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 197.5 What you should see Chest movement and a carbon dioxide trace with bag ventilation through the tube. Senior's pearl Prove ventilation before the cord is clamped. Once the cord is cut there is no second chance on the placenta; the baby goes from a stable fetus to an apnoeic neonate. In a giant mass, the tracheostomy may need to go through or around the tumour, or the mass may need resecting on placental support."],["osec","th:op-197~s2-5","Clamp the cord and close","Exit Procedure · The operation, stage by stage","When the airway is secure and ventilation proven, clamp and divide the cord; the baby goes to the neonatal team. The anaesthetist reverses uterine relaxation (volatile agent reduced, uterotonics given) before the uterus is closed. What you should see A ventilated neonate on the resuscitaire and a uterus contracting."],["osec","th:op-197~s3-1","Crisis drills","Exit Procedure · When it goes wrong","Fetal bradycardia on placental support 1 Act check cord for kinking restore amniotic volume raise maternal blood pressure deepen uterine relaxation 2 Failing clamp the cord airway attempt on the back table (intubation, bronchoscopy, tracheostomy) Cannot intubate, cannot reach the trachea 1 Act rigid ventilating bronchoscope past the obstruction tracheostomy through or below the mass resection of the mass on placental support ECMO if prepared Maternal haemorrhage after delivery 1 Act stop volatile agent oxytocin and other uterotonics uterine massage blood products surgical control"],["osec","th:op-197~s4-1","After surgery","Exit Procedure · Aftercare","The baby goes to neonatal intensive care with the airway secured; plan definitive treatment of the mass (resection, sclerotherapy) and decannulation later. The mother is watched for bleeding and is advised that future deliveries should be by caesarean section."],["ev","th:op-197~s4-2","Exit Procedure: current evidence","Exit Procedure · Aftercare","A systematic review of 235 EXIT procedures for oral or cervical tumours (most commonly teratoma, 46%) found postpartum haemorrhage in 4.7% of mothers and no maternal deaths; failed intubation or tracheostomy occurred in 3.4% of fetuses, and fetal or neonatal death in 17% (Novoa et al., J Pediatr Surg 2020). In a single-centre series of giant neck masses, the airway was secured in all 12 fetuses, mostly by rigid bronchoscopy (Lazar et al., J Pediatr Surg 2011). General anaesthesia was used in most reported cases, but regional techniques have also been successful (Kumar et al., J Anaesthesiol Clin Pharmacol 2019)."],["osec","th:op-197~s4-2","Complications","Exit Procedure · Aftercare","Fetal: hypoxia, death, failed airway, pneumothorax (the neonatal lung apex is high in the neck), injury to the larynx, trachea or oesophagus. Maternal: haemorrhage from uterine atony, infection, wound breakdown, uterine rupture in later pregnancies."],["osec","th:op-197~s5-1","Where the books differ","Exit Procedure · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers the step-by-step team procedure, the airway ladder from laryngoscopy to tracheostomy, and the back-table alternative. Oxford Textbook EXIT among paediatric airway procedures, planned weeks in advance for large obstructing lesions and laryngeal atresia. K. J. Lee EXIT in the management of congenital neck masses and CHAOS. The literature: substantial fetal mortality and low maternal morbidity in published series."],["osec","th:op-197~s5-2","Viva essentials","Exit Procedure · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications and prenatal signs (polyhydramnios, CHAOS). Why partial delivery and uterine relaxation matter. The airway ladder and who does what. Maternal risks and the alternative of back-table delivery."],["osec","th:op-197~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Exit Procedure · For the examination","Common pitfalls Delivering the whole baby. Clamping the cord before ventilation is proven. No rehearsed plan for failed intubation. Forgetting the mother: haemorrhage from atony."],["osec","th:op-198~s1-1","The case at a glance","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Operating note","Paediatric airway endoscopy and foreign bodies Summary Rigid endoscopy Suspected foreign body; biphasic or expiratory stridor (almost always); inspiratory stridor without a cause on flexible laryngoscopy; stenosis, webs and granulation for treatment (laser, balloon, forceps, steroid injection); surveillance in tracheostomy-dependent children (suprastomal granulation, erosion at the tube tip); bacterial tracheitis and plastic bronchitis for debridement; securing a difficult airway with a ventilating bronchoscope Flexible scope The distal airway: chronic cough, recurrent pneumonia, persistent wheeze, haemoptysis, lavage; not usually for removing foreign bodies, but an adjunct for distal ones Difficult if Medically fragile patients (relative); cervical spine instability or fusion, severe Chiari, temporomandibular ankylosis or oral abnormalities that block laryngoscopy (flexible bronchoscopy may be an alternative) Anaesthesia No sedative premedication with stridor or a suspected foreign body; inhalational induction then intravenous access; spontaneous ventilation preferred for foreign bodies, avoiding forceful positive pressure; the endoscopist performs the first laryngoscopy ; intravenous steroid at the start Position Supine, head extended on a shoulder roll in a gel ring; neutral and SSEP-monitored if the cervical spine is unstable Antibiotics Not routinely, unless infection is suspected Tray Age-appropriate laryngoscope (Phillips, Miller or Parsons); 0 degree Hopkins rod; rigid ventilating bronchoscopes with telescope and camera; rigid and flexible suctions; optical forceps "],["osec","th:op-198~s1-2","Consent","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Operating note","What to tell the parents Why: to look into the windpipe and lungs under anaesthetic and remove anything that has been breathed in. Damage to teeth , the throat, voice box or airways; bleeding into the airway. A collapsed lung or air leak in the chest. The object may not come out at once: a second operation after a few days, or rarely an operation on the chest. An overnight stay is usual; a chest radiograph afterwards."],["osec","th:op-198~s1-3","Before you start","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Operating note","Ask about previous laryngoscopy, bronchoscopy, airway surgery or tracheostomy; intubation or intensive care; anaesthetic problems in the family; spine or brain abnormalities; stridor (inspiratory, expiratory or biphasic, its duration, severity and triggers); a witnessed choking episode (present in only about half); cough, choking, gagging, breathlessness, wheeze, hoarseness or dysphagia."],["osec","th:op-198~s1-4","Examination","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Operating note","Check neck extension, mouth opening and Mallampati grade. A laryngeal foreign body causes hoarseness, aphonia, distress or stridor. A tracheal one may give the classic triad: an audible slap , a palpable thud and an asthmatoid wheeze . A bronchial one causes wheeze and reduced air entry on that side. 5 to 40% have no signs at all."],["osec","th:op-198~s1-5","Imaging","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Operating note","Never send a child in significant respiratory distress to radiology: go to theatre. About 80% of airway foreign bodies are radiolucent. Inspiratory and expiratory chest films show air trapping on the affected side (accentuated on expiration); in children who cannot cooperate, lateral decubitus films show trapping when the affected side is down. Neck films may show laryngeal or subglottic objects. The commonest findings are air trapping or hyperinflation and atelectasis, but about 40% are normal . Flexion-extension cervical films in Down syndrome or other instability (their accuracy is debated). Figure 1 Inspiratory (A) and expiratory (B) films of an almond in the right main bronchus showing air trapping and hyperinflation of the right lung; (C) the almond at endoscopy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 198.1 Figure 2 The rigid bronchoscope with optical and non-optical forceps. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 198.2"],["osec","th:op-198~s2-1","Landmarks","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Anatomy you must see","At laryngoscopy: tongue base, vallecula, epiglottis, arytenoids and vocal folds. At bronchoscopy: subglottis, trachea, carina, main and lobar bronchi. Transnasal flexible: nasal cavity, nasopharynx, soft palate, epiglottis, arytenoids, cords. The right main bronchus is the commonest site; the sagittal plane is the widest diameter of the larynx."],["osec","th:op-198~s3-1","Expose the larynx","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · The operation, stage by stage","Protect the upper teeth with a guard, or moist gauze if there are none. Insert the laryngoscope along the right side of the tongue , sweep it left, place the tip in the vallecula , and identify the epiglottis, arytenoids and cords. Apply anti-fog solution. Introduce the telescope or bronchoscope through the mouth and examine the oropharynx, hypopharynx and larynx. Figure 3 Inserting the rigid bronchoscope after direct laryngoscopy, the blade along the right of the tongue and its tip in the vallecula. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 198.3 What you should see The epiglottis, both arytenoids and the cords, with the scope tip in the vallecula."],["osec","th:op-198~s3-2","Pass the glottis","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · The operation, stage by stage","Turn the bronchoscope 90 degrees anticlockwise so the leading edge of its bevel lies in the plane of the cords, pass the tip through, and once in the subglottis turn it 90 degrees clockwise . This protects the cords and vocal processes. Figure 4 Passing the rigid bronchoscope: rotated 90 degrees anticlockwise to align the bevel with the cords (A, B), passed beyond them (C), and rotated back 90 degrees clockwise in the subglottis (D). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 198.4 What you should see The bevel slipping between the cords without touching them, then tracheal rings."],["osec","th:op-198~s3-3","Steady the scope","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · The operation, stage by stage","With the tip in the trachea, remove the laryngoscope and support the bronchoscope between thumb and forefinger to protect the upper gum and teeth. (With a telescope alone, leave the laryngoscope in place.) The side port connects to the anaesthetic circuit, so the child can be ventilated through it, which also makes it an emergency airway. Figure 5 The bronchoscope supported by thumb and forefinger once in the trachea, protecting the upper gum and teeth. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 198.5 What you should see The carina in view and the chest moving with ventilation through the side port."],["osec","th:op-198~s3-4","Examine the bronchi","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · The operation, stage by stage","For the right bronchi, turn the head left and rotate the tip clockwise; for the left , turn the head right and rotate anticlockwise. Clear secretions with a rigid suction through the main channel or a flexible catheter through the side port. With a large leak, change to intravenous anaesthesia to avoid polluting theatre with anaesthetic gas. What you should see Each lobar opening in turn: on the right the upper lobe, then middle and lower; on the left the upper with the lingula, then the lower."],["osec","th:op-198~s3-5","Flexible bronchoscopy","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · The operation, stage by stage","Usually under general anaesthesia, transnasally or through an LMA, tube or tracheostomy (check the size fits; lubricate with silicone; use a right-angled adaptor with a perforated diaphragm to ventilate around it). Apply topical lidocaine to the cords and carina through the side port. Lavage, diagnosis of the lower airway and some laser treatment are possible. What you should see The cords, the trachea and the lobar and segmental bronchi through the flexible scope with the child oxygenating well."],["osec","th:op-198~s3-6","Prepare for the foreign body","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · The operation, stage by stage","Have an experienced endoscopist, anaesthetist and team, and check every instrument beforehand . Ask the family for a duplicate of the object to plan the grip. Suction secretions and study the object's shape and position before touching it. What you should see The object's shape, its presenting part and the swollen mucosa round it."],["osec","th:op-198~s3-7","Grasp it properly","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · The operation, stage by stage","Choose the instrument for the object: optical peanut forceps for nuts, alligator forceps for sharp objects. Grasp under direct telescopic view, avoiding pushing it further down. Keep sharp points inside the bronchoscope or trailing. Rotate the object into the sagittal plane before it reaches the glottis. If it fits inside the sheath, withdraw object, forceps and bronchoscope together , so it does not catch at the glottis, break off or tear the mucosa. Oxymetazoline helps with bleeding or granulation around it. Figure 6 A dry corn kernel in the distal trachea (A), grasped with optical peanut forceps (B), and removed (C). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 198.6 Figure 7 A radiopaque pen clip in the right main bronchus (A, B), grasped with alligator forceps (C), and removed (D). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 198.7 What you should see The forceps blades beyond the object's widest part before they close, then the object, forceps and scope moving out together. The object lost at the glottis If it is dropped in the subglottis or trachea and obstructs the airway, push it back into the right main bronchus so the child can be ventilated, then try again."],["osec","th:op-198~s3-8","Look again","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · The operation, stage by stage","After removal, re-bronchoscope to check for injury, residual fragments or a second object. If bleeding, oedema or granulation prevents complete removal, take what you safely can, treat with antibiotics and steroids for a few days in hospital, and return when the inflammation has settled. What you should see A clear bronchus to its segments on both sides."],["osec","th:op-198~s4-1","Crisis drills","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · When it goes wrong","Obstruction during extraction 1 Act push the object back into the right main bronchus ventilate regroup and retry A child too unwell for imaging 1 Act straight to theatre for rigid endoscopy Cannot remove it 1 Options staged removal after antibiotics and steroids Fogarty catheter or basket for distal objects flexible bronchoscope as an adjunct rarely thoracoscopy or thoracotomy"],["osec","th:op-198~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Not checking the equipment before starting. Grasping hastily, breaking the object or pushing it distally. Letting it dislodge at the glottis or subglottis. Missing a residual fragment or a second object."],["osec","th:op-198~s5-1","After the operation","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Aftercare","Diagnostic bronchoscopy may be a day case, but observe most infants under 6 months overnight with pulse oximetry, and children with significant airway disease on the ward or in intensive care. Most children stay overnight after foreign body removal (older children with normal airways may go home the same day). Obtain a chest radiograph , especially if pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum or atelectasis is possible. Salbutamol for bronchospasm; several doses of steroid for significant oedema; antibiotics if the object was long-standing or infection suspected."],["osec","th:op-198~s5-2","Complications","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Aftercare","Complications Timing Complications During Dental injury; injury to the pharynx, larynx, trachea or bronchi; bleeding into the airway; pneumothorax or pneumomediastinum; failure to remove Early Atelectasis; wheeze; stridor; laryngeal oedema; traumatic laryngitis; pneumonia; post-obstructive pulmonary oedema; lung abscess Late Bronchopleural fistula; bronchial stricture; laryngotracheal stenosis"],["ev","th:op-198~s5-3","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies: current evidence","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Aftercare","Airway foreign bodies. Rigid bronchoscopy under general anaesthesia remains the standard for children; spontaneous and controlled ventilation have similar outcomes in reviews, and the choice rests with the team. A convincing choking history is an indication for bronchoscopy even with a normal chest film. Diagnostic endoscopy. In infants with stridor, microlaryngoscopy and bronchoscopy with spontaneous ventilation show dynamic lesions (laryngomalacia, tracheomalacia) and synchronous lesions found in a substantial minority with laryngomalacia. Optical forceps and a planned team approach reduce complications."],["osec","th:op-198~s5-3","Alternatives","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · Aftercare","Rigid bronchoscopy is the gold standard; radiographs alone are neither sensitive nor specific enough; CT (with or without virtual bronchoscopy) can help with unusual cases; thoracoscopy or thoracotomy rarely."],["osec","th:op-198~s6-1","Which children need rigid endoscopy?","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · For the examination","All the following are indications: suspected foreign body, subglottic web or stricture, a tracheostomy with an obstructing suprastomal granuloma, unexplained biphasic stridor and plastic bronchitis. Laryngomalacia is the exception: it is diagnosed by flexible laryngoscopy and needs rigid endoscopy only if the symptoms are out of proportion to what is seen. The editor stresses a high index of suspicion and a low threshold for rigid endoscopy in suspected foreign bodies."],["osec","th:op-198~s6-2","Viva essentials","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The epidemiology: under 5s, nuts and seeds, right main bronchus, half unwitnessed. Signs: slap, thud, asthmatoid wheeze; reduced air entry; often none. Radiographs: radiolucent objects, expiratory and decubitus films, 40% normal. Anaesthesia: no sedation, spontaneous breathing, endoscopist's first look. Passing the glottis with the 90 degree rotations. Forceps choice, the sagittal plane, withdrawing en bloc. The rescue: push it into the right main bronchus. Postoperative observation and the chest film."],["osec","th:op-198~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Tracheoscopy, Bronchoscopy, and Airway Foreign Bodies · For the examination","Common pitfalls Dismissing a wheezy toddler with a normal radiograph. Bagging hard and driving the object deeper. Stopping after the first object."],["osec","th:op-2~s1-1","The case at a glance","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Operating note","Phonomicrosurgery for benign vocal fold lesions Summary Indications A benign lesion (polyp, cyst, fibrous mass, Reinke oedema) causing a voice limitation that still matters to the patient for work, leisure or social life after all appropriate non-surgical treatment , chiefly voice therapy Not an indication Vocal nodules; a non-specific lesion whose voice has recovered with therapy; a lesion in a patient untroubled by the voice; the wish for \"straight edges\" alone Contraindications Poor compliance with voice therapy or with healthy voice use afterwards; no time for voice rest and reduced voice use after surgery; severe comorbidity Anaesthesia General, with complete muscle relaxation throughout ; a small microlaryngoscopy tube (5.0 to 5.5), placed without a stylet; tracheal jet ventilation if the tube hides the lesion Premedication Intravenous steroid, glycopyrrolate (unless contraindicated) to dry secretions, intravenous paracetamol, an antiemetic if needed Position Supine, neck flexed on the body and head extended on the neck ; ramp the obese patient until the tragus is level with the sternal notch; tape or Velcro over the cricoid or trachea for anterior counterpressure Antibiotics Only for mucosal surgery in a patient with a cardiac or orthopaedic implant Tray Dental guard; the largest laryngoscope that fits, plus special scopes for difficult anatomy; gallows suspension; 0, 30 and 70 degree telescopes (5 mm, about 22 cm); a microscope with adjustable eyepieces; microinstruments of 1 to 2 mm (knives, scissors, cupped and grasping forceps, triangular retractors, elevators, s"],["osec","th:op-2~s1-2","Consent","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to improve the voice by removing a growth on the vocal cord. This is not a cancer operation and it is your choice to have it. The voice may not improve (about 1 to 2%) or may be worse (about 1 to 2%), usually from scarring. There is no reliable cure for vocal fold scar. You must accept this risk before going ahead. Voice rest: complete silence for about a week, then restricted, gentle voice use for another 7 to 10 days. Plan your work and life around it. Voice therapy before and after the operation is part of the treatment. Tongue numbness or altered taste from pressure of the laryngoscope on the lingual nerve: in 10 to 20%, usually gone within 4 weeks. Damage to the teeth (higher with crowns, loose or missing teeth), to the jaw joint, or to the lips and gums. General anaesthesia, bleeding and infection."],["osec","th:op-2~s1-3","Before you start","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Operating note","Take the voice history in detail: hoarseness and vocal fatigue in the speaking and the singing voice, loss of the upper or lower range or trouble at the passaggio (the transition from chest to head voice), strain and increased effort, and above all the patient's vocal demands at work, at leisure and at home. Ask about reflux (globus, throat clearing, excess mucus, cough, heartburn), smoking and anticoagulants. Examine the whole head and neck, then the larynx with stroboscopy , flexible for vocal fold movement and rigid per-oral for lesions, or both. Stroboscopy is indispensable: it shows the mucosal wave along the vibratory margin, how much the lesion disturbs it, how deep the lesion probably lies, and whether there is associated scar , a comorbidity that limits the outcome. Imaging is very rarely needed. The decision to operate is usually made over several weeks with the patient, the family, a speech and language therapist and sometimes a singing teacher. Prepare the patient: one or two sessions of voice therapy if they have had none, stop anticoagulants if medically possible, stop smoking, avoid voice abuse, start acid suppression several weeks before surgery if there are reflux symptoms, and book the time off for voice rest. Senior's pearl The final diagnosis of a benign vocal fold lesion is made by you, in theatre, under the microscope: by its look, by palpation, and by the plane it sits in. Pathologists find these lesions hard to tell apart on histology. Write down what you found."],["osec","th:op-2~s2-1","The layered vocal fold","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Anatomy you must see","From the surface inwards the membranous fold has epithelium , the lamina propria in three layers (superficial, intermediate and deep), and muscle , the thyroarytenoid with the lateral cricoarytenoid; the most medial part of the thyroarytenoid, running from the vocal process to the thyroid cartilage, is the vocalis . The mucosa is the epithelium plus the superficial lamina propria: this is the cover that vibrates. The intermediate and deep layers together form the vocal ligament ; surgically they are one. Figure 1 The layers of the vocal fold in cross-section: epithelium, the superficial, intermediate and deep layers of the lamina propria, and muscle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 2.1"],["osec","th:op-2~s2-2","The two surgical planes","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Anatomy you must see","The subepithelial space (the superficial lamina propria): where most benign lesions lie, and where dissection heals with the least scar. The ligamentous region, near or within the vocal ligament: the home of ligamentous cysts and fibrous masses. Its fibroblasts lay down more scar, so dissection here carries a much greater risk of a stiff fold."],["osec","th:op-2~s2-3","Where lesions sit","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Anatomy you must see","Most benign lesions arise at the midpoint of the membranous fold , halfway between the anterior commissure and the vocal process."],["osec","th:op-2~s2-4","How the voice is made","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Anatomy you must see","Subglottic air forces the closed folds apart and sets up a mucosal wave along the medial edge; elastic recoil and the Bernoulli effect close them again (the myoelastic aerodynamic theory ), 100 to 1200 times a second. A mass on the edge disturbs this cycle, so anything that disrupts the mucosal wave, the lesion or your scar, makes the voice worse."],["osec","th:op-2~s2-5","The lesions and what to do with them","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Anatomy you must see","Benign vocal fold lesions Lesion Recognise it Treatment Vocal nodules Bilateral (not always symmetrical) lesions of the lamina propria at the midpoint, normal epithelium, near-normal wave; breathy, raspy voice from incomplete closure; children, cheerleaders, aggressive voice users Never surgery. They always resolve once the phonotrauma stops and voice therapy is done Non-specific lesion Like nodules but persists after therapy, though the voice has recovered No surgery Polyp Usually unilateral, subepithelial, at the midpoint, sessile or pedunculated, often with a contralateral reactive lesion; from intense phonotrauma or haemorrhage, with dehydration, hormones and anti-inflammatory drugs as possible contributors Microflap excision or truncation if the voice is still impaired after conservative treatment Cyst (subepithelial or ligamentous) Often unilateral, at the edge of the midmembranous fold; encapsulated; from a blocked gland duct, phonotrauma, or congenital; the wave is more reduced when ligamentous Voice care and therapy first; microflap excision if the voice still falls short Fibrous mass Irregular fibrous tissue with no clear border, subepithelial or ligamentous, unilateral or bilateral, with a reactive lesion opposite; chronic phonotrauma or haemorrhage Therapy first; microflap excision if it fails. The ligamentous type does worst Reactive lesion A concave lesion opposite a convex primary lesion on stroboscopy Remove with the primary, or leave to settle: surgeon's choice Reinke oedema Gelatinous material in the subepithelial space, usually bilateral; low-pitched, rou"],["osec","th:op-2~s3-1","Intubate gently","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Talk to the anaesthetist before the case, and be in the room at intubation. A 5.0 or 5.5 microlaryngoscopy tube without a stylet gives you a better view and is less likely to injure the folds. If the tube hides the lesion, use tracheal rather than supraglottic jet ventilation, which neither vibrates the field nor dries the folds. What you should see A small tube lying in the posterior glottis with the membranous folds untouched. An intubation injury A severe tube injury to the vocal folds can ruin the voice for good, far beyond the effect of the lesion. If a fold is injured at intubation, consider postponing the planned surgery."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-2","Position the patient","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Flex the neck on the body and extend the head on the neck (the sniffing position). Ramp the obese patient with a pillow or rolled blankets under the shoulders and head until the tragus is level with the sternal notch . Protect the teeth and alveolus with a guard, an athletic mouthpiece or a moulded splint. Figure 2 Positioning for microsuspension laryngoscopy: the neck flexed on the body and the head extended on the neck, with counterpressure over the front of the neck (arrow) to bring the anterior commissure into view. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 2.2 What you should see The tragus level with the sternal notch, and the teeth covered."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-3","Expose and suspend","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Place the largest laryngoscope that will go in : a large bore is what makes precise, controlled, two-handed microsurgery possible. Use a special laryngoscope for difficult anatomy or for the posterior commissure and subglottis. Suspend it, preferably from a gallows , whose vector of force lets you use a bigger scope with less risk to the teeth than a fulcrum device on the chest. Improve the anterior view with tape or Velcro across the neck at the level of the cricoid or trachea. What you should see Both folds from the anterior commissure to the vocal processes, with the lesion at the centre of a wide field. Pressure on the thyroid cartilage Counterpressure on the thyroid cartilage itself slackens the vocal folds and makes the dissection harder. Press on the cricoid or trachea instead."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-4","Inspect with the telescopes","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Before you touch anything, look with the 0, 30 and 70 degree telescopes, and record the view: the angled scopes show the undersurface and the true extent of the lesion. Palpate the fold to feel whether the lesion is soft or firm, cystic or fibrous. Figure 3 The same lesion before and after microflap surgery, seen with the 0, 30 and 70 degree telescopes (A, B and C before; A2, B2 and C2 after). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 2.3 What you should see The whole lesion on all three telescopes, including its undersurface and the opposite fold."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-5","Set up your body","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Tilt the table head down and adjust the chair before you begin. The favourable position is a laryngoscope at about 40 degrees from the horizontal , a neutral neck , forearms supported on the arms of the chair, and feet flat on the floor or a fixed step. It reduces strain on your body and may improve precision. What you should see A stable, suspended view of both folds at a magnification where your hands rest, not hover."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-6","Inject and incise","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Inject 1:10,000 adrenaline at the base of the lesion: it controls bleeding and hydrodissects the subepithelial plane so you can see it. Make the incision with a sickle or straight knife through the mucosa over, or immediately lateral to, the lesion , not out on the superior surface. Make it long enough to give you exposure. Figure 4 (A) The mucosal incision at the junction of the lesion and the superior surface of the fold. (B) The cordotomy at the lateral edge of the lesion with a sickle knife. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 2.4 What you should see The lesion lifting on a bleb of fluid, and a clean epithelial edge right beside it. The three rules of the microflap Make the epithelial incision as close to the lesion as possible. Do not disturb the normal tissue around it. Stay in the most superficial plane possible."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-7","Raise the microflap","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","With a small curved elevator, develop the plane between the mucosa and the lesion first . It is often easiest to start in front of or behind the lesion and then carry the plane across it. This is the hardest step of the operation; do it slowly. Where the epithelium is badly thinned, as over a polyp, you cannot keep all of it: save as much healthy epithelium as you can. Figure 5 (A, B) Developing the plane with a blunt dissector between the lesion and the deeper tissues. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 2.5 What you should see A thin, translucent flap of mucosa lifting off the lesion, which stays intact beneath it. Going deep too soon Dissecting deep to the lesion before the subepithelial dissection is done removes the countertension you need to separate the flap. Free the mucosa from the lesion first, then the lesion from the ligament."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-8","Free and remove the lesion","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Then separate the lesion from the vocal ligament, usually the easier plane, except for ligamentous cysts and fibrous masses. Divide fibrous bands between flap and lesion, or between lesion and ligament, with small curved microscissors. Remove the lesion and send it for histology. Figure 6 Removal of the lesion and of the redundant mucosa. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 2.6 What you should see The lesion coming away from the ligament in one piece, leaving a flap of epithelium that falls back to cover a straight free edge. Senior's pearl With a cyst, lift the epithelium off it before you dissect deep to it. This keeps the epithelium from tearing. Deep dissection taking a cuff of muscle, stripping the whole epithelium first, or deliberately puncturing the cyst are all wrong."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-9","Inspect, redrape and check the edge","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Retract the flap medially and look for any remaining abnormal tissue. Redrape the flap over the bed. Then palpate and look at the free edge: it should be perfectly straight , with no concavity and no mucosal tag. Hold adrenaline-soaked pledgets on the site for haemostasis. Figure 7 The microflap redraped over the operative site. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 2.7 What you should see A straight, smooth vibratory edge with the flap lying flat on its bed, as in the after views of figure 3."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-10","Truncation, for a pedunculated polyp","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Truncation suits only a pedunculated polyp surrounded by epithelium too abnormal to be worth saving. Do not inject first: the fluid hides the line of excision. Grasp the polyp, draw it gently medially, and cut the stalk exactly at the junction of the lesion with the normal free edge, leaving a small epithelial defect. Hold adrenaline pledgets on it. Figure 8 Truncation of a vocal fold polyp along the dashed line. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 2.8 What you should see A flat free edge with a defect no larger than the base of the stalk. Cutting into the fold Cut flush with the free edge and no deeper. A cut into the deeper fold, or excess epithelium taken, leaves a notch and scar."],["osec","th:op-2~s3-11","Reinke oedema","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · The operation, stage by stage","Through a lateral cordotomy on the superior surface of the fold, suck out and remove most of the gelatinous material from the subepithelial space, then trim the redundant mucosa. Be conservative: overly aggressive removal of the superficial lamina propria leaves permanent scar and hoarseness. The KTP laser is a reasonable alternative. What you should see The fold reduced to a normal contour with a strip of mucosa laid back over the ligament, and an untouched anterior commissure."],["osec","th:op-2~s4-1","Crisis drills","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · When it goes wrong","Airway fire during laser work 1 Stop turn off the oxygen and remove the tube together 2 Flood saline into the airway 3 Assess bronchoscopy for injury, then reintubate 4 Prevent FiO2 under 40%, laser-resistant tube, wet pledget over the cuff, wet towels over the face, saline syringe in reach, eye protection for all Poor exposure of the anterior commissure 1 Scope change to the largest scope that fits, or a special one 2 Neck counterpressure on the cricoid or trachea, not the thyroid cartilage 3 Position check flexion of the neck and extension of the head ramp the obese patient"],["osec","th:op-2~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls A small-bore laryngoscope and a poor view of the glottis. Taking too much epithelium. An incision too far lateral, or too short. Dissecting deep to the lesion before finishing the subepithelial plane."],["osec","th:op-2~s5-1","Voice rest","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Aftercare","Strict voice rest is part of almost every phonomicrosurgical operation: anywhere from 2 to 14 days, usually about 7, depending on how much of the vibratory margin was disturbed, the patient's reliability and your experience. Strict means no talking, whispering, whistling, straining, Valsalva, coughing or sneezing. At the end of it, repeat stroboscopy; once the epithelium has covered the site, move to relative voice rest , typically 5 to 10 minutes of breathy, airy voicing an hour, building up over the following weeks with the speech therapist."],["osec","th:op-2~s5-2","Supporting healing","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Aftercare","Continue acid suppression, keep well hydrated and stay off cigarettes. Postoperative voice therapy is indicated for most patients, both to get the best voice and to stop the phonotraumatic habits that made the lesion in the first place."],["osec","th:op-2~s5-3","Outcomes","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Aftercare","Results are generally very good. In the series of Akbulut and colleagues, most patients with a midmembranous lesion reported a voice handicap back in the normal range after surgery, with 50 to 67% reaching a normal Voice Handicap Index-10 and a mean score under 11, the level of people with no voice complaint. The exception is the ligamentous fibrous mass , which does worse, most likely because of the deeper dissection and the nature of the lesion. Success depends on proper preparation, compliance, surgical precision, the patient's own healing and therapy after the operation."],["osec","th:op-2~s5-4","Complications","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Aftercare","Complications of phonomicrosurgery Complication Notes Dental injury Rare; more likely with poor, missing or capped teeth and a difficult exposure; prevented by a good guard or moulded splint Lingual nerve injury Numbness or altered taste in 10 to 20%, from laryngoscope pressure; usually resolves within 4 weeks; release the suspension at intervals No improvement or a worse voice About 1 to 2% each; scar, or scar already present beside the lesion; deep dissection into the ligament makes it likely Epithelial ingrowth When the flap is not draped back onto its bed and fails to stick; the flap must then be excised Flap necrosis From oedema and rough handling; healing takes longer Postoperative stiffness Several monthly submucosal steroid injections, usually under local anaesthesia, to modify healing Airway fire Only with laser use; see the drill"],["ev","th:op-2~s5-5","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions: current evidence","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Aftercare","Voice therapy first. Systematic reviews show that voice therapy reduces nodule size and improves voice in adults and children, so surgery is kept for lesions that persist after good therapy (Alegria et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2020). Technique. Microflap surgery that preserves the superficial lamina propria and the vocal ligament gives the best voice; cold instruments and fine lasers (CO₂, KTP) give similar results in comparative series. Outcome measures. Record the Voice Handicap Index, perceptual rating and stroboscopy before and after surgery. Voice rest for a few days after surgery is common practice, though its ideal length is not established."],["osec","th:op-2~s5-5","Alternatives","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · Aftercare","Observation , with serial recorded examinations, for a patient whose voice does not trouble them. Voice therapy with a specialist speech and language therapist, to reduce the forces of phonation. Prolonged voice rest is not an alternative."],["osec","th:op-2~s6-1","Viva essentials","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Hirano's body-cover theory and the layers of the vocal fold; mucosa means epithelium plus superficial lamina propria. The two surgical planes and why the ligament scars. Nodules are never operated on. The three rules of the microflap, and why the modern incision is medial, next to the lesion. When to truncate, and why not to inject first. Why stroboscopy is essential. Voice rest: what strict means, how long, and when to move on. Consent: 1 to 2% no better, 1 to 2% worse, lingual nerve 10 to 20%. Why the incision moved The original microflap was a long incision far out on the superior surface with a large, medially based flap; stroboscopy later showed scar across the whole area of dissection. That is why the modern medial (mini) microflap starts right beside the lesion and dissects no healthy tissue."],["osec","th:op-2~s6-2","Common pitfalls","Phonomicrosurgery for Benign Vocal Fold Lesions · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on nodules, or on a patient who has not had voice therapy. Rushing the patient to surgery before full assessment by you and the therapist. Forgetting to discuss voice rest and the time off it needs. A small laryngoscope, a short incision, or one placed too far laterally. Dissecting into the vocal ligament when the lesion does not require it. Aggressive removal of Reinke's tissue."],["osec","th:op-20~s1-1","The case at a glance","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · Operating note","Percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy Summary Indications Intubated adults in intensive care needing prolonged ventilation or airway toilet; can be done in obesity and after a previous tracheostomy by experienced operators Contraindications Cricoid not palpable above the sternal notch; midline neck mass or large goitre; high innominate artery; high ventilatory requirements (high PEEP or FiO2); uncorrected coagulopathy; unstable cervical spine; children; emergency airway (do a cricothyroidotomy or open tracheostomy) Setting ICU, with an anaesthetist or intensivist at the head managing the tube and bronchoscope Anaesthesia General (sedation and paralysis), FiO2 1.0 Tray Percutaneous kit (introducer needle, J-wire, 14F dilator, guiding catheter, single tapered dilator), tracheostomy tube with loading dilator, bronchoscope, open tracheostomy set on standby, ultrasound"],["osec","th:op-20~s1-2","Consent","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient or family Bleeding, usually minor; rarely major vessel injury. Loss of the airway during the procedure (accidental extubation), needing reintubation or conversion to open tracheostomy. False passage or a tube outside the trachea; posterior tracheal wall or oesophageal injury. Pneumothorax, surgical emphysema. Later: stoma infection, tracheal stenosis, scarring."],["osec","th:op-20~s1-3","Before you start","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · Operating note","Palpate the neck with the head extended: thyroid and cricoid cartilages, sternal notch, and enough space between them. Ultrasound shows the trachea, the isthmus and any vessels in the path. Correct clotting, preoxygenate, and check the open set is ready."],["osec","th:op-20~s2-1","Prepare","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Lay out the kit in order on a tray, load the tracheostomy tube onto its dilator, and extend the neck. Plan the incision one to two fingerbreadths below the cricoid. Figure 1 Instruments laid out in order of use. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 20.1 What you should see The cricoid and first tracheal rings felt clearly in the midline."],["osec","th:op-20~s2-2","Incise and spread","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Make a 1.5 to 2 cm horizontal skin incision and spread the fat and strap muscles bluntly in the midline with a curved clamp, without touching the thyroid isthmus. Feel the rings again. What you should see The anterior tracheal wall palpable at the base of the wound."],["osec","th:op-20~s2-3","Withdraw the tube under bronchoscopy","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","The anaesthetist suctions the endotracheal tube, instils lidocaine, loosens the ties, passes the bronchoscope to the tube tip, deflates the cuff and withdraws tube and scope together until the light transilluminates the incision or your finger indentation is seen. Hold the tube firmly from now on. What you should see The incision lit from inside, with the cuff just below the cords. Accidental extubation The tube is now high, with a deflated cuff. One careless movement removes it. A named person holds the tube throughout and is ready to readvance it."],["osec","th:op-20~s2-4","Puncture and pass the wire","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Insert the needle in the midline between the first and second or second and third rings, confirmed in the middle of the anterior wall on the bronchoscope. Pass the J-wire towards the carina, remove the needle and pass the 14F dilator over the wire. Figure 2 The guidewire through the introducer needle, then the 14F dilator. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 20.2 and 20.3 What you should see The needle entering at 12 o'clock between rings, the wire running down the trachea."],["osec","th:op-20~s2-5","Dilate","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Wet the single tapered dilator and pass it with its guiding catheter over the wire in one smooth movement, following the curve, until the 36F mark is seen in the trachea; slight overdilatation helps. Expect some anterior wall collapse; a fractured calcified ring is not a concern. Figure 3 The single tapered dilator advanced over the guiding catheter. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 20.4 What you should see The dilator entering the lumen without tenting or tearing the posterior wall."],["osec","th:op-20~s2-6","Insert the tube","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the dilator and pass the tracheostomy tube on its loading dilator over the wire and catheter. Remove the loading dilator, catheter and wire, insert the inner cannula, inflate the cuff and ventilate. Confirm position with the bronchoscope through the tube and with capnography; then remove the endotracheal tube and secure the tracheostomy. Figure 4 The tube passed over the guiding catheter and wire. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 20.5 What you should see Carina seen through the tube, a normal end-tidal CO2 trace, and equal chest movement. Senior's pearl Confirm with two methods: the bronchoscope down the new tube and a capnograph trace. Remove the endotracheal tube only when both are right."],["osec","th:op-20~s3-1","Crisis drills","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · When it goes wrong","Accidental extubation during the procedure 1 Act readvance the tube through the cords or reintubate if impossible, convert to open tracheostomy or cricothyroidotomy Tube not in the trachea (no CO2, high pressures, emphysema) 1 Act remove it ventilate through the oral tube or reintubate do not keep pushing through a false passage Bleeding 1 Minor pressure, local adrenaline, cuff inflation 2 Major or persistent open the wound and control vessels consider an anomalous vessel or innominate artery"],["osec","th:op-20~s4-1","After surgery","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · Aftercare","Chest X-ray if difficult; humidification, suction, inner cannula care, cuff pressure checks; first tube change at about a week only by skilled staff, since the tract is less formed than after an open tracheostomy and can close quickly if the tube falls out."],["ev","th:op-20~s4-2","Percutaneous Tracheostomy: current evidence","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · Aftercare","A meta-analysis of 14 randomised trials (973 patients) found percutaneous tracheostomy faster than surgical tracheostomy with fewer stoma infections and less inflammation, but more technical difficulty; among percutaneous methods, the multiple and single-step dilatational techniques carried the lowest procedural risk (Putensen et al., Crit Care 2014). A second meta-analysis of 24 trials (1,795 procedures) found no difference in life-threatening events or mortality between percutaneous and open techniques (Klotz et al., Langenbecks Arch Surg 2018)."],["osec","th:op-20~s4-2","Complications","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · Aftercare","Bleeding, accidental extubation, false passage, posterior wall injury, pneumothorax, surgical emphysema, stoma infection, tracheal stenosis, tracheo-innominate fistula (rare)."],["osec","th:op-20~s5-1","Where the books differ","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers single-dilator percutaneous tracheostomy under bronchoscopic guidance, with clear contraindications (impalpable cricoid, midline mass, high innominate, high PEEP). Dhingra and K. J. Lee open tracheostomy as the standard, and percutaneous techniques as an ICU alternative. The literature similar safety to open surgery, quicker, with fewer stoma infections."],["osec","th:op-20~s5-2","Viva essentials","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Contraindications and who should have an open tracheostomy instead. The steps and the role of the bronchoscope. Confirmation of placement. Complications and their management. Why the first change is riskier than after an open procedure."],["osec","th:op-20~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Percutaneous Tracheostomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Attempting it in a neck where the landmarks cannot be felt. Losing the endotracheal tube when withdrawing it. Puncturing off the midline or through a ring. Not having an open tracheostomy set ready."],["osec","th:op-200~s1-1","The case at a glance","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Operating note","Sistrunk procedure for thyroglossal duct cyst Summary Indications A midline neck mass (for diagnosis and treatment); recurrent infection; failed previous excision or incision and drainage; cosmetic concern; possible malignancy Contraindications Comorbidities that make anaesthesia and surgery unsafe; inadequate support for recovery Anaesthesia General; a nasal RAE tube helps if you need to work through the mouth; ask that the patient is not paralysed so the hypoglossal nerve can be watched Position Supine, shoulder roll, neck extended; lower face and neck prepared Antibiotics Clean-contaminated (the pharynx may be entered): at induction and for 24 hours (clindamycin, ampicillin-sulbactam, or cefazolin with metronidazole) Monitoring Standard Imaging Ultrasound first: a thin-walled, anechoic midline cyst, and a normal thyroid in its usual place; CT or MRI for extensive or unclear lesions (MRI can trace the track on fat-saturated T2); nuclear scans now rarely needed Tray Basic soft tissue set; bone cutters or hyoid scissors; headlight; for recurrent or high-risk cysts, a Jennings mouth gag, perforating towel clip, 3.5 inch 18 gauge spinal needle, heavy (No. 2) braided polyester suture, tonsil clamp, and a sterile button or short piece of 14 French catheter; quarter-inch Penrose drain Before theatre Stop anti-inflammatories and other drugs that increase bleeding; medical clearance if needed; treat infection first"],["osec","th:op-200~s1-2","Consent","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Operating note","What to tell the patient (or parents) What it is: removing the cyst together with the middle part of the small bone above it (the hyoid) and a core of tissue from the base of the tongue, because the root of the cyst runs through them. Why not just the cyst: removing only the cyst lets it come back in about half of cases; this operation lowers that to about 1 in 20. Recurrence is still possible, usually within months, and needs another operation. Bleeding , rarely at the tongue base, where a large clot can block the airway; a drain is often used and the patient may stay overnight. Infection , treated with antibiotics or by opening the wound. A small leak of saliva into the neck if the throat is opened, which usually closes within days. The scar in a neck crease, and a dimple if the muscles do not fill the gap. Rarely, a thyroid cancer is found in the cyst; this operation is usually enough treatment for it."],["osec","th:op-200~s1-3","Before you start","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Operating note","1.3.1 The history A midline lump, often enlarging quickly after a cold or sore throat; painful, red and warm if infected. Previous infections , antibiotics, and any incision and drainage (often from a misdiagnosed abscess) or previous excision. Swallowing difficulty, painful swallowing or stridor if large. Vitamin E, anti-inflammatories, herbal products; antibiotic allergies. 1.3.2 The examination A smooth midline lump, usually 1 to 2 cm (up to 10 cm), most often just below the hyoid at the thyrohyoid membrane (about 65%), sometimes above the hyoid or at its level. It moves up on swallowing and on poking out the tongue , because it is tethered to the hyoid and the tongue. Non-tender unless infected; look for previous scars and draining sinuses . Look in the mouth at the base of the tongue. Midline neck lumps in children Lesion Clue Thyroglossal duct cyst Moves with tongue protrusion and swallowing; cystic; most common (about 55% to 70%) Dermoid cyst Does not move with the tongue; contains cheesy, whitish material Ectopic thyroid Solid; may be the only thyroid tissue Lymph node Firm, often with infection elsewhere Plunging ranula, lipoma, haemangioma Each with its own imaging pattern The only thyroid Before removing a solid midline mass, make sure the patient has a normal thyroid elsewhere on ultrasound. Removing ectopic thyroid that is the only thyroid tissue leaves the patient permanently hypothyroid. 1.3.3 Imaging clues to cancer Carcinoma (almost always papillary) occurs in about 1%, more often in adults. Worry about solid or enhancing nodules in the wall and calcificati"],["osec","th:op-200~s2-1","The track","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Anatomy you must see","The thyroid diverticulum starts at the foramen caecum , where the front two-thirds of the tongue meet the back third in the midline, and descends in front of the hyoid and the laryngeal cartilages to sit over the trachea. The track loops close to, and often through, the body of the hyoid , then runs up through the tongue muscle to the foramen caecum; its remnants and secretory tissue are scattered and cannot be seen at surgery, which is why the operation removes a block of tissue. The cyst is lined mainly by ciliated columnar epithelium and contains colloid-like fluid."],["osec","th:op-200~s2-2","Landmarks","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Anatomy you must see","Foramen caecum at the tongue base, the upper limit. Body of the hyoid , of which the central 1 to 2 cm is removed (less in small children). Thyroid notch , marking the midline. Cricoid cartilage , which in a small child can sit high and be mistaken for the hyoid. The lingual arteries lie either side of the midline in the tongue base; straying sideways reaches them. The vallecula lies just behind the hyoid, covered by only a thin layer of tissue."],["osec","th:op-200~s3-1","Incision","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation, stage by stage","Infiltrate a natural upper-neck skin crease with bupivacaine and adrenaline. Make a transverse incision across the midline, usually just below the body of the hyoid; if the cyst is stuck to the skin or the skin is inflamed, include an ellipse of skin. Raise the flaps and go down to the front of the cyst, retracting the strap muscles. Figure 1 A, the incision in a midline skin crease. B, the cyst exposed with the straps retracted, still attached to the hyoid. C, the hyoid held and cleared. D, the hyoid divided with a bone cutter. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 200.1 What you should see The cyst in the midline, with the straps on either side and a stalk running up toward the hyoid."],["osec","th:op-200~s3-2","Cyst or dermoid?","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation, stage by stage","Look at the contents if the cyst opens or you aspirate it. Mucoid or purulent material means a thyroglossal cyst: proceed with Sistrunk. Thick, whitish, cheesy material in a cyst that was never infected suggests a dermoid , for which simple excision is enough. What you should see A cyst in the midline that moves up when the tongue is protruded, lying on or just below the hyoid, deep to the straps. The only thyroid Confirm a normal thyroid gland on ultrasound before the operation. A lump at this site may be ectopic thyroid and the patient's only functioning tissue. Senior's pearl Simple excision of a thyroglossal cyst recurs in about half; a proper Sistrunk in a few per cent. If you are not sure it is a dermoid, do the Sistrunk."],["osec","th:op-200~s3-3","Free the cyst but not its stalk","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation, stage by stage","Free the cyst on its front, lower, side and back surfaces, but leave its attachment to the hyoid intact : the tissue between cyst and hyoid can hold microscopic remnants of the track. Lift the cyst and open a plane behind the body of the hyoid. The vallecula Only a thin layer separates the back of the hyoid from the mucosa of the vallecula. Stay close to the bone, or you will enter the pharynx. What you should see The cyst free on all sides but still attached to the body of the hyoid, with a cuff of strap muscle and fascia left on it."],["osec","th:op-200~s3-4","Divide the hyoid","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation, stage by stage","Dissect just over the front of the hyoid in the midline, then cut the bone on each side of the midline to free a central segment of about 1 to 1.5 cm (needle diathermy in small children; bone cutters or hyoid scissors in older patients). Leave the soft tissue attached to the top of the hyoid , which carries the track upward. What you should see A free central segment of hyoid with the cyst hanging below it and soft tissue still attached above it."],["osec","th:op-200~s3-5","Core out the tongue base","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation, stage by stage","Grasp the free piece of hyoid with an Allis or Leahy clamp and pull it forward and down . With insulated needle diathermy, cut a cylinder of tongue tissue about 1 cm wide upward toward the foramen caecum, stopping just short of the mucosa or including a small disc of it. Take a more generous core if the cyst has been infected before (higher risk of recurrence). Keep checking the midline: a double-gloved finger in the mouth pressing on the foramen caecum helps. The lingual artery Wandering off the midline in the tongue base brings you to a lingual artery and serious bleeding. Check the midline repeatedly. Senior's pearl The cyst is the visible part; recurrence comes from what you cannot see. Keep everything between the cyst, the hyoid and the foramen caecum in one block, and do not trim it. What you should see A core in continuity from cyst to hyoid body to tongue base, with your finger in the mouth feeling the instrument just beneath the foramen caecum."],["osec","th:op-200~s3-6","Close","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation, stage by stage","If you enter the pharynx, close the mucosa with simple interrupted sutures, using the first as a traction stitch to help place the rest. Bring the strap muscles together in the midline to fill the gap left by the hyoid and avoid a dimple. Close the central part of the wound loosely over a quarter-inch Penrose drain , which comes out the next morning, then close the skin in layers. What you should see The specimen in one piece (cyst, central hyoid and tongue core), a closed pharynx if entered, and straps meeting in the midline."],["osec","th:op-200~c4","The operation: traction suture and transhyoid pharyngotomy","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation: traction suture and transhyoid pharyngotomy","Use a guiding traction suture when the risk of recurrence is high (previously operated cysts, or a first operation on a cyst that has been infected) and in large patients with a short, thick neck."],["osec","th:op-200~s4-1","Place the suture before preparing the neck","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation: traction suture and transhyoid pharyngotomy","With the mouth held open by a Jennings gag and the tongue pulled forward with a perforating towel clip, pass a long 18 gauge spinal needle through the skin over the body of the hyoid and out through the tongue at the foramen caecum . Remove the stylet, thread a No. 2 braided polyester suture through the needle, catch its end in the mouth with a tonsil clamp, and withdraw the needle. Tie a stopper (a sterile button or a short length of 14 French catheter) to the end in the mouth, leaving a long tail brought out of the mouth and clamped. Then prepare and drape the neck. Figure 2 A traction suture passed with a spinal needle from the skin over the hyoid to the foramen caecum, to guide the tongue base resection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 200.2 What you should see The suture running in the midline from the skin over the hyoid to the foramen caecum, with the stopper seated on the tongue. Off the midline A needle passed obliquely guides the whole core off the midline toward a lingual artery or the hypoglossal nerve. Check the entry and exit points are both central before you thread it."],["osec","th:op-200~s4-2","Use the suture","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · The operation: traction suture and transhyoid pharyngotomy","During the operation, pull the suture into the wound and pull it forward and down while you core out the tongue around it. It keeps the core in the midline and delivers the upper tongue base into the wound, greatly improving the view of the top of the resection. For a recurrent cyst, include the tongue mucosa at the foramen caecum, then cut the suture and have the anaesthetist remove the part in the mouth. Close the pharyngotomy carefully in layers with interrupted absorbable sutures over a Penrose drain. Alternatively, a curved retractor placed through the mouth into the vallecula pushes the tongue base into the wound. Figure 3 The traction suture delivering the upper tongue base into the wound for the top of the resection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 200.3 Figure 4 For a recurrent cyst, the core includes a cuff of tongue with its mucosa; the pharyngotomy is closed in layers. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 200.4 What you should see A core of tongue centred on the suture, reaching the foramen caecum, and a secure layered closure. Senior's pearl If you enter the pharynx, it is not a disaster: close the mucosa with absorbable sutures, add a muscle layer, drain the neck and keep the patient on a soft diet with antibiotics."],["osec","th:op-200~s5-1","Crisis drills","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · When it goes wrong","Swelling under the chin or difficulty breathing after surgery 1 Suspect a haematoma at the tongue base, which can obstruct the airway catastrophically 2 Act open the wound and release the clot call for anaesthetic help return to theatre to control the bleeding Saliva leaking from the wound 1 Cause breakdown of the tongue base closure (pharyngocutaneous fistula) 2 Act oral antibiotics and dressings most close within days in a healthy patient The lump comes back 1 Cause secretory tissue left in the tongue base (usually, if the hyoid was already removed), forming a pseudocyst anywhere in the front of the neck, mostly within months 2 Act re-excision with a suture-guided transhyoid pharyngotomy or another approach to the tongue base Papillary carcinoma in the specimen 1 Act for a cancer confined to the cyst, the Sistrunk procedure is usually adequate assess the thyroid with ultrasound and discuss in a multidisciplinary meeting for further treatment if needed"],["osec","th:op-200~s6-1","After the operation","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Aftercare","Paracetamol, ibuprofen, and an opioid if needed. Soft diet for a few days if preferred. Drain out the next morning; some patients can go home the same day."],["osec","th:op-200~s6-2","Complications","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Aftercare","Complications of the Sistrunk procedure Complication Notes Recurrence About 5% after Sistrunk, much higher after cyst excision alone; higher after infection Haematoma Rare, but tongue base bleeding can obstruct the airway; loose central closure over a drain Lingual artery bleeding Dissection off the midline Infection Oral antibiotics; drainage if severe Pharyngocutaneous fistula Usually closes within days Skin buttonholing, dimpled scar Adequate incision; straps brought together"],["ev","th:op-200~s6-3","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst: current evidence","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Aftercare","Sistrunk operation. Removing the central body of the hyoid and a core of tissue up to the foramen caecum reduces recurrence from about half after simple cyst excision to under 5%. Recurrence is more likely after previous infection or incision and drainage, so treat infection with antibiotics and operate once settled. Malignancy (usually papillary thyroid carcinoma) is found in about 1% of cysts, mostly in adults; ultrasound before surgery confirms a normal thyroid gland in the neck."],["osec","th:op-200~s6-3","Alternatives","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · Aftercare","Percutaneous ethanol sclerotherapy has had some success in small series and may suit poor surgical candidates, but it can cause inflammation, haematoma, fever and allergy, and leaked sclerosant scars the field if surgery is later needed. Endoscopic transoral excision avoids a scar but has no long-term results yet."],["osec","th:op-200~s7-1","Where the books differ","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra shows the sites of thyroglossal cysts along the track (base of tongue, suprahyoid, subhyoid as the commonest, over the thyroid cartilage, and in front of the cricoid), with the classic sign of movement on protruding the tongue. Lee explains the embryology: the thyroid begins at the foramen caecum, descends in front of the hyoid, and any part of the track can form a cyst closely related to the hyoid; the lateral thyroid primordia come from the fourth and fifth pouches, the C cells from the ultimobranchial bodies, and a pyramidal lobe is present in up to 40%. Myers gives the full Sistrunk technique: deciding cyst versus dermoid from the contents, keeping the cyst attached to the hyoid, removing 1 to 1.5 cm of central hyoid with the tissue above it, a 1 cm core of tongue to the foramen caecum, the suture-guided transhyoid pharyngotomy for recurrent and high-risk cases, and the airway risk of a tongue base haematoma."],["osec","th:op-200~s7-2","Viva essentials","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The embryology of the thyroid and the thyroglossal track. Why the cyst moves with tongue protrusion . The differential diagnosis of a midline neck lump in a child. Why ectopic thyroid must be excluded first. The Sistrunk procedure : what is removed, and why simple excision fails (recurrence about 50% versus about 5%). Landmarks: foramen caecum, hyoid body, thyroid notch, cricoid (and why it can be confused with the hyoid in a child). Carcinoma in a thyroglossal cyst: type, frequency, imaging clues and management. Management of a recurrence ."],["osec","th:op-200~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Thyroglossal Duct Cyst · For the examination","Common pitfalls Excising the cyst alone. Mistaking the cricoid for the hyoid in a small child. Separating the cyst from the hyoid, or the hyoid from the tissue above it. Entering the vallecula behind the hyoid. Straying from the midline into the lingual artery. Too small a tongue core, especially after infection. Leaving a midline dimple by not closing the straps. Removing the only thyroid tissue."],["osec","th:op-201~s1-1","The case at a glance","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · Operating note","Excision of branchial cysts, sinuses and fistulae Summary Indications Any branchial cyst, sinus or fistula, ideally when free of infection; recurrent infection makes surgery more urgent Contraindications Acute infection (treat first; drain only if needed, through a site that can be excised later); an adult \"branchial cyst\" not yet proven benign Imaging Ultrasound for a simple cyst; CT or MRI for first cleft and pouch anomalies; CT after barium swallow or a fistulogram to show a tract Anaesthesia General Position Supine, shoulder roll, head turned away Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring for first cleft type II; recurrent laryngeal nerve monitoring for third and fourth pouch anomalies Tray Lacrimal probes, methylene blue, fine catheter, neck set, parotid set for first cleft anomalies, laryngoscopes and cautery electrodes for pyriform fossa openings, drains"],["osec","th:op-201~s1-2","Consent","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · Operating note","What to tell the patient or family Infection, haematoma or seroma, and the scar. Recurrence if any epithelium is left: about 3% for a first operation on an uninfected lesion, around 20% after previous failed surgery, and higher for tracts than for cysts. Nerve injury: the facial nerve with first cleft anomalies; nerves IX to XII with second to fourth; the recurrent laryngeal nerve with pouch anomalies. A pharyngeal leak if the pharynx is opened and not closed. For pouch anomalies, possible thyroid lobectomy ."],["osec","th:op-201~s1-3","Before you start","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · Operating note","Ask about recurrent swelling, discharge and infection, especially after colds. Examine for pits and openings: in front of the tragus or under the jaw (first cleft), along the anterior border of sternomastoid (second), and look in the ear canal and the tonsillar and pyriform fossae. In an adult with a cystic lateral neck mass, exclude a cystic metastasis from the oropharynx before calling it branchial. Figure 1 The developing branchial apparatus: each anomaly follows its arch. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 201.1 The tracts and their courses Anomaly Opening Course First cleft type I (ectodermal) Preauricular or near the ear Parallel to the canal, lateral to the facial nerve, superficial to the parotid First cleft type II (ecto- and mesodermal) Below the jaw angle Through or close to the parotid and facial nerve to the cartilaginous canal Second cleft (commonest) Anterior border of sternomastoid, lower third Deep to platysma, up along the carotid sheath, between internal and external carotids, over IX and XII, to the tonsillar fossa Third pouch Pyriform fossa (base) Behind the internal carotid, over XII, through the thyrohyoid membrane above the superior laryngeal nerve Fourth pouch Pyriform fossa (apex) Through the cricothyroid region below the superior laryngeal nerve, beside the recurrent laryngeal nerve, often to the left thyroid lobe Figure 2 A type I first branchial anomaly, and a second branchial anomaly with its tract. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 201.2 and 201.3"],["osec","th:op-201~s2-1","Define the tract","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · The operation, stage by stage","Before injecting local anaesthetic, cannulate the opening with a lacrimal probe or fine catheter, or inject methylene blue, so the tract can be felt and seen throughout the dissection. What you should see A probe or blue staining marking the tract from the skin opening."],["osec","th:op-201~s2-2","First cleft, type I","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · The operation, stage by stage","Excise an ellipse around the opening, oriented in or near the auricular crease (or add a separate crease incision). Dissect a cuff of tissue around the tract towards the ear canal, staying superficial to the parotid and temporalis fasciae, without entering it. Shave the tragal or canal cartilage and perichondrium where the tract ends. Figure 3 Excision of a type I tract from a preauricular pit, and the parotidectomy approach for type II. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 201.5 What you should see The tract removed in continuity to its termination at the canal cartilage."],["osec","th:op-201~s2-3","First cleft, type II","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · The operation, stage by stage","Combine an ellipse around the neck opening with a standard parotidectomy incision. Find the facial nerve trunk first, as for a superficial parotidectomy, then follow the tract in relation to the branches (it may pass deep to, between or superficial to them) up to the cartilaginous canal, excising a piece of canal cartilage if needed. What you should see The facial nerve and its branches displayed, with the tract dissected free of them. The facial nerve in a first cleft anomaly A type II tract can run straight through the facial nerve. Never follow it blindly from the neck: identify the trunk first and work forwards with the monitor on."],["osec","th:op-201~s2-4","Second cleft cyst","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · The operation, stage by stage","Use a skin-crease incision over or just below the cyst, incorporating any old drainage scar. Raise subplatysmal flaps, retract sternomastoid and dissect the cyst circumferentially without rupturing it; aspirate a tense cyst to make it easier to handle. Close over a drain. What you should see An intact cyst lifted off the carotid sheath, with the hypoglossal nerve and carotids safe."],["osec","th:op-201~s2-5","Second cleft sinus or fistula","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · The operation, stage by stage","Excise an ellipse around the opening with a cuff of tissue, and follow the tract up along the anterior border of sternomastoid, between the carotids and over the hypoglossal and glossopharyngeal nerves. Use a second, higher \"stepladder\" incision for a long tract rather than a long vertical scar. Most tracts end blindly; a true fistula reaches the tonsillar fossa, where an ipsilateral tonsillectomy may be needed to close it. Figure 4 Stepladder incisions for a long second cleft tract. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 201.6 Figure 5 A fistula opening in the tonsillar fossa. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 201.4 What you should see The whole tract excised to its pharyngeal end, which is ligated or closed."],["osec","th:op-201~s2-6","Third and fourth pouch anomalies","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · The operation, stage by stage","Begin with direct laryngoscopy and pharyngoscopy to find the opening in the pyriform fossa (base for third, apex for fourth) and cannulate it. Endoscopic cautery of the opening (diathermy, silver nitrate or trichloroacetic acid) is now the first treatment for many. For open excision, use a lateral neck incision, identify the thyrohyoid and cricothyroid membranes, follow the tract to the pyriform fossa and close the opening (a pharyngoplasty); an ipsilateral thyroid lobectomy is often needed, with the recurrent laryngeal nerve identified and monitored. Figure 6 Closing the pyriform sinus opening: hypopharyngeal pharyngoplasty. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 201.7 What you should see The tract traced to the pyriform fossa and the opening securely closed, with the recurrent laryngeal nerve intact. Senior's pearl A child with recurrent left-sided neck abscesses or acute suppurative thyroiditis has a pyriform sinus fistula until proved otherwise. Look in the pyriform fossa at the time of drainage, once the inflammation has settled."],["osec","th:op-201~s3-1","Crisis drills","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · When it goes wrong","Tract ruptured during dissection 1 Act wash out excise the surrounding cuff widely 2 Then pursue every visible epithelial remnant warn of recurrence Pharynx opened during pouch surgery 1 Act close in layers consider a sternomastoid muscle flap 2 After nil by mouth or nasogastric feeding briefly watch for a fistula"],["osec","th:op-201~s4-1","After surgery","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · Aftercare","Remove the drain when output is low; antibiotics if there was recent infection. Review for recurrence, especially after previous infection or surgery."],["ev","th:op-201~s4-2","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses: current evidence","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · Aftercare","Branchial anomalies make up about 20% of paediatric congenital neck lesions, and second arch anomalies are by far the commonest; CT fistulography best shows a complete tract when there is a skin opening (Goff et al., Curr Opin Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2012; Adams et al., Insights Imaging 2016). For third and fourth pouch sinuses, a systematic review found success after primary endoscopic cauterisation of 67% to 100%, rising to 78% to 100% after a second cauterisation, comparable to open surgery with less morbidity, supporting cauterisation as the first treatment (Derks et al., Laryngoscope 2016)."],["osec","th:op-201~s4-2","Complications","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · Aftercare","Infection, haematoma, seroma, recurrence, facial nerve paresis, injury to IX to XII or the recurrent laryngeal nerve, pharyngocutaneous fistula."],["osec","th:op-201~s5-1","Where the books differ","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers complete excision by arch, superficial parotidectomy for type II first cleft anomalies, stepladder incisions, and endoscopic cauterisation as an initial option for pouch anomalies with cure rates of about 75% to 78%. Dhingra and K. J. Lee the embryology and the course of each tract between the arch structures. The literature endoscopic cauterisation of pyriform sinus openings is now first-line for third and fourth pouch sinuses."],["osec","th:op-201~s5-2","Viva essentials","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The embryology and the course of each tract relative to the carotids and nerves. Work classification of first cleft anomalies and the facial nerve. Imaging for each anomaly. Recurrent left neck abscess and the pyriform sinus fistula. The cystic metastasis that mimics a branchial cyst in adults."],["osec","th:op-201~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Branchial Cleft Cysts and Sinuses · For the examination","Common pitfalls Incising and draining through a site that cannot be excised later. Following a first cleft tract without finding the facial nerve. Rupturing the cyst or leaving tract epithelium. Calling a cystic neck mass in an adult a branchial cyst without excluding cancer."],["osec","th:op-204~s1-1","The case at a glance","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Operating note","Congenital middle ear malformations Summary Indications Conductive loss with no cause on examination or CT (exploration); a CT finding that explains the loss and can be reconstructed Contraindications Anaesthetic risk; only hearing ear ; a vascular anomaly that cannot be corrected or raises the risk of vascular injury; an otitis-prone child; inner ear causes of conductive or mixed loss (risk of gusher) CT first In every child with conductive loss not due to otitis media: ossicles, facial nerve course, vessels, and inner ear third windows Anaesthesia General in children; local with sedation in selected adults (hearing tested on the table, immediate vestibular feedback); no long-acting relaxants if facial EMG is used Position Supine, head turned, ear toward the surgeon Antibiotics None Monitoring Facial EMG when dissecting along the nerve or if an anomaly is suspected Tray Tympanoplasty and mastoid set; microscope or otoendoscope; CO2 laser (adhesions or laser stapedotomy); drill for bony overhangs; ossicular prostheses Skills Tympanoplasty, ossiculoplasty, stapedectomy"],["osec","th:op-204~s1-2","Consent","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Operating note","What to tell the patient and family Why: to find and correct the cause of a hearing loss present since birth. The ear may be explored and nothing done if the anatomy is unsafe, for example a facial nerve lying over the oval window. Hearing may not improve , or may worsen, rarely to deafness, especially if inner ear fluid gushes out. Dizziness , taste change, a hole in the eardrum. Facial weakness , usually temporary from the local anaesthetic. Bleeding from an abnormal vessel, rarely needing further intervention. Later: the prosthesis may slip or extrude; a cholesteatoma can form. Hearing aids are a good alternative."],["osec","th:op-204~s1-3","Before you start","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Operating note","Take an otological history: the newborn screen, how long the loss has been present (since childhood suggests congenital), otalgia, otorrhoea, tinnitus, vertigo, previous ear surgery, family hearing loss and surgery, speech, language and school, and systemic features suggesting a syndrome. Look for pinna anomalies, preauricular tags or pits, and first arch anomalies (Treacher Collins), which also warn of a difficult airway. Check that the canal is wide enough to work through, look for malleus or vascular anomalies through the drum, and confirm the audiogram with tuning forks."],["osec","th:op-204~s1-4","Tests and CT","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Operating note","Obtain an audiogram, which is hard in children: conductive loss from glue ear is common, so confirm that the air-bone gap persists after the middle ear aerates ; tympanometry helps. CT of the temporal bones in any child with conductive loss not due to otitis media: look at each ossicle and the facial nerve course, and for inner ear causes, where surgery is not indicated. Figure 1 CT of a child with left conductive loss and a large vestibular aqueduct (arrow); no surgery was recommended. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.1 Third windows and the gusher A large vestibular aqueduct or superior canal dehiscence causes an air-bone gap through a third window. A dilated internal auditory canal (X-linked gusher syndrome, linked to POU3F4 ) or a patent cochlear aqueduct means perilymph may gush when the footplate is opened. None of these is corrected by middle ear surgery."],["osec","th:op-204~s2-1","Landmarks","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Anatomy you must see","The malleus; the incus and stapes superstructure; the footplate; the promontory and round window; the cochleariform process and horizontal facial nerve; and aberrant vessels: a high jugular bulb , an aberrant carotid , a persistent stapedial artery ."],["osec","th:op-204~s3-1","Enter the middle ear","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · The operation, stage by stage","Inject the four quadrants of the canal with local anaesthetic and adrenaline. Raise a tympanomeatal flap high enough to remove scutum if needed. Beware a high jugular bulb when entering posteroinferiorly. Identify the landmarks and palpate the ossicles to find the level of fixation. Figure 2 The incision for the tympanomeatal flap. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.2 What you should see The level of fixation: which ossicle moves and which does not when each is touched."],["osec","th:op-204~s3-2","Principles of reconstruction","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · The operation, stage by stage","Use autologous material (incus interposition, cartilage) or prostheses (titanium, Plastipore, hydroxyapatite, bone cement) by preference, in an aerated ear. Size carefully: too short conducts poorly and slips; too long extrudes or subluxes into the vestibule. Some TORPs and PORPs need cartilage against extrusion. What you should see A measured gap and an aerated middle ear with healthy mucosa."],["osec","th:op-204~s3-3","The stapes","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · The operation, stage by stage","The commonest ossicular anomaly, but significant only if hearing is affected (a monopedal stapes may be harmless). Assess the crura, obturator foramen, mobility and incudostapedial attachment. Bony bridges to the promontory: fracture them or divide them with the CO2 laser. Superstructure separate from the footplate: reconstruct with an ossicular or stapes prosthesis according to the remaining ossicles and footplate mobility. Congenital footplate fixation: the footplate blends with the otic capsule with no visible annular ligament; isolated fixation does well, worse with incudomalleal anomalies. A thick footplate may need a drill-out : saucerise the oval window until it is blue-lined, fenestrate, and place a stapes prosthesis. Footplate defects: rare, with inner ear anomalies, presenting with recurrent meningitis or CSF otorrhoea or rhinorrhoea; remove the remnant and obliterate the opening with soft tissue. Figure 3 Stapes anomalies: (A) monopedal; (B) fused crura and a malformed footplate; (C) a bony bridge to the promontory; (D) superstructure separate from the footplate; (E) congenital footplate fixation without an annular ligament. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.3 Figure 4 The stapes of a child with CSF rhinorrhoea and a footplate defect. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.4 What you should see The shape of the stapes, whether its footplate moves, and the oval window niche with the facial nerve above it."],["osec","th:op-204~s3-4","The incus","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · The operation, stage by stage","Hypoplastic long or lenticular process with a fibrous incudostapedial joint , the commonest: reattach with bone cement, or use an incus interposition or PORP. Incudomalleal fusion , common in atresia but also isolated: divide bony attachments with a malleus nipper or laser; mobilising epitympanic fixation tends to refix, so separating the incudostapedial joint and using an interposition or PORP is an alternative. Figure 5 A fibrous incudostapedial joint. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.5 Figure 6 (A) Fused malleus and incus; (B) on CT (arrow). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.6 What you should see The length of the long process and whether it reaches the stapes head."],["osec","th:op-204~s3-5","The malleus","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · The operation, stage by stage","Uncommon. Complete absence occurs only with multiple anomalies. An absent manubrium : separate the incudostapedial joint and place a PORP on the stapes. A malleus detached from the drum : graft medial to the malleus or run a PORP from stapes to drum. Malleus fixation at the scutum or to the incus refixes after simple division, so separate the incudostapedial joint, divide the manubrium from the head, and use a PORP or interposition. Figure 7 (A) Absent manubrium; (B) repaired with a PORP. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.7 What you should see Whether the handle is present and attached to the drum, and whether the head moves in the attic."],["osec","th:op-204~s3-6","The facial nerve","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · The operation, stage by stage","A dehiscent horizontal segment is common enough to be a normal variant: recognise it and avoid it. A prominent overhang can be retracted gently, and a stapes prosthesis may even indent the epineurium. A nerve that splits into branches , lies outside its canal, or crosses the oval window : abandon the operation. The vertical segment may run forward across the hypotympanum. Figure 8 (A) The facial nerve crossing the footplate; (B) on CT (arrow). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.8 What you should see The nerve's course before any instrument goes near the oval window. The nerve over the oval window If the facial nerve covers the footplate, stop. Hearing aids are safer than drilling past it."],["osec","th:op-204~s3-7","The vessels","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · The operation, stage by stage","Aberrant carotid: a mass on the promontory on CT entering the horizontal carotid canal through a dehiscent lateral plate. Not correctable; mistaking it for a glomus tumour and puncturing or biopsying it causes major bleeding and neurological harm. High jugular bulb: reaching above the floor of the canal, occasionally with a diverticulum; usually silent; if it causes pulsatile tinnitus or conductive loss, skeletonise it and push it below the round window with cartilage or cortical bone. Persistent stapedial artery: small remnants stop bleeding on their own; with a large one, reach the footplate in front of or behind it. Figure 9 (A) An aberrant carotid seen through the drum; (B) on CT; (C) a high jugular bulb seen through the drum; (D) a high bulb touching the stapes superstructure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.9 Figure 10 A large persistent stapedial artery. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 204.10 What you should see A pulsating red mass on the promontory, or a blue dome in the hypotympanum: recognised and left alone."],["osec","th:op-204~s3-8","The round window","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · The operation, stage by stage","Congenital absence is rare (failure of the cartilaginous ring that stops bone forming there). Do not drill a new window in the promontory: the risk of sensorineural loss is high. What you should see A smooth promontory with no niche, on CT and in the ear."],["osec","th:op-204~s4-1","Crisis drills","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · When it goes wrong","Perilymph gusher 1 Act head up do not suction the vestibule fascia graft over the opening consider an intraoperative lumbar drain if it continues 2 Afterwards head up at least 30 degrees stool softeners lumbar drain if drainage persists review CT for an inner ear anomaly Vascular injury 1 Jugular bulb packing with Surgicel usually stops it 2 Carotid potentially catastrophic pack the ear, call interventional radiology urgently, cross-match blood Facial weakness after surgery 1 Immediate, with local anaesthetic used observe briefly it wears off 2 Delayed expectant (oedema) 3 Immediate, complete and unexplained consider re-exploration Severe vertigo 1 Think fistula, subluxed footplate, a prosthesis too long in the vestibule sensorineural loss may follow 2 Act consider exploration"],["osec","th:op-204~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Too small a flap and a poor view. Not testing the mobility of each part of the chain. A poorly sized prosthesis."],["osec","th:op-204~s5-1","After the operation","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Aftercare","Ear dressing for 2 to 3 days; limited activity and no water in the canal for 4 weeks (an ear plug or cotton wool with petroleum jelly or antibiotic ointment from day 3). Expect a conductive loss from packing at first; test with a tuning fork."],["osec","th:op-204~s5-2","Complications","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Aftercare","Complications Timing Complication Early Facial weakness; deafness; gusher; vertigo; drum perforation (fascia graft); vascular injury; CSF leak; taste change Late Conductive loss from a displaced or extruded prosthesis; cholesteatoma from trapped epithelium"],["ev","th:op-204~s5-3","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear: current evidence","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Aftercare","Atresia repair. The Jahrsdoerfer score predicts hearing results, and a score of 7 or more is usually required (Jahrsdoerfer et al., Am J Otol 1992). Even in expert hands, many patients do not achieve an air–bone gap under 30 dB long term, and restenosis and facial nerve risk are real, so bone-conduction devices (a headband in infancy, then an implant) or active middle ear implants are now chosen by most families. Microtia. Autologous rib cartilage reconstruction at about eight to ten years remains the standard; porous polyethylene frameworks allow earlier, single-stage reconstruction with a higher exposure risk. Hearing first: start a bone-conduction aid early, even for unilateral atresia."],["osec","th:op-204~s5-3","Alternatives","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · Aftercare","Hearing aids for those unsuitable for surgery; observation for minimal loss."],["osec","th:op-204~s6-1","Congenital footplate fixation or juvenile otosclerosis?","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · For the examination","Stapes surgery in children is uncommon and debated, as aids work well and some prefer the child to share the decision. Juvenile otosclerosis presents later with progressive low-frequency loss and a family history in 53% ; congenital footplate fixation presents earlier with greater loss and a family history in only 9.8% , and is the commoner cause. In a meta-analysis the air-bone gap closed to under 10 dB in 80.2% of juvenile otosclerosis and 54% of congenital fixation (69.9% overall); the two are usually told apart only at surgery. The editor notes how little attention congenital conductive loss has had compared with cochlear implantation, and that timing and technique remain unsettled."],["osec","th:op-204~s6-2","Viva essentials","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for CT for every child with unexplained conductive loss. Inner ear third windows and the gusher ear: when not to operate. The anomalies of stapes, incus and malleus, and the reconstruction for each. Facial nerve variants and when to abandon. Aberrant carotid, high bulb and persistent stapedial artery. No promontory fenestration for an absent round window. Managing a gusher and a carotid injury. Congenital fixation versus juvenile otosclerosis."],["osec","th:op-204~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Congenital Malformations of the Middle Ear · For the examination","Common pitfalls Biopsying a red mass behind the drum before CT. Operating on an only hearing ear. Suctioning an open vestibule."],["osec","th:op-205~s1-1","The case at a glance","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · Operating note","Paediatric cochlear implantation Summary Indications Bilateral severe to profound sensorineural loss with limited benefit from a hearing aid trial; unilateral profound loss with a progressive or at-risk other ear (for example enlarged vestibular aqueduct); expanding criteria for single-sided deafness and partial deafness Timing As early as possible, commonly from about 9 to 12 months for congenital deafness; urgently after meningitis Contraindications Absent cochlea or cochlear nerve (consider auditory brainstem implant); active middle ear infection (treat first); no family commitment to rehabilitation Work-up Audiology including ABR and aided testing; high-resolution CT and MRI (cochlear nerve, malformation, ossification); genetic and CMV testing; eye review; vaccination against pneumococcus and Haemophilus Anaesthesia General Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring; intraoperative impedance and neural response telemetry Tray Mastoid set, otology drill, templates, device-specific insertion tools, fascia and muscle for sealing"],["osec","th:op-205~s1-2","Consent","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · Operating note","What to tell the family Facial nerve injury (rare), taste change from the chorda tympani. Wound problems or device exposure; meningitis (vaccinate); device failure needing reimplantation. Vertigo, usually short-lived. Residual hearing in the implanted ear will probably be lost. Outcomes depend on age at implantation, cause, other disabilities and, above all, consistent use and therapy. MRI restrictions and the magnet."],["osec","th:op-205~s1-3","Before you start","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · Operating note","Confirm the cochlea and nerve on imaging: a hypoplastic or absent cochlear nerve changes the plan. Look for malformations (incomplete partition, common cavity, enlarged vestibular aqueduct), a CSF gusher risk, an abnormal facial nerve course, and early fibrosis or ossification after meningitis. Plan bilateral simultaneous implantation where offered. Mark the receiver site symmetrically for two sides."],["osec","th:op-205~s2-1","Plan the site and the pocket","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · The operation, stage by stage","Shave the scalp, mark the incision and use templates for the receiver and the processor, tattooing the bone with methylene blue. Angle the receiver at least 45 degrees above horizontal, and more vertical in infants so it does not lie over the occiput. Raise an anteriorly based flap to temporalis fascia, then a tight subperiosteal pocket for the receiver; check it with the dummy. Harvest fascia and muscle for sealing. Figure 1 Marking the receiver well with the template and methylene blue. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 205.1 Figure 2 Checking the subperiosteal pocket with a template. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 205.2 What you should see A snug pocket that holds the dummy without sutures, well behind the processor position."],["osec","th:op-205~s2-2","Mastoidectomy and facial recess","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · The operation, stage by stage","Perform a simple mastoidectomy, identify the tegmen, the lateral canal and the short process of the incus, and thin the posterior canal wall to translucency. Open the facial recess: the short process points to it, the incus buttress is its upper limit, the facial nerve posteromedial and the chorda anterolateral. Figure 3 The facial recess before and after opening. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 205.3 What you should see The stapedial tendon, incudostapedial joint and round window niche through the recess. The small child's facial nerve In infants the mastoid tip is undeveloped, so the facial nerve exits superficially at the stylomastoid foramen, and the facial recess is small. Keep the incision and dissection high and the drill on diamonds near the nerve."],["osec","th:op-205~s2-3","Prepare the round window","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · The operation, stage by stage","Clear the promontory mucosa around the niche (adrenaline-soaked Gelfoam stops bleeding), then drill the round window overhang with a 1 to 1.5 mm diamond at low speed until the membrane is seen. Figure 4 Removing the round window overhang to expose the membrane. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 205.4 What you should see The round window membrane facing you through the recess."],["osec","th:op-205~s2-4","Seat the receiver","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · The operation, stage by stage","Drill a shallow well if needed to lower the device profile; in infants the calvarium may be thin enough to expose dura, which is accepted but must not be breached. Secure the receiver with tie-down sutures or a tight pocket. Figure 5 Tie-down sutures over the receiver. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 205.5 What you should see A receiver that does not move, flush with the skull."],["osec","th:op-205~s2-5","Insert the electrode","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the round window membrane (or make a small cochleostomy anterior and inferior to it if the membrane is inaccessible) and insert the electrode slowly along the scala tympani to the device's depth, without force. Seal around the electrode with fascia or muscle. Check impedances and neural responses; take an X-ray if in doubt. Figure 6 Electrode insertion through the round window. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 205.6 Figure 7 A cochleostomy at the basal turn when the round window cannot be used. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 205.9 What you should see Smooth insertion to the marker, sealed entry, and normal telemetry. Senior's pearl Slow, gentle insertion saves the cochlea. Stop at resistance; never push through it. A kinked or buckled electrode belongs in the basal turn only in a malformed or ossified cochlea, never in a normal one."],["osec","th:op-205~s2-6","The ossified or malformed cochlea","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · The operation, stage by stage","After meningitis, drill through obliterated basal turn bone (cochlear drill-out) or use a split or short electrode; implant early before ossification advances. In a malformed cochlea, expect a gusher: prepare to pack the cochleostomy firmly with muscle and fascia, and use an electrode designed for common cavities. Figure 8 Cochlear drill-out for an ossified basal turn. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 205.7 What you should see An electrode inserted as fully as the anatomy allows, with any leak sealed."],["osec","th:op-205~s3-1","Crisis drills","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · When it goes wrong","CSF gusher on opening the cochlea 1 Act head up wait for the flow to slow insert the electrode 2 Seal pack firmly with muscle and fascia around the electrode consider a lumbar drain if it persists Facial weakness on waking 1 Check local anaesthetic effect observe 2 to 3 hours 2 Persisting complete palsy CT and re-exploration steroids for delayed weakness Device exposed or infected 1 Act antibiotics flap repair if small 2 Not settling explant, cutting the electrode and leaving it in the cochlea to keep the lumen for reimplantation"],["osec","th:op-205~s4-1","After surgery","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · Aftercare","Pressure dressing for a day. Switch on at 2 to 4 weeks. The result depends on months to years of programming (mapping), auditory-verbal therapy and full-time use; the family is part of the team. Keep vaccinations up to date."],["ev","th:op-205~s4-2","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation: current evidence","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · Aftercare","Age matters. Children with congenital deafness implanted before 12 months had better receptive language up to 2 years after implantation than those implanted between 12 and 24 months (Mitchell et al., Laryngoscope 2020). Wound complications. A meta-analysis of 21,838 implants found wound complications in 0% to 22% of series overall, slightly less often in children (2.4%) than adults (2.9%) (Ekman et al., Laryngoscope 2023). After meningitis. Among 95 children deafened by meningitis, 32% had cochlear ossification, more often with a longer interval to scanning; CT was 88% sensitive for obliteration found at surgery (Durisin et al., Otol Neurotol 2010), supporting urgent imaging and implantation."],["osec","th:op-205~s4-2","Complications","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · Aftercare","Haematoma, infection, flap breakdown and device exposure, electrode malposition, facial palsy, vertigo, taste change, meningitis, device failure."],["osec","th:op-205~s5-1","Where the books differ","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers the paediatric technique with orientation of the receiver in infants, the round window approach, cochlear drill-out for ossification and management of the gusher. K. J. Lee and the Oxford books: candidacy, imaging for the cochlear nerve and malformations, vaccination, and outcomes by age. The literature: implantation before 12 months improves language outcomes."],["osec","th:op-205~s5-2","Viva essentials","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Candidacy and work-up in a child. Why early implantation matters. Facial recess boundaries and round window access. The meningitis child: urgency and ossification. Malformations and the gusher. Rehabilitation as the deciding factor."],["osec","th:op-205~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Pediatric Cochlear Implantation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Delaying implantation after meningitis. Missing an absent cochlear nerve on MRI. A low incision over a superficial facial nerve in an infant. Forcing the electrode against resistance."],["osec","th:op-206~s1-1","The case at a glance","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Operating note","Cleft palate and velopharyngeal dysfunction Summary Primary palatoplasty Usually at 10 to 14 months (repair at 3 to 5 months is possible, but midface growth costs outweigh any measurable speech gain); delay for developmental or speech delay before verbal communication, or very wide clefts Fistula Persistent symptomatic oral-nasal fistula after repair VPD surgery Persistent, anatomical VPD, usually at 2.5 to 5 years (younger children's language and cooperation limit assessment) Furlow palatoplasty Anteriorly placed levators : a submucous cleft, or a previous repair that did not reposition the muscle Pharyngeal flap Intact levator sling and good lateral wall motion , but a large anteroposterior gap or poor anteroposterior motion Sphincter pharyngoplasty Good anteroposterior motion, poor lateral wall motion Contraindications Anaesthetic risk; uncorrected bleeding disorder or nutritional deficiency; severe OSA , which these operations can worsen (assess and treat first) Preparation Stop antiplatelets; change from bottle to cup or short-spouted sippy cup; counsel on pain, diet and arm restraints Anaesthesia and position General, oral RAE tube taped in the midline; supine with a shoulder roll Antibiotics Ampicillin-sulbactam; clindamycin in allergy Tray Dingman or ratchet gag; marking pen or gentian violet; lidocaine with adrenaline (0.5% 1:200,000, or 1% 1:100,000 in older children); oral set with periosteal and Freer elevators, skin hooks, long tenotomy scissors; long-handled 15 or 15C blade; angled Beaver blade; 3-0 and 4-0 absorbable sutures; bipolar and insulated needle mono"],["osec","th:op-206~s1-2","Consent","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Operating note","What to tell the parents Why: to close the gap between mouth and nose and rebuild the palate muscle so your child can feed and speak normally; or, later, to tighten the valve behind the palate for clearer speech. Bleeding , and swelling that can affect breathing; a night or more in hospital. The repair may break down or leave a small hole (fistula) that may need repair. Speech may still be nasal ; speech therapy continues, and another operation may be needed after at least a year. Snoring or sleep apnoea after a pharyngeal flap or sphincter pharyngoplasty; rarely a narrowing behind the nose needing surgery. Arm restraints for 2 weeks and a smooth liquid diet; no bottles or dummies."],["osec","th:op-206~s1-3","Before you start","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Operating note","Ask when the cleft was diagnosed (palate clefts are harder to see on prenatal ultrasound than lip clefts); breathing problems at birth ( micrognathia with tongue-base obstruction), stridor, stertor, cyanosis or life-threatening events; feeding: breast milk or formula, its caloric density, the bottle (a Haberman or Pigeon teat that does not need suction), how easy and how long each feed takes (long feeds suggest poorly coordinated breathing and swallowing). In older children, speech development, therapy, whether speech is improving, plateauing or worsening, and fatigue through the day. Ask about milk from the nose , hearing screening, otitis media and tubes. Record previous oral, tonsil and adenoid surgery: adenoidectomy unmasks VPD in up to 1 in 1000 children, often improving within months; consider surgery if it lasts over 6 months. Ask about family clefts, heart disease and speech problems."],["osec","th:op-206~s1-4","Examination","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Operating note","The palate: extent and side of the cleft (LAHSHAL or Veau classification); elevation pattern with a gag. A submucous cleft is the triad of bifid uvula, notched hard palate and zona pellucida ; an occult submucous cleft shows only on activation (a vault-shaped elevation, a midline muscle deficiency, anterior levators). The airway: stridor, stertor, work of breathing. Perceptual speech assessment by an experienced speech therapist: still the gold standard; no objective measure correlates reliably with intelligibility. The ears: effusions; tubes can be placed at palate repair. Nasendoscopy above the inferior turbinate for a bird's-eye view of closure on test phrases: palatal, lateral and posterior wall movement, bubbling (a nasal rustle), and adenoid size (adenoidectomy is often useful before a pharyngeal flap). Syndromes: Stickler, 22q11.2 deletion, Treacher Collins."],["osec","th:op-206~s1-5","Closure patterns","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Operating note","Imaging is not routine; speech videofluoroscopy with oral and nasal contrast, with the speech therapist present, suits children who will not tolerate endoscopy and also shows adenoid size. Velopharyngeal closure patterns Pattern Normal population With VPI Coronal (velum moves back, little lateral wall movement) 55% About 45% Sagittal (lateral walls close, little anteroposterior movement) 10 to 15% 10 to 15% Circular, with Passavant's ridge (all walls move) 10 to 20% 20 to 25%"],["osec","th:op-206~s2-1","Landmarks","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Anatomy you must see","Bone: incisive foramen, palatine process of the maxilla, horizontal plate of the palatine bone, greater and lesser palatine foramina , pterygoid hamulus , vomer. Muscle: in the normal palate the levators meet as a transverse sling; in a cleft they run forward to insert on the posterior edge of the hard palate (Veau's muscle, levator with palatopharyngeus). Figure 1 The velopharyngeal valve: (A) normal anatomy (tensor and hamulus, palatoglossus, levator, superior constrictor, palatopharyngeus); (B) a unilateral cleft with the levators attached anteriorly to the hard palate edge. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 206.1"],["osec","th:op-206~s3-1","Set up","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: common steps","Oral RAE tube taped in the midline, sparing the lower lip; shoulder roll; face prepared with povidone-iodine and mouth with chlorhexidine. Insert the Dingman or ratchet gag for the best view, the tube held in the midline on the tongue, and relax the gag every 20 to 30 minutes for tongue perfusion. Mark incisions with a marking pen or a gentian violet stick. Inject submucosally and subperiosteally in the hard palate, avoiding the neurovascular bundles. A 70 degree endoscope on the Mayo stand lets the team watch. At the end, pass an orogastric tube to empty the stomach. What you should see The whole cleft, both hamuli and the uvula halves, with the tube midline and the tongue pink."],["osec","th:op-206~s4-1","Incise","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: two-flap palatoplasty","Medially, leave a strip of mucoperiosteum ( at least 2 mm , more in wide clefts) on the cleft edge for the nasal layer, continuing to the uvula tip on each side. Laterally, include only oral mucoperiosteum, not gingiva. What you should see A marked strip of mucoperiosteum left on the cleft edge and incisions clear of the gum."],["osec","th:op-206~s4-2","Raise the flaps and free the pedicle","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: two-flap palatoplasty","Elevate subperiosteally from anterolateral, identify and preserve the greater palatine neurovascular pedicle , and on both sides of it detach all muscle and tendon from the posterior hard palate so the flaps hang on the pedicle and lateral attachment only. Blunt dissection in the space of Ernst medialises the soft palate further; infracture the hamulus if needed. Gentle traction draws the pedicle out of its foramen, and incising its periosteal cone gives more length. What you should see The greater palatine vessels entering the flap from the foramen, intact, and the flaps reaching the midline without tension."],["osec","th:op-206~s4-3","Free Veau's muscle","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: two-flap palatoplasty","Elevate the nasal mucoperiosteum off the nasal floor. Dissect Veau's muscle off the posterior hard palate edge, the oral mucosa and the nasal mucosa with sharp scissors or a knife, without perforating either layer. What you should see The muscle bundles freed from the bone edge and from both mucosal layers, which remain unperforated."],["osec","th:op-206~s4-4","Close in three layers","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: two-flap palatoplasty","Close the nasal layer with 4-0 absorbable sutures from front to the base of the uvula, knots on the nasal side. Bring the muscle back and to the midline and suture it with 3-0 (the intravelar veloplasty ). Join the uvula with horizontal mattress sutures. Close the oral layer from front to back with 4-0 or 5-0 interrupted or mattress sutures, knots on the oral side, tacking it to the nasal layer at its midpoint and to the gingiva anteriorly. A double-opposing Z-plasty may replace the veloplasty. Figure 2 Two-flap palatoplasty: (A) incisions 1 to 2 mm lateral to the oral-nasal junction; (B) elevating the mucoperiosteal flap; (C) the greater palatine pedicle preserved; (D) the nasal flap elevated; (E, F, G) the levator freed from the hard palate, oral and nasal mucosa; (H) nasal closure; (I) levators sutured together; (J) oral closure, lateral donor sites left open. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 206.2 What you should see Three intact layers, muscle joined across the midline at the back of the soft palate, and no blanching of the flaps."],["osec","th:op-206~s5-1","Mark","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: Furlow palatoplasty","Feel the hamulus on each side, a bony bump just medial and behind the maxillary tuberosity. Mark the oral incisions from the left hamulus to the hard palate edge and from the right hamulus to the base of the uvula , and the midline from the hard palate to the uvula (in an unrepaired cleft, 2 mm lateral to the cleft edge). What you should see Marks from each hamulus making two mirror-image Z shapes."],["osec","th:op-206~s5-2","Oral flaps","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: Furlow palatoplasty","Cut the midline, then the left limb; free the clefted muscle from the hard palate and the nasal mucosa as a posteriorly based oral myomucosal flap . Cut the right limb and raise an anteriorly based oral mucosal flap to the hard palate edge, then free the right muscle from the hard palate, leaving a rim of nasal mucosa. What you should see The muscle in the left flap, and none in the right mucosal flap."],["osec","th:op-206~s5-3","Nasal flaps","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: Furlow palatoplasty","Cut the left nasal layer from the uvula base to just medial to the torus tubarius (an anteriorly based nasal mucosal flap ) and the right from the hard palate edge to the same point (a posteriorly based nasal myomucosal flap ). Secure haemostasis. What you should see Four flaps: on each side one with muscle and one without."],["osec","th:op-206~s5-4","Transpose and close","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: Furlow palatoplasty","Close the nasal layer from front to back: the left anteriorly based flap across to the right hard palate edge, then the right posteriorly based myomucosal flap across toward the left superior constrictor. Join the uvula. Close the oral layer: the right anteriorly based flap to the left hard palate edge, then the left posteriorly based myomucosal flap across to the right. The palate is lengthened , its muscle reoriented transversely and bulked posteriorly . Figure 3 Furlow palatoplasty for a Veau II cleft: (A) the cleft; (B) the oral Z-plasty with the nasal cutbacks in blue; (C) nasal flaps transposed, the posteriorly based nasal myomucosal flap moved back; (D) oral closure with the posteriorly based oral myomucosal flap moved back. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 206.3 What you should see Muscle from both sides overlapping across the midline at the back, and a visibly longer soft palate."],["osec","th:op-206~s6-1","Raise the flap","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: superiorly based pharyngeal flap","Mark a flap on the posterior pharyngeal wall whose tip lies near the lowest point visible with the gag. Inject, split the palate in the midline (slightly more on the oral layer anteriorly), cut the flap to the prevertebral fascia , and raise it as a superiorly based myomucosal flap toward the skull base, its base at or above the level of the hard palate. What you should see The white prevertebral fascia in the bed, and a flap as wide as the posterior wall."],["osec","th:op-206~s6-2","Inset and set the ports","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: superiorly based pharyngeal flap","Secure haemostasis with bipolar, then sew the flap tip to the nasal layer at the front of the split palate with 4-0 sutures, continuing laterally with mattress sutures. Close the oral mucosa over it, adjusting each lateral port with the last sutures and with nasal trumpets as a guide. Check the base of the flap for bleeding, a common site. Pass a nasal trumpet or small tube through each side into the pharynx and secure them; close the donor site with 3-0. Figure 4 Superiorly based pharyngeal flap: (A) elevation with the midline palatal split; (B) inset into the nasal side of the palate; (C) closure of the palate and the pharyngeal donor site; (D) the flap above the palate between its oral and nasal layers. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 206.4 What you should see A port on each side that takes the nasal trumpet, and a dry flap base."],["osec","th:op-206~s7-1","Raise the pillar flaps","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: sphincter pharyngoplasty","Retract the uvula forward with a suture. Mark incisions along each posterior tonsillar pillar and a horizontal inset incision on the posterior pharyngeal wall. Inject, then raise myomucosal flaps from each pillar from below upward, taking as much palatopharyngeus as possible. What you should see Two muscle-bearing flaps based high, at the level of the soft palate."],["osec","th:op-206~s7-2","Inset","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · The operation: sphincter pharyngoplasty","Cut the inset incision to the prevertebral fascia and spread it slightly. Secure haemostasis and sew the flaps into it, end to end or overlapping, to create a single port whose size depends on flap length and overlap. Close the pillar defects. Figure 5 Sphincter pharyngoplasty: (A) incisions on the posterior pillars and the posterior wall above the palate; (B) the flaps elevated and rotated; (C) flaps sutured together, muscle deep; (D) a single port with a new muscular ridge. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 206.5 Combined operations Some surgeons combine Furlow palatoplasty with sphincter pharyngoplasty with good results; the authors do not, for fear of nasopharyngeal stenosis. What you should see A single central port high in the nasopharynx, small but open."],["osec","th:op-206~s8-1","Crisis drills","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · When it goes wrong","Bleeding after surgery 1 Minor often stops, or with oxymetazoline spray 2 Persistent return to theatre the base of a pharyngeal flap is a common source Breakdown of the repair 1 Act continue liquids and wait repair a resulting fistula secondarily"],["osec","th:op-206~s8-2","Common errors in technique","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Inadequate flap elevation, so the closure is under tension. Poor haemostasis, leading to bleeding or haematoma. Not relaxing the gag, leaving a swollen tongue."],["osec","th:op-206~s9-1","After the operation","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Aftercare","Admit until oral intake is adequate. Arm restraints at all times for 2 weeks except when supervised; a smooth liquid diet for 2 weeks; no dummies, bottles or long-spouted cups. Review at 1 to 2 weeks; optional antibiotics for 7 days. Young children sip water after meals; older ones use chlorhexidine twice daily for 14 days."],["osec","th:op-206~s9-2","Complications","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Bleeding Oxymetazoline; theatre if persistent Airway obstruction Early swelling Dehiscence and fistula Wait; repair later Persistent VPI Speech therapy for at least a year before more surgery, as speech may keep improving Obstructive sleep apnoea After flaps and pharyngoplasty Nasopharyngeal stenosis Scarring of the ports; surgical repair"],["ev","th:op-206~s9-3","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction: current evidence","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Aftercare","Timing of palate repair. In the TOPS trial, repair at 6 months gave less velopharyngeal insufficiency at age five than repair at 12 months (8.9% against 15.0%) (Gamble et al., NEJM 2023). Surgery for velopharyngeal insufficiency. A meta-analysis of two randomised trials found a non-significant trend favouring the pharyngeal flap over sphincter pharyngoplasty (Collins et al., J Plast Reconstr Aesthet Surg 2012); Furlow palatoplasty suits small central gaps and a submucous cleft. Choice depends on the closure pattern on nasendoscopy and videofluoroscopy. Obstructive sleep apnoea after pharyngeal flap is a known risk; screen with a sleep history."],["osec","th:op-206~s9-3","Alternatives","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · Aftercare","A speech obturator or palatal lift for those unfit for surgery or with severe OSA; posterior wall augmentation (calcium hydroxylapatite or fat) for small gaps and for pressure VPI in woodwind and brass players."],["osec","th:op-206~s10-1","Clefts and sleep apnoea","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · For the examination","Children with clefts have more sleep-disordered breathing than other children, obviously with midface hypoplasia, micrognathia or glossoptosis, but also in non-syndromic clefts. Robison and Otteson found symptoms in 37.5% of 459 cleft patients, of whom about half had surgery, usually adenotonsillectomy. Assess sleep before any operation that narrows the velopharynx."],["osec","th:op-206~s10-2","Viva essentials","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The velopharyngeal valve and how a cleft disrupts it. Submucous cleft triad and the occult form. Feeding with a cleft: why breast feeding fails, special bottles. Timing: palatoplasty at 10 to 14 months, VPD surgery at 2.5 to 5 years. Closure patterns and matching them to Furlow, pharyngeal flap or sphincter pharyngoplasty. The greater palatine pedicle, the space of Ernst, intravelar veloplasty. The Furlow flaps and what they achieve. Aftercare and complications, including OSA and stenosis."],["osec","th:op-206~s10-3","Common pitfalls","Cleft Palate and Velopharyngeal Dysfunction · For the examination","Common pitfalls Performing a pharyngeal flap in a child with untreated sleep apnoea. Operating for VPD before a proper perceptual speech assessment. Forgetting the adenoid before a pharyngeal flap."],["osec","th:op-207~s1-1","The case at a glance","Microtia · Operating note","Microtia reconstruction with rib cartilage Summary Indications Microtia in a child or adult who wants an ear, with realistic expectations Timing When the chest has enough cartilage and the child is aware of the difference, commonly from about 6 to 10 years; before school teasing if possible Alternatives Watchful waiting; a silicone prosthesis (glued or on osseointegrated anchors); a porous polyethylene framework covered with a temporoparietal fascia flap; fewer-stage autologous techniques (Nagata) Stages Three-stage (framework; lobule transposition and conchal deepening; elevation with a skin graft) or four-stage Anaesthesia General, with an epidural or paravertebral block for the chest Antibiotics Perioperative (cefazolin); dexamethasone Tray Rib harvest set, cartilage carving instruments, sterilised template from the opposite ear, 4-0 clear nylon, drains"],["osec","th:op-207~s1-2","Consent","Microtia · Operating note","What to tell the family Several operations, about two months apart, and the result is an ear that looks natural at conversation distance, not an exact copy. Chest: pain, a scar, a chest wall depression, and a small risk of pneumothorax. Skin loss over the framework, exposing cartilage; it may need a flap to cover it. Infection, haematoma or seroma. Asymmetry, scarring, and a framework that loses definition over time. Hearing is not changed by the reconstruction itself."],["osec","th:op-207~s1-3","Before you start","Microtia · Operating note","Assess hearing in both ears and fit a bone conduction aid on a softband early if needed. Plan with the otologist: a bone-anchored implant or atresiaplasty must not be sited where the framework will go, and atresiaplasty is usually done after the framework. Make a template of the opposite ear from a flipped photograph or a tracing on sterilisable film; in bilateral cases use age- and sex-appropriate dimensions. Mark the ear and the side of rib harvest before induction."],["osec","th:op-207~s2-1","Harvest the rib cartilage","Microtia · The operation, stage by stage","Through a 3 to 4 cm incision over the seventh rib, reach ribs 6 to 8. Handle the cartilage with skin hooks to preserve perichondrium. Take the floating (eighth) rib cartilage for the helix, leaving a little cartilage over the bony junction, and the synchondrosis of ribs 6 and 7, marked from the template, for the base. Stay close to the cartilage and away from the pleura. Check for air leak, place a drain and close in layers. Figure 1 The synchondrosis of the sixth and seventh ribs harvested from the template outline. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 207.1 What you should see Clean cartilage blocks and an intact pleura when the cavity is filled with saline and the lungs inflated. Pneumothorax Fill the wound with saline and ask for a sustained inflation: bubbles mean a pleural tear. Repair it over a catheter evacuated under positive pressure, or place a chest drain, and get a chest X-ray."],["osec","th:op-207~s2-2","Carve the framework","Microtia · The operation, stage by stage","Clean the cartilage of soft tissue. Cut the base from the synchondrosis using the template, thinning it if needed. Thin and curve the floating rib to form the helix and helical crus, then fix it to the edge of the base with 4-0 clear nylon, knots underneath. Sculpt the triangular fossa and scapha with a scalpel, gouge or punch, and fix a separate tragal piece. Figure 2 A carved framework with its helix attached. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 207.2 What you should see A framework whose helix, antihelix, fossae and tragus stand out clearly; definition must be exaggerated because skin softens it. Senior's pearl Do not over-thin the helix. Cartilage that is too thin buckles and warps as it heals, and the ear loses its shape over the following years."],["osec","th:op-207~s2-3","Make the pocket and insert the framework","Microtia · The operation, stage by stage","Make a short incision in front of the vestige and undermine a skin pocket immediately beneath the subdermal plexus, 3 mm beyond the planned outline. Remove the vestigial cartilage without buttonholing the skin. Insert the framework, place a fine suction drain behind it so the skin is sucked down onto every contour, and close. Figure 3 Immediately after stage one, the skin draped over the framework by suction. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 207.3 What you should see The skin shrink-wrapped onto the framework with good colour and capillary return. The subdermal plexus Too thick a pocket gives an ear without definition; too thin, or cauterised skin, dies and exposes the cartilage. Bipolar only, away from the skin surface."],["osec","th:op-207~s2-4","Stage two: lobule and concha","Microtia · The operation, stage by stage","About two months later, rotate the lobule vestige back to its correct position, tucking the lower framework into a pocket in the lobule to keep the ear's height, and deepen the conchal bowl by excising soft tissue. Figure 4 Lobule transposition at the second stage. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 207.4 What you should see A lobule in line with the helix and a defined conchal hollow."],["osec","th:op-207~s2-5","Stage three: elevation","Microtia · The operation, stage by stage","Two months later, incise around the framework 2 mm outside its edge, lift it off the scalp, place a cartilage wedge behind it to set the projection, cover the back of the ear with a turnover flap or fascia, and graft the raw sulcus with full-thickness skin (often from the groin). Figure 5 The third stage creates the postauricular sulcus and projection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 207.5 Figure 6 Before and after reconstruction of a grade III microtia. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 207.6 What you should see An ear standing out from the head at about the same angle as the other side, with a grafted sulcus."],["osec","th:op-207~s3-1","Crisis drills","Microtia · When it goes wrong","Skin over the framework darkens or breaks down 1 Small exposure dressings and antibiotic ointment it may granulate 2 Larger cover early with a local flap or temporoparietal fascia flap and skin graft before the cartilage is lost Haematoma or seroma under the skin 1 Act needle aspiration or drainage check the drain 2 Infection culture, antibiotics drain pus early to save the framework"],["osec","th:op-207~s4-1","After each stage","Microtia · Aftercare","Light, non-compressive dressings; drain suction for a few days; antibiotics; no pressure on the ear while sleeping. Chest pain is the main early discomfort. Protect the ear from trauma during sport."],["ev","th:op-207~s4-2","Microtia: current evidence","Microtia · Aftercare","Complications by technique. In 470 rib-cartilage reconstructions at one centre, complications occurred after 3% of Brent and 12% of Nagata first stages, with a learning curve for both (Fu et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2019). Rib or polyethylene. In a comparison of the two frameworks, porous polyethylene needed fewer operations (about 3.4 against 4.9) and gave better definition and size match, but two of 17 implants became infected and extruded, while cartilage gave better colour match and no extrusion (Constantine et al., JAMA Facial Plast Surg 2014). Psychosocial benefit. Prospective assessment after rib-cartilage reconstruction showed improvements in self-confidence and social life (Steffen et al., J Plast Reconstr Aesthet Surg 2010)."],["osec","th:op-207~s4-2","Complications","Microtia · Aftercare","Pneumothorax, chest wall deformity, infection, haematoma, skin necrosis and framework exposure, resorption or distortion of the framework, scarring and asymmetry."],["osec","th:op-207~s5-1","Where the books differ","Microtia · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers describes the three-stage autologous technique in detail, with the four-stage Brent technique, the Nagata method, polyethylene frameworks and prostheses as alternatives. K. J. Lee and the Oxford books: classify microtia by grade, emphasise early bone conduction hearing and coordinating any atresiaplasty after the framework. The literature: polyethylene gives definition in fewer stages but risks extrusion; autologous reconstruction is durable with a learning curve."],["osec","th:op-207~s5-2","Viva essentials","Microtia · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Grades of microtia and associated anomalies (atresia, first and second arch syndromes). Timing and the reasons for it. The stages and what each achieves. Rib harvest and pneumothorax. Options: rib, polyethylene, prosthesis; pros and cons. Coordination with hearing rehabilitation."],["osec","th:op-207~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Microtia · For the examination","Common pitfalls Placing a bone conduction implant where the ear will go. Cauterising the pocket skin. Building a framework without enough definition. Missing a pleural tear."],["osec","th:op-208~s1-1","The case at a glance","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","Atresiaplasty and canalplasty for stenosis Summary Indications Congenital aural atresia with a good CT grade (Jahrsdoerfer 7 or more) and a family who prefer an unaided ear; congenital canal stenosis with cholesteatoma or a canal under 4 mm; acquired atresia from scarring after infection, trauma or surgery Contraindications Poor CT grade; no identifiable stapes or oval window; uncontrolled ear disease; unfavourable facial nerve course; a family who would rather have a bone conduction device Timing Usually from about 6 years, after rib-cartilage microtia reconstruction so the skin and blood supply are not compromised Anaesthesia General Position Supine, head turned away Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring Tray Microscope, otology drill and diamond burrs, ear set, fascia, dermatome for a split-thickness skin graft, Silastic discs, Merocel packs"],["osec","th:op-208~s1-2","Consent","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","What to tell the family Hearing gain is variable: on average about 25 dB, less than with a bone conduction implant. Stenosis of the new canal is the commonest problem (up to a quarter) and the commonest reason for revision; regular cleaning is lifelong. Lateralisation of the new drum causing hearing to fall again, needing revision (up to about one in five). Facial nerve injury (up to 7%), more likely in poorly formed ears. Sensorineural hearing loss (up to about 7%), usually slight and high-pitched. Infection and discharge from the new canal. Alternatives: a bone conduction hearing device (softband, percutaneous or transcutaneous), or a hearing aid where a canal exists."],["osec","th:op-208~s1-3","Before you start","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · Operating note","Confirm a normal cochlea with bone-conduction testing or ABR in young children; in bilateral cases fit a softband bone conduction aid early for language. Unilateral atresia is not trivial: many children have school difficulties, so monitor the good ear and consider rehabilitation. Obtain a high-resolution temporal bone CT and grade it. Watch congenital stenosis with serial CT even if no operation is planned, because cholesteatoma forms behind a narrow canal. Coordinate with the microtia surgeon: the canal must open where the new auricle will sit. Figure 1 Grade 3 microtia with congenital aural atresia. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 208.1 Figure 2 Coronal CT: the bony atretic plate, the middle ear and the ossicular mass behind it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 208.2 The Jahrsdoerfer CT grading score (10 points) Feature Points Stapes present 2 Oval window open 1 Middle ear space 1 Facial nerve course normal 1 Malleus-incus complex present 1 Mastoid pneumatised 1 Incus-stapes connection 1 Round window normal 1 Appearance of the external ear 1 A score of 8 or more predicts a good result, 7 a fair one and 6 a marginal one; 5 or less is a poor candidate."],["osec","th:op-208~s2-1","Open behind the ear and take fascia","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Make a postauricular incision (placed to protect the reconstructed auricle) and harvest a generous temporalis fascia graft. The canal wall-down mastoid approach used historically is avoided because of the lifelong mastoid cavity. What you should see The linea temporalis, the glenoid fossa in front and the mastoid cortex behind, with no canal."],["osec","th:op-208~s2-2","Drill the new canal","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Start at the level of the linea temporalis, just behind the glenoid fossa, and drill medially, keeping the middle fossa dura above and the jaw joint in front, and staying anterior to the mastoid air cells. Follow the dura and the glenoid towards the epitympanum until the atretic plate and the ossicular mass come into view. What you should see The fused malleus-incus mass in the epitympanum, seen through the thinning atretic bone. The facial nerve is not where you expect it In atresia the facial nerve often runs more anterior and lateral than normal, and its vertical segment can cross the area where the new canal is drilled. Use the monitor, drill with diamond burrs, and study the CT for its course beforehand."],["osec","th:op-208~s2-3","Free the ossicles","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Thin the atretic plate with a diamond burr, then remove the last bone with fine curettes and picks, separating the plate from the ossicular mass without letting the drill touch the ossicles. Brace your hands. Keep the native chain if it moves well; if it is fixed or discontinuous, reconstruct. Figure 3 The malleus-incus complex freed from the atretic plate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 208.3 What you should see A mobile ossicular mass with a visible stapes; if no stapes can be found, stop. Drill energy reaches the cochlea Touching the ossicles with a turning burr transmits energy straight to the inner ear. Thin, then pick."],["osec","th:op-208~s2-4","Build the drum and line the canal","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Lay the temporalis fascia over the ossicular mass as a new drum, tucked under the canal edges. Line the bony canal with a thin split-thickness skin graft, overlapping the fascia slightly to prevent lateralisation, and place a Silastic disc on the drum. Pack with Merocel and drops. Figure 4 The canal lined with split-thickness skin graft, with a Silastic disc over the new drum. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 208.4 What you should see A wide canal, 10 mm or more, fully lined with skin, and a fascia drum sitting on the ossicles."],["osec","th:op-208~s2-5","Make the meatus","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","Excise a disc of tissue from the reconstructed auricle, preserving a pedicled skin flap; bevel the meatus and bone into a smooth funnel; bring the skin graft out and suture it to the meatal skin. A generous meatus is the best defence against stenosis. Figure 5 Final result of microtia reconstruction with rib grafts and atresiaplasty. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 208.5 What you should see A large, round meatus in line with the new canal."],["osec","th:op-208~s2-6","Congenital stenosis and acquired atresia","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · The operation, stage by stage","For congenital stenosis use an endaural (Lempert) or postauricular approach: raise triangular canal flaps and a tympanomeatal flap, drill the bony canal wide, remove any partial atretic plate and canal cholesteatoma, enlarge the hypoplastic drum with an underlay fascia graft and reline bare bone with full- or split-thickness skin. For acquired atresia, excise the fibrous plug through a postauricular approach down to the plane on the drum, widen the canal until the whole drum is visible, and graft the canal, since little skin survives. Explore the middle ear if the conductive loss exceeds what the plug explains (more than 20 to 30 dB)."],["osec","th:op-208~s3-1","Crisis drills","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · When it goes wrong","No stapes or oval window found 1 Stop do not fenestrate the lateral canal or open the labyrinth 2 Plan close and offer a bone conduction device Mastoid cells opened during an anterior approach 1 Small opening seal with fascia or cartilage 2 Large convert to a canal wall-down technique Facial monitor fires 1 Stop irrigate and identify the nerve with a diamond burr 2 Proceed redirect the canal away from it do not chase hearing at the nerve's expense"],["osec","th:op-208~s4-1","After surgery","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","Remove the packs at about 1 to 2 weeks, then keep the canal open with wicks, stents or moulds, sometimes for 6 months. Treat granulation early. Steroid (triamcinolone) injection into the meatus helps prevent or treat stenosis. Test hearing after healing and clean the canal regularly for life."],["ev","th:op-208~s4-2","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal: current evidence","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","Atresiaplasty or a bone conduction implant. A systematic review comparing hearing outcomes found mean gains of about 24 dB after atresiaplasty against about 38 dB with osseointegrated bone conduction devices, with better and more consistent hearing and fewer complications for the implants (Nadaraja et al., Otol Neurotol 2013). Transcutaneous active implants. Active transcutaneous bone conduction implants in children with aural atresia give aided thresholds around 25 dB with good word recognition (Zernotti et al., Int J Pediatr Otorhinolaryngol 2019; Bravo-Torres et al., Int J Audiol 2018). Revision. In 75 ears undergoing revision atresiaplasty, stenosis, lateralisation and persistent conductive loss were the main indications (Oliver et al., Otol Neurotol 2011)."],["osec","th:op-208~s4-2","Complications","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · Aftercare","Canal stenosis (up to 25%); drum lateralisation and recurrent conductive loss (up to 18%); chronic infection and discharge; facial nerve injury (up to 7%); sensorineural loss (up to 7.5%)."],["osec","th:op-208~s5-1","Where the books differ","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers prefers the anterior (lateral) atresiaplasty to avoid a cavity, times it after microtia repair, and presents bone conduction devices as faster with better hearing but cosmetically less desirable. Dhingra and K. J. Lee describe the embryology (first branchial groove and arch), the Jahrsdoerfer grading and the timing with microtia surgery. The literature bone conduction devices give larger and more predictable hearing gains than atresiaplasty."],["osec","th:op-208~s5-2","Viva essentials","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Embryology and why the cochlea is usually normal. The Jahrsdoerfer score and who is a candidate. Timing in relation to microtia repair. The steps of the anterior approach and the facial nerve's abnormal course. Complications and why bone conduction devices are often preferred."],["osec","th:op-208~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Congenital and Acquired Atresia and Stenosis of the External Auditory Canal · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating before the microtia reconstruction. Drilling a narrow canal and meatus. Touching the ossicles with the drill. Continuing when no stapes is found. Forgetting serial CT for cholesteatoma in congenital stenosis."],["osec","th:op-209~s1-1","The case at a glance","Otoplasty · Operating note","Otoplasty for prominent ears Summary Indications Prominent ears causing distress, with realistic expectations; usually from about 5 to 6 years, when the ear is near adult size and before teasing at school; adults at any age Contraindications A child who does not want it; unrealistic expectations; skin infection Non-surgical Moulding (splinting) in the first weeks of life, while maternal oestrogen keeps the cartilage soft Anaesthesia General in children; local in older children and adults Position Supine, head turned, both ears in the field for comparison Tray Fine scissors, rasp or Adson-Brown forceps for scoring, 4-0 clear nylon for Mustardé and conchomastoid sutures, 5-0 nylon for temporary marking sutures, light pressure dressing"],["osec","th:op-209~s1-2","Consent","Otoplasty · Operating note","What to tell the patient or family Haematoma (urgent: pain on one side) and infection of skin or cartilage. Recurrence of some prominence over time; slight overcorrection allows for it. Asymmetry; perfect symmetry is not natural or achievable. Visible or palpable sutures, suture extrusion or granuloma. Contour problems: a sharp fold, the \"telephone\" or \"reverse telephone\" deformity. Scarring, including rarely a keloid."],["osec","th:op-209~s1-3","Before you start","Otoplasty · Operating note","Measure and photograph: helix-to-mastoid distance (normal about 15 to 20 mm) at the top, middle and lobule, the auriculocephalic angle (normally under about 30 degrees), and identify which part is at fault: an absent antihelical fold, a deep concha, a prominent lobule, or several. Figure 1 A typical prominent ear: absent antihelical fold and a deep concha. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 209.1 Figure 2 Normal auricular anatomy to recreate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 209.3"],["osec","th:op-209~s2-1","Excise skin behind the ear","Otoplasty · The operation, stage by stage","Inject local anaesthetic with adrenaline. Excise a fusiform strip of postauricular skin no wider than about 15 mm, biased onto the back of the auricle so the sulcus is not pulled, and dissect down onto the perichondrium behind the antihelix. What you should see The posterior perichondrium exposed over the planned fold."],["osec","th:op-209~s2-2","Mark the new fold","Otoplasty · The operation, stage by stage","Fold the ear back with a finger to show where the antihelix should be, and pass temporary 5-0 nylon mattress sutures from the front through to the back to mark it. What you should see The marking sutures sitting along a fold that looks natural when pressed back."],["osec","th:op-209~s2-3","Weaken the cartilage (optional)","Otoplasty · The operation, stage by stage","If the cartilage is stiff, score the front of the planned fold through a small subperichondrial pocket with a fine rasp or the serrated edge of Adson-Brown forceps, and wash out every cartilage fragment. What you should see A cartilage that bends smoothly along the fold without kinking."],["osec","th:op-209~s2-4","Mustardé sutures for the antihelix","Otoplasty · The operation, stage by stage","Place about three horizontal mattress sutures of 4-0 clear nylon through cartilage and anterior perichondrium, without breaching the front skin, along the new fold. Tighten each until a natural fold forms, then a touch beyond to allow for relaxation. Remove the marking sutures as you go. Add one more for the superior crus if needed. Figure 3 The surgical steps: skin excision, Mustardé sutures and conchomastoid sutures. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 209.4 What you should see A rounded antihelical fold with the helix just visible behind it from the front."],["osec","th:op-209~s2-5","Set back the concha","Otoplasty · The operation, stage by stage","For a deep bowl, place conchomastoid mattress sutures from the back of the concha to the mastoid periosteum, radially and not too far back, so the meatus is not narrowed or the skin tented. Excise a small fusiform strip of conchal cartilage if needed, keeping the perichondrium and canal skin intact. What you should see The concha set back with an open, round ear canal. The narrowed meatus Conchomastoid sutures that pull the concha forwards and backwards in the wrong vector close the ear canal. Check the meatus before closing."],["osec","th:op-209~s2-6","Lobule, close and dress","Otoplasty · The operation, stage by stage","Correct a protruding lobule with a small skin excision behind it (M-plasty) or a suture. Close in layers and apply a lightly compressive, well-padded head dressing that holds the new contour. Figure 4 After correction: a natural antihelix and conchal set-back. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 209.2 What you should see Both ears at similar distances from the head at the top, middle and lobule, viewed from the front and above. Senior's pearl Judge the result from the front, not the side, and correct the upper, middle and lower thirds in proportion. Pinning only the middle produces the telephone deformity; overdoing the top and lobule produces the reverse."],["osec","th:op-209~s3-1","Crisis drills","Otoplasty · When it goes wrong","Severe one-sided pain after otoplasty 1 Act remove the dressing and look now 2 Haematoma evacuate in theatre re-dress 3 Infection or perichondritis culture, antipseudomonal antibiotics, drain any pus"],["osec","th:op-209~s4-1","After surgery","Otoplasty · Aftercare","Keep the head dressing for a few days, then wear a headband at night for several weeks. Avoid contact sports for a while. Remove skin sutures if non-absorbable."],["ev","th:op-209~s4-2","Otoplasty: current evidence","Otoplasty · Aftercare","In 1,060 ears, cartilage-sparing (Mustardé, Furnas) and cartilage-cutting techniques had similar complication rates (about 20%, mostly minor) and similar reoperation rates (6% to 7%), but cartilage-sparing techniques scored better for shape and satisfaction (Smittenberg et al., Plast Reconstr Surg 2018). After suture-technique otoplasty, all adults were satisfied with the appearance and quality-of-life scores improved in both children and adults (Braun et al., Plast Reconstr Surg 2010)."],["osec","th:op-209~s4-2","Complications","Otoplasty · Aftercare","Haematoma, infection, perichondritis, suture extrusion or granuloma, recurrence, asymmetry, contour deformities, meatal narrowing, keloid."],["osec","th:op-209~s5-1","Where the books differ","Otoplasty · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers a cartilage-sparing technique with temporary marking sutures, optional scoring, Mustardé and conchomastoid sutures, and slight overcorrection. K. J. Lee lists the classic techniques by the fault corrected (Mustardé for the fold, Furnas for the concha, cartilage-cutting techniques such as Chongchet for stiff cartilage) and neonatal moulding. The literature sparing and cutting techniques have similar complications, but sparing techniques look better."],["osec","th:op-209~s5-2","Viva essentials","Otoplasty · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The two main faults and how to measure them. Mustardé versus Furnas sutures: what each corrects. Timing, and neonatal moulding. Telephone and reverse telephone deformities. Haematoma after otoplasty as an emergency."],["osec","th:op-209~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Otoplasty · For the examination","Common pitfalls Excising too much skin to pin the ear back. Over-tightening sutures into a sharp fold. Treating only the middle third. Narrowing the meatus with conchal sutures."],["osec","th:op-23~s1-1","The case at a glance","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Operating note","Closure of a tracheocutaneous fistula and revision of a depressed scar Summary Indications A fistula causing symptoms (weak voice, productive cough, saliva or secretions leaking through it, aspiration, pneumonia, infection at the site, an ineffective cough) or cosmetic concern, present for at least 3 months after decannulation ; a depressed, tethered scar alone Contraindications Cannot tolerate the fistula being occluded, or airway obstruction on endoscopy; active infection at the site; chronic cough; the need for positive pressure ventilation (relative) Anaesthesia Adults: local anaesthetic with intravenous sedation preferred (avoids positive pressure during closure and coughing at emergence); general for a difficult airway. Children: general. Keep inspired oxygen low because of the fire risk with diathermy near the airway Position Supine, shoulder roll for maximal neck extension Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin before the incision (the track is colonised) Monitoring Standard; overnight observation for adults, intensive care for children Imaging Non-contrast CT of the neck if endoscopy cannot confirm an adequate tracheal lumen Tray Tracheotomy or neck dissection set; diathermy and suction; a cuffed endotracheal or tracheostomy tube ready; size 3 or 4 metal (Pilling) tracheostomy tube for large defects; Penrose drain or rubber band drain; absorbable sutures Before theatre Flexible laryngoscopy and tracheoscopy to the carina; a 24-hour trial of occluding the fistula; sleep study with the fistula occluded if sleep apnoea is known or suspected"],["osec","th:op-23~s1-2","Consent","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: removing the skin-lined track and the scar, lining the windpipe again, and bringing the neck muscles back together in the middle so the skin no longer sticks to the windpipe. Air leak: air can escape under the skin, around the lung or into the chest in up to about 3 in 100; you will be watched overnight for this. Breathing difficulty , which can mean reopening the wound or, rarely, a tracheostomy again; children are watched in intensive care. The fistula can come back if the closure is not airtight. Infection or the wound coming apart. The scar will still be there, but flatter and usually much better. Sleep apnoea: if you need a CPAP machine, closing the fistula may mean using it long term; it will usually be restarted after about two weeks."],["osec","th:op-23~s1-3","Before you start","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Why and how long the tracheostomy was in place; was it an emergency (often a vertical incision) or planned? How long since decannulation, and any previous attempts to close it (more scar, lower chance of success). Symptoms: breathlessness, a weak voice, productive cough, saliva coming through the hole, swallowing trouble, pneumonia, infections at the site, cosmetic concern. Heart and lung disease , and any need for CPAP or other positive pressure, including for sleep apnoea. Neck radiation, diabetes and vascular disease (poor healing); other neck surgery; smoking (poor healing, and coughing at emergence raises the risk of air leak). 1.3.2 The examination The site: a horizontal or vertical scar, the size of the fistula , any keloid or granulation tissue. Other neck scars; thyromental distance; mouth and pharynx for signs of a difficult airway or sleep apnoea (high arched palate, retracted tongue, Mallampati grade). Flexible laryngoscopy and tracheoscopy down to the carina. Look hardest at the trachea just above the stoma and the subglottis , the commonest sites of granulation and stenosis in children. Awake transnasal tracheoscopy in clinic under nebulised and topical lidocaine shows the airway moving; children who will not tolerate it have formal airway endoscopy at the start of the repair. The fistula that is keeping the patient alive A fistula sometimes persists because the airway above it is too narrow and the hole is acting as a second airway. Close it and the patient may not be able to breathe. Prove the airway is adequate on endoscopy, and make sure "],["osec","th:op-23~s2-1","How the fistula forms","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Anatomy you must see","During tracheostomy the strap muscles are retracted laterally. With a tube in place for months, infection and fibrosis fix them in that lateral position. When the tube comes out and the stoma heals, the skin heals directly onto the trachea , with nothing between them: the scar sinks and moves with swallowing. If skin has grown down the track and met the tracheal mucosa, a lined fistula forms that cannot close on its own."],["osec","th:op-23~s2-2","Risk factors","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Anatomy you must see","Duration of cannulation: about half of patients with a tube for over a year develop a persistent fistula. Previous neck radiation or previous tracheostomy. Infection, poor nutrition and weight loss (loss of subcutaneous tissue). A matured (skin-sutured) stoma has not been shown to increase the risk."],["osec","th:op-23~s2-3","Landmarks","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Anatomy you must see","The thyroid notch , cricoid cartilage and sternum . The strap muscles , found scarred onto the sides of the trachea."],["osec","th:op-23~s3-1","Incision and flaps","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · The operation: adult repair","Make a transverse elliptical incision around the fistula, extending a little to each side. Undermine the skin flaps widely and release the scar holding the skin to the deeper tissues. Figure 2 The tracheostomy scar excised almost down to the fistulous track. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 23.2 What you should see Mobile skin flaps above and below, free of the scar."],["osec","th:op-23~s3-2","Turn in the lining","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · The operation: adult repair","Follow the track down to the trachea. Excise it, but keep just enough of the skin around the opening to turn inward like a trapdoor , so its epithelium forms the inner lining of the front wall of the trachea, and close it airtight. Figure 3 The skin of the track used as a trapdoor flap, turned in to restore the inner lining of the front wall of the trachea. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 23.3 An incomplete seal Without an airtight closure and an inner epithelial lining, the fistula comes back. Check the closure: with the anaesthetist's help, look for bubbles under saline. What you should see The track followed to the tracheal wall, and a rim of skin turned in and sutured so that no air bubbles through saline when the anaesthetist gives a breath. Senior's pearl Before you plan a closure, be sure the patient no longer needs the stoma: endoscope the airway and confirm they tolerated a capped tube or occluded fistula. A closed fistula over an obstructed airway is an emergency."],["osec","th:op-23~s3-3","Bring the straps back to the midline","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · The operation: adult repair","Find the strap muscles, which lie buried in dense scar and stuck to the sides of the trachea. Dissect them completely free of the scar and the trachea, bring them back over the trachea, and sew them together in the midline with absorbable sutures. Figure 4 The strap muscles repositioned in the midline and sutured together; then the subcutaneous tissue and skin are closed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 23.4 What you should see Muscle lying between the trachea and the skin across the whole defect. Senior's pearl The midline strap repair is the heart of this operation. It separates the skin from the trachea, fills the hollow, stops the scar riding with swallowing and reinforces the closure; with it, muscle flaps and Z-plasties are rarely needed. Half-freed straps spring back and the hollow returns."],["osec","th:op-23~s3-4","Drain and close","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · The operation: adult repair","Leave a small rubber band or Penrose drain just deep to the straps, brought out at the side of the incision, so any air leaking from the trachea can escape. Close the subcutaneous tissue without tension and the skin with fine absorbable sutures; a Z-plasty or a geometric broken-line closure makes a smaller, smoother scar if needed. Apply strips to take the tension. What you should see A flat closure with no tension, and the drain sitting deep to the muscles. 3.4.1 A depressed scar without a fistula The same operation, minus the fistula excision: excise the scar, release the skin, free the straps and bring them together in the midline. Returning the straps to their place removes the hollow and stops it coming back. Figure 5 Planned excision of a depressed tracheostomy scar, and its appearance a week after repair. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 23.5"],["osec","th:op-23~s4-1","A defect larger than 4 mm","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · The operation: large defects and children","Closing a large hole in the front of the trachea in one stage risks air leak, pneumothorax and respiratory failure. Instead, dissect out the fistula and scar through a horizontal ellipse and excise the track down to the trachea. If the defect is over 4 mm , place a size 3 or 4 metal tracheostomy tube in the old site. Raise the straps and close them in the midline around the tube , undermine the skin widely, and close it around the tube. Cap the tube in recovery or the next day, decannulate on day 2 , and let the small central area heal by secondary intention. What you should see A tube sitting snugly in the trachea with the straps already reunited around it."],["osec","th:op-23~s4-2","Children","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · The operation: large defects and children","Use the least complex method: under general anaesthetic, excise the epithelium lining the track and let it close by secondary intention. It closes quickly and watertight, so the child can swim and bathe, and avoids a long anaesthetic and handling of a small trachea that could swell and obstruct. Revise the scar later if needed. Systematic review found no difference in closure between primary and secondary closure in children (about 96% and 92%), with similar airway complication rates (under 4%), so secondary closure is preferred. Swelling in a small airway In a child, oedema after handling the trachea can obstruct the airway. Keep the operation small, and monitor the child in intensive care overnight. What you should see A raw, de-epithelialised track with no skin left in its depth, and a small occlusive dressing over it. Senior's pearl Endoscope the child's airway first: suprastomal granulation or collapse is the usual reason a stoma has not closed on its own."],["osec","th:op-23~s5-1","Crisis drills","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · When it goes wrong","Swelling, crackling skin or breathlessness after closure 1 Recognise surgical emphysema, pneumothorax or pneumomediastinum from air escaping through the tracheal closure (up to about 3%), worse with coughing or positive pressure 2 Act open the wound at the bedside and pass a cuffed tracheostomy tube through the stoma give positive pressure through that tube if needed 3 Never give positive pressure by face mask, which forces more air into the tissues 4 Then chest X-ray chest drain for a significant pneumothorax Airway obstruction after closure 1 Act keep a tracheostomy set at the bedside reopen the stoma and reinsert a tube 2 Then reassess the airway endoscopically before any further attempt The fistula comes back 1 Cause an incomplete seal or no inner lining 2 Act repeat repair once healed, ensuring a trapdoor lining and an airtight closure"],["osec","th:op-23~s6-1","After the operation","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Aftercare","Adults: admit to a monitored bed overnight; home the next day. Children: intensive care overnight. Drain out on day 1 ; a tracheostomy tube, if used, out after a capping trial, usually on day 2 . Wound care is minimal; strips off at 7 to 10 days. A tracheostomy set at the bedside. Positive pressure (CPAP) restarted after 2 weeks, unless sleep apnoea is severe, when it is restarted the same evening, accepting a higher risk of airway complications. For patients with chronic cough or lung disease, excising the track and letting it heal by secondary intention lowers the risk of air leak."],["osec","th:op-23~s6-2","Complications","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Aftercare","Complications of fistula closure Complication Notes Subcutaneous emphysema, pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum Up to about 3%; coughing and positive pressure; mostly after single-stage closure of large defects Respiratory compromise Inadequate airway above the stoma; oedema in children Recurrent fistula Incomplete seal, no lining Persistent depressed scar Straps not fully freed or not reunited Infection, dehiscence Colonised track; smoking, diabetes, radiation"],["ev","th:op-23~s6-3","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar: current evidence","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Aftercare","Persistent tracheocutaneous fistula follows long-term tracheostomy, especially in children cannulated for many months. Closure. Comparative series show that excision of the tract with healing by secondary intention and layered primary closure give similar success; layered closure has a small risk of air leak, surgical emphysema and pneumothorax, so many surgeons leave the skin open or use a drain and observe overnight. Confirm that the airway is adequate (by endoscopy and successful capping) before closing."],["osec","th:op-23~s6-3","Alternatives","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · Aftercare","Observation; excision of the track with healing by secondary intention; chemical or diathermy cautery of a very small track where appearance does not matter; and older techniques such as bipedicled or turnover flaps, sternocleidomastoid or sternohyoid muscle flaps, subcutaneous Z-plasty and V-Y advancement. In adults there is little evidence favouring one technique, but primary closure gives the better cosmetic result."],["osec","th:op-23~s7-1","Where the books differ","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra covers this from the tracheostomy side: the tube should not stay longer than needed, because prolonged cannulation causes infection, ulceration, granulation, stenosis and unsightly scars; decannulation follows 24 hours of tolerating a plugged tube (a smaller tube in children), with children watched closely afterwards; and failed decannulation is explained by persistent disease, granulation at or below the stoma, tracheal oedema or subglottic stenosis, an infolded tracheal wall at the stoma, tracheomalacia, or psychological dependence. Lee lists a persistent tracheocutaneous fistula after decannulation among the delayed complications of tracheostomy, with granulation, stomal infection, stenosis, tracheomalacia, tracheo-oesophageal and tracheo-innominate fistulae and tube displacement. Myers gives the repair 3 months' wait after decannulation, airway endoscopy and a 24-hour occlusion trial first, the trapdoor epithelial lining, the midline strap muscle repair, a drain for air, the tube-based method for defects over 4 mm, secondary closure in children, and the bedside drill for air leak."],["osec","th:op-23~s7-2","Viva essentials","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why a tracheocutaneous fistula forms and persists (an epithelium-lined track). Why the scar is depressed (strap muscles scarred laterally). Before closing: airway endoscopy and a 24-hour occlusion trial, and why. The two key steps : an epithelial trapdoor lining and the midline strap repair. The method for a defect over 4 mm . Children: excision with secondary closure, and intensive care monitoring. The air leak drill : open the wound, cuffed tube through the stoma, never mask ventilation. Decannulation principles and causes of failed decannulation."],["osec","th:op-23~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Management of a Tracheocutaneous Fistula and Depressed Scar · For the examination","Common pitfalls Closing a fistula that is serving as an airway. Operating before 3 months. No inner lining, or a closure that is not airtight. Straps left half-freed. No drain for air. Mask ventilation for an air leak. A long, complex repair in a small child."],["osec","th:op-27~s1-1","The case at a glance","Cancer of the Lip · Operating note","Excision and reconstruction of lip cancer Summary Indications Biopsy-proven malignancy of the lip; neck dissection or sentinel node biopsy for risk factors (thick tumours, upper lip, commissure, recurrence); therapeutic neck dissection for nodes Margins Full-thickness excision with clear margins (frozen sections help); vermilionectomy (lip shave) for field change or in situ disease Reconstruction by defect Under about a third (up to half): direct closure (V or W wedge). A third to two-thirds: advancement flaps, Abbe (lip switch) or Estlander (commissure) flap, Karapandzic flap. Over two-thirds: Bernard-Burow (Webster) cheek advancement, Gillies fan flap, free flap (radial forearm with tendon sling) Anaesthesia Local for small wedges; general for flaps and neck surgery Tray Fine plastic instruments, marking pen, 6-0 sutures for vermilion, bipolar"],["osec","th:op-27~s1-2","Consent","Cancer of the Lip · Operating note","What to tell the patient A scar, and a smaller mouth ( microstomia ), especially after large reconstructions. Drooling if the sphincter is weak. Numbness of the lip and chin. Abbe flap: a two-stage operation, with the lips joined for 2 to 3 weeks. Recurrence, and neck treatment if nodes are involved."],["osec","th:op-27~s1-3","Before you start","Cancer of the Lip · Operating note","Mark the vermilion border before injecting (adrenaline blanches it and hides the line). Palpate the neck (lower lip drains to submental and submandibular nodes, crossing the midline; the upper lip to submandibular, parotid and preauricular nodes). Figure 1 Lymphatic drainage of the lip. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 27.1"],["osec","th:op-27~s2-1","Excise","Cancer of the Lip · The operation, stage by stage","Excise the tumour full thickness with clear margins: a V or W-shaped wedge for small lower lip lesions (the W keeps the scar above the mental crease), a rectangle when an advancement flap will close it. Send margins for frozen section. Figure 2 A wedge excision of a lower lip lesion and its closure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 27.2 Figure 3 A large lip cancer marked with frozen section margins. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 27.7 What you should see A full-thickness defect with clear margins and the labial arteries controlled."],["osec","th:op-27~s2-2","Close a small defect in three layers","Cancer of the Lip · The operation, stage by stage","Align the vermilion border first with a single exact suture, then close mucosa, orbicularis oris (to restore the sphincter) and skin with everting sutures. What you should see A continuous vermilion line without a step and a muscle layer closed under the skin. The white roll A step of 1 mm in the vermilion border is visible at conversational distance. Mark it before injecting and align it first."],["osec","th:op-27~s2-3","Medium defects: borrow from the other lip","Cancer of the Lip · The operation, stage by stage","Abbe flap: a full-thickness wedge from the opposite lip, about half the width of the defect, pedicled on the labial artery near the vermilion, is rotated into the defect; the pedicle is divided at 2 to 3 weeks. Estlander flap: the same idea for a defect at the commissure, rotated around it in one stage (it blunts the commissure). Karapandzic flap: circumoral incisions that rotate and advance the remaining lip with its nerves and vessels preserved, keeping a working sphincter at the cost of a smaller mouth. Figure 4 An Abbe flap for an extensive upper lip cancer. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 27.4 Figure 5 Reconstruction of lower lip and commissure defects. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 27.5 and 27.6 What you should see A flap with its labial artery pedicle visible and the orbicularis repaired end to end. Senior's pearl Innervated muscle matters more than appearance. Flaps that keep the orbicularis with its nerve supply (Karapandzic) give a competent mouth; adynamic flaps and free flaps fill the gap but drool."],["osec","th:op-27~s2-4","Large defects","Cancer of the Lip · The operation, stage by stage","For more than about two-thirds of the lip, use bilateral cheek advancement (Bernard-Burow or Webster), fan flaps, or a free radial forearm flap with a palmaris longus tendon sling. Reconstruct the vermilion with a mucosal advancement. What you should see A closed oral sphincter with an opening wide enough for a spoon and, ideally, dentures."],["osec","th:op-27~s3-1","Crisis drills","Cancer of the Lip · When it goes wrong","Margin positive on final histology 1 Act re-excise or radiotherapy Abbe flap pedicle congested 1 Act release tight sutures leech or pinprick bleeding avoid avulsing the pedicle"],["osec","th:op-27~s4-1","After surgery","Cancer of the Lip · Aftercare","Soft diet, mouth care, protect the Abbe pedicle (no wide opening); divide it at 2 to 3 weeks. Commissuroplasty later for microstomia if needed. Sun protection for life."],["ev","th:op-27~s4-2","Cancer of the Lip: current evidence","Cancer of the Lip · Aftercare","Lip and oral cavity cancer remains a major global burden, with ultraviolet exposure and tobacco the main preventable causes of lip cancer (GBD 2019 Lip, Oral, and Pharyngeal Cancer Collaborators, JAMA Oncol 2023). Evidence comparing reconstructive techniques is from case series; the choice rests on defect size and preserving an innervated sphincter."],["osec","th:op-27~s4-2","Complications","Cancer of the Lip · Aftercare","Bleeding, infection, wound breakdown, microstomia, oral incompetence and drooling, numbness, notching of the vermilion, recurrence, nodal disease."],["osec","th:op-27~s5-1","Where the books differ","Cancer of the Lip · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers direct closure under half the lip, advancement and Abbe flaps for a half to two-thirds, larger flaps and free tissue above that, with neck treatment for risk factors. K. J. Lee the classic thresholds (a third, two-thirds), Estlander and Karapandzic flaps, and nodal drainage by lip."],["osec","th:op-27~s5-2","Viva essentials","Cancer of the Lip · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Risk factors and nodal drainage. Reconstruction by defect size and the named flaps. Vermilion alignment. Sphincter function versus mouth opening."],["osec","th:op-27~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Cancer of the Lip · For the examination","Common pitfalls Injecting before marking the vermilion border. Closing skin without repairing the orbicularis. Reconstructions that leave a tiny mouth. Ignoring the neck in high-risk tumours."],["osec","th:op-28~s1-1","The case at a glance","Partial Glossectomy · Operating note","Partial glossectomy Summary Indications T1 and T2 squamous cell carcinoma of the oral tongue ; other tumours arising in the oral tongue Not suitable Locally advanced (most T3 and T4) cancers, which need hemiglossectomy or subtotal glossectomy; unacceptable comorbidity; poor functional status; lack of social support; the patient does not consent Neck Selective dissection of levels I to III, and IV, for stage 1 and 2; comprehensive dissection if imaging shows nodes; bilateral when the cancer nears the midline, the anterior floor of mouth or the base of tongue; sentinel node biopsy is an alternative in selected cN0 cases Anaesthesia General, oral or nasal intubation as access, teeth, site and preference dictate; full muscle relaxation helps mouth opening and stops the tongue moving under diathermy Position Supine Antibiotics Not needed if the mouth will not communicate with the neck; cover oral flora if it will (as with free flap reconstruction) Tray Neck dissection soft tissue set; side mouth retractor and bite block; guarded diathermy tip; headlight Prerequisites Diagnostic endoscopy, oral cavity anatomy, neck dissection"],["osec","th:op-28~s1-2","Consent","Partial Glossectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer with a margin of healthy tongue, usually with an operation on the neck at the same time. Speech and swallowing change; how well they recover depends on doing the exercises afterwards. Bleeding, most dangerous in the first 2 to 3 weeks: if you spit bright red blood or clots at home, call an ambulance. A further operation if the margins are close or involved. Further treatment with radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy, depending on the final pathology. Pain, difficulty swallowing , weight loss, and constipation from painkillers. A tracheostomy if a graft or flap is needed. Infection of the neck if the mouth and neck connect; chest infection from aspiration."],["osec","th:op-28~s1-3","Before you start","Partial Glossectomy · Operating note","Ask how long the lesion has been there and who found it, and whether it has been biopsied. Cancer pain is dull and persistent for weeks or months, \"a sore\"; otalgia suggests deep infiltration. Ask about dysphagia, change of diet, weight loss, dysarthria and a neck lump: a greater symptom burden before treatment predicts worse survival, independent of stage. Record previous treatment and analgesia, smoking and alcohol (how much), cardiac and respiratory symptoms, and the support at home, which will shape rehabilitation. Examine the size and depth of the cancer; whether it is exophytic, infiltrative or ulcerated; extension to the floor of mouth; the teeth; trismus; invasion of the mandible directly or through tooth sockets; extension into the base of tongue on palpation; and the nasopharynx, larynx and hypopharynx with a mirror or endoscope. Palpate the neck for nodes and their levels. Figure 1 (A) An irregular plaque with raised edges suggests invasion at least 2 to 3 mm deep; palpation tells you more. (B) Partial glossectomy removes an adequate margin of mucosa and muscle; at the back of the oral tongue it often needs no formal reconstruction. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 28.1 Feel it Tongue cancer may be a beefy red patch, an ulcer or arise in leukoplakia. A thin cancer (under about 2 to 3 mm) feels different from a deeply infiltrating one, which may be a hard mass under normal mucosa. Deviation of the protruded tongue means hypoglossal nerve infiltration or invasion of the extrinsic muscles. Figure 2 (A) Deviation of the tongue should raise concern about deep inf"],["osec","th:op-28~s1-4","Imaging and preparation","Partial Glossectomy · Operating note","Obtain a chest radiograph. Image the neck and primary with contrast CT or gadolinium MRI when deep (extrinsic) muscle involvement or neck disease is suspected, and CT when bone invasion is a concern. Imaging is more accurate than examination for nodes, and nodes on imaging upstage the cancer and call for a comprehensive rather than selective dissection. Review the outside biopsy and scans. Consider formal detoxification for heavy drinkers with a history of delirium tremens; offer smoking cessation; stop antiplatelet drugs if possible; arrange medical and anaesthetic assessment and dental review with extractions as needed. Cross-match is not usually necessary. Rehabilitation starts before surgery Exercises for trismus, tongue mobility and neck and shoulder movement work best if started before dysfunction sets in. Involving the speech therapist and physiotherapist around the operation improves adherence."],["osec","th:op-28~s2-1","Landmarks","Partial Glossectomy · Anatomy you must see","The lingual artery, met posterolaterally in the tongue as you complete the deep cut: it is the source of both intraoperative bleeding and the dangerous late bleed. The lingual nerve in the floor of the mouth."],["osec","th:op-28~s2-2","Patterns of spread","Partial Glossectomy · Anatomy you must see","Larger and deeper cancers are more likely to metastasise. Lateral cancers usually spread to the same side of the neck, but the risk to the other side rises as the cancer nears the midline; involvement of the anterior floor of mouth or base of tongue brings more bilateral and contralateral metastasis and calls for bilateral neck dissection."],["osec","th:op-28~s3-1","Endoscopy first","Partial Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Under the same anaesthetic, perform direct laryngoscopy and oesophagoscopy to look for a synchronous primary . What you should see A larynx, hypopharynx and oesophagus free of a second tumour, and the tumour's posterior limit."],["osec","th:op-28~s3-2","Airway and exposure","Partial Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","A tracheostomy is rarely needed unless a skin graft or free flap is planned; then make it before the resection, which also improves exposure, and secure the tube to the neck skin to keep the tubing out of the way. Open the mouth and retract the cheek with retractors, use a headlight, and pass a suture through the midline of the tip to draw the tongue out. What you should see The whole tumour delivered forward on the traction suture, with the cheek and lips out of the field."],["osec","th:op-28~s3-3","Mark the margin with your finger","Partial Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Feel along the cancer with your index finger and make the first incisions about 1 cm into normal tongue around what you feel, not just what you see. Use a guarded diathermy tip so the lips are not burnt. Place a silk suture on the anterior specimen for retraction and orientation. What you should see A marked line at least 1 cm beyond the edge you can feel, all the way round. Cutting into the cancer Infiltrating cancer extends further than it looks. Palpate again and again during the resection; a cut through the tumour gives a positive margin and worse local control. Bleeding that hides the field makes this more likely."],["osec","th:op-28~s3-4","Ligate the lingual artery","Partial Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","As the deep cut reaches the back of the wound, clamp and ligate the lingual artery . Deep sutures in the posterior tongue muscle looped around the artery reduce the risk of a severe late bleed. Ligation is more effective than extensive cautery and gives less pain and dysphagia afterwards. Closing the residual tongue primarily does not lower the bleeding risk and makes little difference to function. What you should see A specimen with a cuff of muscle all round, and a dry bed with the lingual artery tied posteriorly."],["osec","th:op-28~s3-5","Check the margins now","Partial Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Ink and open the specimen with the pathologist to judge the gross margin; a gross margin under 5 mm calls for more resection. If any margin is in doubt, assess the whole margin, peripheral and deep, all the way round. Mark the residual tongue on the patient's side with blue dye and excise and orient a further layer 2 to 4 mm thick, mucosa and deep muscle; this layer becomes the true margin and avoids sampling error. While frozen sections are processed, do the neck dissection. What you should see On the cut specimen, a rim of normal muscle around the tumour of at least 5 mm at its narrowest, including the deep margin. Fix it today A close or positive margin is best dealt with during the first operation. If it is found only on final pathology, readmit the patient for further resection."],["osec","th:op-28~s3-6","Close, graft or flap","Partial Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Most T1 and many T2 defects are left to heal by secondary intention . A substantial loss of floor of mouth mucosa tethers the tongue and slurs speech; a split-thickness skin graft from the thigh (0.016 to 0.018 inch), firmly secured over a bolster, or a radial forearm free flap reduces contracture. Hemiglossectomy and subtotal glossectomy defects are usually reconstructed with microvascular tissue from the forearm or thigh. Larger, deeply infiltrating cancers may need a transcervical delivery or a mandibulotomy for safe resection, and bone invasion needs a segmental mandibulectomy. What you should see A dry bed with the lingual artery branches tied, and a tongue tip that still reaches the lips after closure."],["osec","th:op-28~s3-7","The neck dissection","Partial Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","For stage 1 and 2 disease, a selective dissection of levels I, II, III and possibly IV. If the mouth and neck connect, repair the communication, give antibiotics and place a drain. What you should see Levels I to III (and IV) cleared in continuity, with the hypoglossal and lingual nerves seen and preserved. Level IV and skip metastases The editor includes level IV in every elective dissection for lateral tongue cancer, because a small number of patients have skip metastases to level IV with nothing in levels I to III."],["osec","th:op-28~s4-1","Crisis drills","Partial Glossectomy · When it goes wrong","Bleeding during the resection 1 Expect it from the lingual artery, posterolaterally 2 Control clamp and ligate early cautery alone is usually not enough 3 Then resume palpating the margin once the field is dry Bleeding at home in the first 2 to 3 weeks 1 Patient calls spitting bright red blood or clots call an ambulance to the nearest emergency department 2 Assess airway first 3 Treat ligation in theatre with a secure airway is the safest suture ligation at the bedside only for a well seen site in a stable, cooperative patient Close margin on frozen section 1 Mark blue dye on the residual tongue 2 Excise a further oriented layer 2 to 4 mm thick, mucosa and deep muscle 3 Assess the whole margin, peripheral and deep"],["osec","th:op-28~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Partial Glossectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Operating without a confirmed diagnosis, or after inadequate staging. Inadequate or positive margins; cutting through the tumour. Poor haemostasis, which hides the field. Burning the lips with an unguarded diathermy tip; damaging the teeth."],["osec","th:op-28~s5-1","After the operation","Partial Glossectomy · Aftercare","Without reconstruction, most patients take a modified oral diet soon after surgery if pain is controlled; high-calorie drinks prevent weight loss. After a skin graft or free flap, feed by nasogastric tube until the wound has healed. Opioids are usually used rather than NSAIDs because of the bleeding risk, with stool softeners, fluids and laxatives. Arrange a routine speech and swallowing assessment early. Further treatment depends on the final pathology."],["osec","th:op-28~s5-2","Complications","Partial Glossectomy · Aftercare","Complications of partial glossectomy Complication Notes Tongue oedema Uncommon without reconstruction; tracheostomy at the time if reconstructing or if oedema develops; it can cause aspiration Haemorrhage Within 2 to 3 weeks; often needs theatre Neck infection From a communication between mouth and neck; explore and drain Aspiration pneumonia Especially with oedema; a temporary tracheostomy helps pulmonary toilet Dehydration and weight loss Supplement nutrition Pain, dysphagia, dysarthria Expected; reduced by early rehabilitation Constipation From opioids; use a bowel regimen Medical Cardiac events, venous thrombosis"],["ev","th:op-28~s5-3","Partial Glossectomy: current evidence","Partial Glossectomy · Aftercare","Elective neck dissection. In a randomised trial of early oral cancer, elective neck dissection at the time of tumour removal improved overall survival over watchful waiting with therapeutic dissection at relapse (D'Cruz et al., NEJM 2015). Sentinel node biopsy. A meta-analysis of three randomised trials (608 patients) found sentinel node biopsy oncologically non-inferior to elective neck dissection, with better neck and shoulder function, on low-certainty evidence (Gupta et al., Oral Oncol 2022). Staging and margins. The AJCC eighth edition uses depth of invasion (5 and 10 mm thresholds); a histological margin of 5 mm or more is the usual target, with close margins prompting re-excision or adjuvant treatment."],["osec","th:op-28~s5-3","Alternatives","Partial Glossectomy · Aftercare","External beam radiotherapy. Supportive or palliative care."],["osec","th:op-28~s6-1","The elective neck","Partial Glossectomy · For the examination","In the large randomised trial of D'Cruz and colleagues, elective neck dissection in node-negative patients improved overall and disease-free survival compared with waiting for nodes to appear. Nodal metastasis fell from 16.9% at 4 mm depth of invasion to 5.6% at 3 mm , so a neck dissection may be avoidable in cancers 3 mm deep or less , though this needs more study. Sentinel node biopsy finds micrometastases in experienced hands, but unlike selective neck dissection it has not been shown equivalent in a randomised trial. Early-stage disease has 85 to 90% five-year cancer-free survival; nodal metastasis halves it."],["osec","th:op-28~s6-2","Viva essentials","Partial Glossectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three questions before surgery: the primary, the neck, the reconstruction. Depth of invasion and nodal risk; the 3 mm threshold. Signs of deep infiltration: tongue deviation, otalgia, a hard mass. Margin: 1 cm by palpation; gross margin under 5 mm means more resection; the blue-dye re-resection of 2 to 4 mm. Ligation of the lingual artery, and the late bleed. When to leave the wound open, graft or flap. Elective neck levels, including IV, and when to go bilateral. Endoscopy for a synchronous primary."],["osec","th:op-28~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Partial Glossectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Trusting the eye rather than the finger for the margin. Relying on cautery for the lingual artery. Omitting the neck in a thick cancer. Forgetting rehabilitation and support at home."],["osec","th:op-29~s1-1","The case at a glance","Total Glossectomy · Operating note","Total glossectomy Summary Indications Advanced tongue cancer as primary surgery; when partial glossectomy cannot give clear margins; salvage after (chemo)radiotherapy Patient bars Medically unfit; malnourished; cannot give informed consent or face the challenges to speech and swallowing; poor social support; unlikely to complete adjuvant treatment Tumour bars Clear margins unachievable (especially in salvage); distant metastasis Surgical bars Inadequate resecting or reconstructive expertise Add a laryngectomy Cancer in the pre-epiglottic space or epiglottis ; patient unlikely to tolerate aspiration Anaesthesia Nasal intubation with the surgeon present at induction (bulky tumours can make intubation impossible); temporary tracheostomy always; nasogastric tube; no paralysis (to protect accessory and marginal nerves); temperature, fluids and haemoglobin optimised for a free flap; urinary catheter and rectal temperature probe Position Supine, neck extended, set up for two teams at once; anaesthetic machine at the foot Antibiotics At induction and for 24 hours: clindamycin, co-amoxiclav, or a cephalosporin with metronidazole Tray Two sets of diathermy; head and neck set; oscillating saw; plates; power drill; tracheostomy tubes; microvascular set, sutures, clips, heparinised saline, loupes or microscope, tourniquet for a fibula"],["osec","th:op-29~s1-2","Consent","Total Glossectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the whole tongue and the cancer, with the lymph nodes on both sides of the neck, and to rebuild the floor of the mouth. Speech will be changed; eating will be re-learned with the speech therapist, at first by placing soft food at the back of the mouth. Aspiration : food and saliva going into the airway; if the risk is too high, or the cancer reaches the voice box, the voice box will be removed too. A temporary tracheostomy and a feeding tube. Part of the jawbone will be removed along the top edge (marginal mandibulectomy), and the lower teeth may go. The flap may fail, and a second operation may be needed. Radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy afterwards, usually within 6 weeks. You are unlikely to return to full employment."],["osec","th:op-29~s1-3","Before you start","Total Glossectomy · Operating note","Ask about smoking, alcohol and HPV; weight loss and nutrition; whether pain is controlled; pointers to a synchronous primary; lung function and aspiration; deficits of the lingual, hypoglossal and mental nerves; dysphonia or stridor (laryngeal spread); previous cancers, radiotherapy, surgery, trauma or claudication (which affect flap choice); heart and lung disease (can the patient climb a flight of stairs?); immunosuppression; alcohol and substance misuse (withdrawal); anticoagulants; and whether the patient has the mental and social resources to cope. Can this patient survive the operation and its aftermath? Most patients need surgery and then (chemo)radiotherapy, so there is no point operating on someone who will not complete adjuvant treatment. A patient keeping the larynx must have the cardiopulmonary reserve to cope with some aspiration; physical fitness, lung reserve, cognition and attitude all count. Pain, pooling, bleeding and trismus hamper examination; consider giving morphine first. Define the cancer's limits: back to the vallecula and supraglottis, the floor of mouth, the mandible (free, abutting, adherent or invaded), the extrinsic and suprahyoid muscles, and the nerves (lingual: numb anterior tongue and floor of mouth; inferior alveolar: numb mental area; hypoglossal, hard to judge). Assess the height of the mandible (a thin mandible may rule out a marginal and need a segmental resection with fibula), the teeth, both necks and old scars, the rest of the upper aerodigestive tract, and donor sites (obesity may rule out thigh and rectus; vein harvest or peripher"],["osec","th:op-29~s1-4","The mandible, the nerves and the larynx","Total Glossectomy · Operating note","Mandible: if only the periosteum is involved, a marginal mandibulectomy may suffice; once the cortex is invaded, segmental ; once the medulla is invaded, segmental or hemimandibulectomy with at least 2 cm of bone either side of visible cancer. Orthopantomogram or CT shows gross destruction; MRI is preferred for medullary invasion. Perineural spread: a widened inferior alveolar canal on the orthopantomogram, or MRI changes, mean the nerve must be followed and resected proximally until frozen section is clear. Pre-epiglottic space: look on sagittal CT or MRI, or in theatre by feeling for thickening between a finger in the vallecula and a finger on the neck just below the hyoid. If in doubt, consent for laryngectomy. Figure 1 A second primary tongue cancer involving the vallecula and epiglottis, needing total glossectomy with laryngectomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.1 Figure 2 The relations of the hyoid, epiglottis, vallecula and pre-epiglottic space. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.2"],["osec","th:op-29~s1-5","Imaging and preparation","Total Glossectomy · Operating note","Chest radiograph for metastases, lung cancer and cardiopulmonary status; contrast swallow if oesophageal cancer is suspected; orthopantomogram for teeth, bone invasion, mandibular height and the inferior alveolar canal. CT, MRI and PET-CT are not needed in everyone: CT for mandibular or pre-epiglottic involvement or an unclear chest; MRI for extrinsic and suprahyoid muscle invasion, marrow and perineural spread; PET-CT for distant disease. Neck imaging is not needed for nodes because both necks are always dissected. Arrange review by the oncological and reconstructive surgeons, oncologist, speech and swallowing therapist and anaesthetist; book intensive care; stop antiplatelets. Take dental radiographs and refer to a dentist, or remove carious teeth at surgery, to prevent osteoradionecrosis."],["osec","th:op-29~s2-1","Landmarks","Total Glossectomy · Anatomy you must see","The mylohyoid line: the attachment of mylohyoid, the floor of the mouth; mylohyoid and its attachment are resected for clearance. The genial tubercles: genioglossus inserts on the superior, geniohyoid on the inferior. The anterior belly of digastric insertion. The sublingual glands between the submandibular duct and the mandible. The lingual arteries, emerging behind hyoglossus to enter the tongue posterolaterally. The pre-epiglottic space and hyoid. The superior laryngeal nerves crossing the thyrohyoid membrane. The mental nerves at the mental foramina, at risk in the edentulous elderly. Figure 3 The mylohyoid line and the attachments of genioglossus and geniohyoid to the genial tubercles, and of digastric. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.3"],["osec","th:op-29~s3-1","Secure the airway","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Stand by at induction: if the tongue cannot be lifted to see the larynx, fibreoptic intubation may be needed, and failing that intubation through an operating laryngoscope, an emergency tracheostomy or cricothyroidotomy. A temporary tracheostomy is always done, because bleeding, swelling, a bulky flap and loss of the larynx's anterior suspension threaten the airway. What you should see A secure tracheostomy before any resection."],["osec","th:op-29~s3-2","Endoscopy and reassessment","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Perform panendoscopy for synchronous cancers, and reassess the primary (especially its posterior and lateral limits) and the necks. What you should see The posterior and lateral limits of the tumour, and no second primary."],["osec","th:op-29~s3-3","Neck dissections","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Open both necks. Advanced tongue cancer is very likely to have nodal disease, so perform bilateral selective (levels I to IV) or comprehensive dissections, completed before resecting the primary. The author combines transcervical and transoral approaches; others use a midline lip split with a median or paramedian mandibulotomy. Figure 4 The skin incisions on both sides of the neck. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.4 Figure 5 Exposure after bilateral selective neck dissections: mandibular periosteum, anterior belly of digastric, mylohyoid, hypoglossal nerve and hyoid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.5 What you should see Both necks cleared, the lingual and hypoglossal nerves and the lingual arteries identified on each side."],["osec","th:op-29~s3-4","The cervical dissection","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the periosteum along the lower border of the mandible from angle to angle , divide the anterior bellies of digastric and mylohyoid with diathermy, and free them from the bone. Strip the soft tissue off the inner mandible subperiosteally; if the cancer reaches the lateral floor of mouth, strip up to the mylohyoid line. Free geniohyoid from the inferior mental spine. Figure 6 Incising the periosteum along the lower border of the mandible (yellow) and dividing the anterior belly of digastric (green). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.6 What you should see The inner surface of the mandible bare from angle to angle, with the floor-of-mouth muscles released."],["osec","th:op-29~s3-5","The marginal mandibulectomy","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","When tumour abuts the mandible, do the marginal mandibulectomy first, which makes the tongue resection much easier. Incise the gingiva along the alveolar ridge, strip the soft tissue off the outer mandible without exposing its lower half (to keep its blood supply), and protect the mental nerves. Cut along the upper part of the horizontal ramus with a power saw, angled obliquely to include the mylohyoid line , especially if the cancer reaches the lateral floor of mouth. Figure 7 The mylohyoid line and genial attachments; the broken line marks the marginal mandibulectomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.7 Figure 8 Incising along the gingival margin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.8 Figure 9 The osteotomy along the dotted line with a power saw. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.9 What you should see A strip of bone that includes the mylohyoid line, with the lower half of the mandible and the mental nerves intact. Why remove the alveolus? The marginal mandibulectomy matters for clearance, and it also prevents a sump along the inside of the mandible where food and saliva collect, by letting you sew the flap directly to the buccal and labial mucosa."],["osec","th:op-29~s3-6","Deliver and resect","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Deliver the bone strip, floor of mouth and tongue into the neck as one unit , which shows the whole tongue, vallecula and epiglottis. Resect the cancer with monopolar diathermy and adequate margins. Keep as much tongue base mucosa as you safely can, and preserve the superior laryngeal nerves , both for swallowing. Ligate the lingual arteries as you meet them posterolaterally. Examine the specimen and send frozen sections, especially from the tongue base, where tumour and thick, irregular normal mucosa look alike. Figure 10 The bone strip, floor of mouth and tongue dropped into the neck. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.10 Figure 11 Transcervical resection with excellent exposure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.11 Figure 12 Total glossectomy with marginal mandibulectomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.12 What you should see The whole tongue, vallecula and epiglottis in the neck, with margins you can see and feel all round."],["osec","th:op-29~s3-7","Laryngectomy or not?","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","If planned, remove the larynx in continuity. If not, examine the posterior margin: if the epiglottis and pre-epiglottic fat are free and the patient is fit enough to tolerate some aspiration, keep the larynx and reconstruct. Figure 13 The completed resection with a small amount of residual tongue base. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.13 What you should see The epiglottis and pre-epiglottic fat free of tumour at the posterior margin."],["osec","th:op-29~s3-8","Reconstruct with bulk","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Choose a bulky flap that makes a convex floor of mouth bulging up to the hard palate, so the neotongue meets the palate for speech and swallowing and secretions do not pool. It will descend and lose muscle bulk with time, so it should almost touch the palate at the end of surgery. Pectoralis major or latissimus dorsi pedicled flaps, or anterolateral thigh or rectus free flaps, give enough volume; consider a sensate flap. Avoid the radial forearm : too thin, it leaves a concave floor, a sump, spillage and poor articulation. A fibula for the mandible also brings too little soft tissue. Sew the flap edges to the buccal and labial mucosa with continuous absorbable sutures. Figure 14 A large flap making a convex floor of mouth. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.14 Figure 15 Long-term result of a pectoralis major flap: a convex floor without the sump that keeping the lower teeth would have caused. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.15 What you should see A flap dome that almost touches the hard palate, with a watertight suture line."],["osec","th:op-29~s3-9","Suspend the larynx and close","Total Glossectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Dividing all the larynx's attachments to the mandible (digastrics, mylohyoids, geniohyoids, genioglossi) promotes aspiration, impairs swallowing and can cause obstructive sleep apnoea. Suspend the hyoid from the anterior arch of the mandible with two strong non-absorbable sutures. Close the necks over two suction drains and complete the tracheostomy. Figure 16 Laryngeal suspension with non-absorbable sutures. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 29.16 What you should see The hyoid pulled up and forward to the mandible, and the airway on the tracheostomy."],["osec","th:op-29~s4-1","Crisis drills","Total Glossectomy · When it goes wrong","Cannot intubate 1 Prevent surgeon present at induction plan nasal fibreoptic intubation 2 Fallback intubation through an operating laryngoscope emergency tracheostomy or cricothyroidotomy Haematoma under the flap 1 Act return to theatre as soon as possible to save the flap Alcohol withdrawal 1 When onset on days 2 and 3 2 Act involve the physician or intensivist sedation and ventilation may be needed"],["osec","th:op-29~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Total Glossectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Keeping the lower teeth without a marginal mandibulectomy: the teeth have little value without a tongue, and the lateral floor becomes a sump. A flap with too little bulk: it should almost touch the palate at the end. Omitting a laryngectomy in a patient who then aspirates badly."],["osec","th:op-29~s5-1","After the operation","Total Glossectomy · Aftercare","Antibiotics for 24 hours; monitor the flap; remove drains when under 50 mL in 24 hours. Keep the tracheostomy until you are sure no return to theatre is needed (for flap ischaemia, for example) and the airway is adequate, then cork it overnight and remove it if the airway holds. Start nasogastric feeding the morning after surgery. Delay oral feeding until decannulation; then the speech and swallowing therapist teaches the patient to swallow thickened liquids and purée placed at the back of the mouth with a syringe or spoon, until the patient learns to throw the head back and use gravity. Begin postoperative (chemo)radiotherapy within 6 weeks ."],["osec","th:op-29~s5-2","Complications","Total Glossectomy · Aftercare","Complications of total glossectomy Complication Notes Haematoma Theatre quickly, to save the flap Alcohol withdrawal Days 2 to 3; added morbidity, mortality and stay Tracheostomy Blockage, dislodgement, delayed decannulation with a very bulky flap Flap Loss from perfusion failure; too bulky (late decannulation) or too small (poor speech, pooling) Dysphagia and aspiration Especially if the larynx was kept in an unsuitable patient"],["ev","th:op-29~s5-3","Total Glossectomy: current evidence","Total Glossectomy · Aftercare","Function after total glossectomy. With laryngeal preservation and a bulky free flap (anterolateral thigh or rectus abdominis) to fill the oral cavity, many patients regain intelligible speech and an oral diet in published series, although gastrostomy dependence remains common; laryngeal suspension and a cricopharyngeal myotomy aid swallowing. Selection. Older age, poor lung function and radiotherapy reduce the chance of safe swallowing; total glossolaryngectomy may be safer in some. Adjuvant chemoradiation follows for positive margins and extranodal extension."],["osec","th:op-29~s5-3","Alternatives","Total Glossectomy · Aftercare","Always offer non-surgical treatment to patients with advanced tongue cancer."],["osec","th:op-29~s6-1","Quality of life and survival","Total Glossectomy · For the examination","Patients with advanced tongue cancer often have severe pain, poor speech and great weight loss, which may be why Dziegielewski and colleagues found speech and swallowing compared well with the preoperative state, and quality of life scores fell only a little; in the author's own series, eight of eight returned to a full oral diet. Van Lierop and colleagues reported 63%, 38% and 25% alive without disease at 2, 3 and 5 years after primary total glossectomy with radiotherapy for stage IV disease; a review by Navach and colleagues gave 5-year overall and disease-free survival of 79% and 61%. Series are small and selected. The editor would add a laryngectomy if much of the supraglottis must go or lung reserve is poor."],["osec","th:op-29~s6-2","Viva essentials","Total Glossectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Selection: fitness, nutrition, cognition, support, ability to complete adjuvant therapy. When to add a laryngectomy, and how to judge pre-epiglottic involvement. Marginal, segmental or hemimandibulectomy by depth of bone invasion. Perineural spread and how to chase it. The airway plan and the routine tracheostomy. The marginal mandibulectomy to avoid a sump. Flap choice for bulk; why not a radial forearm. Hyoid suspension. The swallowing technique after surgery."],["osec","th:op-29~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Total Glossectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a patient who will not finish radiotherapy. Losing the airway at induction. Sacrificing the superior laryngeal nerves or excess tongue base. A flat or concave floor of mouth."],["osec","th:op-3~s1-1","The case at a glance","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · Operating note","Surgery for recurrent respiratory papillomatosis Summary Presentation Progressive hoarseness, stridor and breathlessness (children often misdiagnosed as asthma or croup); adult-onset disease is usually milder Aim A safe airway and the best voice, preserving normal tissue; not complete removal at all costs Methods Cold steel microsurgery, microdebrider (common in children), KTP or other angiolytic laser (also in clinic for adults), CO2 laser Adjuvants HPV vaccination; intralesional or systemic bevacizumab for aggressive disease; cidofovir (less used now); treat reflux Avoid Tracheostomy where possible (may seed disease into the trachea) Anaesthesia General with a small laser-safe tube, or tubeless (jet or apnoeic) techniques; office procedures under topical anaesthesia in adults Tray Laryngoscopes, telescopes (0, 30, 70 degree), microscope, microlaryngeal instruments, microdebrider with laryngeal blades, laser with safety equipment, wet towels and pledgets"],["osec","th:op-3~s1-2","Consent","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · Operating note","What to tell the patient or family Repeated operations are likely; the disease recurs. Scarring and webbing, especially at the front of the vocal folds, which can worsen voice and airway. Airway fire (rare) when a laser is used. Dental and mucosal injury, sore throat, temporary tongue numbness or taste change from the laryngoscope. Rarely, spread to the lungs or malignant change (more in adults and smokers)."],["osec","th:op-3~s1-3","Before you start","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · Operating note","Look at the whole airway in clinic with a flexible scope and stroboscopy if possible. Score the disease (for example the Derkay staging) to track it over time. Send tissue for HPV typing at the first operation. Plan the anaesthetic airway together, especially with obstructing disease. Figure 1 Papillomas of the larynx. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 3.1 Figure 2 A staging form for recording the extent of disease at each site. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 3.2"],["osec","th:op-3~s2-1","Expose and map","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · The operation, stage by stage","Suspend the largest laryngoscope that fits. Examine the oral cavity, pharynx, larynx, subglottis and trachea with 0, 30 and 70 degree telescopes, map and photograph every site (the ventricles, undersurface of the folds and commissures are best seen with angled scopes), and score the disease. What you should see A complete map of papilloma sites from the supraglottis to the trachea."],["osec","th:op-3~s2-2","Sample","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · The operation, stage by stage","Remove a small piece with cold instruments for histology (and HPV typing at the first operation) before using any energy device. What you should see A clean specimen without thermal artefact."],["osec","th:op-3~s2-3","Remove the papillomas","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · The operation, stage by stage","Use the method suited to the site: a microdebrider shaves bulky disease quickly in children; an angiolytic (KTP) laser coagulates papillomas on the vocal folds with little damage to the vibrating layer; cold steel for discrete lesions. Treat to the surface, not through the vocal ligament. Figure 3 KTP laser treatment of papillomas (here in the office). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 3.3 What you should see An open airway and vocal folds with their smooth edges and underlying layers preserved. The anterior commissure web Treating raw areas on both vocal folds at the anterior commissure in one session creates a web. Leave a small strip on one side and treat it at the next session."],["osec","th:op-3~s2-4","Laser safety","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · The operation, stage by stage","Use a laser-safe tube with a saline-filled cuff, saline pledgets in the subglottis above it, wet towels on the face, the lowest oxygen concentration the patient tolerates, and the laser on standby when not firing. What you should see Wet protection in place and inspired oxygen kept low before the laser fires. Senior's pearl This is a disease of decades. Every session, ask what the patient needs now (airway or voice) and leave everything that does not need treating today. Healthy, scar-free mucosa is worth more than a \"clean\" larynx."],["osec","th:op-3~s3-1","Crisis drills","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · When it goes wrong","Airway fire 1 Act stop the laser stop oxygen remove the tube pour saline 2 Then ventilate by mask, reintubate bronchoscopy to assess the injury Obstructing disease on induction 1 Act spontaneous ventilation induction debulk through a rigid scope or ventilating bronchoscope tracheostomy only as a last resort"],["osec","th:op-3~s4-1","After surgery","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · Aftercare","Voice rest briefly, anti-reflux treatment, regular clinic reviews with endoscopy to plan the next session before the airway narrows. Offer HPV vaccination; refer aggressive or distal disease for systemic adjuvant therapy."],["ev","th:op-3~s4-2","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis: current evidence","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · Aftercare","A meta-analysis of 11 studies (133 patients) found that HPV vaccination given as an adjuvant reduced surgery from about 0.35 to 0.06 procedures per month and extended the interval between operations from about 7 to 34 months (Rosenberg et al., J Infect Dis 2019). A systematic review of 64 patients found systemic bevacizumab prolonged the surgical interval in 95%, with more than half needing no surgery over about 2 years; intralesional bevacizumab was less effective (Pogoda et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2022). Prophylactic HPV vaccination is expected to prevent most juvenile-onset disease: Australia saw a sharp fall in new juvenile cases after its national vaccination programme. Intralesional cidofovir is used less now, because controlled evidence is weak."],["osec","th:op-3~s4-2","Complications","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · Aftercare","Glottic and subglottic stenosis and webs, voice deterioration, airway fire, dental injury, distal spread into the trachea and lungs, malignant change."],["osec","th:op-3~s5-1","Where the books differ","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers staging at each operation, a biopsy first, and removal with KTP laser, microdebrider or cold steel, protecting the anterior commissure. Dhingra and K. J. Lee juvenile and adult-onset forms, HPV 6 and 11, maternal transmission, and avoidance of tracheostomy. The literature HPV vaccine and bevacizumab as effective adjuvants that reduce the number of operations."],["osec","th:op-3~s5-2","Viva essentials","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Virology and transmission. Presentation in a child and why it is missed. Surgical principles : airway and voice, preserve normal tissue, avoid webs. Laser safety and airway fire drill. Adjuvants and their evidence."],["osec","th:op-3~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Recurrent Respiratory Papillomatosis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Aggressive removal leaving bilateral raw anterior commissure. Laser without fire precautions. A tracheostomy that seeds the trachea. No tissue for histology at the first operation."],["osec","th:op-30~s1-1","The case at a glance","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Operating note","Excision of floor of mouth cancer Summary Soft tissue excision only Cancer not approaching or involving the mandible Marginal mandibulectomy Cancer on the periosteum but not the cortex; close to the periosteum without bone invasion; abutting healthy teeth without periodontal ligament involvement Not marginal Bone invasion; gross marrow invasion; recurrence after radiotherapy; a hypoplastic mandible; less than 1 cm of bone would remain Segmental mandibulectomy Marrow invasion; cancer reaching the occlusal surface of an edentulous mandible; an edentulous hypoplastic mandible that would otherwise suit a marginal; cancer abutting diseased teeth with periodontal ligament involvement; to improve exposure Mandibulotomy Better access to anterior cancers; not in an atrophic mandible Airway Nasal intubation; tracheostomy for extensive resections and flaps Position Table turned 180 degrees, shoulder roll, foam head ring; prepare neck, lower face and donor sites Antibiotics Ampicillin-sulbactam (clindamycin in allergy) at the start, every 6 hours, and for 24 hours Monitoring No paralysis during the neck dissection; a non-depolarising relaxant is usually needed for access to the floor of mouth Tray Major head and neck set; oral retractors; reciprocating and oscillating saws; tracheostomy, free flap and dental extraction sets; otological drill; 1% lidocaine with adrenaline; dexamethasone 10 mg; headlight, loupes, microscope"],["osec","th:op-30~s1-2","Consent","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer under the tongue with a margin of healthy tissue, usually with a neck operation. Bone: part of the jawbone may need removing, either a strip along the top or a full segment, rebuilt with bone from the leg or back. Reconstruction: a skin graft, or a flap from the neck, chest or forearm. A temporary tracheostomy and feeding tube for larger operations. Changes to speech and swallowing ; dentures may need implants. Numbness or weakness of the tongue (lingual and hypoglossal nerves), numbness of the chin. A leak of saliva to the neck, bleeding, infection, a fracture or failure of the jaw to heal. Radiotherapy may follow."],["osec","th:op-30~s1-3","Before you start","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Operating note","Age itself is not a contraindication, but comorbidity predicts survival, so involve physicians, oncologists, speech and swallowing therapists, dietitians and anaesthetists. Ask about the lesion (size, onset, growth, pain), an ill-fitting lower denture , speech, swallowing and chewing, signs of perineural spread (a stiff tongue, taste change, numbness), odynophagia, dysphagia, otalgia, bleeding, weight loss and a recent biopsy. Record leukoplakia, dysplasia or lichen planus, sleep apnoea, heart and lung disease, radiotherapy, previous head and neck cancer and treatment to the site or neck, immunosuppression, previous oral, neck or dental surgery, a fixed mandibular fracture, a previous tracheostomy, anaesthetic or bleeding problems in the family, anticoagulants, herbal products, immunosuppressants and allergies; alcohol, tobacco and betel quid ; occupation (especially cooks, and those who speak for a living), support, and whether the patient is a Jehovah's Witness. Figure 1 Squamous cell carcinoma of the floor of the mouth is strongly linked to tobacco and alcohol. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 30.1"],["osec","th:op-30~s1-4","Examination","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Operating note","Malnutrition and cachexia affect half of head and neck cancer patients and independently predict survival. Describe the cancer (exophytic, deeply infiltrating, ulcerated, papillary), its extent toward the tongue muscles (midline or lateral, anterior or lateral), tongue mobility, diameter for staging, its relation to the duct papilla , and the teeth. Feel the mandible Bimanual palpation of the cancer against the mandible predicts cortical invasion better than imaging. Feel how close it is to the lingual periosteum, whether it has breached the cortex, reached the alveolus or buccal surface, or the skin; look for a pathological fracture, and judge mandibular height and thickness and any old plates. Figure 2 (A) An advanced floor of mouth cancer; (B) invading the mandible and the skin of the chin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 30.2 Look for perineural spread (mental numbness, tongue numbness, taste change, reduced tongue movement) and test cranial nerves II to XII. Palpate both necks, note radiation fibrosis and old incisions, and look for a synchronous primary (a unilateral middle ear effusion, stridor, dysphonia). Assess donor sites: fibula, radial forearm, supraclavicular, pectoralis. Figure 3 A patient referred for laryngectomy in whom routine examination found an early floor of mouth cancer, excised and grafted at the same operation. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 30.3"],["osec","th:op-30~s1-5","Imaging and preparation","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Operating note","Contrast CT is first-line, with fine-cut multiplanar images for the mandibular cortex; MRI is rarely needed; an orthopantomogram for teeth and bone; chest CT for metastases and a lung primary; PET-CT is more accurate for nodes and distant disease but too costly as a routine screen. Arrange ultrasound-guided FNA of suspicious nodes, biopsy of the primary (and review of outside slides), panendoscopy (can be at the operation), extraction of carious teeth (also at the operation), nutritional bloods (albumin, half-life about 20 days; prealbumin, about 8 days) and haemoglobin where relevant, and a general surgeon if a feeding tube is planned. The airway In Shaha and colleagues' review of 320 floor of mouth cancers, half the postoperative deaths were from airway compromise . Agree the airway plan with the anaesthetist for induction and for the days after surgery."],["osec","th:op-30~s2-1","Landmarks","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Anatomy you must see","Mylohyoid: the diaphragm of the floor of mouth. Extrinsic tongue muscles: hyoglossus and geniohyoid. The submandibular and sublingual glands , and the duct opening (papilla). The lingual, hypoglossal and mental nerves. The lingual artery. The mandible."],["osec","th:op-30~s3-1","Neck first","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · The operation, stage by stage","Do the neck dissection first. What you should see The neck cleared, with the lingual and hypoglossal nerves and the submandibular duct identified from below."],["osec","th:op-30~s3-2","Soft tissue resection: plan by palpation","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · The operation, stage by stage","Place a bite block and a plastic cheek retractor, and a 2-0 silk through the tongue tip for traction. Palpate bimanually to confirm the stage and plan. Mark at least a 1 cm margin of normal mucosa, then inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline along the line (after palpating, not before). If the submandibular gland is staying, find the opening of Wharton's duct; if the papilla is within the resection, cannulate the duct with a lacrimal probe to help reroute it. What you should see The limits of induration between your fingers, whether the tumour moves on the mandible, and a marked 1 cm margin."],["osec","th:op-30~s3-3","Excise","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · The operation, stage by stage","Incise with a needle-tip diathermy, mark the anterior edge of the specimen with a 2-0 silk, and dissect from front to back. The deep margin lies at the level of the sublingual glands . Remove the specimen and send it for frozen section. Control bleeding by ligation or bipolar diathermy, not monopolar. Figure 4 (A) A 2 × 2 cm ulcerated exophytic cancer; (B) the specimen removed and haemostasis secured; (C) a split skin graft with long silk tie-overs for a Xeroform bolster; (D) the healed graft; (E) full dentures fitted over it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 30.4 What you should see The sublingual gland in the deep margin, the lingual nerve, and a specimen marked for orientation."],["osec","th:op-30~s3-4","The duct","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · The operation, stage by stage","If the gland goes with the neck dissection, the duct is not reconstructed. If the gland stays and its duct is within the resection, reroute it: follow the probe, sew the cut end to the remaining floor of mouth mucosa, open the duct along 8 to 10 mm, bring it up to the mucosa and marsupialise it with 4-0 chromic. What you should see A probe in the duct and saliva flowing from its new opening."],["osec","th:op-30~s3-5","Graft and finish","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · The operation, stage by stage","Resurface with a split skin graft , held by a Xeroform bolster tied with 2-0 silk. Empty the stomach with an orogastric tube and pass a fine-bore feeding tube, fixed to the septum with 3-0 silk. What you should see The graft flat against the bed under the bolster, with no blood beneath it."],["osec","th:op-30~s3-6","Soft tissue resection with marginal mandibulectomy","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the mucosal margins; the bony margins should include 1 to 1.5 cm of normal bone laterally. Resect bone and soft tissue en bloc to orient the pathologist. In the dentate, extract the tooth next to the planned cut and make the vertical cut in the front of the socket , keeping as much bone as possible to support the next tooth. Incise with a needle tip through mucosa and periosteum onto bone, across the alveolus and joined in front; lift the mucoperiosteum with a Freer. Draw the cuts on the bone. Make the vertical cuts with a reciprocating saw, taking only the alveolar process and leaving at least 1 cm of bone along the lower border; join them with a horizontal cut. Cut right through both the buccal and lingual plates , or the bone splinters or fractures. Lift the freed bone up and back with the soft tissue, and complete the resection. Smooth the bone edges with a cutting bur. Reconstruct with a skin graft over soft tissue and bone, a submental or supraclavicular island flap, or a radial forearm flap. Orogastric tube, feeding tube. Figure 5 (A) A second primary floor of mouth cancer 25 years after a first, previously reconstructed with a deltopectoral flap; (B) the old donor site; (C) the floor rebuilt with a deltopectoral flap (left) and a split skin graft (right). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 30.5 Figure 6 (A) Recurrent cancer behind an old skin graft, excised en bloc with an anterior marginal mandibulectomy; (B) a radial forearm flap being raised; (C) the soft, pliable flap does not restrict the tongue. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 30.6 What you shoul"],["osec","th:op-30~s3-7","Extensive soft tissue cancer without bone involvement","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · The operation, stage by stage","Make a tracheostomy and dissect both necks. Then choose: Pull-through: set the lateral margins with a needle tip and sharp dissection; at the level of the sublingual glands, approach the cancer from the neck: raise a subplatysmal flap to the lower border of the mandible, divide mylohyoid and digastric to enter the deep floor of mouth, deliver the floor of mouth into the neck and resect. The hypoglossal and lingual nerves, already found in the neck, can be kept if clearance allows. Reconstruct with pectoralis major, radial forearm or supraclavicular flap. Mandibulotomy , through a visor flap (raise the upper cervical flap to the mandible, incise the gingivobuccal sulcus 1 cm from the bone without stripping the anterior periosteum or injuring the mental nerves, and join them) or a lip split with a notch in the midline of the lower lip for camouflage, carried down to the neck incision. Pre-bend a plate to the native mandible, then make a midline mandibulotomy through both tables with an oscillating saw to expose and remove the cancer. Reconstruct with the plate and a skin graft or flap. What you should see The floor of mouth delivered into the neck with the hypoglossal and lingual nerves in view."],["osec","th:op-30~s3-8","Segmental mandibulectomy","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · The operation, stage by stage","Use a visor flap or lip split; if the cancer has destroyed the anterior mandible and infiltrates the chin skin, take the skin with it. Extract teeth at the osteotomy sites, make vertical cuts at least 1 cm from the tumour with a reciprocating saw at the front of the extraction sockets, free the periosteum, and resect cancer, bone and skin en bloc. Rebuild with a fibula or scapula flap. Figure 7 (A) Floor of mouth cancer destroying the anterior mandible and chin skin; (B) a bivalved scapular osteocutaneous flap replacing the bone; (C) one skin paddle for the floor of mouth; (D) the other for the chin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 30.7 What you should see Bone cuts at least 1 cm clear of tumour, and the condyles held in their preoperative position by the plate."],["osec","th:op-30~s4-1","Crisis drills","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · When it goes wrong","Swelling in the neck or mouth after surgery 1 Recognise intraoral or neck swelling, skin colour change a haematoma 2 Act the airway comes first Saliva in the drain 1 Recognise an orocutaneous fistula watch the drain output and the suture lines"],["osec","th:op-30~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Not orienting the specimen. An overaggressive marginal mandibulectomy leaving under 1 cm of bone. Not cutting fully through both plates, so the remaining bone splinters or fractures. Placing the vertical cut at the back of the socket, leaving the next tooth unsupported."],["osec","th:op-30~s5-1","After a skin graft or local flap","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Aftercare","Remove the bolster on day 5; 15 mL oral rinse four times daily for a week; trickle nasogastric feeds from day 1; clear fluids at 1 week if not irradiated, at 2 weeks if irradiated; remove the drain when under 30 mL in 24 hours; speech and swallowing review; analgesia. Once healed, full dentures can be made; after a marginal mandibulectomy, implants may be needed to hold them."],["osec","th:op-30~s5-2","After a free flap","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Aftercare","Intensive care for 48 hours with hourly flap checks, routine tracheostomy care, a week of clear fluids then 6 weeks of soft diet, and the drain out when under 30 mL a day."],["osec","th:op-30~s5-3","Complications","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Aftercare","Complications Complication Watch for Orocutaneous fistula Drain output, the suture line and skin Haematoma Swelling in the mouth or neck, skin colour Wound infection Pus in the drain, red or discharging wound Systemic infection Vital signs Hypoglossal injury Tongue movement to the midline and sides Lingual nerve injury Tongue sensation Pathological fracture, non-union Bimanual palpation; orthopantomogram if suspected"],["ev","th:op-30~s5-4","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth: current evidence","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Aftercare","Neck. Elective neck dissection improves survival in early oral cancer (D'Cruz et al., NEJM 2015), and floor of mouth tumours often spread to both sides, so bilateral necks are considered for midline tumours. Sentinel node biopsy is an alternative in centres with expertise, though floor of mouth tumours are where the injection site can obscure level I nodes. Mandible. Marginal mandibulectomy suffices when tumour abuts but does not invade the medullary bone; segmental resection is needed for medullary invasion, and the defect is classified (Brown et al., Lancet Oncol 2016) to plan reconstruction."],["osec","th:op-30~s5-4","Alternatives","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · Aftercare","Radiotherapy, chemoradiotherapy, brachytherapy."],["osec","th:op-30~s6-1","The clinically negative neck in early cancer","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · For the examination","T3 and T4 tumours get bilateral neck dissections; T1 and T2 with clinical or radiological nodes get an ipsilateral dissection. For T1 to T2 N0 there are three options. Watchful waiting: about 70% never need treatment, but 30% develop nodes and only about half of those are salvaged. Sentinel node biopsy: promising elsewhere in the mouth, but in the floor of mouth Ross and colleagues found sensitivity of only 80% (near 100% at other subsites) and neck disease in nearly 4% within 2 years. Elective neck dissection: more cost and morbidity, but a randomised trial of T1 to T2 oral cancer showed a 23.6% survival benefit , with eight patients treated to prevent one death."],["osec","th:op-30~s6-2","Viva essentials","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The epidemiology and the 30% occult nodal rate. Bimanual palpation as the best test of cortical invasion. The ladder: soft tissue, marginal, segmental, mandibulotomy, and their indications and contraindications. 1 cm mucosal margins; 1 to 1.5 cm of bone; at least 1 cm of bone left below a marginal. Rerouting Wharton's duct. The airway: half of deaths. The cN0 neck options and the evidence."],["osec","th:op-30~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Excision of Cancer of the Floor of the Mouth · For the examination","Common pitfalls Injecting local anaesthetic before palpating and marking. Using monopolar diathermy for haemostasis in the floor of mouth. Missing cortical invasion and then improvising the reconstruction. A mandibulotomy through an atrophic mandible."],["osec","th:op-36~s1-1","The case at a glance","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Operating note","Transoral robotic surgery for tongue base tumours Summary Indications Any tongue base tumour resectable through the mouth; ideal candidates are those expected to avoid adjuvant chemotherapy or chemoradiotherapy while keeping speech and swallowing Contraindications Deep involvement of more than half the tongue base (function, and the risk of devascularising the tongue by sacrificing both lingual arteries); invasion of the skull base, hyoid, great vessels or mandible; deep extrinsic muscle involvement; large glossotonsillar sulcus cancers risking carotid exposure Caution Previous radiotherapy (planes obliterated, slow healing); severe heart or lung disease (chemoradiotherapy may suit better); inherited coagulopathy (wounds heal by secondary intention) Uncertain exposure A staging examination under anaesthesia with the FK-WO retractor first Neck The authors stage the neck dissection before the robotic resection, ligating the lingual and facial arteries and a third branch (named as the superior laryngeal artery in one place and the superior thyroid in another), which reduces oedema and avoids a pharyngocutaneous fistula Fire safety FiO2 under 30%; a cuffed tube that does not leak; eye goggles Anaesthesia Full paralysis for the retractor; GlideScope oral intubation (awake fibreoptic if difficult); wire-reinforced tube sutured through the nasolabial fold on the side away from the tumour Position Table turned 180 degrees and lowered fully, the crown at the table edge; no shoulder roll Robot Docked on the right at 30 degrees, its foot touching the table base, the camera pivot at th"],["osec","th:op-36~s1-2","Consent","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer at the back of the tongue through the mouth, so its true extent is known and further treatment matched to it. Bleeding in 3 to 8%, from minor oozing to a life-threatening bleed into the airway that needs urgent intubation and theatre; the arteries are tied in the neck beforehand to reduce this. Pain for some weeks; no anti-inflammatory painkillers for 2 weeks. Swallowing difficulty; a feeding tube at first, sometimes at home. Tongue swelling : you may stay on the breathing tube for a day or two. Damaged teeth ; close or positive margins needing more surgery; radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy may still be needed. A leak into the neck if the neck is operated on at the same time."],["osec","th:op-36~s1-3","Before you start","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Operating note","Note age; ask about dysphagia, otalgia, odynophagia, aspiration, weight loss and breathlessness. Existing dysphagia or aspiration may mean prolonged tube feeding and suggests deep submucosal spread; otalgia suggests perineural invasion or epiglottic involvement. Record previous radiotherapy, cardiopulmonary fitness and bleeding disorders; previous biopsies, cervical spine or jaw joint surgery; family bleeding or anaesthetic problems; smoking and alcohol. Stop anticoagulants (warfarin, clopidogrel, aspirin) before and ideally for 2 weeks after surgery; ginkgo, fish oil and vitamin E also raise the risk; long-term steroids slow healing."],["osec","th:op-36~s1-4","Examination and imaging","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Operating note","Examine the tongue base directly and with a flexible scope (glossotonsillar sulcus, vallecula, epiglottis, extension to the midline) and palpate it to judge depth. Count neck nodes and note contralateral or fixed ones (extracapsular spread). Judge mouth opening, mandibular size and position, tongue size and teeth for exposure. MRI appears best for depth of invasion into the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles (CT also shows nodes and extracapsular spread); obvious deep parapharyngeal, extrinsic muscle or hyoid involvement may rule out transoral resection; sagittal images show anterior extent. PET-CT stages the disease."],["osec","th:op-36~s2-1","Landmarks","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Anatomy you must see","The glossotonsillar sulcus , the vallecula , the epiglottis , the hyoid , and the lingual artery with its dorsal lingual branch , met at the lateral edge of the resection about 1 to 2 cm deep ."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-1","Set up the robot","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","Turn and lower the table, the head at the edge. Dock the robot on the right at 30 degrees with its foot touching the table base and the camera pivot at the oral commissure. To avoid collisions, raise the camera arm's pivot inward and to maximum height, lower the scope fully into the mouth, then bring the arm down until the camera touches the posterior pharyngeal wall, and raise the scope for the best view. Set the instrument arms in a V and lower them until the cannula tips reach the retractor frame, then advance the instruments into view. Figure 1 The robotic arms set up for a transoral resection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 36.1 The first two figure captions In the book the captions of figures 36.1 and 36.2 appear exchanged: the text refers to the arm set-up as figure 36.1 and to the FK-WO retractor as figure 36.2, which matches the images. They are captioned here by what they show. What you should see All three arms moving freely in the mouth without touching the teeth, the retractor or each other."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-2","Examine and mark","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","Examine under anaesthesia and palpate the tumour's site, size and mobility. Paint endophytic tumours with methylene blue , as retraction can hide them. Start with the 0 degree scope; use 5 mm instruments, the spatula cautery on the tumour side and the Maryland dissector opposite. What you should see The tumour's limits felt and painted."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-3","Retract","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","Place a midline traction suture in the tongue and a guard on the upper teeth. With gentle forward traction, insert the FK-WO retractor, sliding the lateral tongue under the blade's straight edge with the slotted edge medial, and open it, keeping a cuff of normal tongue between the blade tip and the tumour for an anterior margin. Suspend it from a chest support and laterally from an endoscope holder. Move the tube away from the tumour and fix it with a suture between the lateral tongue and retromolar trigone. Figure 2 The FK-WO retractor. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 36.2 Figure 3 The endotracheal tube lateralised after the retraction suture is placed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 36.3 What you should see The tumour centred with a cuff of normal tongue in front of it under the blade."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-4","The lateral incision","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","The bedside surgeon sits at the head with two baby Yankauers to retract, clear smoke and help with haemostasis. If the sulcus is free, start in the tonsillar fossa and extend to the lateral tongue base for a lateral mucosal margin; if cancer involves the sulcus, take the constrictors and styloglossus with it. Figure 4 The initial incision through the glossotonsillar sulcus to set the lateral margin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 36.4 What you should see The lateral margin through normal mucosa, with constrictor muscle in the depth."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-5","The anterior incision","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","Cut horizontally just below the retractor tip , about 1 cm deep , so the tumour falls back into the field. Figure 5 The horizontal incision at the anterior margin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 36.5 What you should see The tumour dropping back into view as the horizontal cut deepens."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-6","The medial incision","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","Swap the arms so the cautery is on the side away from the tumour, and cut vertically in the midline from the medial end of the horizontal incision down to the vallecula to set the depth, with strong lateral and medial retraction from the assistant. There is usually little bleeding here, and the epiglottis comes fully into view. Figure 6 The midline vertical incision establishing the depth. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 36.6 What you should see The vallecula and epiglottis at the bottom of the midline cut."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-7","Resect medial to lateral, angled forward","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","Resect from medial to lateral along the horizontal incision, angling the instruments forward along an imaginary line from the retractor blade to the hyoid : the tendency is to cut back too soon and truncate the specimen. What you should see A thick specimen with tongue muscle on its deep surface all the way to the vallecula. Undercutting the cancer Cutting straight down at the anterior line rather than angling toward the hyoid undercuts the tumour and thins the anterior muscle margin."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-8","Clip the lingual artery","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","At the lateral tongue base, at a depth of 1 to 2 cm, find the lingual artery and its dorsal lingual branch : isolate them and place a string of clips along the exposed vessel. Clip every vessel over 2 mm in several places. If an artery is cut, the assistant presses on the neck at the greater horn of the hyoid . Figure 7 Exposing and clipping the lingual artery and its dorsal lingual branches. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 36.7 What you should see The lingual artery pulsating in the lateral depth, with clips on it before it is divided."],["osec","th:op-36~s3-9","Margins and finishing","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · The operation, stage by stage","Orient the specimen with the pathologist; check equivocal margins by frozen section; ink close or positive areas on the patient with methylene blue and re-resect. Perform several Valsalvas, secure haemostasis with cautery and clips, and apply Arista powder. Pass a nasogastric tube under vision and fix it away from the wound. If tongue swelling is expected, keep the patient intubated, exchanging the wire-reinforced tube for an evacuation (Hi-Lo Evac) tube, because a biting patient can crush a reinforced tube irreversibly. What you should see A dry bed after repeated Valsalva, with clipped vessels visible and margins confirmed."],["osec","th:op-36~s4-1","Crisis drills","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · When it goes wrong","Major bleeding after surgery 1 Danger aspiration and airway loss rather than exsanguination 2 Position head down and dependent 3 Press externally near the greater horn of the hyoid 4 Airway intubate and pack the oropharynx with gauze cricothyrotomy if intubation fails 5 Then theatre at once to explore and control Teeth loosened by the retractor 1 Act remove loose teeth to prevent aspiration dental referral for chipped or avulsed teeth"],["osec","th:op-36~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Poor exposure from the robot or retractor position. Undercutting at the anterior line instead of angling toward the hyoid. Not clipping the lingual artery and dorsal lingual branch in several places, and not ligating the lingual artery in the neck."],["osec","th:op-36~s5-1","After the operation","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Aftercare","Keep at-risk patients intubated for 24 to 48 hours ; the authors now give a single 10 mg dose of dexamethasone in theatre and more only for oedema (previously 6 mg every 6 to 8 hours then a tapering course). A prophylactic fine-bore feeding tube goes in at surgery; the speech therapist assesses at the bedside, then clear fluids advancing to a soft diet; remove the tube before discharge if intake is adequate, otherwise send the patient home with tube feeds and reassess in 1 to 2 weeks. Involve a dietitian if nutrition is a concern. Expect significant pain: a PCA, then oral opioids; no NSAIDs for 2 weeks . Chlorhexidine (Peridex) swish and spit at least four times a day."],["osec","th:op-36~s5-2","Complications","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Postoperative haemorrhage 3 to 8%; prevent by neck ligation and multiple clips; see the drill Intraoperative bleeding Lingual artery and dorsal lingual branch Close or positive margins Re-resect Pharyngocutaneous fistula With a concurrent neck dissection and extensive sulcus resection Airway oedema Prolonged retraction; stay intubated Dental injury Guards; remove loose teeth"],["ev","th:op-36~s5-3","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue: current evidence","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Aftercare","Surgery or radiotherapy. In the ORATOR trial of early oropharyngeal cancer, primary radiotherapy gave slightly better swallowing scores than transoral robotic surgery with neck dissection, though differences beyond one year were not clinically meaningful (Nichols et al., Lancet Oncol 2019; J Clin Oncol 2022). De-escalation. In E3311, transoral surgery followed by reduced-dose (50 Gy) radiotherapy in intermediate-risk HPV-positive cancer gave excellent survival with good function (Ferris et al., J Clin Oncol 2022). Choice between transoral surgery and chemoradiation is made in the multidisciplinary team, considering function, the likely need for adjuvant treatment, and patient preference."],["osec","th:op-36~s5-3","Alternatives","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · Aftercare","Primary chemoradiotherapy; open approaches (lip-split mandibulotomy, transhyoid pharyngotomy)."],["osec","th:op-36~s6-1","Robotic or open?","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · For the examination","Ford and colleagues (2014) matched 130 cases by stage and adjuvant treatment, with similar HPV status: 3-year recurrence-free survival was 89% after robotic surgery against 73% after open surgery. The editor notes that since 2004 the robot has changed tongue base surgery from rare open procedures to the commonest approach for T1 and T2 cancer, with growing evidence for quality of life and cost, and that dual consoles have made it teachable in defined steps."],["osec","th:op-36~s6-2","Viva essentials","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why a surgical specimen matters: staging and adjuvant decisions. Selection and contraindications, especially more than half the tongue base. Staged neck dissection and arterial ligation. Fire safety and the airway. Robot docking and retractor placement. The incisions: lateral, anterior, medial, then medial to lateral angled to the hyoid. The lingual artery: depth, clips, neck pressure point. Managing a postoperative bleed."],["osec","th:op-36~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Transoral Robotic Surgery for Neoplasms of the Base of Tongue · For the examination","Common pitfalls Forgetting methylene blue for an endophytic tumour. Leaving a biting patient intubated with a reinforced tube. NSAIDs in the first fortnight."],["osec","th:op-4~s1-1","The case at a glance","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Operating note","Vocal fold injection via microlaryngoscopy Summary Indications Symptomatic glottic insufficiency (immobility or paralysis, paresis, atrophy) causing voice, swallowing or breathing problems, or a combination; a trial injection with a temporary material when the benefit of augmentation is uncertain (scar, atrophy, paresis, or neurological disease such as dysarthria) Caution A unilateral immobile fold with reduced movement of the other fold : the airway is already narrow and augmentation may close it. Rarely worth it; if done, inject conservatively, as far anteriorly as possible, under tightly controlled conditions, after counselling everyone about urgent intubation or tracheostomy Relative contraindications Comorbidity that raises the risk of general anaesthesia (injection under local anaesthesia may suit); antiplatelet drugs are not a contraindication but increase the risk of haemorrhage and airway compromise Anaesthesia General endotracheal, with the smallest tube possible (5.0 microlaryngoscopy tube) taped along the upper lip; short-acting muscle relaxant; topical laryngotracheal lidocaine at intubation and again before extubation Premedication Single doses at the start: dexamethasone (laryngeal oedema), ondansetron (nausea), glycopyrrolate (secretions) Position Supine, no shoulder roll ; an adjustable headboard (or the table reversed) lets you flex the neck Antibiotics Not indicated Tray Laryngoscopes of several sizes and types (small calibre, long, one with an anterior lip, a slotted anterior commissure scope); 5, 6 or 7 Fr suctions; headlight box; a reinforced dental gu"],["osec","th:op-4~s1-2","Consent","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to bulk out the weak vocal cord so that the cords meet, improving the voice, swallowing and cough. Expectations: the aim is a normal voice, but symptoms may improve, or resolve, without a complete return to normal. After a trial injection the voice may improve, worsen or stay the same. Further injections may be needed: certainly with temporary materials, and sometimes even after fat. The airway: swelling, bleeding, too much material or spasm of the larynx can obstruct breathing; rarely, a tube back in or a tracheostomy. Bleeding into the cord, usually settling with rest. No improvement, or a worse voice, if the material sits in the wrong place or the amount is wrong. Teeth, gums, lips, jaw joint, and a numb tongue or changed taste from the laryngoscope. With fat injection: pain and bruising at the abdominal harvest site."],["osec","th:op-4~s1-3","Before you start","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Operating note","Ask about the voice (breathy, raspy, quiet, effortful, tiring, tight or painful, and what makes it better or worse), swallowing (liquids, solids or pills, coughing or choking, ever needing the Heimlich manoeuvre, weight loss, diet changes, and which compensatory strategies help), and breathing (breathless on speaking, exertion or rest). Always ask about recent pneumonia. Find the precipitant: thyroid, cervical spine, lung or oesophageal surgery, intubation (tube size and duration), a viral infection, trauma, or none. Note cough, globus, throat clearing, mucus and reflux symptoms; prior treatments and how well they worked; antiplatelet drugs; smoking, which brings coughing at extubation and a risk of the implant being coughed out early; and the patient's vocal demands at work. Examine the neck for scars and range of movement, which affect intubation and exposure; the mouth for trismus, crowns, caps, bridges, partial dentures or loose teeth, and a torus mandibularis, which may force a smaller laryngoscope. Flexible laryngoscopy is mandatory and is better than rigid endoscopy for judging movement; repeated alternating sniff and /i/ shows abduction and adduction. Add stroboscopy , which is better for closure, the size of the gap and a mismatch in height between the folds. Before you book theatre, know: Which side to inject. The size of the gap, which guides how much to inject. Any height mismatch, which guides where you put the material so the folds match. Image only when the cause of immobility is unknown: CT along the course of the recurrent laryngeal nerve, not needed when t"],["osec","th:op-4~s1-4","Choosing the setting and the material","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Operating note","The material, the anaesthetic and the setting (office, theatre or procedure suite) are decided case by case: the patient's fitness, your preference and theirs, how long the weakness is expected to last (early after surgery with good hopes of recovery, or permanent from a malignancy), the need for monitoring, whether this is treatment or a trial, and cost. Under general anaesthesia you gain an immobile larynx, precise placement, full control of the airway and the chance to palpate."],["osec","th:op-4~s2-1","The injection landmarks","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Anatomy you must see","The superior arcuate line: the curved line on the upper surface of the fold where it meets the floor of the ventricle (described in full in the office procedures chapter). The vocal process of the arytenoid at the back of the membranous fold. Point A, the primary site: the intersection of the superior arcuate line with the vocal process. The material goes deep, so that the whole fold is pushed medially, filling from below. Point B, a secondary site if more is needed: the midpoint of the fold along the superior arcuate line. Figure 1 The ideal location for deep vocal fold injection, in coronal (A) and axial (B) view. In B, point A is the intersection of the superior arcuate line with the vocal process, the preferred first site; point B, at the middle of the fold on the same line, is used only if more augmentation is needed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 4.1"],["osec","th:op-4~s2-2","Deep and superficial injection","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Anatomy you must see","Deep (global) augmentation is the usual goal: material deep in the fold, medialising the whole fold. Superficial injection into the superficial lamina propria is done only on purpose, for example steroid for scar. When global augmentation is the goal, superficial injection must be avoided: it damps vibration and worsens the voice."],["osec","th:op-4~s3-1","Intubate and premedicate","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Ask for the smallest tube, a 5.0 microlaryngoscopy tube , taped along the upper lip at the commissure: taped to the lower lip it can limit mouth opening and your exposure. Move the circuit holder down the body, away from the head. Give dexamethasone, ondansetron and glycopyrrolate at the start, and spray the larynx and trachea with plain 4% or 2% lidocaine at intubation. What you should see A small tube taped to the upper lip at the corner of the mouth, out of your line of sight. IV steroid Give the intravenous steroid after the patient is asleep, or very slowly. A rapid push in an awake patient can cause an alarming perineal sensation."],["osec","th:op-4~s3-2","Protect and position","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Protect the eyes and drape the head. Reinforce a plastic dental guard with several layers of tape and put it over the upper teeth; for the edentulous, use a foam pad or moist gauze on the alveolar ridge. A customised guard, or one moulded in theatre from splint material, protects poor or misaligned teeth. No shoulder roll: flex the neck if you need to with the headboard. What you should see Covered eyes and upper teeth, and a neck you can flex from the headboard."],["osec","th:op-4~s3-3","Pass the laryngoscope","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Pass the scope by mouth under direct vision, in the midline to reduce pressure on the tongue, and slide it under the epiglottis while keeping it in its natural position (the Lindholm is the exception: it sits in the vallecula). Pull up and away ; never rock back on the teeth. Start with the scope with the largest distal aperture , such as the largest universal modular glottiscope or a Dedo, so that both folds are seen at once, and move to a smaller one only if you must. If you will inject under a telescope, a slotted anterior commissure scope leaves room for both the telescope and the needle. What you should see Both folds from the anterior commissure to the vocal processes, with the scope lifting, not levering. The guard wears through Check the guard often and change it when it shows wear; a practical rule is a fresh reinforced guard after two changes or manipulations of the laryngoscope."],["osec","th:op-4~s3-4","Suspend","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Use a suspension system fixed to the operating table , which is the most stable. A Lewy arm on a Mayo stand is acceptable if the table is lowered (and tilted head down if helpful) before suspension, all cables and suction tubing are placed first, and everyone knows the stand must not be touched. Avoid supports that rest on the chest. What you should see Both folds from the anterior commissure to the vocal processes at once. Use anterior cricoid pressure with Velcro or tape (manual pressure only if nothing else is available)."],["osec","th:op-4~s3-5","Inspect and palpate","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Examine with 0, 30 and 70 degree telescopes: the anterior commissure, the ventricles, the undersurface and the midmembranous folds. Then palpate : push the vocal process laterally with a curved blunt elevator or a suction tip off suction to test the cricoarytenoid joint, and feel the folds for lesions or the stiffness of scar. What you should see The vocal process moving laterally when pushed (the joint is mobile), and the depth of the glottic gap. Testing the joint When the question is paralysis versus joint fixation, palpate at the start of the anaesthetic, before intubation and before suspension, with the laryngoscope held high in the larynx by hand. The tube and the suspension both confuse the assessment."],["osec","th:op-4~s3-6","Prepare the material","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Follow the package directions for prefabricated gels (Prolaryn, Renu) and collagen products (Cymetra). For fat , harvest abdominal fat by liposuction or open excision; the author rinses it with 2 L of sterile Ringer's lactate and soaks it for 5 minutes in an insulin bath before injection. Needles: a 27 G orotracheal injector, the 24 G needle supplied with the prefabricated materials, or a 14 to 16 G lipoinjection device for fat. What you should see The material loaded with no air in the needle, and fat that is clean and yellow."],["osec","th:op-4~s3-7","Inject","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Use the microscope where possible for the most precise placement (a 0 or 30 degree telescope if you prefer telescopic guidance). Enter at point A , deep, with the bevel facing medially or anteriorly . Inject slowly and steadily, and watch: the infraglottic part of the fold fills first, then the fullness moves up to the medial edge of the fold. Inject at point B only if needed; a second puncture is another route for material to leak out of the first. Figure 2 Before (A) and after (B) bilateral deep injection in a patient with right vocal fold paralysis: global augmentation. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 4.2 What you should see A fold filling first below its edge, then medialising as a whole, with a straight, full edge meeting its partner. The three wrong places Too superficial: the superior surface bubbles up (especially in atrophy) and the fold stiffens. Too lateral: material lost in the paraglottic space adds nothing to closure. Check which way the bevel faces. Too anterior: overaugmentation at the front gives a strained voice and an unhappy patient."],["osec","th:op-4~s3-8","Finish and wake gently","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Suck away any material that has extruded from the puncture. Bleeding usually stops on its own; if not, hold a pledget of 1:10,000 adrenaline on it. Spray the larynx again with lidocaine to lessen laryngospasm and coughing at extubation, and empty the stomach with an orogastric tube to reduce vomiting. Remove the scope and check the teeth and tongue. Then talk to the anaesthetist about a quiet emergence and extubation : coughing and bucking can push the material out. This conversation cannot be overstated. What you should see A straight or slightly convex fold with no material at the puncture, and a dry larynx."],["osec","th:op-4~s4-1","Crisis drills","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · When it goes wrong","Airway compromise after injection 1 Find the cause laryngospasm, oedema, overaugmentation, bleeding or anxiety 2 Laryngospasm intravenous lidocaine push positive pressure ventilation low-dose suxamethonium reintubate with the smallest tube if needed 3 After reintubation observe in recovery, often about an hour, then extubate overnight intubation is less common 4 Oedema prevent with intraoperative intravenous steroid 5 Overaugmentation reintubate if it obstructs early remove excess material surgically if necessary 6 Anxiety an anxiolytic from the anaesthetist Poor view of the folds 1 Too much false fold showing, or the epiglottis folded down reposition the scope, keep the epiglottis in place, flex the head 2 Still poor change to a different or smaller laryngoscope anterior cricoid pressure"],["osec","th:op-4~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls An inadequate view: too much false fold, or the epiglottis flopped down. Superficial injection when deep augmentation was wanted. Bubbling up of the superior surface, especially in atrophy. Injecting too far laterally into the paraglottic space. Injecting too far anteriorly."],["osec","th:op-4~s5-1","After the injection","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Aftercare","Voice rest after deep injection: 3 days after fat, 24 hours after other materials . Patients go home the same day after routine recovery observation; an overnight stay is rarely needed. Paracetamol is usually enough (intravenous paracetamol and intramuscular ketorolac help in theatre); warn fat-injection patients about abdominal discomfort and bruising. Eat and drink normally once recovered from the anaesthetic. Antiplatelet and anticoagulant drugs can usually restart the next day."],["osec","th:op-4~s5-2","Complications","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Aftercare","Complications of vocal fold injection Complication What to do Too little material, or early extrusion Reassess the symptoms, especially swallowing (modified barium swallow); repeat the injection; or voice and swallowing therapy if no more injection is wanted Superficial injection Damped vibration and a worse voice; temporary materials last longer when superficial. Calcium hydroxylapatite leaves a visible white \"chiclet\" under the epithelium that may need microflap removal or suction; clear gels (Prolaryn, Restylane) are hard to see and usually left Too much material Observe: fat partly resorbs early and all temporary materials resorb; if symptomatic, surgical removal of the excess (difficulty depends on the material) Persistent symptoms Look for the reason; voice, swallowing or respiratory retraining therapy; reset expectations Vocal fold haemorrhage Usually self-limiting; extra voice rest Airway compromise See the drill Dental injury Refer to the dentist; prevention is the key Jaw joint pain Soft diet, anti-inflammatories, sometimes a guard; expectant Tongue numbness or altered taste Time and reassurance"],["ev","th:op-4~s5-3","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy: current evidence","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Aftercare","Early injection. In a retrospective study, injection laryngoplasty within six months of onset of unilateral paralysis was associated with fewer later open framework procedures (Friedman et al., Laryngoscope 2010), so temporary injection is offered early, especially when aspiration is a problem. Materials. Carboxymethylcellulose lasts about two to three months, hyaluronic acid four to nine, calcium hydroxylapatite about 18 months, and fat is variable; Teflon is abandoned because of granulomas. Awake injection in clinic through the cricothyroid membrane or the mouth gives results similar to injection under general anaesthesia."],["osec","th:op-4~s5-3","Alternatives","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · Aftercare","Injection under local anaesthesia in the office. Voice therapy and swallowing therapy . Compensatory swallowing strategies: head turn, chin tuck."],["osec","th:op-4~s6-1","The evidence on trial injection","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · For the examination","Trial injection, first described in 2010, asks how much augmentation will help when the result is uncertain. Carroll and Rosen injected a temporary material in 25 patients with atrophy or paresis: 76% improved and 24% did not, and the 10 who went on to permanent augmentation all did well. Young and colleagues (2015) compared trial and long-term augmentation in 19 patients with atrophy: 42% responded well to the trial, and 75% of those also did well long term; of the 58% who responded poorly, 55% also did poorly long term, but 45% still did well after long-term augmentation . So a good trial predicts a good result, but a poor trial does not reliably predict failure."],["osec","th:op-4~s6-2","Viva essentials","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The landmarks : superior arcuate line and vocal process (point A); the midpoint as point B. Deep versus superficial injection, and why superficial injection harms the voice. What you need to know before booking: side, gap, height mismatch. The danger of injecting when the other fold is weak. Why the bevel faces medially or anteriorly. Quiet extubation , and why. The trial injection: what a good and a poor response mean. Managing airway compromise and a superficial calcium hydroxylapatite deposit. The longest-lasting material is autologous fat ."],["osec","th:op-4~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Vocal Fold Injection via Microlaryngoscopy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Injecting without flexible laryngoscopy, or without knowing the side and size of the gap. A shoulder roll, a scope rocked on the teeth, or a worn-out guard. Missing joint fixation by never palpating, or palpating only in suspension. Overfilling the front of the fold. A coughing, bucking extubation."],["osec","th:op-40~s1-1","The case at a glance","Pharyngectomy · Operating note","Pharyngectomy Summary Partial Cancer limited to the aryepiglottic fold and the medial or anterior wall of the pyriform sinus, with good lung function; not if it reaches the apex or the lateral wall Larynx and partial pharynx Pyriform cancer extending to the larynx medially, the apex below, or the lateral wall Total Postcricoid cancer, advanced posterior wall cancer, pyriform cancer crossing the midline posteriorly; with oesophagectomy if it extends into the cervical oesophagus Not operable Extension to the prevertebral fascia (MRI, barium swallow); encasement of the common carotid ; distant metastasis (discuss palliative care) Set-up General; awake fibreoptic intubation or awake tracheostomy if the tumour obstructs; supine, shoulder roll, arms tucked Antibiotics Ampicillin-sulbactam; clindamycin or ciprofloxacin with metronidazole in allergy; for 24 hours Tray Direct laryngoscopy set with rigid oesophagoscope; major head and neck set; microscope if a free flap is needed; tracheoesophageal puncture kit with prosthesis"],["osec","th:op-40~s1-2","Consent","Pharyngectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove a cancer of the lower throat, usually with the voice box, and the lymph nodes on both sides of the neck. Breathing through a permanent stoma if the larynx is removed; voice restored by a valve, now or later. Reconstruction with tissue from the chest, arm, thigh or bowel if not enough throat lining is left. A leak of saliva into the neck, treated by tube feeding and drainage, sometimes another operation. Narrowing of the throat later, which may need stretching. Rupture of the carotid artery : rare, life-threatening, commoner after radiotherapy or with a leak. An underactive thyroid , needing tablets. Radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy afterwards. The operation may be stopped if the cancer is found to surround the carotid."],["osec","th:op-40~s1-3","Before you start","Pharyngectomy · Operating note","Ask about dysphagia, odynophagia, referred otalgia and a neck lump, and how long and how severe; choking or coughing with meals means aspiration; hoarseness or stridor means laryngeal involvement. Record lung and heart function, previous head and neck cancer or radiotherapy, immunosuppression, family history of cancer or blood disorders, anticoagulants, antiplatelets and herbal products, and smoking and alcohol (current drinkers risk withdrawal). Record oxygen saturation and BMI. Examine the mouth and oropharynx for a synchronous primary and the teeth (poor teeth are best extracted at the time of surgery, before radiotherapy). Flexible laryngoscopy confirms the tumour and its extent: does it involve the endolarynx, postcricoid region or posterior wall, and does it obstruct the airway ? Where the nodes go The hypopharynx drains to the upper deep cervical nodes (levels II and III), sometimes the retropharyngeal nodes, and often both sides. Nodal metastasis develops in 60 to 80% , and occult nodes are present in at least 20 to 40% . The risk is bilateral for postcricoid, medial pyriform and posterior wall cancers; pyriform cancers have about a 20% risk to the other side, less from the lateral wall than the medial wall."],["osec","th:op-40~s1-4","Imaging and preparation","Pharyngectomy · Operating note","Contrast CT of the neck is quick and cheap and shows the nodes; CT of the chest looks for lung metastases. MRI shows soft tissue planes better and whether the prevertebral fascia is involved. A barium swallow with videofluoroscopy can show a pyriform sinus fixed to the prevertebral fascia or bone. PET-CT looks for distant disease. Obtain staging scans within a month of surgery , so you do not miss spread into critical structures. Get medical clearance (heart and lungs), check prealbumin and TSH (correctable causes of poor healing), consider a gastrostomy for a malnourished patient who cannot swallow, stop antiplatelets and anticoagulants, and have outside biopsies and scans reviewed."],["osec","th:op-40~s2-1","Landmarks","Pharyngectomy · Anatomy you must see","The hyoid bone: skeletonise it; the pharynx is entered above the hyoid on the side opposite the cancer . The hypoglossal nerve: deep to the greater cornu of the hyoid , just lateral and deep to it; its survival matters for swallowing. Find it during the neck dissection and follow it medially until its relation to the hyoid is clear. The pyriform sinuses: in a partial pharyngectomy, peel the uninvolved sinus off the inner lamella of the thyroid cartilage and keep it whole. Figure 1 Advanced laryngeal cancer needing resection of the medial wall of the same pyriform sinus for a deep margin; keeping the opposite sinus and the lateral wall allows primary closure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 40.1 Figure 2 Pyriform cancer involving the whole lateral wall; the opposite sinus was carefully preserved. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 40.2"],["osec","th:op-40~s3-1","Endoscopy again","Pharyngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Perform direct laryngoscopy and oesophagoscopy for diagnosis beforehand, and again at the start of the operation to plan the cuts. Entry into the pharynx is blind, so you must know exactly where the cancer is. What you should see The tumour's upper and lower limits, the apex of the pyriform sinus, the postcricoid area and the cervical oesophagus."],["osec","th:op-40~s3-2","Partial laryngopharyngectomy","Pharyngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Raise an apron flap and dissect both necks for medial wall cancer; an elective unilateral dissection may do for cancer confined to the lateral wall. Perform a supraglottic laryngectomy , removing the hypopharyngeal cancer with it, with at least a 15 mm mucosal margin all round. Reconstruct by impacting the tongue base into the residual larynx. Pass a nasogastric tube if there is no gastrostomy. Close the residual mucosa indirectly, with a second layer of residual strap and digastric muscle, and drain with suction. What you should see A 15 mm margin all round, with both arytenoids and true folds mobile and intact below the cut."],["osec","th:op-40~s3-3","Laryngectomy with partial pharyngectomy","Pharyngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Raise a superiorly based apron flap through the level of the stoma; dissect levels II to IV on both sides in most cases. Enter the pharynx through the vallecula on the side opposite the cancer . Keep all uninvolved pyriform mucosa: incise the constrictors along the thyroid lamella, release the sinus and peel it gently off the endolarynx. Cut from the vallecula down the aryepiglottic fold to the lateral side of the opposite arytenoid, then make the remaining cuts under direct vision with a 15 to 20 mm margin. Frozen section margins are mandatory. Figure 3 An extensive hypopharyngeal cancer crossing the midline posteriorly, needing laryngectomy and near-total pharyngectomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 40.3 What you should see The tumour seen from the opposite side before any cut is made near it, and a strip of healthy mucosa left."],["osec","th:op-40~s3-4","How much is left?","Pharyngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","With 3 cm of pharyngeal mucosa across, the pharynx can usually be closed primarily, and a primary puncture can be made; with less, add a pedicled or free flap, and make the puncture later, after healing and adjuvant treatment. Mature the stoma and close over bilateral suction drains. Figure 4 A narrow strip of residual hypopharynx that cannot be closed primarily: it must be augmented with a pedicled or free flap, or resected and replaced. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 40.4 What you should see The width of remaining mucosa measured unstretched."],["osec","th:op-40~s3-5","Total laryngopharyngectomy","Pharyngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Apron flap and bilateral neck dissections, always. Enter through the opposite vallecula and release any uninvolved pyriform mucosa. Carry the dissection down beside the epiglottis until the cancer is seen; take a 15 to 20 mm superior margin. Develop the plane between the posterior pharyngeal wall and the prevertebral fascia downward. Under direct vision, secure the inferior margin and divide the larynx and pharynx below. If the cancer enters the cervical oesophagus, a total laryngo-oesophagectomy is needed. Figure 5 A circumferential postcricoid cancer needing total laryngopharyngectomy and reconstruction. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 40.5 What you should see The prevertebral fascia clean behind the specimen, and clear margins above and at the cervical oesophagus."],["osec","th:op-40~s3-6","Reconstruct","Pharyngectomy · The operation, stage by stage","A circumferential defect needs free tissue: a tubed radial forearm flap , or for large defects the jejunum, now largely replaced by other flaps. A posterior wall defect suits the thin, pliable forearm flap as a patch. Pass a nasogastric tube, mature the stoma and close over suction drains. Figure 6 A posterior pharyngeal wall cancer often needs free flap reconstruction; the radial forearm gives thin, pliable tissue. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 40.6 Figure 7 The radial forearm flap for partial pharyngeal reconstruction; it can be tubed for total reconstruction. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 40.7 Figure 8 The jejunum, whose arcades each supply about 25 cm of bowel, as a free flap donor. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 40.8 What you should see A tube or patch inset without tension, with a watertight suture line above and below. Suspected carotid involvement Sometimes encasement only becomes clear in theatre. If you suspect it but cannot confirm it beforehand, start with the neck dissection on that side , so you can abandon the operation before the pharynx is opened."],["osec","th:op-40~s4-1","Crisis drills","Pharyngectomy · When it goes wrong","Pharyngeal leak 1 Small nil by mouth, divert it with a drain, local wound care 2 Persistent or large return to theatre to repair it, often with local or free tissue, especially after radiotherapy 3 Risk factors previous radiotherapy, hypothyroidism Carotid blowout 1 Who after radiotherapy, radical neck dissection, a leak, or a wound infection 2 Act immediate return to theatre 3 Prevent cover the carotid with a pectoralis or free flap in high-risk necks Bleeding 1 During surgery identify and repair the injured vessel at once 2 Later a slipped clip, infection breaking down tissue, or persistent cancer"],["osec","th:op-40~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Pharyngectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Poor assessment of the cancer at laryngoscopy, so the blind entry cuts through it. Inadequate skeletonisation of the hyoid: releasing it helps rotate the larynx and free the constrictors and sinuses, and exposing it protects the hypoglossal nerve. Failing to identify inoperability (carotid, prevertebral fascia) before surgery."],["osec","th:op-40~s5-1","After the operation","Pharyngectomy · Aftercare","Antibiotics for 24 hours. Leave the stoma open to humidified air and clean it often; a soft Silastic laryngectomy tube if oedema or a flap obstructs it. Opioids through the tube or intravenously at first. Antiplatelet drugs can restart on day 1 after primary or local flap closure; after a free flap, as the reconstructive plan dictates. Feed through the nasogastric tube or gastrostomy once bowel sounds return. A barium swallow at 7 to 10 days : if intact, start clear fluids, remove the nasogastric tube and advance the diet."],["osec","th:op-40~s5-2","Complications","Pharyngectomy · Aftercare","Complications of pharyngectomy Complication Notes Hypothyroidism Delays healing and raises the leak risk; thyroid tests at 5 to 7 days and levothyroxine Pharyngeal leak Conservative for small leaks, surgery and flaps for persistent ones Pharyngeal stenosis Late; barium swallow; dilatation Carotid blowout The most devastating; radiotherapy, leak and infection; immediate surgery Hypoglossal injury At the greater horn of the hyoid; impairs swallowing"],["ev","th:op-40~s5-3","Pharyngectomy: current evidence","Pharyngectomy · Aftercare","Larynx preservation. In the EORTC 24891 trial in hypopharyngeal cancer, induction chemotherapy followed by radiotherapy in responders gave survival equivalent to laryngectomy, preserving the larynx in about a third of survivors at ten years (Lefebvre et al., J Natl Cancer Inst 1996; Ann Oncol 2012); TPF induction improved larynx preservation over PF (Pointreau et al., J Natl Cancer Inst 2009). Surgery (total laryngopharyngectomy with reconstruction) remains standard for T4a disease or a non-functional larynx, and for salvage. Reconstruction. Circumferential defects are repaired with a tubed fasciocutaneous free flap (anterolateral thigh or radial forearm) or a free jejunum, or gastric pull-up when the oesophagus is resected."],["osec","th:op-40~s5-3","Alternatives","Pharyngectomy · Aftercare","Radiotherapy for early hypopharyngeal cancer, depending on access, function and expertise. Chemoradiotherapy for advanced cancer, preserving the larynx but without a survival advantage over surgery. Palliative chemoradiotherapy for inoperable or metastatic disease. When to choose surgery If the larynx and pharynx already function poorly, or the patient is older with poor performance status or significant thyroid cartilage invasion, laryngopharyngectomy with adjuvant radiotherapy is often preferred to organ preservation."],["osec","th:op-40~s6-1","Chemoradiotherapy or surgery?","Pharyngectomy · For the examination","Organ-preserving chemoradiotherapy has largely replaced laryngopharyngectomy with adjuvant radiotherapy for patients who can tolerate it, and ASCO guidelines recommend offering organ preservation to most patients where feasible. It preserves the larynx but does not improve survival over surgery with radiotherapy. Discuss every advanced case at a multidisciplinary meeting, with the patient's and family's goals, the toxicities and the possibility of salvage surgery."],["osec","th:op-40~s6-2","Viva essentials","Pharyngectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three subsites and the postcricoid region as the front wall. Nodal spread: 60 to 80%, occult 20 to 40%, when it is bilateral. Contraindications: prevertebral fascia, carotid encasement, distant disease. Which operation for which tumour. Entry through the opposite vallecula; preserve the opposite pyriform sinus. Margins of 15 to 20 mm and mandatory frozen sections. The 3 cm rule for primary closure; primary or secondary puncture. Reconstruction choices. Leak, hypothyroidism and carotid blowout."],["osec","th:op-40~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Pharyngectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Old staging scans. Opening the pharynx before checking the carotid when its involvement is in doubt. Closing a pharynx too narrow to swallow. Missing postoperative hypothyroidism."],["osec","th:op-45~s1-1","The case at a glance","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Operating note","Rigid oesophagoscopy and hypopharyngoscopy Summary Indications: diagnostic Dysphagia, odynophagia or a mass; staging and synchronous primaries in head and neck cancer (panendoscopy); the unknown primary; suspected trauma, stricture or inflammation; biopsy Indications: therapeutic Removal of a foreign body; dilatation of strictures; stent insertion; endoscopic pouch surgery (cricopharyngeal myotomy); laser procedures; excision of benign lesions Contraindications Haemodynamic instability; poor general health; known perforation; inability to obtain informed consent; for rigid endoscopy, an immobile or unstable cervical spine, large osteophytes, trismus, a receding mandible or aortic aneurysm (use flexible endoscopy) Anaesthesia General with an oral endotracheal tube (often a small tube placed to the left corner of the mouth); full relaxation Position Supine; head raised and neck flexed on the chest with the head extended at the atlanto-occipital joint, so mouth, pharynx and oesophagus fall in one line; no shoulder roll Antibiotics Not routine (except gastrostomy) Tray Rigid oesophagoscopes in several lengths and diameters (adult and paediatric), light carriers, 0-degree long telescope and camera, long suction cannulas, cup biopsy forceps, serrated and crocodile (alligator) grasping forceps, optical forceps, bougies and a guidewire or lumen finder, upper tooth guard or wet gauze, lubricant; laryngoscopes (Weerda, Dedo) for the hypopharynx Time 10 to 20 minutes"],["osec","th:op-45~s1-2","Consent","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Sore throat for a day or two. Damage to teeth, crowns or lips ; a broken tooth needs a dentist. Perforation of the oesophagus in about 1%, higher (up to about 6%) when a stricture is dilated or a foreign body removed, and higher with rigid than flexible scopes. It may need a nasogastric tube, antibiotics and fasting, or an operation, and can rarely be life-threatening. Bleeding from biopsy sites. Heart rhythm changes from vagal stimulation, usually brief."],["osec","th:op-45~s1-3","Before you start","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Operating note","Take the dysphagia history: solids, liquids or both, the level the patient points to, onset, progression and duration, regurgitation, weight loss, pain, hoarseness, cough, haemoptysis, heartburn, globus, postnasal drip, night sweats and a neck lump. Note cancer history and radiotherapy to the neck, smoking and alcohol, cardiac and respiratory disease, rheumatological disease (scleroderma), and anything that limits neck movement: arthritis, osteophytes, spinal fusion. Ask about previous chest, abdominal or mediastinal surgery and drugs that dry the mouth. Examine the head and neck: masses, nodes, tenderness, and how far the neck flexes and extends (fibrosis after radiotherapy limits both). Perform flexible nasendoscopy in every patient: pooling of secretions, cord movement, the airway, masses and the dynamic movement of the larynx and pharynx. A contrast swallow shows a pouch, web, stricture, dysmotility, extrinsic compression or a radiolucent foreign body before you instrument. Use barium unless perforation is suspected; then use a water-soluble agent (gastrografin), which does not cause mediastinitis if it leaks (but is harmful if aspirated). A speech and language therapy assessment or modified barium swallow shows the swallow dynamics. CT characterises a mass. Medical clearance for the anaesthetic is usually all the preparation needed. Figure 1 Left: transnasal endoscopy showing a cervical osteophyte (O) pushing the epiglottis (E) forwards. Right: barium swallows showing a pharyngeal pouch, a large anterior osteophyte (black arrow) and an oesophageal web (white arrow). My"],["osec","th:op-45~s1-4","Rigid, flexible or transnasal?","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Operating note","The patient's general condition, the purpose and the patient's cooperation decide. Rigid endoscopy is better for the hypopharynx, cricopharyngeus and cervical oesophagus, where mucosal folds hide lesions from a flexible scope, and takes large suction cannulas and grasping forceps for foreign bodies; it needs a general anaesthetic and a mobile neck. Flexible oesophagoscopy (transoral under sedation or transnasal under topical anaesthesia) reaches the gastro-oesophageal junction and stomach, works in a stiff neck or past osteophytes, is safer, and is preferred for surveillance and for placing a gastrostomy; transnasal endoscopy is done in the clinic and the patient goes straight home."],["osec","th:op-45~s2-1","The oesophagus","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Anatomy you must see","The oesophagus runs 22 to 25 cm from the pharyngo-oesophageal junction at C6 (the lower border of the cricoid) to the cardia at about T11. It curves gently to the left in the neck, returns to the midline around T5, and swings left again in front of the aorta before passing through the diaphragmatic hiatus. It has cervical (cricopharyngeus to the sternal notch), thoracic and abdominal parts, and its wall has mucosa, submucosa, muscularis and adventitia but no serosa , which is why leaks spread freely into the mediastinum. Distances from the upper incisors in an adult Landmark Distance What it means Cricopharyngeus (upper sphincter) About 15 to 16 cm The narrowest point; most perforations and impacted foreign bodies Aortic arch and left main bronchus About 23 to 27 cm Pulsation visible; second constriction Lower sphincter and cardia About 38 to 40 cm Third constriction; red, velvety gastric mucosa beyond"],["osec","th:op-45~s2-2","The hypopharynx","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Anatomy you must see","The piriform sinuses lie either side of the larynx, the postcricoid region behind it, and the posterior pharyngeal wall behind that. Killian's dehiscence , the weak area between the thyropharyngeus and the cricopharyngeus fibres of the inferior constrictor on the posterior wall, is where a pouch arises and where a scope pushed against a closed sphincter perforates. The cervical spine lies directly behind; anterior osteophytes indent the posterior wall."],["osec","th:op-45~s3-1","Position and protect","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","After intubation, position the head: raised on a pillow or headrest with the neck flexed and the head extended at the atlanto-occipital joint (the \"sniffing\" position), so that the axes of mouth, pharynx and oesophagus align; an assistant may hold the head to adjust it during the passage. Protect the upper teeth with a guard or folded wet gauze. Choose the scope (a long adult scope for the oesophagus, a shorter wider one or a laryngoscope for the hypopharynx) and lubricate it. What you should see A guard over the upper teeth and a head the assistant can move. The teeth The upper incisors are the most common thing injured. The scope must never lever on them."],["osec","th:op-45~s3-2","Enter the mouth and find the arytenoids","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Open the mouth with a scissor movement of thumb and index finger. Hold the scope like a pen in your right hand and introduce it from the right side of the tongue , moving to the midline over its dorsum. Rest the scope on the thumb of the left hand , which acts as the fulcrum and protects the teeth, and use the left thumb and index finger to advance it; the right hand only changes the angle. Pass down the posterior pharyngeal wall: you see the epiglottis, then the endotracheal tube, then the arytenoids . Figure 2 The left thumb as the fulcrum and the advancing hand, keeping pressure off the incisors; the right hand only steers. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 45.4 What you should see The epiglottis, the tube passing through the glottis and the two arytenoids behind it. Senior's pearl Your left hand never leaves the scope. If you need both hands for an instrument, an assistant holds it, or you withdraw."],["osec","th:op-45~s3-3","Pass the cricopharyngeus","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Keeping the tip strictly in the midline behind the larynx , lift the larynx forward with the tip (a gentle lever with the left thumb) to open the postcricoid space. The closed sphincter appears as a horizontal slit or rosette. Apply slow, sustained, gentle pressure and wait: the sphincter relaxes and the lumen opens ahead; follow it in. Never force it. If it will not open, ask for more muscle relaxant, ask the anaesthetist to deflate the tube cuff, use a smaller scope, or find the lumen with a fine bougie or guidewire. Figure 3 The scope advanced over the posterior pharyngeal wall; the tongue base and larynx are lifted forwards until the scope lies in the axis of the lumen. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 45.5 What you should see The lumen of the oesophagus opening ahead of the tip, with mucosa on all sides. The commonest site of perforation Pushing against a closed cricopharyngeus drives the tip through Killian's dehiscence or into a pouch. If you cannot see the lumen, you are not in it."],["osec","th:op-45~s3-4","Advance to the cardia","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Once in the oesophagus, advance only with the lumen constantly in view, suctioning secretions with a long cannula. At about 25 cm , pulsation of the aortic arch marks the second constriction; lower the head slightly to keep the lumen in line. Toward the cardia, the head and shoulders are lowered and the head moved slightly to the right, so the scope points toward the left anterior superior iliac spine. The cardia is recognised by redder, velvety, rugose gastric mucosa. What you should see A continuous lumen, aortic pulsation at the mid-oesophagus, and the change to gastric mucosa at about 38 to 40 cm. Resistance Never advance against resistance. A stricture, tumour or angulation is a reason to stop, image or switch to a flexible scope, not to push."],["osec","th:op-45~s3-5","Examine on the way out and biopsy last","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Withdraw slowly, inspecting all four walls; the mucosa is best examined coming out. Take biopsies at the end , so that bleeding does not hide the rest of the examination, and take several from the edge of a lesion with cup forceps. Examine the hypopharynx with a laryngoscope or the scope's tip as you leave: each piriform sinus to its apex, the postcricoid region and the posterior wall. Suction air from the stomach before removing the scope, and check the teeth. Figure 4 Oesophagoscopes and long suction cannulas; below, serrated, cup and crocodile forceps: cups for biopsy, the others for foreign bodies. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 45.3 What you should see All four walls inspected, the depth of any lesion recorded in centimetres from the incisors, and intact teeth."],["osec","th:op-45~s4-1","Technique","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Flexible transnasal oesophagoscopy","Sit the patient upright or slightly reclined. Spray the nose with 4% lidocaine and oxymetazoline, then place pledgets of tetracaine, and give 2% lidocaine gel to gargle and swallow. Pass the scope along the widest nasal passage under vision, avoiding septum and turbinates, to the nasopharynx. Survey the larynx and pharynx, then each piriform sinus. Place the tip in the left piriform sinus and ask the patient to swallow: the scope slips through the cricopharyngeus. Insufflate to open the lumen, inspect the mucosa to the lower sphincter and gastro-oesophageal junction, enter the stomach and retroflex to see the cardia, then examine the oesophagus again on withdrawal. A flexible biopsy or grasping forceps passes down the channel. No cardiac monitoring is needed without sedation, and the patient leaves at once. Where transnasal endoscopy changes management It is most useful in men, obese patients and those with dysphagia after head and neck cancer treatment, and avoids a general anaesthetic. Common errors are poor topical anaesthesia, forcing the scope through the narrow side of the nose, and failing to look on the way out."],["osec","th:op-45~s5-1","Crisis drills","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · When it goes wrong","Suspected perforation during or after the procedure 1 Recognise in theatre surgical emphysema, high airway pressures, reduced breath sounds afterwards pain in the neck, chest or back, fever, tachycardia, odynophagia, crepitus 2 Act now stop chest radiograph nil by mouth nasogastric tube placed with care intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics H2 blocker or proton pump inhibitor 3 Confirm water-soluble contrast swallow when awake and stable CT if unclear 4 Treat small contained cervical leak conservative large or thoracic leak, sepsis or pneumothorax early surgical exploration and repair, drainage, chest drain involve thoracic surgeons Bradycardia or hypotension during endoscopy 1 Recognise vagal reflex from oesophageal stretch 2 Act withdraw the scope it usually settles 3 If it persists atropine (or glycopyrrolate) reassess before continuing Broken or avulsed tooth 1 Retrieve find the fragment check it has not been aspirated (chest radiograph if missing) 2 Act replant an avulsed adult tooth at once urgent dental or maxillofacial opinion wire fixation if needed document and tell the patient"],["osec","th:op-45~s6-1","After the procedure","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Aftercare","Most patients go home the same day after several hours' observation, starting with clear fluids. Before discharge, look for fever, tachycardia, odynophagia, chest pain and crepitus in the neck; tell the patient to return at once if any develop. Antibiotics and strong analgesia are not normally needed. Unexpected pain or fever after oesophagoscopy is a perforation until proven otherwise."],["ev","th:op-45~s6-2","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy: current evidence","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Aftercare","Panendoscopy today. With PET-CT and high-quality cross-sectional imaging, routine rigid oesophagoscopy and bronchoscopy as part of panendoscopy have declined; synchronous primaries are found in a few per cent of patients, mostly in smokers and drinkers, and PET-CT or flexible oesophagoscopy finds most of them. Transnasal oesophagoscopy under local anaesthetic in clinic examines the oesophagus and allows biopsy without sedation. Perforation is the main risk of rigid oesophagoscopy; pain, fever or surgical emphysema afterwards need an urgent contrast study or CT."],["osec","th:op-45~s6-2","Complications","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Dental injury Chipped, loosened or avulsed teeth; dentist; wire fixation Perforation About 1% overall; up to 6% with dilatation or foreign body removal; more with rigid scopes and inexperienced operators; usually at the cricopharyngeus Bleeding From biopsies; usually minor Arrhythmia, hypotension Vagal; withdraw the scope, atropine Pneumothorax, mediastinitis Consequences of perforation Epistaxis Transnasal endoscopy"],["osec","th:op-45~s7-1","Where the books differ","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra lists rigid, flexible and transnasal oesophagoscopy, with contraindications including trismus, cervical spine disease, a receding mandible and aortic aneurysm; positions the head raised with the neck flexed and the head extended at the atlanto-occipital joint, passes the scope in four steps (arytenoids, cricopharyngeal sphincter, aortic arch and left bronchus at 25 cm, cardia, with the scope pointing to the left anterior superior iliac spine), and recognises the cardia by redder, velvety mucosa. Lee emphasises panendoscopy in head and neck cancer and the 15, 25 and 40 cm constrictions. Myers compares rigid and flexible approaches, describes transnasal oesophagoscopy step by step, and quantifies perforation risk (about 1%, up to 6% with dilatation or foreign body removal)."],["osec","th:op-45~s7-2","Viva essentials","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three constrictions and their distances from the incisors. Why the oesophagus leaks into the mediastinum (no serosa). Killian's dehiscence : the weak point and the common site of perforation. Rigid versus flexible: when each is better. Barium versus gastrografin. Signs and management of perforation ."],["osec","th:op-45~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Esophagoscopy and Hypopharyngoscopy · For the examination","Common pitfalls A shoulder roll that extends the neck and kinks the passage. Levering on the upper incisors. Pushing against a closed cricopharyngeus. Advancing without seeing the lumen, or against resistance. Biopsying first and examining through blood. Sending home a patient with unexplained pain or fever."],["osec","th:op-46~s1-1","The case at a glance","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · Operating note","Pharyngeal pouch and cricopharyngeal dysfunction Summary Indications Symptomatic pouch (dysphagia, regurgitation of undigested food, aspiration, weight loss); cricopharyngeal dysfunction with moderate to severe dysphagia and poor sphincter opening on barium or videofluoroscopy, with good pharyngeal propulsion and laryngeal elevation Contraindications to the endoscopic route Poor exposure (limited neck extension or mouth opening, retrognathia); a very small pouch (under about 2 to 3 cm, too short a wall to staple); suspicion of carcinoma in the sac Options Endoscopic stapled or laser diverticulotomy; flexible endoscopic septotomy or peroral endoscopic myotomy (gastroenterology); open myotomy with diverticulectomy, inversion or pexy Work-up Barium swallow (size, position, residue); endoscopy of the pouch lining if there is bleeding or a change in symptoms (carcinoma is rare but reported) Anaesthesia General Tray Weerda diverticuloscope, endoscopes, endoscopic stapler or CO2 laser; open neck set, nasogastric tube, bougie"],["osec","th:op-46~s1-2","Consent","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · Operating note","What to tell the patient Recurrence of symptoms, more often after endoscopic stapling, especially with small pouches. Leak or perforation: mediastinitis (rare but serious), neck infection, a salivary fistula after open surgery. Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury and hoarseness (open surgery). Dental injury from the scope. Conversion to an open operation if the pouch cannot be exposed."],["osec","th:op-46~s1-3","Before you start","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · Operating note","Review the barium swallow for pouch size and depth. Check neck extension, teeth and mouth opening. Keep the patient on clear fluids beforehand to empty the pouch. Figure 1 Zenker's diverticulum behind the oesophagus, and its appearance on barium swallow. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 46.1 and 46.2 and 46.3"],["osec","th:op-46~s2-1","Expose the party wall","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · The operation, stage by stage","Insert the bivalved diverticuloscope with one blade in the oesophagus and the other in the pouch, so the bar (the common wall containing the cricopharyngeus) lies between them. Suck out food debris and inspect the pouch lining. Figure 2 The bar dividing the oesophagus and the pouch. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 46.8 What you should see A clear horizontal bar, oesophageal lumen in front, pouch behind, both visible."],["osec","th:op-46~s2-2","Divide the bar","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · The operation, stage by stage","Place the stapler with its anvil in the pouch and cartridge in the oesophagus, centred on the bar, and fire to divide and seal it down to the floor of the pouch; a second firing may be needed. Laser division is an alternative. Figure 3 The stapler on the bar, and the divided party wall. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 46.9 and 46.10 What you should see The bar divided to near the pouch base, edges sealed by staples, with a wide common lumen. The tip of the pouch The last few millimetres beyond the stapler cartridge cannot be divided safely; pushing further risks a perforation into the mediastinum. Accept a small residual lip."],["osec","th:op-46~s2-3","Open approach: expose the retropharyngeal space","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · The operation, stage by stage","Pass a nasogastric tube. Through a transverse incision at the cricoid level (usually left), raise flaps, separate sternomastoid and the carotid sheath from the larynx and thyroid, divide the middle thyroid vein and omohyoid if needed, and rotate the larynx medially with a hook on the back of the thyroid lamina (not near the cricothyroid joint) to enter the retropharyngeal space. Figure 4 The carotid sheath retracted laterally to expose the prevertebral fascia. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 46.4 What you should see The pouch and the cricopharyngeus behind the larynx, with the recurrent nerve safe in the tracheo-oesophageal groove."],["osec","th:op-46~s2-4","Myotomy","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · The operation, stage by stage","Grasp the pharynx above and below the cricopharyngeus over the nasogastric tube, and divide all the muscle fibres between mucosa and muscle from the inferior constrictor (oblique fibres) through the cricopharyngeus (horizontal fibres) and 1 to 2 cm onto the oesophagus. Figure 5 Muscle fibres separated from the mucosa for the myotomy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 46.5 What you should see Mucosa bulging through a complete longitudinal myotomy, with no intact horizontal fibres. Senior's pearl The myotomy is the operation; the sac is secondary. A pouch removed without dividing the cricopharyngeus recurs or leaks."],["osec","th:op-46~s2-5","Deal with the sac","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · The operation, stage by stage","Small pouches need only the myotomy. For larger ones, excise the sac at its neck with a stapler or by excising and suturing the mucosa (without traction, to avoid narrowing), or invert or suspend it (diverticulopexy) to avoid opening the pharynx. Do not close muscle over the mucosa. Drain and close. Figure 6 The diverticulum dissected free and excised. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 46.6 and 46.7 What you should see A closed mucosal suture line without narrowing, and a complete myotomy."],["osec","th:op-46~s3-1","Crisis drills","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · When it goes wrong","Fever, chest pain or surgical emphysema after endoscopic stapling 1 Act nil by mouth, intravenous antibiotics, contrast swallow and CT 2 Leak or mediastinitis surgical drainage nutrition by nasogastric tube or gastrostomy Salivary leak after open surgery 1 Act nil by mouth, nasogastric feeding open the wound to drain most close in days"],["osec","th:op-46~s4-1","After surgery","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · Aftercare","Endoscopic: fluids the same day or the next, then soft diet. Open: nasogastric tube or nil by mouth for a few days, contrast swallow before feeding if the pharynx was opened. Watch temperature, pain and neck swelling."],["ev","th:op-46~s4-2","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter: current evidence","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · Aftercare","A meta-analysis of 11 studies (596 patients) found endoscopic treatment shorter, with shorter stay and earlier feeding and fewer complications, but a higher recurrence of symptoms than open surgery (Albers et al., Endosc Int Open 2016). Flexible endoscopic septum division achieved success in 91% with adverse events in 11% and recurrence in 11% in a meta-analysis of 813 patients (Ishaq et al., Gastrointest Endosc 2016), and peroral endoscopic myotomy (Z-POEM) is a promising newer option (Zhang et al., Surg Endosc 2022). A 2021 network meta-analysis of 903 patients found fewer persistent or recurrent symptoms after open diverticulectomy than after endoscopic laser diverticulotomy, with no difference between laser and stapling (Bhatt et al., JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2021)."],["osec","th:op-46~s4-2","Complications","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · Aftercare","Perforation and mediastinitis, salivary fistula, bleeding, recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy, dental injury, recurrence."],["osec","th:op-46~s5-1","Where the books differ","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers open cricopharyngeal myotomy with excision, inversion or pexy of the sac, and endoscopic stapler division of the party wall. Dhingra Killian's dehiscence and the classical open diverticulectomy with myotomy. The literature endoscopic division is quicker and safer, open surgery recurs less."],["osec","th:op-46~s5-2","Viva essentials","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Anatomy : Killian's dehiscence, the cricopharyngeus, the recurrent nerve. Symptoms and barium findings. Endoscopic versus open : indications and outcomes. Why the myotomy matters. Complications , especially mediastinitis."],["osec","th:op-46~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Dysfunction of the Upper Esophageal Sphincter · For the examination","Common pitfalls Stapling a pouch too small to hold the stapler. Pushing beyond the end of the bar. Excising the sac without a myotomy. Pulling the sac and narrowing the pharynx."],["osec","th:op-5~s1-1","The case at a glance","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Operating note","Medialisation laryngoplasty and arytenoid adduction Summary Indications Symptomatic glottic insufficiency (dysphonia, breathlessness on speaking, aspiration) from paralysis, paresis, atrophy (presbylarynx) or scar, especially when movement has little or no chance of returning; soft tissue loss of the fold after surgery or trauma Add arytenoid adduction A large posterior glottic gap , a foreshortened (slack) fold, or a significant vertical height mismatch ; it is the only operation that consistently closes the posterior glottis Contraindications Poor abduction of the other fold (airway); malignancy involving the laryngotracheal complex; lesions of the vocal fold; coagulopathy (relative); intact movement of the fold (for arytenoid adduction) Anaesthesia Monitored anaesthesia care : local anaesthetic with light sedation and analgesia, so the patient can phonate; nebulised topical anaesthetic beforehand; glycopyrrolate to dry secretions; dexamethasone 10 mg Position Supine with a modest shoulder roll to extend the neck; make the patient comfortable Antibiotics One prophylactic intravenous dose 30 to 45 minutes before incision Tray Head and neck soft tissue set; Cottle or Freer elevator; caliper; drill with a 1 to 2 mm cutting bur; flexible laryngoscope with C-mount camera and monitor; the implant. Gore-Tex: middle ear instruments (alligator, annulus elevator, curette), Woodson elevator, 1 to 3 mm Kerrison rongeurs. Silastic: Netterville medialisation set and Silastic block. Arytenoid adduction: 4-0 permanent monofilament suture and a Kitner (peanut) dissector Implant Silastic, "],["osec","th:op-5~s1-2","Consent","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to move the weak vocal cord to the middle so the cords meet, for a stronger voice and safer swallowing. You will be awake and asked to speak during the operation: this is how the result is fine-tuned. The other side or an extra procedure: both sides, or an arytenoid adduction, may be needed depending on what is found on the day. The voice will sound slightly strained at the end, because of swelling; it settles over 2 to 3 weeks and is judged at about 4 weeks. Revision may be needed for a persisting gap at the back, too little medialisation, an implant in the wrong place or a height mismatch. Breathing difficulty from swelling, bleeding or a haematoma. Implant problems: movement, or extrusion into the airway (an emergency) or through the skin. Infection , and with arytenoid adduction a leak from the throat into the neck (fistula). A scar in a skin crease of the neck."],["osec","th:op-5~s1-3","Before you start","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Operating note","Ask how the hoarseness began, suddenly or slowly, and what came before it: surgery, intubation, an upper respiratory infection. The voice is typically weak, breathy and effortful; ask about breathlessness on speaking, coughing and choking, especially with liquids, and aspiration pneumonia. In the past history look for neck surgery (thyroid, parathyroid, carotid), cervical spine or thoracic and mediastinal surgery, malignancy (head and neck, thyroid, oesophagus, lung), stroke, and lung disease. Little reserve A patient with COPD or other lung disease has little pulmonary reserve: aspiration from a paralysed fold hurts them sooner, and they need earlier intervention. Examine the head and neck and the cranial nerves. Transnasal flexible laryngoscopy shows the movement of both folds, above all the abduction of the other one, and the airway; stroboscopy shows the closure pattern and whether the folds are level. When paralysis has no clear cause, image with contrast-enhanced CT or MRI from the skull base to the aortic arch , along the whole course of the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Stop anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs (NSAIDs, warfarin, clopidogrel, factor Xa inhibitors) in agreement with the cardiologist or haematologist. Prepare the patient psychologically for an operation on the neck while awake."],["osec","th:op-5~s2-1","For medialisation","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Anatomy you must see","The inferior border of the thyroid ala sets the plane of the vocal fold, and the window is placed in relation to it. The whole muscular tubercle on the inferior border of the ala: its angulation can tilt your idea of the true lower border and misorient the window, so you must expose about 5 mm behind it to see it all. The window site: about 5 to 7 mm behind the midline of the thyroid cartilage and 3 mm above its inferior border, immediately lateral to the vocal fold. Figure 1 The site of the medialisation laryngoplasty window, 3 mm above the inferior border of the thyroid ala. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 5.1"],["osec","th:op-5~s2-2","For arytenoid adduction","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Anatomy you must see","The posterior border of the thyroid ala and its inferior cornu. The cricopharyngeus, attached to the inferior cornu. The pyriform sinus, lying directly over the posterior lamina of the cricoid. The posterior cricoarytenoid muscle on the posterior cricoid lamina, its fibres running from medial and inferior to lateral and superior, converging on the muscular process of the arytenoid, a small ridge that feels and looks like a grain of rice ."],["osec","th:op-5~s3-1","Anaesthetise and sedate","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation, stage by stage","Before the patient comes into theatre, nebulise a 50:50 mixture of 4% lidocaine (2 mg/kg) and 1% tetracaine (0.5 mg/kg) to anaesthetise the upper aerodigestive tract so that the patient tolerates the flexible scope. Give glycopyrrolate unless contraindicated, and one dose of dexamethasone. Pack the more open side of the nose with pledgets of 4% lidocaine and oxymetazoline. Once sedated, inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline superficially and deep over the thyroid ala on the side or sides of surgery. If you plan an arytenoid adduction, add a superior laryngeal nerve block in the thyrohyoid space. What you should see A calm, breathing patient who can still speak, and a clear view of the folds on the flexible scope."],["osec","th:op-5~s3-2","Incise and expose the cartilage","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation, stage by stage","Make a 4 to 5 cm curved incision in a skin crease near the inferior border of the thyroid ala on the operative side, crossing the midline by about 1 cm. Divide the platysma and raise subplatysmal flaps up to the hyoid and down to the cricoid. Divide the midline raphe between the strap muscles and hold the skin and straps apart with two self-retaining retractors or fishhooks. What you should see The thyroid cartilage from the notch to its inferior border, both alae exposed in the midline."],["osec","th:op-5~s3-3","Raise the outer perichondrium and find the tubercle","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the outer perichondrium along the midline and elevate it widely, subperichondrially, back to about 5 mm behind the muscular tubercle . Only when the posterior part of the tubercle is seen can you identify the true inferior border of the cartilage. What you should see The bare thyroid ala from the midline back to behind the muscular tubercle, with its true lower border. A misplaced window If the tubercle is not fully exposed, its angulation misleads you about the true inferior border, the window is set at the wrong angle or height, and the implant medialises the wrong part of the fold."],["osec","th:op-5~s3-4","Mark and confirm the window","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation, stage by stage","With the caliper, mark the site 5 to 7 mm behind the midline and 3 mm above the inferior border. Pass a 25 G needle through the cartilage there and watch it on the flexible scope: it should enter immediately lateral to the vocal fold, not too far back, forward or up. If the ala is too ossified for the needle, drill a small hole with a 1 to 2 mm cutting bur and pass a blunt instrument such as an annulus elevator through it instead. What you should see On the scope, the needle tenting the tissue just lateral to the fold, at the level of its free edge."],["osec","th:op-5~s3-5","Cut the window","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation, stage by stage","For Gore-Tex , a 3 × 3 mm window, with the drill in ossified cartilage or a No. 15 or Beaver blade. For Silastic , typically 6 × 13 mm , with drill or scalpel. Remove the cartilage from the window. What you should see A clean window with the inner perichondrium intact beneath it."],["osec","th:op-5~s3-6","Make the pocket while the patient speaks","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the inner perichondrium above, behind and below to leave an anteriorly based flap , and elevate the paraglottic space backward and downward for the implant. Watch the flexible scope and listen to the voice as you go: they tell you the size, position and kind of implant the fold needs, and this step decides the success of the operation. What you should see The fold moving to the midline along its whole length on the scope as you press, with the voice clearing. The two commonest mistakes Overmedialising anteriorly gives a pressed, strained voice; placing the implant too superiorly pushes the ventricle and the false fold medially and impairs phonation. Keep the bulk where the fold needs it. Keep within the perichondrium With a Gore-Tex strip, keep the dissection inside the inner perichondrium where the implant will lie. Wide undermining lets it migrate to a less favourable position."],["osec","th:op-5~s3-7","Insert and tune the implant","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation, stage by stage","Insert the implant through the window while watching the scope and listening to the voice. Adjust it until the fold is medialised and the glottis closes. Slightly overmedialise : the fold swells during the operation and the swelling will go down. Figure 2 Gore-Tex placed into the paraglottic space through the window, medialising the vocal fold. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 5.2 What you should see A slightly strained voice, a medialised fold and complete glottic closure on the screen: the intended end point. Entering the airway Unrecognised breach of the mucosa, usually of the ventricle, while placing the implant is thought to be the cause of later extrusion into the airway, a dangerous complication. Work in the paraglottic space and look for any breach."],["osec","th:op-5~s3-8","Secure and close","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation, stage by stage","Fix the implant to the adjacent cartilage with a permanent 4-0 Prolene suture. Secure haemostasis; a small drain in the dependent part of the wound is optional. Lay the perichondrium back over the window, bring the strap muscles together in the midline, and close the skin in two layers, dermal and epidermal, for the best scar. What you should see The fold straight in the midline on the scope after the implant is fixed, with no bulge above it."],["osec","th:op-5~s4-1","Extend the exposure","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation: arytenoid adduction","Use the same approach, then divide about 1 cm of the medial sternohyoid just below its insertion on the hyoid to open the posterior view. Continue the subperichondrial elevation from the front two thirds of the ala to its posterior border and skeletonise the inferior cornu. What you should see The posterior border of the ala and the inferior cornu."],["osec","th:op-5~s4-2","Free the back of the larynx","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation: arytenoid adduction","Dissect around the posterior border of the ala from the inferior cornu up toward the greater cornu, keeping on the cartilage so as not to enter the pyriform sinus. Rotate the larynx medially with a hook on the greater cornu to bring its back into view. Release the upper part of the cricopharyngeus from the inferior cornu with bipolar diathermy to expose the pyriform sinus. What you should see The back of the cricoid coming into view as the larynx rotates, with the pyriform mucosa intact and bulging when the patient puffs. Puff out your cheeks Ask the awake patient to puff out the cheeks from time to time: the pyriform sinus inflates and shows you exactly where its walls are."],["osec","th:op-5~s4-3","Lift the pyriform sinus and find the muscle","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation: arytenoid adduction","The pyriform sinus lies directly over the posterior cricoid lamina and the posterior cricoarytenoid muscle, so it is your guide. Dissect it off the cricoid from inferolateral to superomedial until the posterior cricoarytenoid appears, recognised by fibres running from medial-inferior to lateral-superior. Retract the lower part of the sinus and follow the muscle up to the ridge where its fibres end: the muscular process . Figure 3 Identifying the muscular process of the arytenoid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 5.3 What you should see Muscle fibres of the posterior cricoarytenoid converging on a small bony knob, the muscular process. The pyriform sinus A breach of the pyriform sinus leads to a pharyngocutaneous fistula. If you see one, oversew it carefully at once."],["osec","th:op-5~s4-4","Open a posterior window if needed","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation: arytenoid adduction","If the muscular process is hard to reach, remove a small piece of the posterior border of the thyroid cartilage with a rongeur, a posterior thyroplasty window, to help you find it and pass the suture. Figure 4 Removing a small part of the posterior border of the thyroid cartilage with a rongeur to help find and suture the muscular process. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 5.4 What you should see The muscular process through the gap in the posterior border."],["osec","th:op-5~s4-5","Place the adduction suture","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation: arytenoid adduction","Pass a figure-of-eight 4-0 non-absorbable suture (such as Mersilene) through the muscular process. Pull gently and confirm on the flexible scope that you control the arytenoid. If the medialisation window is not yet cut, clamp the two arms, let the larynx return to its position, and make the window as above. What you should see The arytenoid rotating inward and the posterior fold closing when the suture is tensioned."],["osec","th:op-5~s4-6","Bring the suture forward","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation: arytenoid adduction","Through the window, develop a track with an elevator in the paraglottic space from the muscular process to the window. Pass a middle ear alligator back along it and draw both arms forward through the window. Pass one arm through the cricothyroid membrane in front of and below the window, and the other through the thyroid cartilage just in front of the window. What you should see Both suture arms emerging at the front, running freely when pulled. A blocked track Without a clear track from the muscular process to the window, the arytenoid will not rotate properly however you tension the suture."],["osec","th:op-5~s4-7","Tension gently, then medialise","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · The operation: arytenoid adduction","With gentle tension on the sutures and a simulated medialisation through the window, judge the voice and the fold on the scope to set the tension and the amount of medialisation. Little tension is needed : the suture only guides the arytenoid into a good position and holds it there; excessive tension over-rotates it and spoils the voice. When combined with adduction, the fold usually needs less medialisation. Tie the suture, complete the medialisation laryngoplasty, place a small dependent drain and close as before. What you should see On the scope, the vocal process swinging medially and slightly down, with the fold lengthening."],["osec","th:op-5~s5-1","Crisis drills","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · When it goes wrong","Voice only marginally better after the implant 1 Look is there a posterior gap, a height mismatch or a short, slack fold on the scope? 2 Posterior gap or mismatch add an arytenoid adduction now (consented beforehand) 3 Implant too anterior or superior reposition the bulk should lie at the level of the fold, not the false fold 4 Other side consider augmenting the opposite fold if it too is deficient Pyriform sinus opened 1 Confirm look for the mucosal breach, puffing the cheeks 2 Repair oversew carefully at once 3 Afterwards watch the wound for a pharyngocutaneous fistula"],["osec","th:op-5~s5-2","Common errors in technique","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Operating when the airway is already compromised by poor abduction of the other fold. Placing the implant too superiorly or too anteriorly. Breaching the airway with the implant, or the pyriform sinus during the posterior dissection. No clear track for the adduction suture. Too much tension on the adduction suture."],["osec","th:op-5~s6-1","After the operation","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Aftercare","Medialisation is usually a day case; arytenoid adduction often means a 23-hour stay, depending on comorbidity. Give analgesia; if observed overnight, intravenous dexamethasone 6 to 8 mg every 8 hours for 24 hours; after arytenoid adduction, a 6-day tapering course of methylprednisolone. Sit the patient up. No voice rest and no routine antibiotics. Review in clinic at about 4 weeks : oedema of the fold and surrounding tissues takes 3 to 4 weeks to settle, and an earlier examination is not a valid judge of the result."],["osec","th:op-5~s6-2","Complications","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Implant malposition Commonest: too much medialisation and too superior a position . Superior placement prolapses the ventricle and medialises the false fold; anterior overmedialisation gives a pressed, strained voice Undermedialisation Failure to allow for intraoperative oedema, or to recognise that the other side also needed augmentation; hence the slightly strained voice at the end Implant extrusion Medially into the airway (severe, can be an airway emergency; from an unrecognised mucosal breach, usually of the ventricle) or laterally through the skin Pharyngocutaneous fistula From a breach of the pyriform sinus during arytenoid adduction Over-rotation of the arytenoid From excessive suture tension; usually seen and corrected on the table Bleeding, haematoma, airway obstruction, infection General risks of the neck wound Persistent dysphonia Posterior gap, undermedialisation, implant too anterior or superior, height mismatch; may need revision"],["ev","th:op-5~s6-3","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction: current evidence","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Aftercare","Thyroplasty or reinnervation. In a randomised trial, overall voice results were similar after medialisation thyroplasty and ansa cervicalis to recurrent nerve reinnervation at one year, but reinnervation was better under 52 years and thyroplasty better over 52 (Paniello et al., Laryngoscope 2011). Assessment. An international consensus recommends stroboscopy, perceptual rating, maximum phonation time and the Voice Handicap Index before and after surgery (Mattei et al., Eur Ann Otorhinolaryngol Head Neck Dis 2018). Arytenoid adduction is added for a wide posterior gap or a level difference."],["osec","th:op-5~s6-3","Alternatives","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · Aftercare","Observation if the patient has no voice complaint and no dysphagia. Injection laryngoplasty with a long-lasting material such as calcium hydroxylapatite, or with autologous fat."],["osec","th:op-5~s7-1","Evidence and history","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · For the examination","Previous radiotherapy does not significantly change subjective voice results or complications after medialisation laryngoplasty. Isshiki reintroduced medialisation (type I thyroplasty) in 1974; the principle is unchanged: move the paralysed fold to the midline so that the mobile fold can meet it. Zeitels' adduction arytenopexy (1998) is an alternative way to reposition the arytenoid, moving it posteriorly, superiorly and medially on the cricoid."],["osec","th:op-5~s7-2","Viva essentials","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why the patient is awake , and the end point: a slightly strained voice, a medialised fold, complete closure. The window : 5 to 7 mm behind the midline, 3 mm above the inferior border; why the muscular tubercle must be fully seen. The commonest implant errors : too anterior and too superior. Why add arytenoid adduction : posterior gap, short fold, height mismatch; neither injection nor medialisation closes the posterior glottis. How arytenoid adduction works: it mimics the lateral cricoarytenoid . The landmarks behind: pyriform sinus, cricopharyngeus, posterior cricoarytenoid, the \"grain of rice\". Why you review at 4 weeks, not sooner. Thyroplasty medialises the fold; it does not move it anteriorly, posteriorly or superiorly."],["osec","th:op-5~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Medialization Laryngoplasty and Arytenoid Adduction · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not exposing the posterior end of the muscular tubercle. Forgetting to check the other fold's abduction before surgery. Stopping when the voice sounds perfect on the table, and undermedialising. Wide undermining around a Gore-Tex strip. Pulling hard on the adduction suture. Judging the result before the swelling has gone."],["osec","th:op-52~s1-1","The case at a glance","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · Operating note","Drug-induced sleep endoscopy Summary Indications Obstructive sleep apnoea or snoring in patients considering surgery or alternatives after failing or refusing CPAP; planning multilevel surgery; selection for hypoglossal nerve stimulation; assessing failed surgery; selected children with persistent apnoea after adenotonsillectomy Contraindications Severe cardiorespiratory disease or very high anaesthetic risk; pregnancy; allergy to the sedative Prerequisites Sleep study, history, examination including awake nasendoscopy, body mass index, and the patient's goals Sedation Propofol (target-controlled infusion preferred) or midazolam, with or without dexmedetomidine, titrated to loss of response to voice with snoring; BIS monitoring optional Setting Theatre or procedure room with anaesthetic support, full monitoring and airway equipment Reporting VOTE classification: Velum, Oropharynx (lateral walls and tonsils), Tongue base, Epiglottis; degree (none, partial, complete) and direction (anteroposterior, lateral, concentric) Tray Flexible nasolaryngoscope with recording, lidocaine gel, decongestant, demisting solution, jaw-thrust and mandibular advancement simulation, oxygen"],["osec","th:op-52~s1-2","Consent","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · Operating note","What to tell the patient It is a diagnostic test to plan treatment. Nose bleed or discomfort from the scope. Oversedation with low oxygen or a pause in breathing, managed by the anaesthetist. Aspiration (rare)."],["osec","th:op-52~s1-3","Before you start","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · Operating note","Confirm a recent sleep study and awake examination. Decide what each finding would change: no test is needed if no surgery or device is being considered."],["osec","th:op-52~s2-1","Prepare the nose","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Place a cotton applicator with 2% lidocaine gel along the floor of one nostril and another between the inferior and middle turbinates, with the patient upright; avoid anaesthetising the pharynx or the other side, which can worsen collapse artificially. Figure 1 Topical preparation and the scope with lubricant and demister. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 52.1 and 52.2 What you should see A comfortable nostril without numbing of the throat."],["osec","th:op-52~s2-2","Position and set up","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Lie the patient supine on one pillow (as in bed), dim the lights, start monitoring, and record the patient's identity on the video. Figure 2 Room set-up with the surgeon, scope screen and anaesthetist. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 52.3 What you should see A quiet room and a screen showing the scope view for both surgeon and anaesthetist."],["osec","th:op-52~s2-3","Scope first, then sedate","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Pass the scope to the level of the soft palate before giving the sedative, so you see the airway change as sedation deepens. Titrate propofol until the patient stops responding to voice and begins to snore or obstruct. What you should see Snoring and cycles of obstruction and arousal, as in natural sleep. Too deep is not sleep Deep sedation causes collapse that does not happen in natural sleep and suggests surgery that will not work. Titrate slowly to the onset of snoring and obstruction, and use target-controlled infusion if possible."],["osec","th:op-52~s2-4","Examine each level","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Watch through several cycles at the velum, the oropharynx (lateral walls and tonsils), the tongue base and the epiglottis. Record each site's degree and pattern of collapse, especially complete concentric collapse at the velum, which predicts poor response to hypoglossal nerve stimulation. What you should see A clear record of which level closes first, how completely and in which direction."],["osec","th:op-52~s2-5","Test manoeuvres","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Assess the effect of jaw thrust (simulating a mandibular advancement device) and, if relevant, mouth closure, head rotation or the lateral position, which can relieve positional obstruction. Figure 3 Assessing the right lateral position. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 52.4 Figure 4 A CPAP mask adapter allowing the scope to watch the airway on positive pressure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 52.5 What you should see Whether jaw thrust or lateral position opens the airway. Senior's pearl Report with the VOTE system and record the video. The findings only matter if they change the plan: palate surgery for velum collapse, tongue base surgery or devices for tongue base collapse, and no stimulator for complete concentric palatal collapse."],["osec","th:op-52~s3-1","Crisis drills","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · When it goes wrong","Desaturation or apnoea under sedation 1 Act stop the infusion jaw thrust oxygen bag-mask ventilation if needed"],["osec","th:op-52~s4-1","After the test","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · Aftercare","Recover until fully awake; discuss findings and the treatment plan once the patient is alert, with the video."],["ev","th:op-52~s4-2","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy: current evidence","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · Aftercare","The European position paper on DISE, updated in 2017, standardised the indications (selection for surgery or devices, failed treatment), the sedative technique (propofol, ideally target-controlled infusion, or midazolam), the depth of sedation and a common reporting language based on the level, degree and pattern of collapse (De Vito et al., Sleep Breath 2014; De Vito et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2018)."],["osec","th:op-52~s4-2","Complications","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · Aftercare","Epistaxis, oversedation, desaturation, aspiration, laryngospasm (rare)."],["osec","th:op-52~s5-1","Where the books differ","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers a practical DISE technique with careful nasal preparation, scope placement before sedation, and testing of jaw thrust and position. K. J. Lee DISE within the evaluation of obstructive sleep apnoea and the multilevel surgical plan. The European position paper: standard indications, sedation and the VOTE reporting system."],["osec","th:op-52~s5-2","Viva essentials","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why DISE adds to awake examination. Sedation and the danger of going too deep. VOTE classification. How findings change treatment , including hypoglossal nerve stimulation."],["osec","th:op-52~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Drug-Induced Sedated Endoscopy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Over-sedating. Anaesthetising the pharynx. Not recording the examination. Performing DISE when no treatment decision depends on it."],["osec","th:op-53~s1-1","The case at a glance","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · Operating note","UPPP, uvulopalatal flap and anterior palatoplasty Summary Indications Obstructive sleep apnoea without significant central apnoea in a patient who cannot tolerate CPAP; upper airway resistance syndrome (symptoms and arousals without a raised AHI); selected non-apnoeic snoring Choosing the operation UPPP: most excisional; Friedman stage I (and II) anatomy; can help more severe apnoea. Uvulopalatal flap: mild to moderate apnoea or snoring with a thin, atrophic, long palate. Anterior palatoplasty: mild to moderate apnoea or snoring with a shorter palate; can be done under local anaesthetic Contraindications Medically unstable; previous velopharyngeal insufficiency; cleft palate; pharyngeal cancer or radiotherapy Anaesthesia General with an oral tube (an oral RAE or armoured tube resists compression by the gag); the surgeon stays for induction because these patients are often hard to intubate; keep the patient paralysed for jaw relaxation and exposure Position Table turned 90 or 180 degrees, supine with a shoulder roll; anterior palatoplasty under local can be done sitting in the sniffing position Antibiotics Clean-contaminated: from induction for 24 hours (ampicillin-sulbactam, clindamycin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, or a cephalosporin with metronidazole) Monitoring Standard; overnight oximetry for moderate to severe apnoea Investigations Sleep study (home study or polysomnography) to grade apnoea and exclude central apnoea, hypoventilation and other sleep disorders; drug-induced sleep endoscopy (DISE) if the sites of collapse are unclear Tray Tonsillectomy set; McIvor gag; mono"],["osec","th:op-53~s1-2","Consent","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it does: removes the tonsils and trims and tightens the soft palate to widen the airway behind it; it often reduces snoring and apnoea, but may not cure apnoea. Results depend on your anatomy ; you will need a sleep study afterwards even if the snoring stops, and you may still need CPAP or a mouth guard (often more comfortable after surgery). Throat pain for about two weeks, with a soft diet. Bleeding , as after tonsillectomy, which can need a return to theatre. Nasal speech and fluid coming out of the nose when drinking, common for a short time and almost always settling. Rare: permanent nasal speech or regurgitation; narrowing between the nose and throat from scarring; breathing problems after surgery, which is why you may stay overnight. Taste change and a feeling of something in the throat."],["osec","th:op-53~s1-3","Before you start","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Night: snoring (loudness, change with weight and position), witnessed apnoeas, waking, nocturia. Day: sleepiness, fatigue, morning headaches, poor memory and concentration, mood change; a job that needs alertness (driving). Nasal blockage (day, night, lying on one side). Previous sleep studies and CPAP titrations; previous treatment (CPAP, oral appliance, positional therapy, weight loss) and why CPAP failed . Other sleep disorders: central apnoea, hypoventilation from lung disease, restless legs, circadian disorders, insomnia, parasomnias. Hypertension, heart disease, stroke, diabetes, hypothyroidism; previous head and neck surgery or radiation; cleft palate or previous nasal speech . Family history; anticoagulants, alcohol, sedatives, opioids; smoking; the effect on the bed partner. 1.3.2 The examination Body mass index , neck circumference, thyromental angle and hyoid position. Nose: septal deviation, turbinates, valves, polyps (nasal surgery may lower the pressure needed and improve CPAP use). Face: a small mandible or retruded maxilla (consider jaw surgery). Mouth: teeth and bite; palate width, thickness and vertical length; Friedman palate position; tonsil size; prominent lateral pharyngeal walls (palatopharyngeus). Flexible nasendoscopy lying down, or DISE: nasal obstruction, whether the palate hangs obliquely or vertically, its length, tonsils and lateral walls, tongue base and lingual tonsils, epiglottic collapse, and the effect of pushing the jaw forward. Friedman staging and UPPP success Stage Typical anatomy UPPP success (one series) I Large ton"],["osec","th:op-53~s2-1","The soft palate","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · Anatomy you must see","The junction of hard and soft palate , and the genu of the soft palate, where it bends: the sling of the levator veli palatini , which lifts the palate to close the nasopharynx during speech and swallowing. The velum and uvula (with the musculus uvulae). The palatoglossus (anterior pillar) and palatopharyngeus (posterior pillar), and the superior constrictor in the tonsil bed. The genu Cutting palate in front of the genu damages the levator sling: the palate can no longer close the nasopharynx, and the patient is left with nasal speech and fluid coming out of the nose."],["osec","th:op-53~s3-1","Tonsillectomy, preserving the pillars","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · The operation: UPPP","Inject the planned incision lines with 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline. Remove both tonsils without breaching the superior constrictor, keeping the palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus and their mucosa. Do not remove the adenoids at the same time: it raises the risk of nasopharyngeal stenosis. What you should see Two tonsil fossae with intact pillars and constrictor."],["osec","th:op-53~s3-2","Mark and cut the palate","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · The operation: UPPP","Start at the anterior pillar and cut upward in the sagittal plane to the planned coronal incision, about 8 to 10 mm in front of the free edge of the soft palate , and behind the genu . Make the junction of the sagittal and coronal cuts a right angle ; a rounded corner contracts like a purse string. Complete the cuts through the palate and remove the back of the palate with the uvula. Cold dissection with sparing bipolar reduces heat injury and pain. Figure 1 The incision line for UPPP, behind the genu of the soft palate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 53.1 What you should see A square-cornered palatal edge, the genu untouched, and the levator sling intact. The purse string A rounded corner where the coronal and sagittal cuts meet scars into a ring that narrows the airway and can make apnoea worse."],["osec","th:op-53~s3-3","Close","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · The operation: UPPP","Sew the anterior and posterior pillars together on each side to close the tonsil fossae, then close the mucosa of the coronal cut. Use durable sutures such as Vicryl or Monocryl, because swallowing pulls hard on these suture lines. Figure 2 Keep a right angle between the coronal and sagittal closures to keep the lateral oropharynx wide as it heals. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 53.2 Senior's pearl Modern palate surgery reconstructs more than it removes. Keep the posterior pillar mucosa, cut behind the genu, and close at right angles: these three habits prevent most of the bad outcomes. What you should see The fossae closed with the lateral walls pulled forward and outward, the nasopharynx still open behind a palate that reaches the back wall when you lift it. Too much palate Over-resection of palate and uvula causes nasal regurgitation and velopharyngeal insufficiency; scarring of raw posterior pillars causes nasopharyngeal stenosis. Both are far harder to treat than residual snoring."],["osec","th:op-53~s4-1","Plan the flap","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · The operation: uvulopalatal flap","Remove the tonsils as above. Fold the tip of the muscular uvula forward to find the furthest-forward point of the dissection, following the natural S-shaped curve of the back edge of the palate. Do not pull the folded palate tight while planning: tension causes the wound to break down. What you should see The uvula folding forward without tension to a point on the soft palate, well behind the hard palate, with the dimple of the levator sling in front of your planned line. Cutting into the levator The flap is mucosa and submucosa only. Dissection into the muscle of the palate, or in front of the levator dimple, causes velopharyngeal insufficiency."],["osec","th:op-53~s4-2","Raise and fold","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · The operation: uvulopalatal flap","Inject generously and wait several minutes. Incise the oral mucosa along the planned lines from that forward point to the back edge of the palate on each side. With cold dissection and sparing bipolar, remove the oral mucosa as a star-shaped area from the underlying muscle, back to the free edge and the tip of the uvular muscle, including excess uvular mucosa. Fold the uvula forward and stitch the nasal mucosa of its tip to the most anterior point, then close the remaining lines. Figure 3 Raising the oral mucosa off the palatal muscle to make the uvulopalatal flap. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 53.3 Figure 4 The flap folded forward and sutured. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 53.4 Figure 5 The appearance after a uvulopalatal flap. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 53.5 What you should see A shortened, folded palate lifted forward from the back wall of the pharynx, with closures free of tension. A bulky palate Folding a thick, muscular palate leaves an uncomfortable bulk. Keep the flap for the thin, atrophic palate."],["osec","th:op-53~s5-1","Prepare","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · The operation: anterior palatoplasty","Remove the tonsils as above; consider local anaesthetic only if the tonsils are already out or very small (grade 1). Inject generously and wait. Trim the uvula partly or completely according to the anatomy. What you should see Blanched mucosa, tonsil fossae empty, and the free edge of the palate and uvula clearly outlined. Senior's pearl Choose the operation at sleep endoscopy, not in theatre: a palate that collapses front to back needs something different from lateral wall collapse."],["osec","th:op-53~s5-2","Excise and advance","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · The operation: anterior palatoplasty","If the posterior pillars are webbed to the back wall of the pharynx, make a small sagittal cut beside the uvula on each side so the palate is not tethered. Excise a rectangle of mucosa about 1 by 4 cm from the soft palate just behind its junction with the hard palate. Achieve haemostasis, then sew the front and back edges together: as the scar contracts, the whole palate moves forward and stiffens. What you should see A tight closure just behind the hard palate, with the soft palate pulled forward and no tethering behind. Webbing left behind Posterior pillar webs that are not released hold the palate back and blunt the effect of the advancement."],["osec","th:op-53~s6-1","Crisis drills","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · When it goes wrong","Breathing difficulty or low oxygen after surgery 1 Causes swelling of the operated airway, opioid suppression of breathing, residual apnoea 2 Act sit up, oxygen, monitor reduce opioids to the least effective dose restart the patient's own CPAP or oral appliance if they have one 3 Escalate airway team reintubation if needed Bleeding from the tonsil fossae 1 Act as for post-tonsillectomy bleeding resuscitate, examine, and return to theatre if it does not stop The uvulopalatal flap breaks down 1 Act debride the breakdown to prevent further damage and airway obstruction 2 Prevent avoid tension when planning the fold Nasal speech or regurgitation 1 Expect common early and usually resolves 2 Persisting speech and swallowing therapy investigate if it lasts months"],["osec","th:op-53~s7-1","After the operation","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · Aftercare","Antibiotics for 24 hours. Overnight monitoring with oximetry for moderate to severe apnoea; patients may wear their CPAP or oral appliance. Analgesia with paracetamol and the least opioid that works; make sure the patient is drinking and eating before discharge. Soft diet for 10 to 14 days. Repeat sleep study after healing, whatever the snoring is like: residual apnoea is common, and surgery often makes CPAP or an oral appliance easier to use."],["osec","th:op-53~s7-2","Complications","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · Aftercare","Complications of palatal surgery Complication Notes Airway oedema and obstruction Monitor moderate to severe apnoea overnight Opioid respiratory depression Titrate carefully Bleeding As after tonsillectomy Dehydration and poor intake Pain control Flap dehiscence Excess tension in the uvulopalatal flap Velopharyngeal insufficiency Common and temporary; persistent if palate cut in front of the genu Nasopharyngeal stenosis Rare but disabling; avoid adenoidectomy at the same time and keep posterior pillar mucosa Persistent apnoea Multilevel collapse; Friedman stage III"],["ev","th:op-53~s7-3","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications: current evidence","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · Aftercare","Children. In the CHAT trial, adenotonsillectomy for childhood sleep apnoea did not improve attention or executive function more than watchful waiting at seven months, but improved behaviour, symptoms, quality of life and polysomnography (Marcus et al., NEJM 2013); in PATS, for snoring with mild sleep-disordered breathing, it again did not improve executive function but improved behaviour, symptoms and quality of life (Redline et al., JAMA 2023). Adults. CPAP improves sleepiness and quality of life, but in the SAVE trial it did not prevent cardiovascular events (McEvoy et al., NEJM 2016). Tirzepatide reduced the apnoea–hypopnoea index by about half in obese adults in SURMOUNT-OSA (Malhotra et al., NEJM 2024), adding weight loss drugs to the options."],["osec","th:op-53~s7-3","Alternatives","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · Aftercare","CPAP and other forms of positive pressure; nasal surgery to make CPAP easier; weight loss and behaviour change; positional therapy for apnoea that is much worse on the back; a mandibular advancement device (good for mild apnoea and as an alternative in selected patients, though about half still have symptoms or residual apnoea, and the bite can change); skeletal surgery for jaw deficiency; more advanced palatal procedures; tongue base surgery; hypoglossal nerve stimulation; and tracheostomy."],["osec","th:op-53~s8-1","Where the books differ","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra lists the treatments for sleep apnoea, from weight loss, positional therapy, oral appliances and CPAP through palatal surgery (uvulopalatoplasty, UPPP, advancement pharyngoplasty), tongue base surgery, genioglossus advancement, hyoid suspension and maxillomandibular advancement, and calls tracheostomy the gold standard. Lee grades tonsils 0 to 4 and the Friedman palate position I to IV with the tongue resting in the mouth (not protruded, unlike Mallampati, which it says should not be used for sleep apnoea); describes drug-induced sleep endoscopy with the VOTE system; and notes that nasal surgery does not lower the apnoea index but can improve sleep quality and CPAP tolerance. Myers gives the operations step by step, with the Friedman stage success figures (about 81%, 38% and 8%), cutting behind the genu with right-angled corners, avoiding adenoidectomy at the same sitting, and matching the uvulopalatal flap to thin palates and anterior palatoplasty to short ones."],["osec","th:op-53~s8-2","Viva essentials","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The AHI grades and the role of the sleep study . Friedman staging and the matching UPPP success rates. DISE and the VOTE classification. Why you cut behind the genu , and why the corners are square . Why adenoidectomy is not combined with UPPP. UPPP versus uvulopalatal flap versus anterior palatoplasty : which palate suits each. Velopharyngeal insufficiency and nasopharyngeal stenosis . Why a repeat sleep study is needed even if snoring stops."],["osec","th:op-53~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty and Related Modifications · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a Friedman stage III patient and expecting a cure. Missing collapse at the tongue base or epiglottis. Cutting in front of the genu. Rounded corners at the palatal closure. Removing adenoids at the same time. Stripping posterior pillar mucosa. A uvulopalatal flap in a thick palate, or closed under tension. Not repeating the sleep study."],["osec","th:op-56~s1-1","The case at a glance","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Operating note","Hypoglossal nerve stimulation Summary Indications Moderate to severe OSA, not significantly obese, intolerant of or inadequately adherent to PAP or other first-line devices; willing to have an implant; no anticipated need for MRI ; able to use a simple remote control DISE Recommended: anteroposterior palatal collapse responds; complete concentric collapse does poorly Relative contraindications High perioperative risk; anticoagulation; poor nutrition; metal allergy ; occupations with repeated trauma to the neck or chest (contact sports, martial arts, heavy firearms; the generator can be moved away from a rifle stock); concentric collapse; BMI over 32 Device Three implanted parts: a pulse generator in a chest pocket, a stimulation cuff on the distal hypoglossal nerve, a respiratory sensor in an intercostal space facing the pleura Anaesthesia General, oral intubation; videolaryngoscope and fibreoptic available; no long-acting relaxants (nerve monitoring) Position Supine, head turned left, right face, neck and chest exposed; shoulder roll and right trunk roll Monitoring Fine-wire electrodes in genioglossus and hyoglossus Antibiotics A standard intravenous dose before incision Tray Nerve monitor with bipolar stimulating probe; head and neck set (dissection like a parotidectomy); monopolar and bipolar; angled DeBakey forceps and right-angle clamp for the cuff; the three components; sterile telemetry and programmer Skills Neck dissection; submandibular gland excision"],["osec","th:op-56~s1-2","Consent","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to implant a device that moves your tongue forward with each breath during sleep, keeping your airway open; it does not change the anatomy of your throat and can be removed. It is turned on about a month after surgery and adjusted over the following months with a sleep study. About two thirds respond well on sleep studies; more feel better. Bleeding, infection (which may mean removing the device), a collection of fluid. Weakness of the tongue affecting speech or swallowing, or of the lower lip. A collapsed lung (pneumothorax) from the sensor. Tongue soreness , discomfort or disturbed sleep from stimulation. No MRI with this device; avoid heavy lifting with the right arm for 2 to 3 weeks."],["osec","th:op-56~s1-3","Before you start","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Operating note","A full sleep history is the cornerstone: snoring, witnessed apnoeas, gasping, waking, nocturia, morning headache, sleepiness, cognitive problems; when it began and what changed (nasal injury, jaw surgery, weight gain, stroke, change of sleeping position); other sleep disorders (insomnia, restless legs) that confound outcomes; previous treatment and adherence . Excellent PAP use with persisting sleepiness suggests another cause, and surgery may not help. Review the sleep study: home or laboratory; sleep efficiency (poor efficiency suggests unreliable results or insomnia); architecture and continuity (disturbed by drugs, pain or other disease); AHI, RDI, snoring and oxygen saturation; and exclude central apnoea, Cheyne-Stokes respiration and hypoventilation, where surgery may be contraindicated; look for limb movements, seizures and REM behaviour disorder. Device-specific questions Does the patient need regular MRI (multiple sclerosis, a brain tumour)? Any metal allergy ? Previous neck or upper chest surgery or radiotherapy (breast surgery, pectoralis flap, neck dissection)? A job or sport that strikes the neck or chest, or firing heavy guns from the shoulder? Also ask about OSA comorbidities (hypertension, diabetes, myocardial infarction, atrial fibrillation, stroke, depression, reflux, erectile dysfunction), pain syndromes, heart and lung disease, dysphagia, neuromuscular disease, previous nasal, pharyngeal, skeletal or bariatric surgery, anticoagulants, opioids, benzodiazepines, antidepressants, lithium and stimulants, family history, occupation (drivers, pilots and shift "],["osec","th:op-56~s1-4","Examination","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Operating note","Nose: deformity, septum, turbinates, rhinitis, polyps, valve problems; correct nasal obstruction before or with pharyngeal surgery. Face: significant maxillary or mandibular hypoplasia may need orthodontic or orthognathic treatment first; occlusion (Angle class, overjet, overbite). Mouth and oropharynx: tongue scalloping, teeth (for an oral appliance), palatal height and width, tori, soft palate configuration and length, Mallampati or Friedman tongue position, tonsils, lateral walls, uvula. Neck: circumference, hyoid position (low means a longer airway). Chest: deformity, scars. Cranial nerves: XII and VII. Flexible laryngoscopy awake and DISE . Figure 1 Velopharyngeal collapse on DISE: (A) anteroposterior, favourable for stimulation; (B) circumferential, less likely to respond. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 56.1"],["osec","th:op-56~s1-5","Imaging and preparation","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Operating note","Chest radiograph (cardiopulmonary disease, a paralysed hemidiaphragm); facial imaging (cephalogram, orthopantomogram, 3D CT) after jaw surgery or with craniofacial abnormality; sinus imaging if nasal surgery is combined. Consult anaesthesia about a difficult airway; hold or bridge anticoagulants; ask the patient to bring CPAP or an oral appliance."],["osec","th:op-56~s2-1","The physiology","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Anatomy you must see","Pharyngeal afferents run in the superior laryngeal nerve to the nucleus solitarius; efferents run from the hypoglossal nucleus to genioglossus, which normally contracts in response to negative airway pressure. This reflex appears dysfunctional in many with OSA; stimulating the efferent limb restores dilation, and the tongue and palate are coupled through palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus."],["osec","th:op-56~s2-2","Landmarks","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Anatomy you must see","For the cuff: the junction of the digastric tendon with the anteroinferior border of the submandibular gland; the free edge of mylohyoid; the breakpoint between the lateral hypoglossal branches to the retractors (styloglossus, hyoglossus) and the medial branches to the protrusors (genioglossus, geniohyoid); the distal edge of hyoglossus. For the generator: the pectoralis fascia. For the sensor: the fifth or sixth intercostal space, the external intercostal muscle, and the upper border of the lower rib (away from the neurovascular bundle). Figure 2 The implanted system: the pulse generator in the upper chest (asterisk), the cuff electrode on the medial hypoglossal branches (black arrow), and the sensing lead in the intercostal space (white arrow). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 56.2 Figure 3 The hypoglossal branches: the protrusor branches (medial XII, oblique and horizontal genioglossus, geniohyoid) go inside the cuff; the lateral retractor branches (to styloglossus and hyoglossus) stay outside. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 56.5"],["osec","th:op-56~s3-1","Plan the incisions","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · The operation, stage by stage","Usually on the right . Neck: about 3 cm , between the mandibular body and the hyoid in a skin crease, more anterior and superior than a submandibular gland incision . Chest: about 4 cm below the clavicle , centred over pectoralis, medial to the deltopectoral groove so the generator does not restrict the shoulder. Sensor: over the fifth or sixth rib space where the anterior axillary line meets the nipple line. Figure 4 The neck incision (red) in the submandibular triangle, more anterior and superior than for gland excision, ideally in a skin crease. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 56.3 Figure 5 The chest incision about 4 cm below the clavicle, medial to the deltopectoral groove. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 56.4 What you should see Three marked incisions, with the neck mark above the usual submandibular line."],["osec","th:op-56~s3-2","Prepare","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · The operation, stage by stage","Place fine-wire electrodes in genioglossus and hyoglossus; prepare with an iodine-impregnated drape and keep the tongue visible through a clear sterile drape. What you should see The tongue visible through the drape, with electrodes reading."],["osec","th:op-56~s3-3","Find the nerve","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · The operation, stage by stage","Through platysma, expose where the digastric tendon meets the front of the submandibular gland. Retract the gland posterosuperiorly and the free edge of mylohyoid anterosuperiorly to show the hypoglossal nerve on the floor of the triangle; clamp, divide and tie the ranine vein . What you should see The hypoglossal nerve on hyoglossus with its veins, running forward under the mylohyoid."],["osec","th:op-56~s3-4","Place the cuff","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · The operation, stage by stage","With the landmarks and the nerve monitor, include the distal protrusor branches to genioglossus and exclude the retractor branches . Place the cuff around them and fix it to the digastric tendon; include the C1 branch to geniohyoid along the nerve's lower edge if it can be reached. What you should see The tongue stiffening and protruding, with no retraction, when the cuffed branches are stimulated. The wrong branch Including a distal lateral retractor branch in the cuff pulls the tongue back. Check with the monitor and by watching the tongue, and reposition."],["osec","th:op-56~s3-5","The generator pocket","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · The operation, stage by stage","Make a subcutaneous pocket on the pectoralis fascia through the chest incision. What you should see A pocket on the pectoralis fascia that holds the generator snugly."],["osec","th:op-56~s3-6","The respiratory sensor","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · The operation, stage by stage","Through the lateral chest incision, dissect below pectoralis and in front of serratus to the external intercostal muscle. Tunnel between the external and internal intercostals in the fifth or sixth space, insert the sensor facing the pleura , and fix it to the chest wall. What you should see The sensor lying between the two intercostal layers, with no breach of the pleura. The pleura and the bundle Work along the upper border of the lower rib to spare the intercostal neurovascular bundle; a breach of the pleura causes a pneumothorax."],["osec","th:op-56~s3-7","Connect and test","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · The operation, stage by stage","Tunnel both leads to the chest pocket, staying in front of the external jugular vein , connect them to the generator, and fix it to the pectoralis fascia. With the telemetry unit, confirm stimulation of the protrusor branches, a good respiratory waveform, and visible tongue protrusion . Close the three wounds in layers with absorbable sutures, apply Steri-Strips and pressure dressings; no drains . What you should see The tongue coming forward past the teeth on test stimulation, and a clean respiratory trace."],["osec","th:op-56~s4-1","Crisis drills","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · When it goes wrong","Poor tongue protrusion on testing 1 Think a retractor branch inside the cuff, or protrusor branches outside it 2 Act reposition the cuff guided by the monitor and the tongue Bleeding while tunnelling 1 Cause the external jugular vein 2 Prevent tunnel in front of it"],["osec","th:op-56~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls A neck incision too far back, like a gland excision: a long tunnel and poor access to the distal branches. A retractor branch in the cuff. Tunnelling through the external jugular vein. A generator too close to the deltopectoral groove, limiting the shoulder."],["osec","th:op-56~s5-1","After the operation","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Aftercare","Neck and chest radiographs in recovery to document the components. Most go home the same day (no airway surgery, little or no opioid, not obese), on a normal diet, with paracetamol or NSAIDs. Avoid strenuous activity and heavy, repetitive use of the right arm for 2 to 3 weeks ."],["osec","th:op-56~s5-2","Follow-up","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Aftercare","Wound check at 1 to 2 weeks . Activation and programming at about 1 month . A sleep study to assess and titrate at about 2 months (a month after activation). Long-term follow-up of adherence, symptoms and the device."],["osec","th:op-56~s5-3","Complications","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Procedure Bleeding; infection (possibly explanting the device); haematoma or seroma; hypoglossal injury (speech, swallowing); marginal mandibular injury (lower lip); pneumothorax Therapy Tongue abrasion; discomfort or insomnia from stimulation; unfavourable protrusion and poor response"],["ev","th:op-56~s5-4","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy: current evidence","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Aftercare","Multilevel surgery. In the SAMS trial of adults with moderate or severe OSA who had failed CPAP, combined palatal and tongue surgery reduced the apnoea–hypopnoea index from about 48 to 21 and improved sleepiness compared with medical management (MacKay et al., JAMA 2020). Hypoglossal nerve stimulation. In the STAR trial, upper airway stimulation reduced the AHI by about two-thirds at 12 months in selected patients with BMI under about 32 and no complete concentric palatal collapse on drug-induced sleep endoscopy (Strollo et al., NEJM 2014). Selection with drug-induced sleep endoscopy guides which site to treat."],["osec","th:op-56~s5-4","Alternatives","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · Aftercare","PAP or other devices should always remain on offer; upper airway reconstructive surgery; and combinations, especially in severe disease."],["osec","th:op-56~s6-1","Does it last?","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · For the examination","In the multicentre STAR trial , about two thirds met objective responder criteria, more reported better snoring, alertness and sleep-related quality of life, and the results held at 2 and 3 years , with high adherence, very few serious adverse events and no permanent nerve injury. Further work is needed on the ideal phenotype, the procedure and titration."],["osec","th:op-56~s6-2","Viva essentials","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The negative pressure reflex and why stimulating XII works on the palate too. Selection: moderate to severe OSA, BMI, PAP failure, DISE pattern, MRI. The three components and where each goes. The protrusor and retractor branches; nerve monitoring; C1. Landmarks for the sensor and how to avoid the pleura and bundle. Activation at 1 month, titration study at 2 months. STAR outcomes."],["osec","th:op-56~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation Therapy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Implanting a patient with concentric palatal collapse. Missing central apnoea on the sleep study. Forgetting the MRI restriction."],["osec","th:op-60~s1-1","The case at a glance","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · Operating note","Fine-needle aspiration biopsy Summary Indications Any palpable neck mass, solid or cystic, in adults and children; parotid and submandibular masses (especially with pain, facial weakness or numbness); thyroid nodules meeting ultrasound criteria; palpable subcutaneous masses of the face and scalp; non-palpable lesions under ultrasound or CT guidance Contraindications None absolute; anticoagulation increases bruising, so press for longer; even vascular masses (carotid body tumour) can be sampled with a fine needle and pressure, though many surgeons avoid them, especially if anticoagulated Anaesthesia None, or 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline to the skin (buffered 1 in 10 with bicarbonate for children) Position Seated with a headrest; for the thyroid, neck extended on a pillow; for nodes and parotid, head turned so the mass is most superficial Antibiotics None; an alcohol swab is enough Tray 20 ml syringe with 1 ml of air in it, syringe holder (optional), 21- to 25-gauge needles 1 to 1.5 inches long, labelled glass slides (name on the frosted end), 95% alcohol spray or jar fixative, balanced salt solution (not saline) for needle rinses, alcohol swabs, gauze, plaster"],["osec","th:op-60~s1-2","Consent","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · Operating note","What to tell the patient A few sharp scratches, usually tolerable without local anaesthetic. Bruising or a small bleed , more likely on anticoagulants. Infection , rare. An inconclusive result in roughly 1 in 10, which may need a repeat aspiration, a core biopsy, an ultrasound-guided sample or an open biopsy. A faint can happen in any clinic procedure; lie down if you feel unwell."],["osec","th:op-60~s1-3","Before you start","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · Operating note","Ask when the lump appeared and how it has changed, and about pain, numbness, voice change and facial weakness; lumps elsewhere; previous radiation; previous biopsies, cancer treatment or drainage of a neck cyst; travel and infection history (tuberculosis); and anticoagulants. Feel the mass: size, consistency, tenderness, fluctuance, skin change, mobility and its relation to the carotid. A palpable mass needs no imaging first; non-palpable masses and most thyroid nodules are sampled under ultrasound."],["osec","th:op-60~s2-1","Neck nodes","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · What the result means","FNA gives a specific diagnosis in 82% to 96% of lymphadenopathy. Cell blocks allow immunohistochemistry and PCR, and flow cytometry on a needle rinse can diagnose lymphoma, often avoiding an open biopsy (at least two passes are needed for lymphoma work-up). An excision biopsy is still needed when lymphoma typing requires architecture or gene rearrangement studies. The cystic neck mass in an adult A cystic lateral neck mass in an adult with squamous cells on aspiration is a cystic metastasis of squamous cell carcinoma until proven otherwise, usually from the tonsil or tongue base (often HPV-related). It must not be called a branchial cyst."],["osec","th:op-60~s2-2","Salivary glands","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · What the result means","FNA separates benign from malignant salivary lesions with 90% to 95% accuracy, but it is unreliable for the malignant subtype and grade. Low-grade cancers such as acinic cell carcinoma can be read as benign or non-neoplastic, and low-grade lymphoma can be reported as inflammation. A benign result with a normal examination (for example a Warthin tumour in an older smoker) can support observation; a benign result with suspicious clinical or radiological features calls for a repeat FNA or excision."],["osec","th:op-60~s2-3","Thyroid","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · What the result means","Ultrasound pattern and size decide which nodules to aspirate (American Thyroid Association 2015). Thyroid nodules: when to aspirate Ultrasound pattern Features Estimated malignancy risk Aspirate if High suspicion Solid hypoechoic (or a solid hypoechoic part of a cystic nodule) with irregular margins, microcalcifications, taller-than-wide shape, rim calcification with extrusion, or extrathyroidal extension 70% to 90% 1 cm or more Intermediate Hypoechoic solid with smooth margins and none of the above 10% to 20% 1 cm or more Low Isoechoic or hyperechoic solid, or partly cystic with eccentric solid areas, none of the above 5% to 10% 1.5 cm or more Very low Spongiform or partly cystic without suspicious features Below 3% Consider at 2 cm or more, or observe Benign Purely cystic Below 1% Do not biopsy (drain only for symptoms) Results are reported by the Bethesda system : Bethesda categories Category Risk of malignancy I Non-diagnostic or unsatisfactory 1% to 4% II Benign 0% to 3% III Atypia or follicular lesion of undetermined significance 5% to 15% IV Follicular neoplasm or suspicious for one 15% to 30% V Suspicious for malignancy 60% to 75% VI Malignant 97% to 99% FNA cannot distinguish a follicular adenoma from a follicular carcinoma : the difference is capsular or vascular invasion, which only histology shows, so a follicular neoplasm needs a diagnostic lobectomy or molecular testing."],["osec","th:op-60~s3-1","Prepare and position","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Write the patient's name on the frosted end of each slide and lay them out with the fixative before you start. Position the patient comfortably with the head supported. For a node, turn the head so it is superficial; pushing from behind the sternocleidomastoid may bring a deep node forward. For the thyroid, extend the neck; you may stand on the opposite side. Clean the skin with an alcohol swab and, if wished, raise a small bleb of local anaesthetic. Figure 1 The equipment: slides, fixative, a 20 ml syringe in a holder and a 22- to 25-gauge needle. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 60.2 What you should see Labelled slides and fixative within reach, and the mass made as superficial as possible."],["osec","th:op-60~s3-2","Fix the mass","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Hold the mass between the thumb and index finger of your non-dominant hand so it cannot roll away; stretching the skin over it also reduces the pain. In the lateral neck, know where the carotid bulb pulses and keep the needle away from it. Figure 2 The patient seated against a headrest; the surgeon fixes the mass and makes short passes through it until material appears in the hub. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 60.1 What you should see A mass that does not move when you press it, and the carotid pulse located."],["osec","th:op-60~s3-3","Enter and aspirate","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","With 1 ml of air in the syringe (to help expel the sample), insert the needle into the mass, then pull back on the plunger to create a vacuum. Move the needle rapidly in and out within the mass in short strokes of 2 to 3 mm , 15 to 20 times, without changing its direction (redirection hurts and tears vessels) and without leaving the mass (which loses the vacuum). Watch the hub : as soon as material appears, release the vacuum, and only then withdraw the needle. Figure 3 The needle moved in and out of the mass in short vibratory strokes of 2 to 3 mm. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 60.3 What you should see A little material in the hub of the needle, not a syringe full of blood. The bloody sample Strong suction and long passes fill the needle with blood, which dilutes the cells and hides them. If blood appears, reduce or stop suction and continue passes, or switch to the non-aspiration technique. Senior's pearl Release the vacuum before you pull out. Withdrawing under suction sucks the sample up into the syringe barrel, where it is lost."],["osec","th:op-60~s3-4","Non-aspiration (capillary) technique","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","For thyroid nodules and vascular lesions where suction yields only blood, use the needle alone or on a syringe without suction: the same short strokes fill the needle by capillary action, giving a less bloody, more cellular sample. What you should see Material rising into the needle hub by itself, with little blood."],["osec","th:op-60~s3-5","Make the slides","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Detach the needle, fill the syringe with air, reattach, and with the bevel touching the slide, expel the contents onto two slides. Lay the second slide on the first and draw them apart to make a thin smear on each. Fix immediately with 95% alcohol spray held 20 to 25 cm from the slide (or drop into fixative); air-dried smears lose detail. Rinse the needle in balanced salt solution (not saline) for flow cytometry, microbiology or a cell block. Repeat for three or four passes . What you should see Thin, evenly spread smears fixed within seconds, and a needle rinse in the pot. Air drying Slides left in the air before fixing develop drying artefact that makes them unreadable."],["osec","th:op-60~s3-6","Finish","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Press firmly on the site for a minute (several minutes if anticoagulated) and apply a plaster. If a cytologist can assess adequacy while the patient is still there, take further passes for an inadequate sample. What you should see No swelling at the site after a minute of pressure, and labelled, fixed slides with the request form."],["osec","th:op-60~s4-1","Scoring a specimen","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · Adequacy","Specimen inadequacy in ultrasound-guided FNA averages about 9% overall and about 6% with a cytopathologist on site; surgeons' adequacy improves markedly with experience (from about 71% to 94% in one series) but never reaches 100%. A semi-quantitative score (developed for thyroid) adds three grades of 0 to 3: background (blood versus watery colloid), cellularity (fewer than 8 to 10 cellular fragments up to abundant fragments of at least 50 cells on every slide) and preservation (artefact versus well preserved). A total of 4 or less is inadequate (repeat), 5 inferior, 6 to 7 satisfactory and 8 to 9 superior."],["osec","th:op-60~s5-1","Crisis drills","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · When it goes wrong","Non-diagnostic result 1 Repeat a second FNA, ideally under ultrasound with on-site cytology 2 Change technique core needle biopsy when architecture or more tissue is needed 3 Escalate open biopsy after two or more non-diagnostic aspirates A reassuring result that does not fit the clinical picture 1 Do not be reassured a single negative FNA does not outweigh suspicious clinical or radiological findings 2 Act repeat FNA, core biopsy or excision for a neck node in a smoker, examine the upper aerodigestive tract and consider panendoscopy The patient faints 1 Act lie flat, raise the legs, maintain the airway observe until recovered 2 Next time sample with the patient lying down"],["ev","th:op-60~s6-1","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck: current evidence","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · Aftercare","Reporting systems. Thyroid aspirates are reported with the Bethesda system (2023 edition) and salivary aspirates with the Milan system, each giving a risk of malignancy for every category and linking it to management. Ultrasound guidance improves adequacy; core biopsy gives a higher diagnostic yield than FNA when lymphoma is suspected or FNA is non-diagnostic, with a very low risk of tumour seeding. Cystic neck nodes in adults are metastatic HPV-positive oropharyngeal cancer until proven otherwise; p16 and HPV testing can be done on the aspirate."],["osec","th:op-60~s6-1","After the aspiration","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · Aftercare","A plaster is enough. The commonest complication is a non-diagnostic sample ; haematoma and infection are uncommon. Arrange review with the result, and always correlate the cytology with the clinical findings."],["osec","th:op-60~s7-1","Where the books differ","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra places FNA in the work-up of a neck mass after a full ENT examination, and lists excision biopsy of a node only after the primary site has been sought, to avoid seeding and compromising later neck dissection. Lee emphasises ultrasound-guided FNA, the Bethesda system for thyroid cytology and the rule that a cystic neck mass in an adult is malignant until proven otherwise. Myers gives the detailed technique (short strokes, watching the hub, releasing the vacuum, immediate fixation, balanced salt rinse), adequacy scoring and the accuracy figures by site."],["osec","th:op-60~s7-2","Viva essentials","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Technique: fix the mass, short strokes, watch the hub, release the vacuum before withdrawing , fix at once. Why the sample is bloody and how to avoid it (less suction, non-aspiration technique). Bethesda categories and their risks; why FNA cannot diagnose follicular carcinoma. The cystic lateral neck mass in an adult. Salivary FNA: good for benign versus malignant, poor for subtype and grade. What to do with a non-diagnostic or discordant result."],["osec","th:op-60~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Office-Based Procedures: Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy of a Mass in the Head and Neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls Strong suction producing a blood-filled syringe. Redirecting the needle within the mass. Pulling out under vacuum and losing the sample. Air-dried smears. Rinsing the needle in saline instead of balanced salt solution for flow cytometry. Accepting a benign result that does not fit the clinical picture."],["osec","th:op-64~s1-1","The case at a glance","Selective Neck Dissection · Operating note","Selective neck dissection Summary Indications A clinically negative neck with a risk of hidden metastasis above about 15% to 20% when the primary is treated surgically; a transcervical approach to the primary; vessels for a free flap; N1 disease in the first-echelon levels; low-volume N2 disease without fixation; limited residual disease after radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy Contraindications Poor surgical candidate; primary treated without surgery; unresectable primary; fixed nodes; distant metastases Anaesthesia General; avoid paralysis until the nerves at risk are found; agree with the anaesthetist on duration, blood loss and monitoring Position Table turned 90 to 180 degrees; supine, head turned away; a moderate shoulder roll (not hyperextended, to avoid neck pain) Antibiotics Not needed for an isolated neck dissection; guided by the approach to the primary Monitoring Not routine for an isolated neck dissection Imaging CT or MRI of the neck and primary within 30 to 60 days; nodes suspicious by size, shape, irregular enhancement or clustering; ultrasound-guided FNA if it would change the plan; nodes under 1 cm cannot be reliably judged Tray Neck dissection set; vascular sutures for jugular injury; nerve hooks and loops; headlight and loupes; bipolar, monopolar and harmonic or vessel-sealing devices; closed suction drains Before theatre Medical risk assessment; plan the reconstruction of the primary if needed; look for a second primary"],["osec","th:op-64~s1-2","Consent","Selective Neck Dissection · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: removing the lymph nodes from the parts of the neck most likely to hide cancer spread, keeping the main nerve, vein and muscle; the results decide whether you need further treatment. Shoulder weakness and pain , from stretching of the nerve to the shoulder muscle; usually recovers with physiotherapy. Numbness of the neck skin and ear lobe. Weakness of the lower lip (marginal nerve), and less often of the tongue (hypoglossal nerve) or tongue numbness (lingual nerve), when the upper levels are cleared. A milky leak (chyle) from the lymph duct at the base of the neck, mainly on the left. Bleeding , a collection under the skin, infection, swelling of the face and neck, and slow healing, especially after radiotherapy. Hoarseness and low calcium if the central nodes are removed. Rare: a drooping eyelid (Horner syndrome), a paralysed diaphragm, swallowing difficulty."],["osec","th:op-64~s1-3","Before you start","Selective Neck Dissection · Operating note","Examine the primary fully, and look for a second primary . Feel the neck for nodes (accuracy depends on build), noting their level and mobility. Plan which levels to remove from the primary site, the stage, and whether the neck is clinically negative or positive."],["osec","th:op-64~s2-1","The levels","Selective Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","Neck levels and their boundaries Level Nodes Boundaries Ia Submental Between the anterior bellies of digastric, from the mentum to the hyoid Ib Submandibular Mandible above, anterior and posterior bellies of digastric; includes the gland and its node groups IIa and IIb Upper jugular Skull base to hyoid; front edge at the stylohyoid; back at the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid; the accessory nerve divides IIa (in front) from IIb (behind) III Middle jugular Hyoid to the lower border of the cricoid; front edge at the sternohyoid; back at the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid IV Lower jugular Cricoid to the clavicle; same front and back limits Va and Vb Posterior triangle Between sternocleidomastoid, trapezius and clavicle; a horizontal plane at the lower cricoid divides Va (accessory nodes) above from Vb (supraclavicular and transverse cervical nodes) below VI Central Hyoid to suprasternal notch, between the carotid sheaths: prelaryngeal, pretracheal and paratracheal nodes VII and others Superior mediastinal, retropharyngeal, parotid, postauricular, occipital Named by site"],["osec","th:op-64~s2-2","Which levels for which primary","Selective Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","Levels at risk by primary site Level Primary sites Ia Lower lip, front of tongue, anterior mandibular alveolus Ib Oral cavity, front of the nasal cavity, midface skin, submandibular gland II Oral cavity, nasal cavity, nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx, larynx (IIb is involved in under 5% of negative necks and is usually left in elective dissection) III Oral cavity, nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx, larynx IV Larynx, hypopharynx, thyroid, cervical oesophagus V Nasopharynx, oropharynx, skin of the back of the scalp and neck VI Thyroid, glottis and subglottis, hypopharynx, cervical oesophagus The common selective dissections Name Levels Usual use Supraomohyoid I to III (I to IV extended) Oral cavity cancer Lateral II to IV Oropharynx, hypopharynx and larynx Posterolateral II to V with suboccipital and postauricular nodes Skin cancer behind the ear canal (back of the ear, scalp) Central compartment VI Thyroid, glottis and subglottis, piriform sinus, cervical oesophagus, trachea"],["osec","th:op-64~s2-3","Layers and structures","Selective Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","The dissection removes the fatty node-bearing tissue between the superficial and deep layers of deep cervical fascia ; the deep layer stays on the floor of the neck to protect the phrenic nerve and brachial plexus . Erb's point at the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid marks the accessory nerve; the transverse process of the atlas is a palpable guide to the nerve near the jugular vein. The accessory nerve enters the underside of the trapezius about 1 cm from its edge, which tells it from cervical plexus branches. The thoracic duct (left) and right lymphatic duct enter at the base of the neck near the jugular-subclavian junction. The sympathetic trunk lies deep and medial to the carotid; injury causes Horner syndrome."],["osec","th:op-64~s3-1","Incision and flaps","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Plan a transverse incision in a skin crease or a utility incision. Find the platysma before cutting it: its back edge guides you to the external jugular vein and great auricular nerve . Raise flaps just deep to the platysma (an avascular plane that keeps those structures and the fascia on the sternocleidomastoid), to the mandible (level I), the posterior belly of digastric (II), the omohyoid (III), the clavicle (IV), the trapezius and clavicle (V), and the sternal notch and hyoid (VI). Where the platysma is absent, continue in a subcutaneous plane. What you should see Flaps with the platysma on them, the external jugular vein and great auricular nerve lying intact on the sternocleidomastoid."],["osec","th:op-64~s3-2","Level I","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Clear the submental triangle (Ia) between the anterior bellies from the mentum to the hyoid, including the tissue between and beneath them. For Ib , open the gland's investing fascia near the angle of the mandible, where the marginal mandibular nerve leaves the parotid tail; skeletonise it and lift it above the mandible. Then free the gland fascia from the mandible, tie the facial artery and vein, and pull level I down and back while clearing tissue off the anterior digastric and mylohyoid. Tie the vessels to the mylohyoid, retract its back edge forward, and find the lingual nerve , the ganglion, the duct and the hypoglossal nerve on the hyoglossus. With both nerves seen, divide the ganglion branches and the duct as far forward as possible (to include accessory gland tissue along the duct). Figure 1 Level Ib dissection: the marginal nerve lifted with the facial vessels, the mylohyoid retracted, and the lingual nerve, ganglion, duct and hypoglossal nerve exposed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 64.1 The marginal mandibular nerve Its height below the mandible varies. Find it where it leaves the parotid tail near the angle, and keep it in the flap above the mandible before you work on the gland. What you should see The marginal nerve lifted in the flap above the mandible. With the mylohyoid retracted: the lingual nerve looping down to the ganglion, the duct below it, and the hypoglossal nerve with its veins on the hyoglossus. Bare anterior digastric bellies and mylohyoid when level I is cleared. Senior's pearl The mylohyoid's free posterior edge is the door to the deep p"],["osec","th:op-64~s3-3","Levels II to IV: the front of sternocleidomastoid and the accessory nerve","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Lift the fascia off the whole front edge of the sternocleidomastoid, keeping the great auricular nerve, and retract the muscle back to clear its deep surface. (Tie the external jugular vein high if you need to, leaving length for a free flap anastomosis.) Find the accessory nerve where it enters the sternocleidomastoid, handling it gently. Clear the lower border of the posterior belly of digastric and lift it to show the nerve crossing the jugular vein. Figure 2 Levels II and III: the sternocleidomastoid retracted, the accessory nerve, internal jugular vein, great auricular nerve and omohyoid exposed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 64.2 What you should see The accessory nerve crossing in front of the jugular vein (usually) under the digastric, and running into the sternocleidomastoid."],["osec","th:op-64~s3-4","Level IIb, when included","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","For a positive neck, retract the digastric and stylohyoid up and the sternocleidomastoid back, skeletonise the accessory nerve from the jugular vein to the muscle, and clear the fat of IIb from the muscular floor, using cautery in the upper corner but a fine dissector and bipolar near the nerve and vein. Pass the IIb contents under the nerve to join IIa. For an elective dissection, the upper limit of IIa is below where the nerve crosses the vein. Figure 3 Level IIb identified (A) and its fatty tissue freed from the accessory nerve and the muscular floor (B). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 64.3 The accessory nerve Traction and heat injure it as surely as cutting. Shoulder dysfunction is the commonest lasting problem after neck dissection; handle the nerve gently and use bipolar near it. What you should see The accessory nerve skeletonised from the jugular vein to the sternocleidomastoid, with clean splenius and levator scapulae fascia behind it and the IIb fat passed beneath the nerve."],["osec","th:op-64~s3-5","The floor of the neck","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Retract the sternocleidomastoid back and, with the assistant pulling the specimen forward with a swab, cut through the fat beneath the muscle to the muscular floor , keeping the deep fascia intact on the scalenes and levator scapulae. Its back limit is the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid and the cervical plexus branches, which are kept. Divide the posterior belly of omohyoid to clear level III and the jugulo-omohyoid node , which belongs in level III and IV dissections. The transverse cervical vessels lie on the fascia and can be kept. Figure 4 Levels IIa, III and IV: the floor of the neck with the cervical nerves, phrenic nerve on the scalene, the jugular vein, carotid, omohyoid, transverse cervical artery and thoracic duct. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 64.4 What you should see A clean muscular floor covered by its fascia, with the phrenic nerve running down on the scalenus anterior and the cervical rootlets intact."],["osec","th:op-64~s3-6","The jugular vein and the lower limit","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Before opening the carotid sheath, define the lowest point of the dissection: expose the lateral wall of the internal jugular vein low down, then clamp and tie the fatty tissue lateral to it for 2 to 3 cm toward the sternocleidomastoid. This is where the thoracic duct and its branches run, so do not rely on cautery alone. Expose the vein's lateral wall at the top as well, so it cannot twist. Then, with the assistant pulling the contents forward and you holding countertraction, open the sheath sharply along the whole length of the vein with a size 10 blade, tying its branches, and free the tissue from the lateral border of the sternohyoid. Detach the upper belly of omohyoid from the hyoid if it is in the specimen. The twisted jugular vein A vein that twists while you strip the sheath hides its wall, and you can cut into it. Expose its lateral wall at the top and bottom before you start along it. Senior's pearl Traction and countertraction do most of the work. With the specimen pulled forward and the vein held back, a sharp blade clears the jugular chain cleanly and quickly; poor tension leads to tearing and bleeding. What you should see The blue lateral wall of the vein exposed top and bottom, then the vein, vagus and carotid lying clean in the sheath as the specimen comes forward. A dry lower end with no clear or milky fluid welling up."],["osec","th:op-64~s3-7","Level V, when included","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Expose the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid, the clavicle and the front edge of trapezius. Find the accessory nerve at the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid, skeletonise it across the posterior triangle to where it runs under the trapezius edge for 1 to 2 cm before entering it, and bring the level V contents under the nerve . Clear them off splenius capitis, levator scapulae and the scalenes, leaving the deep fascia to protect the brachial plexus; if levels II to IV are also being removed, pass level V under the sternocleidomastoid to come out with them. Figure 5 The accessory nerve identified (A) and skeletonised, with level V brought beneath it (B). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 64.5 What you should see The accessory nerve leaving the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid about 1 cm above the point where the great auricular nerve curls round it (Erb's point), running obliquely down to the trapezius. Deep fascia intact over the brachial plexus and phrenic nerve in the floor. The floor Stay superficial to the prevertebral fascia: beneath it lie the brachial plexus between the scalenes and the phrenic nerve on scalenus anterior. Low in the triangle the transverse cervical vessels and, on the left, the thoracic duct are at risk. Senior's pearl The cervical plexus branches run superficial to the nerve and are thinner; if in doubt, stimulate. The accessory nerve makes the trapezius twitch."],["osec","th:op-64~s3-8","Level VI, when included","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Define the carotid sheaths laterally, the cricoid above, the trachea medially and the innominate artery below. Find and protect both recurrent laryngeal nerves and the parathyroids (frozen section and autotransplantation separate a parathyroid from a node), skeletonise the nerve through the compartment, and remove the tissue deep to it as well. What you should see Both recurrent nerves traced through the compartment, the trachea and oesophagus bare medially, the carotids laterally, and the innominate artery at the lower limit on the right. Parathyroids pink on their pedicles, or reimplanted. Nerve and calcium A bilateral central dissection puts both recurrent nerves and all four parathyroids at risk in one operation. The right nerve runs obliquely and has nodes deep to it; the left runs in the groove. Autotransplant any gland whose supply is in doubt."],["osec","th:op-64~s3-9","Finish","Selective Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the specimen into its levels, label them, and send them separately. Inspect for bleeding and chyle while the anaesthetist does a Valsalva (or presses firmly on the abdomen). Place closed suction drains in the lowest part of the bed, on wall suction while you close so they do not clot; close the platysma and subcutaneous layers with interrupted absorbable sutures, switch to bulb suction to confirm an airtight seal, then close the skin with nylon, Prolene or staples. What you should see A dry bed with no chyle on Valsalva, the specimen labelled by level, and an airtight closure."],["osec","th:op-64~s4-1","Crisis drills","Selective Neck Dissection · When it goes wrong","Chyle in the wound during surgery 1 Act find the leak with Valsalva or abdominal pressure clip, clamp or tie it seal lymphatics with a vessel-sealing device reinforce with a local muscle flap or fibrin glue 2 Check repeat the Valsalva until it stays dry Milky drain output after surgery 1 Recognise rising, milky output, especially once feeding starts (confirm with triglycerides) 2 Act low-fat or medium-chain triglyceride diet, pressure dressing, keep the drain consider octreotide 3 Escalate re-explore for a high-output leak that does not settle A hole in the jugular vein 1 Act press with a finger or swab, head down slightly to avoid air embolism control above and below 2 Repair close with fine vascular suture, or tie the vein if needed (one side only) Shoulder weakness and pain afterwards 1 Likely accessory nerve neuropraxia from traction 2 Act early shoulder physiotherapy from day 1 or 2 review recovery"],["osec","th:op-64~s5-1","After the operation","Selective Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Ointment to the incision; measure and record drain output; remove drains when the output falls. Shoulder physiotherapy from the first or second day. Review the pathology by level: it decides adjuvant radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-64~s5-2","Complications","Selective Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Complications of selective neck dissection Complication Notes Shoulder dysfunction, pain, capsulitis Accessory nerve traction; physiotherapy Chyle leak Thoracic duct (left) or right lymphatic duct Haematoma, infection, sialocele Haemostasis, drains Facial and neck swelling Especially bilateral dissections Flap necrosis, poor healing Previously irradiated neck Marginal mandibular weakness Level Ib Tongue weakness or numbness, slurred speech Hypoglossal or lingual nerve, level Ib Hoarseness, low calcium Recurrent nerve and parathyroids, level VI Skin and ear lobe numbness Cervical plexus, great auricular nerve Horner syndrome Sympathetic trunk Diaphragm paralysis Phrenic nerve, if the deep fascia is breached Dysphagia Extensive or bilateral dissection"],["ev","th:op-64~s5-3","Selective Neck Dissection: current evidence","Selective Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Classification. The AAO-HNS classification describes neck dissection by the levels and structures removed, with sublevels (Robbins et al., Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2008). Selective neck dissection for the clinically node-negative neck removes the levels at risk for the primary site (I to III for oral cavity, II to IV for oropharynx, larynx and hypopharynx); level IIb can often be spared in N0 laryngeal and oropharyngeal cancer, protecting the accessory nerve. Evidence. Elective dissection improved survival in early oral cancer (D'Cruz et al., NEJM 2015), and sentinel node biopsy is a non-inferior alternative in early oral cancer (Gupta et al., Oral Oncol 2022). After chemoradiation, PET-CT at 12 weeks avoids most planned neck dissections (Mehanna et al., NEJM 2016)."],["osec","th:op-64~s5-3","Alternatives and evidence","Selective Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Radiotherapy, or observation, for a negative neck in a poor surgical candidate. Selective dissections grew from the finding that nodes spread along predictable routes from each primary site (Shah's analysis of over 1,000 radical neck dissections), and repeated studies have shown regional control comparable to comprehensive dissection for the negative neck, and for selected positive necks, with better shoulder function and appearance."],["osec","th:op-64~s6-1","Where the books differ","Selective Neck Dissection · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra classifies neck dissections into radical, modified radical (type I keeps the accessory nerve; type II also the jugular vein; type III also the sternocleidomastoid), selective (supraomohyoid I to III for oral cavity, lateral II to IV, posterolateral II to V with postauricular and occipital nodes, anterior compartment VI) and extended; defines selective as keeping one or more node groups and all three non-lymphatic structures. Lee in thyroid cancer, recommends a systematic lateral (levels II to IV, with V if imaging shows disease) dissection for biopsy-proven lateral nodes rather than picking out single nodes, which lowers nodal recurrence and reoperation; and uses a level II to III selective dissection to open the field and sample nodes in carotid body tumour surgery. Myers gives the level boundaries, the levels at risk by primary site (including the low rate in IIb), the level-by-level technique, the lower limit tie for the thoracic duct, the steps to stop the jugular vein twisting, and the labelled, level-by-level specimen."],["osec","th:op-64~s6-2","Viva essentials","Selective Neck Dissection · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The levels and sublevels and their boundaries (what divides IIa from IIb, Va from Vb). The classification of neck dissections and the names of the selective types. Which levels for which primary , and why IIb is usually spared in a negative neck. The threshold for elective neck treatment (occult risk over 15% to 20%). Finding and protecting the accessory nerve (Erb's point, the atlas, entry into trapezius). The thoracic duct and the management of a chyle leak. Why the deep cervical fascia is left on the floor."],["osec","th:op-64~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Selective Neck Dissection · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not finding the marginal nerve before clearing level Ib. Dividing the duct before seeing the lingual and hypoglossal nerves. Traction on the accessory nerve. Stripping the deep fascia off the floor. Cautery alone at the lower limit near the thoracic duct. Letting the jugular vein twist. Sending the specimen without labelling the levels. Leaving drains that clot during closure."],["osec","th:op-65~s1-1","The case at a glance","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Operating note","Modified radical neck dissection Summary Definition Removal of all nodes in levels I to V, keeping one or more of the sternocleidomastoid, internal jugular vein and accessory nerve (Dhingra's types I, II and III keep one, two and three of them) Indications Nodal disease too extensive for a selective dissection where one or more of the three structures can be kept without compromising cancer control; level V is included only with clinical or radiological disease there, or for nasopharyngeal and some oropharyngeal primaries Contraindications A clinically negative neck (selective dissection instead); disease encasing the carotid, invading the deep neck muscles or skull base; distant metastases; unfit for anaesthesia Anaesthesia General, without paralysis so that nerves can be stimulated; the ENT surgeon present at intubation to guide the airway or provide a surgical one Position Supine, head extended and turned away, shoulder support; table turned 90 degrees (180 for bilateral) Antibiotics No clear benefit in clean neck dissection (a trend toward fewer infections with them); needed if the upper aerodigestive tract is opened Monitoring A facial nerve monitor can help find the marginal mandibular nerve in level I Imaging Chest imaging for metastases and lung primaries; CT to stage and to predict involvement of the three structures; PET-CT for stage III and IV disease (changed management in about 14% in one trial); MRI for perineural spread; angiography with balloon occlusion if carotid resection may be needed Tray Head and neck set; bipolar and monopolar diathermy; 2-0 and 3-0 s"],["osec","th:op-65~s1-2","Consent","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: removing all the lymph nodes on one side of the neck, keeping the muscle, vein and shoulder nerve where the cancer allows; the final decision about each may be made during the operation. Shoulder pain and weakness, a dropped shoulder and a winged shoulder blade if the shoulder nerve has to be removed, and sometimes even when it is kept. Neck asymmetry and a hollow if the muscle is removed. Numbness of the neck and ear. Weakness of the lower lip, tongue or voice , swallowing difficulty, and a drooping eyelid, depending on the nerves affected. A chyle leak from the lymph duct at the base of the neck. Bleeding , infection, swelling of the face (especially if both jugular veins are removed), skin flap breakdown, and a salivary leak if the mouth or throat is also operated on. Stroke, blood clots in the legs or lungs, and death are rare but real. Further treatment , such as radiotherapy, may be needed."],["osec","th:op-65~s1-3","Before you start","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Operating note","1.3.1 The history The primary site , which decides the levels at risk: floor-of-mouth cancer rarely reaches level V, nasopharyngeal cancer often does. Symptoms of the primary; stiffness of the neck; breathlessness (could be phrenic involvement, or lung disease from smoking); numb tongue; taste change; swallowing and voice change; weight loss . Previous treatment, cardiovascular disease, stroke, carotid disease, previous carotid or anterior spinal surgery. Antiplatelets and supplements; opioid use (chronic pain, methadone or buprenorphine); alcohol (delirium tremens at 48 to 72 hours); smoking. 1.3.2 The examination Document each involved level , node size and fixation to skin or to the deep muscles. Flexible nasendoscopy of the primary; listen for carotid bruits. Cranial nerves and others invaded by tumour: lower face (marginal nerve), vocal fold and palate (vagus), tongue deviation toward the affected side (hypoglossal), shoulder drop and trapezius wasting (accessory), Horner syndrome (sympathetic trunk), the diaphragm (phrenic, on chest X-ray), and arm power and sensation (brachial plexus). Figure 1 Massive neck nodes, with CT showing the carotid encased and suggesting invasion of the sternocleidomastoid and deep muscles. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 65.1"],["osec","th:op-65~s2-1","The levels","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","Level Ia (submental), Ib (submandibular), II (upper jugular, divided into IIa and IIb by the accessory nerve), III (middle jugular, hyoid to cricoid), IV (lower jugular, cricoid to clavicle), V (posterior triangle, divided at the cricoid into Va and Vb). See the selective neck dissection chapter for the full boundaries. Figure 2 The levels of the neck nodes. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 65.2"],["osec","th:op-65~s2-2","Landmarks","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","The angle of the mandible , mastoid tip , midline, both borders of the sternocleidomastoid and the clavicle. Marginal mandibular nerve: find it over the facial vessels , where the facial artery pulse and the antegonial notch can be felt at the lower border of the mandible; tie the facial vein below the nerve and lift the tissue up into the flap (protecting the nerve and taking the perifacial nodes). Accessory nerve in the posterior triangle: follow the great auricular nerve back to the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid (Erb's point); the accessory nerve appears about 1 cm above it, superficially, because there is no platysma over the posterior triangle. The phrenic nerve on scalenus anterior and the brachial plexus lie under the deep fascia, which is left intact. The thoracic duct at the base of the left neck (on the right in about 5%). The vagus , carotid and sympathetic trunk lie behind and medial to the jugular vein: keep the plane of dissection on the vein."],["osec","th:op-65~s3-1","Incision and flaps","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Prepare from beyond the midline to behind the sternocleidomastoid, from above the mandible to below the clavicle, including the mastoid tip. Mark a hockey-stick (Lahey) incision from the mastoid tip down along the front of the sternocleidomastoid, curving forward toward the midline at about the cricoid, in a skin crease. Hold the upper flap up with sharp hooks and raise it under the platysma , leaving the cervical plexus and external jugular vein down; take a cuff of platysma (or skin) with any node stuck to it. Find and protect the marginal mandibular nerve over the facial vessels, then continue the flap back to the trapezius and down to the clavicle and sternal notch, watching for the accessory nerve in the posterior triangle. What you should see The marginal nerve lifted safely in the upper flap, and the accessory nerve identified about 1 cm above Erb's point."],["osec","th:op-65~s3-2","Level I","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Find the opposite anterior belly of digastric , incise the fascia on its inner side and sweep the submental fat laterally over the near anterior belly and the mylohyoid. Retract the mylohyoid forward and the gland down, and sweep the specimen down with a swab to show the lingual nerve and ganglion; clamp and tie the ganglion branches, then the duct , as far forward as possible. Tie the facial artery again behind the gland, and continue back over the posterior belly of digastric, so that level I hangs on level II. What you should see Both anterior bellies and the mylohyoid bare, the lingual nerve released and sprung upward once the ganglion is divided, the hypoglossal nerve on the hyoglossus, and level I hanging on level II. The lingual and hypoglossal nerves Clamp the ganglion and the duct only after you have seen both nerves; a clamp placed blindly behind the mylohyoid takes the lingual nerve with the duct."],["osec","th:op-65~s3-3","The top of the neck","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Follow the posterior belly of digastric back to the sternocleidomastoid. Find the accessory nerve passing deep to the muscle and over the jugular vein. If it is kept, free the tissue over it back beneath the sternocleidomastoid to the trapezius, and free the inner surface of the muscle above the nerve toward the mastoid tip. If the jugular vein is to be removed, lift the digastric, free the vein, clamp it, tie it twice with 2-0 silk and add a suture ligature . If the sternocleidomastoid is to be removed, detach it from the mastoid and fold it down. If the accessory nerve is to be removed, clamp and tie it at the digastric. Figure 3 After a right level I to IV dissection keeping the submandibular gland, jugular vein and accessory nerve: the cervical rootlets, phrenic nerve, brachial plexus, carotid, vagus and great auricular nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 65.4 The jugular stump A silk tie alone can slip off the upper or lower jugular stump, with catastrophic bleeding. Always tie twice and add a suture ligature. What you should see The posterior belly of digastric as the roof, with the accessory nerve crossing the jugular vein beneath it (in front of the vein in most), the hypoglossal nerve crossing the carotids, and the occipital artery running back. Senior's pearl The posterior belly of digastric is the resident's friend: everything dangerous at the top of the neck (jugular, carotids, the tenth, eleventh and twelfth nerves) lies deep to it."],["osec","th:op-65~s3-4","Levels II and V above the accessory nerve","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Hold a kept accessory nerve down with a nerve hook, pull level II down and dissect deep to the fascia of splenius capitis and levator scapulae, continuing back to the trapezius; once you reach the nerve, pass the specimen beneath it. Senior's pearl Keeping the accessory nerve is what keeps the shoulder working. A nerve cut for convenience leaves a patient with a dropped, painful shoulder for life; take it only when it is involved with tumour. What you should see The accessory nerve held on a hook with the fascia of splenius and levator scapulae clean beneath it, and the specimen passed under the nerve. Traction on the nerve A nerve hook is for holding, not pulling: stretch causes the same shoulder palsy as division."],["osec","th:op-65~s3-5","The posterior triangle and floor","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Work forward from the trapezius. Tie the omohyoid at the trapezius and take it with the specimen. Leave the fascia on the deep muscles , which protects the nerve to levator scapulae and the cervical roots, and stay superficial to the cervical rootlets as you pass the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid, keeping the transverse cervical artery, phrenic nerve and brachial plexus beneath your plane. Figure 4 After a right level I to IV dissection keeping the jugular vein: the phrenic nerve and brachial plexus beneath the preserved deep fascia. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 65.5 What you should see A clean floor with the fascia intact, the phrenic nerve on scalenus anterior and the plexus trunks beneath."],["osec","th:op-65~s3-6","Level IV and the bottom of the neck","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Bluntly separate, clamp and tie the tissue between the omohyoid and the clavicle, so the lymphatic channels are closed and not burnt open. If the sternocleidomastoid is being removed, divide it a finger's breadth above the clavicle and sternum, after finding and protecting the jugular vein. Ask the anaesthetist for positive pressure to show any chyle. The chyle leak The lymphatic channels at the base of the neck are invisible and tear when handled. Tie the tissue in bundles rather than chasing a single channel; if you see clear fluid, plicate or suture the surrounding tissue (3-0 or 4-0 silk, figure-of-eight), with fibrin glue or a muscle flap over it. What you should see The transverse cervical vessels and the fat pad clamped and tied in bundles, the omohyoid crossing, and a dry field when the anaesthetist holds a positive pressure breath. Senior's pearl On the left, treat every strand lateral to the lower jugular as the thoracic duct. Ties, not diathermy."],["osec","th:op-65~s3-7","The carotid sheath","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Approach the sheath from behind. To remove the vein, skeletonise it about 2 cm above the clavicle, clamp and tie it as before. To keep it, dissect the sheath fascia forward over the vein , dividing small tributaries with ties or bipolar, and lift the ansa cervicalis off the sheath and divide it. Keep the plane on the vein to protect the vagus, carotid and sympathetic trunk. The specimen is now attached only over the hyoid, from which it is easily freed. Figure 5 After a right level I to IV dissection: the relationship of the jugular vein to the accessory nerve and the vagus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 65.6 The carotid sinus reflex Handling the carotid can cause profound slowing of the heart and low blood pressure. If it persists, inject plain lidocaine into the carotid bulb. What you should see The vagus lying behind and between the carotid and the jugular, the sympathetic trunk behind the sheath on the prevertebral fascia, and the phrenic nerve on scalenus anterior beneath its fascia. Senior's pearl The cervical plexus rootlets are divided where they emerge, well in front of the phrenic nerve; cut them too deep and you take the phrenic's contribution with them."],["osec","th:op-65~s3-8","Close","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Confirm haemostasis, irrigate with warm saline, place size 10 Jackson-Pratt drains, close the platysma with 4-0 absorbable sutures and the skin with staples. What you should see A dry field without chyle on positive pressure, secure jugular stumps, and the kept structures intact."],["osec","th:op-65~s4-1","Crisis drills","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · When it goes wrong","Chyle in the drain after feeding 1 Confirm milky fluid chylomicrons or triglycerides if unsure watch fluids and electrolytes 2 Conservative pressure dressing, medium-chain triglyceride diet, octreotide parenteral nutrition for leaks over 5 days or over 1 litre a day avoid wound vacuum over exposed great vessels 3 Surgical for failure, high output or electrolyte imbalance re-exploration and ligation, lymphatic embolisation, thoracic duct ligation, local flaps Neck swelling after surgery 1 Small and stable aspirate 2 Expanding emergency exploration and control 3 Prevent careful haemostasis, blood pressure control Shaking, confusion and agitation at 48 to 72 hours 1 Suspect alcohol withdrawal (delirium tremens) 2 Act withdrawal protocol, monitoring, electrolytes and thiamine Calf swelling or breathlessness after surgery 1 Suspect deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism (cancer patients have twice the risk a leading cause of death after surgery) 2 Prevent early walking, stockings and drug prophylaxis, balanced against bleeding"],["osec","th:op-65~s5-1","After the operation","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Drains on bulb suction; remove when 20 ml or less in 8 hours , watching the fluid for chyle. After the drains, a circumferential pressure dressing for 24 hours, but not if a free flap's vessels are in the neck. Staples out at 10 to 14 days . Shoulder rehabilitation. Watch for alcohol withdrawal, and give thromboprophylaxis."],["osec","th:op-65~s5-2","Complications","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Complications of modified radical neck dissection Complication Notes Chyle fistula Level IV; left in about 95% Haematoma Aspirate if small, explore if expanding Shoulder dysfunction Accessory nerve injury or sacrifice Marginal mandibular, hypoglossal, vagal, sympathetic injury Lip weakness, tongue weakness, hoarseness and dysphagia, Horner syndrome Facial and cerebral oedema Especially if both jugular veins are tied Skin flap necrosis Radiotherapy, poor incision design Salivary fistula Combined with oral or pharyngeal surgery Infection Rare unless the aerodigestive tract is opened Stroke Comparable to other major surgery; higher with stroke, diabetes, hypertension Venous thromboembolism Prophylaxis Figure 6 A dropped right shoulder from trapezius paralysis after the accessory nerve was sacrificed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 65.3"],["ev","th:op-65~s5-3","Modified Radical Neck Dissection: current evidence","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Preserving structures. Modified radical neck dissection keeps the accessory nerve, internal jugular vein or sternocleidomastoid when they are not involved, with oncological control similar to radical dissection in non-randomised series and much less shoulder disability. Shoulder. Even with the accessory nerve preserved, many patients develop shoulder pain and weakness; a randomised trial showed that progressive resistance exercise training improved shoulder pain and function after neck dissection (McNeely et al., Cancer 2008). Adjuvant treatment. Extranodal extension or multiple positive nodes need postoperative radiotherapy, with chemotherapy for extranodal extension."],["osec","th:op-65~s5-3","Alternatives and the carotid","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Selective neck dissection; chemoradiotherapy in selected patients; palliation. A carotid encased by tumour was once thought unresectable; with balloon occlusion testing and grafting, resection can be safe and can improve survival over no treatment, but it carries real morbidity, and is most justified when it is the only obstacle to a complete resection."],["osec","th:op-65~s6-1","Where the books differ","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra classifies modified radical neck dissection into type I (keeps the accessory nerve), type II (accessory nerve and internal jugular vein) and type III (all three, including the sternocleidomastoid), and describes the radical neck dissection as removing all nodes from the mandible to the clavicle and from the hyoid and opposite anterior digastric to the trapezius, with the three non-lymphatic structures. Lee describes the steps around a carotid body tumour, including keeping the connections between the hypoglossal and vagus nerves, and having vascular surgery available because the decision to resect the carotid is made during the operation. Myers gives the hockey-stick approach and the order (level I, top of neck, posterior triangle, level IV, carotid sheath), the landmarks for the marginal and accessory nerves, the tie-and-suture rule for the jugular vein, bundle ligation at level IV, lidocaine for the carotid sinus reflex, the drain criteria, and the full management of a chyle leak."],["osec","th:op-65~s6-2","Viva essentials","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The definitions of radical, modified radical (types I to III), selective and extended neck dissection. Suárez and the fascial basis for keeping structures. Finding the accessory nerve in the posterior triangle (Erb's point, about 1 cm above it). Finding the marginal mandibular nerve (facial vessels, antegonial notch). How to tie the jugular vein safely, and why bilateral ligation is dangerous. Chyle leak: prevention, recognition and management ladder. The carotid sinus reflex and its treatment. When to include level V ."],["osec","th:op-65~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Modified Radical Neck Dissection · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not finding the marginal nerve before working in level I. Cutting the accessory nerve in the posterior triangle while raising the flap. A single tie on the jugular stump. Cautery instead of ties at level IV. Dissecting medial to the jugular vein into the vagus, carotid or sympathetic trunk. Stripping the deep fascia from the floor. A tight neck dressing over a free flap pedicle."],["osec","th:op-66~s1-1","The case at a glance","Radical Neck Dissection · Operating note","Radical neck dissection Summary What is removed Nodes of levels I to V, the sternomastoid , the internal jugular vein and the accessory nerve Indications Clinically apparent bulky nodal metastasis involving the sternomastoid, jugular vein and accessory nerve, often high in level II or low in level V; never for an N0 neck Contraindications Invasion of the prevertebral or paraspinal muscles; brachial plexus involvement; complete encasement of the carotid (relative: resection and grafting can be considered after balloon occlusion testing); skull base involvement; an uncontrollable primary; distant metastasis Anaesthesia General, avoiding paralysing agents so motor nerves can be stimulated Position Table turned 180 degrees; shoulder roll; arms tucked; chest prepared in case a pectoralis flap is needed Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin; broader if mucosa is opened with the primary; continue 24 to 48 hours Monitoring Arterial line; central access for long cases; no routine nerve monitor, but a handheld stimulator Tray Head and neck set; monopolar and bipolar diathermy; harmonic scalpel; handheld nerve stimulator; vessel loops"],["osec","th:op-66~s1-2","Consent","Radical Neck Dissection · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the cancer in the neck, together with the muscle, vein and nerve it involves. Shoulder: cutting the accessory nerve weakens and stiffens the shoulder and causes pain (the \"shoulder syndrome\"); you will need physiotherapy. Swelling of the face (lymphoedema). Lip weakness from the nerve to the lower lip, usually temporary. Diaphragm weakness (phrenic nerve), arm weakness (brachial plexus), voice change (vagus nerve): uncommon. Leak of lymph (chyle), bleeding, a blood collection needing return to theatre, infection. Carotid artery rupture : rare but life-threatening, more likely after radiotherapy or a wound breakdown."],["osec","th:op-66~s1-3","Before you start","Radical Neck Dissection · Operating note","Patients usually present with a large neck mass. Ask how long it has been there and how fast it has grown: rapid growth suggests an aggressive cancer needing urgent treatment. Ask about dysphagia, dysphonia, otalgia, epistaxis, haemoptysis and weight loss, which point to the primary. Identify comorbidity, especially previous venous thromboembolism or atrial fibrillation needing coordinated anticoagulation. Find out about previous FNA, open biopsy or neck dissection, read earlier operation and pathology reports, and talk to the previous surgeon if you can. Previous radiotherapy matters: plan incisions to avoid a three-point junction over the carotid and consider covering the carotid with vascularised tissue. Record alcohol, tobacco and drug use; heavy drinkers need close watching for withdrawal. Examine the whole head and neck, the cranial nerves and the larynx with a flexible scope. Look for temporal wasting, old scars and radiation skin changes. Reading the neck mass Fixed, firm, ill-defined: radical dissection is likely. Mobile, well defined: a modified radical dissection may be enough. Site: large matted nodes high in level II or low in level V lie where the sternomastoid, jugular vein and accessory nerve overlap. Skin: if the skin cannot be separated from the mass, plan an extended dissection with skin resection and a reconstruction. Cranial nerves VII and IX to XII: their function shows the extent of the cancer, predicts morbidity and informs the prognosis."],["osec","th:op-66~s1-4","Imaging and preparation","Radical Neck Dissection · Operating note","Contrast CT of the neck in 1 mm sections shows the levels involved and the relation to the great vessels, skin and deep muscles; invasion of the prevertebral or paraspinal muscles or encasement of the carotid may steer you away from surgery. PET-CT excludes distant metastasis, which rules out neck surgery except for palliation. When the carotid is involved and resection is being considered, CT angiography or angiography with balloon occlusion testing . Optimise comorbidity and nutrition, with anaesthetic and medical assessment for cardiopulmonary disease."],["osec","th:op-66~s2-1","The landmarks","Radical Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","The marginal mandibular nerve: found about 1 cm below the antegonial notch where it leaves the parotid, freed and lifted with the flap; the facial vessels below it are tied and reflected up with it (the Hayes Martin manoeuvre ). The posterior belly of digastric: many critical structures lie deep to it. Dissecting over it (the \"digastric tunnel\") is safe; the only structure to tie there is the retromandibular vein. The accessory nerve, found as the sternomastoid is unwrapped, if level II disease allows. The lateral process of C2: a consistent, palpable landmark just below the skull base, where the accessory nerve crosses the jugular vein; the top of the vein lies just in front of it. The omohyoid: the carotid sheath lies just deep to it in the lower neck, as it lies deep to the digastric in the upper neck. The phrenic nerve on the anterior scalene, and the brachial plexus between anterior and middle scalenes: always identify and keep them in view, and stay superficial to the deep cervical fascia . The thoracic duct on the left and the right lymphatic duct on the right. Figure 1 Radical neck dissection: the sternomastoid, internal jugular vein and accessory nerve removed with the specimen (dotted outlines). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.1 Figure 2 The right neck: the sternomastoid, jugular vein and accessory nerve closely associated in level II. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.2"],["osec","th:op-66~s3-1","Mark and incise","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Mark the mastoid tip, the angle of the mandible, the suprasternal notch and the cricoid. Use an extended hockey stick or half-apron incision from the mastoid tip down to a crease in the lower neck; for bilateral dissections continue it to the opposite mastoid as an apron . Figure 3 The hockey stick incision for a unilateral radical or elective neck dissection. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.5 What you should see An incision line that reaches the mastoid tip and the lower neck, with its trifurcation (if any) away from the carotid."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-2","Raise the flaps to the boundaries","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Raise subplatysmal flaps to the limits: above, the lower border of the mandible and a line from the angle to the mastoid tip; in front, the superior belly of omohyoid and anterior belly of digastric; below, the suprasternal notch and clavicle; behind, the anterior border of trapezius . Keep the great auricular nerve and external jugular vein unless involved. What you should see The whole field from mandible to clavicle and from the strap muscles to trapezius, with the platysma in the flap."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-3","Protect the marginal mandibular nerve","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Start blunt dissection about 1 cm below the antegonial notch, with loupes and the stimulator. Free the nerve and all its branches from the submandibular fascia, then tie the facial artery and vein below it and lift them with the flap. Figure 4 (A) The marginal mandibular nerve leaving the parotid tail and crossing the lower border of the submandibular gland. (B) The nerve freed and lifted with the flap after the facial vessels are tied. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.4 What you should see The marginal mandibular nerve lying over the facial vessels, twitching the lower lip when stimulated, then lifted with the flap."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-4","Level I","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the fascia under the nerve, find the anterior belly of digastric and carry the submental fat and nodes down along it. Expose and retract the free edge of mylohyoid , cauterising submental branches of the facial vessels, and draw the submandibular gland down and back to show the lingual nerve , the submandibular ganglion and the duct. Clamp and tie the duct and ganglion, tie the facial vessels at the posterior belly, and deliver level Ib. Figure 5 Mylohyoid retracted to show the lingual nerve, submandibular ganglion and Wharton's duct. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.6 What you should see Bare mylohyoid and anterior digastric, the lingual nerve freed from the ganglion, and the hypoglossal nerve on hyoglossus."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-5","Over the digastric","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Continue over the posterior belly of digastric, tying the retromandibular vein and often the lowest part of the parotid tail, protecting the marginal branch again. Suture-ligate the parotid with silk to prevent a salivary leak or sialocele. What you should see The posterior belly of digastric cleared, with the parotid tail oversewn."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-6","Level II","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Retract the digastric upward, find the accessory nerve and follow it to the sternomastoid (if it is not encased in tumour), isolate the top of the jugular vein, and identify and preserve the hypoglossal nerve . What you should see The accessory nerve, the top of the jugular vein and the hypoglossal nerve crossing both carotids, all under the digastric."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-7","Release the sternomastoid below","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the sternal and clavicular heads about 1 cm above the clavicle and lift the muscle. The omohyoid appears, just in front of the carotid sheath; divide it and follow it toward the midline as the front limit. Figure 6 Dividing the clavicular head of the sternomastoid and the supraclavicular tissue lateral to the omohyoid; the plexus, carotid sheath and phrenic nerve lie safely deep to it. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.7 What you should see The omohyoid crossing the carotid sheath beneath the divided muscle."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-8","Level IV","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Dissect bluntly until the phrenic nerve and brachial plexus are seen. With the phrenic nerve in view, clamp and tie the supraclavicular fat pad and draw it up with the specimen, clamping and tying carefully to avoid the thoracic duct on the left and the right lymphatic duct on the right. Identify the lower jugular vein and separate it from the common carotid and the vagus . What you should see The phrenic nerve on scalenus anterior under its fascia, the brachial plexus behind it, and a dry lymphatic pedicle. The ducts Clamp and tie level IV tissue rather than cutting it: an open thoracic or right lymphatic duct leaks chyle."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-9","Level V","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Continue behind the sternomastoid, releasing the supraclavicular fossa and defining the transverse cervical vessels and brachial plexus, back to the anterior border of trapezius , then up along it toward the skull base. The accessory nerve is usually met here; divide it and bring it forward with the specimen. What you should see The anterior border of trapezius, the transverse cervical vessels, and the brachial plexus still covered by fascia."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-10","Release the sternomastoid above","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the muscle just below the mastoid tip and go down to the fascia over the deep muscles. With trapezius defined, sweep the fibrofatty, node-bearing tissue forward, leaving fascia on the deep muscles to spare as much of the cervical plexus as possible. Divide the cervical plexus branches from the specimen high in the neck, with the phrenic nerve always in view . Figure 7 The cervical nerves (red) leaving the plexus; take care not to strip the plexus off the deep muscles. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.8 What you should see Splenius, levator scapulae and the scalenes still covered by fascia, with the phrenic nerve in view as the plexus roots are cut. The phrenic nerve It lies on the anterior scalene under the deep cervical fascia. Stay superficial to that fascia and keep the nerve in sight as you bring the specimen forward."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-11","Divide the jugular vein","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","As the specimen comes forward to the carotid sheath, define the jugular vein and the vagus clearly before ligation. Tie the vein first below in level IV: doubly clamp it and suture-ligate it, one of the ties being a suture ligature so it cannot slip. Clamp and tie the lymphatic pedicle. Then work up the sheath, lift the digastric, isolate the vein in level II, divide the accessory nerve there, and doubly clamp and suture-ligate the vein again. Tie its branches (common facial, lingual, superior thyroid), which vary. In a difficult neck with a large cancer, the lateral process of C2 finds the vein and the nerve where they cross. What you should see The vein clear of the vagus and carotid before each clamp goes on, and doubly tied stumps above and below. The vagus and air embolism The vagus lies with the vein in the sheath; see it before you clamp. An open jugular vein can draw in air: control both ends securely."],["osec","th:op-66~s3-12","Remove and close","Radical Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Join the specimen to the level I dissection, bring it to the lateral border of the strap muscles and remove it. Irrigate, and ask for a Valsalva to show bleeding or chyle. Place suction drains, close the platysma and staple the skin. What you should see A clean neck from mandible to clavicle and from straps to trapezius, with carotid, vagus, phrenic nerve, brachial plexus and hypoglossal nerve intact, and no chyle on Valsalva."],["osec","th:op-66~s4-1","Crisis drills","Radical Neck Dissection · When it goes wrong","Chyle in the wound 1 During surgery ligate the duct and its tributaries check with Valsalva 2 Low output afterwards medium-chain triglyceride diet, pressure dressing, medical treatment 3 High output or not settling re-explore Expanding haematoma 1 Act back to theatre explore, irrigate, find the vessel 2 If no vessel still evacuate the haematoma and replace the drains, which will have clotted The carotid is encased 1 Before surgery angiography and balloon occlusion testing 2 If collateral flow is adequate resection with vein interposition graft can be offered, with full disclosure of stroke and death risk"],["osec","th:op-66~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Radical Neck Dissection · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Not identifying the marginal mandibular nerve. Not isolating and controlling the jugular vein or its branches. Not ligating the lymphatic pedicle securely. Not identifying the phrenic nerve and brachial plexus."],["osec","th:op-66~s5-1","After the operation","Radical Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Admit to a monitored unit. Continue antibiotics for 24 to 48 hours, keep the drains on suction with accurate records, and start physiotherapy early for the shoulder."],["osec","th:op-66~s5-2","Complications","Radical Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Complications of radical neck dissection Complication Notes Infection Commoner when the aerodigestive tract is open; broad-spectrum antibiotics and cultures, drainage if no improvement Haematoma Usually poor intraoperative haemostasis; return to theatre Marginal mandibular weakness May be neurapraxia or platysma weakness; most keep oral competence; expectant care Phrenic nerve injury Usually tolerated, but a problem with lung disease Brachial plexus injury Avoided by identifying it in level IV; early physiotherapy Chylous fistula Diet, pressure, medical treatment, or re-exploration Shoulder syndrome Accessory nerve sacrifice; physiotherapy Facial lymphoedema From jugular and lymphatic removal Carotid rupture, vagus injury, air embolism, bleeding Intraoperative and early risks Figure 8 Shoulder syndrome after sacrifice of the accessory nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.3"],["ev","th:op-66~s5-3","Radical Neck Dissection: current evidence","Radical Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Rarely needed now. Radical neck dissection is reserved for bulky nodal disease directly invading the accessory nerve, internal jugular vein or sternocleidomastoid; modified radical and selective dissections give similar control when these structures are free. Shoulder syndrome (pain, weakness, winging and limited abduction) follows accessory nerve sacrifice in most patients; early physiotherapy and progressive resistance training help (McNeely et al., Cancer 2008). Bilateral jugular vein ligation carries a risk of facial and cerebral oedema and should be staged, or one vein reconstructed."],["osec","th:op-66~s5-3","Alternatives","Radical Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Modified radical dissection when the findings allow: assess the sternomastoid and accessory nerve early, and preserve any non-lymphatic structure that can safely be kept. Extended dissection when other nodes (paratracheal, suboccipital, mediastinal) or structures (skin, digastric, levator scapulae, hypoglossal or vagus nerve) must be included. Non-operative treatment when the cancer is unresectable (skull base, deep muscles, carotid encasement, distant disease), or the patient cannot or will not have surgery."],["osec","th:op-66~s6-1","Surgery when the carotid is involved","Radical Neck Dissection · For the examination","Carotid involvement was once an absolute bar, with complication and stroke rates over 50%. Angiography and balloon occlusion testing are now standard before resecting and reconstructing the carotid. In Freeman and colleagues' 58 patients who had ligation or resection with saphenous vein grafting, stroke occurred in 20% , median disease-specific and disease-free survival was only 12 months , and 24% developed distant metastasis. Data are limited; surgery may be offered to patients with adequate collateral flow who understand the risks. Figure 9 Extended dissection with resection of the common and internal carotid: (left) cancer on the internal carotid just above the bifurcation (star); (right) saphenous vein interposition graft (arrow). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 66.9"],["osec","th:op-66~s6-2","Viva essentials","Radical Neck Dissection · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for What a radical dissection removes, and the classification of modified, selective and extended dissections. Indications today; no role in the N0 neck. Contraindications, including carotid encasement and how it is assessed. The landmarks: Hayes Martin manoeuvre, digastric, C2, omohyoid, phrenic nerve, brachial plexus. Order of the dissection, and double ligation with a suture ligature at each end of the jugular vein. The ducts and chyle; Valsalva. Shoulder syndrome and early physiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-66~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Radical Neck Dissection · For the examination","Common pitfalls A three-point junction over the carotid in an irradiated neck. Paralysing the patient, so nerves cannot be stimulated. Stripping the deep fascia and the cervical plexus. Losing sight of the phrenic nerve as the specimen comes forward."],["osec","th:op-67~s1-1","The case at a glance","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Operating note","Central compartment neck dissection Summary Boundaries Carotids laterally; the innominate artery and vein below; the prelaryngeal, pretracheal and paratracheal nodes (level VI), often with superior mediastinal nodes (level VII) Thyroid cancer At initial surgery: clinically involved central nodes ; BRAF-positive disease; gross extrathyroidal extension; suspicious paratracheal nodes (for example microcalcifications); lateral neck metastasis at presentation. Also listed: a contralateral nodule or distant metastasis, multinodular goitre with previous head and neck irradiation, first-degree relatives with thyroid cancer Other uses Medullary thyroid carcinoma (with or without nodal disease; may need lateral dissection too); parathyroid carcinoma (with ipsilateral lobectomy); squamous cancer involving the central viscera; recurrence Caution Uncontrolled medical disease; pregnancy (postpone to after delivery, or operate in the second trimester for substantial growth); significant distant metastasis unresponsive to suppression; contralateral vocal fold paralysis Never Unresectable mediastinal disease; intracranial metastasis Anaesthesia General, endotracheal Position Supine, shoulder roll, head in a ring; table moved away or turned 180 degrees; head and back up 10 to 30 degrees; prepare neck and upper chest (whole chest if a sternotomy is possible); arms tucked and padded; face and eyes protected Antibiotics A single preincision dose (cefazolin or clindamycin); none afterwards in clean surgery Monitoring Laryngeal nerve monitoring with a surface-electrode tube is optional; probably "],["osec","th:op-67~s1-2","Consent","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove the lymph nodes around the windpipe that may hold cancer, usually with the thyroid. Low calcium from damage to the parathyroid glands: temporary or, less often, lifelong tablets. Voice change from injury to the recurrent laryngeal nerve; if both are injured, breathing difficulty needing a tube or tracheostomy. Loss of power and pitch in the voice from the superior laryngeal nerve. Bleeding under the wound, which can press on the airway and needs urgent return to theatre. Injury to the windpipe or gullet. A scar in a neck crease."],["osec","th:op-67~s1-3","Before you start","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Operating note","Is this primary or recurrent disease? Central dissection is often a reoperation for regional recurrence of thyroid cancer, so obtain earlier operation and pathology reports. Ask about COPD, cardiovascular disease and gastric bypass or malabsorption (which may need a calcium challenge if PTH is raised, to confirm absorption). Ask about haemoptysis, skin fixation and voice change, which suggest invasion and call for laryngoscopy and imaging. Risk factors for serious morbidity are an existing vocal fold paralysis (injury to the other side could mean tracheostomy), previous neck surgery or radiotherapy, and haemoptysis or cough. Note well-differentiated or medullary thyroid cancer and MEN. Palpate the thyroid bed and paratracheal region (site of masses, movement with swallowing, fixation to skin or deep structures) and the lateral neck. Listen for hoarseness and stridor, and note a low larynx or cricoid. Laryngoscopy is essential : the voice can be normal despite a paralysed fold."],["osec","th:op-67~s1-4","Tests and imaging","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Operating note","Bloods: TSH (the patient should be euthyroid); thyroglobulin and its antibodies (disease burden, especially in reoperation); calcitonin with a family history of medullary cancer or MEN 2, or medullary cytology; PTH. Ultrasound: cheap, no radiation, reliable in experienced hands; microcalcifications suggest malignancy, cystic and spongiform nodules suggest benign; but the thyroid shadows the central neck, so sensitivity for central nodes is low. CT and MRI: retrosternal extension, cervical and mediastinal nodes, invasion of trachea and oesophagus, distant disease; sensitive, specific and less operator-dependent. Iodinated contrast may delay radioiodine for 1 to 3 months. Radionuclide scanning for rising thyroglobulin with negative imaging; PET for radioiodine-negative, thyroglobulin-positive patients. FNA is essential before reoperation: sensitivity 65 to 98%, specificity 72 to 100%, better under ultrasound. Sample suspicious nodes; with several nodules, the largest or most suspicious, and more than one if needed. Molecular markers (BRAF, RAS, RET/PTC, PAX8-PPARγ, galectin-3 and others) may refine indeterminate cytology, but their value is uncertain. Document vocal fold movement, ensure the patient is euthyroid, and map the disease and plan."],["osec","th:op-67~s2-1","Vessels","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","Superior thyroid artery: first branch of the external carotid (from the common carotid in 16%). Inferior thyroid artery: from the thyrocervical trunk. Thyroidea ima artery in the thyrothymic fat, in 2 to 12%, from the innominate, subclavian, right common carotid, internal thoracic or the arch, supplying the lower isthmus. The carotids form the lateral borders; the innominate artery is the lower limit (the right common carotid may arise early); the innominate vein lies below and the jugular system laterally."],["osec","th:op-67~s2-2","The recurrent laryngeal nerve","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","Left: loops under the aortic arch behind the ligamentum arteriosum and rises in the tracheoesophageal groove. Right: leaves the vagus higher, loops round the subclavian and rises more laterally. It passes beneath the inferior constrictor, behind the cricothyroid joint , and enters the larynx below the cricoid, above the first ring. Simon's triangle (common carotid laterally, oesophagus medially, inferior thyroid artery above) contains it. It lies behind or between the branches of the inferior thyroid artery in 80%, and the relationship differs between sides in about half of patients. It is usually near the inferior parathyroid, mostly within 3 mm of Berry's ligament (often lateral to it, sometimes through it), may pass through the thyroid capsule, and lies deep to the tubercle of Zuckerkandl in 98% . A non-recurrent nerve occurs in up to 0.7%, almost always on the right with an aberrant subclavian artery (or on the left in situs inversus), running directly into the larynx from the level of the thyroid cartilage or upper pole. 40 to 65% divide into two or three branches, more than 5 mm below the cricoid."],["osec","th:op-67~s2-3","The superior laryngeal nerve","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","It leaves the nodose ganglion of the vagus about 4 cm above the bifurcation, runs down beside the pharynx behind the carotid sheath, and divides about 2 cm above the upper pole. The internal branch pierces the thyrohyoid membrane and gives sensation and secretomotor supply to the supraglottis, epiglottis, tongue base, pyriform sinus and upper sphincter. The external branch supplies cricothyroid and inferior constrictor; it lies in the sternothyrolaryngeal (Joll's) triangle (midline, upper pole and strap attachments), deep to the superior thyroid artery in 82 to 86%, and lies below or up to 1 cm above the upper pole in 37%."],["osec","th:op-67~s2-4","The parathyroids","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Anatomy you must see","Superior: from the fourth branchial pouch, supplied by the superior or inferior thyroid vessels, usually above the cricoid; these can often be preserved. Inferior: from the third, variable in position, usually below the cricoid; the ones most often needing autotransplantation. Most receive blood from the inferior thyroid artery ."],["osec","th:op-67~s3-1","Mark and incise","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Mark a 5 to 6 cm horizontal incision between cricoid and sternal notch, usually 2 to 3 cm above the notch, in a crease that could be extended for a lateral dissection. Place it higher in young patients , because scars migrate down and one below the clavicle looks poor; if a sternotomy may be needed, at the clavicles or lower. Inject 0.25% bupivacaine with 1:200,000 adrenaline 1 to 2 cm above and below. Cut the dermis with a No. 15 blade and the platysma with diathermy, sparing the communicating anterior jugular veins. What you should see An incision in a crease, symmetrical, with the platysma divided."],["osec","th:op-67~s3-2","Raise and protect the flaps","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Raise subplatysmal flaps just above the investing fascia and anterior jugular veins, up to the thyroid notch and down to the sternal notch, tying large vessels with 2-0 silk. Protect the skin edges with occlusive material such as Steri-Strips sewn on with a running locked 2-0 silk, to prevent heat and instrument injury. Figure 1 Occlusive skin protection with Steri-Strips held by 2-0 silk. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 67.1 What you should see Flaps up to the thyroid notch and down to the sternal notch, with the skin edges covered."],["osec","th:op-67~s3-3","Split the straps","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Open the median raphe from thyroid notch to sternal notch and lift the straps off the thyroid (if present) and trachea with a Freer, a peanut or diathermy. Take the straps with a margin if tumour infiltrates them. In reoperations, or when the straps are stuck to the thyroid or trachea, find the carotid lateral to the straps first, and divide the straps horizontally if needed to reach the paratracheal compartment. What you should see The trachea and thyroid bed in the midline, or the carotid lateral to stuck straps in a reoperation."],["osec","th:op-67~s3-4","Find the carotid and the nerve","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Rotate the thyroid and trachea medially and skeletonise the carotid. With a McCabe dissector, dissect near the cricothyroid joint and in the tracheoesophageal groove to find the recurrent laryngeal nerve ; never close the clamp with its tips buried. Once found, dissect along and directly on its sheath , tracing it from the paratracheal region to cricothyroid, protecting both anterior and posterior branches. In reoperations, start laterally near the carotid. What you should see The recurrent nerve in the tracheo-oesophageal groove entering beneath the inferior constrictor near the cricothyroid joint, stimulating. Find the nerve before you clear the trachea See the nerve before the thyroid or nodes are lifted off the trachea and oesophagus, and keep it in sight. That is the best protection against injury."],["osec","th:op-67~s3-5","Remove the paratracheal packet","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Hold the paratracheal tissue with a Babcock and pull it up and medially, away from the nerve. Remove all fibrofatty tissue and nodes over the trachea and medial to the carotid, down into the superior mediastinum. Figure 2 The paratracheal packet drawn medially away from the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 67.2 What you should see The trachea and oesophagus bare medially, the carotid laterally, the nerve skeletonised throughout, and the innominate artery at the lower limit on the right."],["osec","th:op-67~s3-6","Save the parathyroids","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Dissect each parathyroid laterally with its blood supply . Glands above the cricoid can often be kept. If an inferior gland is devascularised, confirm it on frozen section and autotransplant it; keep a low threshold. Figure 3 Paratracheal dissection with a lateral neck dissection, a parathyroid preserved medial to the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 67.3 Figure 4 Bilateral paratracheal dissection, carotid to carotid, with superior parathyroid tissue preserved. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 67.4 What you should see A trachea cleared from carotid to carotid and down to the innominate artery, with both nerves traced and intact and the superior parathyroids pink on their pedicles."],["osec","th:op-67~s3-7","Haemostasis and closure","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · The operation, stage by stage","Irrigate and ask for a Valsalva; use bipolar diathermy and agents such as Surgicel. A drain is optional. Close the straps with 3-0 Vicryl, leaving a gap below so a haematoma can drain forward; close the platysma carefully and the skin with subcuticular 4-0 Monocryl, with an adhesive and Steri-Strips. What you should see A dry bed on Valsalva, a stimulating nerve, and parathyroids that are pink or have been reimplanted."],["osec","th:op-67~s4-1","Crisis drills","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · When it goes wrong","Haematoma after surgery 1 Recognise usually within 2 hours breathlessness, difficulty swallowing, an expanding neck 2 Act at once check the drain open the wound at the bedside and evacuate the clot to free the airway 3 Then back to theatre to find and stop the bleeding Recurrent laryngeal nerve divided 1 Repair immediate reanastomosis may give tone and reduce atrophy 2 Later early medialisation of the fold 3 Both nerves stridor and distress at extubation reintubate, and tracheostomy may be needed Low calcium 1 Severe symptoms intravenous calcium gluconate with cardiac monitoring 2 Then oral calcium carbonate and vitamin D, temporarily or for life Trachea or oesophagus injured 1 During surgery usually at Berry's ligament or the subcricoid trachea primary repair usually suffices 2 After surgery surgical emphysema or increased drainage, often with wound infection usually self-limiting explore for persistent or large output"],["osec","th:op-67~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Failing to save or to recognise devascularised parathyroids. Failing to identify the recurrent laryngeal nerve and preserve both branches. Leaving tissue over the trachea in the superior mediastinum. Poor haemostasis, which puts nerves and glands at risk. Stretching and burning the skin edges with hot instruments."],["osec","th:op-67~s5-1","After the operation","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Aftercare","After total or completion thyroidectomy, check PTH at 1 hour in recovery; keep the patient in overnight if it is under 15 pg/mL , remembering that calcium may take over 12 hours to follow. Ibuprofen or paracetamol with an opioid for breakthrough pain. Keep the wound dry for 48 hours; review at a week and renew the Steri-Strips. Start thyroid replacement after total thyroidectomy; for radioiodine, withdraw thyroxine (liothyronine can bridge and be stopped 2 weeks before). Radioiodine depends on pathology and metastasis."],["osec","th:op-67~s5-2","Complications","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Complications of central compartment dissection Complication Notes Hypoparathyroidism Risk with total or completion thyroidectomy and previous parathyroid surgery; IV calcium for severe symptoms, oral calcium and vitamin D Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury Weak, hoarse voice with the fold paramedian, possible aspiration; bilateral injury threatens the airway Superior laryngeal nerve injury Poor projection and high-pitch control; voice therapy Haematoma Within 2 hours; open the wound and return to theatre Tracheo-oesophageal injury Usually at Berry's ligament or the subcricoid trachea Bleeding during surgery Anterior jugular veins, the carotid (blunt dissection, know your depth), thyroid surface vessels"],["ev","th:op-67~s5-3","Central Compartment Neck Dissection: current evidence","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Therapeutic and prophylactic. Central neck dissection is recommended for clinically involved central nodes in thyroid cancer; prophylactic dissection in node-negative papillary cancer may be considered for T3 to T4 tumours or when lateral nodes are involved (Haugen et al., Thyroid 2016). Randomised and meta-analytic evidence shows that prophylactic dissection reduces central recurrence only slightly, if at all, but increases temporary and permanent hypoparathyroidism. Medullary carcinoma needs central dissection routinely. Protect the parathyroids and the recurrent laryngeal nerves; autotransplant a devascularised gland."],["osec","th:op-67~s5-3","Alternatives","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · Aftercare","Radioiodine. External beam radiotherapy. Tyrosine kinase inhibitors."],["osec","th:op-67~s6-1","Prophylactic central dissection","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · For the examination","For clinically involved central nodes, dissection is clearly indicated. For the clinically negative central neck there is no proven survival benefit and the effect on local control is equivocal; prophylactic dissection with total thyroidectomy raises hypocalcaemia , but may improve local control, avoid riskier reoperation and stage the disease for radioiodine decisions. A 2013 meta-analysis of 11 studies (2318 papillary cancers) found recurrence of 7.9% after thyroidectomy alone and 4.7% with prophylactic dissection, not statistically significant; high-volume surgeons' cohorts had 3.8%, and the number needed to treat was 31. Because high-volume surgeons have the fewest complications, prophylactic dissection deserves stronger consideration in their hands. Medullary cancer is always an indication."],["osec","th:op-67~s6-2","Viva essentials","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The boundaries of levels VI and VII. The frequency of central nodal disease in thyroid cancer, and the prophylactic debate. The course of each recurrent laryngeal nerve, Simon's triangle, Berry's ligament, the tubercle of Zuckerkandl, and the non-recurrent nerve. The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve and Joll's triangle. Parathyroid embryology and blood supply; autotransplantation. The 1-hour PTH and the 15 pg/mL threshold. Managing a neck haematoma and bilateral nerve palsy."],["osec","th:op-67~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Central Compartment Neck Dissection · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not checking the vocal folds before a reoperation. Operating without FNA proof of recurrence. Clearing the trachea before seeing the nerve. Taking an inferior parathyroid without reimplanting it."],["osec","th:op-73~s1-1","The case at a glance","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Operating note","Excision of carotid body tumour Summary Indications Small, newly found tumours; tumours that are growing, steadily or fast, with or without cranial nerve involvement; tumours causing neuropathy (vocal fold or tongue paralysis), usually Shamblin III needing carotid resection and replacement; secreting tumours Observe instead The elderly, severe comorbidity, refusal of surgery, or failure of balloon occlusion testing ; yearly CT if observed Shamblin I and II Excision in the subadventitial plane Shamblin III Resection of the carotid with vein or synthetic graft, by a vascular surgeon Anaesthesia General, usually without paralysis; arterial line for close blood pressure control. Secreting tumours: alpha and beta blockade for several weeks beforehand Position Supine with a shoulder roll Antibiotics Not indicated (clean surgery) Tray Standard head and neck set; vessel loops for nerves and vessels; a vascular surgeon available on the day"],["osec","th:op-73~s1-2","Consent","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove a tumour growing on the main artery in the neck before it grows larger or affects the nerves. Bleeding , which can be heavy; rarely the artery must be repaired or replaced with a graft by a vascular surgeon. Stroke , especially if the artery has to be resected. Nerve injury: hoarseness and swallowing trouble (vagus), tongue weakness (hypoglossal), voice and swallowing changes (superior laryngeal); worse if two nerves are injured. First bite syndrome: a short sharp pain near the ear and jaw at the start of meals, usually tolerable, treatable with botulinum toxin. Blood pressure swings if both sides are operated on. Genetics: if it is the inherited type, other tumours may develop and relatives may need testing."],["osec","th:op-73~s1-3","Before you start","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Operating note","Most tumours of the post-styloid parapharyngeal space are neural, most of those are paragangliomas, and the commonest paraganglioma is the carotid body tumour. Ask about a lateral neck lump; labile or hard-to-control hypertension , flushing and palpitations; voice change, dysphagia and tongue weakness; and tinnitus on that side from the high flow. Record previous neck surgery, hypertension needing several drugs, phaeochromocytoma, carotid atheroma and TIAs; familial paragangliomas and bleeding or clotting disorders; antiplatelet drugs, herbal products and antibiotic allergies. Examine the neck for a mass on one or both sides and old scars, and the cranial nerves, especially the vagus and hypoglossal . Use a flexible scope to check superior and recurrent laryngeal nerve function and vocal fold movement."],["osec","th:op-73~s1-4","Imaging","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Operating note","Contrast CT gives excellent detail: the tumour enhances intensely and splays the internal and external carotids (the lyre sign); it may show other paragangliomas. A carotid body tumour sits at the bifurcation, whereas a glomus vagale arises behind the carotid, from the vagus. MRI and MR angiography: iso- or hypointense to muscle on T1, larger lesions with a salt and pepper pattern (slow flow and flow voids), intense gadolinium enhancement; hyperintense and speckled with flow voids on T2. Transfemoral angiography remains the gold standard for identifying the tumour and its vascularity. The senior author once found that a presumed carotid body tumour, diagnosed on history, examination and an MR blush, was an aneurysm of the internal carotid . Octreotide or MIBG scintigraphy is sensitive but not specific: other paragangliomas, other neuroendocrine tumours, lymphoma and Merkel cell carcinoma also take it up. It helps in looking for multiple lesions. Figure 1 (A) A neck mass that grew over 10 years; (B) MRI showing a large carotid body tumour with many flow voids; (C) lateral view, the mass reaching the prevertebral fascia. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 73.2 Figure 2 MR angiography in a 21-year-old woman with bilateral carotid body tumours (arrows). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 73.3 Figure 3 Angiogram of the same patient showing a carotid body tumour. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 73.4 Figure 4 MR angiogram of a neck mass mistaken for a carotid body tumour: transfemoral angiography showed an internal carotid aneurysm. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig"],["osec","th:op-73~s1-5","The catecholamine screen","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Operating note","Every patient with a suspected paraganglioma needs a 24-hour urine for catecholamines (vanillylmandelic acid) before surgery. Abnormal: MRI of the abdomen for a phaeochromocytoma, which must be removed first to avoid a potentially fatal intraoperative hypertensive crisis. Abnormal but no phaeochromocytoma: assume a rare secreting paraganglioma. Involve the anaesthetist early and plan for a hypertensive crisis, with alpha and beta blockade for several weeks to prevent both the crisis and circulatory collapse after removal."],["osec","th:op-73~s1-6","Embolisation and the vascular surgeon","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Operating note","The authors do not embolise most tumours: tumours not encasing the artery come off in the subadventitial plane, and they believe embolisation can provoke inflammation that obliterates that plane. Discuss every case with a vascular surgeon , and book the operation for a day when one is available, in case the internal carotid needs a patch or graft."],["osec","th:op-73~s2-1","The Shamblin grades","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Anatomy you must see","Type I: small, easily dissected. Type II: medium, more intimately attached to the artery. Type III: large, encasing and invading the muscular wall of the artery, needing resection and grafting. Figure 5 The Shamblin classification of carotid body tumours, types I to III, with the vagus, hypoglossal and superior laryngeal nerves. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 73.1"],["osec","th:op-73~s2-2","Landmarks","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Anatomy you must see","The upper border of the thyroid cartilage is the most reliable surface landmark for the level of the bifurcation. The internal carotid rises lateral and posterior to the external and has no branches in the neck. The external carotid is more medial and has eight branches; the superior thyroid, ascending pharyngeal, lingual, facial and occipital are the five you are most likely to meet. The vagus, hypoglossal and superior laryngeal nerves must be found and protected before the tumour is touched."],["osec","th:op-73~s2-3","The familial form","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Anatomy you must see","Most tumours are sporadic, but some are inherited ( PGL1 ): autosomal dominant, due to mutations in the SDHD gene (succinate dehydrogenase, a mitochondrial complex II enzyme) on chromosome 11q23 . Familial tumours are multiple (one or both carotid bodies, plus others on unnamed arteries nearby), more often malignant , and more often associated with phaeochromocytoma . Relatives should have genetic testing : a negative test is reassuring; a positive one needs MRI, with removal of any tumour found, and yearly MRI if negative. Inheritance through the father The disease is expressed when the gene comes from the father, whose children each have a 50% chance of inheriting it. The gene passes from mothers too, but because of genomic imprinting it is usually silent in their children. The chapter puts this as a woman being unable to pass the gene on, which is not strictly correct: she can pass it, and her sons can then transmit an active copy to the next generation."],["osec","th:op-73~s3-1","Incision and exposure","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · The operation, stage by stage","Make an incision in a natural skin crease at the level of the upper border of the thyroid cartilage , through the platysma, and raise subplatysmal flaps. Retract the sternomastoid; the submandibular triangle need not be dissected. What you should see The carotid sheath under the retracted sternocleidomastoid at the level of the bifurcation."],["osec","th:op-73~s3-2","Find and loop the nerves and vessels","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · The operation, stage by stage","Open the carotid sheath, dissect the jugular vein free of the carotid, and skeletonise the vagus and hypoglossal nerves , placing vessel loops around them for identification and retraction. Skeletonise the common carotid and pass a Penrose drain around it for proximal control . Figure 6 The operative field: the carotid and the hypoglossal and vagus nerves identified and looped for safety, with the tumour. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 73.6 What you should see Loops around the common carotid, the vagus and the hypoglossal nerve, with the superior laryngeal nerve deep to the bifurcation. Control before you dissect Proximal and distal control of the carotid with loops limits the risk of catastrophic bleeding; careful identification of the nerves before resection keeps the risk of nerve injury low."],["osec","th:op-73~s3-3","Enter the subadventitial plane","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · The operation, stage by stage","A large venous plexus usually runs down from the bifurcation about halfway to the clavicle. Where the carotid is clear of it, start the subadventitial dissection and carry it up to the bifurcation. What you should see The white line: a pale, relatively avascular plane between the tumour and the arterial wall."],["osec","th:op-73~s3-4","Divide the external carotid","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · The operation, stage by stage","Continue up the external carotid about 1 to 2 cm above the bifurcation; clamp, divide and ligate it with a silk tie and a suture ligature. Larger tumours extend deep, even to the prevertebral fascia, and without ligating the external carotid it is very hard to reach that deep plane. Figure 7 Subadventitial dissection along the carotid up to the bifurcation, then ligation of the external carotid. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 73.7 What you should see The external carotid tied and the tumour rotating off the internal carotid, giving a view of its deep surface."],["osec","th:op-73~s3-5","Dissect off the internal carotid","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · The operation, stage by stage","In the same plane, dissect the tumour off the internal carotid and remove it. With the nerves and jugular vein identified and retracted, removal is usually clean, safe and nearly bloodless. What you should see A bare, intact internal carotid with its muscular wall, the external carotid ligated, and the vagus and hypoglossal nerves untouched in their loops. Never peel a Shamblin III tumour When the tumour encases the artery and invades its muscular wall, even precise subadventitial dissection opens the wall and causes massive bleeding. Rule out encasement before surgery and plan resection and grafting instead."],["osec","th:op-73~s4-1","Crisis drills","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · When it goes wrong","Bleeding from the internal carotid 1 Control proximal and distal loops already in place 2 Repair the vascular surgeon repairs it with a patch or graft Hypertensive crisis on the table 1 Cause an unrecognised secreting paraganglioma or phaeochromocytoma 2 Prevent 24-hour urine catecholamines in every patient remove a phaeochromocytoma first alpha and beta blockade for secreting tumours Nerve divided 1 Hypoglossal not severe on its own repair by neurorrhaphy when possible 2 Vagus assess the paralysed fold medialise now or after assessing voice and aspiration a paramedian fold may not need it 3 Both expect substantial dysphagia and aspiration"],["osec","th:op-73~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Attempting subadventitial removal of a Shamblin III tumour. Not identifying and preserving the vagus and hypoglossal nerves. Missing a secreting tumour."],["osec","th:op-73~s5-1","After the operation","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Aftercare","First bite syndrome: a few seconds of pain around the parotid and jaw, worst with the first bite of the day, from sympathetic denervation when the carotid sheath is violated. Most tolerate it; botulinum toxin A into the parotid helps those who do not. After bilateral excision, baroreceptor failure leaves patients at risk of hypertensive crises when sympathetic drive rises: refer for blood pressure management and give anxiolytics. Most tumours are benign and need no long-term follow-up. Malignancy looks the same histologically and is diagnosed by tumour in cervical nodes, invasion of neighbouring structures or distant metastasis; discuss these at a multidisciplinary meeting and follow them long term."],["osec","th:op-73~s5-2","Complications","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Bleeding The key risk, with injury to nerves while controlling it Carotid injury Patch or graft; avoid peeling Shamblin III tumours Hypoglossal injury Repair when possible Vagus injury Vocal fold paralysis; medialisation if needed Combined vagus and hypoglossal injury Substantial dysphagia and aspiration First bite syndrome Botulinum toxin if troublesome Baroreceptor failure After bilateral excision"],["ev","th:op-73~s5-3","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors: current evidence","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Aftercare","Genetics. About a third of head and neck paragangliomas are hereditary, so every patient should be offered SDHx testing. Surgery. The Shamblin grade predicts carotid involvement and the risk of vascular reconstruction and cranial nerve injury; transient lower cranial nerve deficits occur in up to a quarter in series, permanent ones less often. Preoperative embolisation for larger tumours is debated. Alternatives. Observation for small or slow-growing tumours in older patients, and radiotherapy or radiosurgery, which controls most tumours with fewer nerve deficits."],["osec","th:op-73~s5-3","Alternatives","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · Aftercare","Definitive radiotherapy for patients unfit for surgery, or for tumours that grow on CT or cause symptoms such as cranial nerve deficits or hypertension."],["osec","th:op-73~s6-1","Should you embolise first?","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · For the examination","Unresolved and never studied prospectively. Guidelines (Moore and colleagues) give no criteria; Litle and colleagues found no benefit for tumours under 5 cm . Jackson and colleagues' meta-analysis, built from 8 non-randomised quantitative studies out of 2418 screened, found embolisation reduced estimated blood loss by more than half (295 tumours) and nearly halved operative time (174 tumours). The effect may be large, but only a randomised trial will settle it, and the authors of this chapter avoid embolisation for fear of losing the subadventitial plane."],["osec","th:op-73~s6-2","Viva essentials","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The post-styloid space and paragangliomas; carotid body versus glomus vagale versus schwannoma on CT. The lyre sign, salt and pepper MRI, angiography as the gold standard; no biopsy. The Shamblin grades and what each means for surgery. 24-hour urine catecholamines and phaeochromocytoma; alpha and beta blockade. SDHD, paternal transmission and family screening. The subadventitial plane; ligating the external carotid to reach deep tumour. The nerves at risk, and first bite syndrome. The embolisation debate."],["osec","th:op-73~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Excision of Carotid Body Tumors · For the examination","Common pitfalls Biopsying a neck mass that is a carotid body tumour. Operating without a vascular surgeon available. Forgetting that the tumour may be bilateral or multiple. Mistaking an aneurysm for a tumour."],["osec","th:op-75~s1-1","The case at a glance","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · Operating note","Penetrating neck injury Summary Immediate exploration (hard signs) Shock, pulsatile bleeding, expanding haematoma, absent pulse, stroke signs, bruit or thrill, airway compromise or hoarseness with stridor, air bubbling through the wound, extensive surgical emphysema Stable, soft signs or no signs CT angiography of the neck (and chest if low); then endoscopy, contrast swallow or exploration guided by findings and trajectory Wound does not breach platysma Local care and closure Zones (still useful for exposure) I: clavicle to cricoid (thoracic outlet); II: cricoid to mandible angle; III: above the mandible angle to the skull base Airway Secure early: intubation by an expert with a surgical airway ready; awake tracheostomy or intubation through a tracheal wound in laryngotracheal injury Antibiotics For contaminated wounds and aerodigestive injury; tetanus prophylaxis Tray Neck exploration and vascular sets, shunts, Fogarty catheters, rigid and flexible endoscopes, drains"],["osec","th:op-75~s1-2","Consent","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · Operating note","What to tell the patient (often an emergency) Life-saving surgery for bleeding or airway, sometimes a tracheostomy. Stroke from carotid injury or its repair; ligation of a major vessel if repair is impossible. Nerve injury: hoarseness (vagus, recurrent laryngeal), tongue weakness (hypoglossal), shoulder weakness (accessory), Horner syndrome. Leak from a pharyngeal or oesophageal repair, neck infection, mediastinitis; feeding tube. Scarring; further operations."],["osec","th:op-75~s1-3","Before you start","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · Operating note","ATLS: airway with cervical spine care if blunt or gunshot, breathing, circulation (pressure on bleeding, not blind clamping). Do not probe the wound or remove an impaled object outside theatre. Examine for the hard signs and for hoarseness, dysphagia, haemoptysis and haematemesis. Figure 1 A modern algorithm for penetrating neck injury. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 75.5"],["osec","th:op-75~s2-1","Position and incise","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Supine, neck extended and turned away if the spine is cleared, prepare from the jaw to the chest so a sternotomy or clavicular approach can follow. Make an incision along the anterior border of sternomastoid (extend to a collar incision for bilateral injury). What you should see The carotid sheath and the visceral compartment exposed through a long incision."],["osec","th:op-75~s2-2","Get proximal and distal control","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Before opening a haematoma, control the common carotid low in the neck and the internal and external carotids above the injury. Zone I injuries may need the chest opened; zone III injuries may need mandibular subluxation, endovascular balloon occlusion or stenting. What you should see Slings round the vessels above and below the injury before the haematoma is entered. Opening the haematoma first A contained haematoma is tamponading the injury. Open it before proximal and distal control and you will meet torrential bleeding you cannot see."],["osec","th:op-75~s2-3","Repair the vessels","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Repair the carotid if possible (primary repair or interposition graft, shunting if back-pressure is poor); ligation risks stroke and is kept for the moribund or unrepairable vessel. Repair or ligate the internal jugular vein (one side can be ligated safely). External carotid branches may be ligated. What you should see Restored carotid flow with a distal pulse, and secure haemostasis."],["osec","th:op-75~s2-4","Explore the airway and gullet","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Inspect the larynx, trachea, pharynx and oesophagus; flexible or rigid endoscopy on the table helps find small tears. Close pharyngeal and oesophageal tears in two layers, cover them with a muscle flap (sternomastoid), and drain widely; repair tracheal tears and consider a tracheostomy through or below the injury. Place a feeding tube. What you should see Watertight aerodigestive repairs separated from vascular repairs by healthy muscle, with drains. Senior's pearl A missed oesophageal injury is the commonest cause of late death. If trajectory, air in the tissues or dysphagia suggest it, look with both a contrast swallow and endoscopy, or explore."],["osec","th:op-75~s2-5","Soft tissue wounds","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · The operation, stage by stage","Irrigate generously, remove debris and devitalised tissue (scrape embedded particles to prevent tattooing), and close in layers without tension. Repair the facial nerve and parotid duct in facial lacerations at the time. Give antibiotics for bites and contaminated wounds. Figure 2 A dog bite with multiple lacerations, and principles of suture placement. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 75.1 and 75.2 Figure 3 Complex facial injuries and a deep preauricular laceration with facial palsy. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, figs 75.3 and 75.4 What you should see Clean, well-aligned closures, and any nerve or duct injury repaired."],["osec","th:op-75~s3-1","Crisis drills","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · When it goes wrong","Exsanguinating bleeding from the neck 1 Act finger pressure in the wound Foley catheter inserted and inflated as a tamponade 2 Then theatre for proximal control massive transfusion Airway compromise with a neck haematoma 1 Act expert intubation with a surgical airway ready avoid paralysing before you can secure the airway Leak after aerodigestive repair 1 Act nil by mouth, drainage, antibiotics, nutrition CT for collections"],["osec","th:op-75~s4-1","After surgery","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · Aftercare","Neurological observations after carotid repair; nil by mouth and nasogastric feeding after pharyngeal or oesophageal repair, with a contrast swallow before oral intake; voice and swallowing assessment."],["ev","th:op-75~s4-2","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck: current evidence","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · Aftercare","The traditional zone-based mandatory exploration has largely given way to the \"no zone\" approach, in which stable patients are triaged by examination and CT angiography regardless of zone (Shiroff et al., Am Surg 2013). A systematic review of CT angiography in this approach reported sensitivity of 83% to 100% and specificity of 61% to 100% for vascular and aerodigestive injuries (Ibraheem et al., J Trauma Acute Care Surg 2020). An evidence-based review for otolaryngologists set out care pathways from resuscitation to selective exploration (Burgess et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2012)."],["osec","th:op-75~s4-2","Complications","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · Aftercare","Stroke, haemorrhage, airway compromise, leak and mediastinitis, wound infection, nerve palsies, fistula."],["osec","th:op-75~s5-1","Where the books differ","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers immediate exploration for hard signs, CT angiography for soft signs, the soft tissue principles and antibiotic choices for bites. Dhingra and K. J. Lee the neck zones and the classic selective approach with endoscopy and angiography. The literature the \"no zone\" approach with CT angiography for stable patients."],["osec","th:op-75~s5-2","Viva essentials","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Primary survey and airway management. Hard and soft signs. Zones and why they matter for exposure. Proximal and distal control before entering a haematoma. Oesophageal injury : how to find it and how to repair it."],["osec","th:op-75~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Management of Traumatic Soft Tissue and Vascular Injuries to the Neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls Probing the wound in the emergency department. Paralysing for intubation without a surgical airway plan. Entering a haematoma before vascular control. Missing an oesophageal tear."],["osec","th:op-76~s1-1","The case at a glance","Deep Neck Abscesses · Operating note","Drainage of deep neck abscesses Summary Indications An abscess threatening the airway; sepsis; a smaller abscess not improving on intravenous antibiotics; persistent or recurrent collection needing revision Contraindications Haemodynamic instability (resuscitate first) Anaesthesia The airway comes before everything: if it is threatened, an awake tracheostomy (or awake fibreoptic intubation) under local anaesthetic, with the anaesthetist ready to give a general anaesthetic once the airway is secure; no sedation before the airway is secured; avoid muscle relaxants so nerves can be stimulated Position Supine, shoulder roll to extend the neck gently (sitting up until the airway is secure if the patient cannot lie flat) Antibiotics Intravenous, covering aerobes, anaerobes and beta-lactamase producers, started before surgery and adjusted to cultures Monitoring Standard; high dependency or intensive care afterwards for ventilation, sepsis and pain Imaging Contrast CT in 3 mm slices for every patient whose airway allows it; lateral neck X-ray as a quick screen for retropharyngeal swelling; ultrasound for superficial collections and guided aspiration Tray Head and neck set; tracheostomy set; large Penrose drains; culture pots for aerobic, anaerobic and fungal culture (anaerobic samples kept away from air) Before theatre Bedside aspirate for Gram stain and culture if stable; intravenous antibiotics; secure or plan the airway; blood glucose and immune status"],["osec","th:op-76~s1-2","Consent","Deep Neck Abscesses · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: an infection deep in the neck has formed pus that antibiotics alone will not clear, and it can affect breathing and spread to the chest. A breathing tube in the neck (tracheostomy) may be needed to keep the airway safe while the swelling settles; it is usually temporary. Open wounds with drains , dressed several times a day, healing over weeks; scarring. More than one operation may be needed if pus collects again. Nerve injury: facial weakness, hoarseness, shoulder weakness or tongue weakness, depending on the space. Bleeding , including rare, catastrophic bleeding from a major artery weakened by the infection. Swallowing difficulty , sometimes needing a feeding tube for a time. Spread to the chest, clots in the neck veins, and death in severe cases. The cause must also be treated: often a tooth extraction."],["osec","th:op-76~s1-3","Before you start","Deep Neck Abscesses · Operating note","1.3.1 The history How long, and how fast it is changing; pain on swallowing or difficulty swallowing; trismus; voice change; breathing difficulty. Teeth: periodontal disease, fractured or carious teeth, periapical abscess. A recent sore throat or quinsy, upper respiratory infection, trauma, intravenous drug use, or recent neck surgery. Diabetes , HIV, chemotherapy and other immune suppression. Antibiotics already taken; anticoagulants. Previous head and neck cancer: a malignant fistula can masquerade as an abscess. 1.3.2 The examination Airway first: stridor, voice, drooling, positioning, oxygen saturation; flexible laryngoscopy for patency and swelling of the posterior pharyngeal wall. Neck: extent and site of swelling, fluctuance, tenderness, redness, crepitus. Mouth: gingival swelling and pus around carious teeth; swelling of the floor of the mouth with the tongue pushed up and back. Oropharynx: bulging of the palate or tonsil, trismus. Fever and a high white cell count are usual. Sedation can kill A patient sitting up, drooling and working hard to breathe may lose the airway completely if sedated or laid flat. Do not send an unstable airway to the CT scanner; secure the airway first."],["osec","th:op-76~s2-1","The fascial layers","Deep Neck Abscesses · Anatomy you must see","The superficial cervical fascia lies with the platysma; deep neck infections involve the spaces made by the three layers of the deep cervical fascia . Superficial (investing) layer: encircles the neck like a drum, splitting around the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius and the submandibular gland (the marginal mandibular nerve runs in it); above the jaw it splits to enclose the masticator space . Middle (visceral) layer: wraps the thyroid, trachea, oesophagus and constrictors; above, as the buccopharyngeal fascia , it forms the front wall of the retropharyngeal space. Deep layer: splits into the alar layer (the back wall of the retropharyngeal space) and the prevertebral layer. All three layers contribute to the carotid sheath . The deep neck spaces Space Boundaries and contents Usual source How to drain Sublingual Above the mylohyoid, under the floor-of-mouth mucosa Premolars and first molar (roots above the mylohyoid line) Through the mouth, plus the tooth Submandibular Below the mylohyoid Second and third molars (roots below the mylohyoid line) Through the skin Ludwig's angina Both, often both sides Dental Submental incision; awake tracheostomy often needed Parapharyngeal Inverted pyramid from skull base to hyoid, beside the pharynx Tonsil, quinsy, teeth External, at the level of the hyoid Retropharyngeal Between buccopharyngeal and alar fascia, skull base to about T1 to T6; split by a midline raphe Nasopharynx and sinuses (retropharyngeal nodes in children) Through the mouth if small and confined; otherwise external, medial to the carotid sheath Danger space Between alar"],["osec","th:op-76~s2-2","Reading the retropharynx","Deep Neck Abscesses · Anatomy you must see","A retropharyngeal abscess is classically to one side of the midline , because the raphe tethers it; but the raphe can break down, so a midline abscess can still be retropharyngeal. On a lateral neck X-ray the retropharyngeal soft tissue at C2 should be about 4 mm; over 7 mm is abnormal . At C6 up to 14 mm in children and 22 mm in adults is normal."],["osec","th:op-76~s3-1","Secure the airway","Deep Neck Abscesses · The operation, stage by stage","If the airway is threatened, perform an awake tracheostomy under local anaesthetic with the anaesthetist standing by, then give the general anaesthetic once the tube is in. Do not sedate first. In Ludwig's angina, intubation is often impossible because the tongue is pushed up and back and the floor of the mouth is swollen. Figure 3 Ludwig's angina: the tongue pushed upward, and swelling and redness of the submental skin. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 76.2 What you should see A secure airway before anything else is done."],["osec","th:op-76~s3-2","Use the CT as a map","Deep Neck Abscesses · The operation, stage by stage","Before you cut, list every pocket on the CT and decide how you will reach each one; plan the incisions around the spaces involved. Senior's pearl The commonest error is draining one pocket and leaving another. Count the loculations on the scan, then count them again in the wound. What you should see On the scan: each rim-enhancing collection named by its space, the side and position of the carotid sheath relative to it, the airway's calibre, and whether the collection tracks down toward the mediastinum. Mediastinal extension Collections in the retropharyngeal, danger and carotid spaces descend into the chest. A scan that stops at the clavicles misses mediastinitis; extend it and involve the thoracic surgeons."],["osec","th:op-76~s3-3","Choose the incisions","Deep Neck Abscesses · The operation, stage by stage","Ludwig's angina: a submental incision (often with bilateral submandibular incisions); the discharge is often thin and watery rather than frank pus. Submandibular space: a skin incision below the mandible. Parapharyngeal space: an incision at the level of the hyoid; find the posterior belly of digastric, retract it upward, and open the space bluntly above and behind it. Transoral drainage cannot reach the lower part of this space. Retropharyngeal space: an incision placed to reach the lowest pocket, often one or two fingerbreadths above the clavicle when it runs toward the mediastinum; dissect deep and medial to the carotid sheath . A third or fourth branchial pouch abscess with thyroiditis may need a thyroid lobectomy and the tract followed up to the piriform fossa. Several spaces: two or more staggered horizontal incisions, sometimes on both sides. Figure 4 The external approach to the parapharyngeal space at the level of the hyoid, beneath the posterior belly of digastric. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 76.4 The great vessels and nerves The carotid sheath, the vagus, the hypoglossal and accessory nerves and the marginal mandibular nerve all lie close to these spaces, distorted by swelling. Open the spaces bluntly with a finger or closed instrument, and do not use muscle relaxants. What you should see A horizontal incision at least two fingerbreadths below the mandible for submandibular access, placed over the most dependent part of the pocket you are draining."],["osec","th:op-76~s3-4","Open, sample and break down the pockets","Deep Neck Abscesses · The operation, stage by stage","When pus appears, take samples for Gram stain and aerobic, anaerobic and fungal culture, with the anaerobic sample protected from air. Using the CT as your guide, and gentle palpation inside the cavity, open every loculation. What you should see One open cavity with no remaining walls, the full extent seen on CT reached. A clot beside the artery A blood clot near the carotid in an infected field may mean the artery has ruptured, from bacterial toxins eroding its wall. Stop, pack, and call the vascular surgeons into theatre."],["osec","th:op-76~s3-5","Irrigate and drain","Deep Neck Abscesses · The operation, stage by stage","Wash out the cavity with large volumes of saline until the fluid runs clear. Place half-inch Penrose drains into every incision and stitch them to the skin so they cannot fall out. Leave the wounds open to heal by secondary intention, and apply an absorbent dressing that protects the skin. What you should see Clear effluent, a drain lying in the depth of every pocket you listed on the scan, and a patent airway with the swelling no longer tense. Closing the wound A tightly closed wound over an infected space re-collects. Leave it open or loosely approximated around the drains. Senior's pearl Send pus for aerobic and anaerobic culture before you irrigate; antibiotics chosen blindly are the reason some of these patients come back to theatre."],["osec","th:op-76~s3-6","Treat the source","Deep Neck Abscesses · The operation, stage by stage","Extract the offending tooth or teeth (with the dental or maxillofacial team), drain an associated peritonsillar abscess, and plan treatment for any branchial anomaly or other cause. What you should see The causative tooth out, or the tonsil, gland or tract identified and dealt with or planned. Senior's pearl If the patient is not better in 48 hours (fever, white count, swelling), rescan before you blame the antibiotics: the usual cause is an undrained pocket."],["osec","th:op-76~s4-1","Crisis drills","Deep Neck Abscesses · When it goes wrong","Still septic after drainage 1 Look for rising white count, fever, sepsis, a new collection on repeat CT 2 Act involve the infectious diseases team, adjust antibiotics to cultures, extract any remaining dental source 3 Then return to theatre if the scan shows a collection Spiking fever with a tender, swollen neck after a sore throat 1 Suspect Lemierre syndrome septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein, usually Fusobacterium necrophorum, with septic emboli to the lungs and joints 2 Confirm contrast CT a thrombosed vein with a rim of enhancement (the \"O\" sign) 3 Treat prolonged intravenous antibiotics drain associated abscesses anticoagulation is often used, though the evidence is weak ligation of the vein is no longer routine Dusky, numb skin with crepitus 1 Suspect necrotising fasciitis subcutaneous gas, skin anaesthesia, rapid spread 2 Act urgent wide debridement back to healthy, bleeding tissue leave the wound open repeat debridement as needed 3 Note mortality around 23%, over 40% if it reaches the mediastinum reconstruct only after the infection has settled Sudden neck swelling, bruising and falling blood pressure 1 Suspect carotid rupture from infection 2 Act pressure, resuscitation, airway vascular or interventional radiology team immediately"],["osec","th:op-76~s4-2","On the scan","Deep Neck Abscesses · When it goes wrong","Figure 5 A thrombosed internal jugular vein after an untreated quinsy (left), and a multiloculated parapharyngeal abscess with the \"O\" sign of jugular thrombosis (right, arrow). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 76.5"],["osec","th:op-76~s5-1","After the operation","Deep Neck Abscesses · Aftercare","Intensive care if needed for ventilation, sepsis, and pain control and sedation. Change dressings several times a day while the discharge is heavy. Check the microbiology daily and change antibiotics as cultures return. Withdraw the Penrose drains gradually as drainage falls; the wounds heal by secondary intention. Repeat washouts for heavy continuing discharge. Assess swallowing, especially in the elderly; speech therapy and nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding when needed. Remove the tracheostomy once the swelling has settled and the infection has resolved."],["osec","th:op-76~s5-2","Pathogens","Deep Neck Abscesses · Aftercare","The teeth are the commonest source; a source is never found in about half. Children: mostly aerobes, especially Streptococcus pyogenes and Staphylococcus aureus . Adults and odontogenic infections: anaerobes are common (Prevotella, Peptostreptococcus, Fusobacterium, Porphyromonas, Bacteroides); 30% to 40% are mixed. Beta-lactamase producers (S. aureus, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis, Prevotella, Porphyromonas) can protect other organisms from penicillins."],["osec","th:op-76~s5-3","Complications","Deep Neck Abscesses · Aftercare","Complications of deep neck infection Complication Notes Airway obstruction Commonest killer; secure early Mediastinitis Through the retropharyngeal and danger spaces Lemierre syndrome Jugular thrombophlebitis with septic emboli Necrotising fasciitis Diabetes, alcohol, obesity, age, vascular disease Carotid erosion and rupture Bleeding, bruising, shock Cranial nerve palsy IX to XII in the parapharyngeal space; marginal mandibular Recurrent or persistent abscess Missed pockets, untreated source Dysphagia Retro- and parapharyngeal dissection, oedema"],["osec","th:op-76~s5-4","The mimic: calcific tendinitis of longus colli","Deep Neck Abscesses · Aftercare","Severe neck pain and stiffness with painful swallowing and swelling of the back wall of the nasopharynx, but without fever or a raised white count (ESR and CRP may be up). CT shows calcification in front of C1 to C2 with retropharyngeal oedema and no enlarged nodes. It is not an abscess: treat with anti-inflammatory drugs (or steroids), and it resolves in 1 to 2 weeks. Operating on it is a mistake. Figure 6 Calcific tendinitis of longus colli: thickened retropharyngeal tissue at C1 to C2 and the typical calcification. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 76.6"],["ev","th:op-76~s5-5","Deep Neck Abscesses: current evidence","Deep Neck Abscesses · Aftercare","Imaging. Contrast CT is standard, but a rim-enhancing collection is pus in only about three-quarters of cases, so a small collection in a stable patient, especially a child, can be treated with intravenous antibiotics first, with surgery if there is no improvement within 24 to 48 hours. Airway. Ludwig's angina and parapharyngeal infection with airway compromise need early airway control, ideally awake fibreoptic intubation or awake tracheostomy under local anaesthetic. Antibiotics must cover streptococci, anaerobes and S. aureus ; diabetes, intravenous drug use and dental infection are common risk factors."],["osec","th:op-76~s5-5","Alternatives and evidence","Deep Neck Abscesses · Aftercare","Ultrasound-guided aspiration can work for well-defined, single-pocket abscesses in selected patients, with shorter stays. Transoral drainage suits children and small abscesses confined to the retropharyngeal space, but not those spreading into the parapharyngeal space or needing revision. Many children are first treated with intravenous antibiotics; in adults, delaying surgery beyond two days more than doubled morbidity and mortality in one large study. Reasonable indications for surgery in children include airway compromise, complications, an abscess over about 2.2 cm, age under 4, and needing intensive care."],["osec","th:op-76~s6-1","Where the books differ","Deep Neck Abscesses · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra separates the acute retropharyngeal abscess of children (suppurating retropharyngeal nodes, which atrophy after about age 5) from the chronic, tuberculous retropharyngeal or prevertebral abscess from cervical spine caries or tuberculous nodes, which is drained externally behind the sternocleidomastoid and not through the mouth; describes Ludwig's angina in terms of the sublingual, submandibular and submental spaces, the first molar and premolar roots lying above the mylohyoid line, and tracheostomy when the airway is threatened. Lee describes Lemierre syndrome from Fusobacterium necrophorum in young adults, spreading from the oropharynx to the parapharyngeal space and internal jugular vein, with septic emboli to lungs, joints, liver and spleen, and sensitivity to clindamycin, metronidazole and penicillin; notes that retropharyngeal nodes atrophy after about age 5. Myers gives the surgical plan: CT as a map, awake tracheostomy without sedation, the incision for each space, finding the parapharyngeal space beneath the digastric, reaching the retropharynx medial to the carotid sheath, Penrose drains and open wounds, and the variants including necrotising fasciitis, carotid rupture and longus colli tendinitis."],["osec","th:op-76~s6-2","Viva essentials","Deep Neck Abscesses · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three layers of deep cervical fascia and the spaces they make. Which teeth drain into which space, and why (the mylohyoid line). Ludwig's angina : definition and airway management. The retropharyngeal, danger and prevertebral spaces and how far each extends. Normal retropharyngeal thickness at C2 and C6. The approach to the parapharyngeal space . Lemierre syndrome and necrotising fasciitis . Longus colli tendinitis as a mimic."],["osec","th:op-76~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Deep Neck Abscesses · For the examination","Common pitfalls Sedating or laying flat a patient with a threatened airway. Sending an unstable airway to CT. Draining one pocket and missing others. Draining a parapharyngeal abscess through the mouth only. Forgetting the dental source. Missing a malignant fistula in a patient with previous cancer. Operating on calcific tendinitis."],["osec","th:op-78~s1-1","The case at a glance","Open Thyroidectomy · Operating note","Open thyroidectomy Summary Total thyroidectomy Differentiated cancer over 4 cm, with gross extrathyroidal extension, contralateral nodules, nodal or distant metastases; multinodular goitre after neck irradiation or with a first-degree family history of thyroid cancer; medullary carcinoma; resectable anaplastic carcinoma; bilateral indeterminate nodules; compressive multinodular goitre; Graves disease or hyperthyroidism not controlled medically Lobectomy Papillary or follicular cancer under 4 cm that is low risk, single, confined to the gland, with no irradiation and no nodes (1 to 4 cm: lobectomy or total are both options); a single indeterminate nodule (AUS, follicular neoplasm, suspicious), unless the patient prefers a total to avoid a second operation; a benign nodule over 3 to 4 cm; a compressive nodule Contraindications Severe uncontrolled hyperthyroidism (relative: risk of thyroid storm); pregnancy (relative: delay to after delivery, or operate in the second trimester if the nodule grows); prohibitive medical problems Anaesthesia General with an endotracheal tube (a monitoring tube with surface electrodes if nerve monitoring is used); local with sedation is possible in pregnancy or poor cardiac function Position Supine, shoulder roll to extend the neck, head on a ring, back up 10 to 30 degrees, arms tucked and padded; prepare the neck and upper chest (whole chest if sternotomy is possible) Antibiotics Not needed for clean thyroid surgery; a single dose of second-generation cephalosporin or clindamycin is acceptable Monitoring Nerve monitoring optional: an aid to findi"],["osec","th:op-78~s1-2","Consent","Open Thyroidectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: removal of half (lobectomy) or all (total) of the thyroid through a short incision in a skin crease at the base of the neck. Voice change: temporary hoarseness or weakness is common; permanent damage to the voice-box nerve is uncommon. If both nerves stop working, breathing can be affected and a tracheostomy may be needed. Loss of high notes and voice projection from the smaller nerve to the voice box. Low calcium after a total thyroidectomy, causing tingling and cramps, treated with calcium tablets; rarely permanent. Bleeding under the wound , usually in the first hours, which can press on the airway and is an emergency. Lifelong thyroid hormone tablets after a total thyroidectomy, and possibly after a lobectomy. A second operation may be needed if a lobectomy specimen shows cancer. Scar , infection, swallowing discomfort and numbness of the neck skin."],["osec","th:op-78~s1-3","Before you start","Open Thyroidectomy · Operating note","1.3.1 The history The nodule: when it appeared, how fast it is growing (rapid growth of a new or previously stable nodule is worrying), and pain (more often thyroiditis or bleeding into a benign nodule than cancer). Compression: breathlessness, especially lying flat, and persistent dysphagia. Invasion: coughing blood, skin fixation, hoarseness from a paralysed fold. Thyroid function: palpitations, weight change, fatigue, anxiety, poor sleep, menstrual change. Risk factors for cancer: a solitary nodule under age 20 (about 20% malignant) or at 20 to 40 (5% to 10%); men (fewer nodules, more often malignant); two or more first-degree relatives with thyroid cancer; low-dose neck irradiation; Hashimoto thyroiditis (more papillary cancer and lymphoma); features of MEN 2 (phaeochromocytoma, hyperparathyroidism, marfanoid habitus, mucosal neuromas); Gardner and Cowden syndromes. 1.3.2 The examination Palpate the gland for size, shape, firmness, movement on swallowing and fixation; feel the neck for nodes; listen for hoarseness and stridor. Examine the vocal folds in every patient : the voice can be normal with a paralysed fold. 1.3.3 Tests TSH first, to exclude hyper- or hypothyroidism. Thyroglobulin does not separate benign from malignant before surgery. Calcitonin only with a family history of medullary cancer or MEN 2, or medullary cytology. Ultrasound: worrying features are a solid or complex nodule, irregular margins, increased internal vascularity and especially microcalcification ; cystic and spongiform nodules are usually benign. Nuclear scan only if TSH is low: a hyperfunct"],["osec","th:op-78~s2-1","The gland","Open Thyroidectomy · Anatomy you must see","It develops from the floor of the pharynx at the foramen caecum and descends along the thyroglossal duct in front of the hyoid to lie over the second to fourth tracheal rings , within the pretracheal (middle) layer of deep cervical fascia. Lobes about 4 to 5 cm tall; isthmus 12 to 15 mm, absent in 5% to 10%; pyramidal lobe rising from the isthmus in 40% to 50%. Tubercle of Zuckerkandl: a posterolateral projection of each lobe (63% to 78%); the recurrent nerve lies deep to it in 98%. Berry's ligament: the posterior suspensory ligament binding the gland to the cricoid and first two tracheal rings; the nerve lies within 3 mm of it, usually laterally, sometimes through it."],["osec","th:op-78~s2-2","The vessels","Open Thyroidectomy · Anatomy you must see","Superior thyroid artery: first branch of the external carotid. Inferior thyroid artery: from the thyrocervical trunk. Thyroidea ima in 2% to 12%, to the lower isthmus. Superior and middle thyroid veins drain to the internal jugular; inferior veins to the brachiocephalic veins."],["osec","th:op-78~s2-3","The recurrent laryngeal nerve","Open Thyroidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The left loops under the aortic arch and rises in the tracheo-oesophageal groove; the right loops under the subclavian artery and runs a more oblique, lateral course. It passes under the inferior constrictor and behind the cricothyroid joint to enter the larynx below the cricoid. Simon's triangle: carotid laterally, oesophagus medially, inferior thyroid artery above; the nerve lies within it. Relation to the inferior thyroid artery varies, and differs between sides in about half. It often branches below the cricoid (40% to 65% more than 5 mm below it); every branch must be preserved, the anterior branch carrying the motor fibres. Non-recurrent nerve in up to 0.7%, almost always on the right with an aberrant subclavian artery, running directly into the larynx from higher in the neck."],["osec","th:op-78~s2-4","The superior laryngeal nerve","Open Thyroidectomy · Anatomy you must see","Arises from the vagus near the skull base and divides about 2 cm above the upper pole. The internal branch pierces the thyrohyoid membrane (sensation of the supraglottis); the external branch supplies the cricothyroid (pitch and projection) and the inferior constrictor. The external branch runs through the sternothyrolaryngeal (Joll's) triangle , usually deep to the superior thyroid artery, and in about a third lies at or below the top of the upper pole, where it can be caught in the pedicle."],["osec","th:op-78~s2-5","The parathyroids","Open Thyroidectomy · Anatomy you must see","Usually supplied by the inferior thyroid artery ; the superior glands sometimes by the superior thyroid artery. The inferior glands are often close to where the recurrent nerve passes. What you should see Upper gland: behind the upper pole near the cricothyroid joint, dorsal to the plane of the nerve. Lower gland: on or near the lower pole, ventral to the nerve, often in the thyrothymic ligament. A tan-yellow gland that stays pink after you have moved it; a gland turning dark has lost its supply. Devascularised glands The parathyroids' end-arteries come from the inferior thyroid artery. Tying its trunk laterally, or stripping a gland off the capsule, leaves it dead even though it is still in the neck."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-1","Mark and incise","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","Mark a 3 to 5 cm horizontal incision in a skin crease between the cricoid and the sternal notch, usually 2 to 3 cm above the notch; place it higher in young patients, because scars drift down and a scar below the clavicles looks poor. Inject 0.25% bupivacaine with 1:200,000 adrenaline above and below the line. Cut the dermis with a size 15 blade and the platysma with diathermy, avoiding the anterior jugular veins. What you should see The incision in a natural crease, symmetrical about the midline, with the platysma divided and the anterior jugular veins intact beneath it. Senior's pearl Mark the crease with the patient sitting up before induction; once the neck is extended on the table, creases move and an incision that looked central ends up too low."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-2","Skin flaps and skin protection","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","Raise the flaps deep to platysma, keeping the anterior jugular veins and investing fascia down on the straps, as far as the thyroid notch above and the sternal notch below, tying larger veins with 2-0 silk. Protect the skin edges, for example with strips held by a running locking silk suture, so that hot instruments and retractors do not burn them. Figure 1 The skin edges protected by a running locking silk suture after the subplatysmal flaps are raised. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 78.1 What you should see Clean flaps in the right plane, with the anterior jugular veins left on the strap muscles."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-3","Divide the strap muscles","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","Find the midline raphe and divide it from the thyroid notch to the sternal notch, then lift the straps off the gland with a Freer elevator, peanut or diathermy. If the straps are invaded by tumour, take them with a margin. Figure 2 Dividing the strap muscles in the midline. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 78.2 What you should see The pale avascular midline raphe, then the thyroid capsule with its fine vessels shining through as the straps lift off. Sternothyroid peeled off the lobe out to its lateral edge. The wrong plane Staying between the straps and the true capsule is bloodless. Dissecting into the capsule's veins stains the field you need clean for the nerve and the parathyroids."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-4","The superior pedicle","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","Retract the straps up and laterally to show the upper pole (in a large goitre, the sternothyroid may need dividing). Dissect the vessels of the superior pedicle all the way round with a right-angle clamp, confirm the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve is not in the bundle, and seal the pedicle with the ultrasonic device close to the gland , at two or three points before dividing it. A cut stump retracts up into the neck, where bleeding is hard to control; add clips or ties if needed. Figure 3 Sealing the superior vascular pedicle close to the upper pole with the ultrasonic scalpel. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 78.3 What you should see Separate, skeletonised vessels at the upper pole, with no nerve running across them. The external branch Taking the pedicle in a mass, or high above the pole, catches the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. The patient loses the high notes and the power of the voice, which is easily dismissed and hard to treat."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-5","Mobilise the lobe","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","Roll the lobe forward and medially with a peanut and Babcock forceps; seal and divide the middle thyroid vein . What you should see The lobe rolling medially, the middle thyroid vein stretched across to the internal jugular, and the carotid sheath lateral to it. The middle thyroid vein It is short and thin-walled and tears off the jugular with forceful retraction. Seal and divide it before you roll the lobe. Senior's pearl Traction on the lobe opens the tracheo-oesophageal groove for you. Steady medial and anterior traction, not more dissection, is what brings the nerve into view."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-6","Find and follow the recurrent nerve","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","With the gland rotated medially, use a McCabe dissector in the tracheo-oesophageal groove and near the cricothyroid joint to find the nerve. Open the tips only to spread; never close them while buried in tissue. Once found, dissect directly on top of the nerve , parallel to it, from below up to where it enters the larynx under the inferior constrictor, protecting every branch . Figure 4 The recurrent laryngeal nerve found and followed with a McCabe dissector, working directly on the nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 78.4 Figure 5 Following the recurrent nerve along its course showed two branches; both must be traced and protected before the gland is lifted off the trachea. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 78.6 What you should see A white, glistening nerve with a vessel on its surface, running up to the cricothyroid joint, and any branches traced to the larynx. Senior's pearl The best protection for the nerve is to see it and keep seeing it. Find it before the gland is lifted off the trachea, follow every branch, and remember that the anterior branch is the one that moves the fold."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-7","The parathyroids","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","As you work along the capsule, find the parathyroids (small, tan-yellow, with their own vessels) and push them laterally with their blood supply instead of dissecting them off. A gland that turns dark or has lost its vessels is removed, confirmed as parathyroid by frozen section of a small piece, finely minced and placed in a pocket in the sternocleidomastoid or a strap muscle. Never use the sucker near the nerve or the parathyroids. What you should see A tan-yellow gland that stays pink on its own vessels after it is pushed laterally: upper gland dorsal to the nerve near the cricothyroid joint, lower gland ventral to it near the lower pole."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-8","Berry's ligament and the inferior pole","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","With the nerve in view and gently held away with a moist peanut (the ultrasonic blade and the hot fluid it creates can injure it), divide Berry's ligament. Leaving a tiny cuff of thyroid on the nerve is better than injuring it. Look for early branching of the nerve near the lower pole, then seal the inferior vessels close to the gland so the inferior parathyroid keeps its supply. Lift the lobe off the trachea along the pretracheal fascia, keeping the active blade away from the trachea. Figure 6 Lifting the lobe off the trachea with the ultrasonic scalpel while a peanut protects the recurrent nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 78.5 Heat near the nerve Ultrasonic and diathermy instruments spread heat sideways. Keep the active blade facing away from the nerve and the trachea, and keep a peanut between them. What you should see The nerve seen along its whole course into the larynx beneath the inferior constrictor, with any anterior branch preserved, before the ligament is cut. The lobe free on the trachea with the inferior parathyroid left behind on its vessels."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-9","Divide the isthmus","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","Divide the gland at the junction of the isthmus and the opposite lobe, and remove any pyramidal lobe up to the thyroid notch. What you should see The front of the trachea bare from the cricoid down, with no pyramidal lobe left attached above the isthmus. The trachea Divide the isthmus with the plane between it and the trachea opened first; an ultrasonic blade resting on the tracheal wall can burn through it."],["osec","th:op-78~s3-10","Haemostasis and closure","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: lobectomy","Irrigate and ask the anaesthetist for a Valsalva to show bleeding; control it with bipolar. A haemostatic gauze can help; a drain is not usually needed. Close the straps with 3-0 absorbable sutures, leaving a small gap low down so that a haematoma can escape forward rather than compress the airway, then the platysma, and the skin with a 4-0 subcuticular suture and strips. What you should see A dry bed during Valsalva, the nerve and parathyroids intact, and a neat closure."],["osec","th:op-78~s4-1","The second side","Open Thyroidectomy · The operation: total thyroidectomy","Remove the other lobe in exactly the same way, taking even more care with the parathyroids: handling them on both sides is what causes low calcium afterwards. Inspect and feel the thyroid bed for suspicious nodes or remaining thyroid, and send them to pathology. What you should see An intact, stimulating nerve on the first side before you begin the second. At least one, and ideally all, of the parathyroids pink and in place at the end. Both nerves If the signal is lost or the nerve is in doubt on the first side, stop and stage the second lobe: bilateral palsy means a tracheostomy. Senior's pearl In a total thyroidectomy, treat every parathyroid as if it were the last one. 4.1.1 The neck in differentiated cancer Remove the central (level VI) nodes if they are suspicious on imaging or at surgery. Do a selective or modified radical lateral neck dissection only for biopsy-proven lateral nodes; never pick out single nodes. Prophylactic central neck dissection in a node-negative neck has not reduced recurrence or improved survival in meta-analyses and increases low calcium; it is not routinely advised, except possibly for T3 or T4 tumours or with involved lateral nodes. 4.1.2 Invasion of the airway If tumour is stuck to the cartilage, shaving off all visible disease is an option; full-thickness en bloc resection lowers recurrence but has not shown better survival."],["osec","th:op-78~s5-1","Crisis drills","Open Thyroidectomy · When it goes wrong","Neck haematoma 1 Recognise usually within hours swelling, tight dressing, breathlessness, difficulty swallowing, stridor 2 Act at the bedside, open the skin and strap sutures at once and scoop out the clot to relieve the airway call for senior and anaesthetic help 3 Then return to theatre to find and control the bleeding Stridor after extubation following total thyroidectomy 1 Suspect bilateral recurrent nerve palsy (folds near the midline), haematoma, or laryngeal oedema 2 Act reintubate examine the folds 3 Then if both folds are paralysed, tracheostomy or a period of intubation awaiting recovery A recurrent nerve is cut during surgery 1 Act repair it there and then, end to end without tension (or an ansa cervicalis transfer): this restores tone and limits atrophy, though not movement 2 Then consider early medialisation of the paralysed fold Tingling, cramps or a positive Chvostek sign 1 Recognise low calcium after total or completion thyroidectomy (check PTH and calcium, and magnesium) 2 Mild oral calcium carbonate with vitamin D (calcitriol) 3 Severe intravenous calcium gluconate over 10 to 15 minutes with cardiac monitoring Hyperthyroid patient becomes hot, fast and agitated 1 Suspect thyroid storm, mostly in uncontrolled hyperthyroidism 2 Act cool, beta-blocker, antithyroid drugs, steroids, intensive care support"],["osec","th:op-78~s6-1","After the operation","Open Thyroidectomy · Aftercare","After total or completion thyroidectomy, check PTH about an hour after surgery; keep the patient overnight if it is low (under about 15 pg/ml), and remember calcium can take over 12 hours to fall. Pain: ibuprofen or paracetamol, with opioids for breakthrough pain. Wound: may get wet after 48 hours; review at a week and replace the strips. Thyroid hormone for life after a total thyroidectomy; if radioiodine is planned, hormone may be withheld (or short-acting liothyronine used and stopped 2 weeks before) so that TSH rises. Radioiodine depends on the final pathology and metastases."],["osec","th:op-78~s6-2","Complications","Open Thyroidectomy · Aftercare","Complications of thyroidectomy Complication Features and management Haematoma Within hours; open at the bedside, then theatre Recurrent nerve palsy Hoarse, breathy voice, aspiration; fold paramedian; voice therapy, injection or medialisation Bilateral palsy Good voice but stridor after extubation; reintubation, tracheostomy External branch of superior laryngeal nerve Poor projection and loss of high pitch; voice therapy Hypoparathyroidism Temporary or permanent; calcium and vitamin D; IV calcium for severe symptoms Thyroid storm Uncontrolled hyperthyroidism Infection, poor scar Uncommon; protect skin edges"],["ev","th:op-78~s6-3","Open Thyroidectomy: current evidence","Open Thyroidectomy · Aftercare","Nerve monitoring. The International Neural Monitoring Study Group standardised intraoperative monitoring (Randolph et al., Laryngoscope 2011); meta-analyses have not shown a clear reduction in permanent nerve palsy compared with visual identification alone, but loss of signal on the first side allows staged surgery to avoid bilateral palsy. Parathyroids. In the PARAFLUO randomised trial, near-infrared autofluorescence to identify parathyroid glands reduced temporary postoperative hypocalcaemia (Benmiloud et al., JAMA Surg 2020). Haematoma. UK multidisciplinary guidelines recommend early warning signs, a bedside kit, and the SCOOP sequence for an expanding haematoma: skin exposure, cut sutures, open skin, open muscles, pack (Iliff et al., Anaesthesia 2022). Calcium. A day-one PTH level guides calcium supplementation and early discharge."],["osec","th:op-78~s6-3","Alternatives","Open Thyroidectomy · Aftercare","Endoscopic or robotic thyroidectomy through remote incisions; observation with ultrasound every 6 to 12 months for low-risk nodules; radioiodine for toxic nodules and Graves disease; chemotherapy and radiotherapy for anaplastic carcinoma."],["osec","th:op-78~s7-1","Where the books differ","Open Thyroidectomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra covers the thyroid from the ENT side, with the recurrent laryngeal nerve in the context of vocal fold paralysis: bilateral abductor paralysis after thyroidectomy leaves the folds near the midline, with a good voice but stridor and breathlessness needing tracheostomy; it describes the suspensory ligaments, residual thyroid in Berry's ligament as the cause of uptake and raised thyroglobulin after surgery, and ligation of the superior vessels close to the upper pole to protect the external branch, whose injury lowers and bows the fold and loses pitch. Lee gives the Chvostek sign as the most reliable sign of hypocalcaemia, with Trousseau sign and a prolonged QT, and the treatment: intravenous calcium gluconate 1 g over 10 to 15 minutes with cardiac monitoring, checking magnesium, then oral calcium with calcitriol; mild transient hypocalcaemia may not need treatment. Myers adds the full work-up (Bethesda, ultrasound features, molecular markers), indications for lobectomy versus total thyroidectomy, the anatomy of both laryngeal nerves with their landmarks and variants, the step-by-step operation with the ultrasonic scalpel, the 1-hour PTH, and the evidence against routine prophylactic central neck dissection."],["osec","th:op-78~s7-2","Viva essentials","Open Thyroidectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Bethesda categories and their risks; worrying ultrasound features. Indications for lobectomy versus total thyroidectomy . The course of both recurrent nerves , the non-recurrent nerve, and where to find the nerve (Simon's triangle, Zuckerkandl's tubercle, Berry's ligament). The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve , Joll's triangle, and why you tie the pedicle close to the gland. Parathyroid preservation and autotransplantation. The bleeding neck drill. Hypocalcaemia : signs, the PTH check, treatment. Prophylactic central neck dissection : the evidence."],["osec","th:op-78~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Open Thyroidectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not examining the vocal folds before surgery. Taking the superior pedicle in a mass or high above the pole. Lifting the gland off the trachea before seeing the nerve. Missing a branch of the nerve. Closing a dissector's tips buried in tissue. Using the sucker near the nerve or parathyroids. Stripping the parathyroids of their blood supply. Burning the skin edges. Closing the straps tightly with no escape for a haematoma."],["osec","th:op-80~s1-1","The case at a glance","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Operating note","Robotic-assisted thyroidectomy Summary Indications Nodule under 5 cm ; BMI under 35 ; no previous neck surgery; small T1 to T2 well-differentiated papillary or follicular cancer Contraindications Graves' disease ; substernal extension; locally advanced cancer invading the recurrent nerve, tracheo-oesophageal complex or straps; known central or lateral nodal metastasis; medullary cancer Relative Disease needing primary total thyroidectomy (sizeable bilateral multinodular goitre, cancer); suspected central nodes in cancer Approaches Lateral: retroauricular (shorter distance, intuitive for head and neck surgeons, no brachial plexus risk, two working arms) or transaxillary (longer distance, a third arm possible, rare brachial neuropathy, about 2%); midline: bilateral axillo-breast Anaesthesia Laryngeal nerve monitoring tube, placed to the side opposite the operation; circuit toward the feet Position Transaxillary: arm extended, elbow flexed and internally rotated to open the axilla without tension on the plexus , pressure points padded. Retroauricular: arms tucked, head turned away. No shoulder roll Antibiotics None; povidone-iodine skin preparation Robot da Vinci Si; 10 mm 30 degree camera facing down; 8 mm harmonic shears and an 8 mm atraumatic ProGrasp (or 5 mm Maryland) Assistant Countertraction; retracting sternomastoid (retroauricular); long-tip bipolar; smoke suction; preventing arm collisions Tray Long-tip monopolar and bipolar; lighted retractors; headlight; Chung retractor; rolled cotton pledgets; Yankauer suction Skills Experience of open thyroidectomy; robot credent"],["osec","th:op-80~s1-2","Consent","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove half (or all) of the thyroid through a hidden incision behind the ear or in the armpit, avoiding a scar on the neck. Voice change from injury to the recurrent or superior laryngeal nerve. Low calcium if both sides are removed. Bleeding or a collection under the skin. Numbness of the chest wall (armpit approach) or around the ear (ear approach), usually temporary; swelling of the flap for 1 to 2 weeks. Rare injuries to the windpipe, gullet or major neck vessels; arm weakness from stretching of the arm nerves (armpit approach). Conversion to a standard neck incision if needed, which is rare with careful selection. The standard open operation is always an alternative."],["osec","th:op-80~s1-3","Before you start","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Operating note","Ask about previous thyroid or neck surgery, thyroid status (hypo- or hyperthyroid), neck or shoulder problems and keloid or hypertrophic scarring. Record BMI, nodule size and whether nodules are on one or both sides, the range of movement of the neck and shoulder, and vocal fold function by laryngoscopy ."],["osec","th:op-80~s1-4","Investigations","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Operating note","Ultrasound by a specialist: gland size, number of nodules, suspicious features (taller than wide, hypoechoic, peripheral vascularity, microcalcifications, irregular margins) and suspicious central nodes. FNA classified by the Bethesda system; molecular markers for indeterminate nodules (follicular lesion of undetermined significance, follicular neoplasm, suspicious for malignancy) can support an upfront total thyroidectomy rather than diagnostic lobectomy and a later completion. Thyroid scan only for a suspected hot nodule causing thyrotoxicosis. Discuss the approach with the patient: the authors have no fixed preference."],["osec","th:op-80~s2-1","Landmarks","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The omohyoid: find it first; the carotid sheath and internal jugular vein lie deep and medial to its plane. The trachea: the medial limit of dissection. The cricothyroid muscle: marks where the recurrent nerve enters the larynx. The inferior thyroid artery: ligated distally to protect the parathyroid supply; its relation to the recurrent nerve is too inconsistent to use as a landmark."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-1","The transaxillary flap","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Make a 4 to 5 cm incision 5 to 8 mm behind the anterior axillary fold so the scar hides behind it. Raise the flap over pectoralis major to the clavicle, then subplatysmally over the clavicular and sternal heads of sternomastoid to the thyroid cartilage and just past the midline, 12 to 14 cm from the incision. Figure 1 The modified arm position for transaxillary thyroidectomy, the elbow flexed to avoid tension on the axilla. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 80.1 What you should see The flap raised on pectoralis fascia, then under platysma, to the midline of the neck."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-2","The retroauricular flap","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Make a 4 to 5 cm incision about 5 to 8 mm behind the hairline , infiltrated with lidocaine and 1:80,000 adrenaline, and raise a subplatysmal flap toward the suprasternal notch (the future retractor axis) over sternomastoid, preserving the great auricular nerve and external jugular vein , medially past the midline raphe, down to the clavicle and up over the parotid tail to the ramus, 10 to 12 cm from the incision, protecting the marginal mandibular nerve . Figure 2 The planned retroauricular incision (arrow). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 80.2 What you should see The sternocleidomastoid, the great auricular nerve and the external jugular vein on its surface, under the flap."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-3","Build the working space","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Work under direct vision with a long-tip monopolar and a headlight; once the flap is about 5 cm long, bring in lighted retractors, with the Yankauer for smoke and countertraction. What you should see The omohyoid, and the gap between the two heads of sternocleidomastoid (or its anterior border) leading to the straps. Too deep too soon Going deep to the omohyoid prematurely risks the internal jugular vein and its branches. Find the omohyoid first. Perforating the flap Inadequate countertraction while elevating the skin flap can buttonhole it."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-4","Retract and check the height","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Lift sternohyoid off the thyroid bed and insert the Chung retractor under it, fixed to the table. When the flap is suspended, the vertical height from the base of the incision to the flap must be over 5 cm : the robot must not come in until the space is adequate. Figure 3 Adequate working space in the retroauricular approach: the height of elevation over 5 cm (arrow); great auricular nerve (GAN), sternomastoid (SCM). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 80.3 What you should see The thyroid lobe under the lifted straps, in a space more than 5 cm high."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-5","Dock","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Bring the robot in from the opposite side at 30 to 45 degrees to the table, at about the level of the shoulder. Dock the 10 mm camera in the midline and the two working arms at 30 to 45 degrees to it, an isosceles triangle that reduces collisions. The authors prefer the 8 mm atraumatic ProGrasp to the 5 mm Maryland, whose sharp tip can tear the thyroid and cause bleeding that hides the field. See the whole thyroid bed before starting. Figure 4 Docking the robotic arms in the retroauricular approach. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 80.4 Figure 5 The left thyroid bed exposed from the retroauricular approach. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 80.5 What you should see The lobe centred in the camera view with all instruments meeting on it."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-6","The superior pole","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Start at the superior pole, dividing sternothyroid if needed. Define lateral and medial planes, skeletonise the superior thyroid artery and ligate it with the harmonic shears close to the gland ; the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve is not routinely sought, but the magnified view often shows it. Identify the cricothyroid muscle . What you should see The superior vessels skeletonised on the gland with the cricothyroid muscle medial to them. The superior pole Dividing a large bundle of tissue risks incomplete sealing of the vessel and injury to a low-lying external branch (Cernea type 2b). Skeletonise each vessel first."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-7","The parathyroids","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Mobilise the gland from lateral to medial and reflect it toward the trachea, dividing the middle thyroid vein. Free the superior parathyroid with the harmonic shears, then at the inferior pole ligate the inferior thyroid artery as distally as possible and free the inferior parathyroid on its pedicle. What you should see Each parathyroid pink on its own pedicle, left on the bed."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-8","The recurrent nerve and Berry's ligament","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Expose the trachea to set the medial limit. With the gland reflected medially, find the recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheo-oesophageal groove, preferably near the cricothyroid joint , its most consistent position, avoiding the oesophagus. Trace it to Berry's ligament , ligating the small overlying vessels individually with the harmonic shears; lay a wet rolled cotton pledget over the nerve against heat, and use it to dissect bluntly. If the nerve is closely adherent, divide the ligament with fine bipolar. Mobilise the lobe off the trachea, divide the isthmus and remove the lobe. What you should see The white nerve with its vasa nervorum in the groove, followed to its entry under the constrictor, with the trachea medial. Berry's ligament Divide it only after the whole lobe is mobilised, in a near-bloodless field: bleeding here threatens the nerve and leads to haematoma. No sense of touch Without haptic feedback the console surgeon must positively identify the trachea and oesophagus; perforation of the trachea has been reported."],["osec","th:op-80~s3-9","Confirm and close","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Irrigate with warm saline and stimulate the nerve at 0.5 to 1 mA to confirm function. Close over a drain brought out at the edge of the incision. Figure 6 The right thyroid bed after robotic thyroidectomy: the recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN) and common carotid (CCA). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 80.6 What you should see A signal from the nerve at 0.5 to 1 mA and a dry bed."],["osec","th:op-80~s4-1","Crisis drills","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · When it goes wrong","Carotid injury 1 Act direct external pressure on the carotid while the neck is opened conventionally to expose the sheath Internal jugular vein injury 1 Minor tear bipolar or clips 2 Major the vein may have to be sacrificed for haemostasis convert to open if needed Delayed haematoma 1 Act general anaesthesia reopen through the same remote incision, without the robot 10 mm 0 and 30 degree endoscopes bipolar or harmonic shears"],["osec","th:op-80~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Too little countertraction, perforating the flap. Going deep to the omohyoid too early. Dividing a large bundle at the superior pole. Dividing Berry's ligament before the lobe is fully mobilised. Docking before the working space is adequate."],["osec","th:op-80~s5-1","After the operation","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Aftercare","Remove the drain when under 20 mL in 24 hours . No compression dressing is needed; some flap swelling settles within 1 to 2 weeks."],["osec","th:op-80~s5-2","Complications","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Aftercare","Wound infection; haematoma; recurrent laryngeal nerve injury; temporary numbness of the anterior chest (transaxillary) or around the ear (retroauricular); tracheal, oesophageal and vascular injury; brachial plexopathy (transaxillary, avoided by careful positioning)."],["ev","th:op-80~s5-3","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy: current evidence","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Aftercare","Transoral endoscopic thyroidectomy (TOETVA). In a series of 425 patients, the vestibular approach gave operating times, complication rates and nerve palsy rates similar to open surgery, with no visible scar (Anuwong et al., JAMA Surg 2018). Selection. Remote-access approaches suit small nodules and lobes in patients who want to avoid a neck scar; they take longer, cost more and carry approach-specific risks (mental nerve numbness, CO₂ embolism). Open surgery remains standard for large goitres, advanced cancer and central or lateral neck dissection."],["osec","th:op-80~s5-3","Alternatives","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · Aftercare","Open thyroidectomy should always be discussed. Conversion is rarely needed with proper selection, mainly for vascular injury."],["osec","th:op-80~s6-1","The evidence","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · For the examination","There are no randomised trials; evidence comes from large series in high-volume centres, and there is a learning curve. Lang and colleagues' meta-analysis found more temporary recurrent nerve palsy after robotic surgery, with no difference in permanent morbidity; Son and colleagues, who excluded series under 40 patients, found no differences in temporary or permanent nerve injury or hypocalcaemia, which suggests experience matters. Postoperative thyroglobulin did not differ after robotic and open surgery for cancer, but long-term oncological conclusions are premature. The editor calls it the current pinnacle of technological advance in thyroid surgery, set against a century of safe, reproducible open technique."],["osec","th:op-80~s6-2","Viva essentials","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Selection criteria and contraindications (Graves', substernal, medullary, nodes, invasion). Retroauricular versus transaxillary: distance, arms, brachial plexus risk. Positioning to protect the plexus. The working space: flap lengths, the 5 cm height, the Chung retractor. Docking angles. The omohyoid, cricothyroid and trachea as landmarks; why the inferior thyroid artery is not. The recurrent nerve near the cricothyroid joint; protecting it at Berry's ligament; stimulation. Managing vascular injury and haematoma."],["osec","th:op-80~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Robotic-Assisted Thyroidectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Offering it to a patient with a large goitre or high BMI. Promising no scar at all rather than no neck scar. Using a sharp grasper that tears the gland."],["osec","th:op-84~s1-1","The case at a glance","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Operating note","Parathyroidectomy for primary hyperparathyroidism Summary Indications Symptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism, including parathyroid crisis; familial disease; suspected parathyroid cancer; thyroid disease that can be treated at the same operation; and asymptomatic disease with any one of: age under 50, calcium more than 1 mg/dl above the upper limit of normal, bone density T score below -2.5, a vertebral fracture, creatinine clearance under 60 ml/min, 24-hour urine calcium over 400 mg, or kidney stones or nephrocalcinosis on imaging Contraindications A paralysed recurrent nerve on the opposite side; symptomatic cervical disc disease; comorbidity that rules out surgery Anaesthesia General with an endotracheal tube; a superficial cervical plexus block with sedation is possible for a well-localised single adenoma in an unoperated neck, with a real chance of converting to general anaesthesia Position Supine, head up 30 degrees, a small roll under the shoulder blades for slight neck extension Antibiotics Not required for clean neck surgery; at most a single dose before the incision Monitoring Standard; nerve monitoring is optional and has not been shown to reduce vocal fold weakness Imaging Localisation is for planning, never for diagnosis: ultrasound (also screens the thyroid) and sestamibi scan, best when they agree; SPECT-CT, four-dimensional CT or MRI when they do not; selective venous sampling, arteriography or ultrasound-guided aspiration for PTH in difficult or reoperative cases Tray Basic head and neck set; ultrasonic scalpel if thyroid surgery is planned; monopolar and "],["osec","th:op-84~s1-2","Consent","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: removing the overactive parathyroid gland or glands through a small incision low in the neck. Cure is achieved in about 95 to 97 in 100 at experienced centres; in up to 5 in 100 the high calcium persists or returns and further tests or surgery are needed. A larger operation on both sides may be needed if the hormone level does not fall, or if more than one gland is abnormal. Low calcium afterwards, causing tingling and cramps, treated with calcium and vitamin D tablets. Voice change from the nerve to the voice box: uncommon, and usually temporary. Bleeding needing a return to theatre in fewer than 1 in 100. Infection , a scar, and a temporary change in thyroid function. Rarely, the breastbone must be opened to reach a gland in the chest."],["osec","th:op-84~s1-3","Before you start","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Most patients have no symptoms , or vague ones: tiredness, weakness of the thighs and upper arms, aching bones, poor appetite, constipation. Kidneys: thirst, passing a lot of urine, stones. Bones: osteopenia, osteoporosis, fractures, and rarely osteitis fibrosa cystica with bone pain and brown tumours. Gut: peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, gallstones. Heart: hypertension, arrhythmia. Mind: depression, anxiety, poor concentration, psychosis. Compressive symptoms from thyroid disease. Previous neck surgery or neck irradiation. Family history: MEN 1 or 2A, familial isolated hyperparathyroidism, familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia. Drugs: lithium and thiazides cause mild hypercalcaemia. 1.3.2 The examination The neck is usually normal. Feel for thyroid nodules and nodes. A palpable neck mass with a high calcium suggests parathyroid carcinoma. Examine the vocal folds. 1.3.3 The biochemistry Measure calcium, PTH and vitamin D together : a raised calcium with a raised or inappropriately normal PTH makes the diagnosis. 24-hour urine calcium separates it from familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia , which is not helped by surgery. Low phosphate and a mild hyperchloraemic acidosis are common; check creatinine and urea. After gastric bypass, check that the patient can absorb calcium. Causes of primary hyperparathyroidism Cause Share Operation Single adenoma 80% to 90% Focused removal with PTH assay Hyperplasia of all glands 6% to 15% Subtotal (three and a half glands) or total with autotransplantation Double adenoma 2% to 5% Remove both Carcinoma Under 5% Suspect a firm,"],["osec","th:op-84~s2-1","Normal glands","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Anatomy you must see","About 85% of people have four : two superior (fourth pouch) and two inferior (third pouch, descending with the thymus). Up to 15% have more than four, and 1% to 3% of glands are ectopic. A normal gland is the size of a grain of rice or a lentil, weighs 30 to 50 mg , and is light brown against yellow fat. Glands move more freely than thyroid nodules or nodes."],["osec","th:op-84~s2-2","The superior glands","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Anatomy you must see","On the posterolateral surface of the upper two-thirds of the thyroid lobe, near the cricothyroid joint, within about 1 cm of where the recurrent nerve crosses the inferior thyroid artery . They lie behind the plane of the recurrent nerve. When they migrate, they fall backward and down : the tracheo-oesophageal groove, beside or behind the oesophagus, the piriform sinus, and the posterior mediastinum."],["osec","th:op-84~s2-3","The inferior glands","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Anatomy you must see","More variable, because they travel further: at the lower pole of the thyroid or in the thyrothymic tract . They lie in front of the plane of the recurrent nerve. When they are missing, look in the thymus and the front of the upper mediastinum ; less often they are undescended high in the neck, or inside the thyroid. Figure 2 Ectopic sites: superior glands (B partly descended, D tracheo-oesophageal groove, G posterior mediastinum) and inferior glands (A undescended, C inside the thyroid, E at the mediastinal outlet, F in the thymus, H anterior mediastinum). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 84.4"],["osec","th:op-84~s2-4","The recurrent laryngeal nerve","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Anatomy you must see","The right nerve loops under the subclavian artery and runs obliquely to the tracheo-oesophageal groove; the left loops under the aortic arch and rises more vertically. Both enter the larynx near the first tracheal ring."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-1","Baseline PTH and incision","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","Take the baseline PTH before the incision, from a peripheral vein or artery: a central line on the side of the adenoma can give a falsely high level. Infiltrate local anaesthetic and make a Kocher incision in a skin crease between the cricoid and the sternal notch: 3 to 5 cm in the midline for a bilateral exploration, smaller and toward the suspected side for a focused operation. Raise subplatysmal flaps. What you should see A baseline sample sent and timed, and clean flaps above the strap muscles. The wrong sample site A baseline taken from a central vein on the side of the adenoma is falsely high, and makes every later level look like a cure. Senior's pearl Agree the timings with the anaesthetist and the laboratory before you start. The assay is only as good as the labelling and timing of the samples."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-2","Open the midline and mobilise the lobe","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","Divide the midline raphe and separate the strap muscles, retracting them sideways and the thyroid toward the midline. Tie the middle thyroid vein so that the lobe rolls forward and medially, and you can see its whole posterior surface. What you should see The back of the thyroid lobe turned toward you, with the carotid laterally and the paratracheal and retrothyroid spaces open. Blood in the field The plane between the straps and the thyroid capsule bleeds easily, and blood stains everything the same colour as a parathyroid. Dissect bluntly and keep the field dry. Senior's pearl You cannot find a tan gland in a red field. Haemostasis is the most useful localising technique you have."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-3","Find the recurrent nerve","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","Identify the recurrent laryngeal nerve and follow it down toward the carotid and up to the cricothyroid muscle. You need not dissect its whole length in a focused operation, but you must see and protect it whenever you explore beside or behind the oesophagus. What you should see The nerve in the tracheo-oesophageal groove, crossing the inferior thyroid artery. Exploring blind Searching the paraoesophageal and retro-oesophageal spaces without knowing where the nerve is risks dividing it. Senior's pearl The nerve is your map: upper glands lie behind its plane, lower glands in front of it."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-4","Search for the glands","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","In a focused operation, go to the gland the imaging showed. In a bilateral exploration, search in the same order every time . Look and feel: upper glands near the nerve-artery crossing on the back of the upper lobe; lower glands at the lower pole and in the thyrothymic tract. If a gland is missing, open the pretracheal fascia to reach the spaces beside and behind the oesophagus (upper gland), or follow the thymus into the upper mediastinum (lower gland). Never remove a normal gland , even if you cannot find the abnormal one. Figure 3 A parathyroid adenoma. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 84.1 What you should see A soft, tan to reddish-brown, enlarged gland that slides within its fat, quite unlike a firm, fixed thyroid nodule. The white, hard gland A firm, white parathyroid should make you suspect carcinoma. Do not cut into it; stop and get senior help before going further. Senior's pearl A missing upper gland has usually fallen back and down behind the oesophagus; a missing lower gland is usually in the thymus. Search by embryology, not at random."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-5","Help for a hidden gland","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","Radio-guided probe: sestamibi is given 2 hours before surgery and a hand-held gamma probe guides the incision and checks the bed; it cannot exclude several abnormal glands, and thyroid nodules can take up the tracer. Methylene blue stains the glands, but is kept for reoperations because of neurotoxicity. Jugular vein sampling for PTH on each side tells you which side to explore. What you should see One clear signal, stain or lateralising level pointing you to a side or a site."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-6","Remove the gland","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","Dissect all round the enlarged gland before sealing its vessels, without rupturing its capsule. Send it for frozen section , to confirm only that it is parathyroid tissue. Do not stop the search until you have either seen one abnormal and one normal gland, or the PTH has fallen. What you should see An intact gland on its pedicle, removed whole, and a report confirming parathyroid tissue. Sealing before you can see Free the gland all the way round before you seal its vessels, so that you do not catch a nearby structure or leave part of the gland behind."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-7","Prove the cure with PTH","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","PTH has a half-life of 3 to 5 minutes , and rapid assays report within about 20 minutes. Take levels at 10, 15 and 20 minutes after removing the gland. A fall of more than 50% from the baseline at 10 minutes suggests cure, and the later levels should keep falling: a curve that flattens is worrying. You can close the wound while waiting, but keep the patient asleep until the result is satisfactory. What you should see A PTH that has fallen by more than half at 10 minutes and is still falling at 15 and 20. Stopping on a flat curve An unchanged level means that the abnormal tissue is still in the patient. A level that falls and then plateaus suggests a second abnormal gland. Senior's pearl If the hormone has not fallen, look at the other gland on the same side first. If that is also enlarged, the patient has multigland disease: explore all four and do a subtotal parathyroidectomy."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-8","If the PTH does not fall","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","Explore the other gland on the same side, then the opposite side. For hyperplasia , remove three and a half glands, leaving a well-vascularised remnant marked with a clip , or remove all four and autotransplant minced tissue. Mark any glands left in place with clips to help a future surgeon. Over 95% of adenomas can be removed through the neck; a median sternotomy is needed only for a mediastinal gland that cannot be reached from above. Figure 4 Parathyroid hyperplasia, with clips marking the glands. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 84.2 What you should see All four glands accounted for, the remnant viable and clipped. A normal gland devascularised A gland that turns dark has lost its blood supply. Confirm it is parathyroid on frozen section of a fragment, mince it into 1 mm pieces and implant it in the sternocleidomastoid or a strap muscle."],["osec","th:op-84~s3-9","Close","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · The operation, stage by stage","Bring the strap muscles and platysma together with interrupted absorbable sutures, and close the skin with a subcuticular suture or strips. What you should see A dry bed and a neat closure, with the final PTH result in hand."],["osec","th:op-84~s4-1","Crisis drills","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · When it goes wrong","The adenoma is not where the scan showed it 1 Act search the usual sites systematically on that side, then the ectopic sites by embryology 2 Help jugular sampling, gamma probe open the thyroid capsule or remove the lobe only as a last resort for a suspected intrathyroid gland 3 Stop if nothing is found after a thorough exploration close, re-image, and plan a second operation, never removing normal glands Tingling, cramps or spasm after surgery 1 Recognise low calcium, sometimes from \"hungry bones\" taking up calcium after a long-standing adenoma is removed 2 Act oral calcium and calcitriol intravenous calcium if symptoms persist or are severe, with cardiac monitoring Stridor after extubation 1 Suspect both recurrent nerves injured, or a haematoma 2 Act reintubate emergency tracheostomy if that fails examine the vocal folds Neck swelling after surgery 1 Act open the wound and evacuate the clot return to theatre (needed in under 1%) Calcium is high again months later 1 Confirm repeat calcium and PTH, and exclude other causes 2 Localise two imaging methods that agree four-dimensional CT or MRI for ectopic glands venous sampling if needed check the vocal folds 3 Operate a focused approach if localised (a single adenoma is still the commonest cause), dissecting from lateral to medial to avoid scar"],["osec","th:op-84~s5-1","After the operation","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Aftercare","Simple analgesia is usually enough. Review within 2 weeks with calcium, PTH and vitamin D; check TSH too, because handling the thyroid can disturb its function. Repeat calcium and PTH at 6 months to confirm cure (a normal calcium for at least 6 months). Measure bone density at about 18 months ."],["osec","th:op-84~s5-2","Complications","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Aftercare","Complications of parathyroidectomy Complication Notes Persistent or recurrent hyperparathyroidism The commonest, up to 5%; missed adenoma, multigland disease, ectopic or extra glands Hypocalcaemia Tingling and numbness, progressing to tetany, seizures and arrhythmia if untreated Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury Voice therapy and medialisation for one side; airway emergency if both Neck haematoma Under 1% need evacuation Wound infection Open, drain, short course of antibiotics Thyroid dysfunction Overactivity early, underactivity later"],["osec","th:op-84~s5-3","Alternatives","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Aftercare","Surgery is the only cure. For patients who cannot have it: bisphosphonates to protect bone density; calcimimetics such as cinacalcet to lower PTH and calcium; ablation by angiography, ethanol or laser, with temporary or lasting effect; and hydration, which lowers calcium but does not prevent osteoporosis. Patients not operated on need regular checks of calcium, bone density and kidney function."],["ev","th:op-84~s5-4","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism: current evidence","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Aftercare","Who to operate on. The Fifth International Workshop recommends surgery for symptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism and for asymptomatic patients meeting criteria (calcium over 0.25 mmol/l above normal, osteoporosis or vertebral fracture, eGFR under 60, stones or hypercalciuria, age under 50) (Bilezikian et al., J Bone Miner Res 2022). Localisation. Ultrasound and sestamibi first; 4D-CT or ¹⁸F-fluorocholine PET-CT when these are negative or discordant. Focused parathyroidectomy with intraoperative PTH monitoring (a fall of more than 50% at ten minutes) cures about 95 to 98%; the American guideline sets out these standards (Wilhelm et al., JAMA Surg 2016)."],["osec","th:op-84~s5-4","Localisation: the evidence","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · Aftercare","Localising studies compared Study Strength Limit Ultrasound Cheap, no radiation, screens the thyroid; may beat planar sestamibi in expert hands Depends on the operator, gland size and body habit Planar sestamibi The traditional standard; lateralises in over 70% Leaves many scans non-localising; thyroid nodules take up tracer Ultrasound and sestamibi together Excellent accuracy when they agree Both weaker in multigland disease and small adenomas Sestamibi SPECT or SPECT-CT Three-dimensional; better for ectopic glands Cost, radiation; can still miss multigland disease Four-dimensional CT Localises to the correct quadrant Radiation and contrast MRI When others fail; glands dark on T1 and bright on T2 Availability"],["osec","th:op-84~s6-1","Where the books differ","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra covers the parathyroids from the thyroid surgeon's side: they must be identified and preserved in thyroid surgery for benign disease, the superior glands are more constant in position than the inferior, the superior gland lies above the inferior thyroid artery near the cricoid along the posterior border of the thyroid, and both are supplied mainly by the inferior thyroid artery. Lee notes that about 10 minutes after an adenoma is removed the PTH falls to within normal limits, which allows unilateral and minimally invasive surgery; for four-gland hyperplasia it offers subtotal (three and a half gland) resection or total resection with autotransplantation, matched to the severity of the disease; and it explains low calcium after an uneventful adenoma removal as hungry bone syndrome. Myers gives the surgical criteria for asymptomatic disease, the localisation strategy, where the upper and lower glands hide, the peripheral baseline sample, the 10-, 15- and 20-minute PTH protocol with the 50% rule, conversion to bilateral exploration, and the approach to reoperation."],["osec","th:op-84~s6-2","Viva essentials","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The biochemical diagnosis and why familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia must be excluded. The causes and their proportions. Indications for surgery in asymptomatic disease. Embryology and the usual and ectopic sites of the upper and lower glands , and their relation to the recurrent nerve . Localising studies and that they do not make the diagnosis. The intraoperative PTH rule: more than a 50% fall at 10 minutes. Management of multigland disease and MEN (thymectomy in MEN 1). Hungry bone syndrome and the approach to reoperation ."],["osec","th:op-84~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Parathyroidectomy for Primary Hyperparathyroidism and Nonlocalizing Hyperparathyroidism · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia. A focused operation in undiagnosed MEN. A baseline PTH from a central line on the adenoma's side. A bloodstained field. Removing a normal gland. Rupturing an adenoma or a carcinoma. Closing on a PTH that has not fallen, or has plateaued. Leaving retained glands unmarked."],["osec","th:op-88~s1-1","The case at a glance","Salivary Endoscopy · Operating note","Salivary endoscopy Summary Indications Obstruction by calculus or stenosis , compatible on history and imaging; inflammatory disease (Sjögren's syndrome, juvenile recurrent parotitis) frequent or severe enough to warrant intervention; sialadenitis after radioactive iodine Contraindications Acute infection (relative): it may worsen the infection or the duct may perforate. Comorbidity is not a contraindication, because most cases can be done under local anaesthesia or sedation Anaesthesia General is often preferred for comfort; nasal intubation can improve access for hybrid and bilateral cases; sedation or local anaesthesia (including in the office) are possible Position Supine, as for sinus surgery, head turned slightly toward the surgeon Antibiotics Not routine; recommended after recent infection or for combined approaches through mucosa: a first-generation cephalosporin for transfacial parotid work, co-amoxiclav for transoral hybrids, clindamycin in penicillin allergy Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring for parotid hybrid approaches is used by some but has no evidence of benefit Tray Camera and light tower; salivary set with mouth props, dilators of several types, fine scissors and forceps; sweetheart, malleable and self-retaining retractors; 20 mL syringe of saline with IV tubing for irrigation; scopes of 1.1, 1.3 and 1.6 mm; disposable baskets, balloons, dilators and stents; triamcinolone (Kenalog); local anaesthetic without adrenaline as irrigant under local Scopes Diagnostic 0.8 mm; therapeutic 1.1 to 1.6 mm, with rinsing and working channels Skills Endoscopy, basic ope"],["osec","th:op-88~s1-2","Consent","Salivary Endoscopy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove a stone, widen a narrowing or wash out the duct, and to keep the gland. It may not work: the stone may not come out or the problem may persist; removal of the gland may be needed later. A cut inside the mouth or on the face may be needed for a large stone. A tear in the duct, treated with antibiotics or a small tube (stent). Swelling of the gland, cheek or floor of mouth from the rinsing fluid, usually settling; rarely it can affect breathing. Narrowing of the duct afterwards. Infection. Numbness of the tongue (lingual nerve) after removal of a submandibular stone through the mouth, or facial weakness after a parotid approach through the cheek or face. A broken basket or one that sticks on the stone , needing an open operation."],["osec","th:op-88~s1-3","Before you start","Salivary Endoscopy · Operating note","Patients usually complain of swelling and discomfort of the gland at mealtimes; inflammatory disease gives more constant symptoms. Ask about the duration and age at onset, swelling with meals, previous stones, dry eyes or mouth, radioactive iodine, previous salivary surgery, other illness, the family history and anticoagulants. Look for practical obstacles: the teeth, tori and piercings. Palpate all the major glands. Examine the mouth, under the microscope if possible: note the size and position of the submandibular papilla , the quality and quantity of saliva you can express, and feel for stones along Wharton's duct and near the parotid papilla. Treat an acute infection with antibiotics first."],["osec","th:op-88~s1-4","Imaging","Salivary Endoscopy · Operating note","Ultrasound is the first choice outside the United States and increasingly within it: it detects stones down to 2 mm , shows ductal dilatation from any obstruction, and is dynamic, done with food stimulation or intraoral palpation. The anterior part of Wharton's duct is the hardest area to see. Non-contrast CT finds stones over 1 mm but is little help for stenosis unless the duct is greatly dilated; add contrast if a tumour is in the differential. Conventional sialography is invasive and operator-dependent but helps in puzzling obstructions. MR sialography uses saliva as contrast; it is expensive, available in few centres and needs extra set-up."],["osec","th:op-88~s2-1","Landmarks","Salivary Endoscopy · Anatomy you must see","The submandibular papilla lies behind the lower incisors, but its exact position varies: note it in clinic while stimulating the gland with food. The parotid papilla lies in the buccal mucosa opposite the second upper molar . Stensen's duct runs close to the buccal branch of the facial nerve , and curves around the masseter , the hardest part of the parotid endoscopy. Hilar submandibular stones lie close to the lingual nerve , which often has to be moved laterally in a hybrid approach. Two glands, two problems The submandibular duct is hard to enter at its papilla but easy to navigate once inside. The parotid papilla dilates easily, but the duct bends around the masseter; straightening the duct helps you past it."],["osec","th:op-88~s3-1","Choose the setting and find the papilla","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Choose local anaesthesia, sedation or general anaesthesia according to the patient, the disease and comorbidity. View the papilla under magnification. Saliva milked from the gland marks the opening, but there is little of it, so use it sparingly. What you should see A drop of saliva appearing at the papilla when the gland is massaged. A false opening Toothed forceps, injection needles, dilators or guidewires can make a dimple or puncture that looks just like the papilla. Use atraumatic instruments."],["osec","th:op-88~s3-2","Dilate atraumatically","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Dilate the papilla gently, either with rigid dilators of increasing size or by the Seldinger technique over a guidewire. This is the rate-limiting step of the whole procedure. In the parotid, metal dilators are used, and the papilla is usually easier to find. What you should see The papilla accepting the next dilator without blanching or tearing."],["osec","th:op-88~s3-3","Prepare the scope","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Choose the scope by the expected disease and the instruments that must pass through its channel. Outside the patient, check the optics, focus and white balance, and decide which way is up while you hold it in your hand. Flush both ports with saline to clear air bubbles. What you should see A sharp, white-balanced image with your orientation known, and fluid running from the tip."],["osec","th:op-88~s3-4","Enter and navigate","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Insert the scope without force . Stay in the centre of the lumen , irrigating to keep it open; if you lose the lumen you have no real view. Never push without seeing. Remember that Stensen's duct is smaller. What you should see A pale, glistening duct wall with fine vessels and the dark lumen in the centre of the picture; branches at the hilum. Perforation Pushing without a view perforates the duct. Once it has perforated, stop irrigating: fluid escapes into the floor of the mouth or the cheek. Too much irrigation Over-irrigation balloons the gland, can injure the duct, and leaks into the floor of the mouth or the buccal space, swelling the floor of the mouth or the cheek; in the worst case it threatens the airway."],["osec","th:op-88~s3-5","Submandibular stones","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Choose a basket to suit the stone: more wires for small stones, fewer for large . Floating stones can usually be retrieved, and most need a papillotomy to come out. Stones larger than 4 mm in their smallest dimension and not near the papilla need a hybrid approach or fragmentation first. What you should see A stone that moves in the irrigation stream, then the basket open beyond it before you close. Trapped basket syndrome A basket closed on a stone that is not floating may grasp it but not let it go, and the basket is then stuck. Basket only stones that move; a fixed stone needs fragmentation or a hybrid approach."],["osec","th:op-88~s3-6","Parotid stones","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Stones in the ducts proximal to the masseter have a low endoscopic success rate. Non-floating stones over 3 mm may need fragmentation or a hybrid approach. What you should see The stone's position relative to the masseter bend, and whether it moves."],["osec","th:op-88~s3-7","Stenosis","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation, stage by stage","Stenosis is commonest in the parotid ducts . It may be a single diaphragm, the ideal case, or multilevel and severe. Dilate it with a balloon or with scopes in sheaths, then stent the duct. Figure 1 Parotid duct stenosis before and after dilatation, and a salivary duct stent with a guidewire through its lumen to ease introduction into the papilla. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 88.1 What you should see A pale ring or a pinpoint lumen, widening as the balloon inflates, with the duct visible beyond it. Do not break the scope Scopes are fragile. Excess force, or bending the scope over the teeth, breaks it."],["osec","th:op-88~s4-1","Submandibular: transoral sialolithotomy","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation: hybrid approaches","A stone at the papilla can be removed in the office. A medium-sized stone in the middle of the duct or the hilum can be removed by transoral sialolithotomy or fragmented and extracted endoscopically. A large stone in the mid-duct or hilum is removed by transoral sialolithotomy , seeing and protecting the lingual nerve . Afterwards, endoscope the duct to confirm that no fragment is left: residual fragments cause recurrence. The duct need not be repaired; close the mucosa loosely over the incision. What you should see The duct opened on the stone, with the lingual nerve crossing beneath it identified and held away. The lingual nerve At the hilum the lingual nerve lies close to the stone and usually has to be moved laterally. See it before you incise."],["osec","th:op-88~s4-2","Parotid: the transfacial approach","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation: hybrid approaches","Use a mini face-lift incision with standard parotid exposure, protecting the great auricular nerve. The stone must be found exactly: marked with methylene blue under ultrasound, easily palpable, lit by a scope left in the duct, or best of all trapped in a basket so it can be moved within the duct. Raise an anteriorly based U-shaped SMAS flap over it. If the buccal branch of the facial nerve is seen, free it from the point of entry. Open the duct over the stone with a scalpel and enlarge the opening with middle ear scissors only as much as needed. Close the duct watertight , and check it by running water at low pressure through the scope; the SMAS flap is the second layer. Vein grafts have been described for stenosis that will not dilate. What you should see The transilluminated or basket-held stone under the flap, the duct, and the buccal branch freed from it."],["osec","th:op-88~s4-3","Parotid: the transbuccal approach","Salivary Endoscopy · The operation: hybrid approaches","Make a curved incision in front of the parotid papilla and carry it through the buccinator to expose Stensen's duct in the buccal space. Stenosis or stones distal to the anterior border of the masseter can then be treated by sialodochotomy and release of the stone, or by dilating the stenosis under direct vision. Endoscope through the natural opening past the treated segment into the proximal duct, place a guidewire and stent, and repair any duct incision over the stent. Consider botulinum toxin injected into the gland to prevent a sialocele. The same mid-buccal opening also allows endoscopy into the hilum. For a more distal stenosis, complete the incision around the duct opening, dissect the duct from the buccal space and draw it into the mouth (duct stretching), excise the distal 1 to 2 cm and suture the proximal duct to the buccal mucosa: a sialodochoplasty . What you should see Stensen's duct in the buccal space in front of the masseter, opened onto the stone or the stricture."],["osec","th:op-88~s5-1","Crisis drills","Salivary Endoscopy · When it goes wrong","Duct perforated 1 Stop stop irrigating at once 2 Treat antibiotics occasionally a stent 3 Later watch for infection and stenosis Basket stuck on the stone 1 Recognise a fixed stone grasped and the basket will not release 2 Free it open hybrid procedure to retrieve the stone and basket 3 Prevent basket only floating stones of a suitable size Duct avulsed by traction on a basket 1 Recognise the duct comes out with the basket and stone 2 Treat in most cases, removal of the gland 3 Prevent never pull hard on an impacted stone"],["osec","th:op-88~s5-2","Common errors in technique","Salivary Endoscopy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Failing to dilate the papilla. Making a false opening with needles, dilators or guidewires. Losing the centre of the lumen. Pushing without a view and perforating the duct; irrigating on afterwards. Basketing a fixed stone. Breaking the scope with force or by bending it over the teeth. Over-irrigating, swelling the gland, floor of mouth or cheek."],["osec","th:op-88~s6-1","After the procedure","Salivary Endoscopy · Aftercare","Done in the clinic under local anaesthesia or in theatre, and the patient goes home the same day. Give antibiotics if infection was seen during the endoscopy."],["osec","th:op-88~s6-2","Complications","Salivary Endoscopy · Aftercare","Complications of salivary endoscopy Complication Notes Failure Stone not retrieved or disease not controlled; gland excision may follow Duct perforation Antibiotics, occasionally a stent Infection From postoperative swelling of the duct and papilla Blocked basket A non-floating stone grasped but not released; open retrieval Duct avulsion From pulling hard on a basket and stone; usually needs gland removal Stenosis From instrumentation, laser heat or hybrid incisions Airway swelling From irrigation fluid in the floor of the mouth Nerve injury Lingual nerve after submandibular hybrids, buccal branch of the facial nerve after parotid hybrids; rare. No nerve or vessel risk in purely endoscopic work Broken scope Fragile instruments"],["ev","th:op-88~s6-3","Salivary Endoscopy: current evidence","Salivary Endoscopy · Aftercare","Effectiveness. A systematic review found sialendoscopy effective, safe and gland-preserving for obstructive salivary disease, with success in most patients with stones and strictures (Strychowsky et al., Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2012). Size matters. Stones under about 4 to 5 mm in the submandibular duct (3 mm in the parotid) can usually be removed endoscopically; larger stones need a combined transoral or external approach, or laser or intraductal lithotripsy. Gland preservation. Since sialendoscopy, gland excision for stones is needed far less often."],["osec","th:op-88~s6-3","Alternatives","Salivary Endoscopy · Aftercare","Observation. Excision of the gland, for patients whom endoscopy has not helped. Ultrasound-guided botulinum toxin into the parotid to temporise sialadenitis in a patient who does not want parotidectomy."],["osec","th:op-88~s7-1","Does the gland work afterwards?","Salivary Endoscopy · For the examination","Large and hilar submandibular stones were once thought to rule out keeping the gland. After transoral removal of hilar submandibular stones, scintigraphy showed stable or increased salivary flow in up to 66% of glands, objective support for gland-preserving surgery. Flow is not the only measure: relief of obstruction and pain, and freedom from recurrent stones or stenosis, matter as much, and recurrence is what usually leads to salvage excision."],["osec","th:op-88~s7-2","Viva essentials","Salivary Endoscopy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications: stones, stenosis, Sjögren's, juvenile recurrent parotitis, radioiodine sialadenitis. Acute infection as a relative contraindication. Ultrasound as first-line imaging; its limits at the front of Wharton's duct. The papillae: behind the lower incisors, and opposite the second upper molar. Size limits : submandibular stones over 4 mm and parotid stones over 3 mm (not floating) need fragmentation or a hybrid. Stenosis is commonest in the parotid . The trapped basket and how to avoid it. The nerves at risk in hybrid approaches. The book's own numbers In this chapter's text the size threshold is 4 mm for submandibular stones and 3 mm for non-floating parotid stones; one of its review questions instead pairs the parotid duct with stones larger than 4 mm. The thresholds in the text are the ones used here."],["osec","th:op-88~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Salivary Endoscopy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Forcing the scope or pushing blind. Irrigating through a perforation. Pulling hard on an impacted stone. Leaving fragments behind after a sialolithotomy. Forgetting the lingual nerve at the hilum."],["osec","th:op-89~s1-1","The case at a glance","Surgical Management of Ranula · Operating note","Surgery for ranula Summary Indications Any symptomatic ranula; urgently if it compromises the airway; recurrence; infection; speech or swallowing interference; obstructed Wharton's duct; cosmetic concern Types Oral (simple) ranula in the floor of the mouth; plunging ranula in the submandibular or parapharyngeal space, with or without an oral component Imaging Ultrasound, CT or MRI for plunging ranulas (the \"tail sign\" pointing to the sublingual space) Options Intraoral sublingual gland excision with drainage of the pseudocyst (most curative); marsupialisation or micromarsupialisation (children, small ranulas; higher recurrence); sclerotherapy (OK-432); transcervical excision rarely needed Anaesthesia General, nasotracheal tube helps Antibiotics Cover oral flora (for example clindamycin) Tray Head and neck set, intraoral retractors, mouth prop, bipolar, lacrimal probes or salivary dilators and stent for Wharton's duct, sialendoscopes if needed"],["osec","th:op-89~s1-2","Consent","Surgical Management of Ranula · Operating note","What to tell the patient Recurrence, mainly after marsupialisation or if gland is left behind. Numbness or altered taste of the tongue tip (lingual nerve), usually temporary. Submandibular duct injury causing swelling of the submandibular gland when eating; rarely gland removal later. Bleeding, infection, floor of mouth swelling."],["osec","th:op-89~s1-3","Before you start","Surgical Management of Ranula · Operating note","Check the airway with a large or plunging ranula. Image a neck swelling to confirm continuity with the sublingual space and to exclude other cystic neck masses (lymphatic malformation, dermoid, branchial cyst). Figure 1 A bilateral floor of mouth ranula. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 89.1 Figure 2 CT of a plunging ranula. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 89.2"],["osec","th:op-89~s2-1","Protect the duct","Surgical Management of Ranula · The operation, stage by stage","Open the mouth with a prop and retract the tongue. Cannulate Wharton's duct at its papilla with a probe or stent so it can be felt and seen throughout. What you should see The duct marked by its stent, running backwards under the floor of mouth mucosa."],["osec","th:op-89~s2-2","Incise the floor of the mouth","Surgical Management of Ranula · The operation, stage by stage","Incise the mucosa over the sublingual gland lateral to the duct, from near the papilla backwards along the plica sublingualis, staying lateral to the duct. Figure 3 The incision outlined on the floor of the mouth. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 89.3 What you should see The lobulated sublingual gland under thin mucosa, with the pseudocyst wall beneath or beside it."],["osec","th:op-89~s2-3","Dissect the gland","Surgical Management of Ranula · The operation, stage by stage","Free the gland from the inner mandible laterally, then medially off the duct and lingual nerve, working from front to back. The lingual nerve runs under the gland and crosses below the duct from lateral to medial; identify it and keep it. Drain the ranula fluid and remove the pseudocyst wall where it comes easily; it has no epithelium and does not need complete excision. What you should see The whole sublingual gland removed, the duct and lingual nerve intact on the floor of the wound. The lingual nerve and Wharton's duct Both lie directly deep and medial to the sublingual gland in the back of the floor of the mouth. Keep the stent in the duct and find the nerve before dividing anything that crosses the back of the gland."],["osec","th:op-89~s2-4","Plunging ranula","Surgical Management of Ranula · The operation, stage by stage","The same intraoral excision of the sublingual gland usually cures a plunging ranula: drain the neck collection from inside the mouth and the pseudocyst collapses. A cervical approach is reserved for large or recurrent collections not managed intraorally. What you should see The neck swelling collapsing once the gland is out and the fluid drained."],["osec","th:op-89~s2-5","Close","Surgical Management of Ranula · The operation, stage by stage","If the duct was cut, marsupialise or reimplant it into the floor of mouth mucosa (sialodochoplasty) over a stent; otherwise close the mucosa loosely. Figure 4 Marsupialising an interrupted submandibular duct. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 89.4 What you should see A loosely closed floor of mouth, the duct draining. Senior's pearl Treat the source, not the pool. Excising the pseudocyst alone, or deroofing it without removing the gland, gives the highest recurrence rates."],["osec","th:op-89~s3-1","Crisis drills","Surgical Management of Ranula · When it goes wrong","Floor of mouth swelling and airway compromise after surgery 1 Act sit up, assess airway evacuate haematoma secure the airway if needed Submandibular swelling with meals after surgery 1 Act duct stenosis or injury dilate, stent, sialendoscopy gland excision if it persists"],["osec","th:op-89~s4-1","After surgery","Surgical Management of Ranula · Aftercare","Soft diet, chlorhexidine mouthwash, analgesia, antibiotics for oral flora; remove a duct stent at about 2 weeks. Review for recurrence."],["ev","th:op-89~s4-2","Surgical Management of Ranula: current evidence","Surgical Management of Ranula · Aftercare","A proportion meta-analysis of 39 series found intraoral sublingual gland excision the most curative treatment for oral ranula; micromarsupialisation gave comparable but less consistent cure rates; for plunging ranula intraoral and transcervical approaches had similar cure rates, both higher than OK-432 injection, with more morbidity after the transcervical route (Chung et al., Br J Oral Maxillofac Surg 2019). A systematic review of paediatric ranulas found micromarsupialisation and marsupialisation useful first options in children, with gland excision for recurrence (Packiri et al., J Clin Diagn Res 2017)."],["osec","th:op-89~s4-2","Complications","Surgical Management of Ranula · Aftercare","Recurrence, lingual nerve injury, duct stenosis or injury with obstructive sialadenitis, bleeding, haematoma, infection."],["osec","th:op-89~s5-1","Where the books differ","Surgical Management of Ranula · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers excision of the sublingual gland through the mouth with drainage of the pseudocyst, protecting the duct (stented) and lingual nerve. Dhingra and K. J. Lee the ranula as an extravasation pseudocyst of the sublingual gland, the plunging type, and the older marsupialisation. The literature intraoral gland excision gives the highest cure rates for both oral and plunging ranulas."],["osec","th:op-89~s5-2","Viva essentials","Surgical Management of Ranula · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Pathology : extravasation pseudocyst, not a true cyst. Plunging ranula and the differential of a cystic neck mass. Treatment options and why gland excision works. Anatomy of the sublingual space: duct and lingual nerve."],["osec","th:op-89~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Surgical Management of Ranula · For the examination","Common pitfalls Excising the pseudocyst and leaving the gland. A neck approach for a plunging ranula that the mouth could cure. Cutting Wharton's duct without recognising it. Missing the lingual nerve behind the gland."],["osec","th:op-91~s1-1","The case at a glance","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Operating note","Excision of the submandibular gland Summary Indications A mass or suspected neoplasm of the gland (excision is diagnostic when FNA is non-diagnostic and the mass is confined to the gland; more extensive surgery with neck dissection for a known malignancy); chronic sialadenitis with ectatic ducts that has failed gland-sparing treatment; stones not removable endoscopically; chronic drooling in neuromuscular swallowing disorders (bilateral excision with parotid duct ligation) Contraindications Acute infection (planes are lost; drain an abscess, securing the airway if the floor of mouth is raised, and operate later); try sialendoscopy first for stones, stricture, chronic sialadenitis, radiation or Sjögren-related disease Anaesthesia General, without long-acting muscle relaxants so the nerves can be stimulated; local is possible for benign disease in unfit patients Position Shoulder roll, head up slightly and turned away; oral tube taped to the opposite side (nasotracheal tube for the intraoral approach) Antibiotics None for the transcervical (clean) operation; 24 hours of oral-flora cover for the transoral (clean-contaminated) route Monitoring Not routine; a hand-held stimulator helps in malignancy and scarred, chronically infected fields Imaging Ultrasound for stones, dilated ducts and guided FNA; non-contrast CT for stones; contrast CT for abscess or bone involvement; gadolinium MRI for a suspected tumour, including perineural spread (adenoid cystic carcinoma) Tray Head and neck set; bipolar; hand-held nerve stimulator; small right-angled retractors; salivary endoscope if ret"],["osec","th:op-91~s1-2","Consent","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it is: removing the salivary gland under the jaw through a skin crease in the upper neck. Lower lip weakness on that side, usually temporary, from the small branch of the facial nerve that runs near the gland; more likely after repeated infection. Numbness of the side of the tongue (lingual nerve) and, rarely, tongue weakness (hypoglossal nerve). Bleeding under the jaw, which can push the tongue up and affect breathing; it is watched overnight for this reason. A stone left in the remaining duct , causing swelling or infection later. Infection , a salivary leak, and the scar. Dry mouth is usually not noticeable from one gland. The final diagnosis comes from the specimen; a cancer may need further treatment."],["osec","th:op-91~s1-3","Before you start","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Sialadenitis: one-sided redness, swelling and pain under the jaw that worsen with meals, and foul-tasting saliva, especially when the gland is pressed. A mass: tumours here are uncommon but are proportionally more often malignant than in the parotid; weakness of the tongue or lower lip, or numbness of the tongue, suggests malignancy. Drooling: how long, how severe, the cause and whether it will improve. Sjögren syndrome; previous neck irradiation or radioiodine (raising the risk of sialadenitis and salivary tumours); previous drainage, biopsy or stone removal; other neck surgery. Occupational exposure to silica dust or nitrosamines; tobacco and alcohol; antiplatelets, anticoagulants and supplements. 1.3.2 The examination A full head and neck examination; neck nodes need full work-up (about a quarter of submandibular cancers have spread to the neck). Inspect and feel the floor of the mouth for a stone in the end of Wharton's duct. Bimanual palpation: size, discrete mass or stone, tenderness, fixation. Milk the gland and compare the saliva on both sides: cloudy or purulent saliva means sialadenitis; no flow from a big gland means obstruction. Nerves: tongue movement (XII), tongue sensation (lingual), lower lip movement (marginal mandibular). In the elderly, a ptotic gland can drop below and outside the greater cornu of the hyoid and be mistaken for a tumour."],["osec","th:op-91~s2-1","The gland and its duct","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Anatomy you must see","The gland lies in the submandibular triangle , above and lateral to the hyoid and digastric sling, below the mandible; it wraps around the back edge of the mylohyoid , with a superficial part outside it and a deep part inside. Wharton's duct runs forward deep to the mylohyoid, beside the sublingual gland , under the floor-of-mouth mucosa, to open at the sublingual papilla beside the frenulum. The opening is the narrowest point, so stones impact there. About 80% to 90% of salivary stones form here: the duct runs uphill and the saliva is richer in mucin. Figure 1 The opening of Wharton's duct (arrow) beside the frenulum. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 91.1"],["osec","th:op-91~s2-2","The three nerves","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Anatomy you must see","Marginal mandibular nerve: runs beneath the platysma, superficial to the facial vein , over the gland. With the neck extended and the head turned, it lies lower , often right over the gland, and behind the gland it can be well below the mandible. Lingual nerve: a broad band coming from under the mandible, looping down close to the front of the gland and sending branches to the submandibular ganglion , then rising forward to the tongue; it lies above the duct. Hypoglossal nerve: runs forward along the floor of the triangle, deep to the gland and to the posterior belly of digastric, parallel to the duct, with accompanying veins."],["osec","th:op-91~s2-3","Landmarks","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Anatomy you must see","The facial vein on the outer surface of the gland. The anterior belly of digastric , reflected back to show the mylohyoid. The mylohyoid , whose fibres run from back-above to front-below, at a clearly different angle from the digastric; the lingual nerve, duct, sublingual gland and front of the submandibular gland lie just deep to it. The posterior belly of digastric , below which the hypoglossal nerve can be found in a scarred field. The facial artery , which grooves the back-upper surface of the gland."],["osec","th:op-91~s3-1","Incision and flap","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · The operation: transcervical excision","Make the incision in a natural skin crease at the lower border of the gland, usually just above the hyoid; a finger in the mouth pushing the floor of the mouth down helps you find a small gland. Cut through skin, fat and platysma. Once you are through the platysma the marginal nerve is at risk, so aim your dissection at the lower part of the gland. What you should see The capsule of the lower gland, with the facial vein running up over its outer surface."],["osec","th:op-91~s3-2","Protect the marginal mandibular nerve","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · The operation: transcervical excision","Incise the fascia over the lower gland and lift the upper fascial flap off the gland's outer surface. Find the facial vein , tie and divide it low on the gland , and lift the vein upward with the fascia: the nerve, which lies superficial to the vein, is lifted up out of harm's way (the Hayes Martin manoeuvre ). For a malignancy or a neck dissection, dissect the nerve itself off the vein and remove the prevascular and postvascular facial nodes . In a scarred, chronically infected gland, you often need to find the nerve with magnification and a stimulator. Figure 2 The skin and platysma flap lifted off the gland; the marginal mandibular nerve (arrow) crossing the facial vein. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 91.2 Figure 3 Dividing the facial vein low on the gland and lifting the nerve with it; the prevascular and postvascular nodes (arrows). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 91.3 What you should see The nerve lifted up out of the field with the vein, and the gland's capsule bare below it. Senior's pearl For benign disease you do not have to see the nerve; you have to stay out of its plane. Dissect directly on the gland, deep to the fascia, and the nerve stays in the flap above you."],["osec","th:op-91~s3-3","Free the front of the gland and retract the mylohyoid","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · The operation: transcervical excision","Free the upper gland bluntly from the soft tissue beneath the mandible. Free the front of the gland at the anterior belly of digastric: incise the fascia over the anterior belly and reflect it backward, which shows the mylohyoid . Dissect along the back edge of the mylohyoid (it can be hard to find, or partly absent, in a chronically infected gland), divide the small vessels entering it, and pull the muscle forward with a small right-angled retractor to show the deep part of the gland. Figure 4 The back edge of the mylohyoid retracted forward to show Wharton's duct and the lingual nerve. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 91.4 What you should see Deep to the retracted mylohyoid: the front of the gland, the duct with the sublingual gland stuck to it, and the lingual nerve above."],["osec","th:op-91~s3-4","Free the lingual nerve","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · The operation: transcervical excision","Find the lingual nerve as a broad band looping down toward the front of the gland. Divide the branches running from it to the submandibular ganglion so the nerve springs up, away from the gland. Only then look for the duct. The lingual nerve and the duct The nerve lies directly above the duct. Dividing the duct before freeing the nerve, especially in a scarred gland, cuts the nerve and leaves the side of the tongue numb for good. What you should see A flat, broad white band looping down from under the mandible, tethered to the gland by the ganglion, which springs up out of the field once the ganglion branches are cut. Senior's pearl The nerve is found by pulling the gland down and the mylohyoid forward at the same time. If you cannot see the loop, the retraction is wrong; do not start cutting to find it."],["osec","th:op-91~s3-5","Find the hypoglossal nerve, then divide the duct","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · The operation: transcervical excision","Identify the duct in the fascia of the floor of the triangle, and tie its accompanying vein separately. Identify the hypoglossal nerve running forward on the floor of the triangle from beneath the posterior belly of digastric. If inflammation hides it, find it below the posterior belly of digastric and follow it forward. With both nerves safe, follow the duct forward to the floor-of-mouth mucosa and divide it there, feeling bimanually for any stone left in the duct; pass a salivary endoscope retrograde through the duct if you are unsure. Figure 5 The lingual nerve's branches to the ganglion divided to free the nerve; Wharton's duct divided (arrow) and the specimen removed. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 91.5 What you should see The lingual nerve lifted clear above, the hypoglossal nerve safe on the floor of the triangle, and the duct traced right up to the mucosa with no stone in it. A stone left behind A stone left in the remaining duct causes chronic infection after the gland has gone. Follow the duct all the way forward and feel along it before you divide it."],["osec","th:op-91~s3-6","The facial artery","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · The operation: transcervical excision","Pull the gland down, with countertraction above, and free its upper surface from beneath the mandible, staying right on the gland to protect the marginal nerve. The facial artery grooves the back-upper surface: divide and double-tie it where it leaves the gland above and where it enters below. In an uninflamed gland it can be kept by tying each small branch into the gland. Remove the gland. What you should see The artery entering the back of the gland from beneath the posterior belly of digastric and leaving over the mandible, tied twice at each end, and the gland free. The slipped tie The proximal stump of the facial artery retracts under the digastric if its tie slips, and bleeds from a place that is hard to reach. Double-tie or suture-ligate it. Senior's pearl For a benign, uninflamed gland, staying inside the gland's capsule and beneath the facial vein (which carries the marginal nerve up with the flap) protects the nerve without having to dissect it."],["osec","th:op-91~s3-7","Haemostasis and closure","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · The operation: transcervical excision","Inspect the whole triangle for bleeding, especially the front part deep to the mylohyoid. Irrigate, place a suction drain, and close platysma, subcutaneous tissue and skin in separate layers. What you should see A dry triangle, a securely tied facial artery stump, and the three nerves intact."],["osec","th:op-91~s4-1","Intraoral excision","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Other approaches","Through a nasotracheal tube, inject the floor of the mouth, put a probe into the papilla, and incise the floor-of-mouth mucosa from the duct opening back to the lingual side of the retromolar area. Removing the sublingual gland exposes the duct; follow the duct (beside the lingual nerve) and retract the tongue, floor of mouth and lingual nerve to see the mylohyoid. Pull the back edge of the mylohyoid forward to reach the gland, push the gland up from the neck, tie or avoid the facial vessels, remove it, and close the mucosa (with a drain if needed). It avoids a scar and the marginal nerve, but the heavy retraction of the floor of the mouth and lingual nerve often causes temporary tongue numbness (in one series about 81%) and temporary reduced tongue movement from swelling (about 68%), settling within about two weeks."],["osec","th:op-91~s4-2","Endoscopic and robotic excision","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Other approaches","Endoscope-assisted excision through the mouth or a small neck or hairline incision follows the same steps. Robotic excision through a retroauricular incision, or three small neck ports in a balloon-dissected subplatysmal pocket, works from below upward under the capsule to protect the marginal nerve, with clips on the duct and facial vessels. Approaches compared Approach Advantage Drawback Transcervical Familiar, direct view of all three nerves Neck scar; marginal nerve injury (transient in 7.7% to 36% across reports) Intraoral No scar; marginal nerve not at risk Temporary tongue numbness and stiffness; nasotracheal tube; harder to learn Submental or retroauricular Hidden scar; small series show no extra marginal nerve injury Small studies only Endoscopic or robotic Small or hidden incisions Equipment, time; early experience"],["osec","th:op-91~s5-1","Crisis drills","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · When it goes wrong","Swelling under the jaw with breathing difficulty after surgery 1 Recognise expanding neck swelling, a raised floor of mouth and tongue, a drain filling with blood 2 Act call for senior and anaesthetic help open the wound to release the clot if the airway is threatened 3 Then return to theatre to control the bleeding, usually the facial artery stump Lower lip weakness afterwards 1 Likely neuropraxia of the marginal mandibular nerve, especially after chronic infection 2 Act reassure most recover if the nerve was known to be cut, repair or later reanimation Swelling and pus from the duct opening months later 1 Suspect a stone left in the duct remnant 2 Act imaging sialendoscopy or transoral removal of the stone and duct remnant"],["osec","th:op-91~s6-1","After the operation","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Aftercare","Observe overnight, mainly for airway swelling or bleeding , with the head of the bed raised. Remove the drain when the output falls; persistent heavy drainage means re-exploration , because a haematoma here can push the tongue back and obstruct the airway. Check the lower lip, tongue sensation and tongue movement."],["osec","th:op-91~s6-2","Complications","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Aftercare","Complications of submandibular gland excision Complication Cause or prevention Marginal mandibular nerve palsy Commonest nerve injury; dissect on the gland, Hayes Martin manoeuvre, find the nerve in scarred fields Lingual nerve injury Dividing the duct before freeing the nerve Hypoglossal nerve injury Nerve stuck to the deep surface in inflamed glands; find it below the posterior belly Haematoma and airway compromise Facial artery; double ligation, careful haemostasis Retained stone Duct not followed to the mucosa Infection, fistula, poor scar Uncommon Dry mouth Rarely noticeable after one gland"],["ev","th:op-91~s6-3","Excision of the Submandibular Gland: current evidence","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Aftercare","Nerves at risk. The marginal mandibular nerve is temporarily weak in up to about 10% after submandibular gland excision and permanently in a few per cent; the lingual and hypoglossal nerves are injured less often. Dissecting deep to the investing fascia and ligating the facial vein low (the Hayes Martin manoeuvre) protects the marginal nerve. Gland preservation. Sialendoscopy and transoral stone removal have greatly reduced the need for excision for stones (Strychowsky et al., Arch Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2012)."],["osec","th:op-91~s6-3","Alternatives","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · Aftercare","Sialendoscopy has changed the treatment of stones, strictures and chronic sialadenitis, and often saves the gland; transoral removal of stones in the duct is another gland-sparing option. A mass in the gland needs excision for diagnosis and treatment; a patient who declines must at least be followed up."],["osec","th:op-91~s7-1","Where the books differ","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra explains that stones form from calcium phosphate on a mucin or debris matrix, that about 90% occur in the submandibular gland and 10% in the parotid, that about 80% are radio-opaque, and that stones near the duct opening can be removed through the mouth while stones at the hilum or in the gland need the gland removed. Lee notes that sialolithiasis can scar and dilate the ducts, that half of chronic sialadenitis improves with antibiotics, hydration and sialogogues, that removing the stone can restore function and spare the gland, that submandibular stones are usually in the duct (parotid stones in the gland), that sialendoscopy baskets cannot remove stones over 5 mm, and that a Küttner tumour (chronic sclerosing sialadenitis) can mimic a neoplasm. Myers gives the operation protecting the marginal nerve with the Hayes Martin manoeuvre or by identifying it, freeing the lingual nerve before dividing the duct, finding the hypoglossal nerve below the posterior belly of digastric, following the duct to the mucosa, double-tying the facial artery, observing the airway overnight, and the alternative approaches."],["osec","th:op-91~s7-2","Viva essentials","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three nerves at risk, where each lies, and how to protect each. The Hayes Martin manoeuvre , and when you must find the nerve instead. Why stones form here more than in the parotid. The lingual nerve looping around the duct and the order: nerve first, then duct. How to find the hypoglossal nerve in a scarred field. Why the duct is followed to the floor of the mouth. The bleeding neck after submandibular surgery. The role of sialendoscopy ."],["osec","th:op-91~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Excision of the Submandibular Gland · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating during acute infection. Dissecting above the gland's fascia, into the marginal nerve's plane. Forgetting that the nerve lies lower with the head extended and turned. Dividing the duct before freeing the lingual nerve. Not seeing the hypoglossal nerve on the floor of the triangle. Leaving a stone in the duct. A single tie on the facial artery stump."],["osec","th:op-92~s1-1","The case at a glance","Superficial Parotidectomy · Operating note","Superficial parotidectomy Summary Indications Neoplasms of the superficial lobe (about 85% of parotid tumours are benign: pleomorphic adenoma, Warthin tumour; low-grade malignancies); metastatic nodes from skin cancer of the face, scalp and ear; chronic parotitis that has failed medical and minimally invasive treatment Contraindications A poor surgical candidate; tumour in the deep lobe (needs a total or deep-lobe operation); malignancy extending into the parapharyngeal space, mandible, skin or ear canal, or with distant metastases (needs a wider resection or non-surgical treatment) Anaesthesia General; avoid long-acting muscle relaxants so the nerve can be stimulated Position Supine, neck gently extended, head turned away; the whole side of the face and the ear prepared and draped in view Antibiotics Not needed for tumour surgery, even with a suction drain; give them for chronic parotitis Monitoring Facial nerve monitoring is optional, not the standard of care; useful in revisions and chronic parotitis Imaging CT or MRI with contrast for anatomy, extent, nodes and stones; MRI is particularly useful; PET for staging a known malignancy; ultrasound for intraparotid nodes Tray Head and neck and facial plastic sets; fine blunt-tipped dissecting scissors (McCabe dissector); right-angle retractors; nerve stimulator; bipolar; harmonic scalpel if available; suction drain Before theatre Fine-needle aspiration for any suspicious mass; stop aspirin, anti-inflammatories and supplements a week before; agree the plan for the nerve in a malignancy"],["osec","th:op-92~s1-2","Consent","Superficial Parotidectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Facial weakness: temporary weakness of part of the face is common and usually recovers over weeks; permanent weakness is uncommon. In a cancer, the nerve may have to be removed and grafted, and this will be discussed beforehand. Numbness of the ear lobe and the skin around the scar, often long-lasting, because the great auricular nerve is usually divided. Gustatory sweating (Frey syndrome): sweating and flushing of the cheek when eating, which many patients have to some degree; treatable if it bothers them. A hollow in front of the ear. A collection of saliva or fluid (sialocele or seroma) , usually managed by aspiration and settling within weeks. Bleeding or a blood clot , infection, poor healing of the skin flap, and a thick or raised scar. The final diagnosis comes from the specimen; occasionally further treatment (radiotherapy, or more surgery if the margins are involved) is needed. Recurrence , especially of a pleomorphic adenoma if the capsule is breached."],["osec","th:op-92~s1-3","Before you start","Superficial Parotidectomy · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Features of malignancy: pain, facial weakness coming on gradually, rapid growth, fixation. Radiation exposure raises the risk of mucoepidermoid carcinoma. Lymphoma or Sjögren syndrome in the past makes lymphoma more likely. Skin cancer of the face, scalp or ear, or a non-healing skin lesion: think of a metastatic node. A smoker with a cystic mass in the tail: probably a Warthin tumour. A mass present for many years, or recurrent: carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma becomes more likely (malignant change in 5% to 10%). Previous parotid surgery , which makes the nerve harder to find. 1.3.2 The examination A mobile mass suggests a benign tumour; a fixed one is worrying, though benign tumours can be fixed when they pass through the stylomandibular tunnel into the prestyloid parapharyngeal space, or when inflamed. Facial weakness of one or more branches is a hallmark of malignancy. Palpable neck nodes raise concern (occasionally inflammatory). Look in the mouth at the tonsil and soft palate for deep-lobe extension . Look for scars of previous surgery and skin cancers . 1.3.3 Imaging and cytology What the imaging tells you Finding Suggests Irregular edge, infiltration of soft tissue, low T2 signal Malignancy Early enhancement with little washout on dynamic MRI Malignancy Heterogeneous mass with sharp scalloped edges, bright on T2 Pleomorphic adenoma Cystic, high T2 signal, tail of gland, often bilateral Warthin tumour Intraparotid node with normal architecture on ultrasound May be observed FNA is indicated for any suspicious mass; it can mislead (about 10% false pos"],["osec","th:op-92~s2-1","Finding the main trunk","Superficial Parotidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The tragal pointer : the cartilaginous tip of the external auditory canal points anteroinferiorly toward the nerve; the trunk lies about 1 cm deep and slightly inferior to it. The tympanomastoid suture : palpable between the pointer and the mastoid tip; the nerve leaves the stylomastoid foramen just deep to its lower end. This is the most reliable landmark. The posterior belly of digastric : the nerve lies at the level of its attachment to the mastoid, just medial to it; the muscle lies lateral to the nerve as it exits. The styloid process lies deep to the nerve: dissecting straight down toward it takes you past the nerve. Figure 1 A, the modified Blair incision, with the face-lift modification hiding the posterior limb at the hairline. B, dissection with a fine dissector over the tympanomastoid suture, moving toward the parotid. C, the main trunk exposed and followed along its branches. D, the digastric muscle inserting on the mastoid, lateral to the nerve as it leaves the stylomastoid foramen. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 92.1"],["osec","th:op-92~s2-2","Other structures","Superficial Parotidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The great auricular nerve runs up over the sternocleidomastoid to the lobule; it is usually divided, but its posterior branch can sometimes be kept, and it is a ready graft if needed. The spinal accessory nerve enters the deep surface of the sternocleidomastoid; beware it if you rotate muscle fibres to fill the defect. The retromandibular vein runs through the gland, often deep to the nerve's divisions. The superficial musculoaponeurotic system (SMAS) lies beneath the skin flap and can be raised as a separate layer to fill the defect and reduce Frey syndrome."],["osec","th:op-92~s3-1","Incision and skin flap","Superficial Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Use a modified Blair incision : in the preauricular crease to the level of the canal, around the lobule, then down into a neck crease about two fingers below the mandible. For tumours at or above the lobule, it need not extend into the neck. A face-lift variant hides the posterior limb at the hairline. Raise the skin flap with its subcutaneous fat on top of the SMAS; if the SMAS is free of tumour, raise it as a separate, anteriorly based flap to use later. What you should see A healthy, thick skin flap and the SMAS (or parotid fascia) intact over the gland. The thin flap A flap raised without its fat, especially in a smoker, can die at its tip. Keep the fat on the skin."],["osec","th:op-92~s3-2","Free the gland from behind","Superficial Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Clear the front of the upper sternocleidomastoid. Divide the great auricular nerve (or preserve its posterior branch if you can). Dissect along the cartilaginous ear canal, find the pointer , and retract the gland forward. Work parallel to the floor (toward the parotid, not down into the bony canal); dissecting perpendicularly takes you to the styloid, deep to the nerve. Divide the parotidomasseteric fascia to reach the plane in which the nerve runs. What you should see The pointer, the mastoid tip, the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid and the posterior belly of digastric, with the gland lifted forward."],["osec","th:op-92~s3-3","Find the main trunk","Superficial Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Feel for the tympanomastoid suture between the pointer and the mastoid tip. Staying over the suture, divide small bites of fascia and parotid tissue by spreading parallel to where the nerve will run, until the trunk appears as a white, round cord with fine vessels on its surface. Large tumours can push the trunk into a more vertical course, parallel to the sternocleidomastoid. If you cannot find the trunk, find a peripheral branch (usually the marginal mandibular as it crosses the facial vessels, or the buccal along the duct) and follow it back. What you should see The main trunk, about 1 cm deep to the pointer, at the level of the digastric, dividing a short distance on into its upper and lower divisions at the pes anserinus. Senior's pearl Spread, never cut blindly, and spread along the line of the nerve. Use the stimulator sparingly: repeated stimulation and rough handling cause the very neuropraxia you are trying to avoid."],["osec","th:op-92~s3-4","Follow the branches around the tumour","Superficial Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Follow the trunk forward along its branches, dividing the gland tissue lateral to the visible nerve, until you are in soft tissue in front of the tumour. Keep a cuff of normal gland around a benign tumour where you can; on the nerve side there is often only the capsule, so dissect the tumour off the nerve carefully without breaching it. Then divide the remaining gland and remove the specimen. Control small vessels with bipolar or ties near the nerve; a harmonic scalpel speeds the operation. What you should see Every branch running intact from the trunk to the front edge of the dissection, with the tumour lifted off them in its capsule. Breaching the capsule A torn pleomorphic adenoma seeds multiple recurrences that are hard to clear. If the capsule ruptures, irrigate generously and remove every fragment you can see, and record it."],["osec","th:op-92~s3-5","Close","Superficial Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Stimulate the main trunk before closing to confirm that every branch still works. Irrigate and place a suction drain. Fill a visible hollow by sewing the SMAS flap to the sternocleidomastoid, or by rotating some upper sternocleidomastoid fibres forward (beware the accessory nerve); dermal grafts can cause infection and sialocele, and fat grafts partly resorb. Close the subcutaneous layer with 3-0 absorbable sutures and the skin with 6-0 absorbable sutures or glue, then strips and a light parotid dressing. What you should see All branches contracting on stimulation, a dry bed, and a smooth contour."],["osec","th:op-92~s4-1","The options","Superficial Parotidectomy · Variations","Extracapsular dissection: removing a small benign tumour with a thin cuff, without formally dissecting the nerve. It grew out of enucleation (which recurred in over 40% because the capsule was left), and needs a microscope and careful selection. Accessory lobe tumours: endoscopic assistance avoids large facial incisions. Chronic parotitis: removing the whole superficial lobe, with or without tying the duct, cures nearly all."],["osec","th:op-92~s5-1","Crisis drills","Superficial Parotidectomy · When it goes wrong","The face is weak after surgery 1 Nerve seen intact expect full recovery neuropraxia in 4 to 6 weeks, traction injury up to 6 months steroids do not help eye care 2 Nerve not identified re-explore to find and repair it 3 Eye lubricants, taping at night, ophthalmic review if closure is poor A branch is cut during the operation 1 Act freshen both ends with a sharp cut repair the epineurium with 8-0 or 9-0 monofilament under magnification, without tension a conduit can support it 2 Gap interpose a great auricular graft at a delayed repair, sural or medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve 3 Expect at best House-Brackmann III, recovering over 6 to 12 months Sudden swelling or a drain filling with blood 1 Act return to theatre to evacuate the haematoma and control the vessel 2 Why prevents infection and death of the skin flap Swelling under the flap after drain removal 1 Act aspirate with a 16 to 20 gauge needle under aseptic conditions repeat as needed 2 Then botulinum toxin into the remaining gland for a persistent sialocele most settle within 4 to 6 weeks The tumour is malignant on final histology 1 Margins clear no further surgery is needed for a superficial lobe tumour 2 Then multidisciplinary review for neck treatment and radiotherapy by grade and stage"],["osec","th:op-92~s6-1","After the operation","Superficial Parotidectomy · Aftercare","Check facial movement on waking and record each branch. Drain on bulb suction (avoid high suction near the nerve); remove when under 20 ml in 24 hours, then if under 5 ml over the next 8 hours. Keep the dressing on for 24 hours after discharge. No strenuous exercise for 2 weeks; no driving for 1 week."],["osec","th:op-92~s6-2","Complications","Superficial Parotidectomy · Aftercare","Complications of superficial parotidectomy Complication Frequency or management Ear lobe and skin numbness Common; great auricular nerve Temporary facial weakness Common; usually recovers Permanent facial weakness Uncommon; repair or reanimation Frey syndrome Up to 60%, troubling about 10%; antiperspirant, botulinum toxin, interposed vascularised tissue Sialocele or seroma About 5%; aspiration, botulinum toxin, time Infection Under 5% Haematoma Return to theatre Flap necrosis Keep the fat on the flap; smoking increases risk Hypertrophic scar or keloid Early triamcinolone injection with lidocaine Recurrence About 2%, often multifocal, from capsule left behind or spillage; revision ± nerve sacrifice, radiotherapy for widespread disease"],["osec","th:op-92~s6-3","Frey syndrome","Superficial Parotidectomy · Aftercare","After the gland is removed, parasympathetic secretomotor fibres from the auriculotemporal nerve (glossopharyngeal origin, via the otic ganglion) regrow into the sweat glands of the overlying skin, so eating causes sweating and flushing. Confirm with Minor's starch-iodine test. Prevent it by placing vascularised tissue (SMAS flap) between the bed and the skin; treat it with antiperspirant or botulinum toxin injections. Revision surgery for Frey syndrome risks the scarred nerve and is a last resort."],["ev","th:op-92~s6-4","Superficial Parotidectomy: current evidence","Superficial Parotidectomy · Aftercare","Extent of surgery for benign tumours. An updated meta-analysis of 21 studies (2507 patients) found similar recurrence after extracapsular dissection and superficial parotidectomy for pleomorphic adenoma, with less facial palsy and Frey syndrome after extracapsular dissection, for mobile superficial tumours up to 3 cm (Salzano et al., Med Sci 2025). Facial nerve monitoring is widely used; studies suggest it reduces immediate weakness, especially in revision surgery, but evidence for permanent palsy is weak. Cytology is reported with the Milan system. Malignant tumours need total parotidectomy when the deep lobe is involved, with neck dissection and radiotherapy by grade and stage."],["osec","th:op-92~s6-4","Alternatives","Superficial Parotidectomy · Aftercare","A benign tumour in a patient unfit for surgery can be observed; radiotherapy can be considered for a malignancy in a patient who cannot have surgery."],["osec","th:op-92~s7-1","Where the books differ","Superficial Parotidectomy · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra explains that pleomorphic adenomas have finger-like projections through the capsule, so enucleation leaves tumour behind and excision must include a cuff of normal gland, which in the parotid means a superficial parotidectomy; Warthin tumours (men, tail of gland, bilateral in 10%) are treated by superficial parotidectomy, though enucleation is acceptable for them; a severed facial nerve after trauma is found by superficial parotidectomy and repaired under magnification. Lee explains Frey syndrome as auriculotemporal parasympathetic fibres reinnervating the sweat glands (about 20% after parotidectomy in children), and sets out when superficial parotidectomy is part of treating skin cancer and melanoma of the face, scalp and ear with nodal disease. Myers gives the operation itself: the three landmarks to the main trunk, the plane parallel to the floor, sparing use of the stimulator, drain removal criteria, the management of sialocele with aspiration and botulinum toxin, and when to sacrifice the nerve in a malignancy."],["osec","th:op-92~s7-2","Deciding whether to sacrifice the nerve","Superficial Parotidectomy · For the examination","Preserve a normally working nerve whenever possible; FNA (with only about 52% sensitivity for suspicious and malignant lesions) must not decide its fate. The exceptions, which the patient should know about in advance, are a nerve encased by tumour, frozen section evidence of infiltration, or gross tumour that cannot otherwise be removed. A nerve that was already paralysed before surgery is resected with the tumour. Microscopic disease left on the nerve can be treated with radiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-92~s7-3","Viva essentials","Superficial Parotidectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The landmarks for the main trunk : tragal pointer, tympanomastoid suture, posterior belly of digastric, styloid (deep). The retrograde approach from a peripheral branch. Why enucleation of a pleomorphic adenoma recurs. The commonest benign and malignant salivary tumours (pleomorphic adenoma; mucoepidermoid carcinoma). Lymphoma in the differential, and why it must not be operated on unnecessarily. Frey syndrome : mechanism, Minor's test, prevention and treatment. Nerve repair : technique, grafts, the expected best result (House-Brackmann III). When to sacrifice the nerve ."],["osec","th:op-92~s7-4","Common pitfalls","Superficial Parotidectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not considering lymphoma before operating. Dissecting perpendicular, down toward the styloid, past the nerve. Pulling on the nerve, or stimulating it over and over. Cutting rather than spreading over the nerve. Breaching the capsule of a pleomorphic adenoma. A thin skin flap without its fat. Taking out a drain before the output has settled."],["osec","th:op-93~s1-1","The case at a glance","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Operating note","Deep lobe parotidectomy Summary Indications Parotid neoplasms (surgical excision is the treatment); chronic parotitis refractory to treatment (remove all remaining gland, including deep lobe); biopsy or subtotal resection when the likely diagnosis is not surgical (lymphoma, sarcoidosis) Contraindications Comorbidity with high anaesthetic risk, especially when imaging and FNA suggest a benign tumour (a Warthin tumour may be observed) Anaesthesia General, no long-acting paralysis Monitoring Facial nerve EMG is controversial in primary surgery (no proven reduction in weakness) but common and helpful in revisions Tray Monopolar and bipolar diathermy, ultrasonic device if wanted; nerve stimulator; adrenaline pledgets; head and neck set; McCabe dissector; saw and plates if mandibulotomy is possible; loupes or microscope Skills Superficial parotidectomy, parapharyngeal surgery, neck dissection"],["osec","th:op-93~s1-2","Consent","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: to remove a lump deep in the salivary gland, under the facial nerve. Facial weakness is commoner than in superficial surgery because the nerve must be lifted off the tumour; usually temporary. The nerve will not be cut unless cancer is proven to involve it, and even then every effort is made to keep it. A cut through the jaw (mandibulotomy) may be needed for a large or malignant tumour. Numbness of the ear lobe from the great auricular nerve. Bleeding , a collection of fluid or saliva, and first bite syndrome (pain at the angle of the jaw at the start of meals). Radiotherapy may follow for some cancers."],["osec","th:op-93~s1-3","Before you start","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Operating note","Benign tumours (pleomorphic adenoma and Warthin tumour above all) predominate. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the commonest malignancy, with adenoid cystic nearly as common; others include acinic cell, adenocarcinoma, salivary duct carcinoma, primary squamous carcinoma, carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma, and metastases from facial and scalp skin cancers. Lymphoma occurs, especially with Sjögren's syndrome. Deep lobe masses are found on examination or incidentally on imaging. Most benign ones cause little but the lump; significant or worsening pain, trismus, fixity, skin change and facial weakness suggest malignancy, though even benign deep lobe tumours may feel fixed or be impalpable. Ask how the lump was found and how fast it has grown; about twitching or weakness of the face, dysphagia, otalgia, fullness, hearing loss, epistaxis, visual or voice change, and weight loss, fevers or night sweats. Record previous salivary surgery or infection, smoking (Warthin), previous head and neck cancer and radiotherapy (including radioiodine), skin cancers (squamous, melanoma), autoimmune disease and sarcoidosis, a pacemaker or defibrillator (can the patient have MRI?) and anticoagulants. Look for facial asymmetry and weakness, a muffled voice, poor secretion control or airway obstruction; suspicious skin lesions on the face, scalp and ear; a middle ear effusion (possible parapharyngeal extension); trismus, the teeth and mandibular height (if mandibulotomy is possible), and asymmetry of the soft palate, tonsillar fossa and lateral pharyngeal wall. Examine the mass (size, mobility, firmness,"],["osec","th:op-93~s1-4","Imaging","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Operating note","Cross-sectional imaging is recommended for any possible deep lobe tumour. MRI with contrast best shows the tumour, soft tissue and perineural spread; CT with contrast best shows bone (mandible, mastoid, styloid). PET-CT is rarely needed, as salivary tumours vary in FDG avidity, but helps with possible high-grade or metastatic disease. Where is the nerve on the scan? The nerve cannot be seen, but the retromandibular vein can: follow it up from the external jugular (occasionally the common facial) vein on axial images. The nerve lies just superficial to it, so a deep lobe tumour typically pushes the vein laterally and superficially . The styloid, mastoid and posterior digastric are further clues."],["osec","th:op-93~s2-1","The gland","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The parotid lies in front of and below the ear lobe, invested by the superficial layer of deep cervical fascia, which becomes continuous with the SMAS in front. Its posterior and lower margin is the front of the upper sternomastoid; its deep surface touches the mastoid, the ear canal, the ramus and, lower, the posterior digastric and stylohyoid. 70 to 80% of the gland is superficial to the nerve; there is no fascial plane between the \"lobes\", only the nerve."],["osec","th:op-93~s2-2","The facial nerve","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Anatomy you must see","It leaves the stylomastoid foramen , gives small branches to the postauricular muscles and posterior digastric, then divides at the pes anserinus into upper (temporofacial) and lower (cervicofacial) divisions. Landmarks for the trunk: the tragal pointer , the tympanomastoid suture and the posterior belly of digastric ."],["osec","th:op-93~s2-3","The three parts of the deep lobe","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Anatomy you must see","The main part, deep to the nerve between the mastoid behind and the ramus in front: tumours here push the nerve outward. Small anterior portions between the distal branches and the masseter: tumours here look more superficial than they are. A posteroinferior portion joined to the parapharyngeal space through the stylomandibular tunnel : tumours here can grow into the prestyloid parapharyngeal space as a dumbbell."],["osec","th:op-93~s3-1","Prepare and incise","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Prepare so the whole face can be watched, with the whole neck, and across the midline if a mandibulotomy is possible. Make a modified Blair incision from in front of and above the tragus around the ear lobe: extend it back behind the ear for posterior or high lesions, or forward into a transverse crease in the neck for anterior lesions and most deep lobe tumours. Hatch marks help closure. What you should see The whole side of the face visible through a clear drape, and the incision marked with cross-hatches."],["osec","th:op-93~s3-2","Flap, sternomastoid and great auricular nerve","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Raise the anterior flap over the parotid fascia. Free the front of the sternomastoid. Keep the great auricular nerve and its branches if exposure allows; if you divide it, cut it sharply and bury the proximal end in muscle against a painful neuroma. The external jugular vein lies about 1 cm in front of it; keep it unless it limits exposure. What you should see The flap raised on the parotid fascia to the front of the gland, the great auricular nerve on the sternocleidomastoid, and the posterior digastric."],["osec","th:op-93~s3-3","Find the trunk","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Retract the sternomastoid to show the posterior digastric. Open the avascular plane between parotid and tragal cartilage to the pointer , and divide the tail of the gland with bipolar diathermy, using the pointer and digastric as guides. Find the trunk by the pointer, the tympanomastoid suture and the digastric. What you should see The trunk about 1 cm deep and inferomedial to the pointer, just above the digastric, dividing into its two divisions. A displaced trunk A deep lobe tumour near the stylomastoid foramen may push the main trunk laterally and superficially , closer to you than you expect."],["osec","th:op-93~s3-4","Keep the field dry near the nerve","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Use pressure, adrenaline pledgets, well-placed clips and careful bipolar diathermy. Never use monopolar diathermy over nerve branches: it carries current into the nerve. What you should see A field dry enough to see every branch before you cut near it."],["osec","th:op-93~s3-5","Release the branches","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Follow the nerve to expose the branches needed for safe removal. The branches near the tumour usually have to be fully dissected and freed from it so the tumour can be mobilised. Divide parotid tissue over the nerve with bipolar, ultrasonic or cold knife. The superficial lobe can be released from back to front while left pedicled anteriorly. Figure 1 A small left parotid tumour found incidentally on brain imaging: (A) just deep to a main branch of the upper division; (B) the nerve freed from it and the tumour removed (basal cell adenoma). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 93.1 Figure 2 A large deep lobe pleomorphic adenoma: the main trunk lies just deep to the retractor and the tumour deep to the lower division, with superiorly and inferiorly based portions of the superficial lobe kept for closure. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 93.2 What you should see The trunk and divisions draped over the tumour and freed from it, the tumour delivered intact."],["osec","th:op-93~s3-6","Close","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Replace any preserved superficial lobe, which gives an excellent cosmetic result. Place a suction drain along the posterior digastric, away from the nerve. What you should see All branches intact and stimulating, and the superficial lobe back in place over them."],["osec","th:op-93~s3-7","The dumbbell tumour","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · The operation, stage by stage","Tumours from the posteroinferior deep lobe can pass through the stylomandibular tunnel and widen in the parapharyngeal space. A purely parapharyngeal tumour is usually removed through the neck alone; a dumbbell needs both parotid and cervical exposure. Figure 3 MRI (axial T1, fat-suppressed, contrast) of a dumbbell tumour of the deep lobe extending into the parapharyngeal space. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 93.3 Begin as for a deep lobe tumour, with an incision extended further into the neck. Once the trunk is found, feel or see the deep lobe part and free it gently. Approach the parapharyngeal part through the neck: skeletonise the posterior digastric. Leave the submandibular gland, or divide the facial vessels lateral to it to let it fall medially. Retract the digastric down to see the deep parotid and parapharyngeal space. To mobilise the medial part: divide the posterior digastric (at its medial tendinous part if you want to repair it, since cut muscle fibres will not hold sutures), divide the stylomandibular ligament , and press from medial to lateral through the oropharynx. Figure 4 A large epithelial-myoepithelial carcinoma of the deep lobe and parapharyngeal space; the imprint of the stylomandibular ligament marks the junction between the two parts. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 93.4 What you should see The tumour passing behind the mandible through the stylomandibular tunnel, with the trunk and lower division lifted off it."],["osec","th:op-93~s4-1","Crisis drills","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · When it goes wrong","The tumour may be malignant and involves the nerve 1 Confirm frozen section reliably separates high-grade from benign or low-grade tumours 2 Before cutting a nerve require pathological proof of malignancy, even if that means waiting for final histology 3 Even then in a patient with normal function, try hard to keep the nerve in continuity Expanding haematoma 1 Small most can be watched 2 Expanding with airway compromise return to theatre"],["osec","th:op-93~s4-2","Common errors in technique","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · When it goes wrong","Common pitfalls Poor selection: inadequate imaging of extent or metastatic work-up. Operating on malignancy with perineural spread along several nerves that was not assessed or not resected. Taking on deep lobe tumours without enough experience. Resecting a facial nerve that could have been preserved."],["osec","th:op-93~s5-1","After the operation","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Aftercare","Watch the wound and drain overnight; remove the drain on day 1 unless the output is high (over 100 mL total or over 50 mL in the last shift). Antibiotic ointment on the sutures for 3 to 5 days, remembering that some patients react to it with redness. Remove sutures at about a week in a healthy, non-irradiated patient, and start massaging the scar at 2 weeks."],["osec","th:op-93~s5-2","Complications","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Aftercare","Complications Complication Notes Facial weakness Commoner after the manipulation deep lobe tumours need Haematoma Usually observed; theatre if expanding with airway compromise Seroma or sialocele Resolve with conservative care and aspiration First bite syndrome After dissection into the parapharyngeal space or mobilisation of the deep lobe Ear lobe numbness Great auricular nerve"],["ev","th:op-93~s5-3","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy: current evidence","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Aftercare","Deep lobe and parapharyngeal tumours. Most pre-styloid parapharyngeal space tumours are deep lobe pleomorphic adenomas, which can be removed through a transcervical or transcervical–transparotid approach without mandibulotomy in most cases. Post-styloid tumours are usually schwannomas or paragangliomas. Transoral robotic removal is reported for selected benign tumours, with concern about capsule rupture and tumour spillage. Facial nerve dissection is needed when the tumour passes between the nerve's branches."],["osec","th:op-93~s5-3","Outcomes","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · Aftercare","Complete excision cures benign tumours; pleomorphic adenomas are controlled long term with close but clear margins. For malignancy, outcome depends on the histology, grade and margins. Postoperative radiotherapy is indicated for high-grade tumours, positive margins, high T stage and nodal disease, and for adenoid cystic carcinoma even with clear margins, because of its perineural spread and local recurrence."],["osec","th:op-93~s6-1","Is the great auricular nerve worth keeping?","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · For the examination","Numbness around the ear is common, sometimes the commonest complaint after parotidectomy. Preserving the posterior branch gives better sensation in the short term and some long-term improvement, but sensation never returns to normal, and long-term quality of life data do not support routine preservation."],["osec","th:op-93~s6-2","Viva essentials","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The deep lobe is defined by the nerve, not by a fascial plane; its three parts. The retromandibular vein on imaging and what its displacement means. The landmarks for the trunk. Why FNA and imaging matter more for deep lobe tumours. Freeing the branches; no monopolar diathermy near the nerve. The dumbbell tumour and the manoeuvres to deliver it. When to sacrifice the nerve, and postoperative radiotherapy."],["osec","th:op-93~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Deep Lobe Parotidectomy · For the examination","Common pitfalls Expecting the trunk at its usual depth when the tumour has pushed it out. Dividing the digastric through its muscle belly if you want to repair it. Forgetting to prepare across the midline when a mandibulotomy may be needed."],["osec","th:op-95~s1-1","The case at a glance","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Operating note","Septoplasty Summary Indications Symptomatic nasal obstruction with a deviated septum; access to the sinuses for endoscopic sinus surgery; access to the sphenoid and pituitary; access for epistaxis behind a deviation; access for endoscopic dacryocystorhinostomy Contraindications Unfit for general anaesthesia; a bleeding tendency or anticoagulation that cannot be stopped around the operation; acute nasal or sinus infection Anaesthesia General, with an oral RAE tube resting in the midline; local with topical anaesthesia is possible Position Supine; for endoscopic work, head up 20 to 30 degrees and tilted 15 degrees toward the surgeon, with surgeon, patient and screen in one line Antibiotics First-generation cephalosporin; clindamycin or gentamicin if allergic to penicillin Monitoring Standard anaesthetic monitoring; a heart rate around 60 and mean arterial pressure around 60 mm Hg reduce bleeding where safe Imaging Not needed routinely; CT only if another cause of obstruction or sinus disease is suspected Tray Basic septorhinoplasty set (speculums, Woodson and Cottle elevators, double-action scissors and rongeurs, swivel knife, 4 to 6 mm chisel or osteotome, mallet); 0- and 30-degree telescopes, camera and tower for the endoscopic technique; Doyle splints; 4-0 and 5-0 plain gut, 3-0 Prolene Before theatre Stop antiplatelets and herbal supplements; clotting tests if there is a bleeding history; medical clearance where needed; a trial of topical steroid if allergy is suspected"],["osec","th:op-95~s1-2","Consent","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it does: straightens the partition inside the nose to improve breathing; it is done through the nostril and leaves no outside scar. What it does not do: it will not cure allergy or rhinitis, and it will not change the shape of the nose unless a separate rhinoplasty is planned. Bleeding , usually minor; occasionally packing or a return to theatre. A blood clot or infection between the layers of the septum , which must be drained quickly. A hole in the septum (perforation), which can whistle, crust or bleed. A change in the shape of the nose : a dip above the tip if support is lost; uncommon. The blockage may persist or recur, and further surgery may be needed. Rare: a leak of brain fluid, loss of smell (about one in a million), toxic shock syndrome. Splints stay in for about a week; numbness of the upper front teeth can last weeks."],["osec","th:op-95~s1-3","Before you start","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Ask which symptom bothers the patient most, because that guides what you promise: blockage, reduced smell, bleeding, facial pressure, discharge or postnasal drip. How long has it been there, and is it worse on one side? (An S-shaped septum blocks both sides.) What happened with steroid sprays, decongestants and external nasal dilator strips? Ask about allergy, previous nasal surgery (cosmetic or functional: it raises the chance of weak cartilage support and of scarred flaps that tear), previous nasal trauma, medical illness, antiplatelets, herbal products, alcohol and smoking. 1.3.2 The examination Watch the patient breathe quietly and then sniff firmly: does the side wall collapse inwards (static or dynamic valve collapse)? Anterior rhinoscopy: describe the deviation (tilt, curve, angle, spur, groove, where it lies), the position of the caudal septum, and whether the angle of the internal valve is narrowed. Palpate the caudal septum: its position, strength, angle and relationship to the dorsal strut. Endoscopy in three passes: along the floor (inferior turbinate and meatus, nasolacrimal duct opening, nasopharynx with the fossa of Rosenmüller and tubal opening); toward the middle meatus (bulla, uncinate, sphenoethmoidal recess, superior turbinate, sphenoid ostium); and up to the olfactory cleft with a 30-degree scope. Look for polyps, masses and adenoids, and record any existing perforation. Four questions before you book a routine septoplasty Does the nasal valve narrow or collapse? Is the dorsal part of the septum bent? Is the caudal end of the septum of"],["osec","th:op-95~s2-1","The septal framework","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Anatomy you must see","Quadrangular cartilage in front, sitting on the maxillary crest and the anterior nasal spine below. Perpendicular plate of the ethmoid above and behind, joined to the cribriform plate at the skull base. Vomer below and behind. The keystone area at the rhinion, where the cartilage meets the nasal bones and the perpendicular plate; separating the cartilage here drops the bridge of the nose."],["osec","th:op-95~s2-2","The plane and the struts","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Anatomy you must see","The correct plane is beneath the perichondrium over cartilage and beneath the periosteum over bone: the cartilage appears pale blue and the dissection is quick and almost bloodless. At least 1 cm of dorsal strut and 1 cm of caudal strut (an L-shaped strut) must be preserved to hold up the dorsum and the tip. The internal nasal valve (between the septum and upper lateral cartilage) and external valve (the vestibule) are the narrowest, highest-resistance points of the airway: small deviations here matter far more than large ones on the floor. Figure 1 A caudal septum displaced off the anterior nasal spine: excising a small wedge of cartilage lets the caudal strut sit back in the midline over the spine. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.4"],["osec","th:op-95~s3-1","Re-examine, inject and decongest","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: classic septoplasty","After induction, look in the nose again and palpate the caudal septum to confirm what you found in clinic. Infiltrate both sides of the septum with 5 to 10 ml of 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline, then place oxymetazoline-soaked pledgets for 10 to 15 minutes. What you should see A blanched, well-decongested septum and turbinates, and a caudal septum whose position you have felt."],["osec","th:op-95~s3-2","The hemitransfixion incision","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: classic septoplasty","Expose the caudal end of the septum with a small speculum and cut along its free caudal edge with a size 15 blade, usually on the left. If the caudal septum is pushed so far into the right nostril that the left side is hard to reach, use the right. Go through the perichondrium until you see the ice-blue cartilage . Figure 2 The left hemitransfixion incision along the caudal edge of the septum. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.1 What you should see Blue-grey cartilage with no perichondrium on it, and the cut edge of mucoperichondrium clearly separate."],["osec","th:op-95~s3-3","Raise the flaps on both sides","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: classic septoplasty","Start with a Woodson elevator low in the nose under a short speculum, and switch to a Cottle elevator further back, where its flatter profile lets you see past it, using a longer Killian speculum. Raise submucoperichondrial and submucoperiosteal flaps on both sides. In the right plane this is quick and dry. Take particular care over grooves, angles and spurs, where the mucosa is thin and tears easily. Figure 3 The speculum holding the left flap aside: septal cartilage on one side of the elevator, the mucosal flap on the other. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.2 What you should see Smooth white cartilage and bone under a flap that remains intact and does not bleed. Opposing tears A tear on one side is usually harmless; tears on both sides at the same point leave the septum with no cover there, and a perforation follows. Work around angles and spurs patiently, and repair any tear at the end. Senior's pearl Raising both flaps does more than give you room: it releases the mucosal tension that holds the septum bent, and gives you access if you need to reconstruct. Reassess the deviation once both flaps are up; it often looks different."],["osec","th:op-95~s3-4","Separate the cartilage from the bone","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: classic septoplasty","Press steadily with the Cottle elevator at the junction of the quadrangular cartilage and the perpendicular plate; the two separate. Carry the separation up to within 1 to 1.5 cm of the dorsum , and no further. Figure 4 Separating the quadrangular cartilage from the perpendicular plate (left), then the high and low bone cuts with removal of the bent segment between them (right). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.3 The keystone Separating the cartilage all the way to the dorsum disarticulates it at the keystone and the bridge drops (a supratip depression or mild saddle). What you should see The white, rigid perpendicular plate behind the bluish-grey cartilage, and the elevator slipping through to the opposite subperiosteal tunnel with the far flap intact. Senior's pearl Raise the opposite flap from behind this junction, starting on bone: the plane is easier there and the cartilage in front keeps its perichondrium on one side."],["osec","th:op-95~s3-5","Remove the bent bone","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: classic septoplasty","Work from the back of the nose to the front, so that the caudal septum, which supports the tip, is dealt with last. Bent bone is rigid and holds the softer cartilage off the midline, so remove enough of it. Make the upper cut sharply with double-action scissors, high toward the dorsum, and a lower cut low over the crest, then take out the segment between them with a double-action rongeur. The cribriform plate Never twist or pull bone out before the upper cut has been made. The perpendicular plate is attached to the cribriform plate, and avulsing it can fracture the skull base and cause a CSF leak. What you should see Clean edges of bone above and below, both flaps intact, and no bone left pushing the flaps apart at the back when you close them together."],["osec","th:op-95~s3-6","Cartilage and the maxillary crest","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: classic septoplasty","If the cartilage is still off the midline, outline the segment to remove with two cuts under direct vision, one parallel to the dorsum and one parallel to the caudal edge, and remove as little as is needed , keeping the 1 cm dorsal and caudal struts. Straight pieces of removed cartilage can be replaced between the flaps for support, placed above the caudal strut and behind the dorsal strut so they do not overlap and obstruct. Spurs of the maxillary crest that touch the inferior turbinate, large spurs, or a crest that pushes the cartilage across should be removed with a rongeur or a 4 to 6 mm chisel after carefully raising the thin, adherent mucoperiosteum over them; small deviations low on the floor are usually harmless. What you should see A septum that sits in the midline from the back of the nose to the front, with the dorsal and caudal struts intact."],["osec","th:op-95~s3-7","The caudal septum","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: classic septoplasty","Deal with the caudal septum according to its shape: Tilted off the spine, or gently bowed: remove a small wedge of cartilage over the anterior nasal spine, no more than 2 to 3 mm, a little at a time, so the caudal strut sits on the spine without tension. Too little leaves the tilt or a clicking septum; too much lets the strut hang and the supratip settle. Angulated, as after a fracture: a wedge does not remove the angle. Divide the caudal strut vertically through the apex of the angle, then overlap the two segments and suture them, trying both overlap directions to see which opens the airway best; or trim the overlap, bring the ends together, and bridge them with a sutured batten graft of septal cartilage. Rotated so that the caudal edge points sideways: this needs the caudal septum removed and replaced or reconstructed, best through an external septorhinoplasty by an experienced surgeon. Figure 5 A gently bowed caudal septum released by shortening it a little above the spine, so that it straightens into the midline. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.5 Figure 6 An angulated caudal septum divided at the apex; the segments are overlapped and sutured, or set end to end with a batten graft. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.6 Senior's pearl Caudal and dorsal deviations are the commonest reasons a septoplasty fails, because they sit at the valves where resistance is highest. If you cannot see the head of the middle turbinate easily without forcing the speculum open or pushing it deep, the internal valve is still narrow: deal with it. What you should see The caudal "],["osec","th:op-95~s3-8","Check the airway, quilt and splint","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: classic septoplasty","Run through the same checklist every time, from back to front: is the nose open at the back? Is it open at the internal valve? Is it open along the floor? Is the caudal septum straight and sitting in the midline without tension? Then quilt the flaps together with a running through-and-through 4-0 plain gut suture to close the dead space, and close the incision with 5-0 plain gut or through-and-through septocolumellar sutures. Place Doyle splints on both sides, secured with a through-and-through 3-0 Prolene suture. What you should see A straight septum, both valves open, the flaps lying flat with no dead space, and splints sitting without pressure on the turbinates."],["osec","th:op-95~c4","The operation: endoscopic septoplasty","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: endoscopic septoplasty","Endoscopic septoplasty gives an excellent view of posterior and high deviations and suits patients who also need endoscopic sinus surgery. It is also used for isolated spurs."],["osec","th:op-95~s4-1","Stages","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: endoscopic septoplasty","Raise the flap through the hemitransfixion incision far enough (2 to 3 cm) to admit the scope, then continue with a 0-degree telescope, raising the flaps with a Cottle elevator while keeping an instrument or the scope in the plane so that the flap is not handled repeatedly. If the caudal septum is straight, raise the flap on one side only at the front, and cross through the cartilage to the other side further back, just in front of the deviation (measure the distance from the columella with the elevator). Remove the bent cartilage with a Ballenger swivel knife or through-cutting Blakesley forceps, keeping the struts; make the upper bone cut with double-action scissors held with the jaws vertical so that you do not injure the middle turbinates. Figure 7 The endoscopic view with a 0-degree telescope: the mucosal flap on one side of the sucker and the quadrangular cartilage on the other. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.7 Figure 8 Raising the flaps on both sides with a Cottle elevator. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.8 What you should see Once the deviation is removed, the axilla of the middle turbinate on both sides , which you need if frontal sinus surgery is planned. Figure 9 The axilla of the middle turbinate is now visible, giving access to the sinuses, especially the frontal. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.9"],["osec","th:op-95~s4-2","Isolated spurs","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: endoscopic septoplasty","Raise a small C-shaped flap in front of the spur, remove the spur with through-cutting Blakesley or Grünwald forceps, and lay the mucosa back over the defect. There is no need for large flaps. Figure 10 A local flap raised in front of a spur, the spur removed, and the mucosa replaced. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 95.10 What you should see The spur's apex exposed on both sides before it is cut, and mucosa covering the defect at the end. Opposing tears A tear on the convex side is common and harmless if the other flap is intact; tears facing each other become a perforation. Leave or replace cartilage between them."],["osec","th:op-95~s4-3","Closing","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · The operation: endoscopic septoplasty","Close the incision with interrupted 5-0 fast-absorbing sutures and quilt the flaps with 4-0 plain gut. The quilting stitch can include the middle turbinates (a medialisation suture) to stop them drifting laterally and scarring. Place the Doyle splints under endoscopic view so that they do not push the middle turbinate laterally, trimming the upper back corner so it does not cut the medialisation suture. What you should see Flaps lying together with no dead space, a straight septum, and both middle turbinates visible without forcing the speculum. Septal haematoma Blood between the flaps strips the cartilage of its supply; infection then melts it and the dorsum collapses. The quilting stitch is what prevents this."],["osec","th:op-95~s5-1","Crisis drills","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · When it goes wrong","Bleeding after the operation 1 First oxymetazoline spray gentle packing with absorbable haemostatic mesh over the splints 2 Then remove the splints and cauterise a bleeding point if seen 3 Escalate examination and control in theatre Septal haematoma 1 Recognise increasing pain, blockage and a swollen septum on both sides, in the days after surgery 2 Act aspirate with an 18 gauge needle if incomplete, open, drain and quilt or pack early, with antibiotics 3 Why an unrecognised haematoma kills the remaining cartilage and causes a saddle nose Toxic shock syndrome 1 Recognise early fever, vomiting or severe diarrhoea, rash, low blood pressure, cold or ischaemic limbs 2 Act remove and culture any packing or splints anti-staphylococcal antibiotics, fluids and close monitoring 3 Escalate intensive care as needed Clear fluid from the nose 1 Recognise clear watery discharge, especially on bending, after bone was pulled rather than cut 2 Act test for beta-2 transferrin CT to find the defect 3 Treat bed rest and observation for a small leak endoscopic repair if it persists Unexpected bleeding between the flaps at the end 1 Act make a small venting stab in the posterior septal mucosa (if there is no tear) so blood can escape 2 Then quilt carefully to close the dead space"],["osec","th:op-95~s6-1","After the operation","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Aftercare","Most patients go home the same day ; those with obstructive sleep apnoea are better observed overnight. Antibiotic cover against S. aureus while the splints are in. Saline rinses , a high-volume bottle three to four times a day, keep the splints clear; then clean the nostrils with a cotton bud and apply an antibiotic ointment. No nose blowing or strenuous exercise for about a week; sneeze with the mouth open. Splints out at 5 to 7 days. Sinusitis: culture-directed antibiotics, or amoxicillin-clavulanate (doxycycline if allergic to penicillin)."],["osec","th:op-95~s6-2","Complications","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Aftercare","Complications of septoplasty Complication Cause or prevention Bleeding Usually minor; spray, packing, cautery or theatre Septal haematoma and abscess Dead space; prevented by quilting Perforation Opposing mucosal tears; repair tears at the end Supratip depression or saddle Separation at the keystone, or too much dorsal strut removed Columellar retraction Caudal strut not preserved Persistent obstruction Missed caudal or dorsal deviation, valve collapse, too little bone removed Flapping septum Too much framework removed CSF leak Avulsion of the perpendicular plate Loss of smell Very rare Toxic shock syndrome Staphylococcal toxin; rare with modern splints"],["ev","th:op-95~s6-3","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic: current evidence","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Aftercare","Effectiveness. Two randomised trials show septoplasty is better than medical treatment for nasal obstruction with a deviated septum: in the Dutch trial, quality of life improved at 12 and 24 months (van Egmond et al., Lancet 2019), and in the UK NAIROS trial, SNOT-22 scores were 20 points better at six months than with a steroid spray and saline (Carrie et al., Health Technol Assess 2024). Patient selection. The NOSE score, a deviation that matches the symptoms, and a failed trial of a nasal steroid predict benefit. Packs and splints. Trials show that nasal packing after septoplasty can usually be omitted, with quilting sutures or splints instead, causing less pain without more haematoma."],["osec","th:op-95~s6-3","Alternatives","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · Aftercare","Allergy assessment and treatment of rhinitis or turbinate swelling; a trial of steroid spray and antihistamine where allergy is likely. Patients with no inflammatory history and an obvious structural deformity may go straight to surgery. Severe caudal, dorsal or valve problems call for a functional septorhinoplasty. A camouflage onlay graft of crushed cartilage can hide a mild saddle afterwards. Outcomes are now measured with the validated NOSE scale , on which primary and revision septoplasty both show significant improvement and high patient satisfaction."],["osec","th:op-95~s7-1","Where the books differ","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra separates the old submucous resection (SMR) , which removes nearly all the cartilage and bone through bilateral flaps leaving only dorsal and caudal struts and is not done before 17 years, from septoplasty , which keeps as much framework as possible, raises a full flap on one side only, can be done in children, and reshapes cartilage by scoring the concave side, cross-hatching, shaving, wedge excision and repositioning on the crest; describes the Killian incision on the concave side for most deviations and the hemitransfixion (Freer) incision for caudal dislocation; packs the nose for 24 hours; and keeps SMR today only to harvest graft material. Lee places septoplasty among nasal procedures for obstruction and sleep apnoea, stresses that nasal surgery does not lower the apnoea index, though it can improve sleep quality and CPAP tolerance, and that septoplasty is often part of sinus surgery for access. Myers raises flaps on both sides through a hemitransfixion incision as a routine, to release tension and allow reconstruction; uses quilting sutures and Doyle splints instead of packs; gives a back-to-front intraoperative checklist; and classifies caudal deformities into tilted, angulated and rotated, with a correction for each."],["osec","th:op-95~s7-2","Viva essentials","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The difference between SMR and septoplasty , and why septoplasty replaced it. The incisions : Killian, hemitransfixion, transfixion, and a horizontal incision over a spur. The L-strut : at least 1 cm dorsal and 1 cm caudal; what happens if either is lost (supratip depression, columellar retraction). The keystone area and the danger of separating the cartilage up to the dorsum. Why you cut the perpendicular plate before removing it (cribriform plate, CSF leak). The internal and external valves , the Cottle test, and why small deviations there matter. Recognising and draining a septal haematoma . The NOSE scale ."],["osec","th:op-95~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Septoplasty—Classic and Endoscopic · For the examination","Common pitfalls Booking a routine septoplasty for a caudal, dorsal or valve problem. Raising the flap above the perichondrium: bleeding and tears. Tears on both sides at the same point. Separating the cartilage all the way to the dorsum. Twisting out bone before the upper cut. Leaving bent bone that drags the cartilage off the midline. Removing the dorsal or caudal strut. Forgetting to quilt, leaving dead space."],["osec","th:op-96~s1-1","The case at a glance","Nasal Septal Perforation · Operating note","Repair of a nasal septal perforation Summary Indications Symptomatic perforation (crusting, bleeding, whistling, obstruction, pain, smell) not controlled by conservative care; cosmetic deformity from loss of support Contraindications Active vasculitis or granulomatous disease; ongoing cocaine or other intranasal drug use; malignancy not excluded; very large perforation with poor mucosa (consider a button) Conservative first Saline douching, emollient ointment, humidification; a silicone septal button Work-up Endoscopy, biopsy of the edge if the cause is unclear, ESR/CRP, ANCA, ACE, syphilis serology, urine drug screen if suspected Size Measure height and length: under 2 cm closes reliably; over 2 cm is harder Anaesthesia General Tray Endoscopes, septoplasty set, through-cutting forceps, backbiter, interposition graft (acellular dermis, fascia, septal or auricular cartilage), absorbable sutures, Silastic splints"],["osec","th:op-96~s1-2","Consent","Nasal Septal Perforation · Operating note","What to tell the patient Closure is not guaranteed: about 85% to 90% overall, less for large holes; the hole may enlarge or a new one form. Bleeding, infection, septal haematoma. Adhesions, reduced smell, rarely a watering eye. Splints for about 2 to 3 weeks. No cocaine or nose picking afterwards, or the repair will fail."],["osec","th:op-96~s1-3","Before you start","Nasal Septal Perforation · Operating note","Find the cause and treat it. Biopsy the posterior or superior edge, away from the support, if the cause is uncertain. Make sure active disease has been quiet for months and drug use has stopped. Measure the perforation and assess the remaining septal support and the mucosa around it. Figure 1 Anatomy of the septum and its blood supply, which the flaps rely on. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 96.1 Figure 2 An algorithm for managing a septal perforation. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 96.2"],["osec","th:op-96~s2-1","Vasoconstrict and hydrodissect","Nasal Septal Perforation · The operation, stage by stage","Inject both sides of the septum in the subperichondrial and subperiosteal planes and the nasal floors with lidocaine and adrenaline, from back to front, using a \"pull-flash\" technique; wait 10 minutes. Place oxymetazoline pledgets. What you should see Blanched mucosa lifted from the cartilage around the perforation."],["osec","th:op-96~s2-2","Clean and freshen the edge","Nasal Septal Perforation · The operation, stage by stage","Under the endoscope, remove crusts with warm saline irrigation. De-epithelialise the perforation margin all round (through-cutting forceps behind, a blade above and below, a backbiter in front), taking as little tissue as possible. What you should see Raw, bleeding mucosal edges on both sides of the hole, no larger than before. Do not enlarge the hole Every millimetre removed from the edge adds to the defect you must close. Freshen; do not trim."],["osec","th:op-96~s2-3","Raise the flaps","Nasal Septal Perforation · The operation, stage by stage","Through a hemitransfixion incision (or an open rhinoplasty approach for large or anterior holes), elevate subperichondrial flaps on both sides above and below the perforation, and separate the two mucosal layers at its edge. Design the closure: superior and inferior bipedicled advancement flaps (the inferior one extending along the nasal floor) on one side, and an anterior ethmoidal artery flap or advancement flaps on the other. What you should see Mobile, intact flaps on each side that reach across the hole without pulling."],["osec","th:op-96~s2-4","Interpose the graft","Nasal Septal Perforation · The operation, stage by stage","Lay a graft larger than the hole (acellular dermis, temporalis fascia or septal or auricular cartilage) between the two sides as a scaffold. What you should see The graft covering the perforation with a generous overlap."],["osec","th:op-96~s2-5","Close each side without tension","Nasal Septal Perforation · The operation, stage by stage","Advance and suture the flaps to close the mucosa on both sides with fine absorbable sutures. Where both sides cannot be closed, close one completely; the other heals over the graft. Use quilting sutures to close dead space, then insert Silastic splints sutured through the septum. What you should see Both sides closed (or one closed and the other covered by graft), with splints in place. Senior's pearl Tension is the enemy. Release the flaps until they lie across the hole on their own; a closure that needs pulling will break down at the suture line."],["osec","th:op-96~s3-1","Crisis drills","Nasal Septal Perforation · When it goes wrong","Septal haematoma after repair 1 Act evacuate quilting sutures to close the space antibiotics Repair fails 1 Wait at least 6 months for healing 2 Then a septal button, or a second repair with an open approach or a different flap"],["osec","th:op-96~s4-1","After surgery","Nasal Septal Perforation · Aftercare","Oral antibiotics while the splints are in; saline douching and ointment; no nose blowing for 2 weeks; remove splints at 2 to 3 weeks. Examine at 3 months for closure."],["ev","th:op-96~s4-2","Nasal Septal Perforation: current evidence","Nasal Septal Perforation · Aftercare","The size of the perforation is the strongest predictor of closure: failure is commoner above 2 cm, and bilateral vascularised flap cover improves success (Kim and Rhee, Curr Opin Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2012). A systematic review of 329 purely endoscopic repairs (mean size 17 mm) reported closure in 90%, with similar success for one-, two- and three-layer repairs (Gravina et al., Ann Otol Rhinol Laryngol 2023). For large perforations (2 cm or more), a meta-analysis of 408 patients found overall closure of 84%: about 88% with mucosal advancement and 87% with interposition grafts (Cheung et al., Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 2026)."],["osec","th:op-96~s4-2","Complications","Nasal Septal Perforation · Aftercare","Bleeding, septal haematoma or abscess, adhesions, reperforation or enlargement, reduced smell, epiphora, infection."],["osec","th:op-96~s5-1","Where the books differ","Nasal Septal Perforation · For the examination","Where the books differ Myers bilateral intranasal mucosal flaps (advancement on one side, an anterior ethmoidal artery flap on the other), tension-free closure and an acellular dermis interposition graft. Dhingra and K. J. Lee the causes (surgery, trauma, cocaine, granulomatous disease, syphilis) and conservative care, including the septal button. The literature closure rates of 85% to 90%, falling with size above 2 cm."],["osec","th:op-96~s5-2","Viva essentials","Nasal Septal Perforation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Causes and the investigations to find them. Who to repair and who to button. The principles : vascularised flaps, tension-free closure, interposition graft. Prognostic factors: size, cause, mucosa."],["osec","th:op-96~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Nasal Septal Perforation · For the examination","Common pitfalls Repairing a perforation from active disease or continuing cocaine use. Trimming rather than freshening the edge. Closing under tension. Biopsying the anterior edge and enlarging the hole."],["osec","th:op-97~s1-1","The case at a glance","Surgery of the Turbinate · Operating note","Turbinate surgery Summary Indications Inferior turbinate: nasal obstruction from turbinate hypertrophy despite medical treatment, alone or with septoplasty (compensatory hypertrophy on the concave side). Middle turbinate: a large concha bullosa causing obstruction, blocking the ostiomeatal complex, preventing septal correction, or limiting access to the uncinate and bulla in sinus surgery Contraindications Unacceptable anaesthetic risk; an objectively open nose after previous turbinate surgery with persistent subjective blockage (likely empty nose or another cause); a sinonasal tumour, which is treated first Anaesthesia General for submucosal resection with a microdebrider or through-cutting instruments; sedation or local only for low-bleeding-risk techniques such as submucosal coblation, ideally where the airway can be managed Position Supine under general; beach-chair or sitting for awake procedures Antibiotics Not clearly needed without packs or splints; if used, a first-generation cephalosporin, or clindamycin if allergic to penicillin Monitoring No special monitoring Imaging Not needed for obstruction alone; CT if chronic rhinosinusitis or a concha bullosa affecting drainage is suspected Tray 0- and 30-degree telescopes; sharp Molt elevator or blade (or needle-tip diathermy) for the incision; Cottle elevator; through-cutting forceps; microdebrider with 2.9 to 3.5 mm turbinate blades; Frazier sucker; diathermy; for the middle turbinate, a sickle knife and turbinate scissors Before theatre A proper trial of medical treatment and control of allergy or rhinitis; stop antip"],["osec","th:op-97~s1-2","Consent","Surgery of the Turbinate · Operating note","What to tell the patient What it does: reduces the bulk inside the lower (or middle) turbinate to open the airway, while keeping the lining that warms and moistens the air. What it may not do: allergy and rhinitis continue and still need treatment; symptoms can return over time. Bleeding , the commonest problem (around one in ten in early series), usually managed in clinic. Scar bands (adhesions) between the turbinate and septum, more likely when septoplasty is done at the same time; usually cut in clinic. Crusting for a few weeks. Sinus infection. Rare: injury to the tear duct and a watering eye; a CSF leak after middle turbinate surgery. Empty nose: a rare feeling of blockage or suffocation despite a wide nose, which is hard to treat; avoided by keeping the lining."],["osec","th:op-97~s1-3","Before you start","Surgery of the Turbinate · Operating note","1.3.1 The history Fixed or variable blockage? Fixed suggests a bad septal deviation, gross turbinate hypertrophy, polyps or tumour; variable suggests the turbinate's venous sinusoids and the nasal cycle. Worse lying down? Engorgement of the turbinate sinusoids: turbinate reduction is likely to help. Worse with a hard sniff or on exercise? Think of nasal valve collapse. Better with a decongestant spray? A soft-tissue turbinate problem that reduction should help. Better with external nasal strips, cones or lifting the tip? The valve or external framework needs attention, instead of or as well as the turbinates. One side or both? The blockage may be on the side of the septal deviation, or on the opposite side from compensatory turbinate hypertrophy. New one-sided blockage with bleeding must be investigated for a tumour. Previous treatment and surgery: what was done, and how much and for how long it helped. Ask about decongestant overuse. 1.3.2 Comorbidities that keep the nose blocked Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis, chronic rhinosinusitis, adenoid hypertrophy and nasopharyngitis, rhinitis medicamentosa, chemotherapy mucositis, and vasculitis or granulomatous disease (fragile mucosa, more crusting: dissect gently). 1.3.3 The examination Watch quiet and forced breathing for valve collapse; anterior rhinoscopy for the caudal septum, columella and valve; endoscopy for concha bullosa, posterior deviations, polyps and the nasopharynx; and look in the mouth for large tonsils (and occasionally an antrochoanal polyp). Causes of nasal obstruction Common Less common Septal deviation A"],["osec","th:op-97~s2-1","Airflow","Surgery of the Turbinate · Anatomy you must see","Most air passes through the middle of the nasal cavity, over the upper surface of the inferior turbinate and along the body of the middle turbinate to the nasopharynx. Turbinate hypertrophy changes the flow: more turbulence, more negative pressure and more shear on the mucosa."],["osec","th:op-97~s2-2","The inferior turbinate","Surgery of the Turbinate · Anatomy you must see","The anterior head forms the lower boundary of the internal nasal valve , the narrowest part of the nose; reducing it carefully has the biggest effect on airflow. The turbinate bone is irregular, so watch your elevator at all times. The inferior turbinate branch of the posterior nasal artery usually runs within the bone or partly in bone and partly in submucosa; the lower surface has the fewest large vessels. The nasolacrimal duct opens in the inferior meatus about 30 to 40 mm behind the nostril sill ; injuring the lateral wall there causes epiphora. The posterior tip (the mulberry end) bleeds readily."],["osec","th:op-97~s2-3","The middle turbinate","Surgery of the Turbinate · Anatomy you must see","The hiatus semilunaris , with the uncinate process and ethmoid bulla , lies lateral to it; injuring them scars the ostiomeatal complex and causes sinusitis. The turbinate hangs from the cribriform plate ; twisting or avulsing it can tear the skull base. The basal lamella marks the back limit of a concha bullosa resection. More olfactory epithelium may lie on the lateral than the medial lamella of a concha bullosa. Figure 1 The lateral half of a right concha bullosa removed: the uncinate process and ethmoid bulla are left untouched so that the hiatus and infundibulum keep working. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 97.1"],["osec","th:op-97~s3-1","Decongest and inject","Surgery of the Turbinate · The operation: inferior turbinate reduction","Apply 0.05% oxymetazoline pledgets. Inject 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline along the inferomedial surface and the anterior tip of the inferior turbinate. What you should see A shrunken, pale turbinate and a clear view of its head, body and attachment."],["osec","th:op-97~s3-2","Incision and elevation","Surgery of the Turbinate · The operation: inferior turbinate reduction","Under the endoscope, make a stab incision in the front of the turbinate at its attachment to the lateral wall, with the sharp end of a Molt elevator, a blade, needle-tip diathermy or a turbinate microdebrider blade. Raise the mucosa off the medial, inferior and upper surfaces of the bone, keeping the tip of the elevator in view as the bone undulates. The bone can be outfractured from inside the pocket as you go back, which improves the view. Figure 2 Submucosal reduction: incision in the turbinate mucosa, elevation off the bone, removal of bone with biting forceps, microdebrider on the lateral side, and the medial mucosa rolled laterally. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 97.2 What you should see A long submucosal tunnel with the turbinate bone exposed inside it and the covering mucosa intact."],["osec","th:op-97~s3-3","Submucosal resection","Surgery of the Turbinate · The operation: inferior turbinate reduction","Insert a 3.5 mm (or turbinate-specific 2.9 mm) microdebrider blade into the tunnel and remove the excess lateral soft tissue and, if needed, bone, always seeing where the blade is beneath the mucosa. For a very large turbinate, take the lateral mucoperiosteum of the front of the turbinate with its bone (bone is quicker with through-cutting forceps), then drape the medial mucosa laterally over the raw area. The blade that goes through Angled upward, the blade can come out through the top of the inferior turbinate and catch the middle turbinate, or punch a hole that whistles and crusts. Keep the blade low in the pocket and pointed along the turbinate. Figure 3 Wrong and right: a microdebrider angled up out of the inferior turbinate toward the middle turbinate, and the same blade correctly placed within the turbinate pocket. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 97.4 Senior's pearl Preserve the mucosal surface, especially at the anterior head, and avoid cautery on the surface. The aim is a smaller turbinate that still works, not an empty nose. What you should see The turbinate visibly smaller with an unbroken medial mucosal surface, and the blade's movement seen as a ripple beneath the mucosa."],["osec","th:op-97~s3-4","Outfracture","Surgery of the Turbinate · The operation: inferior turbinate reduction","With a broad elevator or septal displacer, push the turbinate laterally under vision. It moves more easily if it was outfractured from inside the pocket during elevation. Splints can be placed to hold it and to reduce venous congestion and swelling. Figure 4 Outfracture of a prominent inferior turbinate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 97.3 What you should see A turbinate lying against the lateral wall with a clear channel along the floor and up to the middle meatus, and an intact medial mucosa."],["osec","th:op-97~s4-1","Inject","Surgery of the Turbinate · The operation: concha bullosa","Inject at the axilla, along the anterior face and posteriorly near the lower edge of the basal lamella. What you should see Blanching of the middle turbinate at the axilla and along its anterior face. The pneumatised turbinate Confirm the concha bullosa on the coronal CT before you cut. A sickle knife pushed into a turbinate that is solid, or into one whose cell extends to the skull base, is how the cribriform plate is injured."],["osec","th:op-97~s4-2","Open the air cell","Surgery of the Turbinate · The operation: concha bullosa","With a sickle knife, cut into the air cell through the front of the turbinate, lateral to its vertical attachment so that you do not twist the cribriform plate. If the air cell lies further back, enter laterally or medially where it is. Extend the cut up and down. The skull base Avulsing the middle turbinate or working too high on it can tear the cribriform plate and cause a CSF leak. Cut sharply, never pull, and keep the cribriform plate in view. What you should see The knife dropping into an air space lined with mucosa, sometimes with pus or polyp inside, and the cell opened from top to bottom. Senior's pearl Remove the lateral half and keep the medial. The medial lamella carries the olfactory epithelium and the attachment to the skull base."],["osec","th:op-97~s4-3","Remove the lamella","Surgery of the Turbinate · The operation: concha bullosa","Remove the lateral (or medial) lamella with through-cutting forceps or the microdebrider, piecemeal or in one piece by extending upper and lower cuts backward. Stop at about the basal lamella. When taking the lateral lamella, protect the uncinate and bulla : a blunt elevator can gently infracture the lateral lamella away from them as you go back. Trim free edges with the microdebrider. What you should see A slimmer middle turbinate with its medial lamella and attachment intact, and an untouched uncinate, bulla and hiatus beside it. Senior's pearl A medial laminectomy opens the concha as well as a lateral one, and a lateral laminectomy has to be done carefully to keep the infundibulum working. Whichever side you take, fix the middle turbinate to the septum (a medialisation suture or controlled scar) if it is unstable, so it does not fall laterally and block the middle meatus."],["osec","th:op-97~s5-1","Crisis drills","Surgery of the Turbinate · When it goes wrong","Bleeding during or after the operation 1 During pressure with decongestant pledgets selective suction diathermy, especially at the posterior tip 2 After targeted absorbable packing or cautery in clinic 3 Escalate return to theatre check clotting and blood pressure Watering eye after surgery 1 Suspect injury to the nasolacrimal duct in the inferior meatus 2 Act refer to ophthalmology dacryocystorhinostomy may be needed Clear fluid from the nose after middle turbinate surgery 1 Suspect CSF leak from a twisted or avulsed middle turbinate 2 Act repair at once endoscopically if seen during surgery test for beta-2 transferrin and image if it appears later A wide nose that feels blocked 1 Recognise empty nose loss of the feeling of airflow, crusting and dryness after over-resection 2 Act humidification, saline and emollients specialist management (treatment is controversial)"],["osec","th:op-97~s6-1","After the operation","Surgery of the Turbinate · Aftercare","Pain is usually controlled with paracetamol or a low-dose opioid; non-steroidal drugs from day 7. Saline irrigation clears mucus, clot and debris and keeps any splints clear. Splints out at 3 to 5 days if used. Adhesions are cut in clinic under topical anaesthesia; sinusitis is treated with antibiotics, guided by endoscopic culture."],["osec","th:op-97~s6-2","Complications","Surgery of the Turbinate · Aftercare","Complications of turbinate surgery Complication Frequency or cause Bleeding Commonest, around 10%; posterior tip and incision Adhesions About 3% to 9%; more with septoplasty, where raw surfaces face each other Acute sinusitis Breach of the upper surface into the middle meatus, adhesions Nasolacrimal duct injury Lateral wall of the inferior meatus, 30 to 40 mm back Through-and-through hole Blade angled out of the turbinate: whistling, crusting Empty nose syndrome Over-resection; rare with modern techniques CSF leak Avulsion or high dissection of the middle turbinate Atrophic rhinitis Total or aggressive partial turbinectomy"],["osec","th:op-97~s6-3","Choosing a technique","Surgery of the Turbinate · Aftercare","Inferior turbinate techniques compared Technique Long-term result Mucosa Submucosal resection, including powered Best reported long-term relief Preserved; mucociliary clearance returns to normal by 6 months Radiofrequency, coblation, laser, cryotherapy, cautery Relief fades more often Measurable damage to mucosal function Partial or total turbinectomy Widens the airway Lost; risk of bleeding, adhesions, atrophic rhinitis and empty nose; out of favour Outfracture Adds to other techniques Preserved"],["ev","th:op-97~s6-4","Surgery of the Turbinate: current evidence","Surgery of the Turbinate · Aftercare","Evidence. Turbinate reduction is common but poorly studied; a Cochrane review found no good randomised trials comparing it with no surgery. In a large randomised comparison of six techniques, submucosal resection with lateral outfracture gave the best long-term airway and preserved mucosal function (Passali et al., Ann Otol Rhinol Laryngol 2003). Mucosa-sparing. Submucosal diathermy, radiofrequency, coblation and microdebrider reduction preserve the mucosa; radical turbinectomy risks crusting, bleeding and empty nose syndrome. Combined surgery. In the NAIROS trial, septoplasty with or without turbinate reduction improved symptoms over medical treatment (Carrie et al., Health Technol Assess 2024)."],["osec","th:op-97~s6-4","Alternatives","Surgery of the Turbinate · Aftercare","Treating rhinitis medically is an excellent alternative if it works; immunotherapy may be enough for significant allergic rhinitis."],["osec","th:op-97~s7-1","Where the books differ","Surgery of the Turbinate · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra lists the methods of reducing hypertrophic turbinates (linear cauterisation, submucosal diathermy, cryosurgery, partial or total turbinectomy, submucous resection of turbinate bone, and lasers, coblation and radiofrequency), warns that excessive removal causes persistent crusting, and notes that compensatory turbinate hypertrophy on the roomy side of a deviated septum often does not reverse after septal correction, so the turbinate is reduced at the same operation. Lee separates atrophic rhinitis from empty nose syndrome , which it describes as obstruction attributed to excessive turbinate removal, and lists turbinate reduction among the nasal procedures for sleep-disordered breathing. Myers prefers powered submucosal resection with endoscopes, gives the reasons (best long-term relief, preserved mucociliary function), adds the outfracture, and sets out how to resect a concha bullosa safely around the ostiomeatal complex and skull base."],["osec","th:op-97~s7-2","Viva essentials","Surgery of the Turbinate · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Functions of the turbinates and why mucosa-sparing techniques are preferred. The techniques of inferior turbinate reduction and which has the best long-term evidence (submucosal resection). Empty nose syndrome versus atrophic rhinitis. The anterior head and the internal nasal valve. Concha bullosa: what it is, why it matters, medial versus lateral laminectomy, and the structures to protect (uncinate, bulla, cribriform plate). The nasolacrimal duct opening , 30 to 40 mm behind the sill. Compensatory hypertrophy on the concave side of a deviated septum."],["osec","th:op-97~s7-3","Common pitfalls","Surgery of the Turbinate · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating on a nose that is already objectively open. Missing valve collapse as the real cause. Not treating allergy and rhinitis first. Stripping the mucosa or cauterising the surface. Angling the microdebrider up out of the inferior turbinate. Twisting or pulling the middle turbinate. Injuring the uncinate or bulla during a lateral laminectomy."],["osec","th:op-98~s1-1","The case at a glance","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Operating note","Endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation Summary Indications Severe one-sided epistaxis that is posterior or has no obvious source; recurrent significant posterior bleeds controlled only by packing, with no treatable cause (elective ligation); devascularising a small vascular tumour such as an early angiofibroma before removal, or a bleeding malignancy; the first step of a transpterygoid skull base approach Contraindications A correctable coagulopathy (treat it first, non-operatively); too unfit for general anaesthesia (packing or embolisation instead); a large vascular tumour blocking access with several feeding vessels (embolisation preferred) Anaesthesia General, with airway protection; awake fibreoptic or rapid sequence intubation if bleeding is active; orogastric tube to empty swallowed blood; throat pack optional; controlled hypotension helps Position Supine, as for sinus surgery, head turned slightly toward the surgeon, tube to the opposite side, reverse Trendelenburg of at least 15 degrees Antibiotics A single dose of first-generation cephalosporin (clindamycin if allergic); continue only if packs have caused sinusitis, covering streptococci and S. aureus Monitoring No special monitoring Imaging Usually none; contrast CT if a tumour is suspected; CT angiography after recent sinus surgery to exclude a carotid pseudoaneurysm; formal angiography only for embolisation, failed surgery, or suspected carotid injury Tray Endoscopic sinus set; monopolar suction diathermy for surface bleeding; pistol-grip endoscopic bipolar; adjustable pistol-grip clip applier with a flat sh"],["osec","th:op-98~s1-2","Consent","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: packing has not stopped the bleeding, and closing the main artery at the back of the nose controls it in about nine out of ten patients or more. Bleeding may come back , needing more packing, a second operation, an operation on the ethmoid arteries, or embolisation. Numbness of the palate or upper teeth, usually partial and recovering over weeks to months. Dryness of the nose and a less watery eye if the nerves behind the artery are injured; uncommon. Crusting, scar bands and sinus infection. A blood transfusion may be needed. Rare: damage to the septum, especially after bilateral ligation. The ethmoid arteries may be cauterised at the same time if the source is unclear."],["osec","th:op-98~s1-3","Before you start: finding the source","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Operating note","1.3.1 The history How did it start? After a blow to the nose, think of the anterior ethmoid artery ; spontaneous heavy bleeding more often comes from the sphenopalatine artery . How much and for how long? Count tissues, cups or bowls. Heavy bleeding comes from a larger artery. Which side, front or back? Bleeding often crosses through the nasopharynx to the other side, but one side usually leads. Mostly forward suggests the anterior ethmoid; mostly into the throat suggests the sphenopalatine; heavy bleeds often do both. Does pressing on the front of the nose stop it? If so, it comes from the small vessels of the anterior septum; if blood still runs down the throat, think of the anterior ethmoid or sphenopalatine artery. Associated symptoms: blockage, visual change, facial numbness, pain or trismus suggest a tumour. Age: severe bleeding under 20 suggests a tumour (angiofibroma in boys) or an inherited bleeding disorder; in the elderly, think of medications. Previous treatment: failure of a previous ligation means a missed branch or a dislodged clip; failure of embolisation points to the anterior ethmoid artery. Medical history: liver or kidney failure, haematological cancer and hypothyroidism all impair clotting. The link with hypertension is disputed: population studies have not shown that it causes epistaxis. Recent sinus surgery: consider an injury to the internal carotid artery with a pseudoaneurysm, especially if heavy bleeding was packed in theatre; and a sphenoidotomy can injure the posterior septal branch running over the face of the sphenoid. Family history: recurren"],["osec","th:op-98~s2-1","The artery and its foramen","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Anatomy you must see","The sphenopalatine foramen lies at the posterosuperior corner of the maxillary sinus , formed where the palatine and sphenoid bones meet, at about the level of the posterior attachment of the middle turbinate . The crista ethmoidalis is a triangular ridge of bone just in front of the foramen, the landmark you feel for. The artery often divides before the foramen and its branches can leave through separate openings (about 40% in cadaver studies); you must find them all. Its two main terminal branches are the posterior septal artery , crossing the face of the sphenoid to the septum, and the posterior lateral nasal artery to the turbinates."],["osec","th:op-98~s2-2","What lies behind it","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Anatomy you must see","In the pterygopalatine fossa, the vessels lie in front of (superficial to) the nerves . Dissecting or cauterising deep to the artery can injure the pterygopalatine ganglion and the vidian nerve : a dry nose and reduced tearing. The greater (descending) palatine nerve runs vertically down the lateral nasal wall below the foramen; drilling or biting bone there numbs the palate. Figure 1 The sphenopalatine artery lies at the level of the posterior attachment of the middle turbinate. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 98.1"],["osec","th:op-98~s3-1","Prepare the nose","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · The operation, stage by stage","Remove the packs and replace them with 0.05% oxymetazoline pledgets. With controlled hypotension, head-up tilt and vasoconstriction, active bleeding usually stops. Examine with a 0-degree endoscope to confirm the landmarks and look for a bleeding point; control surface bleeding temporarily with suction monopolar diathermy. Medialise the middle turbinate to open the middle meatus. Trim a concha bullosa, or do a septoplasty, if either blocks the way. What you should see A clear, decongested middle meatus with the uncinate process, middle turbinate and any active bleeding identified."],["osec","th:op-98~s3-2","Open the maxillary sinus","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · The operation, stage by stage","Do a middle meatal antrostomy: remove the uncinate with backbiting forceps, find the natural ostium, and widen it backward and downward . This shows you the posterior wall of the antrum, which leads you to the foramen, and gives you room to work. Figure 2 After a left middle meatal antrostomy, the mucoperiosteum behind it is raised off the lateral nasal wall (MT, middle turbinate; PWA, posterior wall of the antrum). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 98.2 What you should see The posterior wall of the maxillary sinus through a wide antrostomy."],["osec","th:op-98~s3-3","Raise the flap from below","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · The operation, stage by stage","With a Cottle elevator, raise the mucoperiosteum off the lateral nasal wall below the antrostomy and work backward, staying below the level of the foramen . Feel for the crista ethmoidalis. As the periosteum lifts, the branches of the artery are stretched and become visible. Swap the elevator for a 7 French Frazier sucker, which keeps the vessels on tension, clears blood, and can be left in place so you have a free hand. Figure 3 The sphenopalatine artery exposed at the posterosuperior corner of the maxillary sinus. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 98.3 Figure 4 Endoscopic view of the right sphenopalatine artery (asterisk) as it leaves the foramen. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 98.4 What you should see The crista ethmoidalis, and behind it the artery and its branches emerging from the foramen into the raised flap. Coming in from above Going straight at the foramen from above makes the vessels hard to see and easy to tear. Approach from below, in the subperiosteal plane, where you have room for the sucker and the vessels are stretched toward you. An aberrant branch can come out of the bone here too."],["osec","th:op-98~s3-4","Open the foramen","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · The operation, stage by stage","Place a 1 mm angled Kerrison rongeur into the foramen, superficial to the artery, and remove the bone over it. Stay outside the periosteum that envelops the fossa contents. Continue into the pterygopalatine fossa, taking part of the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus with a 2 mm Kerrison, until you see the main trunk. Figure 5 The foramen enlarged with a rongeur to expose the main trunk of the artery. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 98.5 The wrong rongeur A 2 mm rongeur pushed into the foramen first is likely to cut the artery. Start with the 1 mm. Senior's pearl Find the main trunk before it divides. Wide exposure through an antrostomy is what lets you see the trunk and every branch; limited exposure is why branches are missed and bleeding returns. What you should see The crista ethmoidalis, a small bony spur just in front of the foramen, then the artery as a pulsating cord leaving the foramen with its periosteal sleeve, and its branches fanning out."],["osec","th:op-98~s3-5","Clip or cauterise","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · The operation, stage by stage","Separate the fat around the vessels with a Cottle or nerve hook, shrinking it with bipolar if needed; you do not need to dissect every branch of the maxillary artery. Place a medium clip across the main trunk lateral to the foramen, and a second if possible, then clip or cauterise the two terminal branches. Palm the clip applier as you pass it into the nose so the clip does not bend and drop. Use the tip end of the clip to close the vessel, because clips do not close at their apex. If the vessel tears, or clips will not sit securely, cauterise it thoroughly with bipolar diathermy. Figure 6 Clips on the main trunk of the sphenopalatine artery and on its terminal branches. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 98.6 What you should see A clipped or cauterised trunk and both terminal branches, with no pulsatile bleeding when you remove the sucker. Clips that fall off A clip too small for the vessel, one placed with its apex on the vessel, or a pack pushed in blindly at the end can dislodge clips. Choose the right size and place any pack under vision."],["osec","th:op-98~s3-6","If the source is uncertain, add the anterior ethmoid","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · The operation, stage by stage","When you are not sure the bleeding came from the sphenopalatine artery, cauterise the anterior ethmoid artery endoscopically at the same operation (see the ethmoid artery chapter). What you should see The anterior ethmoid artery on the roof behind the frontal recess, sealed with bipolar. Monopolar and the orbit Use bipolar only on the ethmoid roof, and never cut the anterior ethmoid artery before it is sealed: it retracts into the orbit."],["osec","th:op-98~s3-7","Finish","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · The operation, stage by stage","Irrigate with warm saline, lay a small piece of Gelfoam over the clips, and stitch Silastic splints to the septum to prevent adhesions. Packing is usually unnecessary; if you need it for oozing, place it under vision. What you should see A dry field after the blood pressure has been brought back to normal, clips on the trunk and every branch, and the flap laid back over the foramen. The branch you did not see The artery has two to ten branches and the posterior septal branch often leaves through its own opening. Rebleeding after ligation is usually a missed branch. Senior's pearl Ask the anaesthetist to restore normal pressure before you leave. A field that is dry only while the blood pressure is held low tells you nothing."],["osec","th:op-98~s4-1","Crisis drills","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · When it goes wrong","Bleeding returns after ligation 1 Minor decongestant spray, anterior packing 2 Severe endoscopy to find the side and site 3 Same-side sphenopalatine source return to theatre (allows the anterior ethmoid to be treated too) or embolisation 4 Think a dislodged clip, bleeding proximal to the clip, a missed branch, the anterior ethmoid, or rarely the other side Heavy bleeding after recent sinus surgery 1 Suspect internal carotid injury or pseudoaneurysm 2 Act pack firmly, resuscitate, CT angiography or formal angiography 3 Escalate interventional radiology for the carotid do not explore blindly Torrential bleeding during the dissection 1 Act press with a pledget, raise the head, ask anaesthetics for lower pressure 2 Then find the vessel with suction and coagulate with bipolar or clip"],["osec","th:op-98~s5-1","After the operation","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Aftercare","Check haemoglobin and replace blood as needed; iron for a few weeks if anaemic. Pain is mild: paracetamol; anti-inflammatories a few days later once bleeding is controlled. Antibiotics only if packs caused sinusitis. Saline sprays and irrigations to clear clot. Restart essential anticoagulants or antiplatelets from the first day if there is no bleeding. Splints stay 1 to 2 weeks."],["osec","th:op-98~s5-2","Complications","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Aftercare","Complications of sphenopalatine artery ligation Complication Cause or management Rebleeding Mucosal ooze, missed branch, displaced clip, anterior ethmoid or other side Adhesions Prevented by splints; divide only if symptomatic Sinusitis Packing and instrumentation; scarring of the ostiomeatal complex Palatal numbness Greater palatine nerve injury below the foramen; usually partial and temporary Dry nose, reduced tearing Pterygopalatine ganglion or vidian nerve injury deep to the artery Septal necrosis Rare; bilateral ligation, heavy septal cautery and packing"],["ev","th:op-98~s5-3","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis: current evidence","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Aftercare","First aid and cautery. Most bleeds stop with 10 to 15 minutes of firm pressure on the soft nose, leaning forward; then look for the bleeding point and cauterise one side at a time. Packs. In the NoPAC trial, topical tranexamic acid did not reduce the need for packing (Reuben et al., Ann Emerg Med 2021). The UK national epistaxis audit (INTEGRATE, 2016) found wide variation in practice and supported early endoscopic assessment and targeted cautery or surgery rather than prolonged packing. Anticoagulants. Most patients on anticoagulants can be managed with local measures without reversal; discuss stopping with the prescribing team only when bleeding persists."],["osec","th:op-98~s5-3","Alternatives and evidence","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · Aftercare","Ligation versus embolisation Endoscopic ligation Embolisation Success About 87% to 98%; failures are often anterior ethmoid bleeds treated by adding ethmoid ligation 93% to 100% immediately, falling to about 71% to 89% with later rebleeds Ethmoid arteries Can be treated at the same time Cannot be embolised without risking blindness Main risks Minor: crusting, palatal numbness Stroke, blindness, soft tissue necrosis (under 2%) Best for Most patients fit for anaesthesia; failed embolisation Patients unfit for anaesthesia; failed ligation (though repeat surgery is usually preferred); possibly anticoagulated patients Cost Lower; first-line ligation is the most cost-effective strategy Higher Transmaxillary ligation of the maxillary artery through a sublabial antrostomy is an older alternative, with more morbidity and missed collaterals; it is still useful to devascularise an angiofibroma or a malignancy."],["osec","th:op-98~s6-1","Where the books differ","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra lists ligation of the external carotid, the maxillary artery through a Caldwell-Luc approach, and the ethmoid arteries through a Lynch incision, noting that external carotid ligation has given way to embolisation and more peripheral ligation, and that transantral maxillary artery ligation has been superseded by transnasal endoscopic sphenopalatine ligation (TESPAL). Lee calls TESPAL the preferred method for intractable posterior epistaxis, with a 95% to 98% success rate and a short stay; raises a medially based flap in front of the crista ethmoidalis, cauterises the artery and its branches with bipolar, and considers clips generally unnecessary. Myers adds a maxillary antrostomy for wide exposure, approaches the foramen from below, opens it with a 1 mm Kerrison, and clips the trunk and both terminal branches, with bipolar as the fallback; it also cauterises the anterior ethmoid at the same time when the source is uncertain."],["osec","th:op-98~s6-2","Viva essentials","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for How to tell an anterior ethmoid bleed from a sphenopalatine bleed from the history. The site of the foramen (posterosuperior corner of the antrum, level of the middle turbinate's posterior attachment) and the crista ethmoidalis . That the artery branches before the foramen in about 40%. Why approach from below and why a 1 mm Kerrison. Nerves lie deep to the vessels : what is injured if you go too deep, and the greater palatine nerve below. Success rates and cost compared with embolisation, and why the ethmoid arteries are never embolised . Causes of failure."],["osec","th:op-98~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Sphenopalatine Artery Ligation for Epistaxis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Too little exposure (no antrostomy), so a branch is missed. Approaching the foramen from above. Opening the foramen with a 2 mm rongeur. Dissecting or cauterising deep to the artery. Using a general surgical clip applier, or clips that are too small. Pushing a pack in blindly at the end. Forgetting the anterior ethmoid when the source is unclear."],["osec","th:op-99~s1-1","The case at a glance","Anterior and Posterior · Operating note","Anterior and posterior ethmoid artery ligation Summary Indications Epistaxis not controlled at the bedside, especially bleeding from high in the nose above the middle turbinate; bleeding that continues after a properly performed sphenopalatine ligation with clear unilateral bleeding; facial trauma with suspected anterior ethmoid injury; vascular control before resecting anterior skull base tumours Contraindications Unfit for anaesthesia, a consideration often overridden by life-threatening bleeding Anaesthesia General, with the airway plan agreed beforehand: active bleeding can make intubation difficult Position Supine, head turned slightly toward the surgeon, reverse Trendelenburg, as for endoscopic sinus surgery; for the external approach, the cornea protected by a shield or temporary tarsorrhaphy Antibiotics First- or second-generation cephalosporin; clindamycin if allergic to penicillin Imaging Thin-cut CT (1.25 mm or less) with navigation for the endoscopic approach; none needed for the external approach Tray Endoscopic sinus set with 30- and 70-degree telescopes and angled instruments, angled pistol-grip bipolar forceps, endoscopic clip applier with medium clips, Cottle elevator, Lusk probe or sinus seeker, angled Kerrison rongeurs, J-curette, microdebrider; for the external approach a size 15 blade, skin hooks, a malleable orbital retractor and a 3 mm endoscope Before theatre Group and save or crossmatch, full blood count, clotting (INR, APTT); reverse anticoagulants; control blood pressure; nasal and if needed postnasal packing"],["osec","th:op-99~s1-2","Consent","Anterior and Posterior · Operating note","What to tell the patient Why: packing and cautery have not stopped the bleeding, and these arteries cannot safely be blocked by a radiologist because they share the eye's blood supply. Bleeding may continue or recur , needing packing, a second procedure or other measures. Bruising or bleeding around the eye ; very rarely a collection behind the eye that threatens sight and needs an immediate procedure at the corner of the eye. Injury to the eye socket or its muscles : double vision, watering; optic nerve injury and loss of vision are rare but serious, especially with the posterior artery. CSF leak through the roof of the nose, needing repair. Crusting, adhesions and sinus infection after the endoscopic approach; a scar beside the nose, numbness of the skin and occasionally a web needing a small revision after the external approach. Blood transfusion may be needed."],["osec","th:op-99~s1-3","Before you start","Anterior and Posterior · Operating note","Resuscitate first: in a shocked patient follow the trauma protocol and take the history once they are stable. Then ask where the bleeding started: front (anterior septum, Little's area, then the anterior ethmoid artery) or back into the throat (the sphenopalatine artery and its branches, rarely the posterior ethmoid). Which side? Torrential, sudden bleeding suggests an artery; a steady trickle suggests veins or mucosa. Recurrent winter or seasonal bleeds that stop easily point to Little's area. Estimate the loss in tissues, cups or bowls, and look for light-headedness, palpitations or clamminess. Ask about facial numbness, unilateral obstruction, double vision, fever or weight loss, which suggest a tumour: nasopharyngeal carcinoma in patients of Southeast Asian origin, juvenile angiofibroma in an adolescent boy. Ask about bleeding disorders and bruising, liver disease, previous sinus surgery or septorhinoplasty (cautery after septoplasty risks perforation), a family history of bleeding or telangiectasia, and the drugs that matter: anticoagulants, antiplatelets, alcohol, and supplements such as omega-3, garlic, ginseng and ginkgo. Involve a haematologist for a medical cause or recurrent bleeding despite treatment. Common causes of epistaxis Local Systemic Digital trauma, facial trauma Hypertension Septal deviation and perforation Anticoagulants and antiplatelets Chronic rhinosinusitis, irritants Congenital coagulopathies, platelet dysfunction Foreign bodies Liver dysfunction Neoplasm Leukaemia Vascular malformations (hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia) Medications and su"],["osec","th:op-99~s2-1","Examine properly","Anterior and Posterior · First-line control","Wear a face shield, eye protection, gown and gloves. Sit the patient up, leaning forward over a basin. Irrigate the whole nasal cavity with saline from a 60 ml syringe or squeeze bottle to wash out clots. Examine with anterior rhinoscopy and then a rigid 30-degree endoscope , front to back, including the nasopharynx; a rigid scope lets you suction and work with an instrument in the other hand. Lift off crusts on the anterior septum: the bleeding point is often beneath these biological dressings. Look in the mouth for blood running down the pharynx and for telangiectasia or tumours of the palate, maxilla or tonsil, and examine the cranial nerves. Look before you decongest Examine once before applying a vasoconstrictor. Topical decongestant can put an arterial bleeding point into spasm, so that the examination is falsely negative. Then apply decongestant mixed with lidocaine and look again."],["osec","th:op-99~s2-2","Cautery","Anterior and Posterior · First-line control","When you see the bleeding point, cauterise it: silver nitrate rolled onto the vessel and then around it, starting at the periphery, or bipolar diathermy under the endoscope. Do not cauterise both sides of the septum at the same point, which risks a perforation. In a coagulopathic patient with diffuse oozing, minimise mucosal trauma and prefer absorbable packing; cautery can make diffuse bleeding worse."],["osec","th:op-99~s2-3","Anterior packing","Anterior and Posterior · First-line control","If the bleeding is profuse or the site cannot be found, pack. Modern options are an expanding sponge (Merocel), an inflatable balloon (Rapid Rhino) or an absorbable pack in the coagulopathic patient. The classic pack is about a metre of ribbon gauze (2.5 cm wide in adults, 1.2 cm in children) impregnated with paraffin or bismuth-iodoform paste: fold the first few centimetres on themselves, lay them along the floor, and layer the gauze from floor to roof and from front to back, or in vertical layers from back to front. Place packs under endoscopic view when the source is high, so that they press on the bleeding point. Remove after 24 hours if bleeding has stopped; packs left two to three days need antibiotic cover against sinusitis and toxic shock syndrome."],["osec","th:op-99~s2-4","Posterior packing","Anterior and Posterior · First-line control","For bleeding posteriorly into the throat, use a balloon: a Foley catheter (12 to 14 French) passed along the floor into the nasopharynx, its balloon inflated with 5 to 10 ml of saline (never air) and pulled forward to block the choana, then an anterior pack in front, with the catheter padded at the nostril to prevent columellar and alar necrosis; or a double-balloon device inflated posteriorly then anteriorly. The traditional postnasal pack is a rolled gauze cone with three silk ties: two are pulled through the nose on catheters passed through the nostrils and out of the mouth, the pack is guided into the nasopharynx with a finger, an anterior pack is placed, and the two ties are tied over a dental roll at the columella; the third is left in the mouth to remove it. Every patient with a posterior pack is admitted, monitored with oxygen saturation (posterior packs can depress breathing, especially in the elderly) and given analgesia and antibiotics. Senior's pearl A patient who needs a posterior balloon to stop a bleed is a patient who needs a definitive procedure. Treat the balloon as a bridge to the operating theatre, not as the treatment."],["osec","th:op-99~s2-5","Warm water irrigation","Anterior and Posterior · First-line control","Irrigation with water at 46 to 52 degrees Celsius is a well-documented way of controlling posterior epistaxis without packing; it works through mucosal oedema, reduced intravascular pressure and a faster clotting cascade."],["osec","th:op-99~s3-1","Where the arteries come from","Anterior and Posterior · Anatomy you must see","The anterior and posterior ethmoid arteries are branches of the ophthalmic artery , itself the first branch of the internal carotid. They leave the orbit through foramina in the frontoethmoid suture on the medial orbital wall, cross the roof of the ethmoid sinuses from lateral (orbit) to medial (skull base) , and enter the anterior cranial fossa beside the cribriform plate before descending to supply the upper septum and lateral wall. This direction of flow is why the lateral, orbital end must be controlled: an artery cut medially retracts into the orbit still bleeding. The anterior septum's Kiesselbach plexus (Little's area) is formed by the anterior ethmoid, sphenopalatine, greater palatine and superior labial (facial) arteries; posterior bleeds come mostly from the sphenopalatine artery."],["osec","th:op-99~s3-2","The external measurements (the 24-12-6 rule)","Anterior and Posterior · Anatomy you must see","Along the frontoethmoid suture from the anterior lacrimal crest : the anterior ethmoid foramen is about 20 to 24 mm behind the crest; the posterior ethmoid foramen about 9 to 16 mm (roughly 12 mm) behind that; the optic canal only 4 to 7 mm (roughly 6 mm) behind the posterior foramen. Figure 1 Axial view of the anterior and posterior ethmoid arteries and the optic nerve, with their distances from the anterior lacrimal crest (about 24, 12 and 6 mm). Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 99.3 Figure 2 Parasagittal view: the foramina lie in the frontoethmoid suture line; the modified Lynch incision runs from the medial brow to the level of the medial canthus in the shadow of the orbit. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 99.4"],["osec","th:op-99~s3-3","The endoscopic landmarks","Anterior and Posterior · Anatomy you must see","The anterior ethmoid artery lies just behind the supraorbital ethmoid cell ; the frontal sinus is always anterior and medial to that cell. It lies about 17 to 25 mm behind the axilla of the middle turbinate , and was classically related to the skull base attachment of the basal lamella of the bulla. About one third of anterior ethmoid arteries hang below the skull base in a bony mesentery ; the rest are embedded in the skull base. The posterior ethmoid artery is smaller, usually embedded in bone and often not seen; when visible it lies about 8 mm in front of the face of the sphenoid . A middle ethmoid artery is present in some. On coronal CT, the nipple sign , a small triangular notch in the superomedial orbital wall between the medial rectus and superior oblique , marks the anterior ethmoid canal; the posterior artery's notch lies in the skull base without a muscle relationship. Figure 3 Coronal CT showing the bilateral nipple signs (asterisks) of the anterior ethmoid canals between the superior oblique and medial rectus muscles. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 99.1 Figure 4 Endoscopic view of the anterior, middle and posterior ethmoid arteries crossing the ethmoid roof. Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 99.2"],["osec","th:op-99~s4-1","Prepare the nose","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: endoscopic ligation","After intubation, register the navigation system to the CT. Remove the packs, irrigate the nose copiously with warm water to clear clot, and decongest with pledgets of oxymetazoline or 1:1000 adrenaline. What you should see A clean, decongested nasal cavity with the middle turbinate, uncinate and septum identified, and any bleeding point you can now see. Senior's pearl Look again for a bleeding point before you dissect: a sphenopalatine or septal source seen now saves an ethmoidectomy."],["osec","th:op-99~s4-2","Uncinectomy and anterior ethmoidectomy","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: endoscopic ligation","Remove the uncinate process; a posterior-to-anterior technique limits the risk to the orbit: open with a backbiter to expose the natural maxillary ostium, then take the remaining uncinate off the maxillary line with through-cutting instruments. Place a J-curette into the retrobullar space and remove the ethmoid bulla with gentle push-pull movements, then tidy the edges with the microdebrider. What you should see The lamina papyracea laterally, the basal lamella behind, and the maxillary ostium below. The orbit The lamina papyracea is paper-thin. Keep the instruments' cutting surfaces facing medially, and check with an eye press (pressing on the closed eye while watching the lamina) if in doubt."],["osec","th:op-99~s4-3","Open the frontal recess and find the supraorbital cell","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: endoscopic ligation","Dissect the frontal recess completely with angled telescopes and instruments, identifying the frontal sinus drainage pathway and the supraorbital ethmoid cell . The anterior ethmoid artery, or its bony canal, crosses the roof just behind the supraorbital cell ; confirm with navigation. What you should see The frontal ostium anterior and medial, the supraorbital cell lateral, and behind it the artery in its mesentery or the ridge of its canal in the roof. The skull base The artery enters the cribriform region at the thinnest part of the skull base, the lateral lamella. Rough work here causes a CSF leak."],["osec","th:op-99~s4-4","Expose the artery circumferentially","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: endoscopic ligation","If the artery hangs in a mesentery, it can be controlled there. If it is encased in bone, control it at its exit from the orbit : fracture the lamina papyracea gently with a Cottle elevator, tease the bone flakes off the periorbita with a Lusk probe or seeker, and see the pyramid-shaped neurovascular bundle leaving the orbit. Remove more bone around it with angled Kerrisons, including a little of the skull base cortex, until the artery is free all round. What you should see The whole calibre of the artery, free for 360 degrees, with the periorbita intact laterally. Avulsion and retraction An artery torn or cut before it is controlled retracts into the orbit and bleeds there: a retro-orbital haematoma. Do not grasp or tear it; expose it fully before you seal it."],["osec","th:op-99~s4-5","Seal the artery and finish","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: endoscopic ligation","Seal it with bipolar diathermy across the full calibre (never monopolar this close to the dura) or with two medium clips . For the posterior artery, first complete a posterior ethmoidectomy and expose it the same way; prefer clips, because heat can reach the optic nerve a few millimetres away. Check for bleeding, medialise the middle turbinate to prevent it lateralising and causing sinusitis, and place a middle meatal spacer if wished; formal packing is usually unnecessary. What you should see A sealed artery with no flow beyond the seal, and a dry ethmoid cavity. Senior's pearl Seal the lateral end first. Flow runs from the orbit to the skull base, so a medial seal leaves the orbital end free to bleed and retract."],["osec","th:op-99~s5-1","Protect the eye and mark the incision","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: external (Lynch) ligation","Leave the nasal packs in after intubation and protect the cornea with a shield or temporary tarsorrhaphy. Infiltrate 1% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline along a curvilinear modified Lynch incision in the upper shadow line of the orbit, halfway between the nasal dorsum and the medial canthus, from the medial end of the brow to the level of the canthus. What you should see The incision line in the shadow of the orbit, clear of the medial canthal tendon below."],["osec","th:op-99~s5-2","Incise and elevate the periosteum","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: external (Lynch) ligation","Cut through skin and soft tissue to bone with a size 15 blade, with bipolar ready for the angular vessels , which are often divided. With a Cottle elevator, raise the periosteum backwards off the nasal bone, the frontal process of the maxilla and the lamina papyracea. Keep within three limits: superiorly just above the frontoethmoid suture, inferiorly the lacrimal sac, and laterally the junction of the anterior lacrimal crest and the suture line, following the contour of the medial orbital wall along the line of the suture. What you should see The lacrimal sac in its fossa below, the medial orbital wall, and fine communicating venules between periorbita and bone, which are not the artery. Too high or too low The suture is never actually seen. Elevation too high enters the orbital roof and frontal region; too low misses the foramina and risks the lacrimal sac."],["osec","th:op-99~s5-3","Find the anterior ethmoid artery","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: external (Lynch) ligation","At about 24 mm behind the anterior lacrimal crest, the artery appears as a thick pyramid of soft tissue wrapped in fascia passing from lateral to medial into its foramen. Retract the skin with hooks and the orbital contents gently laterally with a malleable retractor, and develop the tissue planes around and beyond the vessel. A 3 mm endoscope improves the view. What you should see A distinct fascial cone running into a foramen, not a fleeting thin venule."],["osec","th:op-99~s5-4","Ligate, and the posterior artery if needed","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: external (Lynch) ligation","Control the whole calibre in place with two medium clips or bipolar, at the most lateral point. To reach the posterior artery, continue elevation along the suture about 12 mm further back with more orbital retraction; it lies only about 6 mm from the optic nerve, so use clips, not heat , and do not let a clip touch the nerve. What you should see Both arteries clipped at their orbital ends, and no traction on the globe for longer than needed. The optic nerve It lies about 6 mm behind the posterior ethmoid foramen. Stop at the posterior artery; never dissect blindly behind it."],["osec","th:op-99~s5-5","Check and close","Anterior and Posterior · The operation: external (Lynch) ligation","Irrigate the wound and remove the nasal packs; decongest the engorged mucosa with oxymetazoline pledgets and examine the whole nasal cavity endoscopically for further bleeding. Close in two layers and apply adhesive strips or tissue glue. What you should see A dry nasal cavity on endoscopy after the packs are out, and a soft eye with a reactive pupil."],["osec","th:op-99~s6-1","Crisis drills","Anterior and Posterior · When it goes wrong","Retro-orbital haematoma 1 Recognise proptosis, a tense eyelid, pain, reduced vision, a dilated pupil or loss of colour vision 2 Decompress remove all nasal packs lateral canthotomy and inferior cantholysis at the bedside without delay 3 Support measure intraocular pressure mannitol, acetazolamide and dexamethasone urgent ophthalmology but do not wait for them 4 Prevent seal arteries in full before cutting control the lateral end Orbital fat in the field 1 Stop protect the breach, do not suction or debride fat 2 Work around carry on above and below the breach some shrink exposed fat gently with bipolar 3 Afterwards no nose blowing, no forceful saline irrigation, sneeze with the mouth open Clear fluid from the roof 1 Recognise CSF leak from the lateral lamella or skull base 2 Act identify the defect repair at the time with a graft 3 Afterwards bed rest, head up, no straining Bleeding that will not stop 1 Exhaust options bipolar, clips, sphenopalatine ligation if not done 2 Pack formal packing, keep intubated, high-dependency care 3 Next second surgeon embolisation of external carotid branches only, never the ethmoid arteries"],["osec","th:op-99~s7-1","On the ward and at home","Anterior and Posterior · Aftercare","No nose blowing or strenuous activity; start saline irrigation on the first day; review at 7 to 14 days for debridement and removal of any spacer. Simple analgesia (paracetamol with an anti-inflammatory if bleeding allows), with a short course of an opioid if needed. Anticoagulants and antiplatelets are usually restarted on the second day, agreed with the team who prescribe them."],["osec","th:op-99~s7-2","Complications","Anterior and Posterior · Aftercare","Complications Complication Management Retro-orbital haematoma Remove packs, canthotomy and cantholysis, medical decompression, ophthalmology CSF leak Repair (see the CSF leak chapter) Orbital injury, diplopia Protect breach; ophthalmology review Optic nerve injury Prevention: clips not cautery on the posterior artery Synechiae Prevent with debridement; divide in clinic with through-cutting instruments Iatrogenic sinusitis Saline, antibiotics, steroids; revise a stenosed antrostomy if needed Scar web at the Lynch incision Z-plasty in clinic Numbness at the incision Usually recovers by 6 to 12 months; permanent if not by a year"],["ev","th:op-99~s7-3","Anterior and Posterior: current evidence","Anterior and Posterior · Aftercare","Sphenopalatine artery ligation controls posterior epistaxis in about 90 to 98% of patients in published series, and early surgery shortens hospital stay compared with prolonged posterior packing. Embolisation has similar success but carries a small risk of stroke, facial skin or palatal necrosis and blindness, and is usually reserved for patients unfit for anaesthesia or after failed surgery; cost analyses favour endoscopic ligation (Costa et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2019). Anterior ethmoidal artery ligation is added when bleeding comes from high in the nose or after trauma."],["osec","th:op-99~s7-3","Alternatives","Anterior and Posterior · Aftercare","The transcaruncular approach , usually by oculoplastic surgeons, reaches the medial orbital wall without a skin incision. Embolisation of the ethmoid arteries risks blindness and is a last resort when everything else has failed."],["osec","th:op-99~s8-1","Where the books differ","Anterior and Posterior · For the examination","Where the books differ Dhingra manages epistaxis stepwise from pinching and Trotter's position, cautery, anterior ribbon-gauze packing and posterior packing (gauze cone, Foley or double balloon) to endoscopic cautery, septal flap elevation or SMR, and ligation: external carotid (now avoided), maxillary artery via Caldwell-Luc (superseded by endoscopic sphenopalatine ligation) and the ethmoid arteries through a Lynch incision for bleeding above the middle turbinate. Lee emphasises the sphenopalatine artery as the target for posterior bleeding, the anatomy of Little's area, and embolisation for the external carotid system. Myers details the endoscopic approach to the ethmoid arteries behind the supraorbital cell, the 24-12-6 distances for the external approach, the lateral-to-medial flow, and clips rather than heat near the optic nerve."],["osec","th:op-99~s8-2","Viva essentials","Anterior and Posterior · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Why the ethmoid arteries cannot be embolised (ophthalmic artery, blindness). The 24-12-6 mm rule from the anterior lacrimal crest. Endoscopic landmarks: behind the supraorbital ethmoid cell; the nipple sign on CT. Why the lateral end must be controlled. Retro-orbital haematoma : signs and the canthotomy-cantholysis drill. The stepwise management of epistaxis, from first aid to ligation."],["osec","th:op-99~s8-3","Common pitfalls","Anterior and Posterior · For the examination","Common pitfalls Decongesting before the first look and missing the bleeding point. Treating a posterior balloon as the definitive treatment. Using monopolar diathermy on the ethmoid roof. Cutting the artery before sealing its whole circumference. Heat near the optic nerve when sealing the posterior artery. Elevating too high or too low along the medial orbital wall."],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s1-1","Before induction","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · The shared airway","Talk to the anaesthetist about: the lesion and where it is, the CT and the endoscopy findings, the best tube (type, size, route), whether the larynx can be seen, the plan if intubation fails (including who does a tracheostomy and whether the neck should be prepared and marked first), the use of lasers or nerve monitoring, blood loss, throat pack, and extubation. Stay in the anaesthetic room for induction in any airway case."],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s1-2","Airway devices","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · The shared airway","Tubes and airways in ENT Device Use Oral RAE (south-facing) tube Tonsils, mouth and oropharyngeal surgery; sits in the gag's tongue-blade groove Nasal RAE (north-facing) tube Oral cavity, mandible and dental surgery Reinforced (armoured) tube Head turned or flexed; resists kinking Microlaryngeal tube (5.0 to 6.0 mm) Laryngeal surgery: small diameter, adult length Laser-safe tube Laser surgery of the larynx and airway Nerve monitoring (EMG) tube Thyroid and parathyroid surgery Flexible laryngeal mask Tonsils and adenoids in selected children, nasal and ear surgery Tubeless: jet ventilation, high-flow nasal oxygen, intermittent apnoea Laryngeal and tracheal surgery when a tube obstructs the view"],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s1-3","Tubeless techniques","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · The shared airway","Jet ventilation: high-pressure oxygen pulses through a catheter above, through or below the vocal cords (supraglottic, subglottic, transtracheal). Gas must be able to escape: an obstructed outflow causes barotrauma and pneumothorax. High-flow nasal oxygen (for example THRIVE, up to 70 litres per minute) extends safe apnoea while the anaesthetist steps away from the airway; carbon dioxide still rises. Intermittent apnoea: the tube is removed for short periods of surgery and replaced."],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s2-1","The obstructed airway","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · The difficult airway","Tumours of the larynx, pharynx and tongue base, supraglottitis, and deep neck abscesses can make intubation impossible after induction. Options include awake fibreoptic or videolaryngoscopic intubation, inhalational induction keeping spontaneous breathing, and awake tracheostomy (or front-of-neck access) under local anaesthetic. Decide before induction, with the surgeon scrubbed and the neck marked and prepared if failure is possible. \"Can't intubate, can't oxygenate\" Know your hospital's Difficult Airway Society algorithm. If oxygenation fails, the surgeon may be asked to do front-of-neck access immediately (scalpel cricothyroidotomy). Be ready, and keep the kit in the room for every airway case."],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s2-2","Anaesthesia for the surgical field","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · The difficult airway","Total intravenous anaesthesia, controlled low-normal blood pressure, head-up tilt and a smooth heart rate give a better field in sinus and ear surgery. Avoid long-acting muscle relaxants when nerves are monitored. Ask for the blood pressure to be normalised and a Valsalva before closure."],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s3-1","Extubation","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Waking up","Extubation is as risky as intubation in ENT: blood, swelling, packs and airway surgery all threaten the airway at the end of the case. Remove the throat pack and suck out the pharynx under direct vision. Decide with the anaesthetist on awake or deep extubation; a smooth emergence without coughing protects a fresh thyroid bed or a reconstruction. If the airway may swell (extensive surgery, laryngeal oedema, bilateral neck dissection), keep the tube, use an airway exchange catheter, or perform a tracheostomy."],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s3-2","Laryngospasm","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Waking up","Sudden closure of the vocal cords during light anaesthesia, typically at extubation, especially in children and after blood or secretions touch the larynx. Laryngospasm at extubation 1 Act call for help 100% oxygen with tight mask and CPAP jaw thrust (pressure at Larson's point behind the angle of the jaw) 2 Not breaking propofol then suxamethonium (with atropine in children) and reintubate if needed 3 After watch for negative-pressure pulmonary oedema Senior's pearl Say aloud at the end of every airway case what is in the patient's mouth and nose (packs, gauze, patties, splints), and who checked it out. Nothing should stay behind and nothing should fall into the airway at extubation."],["ev","th:b-anaes~s4-1","Anaesthesia and the shared airway: current evidence","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · Evidence","The Fourth National Audit Project collected a year of major airway complications in the UK: 133 events in about 2.9 million general anaesthetics (about 1 in 22,000), with 16 deaths; head and neck cases and obstructed airways were prominent, and airway management was judged good in only 19% (Cook et al., Br J Anaesth 2011). High-flow nasal oxygen (THRIVE) kept patients with difficult airways oxygenated during apnoea of a median of about 14 minutes (Patel and Nouraei, Anaesthesia 2015)."],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s5-1","Viva essentials","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Shared airway principles and the pre-induction discussion. Tubes for each type of ENT operation. Jet ventilation and high-flow nasal oxygen. Obstructing airway tumours: awake options and awake tracheostomy. Laryngospasm management."],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · For the examination","Skills to sign off Discuss the airway plan with the anaesthetist before every case Choose the right tube for tonsil, laryngeal, thyroid and oral surgery Check packs and airway clearance before extubation Plan the anaesthetic for an obstructing laryngeal tumour with the anaesthetist Perform an awake tracheostomy under local anaesthetic"],["bsec","th:b-anaes~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Anaesthesia and the shared airway · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not being in the anaesthetic room for an airway case. No plan B agreed before induction. Jet ventilation without a route for gas to escape. Coughing at extubation after thyroid or neck surgery."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s1-1","Three requirements","Consent · The principles","Capacity: the patient can make this decision at this time. Information: they have been told what they need to know. Voluntary: no pressure from staff, family or circumstances."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s1-2","What to tell the patient","Consent · The principles","The diagnosis and what is likely to happen without treatment. The options, including no treatment and non-surgical treatment, with their benefits and risks. The operation: what it involves, the anaesthetic, the scar, the hospital stay and the recovery. Material risks: common risks, and serious risks even if rare (for example facial weakness after parotidectomy, hearing loss and dizziness after stapes surgery, voice change after thyroidectomy, death after tonsillectomy haemorrhage), and anything this patient has said matters to them (a singer's voice, a musician's hearing, a pilot's balance). Who will do the operation, and that trainees take part under supervision. What happens if something unexpected is found during the operation. Senior's pearl Ask, \"Is there anything about your work or your life that would make a particular complication matter more to you?\" That one question is how you find out what is material for this patient."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s1-3","The Montgomery test","Consent · The principles","In Montgomery v Lanarkshire (2015), the UK Supreme Court held that a doctor must take reasonable care to ensure the patient knows of any material risks and of reasonable alternatives. A risk is material if a reasonable person in the patient's position would attach significance to it, or if the doctor knows (or should know) that this patient would. Other countries' laws differ in detail, but the direction is the same: patient-centred disclosure."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s2-1","Assessing capacity","Consent · Capacity","Assume capacity unless there is reason to doubt it. A patient has capacity for a decision if they can: Understand the information relevant to the decision. Retain it long enough to decide. Use or weigh it as part of making the decision. Communicate the decision (by any means). Capacity is specific to the decision and the time. An unwise decision does not mean lack of capacity."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s2-2","When the patient lacks capacity","Consent · Capacity","Act in their best interests, consulting family, carers, any lasting power of attorney for health and welfare, or an independent advocate, and respecting any valid advance decision to refuse treatment. In an emergency, do what is necessary to save life or prevent serious harm."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s2-3","Children","Consent · Capacity","Consent comes from a person with parental responsibility. In the UK, young people of 16 and 17 are presumed to have capacity, and a child under 16 may consent if they have enough understanding and maturity (Gillick competence). Involve the child in every decision in a way they understand."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s3-1","Who should take consent","Consent · The practice","The surgeon doing the operation, or someone who could do it, or someone trained and delegated to take consent for that procedure who knows its risks. If you cannot answer the patient's questions, you are not the right person to consent them."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s3-2","When and how","Consent · The practice","For elective surgery, discuss in clinic and confirm on the day; do not leave the first discussion to the morning of a major operation. Use plain language and numbers (\"about 1 in 100\"), give written information, and check understanding by asking the patient to explain it back. Use a professional interpreter, not a family member, when there is a language barrier. Write the risks discussed on the form and in the notes, including any the patient was particularly concerned about. Separate consent for photographs, video and teaching."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s3-3","Withdrawal","Consent · The practice","A patient with capacity can withdraw consent at any time, even on the operating table before induction. Stop, discuss, and document. Consent on the trolley Signing a form in the anaesthetic room, sedated, anxious and without time to think, is not valid consent for elective surgery. If the patient has not been properly consented, postpone."],["ev","th:b-consent~s4-1","Consent: current evidence","Consent · Evidence","A systematic review of 52 trials of interventions to improve patients' understanding of consent found that interactive approaches worked best: every study using verbal discussion with teach-back or test-and-feedback improved comprehension, compared with fewer than half of written interventions (Glaser et al., Med Decis Making 2020)."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s5-1","Viva essentials","Consent · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Valid consent: capacity, information, voluntary. Montgomery and material risk. Capacity assessment and best interests. Gillick competence. Material risks for common ENT operations."],["bsec","th:b-consent~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Consent · For the examination","Skills to sign off Take consent for grommets, tonsillectomy and septoplasty, including all material risks Assess capacity and document it Consent using a professional interpreter Consent for thyroidectomy, parotidectomy and ear surgery Hold a consent conversation for major head and neck cancer surgery, including alternatives and no treatment"],["bsec","th:b-consent~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Consent · For the examination","Common pitfalls Consenting for an operation you do not understand. Leaving out the option of no treatment. A signature in the anaesthetic room as the only consent. Using family members as interpreters."],["bsec","th:b-crises~s1-1","What should make you stop","Theatre emergencies · Recognise","Sudden fall in blood pressure, rising airway pressures, falling saturations, a rising end-tidal carbon dioxide, a rash or swelling, unexplained tachycardia, a flash or smoke in the airway, sudden pulsatile bleeding, or a gasp of air into an open vein. Say what you see out loud and stop operating until the team knows what is happening."],["bsec","th:b-crises~s2-1","Anaphylaxis","Theatre emergencies · Drills","Anaphylaxis under anaesthesia 1 Call declare \"anaphylaxis\" call for help stop the suspected trigger (antibiotic, chlorhexidine, muscle relaxant, dye) 2 ABC 100% oxygen raise the legs fluid bolus 3 Adrenaline IV 50 micrograms boluses by the anaesthetist (or IM 0.5 mg if no IV) repeat infusion if needed 4 Then consider stopping or finishing surgery quickly mast cell tryptase at once, 1 to 2 hours and 24 hours allergy clinic referral Chlorhexidine (skin preparation, catheter gel, coated lines) is a hidden cause; ask about previous reactions."],["bsec","th:b-crises~s2-2","Malignant hyperthermia","Theatre emergencies · Drills","An inherited reaction to volatile anaesthetics and suxamethonium. Signs: unexplained rise in end-tidal carbon dioxide, tachycardia, muscle rigidity (including jaw spasm), then rising temperature, acidosis and high potassium. Malignant hyperthermia 1 Act stop volatile agents call for help hyperventilate with 100% oxygen switch to intravenous anaesthesia 2 Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV, then 1 mg/kg repeated until the signs settle (up to 10 mg/kg) 3 Support cool treat high potassium and arrhythmias finish surgery quickly critical care family testing"],["bsec","th:b-crises~s2-3","Massive haemorrhage","Theatre emergencies · Drills","Massive bleeding in theatre 1 Control pressure with packs or fingers tell the team call a senior and vascular help if needed 2 Activate the major haemorrhage protocol two large-bore cannulas group and crossmatch 3 Replace red cells and plasma about 1 1 platelets and cryoprecipitate as guided by tests tranexamic acid 1 g IV calcium 4 Prevent hypothermia (warmed fluids) and acidosis monitor clotting with near-patient tests"],["bsec","th:b-crises~s2-4","Carotid blowout","Theatre emergencies · Drills","Rupture of the carotid artery, almost always after radiotherapy or surgery with wound breakdown, fistula or tumour. Often preceded by a \"sentinel bleed\" or exposed carotid. Carotid blowout 1 Act direct pressure (finger or pack) call for help head down 2 Airway protect it cuffed tube or tracheostomy tube below the bleeding, cuff inflated 3 Fluids two large-bore cannulas major haemorrhage protocol 4 Definitive interventional radiology (stent graft or embolisation) if available surgical ligation if not The sentinel bleed A small bleed from the neck or mouth in an irradiated patient with a fistula or exposed carotid can be the warning before a fatal blowout. Admit, crossmatch, keep pressure kits at the bedside, and arrange urgent imaging and interventional radiology."],["bsec","th:b-crises~s2-5","Post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage","Theatre emergencies · Drills","Bleeding after tonsillectomy 1 First sit the patient up suction IV access and bloods (count, crossmatch) fluids 2 Local remove clot hydrogen peroxide gargle or adrenaline-soaked swab on the fossa pressure with a swab on a forceps 3 Theatre if it continues or is heavy rapid sequence induction by a senior anaesthetist, two suctions ready 4 In children small blood volume, swallowed blood hides the loss, and a full stomach of blood at induction"],["bsec","th:b-crises~s2-6","Venous air embolism","Theatre emergencies · Drills","When a large neck vein (internal jugular, sigmoid sinus) is opened with the head up, air can be sucked in, causing a sudden fall in end-tidal carbon dioxide, hypotension and arrhythmia. Flood the field with saline, cover the vein, lower the head, compress the jugular veins if possible, tell the anaesthetist, and aspirate air through a central line if one is present."],["bsec","th:b-crises~s2-7","Cardiac arrest in theatre","Theatre emergencies · Drills","Stop surgery, start chest compressions, follow advanced life support, and look for the cause: bleeding, tension pneumothorax (after neck or tracheostomy surgery), anaphylaxis, local anaesthetic toxicity, air embolism, airway loss, potassium. Drape removal and access to the chest must be quick."],["bsec","th:b-crises~s3-1","Readiness","Theatre emergencies · Prepare","Know where the difficult airway trolley, the defibrillator, the dantrolene, the lipid emulsion and the major haemorrhage protocol are kept. Rehearse with your team in simulation. In every head and neck cancer case, keep a plan for the carotid and the airway. Senior's pearl In a crisis, the person who leads should step back from the hands-on task so they can see the whole picture. If you are holding pressure on the carotid, someone else should be leading."],["ev","th:b-crises~s4-1","Theatre emergencies: current evidence","Theatre emergencies · Evidence","The national audit of perioperative anaphylaxis (NAP6) found an incidence of about 1 in 10,000 anaesthetics; antibiotics (especially teicoplanin), muscle relaxants, chlorhexidine and Patent Blue dye were the main causes, and hypotension was the commonest first sign (Harper et al., Br J Anaesth 2018). Among 1,554 patients having re-irradiation of the head and neck, carotid blowout occurred in 2.6%, and 76% were fatal (McDonald et al., Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys 2012). For acute blowout, both embolisation and stent grafts were effective, with more procedural strokes after embolisation of the internal or common carotid and more re-bleeding after stent grafts (Wong et al., Head Neck 2018)."],["bsec","th:b-crises~s5-1","Viva essentials","Theatre emergencies · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Anaphylaxis under anaesthesia and its common triggers. Malignant hyperthermia signs and dantrolene. Major haemorrhage protocol. Carotid blowout: sentinel bleed and management. Post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage in a child."],["bsec","th:b-crises~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Theatre emergencies · For the examination","Skills to sign off Know the location of all emergency drugs and equipment in my theatre Manage a post-tonsillectomy bleed from the ward to theatre Take part in a simulated anaphylaxis, airway fire and massive haemorrhage Lead the response to a carotid blowout or sentinel bleed"],["bsec","th:b-crises~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Theatre emergencies · For the examination","Common pitfalls Continuing to operate during an unexplained deterioration. Missing chlorhexidine as an allergen. Ignoring a sentinel bleed. The leader doing a hands-on task."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s1-1","Crisis resource management","Leading in a crisis · The principles","Know the environment: where the kit, drugs and help are. Anticipate and plan: brief for what could go wrong. Call for help early: the cost of calling too soon is small; too late is large. Lead and follow: one named leader; everyone else follows and speaks up. Communicate clearly: names, closed loops, one voice at a time. Distribute the work: give each person a task. Use all the resources: people, cognitive aids, equipment, other specialties. Re-evaluate repeatedly: \"Is our diagnosis still right? What are we missing?\" Avoid fixation: stepping back stops the whole team chasing one wrong idea."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s1-2","Situational awareness","Leading in a crisis · The principles","Three levels: perceiving what is happening (the numbers, the field, the people), understanding what it means, and predicting what will happen next. Fatigue, stress and fixation on one task (a bleeding point, a difficult intubation) narrow it."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s2-1","Closed-loop communication","Leading in a crisis · Communication","Address a person by name, give a clear instruction, and expect it repeated back and confirmed when done: \"Sara, please give 1 g of tranexamic acid IV.\" \"1 g of tranexamic acid IV, given.\" Messages shouted to \"someone\" are not received."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s2-2","SBAR","Leading in a crisis · Communication","SBAR handover or call for help Part What to say Situation Who you are, where, which patient, and the problem now Background Operation, relevant history, what has happened Assessment Your observations and what you think is going on Recommendation What you need: come now, advice, a decision"],["bsec","th:b-crm~s2-3","Speaking up: graded assertiveness","Leading in a crisis · Communication","If you are worried and not heard, escalate: probe (\"Have you seen the saturation?\"), alert (\"I'm concerned the saturation is falling\"), challenge (\"Please stop; this is unsafe\"), emergency (\"Stop now\"). Any member of the team, however junior, can and should stop a procedure that is unsafe. Senior's pearl Make it easy for others to stop you. Say at the brief, \"If anyone sees anything that worries them, tell me, and I'll listen.\" Then thank the first person who does."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s3-1","Cognitive aids","Leading in a crisis · Tools","Under stress, memory fails. Use emergency checklists (quick reference handbooks) for anaphylaxis, malignant hyperthermia, local anaesthetic toxicity and airway fire; assign one person to read them aloud. They do not insult your knowledge; they stop the team missing a step."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s3-2","Ten seconds for ten minutes","Leading in a crisis · Tools","When the situation is unclear, take ten seconds to stop, share what you see, agree the problem and the plan, and allocate tasks. It saves minutes of uncoordinated activity."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s4-1","The hot debrief","Leading in a crisis · After the crisis","Straight after the event, for a few minutes: what happened, what went well, what could be better, and does anyone need support. Report the incident, tell the patient (duty of candour) and follow up."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s4-2","The second victim","Leading in a crisis · After the crisis","Surgeons and staff involved in a serious adverse event often suffer guilt, anxiety and sleeplessness. Check on your colleagues and yourself, and use support services; it is not weakness."],["ev","th:b-crm~s5-1","Leading in a crisis: current evidence","Leading in a crisis · Evidence","In 106 simulated operating-room crises, teams with crisis checklists missed 6% of life-saving steps compared with 23% without them (Arriaga et al., N Engl J Med 2013). A systematic review found crisis resource management simulation training improved team performance in simulation, with growing evidence of transfer to clinical care (Boet et al., Can J Anaesth 2014)."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s6-1","Viva essentials","Leading in a crisis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Principles of crisis resource management. SBAR and closed-loop communication. Graded assertiveness. Cognitive aids and their evidence. Debrief and supporting colleagues."],["bsec","th:b-crm~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Leading in a crisis · For the examination","Skills to sign off Hand over or call for help using SBAR Use closed-loop communication in theatre Speak up using graded assertiveness Lead a team through a simulated crisis Run a hot debrief after a real event"],["bsec","th:b-crm~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Leading in a crisis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Calling for help too late. Nobody leading, or two people leading. Instructions to \"someone\". Fixating on one problem."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s1-1","The questions to ask","Decisions, complications and candour · Deciding to operate","Is the diagnosis certain? Imaging, histology and examination agree. Will the operation help this patient? The expected benefit for their symptoms, quality of life or survival, compared with the alternatives (including watching and waiting). Can they withstand it? Fitness, frailty, nutrition, the anaesthetic risk. What does the patient want? Their goals and priorities, which may differ from yours. Am I the right surgeon, in the right place? Volume, team, equipment and critical care."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s1-2","Shared decision-making","Decisions, complications and candour · Deciding to operate","Set out the options, including doing nothing, with their likely benefits and harms in numbers the patient can understand; ask what matters most to them; decide together. Decision aids and the \"BRAN\" questions (Benefits, Risks, Alternatives, and what if I do Nothing?) help."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s1-3","The multidisciplinary team","Decisions, complications and candour · Deciding to operate","Head and neck cancer, skull base and complex airway cases are discussed in a multidisciplinary meeting (surgeons, oncologists, radiologists, pathologists, specialist nurses, speech and language therapists, dietitians, restorative dentists). The decision is the patient's, informed by the team's recommendation."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s1-4","When not to operate","Decisions, complications and candour · Deciding to operate","When the risks outweigh the benefits for this patient, when the operation cannot achieve the patient's goals, when the patient does not want it, or near the end of life when comfort matters more than cure. Saying no, and explaining why, is part of good surgery; offer a second opinion if the patient wants one. Senior's pearl Ask yourself: \"If this were my parent, would I advise this operation, from this surgeon, in this hospital?\" If the answer is not a clear yes, slow down."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s2-1","Failure to rescue","Decisions, complications and candour · Complications","Hospitals differ less in how many complications they have than in how well they rescue patients from them. Rescue depends on early recognition (observations, early warning scores, nurses who are heard), early senior review, decisive action (return to theatre, radiology, critical care) and a culture where concerns are escalated."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s2-2","When your patient has a complication","Decisions, complications and candour · Complications","See the patient yourself, early. Be honest with the patient and family: what happened, what it means, what you are doing. Involve senior colleagues and other specialties without delay; your pride is not a reason to wait. Record it accurately, report it, and discuss it at the morbidity and mortality meeting. Denial The most dangerous moment is believing your patient cannot have a complication because the operation went well. Tachycardia, fever, pain out of proportion or a patient who \"isn't right\" after surgery is a complication until proven otherwise."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s3-1","The duty of candour","Decisions, complications and candour · Candour","Every doctor has a professional duty to be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong with their care that causes, or could cause, harm or distress. In England there is also a statutory duty for organisations when a notifiable safety incident causes moderate or greater harm: tell the patient (or family) as soon as reasonably possible, apologise, explain what is known, say what further enquiries will happen, and follow up in writing. Saying sorry Find a quiet place and enough time; bring a senior and a nurse if appropriate. Explain what happened in plain language, without speculation or blame. Say sorry. An apology is not an admission of legal liability. Explain what will be done now and to prevent it happening again. Offer support and a named contact, and document the conversation."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s3-2","Complaints","Decisions, complications and candour · Candour","Respond promptly and personally, listen, explain, apologise where appropriate, and learn. Most complaints are about communication rather than technical care."],["ev","th:b-decide~s4-1","Decisions, complications and candour: current evidence","Decisions, complications and candour · Evidence","In 84,730 patients having inpatient general and vascular surgery, hospitals with the highest and lowest mortality had similar complication rates (major complications 18.2% vs 16.2%), but patients with a major complication were almost twice as likely to die in high-mortality hospitals (21.4% vs 12.5%), showing that rescue, not avoidance, drives the difference (Ghaferi et al., N Engl J Med 2009). A systematic review of shared decision-making in elective surgery found it improved decision quality and knowledge and reduced decisional conflict (Boss et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2016)."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s5-1","Viva essentials","Decisions, complications and candour · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Shared decision-making and the role of the MDT. When not to operate. Failure to rescue. Duty of candour and how to apologise. Managing complaints."],["bsec","th:b-decide~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Decisions, complications and candour · For the examination","Skills to sign off Present options including no treatment in a way the patient understands Escalate a deteriorating post-operative patient early Tell a patient about a complication and apologise, with a senior Present a case at the multidisciplinary meeting Decide not to operate and explain why"],["bsec","th:b-decide~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Decisions, complications and candour · For the examination","Common pitfalls Operating because you can, not because you should. Delaying senior or other specialty help. Avoiding the patient after a complication. Treating an apology as an admission of liability."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s1-1","Halsted's principles","Dissection and tissue handling · Principles","Handle tissue gently. Achieve meticulous haemostasis. Preserve the blood supply. Use strict asepsis. Close without tension. Bring tissue together accurately. Obliterate dead space."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s1-2","Holding tissue","Dissection and tissue handling · Principles","Toothed forceps (Adson, Gillies) grip skin and fascia with less crushing than non-toothed forceps, which slip and tempt you to squeeze. Hold the dermis or the cut edge, never the epidermis, and never crush a nerve, a vessel you want to keep, or the tip of a flap. Fine skin hooks are kinder still for skin edges."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s1-3","Traction and countertraction","Dissection and tissue handling · Principles","Planes open when tissue is put under gentle tension in opposite directions: your forceps pull one way, the assistant's retractor or forceps the other. Without tension, scissors and diathermy push tissue around instead of separating it. Most of the time a stuck dissection needs better traction, not more force."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s2-1","Sharp dissection","Dissection and tissue handling · Techniques","With scalpel or scissors under direct vision. It is precise and causes the least tissue damage. Use the belly of the blade or the tips of the scissors, cutting only what you can see."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s2-2","Blunt dissection","Dissection and tissue handling · Techniques","Separating tissue by spreading (scissors or artery forceps opened in the plane), pushing with a swab or finger, or stroking with a dissector or elevator. It finds and follows loose areolar planes and is safer around structures you cannot yet see, but it tears tissue if forced and can avulse small vessels. Spread and cut with scissors Insert the closed tips into the plane, parallel to important structures (nerves, vessels). Spread gently to open the plane and see what is in it. Withdraw slightly, then cut only the thin, clear tissue you have opened and seen. Repeat, keeping the tips in view at all times."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s2-3","Subperiosteal and subperichondrial dissection","Dissection and tissue handling · Techniques","Under the periosteum and perichondrium, the plane is bloodless and safe, and it protects the soft tissues outside it. Use a Freer, Howarth or Cottle elevator against the bone or cartilage, keeping the edge in contact with the hard surface (septoplasty, mastoid, orbit, maxilla)."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s3-1","Finding and following a nerve","Dissection and tissue handling · Nerves and vessels","Know where the nerve should be, and find it at a reliable landmark before it is at risk (the facial nerve trunk at the tragal pointer and posterior belly of digastric, the recurrent laryngeal nerve in Beahrs' triangle, the accessory nerve at the jugular vein or Erb's point). Dissect on top of the nerve and parallel to it, with the scissor tips spreading along its line. Do not strip it of its surrounding tissue more than you need; it carries its blood supply. Do not use monopolar diathermy close to it; use bipolar sparingly and away from it. Use a nerve monitor where available, but trust your eyes over the beeps. Never cut what you cannot identify In the neck, any cord-like structure could be a nerve, a duct or an artery. If you are not sure what it is, follow it until you are."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s3-2","Vessels","Dissection and tissue handling · Nerves and vessels","Dissect vessels in the adventitial plane, clear them enough to place a clip or tie with a margin, and tie in continuity before dividing. Large veins (internal jugular) tear easily under traction: hold them with gentle, broad pressure, not forceps."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s4-1","Assisting well","Dissection and tissue handling · The assistant","Watch the operation, not the screen or the clock; anticipate the next step. Retract to show, not to pull: steady, gentle, and moved only when asked. Keep the field dry with suction or a swab, dabbing rather than wiping. Cut sutures to the length asked (usually 3 to 5 mm for ties; longer for monofilament). Say when you are tired or cannot see; a slipping retractor is dangerous. Senior's pearl When you are lost, stop and go back to something you know. Enlarge the exposure, find a landmark you are sure of, and dissect from known to unknown. The dangerous moment is pushing on in the wrong plane because you think you are nearly there."],["ev","th:b-dissect~s5-1","Dissection and tissue handling: current evidence","Dissection and tissue handling · Evidence","A meta-analysis of 14 randomised trials (2,541 patients) found skin incisions made with cutting diathermy were quicker and bled less than scalpel incisions, without more wound complications or pain (Ly et al., Br J Surg 2012). In a study of video-rated operative skill, surgeons in the lowest quartile of technical skill had nearly three times the complication rate of those in the highest quartile (14.5% vs 5.2%), showing that how tissue is handled affects outcomes (Birkmeyer et al., N Engl J Med 2013)."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s6-1","Viva essentials","Dissection and tissue handling · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Halsted's principles. Sharp versus blunt dissection. Planes of the neck and how to find them. How to find and protect a nerve."],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Dissection and tissue handling · For the examination","Skills to sign off Hold tissue with toothed forceps without crushing Assist with traction, suction and cutting sutures Raise subplatysmal flaps in the neck Elevate subperichondrial and subperiosteal flaps Identify and follow the facial, recurrent laryngeal and accessory nerves"],["bsec","th:b-dissect~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Dissection and tissue handling · For the examination","Common pitfalls Dissecting without traction and countertraction. Cutting tissue you have not seen. Crushing tissue with non-toothed forceps. Pushing on in the wrong plane."],["bsec","th:b-drains~s1-1","Types of drain","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · Drains","Drains in the head and neck Type How it works Typical uses Closed suction (Redivac, Jackson-Pratt) Vacuum bottle or bulb pulls fluid out and the skin flap down onto the bed Neck dissection, parotidectomy, free flap donor and recipient sites, large neck operations Passive (corrugated, Penrose, Yeates) Fluid runs out by gravity and capillary action Abscess cavities, infected or contaminated wounds"],["bsec","th:b-drains~s1-2","Using a suction drain","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · Drains","Bring it out through a separate stab incision in a dependent, hidden position, away from the main incision and the great vessels. Lay it where fluid collects, but not against a vascular anastomosis or directly over the carotid or a nerve. Secure it with a non-absorbable suture to the skin. Check it holds vacuum after closure; a drain that does not hold suction means an air leak (an open wound edge or a pharyngeal fistula). Record the volume and character every shift."],["bsec","th:b-drains~s1-3","When to remove it","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · Drains","Usually when the output falls below about 25 to 50 ml in 24 hours (units differ), or earlier for small operations. Chylous fluid (milky, rising on feeding, triglycerides high) after a left neck dissection means a chyle leak, and the drain stays. The drain will not save you from a haematoma A blocked drain, or a clot faster than the drain can remove it, still causes a neck haematoma that can obstruct the airway. Watch the neck, not just the bottle."],["bsec","th:b-drains~s2-1","Dressings","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · Dressings and bandages","Most head and neck incisions need only a light, non-adherent dressing or adhesive strips and glue; keep them dry for 48 hours. Leave flap dressings loose so the flap can be seen and no pressure falls on the pedicle."],["bsec","th:b-drains~s2-2","Head bandages","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · Dressings and bandages","A firm mastoid or head bandage after ear surgery or otoplasty prevents haematoma under the flap or pinna; usually left for 24 hours after mastoid surgery and up to a week after otoplasty (with a lighter headband for several weeks at night). Check the patient is not in pain under it: severe pain means haematoma or a bandage that is too tight."],["bsec","th:b-drains~s3-1","Nasal packs","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · Packs and splints","Non-absorbable packs (expanding sponge, inflatable, ribbon gauze with paraffin or BIPP) for epistaxis and sometimes after nasal surgery; usually removed after 24 to 48 hours. Absorbable or dissolvable packs (gelatin, carboxymethylcellulose, chitosan, steroid-eluting stents) after sinus surgery. Quilting sutures (a continuous trans-septal suture) instead of packs after septoplasty hold the flaps together. Septal splints (silicone sheets) after septal surgery or with turbinate surgery to prevent adhesions; removed after about a week. The pack that moves backwards A nasal pack, especially a posterior pack, can slip into the pharynx and obstruct the airway. Secure it with a string or tie at the nostril and check it is visible. Posterior packs cause hypoxia and need observation in hospital."],["bsec","th:b-drains~s3-2","Ear packs","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · Packs and splints","After tympanoplasty and canal surgery, gelatin sponge holds the graft in the middle ear and canal, with a ribbon gauze or antibiotic-soaked dressing in the canal (BIPP or antibiotic ointment), removed at 1 to 3 weeks."],["ev","th:b-drains~s4-1","Drains, dressings, packs and splints: current evidence","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · Evidence","A meta-analysis of 25 randomised trials (2,939 patients) found drains after thyroid surgery did not reduce reoperation for haematoma or wound collections, but did lengthen hospital stay, increase pain and more than double wound infection (relative risk 2.53) (Woods et al., Br J Surg 2014). After septoplasty, a meta-analysis of 16 studies found nasal packing did not reduce bleeding, haematoma, perforation, adhesions or residual deviation, but did increase infection (Banglawala et al., Int Forum Allergy Rhinol 2013). Trans-septal quilting sutures are an effective alternative with less pain (Certal et al., Rhinology 2012)."],["bsec","th:b-drains~s5-1","Viva essentials","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Indications for drains, and the evidence after thyroidectomy. Drain types and when to remove them. Nasal packing after septoplasty and alternatives. Chyle leak recognition. Dangers of posterior nasal packs."],["bsec","th:b-drains~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · For the examination","Skills to sign off Insert and secure a suction drain through a separate stab incision Apply a mastoid head bandage Insert anterior nasal packs for epistaxis Place a quilting suture and septal splints after septoplasty Decide when a drain is needed and when to remove it"],["bsec","th:b-drains~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Drains, dressings, packs and splints · For the examination","Common pitfalls Routine drains and packs without a reason. A drain against an anastomosis or the carotid. An unsecured posterior pack. Trusting a drain bottle over the look of the neck."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s1-1","How diathermy works","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Electrosurgery","High-frequency alternating current (about 300 kHz to 3 MHz, too fast to stimulate nerves and muscle) heats tissue where the current is concentrated. The effect depends on the current density and the waveform: Cut: a continuous waveform at lower voltage vaporises cells; little coagulation. Coagulation: an interrupted waveform at higher voltage dries and seals; more spread of heat. Blend: a mixture. Figure 1 Monopolar current passes through the patient to a return plate; bipolar current passes only between the tips. Drawn for this app"],["bsec","th:b-energy~s1-2","Monopolar","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Electrosurgery","The active electrode (pencil or needle) concentrates current at its tip; it returns through the body to a large plate, where the current density is low. Place the plate on dry, shaved, well-vascularised skin over muscle (thigh or upper arm), close to the field, away from metal implants and ECG electrodes. Burns Burns happen where current concentrates unintentionally: a partly detached plate, contact with metal on the table, pooled alcohol skin preparation, or current running along a narrow pedicle (a finger, the penis, a narrow tissue bridge). In the head and neck, monopolar diathermy near the facial nerve, the recurrent laryngeal nerve or in the middle ear can injure nerves at a distance from the tip."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s1-3","Bipolar","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Electrosurgery","The current passes only between the two tips of the forceps, so very little spreads to the surrounding tissue. Use bipolar near nerves (facial, recurrent laryngeal, accessory), in the ear, on the flap pedicle, and in patients with pacemakers. Keep the tips clean and slightly apart, irrigate to reduce charring and sticking, and grip only the vessel."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s1-4","Pacemakers and defibrillators","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Electrosurgery","Electromagnetic interference can inhibit a pacemaker or trigger an implanted defibrillator to shock. Find out the device type, why it was fitted and whether the patient is pacing-dependent; arrange a check by the cardiac physiology team before and after. Prefer bipolar; if monopolar is needed, use short bursts, the lowest effective power, and place the plate so the current path does not cross the device. Defibrillator shock therapies are usually suspended for the operation, with external defibrillator pads on and the patient monitored, and restarted afterwards. Head and neck surgery is often close to the device in the upper chest, which makes all of this more important."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s2-1","Coblation","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Advanced energy devices","Radiofrequency energy passed through saline creates a plasma field that breaks molecular bonds at about 40 to 70 degrees Celsius, lower than diathermy (about 400 degrees). It dissects and coagulates with less thermal spread. Used for tonsillectomy (including intracapsular tonsillotomy), adenoidectomy, turbinate reduction and some laryngeal and tongue base work."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s2-2","Ultrasonic devices and vessel sealers","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Advanced energy devices","Devices you will meet in the neck Device How it works Main uses Notes Harmonic scalpel Blade vibrating at about 55,000 times a second; coagulates proteins at 50 to 100 degrees Thyroidectomy, neck dissection, transoral robotic surgery Seals vessels up to about 5 mm; the active blade stays hot after use LigaSure Bipolar current with pressure and feedback that fuses vessel walls Thyroidectomy, neck dissection, flaps Seals vessels up to about 7 mm Coblation Plasma in saline Tonsils, adenoids, turbinates Low temperature, less spread Diathermy scissors and needles Monopolar or bipolar cutting Tonsils, flaps, neck Cheap, more thermal spread Senior's pearl Every device spreads heat. Keep the active jaw of an ultrasonic device away from the recurrent laryngeal nerve, let it cool before touching other tissue, and never use an energy device to \"find\" a bleeding point in a pool of blood."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s2-3","Smoke","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Advanced energy devices","Diathermy and laser smoke contains fine particles, hydrocarbons and sometimes viral material. Use local smoke extraction (a suction device near the tip) and masks; extraction matters most with lasers and in long cases."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s3-1","Laser physics in one minute","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Lasers","A laser produces light of a single wavelength, in phase and in a narrow beam. The wavelength decides what absorbs it (the chromophore: water, haemoglobin or pigment) and so how deep it acts. Power density (watts per area) decides whether it cuts or coagulates: a small focused spot cuts, a defocused spot coagulates. Figure 2 The main surgical lasers in ENT and their targets. Drawn for this app"],["bsec","th:b-energy~s3-2","Laser safety","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Lasers","A trained laser protection supervisor, warning signs on the doors, and the key controlled. Eye protection for everyone in the room, matched to the wavelength; the patient's eyes covered with wet pads. Laser-safe endotracheal tube with the cuff filled with saline (often with methylene blue, so a puncture shows), or a tubeless technique. Wet swabs around the field and in the subglottis, wet towels on the face. Lowest inspired oxygen the patient tolerates (ideally under 30%), no nitrous oxide. The laser on standby whenever it is not being fired, and fired only when the aiming beam is on target. Airway fire Fire needs fuel (the tube, drapes, swabs), an oxidiser (oxygen, nitrous oxide) and an ignition source (laser or diathermy). In the airway, all three meet. If it happens: stop the laser, stop the gas flow, remove the tube, pour saline into the airway, then ventilate by mask, re-intubate and examine the airway with a bronchoscope."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s4-1","Crisis drills","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · When it goes wrong","Fire in the airway 1 Act stop laser stop ventilation and oxygen remove the tube saline into the airway 2 Then mask ventilation with air, then oxygen re-intubate bronchoscopy keep the tube to examine Burn found under the return plate at the end 1 Act document and photograph check the plate and machine report as an incident tell the patient plastic surgery or dressings"],["ev","th:b-energy~s5-1","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers: current evidence","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · Evidence","In 15,734 tonsillectomies in the Swedish national register, every \"hot\" technique carried a higher risk of late haemorrhage than cold dissection with cold haemostasis: 2.8 times after cold dissection with diathermy haemostasis, 3.2 times after Coblation and 4.3 times after diathermy scissors (Söderman et al., Clin Otolaryngol 2015). In open thyroidectomy, a meta-analysis found the Harmonic Focus shortened operating time by about 22 minutes and reduced blood loss and hospital stay compared with conventional techniques, without more complications (Cannizzaro et al., Head Neck 2016). A systematic review found surgical smoke contains respirable ultrafine particles and potentially carcinogenic hydrocarbons, supporting smoke extraction (Mowbray et al., Surg Endosc 2013)."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s6-1","Viva essentials","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Monopolar versus bipolar: the current path and when to use each. Causes of diathermy burns. Pacemakers and defibrillators. Laser wavelengths and their uses in ENT. Laser safety and the airway fire drill."],["bsec","th:b-energy~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · For the examination","Skills to sign off Place a return plate correctly and check the diathermy settings Use bipolar diathermy cleanly, without charring or sticking Use Coblation for tonsillectomy or adenoidectomy Use an ultrasonic or vessel-sealing device in the neck Set up and use a laser with full safety precautions Lead the airway fire drill"],["bsec","th:b-energy~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Diathermy, energy devices and lasers · For the examination","Common pitfalls Monopolar diathermy near nerves or in a patient with a pacemaker without precautions. Firing a laser at high oxygen concentrations. Wrong eye protection for the laser in use. Touching tissue with a hot ultrasonic blade."],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s1-1","Preparation","Haemostasis and blood loss · Before the knife","Medicines: stop or bridge anticoagulants and antiplatelets according to the bleeding risk of the operation and the reason for the drug (see the pre-operative assessment chapter); ask about herbal supplements (ginkgo, garlic, fish oil) and non-steroidal drugs. History: easy bruising, heavy periods, bleeding after dental extraction or previous surgery, and family history suggest a bleeding disorder (von Willebrand disease is the commonest); test before elective surgery if the history is positive. Position: head up 15 to 20 degrees for sinus, skull base and thyroid surgery. Anaesthesia: controlled low-normal blood pressure, a slow heart rate, normocapnia, and total intravenous anaesthesia for endoscopic sinus surgery. Vasoconstriction: topical adrenaline (1:1,000 to 1:10,000 on patties) or cocaine in the nose, and local anaesthetic with adrenaline (1:80,000 to 1:200,000) injected into the field, waiting 10 minutes for the effect."],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s2-1","The haemostasis ladder","Haemostasis and blood loss · During the operation","1 Pressure Firm pressure with a swab or patty for 2 to 5 minutes stops most capillary and venous oozing 2 Position and patience Head up, warm saline irrigation (about 40 degrees) in the nose and ear 3 Diathermy Bipolar for precision near nerves; monopolar or suction diathermy for larger raw surfaces 4 Clips and ties For named vessels: clip, tie in continuity, transfix large vessels and pedicles 5 Sutures Figure-of-eight or under-running suture for a bleeder in a tissue bed 6 Topical agents Oxidised cellulose, gelatin sponge, gelatin-thrombin matrix, bone wax 7 Systemic Tranexamic acid; correct coagulation; blood products 8 Vessel control Ligation or embolisation of the feeding artery"],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s2-2","Topical haemostats","Haemostasis and blood loss · During the operation","Topical agents Agent Examples How it works Uses and cautions Oxidised regenerated cellulose Surgicel A scaffold for clot; acidic, mildly antibacterial Oozing beds (thyroid, tonsil, sinus); swells, avoid in tight bony canals near nerves Gelatin sponge Gelfoam, Spongostan Absorbs blood and provides a matrix Middle ear packing, sinus, dural support Gelatin-thrombin matrix Floseal, Surgiflo Granules plus thrombin; fill irregular surfaces Sinus and skull base bleeding, especially from mucosa and bone Bone wax Plugs bone channels mechanically Bleeding from bone, emissary veins in the mastoid; keep small, it is a foreign body Adrenaline Patties 1:1,000 to 1:10,000 Vasoconstriction Nose, ear; watch the heart rate and rhythm Controlling a bleeder you cannot see Press with a swab or finger to stop the bleeding. Tell the anaesthetist and ask for suction and a second suction if needed. Improve the exposure (retraction, light, enlarge the incision). Release slowly while suctioning, find the point, and pick it up precisely with forceps or a clip. Coagulate, clip or tie only what you can see; never clip or diathermy blindly in a pool of blood. Blind clipping Blind clips and diathermy in the neck injure the recurrent laryngeal, vagus, hypoglossal and accessory nerves and tear larger vessels. Pressure first, then see, then control."],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s3-1","Estimating loss","Haemostasis and blood loss · Blood loss","Weigh swabs (1 g roughly equals 1 ml), measure suction (subtracting irrigation) and look at the drapes. In children, small volumes matter: blood volume is about 80 ml per kilogram (about 85 to 90 ml/kg in neonates), so a 15 kg child has about 1,200 ml, and 120 ml is 10% of it."],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s3-2","Transfusion","Haemostasis and blood loss · Blood loss","Most patients tolerate a haemoglobin of 70 g/L (80 g/L in cardiovascular disease, often higher in head and neck free flap patients by local policy). Give tranexamic acid early when bleeding is significant. Massive haemorrhage is covered in the theatre emergencies chapter."],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s4-1","Checking haemostasis","Haemostasis and blood loss · Before closing","Before closure, ask the anaesthetist to bring the blood pressure back to normal and, in the neck, to perform a Valsalva manoeuvre (raising venous pressure to reveal open veins). Irrigate, wait, look again. Place a drain where dead space or oozing remains. Senior's pearl The wound will bleed at the blood pressure the patient has in recovery, not the one they had under hypotensive anaesthesia. Test it at normal pressure before you close."],["ev","th:b-haemostasis~s5-1","Haemostasis and blood loss: current evidence","Haemostasis and blood loss · Evidence","In endoscopic sinus surgery, tranexamic acid reduced blood loss and operating time and improved the surgical field without more thrombotic events (Kim et al., Laryngoscope 2019), and total intravenous anaesthesia gave a better field and less blood loss than inhalational anaesthesia in a meta-analysis of 15 trials (Lu et al., Laryngoscope 2020). In tonsillectomy, pooled data from ten studies showed tranexamic acid reduced intraoperative blood loss and post-operative haemorrhage (risk ratio 0.42), although higher-quality trials are still needed (Kuo et al., Ann Otol Rhinol Laryngol 2022)."],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s6-1","Viva essentials","Haemostasis and blood loss · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Pre-operative assessment of bleeding risk. Methods of haemostasis, from pressure to ligation. Topical agents and when to use them. Improving the field in sinus surgery. Blood volume and transfusion thresholds."],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Haemostasis and blood loss · For the examination","Skills to sign off Use pressure, bipolar and clips to control bleeding in a clear field Choose and use topical haemostats Control a bleeding vessel in the neck under pressure without blind clipping Optimise the field in sinus surgery with the anaesthetist"],["bsec","th:b-haemostasis~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Haemostasis and blood loss · For the examination","Common pitfalls Clipping or diathermying blindly in a pool of blood. Closing at low blood pressure without a Valsalva test. Bone wax in excess. Forgetting how little blood a small child can lose."],["bsec","th:b-human~s1-1","Non-technical skills","Human factors, audit and improvement · Human factors","NOTSS: the four categories Category Elements Situation awareness Gathering information, understanding it, projecting ahead Decision-making Considering options, selecting and communicating one, reviewing it Communication and teamwork Exchanging information, shared understanding, coordinating Leadership Setting standards, supporting others, coping with pressure"],["bsec","th:b-human~s1-2","Fatigue and stress","Human factors, audit and improvement · Human factors","After about 17 hours awake, performance falls to roughly the level of someone at the drink-drive limit, and after 24 hours it is worse. Check yourself with HALT (hungry, angry, late, tired) before a difficult task. Take breaks, eat, drink, hand over when you are not safe, and avoid scheduling complex elective cases after a night on call."],["bsec","th:b-human~s1-3","Cognitive biases","Human factors, audit and improvement · Human factors","Common biases in surgery Bias What happens Antidote Anchoring Sticking with the first diagnosis Ask \"What else could it be?\" Confirmation Seeing only what supports your view Look for evidence against it Availability Overweighting a recent or memorable case Use base rates and guidelines Sunk cost Pushing on because you have come so far Re-decide as if starting fresh Overconfidence Not checking, not asking Checklists, second opinions"],["bsec","th:b-human~s1-4","Designing out error","Human factors, audit and improvement · Human factors","People are the weakest barrier; design is stronger. Standardise equipment and trays, use checklists and protocols, label everything, reduce interruptions at critical moments (a \"sterile cockpit\" during the time out and critical steps), and make the safe action the easy one."],["bsec","th:b-human~s2-1","Audit","Human factors, audit and improvement · Learning from outcomes","Audit measures practice against a standard, makes a change, and measures again (the audit cycle). Examples in ENT: post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage rates against national figures, post-thyroidectomy hypocalcaemia, time from referral to cancer diagnosis, documentation in operation notes. The audit cycle Choose a topic that matters and a published standard. Agree criteria and a target (for example, \"95% of operation notes record VTE prophylaxis\"). Collect data on current practice. Compare with the standard and find the reasons for the gap. Make a change. Re-audit to close the loop."],["bsec","th:b-human~s2-2","Morbidity and mortality meetings","Human factors, audit and improvement · Learning from outcomes","Present complications and deaths openly to learn, not to blame: what happened, what was expected, what could be different, and what will change. Track your own complication rates throughout your career."],["bsec","th:b-human~s2-3","Incident reporting","Human factors, audit and improvement · Learning from outcomes","Report incidents and near misses through your hospital's system. Near misses are free lessons; a culture that reports them is a safe culture."],["bsec","th:b-human~s3-1","Quality improvement","Human factors, audit and improvement · Improvement","Use the model for improvement: what are we trying to accomplish, how will we know a change is an improvement, and what changes can we make? Then test changes in small Plan-Do-Study-Act cycles, measure with run charts, and spread what works."],["bsec","th:b-human~s3-2","Audit, research and service evaluation","Human factors, audit and improvement · Improvement","Audit compares practice with a standard; research creates new knowledge and needs ethics approval; service evaluation describes current practice without a standard. Know which you are doing before you start."],["bsec","th:b-human~s4-1","Wellbeing","Human factors, audit and improvement · Looking after yourself","Burnout (emotional exhaustion, cynicism, a reduced sense of achievement) is common in surgical training and affects patient care. Protect sleep and time off, keep up friendships and interests outside medicine, seek a mentor, talk about difficult cases, and use support services early. Ask colleagues how they are, and mean it. Senior's pearl The surgeon who never admits to being tired, upset or out of their depth is the most dangerous one in the hospital. Saying \"I need a break\" or \"I need help\" is professional."],["ev","th:b-human~s5-1","Human factors, audit and improvement: current evidence","Human factors, audit and improvement · Evidence","A systematic review of otolaryngology trainees found an average burnout rate of 58.6% (range about 30% to 86%), linked to long working hours; protected non-clinical time and formal mentorship reduced burnout (Lawlor et al., World J Otorhinolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2022). In a classic experiment, staying awake for 17 hours impaired performance as much as a blood alcohol concentration of 0.05%, and 24 hours as much as 0.1% (Dawson and Reid, Nature 1997)."],["bsec","th:b-human~s6-1","Viva essentials","Human factors, audit and improvement · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for NOTSS categories. Fatigue and its effects. Cognitive biases and how to counter them. The audit cycle and the difference from research. Quality improvement and PDSA. Burnout and wellbeing."],["bsec","th:b-human~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Human factors, audit and improvement · For the examination","Skills to sign off Recognise when I am too tired or stressed to work safely and act on it Report an incident or near miss Present at a morbidity and mortality meeting Complete an audit cycle including re-audit Lead a quality improvement project with PDSA cycles Track and review my own outcomes"],["bsec","th:b-human~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Human factors, audit and improvement · For the examination","Common pitfalls Working on when unsafe through tiredness. Anchoring on the first diagnosis. Audits that never close the loop. Blame instead of learning in morbidity meetings."],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s1-1","Relaxed skin tension lines","Incisions in the head and neck · Principles","The relaxed skin tension lines run at right angles to the pull of the underlying muscles and correspond to the wrinkles that appear with age (forehead creases, crow's feet, nasolabial folds, horizontal neck creases). Incisions placed in or parallel to them heal with the narrowest scars. Langer's lines, mapped in cadavers, do not always match them on the face; the relaxed skin tension lines are the ones to use. Ask the patient to frown, smile or bend the neck to show the creases before marking."],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s1-2","Facial aesthetic units","Incisions in the head and neck · Principles","The face is a set of units (forehead, eyelids, cheeks, nose, lips, chin) with their subunits. Scars on the border between units, or replacement of a whole subunit, hide better than scars across the middle of one."],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s1-3","Making the incision","Incisions in the head and neck · Principles","A clean incision Mark with a skin pen before injecting (local anaesthetic distorts landmarks), including any planned extension. Stretch the skin between finger and thumb across the line. Hold the scalpel like a pen for fine work or like a table knife for long incisions, and use the belly of a 10 or 15 blade, not the tip. Cut perpendicular to the skin in one confident stroke to the right depth, except in hair-bearing skin, where you bevel parallel to the follicles. Do not saw or make multiple shallow cuts, which leave ragged edges."],["bsec","th:b-incisions~c2","The incisions","Incisions in the head and neck · The incisions","A map of the head and neck. Figure 1 Common incisions. Each lies in a crease or behind a natural landmark. Drawn for this app"],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s2-1","The neck","Incisions in the head and neck · The incisions","Collar (Kocher) incision: thyroid, parathyroid, larynx; in a natural crease about two fingerbreadths above the sternal notch. Tracheostomy: horizontal, midway between the cricoid and the sternal notch (vertical in an emergency). Submandibular: parallel to and at least 2 cm (two fingerbreadths) below the lower border of the mandible, in a crease, to stay below the marginal mandibular nerve. Neck dissection: an apron (from mastoid down and across), a hockey stick or utility incision, or two parallel MacFee incisions in an irradiated neck; avoid three-point junctions over the carotid artery, where skin necrosis exposes the vessel."],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s2-2","The face and ear","Incisions in the head and neck · The incisions","Modified Blair (parotid): preauricular in the crease, around the lobule, then down in a neck crease; or a facelift incision hidden behind the ear and into the hairline. Postauricular: a few millimetres behind the postauricular sulcus, for mastoid and middle ear surgery; endaural and permeatal incisions for smaller ear operations. Nose and midface: columellar (open rhinoplasty), lateral rhinotomy along the side of the nose (Weber-Fergusson extends it under the eye and through the lip), sublabial (inside the upper lip) and midfacial degloving with no external scar. Coronal: in the hair from ear to ear, for frontal sinus and upper facial skeleton. Eyelid and orbit: transconjunctival, subciliary, upper lid crease. Nerves under the line The marginal mandibular nerve runs at or below the lower border of the mandible in a substantial proportion of people, more often behind the facial artery; the frontal (temporal) branch crosses the zygomatic arch roughly from a point below the tragus to above the lateral eyebrow; the great auricular nerve crosses sternomastoid about 6 cm below the external ear canal; the accessory nerve emerges from behind sternomastoid near Erb's point. Plan incisions and planes around them."],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s3-1","Designing skin flaps","Incisions in the head and neck · Flaps of skin","Keep the base broad and the flap no longer than its blood supply allows; on the face a ratio of up to about 4:1 survives, on the neck less, and much less in irradiated skin. Raise neck flaps in the subplatysmal plane, where the vessels run with the platysma. Round the corners of flaps; sharp points necrose. Avoid incisions that leave a skin bridge between two parallel cuts too narrow to survive. Senior's pearl Plan for the operation you might have to do, not the one you hope to do. A thyroid incision that cannot be extended to a neck dissection, or a parotid incision that cannot reach the neck, turns a complication into a second scar."],["ev","th:b-incisions~s4-1","Incisions in the head and neck: current evidence","Incisions in the head and neck · Evidence","A systematic review and meta-analysis of 28 anatomical studies (1,861 sides) found that at least one branch of the marginal mandibular nerve ran below the lower border of the mandible in 39% of cases, and that the nerve often has two or more branches (Marcuzzo et al., Clin Anat 2020). In a cadaveric study, the lowest branch ran up to 17.7 mm below the mandible (mean about 7 mm), supporting submandibular incisions at least 2 cm below the bone (Anthony et al., Patient Saf Surg 2018)."],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s5-1","Viva essentials","Incisions in the head and neck · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Relaxed skin tension lines versus Langer's lines. Incisions for thyroid, parotid, submandibular gland, neck dissection and ear. Nerves at risk and how incisions avoid them. Flap design in the neck, including after radiotherapy."],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Incisions in the head and neck · For the examination","Skills to sign off Mark an incision in the relaxed skin tension lines before injecting Make a clean, single-stroke skin incision Plan and mark a postauricular, collar and submandibular incision Plan incisions for neck dissection, including the irradiated neck Plan parotid and facelift incisions"],["bsec","th:b-incisions~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Incisions in the head and neck · For the examination","Common pitfalls Marking after injecting local anaesthetic. A submandibular incision too close to the mandible. Three-point junctions over the carotid. An incision that cannot be extended."],["bsec","th:b-infection~s1-1","Hand hygiene","Infection control and sharps injuries · Everyday precautions","Clean your hands at the five moments: before touching a patient, before a clean or aseptic procedure, after body fluid exposure, after touching a patient, and after touching the patient's surroundings. Use alcohol rub unless the hands are visibly soiled or after contact with Clostridioides difficile or norovirus, when soap and water are needed. Bare below the elbows on the ward."],["bsec","th:b-infection~s1-2","Personal protective equipment","Infection control and sharps injuries · Everyday precautions","Gloves and apron for contact with blood, body fluids, mucosa and broken skin. Eye protection for anything that splashes: suction, irrigation, drilling, tonsillectomy, tracheostomy. Surgical mask for routine work; a fitted respirator (FFP3 or N95) for aerosol-generating procedures in patients with airborne or high-risk respiratory infections."],["bsec","th:b-infection~s1-3","Aerosol-generating procedures in ENT","Infection control and sharps injuries · Everyday precautions","Tracheostomy insertion and open suction, high-speed drilling, powered instruments in the nose (microdebrider), laser and diathermy plume, and endoscopy that triggers coughing or sneezing generate aerosols. Use the protection your hospital specifies for the infection risk, keep the minimum number of staff in the room, and consider topical anaesthetic and vasoconstrictor sprays that provoke less sneezing."],["bsec","th:b-infection~s1-4","Specific organisms","Infection control and sharps injuries · Everyday precautions","MRSA: screen before major and implant surgery, decolonise carriers (nasal mupirocin and chlorhexidine washes), and give prophylaxis that covers it when appropriate. Clostridioides difficile: isolate, soap and water, review antibiotics. Prions (Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease): standard sterilisation does not destroy them; instruments used on lymphoid tissue (tonsils, adenoids) are tracked or single-use according to national policy, and patients at increased risk need special handling of instruments."],["bsec","th:b-infection~s2-1","Safe sharps practice","Infection control and sharps injuries · Sharps","Never re-sheath a needle by hand; use the needle holder or a sheathing device. Use a neutral zone (a kidney dish) to pass sharps rather than hand to hand. Load and unload blades with an instrument, never fingers. Use blunt-tipped needles for fascia and muscle where possible, and double gloves. Say \"sharp\" when passing one back, and dispose of sharps yourself into a sharps bin. Never use your fingers to guide a needle in a deep space; retract with an instrument."],["bsec","th:b-infection~s2-2","Transmission risk after a needlestick","Infection control and sharps injuries · Sharps","Approximate risk after a single injury from a source with the virus Virus Risk What reduces it Hepatitis B Up to about 1 in 3 if the source is e-antigen positive Vaccination; immunoglobulin and booster after exposure Hepatitis C About 1 in 50 No prophylaxis; follow-up testing and early treatment HIV About 1 in 300 Post-exposure prophylaxis within hours; negligible if the source has an undetectable viral load After a needlestick or splash 1 Now encourage bleeding, wash with soap and running water (do not scrub or suck) wash eyes or mouth with water remove gloves 2 Report tell the theatre coordinator occupational health or emergency department immediately 3 Assess risk of the injury and the source (consent to test the source patient) 4 Treat HIV post-exposure prophylaxis ideally within 1 hour (up to 72 hours) if indicated hepatitis B booster or immunoglobulin as needed 5 Follow up blood tests at baseline and later support Report every injury Reporting is how you get prophylaxis in time, how the source gets tested, and how the hospital changes what caused it. A quarter or more of surgical residents do not report their injuries."],["ev","th:b-infection~s3-1","Infection control and sharps injuries: current evidence","Infection control and sharps injuries · Evidence","In a national survey of 7,395 general surgery residents, 27.7% had a needlestick in the previous 6 months, mostly self-inflicted in theatre with solid suture needles; carelessness and feeling rushed were the common causes, long working hours increased the risk, and 28.7% did not report the injury (Yang et al., J Am Coll Surg 2019). Reviews during the COVID-19 pandemic identified tracheostomy, drilling, powered nasal instruments and endoscopy as the main aerosol-generating ENT procedures (Mick et al., J Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2020)."],["bsec","th:b-infection~s4-1","Viva essentials","Infection control and sharps injuries · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Hand hygiene moments. Aerosol-generating procedures in ENT and the right protection. Needlestick management and transmission risks. Prions and tonsil instruments."],["bsec","th:b-infection~s4-2","Skills to sign off","Infection control and sharps injuries · For the examination","Skills to sign off Clean hands at the five moments without reminders Choose protective equipment for routine work and aerosol-generating procedures Handle and pass sharps safely using a neutral zone Know the needlestick pathway in my hospital and how to reach occupational health"],["bsec","th:b-infection~s4-3","Common pitfalls","Infection control and sharps injuries · For the examination","Common pitfalls Re-sheathing needles by hand. Guiding a needle with your fingers. Not reporting a needlestick because you are busy. No eye protection during drilling and suction."],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s1-1","Holding instruments","ENT instruments and trays · The basic set","Scalpel: pen grip for fine incisions, table-knife grip (index finger on the back) for long ones. Scissors and needle holders: thumb and ring finger in the rings, index finger along the shaft for control (the tripod grip); turn from the wrist, not the elbow. Forceps: like a pen, between thumb and index and middle fingers, never in the palm. Pass instruments handle first, into the open hand, firmly enough that the receiver feels it, and sharp edges away from both of you."],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s1-2","Scalpel blades","ENT instruments and trays · The basic set","Figure 1 The four blades used most in ENT. Drawn for this app"],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s1-3","The basic instruments","ENT instruments and trays · The basic set","The basic set Instrument Use Scalpel handle (No. 3 takes blades 10 to 15) Skin and fine incisions McIndoe and Metzenbaum scissors Fine, curved dissecting scissors for tissue; never for sutures Mayo scissors Heavy tissue, cutting sutures and dressings Adson and Gillies toothed forceps Holding skin and fascia DeBakey (atraumatic) forceps Vessels and bowel-like tissue Artery forceps (Mosquito, Dunhill, Spencer Wells) Clamping vessels, holding ties Needle holders (Mayo-Hegar, Crile-Wood, Halsey) Holding needles; fine ones for the face Retractors: Langenbeck, Czerny, Kilner cat's paw, skin hooks, self-retaining (West, Weitlaner, Travers) Exposure Bipolar forceps and diathermy pencil Haemostasis Suction (Yankauer, Frazier, Zoellner) Clearing the field; fine suction for the ear and nose Figure 2 Adson forceps, toothed and non-toothed, and a fine needle holder; gold handles mark tungsten carbide jaws. SnowBink, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons; Rschiedon at Dutch Wikipedia (Original text: Rschiedon), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons"],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s2-1","Ear","ENT instruments and trays · The ENT trays","Aural specula, a microscope, fine suction (sizes 18 to 24 French and smaller), Jobson Horne probe and wax hook, crocodile (Hartmann) forceps, Rosen needle, round knife, sickle knife, Plester or House curettes, cup forceps, Bellucci scissors, Wullstein or Weitlaner self-retaining retractor, drill with cutting and diamond burrs, and Rosen or Fisch elevators. Figure 3 An aural speculum. Netha Hussain, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons Figure 4 A Jobson Horne probe with a ring curette at one end, used for wax and debris. Sarindam7, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons Figure 5 Crocodile (Hartmann) aural forceps, for foreign bodies and dressings in the ear canal. Sarindam7, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons"],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s2-2","Nose and sinuses","ENT instruments and trays · The ENT trays","Thudichum and Killian nasal specula, Tilley's nasal dressing forceps, Freer and Cottle elevators, Killian septal speculum, Ballenger swivel knife, Luc's or Tilley-Henckel forceps, Blakesley (straight and up-biting) forceps, through-cutting forceps, backbiter, sickle knife, microdebrider, Kerrison punch, Walsham and Asch forceps for nasal fractures, and endoscopes with suction (Frazier). Figure 6 Tilley's nasal dressing forceps: angled so the hand stays out of the line of sight. Netha Hussain, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons"],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s2-3","Tonsils and adenoids","ENT instruments and trays · The ENT trays","Boyle-Davis mouth gag with tongue blades and Draffin rods, Denis Browne tonsil holding forceps, Mollison dissector and pillar retractor, Gwynne-Evans dissector, Eves tonsil snare, Negus or Birkett artery forceps and Negus knot pusher, adenoid curettes (St Clair Thomson), suction diathermy or Coblation wand. Figure 7 Denis Browne tonsil holding forceps grip the tonsil without cutting it. Netha Hussain, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons Figure 8 Curved artery forceps for the tonsil bed: Negus forceps, then a tonsil artery forceps. Netha Hussain, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons; Sarindam7, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons Figure 9 The Eves tonsil snare, used to cut the lower pole. Netha Hussain, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons"],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s2-4","Larynx and airway","ENT instruments and trays · The ENT trays","Laryngoscopes (Dedo, Kleinsasser, Lindholm, Benjamin for children), suspension and chest support, microlaryngeal cup forceps, scissors and suction, Hopkins rod telescopes, rigid bronchoscopes and oesophagoscopes with optical forceps, tracheostomy set (cricoid hook, tracheal dilator such as Trousseau, tracheostomy tubes of several sizes with introducers)."],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s2-5","Neck","ENT instruments and trays · The ENT trays","Self-retaining neck retractors (Joll for thyroid), Langenbeck and Czerny retractors, Lahey or Kocher forceps, fine right-angled forceps (Lahey, Mixter) for passing ties, vessel loops, nerve stimulator, bipolar and energy devices. Figure 10 Test yourself on a mixed tray: a Thudichum nasal speculum (2), an aural speculum (3) and a metal tracheostomy tube (6), with other ENT instruments. Ask the scrub practitioner to name the rest with you. Netha Hussain, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons Senior's pearl If you want something unusual, ask for it at the team brief, not when you need it. The circulating practitioner can find a Kerrison punch in two minutes before the start, but not in thirty seconds while you are bleeding."],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s3-1","Looking after instruments","ENT instruments and trays · Care of instruments","Fine instruments (ear, microlaryngeal, microvascular) bend and blunt easily: never use fine scissors on sutures or dressings, never grip a needle with fine forceps, and wipe instruments clean of blood during the case so they do not stick. Report any damaged instrument instead of putting it back on the tray."],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s3-2","Prion precautions","ENT instruments and trays · Care of instruments","Instruments used on tonsils and adenoids can carry prions (variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease) that survive normal sterilisation; follow your hospital's tracking policy, and single-use instruments are used in some countries."],["ev","th:b-instruments~s4-1","ENT instruments and trays: current evidence","ENT instruments and trays · Evidence","Single-use tonsil instruments. The UK introduced compulsory single-use instruments for tonsillectomy in 2001 to reduce the theoretical risk of variant CJD, but withdrew the policy within a year after a rise in post-tonsillectomy bleeding and other complications with the new instruments; reusable instruments with full tracking are now standard. Tray rationalisation. Quality-improvement studies in several specialties show that removing rarely used instruments from standard trays cuts processing time and cost without affecting safety. Counts. The WHO Surgical Safety Checklist, which includes instrument, swab and needle counts, reduced complications and deaths in a multinational study (Haynes et al., NEJM 2009)."],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s5-1","Viva essentials","ENT instruments and trays · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Name and use of instruments from each ENT tray (a common viva and OSCE station). Blade numbers. How to hold scissors, forceps and needle holders. Instrument care and prion precautions."],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s5-2","Skills to sign off","ENT instruments and trays · For the examination","Skills to sign off Name every instrument on the basic, ear, nose and tonsil trays Hold scalpel, scissors, forceps and needle holder correctly Name the laryngoscopy, bronchoscopy and tracheostomy instruments Name the neck dissection and thyroid instruments"],["bsec","th:b-instruments~s5-3","Common pitfalls","ENT instruments and trays · For the examination","Common pitfalls Cutting sutures with dissecting scissors. Palming forceps. Asking for unusual instruments only when you need them. Using the wrong size of suction in the ear."],["bsec","th:b-knots~s1-1","Which knot","Knots and wound closure · Knots","Square (reef) knot: two throws in opposite directions, lying flat. The basic secure knot. Surgeon's knot: a double first throw, which holds while you lay the second; useful under tension. Granny knot: two throws in the same direction; it slips. If your knot does not lie flat, you have tied a granny. Slip (sliding) knot: throws run down one strand; useful to snug a tie deep in a hole, but must be \"locked\" with square throws on top."],["bsec","th:b-knots~s1-2","How many throws","Knots and wound closure · Knots","Braided sutures (Vicryl, silk) hold with three flat throws. Monofilaments (nylon, polypropylene, PDS) are slippery and need four to six. Lay each throw square, pull the strands in opposite directions in the line of the knot, and do not saw one strand over the other, which weakens it."],["bsec","th:b-knots~s1-3","Three ways to tie","Knots and wound closure · Knots","Practise all three Two-handed tie: the most secure and controllable; use for ties on vessels and anything important. One-handed tie: fast, and leaves the other hand free to hold tension; learn it with both hands. Instrument tie: wrap the long end around the needle holder, grab the short end, pull through; alternate the direction of each wrap. Economical with suture and the standard for skin."],["bsec","th:b-knots~s1-4","Ties deep in a wound","Knots and wound closure · Knots","For a vessel in the neck or tonsil fossa, pass the tie around the clip, lay the first throw, and push it down to the vessel with the tip of your index finger, keeping both strands in a straight line with the knot, so you do not pull the vessel out. Ask the assistant to release the clip slowly as the first throw tightens. Transfix (pass a needle through the vessel or pedicle) large vessels and pedicles so the tie cannot slip off. Do not lift the vessel Pulling up on a tie in a deep wound tears the vessel at its origin, which you then cannot see. Push the knot down; do not pull the vessel up."],["bsec","th:b-knots~s2-1","Principles of closure","Knots and wound closure · Stitches","Close in layers: deep tissue (platysma, galea, muscle) takes the tension; skin sutures only align the edges. No dead space: cavities collect blood and serum; close them or drain them. No tension: undermine if needed; a tight closure leaves a wide scar or necrosis. Slight eversion: edges turned slightly outwards flatten as the scar contracts; inverted edges leave a groove. Equal bites: the same depth and distance from the edge on both sides, so the edges meet at the same height. Figure 1 The stitches you will use most, in cross-section. Drawn for this app"],["bsec","th:b-knots~s2-2","The stitches","Knots and wound closure · Stitches","Stitches and when to use them Stitch Use Notes Simple interrupted Most skin wounds, mucosa Each stitch independent; adjust as you go Vertical mattress Thin or lax skin that inverts, tension Everts edges; can mark the skin if tight Horizontal mattress Fragile skin, haemostasis, flap edges Spreads tension; also for septal quilting and tonsil pillars Continuous (running) Long, straight, low-tension wounds, mucosa Fast; one break loosens the whole line Subcuticular Neck and face incisions No cross-hatching; usually absorbable Monocryl, or pull-out polypropylene Half-buried (corner) Points of flaps Protects the blood supply to the tip Subcuticular closure of a neck incision Close platysma with interrupted buried 3-0 or 4-0 Vicryl so the skin edges sit together without tension. Start at one end with a buried knot or an anchor outside the wound. Take horizontal bites in the dermis, alternating sides, each entering opposite where the last one left, slightly back. Keep every bite at the same depth so the edges stay level. Finish with a buried knot, then adhesive strips or glue. Senior's pearl The best stitch is the one that brings the edges together without tension at the same height. If the edges do not meet before you put the skin stitch in, the problem is in the deep layer, not the skin."],["bsec","th:b-knots~s3-1","Recognising trouble","Knots and wound closure · When it goes wrong","Stitch marks (\"railroad tracks\"): sutures tied too tight or left too long. Dehiscence: tension, infection, haematoma or poor tissue (radiotherapy, steroids, diabetes). Inverted edges and step deformities: unequal bites. Suture abscess or spitting stitch: buried braided sutures too close to the surface."],["ev","th:b-knots~s4-1","Knots and wound closure: current evidence","Knots and wound closure · Evidence","In a laboratory study of sutures used in the mouth, surgeon's knots were the most secure; square and surgeon's knots needed at least four throws and sliding knots at least five, with nothing gained after five (Silver et al., J Oral Maxillofac Surg 2016). A Cochrane review of 33 trials found sutures better than tissue adhesive at preventing wound breakdown (one extra breakdown for every 43 wounds closed with glue), with no difference in infection or appearance (Dumville et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2014)."],["bsec","th:b-knots~s5-1","Viva essentials","Knots and wound closure · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Square versus granny knots and the number of throws. Deep tie technique and transfixion. Principles of wound closure. When to use each stitch."],["bsec","th:b-knots~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Knots and wound closure · For the examination","Skills to sign off Tie square knots two-handed, one-handed (both hands) and with instruments Tie a secure deep tie on a vessel in a narrow space Close skin with simple interrupted and vertical mattress sutures Close a neck incision in layers with a subcuticular stitch Transfix a pedicle"],["bsec","th:b-knots~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Knots and wound closure · For the examination","Common pitfalls Granny knots that slip. Too few throws on monofilament. Pulling a vessel up to tie it. Closing skin under tension instead of fixing the deep layer."],["bsec","th:b-la~s1-1","How local anaesthetics work","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · The drugs","They block voltage-gated sodium channels from inside the nerve, stopping conduction; small, unmyelinated pain fibres are blocked first. They are weak bases and work less well in acidic, infected tissue. Adding adrenaline slows absorption, prolongs the block, raises the safe dose and reduces bleeding."],["bsec","th:b-la~s1-2","Maximum doses","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · The drugs","Maximum doses in adults (use lean body weight in obesity) Drug Plain With adrenaline Notes Lidocaine 3 mg/kg (maximum 200 mg) 7 mg/kg (maximum 500 mg) Fast onset, 1 to 2 hours Bupivacaine, levobupivacaine 2 mg/kg (maximum 150 mg) 2 mg/kg (maximum 150 mg) Slow onset, 4 to 8 hours; most cardiotoxic Prilocaine 6 mg/kg (maximum 400 mg) 8 mg/kg Methaemoglobinaemia at high doses Cocaine (topical) 1.5 mg/kg (maximum 100 mg) Vasoconstrictor and anaesthetic; avoid in ischaemic heart disease and with adrenaline-type drugs Converting percent to milligrams A 1% solution contains 1 g in 100 ml, which is 10 mg per ml . So 2% lidocaine is 20 mg/ml, and 0.5% bupivacaine is 5 mg/ml. Example: a 70 kg adult may have 7 x 70 = 490 mg of lidocaine with adrenaline, which is 49 ml of 1% or 24.5 ml of 2%. Adrenaline 1:200,000 is 5 micrograms per ml; 1:80,000 (dental cartridges) is 12.5 micrograms per ml. Doses of different local anaesthetics add together as fractions of their maximum. Children and the frail Calculate the dose in milligrams for every child before you draw up, and write it down. A small child's maximum dose is a few millilitres of 1% lidocaine. Reduce doses in the elderly, in liver and heart failure, and for injections into very vascular areas."],["bsec","th:b-la~s2-1","A painless injection","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · Technique","Warm the solution to body temperature and buffer lidocaine with bicarbonate (1 ml of 8.4% to 10 ml of 1% lidocaine) to reduce stinging. Use the finest needle (27 to 30 gauge), inject slowly, and start in the dermis-subcutaneous junction. In a wound, inject through the cut edge, not intact skin. Advance into tissue that is already numb; aspirate before injecting near vessels. Wait: adrenaline needs 7 to 10 minutes to blanch the field."],["bsec","th:b-la~s2-2","Blocks for the ear","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · Technique","Figure 1 The four sensory nerves of the ear and where to inject. Drawn for this app Infiltrate in front of the tragus and helical root (auriculotemporal nerve), behind the pinna (great auricular and lesser occipital nerves), and into the four quadrants of the canal at the bony-cartilaginous junction (including Arnold's branch of the vagus on the posterior wall). For the tympanic membrane alone, a canal injection or topical phenol-based or EMLA-type preparations are used."],["bsec","th:b-la~s2-3","The nose","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · Technique","Topical: cocaine paste or Moffett's solution (cocaine, adrenaline and bicarbonate), or co-phenylcaine spray (lidocaine with phenylephrine), on patties placed along the floor, under the middle turbinate (sphenopalatine area) and high anteriorly (anterior ethmoidal nerve). Infiltration: lidocaine with adrenaline into the septum, the columella, the lateral wall and the base of the nasal bones for fractures; an infraorbital block for the side of the nose and upper lip."],["bsec","th:b-la~s2-4","Face and neck","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · Technique","Supraorbital and supratrochlear blocks for the forehead and scalp. Infraorbital block (via the mouth or through the skin) for the cheek, side of the nose and upper lip; mental block for the lower lip and chin. Superficial cervical plexus block at the midpoint of the posterior border of sternomastoid for neck skin (used in thyroid and carotid surgery and awake tracheostomy)."],["bsec","th:b-la~s2-5","The airway","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · Technique","For awake fibreoptic intubation and office laryngeal procedures: topical lidocaine to the pharynx, superior laryngeal nerve blocks at the greater horn of the hyoid, and transtracheal lidocaine through the cricothyroid membrane, which makes the patient cough and spreads it above and below the cords."],["bsec","th:b-la~s3-1","Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · Toxicity","Early signs: tingling around the mouth, a metallic taste, tinnitus, light-headedness, slurred speech. Then agitation, twitching and seizures, and then arrhythmia and cardiovascular collapse. Bupivacaine can stop the heart without warning signs first, and toxicity can appear up to an hour after injection. Suspected local anaesthetic toxicity 1 Act stop injecting call for help airway, 100% oxygen IV access benzodiazepine or propofol for seizures 2 Lipid 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 ml/kg IV over 1 minute then infusion 15 ml/kg/hour repeat bolus twice at 5 minute intervals and double the infusion if unstable (maximum 12 ml/kg in total) 3 Arrest CPR (may be prolonged, over an hour) amiodarone for arrhythmias, not lidocaine consider bypass Senior's pearl Before you inject in a long case, say the total dose aloud to the anaesthetist; they may already have given lidocaine intravenously or in the airway spray, and the doses add up."],["ev","th:b-la~s4-1","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks: current evidence","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · Evidence","In 93 published cases of local anaesthetic toxicity over 30 years, 60% had the classic progression from nervous system symptoms to cardiac collapse, but the rest had delayed onset or cardiovascular signs alone, so toxicity can appear late and without warning (Di Gregorio et al., Reg Anesth Pain Med 2010). A review of later case reports and registries confirmed that a growing share of cases occur outside the operating theatre and many involve delayed presentation (Gitman et al., Reg Anesth Pain Med 2018). Lipid emulsion is now standard treatment in national guidance."],["bsec","th:b-la~s5-1","Viva essentials","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Maximum doses and the percent-to-milligram calculation. Mechanism and the effect of adrenaline. Ear and nose anaesthesia. Toxicity: signs and the lipid regimen. Cocaine in the nose: dose and cautions."],["bsec","th:b-la~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · For the examination","Skills to sign off Calculate maximum doses in mg and ml for an adult and a child Give a painless skin infiltration Anaesthetise the nose topically and by infiltration for a nasal fracture Perform a four-quadrant ear canal block and postauricular infiltration Perform infraorbital, mental and superficial cervical plexus blocks Lead the response to local anaesthetic toxicity"],["bsec","th:b-la~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Local anaesthesia and nerve blocks · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not calculating the dose for a child. Forgetting lidocaine already given by the anaesthetist. Not waiting for adrenaline to work. Missing late toxicity after a large block."],["bsec","th:b-list~s1-1","Building the list","Planning and running a list · Planning","Estimate time honestly for each case, including anaesthesia and turnover, from your own data, not from hope. Mix cases so the list fits the session and so a long case does not push day cases past the time they can go home. Book what each case needs: microscope, laser and laser-trained staff, nerve monitor, navigation, drill, Coblation, frozen section, blood, implants, interpreters, high-dependency or intensive care beds, and the right assistant. Confirm the patients are ready: pre-assessment done, anticoagulants stopped, tests reviewed, consent discussed."],["bsec","th:b-list~s1-2","Ordering the cases","Planning and running a list · Planning","Common principles for ordering a list Put first Why Children (and youngest first) Shorter fasting, less distress Patients with diabetes Shorter fasting, less disruption of insulin Latex allergy Latex-free room before any exposure Major cases needing beds or critical care Time to recover and to manage problems in working hours Day cases that need time to recover before discharge Enough time to go home the same day Put last Known infection (MRSA, active infection) Cleaning after the case Local practice varies; agree the order with the anaesthetist and the theatre team, and do not change it on the day without telling them."],["bsec","th:b-list~s2-1","Starting well","Planning and running a list · On the day","Arrive early, see every patient, confirm consent and marking, and lead the team brief with a realistic plan for the day. Make decisions about trainees early: which case each will do, and which parts."],["bsec","th:b-list~s2-2","Keeping time","Planning and running a list · On the day","Overlapping tasks: the next patient sent for while the current one is being closed, the junior closing while you consent the next. Avoid unnecessary delays: missing equipment, unchecked consent, unmarked sites, waiting for notes. If the list is going to overrun, decide early with the team which case to cancel and tell the patient yourself. Never start a major case that will clearly run into the night without a reason. Senior's pearl A list that finishes on time is not the one where everyone hurries; it is the one where nothing is missing at the start of each case. Planning saves more minutes than speed."],["bsec","th:b-list~s2-3","Supervising trainees","Planning and running a list · On the day","Match the case to the trainee's level, agree the parts they will do, give them the time to do it safely (a list with trainees runs slower, so plan for it), take over when patient safety needs it, and give feedback at the end. Leave the theatre only when you trust the trainee with what remains and you are immediately available."],["bsec","th:b-list~s3-1","The emergency patient","Planning and running a list · Emergencies","Book emergency cases according to clinical urgency categories: immediate (life-threatening: airway obstruction, bleeding), urgent (within hours: abscess, foreign body), and expedited (within days). Communicate with the emergency theatre coordinator, and do not let elective work delay a patient who needs an airway or bleeding controlled."],["ev","th:b-list~s4-1","Planning and running a list: current evidence","Planning and running a list · Evidence","A meta-analysis of 33 studies (306,635 patients) found about 18% of operations worldwide are cancelled on the intended day, mostly for avoidable reasons such as lack of theatre time or facilities, patients not attending, and changes in medical condition (Abate et al., Int J Surg Open 2020). A scoping review of 129 studies in otolaryngology identified ways to improve theatre efficiency, including standardised equipment and teams, local anaesthesia and laryngeal masks where suitable, procedures outside the main theatre, and better scheduling (Hathi et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2024)."],["bsec","th:b-list~s5-1","Viva essentials","Planning and running a list · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Order of a list and why. Preventing cancellations on the day. Supervising trainees safely. Urgency categories for emergency surgery."],["bsec","th:b-list~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Planning and running a list · For the examination","Skills to sign off Prepare a list: check patients, consent, marking and equipment Plan the order and timing of a mixed ENT list Run a list with a junior trainee and keep to time Decide on a cancellation early and speak to the patient"],["bsec","th:b-list~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Planning and running a list · For the examination","Common pitfalls Optimistic timings. Equipment not booked. Starting a long case late in the day without a reason. Not planning trainee cases."],["bsec","th:b-never~s1-1","The list","Never events and nerve monitoring · Never events","Never events relevant to ENT Event ENT examples Main safeguard Wrong-site surgery Wrong ear, wrong side of the nose or sinus, wrong parotid or thyroid lobe, wrong-side grommet, wrong patient Marking, consent, imaging and audiogram check, time out Wrong implant Wrong grommet, prosthesis length, cochlear implant side or model Implant check before opening and at time out Retained foreign object Throat pack, nasal pack or patty, neuro-patty, swab in the tonsil fossa or neck, guidewire Counts, visual and written reminders, sign out Wrong route of medication Intravenous drug given intrathecally or into an ear, topical drug injected Labelled syringes, read-back Misplaced nasogastric tube After head and neck surgery Confirm position by aspirate pH or X-ray before use"],["bsec","th:b-never~s1-2","Why they happen","Never events and nerve monitoring · Never events","Almost never through one person's ignorance; usually a chain of small failures: the list says left, the consent says right, the mark has washed off, the team is rushed, the junior did not feel able to speak, and the time out was skipped. Each safeguard is a slice of cheese with holes; harm happens when the holes line up. The bilateral operation that wasn't Grommets, sinus surgery and nasal operations are often bilateral, so a team gets used to not checking the side. When it is one side, say so at every check, and mark it."],["bsec","th:b-never~s1-3","Preventing them","Never events and nerve monitoring · Never events","Mark the site with the patient, before premedication, and check against the consent, letter and imaging. Display the imaging, and the audiogram for ear surgery, in theatre. Make the time out a real pause, with every person engaged. Count everything that can be left: patties are counted like swabs, with tails left outside the nose. Confirm removal of throat packs and nasal packs at sign out and before extubation. Never skip a check because the list is behind."],["bsec","th:b-never~s2-1","How it works","Never events and nerve monitoring · Nerve monitoring","Electrodes record muscle activity (electromyography): needle electrodes in the orbicularis oculi and oris for the facial nerve; surface electrodes on the endotracheal tube touching the vocal cords for the recurrent laryngeal nerve. A stimulating probe tells you whether a structure is the nerve, and an alarm warns of mechanical irritation."],["bsec","th:b-never~s2-2","Using it well","Never events and nerve monitoring · Nerve monitoring","Tell the anaesthetist: no long-acting muscle relaxant after induction. Check the system works at the start (tap the face; stimulate the vagus nerve before and after thyroid dissection). Use the lowest current that identifies the nerve (often 0.5 to 1 mA for confirmation, 2 to 3 mA for searching). In thyroid surgery, loss of signal on the first side should make you consider stopping and doing the second side later, to avoid bilateral palsy. Senior's pearl The monitor helps you find the nerve and warns you, but it cannot see. A silent monitor does not prove the nerve is safe, and an alarm does not prove it is damaged. Anatomy comes first."],["bsec","th:b-never~s2-3","Where it is used","Never events and nerve monitoring · Nerve monitoring","Parotid and revision ear surgery, facial nerve surgery, acoustic neuroma and skull base surgery (facial nerve); thyroid and parathyroid surgery (recurrent laryngeal nerve, vagus); neck dissection (accessory nerve, in some units)."],["ev","th:b-never~s3-1","Never events and nerve monitoring: current evidence","Never events and nerve monitoring · Evidence","A systematic review of surgical never events in the USA estimated wrong-site surgery at about 0.09 and retained items at about 1.32 per 10,000 operations, with inadequate communication the commonest root cause (Hempel et al., JAMA Surg 2015). In primary parotidectomy, facial nerve monitoring reduced immediate post-operative weakness (22.5% vs 34.9%; one case prevented for every 9 monitored) but not permanent weakness (Sood et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2015). In 9,203 thyroidectomies, monitoring reduced overall and transient recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy, without a significant difference in permanent palsy (Yang et al., Int J Surg 2017)."],["bsec","th:b-never~s4-1","Viva essentials","Never events and nerve monitoring · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Never events relevant to ENT and how to prevent them. The Swiss cheese model. Nerve monitoring: how it works and its limits. Loss of signal in thyroid surgery and staged surgery."],["bsec","th:b-never~s4-2","Skills to sign off","Never events and nerve monitoring · For the examination","Skills to sign off Check side against consent, letter and imaging for every case Confirm removal of all packs and patties at sign out Set up and test facial nerve monitoring Use recurrent laryngeal nerve monitoring with vagal stimulation in thyroid surgery Decide on staging after loss of signal"],["bsec","th:b-never~s4-3","Common pitfalls","Never events and nerve monitoring · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not checking the side in \"usually bilateral\" operations. Patties without tails or counts. A long-acting relaxant during nerve monitoring. Trusting a silent monitor over your eyes."],["bsec","th:b-opnote~s1-1","Required content","Operation notes and documentation · The operation note","The contents of an operation note Item Example Patient identity, date and time Name, hospital number, date of birth Elective or emergency Names Operating surgeon, assistants, anaesthetist; who supervised Operation performed Right modified radical mastoidectomy and type III tympanoplasty Indication and diagnosis Right attic cholesteatoma Anaesthetic General, local, or both; local anaesthetic agent and dose Position, preparation and antibiotics Including timing of prophylaxis Incision Postauricular Findings Cholesteatoma in attic and antrum; eroded incus; dehiscent tympanic facial nerve; dura intact Procedure Each step, in order, including what was removed, added or altered Complications and problems Bleeding from emissary vein controlled with wax; anything unexpected and how it was dealt with Implants and grafts Type, size, serial and lot numbers (stickers) Closure Layers, sutures, drains, packs, dressings Blood loss Estimated Specimens What was sent, where Post-operative instructions Observations, position, drains and packs, antibiotics, VTE prophylaxis, diet, specific checks, follow-up Signature Name, grade, registration number Senior's pearl Write the findings as if the next surgeon will open the ear in ten years with only your note: is the facial nerve dehiscent, where is the dura low, which ossicles were left, what is the state of the stapes? These details change their operation."],["bsec","th:b-opnote~s1-2","Writing it well","Operation notes and documentation · The operation note","Write it straight after the operation, before the next case. Be factual and specific; avoid \"uneventful\" and \"routine\" instead of findings. Record any deviation from the plan and the reason (for example, a planned partial thyroidectomy converted to total). Draw a diagram if it helps (sinus findings, a tumour map). Never alter a note later; add a dated, signed addendum. Complications go in the note If something went wrong, record it accurately and what you did about it. An honest note is your protection; an incomplete or altered one is not."],["bsec","th:b-opnote~s2-1","Clinic letters","Operation notes and documentation · Other documents","Many units now write directly to the patient, copied to the family doctor, in plain language: what was found, what it means, what was agreed and what happens next. Avoid abbreviations and jargon, and put actions for others in clear bullet points."],["bsec","th:b-opnote~s2-2","Discharge summaries","Operation notes and documentation · Other documents","Diagnosis, operation, complications, medicines (new, changed and stopped, including the duration of antibiotics and anticoagulants), follow-up, pending results, and what the family doctor should do."],["bsec","th:b-opnote~s2-3","Recording difficult conversations","Operation notes and documentation · Other documents","Document breaking bad news, consent discussions, refusals of treatment and complaints with the date, who was present, what was said (including risks and options), and the patient's understanding and decision."],["ev","th:b-opnote~s3-1","Operation notes and documentation: current evidence","Operation notes and documentation · Evidence","A review of 1,092 operation notes in nine hospitals found operation titles and surgeons' names almost always recorded, but the diagnosis was missing in about a quarter, the findings in a fifth, and instructions on post-operative antibiotics and VTE prophylaxis in about half; typed proformas improved completeness (Severn Audit and Research Collaborative in Orthopaedics, Patient Saf Surg 2016)."],["bsec","th:b-opnote~s4-1","Viva essentials","Operation notes and documentation · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Contents of an operation note. Recording complications and implants. Corrections and addenda. Writing to patients."],["bsec","th:b-opnote~s4-2","Skills to sign off","Operation notes and documentation · For the examination","Skills to sign off Write a complete operation note straight after every case Write a discharge summary with clear medicine changes Write a plain-language clinic letter to a patient Document a consent discussion and a complication honestly"],["bsec","th:b-opnote~s4-3","Common pitfalls","Operation notes and documentation · For the examination","Common pitfalls \"Uneventful procedure\" instead of findings. No post-operative instructions. Missing implant details. Writing the note at the end of the list from memory."],["bsec","th:b-optics~s1-1","The parts","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · The operating microscope","Objective lens: sets the working distance (the distance from the lens to the field): usually 200 to 250 mm for the ear, 400 mm for the larynx, leaving room for instruments. Eyepieces: usually 10x or 12.5x, each with a dioptre setting for your eyes. Magnification changer: steps or zoom, typically from about 4x to 40x total. Light: coaxial halogen, xenon or LED, with a fibre-optic cable. Arm and brakes, often with a balance setting and a foot or handle control; a camera and screen for the team. Figure 1 An operating microscope in use. Sit upright with the eyepieces brought to you, not your neck bent to them. U.S. Navy photo by Mass Communication Specialist 3rd Class Joshua Valcarcel, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"],["bsec","th:b-optics~s1-2","Setting it up","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · The operating microscope","Set-up before scrubbing Choose the objective for the operation (250 mm for ear, 400 mm for larynx) and fit a sterile drape if you will handle it scrubbed. Set both eyepieces to zero dioptres, then set the interpupillary distance until you see a single round image. Focus on a flat object at the highest magnification, then drop to the lowest magnification and, without moving the focus, adjust each eyepiece's dioptre until it is sharp. Balance the head so it stays where you leave it with the brakes loose. Adjust the chair and the eyepiece angle so you sit upright with your forearms supported. Senior's pearl Start every case at low magnification to orientate, increase it for fine work, and come back down when you lose your bearings. Most beginners work too high, lose depth of field, and spend their time refocusing."],["bsec","th:b-optics~s2-1","Rigid endoscopes","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · Endoscopes","Modern rigid endoscopes use the Hopkins rod-lens system: glass rods with air gaps as lenses, giving a bright, wide, sharp image. Adult nasal scopes are usually 4 mm, paediatric and ear scopes 2.7 to 3 mm. The angle of view (0, 30, 45 or 70 degrees) tells you where the scope looks relative to its shaft. Figure 2 The angle of the lens decides where the scope looks. Drawn for this app"],["bsec","th:b-optics~s2-2","Setting up the stack","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · Endoscopes","Connect the light cable and camera head; focus the camera on gauze. White balance on white gauze before every case, or colours (especially the red of bleeding and the white of fat) will be wrong. Prevent fogging with warm saline or anti-fog solution, and clean the lens with a moist swab. Rotate the light post to change where an angled scope looks without moving the camera's horizon. The light source burns Xenon and halogen light sources make the scope tip and cable end hot enough to burn skin and set drapes alight. Put the light on standby whenever the scope is out of the patient, and never lay the tip or cable on the drapes."],["bsec","th:b-optics~s2-3","Handling the endoscope","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · Endoscopes","Hold the scope in the non-dominant hand with the camera held so the screen horizon is level, rest the hand on the patient's face or nose, and slide along the septum or floor without touching the mucosa. Instruments go in beside the scope, not across it. Keep the tip clean, step back when the view is lost, and never advance blind."],["bsec","th:b-optics~s3-1","The drill and its burrs","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · The drill","High-speed electric or pneumatic drills run at up to about 80,000 revolutions a minute. Cutting burrs (fluted) remove bone quickly; diamond burrs (coated) polish and are used near the facial nerve, dura, sigmoid sinus and inner ear, where their slower bite and heat-sealing of bone bleeding are an advantage. Figure 3 Choose the burr for the bone and the neighbourhood; stroke parallel to important structures. Drawn for this app Safe drilling Hold the handpiece like a pen, with the hand supported. Use the largest burr that fits: small burrs dig holes and slip. Move in long, even strokes using the side of the burr, not the tip, parallel to the structures you must protect. Irrigate constantly with suction to cool the bone and keep the view clear. Let the burr cut; do not press. Stop the drill before taking it out of the wound. Check the direction of rotation: the burr can run along a surface and jump if it rolls the wrong way. Heat and noise A dry diamond burr can heat the facial nerve enough to injure it. The drill also transmits noise through the skull to both ears, and touching the ossicular chain with a running burr can cause sensorineural hearing loss."],["bsec","th:b-optics~s4-1","Protecting yourself","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · Your body","Neck and shoulder pain is common among ENT surgeons and usually starts in training. Sit or stand upright, bring the microscope and screen to your eye level, keep the screen straight ahead, support your forearms, change position between cases, and use foot pedals without leaning."],["ev","th:b-optics~s5-1","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill: current evidence","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · Evidence","In a national survey of otolaryngologists, 63.9% reported work-related musculoskeletal symptoms, most often in the neck and shoulders and usually beginning in residency; only a third had been taught ergonomics, and 69.6% of those who applied ergonomic principles improved (Ho et al., Laryngoscope 2018)."],["bsec","th:b-optics~s6-1","Viva essentials","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Microscope parts, working distance and set-up. Endoscope angles and their uses. Cutting versus diamond burrs and safe drilling. Risks: light source burns, thermal nerve injury, drill noise."],["bsec","th:b-optics~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · For the examination","Skills to sign off Set up the microscope: objective, interpupillary distance, dioptres, balance Set up the endoscopy stack with white balance and anti-fog Hold a 0 and 30 degree scope and examine the nose without touching the mucosa Drill a cortical mastoidectomy on a temporal bone Use angled scopes for frontal recess and maxillary work"],["bsec","th:b-optics~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Microscope, endoscopes and the drill · For the examination","Common pitfalls Dioptres not set: blurred, tiring vision. No white balance before endoscopy. A hot light cable on the drapes. Drilling towards a nerve with a small cutting burr."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s1-1","The child with a cold","The child in theatre · Before the operation","Children often arrive with a runny nose. A mild upper respiratory infection without fever, cough or chest signs is usually not a reason to cancel short procedures such as grommets. Postpone (usually 2 to 4 weeks) if there is fever, a productive cough, wheeze or chest signs, a recent lower respiratory infection, or if the child is unwell. Airway operations and sleep apnoea surgery need a lower threshold. Make the decision with the anaesthetist."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s1-2","What to check","The child in theatre · Before the operation","Weight on the day, written on the chart: every drug and fluid depends on it. Loose teeth (ages about 6 to 12): note them, warn the family, and remove a very loose tooth under anaesthesia to stop it being inhaled. Bleeding history before tonsillectomy (family history, easy bruising). Sleep apnoea severity , syndromes (Down syndrome, craniofacial), obesity, prematurity and age, which decide whether the child can go home the same day. Safeguarding: bruises, inconsistent stories, or a parent who does not have parental responsibility."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s1-3","Fasting","The child in theatre · Before the operation","Solid food 6 hours, breast milk 4 hours, clear fluids until 1 hour before anaesthesia. Encourage clear fluids up to that point; children fasted for too long arrive dehydrated and distressed."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s2-1","Blood volume","The child in theatre · Numbers","About 80 ml/kg in children (about 85 to 90 ml/kg in neonates). A 20 kg child has about 1,600 ml; a loss of 160 ml is 10% of their blood. Children compensate well and then decompensate suddenly: a rising heart rate is the early sign, and low blood pressure is a late one."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s2-2","Fluids","The child in theatre · Numbers","Maintenance by the 4-2-1 rule: 4 ml/kg/hour for the first 10 kg, 2 ml/kg/hour for the next 10 kg, and 1 ml/kg/hour for each kg above 20 kg. Use isotonic fluids (such as Hartmann's or 0.9% saline with glucose as needed); hypotonic fluids after tonsillectomy, combined with the stress response, have caused fatal hyponatraemia. A bolus for shock is 10 ml/kg of isotonic crystalloid, repeated as needed."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s2-3","Analgesia","The child in theatre · Numbers","Common doses Drug Dose Notes Paracetamol 15 mg/kg every 4 to 6 hours Maximum 60 to 75 mg/kg/day depending on age and route Ibuprofen 10 mg/kg every 6 to 8 hours Maximum 30 mg/kg/day; widely used after tonsillectomy Dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg once (maximum 8 to 10 mg) Reduces vomiting and swelling after tonsillectomy Morphine By weight, in hospital only For severe pain; caution in sleep apnoea Codeine Do not use Contraindicated under 12, and after tonsillectomy or adenoidectomy for sleep apnoea under 18 Codeine and children Codeine is converted to morphine in the liver at very different rates. Ultra-rapid metabolisers have died after tonsillectomy from respiratory depression. Use paracetamol and ibuprofen, with short courses of oral morphine in hospital only when needed."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s3-1","Consent and the family","The child in theatre · On the day","Consent comes from someone with parental responsibility, and from the child too when they understand (in the UK, a child under 16 may consent if they have sufficient understanding, known as Gillick competence). Explain what will happen in words the child understands. Most units let a parent stay for induction, and play specialists help a frightened child."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s3-2","The children's theatre","The child in theatre · On the day","Equipment sized for children: tubes, laryngoscopes, bronchoscopes, tracheostomy tubes. Warmers and blankets: small children lose heat quickly. Smaller doses, smaller syringes, and a weight-based drug chart for emergencies. Separate recovery space with parents brought in early."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s4-1","Day case or overnight","The child in theatre · After the operation","Most children go home the same day. Keep overnight (with monitoring) children under about 3 years having tonsillectomy, those with severe sleep apnoea, significant comorbidities (Down syndrome, craniofacial syndromes, neuromuscular or cardiac disease, obesity), bleeding disorders, those who live far from hospital, and any child with airway concerns. Children with severe sleep apnoea may need a high-dependency bed. Senior's pearl The child who is quiet and sleepy in recovery after tonsillectomy for sleep apnoea may be obstructing or narcotised, not comfortable. Watch saturations, not just the pain score."],["ev","th:b-paeds~s5-1","The child in theatre: current evidence","The child in theatre · Evidence","In 9,297 children having anaesthesia, a positive respiratory history (night cough, wheeze on exercise, recurrent wheeze or eczema) greatly increased the risk of bronchospasm (relative risk 8.46) and other respiratory adverse events, which can be anticipated at pre-operative assessment (von Ungern-Sternberg et al., Lancet 2010). A meta-analysis found a small increase in bleeding after tonsillectomy with ibuprofen (odds ratio 1.38) (Stokes et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019), while the American tonsillectomy guideline still recommends ibuprofen and paracetamol as first-line analgesia and a single dose of dexamethasone (Mitchell et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019)."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s6-1","Viva essentials","The child in theatre · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The child with a cold: proceed or postpone. Blood volume and fluids , including hyponatraemia after tonsillectomy. Analgesia doses and why codeine is banned. Who stays overnight after tonsillectomy. Consent in children."],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s6-2","Skills to sign off","The child in theatre · For the examination","Skills to sign off Assess a child on the morning of surgery, including teeth and respiratory symptoms Calculate blood volume, maintenance fluids and analgesia by weight Decide day case or overnight stay after tonsillectomy Take consent from parents and involve the child"],["bsec","th:b-paeds~s6-3","Common pitfalls","The child in theatre · For the examination","Common pitfalls Not weighing the child on the day. Hypotonic fluids after tonsillectomy. Prescribing codeine. Sending home a young child with severe sleep apnoea."],["bsec","th:b-position~s1-1","The aims","Positioning the ENT patient · Principles","Access: the field square to the surgeon's line of sight and within easy reach. Less bleeding: the head above the heart lowers venous pressure in the field. A safe airway: the tube secure and visible, with no kinking when the head turns. No injury: eyes protected, nerves and pressure points padded, joints within their normal range."],["bsec","th:b-position~s1-2","Who does what","Positioning the ENT patient · Principles","The anaesthetic team controls the head and airway during moves; the surgeon decides the final position and checks it before draping; everyone checks pressure points. Move the patient with enough hands and a slide sheet, and say \"ready, steady, move\" so all move together. Figure 1 The three positions used most often in ENT. Drawn for this app"],["bsec","th:b-position~s2-1","Neck extended with a shoulder roll","Positioning the ENT patient · The positions","For thyroid, parathyroid, tracheostomy, laryngectomy and most neck operations. A sandbag or roll under the shoulders and a head ring let the neck extend and bring the trachea forward. Extend only as far as is comfortable; over-extension strains the cervical spine and can cause post-operative neck pain. The unstable neck Rheumatoid arthritis, Down syndrome (atlantoaxial instability), ankylosing spondylitis, previous cervical fusion and trauma all limit how far the neck can be moved safely. Ask, examine the range of movement while the patient is awake, and position within it."],["bsec","th:b-position~s2-2","Head up (reverse Trendelenburg)","Positioning the ENT patient · The positions","For endoscopic sinus surgery, skull base surgery and thyroid surgery. Tilting the whole table 15 to 20 degrees head up reduces venous pressure in the field and improves the endoscopic view. The head is often turned slightly towards the surgeon. Watch blood pressure: tilting reduces venous return and can drop it."],["bsec","th:b-position~s2-3","Head turned for ear and parotid surgery","Positioning the ENT patient · The positions","Turn the head away from the operated side on a head ring, ideally with the body slightly tilted or a wedge under the shoulder so that the neck itself is not twisted to its limit. Check the neck's comfortable range of rotation before induction. The ear should be horizontal and uppermost, with the surgeon seated and the microscope easily brought in."],["bsec","th:b-position~s2-4","Tonsils, adenoids and the mouth","Positioning the ENT patient · The positions","Supine with a shoulder roll and the neck extended (the Rose position), and a mouth gag (Boyle-Davis) inserted with the tongue blade in the midline, opened gently and suspended on Draffin rods or a stand. Check that the gag has not kinked or dislodged the tube, that the lips and teeth are clear of the blade, and that the eyes are protected. Figure 2 A Boyle-Davis mouth gag. Check the tube under the tongue blade every time it is opened or moved. Netha Hussain, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons"],["bsec","th:b-position~s2-5","Microlaryngoscopy and rigid endoscopy","Positioning the ENT patient · The positions","Supine with the head on a ring, the neck flexed on the chest and the head extended at the atlanto-occipital joint (the \"sniffing\" position) to line up the mouth, pharynx and larynx. A tooth guard protects the upper teeth. Suspend the laryngoscope on a chest support or table bracket, never on the chest wall directly in a small child."],["bsec","th:b-position~s2-6","Arms, eyes and pressure points","Positioning the ENT patient · The positions","Arms: tucked at the sides with the palms facing the body, or on arm boards abducted less than 90 degrees to protect the brachial plexus; pad the elbows to protect the ulnar nerve. Eyes: taped closed, padded if out of the field, and never pressed on by the surgeon's hands or instruments. Pressure points: occiput, sacrum, heels and elbows; use gel pads for long cases. Legs: uncrossed; calf compression devices for VTE prophylaxis in adults. Senior's pearl Before the drapes go on, look at the patient from the foot of the table: is the head straight, is the tube free, are the eyes covered, are the arms in a safe position? Once draped, nobody sees these again until the end."],["bsec","th:b-position~s3-1","Injuries from positioning","Positioning the ENT patient · When it goes wrong","Positioning injuries Injury Cause Prevention Corneal abrasion Open eye, drape or hand pressure, chlorhexidine Tape, lubricate, pad; aqueous preparation near the eye Ulnar neuropathy Pressure at the elbow, pronated forearm Pad the elbow, supinate or neutral forearm Brachial plexus injury Arm abducted over 90 degrees, head turned away Limit abduction, avoid combined extremes Neck pain, cervical injury Over-extension or rotation Stay within the awake range Pressure sores Long operations, thin patients Gel pads, check heels and occiput Dental injury Gags, laryngoscopes Tooth guard; note loose or crowned teeth before Blood pressure falls after head-up tilt 1 Act tell the anaesthetist reduce the tilt fluids or vasopressor as needed"],["ev","th:b-position~s4-1","Positioning the ENT patient: current evidence","Positioning the ENT patient · Evidence","In a randomised trial of endoscopic sinus surgery, a 15 degree head-up position gave a better surgical field and less blood loss than the flat position (Hathorn et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2013). Another trial compared 5, 10 and 20 degrees and found the best field and lowest blood loss at 20 degrees (Gan et al., Int Forum Allergy Rhinol 2014)."],["bsec","th:b-position~s5-1","Viva essentials","Positioning the ENT patient · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Position for each common operation and why. Head-up tilt and bleeding in sinus surgery. Patients at risk: rheumatoid arthritis, Down syndrome, cervical disease. Nerve and eye injuries and how to prevent them."],["bsec","th:b-position~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Positioning the ENT patient · For the examination","Skills to sign off Position a patient for tonsillectomy and insert a mouth gag safely Position for ear, sinus and neck surgery Check eyes, tube, arms and pressure points before draping Assess neck movement and position a patient with an unstable neck Set up suspension laryngoscopy"],["bsec","th:b-position~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Positioning the ENT patient · For the examination","Common pitfalls Turning the head beyond its awake range. A gag that kinks the tube. Arms abducted beyond 90 degrees. Forgetting the eyes once the drapes are on."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s1-1","The post-operative plan","Post-operative care · Instructions","Write it in the operation note: observations (frequency and what to escalate), position (head up 30 degrees after neck surgery), drains and packs (when to remove), diet and fluids, analgesia and antiemetics, antibiotics and VTE prophylaxis, specific checks (voice, calcium, facial movement, flap observations, tracheostomy care), and follow-up."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s1-2","The early warning score","Post-operative care · Instructions","NEWS2 scores breathing rate, oxygen saturation, oxygen use, blood pressure, pulse, consciousness or new confusion, and temperature. A total of 5 or more, or 3 in any single parameter, needs urgent medical review; 7 or more needs an emergency response, usually with critical care. A rising score is often the first sign of bleeding, sepsis or airway trouble."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s2-1","Analgesia","Post-operative care · Comfort","Multimodal: regular paracetamol plus an NSAID (ibuprofen) unless contraindicated, local anaesthetic infiltration at the end of the operation, and opioids only for breakthrough pain. Sleep apnoea: opioids worsen obstruction; use the lowest dose, monitor saturations, and prefer non-opioid analgesia. Tonsillectomy pain peaks around days 5 to 7 and is often referred to the ears; give regular analgesia for a week or more and advise eating normally."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s2-2","Nausea and vomiting","Post-operative care · Comfort","Risk factors (Apfel): female sex, non-smoker, previous PONV or motion sickness, and post-operative opioids; ENT and especially middle ear and tonsil surgery add risk. Give prophylaxis according to risk: dexamethasone at induction and ondansetron at the end are common; add cyclizine or another class for high-risk patients. Vomiting after tonsillectomy can cause bleeding; after neck surgery, a haematoma."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s2-3","Fluids","Post-operative care · Comfort","Encourage eating and drinking early. If intravenous fluid is needed for maintenance in adults: about 25 to 30 ml/kg/day of water, with about 1 mmol/kg/day of sodium, potassium and chloride and 50 to 100 g/day of glucose. Check electrolytes daily while on intravenous fluids, and watch for hyponatraemia, especially in children and the elderly."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s3-1","The checks you must not miss","Post-operative care · Operation-specific checks","Post-operative checks by operation Operation Check Why Thyroid and parathyroid Neck swelling, stridor, voice; calcium or parathyroid hormone Haematoma, nerve palsy, hypocalcaemia Parotid Facial movement in each branch Facial nerve injury Neck dissection Drain volume and character, shoulder movement Haematoma, chyle leak, accessory nerve Free flap Colour, capillary refill, temperature, Doppler signal, every hour at first Failing flap, salvageable if found early Tracheostomy Humidification, suction, inner tube, tapes, spare tubes and obturator at the bedside Blocked or displaced tube Tonsillectomy Bleeding, swallowing, drinking Primary haemorrhage, dehydration Sinus surgery Vision, eye movement, proptosis, clear watery discharge Orbital haematoma, CSF leak Ear surgery Facial movement, vertigo, nystagmus, hearing (tuning fork) Facial nerve or inner ear injury Laryngectomy Flap, drain, feeding, stoma, alcohol withdrawal Fistula, delirium The neck haematoma After thyroid, parathyroid or neck surgery, increasing neck swelling, pain, difficulty breathing or swallowing, or stridor means an expanding haematoma until proven otherwise. Call for help, open the wound at the bedside (skin, subcutaneous tissue and strap muscles) to release the pressure, and return to theatre. Do not wait for imaging."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s3-2","The deteriorating patient","Post-operative care · Operation-specific checks","Assess with ABCDE, give oxygen, check the observations and NEWS2, look at the wound, drains and airway, send bloods (including a gas and lactate), and escalate early with an SBAR handover. Do not leave an unwell post-operative patient without a plan and a review time. Senior's pearl On the evening ward round after a big list, see every patient, not just the notes. Look at the neck, the flap, the tracheostomy and the drain bottle yourself."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s4-1","Discharge criteria","Post-operative care · Going home","Eating and drinking, pain controlled on oral analgesia, observations normal, wound and drains checked, specific checks done (calcium after total thyroidectomy, voice, facial nerve), and written advice: what is normal, what to watch for (bleeding, fever, breathing problems), who to call, and when to come back."],["ev","th:b-postop~s5-1","Post-operative care: current evidence","Post-operative care · Evidence","A meta-analysis of 18 studies (2,630 patients) found enhanced recovery protocols for head and neck free flap surgery shortened hospital stay by about 4 days and reduced readmissions (odds ratio 0.64) and wound complications (relative risk 0.41), without more reoperations or deaths (Chorath et al., Oral Oncol 2021). The ERAS Society recommendations for major head and neck cancer surgery include carbohydrate loading, multimodal analgesia, early mobilisation, early feeding and avoiding unnecessary drains and lines (Dort et al., JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017). International consensus guidelines recommend risk-based PONV prophylaxis using drugs from different classes (Gan et al., Anesth Analg 2020)."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s6-1","Viva essentials","Post-operative care · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Post-operative instructions for common ENT operations. NEWS2 and escalation. Analgesia and PONV , including sleep apnoea. Neck haematoma: recognition and bedside management. Operation-specific checks."],["bsec","th:b-postop~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Post-operative care · For the examination","Skills to sign off Write clear post-operative instructions in the operation note Prescribe multimodal analgesia, antiemetics and maintenance fluids Recognise and escalate a deteriorating patient with NEWS2 and SBAR Open a neck wound at the bedside for an expanding haematoma Monitor a free flap and recognise failure"],["bsec","th:b-postop~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Post-operative care · For the examination","Common pitfalls Heavy opioids in sleep apnoea. Waiting for imaging in a neck haematoma. Forgetting calcium after total thyroidectomy. Reviewing notes instead of patients."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s1-1","What to cover","Pre-operative assessment · The assessment","The ENT problem: confirm the diagnosis, the side, the indication and the plan; check imaging, audiograms and biopsies are available. Cardiorespiratory: exercise tolerance (can they climb two flights of stairs?), angina, heart failure, valve disease, pacemakers, asthma, COPD. Airway: previous difficult intubation, mouth opening, neck movement, stridor, tumours of the larynx or pharynx; tell the anaesthetist early. Sleep apnoea: STOP-BANG questions (snoring, tiredness, observed apnoea, high blood pressure, BMI over 35, age over 50, neck over 40 cm, male); a score of 5 or more suggests high risk. Bleeding: personal and family history of bleeding, anticoagulants, antiplatelets, supplements. Medicines and allergies: including latex, chlorhexidine, iodine and previous anaesthetic reactions (malignant hyperthermia, suxamethonium apnoea in the family). Social: smoking, alcohol, who is at home, transport after day surgery."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s1-2","ASA grade","Pre-operative assessment · The assessment","ASA physical status Grade Description Example ASA 1 Normal healthy patient Fit adult for septoplasty ASA 2 Mild systemic disease Controlled hypertension or asthma, smoker, BMI 30 to 40 ASA 3 Severe systemic disease Poorly controlled diabetes, COPD, BMI over 40, stable angina ASA 4 Severe disease that is a constant threat to life Recent heart attack, severe heart failure ASA 5 Moribund, not expected to survive without the operation Ruptured aneurysm ASA 6 Brain-dead organ donor Add \"E\" for an emergency operation."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s2-1","Routine tests by risk","Pre-operative assessment · Tests","NICE advises tests according to the ASA grade and the size of the operation, not by habit. A simplified guide (adults) Operation ASA 1 ASA 2 ASA 3 or 4 Minor (skin lesion excision, nasal cautery) None None Consider kidney function if at risk of kidney injury Intermediate (tonsillectomy, septoplasty, sinus surgery) None Consider kidney function if at risk Blood count and kidney function; ECG if cardiac, renal or diabetic disease Major or complex (thyroidectomy, neck dissection, laryngectomy, free flaps) Blood count; consider kidney function and ECG over 65 Blood count, kidney function, ECG Blood count, kidney function, ECG; consider clotting and lung function Group and save or crossmatch for major head and neck surgery, according to local blood loss policies. HbA1c for people with diabetes if not done in the last 3 months. Pregnancy test on the day for women who could be pregnant. Sickle cell test when the history suggests it and status is unknown. Clotting tests only when there is a history of bleeding, liver disease or anticoagulation."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s3-1","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets","Pre-operative assessment · Medicines","Common plans (always weigh the bleeding risk of the operation against the reason for the drug) Drug Usual plan before elective surgery with significant bleeding risk Warfarin Stop 5 days before; INR checked the day before (under 1.5); bridge with low molecular weight heparin only for high thrombotic risk (mechanical mitral valve, VTE in the last 3 months) Apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban Stop 2 days before (longer in kidney impairment); no bridging Dabigatran Stop 2 to 4 days before depending on kidney function; no bridging Aspirin Usually continued; stop 7 days before only where bleeding would be disastrous and the indication allows Clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor Stop 5 to 7 days before; never stop within the first months after a coronary stent without cardiology advice The recent stent Stopping dual antiplatelet therapy early after a coronary stent can cause stent thrombosis and a heart attack. Defer elective surgery or discuss with the cardiologist; never stop it on your own."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s3-2","Diabetes","Pre-operative assessment · Medicines","Aim for HbA1c under about 69 mmol/mol (8.5%) before elective surgery; refer for optimisation if higher. Put people with diabetes first on the list to shorten fasting. SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin): omit the day before and the day of surgery because of the risk of ketoacidosis with normal glucose. Insulin: usually continue long-acting insulin at about 80% of the dose; omit short-acting while fasting; use a variable-rate insulin infusion if more than one meal will be missed or control is poor. Most other oral drugs are omitted on the morning of surgery (metformin is often continued if only one meal is missed)."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s3-3","Other medicines","Pre-operative assessment · Medicines","Continue most cardiac drugs, inhalers and antiepileptics on the morning of surgery. Patients on long-term steroids need cover. Stop combined oral contraceptives and HRT about 4 weeks before major surgery only if VTE risk is high, otherwise use prophylaxis."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s3-4","Fasting","Pre-operative assessment · Medicines","Adults: no food for 6 hours, clear fluids allowed until 2 hours before. Children: food 6 hours, breast milk 4 hours, clear fluids until 1 hour before (current UK and European paediatric guidance). Prolonged fasting makes children and the elderly dehydrated and unwell."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s4-1","Before major head and neck surgery","Pre-operative assessment · Optimising","Stop smoking and reduce alcohol (withdrawal after a laryngectomy is dangerous), correct anaemia (iron), improve nutrition (dietitian, feeding tube if needed), treat dental disease before radiotherapy, assess frailty and swallowing, and plan for post-operative critical care. Prehabilitation (exercise and nutrition) improves fitness before major surgery. Senior's pearl The best time to find a problem is the clinic where you list the patient, not the morning of surgery. Book the anaesthetic assessment, the sleep study or the cardiology opinion when you decide to operate."],["ev","th:b-preop~s5-1","Pre-operative assessment: current evidence","Pre-operative assessment · Evidence","In the BRIDGE trial of 1,884 patients with atrial fibrillation whose warfarin was stopped for surgery, no bridging was as safe as bridging for arterial thromboembolism (0.4% vs 0.3%) and caused much less major bleeding (1.3% vs 3.2%) (Douketis et al., N Engl J Med 2015). In the PAUSE study of 3,007 patients on direct oral anticoagulants, a simple standard interruption plan without bridging or blood tests gave low rates of major bleeding (about 1 to 2%) and stroke (under 1%) (Douketis et al., JAMA Intern Med 2019)."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s6-1","Viva essentials","Pre-operative assessment · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for ASA grades. Which tests for which patient. Anticoagulant and antiplatelet plans, and the recent stent. Diabetes management, including SGLT2 inhibitors. Sleep apnoea screening and fasting times."],["bsec","th:b-preop~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Pre-operative assessment · For the examination","Skills to sign off Take a focused pre-operative history and assign an ASA grade Request only the tests NICE recommends Plan anticoagulant and antiplatelet management for an ENT operation Plan diabetes management for a day case and a major case Optimise a patient for major head and neck surgery"],["bsec","th:b-preop~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Pre-operative assessment · For the examination","Common pitfalls Routine blood tests in fit patients for minor surgery. Stopping antiplatelets after a recent stent. Forgetting SGLT2 inhibitors. Missing sleep apnoea or a difficult airway until the day."],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s1-1","Wound classes","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · Infection","Wound classification Class Definition ENT examples Typical infection risk Clean No mucosa of the aerodigestive tract opened, no inflammation Thyroidectomy, parotidectomy, neck lump excision, otoplasty Low (under 2%) Clean-contaminated Mucosa of the mouth, pharynx, larynx or sinuses opened under controlled conditions Laryngectomy, glossectomy with neck dissection, sinus surgery, tonsillectomy Higher; up to 20 to 40% in major head and neck cancer surgery without prophylaxis Contaminated Fresh trauma, major break in sterility, acute inflammation Open fractures into the mouth High Dirty Established infection or pus Neck abscess drainage Treat, not prophylaxis"],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s1-2","Who needs prophylaxis","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · Infection","Yes: major clean-contaminated head and neck surgery (opening the pharynx or larynx with or without a flap), implants (cochlear implants, bone-anchored devices), and many clean operations with risk factors. Usually no: tonsillectomy, adenoidectomy, routine sinus surgery, septoplasty, uncomplicated thyroid and parotid surgery, grommets, ear surgery without implants. Follow local policy, which reflects local bacteria and resistance."],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s1-3","How to give it","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · Infection","Timing: intravenously within 60 minutes before the incision, so tissue levels are high when the wound is opened. Duration: a single dose for most operations; no more than 24 hours for major head and neck surgery. Longer courses add resistance and Clostridioides difficile, not protection. Repeat the dose in long operations (for example, cefuroxime every 4 hours) or with major blood loss. Choice for clean-contaminated head and neck surgery: co-amoxiclav, or cefuroxime or cefazolin with metronidazole, to cover mouth anaerobes and streptococci; follow local policy for penicillin allergy. Clindamycin in free flap surgery Patients given clindamycin for \"penicillin allergy\" in head and neck free flap surgery have higher wound infection rates. Check whether the allergy is real; most patients labelled penicillin-allergic are not, and a cephalosporin is usually safe unless the reaction was severe."],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s1-4","Other ways to prevent infection","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · Infection","Skin preparation with alcohol-based chlorhexidine where safe, clipping not shaving, normal body temperature, glucose control, oxygen, stopping smoking, mouth care and chlorhexidine mouthwash before oral cancer surgery, and careful handling of tissue."],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s2-1","Assessing the risk","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · Thrombosis","Assess every patient on admission and again if things change. Risk rises with major surgery over 90 minutes, cancer and chemotherapy, age over 60, obesity, previous or family VTE, immobility, oestrogens, pregnancy and dehydration; the Caprini score is widely used in surgery. Bleeding risk is assessed at the same time."],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s2-2","Who needs what","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · Thrombosis","A practical approach Patient Prophylaxis Short ENT procedure, mobile the same day, low risk Early mobilisation and hydration; no drugs Intermediate surgery with risk factors Mechanical (intermittent pneumatic compression) during surgery; consider low molecular weight heparin Major head and neck cancer surgery, free flaps Intermittent pneumatic compression plus low molecular weight heparin (started 6 to 12 hours after surgery when bleeding allows), continued until mobile Anti-embolism stockings are no longer used routinely in addition to heparin; they can damage skin in patients with arterial disease. Senior's pearl Weigh the clot against the bleed. A neck haematoma after a thyroidectomy can kill quickly, while VTE after a day-case thyroidectomy is rare; the opposite balance applies to a 10-hour laryngectomy with a free flap."],["ev","th:b-prophylaxis~s3-1","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis: current evidence","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · Evidence","A Cochrane review of ten trials (1,035 participants) found antibiotics after tonsillectomy did not reduce pain, analgesic use or secondary haemorrhage, though they reduced fever (Dhiwakar et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2012). In free flap reconstruction, patients given clindamycin had significantly more surgical site infections than those given other regimens (Pool et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2016). A meta-analysis of 618,264 otolaryngology patients found VTE in 0.4% overall; chemoprophylaxis did not reduce VTE in lower-risk patients but increased bleeding (odds ratio 3.78), while cancer and free flap patients had a much higher risk (odds ratio 6.28) (Moubayed et al., Head Neck 2017). The GAPS trial showed stockings added nothing to heparin in elective surgery (Shalhoub et al., BMJ 2020)."],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s4-1","Viva essentials","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Wound classes with ENT examples. Which ENT operations need antibiotic prophylaxis, the timing and duration. Penicillin allergy and clindamycin. VTE risk assessment and prophylaxis for head and neck cancer surgery."],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s4-2","Skills to sign off","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · For the examination","Skills to sign off Classify the wound for any ENT operation Prescribe prophylactic antibiotics with correct timing and duration Complete a VTE risk assessment and prescribe prophylaxis Plan antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis for a free flap reconstruction"],["bsec","th:b-prophylaxis~s4-3","Common pitfalls","Antibiotic and VTE prophylaxis · For the examination","Common pitfalls Routine antibiotics after tonsillectomy or septoplasty. Antibiotic given after the incision, or continued for a week. Clindamycin for an unverified penicillin allergy. Heparin given too early after neck surgery."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s1-1","Before you scrub","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · Hands","Remove rings, watches and bracelets; nails short, no varnish or artificial nails. Put on a theatre cap covering all hair and a mask covering nose and mouth; eye protection for anything that splashes (drilling, tonsils, neck). Open your gown and gloves (or ask the circulating practitioner to) onto a clean surface before you start. Check your hands for cuts or infection: do not scrub with an infected lesion."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s1-2","The surgical scrub","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · Hands","The antiseptic scrub Wash hands and forearms with the antiseptic (chlorhexidine 4% or povidone-iodine 7.5%) and clean under each nail with a nail pick under running water at the first scrub of the day. Apply antiseptic and scrub each side of each finger, between the fingers, the palm and back of the hand, then the forearm to just below the elbow, for the time the product specifies (usually 2 to 5 minutes in total). Keep the hands above the elbows throughout so water runs off the elbows, not back onto the hands. Rinse from fingertips to elbows, one direction only, and turn off the tap with the elbow. Dry with a sterile towel: one end for one hand and forearm, moving from fingers to elbow, the other end for the other side. Brushes damage skin and are not needed for the skin itself; use a sponge or pick only for the nails. Alcohol-based surgical hand rubs are an equal alternative: wash with plain soap at the start of the day, then apply the rub to dry hands and forearms for the time stated by the manufacturer, and let it dry completely before gloving. Figure 1 Scrubbing with povidone-iodine: fingers, web spaces, palms and backs of the hands, then the forearms, with the hands kept above the elbows. Master Sgt. Mallory Chenzira, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s1-3","Gowning and gloving","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · Hands","Gown and closed gloving Pick up the folded gown by its inside, step back from the trolley, and let it unfold with the inside towards you. Slide your arms into the sleeves without pushing your hands out of the cuffs; the circulating practitioner ties the back. With your hands still inside the sleeves, pick up the first glove through the sleeve, lay it palm to palm on the opposite cuff, and pull it over the cuff as you push the hand through. Glove the other hand the same way, then pull the gloves up over the cuffs. Hand the tag of the waist tie to a scrubbed colleague (or the circulator using the tag card), turn, and tie it at the front. Many surgeons now wear two pairs of gloves (an indicator pair underneath shows a coloured spot when the outer glove is punctured). Change the outer gloves if they are punctured, after draping, and before handling implants. Figure 2 Once gowned, only the front from chest to table level, the sleeves and the gloved hands are sterile. Drawn for this app Contaminated? Say so and change If a glove touches anything non-sterile, or you are unsure, say so and change it. Nobody minds a changed glove; everybody minds an infected wound."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s2-1","Skin preparation","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · The field","Prepare from the incision outwards in widening circles or strips, covering any area that might become part of the field (including a possible extension of the incision or a drain site), and allow the solution to dry before draping. Skin preparation in the head and neck Site Usual choice Why Neck, scalp, trunk, donor sites Chlorhexidine 2% in alcohol (or povidone-iodine in alcohol) Lowest wound infection rates; must dry fully before diathermy Near the eyes, the face Aqueous povidone-iodine 10% Chlorhexidine and alcohol injure the cornea Ear, especially with a perforation or an open middle ear Aqueous povidone-iodine 10% Chlorhexidine is ototoxic if it reaches the middle and inner ear Mouth, nose and mucosa Often none, or dilute aqueous povidone-iodine Mucosa tolerates only aqueous agents Alcohol and fire Alcohol-based preparations that pool under drapes, in the hair or in the ear can ignite with diathermy or a laser. Use the smallest volume, avoid pooling, and wait until it is fully dry (usually at least 3 minutes, longer in hair)."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s2-2","Hair","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · The field","Do not remove hair routinely. If it is in the way, clip it (never shave with a razor, which raises infection rates) on the day of surgery. For ear surgery, a strip of clipped hair behind the ear is usually enough; tape or gel the rest back."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s2-3","Protecting the eyes","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · The field","Close the eyelids with tape (and lubricant if the operation is long), and cover them with pads if the face is not part of the field. In endoscopic sinus surgery, leave the eyes taped but visible through the drapes so that the orbit can be watched for proptosis or bruising and the eye can be ballotted."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s2-4","Draping","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · The field","Drape so that only the field needed is exposed, with the drapes fixed so they do not slip during the operation. Head drape (turban): a towel wrapped around the head and secured, often under the patient's head with a split sheet over the body. Ear: head turned away, an adhesive drape over the ear and the hair. Nose and sinuses: the face from the brow to the upper lip exposed, eyes visible. Neck: from the chin and mandible to the clavicles; leave the face visible under a clear drape when the facial nerve is monitored (parotid surgery) so you can see twitching. Flaps: prepare and drape the donor site (forearm, thigh) at the same time. Senior's pearl Think about everything you might need before you drape: a possible tracheostomy, a neck incision extended to the mastoid, a fat graft from the abdomen, a cartilage graft from the ear. Re-preparing a patient half-way through is slow and risks the field."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s3-1","Rules of the sterile field","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · Keeping it sterile","Sterile touches only sterile; when in doubt, it is contaminated. A sterile person faces the sterile field, and two scrubbed people pass each other back to back or front to front. Do not reach over a sterile field if you are unscrubbed, or behind your back if you are scrubbed. The top of the instrument trolley and the drapes at table level are sterile; anything that falls below table level is not. A wet drape lets bacteria through (strike-through): cover it with a dry one. Hands stay in front of you, above the waist, and not folded under the arms."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s3-2","Taking off the gown","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · Keeping it sterile","Untie the gown, pull it off from the shoulders inside out, then remove gloves with the glove-to-glove and skin-to-skin technique so the dirty outer surface never touches your skin. Clean your hands afterwards."],["ev","th:b-scrub~s4-1","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field: current evidence","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · Evidence","A Cochrane review found no clear difference in wound infection between alcohol-based hand rubs and aqueous scrubs, and that the duration and products matter less than doing it properly (Tanner et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2016). Skin preparation with chlorhexidine-alcohol reduced wound infection compared with aqueous povidone-iodine (9.5% vs 16.1%) in clean-contaminated surgery (Darouiche et al., N Engl J Med 2010), and the WHO recommends alcohol-based chlorhexidine for skin preparation. A systematic review of chlorhexidine on the head and neck found 14 cases of sensorineural hearing loss from chlorhexidine reaching the ear and 38 cases of eye injury, almost all under general anaesthesia (Shive et al., Dermatol Surg 2021). Double gloving reduced glove perforations (rate ratio 0.29) and blood on the skin (0.35) without affecting dexterity (Mischke et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2014)."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s5-1","Viva essentials","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Steps of the surgical scrub and closed gloving. What is sterile on a gowned person. Skin preparation choices near the eye and ear, and why. Fire risk with alcohol preparations. Hair removal: clip, don't shave."],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · For the examination","Skills to sign off Perform a surgical scrub with antiseptic and with an alcohol rub Gown and glove using the closed technique without help Prepare the skin for ear, nose and neck surgery with the right agent Drape for ear, sinus and neck surgery Recognise contamination and correct it without being told"],["bsec","th:b-scrub~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Scrubbing, gowning, gloving and the sterile field · For the examination","Common pitfalls Chlorhexidine in an open ear or near the eye. Draping before alcohol preparation has dried. Folding arms into the armpits while waiting. Draping the eyes out of view in sinus surgery."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s1-1","The three stages","Building surgical skill · How skill is learned","Cognitive: you understand the steps and think about each movement; slow, error-prone, exhausting. Associative: the steps link up; fewer errors, but you still need attention for each part. Autonomous: the movement runs without conscious thought, so your attention is free for the anatomy, the bleeding and the team. The aim of practice outside theatre is to reach the autonomous stage for basic tasks (knots, instrument handling, drilling strokes, holding a scope) before you are asked to do them on a patient."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s1-2","Deliberate practice","Building surgical skill · How skill is learned","Break the operation into tasks and practise the one you are worst at, not the one you enjoy. Set a specific goal for each session (\"ten square one-handed ties with the left hand under 60 seconds\"). Get feedback: from a trainer, a video, a simulator score, or a checklist. Practise a little often rather than a lot rarely."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s2-1","The skills you can build outside theatre","Building surgical skill · Where to practise","Simulation in ENT Skill How to practise Knots and suturing Knot board, suture pads, pigs' feet; time yourself Microscope and microsurgery Practising under the microscope with grains of rice, folding paper, cutting a grape skin; microvascular courses on chicken thigh vessels Temporal bone drilling Cadaveric temporal bone lab, 3D-printed bones, virtual reality simulators Endoscopic sinus surgery Sinus models, cadaver heads, sheep heads; scope handling with a box trainer Airway Airway mannequins for rigid bronchoscopy and foreign bodies; front-of-neck access kits Flaps Pig's feet and skin models for local flaps Teamwork and crises High-fidelity simulation of airway fire, carotid blowout, can't intubate can't oxygenate"],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s2-2","In the temporal bone lab","Building surgical skill · Where to practise","Dissect with a plan: cortical mastoidectomy, then posterior tympanotomy, labyrinthectomy and facial nerve decompression, identifying each landmark in order. Drill one bone fully rather than many bones partly, and dissect the same steps you will be asked to do next in theatre."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s2-3","Mental rehearsal","Building surgical skill · Where to practise","The night before an operation, close your eyes and walk through it step by step: the position, the incision, each landmark, the dangers and what you will do if they happen. Surgeons and athletes who rehearse mentally perform better and more calmly. This app's Theatre section (\"Scrub in\" and \"What comes next?\") is built for this."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s2-4","Using video","Building surgical skill · Where to practise","Watch expert videos of the operation before the case, and record your own (with consent and according to local policy) to watch afterwards with a trainer. Seeing your own wasted movements is the fastest way to remove them."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s3-1","Before, during and after","Building surgical skill · In theatre","One case, three conversations Before: read the chapter, rehearse it, and agree with your trainer which part you will do and what you want to learn. During: ask for the step you agreed, say what you are going to do before you do it, and accept being stopped. After: ask for one thing you did well and one to improve, record it, and log the case with your role. Senior's pearl The best trainees prepare so thoroughly that the trainer only has to watch. Know the steps, the instruments and the dangers before you ask for the knife."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s3-2","Learning curves","Building surgical skill · In theatre","Every operation has a learning curve: complication rates and operating time fall as the number of cases rises, then plateau. Recognise where you are on the curve for each operation, and stay supervised until you are past its steep part."],["ev","th:b-skilllab~s4-1","Building surgical skill: current evidence","Building surgical skill · Evidence","A meta-analysis found that virtual reality temporal bone simulation improved trainees' mastoidectomy performance (standardised mean difference 0.87) (Lui et al., Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2017). A systematic review identified 64 otolaryngology simulators (temporal bone, larynx and pharynx, sinus surgery), most with some validation but few with evidence of transfer to patient outcomes (Musbahi et al., J Surg Educ 2017). In another specialty, video-rated technical skill strongly predicted complication rates, supporting deliberate skills training (Birkmeyer et al., N Engl J Med 2013)."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s5-1","Viva essentials","Building surgical skill · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Stages of motor learning and deliberate practice. Simulation options in ENT and their evidence. Learning curves and supervision. How to get feedback in theatre."],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Building surgical skill · For the examination","Skills to sign off Tie ten one-handed square knots with each hand in under a minute Complete a cortical mastoidectomy on a temporal bone with all landmarks Complete a sinus dissection on a model or cadaver Rehearse each operation mentally before the list Review a video of my own operation with a trainer"],["bsec","th:b-skilllab~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Building surgical skill · For the examination","Common pitfalls Practising only what you are already good at. Arriving in theatre without having read or rehearsed the operation. No feedback after the case. Logging cases without reflecting on them."],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s1-1","The properties that matter","Sutures and needles · The material","Absorbable or non-absorbable: absorbable sutures lose strength and are broken down (by hydrolysis for synthetic materials); non-absorbable ones stay unless removed. Strength over time: how long the suture holds the wound, which must match how fast that tissue heals. Monofilament or braided: monofilaments slide easily, harbour fewer bacteria and cause less reaction but have \"memory\" and need more throws; braided sutures handle and knot easily but wick bacteria. Tissue reaction: natural materials (silk, catgut) cause more inflammation than synthetic ones. Size: in the USP system, the more zeros the finer: 2-0 is thicker than 4-0, which is thicker than 6-0."],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s1-2","The sutures you will use","Sutures and needles · The material","Common sutures Suture Type Strength and absorption Typical ENT uses Polyglactin 910 (Vicryl) Absorbable, braided About 50% strength at 3 weeks; absorbed in about 2 months Ties, deep layers, platysma, mucosa Rapid polyglactin (Vicryl Rapide) Absorbable, braided About 50% at 5 days; gone in about 6 weeks Mucosa, tonsil and septal quilting, children's skin Poliglecaprone 25 (Monocryl) Absorbable, monofilament Most strength lost by 2 to 3 weeks; absorbed in about 3 to 4 months Subcuticular skin closure Polydioxanone (PDS) Absorbable, monofilament Holds for 4 to 6 weeks; absorbed in about 6 months Tension-bearing deep layers, cartilage, pharyngeal repair, tracheal anastomosis Polypropylene (Prolene) Non-absorbable, monofilament Permanent Skin, vessels, pull-out subcuticular closure Nylon (Ethilon) Non-absorbable, monofilament Permanent, slowly weakens Facial skin, microvascular anastomosis (8-0 to 10-0) Silk Non-absorbable, braided Permanent but loses strength; high reaction Securing drains and tracheostomy tubes, ties Senior's pearl Match the suture to the tissue's healing, not to habit. Mucosa heals in days, so a rapidly absorbed suture is enough; fascia and cartilage take weeks, so they need something that holds for weeks. Skin needs the finest suture that will hold the edges."],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s2-1","Needle types","Sutures and needles · The needle","Figure 1 Needle cross-sections and curves, and how to load the needle holder. Drawn for this app Round-bodied (taper): pushes tissue aside; for mucosa, vessels, fascia and anything that tears. Reverse cutting: the third cutting edge is on the outer curve, so the needle track does not cut towards the wound edge; the standard skin needle. Conventional cutting: for tough tissue such as cartilage and periosteum. Curvature: 3/8 circle for skin, 1/2 circle for most deeper work, 5/8 circle for deep or confined spaces such as the oral cavity and pharynx."],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s2-2","Loading and passing","Sutures and needles · The needle","Grip the needle about a third of the way from the swaged end with the tips of the needle holder, at a right angle to the jaws. Enter the tissue at 90 degrees and follow the curve of the needle with your wrist, rather than pushing it straight. Hand needles back to the scrub practitioner on the needle holder, never loose."],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s3-1","Sutures by site","Sutures and needles · The choice","A practical guide Site Deep layer Skin or surface Removal Face 5-0 absorbable (Monocryl or Vicryl) 6-0 nylon or polypropylene, or 5-0 to 6-0 rapid absorbable 5 days Neck 3-0 or 4-0 Vicryl to platysma 4-0 or 5-0 subcuticular Monocryl, or staples or clips 5 to 7 days if non-absorbable Scalp 2-0 or 3-0 Vicryl to galea Staples or 3-0 non-absorbable 7 to 10 days Ear (postauricular) 4-0 Vicryl 4-0 to 5-0 absorbable or non-absorbable 7 days Oral and pharyngeal mucosa 3-0 Vicryl or PDS (pharynx) 3-0 or 4-0 rapid absorbable Not removed Septum and nasal mucosa 4-0 rapid absorbable quilting Not removed Vessels 2-0 or 3-0 ties; 8-0 to 10-0 nylon for microvascular anastomosis Not removed Drains and tracheostomy tubes Silk or nylon to the skin When the drain comes out Infection and the suture A braided suture in a contaminated field (the mouth, an infected neck) carries bacteria into the depth of the wound. Prefer monofilaments where infection matters, and do not leave unnecessary buried sutures."],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s3-2","Alternatives to sutures","Sutures and needles · The choice","Staples are quick on the scalp and neck. Skin adhesive (cyanoacrylate glue) and adhesive strips close small, low-tension wounds in children and support subcuticular closures. Triclosan-coated sutures reduce wound infection."],["ev","th:b-sutures~s4-1","Sutures and needles: current evidence","Sutures and needles · Evidence","For facial wounds, a meta-analysis found no difference between absorbable and non-absorbable skin sutures in cosmetic results, infection, dehiscence or stitch marks (Gillanders et al., J Plast Reconstr Aesthet Surg 2018). A meta-analysis of 25 randomised trials (11,957 patients) found triclosan-coated sutures reduced surgical site infection (relative risk 0.73) in both clean and contaminated surgery (Ahmed et al., BMJ Open 2019), and the WHO recommends them."],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s5-1","Viva essentials","Sutures and needles · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Classification of sutures: absorbable and non-absorbable, monofilament and braided. Strength over time of the common sutures. Needle types and when to use them. Choice and removal times for the face, neck and scalp."],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Sutures and needles · For the examination","Skills to sign off Name and choose the right suture for skin, mucosa, deep layers and ties Load and pass a needle correctly and return it safely Choose sutures for a flap inset and a pharyngeal repair"],["bsec","th:b-sutures~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Sutures and needles · For the examination","Common pitfalls Thick sutures on the face. Braided sutures in an infected wound. Leaving facial skin sutures longer than 5 days. Pushing a curved needle straight."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s1-1","The BID model","Teaching and assessing · Teaching in theatre","Brief, teach, debrief Brief (before the case, 2 minutes): what does the trainee want to learn? Which part will they do? What should they watch out for? Intraoperative teaching: teach to the learning goal; let them do as much as is safe; talk through key steps, landmarks and decisions. Debrief (after the case, 2 minutes): one thing done well, one thing to work on, and a specific action for next time; record it."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s1-2","Levels of supervision","Teaching and assessing · Teaching in theatre","The Zwisch scale describes how much the trainer does: Show and tell: the trainer operates and explains. Active help: the trainee operates with the trainer leading, guiding each step. Passive help: the trainee leads; the trainer assists and helps only when needed. Supervision only: the trainee operates independently; the trainer watches or is nearby. Decide the level for each part of each case, and move the trainee up as they show they can. Senior's pearl Say how you will take over before the case: \"If I say stop, stop, and we'll swap.\" The trainee will operate more calmly if the rules are clear, and you will step in earlier and more gently."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s1-3","Taking over","Teaching and assessing · Teaching in theatre","Take over when patient safety needs it, not because the trainee is slower than you. Do it calmly, explain why afterwards, and give the case back when the difficult part is past."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s2-1","Good feedback","Teaching and assessing · Feedback","Timely: straight after, while it is fresh. Specific: \"Your bites in the platysma were uneven on the left,\" not \"fine\". Balanced: what went well and what to improve. Actionable: one or two clear things to try next time. Two-way: ask the trainee first how they think it went."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s2-2","Asking for feedback","Teaching and assessing · Feedback","As a trainee, ask for it: \"Can you tell me one thing I could do better in that closure?\" Specific questions get useful answers."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s3-1","Workplace-based assessments","Teaching and assessing · Assessment","Common tools Tool What it assesses Procedure-based assessment (PBA) Performance of a whole operation, rated by level of independence Direct observation of procedural skills (DOPS) Smaller procedures (nasendoscopy, packing, ear microsuction) Case-based discussion (CBD) Clinical reasoning from a case the trainee managed Clinical evaluation exercise (CEX) Clinical encounters such as history and examination Multi-source feedback (MSF) Team-working and professional behaviour, from colleagues Logbook Volume and level of involvement in operations Assessments are tools for learning as much as for judgement. Fill them in honestly; a falsely good assessment harms the trainee and future patients."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s3-2","The trainee in difficulty","Teaching and assessing · Assessment","Look for the cause (knowledge, skills, attitude, health, personal life, the training environment), talk early and privately, set clear goals with support, document, and involve the training programme director. Most trainees in difficulty recover with timely help."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s4-1","Everyday teaching","Teaching and assessing · Teaching outside theatre","Use real patients: one learning point per patient in clinic, a short teaching round on the ward, a five-minute \"what would you do?\" during on-call. The one-minute preceptor approach is quick: get a commitment, probe the reasoning, teach a general rule, praise what was right, correct mistakes."],["ev","th:b-teach~s5-1","Teaching and assessing: current evidence","Teaching and assessing · Evidence","A systematic review of 34 studies of intraoperative teaching found that effective teaching spans three phases (before, during and after the case), that trainers often rate their own teaching more highly than trainees do, and that training faculty in teaching techniques improved trainee ratings (Timberlake et al., Ann Surg 2017). The Zwisch scale, which grades how much autonomy the trainee is given in theatre, was shown to be reliable, valid and feasible (George et al., J Surg Educ 2014)."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s6-1","Viva essentials","Teaching and assessing · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for BID model and levels of supervision. Feedback principles. Workplace-based assessments. The trainee in difficulty."],["bsec","th:b-teach~s6-2","Skills to sign off","Teaching and assessing · For the examination","Skills to sign off Ask for specific feedback after every case Teach a medical student in clinic Brief and debrief a junior trainee for a case Supervise a junior through an operation at the right level of help Complete honest workplace-based assessments Support a trainee in difficulty"],["bsec","th:b-teach~s6-3","Common pitfalls","Teaching and assessing · For the examination","Common pitfalls Taking over too early, or too late. Vague feedback (\"good job\"). Assessments filled in without observation. No brief or debrief."],["bsec","th:b-team~s1-1","The theatre team","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · The people","Roles in an ENT theatre Role What they do What they need from you Operating surgeon Leads the operation and the team brief; marks the site; decides the plan A clear account of the patient, consent and imaging Assistant Retracts, suctions, cuts sutures, holds the camera; learns To be told what you want before you need it Anaesthetist Anaesthesia, airway, analgesia, fluids; co-leads the brief The plan for the airway, positioning, bleeding and duration Anaesthetic practitioner (ODP or nurse) Helps with induction, airway equipment and monitoring Notice of special tubes, throat packs, nerve monitoring Scrub practitioner Prepares and controls the sterile instruments; counts Instruments named correctly, sharps handed back safely Circulating practitioner Unscrubbed; opens sterile items, records counts, fetches Early warning of what extra kit you will need Recovery staff Wake-up and early post-operative care A handover: operation, packs, drains, analgesia, concerns Introduce yourself to every member by name and role at the start of the day. In many units names are written on a whiteboard; use them. A team that knows each other's names speaks up more readily when something is wrong."],["bsec","th:b-team~s1-2","Theatre etiquette","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · The people","Before the list: read the list, know each patient, check consent forms, imaging, blood results and that the site is marked. Arrive early and help with positioning; leaving the patient's transfer to others is a bad habit. In the room: keep conversation quiet and relevant during critical steps, phone silent, and never reach over the sterile field. Ask the scrub practitioner before helping yourself to anything on the trolley, and say \"sharp\" when you pass back a blade or needle. Never leave without handing over and writing the operation note."],["bsec","th:b-team~s1-3","The layout for head and neck surgery","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · The people","For most ENT operations the operating table is turned 90 to 180 degrees so the patient's head is away from the anaesthetic machine, with a long breathing circuit. The surgeon sits or stands at the head, the screen or microscope sits in the surgeon's line of sight, and the scrub practitioner stands on the side of the operating hand. This takes the anaesthetist away from the airway, which is why airway checks and tube security at the start matter more in ENT than in most specialties. Figure 1 A typical layout for head and neck surgery. The anaesthetist is away from the head, so the airway must be secure before the drapes go on. Drawn for this app Figure 2 Before the list: the anaesthetist prepares lines while the scrub practitioner opens a sterile set. Master Sgt. Mallory Chenzira, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"],["bsec","th:b-team~s2-1","The team brief","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · The safety system","A short meeting of the whole team at the start of the list, led by the lead surgeon and lead anaesthetist. For each patient: the plan, the side, the airway, any allergies, anticoagulants, special equipment (microscope, navigation, nerve monitor, laser), implants, expected blood loss and length, and the post-operative destination. Everyone states their name and role and is invited to raise concerns."],["bsec","th:b-team~s2-2","The WHO surgical safety checklist","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · The safety system","The three pauses Pause When Key items Sign in Before induction Identity, procedure, site and consent confirmed with the patient; site marked; anaesthetic machine and drugs checked; allergies; airway and aspiration risk; expected blood loss Time out Before the incision Team introduced; patient, procedure and site confirmed aloud; antibiotics given in the last 60 minutes; critical steps and anticipated problems from surgeon, anaesthetist and scrub practitioner; imaging displayed Sign out Before anyone leaves the room Procedure recorded; instrument, swab and needle counts correct; specimens labelled; equipment problems; key concerns for recovery Senior's pearl The checklist works only if people mean it. Stop, face each other, read it aloud, and make it safe for the most junior person to say \"wait\". A rushed time out that nobody listens to is worse than none, because it gives false reassurance."],["bsec","th:b-team~s2-3","Site marking and laterality","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · The safety system","The operating surgeon (or a delegated member of the team who will be present) marks the site with an indelible arrow before the patient leaves the ward, with the patient awake and agreeing, and checks it against the consent form and imaging. Ears, parotids, neck dissections, thyroid lobes, sinus surgery on one side and tympanostomy tubes in one ear all carry a real risk of wrong-side surgery. Mark the side even for midline operations that are done on one side of the nose. Check the side three times Wrong-site surgery is a never event. Check the side against the consent form, the clinic letter and the imaging or audiogram, not against memory or the list alone. If the mark has washed off under the drapes, stop and re-check before you start."],["bsec","th:b-team~s2-4","Counts, packs and items left behind","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · The safety system","The scrub and circulating practitioners count swabs, needles, blades and instruments before the start, before closing a cavity or the skin, and at the end; the count is recorded on a board. ENT has its own hazards: nasal packs, patties and neuro-patties in the sinuses and skull base, ear wicks, gauze in the tonsil fossa, and throat packs. If a count is wrong, stop closing, search the field and the floor, and get an X-ray if the item is not found. Throat packs are no longer routine. Use one only for a reason (for example, heavy oral bleeding). When one is used, record it with at least one visual reminder (a label on the patient or the tube) and one documentary check (the count board), and confirm its removal at sign out."],["bsec","th:b-team~s2-5","The debrief","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · The safety system","At the end of the list, two minutes: what went well, what did not, equipment problems, and who will act on them. This is how a team learns."],["bsec","th:b-team~s3-1","Speaking up","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · When it goes wrong","If you see a risk (a contaminated instrument, a wrong side, a drug about to be given that the patient is allergic to), say so clearly, using the person's name: \" Mr Ahmed, I'm concerned: the consent says left ear \". Graded assertiveness escalates from a question to a concern to a firm \"stop\" if you are not heard. Count discrepancy at closure 1 Act stop closing tell the surgeon and team search field, drapes, bins and floor 2 Not found intra-operative X-ray record and report"],["ev","th:b-team~s4-1","The theatre team and the WHO checklist: current evidence","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · Evidence","In the original eight-hospital WHO study, introducing the checklist cut major complications from 11.0% to 7.0% and in-hospital deaths from 1.5% to 0.8% (Haynes et al., N Engl J Med 2009). A meta-analysis found reductions in any complication (risk ratio 0.59), surgical site infection (0.57) and mortality (0.77), with the greatest benefit where adherence was high (Bergs et al., Br J Surg 2014). In 44,814 patients in 27 countries, checklist use was associated with lower mortality (odds ratio 0.49), although this may partly reflect better hospitals (Abbott et al., Br J Anaesth 2018). A systematic review by the Difficult Airway Society, BAOMS and ENT UK found no good evidence for routine throat packs and recommended using them only for a specific reason, with visual and documentary checks (Athanassoglou et al., Anaesthesia 2018)."],["bsec","th:b-team~s5-1","Viva essentials","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for The three pauses of the WHO checklist and what is checked at each. Who marks the site and when. Counts: when, by whom, and what to do if one is wrong. Throat pack policy. Never events relevant to ENT."],["bsec","th:b-team~s5-2","Skills to sign off","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · For the examination","Skills to sign off Introduce myself and learn every team member's name and role Check consent, marking and imaging for every patient before the list Mark an operative site correctly Take part in sign in, time out and sign out Lead the team brief and time out Lead a debrief and act on what it finds"],["bsec","th:b-team~s5-3","Common pitfalls","The theatre team and the WHO checklist · For the examination","Common pitfalls Treating the time out as a formality. Marking the side from the theatre list instead of the notes and imaging. Forgetting the throat pack or nasal patties at the end. Not speaking up because you are junior."],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s1-1","The phases","Wound healing and scars · How wounds heal","Figure 1 The phases of wound healing and the strength of the scar over time. Drawn for this app Haemostasis: platelets and fibrin seal the wound within minutes and release growth factors. Inflammation (days 0 to 5): neutrophils clear bacteria; macrophages, the key cells, clear debris and drive the next phase. Proliferation (day 3 to week 3): fibroblasts lay down collagen (type III first), new vessels form granulation tissue, epithelium covers the surface (within 48 hours in a sutured wound), and myofibroblasts contract the wound. Remodelling (week 3 to a year or more): type III collagen is replaced by stronger type I, cross-linked along lines of stress; the scar flattens and fades. A wound has only about 20% of its final strength at 3 weeks, and never regains more than about 80% of the strength of normal skin."],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s1-2","Primary, secondary and delayed closure","Wound healing and scars · How wounds heal","Primary intention: edges brought together (most surgical wounds); quick, small scar. Secondary intention: left open to granulate and contract (infected cavities, some concave facial defects such as the medial canthus and conchal bowl, which heal well this way). Delayed primary closure: left open for a few days (contaminated wounds, bites) then closed."],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s1-3","Mucosa heals differently","Wound healing and scars · How wounds heal","Oral and nasal mucosa heal faster and with less scarring than skin, which is why rapidly absorbed sutures are enough there. But the mouth is contaminated, and saliva in a neck wound after pharyngeal surgery means a fistula until proven otherwise."],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s2-1","Factors that impair healing","Wound healing and scars · What gets in the way","Factors that impair healing Local General Infection Smoking Haematoma and seroma Diabetes Tension Corticosteroids and immunosuppression Poor blood supply, tight sutures Malnutrition, low albumin, vitamin C and zinc deficiency Previous radiotherapy Age Foreign bodies, dead tissue Anaemia, jaundice, uraemia Movement Chemotherapy The irradiated neck Radiotherapy causes progressive small-vessel damage (endarteritis), fibrosis and low oxygen in the tissue for life. Wounds heal slowly, flaps fail, fistulas form after pharyngeal surgery, and the carotid may be exposed if the skin breaks down. Plan incisions carefully, bring in vascularised tissue (a pectoralis major or free flap) and expect complications."],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s3-1","Hypertrophic scars and keloids","Wound healing and scars · Scars","Two kinds of raised scar Feature Hypertrophic scar Keloid Extent Stays within the wound Grows beyond the wound into normal skin Timing Weeks after injury Months, may keep growing Course Often improves over 1 to 2 years Rarely regresses; recurs after excision alone Sites Across tension lines, over joints Earlobes (piercing), jaw line, chest, shoulders Who Anyone Darker skin, family history, aged 10 to 30"],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s3-2","Managing scars","Wound healing and scars · Scars","Prevention: tension-free closure in skin creases, eversion, early suture removal on the face, sun protection for a year, silicone gel or sheets, massage after 2 to 3 weeks. Hypertrophic scars: silicone, pressure, intralesional triamcinolone; revision if still poor after a year. Keloids: intralesional triamcinolone (10 to 40 mg/ml every 4 to 6 weeks), excision (intramarginal, leaving a rim of keloid) combined with steroid injection or post-operative radiotherapy, pressure earrings for earlobe keloids, cryotherapy, 5-fluorouracil. Senior's pearl Never excise a keloid without a plan to stop it coming back. Excision alone recurs in most cases and the new keloid is often bigger."],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s3-3","Removing sutures","Wound healing and scars · Scars","Face 5 days, neck 5 to 7 days, scalp 7 to 10 days, and longer in irradiated tissue, steroids or tension. Support the wound with adhesive strips after removal."],["ev","th:b-wounds~s4-1","Wound healing and scars: current evidence","Wound healing and scars · Evidence","A meta-analysis of 140 cohort studies (479,150 patients) found smokers had more wound necrosis (odds ratio 3.60), delayed healing and dehiscence (2.07) and wound infection (1.79) than non-smokers; stopping smoking before surgery reduced wound infection in randomised trials (odds ratio 0.43) (Sørensen, Arch Surg 2012). For ear keloids, a meta-analysis of 25 studies found recurrence after excision of about 15% with triamcinolone, similar to excision with radiotherapy (Shin et al., Plast Reconstr Surg 2016). A Cochrane review found silicone gel sheeting may help prevent abnormal scarring in high-risk patients, but the evidence is of poor quality (O'Brien et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2013)."],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s5-1","Viva essentials","Wound healing and scars · For the examination","What the examiner is looking for Phases of healing and the cells involved. Factors affecting healing, especially radiotherapy and smoking. Hypertrophic scar versus keloid. Management of a keloid of the earlobe. Suture removal times."],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s5-2","Skills to sign off","Wound healing and scars · For the examination","Skills to sign off Remove sutures at the right time and support the wound Assess a wound for infection, dehiscence or haematoma Inject a keloid with triamcinolone Plan excision and adjuvant treatment of an earlobe keloid"],["bsec","th:b-wounds~s5-3","Common pitfalls","Wound healing and scars · For the examination","Common pitfalls Excising a keloid without adjuvant treatment. Ignoring smoking before elective surgery. Assuming an irradiated neck will heal like a normal one. Leaving facial sutures too long."]];
